{"D286/pa_sim/sim":{"body":"<h2>Practice Assessment — 50-Question Simulation</h2><p>Fifty questions curated from every unit of this course for full-breadth coverage. Take it timed (75 minutes), closed-book, on your scratch space. Review every miss — each explanation names the exact rule tested.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 50 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D286/oa_sim/sim":{"body":"<h2>Objective Assessment — 50-Question Simulation</h2><p>Led by this course’s exam-calibrated final bank, filled to 50 with zero overlap against your PA. Timed, closed-book. Score 85%+ here and you are walking into the real OA over-prepared.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 841 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D286/hard_drill/sim":{"body":"<h2>🔥 Hard Drill</h2><p>A shuffled set of this course’s <strong>hardest</strong> questions only — predict-the-output, multi-step scenarios, and compute/trace items at OA difficulty. Use it to stress-test the topics that trip you up. Miss one? The explanation names the exact rule tested, and it’s auto-added to your flashcards.</p>"},"D286/weak_drill/sim":{"body":"<h2>🎯 Weak Spots</h2><p>A practice set drawn from the <strong>topics you score lowest on</strong> — computed live from your quiz history (any topic under 80% accuracy, weakest first). The more you practice, the more precisely this targets your gaps. No history in this course yet? It starts you on the hardest questions until it learns where you struggle.</p>"},"D286/review_missed/sim":{"body":"<h2>🔁 Review Missed</h2><p>Every question you’ve <strong>answered incorrectly</strong> in this course, gathered in one place so you can retry them (most-recent misses first, up to 30). Get one right here and you’ll see the explanation reinforce the exact rule tested. Nothing missed yet? It falls back to this course’s hardest questions.</p>"},"D286/uadv/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-generics"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Generics (types as parameters)</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — why &lt;String&gt; saves you</div><pre>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
names.add("Ana");
// names.add(42);          ← COMPILE error — caught before running
String first = names.get(0);   // no cast needed

// without generics (old style):
ArrayList raw = new ArrayList();
raw.add("Ana"); raw.add(42);            // both allowed!
String s = (String) raw.get(1);         // compiles… CRASHES at runtime</pre>Generics move type errors from RUNTIME to COMPILE TIME and remove casting. The &lt;&gt; &ldquo;diamond&rdquo; on the right lets the compiler infer the type parameter.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">GENERIC METHODS &amp; BOUNDS</div><code>static &lt;T&gt; T first(List&lt;T&gt; list)</code> works for ANY element type. Bounds constrain it: <code>&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> = &ldquo;any T that can compare itself&rdquo; — required to write a generic max/sort. Primitives can&rsquo;t be type arguments: use Integer, not int (autoboxing bridges the gap).</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ ArrayList&lt;int&gt; DOES NOT COMPILE</div>Type parameters must be reference types: ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;. Java auto-boxes int ↔ Integer on the way in and out, so usage still looks natural.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>The main benefit of ArrayList&lt;String&gt; over a raw ArrayList is:<ol type="A"><li>Type errors are caught at compile time</li><li>It runs faster</li><li>It can hold more items</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> The compiler enforces the element type — no bad adds, no casts, no runtime ClassCastException.</div></details></div></div><h3>Generics — type-safe collections &amp; methods</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>A generic is a <strong>type placeholder</strong>. Instead of writing a separate <code>Box</code> class for a String, an Integer, and a Dog, you write <code>Box&lt;T&gt;</code> once and let the caller fill in <code>T</code>. <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code> is just <code>ArrayList&lt;T&gt;</code> with <code>T</code> set to <code>String</code> — so the list can only hold Strings, and the compiler enforces it.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>The angle brackets after a type — <code>&lt;String&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Integer&gt;</code> — are the <strong>type argument</strong>. They give you two things: (1) the compiler <strong>rejects the wrong type at compile time</strong> instead of throwing a <code>ClassCastException</code> at runtime, and (2) you get elements back already typed, so <strong>no casting</strong> is needed.</p><p>Generics work only with <strong>reference types</strong>, never primitives: you write <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, not <code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code>. Autoboxing converts <code>int</code> ↔ <code>Integer</code> automatically.</p></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — with vs. without generics</div><table><tr><th>Raw (old, unsafe)</th><th>Generic (safe)</th></tr><tr><td><code>ArrayList list = new ArrayList();</code></td><td><code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>list.add("hi"); list.add(42);</code> — both allowed</td><td><code>list.add("hi");</code> ✓ &nbsp; <code>list.add(42);</code> ✗ compile error</td></tr><tr><td><code>String s = (String) list.get(0);</code> — cast needed</td><td><code>String s = list.get(0);</code> — no cast</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>You can write your <strong>own</strong> generic type. The <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> after the class name declares the placeholder:</p><p><code>class Box&lt;T&gt; { private T item; void set(T x){ item = x; } T get(){ return item; } }</code></p><p>A <strong>generic method</strong> declares its own placeholder before the return type: <code>public static &lt;T&gt; T first(T[] a){ return a[0]; }</code>. A <strong>bounded</strong> type restricts it: <code>&lt;T extends Number&gt;</code> means "T must be Number or a subclass," so you can safely call <code>.doubleValue()</code>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><code>&lt;T&gt;</code> is a <span class="key-term">type parameter</span>; <code>&lt;String&gt;</code> is a <span class="key-term">type argument</span>. <code>&lt;&gt;</code> on the right is the <span class="key-term">diamond operator</span> (Java infers the type). Generics give <span class="key-term">compile-time type safety</span> and remove casts. Common letters: <code>T</code> type, <code>E</code> element, <code>K</code>/<code>V</code> key/value.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON TRAP</div><p>You cannot use a primitive as a type argument (<code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code> won't compile — use <code>Integer</code>). And generics are checked by the compiler only; at runtime the type is "erased" (<em>type erasure</em>), which is why you can't write <code>new T()</code> or <code>new T[10]</code>.</p></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Why does <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code> not need a cast on <code>get()</code>?<ol type="A"><li>The compiler already knows every element is a String</li><li>ArrayList stores the class name with each element</li><li>Casting is done automatically at runtime</li><li>Strings never need casting</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> The type argument tells the compiler the element type, so <code>get()</code> returns <code>String</code> directly — no cast, and a wrong-type <code>add()</code> is caught at compile time.</div></details></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — generics — why the compiler, not the runtime, catches the bug</div><h3>The problem generics solve</h3><p>Before generics, a collection held <code>Object</code>, so anything could go in and every retrieval needed a cast. A wrong cast compiled fine and blew up at run time with <code>ClassCastException</code>. Generics move that failure to <strong>compile time</strong>: <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> refuses a non-String at the point you write it, and retrieval needs no cast.</p><h3>Type erasure</h3><p>Generics are enforced by the compiler and then <strong>erased</strong> — the bytecode has no type parameter. That explains several rules: you cannot create <code>new T[]</code>, you cannot test <code>x instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code>, and <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> are the same class at run time.</p><h3>Generic methods and bounds</h3><p>You can parameterize a single method: <code>&lt;T&gt; void printAll(List&lt;T&gt; items)</code>. A <strong>bounded</strong> parameter constrains it — <code>&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> guarantees the type supports <code>compareTo</code>, so a generic <code>max()</code> can compare safely.</p><h3>The inheritance trap</h3><p><code>List&lt;Dog&gt;</code> is <strong>not</strong> a subtype of <code>List&lt;Animal&gt;</code>, even though Dog is an Animal — generics are <em>invariant</em>. If it were allowed you could insert a Cat into a list of Dogs through the Animal reference. Wildcards restore flexibility safely: <code>&lt;? extends Animal&gt;</code> to read, <code>&lt;? super Dog&gt;</code> to write.</p></div>"},"D286/uadv/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-objcontract"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — equals(), hashCode() &amp; toString()</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — what breaks when you skip them</div><pre>public class Point {
    int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
}
Point a = new Point(1, 2), b = new Point(1, 2);
System.out.println(a.equals(b));   // false! (inherited == identity)
System.out.println(a);             // Point@6d06d69c (default toString)</pre>Without overrides, equals() means &ldquo;same object in memory&rdquo; and printing shows class@hash. Override them:<br><pre>@Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Point)) return false;
    Point p = (Point) o;
    return x == p.x &amp;&amp; y == p.y;
}
@Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(x, y); }
@Override public String toString() { return "(" + x + "," + y + ")"; }</pre>Now a.equals(b) is true and printing shows (1,2).</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ equals AND hashCode TRAVEL TOGETHER</div>Equal objects MUST have equal hash codes — override one without the other and HashSet/HashMap &ldquo;lose&rdquo; your objects (contains() returns false for an equal object). This pairing rule is the section&rsquo;s most-tested fact.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>If you override equals(), you must also override:<ol type="A"><li>hashCode()</li><li>toString()</li><li>main()</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — hashCode()</b>, so equal objects hash identically and hash-based collections behave.</div></details></div></div><h3>equals(), hashCode() &amp; toString() — the Object contract</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Every class in Java secretly <code>extends Object</code>, inheriting three methods worth overriding: <code>toString()</code> (how the object prints), <code>equals()</code> (when two objects count as "the same"), and <code>hashCode()</code> (a number used by hash-based collections). The defaults compare <em>memory addresses</em>, which is rarely what you want.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>toString()</strong> — the default prints something like <code>Dog@1b6d3586</code> (class name + hash). Override it to return something readable; <code>System.out.println(dog)</code> and string concatenation call it automatically.</p><p><strong>equals(Object o)</strong> — default <code>==</code> checks if two references point to the <em>same object</em>. Override it to compare <em>field values</em> so two different <code>Point(3,4)</code> objects are considered equal.</p><p><strong>hashCode()</strong> — returns an int bucket used by <code>HashMap</code> and <code>HashSet</code>. It must be consistent with <code>equals()</code>.</p></div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE equals/hashCode CONTRACT</div>If <code>a.equals(b)</code> is true, then <code>a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()</code> MUST also be true. Override <strong>both together</strong> — never just one. (The reverse isn't required: equal hashes don't force equal objects — that's a hash collision.)</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — why override both</div><p>You put two <code>Point(1,2)</code> objects in a <code>HashSet</code>. If you override <code>equals()</code> but NOT <code>hashCode()</code>, the two points land in different buckets (different default hashes), so the set stores both — the duplicate is never detected. Overriding <code>hashCode()</code> to match <code>equals()</code> sends equal points to the same bucket, and the set correctly keeps one.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><code>==</code> compares <span class="key-term">references</span> (identity); <code>.equals()</code> compares <span class="key-term">values</span> when overridden. <code>String</code>, <code>Integer</code>, and the wrapper classes already override <code>equals()</code>/<code>hashCode()</code> — that's why <code>"ab".equals("ab")</code> is true. Always use <code>.equals()</code> for object content comparison.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON TRAP</div><p>The signature MUST be <code>public boolean equals(Object o)</code> — with parameter type <code>Object</code>. Writing <code>equals(Point p)</code> instead <em>overloads</em> rather than <em>overrides</em>, so collections (which call <code>equals(Object)</code>) won't use it. Add <code>@Override</code> and the compiler will catch the mistake.</p></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>You override equals() but not hashCode(). What breaks?<ol type="A"><li>Nothing</li><li>HashSet/HashMap may store logical duplicates</li><li>The program won't compile</li><li>toString stops working</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>B.</b> Equal objects can produce different hash codes, so they land in different buckets and hash-based collections fail to detect them as duplicates. Override both together.</div></details></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — the Object contract — equals, hashCode and toString</div><h3>Why you must override equals</h3><p>The inherited <code>Object.equals()</code> compares <strong>references</strong>, so two objects with identical field values are "unequal" by default. If your class represents a value — a Point, a Money, a Student — you override <code>equals()</code> to compare contents. Until you do, <code>list.contains(myObject)</code> and <code>list.remove(myObject)</code> will fail even when a matching object is present.</p><h3>The rule that catches everyone</h3><p><strong>If you override <code>equals()</code>, you must override <code>hashCode()</code>.</strong> The contract: equal objects <em>must</em> return equal hash codes. Hash-based collections (<code>HashMap</code>, <code>HashSet</code>) find the bucket by hash code and only then compare with equals. Override equals alone and two equal objects land in different buckets — so a HashSet accepts an apparent duplicate and a HashMap lookup silently misses. Note the converse is allowed: equal hash codes do not require equality (that is just a collision).</p><h3>Writing equals correctly</h3><p>The contract requires it to be reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent, and false for <code>null</code>. In practice: check <code>this == obj</code> first, reject null and mismatched types, cast, then compare fields — using <code>.equals()</code> for object fields and <code>==</code> for primitives.</p><h3>toString</h3><p>The default prints <code>ClassName@1b6d3586</code> — the class and a hash in hex, useless in a log. Overriding <code>toString()</code> to return meaningful text pays off everywhere, because <code>System.out.println(obj)</code> and string concatenation call it automatically.</p></div>"},"D286/uadv/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-comparable"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Comparable &amp; Comparator</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">TWO WAYS TO DEFINE ORDER</div>&bull; <b>Comparable</b> — the class defines its OWN natural order: implement <code>compareTo(other)</code>; then Collections.sort(list) just works. ONE order per class.<br>&bull; <b>Comparator</b> — a SEPARATE object defines an order: as many different orders as you like, passed to sort.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — both, on a Student</div><pre>class Student implements Comparable&lt;Student&gt; {
    String name; double gpa;
    public int compareTo(Student o) {
        return this.name.compareTo(o.name);      // natural: by name
    }
}

Collections.sort(students);                       // by name (Comparable)

students.sort(Comparator.comparingDouble(s -&gt; s.gpa));         // by GPA
students.sort(Comparator.comparingDouble((Student s) -&gt; s.gpa)
                        .reversed());                          // GPA high→low</pre>compareTo returns negative (this first), zero (tie), positive (other first). Comparators bolt on alternative orders without touching the class.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ RETURN SIGN, NOT VALUE</div>Only the SIGN of compareTo matters: −, 0, +. Returning a−b works for ints (overflow aside); for doubles use Double.compare(a, b). &ldquo;One natural order = Comparable; many external orders = Comparator&rdquo; answers most questions here.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>To sort the same list three different ways, you&rsquo;d use:<ol type="A"><li>Three Comparators</li><li>Three compareTo methods in the class</li><li>Three copies of the class</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> A class gets ONE compareTo (natural order); every extra ordering is a Comparator.</div></details></div></div><h3>Comparable &amp; Comparator — sorting your own objects</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p><code>Collections.sort()</code> and <code>Arrays.sort()</code> know how to order numbers and Strings, but they have no idea whether one <code>Employee</code> comes before another. You teach them by supplying an ordering — either <strong>built into</strong> the class (<code>Comparable</code>) or as a <strong>separate rule</strong> (<code>Comparator</code>).</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>Comparable&lt;T&gt;</strong> — the class implements it and defines <code>public int compareTo(T other)</code>. This is the object's <em>natural ordering</em> (String's is alphabetical, Integer's is numeric). <code>Collections.sort(list)</code> uses it with no extra argument.</p><p><strong>Comparator&lt;T&gt;</strong> — a <em>separate</em> object defining <code>public int compare(T a, T b)</code>. Pass it as a second argument: <code>Collections.sort(list, bySalary)</code>. Use it when you want multiple orderings or can't modify the class.</p></div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE RETURN-VALUE RULE (both methods)</div>Return a <strong>negative</strong> int if this/first should come BEFORE, <strong>zero</strong> if they are equal in order, <strong>positive</strong> if it should come AFTER. A common trick for numbers is <code>Integer.compare(a, b)</code> (never <code>a - b</code>, which can overflow).</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — natural vs custom order</div><table><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Code</th></tr><tr><td>Sort employees by natural order (name)</td><td><code>class Employee implements Comparable&lt;Employee&gt; { public int compareTo(Employee e){ return name.compareTo(e.name);} }</code> then <code>Collections.sort(list);</code></td></tr><tr><td>Sort by salary instead</td><td><code>Collections.sort(list, (a,b) -&gt; Integer.compare(a.salary, b.salary));</code></td></tr><tr><td>Reverse salary</td><td><code>list.sort(Comparator.comparingInt((Employee e) -&gt; e.salary).reversed());</code></td></tr></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">Comparable</span> = <code>compareTo(other)</code>, one natural ordering, implemented BY the class. <span class="key-term">Comparator</span> = <code>compare(a, b)</code>, an external/alternate ordering, passed TO sort. Mnemonic: Compar<strong>able</strong> = the object is able to compare <em>itself</em>; Compar<strong>ator</strong> = a separate <em>-ator</em> object that compares two others.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON TRAP</div><p>Sorting objects that don't implement <code>Comparable</code> with a plain <code>Collections.sort(list)</code> throws a <code>ClassCastException</code> at runtime. Either implement <code>Comparable</code> or pass a <code>Comparator</code>.</p></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>compareTo returns a positive number. What does that mean?<ol type="A"><li>this object comes before the other</li><li>this object comes after the other</li><li>they are equal</li><li>an error occurred</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>B.</b> Positive means "this" is greater / comes after; negative means before; zero means equal in order.</div></details></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — Comparable vs Comparator — one natural order, many alternatives</div><h3>The difference in one line</h3><p><strong>Comparable</strong> is implemented <em>by the class itself</em> and defines its single natural ordering via <code>compareTo(other)</code>. <strong>Comparator</strong> is a <em>separate</em> object defining one of many alternative orderings via <code>compare(a, b)</code>. A Student can be Comparable by ID, while separate Comparators sort by name, GPA or enrollment date.</p><h3>The return-value convention</h3><p>Both return an <code>int</code>, and the sign is what matters: <strong>negative</strong> means this comes first, <strong>zero</strong> means equal ordering, <strong>positive</strong> means this comes after. For numeric fields <code>Integer.compare(a, b)</code> is safer than <code>a - b</code>, which can overflow for extreme values.</p><h3>Where they get used</h3><p><code>Collections.sort(list)</code> and <code>Arrays.sort(arr)</code> require Comparable elements, and throw <code>ClassCastException</code> at run time if the type does not implement it. Pass a Comparator as the second argument to override the natural order. <code>TreeMap</code> and <code>TreeSet</code> keep keys sorted the same way.</p><h3>Consistency with equals</h3><p>Best practice is that <code>compareTo</code> returns 0 exactly when <code>equals</code> returns true. When they disagree, sorted collections behave surprisingly — <code>TreeSet</code> treats "compareTo == 0" as duplicate and silently drops an element that <code>equals</code> considers distinct.</p></div>"},"D286/uadv/s4":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-fileio"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — try-with-resources &amp; File I/O</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — read a file the modern way</div><pre>try (Scanner fin = new Scanner(new File("scores.txt"))) {
    while (fin.hasNextInt()) {
        total += fin.nextInt();
    }
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.out.println("missing file");
}
// fin.close() happened AUTOMATICALLY — even if an exception hit</pre>Declaring the resource inside try(...) means Java closes it for you on every exit path. That&rsquo;s the whole point of try-with-resources: no leaked file handles, no finally-block close boilerplate.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHAT TO KNOW</div>&bull; The resource must implement <b>AutoCloseable</b> (Scanner, readers/writers, streams all do).<br>&bull; FileNotFoundException / IOException are <b>checked</b>: catch or declare throws.<br>&bull; Writing: <code>try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter("out.txt")) { out.println("hi"); }</code>.<br>&bull; hasNextInt()/hasNextLine() guard reads so you stop at end-of-file cleanly.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ WHY NOT JUST close() AT THE END?</div>If an exception fires before your close() line, the file stays open (a resource leak). try-with-resources closes on success AND on failure — which is why it&rsquo;s the exam&rsquo;s &ldquo;best practice&rdquo; answer.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A resource declared in try (...) is closed:<ol type="A"><li>Automatically, even when an exception occurs</li><li>Only if you call close()</li><li>Never</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Auto-close on every exit path is the guarantee of try-with-resources.</div></details></div></div><h3>try-with-resources &amp; File I/O</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>When you open a file, a <code>Scanner</code>, or any resource, you must <strong>close it</strong> or you leak file handles. The old way — closing in a <code>finally</code> block — is verbose and easy to get wrong. <strong>try-with-resources</strong> closes the resource for you, automatically, even if an exception is thrown.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>Declare the resource in parentheses right after <code>try</code>:</p><p><code>try (Scanner fileIn = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;while (fileIn.hasNextLine()) System.out.println(fileIn.nextLine());<br>}</code></p><p>When the block ends — normally OR via exception — Java calls <code>close()</code> automatically. Any resource whose class implements <code>AutoCloseable</code> (Scanner, BufferedReader, PrintWriter, FileWriter…) works this way. You can declare several, separated by semicolons; they close in reverse order.</p></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — read then write, safely</div><table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Code</th></tr><tr><td>Read a file line by line</td><td><code>try (Scanner s = new Scanner(new File("in.txt"))) { while (s.hasNextLine()) process(s.nextLine()); }</code></td></tr><tr><td>Write to a file</td><td><code>try (PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter("out.txt")) { pw.println("Hello"); }</code></td></tr></table><p>No explicit <code>close()</code> — the resource is released as soon as the block exits.</p></div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">CHECKED EXCEPTIONS FOR FILES</div>File operations throw <strong>checked</strong> exceptions: <code>FileNotFoundException</code> (reading a missing file) and <code>IOException</code>. You must either <code>catch</code> them or declare <code>throws IOException</code> on the method — the compiler forces it.</div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">try-with-resources</span> = resource declared in <code>try(...)</code>, auto-closed via <code>AutoCloseable.close()</code>. It replaces the <code>finally { resource.close(); }</code> pattern. <code>Scanner(new File(f))</code> reads a file; <code>Scanner(System.in)</code> reads the keyboard. <code>PrintWriter</code>/<code>FileWriter</code> write. Reading a missing file → <code>FileNotFoundException</code> (a checked exception).</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON TRAP</div><p><code>new Scanner("data.txt")</code> does NOT open a file — it scans the literal string "data.txt"! You must wrap a <code>File</code>: <code>new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))</code>. That's a classic OA trap.</p></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>What is the main advantage of try-with-resources?<ol type="A"><li>It runs faster</li><li>It closes the resource automatically, even on exception</li><li>It ignores exceptions</li><li>It avoids importing Scanner</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>B.</b> The resource declared in <code>try(...)</code> is closed automatically when the block exits — normally or via an exception — preventing leaks without a finally block.</div></details></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — try-with-resources and file I/O</div><h3>The leak that finally was invented to stop</h3><p>Open files, streams and sockets hold operating-system resources that must be released. Closing them in <code>finally</code> works but is verbose and easy to get wrong — especially when the close itself can throw. <strong>try-with-resources</strong> replaces all of it: <code>try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(file)) { ... }</code> closes the resource automatically when the block exits, normally or by exception.</p><p>The mechanism is an interface: any class implementing <code>AutoCloseable</code> can be declared in the parentheses. Multiple resources are closed in <strong>reverse order</strong> of declaration.</p><h3>Reading and writing</h3><p>Reading a file throws the <strong>checked</strong> <code>IOException</code> (or <code>FileNotFoundException</code>), so the compiler forces you to catch it or declare <code>throws</code> — a direct application of the checked/unchecked rule from Unit 4. Typical patterns are <code>Scanner</code> or <code>BufferedReader</code> for input and <code>PrintWriter</code> or <code>FileWriter</code> for output.</p><p>Two practical points that show up in questions: opening a writer in the default mode <strong>overwrites</strong> the file, so appending requires the append flag; and buffered writers hold data in memory until flushed, so failing to close (or flush) can lose the last chunk of output entirely.</p></div>"},"D286/uadv/s5":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-we-nested"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Nested, Inner &amp; Anonymous Classes</h3><p>Java lets you define a class <b>inside</b> another class. Three kinds matter, and the difference is what they can see and when you use them.</p><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE THREE KINDS</div>&bull; <b>Static nested class</b> — declared <code>static</code> inside the outer class. It does NOT see the outer instance&rsquo;s fields; it&rsquo;s just a namespaced helper: <code>Outer.Nested n = new Outer.Nested();</code><br>&bull; <b>Inner class</b> (non-static) — tied to an INSTANCE of the outer class and can read its fields directly. Created via an outer object.<br>&bull; <b>Anonymous class</b> — a one-off, unnamed class defined at the point of use, usually to implement an interface inline.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — an anonymous class implementing an interface</div><pre>Runnable task = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("running!");
    }
};
task.run();   // prints: running!</pre>Reading it: <code>new Runnable() { ... }</code> creates an object of a brand-new unnamed class that implements Runnable, overriding run() on the spot. Since Java 8 the same thing is usually written as a lambda: <code>Runnable task = () -&gt; System.out.println("running!");</code></div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ STATIC NESTED ≠ INNER</div>The exam tell: a <b>static nested</b> class cannot touch the outer object&rsquo;s instance fields (there is no outer object); an <b>inner</b> class can. If the code accesses outer instance state, it must be a (non-static) inner class.</div></div><h3>Nested, inner &amp; anonymous classes</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Sometimes a helper class only makes sense <em>inside</em> another class. Java lets you declare a class within a class. There are four flavors, and the OA mostly wants you to tell them apart: <strong>static nested</strong>, <strong>inner</strong> (non-static), <strong>local</strong> (inside a method), and <strong>anonymous</strong> (a class with no name, created on the spot).</p></div>
<div class="tb-def"><div class="tb-def-term">Static nested class</div><div class="tb-def-body">Declared <code>static</code> inside another class. Does NOT need an outer instance; behaves like a top-level class scoped inside its outer class: <code>Outer.Nested n = new Outer.Nested();</code></div></div>
<div class="tb-def"><div class="tb-def-term">Inner (non-static) class</div><div class="tb-def-body">Non-static; each inner object is tied to an <strong>outer instance</strong> and can access its private fields. Created via an outer object: <code>Outer.Inner i = outer.new Inner();</code></div></div>
<div class="tb-def"><div class="tb-def-term">Local class</div><div class="tb-def-body">Declared inside a method body; visible only within that method.</div></div>
<div class="tb-def"><div class="tb-def-term">Anonymous class</div><div class="tb-def-body">A one-off class with no name, declared and instantiated at once — usually to implement an interface or extend a class inline: <code>Runnable r = new Runnable(){ public void run(){ ... } };</code></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>The big distinction is <strong>static nested vs inner</strong>. A static nested class is independent — no outer object required. A (non-static) inner class always belongs to an instance of the outer class and can read that instance's private members directly. That's why inner classes are handy for tightly-coupled helpers (like an iterator over the outer list).</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">static nested</span> = no outer instance needed (<code>new Outer.Nested()</code>). <span class="key-term">inner</span> = needs an outer instance (<code>outer.new Inner()</code>), can access outer's privates. <span class="key-term">anonymous</span> = no name, defined and instantiated in one expression, often for a listener/callback. Modern code frequently replaces small anonymous classes with <em>lambdas</em>.</p></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Which class type does NOT require an instance of the outer class to create?<ol type="A"><li>Non-static inner class</li><li>Static nested class</li><li>Neither can be created</li><li>Both always need one</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>B.</b> A static nested class is created like a normal class (<code>new Outer.Nested()</code>) — no outer instance. A non-static inner class requires an outer instance.</div></details></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — nested, inner and anonymous classes</div><h3>The four kinds, and what distinguishes them</h3><ul><li><strong>Static nested class</strong> — declared <code>static</code> inside another class. It is just a top-level class in a namespace: it has <em>no</em> reference to an outer instance and cannot touch outer instance fields.</li><li><strong>Inner (non-static nested) class</strong> — holds an implicit reference to the outer object, so it <em>can</em> use the outer instance fields. It requires an outer instance to exist: <code>outer.new Inner()</code>.</li><li><strong>Local class</strong> — declared inside a method, visible only there.</li><li><strong>Anonymous class</strong> — declared and instantiated in one expression, with no name, typically to implement an interface on the spot (a Comparator, an event listener).</li></ul><h3>The distinction that gets tested</h3><p>An inner class can access the enclosing object's private members — including via <code>Outer.this.field</code> when names collide. A static nested class cannot, because there is no enclosing instance. If a nested class does not need outer state, <strong>make it static</strong>: the hidden reference in an inner class keeps the outer object alive and is a known source of memory leaks.</p><h3>Why anonymous classes exist</h3><p>They avoid a named file for a one-use implementation. A local or anonymous class may only use local variables that are <code>final</code> or <em>effectively final</em> — because the value is captured (copied), so allowing reassignment would make the two copies disagree.</p></div>"},"D286/challenge/c1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-gloss-flow"><h3>📖 Exam terms — compilation, parsing &amp; loop patterns (with examples)</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">COMPILATION — what actually runs</div><code>javac Hello.java</code> compiles source into <b>bytecode</b> (Hello.class); <code>java Hello</code> starts the <b>JVM</b>, which executes that bytecode. Java is compiled ONCE to bytecode, then run anywhere a JVM exists — that&rsquo;s &ldquo;write once, run anywhere.&rdquo;</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">PARSING — text to number</div><code>int n = Integer.parseInt("42");</code> turns the STRING "42" into the int 42. Feed it "4x" and it throws <b>NumberFormatException</b>. (Double.parseDouble for decimals; Scanner&rsquo;s nextInt parses as it reads.)</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE for LOOP&rsquo;S THREE COMPONENTS</div><code>for (init; condition; update)</code> — run init once, test the condition before each pass, run the update after each pass. <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)</code>: i starts 0, loops while i&lt;5, bumps i each time.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — countdown loop</div><pre>for (int i = 5; i &gt;= 1; i--) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}</pre>Start high, test i ≥ 1, step DOWN with i--. Output: <b>5 4 3 2 1</b>. (Forgetting to flip the condition when counting down gives a loop that never runs.)</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — the accumulate pattern</div><pre>int total = 0;
int[] prices = {3, 5, 2};
for (int p : prices) {
    total += p;      // running sum: 3, then 8, then 10
}
System.out.println(total);   // 10</pre>Initialize OUTSIDE the loop, add INSIDE. The same shape computes counts, sums, max — it&rsquo;s the most reused pattern in the course.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — recursion, traced properly</div><pre>int fact(int n) {
    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;      // base case — STOPS the calls
    return n * fact(n - 1);    // smaller problem
}</pre>Trace fact(3): fact(3) = 3 · fact(2) = 3 · 2 · fact(1) = 3 · 2 · 1 = <b>6</b>. Every recursive method needs a base case and a call that moves toward it — remove either and you get a StackOverflowError.</div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-we-chal1"><h3>📖 Worked example — trace a program exactly like the exam</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — what is printed?</div><pre>int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {
    total += i * 2;
    System.out.print(total + " ");
}</pre>Trace with a table — one row per loop pass:<br>&bull; i = 1: total = 0 + 2 = 2 → prints &ldquo;2 &rdquo;<br>&bull; i = 2: total = 2 + 4 = 6 → prints &ldquo;6 &rdquo;<br>&bull; i = 3: total = 6 + 6 = 12 → prints &ldquo;12 &rdquo;<br>Output: <b>2 6 12</b>. The discipline: write the variable values down EVERY pass; never run the loop in your head all at once.</div></div><h3>Why tracing beats memorizing</h3><p>The D286 OA rewards people who can <strong>run the code in their head</strong>. Java's arithmetic is driven by the <em>compile-time types of the operands</em>, not the values. <code>7/2</code> is <code>int</code> division and yields <code>3</code>; only if one side is a <code>double</code> literal (<code>7.0/2</code>) do you get <code>3.5</code>. An <code>int</code> silently wraps on overflow: <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1</code> becomes the most negative <code>int</code>.</p><h4>Promotion and precedence</h4><p>In any numeric expression, operands narrower than <code>int</code> (<code>byte</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>char</code>) are promoted to <code>int</code> first. So <code>'A' + 1</code> is <code>66</code>, an <code>int</code>, not the char <code>'B'</code>. Precedence binds <code>*</code> and <code>/</code> tighter than <code>+</code> and <code>-</code>, so <code>2 + 3 * 4 - 8 / 2</code> is <code>2 + 12 - 4 = 10</code>.</p><h4>Identity vs equality</h4><p><code>==</code> on reference types compares <em>object identity</em>; <code>.equals()</code> compares <em>content</em>. A string literal is interned, but <code>new String("hi")</code> is a fresh object, so <code>==</code> is <code>false</code> while <code>.equals()</code> is <code>true</code>.</p><h4>Control flow traps</h4><p>A <code>switch</code> without <code>break</code> <strong>falls through</strong> into later cases. A <em>labeled</em> <code>break</code> exits the named outer loop, not just the inner one. Overload resolution prefers the most specific applicable method using widening (<code>char</code> to <code>int</code> before <code>long</code> before <code>Object</code>) before boxing. Finally, Java is strictly <strong>pass-by-value</strong>: a method can mutate the object an array reference points to, but reassigning a primitive parameter never changes the caller's variable. Short-circuit <code>&amp;&amp;</code> skips its right side when the left is false, which can prevent an <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>.</p>"},"D286/challenge/c2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-gloss-objects"><h3>📖 Exam terms — objects, failures &amp; containers (with examples)</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">ACCESSORS &amp; MUTATORS (getters/setters)</div>Fields stay <b>private</b>; the class exposes <b>accessors</b> (getName — read) and <b>mutators</b> (setName — change). That&rsquo;s encapsulation: outside code goes through methods, so the class can validate changes.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — <code>this</code> disambiguation</div><pre>public class Part {
    private String name;
    public Part(String name) {
        this.name = name;   // this.name = the FIELD; name = the parameter
    }
}</pre>When a parameter has the same name as a field, the parameter wins inside the method — <b>this.</b> reaches past it to the object&rsquo;s field. Without <code>this</code>, the line name = name assigns the parameter to itself and the field stays null.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">AIOOBE — ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</div>Valid indices run 0 to length−1. <code>int[] a = new int[3];</code> then <code>a[3]</code> throws <b>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</b> at RUNTIME (it compiles fine). Classic causes: using &lt;= in a loop condition, or indexing with length.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">ARRAYLIST RESIZING</div>An ArrayList grows automatically: when its internal array fills, it allocates a bigger one and copies the elements over. You never set a size — that&rsquo;s the difference from a plain array, whose length is fixed forever at creation.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">CONTAINMENT (has-a) vs INHERITANCE (is-a)</div><b>Containment/composition</b>: a class holds another as a field — a Car HAS-An Engine. <b>Inheritance</b>: a class IS-A specialized parent — a Truck IS-A Vehicle (extends). Exam tell: &ldquo;has a&rdquo; → field; &ldquo;is a&rdquo; → extends.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — the equals contract</div>A correct equals() must be:<br>&bull; <b>Reflexive:</b> x.equals(x) is true.<br>&bull; <b>Symmetric:</b> x.equals(y) ⇔ y.equals(x).<br>&bull; <b>Transitive:</b> x.equals(y) and y.equals(z) ⇒ x.equals(z).<br>And whenever you override equals, <b>override hashCode too</b> — equal objects must share a hash code, or HashSet/HashMap misplace them. (Same three words as relation properties in discrete math — same meaning.)</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 3 — string performance</div><pre>String s = "";
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) s += i;        // SLOW: copies every pass

StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) sb.append(i);  // FAST: edits in place
String done = sb.toString();</pre>String is <b>immutable</b> — each += builds a whole new string, so a loop of appends is O(n²) copying. <b>StringBuilder</b> mutates one buffer and turns the loop O(n). Rule: concatenating in a loop → StringBuilder.</div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-we-chal2"><h3>📖 Worked example — objects and references, the classic trap</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — two references, one object</div><pre>int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = a;        // b points to the SAME array
b[0] = 99;
System.out.println(a[0]);   // ?</pre><code>b = a</code> copies the <b>reference</b>, not the array. Both names point at one array in memory, so changing b[0] changes what a sees: it prints <b>99</b>.<br><br>Contrast with primitives: <code>int x = 5; int y = x; y = 9;</code> leaves x = 5, because primitives copy the VALUE. Reference-vs-value is the single most-tested Java idea.</div></div><h3>Objects, failures, and containers</h3><p>Construction runs <strong>bottom-up the class hierarchy but top-down in execution</strong>: <code>this(...)</code> and <code>super(...)</code> must be the first statement, so the superclass constructor body finishes before the subclass body runs. Only <em>instance methods</em> are polymorphic. <strong>Fields and static methods are resolved by the reference's compile-time type</strong> (hiding), while overridden instance methods dispatch on the runtime object.</p><h4>Interfaces and exceptions</h4><p>When two interfaces supply the same <code>default</code> method, the implementing class <em>must</em> override it and may call <code>A.super.m()</code> explicitly. Exceptions split into <strong>checked</strong> (subclasses of <code>Exception</code> that are not <code>RuntimeException</code>, e.g. <code>IOException</code> — must be declared or caught) and <strong>unchecked</strong> (<code>RuntimeException</code> and its subclasses like <code>NullPointerException</code>). A <code>finally</code> block always runs; a <code>return</code> inside <code>finally</code> <em>replaces</em> any value the <code>try</code> was returning.</p><h4>Collections gotchas</h4><p>Autoboxing caches <code>Integer</code> objects for <code>-128..127</code>, so <code>==</code> is <code>true</code> for <code>127</code> but <code>false</code> for <code>128</code> — always use <code>.equals()</code> for value comparison. <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;.remove(int)</code> removes by <em>index</em>, while <code>remove(Object)</code> removes by value; <code>list.remove(1)</code> deletes index 1. If you override <code>equals</code> but not <code>hashCode</code>, hash-based lookups fail because equal objects land in different buckets. Generics use <strong>type erasure</strong>: <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> are the same class at runtime, generic types are <em>invariant</em> (a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> is not a <code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code>), and you cannot write <code>new T()</code>. Prefer an <code>ArrayList</code> over a raw array when you need dynamic sizing, and remember it stores boxed <code>Integer</code> objects, not primitives — which is exactly why the <code>remove</code> overload and <code>==</code> traps above bite so often.</p>"},"D286/pa_fresh/sim":{"body":"<h2>🆕 Fresh Questions — Brand-New Practice</h2><p>40 brand-new questions written for this course and mapped across its competencies — not repeated anywhere else in the app. Take it timed and closed-book, then review every miss.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 40 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D286/aistote/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-visual="aistote-method">
<h3>🧠 The Aistote Method (J-Method)</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE IDEA</div>The Aistote app is built on two of the most evidence-backed study techniques — <b>active recall</b> and <b>spaced repetition</b> — run in short, intense <b>25-minute</b> blocks (its "J-Method"). Instead of slowly re-reading, you spend the time <b>testing yourself</b> and re-testing what you miss. This app already has every tool the method needs.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE 3-STEP LOOP</div>
<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What you do</th><th>Where in this app</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>1. Convert</b></td><td>Turn the material into questions instead of re-reading it.</td><td>Open a section, skim the lesson once, then jump to its <b>Quiz</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>2. Recall</b></td><td>Answer from memory first — no peeking — then check.</td><td>Take the section Quiz, or a <b>PA / OA simulation</b>; rate your confidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>3. Space it</b></td><td>Re-test only what you missed, on a growing schedule.</td><td>Every miss becomes a <b>Flashcard</b>; review them on the app's spaced-repetition schedule.</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">A 25-MINUTE J-METHOD SESSION</div>
<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>Do this</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">0–3 min</td><td>Skim the lesson's key terms and the big idea — don't memorize yet, just get the lay of the land.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3–18 min</td><td>Take the section quiz (or a 15-question drill). Try to <b>recall the answer first</b>, reveal it after.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">18–23 min</td><td>Redo <b>only</b> the questions you got wrong.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">23–25 min</td><td>Send the stubborn ones to <b>Flashcards</b> and note tomorrow's quick review.</td></tr></table>
Start the block with the <b>⚡ Focus</b> timer (25 min) on any lesson — that is your J-Method session.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE ONE RULE</div>Always try to answer <b>from memory before you look</b>. The effort of retrieving is what actually builds the memory — re-reading feels productive but barely moves it.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHY IT WORKS</div><b>Active recall</b> (testing yourself) forces your brain to reconstruct the answer, which strengthens it far more than recognition. <b>Spaced repetition</b> reviews each fact just as you're about to forget it, moving it into long-term memory with the fewest reps. The 25-minute block simply makes you actually do both, every day.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">DAILY RHYTHM</div>Two or three 25-minute J-Method blocks a day (new material in one, missed-question review in the next) beats a single long cram session — and it's how the Aistote method delivers "learn it in 25 minutes" instead of hours.</div>
</div>"},"D286/userdocs/add":{"body":"<h3 style="margin-top:0;">➕ Add a document to this class</h3><p style="font-size:13.5px; color:var(--text-3); line-height:1.6;">Paste your document below (or choose a .txt file). It will be kept <b>word-for-word, exactly as you add it</b>. Word/PDF files: open them, select all, copy, and paste here — that preserves your text precisely.</p><div data-docform="D286" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px; max-width:720px;"><input data-doc-title type="text" placeholder="Title (e.g., Unit 3 Sample Test)" style="padding:11px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:14px;"><div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; font-size:13.5px;"><label style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; cursor:pointer;"><input data-doc-type type="radio" name="doctype-D286" value="notes" checked> 📒 Notes (shown as-is)</label><label style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; cursor:pointer;"><input data-doc-type type="radio" name="doctype-D286" value="test"> 📝 Practice test (answers held until the end)</label></div><textarea data-doc-content rows="12" placeholder="Paste the document here, exactly as you want it shown…" style="padding:12px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; resize:vertical;"></textarea><textarea data-doc-key rows="4" placeholder="(Practice tests only, optional) Paste the ANSWER KEY here — it will be hidden until you open it at the end. You can skip this if your document already has a line saying “Answer Key” before the answers." style="padding:12px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.6; resize:vertical;"></textarea><div style="display:flex; gap:10px; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap;"><label style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-3); cursor:pointer; padding:9px 13px; border:1px dashed var(--border); border-radius:10px;">📎 Choose .txt file<input data-doc-file type="file" accept=".txt,.md,.csv,text/plain" style="display:none;"></label><button data-doc-save="D286" style="border:none; background:var(--accent, #6366f1); color:#fff; font-weight:700; font-size:14px; padding:11px 20px; border-radius:10px; cursor:pointer;">Save document</button><span data-doc-msg style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-4);"></span></div></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_1":{"body":"<p>A program is a list of instructions a computer carries out in order, one after another, without judgement. That last part is the whole difficulty of programming: the machine does exactly what you wrote, not what you meant, so almost every bug you will ever write is a gap between those two things.</p><p>Java specifically is <em>compiled then interpreted</em>. You write <code>.java</code> source, the compiler (<code>javac</code>) turns it into <strong>bytecode</strong> in a <code>.class</code> file, and the <strong>JVM</strong> runs that bytecode on whatever machine it is installed on. That two-step is why the same compiled Java file runs on Windows, macOS and Linux unchanged — the phrase the exam uses is "write once, run anywhere".</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Hello.java   --javac--&gt;   Hello.class   --java (JVM)--&gt;   output
 (source)                    (bytecode)                     (running program)</pre><h3>Inside the JVM: interpretation and JIT</h3><p>The JVM does not simply interpret bytecode line by line for ever. It starts by interpreting, and watches which methods run often — the <em>hot</em> ones. Those get handed to the <strong>JIT (just-in-time) compiler</strong>, which translates them into native machine code for the actual processor, once, and reuses that from then on.</p><p>That is why a Java program is often slow for its first second and fast afterwards, and why the old claim that "Java is slow because it is interpreted" is out of date. Bytecode gives portability; the JIT gives back most of the speed.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Tool</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Is</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Contains</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JDK</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Java Development Kit — to <em>write</em> Java</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compiler (javac), tools, and a JRE</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JRE</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Java Runtime Environment — to <em>run</em> Java</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the JVM and the standard libraries</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JVM</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Java Virtual Machine — executes bytecode</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the interpreter and the JIT compiler</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The nesting is the exam point: JDK contains the JRE, which contains the JVM. To compile you need the JDK; to run only, the JRE is enough.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>Why the two-step matters</h3><p>The compile-then-run split is the reason two different kinds of failure exist, and questions rely on it. If <code>javac</code> refuses, <strong>no class file is produced and nothing runs</strong> — so a "compile error" never produces partial output. If compilation succeeds, any failure afterwards happens while the JVM is executing, and by then output may already have appeared. When a question shows some output followed by a crash, it is describing a runtime error; when it shows none at all, suspect a compile error.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Program:</strong> an ordered list of instructions a computer executes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Compiler:</strong> a tool that translates source code into another form before the program runs; in Java, into bytecode</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bytecode:</strong> the platform-independent instructions in a .class file</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JVM:</strong> the Java Virtual Machine — the program that executes bytecode on a particular operating system</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Interpreter:</strong> a program that executes instructions directly rather than translating them ahead of time</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Everything in this course sits on top of this loop: edit, compile, run, read the message, edit again. When a section later says something is a "compile error" it means <code>javac</code> refused and no <code>.class</code> file was produced, so the program never ran at all — a distinction that decides several exam questions in 1.4 and 2.2.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — where a Java program can die — and what you see when it does</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 264" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk203a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="26" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ONE SOURCE FILE, TWO PROGRAMS, TWO PLACES TO FAIL</text><rect x="16" y="74" width="122" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="77" y="96.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Payroll.java</text><text x="77" y="111.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">text you typed</text><line x1="140" y1="100" x2="172" y2="100" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk203a)"/><rect x="174" y="74" width="104" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="226" y="96.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">javac</text><text x="226" y="111.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the compiler</text><line x1="280" y1="100" x2="312" y2="100" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk203a)"/><rect x="314" y="74" width="124" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="96.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Payroll.class</text><text x="376" y="111.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">bytecode</text><line x1="440" y1="100" x2="472" y2="100" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk203a)"/><rect x="474" y="74" width="104" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="526" y="96.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">java</text><text x="526" y="111.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the JVM</text><line x1="580" y1="100" x2="612" y2="100" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk203a)"/><rect x="614" y="74" width="124" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="676" y="96.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">output</text><text x="676" y="111.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">what you see</text><line x1="226" y1="126" x2="226" y2="168" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk203a)"/><rect x="96" y="170" width="262" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="227" y="189.2" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">COMPILE ERROR stops here</text><text x="227" y="202.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no .class is written, so the JVM</text><text x="227" y="215.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">never starts — output is empty</text><line x1="526" y1="126" x2="526" y2="168" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk203a)"/><rect x="400" y="170" width="300" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="550" y="189.2" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">RUNTIME ERROR stops here</text><text x="550" y="202.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">lines before the fault already printed,</text><text x="550" y="215.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">so you see partial output, then a trace</text><text x="376" y="250" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">A "compile error" question can never have partial output as its answer.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a compile error produces no class file and no output</div><p>The file below has one fault: line 4 is missing its semicolon. Watch what the machine actually does with it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Payroll {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int hours = 38;
        int rate = 17
        int gross = hours * rate;
        System.out.println("Gross: " + gross);
    }
}</pre><p>Step 1 — run the compiler. This is the real message from javac 21:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Payroll.java
Payroll.java:4: error: ';' expected
        int rate = 17
                     ^
1 error</pre><p>Step 2 — look for the class file. There is none:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ ls Payroll.class
ls: cannot access 'Payroll.class': No such file or directory</pre><p>Step 3 — there is nothing to run. The program never executed, so it printed nothing at all — not even the lines above the fault. That is the whole point of the two-step pipeline: <strong>javac either produces a complete .class or it produces none</strong>.</p><p>Notice also where the caret points: at the <em>end</em> of line 4, not at line 5. The compiler read <code>int rate = 17</code>, expected a semicolon next, and found <code>int</code> instead — so it complains at the position where the semicolon should have been.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a runtime error prints everything up to the moment it fails</div><p>This one compiles cleanly. Every type is legal and every statement is well formed — the fault is a value, and values are not the compiler's business.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("Loading roster...");
int students = 5;
int groups = 0;
System.out.println(students / groups);
System.out.println("done");</pre><p>Step 1 — javac accepts it and writes the class file. Step 2 — the JVM starts and executes line by line. Line 1 prints. Lines 2 and 3 store values. Line 4 divides 5 by 0, and integer division by zero throws.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
Loading roster...
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero</pre><p>Two things to read off that. First, <code>Loading roster...</code> <em>did</em> appear — the failure happened halfway through, so the earlier output is real. Second, <code>done</code> never printed: an uncaught exception ends the program on the spot, it does not skip the bad line and carry on.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>When a question says "this code has a compile error", the answer can never be "it prints X then crashes". No class file means no execution at all. Conversely, an answer showing some output followed by an exception name is describing a <em>runtime</em> error, and that means the code compiled fine — so do not go hunting for a missing semicolon.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Diagnose from the shape of the output. <strong>No output at all</strong> plus a message naming a line and a rule = compile error. <strong>Some output, then a line starting <code>Exception in thread "main"</code></strong> = runtime error. <strong>Full output, wrong numbers</strong> = logic error. You can answer most Unit 1 error questions from that alone, without reading the code closely.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_2":{"body":"<p>Every Java program is built from the same three nested wrappers, and knowing which one an error is complaining about saves you most of the guessing. The outer wrapper is a <strong>class</strong>, inside it is the <strong>main method</strong> where execution begins, and inside that are <strong>statements</strong>, each ending in a semicolon.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Main {                         // 1. the class, name must match Main.java
    public static void main(String[] args) {   // 2. where the JVM starts
        System.out.println("Hello");           // 3. one statement, ends with ;
    }
}</pre><p><code>System.out.println</code> reads left to right as: in the <code>System</code> class, take the standard <code>out</code> stream, call <code>println</code> on it. The <code>ln</code> is the only difference from <code>print</code> — it adds a newline after the text. That one character is the entire content of a surprising number of "what is the exact output" questions.</p><p>Input is the mirror image, and needs a <code>Scanner</code>:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import java.util.Scanner;                 // must be above the class

Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = scnr.nextInt();                 // reads one int
String name = scnr.next();                // reads one word
String line = scnr.nextLine();            // reads the rest of the line</pre><h3>Reading the structure back</h3><p>Almost every "what is wrong with this code" question in Unit 1 is answered by checking the three wrappers in order: is the class name identical to the file name, is the <code>main</code> signature exactly <code>public static void main(String[] args)</code>, and does every statement end in a semicolon. Those three account for most early compile errors, and the signature is the one people mistype — dropping <code>static</code> compiles fine and then fails at launch with "main method not found", which is a runtime failure rather than a compile one.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Class:</strong> the outer container for Java code; the file name must match the public class name exactly, including case</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>main method:</strong> the method the JVM calls first; its signature is always public static void main(String[] args)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Statement:</strong> a single instruction, terminated by a semicolon</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Scanner:</strong> the standard class for reading typed input, constructed with System.in</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Mixing <code>nextInt()</code> and <code>nextLine()</code> is the classic input bug. <code>nextInt()</code> consumes the number but leaves the newline sitting in the buffer, so the next <code>nextLine()</code> returns an empty string instead of waiting. The fix is an extra throwaway <code>nextLine()</code> after the number.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the anatomy of a Java program, and the three rules attached to it</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 740 300" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk204a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="370" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">THE THREE WRAPPERS, AND WHAT EACH ONE IS CHECKED AGAINST</text><rect x="20" y="42" width="500" height="226" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="32" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Payroll.java</text><text x="120" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">— the file on disk</text><rect x="44" y="76" width="452" height="176" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="56" y="98" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public class Payroll {</text><rect x="68" y="110" width="408" height="108" rx="8" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="80" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public static void main(String[] args) {</text><rect x="94" y="144" width="366" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="106" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">System.out.println("Gross: " + gross);</text><text x="80" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}</text><text x="56" y="240" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}</text><circle cx="548" cy="58" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="548" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="566" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">file name must equal the class name</text><text x="566" y="76" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">(capitals included)</text><circle cx="548" cy="98" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="548" y="102" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="566" y="102" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the class — outer container</text><circle cx="548" cy="132" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="548" y="136" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="566" y="136" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the JVM starts here, and only</text><text x="566" y="150" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">at this exact signature</text><circle cx="548" cy="170" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="548" y="174" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="566" y="174" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a statement — ends in ;</text><line x1="520" y1="58" x2="534" y2="58" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="500" y1="98" x2="534" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="478" y1="132" x2="534" y2="132" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="462" y1="170" x2="534" y2="170" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="370" y="288" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Rename the file and javac says: class Payroll is public, should be declared in a file named Payroll.java</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — print versus println — the one character that changes the output</div><p>Both of these print the same two words. Only the line breaks differ, and "what is the exact output" questions live entirely in that difference.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.print("Ada");
System.out.print("Lovelace");
System.out.println();
System.out.println("Ada");
System.out.println("Lovelace");</pre><p>Trace it. <code>print</code> writes the characters and leaves the cursor where it is, so the first two calls land on the same line with nothing between them — no space is inserted for you. The bare <code>println()</code> then ends that line. The last two calls each write their text and then end the line, so they occupy two lines.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
AdaLovelace
Ada
Lovelace</pre><p>Three lines of output from five statements. If you expected <code>Ada Lovelace</code> on the first line, the missing space is the lesson: concatenation and printing add exactly what you wrote and nothing else.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why nextInt() followed by nextLine() reads an empty string</div><p>The user types <code>37</code>, presses Enter, types <code>Grace Hopper</code>, presses Enter. Here is the version that looks right and is not:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = scnr.nextInt();
String name = scnr.nextLine();
System.out.println("age=" + age);
System.out.println("name=[" + name + "]");</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
age=37
name=[]</pre><p>The reason is the position of the cursor in the input stream. <code>nextInt()</code> consumes the characters <code>3</code> and <code>7</code> and stops — it does not consume the Enter that followed them. So the newline is still waiting, and <code>nextLine()</code> does exactly what it is asked: it reads to the end of the current line, which is now empty, and returns <code>""</code> immediately without waiting for the user.</p><p>The fix is one throwaway call that swallows the leftover newline:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = scnr.nextInt();
String rest = scnr.nextLine();   // consumes the newline left by nextInt
String name = scnr.nextLine();   // now reads the real line
System.out.println("age=" + age);
System.out.println("rest=[" + rest + "]");
System.out.println("name=[" + name + "]");</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
age=37
rest=[]
name=[Grace Hopper]</pre><p><code>rest</code> is the empty string — that is the leftover newline, now safely consumed — and <code>name</code> finally holds the full line, spaces included.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Memorise the signature as one unbroken string: <code>public static void main(String[] args)</code>. Every word earns a question. Drop <code>static</code> and the file still <em>compiles</em>; it fails at launch with <code>Error: Main method is not static in class ...</code>, which makes it a runtime failure, not a compile error. Change <code>String[] args</code> to anything else and the JVM reports that it cannot find a main method at all.</p></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u1_z1_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A local variable has no default value — a field does</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(fieldInt);
System.out.println(fieldBool);
System.out.println(fieldRef);
System.out.println(fieldDouble);

int local;
local = 5;
System.out.println(local);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0
false
null
0.0
5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Fields are initialised for you: <code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>0.0</code>.</li><li>A <strong>local</strong> variable gets none of that. Writing <code>int local; System.out.println(local);</code> is a <em>compile error</em> — "variable local might not have been initialized" — not a run-time surprise and not a printed 0.</li><li>That is why the assignment above comes before the use. The compiler tracks whether every path assigns the variable before it is read.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>This is a favourite exam question because the two rules look alike but are not. <strong>Fields</strong> default (0 / false / null). <strong>Locals</strong> do not, and using one before assignment fails to compile. If a question shows <code>int x; System.out.println(x);</code> inside a method, the answer is "does not compile" — never 0.</div>"},"D286/u1/z1_3":{"body":"<p>Comments and whitespace are invisible to the compiler and essential to the reader, which makes them easy to dismiss and easy to test. Java strips them out entirely before the program runs, so they can never change behaviour — only readability.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// single-line: everything after // on this line is ignored

/* multi-line:
   ignored until the closing marker */

/** Javadoc: same as multi-line, but tools read it
 *  to generate documentation. See 6.22 and 7.17. */</pre><p>Whitespace — spaces, tabs, blank lines — is free-form in Java. You could write an entire program on one line and it would compile. The convention is one statement per line and four spaces of indentation per nesting level, because the indentation is how a human sees the block structure that the braces define.</p><h3>What comments cost and do not cost</h3><p>Because comments are stripped before compilation, they cannot slow a program down or change its behaviour — a fact questions occasionally test directly. What they can do is mislead: a comment that no longer matches the code is worse than none, because a reader trusts it. The convention that survives is to comment the <em>why</em> rather than the <em>what</em>. <code>// add 1 to i</code> repeats the code; <code>// skip the header row</code> explains a decision the code cannot state.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Comment:</strong> text ignored by the compiler, written for people reading the code</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Whitespace:</strong> spaces, tabs and newlines; free-form in Java, so it affects readability but never behaviour</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Javadoc comment:</strong> a /** */ comment that documentation tools extract into API docs</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Watch for questions where indentation misleads. Java decides what belongs to an <code>if</code> by braces, not by how the code is lined up, so a line that <em>looks</em> like it is inside the branch may not be — the specific trap is spelled out in 3.8.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — comments and whitespace never reach the generated bytecode</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 202" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk205a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="360" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WHAT REACHES THE COMPILER</text><rect x="16" y="38" width="268" height="132" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="28" y="56" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what you wrote</text><text x="28" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace" font-style="italic">// pay for the week</text><text x="28" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int hours = 38;</text><text x="140" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace" font-style="italic">/* hrs */</text><text x="28" y="114" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace"></text><text x="28" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int pay = hours * 17;</text><text x="28" y="156" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-style="italic">(blank line above this one)</text><line x1="288" y1="104" x2="336" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk205a)"/><rect x="338" y="78" width="108" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="392" y="100.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">javac</text><text x="392" y="115.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">scanner</text><text x="392" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">strips comments,</text><text x="392" y="160" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">collapses whitespace</text><line x1="450" y1="104" x2="498" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk205a)"/><rect x="500" y="38" width="236" height="132" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="512" y="56" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the token stream it compiles</text><rect x="512" y="70" width="33.6" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="528.8" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><rect x="551.6" y="70" width="48" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="575.6" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">hours</text><rect x="605.6" y="70" width="19.2" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="615.2" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">=</text><rect x="630.8000000000001" y="70" width="26.4" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="644.0000000000001" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">38</text><rect x="663.2" y="70" width="19.2" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="672.8000000000001" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">;</text><rect x="688.4000000000001" y="70" width="33.6" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="705.2" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><rect x="512" y="100" width="33.6" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="528.8" y="115" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">pay</text><rect x="551.6" y="100" width="19.2" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="561.2" y="115" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">=</text><rect x="576.8000000000001" y="100" width="48" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="600.8000000000001" y="115" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">hours</text><rect x="630.8000000000001" y="100" width="19.2" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="640.4000000000001" y="115" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">*</text><rect x="656.0000000000001" y="100" width="26.4" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="669.2000000000002" y="115" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">17</text><rect x="688.4000000000001" y="100" width="19.2" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="698.0000000000001" y="115" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">;</text><text x="360" y="190" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The comments and the blank line are gone before compilation begins — they cannot affect behaviour or speed.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the same program written unreadably still compiles and prints the same thing</div><p>Whitespace in Java is free-form, which means the layout carries no meaning to the compiler. This entire program body is one line:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int hours=38;int rate=17;System.out.println("Pay: "+hours*rate);/* all on one line */</pre><p>Trace it as three statements, because that is what the semicolons say it is: store 38, store 17, then evaluate <code>"Pay: " + hours * rate</code>. Multiplication binds tighter than the <code>+</code>, so 38 × 17 = 646 happens first, and only then is 646 joined onto the text.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
Pay: 646</pre><p>Reformatted across four indented lines, the output is byte-for-byte identical. The indentation was for you, not for javac — which is exactly why an exam question can indent a line misleadingly and still be a fair question.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — where a comment is not a comment</div><p>The comment markers only count when the compiler is reading code. Inside a string literal they are ordinary characters:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("See https://www.wgu.edu/catalog");
System.out.println("a" /* mid-expression comment */ + "b");</pre><p>Line 1 contains <code>//</code> in the middle of <code>https://</code>. If the compiler treated that as a comment, the closing quote and the semicolon would vanish and the file would not compile. It does not — the <code>//</code> is inside a string literal, so it is just text.</p><p>Line 2 shows the reverse: a <code>/* ... */</code> comment sitting in the middle of an expression is stripped, and the two string literals close up around the gap as though it was never there.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
See https://www.wgu.edu/catalog
ab</pre><p>So the rule is positional: <code>//</code> and <code>/*</code> start a comment everywhere <em>except</em> inside a string or character literal, and a quote inside a comment is likewise just a character. This is why a stray unclosed quote can produce a cascade of nonsense errors — the compiler's idea of where code ends and text begins has been shifted.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_4":{"body":"<p>Errors are worth sorting into three kinds, because the kind tells you where to look. Two of them stop you before the program runs; the third one lets the program run and produce the wrong answer, which is why it is the dangerous one.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int x = "hello";       // COMPILE  error — types do not match, no .class produced
int y = 5 / 0;         // RUNTIME  error — compiles, then throws ArithmeticException
int avg = a + b / 2;   // LOGIC    error — runs happily, answer is simply wrong</pre><p>A <strong>warning</strong> sits below all three: the compiler is uneasy but produces the class file anyway. Unchecked casts and deprecated methods are the usual causes. Warnings do not stop you, so they accumulate quietly.</p><h3>Which error you are looking at</h3><p>The practical value of the three-way split is that it tells you where to look before you start looking. A compile error names a line and a rule, so you read that line and the one above it. A runtime error names an exception type, and the type is the diagnosis — <code>ArithmeticException</code> means a division, <code>NullPointerException</code> means something was never assigned, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> means a bound was wrong. A logic error names nothing at all, which is why it needs a trace table rather than a message.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Syntax / compile-time error:</strong> code that breaks the rules of the language; the compiler refuses and nothing runs</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Runtime error:</strong> the program compiles but fails while running, usually by throwing an exception</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Logic error:</strong> the program compiles and runs but computes the wrong result</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Warning:</strong> a compiler complaint that does not prevent compilation</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This split reappears constantly. Unit 2 hinges on it (an uninitialised local is a <em>compile</em> error while integer overflow is a silent <em>logic</em> error), and Unit 12 exists entirely to handle the second category deliberately rather than by accident.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Intro to CS → from source code to running program</strong></p><i data-tba_z1_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — reading the symptom backwards to the kind of error</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 292" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk206a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="276" y="16" width="200" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="37.025" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the program misbehaved</text><line x1="376" y1="50" x2="376" y2="74" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk206a)"/><rect x="256" y="76" width="240" height="38" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="99.025" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Did javac produce a .class file?</text><line x1="256" y1="95" x2="150" y2="95" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="150" y1="95" x2="150" y2="132" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk206a)"/><text x="200" y="88" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">no</text><rect x="40" y="134" width="220" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="150" y="153.2" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">COMPILE-TIME ERROR</text><text x="150" y="166.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">javac named a line and a rule;</text><text x="150" y="179.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">nothing ran, output is empty</text><line x1="496" y1="95" x2="620" y2="95" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="620" y1="95" x2="620" y2="132" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk206a)"/><text x="560" y="88" fill="var(--success)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><rect x="510" y="134" width="226" height="38" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="623" y="157.025" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Did it stop with an exception?</text><line x1="623" y1="172" x2="623" y2="200" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk206a)"/><text x="600" y="190" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">yes</text><rect x="510" y="202" width="226" height="54" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="623" y="220.2" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">RUNTIME ERROR</text><text x="623" y="233.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">partial output, then a stack trace;</text><text x="623" y="246.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the exception type is the diagnosis</text><line x1="510" y1="153" x2="300" y2="153" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="300" y1="153" x2="300" y2="200" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk206a)"/><text x="400" y="146" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">no — it ran to the end</text><rect x="190" y="202" width="220" height="54" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="300" y="220.2" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LOGIC ERROR</text><text x="300" y="233.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">full output, wrong answer,</text><text x="300" y="246.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no message anywhere</text><text x="376" y="278" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">A warning is none of these: javac complains and writes the .class anyway.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a runtime error, read from its own message</div><p>Every type here is correct, so javac has no objection. The fault is an index that does not exist, and only the running program can discover that.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int[] scores = {88, 92, 79};
System.out.println("first: " + scores[0]);
System.out.println("fourth: " + scores[3]);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
first: 88
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 3 out of bounds for length 3</pre><p>Read the message as three separate facts. <strong>The type</strong> — <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> — says the fault is an index. <strong>The detail</strong> — "Index 3 out of bounds for length 3" — says which index and what the limit was, and reading it carefully answers the question on its own: three elements are numbered 0, 1, 2. <strong>The partial output</strong> — <code>first: 88</code> — says the program got that far, which locates the failure at the line after it.</p><p>That triple is the whole technique for runtime errors: type tells you the category, detail tells you the value, and the last line of output tells you the place.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a logic error: the code that runs perfectly and is wrong</div><p>Two students score 88 and 92. Their average is obviously 90. This code is entirely legal Java:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int a = 88, b = 92;
int wrong = a + b / 2;
int right = (a + b) / 2;
System.out.println(wrong);
System.out.println(right);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
134
90</pre><p>Step by step: <code>/</code> binds tighter than <code>+</code>, so <code>a + b / 2</code> means <code>88 + (92 / 2)</code> = <code>88 + 46</code> = <code>134</code>. The average of two scores between 0 and 100 came out as 134, and nothing anywhere reported a problem — no red underline, no exception, no warning.</p><p>That is the defining property of a logic error: the only detector is a value you worked out beforehand. Had you not known the answer was 90, <code>134</code> would simply have been the answer.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A missing semicolon is a compile error even when the offending line looks perfect, because the compiler reports it where the semicolon <em>should have been</em>. Given <code>int rate = 17</code> on line 4 followed by another statement on line 5, javac says <code>Payroll.java:4: error: ';' expected</code> with the caret past the end of line 4. Students read line 4, see nothing wrong with the assignment, and start editing line 5. The caret position, not the line number alone, is the thing to read.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Learn the four common exception types as a lookup table, because the type <em>is</em> the diagnosis: <code>ArithmeticException</code> ⇒ integer division or modulo by zero; <code>NullPointerException</code> ⇒ a method called on something never assigned; <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> ⇒ an index outside 0..length−1; <code>NumberFormatException</code> ⇒ <code>Integer.parseInt</code> handed something that is not a number. Questions usually give the message and ask for the cause.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_5":{"body":"<p>A computer only ever manipulates numbers, and only ever in binary. Text, images and instructions are all encodings on top of that, which is the single fact that makes 2.13 (binary), 2.14 (characters) and 2.16 (overflow) make sense rather than feel arbitrary.</p><p>The layers are worth naming from the bottom up: transistors form logic gates, gates form circuits, circuits execute machine instructions, machine instructions are what your bytecode is eventually turned into. Each layer hides the one below it, which is exactly why you can write <code>x + y</code> without knowing any of this — until a value overflows and the hidden layer shows through.</p><h3>Why everything reduces to numbers</h3><p>Saying a computer stores only numbers sounds like trivia until it explains behaviour. Text is numbers under an encoding, so comparing strings compares character codes and <code>'Z'</code> sorts before <code>'a'</code>. Colours are numbers, so image files are arrays. And because every type is a fixed number of bits, every numeric type has a hard ceiling — which is not a Java quirk but the hardware showing through, and it is what makes integer overflow in 2.16 possible at all.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bit:</strong> a single binary digit, 0 or 1</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Byte:</strong> eight bits; the smallest normally addressable unit of memory</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Machine instruction:</strong> a single operation the processor executes directly</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Memory:</strong> numbered storage locations, each holding a byte</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Because every type is a fixed number of bits, every numeric type has a hard maximum. That is not a Java quirk — it is the hardware showing through, and it is why <code>int</code> stops at about 2.1 billion (2.13, 2.16).</p></div><i data-tba_z1_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the abstraction stack, and the one place it leaks</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 274" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk207a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="360" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">EVERY LAYER HIDES THE ONE BELOW — UNTIL IT DOES NOT</text><rect x="30" y="38" width="300" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="42" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">your statement</text><text x="42" y="68" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int total = a + b;</text><text x="346" y="61" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what you write</text><line x1="180" y1="74" x2="180" y2="84" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="30" y="84" width="300" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="42" y="100" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">bytecode</text><text x="42" y="114" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">iload_1  iload_2  iadd  istore_3</text><text x="346" y="107" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what javac produces</text><line x1="180" y1="120" x2="180" y2="130" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="30" y="130" width="300" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="42" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">machine instructions</text><text x="42" y="160" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ADD r1, r2, r3</text><text x="346" y="153" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what the JIT hands the CPU</text><line x1="180" y1="166" x2="180" y2="176" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="30" y="176" width="300" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="42" y="192" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">logic circuits</text><text x="42" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a 32-bit adder</text><text x="346" y="199" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">gates wired together</text><line x1="180" y1="212" x2="180" y2="222" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="30" y="222" width="300" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="42" y="238" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">transistors</text><text x="42" y="252" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">switches, on or off</text><text x="346" y="245" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the physical layer</text><rect x="520" y="38" width="210" height="220" rx="9" fill="none" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="625" y="60" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">where the bottom shows through</text><text x="534" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a fixed number of transistors</text><text x="534" y="100" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">means a fixed number of bits</text><text x="534" y="122" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a fixed number of bits means</text><text x="534" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a largest representable value</text><text x="534" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int stops at 2,147,483,647</text><text x="534" y="182" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">so 2147483647 + 1 wraps to</text><text x="534" y="198" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">-2147483648 — no error</text><text x="534" y="222" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">That is section 2.16, and it is</text><text x="534" y="238" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">hardware, not a Java quirk.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a character is a number wearing a costume</div><p>The claim "a computer only stores numbers" is testable in four lines. A <code>char</code> holds a Unicode code value; printing it shows a letter, and doing arithmetic on it shows the number.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">char grade = 'B';
System.out.println(grade);
System.out.println((int) grade);
System.out.println(grade + 1);
System.out.println((char)(grade + 1));</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
B
66
67
C</pre><p>Line by line: printing the <code>char</code> itself shows <code>B</code>, because <code>println</code> has a version that formats a char as a character. Casting to <code>int</code> exposes the stored number, 66. Adding 1 <em>promotes</em> the char to an int first, so the expression has type <code>int</code> and prints <code>67</code> — not <code>C</code>. Casting the result back to <code>char</code> finally gives <code>C</code>.</p><p>So the same 66 bits are a letter or a number depending only on how they are interpreted. Nothing about the storage changed between line 2 and line 3.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting the values a fixed number of bits can hold</div><p>If a type is <em>n</em> bits wide it has exactly 2<sup>n</sup> distinct bit patterns, so it has exactly 2<sup>n</sup> possible values — no more. For signed whole numbers Java splits them evenly around zero: 2<sup>n−1</sup> negatives, zero, and 2<sup>n−1</sup>−1 positives.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE);
System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1);
System.out.println(Byte.MAX_VALUE);
System.out.println(Integer.toBinaryString(11));</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
2147483647
-2147483648
127
1011</pre><p>Work the arithmetic yourself. An <code>int</code> is 32 bits, so 2<sup>32</sup> = 4,294,967,296 patterns; half of them are negative, giving a top value of 2<sup>31</sup>−1 = 2,147,483,647 — exactly line 1. A <code>byte</code> is 8 bits, so 2<sup>7</sup>−1 = 127 — exactly line 3.</p><p>Line 2 is the leak. Adding one to the largest pattern carries out of the top bit; the carry has nowhere to go and is discarded, and the sign bit flips. The answer is the most negative int, printed with no warning whatsoever. Line 4 confirms the underlying representation: 11 in binary is <code>1011</code>, which is 8 + 2 + 1.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>"The computer stores the letter B" is the wrong mental model and it makes several later sections look arbitrary. It stores 66. That is why <code>'A' &lt; 'a'</code> is true (65 &lt; 97), why <code>'7' - '0'</code> gives 7, and why <code>System.out.println('A' + 1)</code> prints <code>66</code> rather than <code>B</code>. Whenever character behaviour surprises you, replace each character with its code and re-read the line.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>The exam turns bit width into range questions. Memorise one worked chain rather than four numbers: <em>n</em> bits ⇒ 2<sup>n</sup> patterns ⇒ signed range −2<sup>n−1</sup> to 2<sup>n−1</sup>−1. From that, 8 bits gives −128..127, 16 bits gives −32,768..32,767, and 32 bits gives roughly ±2.1 billion — all three derived, none memorised.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_6":{"body":"<p>An IDE bundles the editor, the compiler and the debugger into one window so that the edit-compile-run loop takes a keystroke instead of three commands. Nothing it does is magic: everything an IDE performs, <code>javac</code> and <code>java</code> can do from a terminal, which is worth remembering when a question asks what is actually required to run Java.</p><p>What an IDE genuinely adds is speed of feedback — it underlines a type error as you type rather than at compile time — plus refactoring, autocompletion and a visual debugger. What it cannot do is find logic errors for you, because to the IDE a wrong answer looks exactly like a right one.</p><h3>The IDEs you will meet by name</h3><p>Exam questions ask you to recognise a Java IDE rather than to use one, so the names are worth knowing. <strong>Eclipse</strong>, <strong>IntelliJ IDEA</strong>, <strong>NetBeans</strong> and <strong>Visual Studio Code</strong> are the common ones; BlueJ and DrJava are teaching-oriented. Note what is <em>not</em> an IDE: a plain text editor such as Notepad, and the JDK itself, which is a toolchain rather than an environment.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>What the IDE is not doing for you</h3><p>An IDE removes typing, not thinking. It will underline a type error as you type, autocomplete a method name and jump to a definition — all of which catch <em>syntax</em> problems early. It cannot tell you that your average is computed with integer division, because to the tool a wrong number looks exactly like a right one. That boundary is worth holding: the IDE handles the errors the compiler would have caught anyway, and the ones that actually cost marks are the ones it cannot see.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>IDE:</strong> Integrated Development Environment — editor, compiler and debugger in one application</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JDK:</strong> Java Development Kit — the compiler and tools; required to write Java</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JRE:</strong> Java Runtime Environment — the JVM and libraries; enough to run Java, not to compile it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Debugger:</strong> a tool that pauses a running program so you can inspect variables</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>JDK versus JRE is a favourite question. To <em>compile</em> you need the JDK; to only <em>run</em> a compiled program the JRE is enough. The JDK contains a JRE, so a machine with the JDK can do both.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → JVM, JRE, and JDK</strong></p><i data-d286f_pipeline="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>What actually happens when you press Run</h3><p>Two separate programs are involved, and the exam expects you to name which does what.</p><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — source to bytecode to execution</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 146" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk63a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="16" y="40" width="122" height="58" rx="10" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="77" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Hello.java</text><text x="77" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">source you write</text><line x1="138" y1="69" x2="164" y2="69" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk63a)"/><rect x="166" y="40" width="122" height="58" rx="10" fill="#f59e0b18" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="227" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">javac</text><text x="227" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the compiler</text><line x1="288" y1="69" x2="314" y2="69" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk63a)"/><rect x="316" y="40" width="122" height="58" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e918" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="377" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Hello.class</text><text x="377" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">bytecode</text><line x1="438" y1="69" x2="464" y2="69" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk63a)"/><rect x="466" y="40" width="122" height="58" rx="10" fill="#22c55e18" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="527" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">JVM</text><text x="527" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">runs it on THIS machine</text><text x="320" y="128" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the .class file is the same on every machine — only the JVM differs</text></svg></div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Step</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Tool</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Produces</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Compile</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>javac Hello.java</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>Hello.class</code> — bytecode</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Run</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>java Hello</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the JVM executes the bytecode</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Write once, run anywhere</strong> is exactly this split: javac targets the JVM rather than any real processor, so the bytecode is portable and only the JVM has to be rewritten per platform.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>The file name must match the public class exactly, capitals included — <code>Hello.java</code> for <code>public class Hello</code>. And you run <code>java Hello</code>, not <code>java Hello.class</code>.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what is inside the JDK, and which layer each task needs</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 262" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk208a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="360" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM — EACH ONE CONTAINS THE NEXT</text><rect x="24" y="40" width="430" height="210" rx="12" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="40" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JDK — Java Development Kit</text><text x="40" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">you need this to COMPILE</text><rect x="48" y="88" width="382" height="148" rx="10" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="64" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JRE — Java Runtime Environment</text><text x="64" y="126" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">enough to RUN an existing .class</text><rect x="72" y="136" width="334" height="88" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="88" y="158" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JVM — Java Virtual Machine</text><text x="88" y="176" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the interpreter + the JIT compiler</text><text x="88" y="194" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">executes bytecode, one platform at a time</text><text x="88" y="212" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-style="italic">this is what "run anywhere" means</text><text x="416" y="110" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">+ the standard library</text><text x="438" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">+ javac, javap, javadoc, jdb</text><rect x="478" y="40" width="256" height="210" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="492" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">what each one lets you do</text><text x="492" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">write and edit .java</text><text x="492" y="102" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">any text editor</text><text x="492" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compile .java → .class</text><text x="492" y="134" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JDK only</text><text x="492" y="152" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">run an existing .class</text><text x="492" y="166" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JRE is enough</text><text x="492" y="184" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">step through in a debugger</text><text x="492" y="198" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JDK (or an IDE)</text><text x="492" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">find a logic error for you</text><text x="492" y="230" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">nothing does</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the whole edit-compile-run loop with no IDE at all</div><p>An IDE is a convenience over two commands. Here is the entire loop in a terminal — this is the real transcript, and the IDE's Run button does exactly this behind the scenes.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, WGU!");
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Hello.java          # the compiler; silence means success
$ ls
Hello.class  Hello.java
$ java Hello                # note: no .class on the end
Hello, WGU!</pre><p>Three details are examined. The compiler is given the <em>file name</em> <code>Hello.java</code>; the launcher is given the <em>class name</em> <code>Hello</code>, with no extension — <code>java Hello.class</code> fails. And <code>javac</code> printing nothing is success: compilers report problems, not progress.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the error an IDE cannot underline</div><p>Twenty questions, seventeen correct. The percentage is obviously 85. This compiles with no error, no warning, and no red underline anywhere:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int correct = 17;
int items = 20;
int percent = correct / items * 100;
System.out.println("Score: " + percent + "%");</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
Score: 0%</pre><p>Trace it in the order Java actually uses. <code>/</code> and <code>*</code> have equal precedence, so evaluation runs strictly left to right: <code>17 / 20</code> is computed first, both operands are <code>int</code>, so the result is the truncated <code>0</code>. Then <code>0 * 100</code> is <code>0</code>.</p><p>The tool cannot help here, because every type is legal and every value is a valid int. To the IDE a wrong number looks exactly like a right one — which is precisely the boundary the section is drawing. Writing <code>correct * 100 / items</code> gives 85, because the multiplication happens while the value is still large enough to survive truncation.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_7":{"body":"<p>Following one statement through the hardware explains why some operations are thousands of times slower than others. The processor fetches an instruction from memory, decodes it, executes it, and stores the result — the fetch-decode-execute cycle — billions of times per second.</p><p>The speed differences come from distance. Registers live inside the processor and are effectively instant. Cache is close and fast. Main memory (RAM) is further and noticeably slower. Disk is slower again by orders of magnitude, which is why file operations in Unit 9 are treated so differently from ordinary variable access.</p><h3>Input, output and storage devices</h3><p>The hardware categories are asked directly. <strong>Input devices</strong> send data in — keyboard, mouse, scanner, microphone, camera. <strong>Output devices</strong> send results out — <strong>monitor</strong>, printer, speakers. A few are both, and touchscreens are the standard example. <strong>Storage devices</strong> — hard disk, SSD, USB drive — are a separate category again, because they retain data rather than moving it in or out.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>Why the speed ladder matters to your code</h3><p>The distances in the hardware explain performance differences you will meet later. A variable lives in registers or cache and is effectively free to read. An object on the heap is slightly further away. A file is thousands of times slower again, which is why Unit 9 treats file access so differently from ordinary assignment, and why reading a file line by line inside a loop is a very different proposition from reading a variable inside one.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>CPU:</strong> the processor; fetches, decodes and executes instructions</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Register:</strong> a tiny storage location inside the CPU; the fastest memory there is</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>RAM:</strong> main memory; fast, addressable, and erased when power is lost</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Storage:</strong> disk or SSD; slow compared with RAM but survives power loss</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>RAM being volatile is the reason files exist at all. Everything your program holds in variables disappears when it ends, so anything that must outlive the run has to be written to a file — the problem Unit 9 solves.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — distance from the processor is the whole explanation for speed</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 382" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk209a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">THE MEMORY LADDER — WHY SOME LINES OF CODE COST NOTHING AND SOME COST EVERYTHING</text><text x="20" y="58" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">CPU register</text><text x="20" y="72" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">inside the core</text><rect x="196" y="48" width="26" height="24" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="232" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">~0.3 ns</text><text x="20" y="102" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">L1 / L2 cache</text><text x="20" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">on the chip</text><rect x="196" y="92" width="64" height="24" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="270" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">~1-10 ns</text><text x="20" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">main memory (RAM)</text><text x="20" y="160" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">on the board</text><rect x="196" y="136" width="132" height="24" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="338" y="153" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">~80 ns</text><text x="20" y="190" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">SSD</text><text x="20" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">over a bus</text><rect x="196" y="180" width="300" height="24" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="506" y="197" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">~100,000 ns</text><text x="20" y="234" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">spinning hard disk</text><text x="20" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a moving arm</text><rect x="196" y="224" width="452" height="24" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="658" y="241" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">~10,000,000 ns</text><line x1="192" y1="40" x2="192" y2="262" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="376" y="282" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">bar length grows with the log of the access time — each step down is roughly 10x to 100x slower</text><rect x="16" y="296" width="720" height="74" rx="9" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="30" y="320" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">a local variable</text><text x="30" y="338" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">lives on the top two rungs</text><text x="30" y="354" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reading it is effectively free</text><text x="258" y="320" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">an object</text><text x="258" y="338" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">lives in RAM</text><text x="258" y="354" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">cheap, but not free</text><text x="486" y="320" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">a file</text><text x="486" y="338" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">lives at the very bottom</text><text x="486" y="354" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reading one in a loop is a different thing</text><line x1="250" y1="302" x2="250" y2="364" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><line x1="478" y1="302" x2="478" y2="364" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — one Java statement, expanded into the steps the processor runs</div><p>The fetch–decode–execute cycle is not a metaphor. Compile this class and disassemble it with <code>javap -c</code> — a JDK tool — and the individual steps are printed out.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        int price = 42;
        total = total + price;
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Demo.java
$ javap -c Demo
  public static void main(java.lang.String[]);
    Code:
       0: iconst_0
       1: istore_1
       2: bipush        42
       4: istore_2
       5: iload_1
       6: iload_2
       7: iadd
       8: istore_1
       9: getstatic     #7   // System.out
      12: iload_1
      13: invokevirtual #13  // println
      16: return</pre><p>Read the middle four instructions against the source line <code>total = total + price;</code>. <code>iload_1</code> fetches <code>total</code> into the working area. <code>iload_2</code> fetches <code>price</code>. <code>iadd</code> executes the addition. <code>istore_1</code> stores the result back into <code>total</code>. Fetch, fetch, execute, store — one readable line of Java became four separate machine-level steps, each one moving a value between storage and the processor.</p><p>That is also why the memory ladder matters: every <code>iload</code> and <code>istore</code> is a trip to storage, and the price of that trip depends entirely on which rung the value is sitting on.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — RAM is erased, disk is not — demonstrated in one program</div><p>The reason Unit 9 exists is visible in nine lines. A value is held in a variable, written to a file, then destroyed in memory and read back from disk.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int reading = 72;
System.out.println("in RAM: " + reading);

PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter("sensor.txt");
out.println(reading);
out.close();

reading = 0;                       // the variable is gone
System.out.println("variable cleared: " + reading);

BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("sensor.txt"));
System.out.println("read back from disk: " + in.readLine());
in.close();</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
in RAM: 72
variable cleared: 0
read back from disk: 72</pre><p>The variable genuinely lost its value — line 2 of the output proves it. The 72 survived anyway, because a copy had been pushed down the ladder to storage, which does not depend on power or on the program still running.</p><p>Note what it cost. The two file operations are the slowest lines in the program by several orders of magnitude, and they are the only ones that needed <code>close()</code> and a <code>throws</code> declaration. Persistence is bought with time and ceremony; that trade is the whole content of the file chapter.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>"Variables are stored on the hard disk" is a common wrong answer. Variables live in registers, cache and RAM — all volatile. Nothing your program holds in a variable survives the program ending, let alone the power going off. The related confusion is memory versus storage: RAM is measured in gigabytes and cleared on shutdown, storage is measured in hundreds of gigabytes and is not.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Two orderings are worth being able to write from memory. <strong>Speed, fastest first:</strong> register, cache, RAM, SSD, hard disk. <strong>The cycle, in order:</strong> fetch, decode, execute, store. Questions are usually "which is faster" or "what comes after decode", and both are answered straight from those lists.</p></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u1_z1_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the hardware a program actually runs on</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk435a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">CPU</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">executes the instructions</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">RAM</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fast, volatile working memory</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">storage</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">disk or SSD — survives power off</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">motherboard</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the main circuit board joining them</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">bus</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the pathways the board provides</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">peripheral</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">any attached device that is not the core</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Where a running program's data actually sits</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] inMemory = new int[3];
inMemory[0] = 42;
System.out.println("in RAM while running: " + inMemory[0]);

java.io.PrintWriter out = new java.io.PrintWriter("kept.txt");
out.println(42);
out.close();

java.util.Scanner in = new java.util.Scanner(new java.io.File("kept.txt"));
System.out.println("read back from storage: " + in.nextInt());
in.close();</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">in RAM while running: 42
read back from storage: 42</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The array lives in RAM. RAM is <strong>volatile</strong> — cut the power and it is gone, which is why the value has to be written somewhere else to survive.</li><li>Writing to a file puts the value on storage, which persists; reading it back proves it outlived the variable.</li><li>The <strong>motherboard</strong> is the main circuit board holding the CPU, RAM and the sockets and buses connecting them. A <strong>peripheral</strong> is a device attached to the machine that is not part of that core — a keyboard, printer, or external drive.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Keep the split straight: RAM is fast and volatile and holds your variables while the program runs; storage is slower and persistent and holds files between runs. The motherboard connects the components, and peripherals are what plug into it.</div>"},"D286/u1/z1_8":{"body":"<p>Java was released by Sun Microsystems in 1995, designed around a goal earlier languages had not met: compile once, run on any machine. C and C++ compiled straight to machine code, which meant recompiling for every platform; Java compiled to bytecode and let the JVM absorb the differences.</p><p>Its other design decisions were reactions to C++ pain points. Automatic garbage collection removed manual memory freeing (Unit 8). Removing pointer arithmetic removed a whole family of crashes. Enforced exception handling made failure explicit (Unit 12). Generics arrived later, in Java 5 (Unit 13), and <code>var</code> later still, in Java 10 (2.22).</p><h3>What the design decisions bought</h3><p>Each Java feature the exam mentions was a response to a specific pain. Automatic garbage collection removed the crashes caused by freeing memory twice or never. Dropping pointer arithmetic removed a family of security holes. Checked exceptions made failure visible in the method signature rather than in a comment. Bytecode removed the need to ship a different binary per platform. If a question asks what a feature is <em>for</em>, the answer is nearly always "it removed a whole class of bug that the previous language allowed".</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Platform independence:</strong> the property that compiled bytecode runs unchanged on any machine with a JVM</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Garbage collection:</strong> automatic reclaiming of memory no longer reachable by the program</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Java SE:</strong> Java Standard Edition — the core language and libraries this course covers</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>The exam does not ask for release dates. It asks what problem a feature solved — platform independence, automatic memory management, explicit error handling. Learn the "why", not the timeline.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → JVM, JRE, and JDK</strong></p><i data-tba_z1_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what "write once, run anywhere" actually moves</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 276" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk210a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="190" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">C / C++ — compile per platform</text><rect x="120" y="40" width="140" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="190" y="61.025" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">payroll.c</text><line x1="190" y1="74" x2="76" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="30" y="106" width="92" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="76" y="124.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">compiler 1</text><line x1="76" y1="136" x2="76" y2="160" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="26" y="162" width="100" height="42" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="76" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">payroll.exe</text><text x="76" y="193" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Windows only</text><line x1="190" y1="74" x2="192" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="146" y="106" width="92" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="192" y="124.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">compiler 2</text><line x1="192" y1="136" x2="192" y2="160" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="142" y="162" width="100" height="42" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="192" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">payroll.exe</text><text x="192" y="193" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">macOS only</text><line x1="190" y1="74" x2="308" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="262" y="106" width="92" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="308" y="124.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">compiler 3</text><line x1="308" y1="136" x2="308" y2="160" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="258" y="162" width="100" height="42" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="308" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">payroll.exe</text><text x="308" y="193" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Linux only</text><text x="190" y="226" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">three builds, three binaries, three chances to differ</text><line x1="388" y1="16" x2="388" y2="240" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="580" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JAVA — compile once, ship the bytecode</text><rect x="510" y="40" width="140" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="580" y="61.025" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Payroll.java</text><line x1="580" y1="74" x2="580" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="520" y="98" width="120" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="580" y="116.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">javac (once)</text><line x1="580" y1="128" x2="580" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="508" y="152" width="144" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="580" y="170.675" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Payroll.class</text><line x1="580" y1="182" x2="470" y2="206" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="424" y="208" width="92" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="470" y="222.075" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JVM</text><text x="470" y="234.575" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Windows</text><line x1="580" y1="182" x2="586" y2="206" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="540" y="208" width="92" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="586" y="222.075" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JVM</text><text x="586" y="234.575" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text><line x1="580" y1="182" x2="702" y2="206" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk210a)"/><rect x="656" y="208" width="92" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="702" y="222.075" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JVM</text><text x="702" y="234.575" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Linux</text><text x="580" y="262" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">one build; only the JVM is rewritten per platform — and not by you</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a class file is aimed at a JVM version — and you can read which one</div><p>"Platform independent" does not mean "version independent". Compile the smallest possible class and inspect the header that javac wrote into it:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Hello.java
$ javap -v Hello.class
  Compiled from "Hello.java"
public class Hello
  minor version: 0
  major version: 65
  flags: (0x0021) ACC_PUBLIC, ACC_SUPER</pre><p>Major version 65 is Java 21 (the scheme is simple: 52 = Java 8, 55 = Java 11, 61 = Java 17, 65 = Java 21). That number is a floor, not a preference. Hand this exact file to a Java 17 JVM and it refuses to load it with <code>UnsupportedClassVersionError</code> — a runtime failure, not a compile one, because the compiler is long finished by then.</p><p>So the promise is precise: the same bytecode runs on any <em>operating system</em> that has a JVM of at least that version. The portability Java bought is across platforms, and the price is a version contract that shows up in the class file itself.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a feature that did not exist yet — the same file, two compilers</div><p><code>var</code> arrived in Java 10, and the compiler can be told to pretend it is older. That makes the history concrete rather than a date to memorise:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Modern {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var total = 1250;
        System.out.println("Total: " + total);
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac --release 8 Modern.java
Modern.java:3: error: cannot find symbol
        var total = 1250;
        ^
  symbol:   class var
  location: class Modern
1 error</pre><p>Read that message closely, because it says exactly what "the feature did not exist" means. The Java 8 compiler does not know <code>var</code> is a keyword at all — it assumes you meant a class called <code>var</code> and reports that no such class exists.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Modern.java        # the modern compiler, no --release flag
$ java Modern
Total: 1250</pre><p>Same file, same text, two different answers. Every feature the exam mentions — generics in Java 5, <code>var</code> in Java 10 — works this way: it is a rule the compiler learned on a specific date, not a change in the hardware.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Three confusions in this section are worth separating. <strong>Bytecode is not machine code</strong> — the CPU cannot run a .class file; the JVM has to. <strong>Portable means portable across operating systems, not across JVM versions</strong> — newer bytecode on an older JVM throws <code>UnsupportedClassVersionError</code>. And <strong>"Java is slow because it is interpreted"</strong> has been out of date for decades: the JVM interprets at first and then JIT-compiles the hot methods to native code, which is why a Java program is often slow for its first second and fast afterwards.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_9":{"body":"<p>Problem solving is the part that does not look like programming and decides whether the programming works. The order that survives contact with real problems is: understand the problem, work one example by hand, write the steps in plain language, then translate those steps into code — and only then worry about syntax.</p><p>Working an example by hand is the step people skip and the step that catches the most bugs. If you cannot compute the answer for one specific input on paper, no amount of Java will get you there, because you do not yet know what you are asking the machine to do.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Problem: average of three test scores
By hand: (88 + 92 + 79) / 3  -&gt;  259 / 3  -&gt;  86.33
Steps:   1. read three scores   2. add them   3. divide by 3   4. print
Trap:    if all four are int, step 3 gives 86 — see 2.5 and 2.11</pre><h3>Why the hand-trace is not optional</h3><p>Working one example by hand feels like a delay and is the fastest route to a correct program, because it converts a vague understanding into a specific expectation. Once you know that three scores of 88, 92 and 79 must give 86.33, you have a test: if the program prints 86, you know immediately that the division truncated, and you know where to look. Without that number you only know the output "looks wrong", which is a far harder position to debug from — and on an exam it is the same technique, since a trace table answers the question directly.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Algorithm:</strong> a finite sequence of unambiguous steps that solves a problem</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pseudocode:</strong> steps written in structured plain language rather than a programming language</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Decomposition:</strong> breaking a problem into smaller sub-problems that can be solved separately</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Trace:</strong> stepping through code by hand, tracking each variable value</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Tracing by hand is also the exam skill. Most "what does this print" questions are answered by writing the variables in a column and updating them line by line — not by recognising the pattern.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why working one case by hand comes before writing any code</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 280" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk211a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="16" y="40" width="166" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="99" y="59.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1. understand</text><text x="99" y="74.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">what exactly is asked?</text><line x1="182" y1="63" x2="200" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk211a)"/><rect x="202" y="40" width="166" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="285" y="59.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2. work ONE case</text><text x="285" y="74.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">by hand, on paper</text><line x1="368" y1="63" x2="386" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk211a)"/><rect x="388" y="40" width="166" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="471" y="59.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3. write the steps</text><text x="471" y="74.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">plain language / pseudocode</text><line x1="554" y1="63" x2="572" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk211a)"/><rect x="574" y="40" width="166" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="657" y="59.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4. translate</text><text x="657" y="74.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">now, and only now, Java</text><text x="376" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">STEP 2 IS THE ONE PEOPLE SKIP, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT PAYS</text><rect x="16" y="104" width="352" height="138" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="30" y="126" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the hand-worked case becomes the test</text><text x="30" y="150" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">scores 88, 92, 79</text><text x="30" y="168" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">88 + 92 + 79  =  259</text><text x="30" y="186" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">259 / 3       =  86.33</text><rect x="24" y="198" width="336" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="36" y="220" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">EXPECTED: 86.33 — written down before any code exists</text><line x1="372" y1="172" x2="400" y2="172" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk211a)"/><rect x="404" y="104" width="332" height="138" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="418" y="126" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">now the program can be judged</text><rect x="412" y="138" width="316" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="424" y="154" fill="var(--success)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">prints 86.33</text><text x="424" y="170" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">matches — the logic is right</text><rect x="412" y="184" width="316" height="54" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="424" y="202" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">prints 86</text><text x="424" y="218" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">does not match — and the gap names</text><text x="424" y="232" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the bug: the division truncated</text><text x="376" y="266" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Without the expected value, "86" is just an answer. With it, "86" is a diagnosis.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the average of three test scores, done both ways</div><p>Follow the four steps. <strong>Understand:</strong> add three scores, divide by three. <strong>By hand:</strong> 88 + 92 + 79 = 259, and 259 ÷ 3 = 86.33. <strong>Steps:</strong> read three, total them, divide, print. <strong>Translate:</strong></p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int s1 = 88, s2 = 92, s3 = 79;
int total = s1 + s2 + s3;
System.out.println("total = " + total);

int avgInt = total / 3;
System.out.println("int average    = " + avgInt);

double avgD = (double) total / 3;
System.out.println("double average = " + avgD);
System.out.printf("rounded        = %.2f%n", avgD);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
total = 259
int average    = 86
double average = 86.33333333333333
rounded        = 86.33</pre><p>The hand-worked 259 confirms the totalling. Then the two averages diverge, and the hand-worked 86.33 tells you instantly which one is wrong: <code>total / 3</code> has two integer operands, so the .33 is discarded before it is ever stored. Casting one operand to <code>double</code> first keeps it.</p><p>Had you not computed 86.33 in advance, <code>86</code> would have looked entirely plausible — which is exactly the failure step 2 prevents.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — decomposing a problem into two operations you already have</div><p>"A project takes 45 days. Express that as whole weeks plus leftover days." There is no <code>weeks()</code> method; the problem has to be broken into pieces the language already provides.</p><p><strong>By hand first:</strong> 45 ÷ 7 is 6 remainder 3, so 6 weeks and 3 days. Two separate quantities, so two separate operations: the whole part of the division, and the remainder.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int days = 45;
int weeks = days / 7;       // whole groups: integer division keeps only the whole part
int leftover = days % 7;    // what did not fill a group
System.out.println(days + " days = " + weeks + " weeks and " + leftover + " days");</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
45 days = 6 weeks and 3 days</pre><p>This is the shape decomposition usually takes: not one clever operation, but two ordinary ones applied to the same value. Note also that integer division — the villain of the previous example — is exactly the right tool here, because "whole weeks" genuinely means "throw the remainder away". The operation is not wrong or right on its own; it is right when the hand-worked answer says the fraction should be discarded.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>For any "what does this print" question, build a two-column trace table: variable names across the top, one row per statement, and update only the variables that change. It is slower for the first two lines and much faster after that, and it is immune to the pattern-matching mistakes that the distractors are built to catch. Write the table even when you are confident — the questions are designed so that confidence is the trap.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_10":{"body":"<p>The practical case for programming in this degree is narrower than the general one: D286 is the gate to every later software course, and the concepts here are the vocabulary those courses assume. Loops and arrays in Units 4 and 5 become the data structures course. Classes in Unit 7 become the object-oriented design course. Big-O in 17.3 becomes algorithms.</p><p>Within Java itself, the payoff is automation of anything repetitive and exact — which is precisely what a loop is. The rest of the course is a series of tools for describing that repetition more precisely.</p><h3>Where the effort pays</h3><p>Weighting revision by exam weight rather than by unit count is worth doing deliberately. Units 2 to 7 — variables, branches, loops, arrays, methods and classes — carry most of the questions, and they are also the units everything later is built from: exceptions are branches on failure, collections are arrays that grow, and inheritance is classes reusing classes. Time spent on Unit 2 pays twice, once directly and once through everything that assumes it.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Automation:</strong> having a program perform a repetitive task reliably and identically each time</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Abstraction:</strong> hiding detail behind a name so it can be used without being re-derived</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Weight your revision by exam weight, not by section count. Units 2 to 7 — variables, branches, loops, arrays, methods, classes — carry the majority of the questions. Units 15, 16 and 18 carry far fewer.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the dependency graph the rest of the course hangs off</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 310" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk212a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WHAT EACH UNIT IS LOAD-BEARING FOR</text><rect x="284" y="40" width="184" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="56.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Unit 2 — variables</text><text x="376" y="71.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">types, arithmetic</text><line x1="376" y1="80" x2="376" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="284" y="106" width="184" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="122.69999999999999" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Unit 3 — branches</text><text x="376" y="137.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">conditions, booleans</text><line x1="376" y1="146" x2="376" y2="170" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="284" y="172" width="184" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="188.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Units 4–5 — loops, arrays</text><text x="376" y="203.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">repetition over data</text><line x1="376" y1="212" x2="376" y2="236" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="284" y="238" width="184" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="254.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Units 6–7 — methods, classes</text><text x="376" y="269.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">naming and packaging it</text><rect x="30" y="106" width="152" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="106" y="122.69999999999999" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">exceptions (12)</text><text x="106" y="137.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a branch on failure</text><line x1="284" y1="126" x2="186" y2="126" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="30" y="172" width="152" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="106" y="188.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">collections (13)</text><text x="106" y="203.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">arrays that grow</text><line x1="284" y1="192" x2="186" y2="192" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="30" y="238" width="152" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="106" y="254.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">inheritance (11)</text><text x="106" y="269.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">classes reusing classes</text><line x1="284" y1="258" x2="186" y2="258" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="522" y="106" width="152" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="598" y="122.69999999999999" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">string handling</text><text x="598" y="137.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">branches + methods</text><line x1="468" y1="126" x2="518" y2="126" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="522" y="172" width="152" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="598" y="188.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">file I/O (9)</text><text x="598" y="203.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">loops over lines</text><line x1="468" y1="192" x2="518" y2="192" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><rect x="522" y="238" width="152" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="598" y="254.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">algorithms (17)</text><text x="598" y="269.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">loops you can count</text><line x1="468" y1="258" x2="518" y2="258" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk212a)"/><text x="376" y="298" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Everything later is these four boxes recombined — which is why time spent on Unit 2 is paid back twice.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — automation: the same calculation, exactly, every time</div><p>The case for a program is not that it is clever but that it is repeatable. Sales tax on a $12.99 item at 7.25%:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">double price = 12.99;
double taxRate = 0.0725;
double tax = price * taxRate;
System.out.printf("tax  = %.4f%n", tax);
System.out.printf("total = %.2f%n", price + tax);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
tax  = 0.9418
total = 13.93</pre><p>Two things worth noticing beyond the arithmetic. The exact tax is 0.941775, and <code>%.4f</code> shows 0.9418 — the format string rounds for display without changing the stored value, which is why the total uses the full precision and comes out at 13.93 rather than 13.9318 rounded twice.</p><p>And the point of the variables: change <code>taxRate</code> to 0.08 and every figure updates consistently. Done by hand across a hundred items, a rate change is a hundred chances to make an arithmetic slip; done here it is one character.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — abstraction: a name that hides a calculation you no longer have to do</div><p>Square roots are computed by repeated refinement. Here are two rounds of that refinement done by hand, next to the library call that does the whole thing:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">double n = 2;
double guess = 1;

guess = (guess + n / guess) / 2;
System.out.println("one step by hand:  " + guess);

guess = (guess + n / guess) / 2;
System.out.println("two steps by hand: " + guess);

System.out.println("Math.sqrt(2):      " + Math.sqrt(2));</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
one step by hand:  1.5
two steps by hand: 1.4166666666666665
Math.sqrt(2):      1.4142135623730951</pre><p>Two rounds of hand work gets three digits right. <code>Math.sqrt</code> gets sixteen, in one call, and you never had to know the method it used.</p><p>That is what abstraction buys: a name standing in for a procedure, so the procedure stops being your problem. The same move happens at every scale in this course — a method name hides a block of statements (Unit 6), a class name hides a set of fields and methods (Unit 7), and <code>System.out.println</code> has been hiding the entire output system since your first line of code.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>The commonest revision mistake here is treating all eighteen units as equally weighted because they are equally numbered. They are not. Units 2 to 7 carry the bulk of the questions <em>and</em> everything later is built from them, so a gap in Unit 2 costs marks twice — once in its own questions and again in every loop, array and method question that assumes it. If your time is limited, the correct order is not "start at 1 and go forwards", it is "make Units 2–7 solid first".</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_11":{"body":"<p>Two of the specific ways Java is unforgiving are worth isolating early, because they cause errors that read as mysterious. The first is that Java is <strong>case-sensitive</strong> everywhere: <code>Count</code>, <code>count</code> and <code>COUNT</code> are three different names, and <code>system.out.println</code> simply does not exist.</p><p>The second is that whitespace is free-form but <strong>punctuation is not</strong>. A missing semicolon, an unmatched brace, or a missing closing quote produces an error message that often points at the following line, because that is where the compiler finally noticed something was wrong.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">System.out.println("hi")     // missing ;   error reported on the NEXT line
if (x &gt; 5) {                 // missing }   error reported at end of file
String s = "unterminated ;   // missing "   error mentions an illegal character</pre><h3>Where the compiler points, and where the error is</h3><p>The two rules combine into a practical habit. Because Java is case-sensitive, an identifier that "looks right" may still be wrong, and the compiler will report it as an unknown symbol rather than a typo. Because punctuation errors are noticed late, the reported line is often one below the real fault. So the routine when a message makes no sense is: check the line above for a missing semicolon or brace, then check the spelling and capitalisation of every name on the reported line. Between them those two checks resolve most early compile errors.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Case sensitivity:</strong> the rule that upper and lower case letters are distinct in identifiers and keywords</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Syntax:</strong> the grammar rules of the language; violations stop compilation</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>When the compiler points at a line that looks fine, check the line <em>above</em> it. Missing semicolons and unclosed braces are almost always reported one line late.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_11="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a missing brace is reported at the end of the file, not at the brace</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 290" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk213a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WHERE THE COMPILER POINTS vs. WHERE THE FAULT IS</text><rect x="16" y="38" width="430" height="216" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="38" y="54" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><text x="46" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public class Roster {</text><text x="38" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="46" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;public static void main(String[] args) {</text><text x="38" y="102" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><text x="46" y="102" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;int students = 24;</text><text x="38" y="126" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><text x="46" y="126" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;if (students &gt; 20) {</text><text x="38" y="150" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="46" y="150" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;System.out.println("Split into two sections");</text><text x="38" y="174" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6</text><text x="46" y="174" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;System.out.println("Roster complete");</text><text x="38" y="198" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7</text><text x="46" y="198" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;}</text><text x="38" y="222" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="46" y="222" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}</text><rect x="42" y="116" width="258" height="20" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><rect x="42" y="212" width="40" height="20" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="478" y1="126" x2="306" y2="126" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk213a)"/><rect x="480" y="96" width="258" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="609" y="116.69999999999999" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">THE FAULT IS ON LINE 4</text><text x="609" y="130.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">this { is never closed —</text><text x="609" y="143.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">line 6 was meant to be inside it</text><line x1="478" y1="222" x2="90" y2="222" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk213a)"/><rect x="480" y="190" width="258" height="64" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="609" y="212.2" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JAVAC POINTS AT LINE 8</text><text x="609" y="226.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Roster.java:8: error:</text><text x="609" y="240.2" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">reached end of file while parsing</text><text x="376" y="276" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The reported line is the last place the fault could be detected, never the first place it existed.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — one wrong capital letter, and the message that follows</div><p>Java is case-sensitive everywhere — in keywords, in class names and in method names. This file has exactly one capital letter out of place:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Greeter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "Ada";
        System.out.Println("Hello, " + name);
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Greeter.java
Greeter.java:4: error: cannot find symbol
        System.out.Println("Hello, " + name);
                  ^
  symbol:   method Println(String)
  location: variable out of type PrintStream
1 error</pre><p>The message is worth decoding, because "cannot find symbol" is the message you will see most often in this course. <strong>symbol:</strong> names what it looked for — a method called <code>Println</code> taking a String. <strong>location:</strong> names where it looked — on <code>out</code>, which is a <code>PrintStream</code>. The caret sits under the dot before the name.</p><p>So the compiler is not saying "you made a typo"; it has no concept of a typo. It is saying "there is no such thing here". Whenever you see <code>cannot find symbol</code>, check spelling and capitalisation of the name under the caret before anything else.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — an unclosed brace, and why the line number is useless</div><p>Here the mistake is a missing closing brace for the <code>if</code> block on line 4:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Roster {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int students = 24;
        if (students &gt; 20) {
            System.out.println("Split into two sections");
        System.out.println("Roster complete");
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
$ javac Roster.java
Roster.java:8: error: reached end of file while parsing
}
 ^
1 error</pre><p>Trace what the compiler did. It opened a brace at line 1 for the class, another at line 2 for <code>main</code>, another at line 4 for the <code>if</code>. It then read three closing braces — lines 7 and 8 close the <code>if</code> and <code>main</code> — and reached the end of the file still owing one. Only at that moment could it know something was wrong, so line 8 is where it complains.</p><p>The reported line is therefore the <em>last</em> place the error could have been detected, not the first place it existed. Nothing on line 8 needs fixing. The habit that follows: for "reached end of file while parsing", count braces from the top; for <code>';' expected</code>, look at the end of the line named and at the line above it.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Two messages, two fixed responses, and between them they cover most Unit 1 compile errors. <code>cannot find symbol</code> ⇒ a name is misspelled, miscapitalised, or not declared yet — look at the token under the caret. <code>';' expected</code> or <code>reached end of file while parsing</code> ⇒ punctuation — look at the end of the named line and upwards, never below it. Answering "which line has the error" by trusting the reported number is the mistake these questions are built to catch.</p></div>"},"D286/u1/z1_12":{"body":"<p>This section puts 1.2 and 1.3 together in one worked program: read two names, join them, print a sentence. Nothing new appears — it exists so you see the whole loop of import, class, main, input, output at real size.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import java.util.Scanner;

public class MarriedNames {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter first name:");
        String first = scnr.next();
        System.out.println("Enter last name:");
        String last  = scnr.next();

        System.out.println("Mr. and Mrs. " + first + " " + last);
    }
}</pre><p>The <code>+</code> here is doing something different from arithmetic: when either operand is a String, <code>+</code> concatenates instead of adding. That double meaning is set up properly in 2.15, and it is the reason <code>"total: " + 3 + 4</code> prints <code>total: 34</code> rather than <code>total: 7</code>.</p><h3>Why this example is placed here</h3><p>The program is deliberately small so that the whole loop is visible at once: import at the top, class, <code>main</code>, read, compute, print. It is also the first place the double meaning of <code>+</code> appears — joining text rather than adding numbers — which is worth noticing now because it becomes a trap in 2.15. And the choice of <code>next()</code> over <code>nextLine()</code> is doing real work: the program depends on reading exactly one word at a time, and swapping the two would break it in a way that produces no error message at all.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Concatenation:</strong> joining strings with +, producing a new String</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>next():</strong> Scanner method that reads a single whitespace-delimited word</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Prompt:</strong> output printed to tell the user what input is expected</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Use <code>next()</code> for one word and <code>nextLine()</code> for a whole line including spaces. A full name typed as "Ada Lovelace" needs <code>nextLine()</code>; two separate <code>next()</code> calls would split it — which is exactly what this program relies on.</p></div><i data-tba_z1_12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the data path through the married-names program</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 292" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk214a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FROM KEYSTROKES TO ONE LINE OF OUTPUT</text><rect x="16" y="40" width="250" height="70" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="28" y="60" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what the user typed</text><text x="28" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Ada⏎Lovelace⏎</text><text x="146" y="102" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">one stream of characters</text><line x1="268" y1="75" x2="300" y2="75" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk214a)"/><rect x="302" y="52" width="130" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="367" y="71.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">scnr.next()</text><text x="367" y="86.7" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">stops at whitespace</text><line x1="434" y1="75" x2="466" y2="75" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk214a)"/><rect x="468" y="40" width="268" height="70" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="480" y="60" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">two String variables</text><rect x="480" y="70" width="118" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="488" y="89" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">first = "Ada"</text><rect x="606" y="70" width="118" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="614" y="89" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">last = "Lovelace"</text><line x1="602" y1="112" x2="602" y2="136" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk214a)"/><rect x="150" y="138" width="452" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="162" y="158" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the + operator joins EXACTLY what it is given</text><text x="162" y="182" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"Mr. and Mrs. " + first + " " + last</text><rect x="344" y="166" width="30" height="24" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="359" y="212" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">that quoted space is the only space in the printed line</text><line x1="376" y1="220" x2="376" y2="240" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk214a)"/><rect x="200" y="242" width="352" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="263.2" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Mr. and Mrs. Ada Lovelace</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — running the program, prompts and all</div><p>The complete program, run with <code>Ada</code> and <code>Lovelace</code> typed on separate lines:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.Scanner;

public class MarriedNames {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter first name:");
        String first = scnr.next();
        System.out.println("Enter last name:");
        String last  = scnr.next();
        System.out.println("Mr. and Mrs. " + first + " " + last);
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
Enter first name:
Enter last name:
Mr. and Mrs. Ada Lovelace</pre><p>Three lines of output, and the prompts are two of them — a prompt is just output, so it counts in "what does this print" questions. The typed input itself is <em>not</em> in the program's output; it appears on your screen because the terminal echoes what you type, not because the program printed it. That distinction catches people out on trace questions that supply the input separately.</p><p>Count the spaces in the last line. There is one after <code>Mrs.</code> because the literal <code>"Mr. and Mrs. "</code> ends with a space, and one between the names because <code>+ " " +</code> put it there. Remove that middle piece and the output becomes <code>Mr. and Mrs. AdaLovelace</code>.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the + that adds and the + that joins</div><p>The same symbol does two different jobs, and which one it does depends on the operand types — evaluated strictly left to right.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("total: " + 3 + 4);
System.out.println("total: " + (3 + 4));
System.out.println(3 + 4 + " total");</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
total: 34
total: 7
7 total</pre><p>Line 1: the leftmost <code>+</code> has a String on its left, so it concatenates, producing <code>"total: 3"</code>. The next <code>+</code> now also has a String on its left, so it concatenates too, giving <code>"total: 34"</code>. No addition ever happened.</p><p>Line 2: parentheses force <code>3 + 4</code> to be evaluated first. Both operands are <code>int</code>, so that <code>+</code> adds, giving 7, and only then is the 7 joined onto the text.</p><p>Line 3: the leftmost <code>+</code> has two ints, so it adds — giving 7 — and the String appears only in the second <code>+</code>. Same three values, three arrangements, three answers. The rule to carry: once a String has appeared on the left, every <code>+</code> after it in that expression joins rather than adds.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_1":{"body":"<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a named box in memory that holds one value at a time. Three things happen to it, and keeping them apart explains most early confusion: you <em>declare</em> it (give it a name and a type), you <em>initialize</em> it (put the first value in), and you <em>assign</em> to it (replace whatever is there).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int x;          // declaration — the box exists, nothing in it yet
x = 5;          // assignment — 5 goes in
int y = 7;      // declaration + initialization together
y = 9;          // assignment — 7 is gone, replaced by 9</pre><p>The equals sign is the source of the confusion. It is not a statement of fact, it is an instruction: <em>work out the right-hand side, then put the result in the left</em>. That is why <code>x = x + 1</code> is perfectly sensible in code and nonsense in algebra.</p><h3>Why the three operations are worth separating</h3><p>Declaration, initialization and assignment get conflated because a single line often does two of them. Keeping them apart explains error messages precisely: "already defined" means you declared twice, "might not have been initialized" means you declared but never gave a value, and a wrong result with no message at all means you assigned something you did not intend. The equals sign is not a claim of equality but an instruction — evaluate the right, store into the left — which is why <code>x = x + 1</code> is ordinary code and nonsense as algebra.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Variable:</strong> a named location in memory holding one value of a declared type</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Declaration:</strong> names a variable and its type</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Initialization:</strong> giving a variable its first value at the moment of declaration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Assignment:</strong> storing a value into an existing variable, replacing what was there</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Assignment copies the <strong>value</strong> for primitives. <code>int b = a;</code> gives b its own copy, so changing <code>a</code> afterwards leaves <code>b</code> alone. For objects, what gets copied is the reference — the same distinction returns in 2.15 with Strings and again in Unit 7.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — declare, initialize, assign — and what the equals sign really means</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 310" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk215a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ONE SLOT IN MEMORY, THREE THINGS THAT HAPPEN TO IT</text><text x="16" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int price;</text><text x="16" y="74" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">DECLARE</text><text x="16" y="88" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the slot exists</text><rect x="16" y="98" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="76" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">?</text><text x="76" y="154" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">price</text><line x1="146" y1="118" x2="196" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk215a)"/><text x="202" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">price = 1299;</text><text x="202" y="74" fill="var(--success)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">INITIALIZE</text><text x="202" y="88" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">first value goes in</text><rect x="202" y="98" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="262" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1299</text><text x="262" y="154" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">price</text><line x1="332" y1="118" x2="382" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk215a)"/><text x="388" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">price = 1499;</text><text x="388" y="74" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">ASSIGN</text><text x="388" y="88" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">1299 is gone forever</text><rect x="388" y="98" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="448" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1499</text><text x="448" y="154" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">price</text><line x1="518" y1="118" x2="568" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk215a)"/><text x="574" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">price = price + 100;</text><text x="574" y="74" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">ASSIGN</text><text x="574" y="88" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">right side first, then store</text><rect x="574" y="98" width="120" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="634" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1599</text><text x="634" y="154" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">price</text><rect x="16" y="176" width="720" height="122" rx="9" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="30" y="200" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">= is an instruction, not a claim of equality</text><text x="30" y="232" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">price&#160;&#160;=&#160;&#160;price + 100</text><line x1="58" y1="240" x2="58" y2="258" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk215a)"/><text x="30" y="274" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">2. store here, last</text><line x1="184" y1="240" x2="184" y2="258" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk215a)"/><text x="156" y="274" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">1. work this out first</text><text x="420" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">That order is why x = x + 1 is ordinary code</text><text x="420" y="226" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">and nonsense as algebra — and why a swap</text><text x="420" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">needs a third variable: the moment you write</text><text x="420" y="270" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">front = back, the old front is unrecoverable.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — following one variable through every operation</div><p>Three lines, three different things happening to the same slot. Track the value after each statement.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int quantity = 3;
int unitCents = 1299;
System.out.println("quantity=" + quantity + " unitCents=" + unitCents);

quantity = quantity + 2;
System.out.println("after +2: quantity=" + quantity);

int totalCents = quantity * unitCents;
System.out.println("totalCents=" + totalCents);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
quantity=3 unitCents=1299
after +2: quantity=5
totalCents=6495</pre><p>Line 1 is a <strong>declaration with initialization</strong>: the name <code>quantity</code> and the type <code>int</code> are fixed, and 3 is put in. Line 4 is a pure <strong>assignment</strong>: the right-hand side <code>quantity + 2</code> is evaluated using the <em>current</em> contents (3), giving 5, and only then is 5 stored back. The old 3 is not recoverable — nothing kept a copy.</p><p>Line 7 declares a new slot and initialises it from an expression: 5 × 1299 = 6495. Note that <code>quantity</code> was read here, not changed; reading a variable never alters it.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why swapping two variables needs a third</div><p>The order "evaluate right, then store into left" has a consequence that is examined regularly. Swapping two values by the obvious route does not work:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int front = 42, back = 17;
System.out.println("before: front=" + front + " back=" + back);

front = back;    // front is now 17 — the 42 is gone
back  = front;   // back = 17, because that is what front now holds
System.out.println("naive : front=" + front + " back=" + back);

int a = 42, b = 17;
int temp = a;    // park the value that is about to be overwritten
a = b;
b = temp;
System.out.println("temp  : a=" + a + " b=" + b);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
before: front=42 back=17
naive : front=17 back=17
temp  : a=17 b=42</pre><p>Trace the naive version one statement at a time. After <code>front = back</code>, <code>front</code> holds 17 and the 42 no longer exists anywhere — a slot holds exactly one value, and the previous one is not stored elsewhere. So the next line copies 17 onto 17.</p><p>The three-line version works because <code>temp</code> preserves the value that is about to be destroyed. This is the standard swap idiom, and the reason it needs a third variable is precisely the assignment rule from the figure.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Two different confusions live on this one symbol. First, <code>=</code> is assignment and <code>==</code> is comparison — <code>if (x = 5)</code> does not even compile for an <code>int</code>, because an assignment produces an <code>int</code> where <code>if</code> demands a <code>boolean</code>. Second, assignment does not create a link. <code>int copy = original;</code> takes a snapshot; changing <code>original</code> afterwards leaves <code>copy</code> alone. Running <code>int original = 100; int copy = original; original = 250;</code> prints <code>original=250 copy=100</code>.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_2":{"body":"<p><code>int</code> is the default choice for whole numbers, and the one you will use most. It holds a value from about -2.1 billion to +2.1 billion, which is enough for counts, indexes and years, but not for populations of the world or amounts in cents.</p><p>The detail that catches people is <strong>what happens before you assign anything</strong>, and it depends on where the variable lives:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Demo {
    static int field;                 // FIELD  — automatically 0

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int local;                    // LOCAL  — no default at all
        System.out.println(field);    // prints 0
        System.out.println(local);    // COMPILE ERROR:
                                      // variable local might not have been initialized
    }
}</pre><p>The compiler is deliberately strict here. It rejects any path where a local <em>might</em> be unread, even one you can prove never happens — which is why an <code>if</code> without an <code>else</code> can trigger it.</p><h3>Where the compiler is strict on purpose</h3><p>The rule that locals must be assigned before use is stricter than it first appears: the compiler rejects any path where the variable <em>might</em> be unread, even one you can prove never happens. An <code>if</code> without an <code>else</code> triggers it, because as far as the compiler is concerned the condition might be false. The fix is to give the variable a value at declaration, or to make every branch assign one. Fields escape this because they are given type defaults automatically — which is the whole distinction the exam tests.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>int:</strong> a 32-bit whole-number type, roughly -2.1 to +2.1 billion</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Field:</strong> a variable belonging to a class or object; gets a default value automatically</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Local variable:</strong> a variable declared inside a method; has no default and must be assigned before use</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Reading a local variable before assigning it is a <strong>compile</strong> error, not a runtime one — the message is "variable might not have been initialized". Remember it as: <em>fields get defaults, locals do not.</em></p></div><i data-d286f_valref="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>What a variable actually holds</h3><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a primitive stores the value; a reference stores the way to it</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 580 168" style="width:100%; max-width:580px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk67a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="30" y="40" width="200" height="118" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="130" y="30" fill="#6366f1" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PRIMITIVE — holds the value</text><rect x="56" y="62" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="89" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="89" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a</text><rect x="140" y="62" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="173" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="173" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">b</text><text x="130" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">int b = a  copies the 5</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="250" height="118" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="425" y="30" fill="#b45309" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">REFERENCE — holds an arrow</text><rect x="318" y="62" width="60" height="34" rx="7" fill="#f59e0b18" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="348" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x</text><rect x="318" y="106" width="60" height="34" rx="7" fill="#f59e0b18" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="348" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">y</text><rect x="462" y="78" width="74" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="499" y="105" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">object</text><line x1="378" y1="79" x2="462" y2="96" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk67a)"/><line x1="378" y1="123" x2="462" y2="106" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk67a)"/><text x="425" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">both names point at the SAME object</text></svg></div></div><p>This one distinction explains a whole family of exam questions. Assigning a primitive copies the value, so the two are independent afterwards. Assigning a reference copies the <em>arrow</em>, so both names reach the same object and a change through one is visible through the other.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Primitive (int, double, char, boolean…)</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Reference (String, arrays, objects)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">A variable holds</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the value itself</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the address of an object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>b = a</code> copies</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the value</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the arrow, not the object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Changing b affects a?</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>No</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Yes</strong>, if you change the object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Default value of a field</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0 / 0.0 / '\u0000' / false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>null</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Compared with <code>==</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">values</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>addresses</strong> — use <code>.equals</code> for content</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A <em>local</em> variable has no default at all — Java refuses to compile a read before assignment. Only fields are auto-initialised, and that difference is examined directly.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why a local variable "might not have been initialized"</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 318" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk216a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">DEFINITE ASSIGNMENT — THE COMPILER CHECKS EVERY PATH, NOT THE LIKELY ONE</text><rect x="16" y="38" width="356" height="250" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="30" y="60" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">if with no else</text><text x="30" y="82" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int shipping;</text><text x="30" y="98" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (weightKg &gt; 10) {</text><text x="30" y="114" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;shipping = 15;</text><text x="30" y="130" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}</text><text x="30" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">System.out.println(shipping);</text><circle cx="120" cy="176" r="15" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="120" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">if</text><line x1="108" y1="188" x2="62" y2="212" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk216a)"/><text x="72" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">true</text><line x1="132" y1="188" x2="250" y2="212" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk216a)"/><text x="206" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">false</text><rect x="20" y="214" width="90" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="65" y="234.325" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">shipping = 15</text><rect x="206" y="214" width="96" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="254" y="234.325" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">nothing runs</text><text x="194" y="272" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one path leaves the slot empty ⇒ javac refuses</text><rect x="388" y="38" width="348" height="250" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="402" y="60" fill="var(--success)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">if with an else</text><text x="402" y="82" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int shipping;</text><text x="402" y="98" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (weightKg &gt; 10) {</text><text x="402" y="114" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;shipping = 15;</text><text x="402" y="130" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">} else { shipping = 5; }</text><text x="402" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">System.out.println(shipping);</text><circle cx="492" cy="176" r="15" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="492" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">if</text><line x1="480" y1="188" x2="434" y2="212" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk216a)"/><text x="444" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">true</text><line x1="504" y1="188" x2="622" y2="212" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk216a)"/><text x="578" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">false</text><rect x="392" y="214" width="90" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="437" y="234.325" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">shipping = 15</text><rect x="578" y="214" width="90" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="623" y="234.325" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">shipping = 5</text><text x="562" y="272" fill="var(--success)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">every path assigns ⇒ javac is satisfied</text><text x="376" y="306" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Fields escape the check entirely — they are given a type default before any code runs.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — fields get defaults, locals get a compile error</div><p>Same four types, two different places to declare them. First as fields of the class:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">public class Demo {
    static int totalOrders;
    static double averageValue;
    static String lastCustomer;
    static boolean isOpen;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(totalOrders);
        System.out.println(averageValue);
        System.out.println(lastCustomer);
        System.out.println(isOpen);
    }
}</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
0
0.0
null
false</pre><p>Those four values are the type defaults: 0 for whole-number types, 0.0 for decimals, <code>null</code> for any reference type, <code>false</code> for <code>boolean</code>. Nothing in the program assigned them; the JVM did it while creating the class.</p><p>Now move one of them inside <code>main</code>, making it a local:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int shipping;
System.out.println(shipping);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
Demo.java:5: error: variable shipping might not have been initialized
System.out.println(shipping);
                   ^
1 error</pre><p>No <code>.class</code> file, no output, no default. The distinction is not stylistic: fields are auto-initialised, locals are not, and reading an unassigned local is caught at compile time rather than producing garbage at run time.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the branch that "obviously" always runs, and the compiler that disagrees</div><p>Here <code>weightKg</code> is 12, so any reader can see the branch is taken and <code>shipping</code> ends up as 15. javac still refuses.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int weightKg = 12;
int shipping;
if (weightKg &gt; 10) {
    shipping = 15;
}
System.out.println(shipping);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
Demo.java:9: error: variable shipping might not have been initialized
System.out.println(shipping);
                   ^
1 error</pre><p>The rule the compiler applies is <em>definite assignment</em>: a local must be assigned on <strong>every</strong> path that reaches the read. It deliberately does not evaluate <code>weightKg > 10</code> to see whether the false path is possible — it only checks that a false path exists in the code.</p><p>Adding the missing path fixes it:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int weightKg = 12;
int shipping;
if (weightKg &gt; 10) {
    shipping = 15;
} else {
    shipping = 5;
}
System.out.println(shipping);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
15</pre><p>The other fix is to initialise at the declaration — <code>int shipping = 0;</code> — which removes the error by making every path start with a value. Both are legitimate; the second is safer when more branches are added later.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Remember the two halves as one sentence: <strong>fields get type defaults, locals get a compile error</strong>. The exact message, <code>variable x might not have been initialized</code>, is quoted verbatim in questions, and the word "might" is the giveaway — it means the compiler found <em>a</em> path, not necessarily the path your data takes. If a question shows an <code>if</code> without an <code>else</code> assigning a local, the answer is a compile error, not the value the branch would have produced.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_3":{"body":"<p>An <strong>identifier</strong> is any name you invent — variables, methods, classes. Two separate sets of rules apply to them, and exam questions deliberately mix the two up: what the compiler <em>enforces</em>, and what programmers merely <em>agree on</em>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// LEGAL — the compiler accepts all of these
int count;      int _total;     int $rate;
int myValue2;   int Total;      int CLASS;

// ILLEGAL — the compiler rejects all of these
int 2ndPlace;   // may not start with a digit
int my-score;   // hyphens are not allowed
int class;      // reserved word
int my score;   // no spaces</pre><p>Conventions sit on top of that. Nothing breaks if you ignore them, but every reader and grader expects <code>camelCase</code> for variables and methods, <code>PascalCase</code> for classes, and <code>ALL_CAPS</code> for the constants you will meet in 2.9.</p><h3>Rules versus conventions</h3><p>Identifier questions are nearly always testing which of the two you are looking at. The <em>rules</em> are enforced by the compiler: start with a letter, underscore or dollar sign, no spaces, not a reserved word. Break one and the code will not compile. The <em>conventions</em> are agreements the compiler ignores entirely — camelCase for variables, PascalCase for classes, ALL_CAPS for constants. Break one and the code runs perfectly and reads badly. A question asking which name "will not compile" is about rules; one asking which is "best" or "conventional" is about style.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Identifier:</strong> a programmer-chosen name for a variable, method or class</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reserved word:</strong> a name Java has claimed for itself, such as class, int, for or new — never usable as an identifier</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>camelCase:</strong> the convention for variables and methods: first word lowercase, later words capitalized</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Watch the wording. <code>int Total;</code> is <strong>legal</strong> but breaks convention; <code>int 1st;</code> is <strong>illegal</strong>. If a question asks which are "valid", it is asking about the rules, not the style. And identifiers are case sensitive, so <code>count</code> and <code>Count</code> are two different variables.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the two filters every identifier passes through</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 382" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk217a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">TWO SEPARATE FILTERS: ONE THE COMPILER ENFORCES, ONE PEOPLE DO</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="152" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="376" y="59.85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a name you invented</text><line x1="376" y1="72" x2="376" y2="92" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk217a)"/><rect x="206" y="94" width="340" height="128" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="376" y="114" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FILTER 1 — THE RULES (javac)</text><text x="220" y="136" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">1. first character is a letter, _ or $</text><text x="220" y="154" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">2. after that: letters, digits, _ or $ only</text><text x="220" y="172" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">3. not a reserved word (class, int, for, new…)</text><text x="220" y="190" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">4. not a lone _  (a keyword since Java 9)</text><text x="220" y="210" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">break one ⇒ the file does not compile</text><line x1="376" y1="222" x2="376" y2="242" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk217a)"/><rect x="206" y="244" width="340" height="106" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="376" y="264" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FILTER 2 — THE CONVENTIONS (people)</text><text x="220" y="286" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">camelCase for variables and methods</text><text x="220" y="304" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">PascalCase for classes</text><text x="220" y="322" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">ALL_CAPS for constants</text><text x="220" y="340" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">break one ⇒ it compiles and reads badly</text><text x="96" y="114" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">rejected by javac</text><rect x="24" y="126" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="32" y="141" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2ndPlace</text><rect x="24" y="152" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="32" y="167" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">my-score</text><rect x="24" y="178" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="32" y="193" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">class</text><rect x="24" y="204" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="32" y="219" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">my score</text><rect x="24" y="230" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="32" y="245" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">_</text><text x="660" y="114" fill="var(--success)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">accepted by javac</text><rect x="586" y="126" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="594" y="141" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">count</text><rect x="586" y="152" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="594" y="167" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">_total</text><rect x="586" y="178" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="594" y="193" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">$rate</text><rect x="586" y="204" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="594" y="219" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">myValue2</text><rect x="586" y="230" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="594" y="245" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Total</text><rect x="586" y="256" width="148" height="20" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="594" y="271" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">CLASS</text><text x="660" y="302" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the last two are legal</text><text x="660" y="316" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">and break convention</text><text x="376" y="370" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">"Which will not compile?" is filter 1. "Which is best?" is filter 2. Read which one the question is asking.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — sorting a list of names into legal, illegal and merely ugly</div><p>Take six names the compiler accepts. Nothing here is good style, but every one of them compiles:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int count = 1;
int _total = 2;
int $rate = 3;
int myValue2 = 4;
int Total = 5;
int CLASS = 6;
System.out.println(count + _total + $rate + myValue2 + Total + CLASS);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
21</pre><p>The sum 1+2+3+4+5+6 = 21 proves all six exist as separate variables. Now the illegal ones, each with the real message javac produces:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
int 2ndPlace = 5;
  error: not a statement          — a name may not start with a digit

int class = 5;
  error: not a statement          — class is a reserved word
  error: &lt;identifier&gt; expected

int _ = 5;
  error: as of release 9, '_' is a keyword, and may not be used as an identifier</pre><p>Notice how unhelpful these messages are compared with the missing-semicolon one. The compiler cannot say "bad identifier" because, having failed to read a name, it no longer knows what kind of statement it is looking at — which is why one bad name can produce four or five errors at once. Fix the first and the rest usually vanish.</p><p>And <code>CLASS</code> is worth a second look: it is legal, because only the exact lowercase <code>class</code> is reserved. <code>Total</code> and <code>CLASS</code> are filter-1 legal and filter-2 wrong.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — case sensitivity makes two names, not one</div><p>Because identifiers are case-sensitive, a capitalisation slip does not produce an error — it produces a second, unrelated variable.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int totalPrice = 120;
int totalprice = 7;
System.out.println("totalPrice=" + totalPrice);
System.out.println("totalprice=" + totalprice);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
totalPrice=120
totalprice=7</pre><p>Two declarations, two slots, two values — the compiler sees no relationship between them at all. That is the good case, where both are declared. The dangerous case is a program that declares <code>totalPrice</code> and then reads <code>totalprice</code>, which produces <code>cannot find symbol</code> and sends you hunting for a missing declaration when the real fault is one capital letter.</p><p>The same rule applies to everything: <code>String</code> and <code>string</code> are not the same type, <code>System</code> and <code>system</code> are not the same class, and <code>println</code> and <code>Println</code> are not the same method.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Three specific traps here. <strong>Reserved words</strong> — <code>class</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>new</code>, <code>static</code>, <code>void</code> — are illegal as names, but capitalised versions like <code>Class</code> or <code>CLASS</code> are perfectly legal, which questions exploit. <strong>A single underscore</strong> <code>_</code> was legal in old Java and has been a keyword since Java 9, so it is now rejected. And <strong><code>true</code>, <code>false</code> and <code>null</code></strong> are not technically keywords but are still reserved literals, so they cannot be used as identifiers either.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_4":{"body":"<p>Arithmetic in Java follows the precedence you already know from maths, with one addition and one trap. Multiplication, division and <strong>modulo</strong> bind tighter than addition and subtraction, and parentheses beat everything.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">2 + 3 * 4        = 14      // * before +
10 - 4 / 2       = 8       // / before -
10 + 7 % 3       = 11      // % ranks with * and /
-3 + 5           = 2       // unary minus applies to 3 first
(2 + 3) * 4      = 20      // parentheses win</pre><p>The trap is <strong>equal precedence</strong>. When two operators rank the same, Java works strictly left to right — it does not group them the way your eye wants to:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">20 / 5 * 2       = 8       // (20/5)*2, NOT 20/(5*2)
100 / 10 / 2     = 5       // (100/10)/2
10 - 4 - 3       = 3       // (10-4)-3</pre><h3>Precedence before arithmetic</h3><p>Expression questions are decided before any arithmetic happens, by the order the operators bind in. Parentheses first, then multiplication, division and modulo left to right, then addition and subtraction. The consequence that costs marks is the invisible division bar: a formula written as a fraction has implied parentheses round the whole numerator, so <code>(a + b + c) / 3</code> is right and <code>a + b + c / 3</code> divides only the last term. When a computed average is wildly too small, that is almost always the cause.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Operator precedence:</strong> the order in which operators are applied when an expression has several</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Associativity:</strong> the direction operators of equal precedence are applied — in Java, left to right</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Modulo (%):</strong> the remainder after integer division; ranks with multiplication and division</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>20 / 5 * 2</code> is <strong>8</strong>, not 2 — the most commonly missed line in this unit, because people read the whole <code>5 * 2</code> as the divisor. Note too that <code>^</code> is <strong>bitwise XOR</strong>, not a power operator: use <code>Math.pow</code> or plain multiplication.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why 20 / 5 * 2 is 8 and not 2</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 330" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk218a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">EQUAL PRECEDENCE MEANS STRICTLY LEFT TO RIGHT</text><rect x="16" y="38" width="356" height="150" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="194" y="60" fill="var(--success)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what Java does</text><text x="194" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">20 / 5 * 2</text><line x1="112" y1="96" x2="172" y2="96" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="142" y="110" fill="var(--success)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">step 1</text><line x1="194" y1="116" x2="194" y2="132" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk218a)"/><text x="194" y="152" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4 * 2</text><line x1="240" y1="147" x2="276" y2="147" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk218a)"/><text x="310" y="152" fill="var(--success)" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="194" y="178" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">left operator first, always</text><rect x="388" y="38" width="348" height="150" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="562" y="60" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what the eye wants</text><text x="562" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">20 / (5 * 2)</text><line x1="538" y1="96" x2="610" y2="96" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="574" y="110" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">step 1</text><line x1="562" y1="116" x2="562" y2="132" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk218a)"/><text x="562" y="152" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">20 / 10</text><line x1="614" y1="147" x2="650" y2="147" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk218a)"/><text x="684" y="152" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="562" y="178" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">only if you write the brackets</text><rect x="16" y="200" width="720" height="118" rx="9" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="30" y="222" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the binding order, tightest first</text><text x="30" y="244" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><text x="56" y="244" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">( )</text><text x="250" y="244" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">parentheses</text><text x="430" y="244" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">inside out</text><text x="30" y="259" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="56" y="259" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">unary  -x  !x  (cast)</text><text x="250" y="259" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sign, negation, casts</text><text x="430" y="259" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">right to left</text><text x="30" y="274" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><text x="56" y="274" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">*  /  %</text><text x="250" y="274" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">multiplicative</text><text x="430" y="274" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">LEFT TO RIGHT</text><text x="30" y="289" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><text x="56" y="289" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">+  -</text><text x="250" y="289" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">additive</text><text x="430" y="289" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">LEFT TO RIGHT</text><text x="30" y="304" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="56" y="304" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">=  +=  -=  *=  /=</text><text x="250" y="304" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">assignment, last of all</text><text x="430" y="304" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">right to left</text><text x="560" y="268" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">% ranks with * and /,</text><text x="560" y="284" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not with + and -</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — eight expressions, evaluated by the rules rather than by eye</div><p>Work each one out before reading the output. The only two rules needed are: higher precedence binds first, and equal precedence goes left to right.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(2 + 3 * 4);
System.out.println(10 - 4 / 2);
System.out.println(10 + 7 % 3);
System.out.println(-3 + 5);
System.out.println((2 + 3) * 4);
System.out.println(20 / 5 * 2);
System.out.println(100 / 10 / 2);
System.out.println(10 - 4 - 3);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
14
8
11
2
20
8
5
3</pre><p>The first four are precedence: <code>3 * 4</code> before the <code>+</code> gives 12 + 2 = 14; <code>4 / 2</code> before the <code>-</code> gives 10 − 2 = 8; <code>7 % 3</code> is 1, ranking with <code>*</code> and <code>/</code>, so 10 + 1 = 11; unary minus applies to the 3 alone, so −3 + 5 = 2.</p><p>The last three are associativity, and they are the ones that catch people. <code>20 / 5 * 2</code> is <code>(20 / 5) * 2</code> = 8, <em>not</em> <code>20 / (5 * 2)</code> = 2. <code>100 / 10 / 2</code> is <code>(100 / 10) / 2</code> = 5, not <code>100 / (10 / 2)</code> = 20. <code>10 - 4 - 3</code> is <code>(10 - 4) - 3</code> = 3, not <code>10 - (4 - 3)</code> = 9. In each case the wrong reading is the one that groups the two rightmost operands.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the division bar that exists on paper and not in code</div><p>A formula written as a fraction has invisible brackets round the whole numerator. Typing it out left to right loses them, and the answer stays plausible enough not to notice.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">double a = 88, b = 92, c = 79;
System.out.println(a + b + c / 3);
System.out.println((a + b + c) / 3);

double celsius = 37.0;
System.out.println(celsius * 9 / 5 + 32);
System.out.println(celsius * (9 / 5) + 32);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
206.33333333333334
86.33333333333333
98.6
69.0</pre><p>Line 1: <code>/</code> binds tighter, so only <code>c / 3</code> is divided — the expression means 88 + 92 + (79/3) = 206.33. Line 2 restores the numerator brackets and gives the real average, 86.33.</p><p>Lines 3 and 4 show the mirror-image mistake. <code>celsius * 9 / 5</code> is <code>(37.0 * 9) / 5</code> = 333 / 5 = 66.6, plus 32 = 98.6 — correct. Adding "helpful" brackets around <code>9 / 5</code> changes it into integer division: <code>9 / 5</code> with two int literals is 1, so the result collapses to 37 + 32 = 69.0.</p><p>The lesson is not "always add brackets" but "add brackets where the paper formula has them, and check what types are inside the ones you add".</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>When a computed value is wildly too small, check for a missing numerator bracket; when it is wildly too large, check for a missing denominator bracket. On the exam, rewrite the expression with every implied bracket made explicit before evaluating anything — <code>20 / 5 * 2</code> becomes <code>(20 / 5) * 2</code> on your paper, and the arithmetic then has only one possible reading. Note also that <code>^</code> is bitwise XOR, never a power operator: use <code>Math.pow</code> or repeated multiplication.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_5":{"body":"<p>This is the single most expensive rule in the unit, because it produces a wrong answer <em>silently</em>. When both operands of <code>/</code> are integer types, Java performs <strong>integer division</strong>: it computes the whole-number part and throws the remainder away. No warning, no exception.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int a = 7, b = 2;

int    r1 = a / b;              // 3     truncated
double r2 = a / b;              // 3.0   the division happened FIRST, in int
double r3 = (double) a / b;     // 3.5   CORRECT — cast BEFORE dividing
double r4 = (double)(a / b);    // 3.0   WRONG — cast AFTER the damage
double r5 = a / 2.0;            // 3.5   one double operand is enough</pre><p>Look carefully at <code>r2</code>. Storing the result in a <code>double</code> does not help, because the division has already finished by the time the assignment happens. Widening cannot recover a digit that was discarded — which is why the fix is always to change one <em>operand</em>, never the result type.</p><p>Truncation also moves toward zero rather than downward, so it behaves differently from rounding on negatives:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">  7 / 2  =  3        // not 3.5, and not 4
 -7 / 2  = -3        // toward zero, so not -4</pre><h3>The one rule that decides the most questions</h3><p>Integer division discarding the remainder is the single most examined behaviour in this unit, and it is silent — no warning, no error, just a smaller number. The rule is that <code>int / int</code> is always an <code>int</code>, so <code>7 / 2</code> is 3 and <code>1 / 2</code> is 0. Declaring the result as a <code>double</code> does not save you, because the truncation happens first and the whole number is then widened. To get a fractional answer at least one operand must be a decimal — either a literal like <code>2.0</code> or a cast like <code>(double) a</code>.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Integer division:</strong> division where both operands are integer types; the fractional part is discarded</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Truncation:</strong> discarding the fractional part, always toward zero</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Cast:</strong> explicitly converting a value to another type, written (double) x</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>double avg = sum / count;</code> with two ints looks right and even produces a <code>double</code> — but a truncated one. This one line is behind almost every wrong-average answer. The fix is <code>(double) sum / count</code>: <strong>cast one operand before the division</strong>.</p></div><i data-d286f_prec="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>Precedence, drawn</h3><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — 2 + 3 * 4 as an expression tree</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 560 228" style="width:100%; max-width:560px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk66a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><line x1="150" y1="52" x2="96" y2="116" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="150" y1="52" x2="204" y2="116" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="204" y1="134" x2="168" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="204" y1="134" x2="244" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="150" cy="52" r="19" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="150" y="57" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">+</text><circle cx="96" cy="116" r="19" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="96" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><circle cx="204" cy="116" r="19" fill="#f59e0b44" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="204" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">*</text><circle cx="168" cy="190" r="19" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="168" y="195" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><circle cx="244" cy="190" r="19" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="244" y="195" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><text x="300" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">*  binds tighter, so it is</text><text x="300" y="134" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">DEEPER in the tree and</text><text x="300" y="152" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">evaluated first</text><text x="300" y="184" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2 + 3 * 4  =  14</text><text x="300" y="208" fill="#6366f1" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(2 + 3) * 4  =  20</text></svg></div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Level</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Operators</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Associativity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1 (tightest)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>()</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>++  --  (cast)  !  unary -</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">right to left</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>*  /  %</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">left to right</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>+  -</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">left to right</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>&lt;  &lt;=  &gt;  &gt;=</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">left to right</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>==  !=</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">left to right</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>&amp;&amp;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">left to right</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">8</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>||</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">left to right</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">9 (loosest)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>=  +=  -=  *=  /=  %=</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>right to left</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>When unsure, add brackets. They cost nothing at run time and they remove the entire category of precedence bugs — and the exam cannot mark you down for being explicit.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — int / int truncates before anything else can help</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 288" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk219a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WHERE YOU PUT THE CAST DECIDES WHETHER THE .5 SURVIVES</text><text x="20" y="52" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the expression</text><text x="330" y="52" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">left</text><text x="386" y="52" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">right</text><text x="486" y="52" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what actually runs</text><text x="596" y="52" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">result</text><line x1="16" y1="58" x2="736" y2="58" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="20" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int r1 = a / b;</text><rect x="306" y="65" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="330" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><rect x="362" y="65" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="386" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><line x1="414" y1="74" x2="434" y2="74" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk219a)"/><text x="500" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7 / 2 in int</text><rect x="572" y="63" width="48" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="596" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><text x="630" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">truncated on the way in</text><line x1="16" y1="98" x2="736" y2="98" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="20" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double r2 = a / b;</text><rect x="306" y="107" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="330" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><rect x="362" y="107" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="386" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><line x1="414" y1="116" x2="434" y2="116" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk219a)"/><text x="500" y="120" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7 / 2 in int</text><rect x="572" y="105" width="48" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="596" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3.0</text><text x="630" y="120" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">widened AFTER the loss</text><line x1="16" y1="140" x2="736" y2="140" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="20" y="162" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double r3 = (double) a / b;</text><rect x="306" y="149" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="330" y="162" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double</text><rect x="362" y="149" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="386" y="162" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><line x1="414" y1="158" x2="434" y2="158" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk219a)"/><text x="500" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7.0 / 2 in double</text><rect x="572" y="147" width="48" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="596" y="163" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3.5</text><text x="630" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">cast before the divide</text><line x1="16" y1="182" x2="736" y2="182" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="20" y="204" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double r4 = (double)(a / b);</text><rect x="306" y="191" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="330" y="204" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><rect x="362" y="191" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="386" y="204" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><line x1="414" y1="200" x2="434" y2="200" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk219a)"/><text x="500" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7 / 2 in int</text><rect x="572" y="189" width="48" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="596" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3.0</text><text x="630" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">cast after the damage</text><line x1="16" y1="224" x2="736" y2="224" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="20" y="246" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double r5 = a / 2.0;</text><rect x="306" y="233" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="330" y="246" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><rect x="362" y="233" width="48" height="19" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="386" y="246" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double</text><line x1="414" y1="242" x2="434" y2="242" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk219a)"/><text x="500" y="246" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7 / 2.0 in double</text><rect x="572" y="231" width="48" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="596" y="247" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3.5</text><text x="630" y="246" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one decimal operand is enough</text><text x="376" y="274" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The division happens first and the result type is decided by the operands — never by the variable you store it in.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the five ways to divide 7 by 2, and the three that lose the half</div><p>One pair of values, five expressions. The only thing that varies is where the decimal type appears.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int a = 7, b = 2;
int    r1 = a / b;
double r2 = a / b;
double r3 = (double) a / b;
double r4 = (double)(a / b);
double r5 = a / 2.0;

System.out.println(r1);
System.out.println(r2);
System.out.println(r3);
System.out.println(r4);
System.out.println(r5);
System.out.println(7 / 2);
System.out.println(-7 / 2);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
3
3.0
3.5
3.0
3.5
3
-3</pre><p><code>r1</code> is the plain case: two ints, so integer division, so 3. <code>r2</code> is the one that surprises people — the answer is a <code>double</code> and it is still 3.0, because the division finished in int arithmetic before the assignment ever happened. Widening cannot restore a digit that was already discarded.</p><p><code>r3</code> casts <em>an operand</em>, so the expression becomes <code>7.0 / 2</code>; the int 2 is promoted and the division runs in double, giving 3.5. <code>r4</code> casts the <em>result</em>, which is 3 by then, so it prints 3.0 — the cast is in the wrong place. <code>r5</code> shows the simpler fix: make one literal a decimal.</p><p>The last line is the negative case. Truncation moves toward zero, not downward, so <code>-7 / 2</code> is −3 rather than −4.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — splitting a price in cents into dollars and cents</div><p>Integer division is not always the villain. When the question genuinely is "how many whole units", it is the right tool — and <code>%</code> supplies what is left over.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int totalCents = 1299;
int dollars = totalCents / 100;   // whole dollars
int cents   = totalCents % 100;   // the leftover
System.out.println("$" + dollars + "." + cents);

double wrong = totalCents / 100;    // both int — truncates first
double right = totalCents / 100.0;  // one decimal operand
System.out.println("wrong=" + wrong);
System.out.println("right=" + right);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
$12.99
wrong=12.0
right=12.99</pre><p>Line 2 wants the whole part, so int division is exactly correct: 1299 / 100 = 12. Line 3 wants the remainder: 1299 % 100 = 99. Together they reconstruct the value with no floating point anywhere, which is why money is normally stored in whole cents.</p><p>Lines 6 and 7 are the same expression twice, differing by a single character. <code>100</code> is an <code>int</code> so the division truncates to 12 and then widens to 12.0; <code>100.0</code> is a <code>double</code> so the int 1299 is promoted and the answer keeps its fraction. Declaring the variable <code>double</code> did nothing for the first one.</p><p>One caution on the first output: <code>"$" + dollars + "." + cents</code> would print <code>$12.9</code> for 1209 cents, because 9 has no leading zero. Formatting is a separate job from arithmetic — <code>printf("$%d.%02d%n", dollars, cents)</code> handles it.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_6":{"body":"<p>This example puts 2.4 and 2.5 together on a real calculation. BMI is weight divided by height squared, and every decision in these nine lines is one the exam tests.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);

double weightKg = in.nextDouble();
double heightM  = in.nextDouble();

if (heightM &lt;= 0) {
    System.out.println("Height must be positive.");
    return;
}

double bmi = weightKg / (heightM * heightM);
System.out.printf("BMI: %.1f%n", bmi);</pre><p><strong>Why <code>double</code> and not <code>int</code>:</strong> 70.5 kg would truncate to 70 (2.5). <strong>Why the parentheses:</strong> without them <code>w / h * h</code> runs left to right and cancels back to <code>w</code> (2.4). <strong>Why the guard:</strong> a zero height gives <code>Infinity</code> for doubles rather than an exception, so the bad value spreads silently instead of stopping. <strong>Why printf:</strong> a raw <code>println</code> would print 22.857142857142858.</p><h3>Reading a worked example for the trap</h3><p>Worked examples in this course are constructed so that one specific mistake produces a plausible wrong answer. Here it is the division: a BMI or average computed with two integers loses the fraction and produces a number that looks reasonable enough not to notice. The habit worth taking from this is to check every division in a formula and ask what types are on each side before trusting the result — which is faster than tracing the whole calculation.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Formatted output:</strong> printf with a format string such as "%.1f" to control decimal places</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Guard clause:</strong> an early check that rejects invalid input before the real work begins</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Infinity:</strong> the double result of dividing by 0.0 — no exception is thrown</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>w / h * h</code> is not a typo you can catch by reading — it computes exactly <code>w</code>, and for realistic inputs that looks like a plausible BMI. It is a <strong>logic error</strong>: it compiles, it runs, and it lies. Only checking against a hand-computed answer finds it.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — every decision in the BMI line, and the wrong answer it prevents</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 284" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk220a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">BMI = WEIGHT / (HEIGHT x HEIGHT) — AND WHAT EACH DECISION PROTECTS</text><rect x="16" y="44" width="130" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="81" y="62.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">weightKg</text><text x="81" y="77.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">70.5</text><rect x="16" y="100" width="130" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="81" y="118.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">heightM</text><text x="81" y="133.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">1.75</text><line x1="148" y1="66" x2="210" y2="84" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk220a)"/><line x1="148" y1="122" x2="210" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk220a)"/><rect x="212" y="74" width="150" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="287" y="92.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">h * h</text><text x="287" y="107.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">3.0625</text><line x1="364" y1="96" x2="420" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk220a)"/><rect x="422" y="74" width="150" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="497" y="92.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">w / that</text><text x="497" y="107.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">23.0204...</text><line x1="574" y1="96" x2="620" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk220a)"/><rect x="622" y="74" width="114" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="679" y="92.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">printf %.1f</text><text x="679" y="107.7" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">23.0</text><text x="376" y="136" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">brackets keep the two heights together — division and multiplication rank equally, so without them Java goes left to right</text><rect x="16" y="156" width="356" height="116" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="194" y="178" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">drop the brackets</text><text x="194" y="202" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">w / h * h</text><text x="194" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(70.5 / 1.75) * 1.75</text><text x="194" y="246" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">= 70.5  — the weight, unchanged</text><text x="194" y="264" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">a plausible-looking BMI for nobody</text><rect x="388" y="156" width="348" height="116" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="562" y="178" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">height entered as 0</text><text x="562" y="202" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">70.5 / 0.0</text><text x="562" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">doubles do not throw — they produce</text><text x="562" y="246" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Infinity, and Infinity > 25 is true</text><text x="562" y="264" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">so the bad value flows on and is classified</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the calculation, and the version that quietly returns the weight</div><p>A person weighing 70.5 kg and standing 1.75 m tall. Work it by hand first: 1.75 × 1.75 = 3.0625, and 70.5 ÷ 3.0625 = 23.02.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">double weightKg = 70.5;
double heightM  = 1.75;

double bmi = weightKg / (heightM * heightM);
System.out.println(bmi);
System.out.printf("BMI: %.1f%n", bmi);

double wrong = weightKg / heightM * heightM;   // brackets removed
System.out.println("w / h * h = " + wrong);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
23.020408163265305
BMI: 23.0
w / h * h = 70.5</pre><p>The first line shows why <code>printf</code> is in the program at all: the raw <code>double</code> carries seventeen digits, none of which a person wants. <code>%.1f</code> rounds for display only — <code>bmi</code> itself is unchanged.</p><p>The last line is the trap in its purest form. <code>/</code> and <code>*</code> have equal precedence, so <code>weightKg / heightM * heightM</code> means <code>(weightKg / heightM) * heightM</code> — the two heights cancel and the expression returns the weight. It compiles, it runs, and for an adult it produces a number in a believable BMI range. Only checking against the hand-computed 23.02 catches it.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — what a height of zero actually does</div><p>The guard clause exists because floating-point division by zero does not behave like integer division by zero. Remove the guard and watch what happens:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">double weightKg = 70.5;
double heightM  = 0.0;
double bmi = weightKg / (heightM * heightM);
System.out.println("bmi = " + bmi);
System.out.println("bmi &gt; 25? " + (bmi &gt; 25));
System.out.printf("BMI: %.1f%n", bmi);

int bad = 70 / 0;    // the integer version, for contrast</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
bmi = Infinity
bmi &gt; 25? true
BMI: Infinity
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero</pre><p>Three lines of output appeared before anything went wrong. Dividing a <code>double</code> by zero produces the special value <code>Infinity</code> — no exception, no warning. Worse, <code>Infinity</code> compares as larger than everything, so a later <code>if (bmi > 25)</code> classifies this nonsense as overweight and the program reports it confidently.</p><p>Only the last line throws, and only because both operands there are <code>int</code>. So the two types fail in opposite ways: integer division by zero stops the program loudly, floating-point division by zero corrupts the answer silently. The guard clause converts the silent failure into a message you control.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Questions on worked examples like this one almost never ask you to recompute the formula. They ask which single change breaks it: removing the brackets, using <code>int</code> for a measurement, or dropping the guard. Scan for those three before doing any arithmetic. And remember the pair of behaviours for division by zero — <code>int</code> throws <code>ArithmeticException</code>, <code>double</code> gives <code>Infinity</code> or <code>NaN</code> — because that contrast is a question on its own.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_7":{"body":"<p><code>double</code> is the default for anything with a fractional part. It stores values in <strong>binary</strong>, and that single fact causes the behaviour that surprises everyone: just as one third cannot be written exactly in decimal, one tenth cannot be written exactly in binary. It repeats forever and gets cut off.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);            // 0.30000000000000004
System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3);     // false</pre><p>So doubles are approximations, and the practical consequence is that you must never test them for exact equality. Compare the <em>difference</em> against a small tolerance instead:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (Math.abs(a - b) &lt; 0.0001) { ... }     // "close enough to equal"</pre><p>Two smaller rules follow from the type system. A decimal literal is a <code>double</code> by default, so assigning one to a <code>float</code> needs an <code>f</code> suffix. And doubles do not crash on overflow — they produce special values instead:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">float f = 3.14;      // ERROR — 3.14 is a double
float f = 3.14f;     // fine

1.0 / 0              // Infinity, no exception
0.0 / 0              // NaN — "not a number"</pre><h3>Why floating point is approximate</h3><p>A <code>double</code> stores a number as a sign, an exponent and a mantissa — scientific notation in binary. That buys enormous range and costs exactness, because any fraction whose denominator is not a power of two cannot be represented, exactly as one third cannot be written in decimal. So <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is not <code>0.3</code>, adding a tiny number to a huge one can lose it entirely, and money is never stored as a double. Those are not bugs to work round; they are the format, and 3.18 is where the consequence becomes a rule.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>double:</strong> a 64-bit floating-point type, about 15 significant digits — the default for decimals</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Epsilon comparison:</strong> testing Math.abs(a - b) &lt; tolerance instead of a == b</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>NaN:</strong> the "not a number" result of an undefined operation such as 0.0/0 or Math.sqrt(-1)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>NaN</code> is not equal to anything, <strong>including itself</strong>. So a NaN produced upstream does not throw — it quietly makes every later comparison false, sending an if-else chain down the wrong branch. Test for it with <code>Double.isNaN(x)</code>.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why a double is an approximation, and where the error comes from</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 308" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk221a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">A DOUBLE IS SCIENTIFIC NOTATION IN BINARY — 64 BITS, SPLIT THREE WAYS</text><rect x="24" y="44" width="30" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="39" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">s</text><rect x="56" y="44" width="150" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="131" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">exponent</text><rect x="208" y="44" width="508" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="462" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">mantissa (the digits)</text><text x="39" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">1 bit</text><text x="131" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">11 bits</text><text x="462" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">52 bits</text><text x="39" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">sign</text><text x="131" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">how far to shift</text><text x="462" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">about 15-16 decimal digits of accuracy</text><line x1="16" y1="134" x2="736" y2="134" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="20" y="158" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">why 0.1 cannot fit</text><text x="20" y="182" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">in decimal:  1/3  =  0.3333333...  never terminates</text><text x="20" y="202" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">in binary:   1/10 =  0.000110011001100...  never terminates</text><text x="20" y="222" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a binary fraction terminates only when the denominator</text><text x="20" y="238" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">is a power of two — 1/2, 1/4, 1/8 are exact; 1/10 is not</text><rect x="20" y="250" width="340" height="24" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="30" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">so 0.1 is stored as the nearest 52-bit approximation</text><rect x="388" y="148" width="348" height="128" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--danger)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="562" y="170" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the consequence, exactly</text><text x="562" y="196" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.1 + 0.2</text><line x1="562" y1="204" x2="562" y2="220" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk221a)"/><text x="562" y="238" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.30000000000000004</text><text x="562" y="262" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3   is   false</text><text x="376" y="296" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">This is not a bug to work round; it is the storage format. The remedy is a tolerance, never more precision.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the arithmetic that does not add up, and the comparison that fixes it</div><p>Every one of these is exact arithmetic in decimal and inexact in binary.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);
System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3);
System.out.println(Math.abs((0.1 + 0.2) - 0.3) &lt; 0.0001);
System.out.println(1.0 / 3.0);
System.out.println(0.1 + 0.7);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
0.30000000000000004
false
true
0.3333333333333333
0.7999999999999999</pre><p>Line 1 shows the error itself — the stored values for 0.1 and 0.2 are each a hair off, and adding them accumulates the discrepancy into the sixteenth decimal place. Line 2 is the practical damage: an exact equality test on values that should match returns <code>false</code>.</p><p>Line 3 is the fix and the pattern to memorise: subtract, take the absolute value, and compare against a small tolerance. <code>Math.abs</code> is needed because you do not know which of the two is larger.</p><p>Line 5 is included because it errs in the <em>other</em> direction — 0.799999… rather than 0.800000…4. There is no rule about which way the error goes, which is exactly why <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> comparisons on computed doubles are also unreliable at the boundary.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the values a double produces instead of crashing</div><p>Integer arithmetic throws when it cannot answer. Floating-point arithmetic answers anyway, with three special values:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(1.0 / 0);
System.out.println(-1.0 / 0);
System.out.println(0.0 / 0);

double nan = 0.0 / 0;
System.out.println(nan == nan);
System.out.println(Double.isNaN(nan));
System.out.println(Math.sqrt(-4));</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
Infinity
-Infinity
NaN
false
true
NaN</pre><p>Note line 4 carefully: <code>NaN == NaN</code> is <code>false</code>. NaN is not equal to anything, including itself, which is the only reason <code>Double.isNaN</code> exists — you cannot test for it with <code>==</code>.</p><p>The danger is what this does to branching. A NaN produced early makes <em>every</em> later comparison false: <code>nan > 0</code>, <code>nan < 0</code> and <code>nan == 0</code> are all false at once, so an if-else chain falls through to whichever branch handles "none of the above". The bad value never announces itself; it just steers the program.</p><p>One more rule from the same section, this time caught at compile time:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">float f = 3.14;   // ERROR: incompatible types: possible lossy conversion from double to float</pre><p>A decimal literal is a <code>double</code> by default, and a double does not fit in a 32-bit float, so it needs the suffix: <code>float f = 3.14f;</code>.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Three facts carry nearly every <code>double</code> question. <strong>Never compare computed doubles with <code>==</code></strong> — use <code>Math.abs(a - b) &lt; EPSILON</code>. <strong>Division by zero differs by type</strong> — <code>int</code> throws, <code>double</code> gives <code>Infinity</code> or <code>NaN</code>. <strong>A decimal literal is a <code>double</code></strong>, so a <code>float</code> needs an <code>f</code> suffix. If a question shows <code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3</code>, the answer is <code>false</code> every single time.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_8":{"body":"<p>Scientific notation is a compact way of writing very large or very small numbers, and Java reads the <code>e</code> as "times ten to the power of". It is a convenience for the literal only — the value is an ordinary number once stored.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">2.5e3    // 2500.0
1e-3     // 0.001      — a negative exponent divides
6.02e23  // readable without counting 23 zeros</pre><p>The rule that costs marks is the <strong>type</strong>: any literal written this way is a <code>double</code>, even when the value is a whole number. That connects straight back to 2.12 — assigning it to an <code>int</code> needs an explicit cast.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int n = 1e3;          // ERROR — 1e3 is the double 1000.0
int n = (int) 1e3;    // fine

int million = 1_000_000;   // underscores are allowed for readability
int bad     = 1,000,000;   // commas are NOT</pre><h3>Where scientific notation appears</h3><p>Scientific notation matters less as a way of writing numbers and more as an explanation of how a <code>double</code> works internally. <code>6.02e23</code> is a mantissa of 6.02 and an exponent of 23, and that is precisely the structure the bits are divided into. Recognising that makes the range and precision trade obvious: the exponent buys reach, the mantissa buys accuracy, and the total number of bits is fixed, so one is always paid for with the other.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Scientific notation:</strong> a literal of the form mEn meaning m x 10 to the power n</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Digit separator:</strong> an underscore inside a numeric literal, ignored by the compiler, used for readability</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>6.02 * 10e23</code> is <strong>wrong</strong> for 6.02 x 10²³ — it means 6.02 x 10 x 10²³, one power too many. The <code>e</code> already contains the "times ten to the", so the correct literal is simply <code>6.02e23</code>.</p></div><i data-tba_z2_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — scientific notation: what each part of the literal means</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 752 374" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk222a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="376" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">READING A LITERAL WRITTEN IN SCIENTIFIC NOTATION</text><text x="376" y="74" fill="var(--text)" font-size="34" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6.02e23</text><line x1="300" y1="88" x2="300" y2="118" stroke="var(--accent-2)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="300" y="136" fill="var(--accent-2)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">mantissa</text><text x="300" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the digits</text><line x1="376" y1="88" x2="376" y2="106" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="376" y="124" fill="var(--warning)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">e = "times ten to the"</text><line x1="438" y1="88" x2="438" y2="118" stroke="var(--success)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="452" y="136" fill="var(--success)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">exponent</text><text x="452" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">how many places to shift</text><line x1="16" y1="172" x2="736" y2="172" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="24" y="198" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2.5e3</text><line x1="140" y1="194" x2="168" y2="194" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="176" y="198" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">2.5 x 10³</text><line x1="300" y1="194" x2="328" y2="194" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="336" y="198" fill="var(--success)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2500.0</text><text x="516" y="198" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">positive exponent shifts right</text><text x="24" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1e-3</text><line x1="140" y1="224" x2="168" y2="224" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="176" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">1 x 10⁻³</text><line x1="300" y1="224" x2="328" y2="224" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="336" y="228" fill="var(--success)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.001</text><text x="516" y="228" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">negative exponent shifts left</text><text x="24" y="258" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6.02e23</text><line x1="140" y1="254" x2="168" y2="254" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="176" y="258" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">6.02 x 10²³</text><line x1="300" y1="254" x2="328" y2="254" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="336" y="258" fill="var(--success)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6.02E23</text><text x="516" y="258" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">printed back in the same form</text><text x="24" y="288" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6.02 * 10e23</text><line x1="140" y1="284" x2="168" y2="284" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="176" y="288" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">6.02 x 10 x 10²³</text><line x1="300" y1="284" x2="328" y2="284" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk222a)"/><text x="336" y="288" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6.019999999999999E24</text><text x="516" y="288" fill="var(--danger)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">ONE POWER TOO MANY</text><rect x="16" y="322" width="720" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--warning)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="376" y="340" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">every literal written this way is a double — even 1e3, which is the value 1000.0</text><text x="376" y="356" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">so int n = 1e3; does not compile; int n = (int) 1e3; does</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — six literals and the values they really stand for</div><p>The <code>e</code> is not a variable and not multiplication — it is part of the literal, and it means "times ten to the power of".</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(2.5e3);
System.out.println(1e-3);
System.out.println(6.02e23);
System.out.println(6.02 * 10e23);
System.out.println(1_000_000);
System.out.println(2.5e3 == 2500.0);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
2500.0
0.001
6.02E23
6.019999999999999E24
1000000
true</pre><p>Line 1: 2.5 × 10³ = 2500, printed as <code>2500.0</code> because it is a <code>double</code>. Line 2: a negative exponent divides, giving 0.001. Line 3 prints in scientific form itself, because Java switches to that notation for very large and very small magnitudes.</p><p>Line 4 is the classic error, and the output makes it unmistakable. <code>10e23</code> already means 10 × 10²³ = 10²⁴, so multiplying by 6.02 gives 6.02 × 10²⁴ — ten times too big. Writing Avogadro's number as <code>6.02 * 10e23</code> instead of <code>6.02e23</code> is off by a factor of ten, and the stray <code>...99999</code> is the ordinary floating-point error from section 2.7.</p><p>Line 5 shows underscores as digit separators — the compiler ignores them entirely, so <code>1_000_000</code> is the int 1000000. Commas are <em>not</em> allowed. Line 6 confirms the value is exactly 2500.0, since 2500 is representable exactly in binary.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the type rule that trips the assignment up</div><p>The value <code>1e3</code> is a whole number, which makes the next line look reasonable:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int n = 1e3;</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--accent);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">TERMINAL</span>
error: incompatible types: possible lossy conversion from double to int
int n = 1e3;
        ^</pre><p>The compiler is not objecting to the value — 1000 fits in an <code>int</code> comfortably. It is objecting to the <em>type</em>: any literal with an <code>e</code> or a decimal point is a <code>double</code>, and narrowing from double to int is never automatic, because in general it would lose data. The compiler applies the rule to the type, not to the particular value.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;">int n = (int) 1e3;
System.out.println(n);

long bytes = 2_000_000_000L;
System.out.println(bytes);

double tiny = 4.5e-8;
System.out.println(tiny);</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:10px 12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid var(--success);"><span style="font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.06em; opacity:.65;">ACTUAL OUTPUT</span>
1000
2000000000
4.5E-8</pre><p>The cast is you accepting responsibility for any loss; here there is none. The other two lines show the neighbouring literal rules: <code>L</code> marks a <code>long</code>, and very small doubles print back in scientific notation.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Two things get asked. <strong>What is the value</strong> — count the exponent as places to shift the decimal point, right for positive and left for negative. <strong>What is the type</strong> — anything with <code>e</code> or a decimal point is a <code>double</code>, so assigning it to an <code>int</code> or <code>float</code> needs a cast or a suffix. And watch for <code>10e23</code>: if the mantissa already ends in a power of ten, the answer is off by that factor.</p></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_9":{"body":"<p>Some values should never change once set — a tax rate, a maximum size, the number of days in a week. Marking them <code>final</code> lets the compiler enforce that, turning a rule you were relying on into one the language guarantees.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">final double TAX_RATE = 0.07;
TAX_RATE = 0.08;          // COMPILE ERROR — cannot assign to a final variable</pre><p>The reason to bother is not performance. It is that a rate change becomes a <strong>one-line edit</strong> instead of a hunt through the file, and that the name explains the number to whoever reads it next. <code>if (x &gt; 86400)</code> means nothing; <code>if (x &gt; SECONDS_PER_DAY)</code> explains itself.</p><p>What <code>final</code> guarantees is narrower than people assume. It locks the <em>variable</em>, not the object the variable points at:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">final int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
a[0] = 99;        // ALLOWED — the contents are not final
a = new int[5];   // ERROR   — repointing the variable is what is blocked</pre><h3>What a constant is really for</h3><p>A named constant is worth three things and only one of them is convenience. It <em>documents</em> — <code>LEGAL_AGE</code> says what 18 means. It <em>centralises</em> — one edit changes every use, so a rate change cannot be half-applied. And it <em>protects</em> — <code>final</code> turns an accidental reassignment into a compile error rather than a silent bug. The alternative, a bare number scattered through the code, is the magic-number anti-pattern, and the reason it is worth naming is that nobody can safely find all the copies later.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>final:</strong> a modifier preventing a variable from being reassigned after its first value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Named constant:</strong> a final variable given a meaningful name, conventionally in ALL_CAPS</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Magic number:</strong> an unexplained numeric literal sitting directly in the code</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>For a primitive, "the variable" and "the value" are the same thing, so <code>final</code> feels absolute. For an array or object they are not, and the contents stay editable. That distinction is the whole reason <code>final</code> fields holding mutable objects are not as safe as they look — a point that returns in Unit 7.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what final actually protects</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk345a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">final int MAX = 50;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the NAME is bound once</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">MAX = 60;</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compile error — reassignment</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">final int[] a = {1,2};</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the REFERENCE is fixed</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[0] = 99;</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">allowed — contents are not final</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Declaring and using a constant</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">final int MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS = 3;
int attempts = 4;
System.out.println(attempts &gt; MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS);
System.out.println("limit is " + MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
limit is 3</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>final</code> says this name may be assigned exactly once.</li><li>The comparison reads clearly because the number has a name.</li><li>Change the policy in one place and every use follows.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — final protects the reference, not the object</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">final int[] scores = {90, 80};
scores[0] = 55;
System.out.println(scores[0] + "," + scores[1]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">55,80</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>scores</code> may never point at a different array.</li><li>But the array it points at is still mutable, so element 0 can change.</li><li>This distinction is worth a mark on its own.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Thinking <code>final</code> freezes the contents. It freezes the <em>binding</em>. <code>final int[] a</code> means you cannot say <code>a = somethingElse</code>, but <code>a[0] = 99</code> is perfectly legal.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Constants are written <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code> by convention, and a <code>final</code> field with no initial value must be assigned in the constructor or the code will not compile.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_10":{"body":"<p>The <code>Math</code> class collects the arithmetic that operators cannot express. Every method is <code>static</code>, so you call it on the class itself, and <code>Math</code> lives in <code>java.lang</code>, so it needs no import.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Math.abs(-7)        // 7        int in, int out
Math.pow(2, 3)      // 8.0      ALWAYS returns double
Math.sqrt(16)       // 4.0      always double
Math.max(3, 9)      // 9
Math.round(5.6)     // 6        rounds — unlike a cast, which truncates
Math.random()       // [0.0, 1.0)  — 0 possible, 1.0 not</pre><p>Two of those return types cause most of the errors here. <code>pow</code> and <code>sqrt</code> hand back a <code>double</code> whatever you feed them, so storing the result in an <code>int</code> needs a cast (2.12):</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int n = Math.pow(2, 3);          // ERROR — double will not narrow on its own
int n = (int) Math.pow(2, 3);    // 8</pre><h3>Reading library methods rather than memorising them</h3><p>The <code>Math</code> methods are all <code>static</code>, so they are called on the class and never on an object. Beyond that, the useful skill is reading the signature: <code>Math.pow</code> returns a <code>double</code> even for whole numbers, which is why <code>Math.pow(2, 3)</code> gives <code>8.0</code> rather than <code>8</code>. <code>Math.round</code> returns a <code>long</code>, so assigning it to an <code>int</code> needs a cast. Those return types are what questions test, more often than what the methods compute.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>static method:</strong> a method called on the class rather than on an object, as in Math.sqrt(x)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Math.round:</strong> rounds to the nearest whole number, in contrast to a cast, which truncates</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>Math.sqrt</code> of a negative returns <code>NaN</code> rather than throwing. Nothing stops, nothing warns — the NaN simply flows into the rest of the calculation and poisons every comparison downstream, exactly as described in 2.7.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the Math methods that actually appear</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk346a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.pow(b, e)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns double — always</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.sqrt(x)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns double</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.abs(x)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">keeps the argument's type</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.max / min</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">keeps the argument's type</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.round(x)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">double -&gt; long, float -&gt; int</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Math.pow always returns a double</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int base = 5;
double squared = Math.pow(base, 2);
System.out.println(squared);
int asInt = (int) Math.pow(base, 2);
System.out.println(asInt);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">25.0
25</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Math.pow</code> returns <code>double</code> whatever you feed it.</li><li>So it prints with a decimal point.</li><li>Assigning it to an <code>int</code> needs an explicit cast.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Rounding is not truncation</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Math.round(2.5));
System.out.println(Math.round(-2.5));
System.out.println((int) 2.9);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3
-2
2</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Math.round</code> rounds half <em>up</em> — towards positive infinity.</li><li>So −2.5 rounds to −2, not −3.</li><li>A cast truncates instead, discarding the fraction entirely.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>The exam's favourite is that <code>Math.pow</code> returns a <code>double</code>. <code>int x = Math.pow(2,3);</code> does not compile without a cast.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_11":{"body":"<p>Modulo gives the <strong>remainder</strong> after integer division, and it is far more useful than that description suggests. It is the standard way to test divisibility, to pull digits apart, and to wrap a value around a range.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">17 % 5    =  2        // 17 = 3*5 + 2
10 % 2    =  0        // no remainder, so 10 is even
 7 % 10   =  7        // when a &lt; b, the answer is a</pre><p>The three patterns worth memorising:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">n % 2 == 0                 // is n even?
n % 10                     // the last digit of n
n / 10                     // n with the last digit removed

// together they walk every digit
while (n &gt; 0) {
    int digit = n % 10;
    n = n / 10;
}</pre><p>Negatives behave in a way that trips a very common test. The sign of the result follows the <strong>left</strong> operand, not the right:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">-7 % 3    = -1        // not 2
 7 % -3   =  1</pre><h3>What modulo is actually for</h3><p>Modulo looks like a niche operator and does three jobs you will use constantly. <code>n % 2 == 0</code> tests whether a number is even. <code>n % 10</code> extracts the last digit, and <code>n / 10</code> removes it, which together let a loop walk the digits of a number. And <code>i % k</code> cycles through <code>0…k−1</code> as <code>i</code> increases, which is how anything wraps round — clock arithmetic, array indices, alternating rows. In Java the result takes the sign of the left operand, so <code>−7 % 2</code> is <code>−1</code>, and that is why <code>n % 2 == 1</code> fails to detect negative odd numbers.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Modulo (%):</strong> the remainder left after integer division</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Divisibility test:</strong> n % d == 0 means d divides n exactly</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Because <code>-7 % 2</code> is <code>-1</code> and not <code>1</code>, the test <code>n % 2 == 1</code> is <strong>false for negative odd numbers</strong>. Use <code>n % 2 != 0</code> to test for odd. And <code>5 % 0</code> throws <code>ArithmeticException</code>, exactly like dividing by zero.</p></div><i data-d286f_divmod="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>Division and remainder are one calculation</h3><p>Integer division answers <em>how many whole groups</em>; <code>%</code> answers <em>what is left</em>. They always satisfy <code>(a / b) * b + (a % b) == a</code>.</p><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — 17 items in groups of 5</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 172" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk65a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="24" y="30" width="176" height="104" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><circle cx="40" cy="48" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="74" cy="48" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="108" cy="48" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="142" cy="48" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="176" cy="48" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="40" cy="82" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="74" cy="82" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="108" cy="82" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="142" cy="82" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="176" cy="82" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="40" cy="116" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="74" cy="116" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="108" cy="116" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="142" cy="116" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="176" cy="116" r="12" fill="#6366f144" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="112" y="152" fill="#6366f1" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3 full groups of 5</text><rect x="214" y="64" width="62" height="36" rx="9" fill="none" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><circle cx="232" cy="82" r="12" fill="#f59e0b55" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="258" cy="82" r="12" fill="#f59e0b55" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="245" y="116" fill="#b45309" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2 left over</text><text x="330" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">17 / 5  =  3</text><text x="330" y="76" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">how many whole groups</text><text x="330" y="108" fill="#b45309" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">17 % 5  =  2</text><text x="330" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what is left over</text></svg></div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Expression</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Value</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>17 / 5</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">truncated, not rounded — 3.4 becomes 3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>17 % 5</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the leftover</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>-7 / 2</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>-3</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">truncation is toward zero, so not -4</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>-7 % 2</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>-1</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the remainder takes the sign of the LEFT operand</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>n % 2 == 0</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">even test</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing left over after halving</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>The two negative rows are the ones that catch people, because several other languages answer -4 and 1 there. Java truncates toward zero and keeps the dividend's sign.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_11="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One operand decides the whole division</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(7 / 2);
System.out.println(7 / 2.0);
System.out.println(7 % 2);
System.out.println((double) 7 / 2);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3
3.5
1
3.5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>7 / 2</code> — both int, so integer division truncates to 3.</li><li><code>7 / 2.0</code> — one double, so real division gives 3.5.</li><li><code>%</code> gives the remainder, 1.</li><li>Casting one operand first is the usual fix.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Splitting a total into units</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int totalMinutes = 145;
int hours = totalMinutes / 60;
int minutes = totalMinutes % 60;
System.out.println(hours + "h " + minutes + "m");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2h 25m</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Integer division gives how many whole hours fit.</li><li>Modulo gives what is left over.</li><li>Division and remainder together are how any total is broken into units.</li></ol></div>"},"D286/u2/z2_12":{"body":"<p>Java converts between numeric types in two directions, and it treats them very differently depending on whether information can be lost.</p><p><strong>Widening</strong> moves to a larger type and happens automatically, because nothing can be lost. <strong>Narrowing</strong> moves to a smaller type and requires an explicit cast — the cast is you telling the compiler you accept the loss.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">byte -&gt; short -&gt; int -&gt; long -&gt; float -&gt; double      // widening: automatic

int n = 5;
double d = n;        // fine, implicit

double x = 5.9;
int m = x;           // ERROR: possible lossy conversion
int m = (int) x;     // 5 — and note it TRUNCATES, it does not round</pre><p>A cast silences the compiler; it does not prevent the loss. That distinction decides where you put it, and it is the same lesson as 2.5: convert <em>before</em> the operation, not after.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">long r = (long) a * b;     // CORRECT — widened first, so the multiply is in long
long r = (long)(a * b);    // WRONG   — overflows in int, then widens the wrong answer</pre><h3>Which conversions are free and which are not</h3><p>Widening — <code>int</code> to <code>double</code>, <code>char</code> to <code>int</code> — is automatic because the target can hold everything the source could, so nothing is lost. Narrowing is the reverse and always needs an explicit cast, because information will be discarded and the language wants you to say you meant it. The specific behaviour to remember is that a cast from a decimal type <strong>truncates toward zero</strong> rather than rounding: <code>(int) 3.9</code> is 3, and <code>(int) −3.9</code> is −3, not −4.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Widening:</strong> converting to a larger type; automatic and lossless</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Narrowing:</strong> converting to a smaller type; requires an explicit cast and may lose data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Promotion:</strong> when operands of different types meet, the smaller is widened before the operation</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Promotion is why <code>5 / 2.0</code> gives 2.5 and why <code>'a' + 1</code> gives the int 98 rather than a char. When two types meet in an expression, the smaller one is widened first — then the operation runs in the larger type.</p></div><i data-d286f_convert="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>Which conversions Java will do for you</h3><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — widening is free, narrowing is not</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 140" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk64a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="46" width="84" height="34" rx="9" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="62" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">byte</text><line x1="104" y1="63" x2="138" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk64a)"/><rect x="124" y="46" width="84" height="34" rx="9" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="166" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">short</text><line x1="208" y1="63" x2="242" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk64a)"/><rect x="228" y="46" width="84" height="34" rx="9" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="270" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><line x1="312" y1="63" x2="346" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk64a)"/><rect x="332" y="46" width="84" height="34" rx="9" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="374" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">long</text><line x1="416" y1="63" x2="450" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk64a)"/><rect x="436" y="46" width="84" height="34" rx="9" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="478" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">float</text><line x1="520" y1="63" x2="554" y2="63" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk64a)"/><rect x="540" y="46" width="84" height="34" rx="9" fill="#6366f118" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="582" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double</text><text x="320" y="26" fill="#16a34a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WIDENING  —  automatic, nothing is lost</text><line x1="624" y1="100" x2="30" y2="100" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="6 4" marker-end="url(#fk64a)"/><text x="320" y="122" fill="#dc2626" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">NARROWING  —  needs an explicit (cast), and can lose data</text></svg></div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Conversion</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Allowed?</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>double d = 3;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Yes</strong>, automatic</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">int fits inside double without loss</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>int x = 3.5;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>No</strong> — compile error</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the .5 would be silently destroyed</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>int x = (int) 3.5;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Yes</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the cast says you accept the loss — x becomes 3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>long L = 5;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Yes</strong>, automatic</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">widening</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>int n = 5L;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>No</strong> — needs <code>(int)</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">narrowing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Casting a double to int <strong>truncates toward zero</strong>; it does not round. <code>(int) 3.99</code> is 3 and <code>(int) -3.99</code> is -3. Use <code>Math.round</code> when you want rounding.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Widening happens by itself; narrowing must be demanded</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int i = 42;
double d = i;
System.out.println(d);
double price = 9.87;
int truncated = (int) price;
System.out.println(truncated);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">42.0
9</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>int to double is widening — no information can be lost, so Java does it silently.</li><li>double to int is narrowing — the fraction is lost, so you must write the cast.</li><li>The cast truncates towards zero. It does not round.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Mixed arithmetic promotes before it computes</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int count = 3;
double total = 10.0;
System.out.println(total / count);
System.out.println(1 / 2 * 2.0);
System.out.println(2.0 * 1 / 2);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3.3333333333333335
0.0
1.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>count</code> is promoted to double, so the division is real.</li><li>In <code>1 / 2 * 2.0</code> the integer division happens FIRST and gives 0.</li><li>Reordering so a double leads keeps the precision.</li></ol></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u2_z2_12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Declared is not the same as initialized</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int declaredAndSet = 7;
System.out.println(declaredAndSet);

int later;
boolean condition = true;
if (condition) {
    later = 1;
} else {
    later = 2;
}
System.out.println(later);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">7
1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Declaring <code>int later;</code> reserves the name but stores nothing in it.</li><li>Both branches of the <code>if</code> assign it, so by the time it is printed the compiler can prove it has a value — this compiles.</li><li>Delete the <code>else</code> and it stops compiling, because one path would reach the print with nothing assigned. The compiler requires <em>every</em> path to assign, not just the likely one.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming an <code>int</code> local starts at 0 the way a field does. It does not start at anything — reading it before assignment is a compile error, so the program never runs at all.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_13":{"body":"<p>Computers store everything in <strong>binary</strong>, because two states are far easier to build reliably than ten. Each position is a power of two, and reading a binary number is just adding up the positions that are switched on.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Position:   128  64  32  16   8   4   2   1
Binary 1011:                  1   0   1   1   = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11
Binary 11111111:                              = 255</pre><p>Going the other way, repeatedly subtract the largest power of two that fits: 10 needs an 8 (leaving 2) and a 2 (leaving 0), so 10 is <code>1010</code>.</p><p>This is also where the type sizes in 2.17 come from. A type with <em>n</em> bits can hold 2ⁿ distinct values, which is exactly why an <code>int</code> stops near 2.1 billion — and why it overflows the way 2.16 describes.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">4 bits  = 2^4  = 16 values
8 bits  = 2^8  = 256 values
32 bits = 2^32 = about 4.3 billion  -&gt;  int spans -2.1bn to +2.1bn</pre><h3>Why bit width shows up later</h3><p>Binary is worth understanding here rather than memorising, because two later topics depend on it. Every numeric type is a fixed number of bits, so each has a hard maximum — which is exactly what overflow in 2.16 is. And the widths in 2.17 are not arbitrary trivia: they are the reason an <code>int</code> stops near 2.1 billion and a <code>long</code> does not. The single fact worth carrying is that binary place values double leftward — 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 — which is enough to read any byte.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bit:</strong> a single binary digit, 0 or 1</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Byte:</strong> eight bits, able to represent 256 distinct values</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Hexadecimal:</strong> base 16, written with the prefix 0x — each hex digit is exactly four bits</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A leading zero on an integer literal makes it <strong>octal</strong>, not decimal: <code>int x = 010;</code> is 8, not 10. It compiles silently, which makes it a genuinely nasty bug. <code>0x</code> means hexadecimal and <code>0b</code> means binary.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_13="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — place values in a byte</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 94" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk347a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">each place is twice the one to its right</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0  0  1  0 1 1 0 1</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">= 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 45</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Converting by subtracting place values</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Integer.toBinaryString(45));
System.out.println(Integer.parseInt("101101", 2));
System.out.println(0b101101);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">101101
45
45</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>45 = 32 + 8 + 4 + 1, so the bits at those places are 1.</li><li><code>parseInt</code> with radix 2 reads a binary string back.</li><li>A <code>0b</code> literal writes binary directly in source.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why 8 bits stop at 255</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Math.pow(2, 8));
System.out.println((int) Math.pow(2, 8) - 1);
System.out.println(Integer.toBinaryString(255));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">256.0
255
11111111</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>n bits give 2^n distinct patterns.</li><li>Counting from 0, the largest is 2^n − 1.</li><li>255 is eight 1s — every place value filled.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Two facts cover most questions: n bits give 2^n values, and the largest unsigned value is 2^n − 1. Do not confuse the count with the maximum.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_14":{"body":"<p>A <code>char</code> holds one character, but underneath it is a 16-bit <em>number</em> — the Unicode code value. That dual nature explains every behaviour in this section: chars compare, sort and do arithmetic like numbers, and only print like letters.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">char c = 'A';
System.out.println(c);          // A
System.out.println((int) c);    // 65   the code value
System.out.println(c + 1);      // 66   char PROMOTES to int in arithmetic
System.out.println((char)(c + 1)); // B   cast back to see the letter</pre><p>You rarely need the code values themselves. What you need are the <strong>differences</strong>, because those turn a character into the number it represents:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">char digit = '7';
int value = digit - '0';        // 7  — NOT (int) digit, which is 55

char letter = 'd';
int position = letter - 'a';    // 3  — d is the 4th letter, zero-indexed</pre><p>For classification, the <code>Character</code> class saves you from remembering ranges: <code>isDigit</code>, <code>isLetter</code>, <code>isLetterOrDigit</code>, <code>isWhitespace</code>, <code>toUpperCase</code>.</p><h3>Why char arithmetic works at all</h3><p>A <code>char</code> holds a number, so arithmetic on it is ordinary arithmetic on that number. That explains the whole family of behaviours: <code>'A' + 1</code> is 66 because the addition promotes the char to an int, and getting back to a character requires a cast. It also explains the digit idiom — <code>'7' − '0'</code> is 7, because subtracting the code for zero from the code for seven leaves the numeric value. The trap is that <code>System.out.println('A' + 1)</code> prints <strong>66</strong>, not <code>B</code>, because the expression is an int by the time it reaches the print.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>char:</strong> a 16-bit type holding one Unicode character, written in single quotes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Code value:</strong> the number a character is stored as — 48 for '0', 65 for 'A', 97 for 'a'</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Promotion:</strong> a char automatically becomes an int when used in arithmetic</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>(int) '7'</code> is <strong>55</strong>, not 7 — it gives the code value, not the digit. Subtract <code>'0'</code> to get the numeric value. And <code>'a'</code> and <code>"a"</code> are different types entirely: single quotes make a char, double quotes make a String.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_14="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a char is a number with a costume</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk348a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">'A'</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">65</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">'B'</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">66</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">'a'</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">97</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">'0'</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">48</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">'A' + 1</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">66 -&gt; an int, not 'B'</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Arithmetic on a char produces an int</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">char c = 'A';
System.out.println((int) c);
System.out.println(c + 1);
System.out.println((char) (c + 1));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">65
66
B</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A char holds a numeric code — 'A' is 65.</li><li>Adding 1 promotes to int, so it prints 66.</li><li>Cast back to char to see the letter.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Converting a digit character to its value</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">char digit = '7';
System.out.println(digit - '0');
System.out.println(Character.isDigit(digit));
System.out.println(Character.toUpperCase('m'));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">7
