M</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>'7' is code 55 and '0' is 48, so the difference is the digit's value.</li><li>This works for any digit because the codes are consecutive.</li><li>The Character class carries the tests and conversions.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Remember '0' is 48 and 'A' is 65, and that lower-case letters come after upper-case ('a' is 97). Any question about ordering follows from those.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_15":{"body":"<p>A <code>String</code> is a sequence of characters, and it is <strong>immutable</strong> — once created, its contents can never change. Every method that looks like it edits a String actually returns a brand-new one and leaves the original untouched.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String s = "WGU";
s.concat(" rocks");
System.out.println(s);          // WGU  — unchanged!

s = s.concat(" rocks");         // you must ASSIGN the result
System.out.println(s);          // WGU rocks</pre><p>That applies to all of them — <code>toUpperCase</code>, <code>trim</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>substring</code>. Calling one without using the result does nothing at all, silently.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">"Hello".length()           // 5    a METHOD for Strings (arrays use .length)
"Hello".charAt(0)          // 'H'  zero-indexed
"Hello".substring(1, 3)    // "el" start INCLUSIVE, end EXCLUSIVE
"Hello".indexOf("l")       // 2    or -1 if absent
"  hi  ".trim()            // "hi"</pre><p>Immutability also decides how you compare Strings. Because two Strings with the same text can be two different objects, <code>==</code> asks the wrong question — it asks whether they are the same object, not whether they read the same.</p><h3>Why immutability is the whole story</h3><p>Every surprising String behaviour comes from one fact: no method ever changes the string it is called on. <code>toUpperCase</code>, <code>trim</code>, <code>replace</code> and <code>substring</code> all return a <em>new</em> String and leave the original untouched, which is why forgetting to assign the result is the most common String bug there is. It is also why <code>+=</code> in a loop is expensive — each pass allocates another String — and why <code>StringBuilder</code> exists. And it is why <code>==</code> is unreliable: two Strings with the same characters may or may not be the same object.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>String:</strong> an immutable sequence of characters, written in double quotes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Immutable:</strong> cannot be changed after creation; operations return a new value instead</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>equals:</strong> the method that compares String contents, as opposed to == which compares identity</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Two consequences bite constantly. <strong>Comparison:</strong> use <code>.equals()</code>, because <code>==</code> can be false for identical text. <strong>Performance:</strong> <code>s += x</code> inside a loop builds a whole new String every pass, making the loop O(n²) — which is what <code>StringBuilder</code> exists to fix.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_15="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a String is indexed from 0</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk349a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">J  a  v  a</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the characters</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0  1  2  3</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the indexes</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">length() = 4</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">last index = length() - 1 = 3</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Length, index, and the last position</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "Java";
System.out.println(s.length());
