CAT</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>toUpperCase()</code> RETURNS a new String; it cannot change the old one.</li><li>Ignoring the return value therefore does nothing at all.</li><li>Assigning the result back is what makes the change visible.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><code>substring(a, b)</code> is inclusive of a and exclusive of b, so its length is b − a. That off-by-one is the most tested detail in this section.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_16":{"body":"<p>An <code>int</code> has a fixed 32 bits (2.13), so there is a largest value it can hold. Go past it and Java does not throw, warn, or stop — it <strong>wraps around</strong> to the most negative value, like an odometer rolling over.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int max = Integer.MAX_VALUE;      // 2,147,483,647
System.out.println(max + 1);      // -2147483648

int a = 2_000_000_000;
System.out.println(a + a);        // -294967296   silently negative</pre><p>Because nothing is thrown, an overflowed value simply flows onward as a plausible-looking wrong number. The defence is to choose a big enough type <em>before</em> the arithmetic, which brings back the cast-placement rule from 2.12:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">long r = (long) a * b;     // CORRECT — widened before multiplying
long r = (long)(a * b);    // WRONG   — already overflowed in int</pre><h3>Why overflow is silent</h3><p>Overflow produces no exception and no warning. The arithmetic simply wraps: add 1 to <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code> and you get <code>Integer.MIN_VALUE</code>, because the carry out of the top bit is discarded and the sign bit flips. That silence is what makes it dangerous — the program continues confidently with a negative total. The practical defences are to use <code>long</code> when a value might exceed about two billion, and to be suspicious of any total that comes out negative when every input was positive.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Overflow:</strong> exceeding a type's range, causing the value to wrap to the opposite end</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Integer.MAX_VALUE:</strong> 2,147,483,647 — the largest int</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>long:</strong> a 64-bit whole-number type, up to about 9.2 quintillion</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>You <strong>cannot catch</strong> overflow — no exception is thrown, so try/catch is useless. Either use <code>long</code> from the start, or use <code>Math.addExact</code> / <code>Math.multiplyExact</code>, which <em>do</em> throw when the result will not fit.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_16="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — int wraps around at its limits</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk350a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2147483647</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Integer.MAX_VALUE</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">+ 1</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">wraps to...</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">-2147483648</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Integer.MIN_VALUE</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Adding one to the maximum</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int max = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
System.out.println(max);
System.out.println(max + 1);
System.out.println(max + 1 == Integer.MIN_VALUE);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2147483647
