4000000000</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Two ints are added AS ints, so the overflow happens before any assignment.</li><li>Making the variables long avoids it.</li><li>Casting one operand first also works — casting the result afterwards would be too late.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Overflow is silent. If a question shows a large int calculation and offers a negative answer, that is almost certainly the intended one.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_17":{"body":"<p>Java has exactly <strong>eight primitive types</strong>, and everything else in the language — <code>String</code>, arrays, your own classes — is an object. Choosing between the primitives comes down to two questions: does the value have a fractional part, and how large can it get?</p><div class="lb-table-wrap"><table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:14px;"><tbody><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Type</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Bits</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:6px;">Holds</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>byte</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">8</td><td style="padding:6px;">-128 to 127</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>short</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">16</td><td style="padding:6px;">-32,768 to 32,767</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>int</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">32</td><td style="padding:6px;">±2.1 billion — the default</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>long</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">64</td><td style="padding:6px;">±9.2 quintillion — needs the L suffix</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>float</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">32</td><td style="padding:6px;">~7 digits — needs the f suffix</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>double</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">64</td><td style="padding:6px;">~15 digits — the default for decimals</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>char</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">16</td><td style="padding:6px;">one Unicode character</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px;"><code>boolean</code></td><td style="padding:6px;">—</td><td style="padding:6px;">true or false only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The bit counts are not trivia — they are where the ranges come from (2.13) and why overflow happens where it does (2.16). Note also that Java fixes these sizes on every platform, unlike C.</p><h3>Choosing a numeric type</h3><p>The choice is nearly always <code>int</code> for whole numbers and <code>double</code> for decimals, and the exceptions are worth knowing because questions are built on them. Use <code>long</code> when the value can exceed about 2.1 billion — populations, milliseconds since 1970, file sizes in bytes. Use <code>byte</code> and <code>short</code> only when memory genuinely matters, which in this course means almost never. And use neither <code>float</code> nor <code>double</code> for money: store whole cents in an <code>int</code> or <code>long</code>, because a rounding error in a balance is a real error.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Primitive type:</strong> one of the eight built-in value types; not an object and never null</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reference type:</strong> everything else — String, arrays, and any class; holds a reference to an object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Literal suffix:</strong> L marks a long literal, f marks a float literal</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>String</code> is a <strong>reference type, not a primitive</strong> — a very frequent exam question. It behaves a little like one because it is immutable and has literal syntax, but it is an object, which is why it uses <code>.equals()</code> and can be <code>null</code>.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_17="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the ranges worth memorising</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk351a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">byte</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">8 bits, -128 to 127</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">short</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">16 bits</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">32 bits, about +/- 2.1 billion</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">long</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">64 bits</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">float / double</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">32 / 64 bits, approximate</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Choosing between int, long and double</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int people = 250;
long nationalDebt = 34_000_000_000L;
double weight = 72.5;
System.out.println(people + " " + nationalDebt + " " + weight);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">250 34000000000 72.5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A count that fits comfortably: int.</li><li>A value beyond ~2.1 billion: long, and the literal needs the L suffix.</li><li>A measurement with a fraction: double.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — float and double are approximate</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);
System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3);
System.out.println(Math.abs((0.1 + 0.2) - 0.3) &lt; 1e-9);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0.30000000000000004
false
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>0.1 has no exact binary representation, so the sum is a hair off.</li><li>Comparing with == therefore fails.</li><li>Compare within a tolerance instead.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing <code>long big = 34000000000;</code> without the <code>L</code>. The literal is treated as an <code>int</code> first, and the compiler rejects it before the assignment ever happens.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_18":{"body":"<p>Java randomness is <strong>pseudorandom</strong>: an algorithm produces a sequence that passes statistical tests but is completely determined by a starting <em>seed</em>. That is a feature, not a limitation — a fixed seed makes a buggy run reproducible while you debug it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Random r = new Random();

r.nextInt(6)          // 0 to 5    the bound is EXCLUSIVE
r.nextInt(6) + 1      // 1 to 6    a die roll
r.nextDouble()        // [0.0, 1.0)

Random r = new Random(42);   // fixed seed -&gt; identical sequence every run</pre><p>The general shape for any inclusive range, which is worth memorising because the off-by-one is so easy:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// random int from min to max INCLUSIVE
int v = r.nextInt(max - min + 1) + min;

// example: 10 to 20 -&gt; 11 possible values
int v = r.nextInt(11) + 10;</pre><h3>Getting a random number in the right range</h3><p>The method that matters is <code>nextInt(n)</code>, and its range is the part questions test: it returns a value from <strong>0 to n−1 inclusive</strong>, never n itself. So a die roll is <code>rand.nextInt(6) + 1</code>, and a value between <code>lo</code> and <code>hi</code> inclusive is <code>rand.nextInt(hi − lo + 1) + lo</code>. The <code>+ 1</code> inside the parentheses is the part people drop, and it is what makes the top of the range reachable. Seeding with <code>new Random(42)</code> gives the same sequence every run, which is what makes randomised code testable.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pseudorandom:</strong> generated by a deterministic algorithm from a seed, not physically random</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Seed:</strong> the starting value that determines the whole sequence</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Exclusive bound:</strong> nextInt(n) returns 0 up to n-1; n itself never appears</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Forgetting the <code>+ 1</code> is the classic die bug: <code>nextInt(6)</code> returns 0 through 5 and <strong>never returns 6</strong>. Whenever you write a range, check both ends by hand — what is the smallest value it can give, and the largest?</p></div><i data-d286_z2_18="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — nextInt's argument is a COUNT, not a maximum</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk352a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt(6)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0,1,2,3,4,5</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt(6) + 1</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">1..6 — a die</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt(b - a + 1) + a</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">any range a..b</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A fixed seed makes randomness repeatable</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.Random;
Random r = new Random(42);
System.out.println(r.nextInt(100));
System.out.println(r.nextInt(100));
Random again = new Random(42);
System.out.println(again.nextInt(100));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">30
63
30</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The same seed always produces the same sequence.</li><li>That is what makes a test involving randomness repeatable.</li><li>A new Random with the same seed restarts the sequence.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Building a range</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.Random;
Random r = new Random(7);
int die = r.nextInt(6) + 1;
System.out.println(die &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; die &lt;= 6);
int between50and60 = r.nextInt(11) + 50;
System.out.println(between50and60 &gt;= 50 &amp;&amp; between50and60 &lt;= 60);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>nextInt(6)</code> gives 0–5, so add 1 for a die.</li><li>For a..b inclusive the count is b − a + 1.</li><li>50..60 is 11 values, hence nextInt(11) + 50.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><code>nextInt(n)</code> excludes n. The off-by-one — writing <code>nextInt(6) + 1</code> versus <code>nextInt(7)</code> — is what the questions test.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_19":{"body":"<p>The API documentation is the reference for every class in the standard library, and reading a method signature is a skill worth ten minutes of practice. Everything you need is in one line.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public int indexOf(String str, int fromIndex)
   |     |      |         |
   |     |      |         +-- parameters, in order, with their types
   |     |      +-- the method name
   |     +-- RETURN TYPE — what you get back
   +-- access — callable from anywhere</pre><p>Four things to extract before using an unfamiliar method: what it <strong>returns</strong> (<code>void</code> means it acts rather than produces), whether it is <strong>static</strong> (class name versus object), what the <strong>boundaries</strong> mean, and what <strong>special values</strong> signal failure — <code>-1</code> for <code>indexOf</code>, <code>null</code> for a missing map key.</p><h3>What to read in a method signature</h3><p>Two lines of any API entry decide whether you can use it correctly. The <strong>return type</strong> tells you whether the result must be assigned — the mistake that makes <code>s.trim();</code> on its own do nothing. The <strong>throws clause</strong> tells you what can fail and whether the compiler will force you to handle it. Everything else is detail you can look up. Getting into the habit of reading those two before calling something unfamiliar removes a whole category of bug.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Signature:</strong> the method name together with its parameter types</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Return type:</strong> the type of value the method hands back; void means none</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>API:</strong> the published set of classes and methods available to you, with their contracts</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>A guessed method name that does not exist is <strong>harmless</strong> — the compiler rejects it. The dangerous case is a method that <em>does</em> exist but behaves slightly differently than you assumed: <code>substring(1,3)</code> returning "el" rather than "ell" is the classic, and only the docs tell you the end index is exclusive.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_19="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — how to read a method signature</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk353a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public static double pow(double a, double b)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start"></text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public static</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">call it as Math.pow(...) — no object needed</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what it RETURNS — the type you can assign</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(double a, double b)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what it demands, in order</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reading the return type before you assign</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Math.pow(2, 10));
System.out.println(Math.max(3, 7));
System.out.println(Math.max(3.0, 7));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1024.0
7
7.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The docs say pow returns double, so it prints 1024.0 and not 1024.</li><li>Math.max has overloads; with two ints it returns an int.</li><li>With one double it uses the double overload, so the result prints as a double.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — static or not decides how you call it</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "hello";
System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
System.out.println(String.valueOf(42).length());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">HELLO
2</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>toUpperCase</code> is an instance method — call it ON a String.</li><li><code>valueOf</code> is static — call it on the class itself.</li><li>The word <code>static</code> in the signature is what tells you which.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming a method changes the object it is called on. The documentation's return type is the giveaway: if a String method returns a String, it is handing you a new one and leaving the original alone.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_20":{"body":"<p>Debugging is finding the <em>cause</em> of wrong behaviour, not changing code until the symptom disappears. A repeatable procedure beats intuition, especially under exam pressure.</p><p><strong>1.</strong> Reproduce it with one specific input — if you cannot reproduce it, you cannot confirm a fix. <strong>2.</strong> Read the top line of the stack trace: the exception type and line number say what and where. <strong>3.</strong> Print state at the midpoint of the suspect region, then halve again — each check eliminates half the code. <strong>4.</strong> Compare actual against expected. <strong>5.</strong> Change one thing at a time. <strong>6.</strong> Re-run earlier tests, because fixes routinely break something else.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// print the state, not just the answer
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    total += a[i];
    System.out.println("i=" + i + " a[i]=" + a[i] + " total=" + total);
}</pre><h3>Debugging as a procedure</h3><p>Debugging becomes reliable when it stops being inspection and becomes a procedure: reproduce the failure, narrow it to the smallest input that still fails, print or inspect the values around that point, form one hypothesis, change one thing, and re-test. Changing several things at once is what makes a bug appear to move rather than disappear. The single most useful tool is still a print statement showing a variable together with the loop index that produced it — a value on its own rarely tells you which pass went wrong.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stack trace:</strong> the list of method calls active when an exception was thrown, innermost first</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Regression:</strong> a bug introduced by a change that previously worked</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reproduce:</strong> to trigger the fault reliably with a known input</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Diagnose by symptom. A result <strong>one too small</strong> is a loop-bound error. A result of <strong>0</strong> means the accumulator was declared inside the loop and reset every pass. A total equal to the <strong>last value only</strong> means <code>=</code> was used where <code>+=</code> was meant.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_20="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — three kinds of error, found at three different times</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk354a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">syntax error</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compile time — missing semicolon</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">runtime error</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">while running — divide by zero</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">logic error</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">never reported — wrong answer</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A logic error runs perfectly and is still wrong</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] scores = {80, 90, 100};
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; scores.length; i++) sum += scores[i];
System.out.println(sum / scores.length);
System.out.println((double) sum / scores.length);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">90
90.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both lines compile and both run.</li><li>The first prints an integer average because both operands are int.</li><li>Nothing reports it — only checking the value against an expectation finds it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Tracing by printing the state</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {
    total += i;
    System.out.println("i=" + i + " total=" + total);
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">i=1 total=1
i=2 total=3
i=3 total=6</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Print the variables that change, inside the loop.</li><li>The table it produces shows exactly where a value first goes wrong.</li><li>This is the cheapest debugging technique there is.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Treating "it compiled" as "it works". The compiler checks grammar, not intent — a logic error passes every compile and produces a confidently wrong answer.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_21":{"body":"<p>None of this is enforced by the compiler. It is enforced by every person who reads the code afterwards — and since most programming time is spent reading rather than writing, that is the majority of a program's life.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">class BankAccount        // PascalCase for classes
int studentCount         // camelCase for variables
void calculateTotal()    // camelCase verb for methods
final int MAX_SIZE       // ALL_CAPS for constants

if (x &gt; 5) {             // brace at the end of the line
    doSomething();       // 4-space indent per level
}                        // one statement per line</pre><p>The naming test that matters: <strong>can a reader tell what it holds without tracing the code?</strong> <code>int d</code> fails, <code>int daysRemaining</code> passes. Short names like <code>i</code> are fine for a tight, obvious loop counter and nowhere else.</p><h3>Why style is examined at all</h3><p>Style questions look like opinion and are actually about a shared convention the exam expects you to know. camelCase for variables and methods, PascalCase for classes, ALL_CAPS_WITH_UNDERSCORES for constants. The reason it is worth marks is that these conventions carry information: seeing <code>MAX_SIZE</code> tells a reader it cannot change, and seeing <code>BankAccount</code> tells them it is a type rather than a value. A name that follows the convention is doing part of the documenting.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>camelCase:</strong> variables and methods: studentCount, calculateTotal</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>PascalCase:</strong> class names: BankAccount, StudentRecord</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ALL_CAPS:</strong> constants: MAX_SIZE, TAX_RATE</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>A boolean reads best with an <code>is</code> or <code>has</code> prefix, because it makes the call site read as a sentence: <code>if (isValid(domain))</code> rather than <code>if (validate(domain))</code>. Methods that return a value should be named for what they return; methods that act should be named for the action.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_21="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the naming conventions Java expects</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk355a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ClassName</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">UpperCamelCase</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">methodName, variableName</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">lowerCamelCase</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">CONSTANT_NAME</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">packagename</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">all lower case</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Naming that carries meaning</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int d = 30;
int daysRemaining = 30;
System.out.println(d + " " + daysRemaining);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">30 30</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both compile and both behave identically.</li><li>Only the second tells the next reader what the number means.</li><li>Style is about the reader, not the compiler.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Whitespace and braces do not change behaviour</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int x = 5;
if (x &gt; 3) { System.out.println("big"); }
if (x &gt; 3)
    System.out.println("big again");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">big
big again</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java ignores the layout entirely.</li><li>Both forms run the same.</li><li>Braces are still recommended, because adding a second line to the second form silently breaks it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Omitting the braces on a single-statement <code>if</code>, then adding a second line later. Only the first line stays inside the branch — the second runs unconditionally, and the indentation hides it.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Conventions are not enforced by the compiler but ARE assessed: class names capitalised, variables and methods lowerCamelCase, constants UPPER_SNAKE_CASE.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_22":{"body":"<p><code>var</code> asks the compiler to work out a local variable's type from its initializer. Java stays <strong>statically typed</strong> — the type is fixed permanently at that moment, and the compiled result is identical to writing the type out. This is not the dynamic <code>var</code> of JavaScript.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">var name = "Ana";        // inferred as String — permanently
var count = 5;           // inferred as int

name = 42;               // COMPILE ERROR — name is a String, always</pre><p>The restrictions all follow from that one idea: if there is nothing to infer from, <code>var</code> cannot be used.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">var x;                   // ERROR — no initializer to infer from
var y = null;            // ERROR — null could be any reference type

// locals only
var field = 1;           // ERROR at class level
void f(var p) { }        // ERROR — parameters need explicit types</pre><h3>What var does and does not do</h3><p><code>var</code> is not a dynamic type and does not make Java loosely typed. The compiler infers the type from the initialiser at compile time and fixes it permanently, so <code>var n = 5</code> is an <code>int</code> and cannot later hold a String. Two restrictions follow and are examined: it works only for <em>local</em> variables, not fields or parameters, and it requires an initialiser on the same line, because otherwise there is nothing to infer from.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type inference:</strong> the compiler deducing a type instead of you writing it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Statically typed:</strong> every variable has one type, fixed at compile time</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>var:</strong> a reserved type name usable only for local variables with an initializer</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Use <code>var</code> when the type is obvious from the right-hand side — <code>var list = new ArrayList&lt;String&gt;()</code> — and avoid it when it is not. <code>var total = compute();</code> hides exactly the information the reader needs.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_22="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — var is inferred, not dynamic</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk356a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">var n = 5;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">n is int, permanently</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">n = "five";</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compile error</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">var x;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">error — nothing to infer from</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">var list = new ArrayList&lt;String&gt;();</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fine — the right side says the type</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The type is fixed at the declaration</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">var count = 5;
count = 10;
System.out.println(count);
var name = "Priya";
System.out.println(name.length());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">10
5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>count</code> is inferred as int from the literal 5.</li><li>It stays an int — assigning a String would not compile.</li><li><code>name</code> is a String, so String methods are available.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — var needs something to infer from</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">var total = 0;
for (var i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) total += i;
System.out.println(total);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">10</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each var has an initialiser, so each type is knowable.</li><li><code>var i</code> in a for-loop is inferred as int.</li><li>A declaration with no initial value cannot use var at all.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Reading <code>var</code> as "any type", as in a dynamically typed language. Java infers the type once, at compile time, and then enforces it exactly as if you had written it out.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_23":{"body":"<p>This example brings the whole unit together on one calculation. Every line uses a rule from an earlier section, and the mistakes available here are the mistakes those sections warned about.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">final double OVERTIME_THRESHOLD = 40.0;     // 2.9  named constants
final double OVERTIME_MULTIPLIER = 1.5;
final double TAX_RATE = 0.20;

double rate  = 20.0;                        // 2.7  double, not int
double hours = 45.0;

double gross;
if (hours &gt; OVERTIME_THRESHOLD) {
    double regular  = rate * OVERTIME_THRESHOLD;
    double overtime = rate * OVERTIME_MULTIPLIER * (hours - OVERTIME_THRESHOLD);
    gross = regular + overtime;             // 2.4  parenthesised correctly
} else {
    gross = rate * hours;
}

double net = gross * (1 - TAX_RATE);
System.out.printf("Gross: %.2f  Net: %.2f%n", gross, net);   // 950.00  760.00</pre><p>Check the overtime arithmetic by hand: 40 hours at 20.00 is 800, plus 5 hours at 30.00 is 150, giving 950. The common wrong version applies the 1.5 multiplier to all 45 hours and produces 1350.</p><h3>What the example is checking</h3><p>The salary calculation exists to test whether the arithmetic rules of this unit survive contact with a realistic formula. The rate is a decimal, the salary may be entered as a whole number, and the result must keep its fraction — so the types on each side of every operation decide the answer. Working it with all-integer arithmetic produces a number that is plausible and wrong, which is exactly the failure the question is built around.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Gross pay:</strong> total earnings before deductions</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Net pay:</strong> what remains after tax — gross x (1 - rate)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Time and a half:</strong> the overtime multiplier applied only to hours beyond the threshold</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Keeping 80% is <code>gross * (1 - TAX_RATE)</code>, or equivalently <code>gross - gross * TAX_RATE</code>. Writing <code>gross - TAX_RATE</code> subtracts twenty <strong>cents</strong> rather than twenty percent — it compiles, runs, and is wrong by an amount small enough to look believable.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_23="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the shape of every calculation program</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk357a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">read input</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Scanner</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">compute</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">arithmetic on the right types</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">output</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">printf or println</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Annual to monthly, with the division trap</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int annualSalary = 55000;
System.out.println(annualSalary / 12);
System.out.println(annualSalary / 12.0);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", annualSalary / 12.0);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4583
4583.333333333333
4583.33</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both int, so the first division truncates — the cents vanish.</li><li>Dividing by 12.0 keeps them.</li><li><code>printf("%.2f")</code> formats to two decimal places for money.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Applying a percentage raise</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double salary = 55000;
double raised = salary * 1.04;
System.out.println(raised);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", raised);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">57200.0
57200.00</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A 4% rise multiplies by 1.04.</li><li>The raw double carries more digits than you want to show.</li><li>Formatting at the point of OUTPUT keeps the stored value exact.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Money questions almost always hinge on integer division. If both operands are int, the cents are gone before formatting can help.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_24":{"body":"<p>The same program written twice shows what variables actually buy you. The first version hard-codes the numbers into every expression; the second names them once.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// version 1 — literals everywhere
System.out.println(20.0 * 45.0);
System.out.println(20.0 * 45.0 * 0.8);

// version 2 — named variables
double rate = 20.0, hours = 45.0;
double gross = rate * hours;
double net   = gross * 0.8;
System.out.println(gross);
System.out.println(net);</pre><p>Three concrete gains, and the exam asks about all of them conceptually. <strong>One place to change:</strong> a new rate is a single edit rather than a search. <strong>No recomputation:</strong> <code>gross</code> is calculated once and reused, so the two outputs cannot disagree. <strong>The names document the arithmetic:</strong> <code>rate * hours</code> needs no comment.</p><h3>Why the same program is repeated with variables</h3><p>Rewriting a hard-coded calculation with variables is not busywork; it is the step that makes a program reusable rather than a one-off answer. It also introduces the arc this course follows for the rest of its length: literals become variables here, variables become parameters in Unit 6, and parameters become fields in Unit 7. Each step widens where the value can come from, and recognising that progression makes the later units feel like continuations rather than new topics.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Hard-coded value:</strong> a literal written directly into an expression rather than stored in a variable</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Single point of change:</strong> the property that one edit updates every use</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is the first step of an arc that runs through the whole course. Literals become <strong>variables</strong> here in Unit 2; variables become <strong>parameters</strong> in Unit 6; parameters become <strong>fields of an object</strong> in Unit 7. Each step moves the data closer to the logic that owns it.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_24="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why a named variable beats a repeated literal</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk358a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">total * 0.07</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what is 0.07?</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">TAX_RATE</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">named once, changed once</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">repeated literal</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one missed edit = a bug</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Naming the rate</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">final double TAX_RATE = 0.07;
double gross = 4583.33;
double tax = gross * TAX_RATE;
double net = gross - tax;
System.out.printf("%.2f %.2f%n", tax, net);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">320.83 4262.50</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The rate appears once and is named.</li><li>Every use reads as intent rather than as a magic number.</li><li>Changing the rate is a one-line edit that cannot be applied inconsistently.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Order of operations still applies</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double gross = 1000;
System.out.println(gross - gross * 0.07);
System.out.println((gross - gross) * 0.07);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">930.0
0.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Multiplication binds tighter than subtraction, so the tax is computed first.</li><li>Adding brackets changes the meaning completely.</li><li>When in doubt, bracket what you mean.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Repeating a literal like <code>0.07</code> in several places. When the rate changes, one occurrence gets missed and the program produces two different answers for the same quantity.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>If a value appears twice, name it. Marks in the performance assessment are lost for magic numbers more often than for wrong arithmetic.</div>"},"D286/u2/z2_25":{"body":"<p>Reading input turns a fixed program into one that works for anybody, and <code>Scanner</code> is how Java does it. The only real decision is how much you want to read at a time.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);

String first = in.next();       // ONE word — stops at whitespace
String last  = in.next();
System.out.println(first + " " + last);

String full = in.nextLine();    // the WHOLE line, spaces included</pre><p>Joining the pieces uses <code>+</code>, which concatenates exactly what it is given and never adds a space of its own — so every space in the output has to exist as a literal in the code:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">first + last            // "AnnLee"
first + " " + last      // "Ann Lee"
last + ", " + first     // "Lee, Ann"</pre><h3>What changed from the Unit 1 version</h3><p>Compared with the version in 1.12, only the storage has changed — the logic is identical. That is deliberate: it shows that variables do not add capability, they add flexibility. The program can now be adapted by changing one assignment rather than by editing the middle of a print statement, and the values can come from input rather than from the source code. It is the smallest possible demonstration of why the previous twenty-four sections were worth reading.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Scanner:</strong> the class that reads input, constructed with new Scanner(System.in)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>next():</strong> reads one whitespace-delimited token</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>nextLine():</strong> reads everything up to the end of the current line</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Mixing the two is the classic input bug. <code>nextInt()</code> and <code>next()</code> stop <em>before</em> the newline and leave it behind, so a following <code>nextLine()</code> reads that leftover and returns an <strong>empty String</strong> with no error — the program looks like it skipped a prompt. Call <code>in.nextLine()</code> once to discard the newline before reading the real line.</p></div><i data-d286_z2_25="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — + means two different things</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk359a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"a" + "b"</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">concatenation -&gt; "ab"</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1 + 2</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">addition -&gt; 3</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"Total: " + 1 + 2</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">left to right -&gt; "Total: 12"</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"Total: " + (1 + 2)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">brackets first -&gt; "Total: 3"</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Building a full name</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String first = "Ana";
String last = "Marsh";
String full = first + " " + last;
System.out.println(full);
System.out.println(full.length());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Ana Marsh
9</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>+</code> between Strings joins them.</li><li>The space is a String literal of its own.</li><li>The length counts that space too.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — When + stops adding and starts joining</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int a = 1, b = 2;
System.out.println("sum: " + a + b);
System.out.println("sum: " + (a + b));
System.out.println(a + b + " total");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">sum: 12
sum: 3
3 total</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Evaluation runs left to right: once a String appears, everything after it is joined.</li><li>Brackets force the addition to happen first.</li><li>Before any String appears, + is still addition.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Whenever a question mixes a String with numbers and +, work left to right and switch to concatenation at the first String.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_1":{"body":"<p>Everything in Unit 2 ran straight down the page — every statement, every time. A <strong>branch</strong> is the first thing that changes that: it lets the program choose a path based on a condition, so the same code produces different behaviour for different data.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (temp &gt; 100) {
    System.out.println("boiling");     // runs only when the condition is true
} else if (temp &gt; 0) {
    System.out.println("liquid");      // checked only if the first was false
} else {
    System.out.println("frozen");      // runs when every condition above failed
}</pre><p>The chain rule is what makes <code>else if</code> different from separate <code>if</code> statements: <strong>at most one</strong> block in a chain runs, and evaluation stops at the first true condition. Separate <code>if</code> statements each get tested independently, so several can run. This distinction is the entire content of 3.6 and half of 3.8.</p><h3>Why the chain rule is the whole unit</h3><p>The difference between an <code>if-else</code> chain and a run of separate <code>if</code> statements decides more questions in this unit than any other single fact. In a chain, evaluation stops at the first true condition and <strong>at most one</strong> block runs — every later condition is skipped even if it would also be true. With separate statements each is tested independently, so several can fire. Neither is more correct; the choice depends on whether the cases are alternatives or independent adjustments, and reading which one the code uses is the first thing to establish in any trace.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Branch:</strong> a block of statements that runs only when a condition is true</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Condition:</strong> an expression that evaluates to true or false</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>if-else chain:</strong> a sequence of conditions where the first true one runs and the rest are skipped</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Relational operator:</strong> one of &lt;, &gt;, &lt;=, &gt;=, ==, != — produces a boolean</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Branching plus the looping in Unit 4 is the whole of control flow. Every program you write for the rest of the course is variables (Unit 2) moved around by branches and loops.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286fx1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — IF-ELSE IS A FORK IN THE ROAD</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 208" style="width:100%; max-width:560px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk68a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="180" y="8" width="120" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="240" y="27" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int x = 15;</text><line x1="240" y1="38" x2="240" y2="58" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk68a)"/><polygon points="240,60 320,95 240,130 160,95" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="240" y="92" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">x > 10 ?</text><text x="240" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">(condition)</text><line x1="320" y1="95" x2="390" y2="95" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk68a)"/><text x="348" y="87" fill="#22c55e" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">true</text><rect x="390" y="78" width="110" height="34" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="445" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">if branch runs</text><line x1="160" y1="95" x2="90" y2="95" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk68a)"/><text x="128" y="87" fill="#ef4444" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">false</text><rect x="20" y="78" width="110" height="34" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="75" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">else branch</text><line x1="445" y1="112" x2="445" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="75" y1="112" x2="75" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="75" y1="150" x2="240" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="445" y1="150" x2="246" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk68a)"/><rect x="180" y="152" width="120" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="240" y="171" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">code after if-else</text><text x="240" y="200" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Exactly one branch runs, then both paths rejoin.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>On the OA, trace with the given value <b>before</b> reading the answer choices: write down which branch runs, then evaluate only that branch. Half the distractors are the output of the branch that did <i>not</i> run.</div><i data-d286_z3_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Only one branch of a chain ever runs</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int score = 95;
if (score &gt;= 90) System.out.println("A");
else if (score &gt;= 80) System.out.println("B");
else if (score &gt;= 70) System.out.println("C");
else System.out.println("F");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">A</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The first true test wins and the rest are skipped.</li><li>95 is also >= 80, but that line is never reached.</li><li>That is the whole point of else-if: the tests are mutually exclusive.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Separate ifs are not a chain</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int score = 95;
if (score &gt;= 90) System.out.println("A");
if (score &gt;= 80) System.out.println("B");
if (score &gt;= 70) System.out.println("C");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">A
B
C</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Without else, each if is tested independently.</li><li>95 satisfies all three, so all three print.</li><li>Dropping the elses changes the meaning entirely.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing a grade chain as separate <code>if</code> statements. Every condition is then evaluated, so a high score matches several branches and the program prints more than one answer.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_2":{"body":"<p>Testing "are these equal" is where Java splits into two rules that look identical and behave differently, and it is worth learning now rather than in 3.13 when the bug appears.</p><p>For <strong>primitives</strong>, <code>==</code> compares values and is correct. For <strong>objects</strong> — including Strings — <code>==</code> compares references: it asks "are these the same object in memory", not "do these hold the same characters".</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int a = 5, b = 5;
a == b                     // true — primitives compare by value

String s1 = "cat";
String s2 = "cat";
String s3 = new String("cat");
s1 == s2                   // true  — but only because the pool reused one object
s1 == s3                   // FALSE — different objects, same characters
s1.equals(s3)              // true  — .equals compares contents</pre><p>The <code>s1 == s2</code> case is a trap dressed as a rule. Java keeps identical string literals in a shared pool, so two literals often are the same object — which makes <code>==</code> appear to work right up until a String comes from input or <code>new</code>, and then it silently stops working.</p><h3>Why == on Strings appears to work</h3><p>The reason this trap survives is that <code>==</code> on Strings often gives the right answer by accident. Java keeps identical string literals in a shared pool, so two literals with the same characters usually are the same object and <code>==</code> reports true. The moment a String comes from input, from concatenation at run time, or from <code>new</code>, it is a different object and the comparison silently starts returning false. Code that worked in testing then fails on real data — which is exactly why the rule is to use <code>.equals()</code> always, not just when it seems necessary.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>==:</strong> compares values for primitives, and reference identity for objects</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>.equals():</strong> compares contents; the correct choice for Strings and other objects</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>String pool:</strong> a shared store of string literals, which is why two identical literals can be the same object</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>==</code> on Strings is the single most common Java error on this exam. Always use <code>.equals()</code>. And do not confuse <code>=</code> with <code>==</code> — <code>if (x = 5)</code> does not compile in Java for ints, because the assignment produces an <code>int</code> and <code>if</code> requires a <code>boolean</code>.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — == compares what the variable holds</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk328a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int a == int b</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compares the NUMBERS — correct</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">String s == String t</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compares REFERENCES — usually wrong</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">s.equals(t)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compares the characters — correct</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — = assigns, == compares</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int a = 5;
int b = 5;
System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a != b);
boolean same = (a == b);
System.out.println(same);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>==</code> asks a question and produces a boolean.</li><li><code>=</code> would instead store a value.</li><li>A comparison can be stored in a boolean variable.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Equality for numbers and for text</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int x = 3;
String s = "abc";
String t = "ab" + "c";
System.out.println(x == 3);
System.out.println(s.equals(t));
System.out.println(s.equalsIgnoreCase("ABC"));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>For primitives, == compares the values themselves.</li><li>For Strings, always use <code>.equals</code> to compare contents.</li><li><code>equalsIgnoreCase</code> ignores letter case.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>In a condition, <code>if (x = 5)</code> does not compile in Java for ints — it is an assignment, not a boolean. With <code>boolean</code> variables it DOES compile and is a real bug, so read carefully.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_3":{"body":"<p>Ranges are the natural next step from equality, and the mistake is importing mathematical notation. Java has no <code>0 &lt;= x &lt;= 100</code>. That expression parses as <code>(0 &lt;= x) &lt;= 100</code>, which compares a boolean with an int and fails to compile.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (0 &lt;= x &amp;&amp; x &lt;= 100) { ... }    // correct: two comparisons joined by &amp;&amp;</pre><p>The four relational operators to keep straight are <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code> and <code>&gt;=</code>, and the boundary is where the exam lives. "At least 18" is <code>&gt;= 18</code>; "over 18" is <code>&gt; 18</code>. Read the wording, not the number.</p><h3>Reading the boundary in the wording</h3><p>Range questions almost always turn on a single word. "At least 18" and "18 or over" are <code>&gt;= 18</code>; "over 18" and "more than 18" are <code>&gt; 18</code>. "Between 10 and 20" is ambiguous in English and the question will disambiguate it with "inclusive" or by the answer options. The reliable technique is to test the boundary value itself: work out what the code does at exactly 18, and compare that with what the sentence says should happen at 18. If they differ, the operator is wrong.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Range check:</strong> testing that a value falls between two bounds, written as two comparisons joined by &amp;&amp;</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Inclusive bound:</strong> a boundary value that counts as inside the range — uses &lt;= or &gt;=</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Exclusive bound:</strong> a boundary value that counts as outside the range — uses &lt; or &gt;</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>When you check a range question, test the boundary itself. If the answer differs at exactly 18, the question is about <code>&gt;</code> versus <code>&gt;=</code> and nothing else.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a range needs two bounds</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk329a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x &gt; 10</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">only the lower edge</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x &gt; 10 &amp;&amp; x &lt; 20</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">both edges — a real range</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">10 &lt; x &lt; 20</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">NOT legal Java</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Both ends must be stated</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int t = 15;
System.out.println(t &gt; 10 &amp;&amp; t &lt; 20);
System.out.println(t &gt;= 15 &amp;&amp; t &lt;= 15);
System.out.println(t &gt; 20 &amp;&amp; t &lt; 10);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
true
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Two comparisons joined by && express a range.</li><li>The bounds may coincide, which selects a single value.</li><li>An impossible range is legal code that is simply always false.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Ordering the tests removes the second bound</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int t = 15;
if (t &lt; 10) System.out.println("low");
else if (t &lt; 20) System.out.println("middle");
else System.out.println("high");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">middle</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Reaching the second test already means t >= 10.</li><li>So only the upper bound needs stating.</li><li>An ordered chain is why range code is usually shorter than it looks.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing <code>10 &lt; x &lt; 20</code>. Java evaluates <code>10 &lt; x</code> to a boolean and then tries to compare a boolean with 20, which does not compile. Each bound needs its own full comparison.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_4":{"body":"<p>With a range you can also order the checks so each one does less work. In an <code>else if</code> chain every later condition already knows that all the earlier ones were false, so you never need to restate the lower bound.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (score &gt;= 90) {
    grade = 'A';
} else if (score &gt;= 80) {          // we already know score &lt; 90
    grade = 'B';
} else if (score &gt;= 70) {
    grade = 'C';
} else {
    grade = 'F';
}</pre><p>Order is not optional here. Reverse it — put <code>score &gt;= 70</code> first — and everything above 70 becomes a C, because the first true condition wins and the rest are never tested. The chain reads correctly only from the most restrictive condition to the least.</p><h3>Why ordering a chain is a correctness issue</h3><p>In a chain, each condition is only reached when every earlier one failed, so the conditions carry hidden context. Written from most restrictive to least — 90, then 80, then 70 — each test needs only its lower bound, because the upper bound is implied by the ones above. Reverse the order and the chain still compiles, still runs, and gives everyone above 70 a C, because <code>score &gt;= 70</code> is now the first true condition for a score of 95. It is a logic error of the purest kind: no message, no crash, just wrong grades.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Cascading conditions:</strong> an else-if chain ordered so each condition assumes all previous ones were false</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Mutually exclusive:</strong> conditions where at most one can be true for any input</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Writing the grade chain from lowest to highest is the classic version of this bug. It compiles, it runs, and every score above 70 gets a C — a logic error (1.4) that no compiler will catch.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — where the boundary belongs</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk330a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x &gt;= 90</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">90 is included</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x &gt; 90</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">90 is excluded</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">overlapping ranges</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the first match wins</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a gap between ranges</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">some values match nothing</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Testing the boundary itself</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] marks = {89, 90, 91};
for (int m : marks) {
    String g = (m &gt;= 90) ? "A" : "B";
    System.out.println(m + " -&gt; " + g);
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">89 -&gt; B
90 -&gt; A
91 -&gt; A</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Always test the exact boundary value.</li><li>89 falls below, 90 and 91 are at or above.</li><li>Most range bugs live on one value, not across the whole range.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A chain must cover everything</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int age = 65;
if (age &lt; 13) System.out.println("child");
else if (age &lt; 20) System.out.println("teen");
else if (age &lt; 65) System.out.println("adult");
else System.out.println("senior");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">senior</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each test assumes the previous ones failed.</li><li>65 is not < 65, so it falls through to the final else.</li><li>The bare else is what guarantees every value is handled.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Test three values for any range question: just below the boundary, exactly on it, and just above. That finds nearly every off-by-one.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_5":{"body":"<p>Once ranges get more complicated than a single band, the logical operators do the joining. There are three, and the truth of each is worth holding in one sentence rather than a table.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">&amp;&amp;   AND   true only when BOTH sides are true
||   OR    true when EITHER side is true
!    NOT   flips true to false and false to true

if (age &gt;= 13 &amp;&amp; age &lt;= 19)        // teenager: both must hold
if (day == 6  || day == 7)         // weekend: either will do
if (!isValid)                       // shorthand for isValid == false</pre><p>Precedence matters when they mix: <code>!</code> binds tightest, then <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, then <code>||</code>. So <code>a || b &amp;&amp; c</code> means <code>a || (b &amp;&amp; c)</code>. Parenthesise when you mix them — not because Java is unclear, but because a reader will read it in English order and get it wrong.</p><h3>Precedence between the logical operators</h3><p>When <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> appear in the same expression, <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds tighter — so <code>a || b &amp;&amp; c</code> means <code>a || (b &amp;&amp; c)</code>, not <code>(a || b) &amp;&amp; c</code>. English reads it the other way round, which is why this is worth parenthesising even when the default happens to be what you meant. The related skill is negation: <code>!(a &amp;&amp; b)</code> becomes <code>!a || !b</code> and <code>!(a || b)</code> becomes <code>!a &amp;&amp; !b</code>. The operator flips along with each side, and forgetting to flip it is the standard wrong answer.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>&amp;&amp; (AND):</strong> true only when both operands are true</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>|| (OR):</strong> true when at least one operand is true</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>! (NOT):</strong> inverts a boolean</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Operator precedence:</strong> the order operators bind in: ! before &amp;&amp; before ||</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Negating a compound condition follows De Morgan: <code>!(a &amp;&amp; b)</code> is <code>!a || !b</code>, and <code>!(a || b)</code> is <code>!a &amp;&amp; !b</code>. The operator flips along with each side — forgetting to flip it is the standard wrong answer.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286fx2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A RANGE IS A BAND ON THE NUMBER LINE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 126" style="width:100%; max-width:520px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk69a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><line x1="30" y1="70" x2="450" y2="70" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8"/><line x1="40" y1="63" x2="40" y2="77" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="40" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><line x1="170" y1="63" x2="170" y2="77" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="170" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">20</text><line x1="300" y1="63" x2="300" y2="77" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="300" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40</text><line x1="430" y1="63" x2="430" y2="77" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="430" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">60</text><rect x="170" y="56" width="130" height="28" rx="5" fill="#6366f155" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="235" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x &gt;= 20 &amp;&amp; x &lt;= 40</text><line x1="235" y1="46" x2="235" y2="54" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk69a)"/><text x="240" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">AND selects the band where BOTH tests hold. OR (x < 20 || x > 40) selects everything OUTSIDE the band.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><code>20 &lt;= x &lt;= 40</code> is not legal Java — the compiler reads it left to right, gets a boolean, and cannot compare a boolean to 40. A range always needs two comparisons joined by <code>&amp;&amp;</code>.</div><i data-d286_z3_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — && narrows, || widens</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int n = 25;
System.out.println(n &gt; 10 &amp;&amp; n &lt; 30);
System.out.println(n &lt; 10 || n &gt; 30);
System.out.println(!(n &gt; 10 &amp;&amp; n &lt; 30));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>&& requires both sides — it selects the overlap.</li><li>|| requires either — it selects everything outside.</li><li>Negating a range gives exactly the outside of it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — De Morgan in Java</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int n = 5;
System.out.println(!(n &gt; 10 &amp;&amp; n &lt; 30));
System.out.println(n &lt;= 10 || n &gt;= 30);
System.out.println(!(n &gt; 10 || n &lt; 3));
System.out.println(n &lt;= 10 &amp;&amp; n &gt;= 3);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
true
true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Negating an AND gives an OR of the negations.</li><li>Negating an OR gives an AND of the negations.</li><li>Each pair prints the same, which is what equivalence means.</li></ol></div>"},"D286/u3/z3_6":{"body":"<p>A range with a gap is where separate <code>if</code> statements and an <code>if-else</code> chain stop being interchangeable. If the valid values are 1–10 and 20–30, the gap between them has to be expressed, not assumed.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// two accepted bands, everything else rejected
if ((n &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 10) || (n &gt;= 20 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 30)) {
    System.out.println("accepted");
} else {
    System.out.println("rejected");     // covers the 11-19 gap AND everything outside
}</pre><p>Note that the <code>else</code> is doing real work: it catches the gap and both outer regions at once. Trying to enumerate the rejected values instead is how ranges end up with holes.</p><h3>Why the else does the work</h3><p>With a gap in the accepted range, the temptation is to enumerate the rejected values, and that is where holes appear — it is easy to cover 11 to 19 and forget everything above 30. Expressing the <em>accepted</em> bands and letting a single <code>else</code> catch everything else is both shorter and complete by construction, because "not accepted" needs no enumeration. That is a general habit worth carrying: describe the smaller, well-defined set and let the else handle the remainder.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Gap:</strong> a span of values between two accepted ranges that must be excluded</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Compound condition:</strong> a condition built from several comparisons joined by logical operators</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Dropping the inner parentheses changes the meaning. Because <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds tighter than <code>||</code>, <code>n &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 10 || n &gt;= 20 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 30</code> happens to be correct here — but the moment the shape changes it will not be, so parenthesise deliberately.</p></div><i data-d286_z3_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a gap is an OR of two ranges</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk331a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0-17</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">child fare</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">18-64</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">standard fare</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">65+</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">senior fare</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">age &lt; 18 || age &gt;= 65</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the two discounted bands</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Two separate bands, joined with ||</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] ages = {10, 30, 70};
for (int a : ages) {
    boolean discount = (a &lt; 18) || (a &gt;= 65);
    System.out.println(a + " discount? " + discount);
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">10 discount? true
30 discount? false
70 discount? true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The qualifying ages are not one continuous range.</li><li>|| joins the two bands into a single condition.</li><li>The middle band is what is left over.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same thing as a chain</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int a = 70;
if (a &lt; 18) System.out.println("child");
else if (a &lt; 65) System.out.println("standard");
else System.out.println("senior");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">senior</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Ordering the bands lets each test state only one bound.</li><li>70 fails both earlier tests and lands in the else.</li><li>A chain is usually clearer than one long boolean expression.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>When a question describes a discount for the young AND the old, the condition is an <strong>OR</strong> of two ranges, not an AND. An AND there is always false.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_7":{"body":"<p>Sometimes the checks are genuinely independent — a product can be both on sale and in stock — and then <em>separate</em> <code>if</code> statements are right, precisely because you want more than one to run.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// independent features: each is tested, several can fire
if (hasDiscount) { price -= 5; }
if (isMember)    { price -= 3; }
if (isBulk)      { price -= 2; }

// versus a chain: at most ONE of these ever runs
if (hasDiscount)      { price -= 5; }
else if (isMember)    { price -= 3; }</pre><p>So the choice between <code>else if</code> and separate <code>if</code>s is not stylistic. Ask: can two of these be true at once, and if so should both take effect? Yes means separate statements; no means a chain.</p><h3>Choosing between a chain and separate ifs</h3><p>The decision has a test that always works: can two of these conditions be true at the same time, and if so should both take effect? Discounts stack, so they are separate <code>if</code> statements. Grade bands do not, so they are a chain. Getting this wrong produces bugs in opposite directions — a chain where you wanted stacking applies only the first discount, and separate statements where you wanted a chain apply every band at once. Both compile, and neither reports anything.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Independent conditions:</strong> conditions that can be true simultaneously and should each take effect</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Nested if:</strong> an if placed inside another, used when the inner test only makes sense if the outer one passed</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This one decision quietly determines the output of many trace questions. When you read code in an exam, mark the chains first — everything in a chain after a true condition is dead.</p></div><i data-d286_z3_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — independent features need independent ifs</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk332a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">has sunroof</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">check separately</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">has heated seats</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">check separately</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">else-if chain</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">wrong — only one would count</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">separate ifs</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">right — all can apply</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Several features can be true at once</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">boolean sunroof = true, heatedSeats = true, towbar = false;
int extra = 0;
if (sunroof) extra += 900;
if (heatedSeats) extra += 400;
if (towbar) extra += 550;
System.out.println("extras cost " + extra);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">extras cost 1300</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each feature is independent, so each gets its own if.</li><li>The costs accumulate.</li><li>An else-if chain would have counted only the first.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same code written wrongly</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">boolean sunroof = true, heatedSeats = true;
int extra = 0;
if (sunroof) extra += 900;
else if (heatedSeats) extra += 400;
System.out.println(extra);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">900</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>else if</code> makes the options exclusive.</li><li>The heated seats are never charged for.</li><li>Ask whether the cases exclude one another; if not, do not use else.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Using an else-if chain for features that can coexist. Only one branch runs, so every option after the first is silently ignored.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Mutually exclusive (a grade band, a category) → else-if chain. Independently true (features, permissions, flags) → separate ifs.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_8":{"body":"<p>The branching errors that cost marks are not exotic; they are four specific things, and all four compile cleanly.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (x &gt; 5);                    // 1. stray semicolon — the branch body is empty,
    System.out.println("hi");  //    so this line ALWAYS prints

if (x &gt; 5)
    a = 1;
    b = 2;                     // 2. no braces — b = 2 is NOT in the branch,
                               //    despite the indentation

if (name == "Ada")             // 3. == on a String — compares references (3.2)

if (x = 5)                     // 4. = instead of == — will not compile for int</pre><p>Three of the four are silent. The compiler is happy with an empty branch body, happy with a one-statement branch followed by an unrelated statement, and happy comparing references. Only the last one is caught for you.</p><h3>Why three of the four are silent</h3><p>The branching errors that matter are the ones the compiler accepts. A stray semicolon after <code>if (x &gt; 5);</code> makes the branch body empty, so the indented line below runs unconditionally. Omitting braces means only the first statement belongs to the branch, however the code is indented — Java reads braces, not layout. And <code>==</code> on a String compares references. Only <code>if (x = 5)</code> is caught, and only because an assignment produces an <code>int</code> where a <code>boolean</code> is required. Using braces always removes the first two permanently.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Empty statement:</strong> a lone semicolon; legal Java, and the cause of the if(x&gt;5); bug</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Dangling else:</strong> an else binds to the nearest unmatched if, not to the one the indentation suggests</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Always use braces, even for one statement. It costs two characters and removes bug 1 and bug 2 permanently.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — three that compile and still misbehave</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk333a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (x = 5)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">assignment — only compiles for booleans</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (x &gt; 5);</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">stray semicolon ends the if</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (a) stmt1; stmt2;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">stmt2 is NOT in the branch</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The stray semicolon</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int x = 3;
if (x &gt; 5);
    System.out.println("runs anyway");
System.out.println("and so does this");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">runs anyway
and so does this</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The semicolon right after the condition IS the body — an empty statement.</li><li>The indented line is therefore outside the if entirely.</li><li>It compiles without complaint and prints even though 3 is not > 5.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Missing braces around two statements</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int t = 2;
if (t &gt; 5)
    System.out.println("hot");
    System.out.println("fan on");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">fan on</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Only the FIRST statement belongs to the if.</li><li>The second runs unconditionally, whatever the indentation suggests.</li><li>Braces would have made the intent enforceable.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Whenever a question shows an if whose output looks impossible, check for a semicolon straight after the condition or a missing pair of braces. Those two account for most of them.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_9":{"body":"<p>A toll calculation is the standard worked example because it forces every idea in the unit into one small program: a chain, a range with boundaries, and a compound condition.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// weekday rush hour (6-9 or 16-19) costs more; weekends are flat
double toll;
boolean weekday   = (day &gt;= 1 &amp;&amp; day &lt;= 5);
boolean rushHour  = (hour &gt;= 6 &amp;&amp; hour &lt;= 9) || (hour &gt;= 16 &amp;&amp; hour &lt;= 19);

if (weekday &amp;&amp; rushHour) {
    toll = 5.00;
} else if (weekday) {
    toll = 2.50;
} else {
    toll = 1.00;
}</pre><p>Naming the two conditions as booleans first is the technique worth copying. It turns an unreadable four-way compound test into two named ideas, and the chain then reads as ordinary English — which is also how you avoid ordering it wrongly.</p><h3>Naming conditions before using them</h3><p>The technique this example demonstrates is worth more than the toll rules. Assigning a compound condition to a well-named boolean first — <code>weekday</code>, <code>rushHour</code> — turns an unreadable four-clause test into two named ideas, after which the chain reads as English and its ordering can be checked by eye. It also makes tracing far faster: work out the two booleans for the given input once, then walk the chain, instead of re-evaluating a long condition at every step.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Boolean variable:</strong> a variable holding true or false, often used to name a condition</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Decision table:</strong> a listing of every input combination and its expected result, used to check a chain is complete</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>When a trace question has a chain this long, write out the boolean sub-conditions for the given input first. The chain then resolves in one pass instead of re-reading each condition.</p></div><i data-d286_z3_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — ordered bands, one charge each</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk334a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">weight &lt;= 3500</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">car rate</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;= 7500</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">van rate</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">above</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">lorry rate</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">order matters</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">test the narrowest first</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Charging by weight band</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] weights = {1200, 5000, 12000};
for (int w : weights) {
    double toll;
    if (w &lt;= 3500) toll = 2.40;
    else if (w &lt;= 7500) toll = 5.80;
    else toll = 11.50;
    System.out.printf("%d kg -&gt; %.2f%n", w, toll);
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1200 kg -&gt; 2.40
5000 kg -&gt; 5.80
12000 kg -&gt; 11.50</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Bands are ordered lightest first, so each test needs one bound.</li><li>The first matching band sets the toll and the rest are skipped.</li><li>The final else catches everything heavier.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — An off-peak discount on top</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double toll = 5.80;
boolean offPeak = true;
if (offPeak) toll = toll * 0.80;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", toll);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4.64</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The discount is independent of the band, so it is a separate if.</li><li>Multiplying by 0.80 takes 20% off.</li><li>Combining it into the band chain would have been wrong.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Ordering the bands from heaviest to lightest while still using <code>&lt;=</code>. The first test then catches everything and every vehicle pays the same toll.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_10":{"body":"<p>Order of evaluation decides what an expression means before any branching happens. Java evaluates in a fixed precedence order, and it is short enough to memorise for the exam.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">1.  ()                       parentheses
2.  ++  --  !  (unary)       including unary minus
3.  *  /  %                  multiplicative
4.  +  -                     additive
5.  &lt;  &lt;=  &gt;  &gt;=             relational
6.  ==  !=                   equality
7.  &amp;&amp;                       logical AND
8.  ||                       logical OR
9.  =  +=  -=  *=  /=        assignment (right to left)</pre><p>Two consequences catch people. Arithmetic always resolves before comparison, so <code>a + b &gt; c</code> is <code>(a + b) &gt; c</code>. And comparison always resolves before <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, so <code>x &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; y &gt; 0</code> needs no inner parentheses.</p><h3>What the precedence table is really for</h3><p>You will not be asked to recite the table; you will be asked what an expression evaluates to, and that is the same thing. The two consequences that appear most are that arithmetic always finishes before comparison — so <code>a + b &gt; c</code> is <code>(a + b) &gt; c</code> — and that comparison always finishes before the logical operators, so <code>x &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; y &gt; 0</code> needs no inner parentheses. Assignment being right-associative is the third: <code>a = b = c = 5</code> works because the rightmost assignment happens first and produces a value.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Precedence:</strong> which operator binds first when several appear in one expression</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Associativity:</strong> the direction operators of equal precedence group in; left to right for arithmetic, right to left for assignment</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>a = b = 0</code> works because assignment is right-associative: <code>b = 0</code> happens first and produces 0, which is then assigned to <code>a</code>.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what binds tightest</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk335a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">( )</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">brackets first</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">! </text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">then NOT</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">* / %</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">then multiply/divide</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">+ -</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">then add/subtract</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">then comparisons</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&amp;&amp; then ||</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">AND before OR</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Arithmetic before comparison, comparison before &&</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int a = 2, b = 3, c = 10;
System.out.println(a + b * 2);
System.out.println(a + b &lt; c);
System.out.println(a &lt; b &amp;&amp; b &lt; c);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">8
true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>b * 2</code> is computed before the addition.</li><li><code>a + b</code> is computed before the comparison with c.</li><li>Both comparisons finish before && combines them.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — && binds tighter than ||</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">boolean p = true, q = false, r = false;
System.out.println(p || q &amp;&amp; r);
System.out.println((p || q) &amp;&amp; r);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The first reads as <code>p || (q && r)</code>, which is true.</li><li>Forcing the OR first makes it false.</li><li>Brackets are the only reliable way to say which you meant.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming <code>||</code> and <code>&amp;&amp;</code> have equal precedence and run left to right. <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds tighter, so <code>p || q &amp;&amp; r</code> is <code>p || (q &amp;&amp; r)</code>.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_11":{"body":"<p>A <code>switch</code> is a chain restricted to one thing: comparing a single variable against a list of constant values. That restriction buys readability, and costs you the ability to test ranges — a <code>switch</code> cannot express "greater than 90".</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">switch (day) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
    case 3:
    case 4:
    case 5:
        System.out.println("weekday");
        break;                       // without break, execution FALLS THROUGH
    case 6:
    case 7:
        System.out.println("weekend");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("invalid");
}</pre><p>Fall-through is the defining behaviour and the source of nearly every switch question. Without <code>break</code>, control continues into the next case regardless of whether that case matched. Stacked empty cases (1 through 5 above) use this on purpose; a missing <code>break</code> anywhere else is a bug.</p><h3>When a switch is the wrong tool</h3><p>A <code>switch</code> compares one value against a list of compile-time constants, and everything it cannot do follows from that. It cannot test a range, so grade bands must stay an <code>if</code> chain. It cannot use a variable as a case label. It does not work with <code>double</code> or <code>boolean</code>. What it does buy is readability when there genuinely are many discrete values, and the stacked-case idiom — several <code>case</code> labels sharing one body — which is the one legitimate use of fall-through. Everywhere else, a missing <code>break</code> is a bug that produces extra output rather than an error.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>switch:</strong> a multi-way branch comparing one expression against constant case labels</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>case:</strong> one labelled entry point in a switch</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>break:</strong> exits the switch; without it, execution falls through to the next case</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>default:</strong> the case that runs when nothing else matched; optional, and may appear anywhere</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A switch works with <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>String</code> and <code>enum</code>, but <strong>not</strong> with <code>double</code> or <code>boolean</code>. And every case label must be a compile-time constant — a variable is not allowed.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286fx3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — SWITCH: BREAK EXITS, NO BREAK FALLS THROUGH</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 240" style="width:100%; max-width:520px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk70a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="30" y="10" width="150" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="105" y="29" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">switch (choice)</text><rect x="30" y="58" width="190" height="34" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="125" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">case 1: ... break;</text><rect x="30" y="118" width="190" height="34" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="125" y="139" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">case 2: ...        </text><rect x="30" y="178" width="190" height="34" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="125" y="199" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">case 3: ... break;</text><line x1="105" y1="40" x2="105" y2="56" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk70a)"/><line x1="125" y1="92" x2="125" y2="116" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk70a)"/><line x1="125" y1="152" x2="125" y2="176" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk70a)"/><rect x="222" y="118" width="14" height="34" fill="#ef444433"/><text x="310" y="130" fill="#ef4444" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">no break — execution</text><text x="310" y="144" fill="#ef4444" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">FALLS THROUGH into case 3</text><line x1="220" y1="75" x2="330" y2="75" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk70a)"/><text x="276" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">break</text><line x1="220" y1="195" x2="330" y2="195" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk70a)"/><text x="276" y="187" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">break</text><rect x="332" y="120" width="0.1" height="0.1" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="332" y="58" width="120" height="154" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="392" y="140" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">after the</text><text x="392" y="155" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">switch</text><text x="240" y="232" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">break exits the switch. Omitting it runs the NEXT case body too — the classic exam trap.</text></svg></div></div><i data-d286_z3_11="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Fall-through when break is missing</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Mon");
    case 2: System.out.println("Tue");
    case 3: System.out.println("Wed");
        break;
    default: System.out.println("other");
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Tue
Wed</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Execution ENTERS at the matching case.</li><li>With no break it keeps running into the cases below.</li><li>So case 2 prints Tue and then Wed, stopping at the break.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Deliberate grouping, and the arrow form</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">char grade = 'B';
switch (grade) {
    case 'A': case 'B': System.out.println("pass"); break;
    case 'C': System.out.println("borderline"); break;
    default: System.out.println("fail");
}
int m = 4;
String season = switch (m) {
    case 12, 1, 2 -&gt; "winter";
    case 3, 4, 5 -&gt; "spring";
    default -&gt; "other";
};
System.out.println(season);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">pass
spring</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Stacked labels with no code between them share one body deliberately.</li><li>The arrow form does not fall through at all.</li><li>It can also produce a value, assigned straight into a variable.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Any switch question showing more output than expected is testing fall-through. Trace from the matching label downwards until you meet a <code>break</code>.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_12":{"body":"<p><code>boolean</code> is the type every condition in this unit has actually been producing. It holds exactly two values, <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>, and — unlike C — Java will not accept a number in its place.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">boolean isValid = (age &gt;= 18);       // a comparison IS a boolean value
boolean canVote = isValid &amp;&amp; isRegistered;

if (isValid) { ... }                 // idiomatic
if (isValid == true) { ... }         // legal but redundant
if (1) { ... }                       // COMPILE ERROR — int is not boolean</pre><p>Because a comparison is a value, you can store it, pass it and return it. That is what makes 3.9 readable, and it is the basis of the boolean-returning methods in 6.5.</p><h3>Booleans as values, not just conditions</h3><p>The step that makes conditions manageable is realising a comparison <em>is</em> a value. <code>age &gt;= 18</code> produces a boolean that can be stored, passed to a method, returned from one, or combined with others — which is what makes the named-condition technique in 3.9 possible and what makes predicate methods in 6.5 natural. It also explains why <code>if (isValid == true)</code> is redundant: <code>isValid</code> already is the boolean the <code>if</code> wants.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>boolean:</strong> a primitive type with exactly two values, true and false</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Boolean expression:</strong> any expression producing a boolean, including comparisons and logical combinations</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Flag:</strong> a boolean variable used to record whether something happened</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>A boolean field defaults to <code>false</code>; a boolean local, like every local, has no default at all (2.2). The wrapper class <code>Boolean</code> defaults to <code>null</code>, which is a third possibility and a source of runtime failures (7.13).</p></div><i data-d286_z3_12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a condition IS a boolean</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk336a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">boolean ok = x &gt; 5;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">store the answer</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (ok)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">no == needed</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (ok == true)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">legal but redundant</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if (!ok)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the negation</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Storing and negating a condition</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int stock = 0;
boolean inStock = stock &gt; 0;
System.out.println(inStock);
System.out.println(!inStock);
if (!inStock) System.out.println("reorder");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">false
true
reorder</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The comparison produces a boolean, which can be named.</li><li><code>!</code> flips it.</li><li>A well-named boolean makes the if read like a sentence.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Comparing to true is redundant</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">boolean ready = true;
if (ready) System.out.println("go");
if (ready == true) System.out.println("also go");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">go
also go</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>ready</code> is already a boolean.</li><li><code>ready == true</code> produces the same boolean again.</li><li>The shorter form is preferred, and avoids the <code>=</code> typo.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing <code>if (ready = true)</code> with one equals sign. For booleans this COMPILES — it assigns true and then tests it — so the branch always runs and the compiler never warns you.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_13":{"body":"<p>String comparison is where 3.2 stops being theoretical. Java gives you three methods, and choosing between them is a real decision rather than a style question.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">s1.equals(s2)             // exact content match, case-sensitive -&gt; boolean
s1.equalsIgnoreCase(s2)   // content match, ignoring case            -&gt; boolean
s1.compareTo(s2)          // dictionary order                        -&gt; int

"apple".compareTo("banana")   // negative — apple comes first
"banana".compareTo("apple")   // positive
"apple".compareTo("apple")    // 0 — equal</pre><p><code>compareTo</code> returns an <code>int</code>, not a boolean, and the exact number is not meaningful — only its sign is. It is the method that makes sorting possible, which is why it returns in 13.5 with the <code>Comparable</code> interface.</p><h3>Choosing between the three comparisons</h3><p>The three String comparisons answer different questions and questions test whether you picked the right one. <code>equals</code> asks "are these identical", <code>equalsIgnoreCase</code> asks "identical apart from case", and <code>compareTo</code> asks "which comes first in dictionary order". Only the last returns an <code>int</code>, and only its <em>sign</em> is meaningful — the magnitude is an implementation detail. Putting the literal on the left, as <code>"yes".equals(input)</code>, is worth doing habitually because it cannot throw when the variable is null.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>equals():</strong> content comparison, case-sensitive, returns boolean</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>equalsIgnoreCase():</strong> content comparison ignoring letter case</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>compareTo():</strong> returns negative, zero or positive to indicate dictionary order</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Lexicographic order:</strong> dictionary ordering based on character codes, so all uppercase letters sort before all lowercase</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Calling a method on a null String throws <code>NullPointerException</code>. Writing the literal first — <code>"yes".equals(input)</code> instead of <code>input.equals("yes")</code> — is safe even when <code>input</code> is null.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → Operators, Strings, and Scanner</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_13="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — == against .equals</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk337a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">s1 == s2</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">same object?</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">s1.equals(s2)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">same characters?</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">literals</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">may share one object — == can look right</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new String / input</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">different objects — == fails</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why == sometimes appears to work</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String a = "hello";
String b = "hello";
String c = new String("hello");
System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a == c);
System.out.println(a.equals(c));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Identical literals are pooled, so a and b are the same object and == is true.</li><li><code>new String</code> forces a separate object, so == is false.</li><li><code>.equals</code> compares the characters and is true either way.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Ordering text with compareTo</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("apple".compareTo("banana"));
System.out.println("banana".compareTo("apple"));
System.out.println("apple".compareTo("apple"));
System.out.println("Apple".equalsIgnoreCase("APPLE"));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">-1
1
0
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A negative result means the first sorts before the second.</li><li>Positive means after, zero means equal.</li><li>Only the SIGN matters, not the size of the number.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Always compare Strings with <code>.equals</code>. The == form works often enough with literals to pass a casual test and then fail on input read from a Scanner.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_14":{"body":"<p>Strings are indexed like arrays and, crucially, are <strong>immutable</strong>: no method changes the string it is called on. Every one of them returns a new String and leaves the original untouched.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String s = "Hello";

s.length()          // 5
s.charAt(0)         // 'H'      — indexes start at 0, so the last is length()-1
s.substring(1, 3)   // "el"     — from index 1 UP TO but NOT including 3
s.substring(2)      // "llo"    — from index 2 to the end
s.toUpperCase()     // "HELLO"  — s is still "Hello"

s = s.toUpperCase();   // to keep the result you must assign it</pre><p>The half-open range in <code>substring</code> is not an inconsistency — it is the same convention as loop bounds in Unit 4, and it means <code>substring(a, b)</code> always has length <code>b - a</code>.</p><h3>Why the half-open range is consistent</h3><p><code>substring(a, b)</code> including the start and excluding the end looks like an inconsistency and is the same convention as loop bounds and array indices. Its practical advantage is that the length of the result is always <code>b − a</code>, with no adjustment, and that consecutive substrings tile without overlap: <code>substring(0,3)</code> and <code>substring(3,6)</code> fit together exactly. Once you expect it, the off-by-one disappears. The other half of this section is immutability — every method returns a new String, so an unassigned call does nothing at all.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Immutable:</strong> cannot be changed after creation; every String method returns a new String</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Index:</strong> the position of a character, counting from 0</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>substring(start, end):</strong> the characters from start up to but not including end</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>charAt(i):</strong> the single char at position i</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Forgetting to assign the result is the number one String bug: <code>s.toUpperCase();</code> on its own does nothing. And an index equal to <code>length()</code> throws <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> — the last valid index is <code>length() - 1</code>.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → Operators, Strings, and Scanner</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_14="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — index positions in "Java"</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk338a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">characters</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">J  a  v  a</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">index</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0  1  2  3</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">length()</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">4</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">last valid index</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">length() - 1 = 3</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reading characters and slices</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "Programming";
System.out.println(s.length());
System.out.println(s.charAt(0));
System.out.println(s.substring(0, 7));
System.out.println(s.substring(7));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">11
P
Program
ming</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>substring(a, b)</code> stops just BEFORE b.</li><li>So (0, 7) gives seven characters.</li><li>One argument means from there to the end.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The index that does not exist</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "Java";
System.out.println(s.charAt(s.length() - 1));
try {
    System.out.println(s.charAt(s.length()));
} catch (StringIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("out of bounds");
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">a
out of bounds</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The last character sits at length() − 1.</li><li>Asking for index length() is one past the end.</li><li>Java throws rather than returning anything.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>The length of <code>substring(a, b)</code> is exactly b − a. Use that to check any substring answer in one step.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_15":{"body":"<p><code>char</code> is a numeric type wearing a costume. It stores a 16-bit Unicode code, which is why arithmetic on characters works and why <code>'A'</code> and <code>65</code> are interchangeable in the right context.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">char c = 'A';
int  n = c;              // 65 — automatic widening
char next = (char)(c + 1);   // 'B' — the cast is required to get back to char

'0' is 48    'A' is 65    'a' is 97      // the three anchors worth knowing

int digit = '7' - '0';   // 7 — converts a digit character to its value</pre><p>The <code>Character</code> class supplies the tests you would otherwise write with ranges:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Character.isDigit(c)       Character.isLetter(c)
Character.isUpperCase(c)   Character.isWhitespace(c)
Character.toUpperCase(c)   Character.toLowerCase(c)</pre><h3>Why characters and integers are interchangeable</h3><p>A <code>char</code> is a 16-bit number, so every behaviour in this section follows from ordinary arithmetic. Comparisons work because codes are ordered — which is why <code>'Z' &lt; 'a'</code> is true, since uppercase codes come first. Ranges work for the same reason, so <code>c &gt;= 'a' &amp;&amp; c &lt;= 'z'</code> tests for a lowercase letter. And the <code>Character</code> methods exist to say the same thing more readably: <code>Character.isDigit(c)</code> is clearer than comparing against <code>'0'</code> and <code>'9'</code>, and it is correct for characters beyond ASCII where the range test is not.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>char:</strong> a 16-bit primitive holding a single Unicode character; single quotes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ASCII:</strong> the character encoding covering the first 128 codes; the basis for the numeric values above</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Character class:</strong> the utility class of static methods for classifying and converting characters</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Single quotes make a <code>char</code>, double quotes make a <code>String</code>. <code>'A'</code> and <code>"A"</code> are different types, and <code>char c = "A";</code> does not compile.</p></div><i data-d286_z3_15="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the Character tests worth knowing</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk339a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isDigit(c)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">'0'-'9'</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isLetter(c)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a letter</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isUpperCase / isLowerCase</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">case test</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isWhitespace(c)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">space, tab, newline</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">toUpperCase(c)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns a char</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Classifying each character of a string</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "a1 B";
for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
    char c = s.charAt(i);
    System.out.println(c + " digit=" + Character.isDigit(c) + " letter=" + Character.isLetter(c));
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">a digit=false letter=true
1 digit=true letter=false
  digit=false letter=false
B digit=false letter=true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>charAt</code> pulls one character out at a time.</li><li>The Character class answers the questions about it.</li><li>A space is neither a digit nor a letter.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Changing case returns a new value</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">char c = 'q';
System.out.println(Character.toUpperCase(c));
System.out.println(c);
System.out.println(Character.isUpperCase('Q'));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Q
q
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>toUpperCase</code> RETURNS the converted character.</li><li>The original variable is unchanged.</li><li>Assign the result if you want to keep it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><code>Character.isDigit(c)</code> tests a char. <code>Character.getNumericValue(c)</code> — or <code>c - '0'</code> — converts it to the number it represents. Questions often offer both and mean the second.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_16":{"body":"<p>Searching within a String is the other half of 3.14, and the whole family shares one convention: <strong>-1 means not found</strong>. Nothing throws; you get a sentinel value you have to check.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String s = "hello world";

s.indexOf("o")        // 4   — first occurrence
s.indexOf("o", 5)     // 7   — first occurrence at or after index 5
s.lastIndexOf("o")    // 7
s.indexOf("z")        // -1  — NOT FOUND
s.contains("wor")     // true
s.replace("l", "L")   // "heLLo worLd" — a NEW string, s is unchanged</pre><p>Because <code>indexOf</code> returns -1 rather than throwing, forgetting to check it turns into a <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> later, when the -1 is handed to <code>substring</code>. Test the result before you use it.</p><h3>Why −1 rather than an exception</h3><p>The whole <code>indexOf</code> family returns <strong>−1</strong> for "not found" rather than throwing, which makes it cheap to call and dangerous to ignore. The failure mode is delayed: <code>indexOf</code> quietly returns −1, the value is passed to <code>substring</code> or used as an index, and <em>that</em> throws — so the exception appears on a line that is not where the mistake was. Testing the result before using it is what keeps the error where it happened. The other detail questions test is that <code>replace</code> replaces every occurrence, not just the first.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>indexOf():</strong> the position of the first match, or -1 if absent</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>lastIndexOf():</strong> the position of the last match, or -1</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>contains():</strong> true if the substring appears anywhere</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>replace():</strong> returns a new String with every occurrence replaced</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>replace</code> replaces <em>all</em> occurrences, not just the first. If a question shows only the first one changing, the answer is wrong.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → Operators, Strings, and Scanner</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_16="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what each search returns</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk340a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">indexOf("x")</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">position, or -1 if absent</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">contains("x")</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">true / false</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">replace(a, b)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a NEW string</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the original</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">unchanged — Strings are immutable</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — indexOf returns a position, or -1</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "order-4821-ship";
System.out.println(s.indexOf("4821"));
System.out.println(s.indexOf("9999"));
System.out.println(s.contains("ship"));
System.out.println(s.substring(s.indexOf("-") + 1, s.lastIndexOf("-")));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">6
-1
true
4821</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A found substring reports where it starts.</li><li>Absent gives −1, which is why you must test before using it as an index.</li><li>indexOf and lastIndexOf together pull out the middle field.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — replace does not modify the original</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = "grey shoes, grey hat";
s.replace("grey", "blue");
System.out.println(s);
String t = s.replace("grey", "blue");
System.out.println(t);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">grey shoes, grey hat
blue shoes, blue hat</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The first call computes a new String and throws it away.</li><li>The original is untouched, because Strings are immutable.</li><li>Capturing the return value is what makes the change stick.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Using the result of <code>indexOf</code> without checking for −1. Passing −1 to <code>substring</code> throws, and the message points at the substring rather than at the failed search.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_17":{"body":"<p>The conditional operator compresses a two-way <code>if-else</code> that assigns to one variable into a single expression. It is the only ternary operator in Java, which is why it is often just called "the ternary".</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int max = (a &gt; b) ? a : b;

// identical to
int max;
if (a &gt; b) { max = a; } else { max = b; }</pre><p>It is an <em>expression</em>, so it produces a value and can be used anywhere a value fits — inside a <code>println</code>, as an argument, inside another ternary. That last option is where readability collapses; nesting more than one is where an <code>if</code> is simply better.</p><h3>When the ternary earns its place</h3><p>The conditional operator is an <em>expression</em>, so it produces a value and fits where a value fits — inside a <code>println</code>, as an argument, on the right of an assignment. That is exactly when it is worth using: assigning one of two values to one variable. It stops being worth it the moment it nests, because a ternary inside a ternary is harder to read than the <code>if</code> it replaced. Both branches must also produce compatible types, which is why it cannot contain a statement that returns nothing.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Conditional operator:</strong> condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse — an expression, not a statement</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Ternary:</strong> an operator taking three operands; Java has exactly one</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Both branches must produce compatible types, and the whole thing must produce a value — so a ternary cannot contain a statement like <code>System.out.println</code> used as a branch result of type void.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_17="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the ternary is an expression</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk341a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">cond ? a : b</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">produces a VALUE</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if/else</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">performs an ACTION</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int x = c ? 1 : 2;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">assignable</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nested ternaries</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">legal, but hard to read</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Choosing a value inline</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int stock = 0;
String label = (stock &gt; 0) ? "In stock" : "Sold out";
System.out.println(label);
int a = 7, b = 4;
System.out.println(a &gt; b ? a : b);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Sold out
7</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The condition comes first, then the value if true, then if false.</li><li>The whole thing evaluates to one value.</li><li>That is why it can sit on the right of an assignment.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same logic written both ways</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int n = -5;
String sign = (n &gt;= 0) ? "non-negative" : "negative";
System.out.println(sign);
String sign2;
if (n &gt;= 0) sign2 = "non-negative";
else sign2 = "negative";
System.out.println(sign2);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">negative
negative</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both produce exactly the same result.</li><li>The ternary is shorter when the ONLY job is picking a value.</li><li>An if/else is clearer as soon as either branch does more than one thing.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Reaching for a ternary when each branch has to perform several statements. It cannot hold statements, only values — the attempt does not compile, and forcing it produces unreadable code.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_18":{"body":"<p>Comparing floating-point values with <code>==</code> is the one comparison that is wrong for a reason unrelated to references. Values like 0.1 have no exact binary representation, so arithmetic accumulates a tiny error, and two numbers that should match do not.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3        // FALSE
0.1 + 0.2               // actually 0.30000000000000004

// the correct test: is the difference small enough
final double EPSILON = 0.0001;
if (Math.abs(a - b) &lt; EPSILON) {
    // treat as equal
}</pre><p>This is the same underlying fact as 2.7: <code>double</code> stores an approximation. It is not a rounding bug you can avoid by being careful — it is how binary fractions work, and it is why money is normally stored in whole cents as an <code>int</code>.</p><h3>Why == on doubles is a rule, not a caution</h3><p>Comparing computed doubles with <code>==</code> fails for a structural reason, not an occasional one: values like 0.1 have no exact binary representation, so arithmetic accumulates a tiny error and two numbers that should match do not. It is not fixable by being careful, which is why the remedy is a tolerance — <code>Math.abs(a − b) &lt; EPSILON</code> — rather than more precision. The same fact is why money is stored as whole cents in an integer, and it connects directly back to 2.7.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Epsilon:</strong> a small tolerance value; two doubles are treated as equal when their difference is below it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Math.abs():</strong> absolute value; used so the comparison works regardless of which is larger</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Representation error:</strong> the gap between a decimal value and its nearest binary approximation</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Never write <code>if (total == 0.3)</code> for a computed double. Compare against a tolerance, or use <code>int</code> cents, or <code>BigDecimal</code>.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → Primitive Types and Type Casting</strong></p><i data-d286_z3_18="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — compare within a tolerance</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk342a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a == b</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">unreliable for double</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.abs(a - b) &lt; 1e-9</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the reliable test</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.1 + 0.2</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0.30000000000000004</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">money</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">use int cents or BigDecimal</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why == fails on doubles</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double a = 0.1 + 0.2;
double b = 0.3;
System.out.println(a);
System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(Math.abs(a - b) &lt; 0.000000001);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0.30000000000000004
false
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>0.1 and 0.2 have no exact binary form, so the sum is slightly off.</li><li>== demands the bits match exactly, and they do not.</li><li>Comparing the difference against a small tolerance is the fix.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Money is safest in whole units</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) total += 0.1;
System.out.println(total);
System.out.println(total == 1.0);
int cents = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) cents += 10;
System.out.println(cents);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0.9999999999999999
false
100</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Ten additions accumulate ten small errors.</li><li>The total is not exactly 1.0, so the test fails.</li><li>Counting in whole cents avoids the problem entirely.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Never compare doubles with == in an exam answer. The expected answer is always a tolerance test, or storing the value as an integer number of the smallest unit.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_19":{"body":"<p>Short-circuit evaluation is a promise Java makes about how much of a condition it bothers to run: with <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, if the left side is false the right side is never evaluated, because the answer is already settled. With <code>||</code>, a true left side settles it the same way.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (count != 0 &amp;&amp; total / count &gt; 5) { ... }
//   ^ if count is 0, the division never happens — no ArithmeticException

if (s != null &amp;&amp; s.length() &gt; 0) { ... }
//   ^ the null check GUARDS the method call — order is not optional here</pre><p>This is why the guard has to come first. Swap the two halves and the protection disappears entirely, because the side that would crash gets evaluated before the side that would have prevented it.</p><h3>Short-circuiting as a tool</h3><p>Short-circuit evaluation is usually introduced as an efficiency detail and is more useful as a correctness tool. Because a false left operand of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> means the right is never evaluated, a guard placed first makes the following test safe: <code>count != 0 &amp;&amp; total / count &gt; 5</code> cannot divide by zero, and <code>s != null &amp;&amp; s.length() &gt; 0</code> cannot throw. Reverse the order and the protection vanishes entirely. Exam questions that count how many times a method is called inside a condition are testing exactly this.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Short-circuit evaluation:</strong> skipping the right operand when the left already determines the result</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Guard condition:</strong> a test placed first specifically to make the following test safe</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>&amp; and |:</strong> the non-short-circuiting versions; both sides are always evaluated</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If an exam question counts how many times a method is called inside a condition, short-circuiting is the point. Work left to right and stop the moment the result is determined.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → if/else and switch Statements</strong></p><i data-d286fx4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — SHORT CIRCUIT: THE RIGHT SIDE MAY NEVER RUN</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 136" style="width:100%; max-width:520px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk71a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="20" y="30" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(x != 0)  &amp;&amp;  (total / x &gt; 2)</text><rect x="14" y="12" width="92" height="26" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="160" y="12" width="150" height="26" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="60" y1="44" x2="60" y2="70" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk71a)"/><text x="60" y="86" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">false?</text><line x1="84" y1="82" x2="200" y2="82" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk71a)"/><text x="288" y="86" fill="#ef4444" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">right side is SKIPPED</text><text x="288" y="102" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">whole thing is false — no ÷0 crash</text><text x="240" y="128" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">&& stops at the first false; || stops at the first true. Java guarantees left-to-right.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>This is why <code>(x != 0) &amp;&amp; (total / x &gt; 2)</code> is safe but the reversed order crashes when x is 0. If a question swaps the operand order, the answer usually changes.</div><i data-d286_z3_19="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The right side may never run</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int count = 0, total = 50;
if (count != 0 &amp;&amp; total / count &gt; 5) System.out.println("high");
else System.out.println("skipped safely");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">skipped safely</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>count != 0</code> is false.</li><li>&& already knows the answer, so the division is never evaluated.</li><li>Swapping the two conditions would divide by zero and crash.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Watching the short circuit happen</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(check(false) &amp;&amp; check(true));
System.out.println("---");
System.out.println(check(true) || check(false));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">evaluated false
false
---
evaluated true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The first prints only one 'evaluated' line — the right side of && is skipped.</li><li>The second also prints only one — || stops as soon as it sees true.</li><li>Only the side that can still change the answer is evaluated.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Putting a side effect on the right of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> or <code>||</code> — an increment, or a method that writes something. It may never run, and the bug appears only for certain inputs.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_20":{"body":"<p>This worked example runs the whole unit through a salary problem: a rate chosen by a chain, then a discount applied only under a compound condition.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">double rate;
if (salary &gt;= 100000)      { rate = 0.28; }
else if (salary &gt;= 50000)  { rate = 0.22; }
else if (salary &gt;= 25000)  { rate = 0.15; }
else                       { rate = 0.10; }

double tax = salary * rate;

// the sale discount is independent, so it is a separate if
if (isMember &amp;&amp; purchase &gt; 100) {
    purchase *= 0.90;
}</pre><p>Notice the two structures side by side and doing different jobs: the chain picks exactly one rate (3.4), while the separate <code>if</code> applies an independent adjustment (3.7). Getting this pairing right is what the section is testing.</p><h3>Two structures doing different jobs</h3><p>This example puts a chain and an independent <code>if</code> side by side deliberately. The tax rate is one of several mutually exclusive bands, so it is a chain ordered highest-first. The member discount is an independent adjustment that should apply on top, so it is a separate statement. Swapping either produces a program that runs and is wrong: a chain for the discount would suppress it whenever a band matched, and separate statements for the bands would apply every rate in turn.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Tiered rate:</strong> a value selected from bands by an ordered else-if chain</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Compound discount condition:</strong> two requirements joined by &amp;&amp;, both of which must hold</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Ordering the chain lowest-first breaks it silently, and using <code>||</code> where the wording says "and" gives the discount to everyone. Read the requirement sentence once per operator.</p></div><i data-d286_z3_20="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — band first, then adjustments</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk343a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">find the band</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">else-if chain</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">apply discounts</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">separate ifs</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">format at the end</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">printf %.2f</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A tiered commission</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double sales = 12000;
double rate;
if (sales &lt; 5000) rate = 0.02;
else if (sales &lt; 20000) rate = 0.05;
else rate = 0.08;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", sales * rate);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">600.00</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The bands are mutually exclusive, so an else-if chain is right.</li><li>12000 fails the first test and passes the second.</li><li>Formatting happens once, at output.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Stacking an independent discount</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double price = 250.00;
boolean member = true;
boolean clearance = true;
if (member) price *= 0.90;
if (clearance) price *= 0.80;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", price);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">180.00</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both discounts can apply, so each gets its own if.</li><li>They compound: 10% off, then 20% off what remains.</li><li>That is not the same as a single 30% off.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Read carefully whether discounts stack or replace each other. Two 10% discounts applied in turn give 19% off, not 20% — a favourite exam distinction.</div>"},"D286/u3/z3_21":{"body":"<p>The final example combines the String methods of 3.13 to 3.16 with branching: given a name and a list, decide whether it matches, ignoring case and surrounding spaces.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String input = scnr.nextLine().trim();          // strip stray spaces first

if (input.equalsIgnoreCase("Ada")) {
    System.out.println("exact match");
} else if (input.toLowerCase().contains("ad")) {
    System.out.println("partial match at index " + input.toLowerCase().indexOf("ad"));
} else {
    System.out.println("no match");
}</pre><p>The order of operations in the second branch matters: lowercase first, then search, so that "AD", "Ad" and "ad" all match. Doing it the other way — searching then lowercasing — would search the original case and miss.</p><h3>The rest of the String search family</h3><p>Beyond <code>indexOf</code> and <code>contains</code>, three more methods appear constantly in matching problems and all return a boolean.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">s.startsWith("A")     true if the string begins with "A"
s.endsWith(".com")    true if it ends with ".com"
s.isEmpty()           true if the length is 0
s.isBlank()           true if it is empty or only whitespace (Java 11+)</pre><p><code>startsWith</code> and <code>endsWith</code> are case-sensitive, like <code>equals</code>, so a case-insensitive prefix test means normalising first: <code>s.toLowerCase().startsWith("a")</code>.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><h3>Normalise, then compare</h3><p>The order of operations in the matching logic is what makes it work. Trimming first removes stray spaces that would defeat any comparison; lowercasing before searching means every capitalisation of the target matches. Searching first and lowercasing afterwards would search the original case and miss. That sequence — normalise the input, then test it — is the general habit worth taking from this section, and it is the reason <code>equalsIgnoreCase</code> exists at all.</p><i data-deep286="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>trim():</strong> returns a new String with leading and trailing whitespace removed</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Normalisation:</strong> converting input to a consistent form (case, spacing) before comparing it</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Normalise, then compare. That single habit removes most String-matching bugs, and it is the same reasoning that makes <code>equalsIgnoreCase</code> exist in the first place.</p></div><i data-d286_z3_21="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — searching with a flag</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk344a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">found = false</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">before the loop</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">compare with .equals</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">never ==</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">found = true; break;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">stop at the first hit</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">after the loop</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">report on the flag</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Searching a list of names</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] names = {"Ada", "Grace", "Alan"};
String target = "Grace";
boolean found = false;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; names.length; i++) {
    if (names[i].equals(target)) { found = true; break; }
}
System.out.println(found ? "found" : "not found");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">found</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The flag starts false — the assumption before any evidence.</li><li><code>.equals</code> compares the characters, which is what matters.</li><li><code>break</code> stops as soon as the answer is known.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reporting where it was found</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] names = {"Ada", "Grace", "Alan"};
int index = -1;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; names.length; i++) {
    if (names[i].equalsIgnoreCase("alan")) { index = i; break; }
}
System.out.println(index);
System.out.println(index &gt;= 0 ? "at " + index : "absent");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2
at 2</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>−1 is the conventional 'not found' marker, matching indexOf.</li><li>equalsIgnoreCase makes the search case-insensitive.</li><li>Testing index >= 0 is how you distinguish found from absent.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Comparing the names with <code>==</code>. It compares object identity, so a name read from input will not match a literal even when the text is identical.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Set the flag or index BEFORE the loop, never inside it. Declaring it inside means it is destroyed on each pass and the result is unreadable afterwards.</div>"},"D286/u4/z4_1":{"body":"<p>A branch chooses whether a block runs. A <strong>loop</strong> chooses how many times. That is the only difference, and it is the reason loops are where beginners stall: an <code>if</code> you can read once, but a loop you have to trace.</p><p>Every loop, whatever its syntax, has the same three moving parts. Miss any one of them and you get an infinite loop or a loop that never starts.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int i = 0;              // 1. INITIALISE  — set up the loop variable
while (i &lt; 5) {         // 2. CONDITION   — checked BEFORE each pass
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;                // 3. UPDATE      — move toward making the condition false
}</pre><p>Java has three loop forms and they are interchangeable in power: <code>while</code> when you do not know the count in advance, <code>for</code> when you do, and <code>do-while</code> when the body must run at least once (19.1). Choosing between them is about clarity, never about capability.</p><h3>The three moving parts, named</h3><p>Every loop — whatever the syntax — is made of the same three things, and naming them is what lets you debug one you did not write.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Part</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Does</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">If you get it wrong</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Initialisation</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">sets the starting value before the first test</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the loop starts in the wrong place, or does not compile</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Condition</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">is tested before each pass; the loop runs while it is true</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the loop never starts, or never stops</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Update</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">changes something the condition depends on</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>infinite loop</strong> — the classic cause</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int i = 0;              // initialisation
while (i &lt; 5) {         // condition
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;                // update  &lt;- forget this and it never ends
}</pre><p>An infinite loop is almost always a missing or ineffective update: the condition tests something the body never changes. The other half of the pair is a loop that never runs at all, which happens when the condition is already false at the first test — often correct behaviour, not a bug, when the data is empty.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Loop:</strong> a construct that repeats a block of statements while a condition holds</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Iteration:</strong> one pass through the loop body</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Loop variable:</strong> the variable the condition tests and the update changes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Infinite loop:</strong> a loop whose condition never becomes false, usually because the update is missing or moves the wrong way</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Nearly every trace question in the exam is a loop question. The reliable method is a table: one column per variable, one row per iteration. It is slower than reading and it is right far more often.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz41="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a loop is a branch with one extra arrow — the one going back</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 300" style="width:100%; max-width:660px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk223a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><line x1="304" y1="14" x2="304" y2="288" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 5"/><text x="175" y="24" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">BRANCH — an if</text><line x1="175" y1="38" x2="175" y2="57" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><path d="M175,59 L259,86 L175,113 L91,86 Z" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="175" y="91" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">hours &gt; 40</text><text x="182" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">true</text><line x1="175" y1="113" x2="175" y2="142" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><rect x="112" y="142" width="126" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="175" y="167" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">add overtime</text><line x1="175" y1="182" x2="175" y2="212" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><line x1="91" y1="86" x2="52" y2="86" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="52" y1="86" x2="52" y2="199" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="52" y1="199" x2="168" y2="199" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><text x="56" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">false</text><rect x="112" y="212" width="126" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="175" y="235" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">print the pay</text><text x="175" y="272" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the block runs 0 or 1 times</text><text x="487" y="24" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LOOP — a while</text><line x1="487" y1="38" x2="487" y2="57" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><path d="M487,59 L571,86 L487,113 L403,86 Z" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="487" y="91" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">hours &gt; 0</text><text x="494" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">true</text><line x1="487" y1="113" x2="487" y2="142" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><rect x="424" y="142" width="126" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="487" y="167" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">pay one hour</text><path d="M424,162 C368,162 368,86 401,86" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><rect x="326.2" y="187" width="123.6" height="22" rx="11" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.16"/><text x="388" y="202" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">back to the test</text><line x1="573" y1="86" x2="622" y2="86" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="622" y1="86" x2="622" y2="230" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="622" y1="230" x2="552" y2="230" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk223a)"/><text x="577" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">false</text><rect x="424" y="212" width="126" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="487" y="235" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">print the pay</text><text x="487" y="272" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the block runs 0, 1 or 500 times</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — folding a repeated block into a loop</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A warehouse program scans four crates. Written out by hand it is the same two lines copied four times, with only the crate number changing — which is the signal that a loop is wanted.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int crate = 1;                          // 1. INITIALISE
while (crate &lt;= 4) {                    // 2. CONDITION
    System.out.println("Crate " + crate + " scanned");
    crate++;                            // 3. UPDATE
}
System.out.println("Total crates: " + (crate - 1));</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Test #</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">crate</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">crate &lt;= 4</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "Crate 1 scanned", crate becomes 2</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "Crate 2 scanned", crate becomes 3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "Crate 3 scanned", crate becomes 4</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "Crate 4 scanned", crate becomes 5</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>false</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">loop exits — the body does not run</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>Crate 1 scanned
Crate 2 scanned
Crate 3 scanned
Crate 4 scanned
Total crates: 4</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two counts come out of that table and the exam asks for both. The body ran <strong>four</strong> times; the condition was evaluated <strong>five</strong> times, because the failing test is what ends the loop. And <code>crate</code> is left holding 5, not 4 — which is why the last line subtracts one.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: four passes, five tests, and crate ends at 5</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — naming the three parts in code you did not write</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">An electric van charges from 12 percent, gaining 17 points every 10 minutes. How long until it reaches 80?</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int minutes = 0;
int charge  = 12;
while (charge &lt; 80) {
    charge  += 17;
    minutes += 10;
}
System.out.println(charge + " " + minutes);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Initialisation</strong> is the two declarations above the loop: <code>charge = 12</code> is the value the condition will test, <code>minutes = 0</code> is the accumulator.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Condition</strong> is <code>charge &lt; 80</code>. It is tested before every pass, including the first.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Update</strong> is <code>charge += 17</code>. Note that <code>minutes += 10</code> is <em>not</em> the update — the condition does not test it, so it could never stop the loop.</li></ol><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Pass</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">charge at the test</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">charge &lt; 80</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">charge after</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">minutes after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">12</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">29</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">10</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">29</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">46</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">20</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">46</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">63</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">30</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">63</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">80</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">40</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">80</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>false</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>80 40</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The charge overshoots to exactly 80 and the loop stops there. Had the condition been <code>charge != 80</code> instead of <code>charge &lt; 80</code>, a starting value of 13 would step 13, 30, 47, 64, 81 — straight past 80 without ever equalling it, and the loop would never end. Prefer <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&gt;</code> over <code>!=</code> whenever the update takes steps larger than one.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: charge = 80, minutes = 40, after four passes</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The update has to change something the <em>condition</em> tests. A loop whose body busily changes five variables still runs forever if none of them is the one in the condition — which is why the first thing to do with a suspected infinite loop is read the condition, then hunt for that exact variable in the body.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Loop questions come in three shapes and all three are answered by the same trace table: <em>what does it print</em>, <em>how many times does the body run</em>, and <em>what is the loop variable afterwards</em>. Write one row per test — not per pass — and the third question answers itself, because the last row holds the value that failed the condition.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_2":{"body":"<p><code>while</code> is the plainest form: test, run, repeat. The test happens <em>before</em> the body, so a while loop can run zero times — which is usually what you want when the data might be empty.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int total = 0;
int n = scnr.nextInt();

while (n != -1) {          // -1 is a SENTINEL: a value meaning "stop"
    total += n;
    n = scnr.nextInt();    // the update MUST be inside the loop
}
System.out.println(total);</pre><p>The structure above — read one, then loop while it is not the sentinel, reading the next at the bottom — is called a priming read, and it is the standard shape for input-driven loops. Reading only at the top of the loop instead would test the same value forever.</p><h3>while and do-while side by side</h3><p>The two forms differ in one thing only: <em>when</em> the test happens. That single difference decides whether the body can run zero times.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">while (cond) { body }      // test FIRST  -&gt; body runs 0 or more times
do { body } while (cond);  // test AFTER  -&gt; body runs 1 or more times</pre><p>So <code>do-while</code> is the right choice exactly when the body must happen at least once before there is anything to test — showing a menu, then asking whether to show it again; reading a first value, then deciding whether to read more. Note the semicolon after the closing <code>while</code> of a do-while; it is required, and leaving it off is a compile error rather than a silent bug.</p><p><code>while (true)</code> with a <code>break</code> inside is a deliberate infinite loop, and it is legitimate when the exit condition only becomes knowable in the middle of the body. It is not the same as an accidental infinite loop, and the exam distinguishes them by whether a <code>break</code> is reachable.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>while loop:</strong> repeats while a condition is true, testing before each iteration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Sentinel value:</strong> a special input value that signals the end of data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Priming read:</strong> reading the first value before the loop so the condition has something to test</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pre-test loop:</strong> a loop that checks its condition before the first iteration, so it may run zero times</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Forgetting the update inside the body is the standard infinite loop. If the loop variable is never changed, the condition can never change either.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → while and do-while Loops</strong></p><i data-d286fx5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHILE: TEST, BODY, BACK TO THE TEST</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 470 100" style="width:100%; max-width:500px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk72a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="150" y="8" width="130" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="215" y="27" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i &lt; 3 ?</text><line x1="280" y1="23" x2="360" y2="23" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk72a)"/><text x="318" y="15" fill="#22c55e" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">true</text><rect x="360" y="8" width="100" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="410" y="27" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">body runs</text><line x1="410" y1="38" x2="410" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="410" y1="68" x2="215" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="215" y1="68" x2="215" y2="40" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk72a)"/><text x="312" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">back to the test</text><line x1="150" y1="23" x2="70" y2="23" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk72a)"/><text x="112" y="15" fill="#ef4444" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">false</text><rect x="10" y="8" width="60" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="40" y="27" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">exit</text><text x="235" y="92" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">A while loop tests FIRST. If the test is false on arrival, the body never runs at all.</text></svg></div></div><p style="margin-top:14px;">Tracing <code>int i = 0; while (i &lt; 3) { System.out.print(i + " "); i++; }</code> one row per pass:</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">iteration</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">i before test</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">i < 3 ?</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">what runs</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">output so far</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">print i, then i++</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">print i, then i++</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0 1</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">print i, then i++</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0 1 2</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><b>false</b></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">loop exits</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0 1 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>The loop body ran 3 times but the TEST ran 4 times — the final, failing test is what ends the loop. Questions that ask "how many times is the condition evaluated?" want that extra one.</div><i data-tbd286bz42="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the priming read: which values are read, and which are actually processed</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 296" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk224a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Scanner input stream:  48  52  61  -1</text><text x="30" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">READ</text><rect x="60" y="72" width="96" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="108" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">48</text><text x="108" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">read 1</text><rect x="205" y="72" width="96" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">52</text><text x="253" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">read 2</text><rect x="350" y="72" width="96" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="398" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">61</text><text x="398" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">read 3</text><rect x="495" y="72" width="96" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="543" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">-1</text><text x="543" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">read 4</text><text x="30" y="160" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">BODY</text><line x1="108" y1="140" x2="108" y2="168" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk224a)"/><rect x="60" y="168" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="108" y="193" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">total = 48</text><line x1="253" y1="140" x2="253" y2="168" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk224a)"/><rect x="205" y="168" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="193" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">total = 100</text><line x1="398" y1="140" x2="398" y2="168" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk224a)"/><rect x="350" y="168" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="398" y="193" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">total = 161</text><line x1="543" y1="140" x2="543" y2="168" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><rect x="495" y="168" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="543" y="193" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">never added</text><rect x="28" y="226" width="300" height="58" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="44" y="250" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">read 1 is the PRIMING READ —</text><text x="44" y="268" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">it happens before the loop is ever tested</text><rect x="348" y="226" width="300" height="58" rx="10" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="364" y="250" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">reads 2-4 sit at the BOTTOM of the body,</text><text x="364" y="268" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">so the next value is ready for the next test</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a sentinel loop, read by read</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A clinic records waiting times in minutes and types −1 to finish. A <code>Scanner</code> can be built over a fixed String, which is how a snippet like this is tested without typing anything.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">Scanner scnr = new Scanner("48 52 61 -1");
int total = 0;
int reading = scnr.nextInt();       // priming read
while (reading != -1) {
    total += reading;
    reading = scnr.nextInt();       // read the NEXT one, at the bottom
}
System.out.println("Total: " + total);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Test #</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">reading</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">reading != -1</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">total after the body</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">48</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">48</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">52</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">100</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">61</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">161</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">−1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>false</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">161 — the body never runs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>Total: 161</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Four values were read and three were added. The sentinel is consumed by the last <code>nextInt()</code> inside the body and then fails the test, so it never reaches <code>total</code>. Move that read to the top of the body instead and <code>reading</code> is tested before it is ever updated on the first pass, so the priming value is skipped and the total comes out as 113.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: Total: 161</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the zero-pass case, and the one loop that cannot have it</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The stockroom is empty. Both loops below are asked to process the stock; only one of them notices there is nothing to process.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int stock = 0, passes = 0;
while (stock &gt; 0) { stock--; passes++; }
System.out.println("while ran " + passes);

int stock2 = 0, passes2 = 0;
do { stock2--; passes2++; } while (stock2 &gt; 0);
System.out.println("do-while ran " + passes2);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>while</code> tests <code>0 &gt; 0</code> first. It is false on arrival, so the body is skipped entirely and <code>passes</code> stays 0.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>do-while</code> runs its body <em>before</em> testing anything, so <code>stock2</code> is decremented to −1 and <code>passes2</code> reaches 1.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Only then is <code>-1 &gt; 0</code> evaluated; it is false and the loop stops with one pass already spent.</li></ol><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>while ran 0
do-while ran 1</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The do-while did not just count one extra pass — it took stock <em>negative</em>. That is the whole risk of a post-test loop on possibly-empty data, and the reason <code>while</code> is the default choice unless the body genuinely must happen once.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 0 and 1 — the pre-test loop can run zero times, the post-test loop cannot</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">When a question hands you a while loop and asks for a count, decide first whether it can run <strong>zero</strong> times: evaluate the condition with the starting values before you trace anything. Half the wrong answers on these come from assuming at least one pass. And remember the count of <em>tests</em> is always one more than the count of <em>passes</em>.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_3":{"body":"<p>Two patterns cover most of what while loops are actually used for, and both are worth recognising instantly because the exam reuses them constantly.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// PATTERN 1 — accumulate: build a running total
int sum = 0, i = 1;
while (i &lt;= 10) { sum += i; i++; }        // sum is 55

// PATTERN 2 — validate: keep asking until the input is acceptable
int age = scnr.nextInt();
while (age &lt; 0 || age &gt; 120) {
    System.out.println("Invalid, try again:");
    age = scnr.nextInt();
}</pre><p>A third, subtler pattern is digit extraction, which shows up in almost every course that teaches loops, and depends entirely on the integer division and modulo from 2.11:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int n = 4372, count = 0;
while (n &gt; 0) {
    int digit = n % 10;      // 2, then 7, then 3, then 4  — last digit
    n = n / 10;              // 437, 43, 4, 0              — chop it off
    count++;
}                            // count is 4</pre><h3>The two loop patterns, written out</h3><p>A <strong>sentinel loop</strong> reads until a special value says stop. The sentinel is never processed — it is a signal, not data — which is why the read happens before the test.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int total = 0;
int value = scnr.nextInt();       // priming read
while (value != -1) {             // -1 is the sentinel
    total += value;               // the sentinel never reaches here
    value = scnr.nextInt();       // read the next one at the BOTTOM
}</pre><p>An <strong>accumulator</strong> is a variable declared <em>before</em> the loop that gathers a result across passes — a running total, a count, a largest-so-far. Declaring it inside the loop resets it every pass, which is the single most common accumulator bug.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int count = 0, sum = 0, max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
for (int n : values) {
    sum += n;                     // total
    count++;                      // how many
    if (n &gt; max) max = n;         // largest so far
}
double average = count == 0 ? 0 : (double) sum / count;   // guard the empty case</pre><p>The average line carries two exam points at once: divide only after checking the count is not zero, and cast to <code>double</code> or integer division throws the fraction away.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Accumulator:</strong> a variable holding a running total or product across iterations</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Input validation loop:</strong> a loop that repeats until the user supplies acceptable data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Digit extraction:</strong> peeling digits off a number with % 10 and / 10</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>An accumulator for a sum starts at 0; for a product it must start at 1, since anything times zero is zero. Getting that initial value wrong is a favourite exam distractor.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → while and do-while Loops</strong></p><i data-tbd286bz43="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — digit extraction: % 10 takes the digit, / 10 removes it</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 292" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk225a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int n = 40295;   while (n > 0) { sum += n % 10;  n = n / 10; }</text><text x="22" y="48" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">digit = n % 10</text><rect x="34" y="56" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="65" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><line x1="65" y1="90" x2="65" y2="116" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><rect x="144" y="56" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="175" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><line x1="175" y1="90" x2="175" y2="116" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><rect x="254" y="56" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="285" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><line x1="285" y1="90" x2="285" y2="116" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><rect x="364" y="56" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="395" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><line x1="395" y1="90" x2="395" y2="116" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><rect x="474" y="56" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="505" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><line x1="505" y1="90" x2="505" y2="116" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><text x="22" y="110" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">n</text><rect x="22" y="116" width="86" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="65" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40295</text><line x1="108" y1="137" x2="132" y2="137" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><text x="120" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">/ 10</text><rect x="132" y="116" width="86" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="175" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4029</text><line x1="218" y1="137" x2="242" y2="137" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><text x="230" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">/ 10</text><rect x="242" y="116" width="86" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="285" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">402</text><line x1="328" y1="137" x2="352" y2="137" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><text x="340" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">/ 10</text><rect x="352" y="116" width="86" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="395" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40</text><line x1="438" y1="137" x2="462" y2="137" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><text x="450" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">/ 10</text><rect x="462" y="116" width="86" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="505" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><line x1="548" y1="137" x2="572" y2="137" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk225a)"/><text x="560" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">/ 10</text><rect x="572" y="116" width="86" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="615" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><text x="615" y="178" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">test fails</text><text x="22" y="200" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">sum</text><rect x="34" y="206" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="65" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><rect x="144" y="206" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="175" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">14</text><rect x="254" y="206" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="285" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="364" y="206" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="395" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="474" y="206" width="62" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="505" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">20</text><text x="340" y="274" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">% 10 reads the last digit;  / 10 throws it away.  The loop ends when nothing is left.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting digits and summing them in one pass</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">An electricity meter reads 40295. How many digits, and what do they add up to? This is the standard check-digit shape, and it needs no Strings at all.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int reading = 40295;
int n = reading, digits = 0, sum = 0;
while (n &gt; 0) {
    int d = n % 10;      // the last digit
    sum += d;
    n = n / 10;          // integer division drops it
    digits++;
}
System.out.println(digits + " digits, sum " + sum);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Pass</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">n at the test</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">d = n % 10</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">sum</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">n = n / 10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">40295</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4029</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4029</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">9</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">14</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">402</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">402</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">16</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">40</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">40</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">16</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">20</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">test fails, loop ends</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>5 digits, sum 20</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two details carry the whole thing. <code>n / 10</code> is <em>integer</em> division, so 4029 / 10 is 402 and the remainder is simply gone — that is what makes it a shift. And the digits come out backwards, last first, which is why this loop is also the standard way to reverse a number.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 5 digits, summing to 20</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why a product accumulator cannot start at zero</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The same loop maintaining two accumulators at once — one adding, one multiplying — shows why their starting values are different.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int sum = 0, product = 1, k = 1;
while (k &lt;= 5) {
    sum     += k;
    product *= k;
    k++;
}
System.out.println(sum + " " + product);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">k</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">sum += k</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">product *= k</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0 + 1 = 1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1 × 1 = 1</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1 + 2 = 3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1 × 2 = 2</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3 + 3 = 6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2 × 3 = 6</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">6 + 4 = 10</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">6 × 4 = 24</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">10 + 5 = 15</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">24 × 5 = 120</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>15 120</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Each accumulator starts at its own <em>identity</em> — the value that changes nothing under its operation. Zero is the identity for addition, one is the identity for multiplication. Start <code>product</code> at 0 and every multiplication produces 0, so the answer is 0 no matter what the data is; start <code>sum</code> at 1 and every total is one too big.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 15 and 120</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">An input-validation loop must re-read inside the body. Writing <code>while (age &lt; 0) { System.out.println("Invalid"); }</code> with no second read prints the message forever, because the value being tested never changes. The symptom is a screen filling with the same line — and it is the same missing-update fault as any other infinite loop, just disguised as an input problem.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_4":{"body":"<p>A <code>for</code> loop is a <code>while</code> loop with the three parts collected onto one line. Nothing about it is more powerful — it is more readable, and it keeps the loop variable from leaking, which matters in 4.10.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
//   ^init      ^cond  ^update
    System.out.println(i);         // prints 0 1 2 3 4
}

// exactly equivalent to
int i = 0;
while (i &lt; 5) { System.out.println(i); i++; }</pre><p>The order of events per pass is: condition, body, update. So the update runs <em>after</em> the last statement of the body, and the condition is checked once more after that — which is why <code>i</code> ends up as 5, not 4, when the loop finishes.</p><p>The two idiomatic bounds are worth memorising as shapes rather than derived each time:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt;  n; i++)   // n iterations, i from 0 to n-1   &lt;- arrays
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++)   // n iterations, i from 1 to n     &lt;- counting</pre><h3>Why i ends at 5</h3><p>The order of events in a <code>for</code> loop is fixed, and tracing it once removes most off-by-one confusion.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) { ... }

  1  i = 0                    initialisation, once only
  2  is i &lt; 5?  yes  -&gt; body, then i++     (i = 1)
  3  is i &lt; 5?  yes  -&gt; body, then i++     (i = 2)
     ...
  6  is i &lt; 5?  yes  -&gt; body, then i++     (i = 5)
  7  is i &lt; 5?  NO   -&gt; loop ends</pre><p>The body ran five times, with <code>i</code> taking 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — but <code>i</code> finishes holding <strong>5</strong>, because the update runs before the final test. That final increment is the whole reason the loop stops.</p><p>Two bounds cover nearly everything: <code>i &lt; n</code> runs <em>n</em> times and is what you want for an array of length <em>n</em>; <code>i &lt;= n</code> runs <em>n</em> + 1 times and is the classic off-by-one when applied to an array, because index <em>n</em> does not exist.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>for loop:</strong> a loop with initialisation, condition and update declared in its header</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Loop control variable:</strong> the variable declared in the init part; scoped to the loop only</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Off-by-one error:</strong> looping one time too many or too few, usually from &lt; versus &lt;=</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt;= n; i++)</code> runs n+1 times, and with an array of length n it throws <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> on the final pass. Use <code>&lt;</code> with <code>length</code> — never <code>&lt;=</code>.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-d286fx6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE FOUR PARTS OF A FOR LOOP, IN ORDER</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 460 170" style="width:100%; max-width:500px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk73a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="30" y="34" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (</text><text x="76" y="34" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int i = 0</text><text x="156" y="34" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">;</text><text x="170" y="34" fill="#22c55e" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i &lt; 5</text><text x="222" y="34" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">;</text><text x="236" y="34" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i++</text><text x="272" y="34" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">) { body }</text><line x1="112" y1="44" x2="112" y2="66" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk73a)"/><text x="112" y="82" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">① init</text><text x="112" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">once, first</text><line x1="194" y1="44" x2="194" y2="66" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk73a)"/><text x="196" y="82" fill="#22c55e" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">② test</text><text x="196" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">before every pass</text><line x1="252" y1="44" x2="252" y2="110" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk73a)"/><text x="268" y="124" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">④ update — AFTER the body,</text><text x="268" y="138" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">then back to the test ②</text><line x1="320" y1="44" x2="320" y2="66" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk73a)"/><text x="322" y="82" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">③ body</text><text x="230" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Order: ① once, then ② ③ ④, ② ③ ④, ... until the test fails. i is 5 when the loop ends.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbd286bz44="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — &lt; against &lt;= : the same header, one extra visit</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 322" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk226a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)     versus     for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++)</text><line x1="60" y1="96" x2="620" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="90" y1="90" x2="90" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="90" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><line x1="178" y1="90" x2="178" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="178" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><line x1="266" y1="90" x2="266" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="266" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><line x1="354" y1="90" x2="354" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="354" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><line x1="442" y1="90" x2="442" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="442" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><line x1="530" y1="90" x2="530" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="530" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><rect x="72" y="108" width="388" height="26" rx="13" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.16"/><text x="266" y="126" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">i < 5 visits 0 1 2 3 4  —  five passes</text><rect x="490" y="108" width="98" height="26" rx="13" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.16"/><text x="539" y="126" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">i <= 5 also visits 5</text><text x="60" y="172" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">an array of length 5:</text><rect x="56" y="190" width="68" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="90" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">72</text><text x="90" y="250" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[0]</text><rect x="144" y="190" width="68" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="178" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">85</text><text x="178" y="250" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[1]</text><rect x="232" y="190" width="68" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="266" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">91</text><text x="266" y="250" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[2]</text><rect x="320" y="190" width="68" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="354" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">60</text><text x="354" y="250" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[3]</text><rect x="408" y="190" width="68" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="442" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">78</text><text x="442" y="250" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[4]</text><rect x="496" y="190" width="68" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="530" y="218" fill="#e11d48" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✖</text><text x="530" y="250" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[5]</text><text x="340" y="278" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a[5] throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 5 out of bounds for length 5</text><text x="340" y="306" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">The last valid index is length - 1.  <= reaches one past the end, every time.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting passes and the value left behind</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A site logs twice as many visits each week as the week number. Six weeks, then the total.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int hits = 0;
for (int week = 1; week &lt;= 6; week++) {
    hits += week * 2;
}
System.out.println(hits);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Pass</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">week</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">week &lt;= 6</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">hits += week * 2</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">hits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">+2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">+4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">6</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">+6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">12</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">+8</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">20</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">+10</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">30</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">true</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">+12</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">42</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">—</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>false</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">loop ends</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">42</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>42</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Here <code>&lt;=</code> is correct, because the loop is counting weeks for a human — 1 through 6 — not indexing anything. That is the honest distinction: <code>i &lt; n</code> for array positions, <code>i &lt;= n</code> for counting <em>n</em> things starting at one.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 42</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — where the loop variable goes when the loop ends</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A theatre has four rows of three seats. The loop variable is declared <em>outside</em> the header here, which is the only way to read it afterwards.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int seats = 0;
int row;                             // declared OUTSIDE
for (row = 0; row &lt; 4; row++) {
    seats += 3;
}
System.out.println("rows " + row + ", seats " + seats);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>row</code> takes the values 0, 1, 2, 3 inside the body — four passes, so <code>seats</code> reaches 12.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">After the fourth pass the update runs once more, making <code>row</code> equal to 4.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Only then is <code>4 &lt; 4</code> tested. It is false, so the loop ends with <code>row</code> holding 4 — one past the last value the body ever saw.</li></ol><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>rows 4, seats 12</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Written the usual way, <code>for (int row = 0; ...)</code>, that final 4 is unreachable: the variable is scoped to the loop and the line below would not compile. Both forms are correct; the choice is simply whether the caller needs the value afterwards.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: rows 4, seats 12 — the loop variable ends one past its last body value</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">For "how many times does this run", use the formula rather than tracing: <code>for (i = start; i &lt; end; i += step)</code> runs ⌈(end − start) / step⌉ times. For <code>i = 0; i &lt; n</code> that is simply <em>n</em>; for <code>i = 1; i &lt;= n</code> it is also <em>n</em>. Trace only when the update is irregular or the body changes the loop variable.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_5":{"body":"<p>Once the shape is fixed, the variations are all in the update expression, and each one is a small idiom with a specific use.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 10; i &gt; 0; i--)      // countdown: 10 down to 1
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 20; i += 2)   // every second value: 0 2 4 ...
for (int i = 1; i &lt; 100; i *= 2)  // doubling: 1 2 4 8 16 32 64
for (int i = 0, j = 9; i &lt; j; i++, j--)   // two variables moving toward each other</pre><p>That last one is the shape behind reversing and palindrome checks, and it returns in 5.7 for arrays. The comma form is the only place Java lets you declare or update more than one thing in a for header.</p><p>The iteration count is worth being able to compute without tracing: for <code>i = start; i &lt; end; i += step</code>, the count is <code>ceil((end - start) / step)</code>.</p><h3>The update expression is where the variations live</h3><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Header</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Runs</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><em>n</em> times, 0 to n−1</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">walking an array forward</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>for (int i = n - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><em>n</em> times, backwards</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">reversing, removing while iterating</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i += 2)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">every second item</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">alternate elements</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><em>n</em> times, 1 to n</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">counting things for people, not indexes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Counting down needs <code>&gt;=</code> rather than <code>&gt;</code> if index 0 is to be included — using <code>&gt;</code> silently skips the first element, which is a bug that only shows up at one end of the data.</p><p>A stray semicolon after the header, <code>for (...) ;</code>, gives the loop an empty body: it runs to completion doing nothing, then the block below it executes once. It compiles, so nothing warns you.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Step:</strong> the amount the loop variable changes each iteration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Countdown loop:</strong> a loop that decrements toward a lower bound</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Two-pointer loop:</strong> a loop with one index moving forward and another backward</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>An empty section in the header is legal: <code>for (;;)</code> is an intentional infinite loop, escaped with <code>break</code> (4.9).</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-tbd286bz45="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — four update expressions, and exactly which values each one visits</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 372" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk227a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the update expression decides which values the body ever sees</text><text x="20" y="56" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int t = 5;  t < 40;  t += 7)</text><line x1="258" y1="72" x2="580" y2="72" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="258" cy="72" r="5.5" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="258" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><circle cx="338" cy="72" r="5.5" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="338" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12</text><circle cx="418" cy="72" r="5.5" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="418" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">19</text><circle cx="498" cy="72" r="5.5" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="498" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">26</text><circle cx="578" cy="72" r="5.5" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="578" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">33</text><text x="600" y="76" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">5 passes</text><text x="20" y="122" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 0;  i < 12;  i += 2)</text><line x1="258" y1="138" x2="580" y2="138" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="258" cy="138" r="5.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="258" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><circle cx="322" cy="138" r="5.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="322" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><circle cx="386" cy="138" r="5.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="386" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><circle cx="450" cy="138" r="5.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="450" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6</text><circle cx="514" cy="138" r="5.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="514" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><circle cx="578" cy="138" r="5.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="578" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">10</text><text x="600" y="142" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">6 passes</text><text x="20" y="188" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 1;  i < 100; i *= 2)</text><line x1="258" y1="204" x2="580" y2="204" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="258" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="258" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><circle cx="311.3333333333333" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="311.3333333333333" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><circle cx="364.6666666666667" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="364.6666666666667" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><circle cx="418" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="418" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><circle cx="471.33333333333337" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="471.33333333333337" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><circle cx="524.6666666666667" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="524.6666666666667" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">32</text><circle cx="578" cy="204" r="5.5" fill="#8b5cf6" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="578" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">64</text><text x="600" y="208" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">7 passes</text><text x="20" y="254" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 4;  i >= 0; i--)</text><line x1="258" y1="270" x2="580" y2="270" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="258" cy="270" r="5.5" fill="#e8795a" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="258" y="260" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><circle cx="338" cy="270" r="5.5" fill="#e8795a" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="338" y="260" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><circle cx="418" cy="270" r="5.5" fill="#e8795a" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="418" y="260" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><circle cx="498" cy="270" r="5.5" fill="#e8795a" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="498" y="260" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><circle cx="578" cy="270" r="5.5" fill="#e8795a" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="578" y="260" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><text x="600" y="274" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">5 passes</text><rect x="20" y="316" width="640" height="44" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="340" y="344" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">for (int i = 4; i > 0; i--) stops at 1 — index 0 is never visited. Use >= to include it.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting the passes of a stepped loop without tracing</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A bus leaves at minute 5 and then every 7 minutes, up to but not including minute 40. How many departures, and at what times?</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int count = 0;
for (int t = 5; t &lt; 40; t += 7) {
    count++;
    System.out.print(t + " ");
}
System.out.println("| count " + count);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Apply the formula: ⌈(end − start) / step⌉ = ⌈(40 − 5) / 7⌉ = ⌈5⌉ = <strong>5</strong> passes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The visited values are start + 0·step, start + 1·step, … : 5, 12, 19, 26, 33.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The next value would be 40, and <code>40 &lt; 40</code> is false, so it is never visited — which is the check that the count of 5 is right.</li></ol><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>5 12 19 26 33 | count 5</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Notice that <code>t</code> lands exactly on the bound here. Had the header read <code>t &lt;= 40</code> there would be a sixth departure at 40; had the step been 5 instead of 7, ⌈35/5⌉ = 7 passes ending at 35. The formula survives both changes without tracing.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 5 departures: 5, 12, 19, 26, 33</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the countdown boundary: &gt; against &gt;=</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A launch counter counts 4 down to 0. The two headers differ by one character and by one value.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">for (int i = 4; i &gt; 0; i--) System.out.print(i + " ");
System.out.println();
for (int i = 4; i &gt;= 0; i--) System.out.print(i + " ");</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Header</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Values visited</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Passes</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Right for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>i &gt; 0</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4 3 2 1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">counting <em>things</em> down to one</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>i &gt;= 0</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4 3 2 1 0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">walking <em>indexes</em> back to the start</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>4 3 2 1 
4 3 2 1 0 </div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">On an array of length 5 the second form is the correct backwards walk, because index 0 is a real element. The first form silently skips the first element — and silently is the problem: nothing throws, the array is simply one short in every result.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 4 3 2 1 then 4 3 2 1 0</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A semicolon straight after the header — <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++);</code> — gives the loop an <em>empty body</em>. It compiles, runs its five passes doing nothing, and then executes the block below it exactly once. The give-away in the output is a result that looks like a single pass with the final value of the loop variable, and the give-away in the source is a brace-opening line that ends in <code>;{</code>.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_6":{"body":"<p>Looping over a String is the first real use of the 3.14 methods together. The bound is <code>length()</code> — with parentheses, because on a String it is a method, unlike an array where <code>length</code> is a field (5.2).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String s = "hello world";
int vowels = 0;

for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
    char c = s.charAt(i);
    if (c=='a' || c=='e' || c=='i' || c=='o' || c=='u') {
        vowels++;
    }
}</pre><p>Building a String inside a loop is the one place where immutability (3.14) has a cost: each <code>+=</code> creates a whole new String, so a loop of n concatenations does n allocations. <code>StringBuilder</code> exists for exactly this.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) { sb.append(i); }
String out = sb.toString();      // "01234"</pre><h3>Walking a String, and why StringBuilder exists</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String s = "hello";
for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {     // length() — a METHOD on String
    char c = s.charAt(i);
    System.out.print(c);
}

int[] a = new int[5];
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { ... } // length — a FIELD on an array</pre><p><code>s.length()</code> with parentheses on a String, <code>a.length</code> without on an array. The exam asks this directly, and the compiler error for getting it the wrong way round names the other form, so it is easy to recognise once seen.</p><p>Because a String cannot be changed, <code>result += c</code> inside a loop does not append — it builds a brand-new String each pass and throws the old one away. Over <em>n</em> characters that is <em>n</em> allocations and roughly <em>n²</em> character copies.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = s.length() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) sb.append(s.charAt(i));
String reversed = sb.toString();          // one buffer, appended in place</pre><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>length():</strong> the number of characters in a String — a method, so it needs parentheses</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>StringBuilder:</strong> a mutable string buffer; append() modifies it in place instead of creating a new object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reverse loop:</strong> iterating from length()-1 down to 0, used to reverse or check palindromes</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>s.length</code> (no parentheses) does not compile for a String, and <code>arr.length()</code> does not compile for an array. String uses a method; array uses a field. The exam tests this pair directly.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz46="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a String is indexed like an array — and += rebuilds it every pass</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 318" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk228a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">String code = "WGU-286";</text><rect x="88" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="116" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">W</text><text x="116" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[0]</text><rect x="152" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="180" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">G</text><text x="180" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[1]</text><rect x="216" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="244" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">U</text><text x="244" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[2]</text><rect x="280" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="308" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">-</text><text x="308" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[3]</text><rect x="344" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="372" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="372" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[4]</text><rect x="408" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="436" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="436" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[5]</text><rect x="472" y="44" width="56" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="500" y="71" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">6</text><text x="500" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[6]</text><line x1="394" y1="118" x2="394" y2="96" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk228a)"/><text x="394" y="134" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">code.charAt(4) is '2'</text><line x1="88" y1="34" x2="536" y2="34" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="344" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="1" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="552" y="70" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">code.length()</text><text x="552" y="86" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">is 7 — a METHOD</text><rect x="20" y="156" width="316" height="150" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="32" y="174" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">result += c   — a NEW String every pass</text><rect x="36" y="194" width="64" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="68" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"W"</text><text x="68" y="246" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">discarded</text><rect x="110" y="194" width="64" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="142" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"WG"</text><text x="142" y="246" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">discarded</text><rect x="184" y="194" width="64" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="216" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"WGU"</text><text x="216" y="246" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">discarded</text><rect x="258" y="194" width="64" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="290" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"WGU-"</text><text x="290" y="246" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">discarded</text><text x="178" y="282" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4 objects built, 3 thrown away</text><rect x="348" y="156" width="312" height="150" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="360" y="174" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">StringBuilder — ONE buffer, appended in place</text><rect x="372" y="190" width="264" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="504" y="218" fill="var(--text)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">W G U -</text><line x1="392" y1="258" x2="392" y2="238" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk228a)"/><line x1="452" y1="258" x2="452" y2="238" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk228a)"/><line x1="512" y1="258" x2="512" y2="238" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk228a)"/><line x1="572" y1="258" x2="572" y2="238" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk228a)"/><text x="504" y="282" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">append() extends the same buffer</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — walking a String and counting what you find</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">How many characters and how many vowels are in a course title? The loop bound is <code>length()</code> with parentheses, because a String is an object and that is a method call.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">String course = "Java Fundamentals";
int vowels = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; course.length(); i++) {
    char c = Character.toLowerCase(course.charAt(i));
    if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') {
        vowels++;
    }
}
System.out.println(course.length() + " chars, " + vowels + " vowels");</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The title is 17 characters including the space, so <code>i</code> runs 0 through 16.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>charAt(i)</code> hands back one <code>char</code>; <code>Character.toLowerCase</code> means the test does not need ten cases for the capitals.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The vowels are the a and a of "Java" and the u, a, e, a of "Fundamentals" — six in all.</li></ol><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>17 chars, 6 vowels</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Writing <code>i &lt;= course.length()</code> here would throw <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> on the last pass, for exactly the reason an array does: the valid indexes stop at <code>length() - 1</code>.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 17 characters, 6 vowels</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reversing a String with a StringBuilder</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Walking a String backwards is the standard reverse, and it is where a StringBuilder earns its place.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">String code = "WGU-286";
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = code.length() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {
    sb.append(code.charAt(i));
}
System.out.println(sb.toString());</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">i</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">code.charAt(i)</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">buffer after append</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"6"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">8</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"68"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"682"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">-</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"682-"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">U</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"682-U"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">G</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"682-UG"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">W</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">"682-UGW"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>682-UGW</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two bounds are load-bearing. The start is <code>length() - 1</code> — index 7 does not exist — and the end is <code>i &gt;= 0</code>, not <code>i &gt; 0</code>, or the W at index 0 is dropped and the answer is silently one character short. Written with <code>result += ...</code> instead, the output is identical but seven separate String objects are created and six immediately discarded.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 682-UGW</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Whenever a snippet mixes Strings and arrays, check the length forms first: <code>s.length()</code> for a String, <code>a.length</code> for an array, and later <code>list.size()</code> for an ArrayList. Exam questions that look like loop questions are frequently this question wearing a disguise, and the wrong form is a <em>compile</em> error, so "does not compile" is a live answer.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_7":{"body":"<p>Nesting a loop inside a loop multiplies the iterations: an outer loop of n and an inner loop of m runs the inner body n times m times. The inner loop restarts completely on every pass of the outer one — that restart is the part people trace wrongly.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int row = 1; row &lt;= 3; row++) {
    for (int col = 1; col &lt;= 4; col++) {
        System.out.print(row * col + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();        // ends the row — belongs to the OUTER loop
}
// 1 2 3 4
// 2 4 6 8
// 3 6 9 12</pre><p>Which loop a statement belongs to is decided by braces, and it is the usual bug: put the <code>println()</code> inside the inner loop and every number lands on its own line. This is the same brace-versus-indentation issue as 3.8, with more visible consequences.</p><h3>Tracing a nested loop</h3><p>The inner loop runs to completion for <em>every single pass</em> of the outer one, then starts over from its own initialisation. Writing out the first few pairs is faster than reasoning about it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int r = 1; r &lt;= 3; r++) {
    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= 2; c++) {
        System.out.print(r + "" + c + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();          // OUTER loop — once per row
}

11 12
21 22
31 32                              3 x 2 = 6 inner passes</pre><p>Move that <code>println()</code> inside the inner loop and every pair lands on its own line; delete the braces around the inner body and only the first statement belongs to it. Total iterations of the inner body is outer count × inner count — so a nested pair over the same <em>n</em> is <em>n</em>², which is where O(n²) comes from.</p><p>A triangle pattern is the same shape with the inner bound depending on the outer variable: <code>for (int c = 1; c &lt;= r; c++)</code> gives 1, then 2, then 3 stars.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Nested loop:</strong> a loop contained in the body of another loop</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Outer / inner loop:</strong> the containing loop and the contained one; the inner runs fully for each outer pass</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Triangular pattern:</strong> a nested loop whose inner bound depends on the outer variable, e.g. j &lt;= i</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>For total-iteration questions, multiply the counts — unless the inner bound depends on the outer variable. <code>for i in 1..n { for j in 1..i }</code> runs n(n+1)/2 times, not n². This is the same sum that makes selection sort O(n²) in 17.6.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-d286fx7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — NESTED LOOPS: OUTER PICKS THE ROW, INNER WALKS IT</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 530 204" style="width:100%; max-width:560px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk74a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="120" y="30" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="146" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="182" y="30" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="208" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="244" y="30" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="270" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="306" y="30" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="332" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><rect x="120" y="82" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="146" y="108" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">5</text><rect x="182" y="82" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="208" y="108" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">6</text><rect x="244" y="82" width="52" height="42" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="270" y="108" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">7</text><rect x="306" y="82" width="52" hei