10</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">4 × 4 = 16 would be the answer for a square; the real answer is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10, which is <em>n</em>(<em>n</em> + 1) / 2 = 4 × 5 / 2. That sum is exactly the comparison count of selection sort, which is why the same arithmetic reappears in the sorting unit.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 10 stars, not 16</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The inner loop's initialisation runs again on every outer pass — that is what "restarts" means. Hoisting it out, <code>int c = 1;</code> above the outer loop, means <code>c</code> is already past its bound after the first row, so rows two onward print nothing at all. The output is one correct row followed by blank lines, which looks like a printing bug and is really a declaration in the wrong place.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_8":{"body":"<p>Developing incrementally means writing the smallest thing that runs, checking it, then adding one piece. It sounds like advice; it is actually a debugging strategy, because a bug in code you just added is far cheaper to find than a bug somewhere in 60 lines.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// step 1: get the loop running at all
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { System.out.println(i); }

// step 2: add the real work, keep the print
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { sum += arr[i]; System.out.println(i + " " + sum); }

// step 3: remove the trace print once it is correct</pre><p>The temporary print inside the loop is the cheapest debugger there is, and 11.4 makes the same point for recursion. Print the loop variable together with whatever it changed — a value alone rarely tells you which pass produced it.</p><h3>Stubs, and what "smallest thing that runs" means</h3><p>A <strong>stub</strong> is a method that exists with the right name and signature but does something trivial — returns 0, prints a fixed line — so the rest of the program can be compiled and run before that part is written.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double taxFor(double pay) {
    return 0.0;                   // stub: correct shape, wrong answer, compiles
}</pre><p>That is what makes incremental development a debugging strategy rather than advice. You always have a running program, so when it breaks you know the cause is in the few lines you just added. The alternative — writing sixty lines and then compiling — gives you a list of errors with no ordering and no way to tell which are consequences of which.</p><p>Hard-coding the input first is the same idea applied to data: get the calculation right with a fixed value you can check by hand, and only then read the value from the user.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Incremental development:</strong> building a program in small verified steps rather than all at once</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Trace output:</strong> temporary print statements used to observe variable values while running</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stub:</strong> a placeholder implementation that lets the surrounding code run before the real logic exists</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>On a timed exam the same habit works in reverse: when a loop question looks tangled, trace two iterations by hand rather than trying to see the whole answer at once.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz48="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why small steps are a debugging strategy, not just tidiness</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 308" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk230a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="180" y="24" fill="#10b981" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">INCREMENTAL — always running</text><text x="510" y="24" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ALL AT ONCE</text><rect x="60" y="52" width="232" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="48" cy="73" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="48" y="77" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="74" y="72" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">empty loop + a trace print</text><text x="74" y="87" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">6 lines since the last run</text><text x="302" y="78" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">✓ runs</text><line x1="176" y1="94" x2="176" y2="104" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk230a)"/><rect x="60" y="108" width="232" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="48" cy="129" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="48" y="133" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="74" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">add the stub  taxFor() → 0.0</text><text x="74" y="143" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">4 lines since the last run</text><text x="302" y="134" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">✓ runs</text><line x1="176" y1="150" x2="176" y2="160" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk230a)"/><rect x="60" y="164" width="232" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="48" cy="185" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="48" y="189" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="74" y="184" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">add the real bracket logic</text><text x="74" y="199" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">5 lines since the last run</text><text x="302" y="190" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">✓ runs</text><line x1="176" y1="206" x2="176" y2="216" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk230a)"/><rect x="60" y="220" width="232" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="48" cy="241" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="48" y="245" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="74" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">delete the trace prints</text><text x="74" y="255" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">−2 lines since the last run</text><text x="302" y="246" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">✓ runs</text><text x="180" y="292" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a bug can only be in the last few lines</text><rect x="400" y="52" width="230" height="154" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="515" y="76" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">60 lines written</text><text x="515" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">before the first compile</text><text x="515" y="124" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">error: cannot find symbol</text><text x="515" y="144" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">error: incompatible types</text><text x="515" y="164" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">error: ';' expected</text><text x="515" y="184" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">... 11 more</text><line x1="515" y1="214" x2="515" y2="240" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk230a)"/><text x="515" y="262" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">which error caused which?</text><text x="515" y="292" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">no ordering, no last-known-good</text><line x1="346" y1="40" x2="346" y2="276" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 5"/></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — building a payroll loop in two verified steps</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Step one is not the program. It is the smallest thing that <em>runs</em>: the loop shape, a stub value, and a trace print that shows the loop is going round the right number of times.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">// STEP 1 — shape only. pay is a stub that returns nothing useful yet.
double total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {
    double pay = 0.0;                       // stub
    total += pay;
    System.out.println("pass " + i + " total " + total);
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>pass 1 total 0.0
pass 2 total 0.0
pass 3 total 0.0</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three passes, the accumulator surviving between them, no crash. Everything structural is now known to be right, so step two can only break the arithmetic.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">// STEP 2 — the real calculation, trace print kept for one more run.
double total = 0;
double[] hours = {38, 46, 40};
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {
    double h = hours[i - 1];
    double pay = (h &lt;= 40) ? h * 18.50
                           : 40 * 18.50 + (h - 40) * 18.50 * 1.5;
    total += pay;
    System.out.printf("pass %d pay %.2f total %.2f%n", i, pay, total);
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>pass 1 pay 703.00 total 703.00
pass 2 pay 906.50 total 1609.50
pass 3 pay 740.00 total 2349.50</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Pass 1 is checkable by hand — 38 × 18.50 = 703.00 — so the ordinary case is right. Pass 2 is the overtime case and pass 3 is the boundary at exactly 40 hours, which correctly takes the <em>no</em>-overtime branch because the test is <code>&lt;=</code>. Only now are the trace prints deleted.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 703.00, 906.50 and 740.00, totalling 2349.50</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — hard-coding the input to check the formula by hand</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Before any of that, the overtime formula itself is worth pinning down with one fixed value you can verify on paper — 46 hours at £18.50.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">double hours = 46, rate = 18.50;
double pay = 40 * rate + (hours - 40) * rate * 1.5;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", pay);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", hours * rate * 1.5);   // the tempting wrong version</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">By hand: the first 40 hours are 40 × 18.50 = 740.00.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The remaining 6 hours are at time-and-a-half: 6 × 18.50 × 1.5 = 166.50.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Total 906.50 — and that is what the first line prints, so the formula is confirmed.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The second line applies 1.5 to <em>all</em> 46 hours and gives 1276.50, overpaying by 370. It is a plausible-looking line that no compiler will ever question.</li></ol><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>906.50
1276.50</div><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 906.50 is correct; the one-line version overpays by 370.00</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Trace prints that show only a value are nearly useless. <code>System.out.println(total);</code> tells you a number is wrong but not which pass produced it. Print the loop variable beside it — <code>println("i=" + i + " total=" + total)</code> — and the failing iteration names itself.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The exam version of this habit is to trace <strong>two</strong> iterations of a tangled loop rather than trying to see the whole answer at once. Two rows of a trace table almost always reveal the pattern, and the remaining passes can then be reasoned about instead of simulated.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_9":{"body":"<p><code>break</code> and <code>continue</code> interrupt the normal flow of a loop, and the difference between them is exactly one word: <code>break</code> leaves the loop, <code>continue</code> skips to the next iteration.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5) { break; }       // stops the loop entirely — prints 0 1 2 3 4
    System.out.println(i);
}

for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) { continue; }  // skips the rest of THIS pass
    System.out.println(i);         // prints 1 3 5 7 9
}</pre><p>In a nested loop, both affect only the loop they sit directly inside — <code>break</code> in an inner loop returns to the outer loop, it does not leave both. And in a <code>while</code> loop <code>continue</code> is dangerous: it jumps past the update if the update is at the bottom of the body, which turns the loop infinite.</p><h3>Where continue jumps to</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    if (i == 2) continue;         // skip the REST of this pass
    System.out.print(i);          // i++ still runs
}                                 // prints 0134

int i = 0;
while (i &lt; 5) {
    if (i == 2) continue;         // DANGER: i++ below is skipped too
    System.out.print(i);
    i++;                          // never reached when i == 2 -&gt; infinite
}</pre><p>In a <code>for</code> loop <code>continue</code> jumps to the <em>update</em>, so the loop still advances. In a <code>while</code> loop it jumps to the <em>condition</em>, skipping any update at the bottom of the body — which hangs the program. That asymmetry is the point of the topic.</p><p>In nested loops, an unlabelled <code>break</code> leaves only the loop it sits directly inside. To leave both, Java allows a label: <code>outer: for (...) { for (...) { break outer; } }</code>.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>break:</strong> exits the innermost enclosing loop or switch immediately</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>continue:</strong> skips the remainder of the current iteration and proceeds to the next</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Early exit:</strong> leaving a loop as soon as the answer is known, e.g. a found flag in a search</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>continue</code> in a <code>for</code> loop still runs the update, because the update is part of the header. <code>continue</code> in a <code>while</code> loop does not. That asymmetry is a favourite exam question.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-tbd286bz49="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — break leaves the loop; continue leaves only the pass — and in a while it skips the update</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 400" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk231a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { if (i == 2) ???; System.out.print(i); }</text><text x="20" y="109" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">break</text><rect x="130" y="86" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="161" y="111" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=0</text><text x="161" y="140" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prints 0</text><line x1="192" y1="106" x2="226" y2="106" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><rect x="226" y="86" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="257" y="111" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=1</text><text x="257" y="140" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prints 1</text><line x1="288" y1="106" x2="322" y2="106" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><rect x="322" y="86" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(245,158,11,0.15)" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="353" y="111" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=2</text><text x="353" y="140" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">exit</text><rect x="418" y="86" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="449" y="111" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=3</text><text x="449" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">never</text><rect x="514" y="86" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="545" y="111" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=4</text><text x="545" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">never</text><line x1="353" y1="86" x2="353" y2="58" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><text x="353" y="50" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">leaves the loop entirely — prints 01</text><text x="20" y="221" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">continue</text><rect x="130" y="198" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="161" y="223" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=0</text><text x="161" y="252" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prints 0</text><line x1="192" y1="218" x2="226" y2="218" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><rect x="226" y="198" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="257" y="223" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=1</text><text x="257" y="252" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prints 1</text><line x1="288" y1="218" x2="322" y2="218" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><rect x="322" y="198" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(245,158,11,0.15)" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="353" y="223" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=2</text><text x="353" y="252" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">skip print</text><line x1="384" y1="218" x2="418" y2="218" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><rect x="418" y="198" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="449" y="223" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=3</text><text x="449" y="252" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prints 3</text><line x1="480" y1="218" x2="514" y2="218" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><rect x="514" y="198" width="62" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="545" y="223" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i=4</text><text x="545" y="252" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prints 4</text><rect x="329.3" y="169" width="143.39999999999998" height="22" rx="11" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.16"/><text x="401" y="184" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">continue lands here</text><line x1="401" y1="191" x2="401" y2="210" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk231a)"/><text x="388" y="282" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">jumps to i++ — the loop still advances. Prints 0134</text><rect x="20" y="300" width="640" height="88" rx="10" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.10" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="32" y="318" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the same continue inside a while</text><text x="36" y="344" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int i = 0;  while (i < 5) { if (i == 2) continue;  System.out.print(i);  i++; }</text><text x="36" y="370" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">continue jumps to the CONDITION, so i++ at the bottom never runs: i stays 2 forever.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — break inside a nested loop leaves only the inner one</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">This is the single most-tested fact about <code>break</code>: it is not a "leave everything" instruction.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">for (int r = 1; r &lt;= 3; r++) {
    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= 3; c++) {
        if (c == 2) break;          // leaves the INNER loop only
        System.out.print(r + "" + c + " ");
    }
}</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">r</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">c</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "11 "</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>break</strong> — inner loop ends, outer continues</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "21 "</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">break again</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">prints "31 "</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">break — inner loop ends, outer loop is now finished too</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>11 21 31 </div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The outer loop ran all three of its passes. If the intention really is to leave both, Java needs a label: <code>outer: for (...) { for (...) { break outer; } }</code>. A plain <code>break</code> answering "it stops the program" or "it stops both loops" is the classic wrong option.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 11 21 31</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — continue in a for loop still runs the update</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A totals routine skips every multiple of three. In a <code>for</code> loop this is safe, because the update lives in the header where <code>continue</code> cannot bypass it.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int sum = 0;
for (int n = 1; n &lt;= 8; n++) {
    if (n % 3 == 0) continue;      // skip the rest of THIS pass
    sum += n;
    System.out.print(n + " ");
}
System.out.println("| sum " + sum);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">n</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">n % 3 == 0</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Action</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">sum</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add and print</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add and print</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>true</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">continue — nothing added</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add and print</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">7</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add and print</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">12</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>true</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">continue</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">12</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add and print</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">19</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">8</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">false</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add and print</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">27</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>1 2 4 5 7 8 | sum 27</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Rewrite the same loop as a <code>while</code> with <code>n++</code> as the last statement of the body and it hangs: on <code>n == 3</code> the <code>continue</code> jumps straight back to the condition with <code>n</code> still 3, forever. The fix is either a <code>for</code>, or an update placed before the <code>continue</code>.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 1 2 4 5 7 8, sum 27</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">For a <code>break</code> question, ask <em>which</em> loop it sits directly inside — that is the only one it leaves. For a <code>continue</code> question, ask <em>where the update lives</em>: in a for header it still runs, at the bottom of a while body it does not. Those two questions answer almost every break/continue item on the exam.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_10":{"body":"<p>Scope is the region of code where a name exists. The rule is simple and its consequences are not: a variable exists from its declaration to the closing brace of the block containing it, and nowhere else.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    int temp = i * 2;         // temp is created and destroyed each pass
}
System.out.println(i);        // ERROR — i does not exist out here
System.out.println(temp);     // ERROR — neither does temp

int j;
for (j = 0; j &lt; 5; j++) { }   // declared OUTSIDE, so it survives
System.out.println(j);        // 5 — usable</pre><p>So if you need the loop variable after the loop — the index where a search succeeded, for instance — declare it before the loop. That is the whole reason the "found index" pattern in 5.4 declares its variable outside.</p><h3>Scope, lifetime, and shadowing</h3><p>Scope answers <em>where can this name be seen</em>; lifetime answers <em>when does the value exist</em>. For a local variable they coincide — it is created when execution reaches the declaration and destroyed at the closing brace — which is why a variable declared inside a loop is genuinely a new variable each pass.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
    int temp = i * 2;             // new variable every pass
}
// i and temp are both gone here — using either is a compile error

int found = -1;                   // declared OUTSIDE so it survives the loop
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] == target) { found = i; break; }
}
if (found &gt;= 0) { ... }</pre><p>Declaring a name inside a block when the same name already exists outside is <strong>shadowing</strong>: the inner one hides the outer for the rest of that block. It compiles, so the symptom is an outer variable that mysteriously never changes.</p><p>The working rule is to declare a variable in the smallest block that needs it. Narrow scope means fewer places a value can be corrupted, and the compiler catches misuse instead of the value silently surviving into code that should not see it.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Scope:</strong> the region of code in which a name is visible</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Block scope:</strong> the rule that a variable declared in a block exists only until that block closes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Shadowing:</strong> an inner declaration hiding an outer variable of the same name</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Declaring a variable inside a loop resets it every pass. If your accumulator keeps coming out as one iteration’s worth of value, its declaration is in the wrong place.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-tbd286bz410="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — nested blocks: an inner block sees out, an outer block cannot see in</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 336" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk232a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a name exists from its declaration to the closing brace of its block</text><rect x="24" y="44" width="500" height="220" rx="10" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.10" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="36" y="62" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">method block</text><text x="42" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int found = -1;</text><rect x="48" y="100" width="452" height="118" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="60" y="118" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">for block   for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {</text><text x="66" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int temp = i * 2;</text><rect x="72" y="156" width="404" height="52" rx="10" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="84" y="174" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">if block</text><text x="90" y="192" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">found = i;    // reaches OUT to the method block  ✓</text><text x="42" y="244" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">System.out.println(found);   // ✓ still alive</text><text x="556" y="60" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">alive for</text><rect x="536" y="76" width="16" height="176" rx="8" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.35"/><text x="560" y="130" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">found</text><rect x="562" y="132" width="16" height="86" rx="8" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.35"/><text x="586" y="176" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">i, temp</text><text x="586" y="192" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reborn each</text><text x="586" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">pass</text><rect x="24" y="278" width="636" height="46" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="342" y="306" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">println(i) or println(temp) after the loop:  compile error — the names no longer exist.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the accumulator that keeps coming out too small</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The same addition written with the total declared in two different places. Both compile; only one is a total.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int totalOutside = 0;                       // OUTSIDE the loop
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) { totalOutside += i; }
System.out.println(totalOutside);

for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) {
    int totalInside = 0;                    // INSIDE — a NEW variable each pass
    totalInside += i;
    System.out.print(totalInside + " ");
}</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Pass</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">totalOutside after</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">totalInside after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1 — created, used, destroyed</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2 — a brand-new variable</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">10</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">4</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>10
1 2 3 4 </div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The inner version is not "resetting" a variable; it is <em>creating a different one</em> on every pass, because the declaration is inside the block. That is why the printed values are just the loop variable back again — the symptom to recognise is a total that always equals the last item.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 10, then 1 2 3 4</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a search result has to outlive the loop that found it</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Room codes are stored as a String; find where the letter C appears.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">String rooms = "B4C7";
int found = -1;                     // OUTSIDE — must survive the loop
for (int i = 0; i &lt; rooms.length(); i++) {
    if (rooms.charAt(i) == 'C') { found = i; break; }
}
System.out.println(found);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>i</code> is declared in the header, so it exists only inside the loop — printing it afterwards would not compile.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>found</code> is declared before the loop, so the assignment inside the <code>if</code> reaches outward and the value survives.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">It starts at −1, the conventional "not found" marker, so the code after the loop can distinguish "no match" from "matched at index 0".</li></ol><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>2</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Declaring <code>int found</code> inside the loop instead would not even compile at the <code>println</code> — which is the good outcome. The dangerous variant is declaring it inside and <em>also</em> printing inside: it compiles, and prints a result for every pass instead of one answer.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 2</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Shadowing is the silent version of this. Declaring <code>int count = 0;</code> inside a loop when a <code>count</code> already exists outside compiles in some arrangements and leaves the outer variable untouched forever. The symptom is an outer variable that stubbornly reads 0 at the end. If a variable never changes when you can see the line that changes it, look for a second declaration of the same name.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_11":{"body":"<p>An <code>enum</code> is a type whose values are a fixed named list. It exists so that a set of constants — days, states, suits — becomes a real type the compiler can check, instead of loose ints that anything can be assigned to.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN }

Day today = Day.WED;

switch (today) {
    case MON: case TUE: case WED: case THU: case FRI:
        System.out.println("weekday"); break;
    default:
        System.out.println("weekend");
}

for (Day d : Day.values()) { System.out.println(d); }   // every value in order</pre><p>The advantage over <code>int</code> constants is compile-time safety: <code>Day d = 9;</code> will not compile, whereas <code>int day = 9;</code> happily accepts a nonsense day. Inside a <code>switch</code> the case labels drop the type name — <code>case MON</code>, not <code>case Day.MON</code>.</p><h3>What an enum gives you beyond a list of names</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI }

Day d = Day.WED;
switch (d) {
    case MON: ... break;          // no "Day." prefix inside a switch
    case WED: ... break;
}

for (Day each : Day.values()) { ... }   // values() — every constant, in order
Day.valueOf("TUE")                       // String -&gt; enum (exact match, or throws)
d.ordinal()                              // position: MON=0, TUE=1, WED=2
d.name()                                 // "WED"</pre><p>Compare enum constants with <code>==</code>, not <code>equals</code> — there is exactly one object per constant, so reference comparison is both correct and the convention.</p><p><code>ordinal()</code> is worth treating with suspicion: it returns the declaration position, so reordering the constants silently changes every stored ordinal. If a number matters, give the enum a field for it rather than relying on position.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">enum Planet {
    MERCURY(3.30e23), VENUS(4.87e24);
    private final double mass;
    Planet(double m) { mass = m; }
    public double mass() { return mass; }
}</pre><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>enum:</strong> a type whose instances are a fixed, named set of constants</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>values():</strong> the automatically provided method returning an array of all constants, in declaration order</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ordinal():</strong> the zero-based position of a constant in its declaration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>name():</strong> the constant’s identifier as a String</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Enum constants are conventionally ALL_CAPS, matching the constant naming from 2.9 — and for the same reason, since they are constants.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz411="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — an enum is a closed set of constant objects the compiler can police</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 302" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk233a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">enum Shift { MORNING, AFTERNOON, NIGHT }</text><rect x="66" y="52" width="160" height="74" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="146" y="78" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">MORNING</text><text x="146" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ordinal() = 0</text><text x="146" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">exactly ONE object</text><rect x="256" y="52" width="160" height="74" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="336" y="78" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">AFTERNOON</text><text x="336" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ordinal() = 1</text><text x="336" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">exactly ONE object</text><rect x="446" y="52" width="160" height="74" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="526" y="78" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">NIGHT</text><text x="526" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ordinal() = 2</text><text x="526" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">exactly ONE object</text><text x="46" y="152" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">Shift.values() returns all three, in order</text><rect x="46" y="200" width="208" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="150" y="225" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Shift s = Shift.NIGHT;</text><text x="150" y="260" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a variable holds a reference to</text><text x="150" y="276" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">one of the three — nothing else</text><line x1="254" y1="208" x2="476" y2="130" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="5 4" marker-end="url(#fk233a)"/><rect x="300" y="194" width="360" height="96" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="312" y="212" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the same idea with a bare int</text><text x="320" y="234" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int shift = 9;      // compiles. There is no shift 9.</text><text x="320" y="262" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Shift s = 9;        // does NOT compile — caught here,</text><text x="320" y="280" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">                    not three months later</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — values(), ordinal() and an enum in a switch</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A rota program models the three shifts. Because the set is fixed and named, the compiler can check every use of it.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">enum Shift { MORNING, AFTERNOON, NIGHT }

for (Shift s : Shift.values()) {
    System.out.println(s + " " + s.ordinal());
}
Shift s = Shift.valueOf("NIGHT");
switch (s) {
    case NIGHT: System.out.println("premium"); break;   // no "Shift." prefix
    default:    System.out.println("standard");
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>MORNING 0
AFTERNOON 1
NIGHT 2
premium</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>values()</code> returns an array of every constant in declaration order, so the enhanced for loop visits them once each.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Printing an enum prints its <code>name()</code> — "MORNING", not a number. That is the free <code>toString</code> every enum gets.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>ordinal()</code> is the declaration position, counting from 0.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Inside a <code>switch</code> the case labels are bare: <code>case NIGHT:</code>. Writing <code>case Shift.NIGHT:</code> is a compile error, which surprises people who expect the opposite.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: MORNING 0 / AFTERNOON 1 / NIGHT 2 / premium</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — comparing enum constants, and why ordinal is fragile</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">There is exactly one object per constant, which changes how you compare them.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">Shift a = Shift.AFTERNOON;
Shift b = Shift.valueOf("AFTERNOON");
System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a.name() + " " + a.ordinal() + " " + Shift.values().length);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>true
AFTERNOON 1 3</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>a == b</code> is <strong>true</strong> — unlike Strings, where the same comparison is a trap. <code>valueOf</code> does not build anything; it looks up the one existing constant, so both variables hold the same reference. That is why <code>==</code> is the idiomatic comparison for enums and <code>equals</code> is merely equivalent.</p><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The fragile part is <code>ordinal()</code>. AFTERNOON is 1 only because it is declared second. Insert an EVENING constant before it and every stored 1 now means something else, with nothing to warn you. If a number matters — a shift multiplier, a planet's mass — give the enum a field for it and a constructor, rather than leaning on declaration order.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: true, then AFTERNOON 1 3</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>valueOf</code> requires an <em>exact</em> match, capitals included. <code>Shift.valueOf("night")</code> compiles perfectly and throws <code>IllegalArgumentException: No enum constant Shift.night</code> at run time. Normalise with <code>toUpperCase()</code> before converting user input, or check against <code>values()</code> first.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_12":{"body":"<p>This example replaces the fixed calculation from 3.20 with a loop over many employees, which is what makes the tiered-rate chain worth writing once.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">double totalPayroll = 0;
int n = scnr.nextInt();

for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
    double salary = scnr.nextDouble();

    double rate;
    if      (salary &gt;= 100000) { rate = 0.28; }
    else if (salary &gt;=  50000) { rate = 0.22; }
    else                       { rate = 0.15; }

    totalPayroll += salary * (1 - rate);
}
System.out.printf("Total: %.2f%n", totalPayroll);</pre><p>Two things are placed deliberately. <code>totalPayroll</code> is declared before the loop, so it accumulates (4.10); <code>rate</code> is declared inside, because each employee needs a fresh one. Swapping those placements is the bug this example is designed to teach.</p><h3>Reading the placement decisions</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">double totalPayroll = 0.0;            // OUTSIDE — accumulates across employees
for (int e = 0; e &lt; count; e++) {
    double rate = baseRate;           // INSIDE — fresh for each employee
    double hours = scnr.nextDouble();
    double pay = (hours &lt;= 40)
               ? hours * rate
               : 40 * rate + (hours - 40) * rate * 1.5;
    totalPayroll += pay;
}
System.out.printf("Total: $%.2f%n", totalPayroll);</pre><p>Move <code>totalPayroll</code> inside and it resets every pass, so the printed total is the last employee's pay. Move <code>rate</code> outside and any change one employee makes to it carries into the next. Those two mistakes look identical in the source and produce completely different wrong answers, which is why the example exists.</p><p>The overtime line is the tiered-rate idea from 3.20 applied per pass: the first 40 hours at the base rate, everything beyond at 1.5×. Computing <code>hours * rate * 1.5</code> for an overtime week overpays the first 40 hours as well.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>printf:</strong> formatted output; %.2f prints two decimal places, %n a newline (see 9.2)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Loop-invariant declaration:</strong> a variable declared outside the loop because its value must persist across iterations</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Declaring the accumulator inside the loop resets it to zero every pass, so the final total equals only the last employee. It compiles, it runs, and the answer is silently wrong.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz412="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — one declaration moved three lines changes the answer completely</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 312" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk234a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="170" y="24" fill="#10b981" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">declared OUTSIDE — accumulates</text><text x="510" y="24" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">declared INSIDE — resets</text><line x1="340" y1="40" x2="340" y2="288" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 5"/><rect x="40" y="44" width="262" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="171" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double total = 0;</text><rect x="380" y="44" width="262" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="511" y="66" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (...) {</text><text x="24" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">e1</text><rect x="52" y="96" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="100" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40800</text><line x1="148" y1="116" x2="178" y2="116" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk234a)"/><rect x="178" y="96" width="124" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="240" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40800</text><text x="240" y="150" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">total carries over</text><rect x="380" y="92" width="262" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="511" y="112" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double total = 0;   ← reborn</text><rect x="392" y="126" width="96" height="28" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="440" y="145" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40800</text><line x1="488" y1="140" x2="516" y2="140" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk234a)"/><rect x="516" y="126" width="116" height="28" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="574" y="145" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40800</text><text x="24" y="180" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">e2</text><rect x="52" y="158" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="100" y="183" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">75600</text><line x1="148" y1="178" x2="178" y2="178" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk234a)"/><rect x="178" y="158" width="124" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="240" y="183" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">116400</text><text x="240" y="212" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">total carries over</text><rect x="380" y="154" width="262" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="511" y="174" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double total = 0;   ← reborn</text><rect x="392" y="188" width="96" height="28" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="440" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">75600</text><line x1="488" y1="202" x2="516" y2="202" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk234a)"/><rect x="516" y="188" width="116" height="28" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="574" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">75600</text><text x="24" y="242" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">e3</text><rect x="52" y="220" width="96" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="100" y="245" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">48360</text><line x1="148" y1="240" x2="178" y2="240" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk234a)"/><rect x="178" y="220" width="124" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="240" y="245" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">164760</text><rect x="380" y="216" width="262" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="511" y="236" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double total = 0;   ← reborn</text><rect x="392" y="250" width="96" height="28" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="440" y="269" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">48360</text><line x1="488" y1="264" x2="516" y2="264" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk234a)"/><rect x="516" y="250" width="116" height="28" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="574" y="269" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">48360</text><text x="171" y="300" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ends at 164760 — the payroll</text><text x="511" y="300" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ends at 48360 — the last employee</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a tiered-rate payroll over four employees</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Four salaries, three tax bands, one running total. The <code>Scanner</code> is built over a fixed String so the run is reproducible.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">Scanner scnr = new Scanner("48000 105000 62000 24000");
double totalPayroll = 0;                        // OUTSIDE — accumulates
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
    double salary = scnr.nextDouble();
    double rate;                                // INSIDE — fresh per employee
    if      (salary &gt;= 100000) { rate = 0.28; }
    else if (salary &gt;=  50000) { rate = 0.22; }
    else                       { rate = 0.15; }
    double net = salary * (1 - rate);
    System.out.printf("%.2f at %.0f%% -&gt; %.2f%n", salary, rate * 100, net);
    totalPayroll += net;
}
System.out.printf("Total: %.2f%n", totalPayroll);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>48000.00 at 15% -&gt; 40800.00
105000.00 at 28% -&gt; 75600.00
62000.00 at 22% -&gt; 48360.00
24000.00 at 15% -&gt; 20400.00
Total: 185160.00</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">48000 fails both <code>&gt;=</code> tests, so it falls to the <code>else</code> and pays 15 percent: 48000 × 0.85 = 40800.00.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">105000 matches the first test, 28 percent: 105000 × 0.72 = 75600.00.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">62000 fails the first and matches the second, 22 percent: 62000 × 0.78 = 48360.00.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">24000 takes the <code>else</code> again: 24000 × 0.85 = 20400.00, and the four nets sum to 185160.00.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The band order is not decorative. Test 50000 before 100000 and every high earner matches the 22 percent band first and stops there, so the top rate is never applied — a silent under-taxing bug with no error anywhere.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: Total: 185160.00</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the same program with the accumulator one line lower</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Move the declaration inside the loop and the program still compiles, still runs, and reports the wrong number.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">Scanner scnr = new Scanner("48000 105000 62000");
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    double totalPayroll = 0;                    // INSIDE — the bug
    double salary = scnr.nextDouble();
    totalPayroll += salary;
    System.out.printf("after employee %d total = %.1f%n", i + 1, totalPayroll);
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>after employee 1 total = 48000.0
after employee 2 total = 105000.0
after employee 3 total = 62000.0</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Every "total" is simply that employee's salary. The declaration runs on every pass, so a fresh variable is created and set to 0 before the addition — the previous pass's value was destroyed when the block closed. The tell-tale is a running total that equals the current item rather than the sum so far.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: each line equals that one salary — the totals never accumulate</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">For questions about where a declaration belongs, ask one thing: <em>must this value survive from one pass to the next?</em> Accumulators, counters, best-so-far and found-index all say yes and go above the loop. Per-item working values — this employee's rate, this character, this row's subtotal — say no and go inside, where a fresh one every pass is exactly what you want.</p></div>"},"D286/u4/z4_13":{"body":"<p>Domain validation puts loops and Strings together: walk the characters, and reject the whole string the moment any character breaks a rule.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String domain = scnr.next();
boolean valid = domain.length() &gt;= 3 &amp;&amp; domain.contains(".");

for (int i = 0; i &lt; domain.length() &amp;&amp; valid; i++) {
    char c = domain.charAt(i);
    if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(c) &amp;&amp; c != '.' &amp;&amp; c != '-') {
        valid = false;                 // the condition ends the loop next check
    }
}
System.out.println(valid ? "valid" : "invalid");</pre><p>The flag appears in the loop condition, so the loop stops as soon as it is falsified — the same early-exit idea as <code>break</code> in 4.9, expressed without one. Either is fine; what is not fine is continuing to scan after the answer is known and then overwriting it.</p><h3>Validating with a flag versus breaking out</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">boolean valid = domain.length() &gt; 0;
boolean hasDot = false;
int i = 0;
while (valid &amp;&amp; i &lt; domain.length()) {      // stops the moment valid turns false
    char c = domain.charAt(i);
    if (c == '.') hasDot = true;
    else if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(c) &amp;&amp; c != '-') valid = false;
    i++;
}
valid = valid &amp;&amp; hasDot;</pre><p>Two rules are being checked at once and they behave differently. "Every character must be legal" fails on the <em>first</em> counter-example, so it can stop early. "At least one dot" cannot be decided until the whole string has been read, so it must be a flag that survives the loop.</p><p>Putting <code>valid</code> in the loop condition is early exit without <code>break</code> — equivalent here, and it keeps the exit rule visible at the top. What is genuinely wrong is scanning to the end and then overwriting <code>valid</code> in a later pass, which is what happens if the final line is written <code>valid = hasDot;</code> instead of combining the two.</p><i data-d286u4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Validation flag:</strong> a boolean set to false the first time a rule is broken and never reset</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Early termination:</strong> stopping a loop once the result is determined</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Never set the flag back to <code>true</code> inside the loop. A flag that can flip both ways ends up reporting only the last character, which is the standard broken version of this pattern.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz413="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a validation flag falls once and stays down</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 356" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id