ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int t = a[1];</text><rect x="178" y="120" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="205" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><rect x="240" y="120" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="267" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="302" y="120" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="364" y="120" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="391" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="426" y="120" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="453" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">23</text><rect x="514" y="120" width="62" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="545" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="20" y="202" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[1] = a[3];</text><rect x="178" y="182" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="205" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><rect x="240" y="182" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="267" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="302" y="182" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="364" y="182" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="391" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="426" y="182" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="453" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">23</text><rect x="514" y="182" width="62" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="545" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="20" y="264" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[3] = t;</text><rect x="178" y="244" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="205" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><rect x="240" y="244" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="267" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="302" y="244" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="364" y="244" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="391" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="426" y="244" width="54" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="453" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">23</text><rect x="514" y="244" width="62" height="36" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="545" y="267" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="20" y="302" width="640" height="62" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="340" y="330" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Without temp:  a[1] = a[3];  a[3] = a[1];  — the second line reads the value</text><text x="340" y="352" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the first line already overwrote, so both slots end up holding 16.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — watching the value disappear</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Both versions are run side by side on the same starting values, so the loss is visible rather than argued.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int x = 3, y = 7;

int a = x, b = y;
a = b;                       // a is now 7 — the 3 is GONE
b = a;                       // b gets 7 back from a
System.out.println("broken: a=" + a + " b=" + b);

int c = x, d = y;
int temp = c;                // save the value about to be overwritten
c = d;
d = temp;
System.out.println("correct: c=" + c + " d=" + d);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Statement</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">a</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">b</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">temp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">start</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>a = b;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>7</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">— the 3 no longer exists anywhere</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>b = a;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">7</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">— b is assigned its own value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>broken: a=7 b=7
correct: c=7 d=3</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The order in the correct version is fixed and worth memorising as <em>save, overwrite, restore</em>. Any other order loses a value: <code>temp = c; d = temp; c = d;</code> leaves both holding 3.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 7 and 7 from the broken version; 7 and 3 from the correct one</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — swapping array elements, and the no-temp trick</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Inside an array the swap is identical, just with subscripts — and this three-line block is the primitive underneath reversing and every exchange sort.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16, 23};
int i = 1, j = 3;
int t = a[i];
a[i] = a[j];
a[j] = t;
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));

double p = 0.1, q = 0.3;                    // the "clever" arithmetic swap
p = p + q;  q = p - q;  p = p - q;
System.out.println("doubles after the no-temp trick: " + p + " " + q);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>[4, 16, 15, 8, 23]
doubles after the no-temp trick: 0.3 0.10000000000000003</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The array swap exchanged positions 1 and 3 and left everything else alone. The arithmetic trick — <code>p = p + q; q = p - q; p = p - q;</code> — did move the values, but look at what came back: 0.1 became 0.10000000000000003, because two floating-point roundings do not cancel. It also cannot be used at all on Strings or objects, and it is unreadable. Use the temporary.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: [4, 16, 15, 8, 23], and a corrupted 0.1 from the trick</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A method cannot swap two <code>int</code> parameters. <code>swap(a, b)</code> exchanges the method's own copies and the caller sees nothing, because primitives are passed by value. It <em>can</em> swap two elements of an array it is given, because the array reference reaches the one real array — which is the same distinction that governs every parameter question in Unit 6.</p></div>"},"D286/u5/z5_7":{"body":"<p>Three operations on whole arrays behave differently from what the syntax suggests, and each has a specific correct form.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// COPYING — this does NOT copy
int[] b = a;             // b and a now refer to the SAME array
b[0] = 99;               // a[0] is also 99

// copy properly
int[] b = new int[a.length];
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { b[i] = a[i]; }
// or
int[] b = Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length);

// COMPARING — this compares references, not contents
a == b                   // almost always false
Arrays.equals(a, b)      // compares element by element   &lt;- correct</pre><p>Both traps are the same trap as <code>==</code> on Strings in 3.2, for the same reason: an array variable holds a reference, so plain assignment and <code>==</code> operate on the reference and not on the contents. The idea is set out fully in 7.10 and 7.12.</p><h3>Assignment, comparison and copying</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = a;                 // NOT a copy — two names, one array
b[0] = 99;                   // a[0] is now 99 as well

int[] c = Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length);       // a real copy
int[] d = a.clone();                        // also a real copy (1D)

a == c                       // false — different objects, even if equal
Arrays.equals(a, c)          // true  — compares element by element</pre><p>An array variable holds a <em>reference</em>. Plain assignment copies the reference, so both names reach the same array; <code>==</code> compares references, so it asks "the same array?" and never "the same contents?". This is the identical distinction as <code>==</code> versus <code>.equals</code> on Strings.</p><p>For a 2D array, <code>clone()</code> and <code>copyOf</code> are <strong>shallow</strong>: the new outer array holds references to the <em>same</em> rows, so changing an element still shows through both. A deep copy needs a loop that copies each row.</p><p>Removing an element means shifting everything after it left by one and treating the array as one shorter — the length itself cannot change.</p><i data-d286u5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reference copy (shallow):</strong> copying the reference, leaving one array with two names</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Element copy (deep):</strong> copying each element into a separate array</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Arrays.copyOf:</strong> library method returning a new array with the copied contents</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Arrays.equals:</strong> compares two arrays element by element</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Modifying an array through a second variable and being surprised the first one changed is the classic aliasing bug. If two names point at one array, there is only one array.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → 1D Arrays</strong></p><i data-tbd286bz57="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — assignment copies the arrow; copyOf copies the array</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 306" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk242a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int[] a = {5, 9, 2};    int[] b = a;    int[] c = Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length);</text><rect x="50" y="60" width="108" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="82" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a  ●———</text><rect x="50" y="108" width="108" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="130" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">b  ●———</text><rect x="50" y="210" width="108" height="34" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="232" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">c  ●———</text><line x1="158" y1="77" x2="276" y2="100" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk242a)"/><line x1="158" y1="125" x2="276" y2="110" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk242a)"/><line x1="158" y1="227" x2="276" y2="220" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk242a)"/><rect x="280" y="66" width="240" height="80" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="292" y="84" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">ONE array on the heap</text><rect x="300" y="92" width="60" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.13)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="330" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">99</text><rect x="370" y="92" width="60" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="400" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><rect x="440" y="92" width="60" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="470" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="400" y="162" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">b[0] = 99 changes what a sees too</text><rect x="280" y="186" width="240" height="80" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="292" y="204" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">a SECOND array — a real copy</text><rect x="300" y="212" width="60" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="330" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><rect x="370" y="212" width="60" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="400" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><rect x="440" y="212" width="60" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="470" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><rect x="536" y="66" width="128" height="200" rx="10" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.10" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="600" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a == b</text><text x="600" y="114" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">true</text><text x="600" y="148" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a == c</text><text x="600" y="166" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">false</text><text x="600" y="200" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Arrays.equals</text><text x="600" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(a, c)</text><text x="600" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">false — the</text><text x="600" y="250" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">contents differ now</text><text x="340" y="292" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">== compares the ARROWS.  Arrays.equals compares the CONTENTS.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — two names, one array</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The aliasing demonstration, with a real copy alongside it for contrast.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[] a = {5, 9, 2};
int[] b = a;                              // NOT a copy
int[] c = Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length);     // a real copy
b[0] = 99;
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a) + " " + Arrays.toString(b) + " " + Arrays.toString(c));
System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (a == c) + " " + Arrays.equals(a, c));</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>[99, 9, 2] [99, 9, 2] [5, 9, 2]
true false false</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Writing through <code>b</code> changed what <code>a</code> reports, because there is only one array and both variables hold its address.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>c</code> was taken before the write, so it still holds 5 — a genuinely separate array.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>a == b</code> is true: the same address. <code>a == c</code> is false: different addresses, even though the contents were identical at the moment of copying.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Arrays.equals(a, c)</code> is false <em>now</em> only because <code>a[0]</code> has since become 99. Immediately after the copy it would have been true.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The final line of the run makes the point completely: comparing <code>a</code> with a freshly written literal <code>{99, 9, 2}</code> gives <code>false</code> for <code>==</code> and <code>true</code> for <code>Arrays.equals</code> — identical contents, different objects.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: [99, 9, 2] [99, 9, 2] [5, 9, 2] — then true, false, false</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why clone() is not enough for a 2D array</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A 2D array is an array of references to rows, so copying the outer array copies the references and not the rows.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[][] g = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};

int[][] shallow = g.clone();              // copies the ROW REFERENCES only
shallow[0][0] = 99;
System.out.println("after shallow write, g[0][0] = " + g[0][0]);

int[][] deep = new int[g.length][];       // copy each row separately
for (int r = 0; r &lt; g.length; r++) deep[r] = Arrays.copyOf(g[r], g[r].length);
deep[1][1] = 77;
System.out.println("after deep write,    g[1][1] = " + g[1][1]);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>after shallow write, g[0][0] = 99
after deep write,    g[1][1] = 4</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The shallow copy produced a new outer array whose two slots point at the <em>original</em> rows, so writing into it wrote into <code>g</code>. The deep copy allocated a fresh row for each one, so <code>g</code> is untouched. <code>Arrays.copyOf</code> and <code>clone()</code> behave identically here — both are one level deep, which is enough for a 1D array of primitives and never enough for a 2D one.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 99 after the shallow write; 4 after the deep write</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Any exam question showing <code>b = a</code> followed by a write through <code>b</code> is asking about aliasing, and the answer is that both variables report the change. Any question comparing two arrays with <code>==</code> is answered <code>false</code> unless one was assigned from the other. Both are the same rule as <code>==</code> on Strings, for the same reason.</p></div>"},"D286/u5/z5_8":{"body":"<p>Reversing an array in place is the standard debugging example because the obvious version is wrong in an instructive way.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// WRONG — this reverses, then reverses back to the original
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    int temp = a[i];
    a[i] = a[a.length - 1 - i];
    a[a.length - 1 - i] = temp;
}

// RIGHT — stop at the middle
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length / 2; i++) {
    int temp = a[i];
    a[i] = a[a.length - 1 - i];
    a[a.length - 1 - i] = temp;
}</pre><p>Going the whole way swaps every pair twice, and the second swap undoes the first. Halving the bound is the fix, and integer division handles an odd length correctly for free: the middle element has nothing to swap with and is left alone.</p><h3>Why the bound is half</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// WRONG — swaps every pair twice, ending where it started
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[a.length-1-i]; a[a.length-1-i] = t;
}

// RIGHT
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length / 2; i++) {
    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[a.length-1-i]; a[a.length-1-i] = t;
}</pre><p>Each swap handles <em>two</em> elements — <code>i</code> and its mirror — so running the full length processes every pair a second time from the other end and undoes it. Half the length is exactly one pass per pair.</p><p>Integer division handles an odd length for free: length 5 gives <code>5 / 2 = 2</code>, so <code>i</code> takes 0 and 1, and the middle element at index 2 is left where it is — which is correct, since its mirror is itself.</p><p>The mirror index is <code>a.length - 1 - i</code>. Writing <code>a.length - i</code> is off by one and throws on the very first pass, which at least fails loudly.</p><i data-d286u5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>In-place algorithm:</strong> one that rearranges the existing array without allocating another</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Mirror index:</strong> the position length - 1 - i, which pairs with i from the other end</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>For a length-5 array, <code>a.length / 2</code> is 2, so <code>i</code> takes 0 and 1 and index 2 stays put — exactly right. Trust the integer division here rather than special-casing odd lengths.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz58="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — running the whole length swaps every pair twice and undoes the reversal</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 434" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk243a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16};    swap a[i] with a[a.length - 1 - i]</text><text x="24" y="72" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">start</text><rect x="220" y="50" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="75" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><rect x="296" y="50" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="75" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="372" y="50" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="405" y="75" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="448" y="50" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="481" y="75" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><text x="24" y="138" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i = 0: swap [0] and [3]</text><rect x="220" y="116" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="141" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="296" y="116" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="141" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="372" y="116" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="405" y="141" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="448" y="116" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="481" y="141" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><text x="24" y="204" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i = 1: swap [1] and [2]</text><rect x="220" y="182" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="296" y="182" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="372" y="182" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="405" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="448" y="182" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="481" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><text x="560" y="218" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">← reversed</text><text x="24" y="270" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i = 2: swaps [2] and [1] BACK</text><rect x="220" y="248" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><rect x="296" y="248" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="372" y="248" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="405" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="448" y="248" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="481" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><text x="24" y="336" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">i = 3: swaps [3] and [0] BACK</text><rect x="220" y="314" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="253" y="339" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><rect x="296" y="314" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="329" y="339" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="372" y="314" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="405" y="339" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><rect x="448" y="314" width="66" height="40" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="481" y="339" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">16</text><text x="560" y="350" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">← the original</text><line x1="200" y1="232" x2="660" y2="232" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><text x="112" y="232" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">stop here:  i < a.length / 2</text><rect x="24" y="372" width="636" height="50" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="342" y="402" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Each swap handles TWO elements, so the bound is half the length — 4 / 2 = 2 passes.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the two bounds, run side by side</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The only difference between the working reverse and the broken one is <code>a.length</code> against <code>a.length / 2</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16};
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {                 // WRONG
    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[a.length-1-i]; a[a.length-1-i] = t;
}
System.out.println("full length: " + Arrays.toString(a));

int[] b = {4, 8, 15, 16};
for (int i = 0; i &lt; b.length / 2; i++) {             // RIGHT
    int t = b[i]; b[i] = b[b.length-1-i]; b[b.length-1-i] = t;
}
System.out.println("half length: " + Arrays.toString(b));</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>full length: [4, 8, 15, 16]
half length: [16, 15, 8, 4]</div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">i</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">mirror index</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">array after the swap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">[16, 8, 15, 4]</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">[16, 15, 8, 4] — <strong>reversed here</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">[16, 8, 15, 4] — undoes pass 1</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">[4, 8, 15, 16] — undoes pass 0, back to the start</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The broken version is not "not reversing" — it reverses correctly halfway through and then reverses again. That is why the symptom is an array that looks completely untouched, which sends people hunting for a swap that is not happening at all.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: the full-length loop gives back the original; the half-length loop reverses it</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — an odd length, and the element with no partner</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Five elements, so one of them is its own mirror. Integer division handles it with no special case.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16, 23};
System.out.println("bound is " + (a.length / 2));
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length / 2; i++) {
    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[a.length-1-i]; a[a.length-1-i] = t;
    System.out.println("i=" + i + " -&gt; " + Arrays.toString(a));
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>bound is 2
i=0 -&gt; [23, 8, 15, 16, 4]
i=1 -&gt; [23, 16, 15, 8, 4]</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>5 / 2</code> is integer division, so the bound is 2 and <code>i</code> takes 0 and 1.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Pass 0 exchanges indexes 0 and 4; pass 1 exchanges 1 and 3.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Index 2 is never touched — and it should not be, because its mirror (<code>5 - 1 - 2</code>) is 2, itself. Swapping an element with itself is a waste at best and, written carelessly, a way to zero it out.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The mirror expression is <code>a.length - 1 - i</code>. Writing <code>a.length - i</code> is off by one and throws on the very first pass, which is the friendly failure — you find it immediately instead of shipping a subtly wrong array.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: [23, 16, 15, 8, 4] after two passes, with index 2 left in place</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Reversing into a <em>second</em> array is a different algorithm and needs no halving: <code>for (i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) out[i] = a[a.length - 1 - i];</code> runs the full length and is correct, because it never writes back into what it is reading. Halving <em>that</em> loop fills only half the output. The bound depends on whether the reversal is in place, so read that first.</p></div>"},"D286/u5/z5_9":{"body":"<p>A two-dimensional array is an array of arrays: the outer array holds references to rows, and each row is itself an array. Once you picture it that way, the syntax follows.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[][] grid = new int[3][4];        // 3 rows, 4 columns

grid[1][2] = 7;                       // row 1, column 2

grid.length        // 3  — number of ROWS
grid[0].length     // 4  — number of COLUMNS in row 0

int[][] g = { {1, 2, 3},
              {4, 5, 6} };            // literal form

for (int r = 0; r &lt; g.length; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; g[r].length; c++) {
        System.out.print(g[r][c] + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();             // ends the row — outer loop (4.7)
}</pre><p>Because rows are separate arrays, they need not all be the same length — a <strong>jagged</strong> array. That is why the inner bound is <code>g[r].length</code> and not <code>g[0].length</code>: writing the latter is correct for rectangular data and breaks the moment it is not.</p><h3>Rows, columns, and the two bounds</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[][] g = new int[3][4];        // 3 rows, each of 4 columns
g[1][2] = 7;                      // row 1, column 2

g.length        // 3  — number of ROWS
g[0].length     // 4  — number of columns in row 0

for (int r = 0; r &lt; g.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; g[r].length; c++)     // g[r], not g[0]
        System.out.print(g[r][c] + " ");</pre><p>The first index is always the row, and <code>g.length</code> counts rows rather than total cells — a 3×4 grid has <code>g.length == 3</code> and 12 elements. Total cells is rows × columns.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[][] jag = new int[3][];       // rows not yet created
jag[0] = new int[2];
jag[1] = new int[5];              // rows may differ in length</pre><p>Because each row is its own array, rows can have different lengths — a <strong>jagged</strong> array. That is why the inner bound must be <code>g[r].length</code>: <code>g[0].length</code> is right only for rectangular data and throws the moment it is not.</p><i data-d286u5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>2D array:</strong> an array whose elements are themselves arrays</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Row-major order:</strong> the convention that the first index selects the row</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Jagged array:</strong> a 2D array whose rows have different lengths</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>grid[r].length:</strong> the length of one specific row</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>grid.length</code> is the row count, never the total number of elements. For a 3×4 grid the total is <code>grid.length * grid[0].length</code> = 12.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → 2D Arrays and ArrayList</strong></p><i data-d286fx10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A 2D ARRAY IS ROWS OF ARRAYS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 530 176" style="width:100%; max-width:560px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk77a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="30" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int[][] m = { {5, 8, 2}, {9, 1, 7} };</text><rect x="120" y="42" width="70" height="44" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="155" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="155" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[0][0]</text><rect x="190" y="42" width="70" height="44" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="225" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="225" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[0][1]</text><rect x="260" y="42" width="70" height="44" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="295" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="295" y="78" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[0][2]</text><rect x="120" y="96" width="70" height="44" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="155" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><text x="155" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[1][0]</text><rect x="190" y="96" width="70" height="44" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="225" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><text x="225" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[1][1]</text><rect x="260" y="96" width="70" height="44" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="295" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7</text><text x="295" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[1][2]</text><text x="104" y="68" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">row 0</text><text x="104" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">row 1</text><text x="348" y="78" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m.length = 2 (rows)</text><text x="348" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">m[0].length = 3 (cols)</text><text x="265" y="168" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">First bracket picks the row, second picks the column. m[1][2] is 7 — row 1, column 2.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbd286bz59="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a jagged array: why the inner bound must be jag[r].length</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 352" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk244a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int[][] jag = new int[3][];   // the rows are separate arrays, created one by one</text><rect x="40" y="60" width="128" height="172" rx="7" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="84" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">jag</text><text x="104" y="102" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">jag.length = 3</text><rect x="56" y="118" width="96" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">jag[0]  ●</text><line x1="152" y1="133" x2="214" y2="133" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7" marker-end="url(#fk244a)"/><rect x="216" y="118" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="240" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><rect x="272" y="118" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="296" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><text x="336" y="138" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">jag[0].length = 2</text><rect x="56" y="156" width="96" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">jag[1]  ●</text><line x1="152" y1="171" x2="214" y2="171" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7" marker-end="url(#fk244a)"/><rect x="216" y="156" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="240" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><rect x="272" y="156" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="296" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><rect x="328" y="156" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="352" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><rect x="384" y="156" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="408" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><rect x="440" y="156" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="464" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="504" y="176" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">jag[1].length = 5</text><rect x="56" y="194" width="96" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="104" y="214" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">jag[2]  ●</text><line x1="152" y1="209" x2="214" y2="209" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7" marker-end="url(#fk244a)"/><rect x="216" y="194" width="48" height="30" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="240" y="214" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><text x="280" y="214" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">jag[2].length = 1</text><rect x="40" y="248" width="620" height="92" rx="10" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.10" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="348" y="276" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (c = 0; c < jag[r].length; c++)   ✓  each row asked for its own length</text><text x="348" y="302" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (c = 0; c < jag[0].length; c++)   ✗  row 1 stops at 2, row 2 throws</text><text x="348" y="328" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Index 1 out of bounds for length 1</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — row totals and a column total on a rectangular grid</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Four stores, three quarters of sales. The first subscript is always the row.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[][] sales = { {120, 95, 140},
                  { 88, 210,  76},
                  {150, 130, 190},
                  { 60,  45,  80} };
System.out.println("rows " + sales.length + ", cols " + sales[0].length);
for (int r = 0; r &lt; sales.length; r++) {
    int rowTotal = 0;
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; sales[r].length; c++) rowTotal += sales[r][c];
    System.out.println("store " + r + " total " + rowTotal);
}
int colTotal = 0;
for (int r = 0; r &lt; sales.length; r++) colTotal += sales[r][1];   // fix the COLUMN
System.out.println("column 1 total " + colTotal);
System.out.println("sales[2][0] = " + sales[2][0]);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>rows 4, cols 3
store 0 total 355
store 1 total 374
store 2 total 470
store 3 total 185
column 1 total 480
sales[2][0] = 150</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>sales.length</code> is 4 — the number of <em>rows</em>, not the 12 elements. Total cells is rows × columns.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The row total fixes <code>r</code> and walks <code>c</code>; the column total does the opposite, fixing the second subscript at 1 and walking <code>r</code>. Swapping those is the standard error.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>rowTotal</code> is declared <em>inside</em> the outer loop, so each store starts from zero — the accumulator-placement rule from Unit 4, now one level deeper.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>sales[2][0]</code> is 150: row 2 (the third store), column 0 (the first quarter).</li></ol><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: row totals 355, 374, 470, 185; column 1 totals 480</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a jagged array, and the bound that breaks it</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Rows are separate arrays, so they need not be the same length — and code that assumes they are fails in two different ways.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[][] jag = new int[3][];        // three row slots, no rows yet
jag[0] = new int[] {5, 8};
jag[1] = new int[] {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
jag[2] = new int[] {9};
for (int r = 0; r &lt; jag.length; r++)
    System.out.println("row " + r + " length " + jag[r].length + " " + Arrays.toString(jag[r]));
int total = 0;
for (int r = 0; r &lt; jag.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; jag[r].length; c++) total += jag[r][c];
System.out.println("total " + total);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>row 0 length 2 [5, 8]
row 1 length 5 [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
row 2 length 1 [9]
total 37</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Now replace the inner bound with <code>jag[0].length</code>, which is 2. Row 0 is fine, row 1 is silently truncated to its first two elements, and row 2 — which has only one element — throws <code>Index 1 out of bounds for length 1</code>. One bound produces both a silent wrong answer and a crash, depending on the row.</p><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>new int[3][]</code> with the second bracket empty creates the outer array only: its three slots are <code>null</code> until a row is assigned. <code>new int[3][4]</code> creates all four rows at once, every element 0.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: lengths 2, 5 and 1; total 37</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">For a 2D trace question, write the grid out with its row and column indexes labelled before doing anything else. Then check which subscript the loop is holding still: an inner loop over the second subscript walks a <em>row</em>, an inner loop over the first walks a <em>column</em>. Half the marks on these questions turn on that one distinction.</p></div>"},"D286/u5/z5_10":{"body":"<p>The enhanced <code>for</code> loop — the "for-each" — removes the index when you do not need it. It reads each element in turn, from first to last, and there is no counter to get wrong.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int value : a) {
    System.out.println(value);
}

// same thing with an index
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { System.out.println(a[i]); }</pre><p>The restriction is the whole point of the trade: <code>value</code> is a <em>copy</em> of the element, so assigning to it changes nothing in the array. Modifying elements needs the indexed form.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int value : a) { value = 0; }      // array is UNCHANGED
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { a[i] = 0; }   // this works</pre><h3>What the for-each can and cannot do</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int x : a) sum += x;             // reading — ideal

for (int x : a) x = x * 2;            // does NOTHING to the array
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) a[i] = a[i] * 2;   // this works</pre><p>The loop variable is a fresh copy of each element, so assigning to it changes the copy and nothing else. For objects the copy is a copy of the <em>reference</em>, which means you cannot replace an element but you can still call a method that mutates the object it points at.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Use</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Which loop</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">reading every element, order irrelevant</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">for-each — no index to get wrong</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">assigning to elements</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">indexed <code>for</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">needing the position</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">indexed <code>for</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">going backwards, or skipping</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">indexed <code>for</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">comparing neighbours <code>a[i]</code> and <code>a[i+1]</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">indexed <code>for</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A for-each over an empty array runs zero times and is safe. A for-each over a <code>null</code> array throws a <code>NullPointerException</code> — empty and null are not the same thing.</p><i data-d286u5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Enhanced for loop:</strong> for (Type v : collection) — iterates every element without an index</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Read-only iteration:</strong> the enhanced loop gives a copy for primitives, so writes to the variable do not persist</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Use the enhanced loop when you only read, and every element, in order. Use the indexed loop when you need to write, need the index, need to skip, or need to go backwards. The same loop works on collections in 14.1.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-tbd286bz510="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the for-each variable is a copy, which is exactly why it cannot write back</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 336" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk245a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int v : readings) { ... }    — v is a COPY of each element</text><rect x="40" y="48" width="380" height="96" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="52" y="66" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the array — the real storage</text><rect x="62" y="82" width="74" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="99" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">14</text><text x="99" y="142" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[0]</text><rect x="150" y="82" width="74" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="187" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">22</text><text x="187" y="142" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[1]</text><rect x="238" y="82" width="74" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="275" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><text x="275" y="142" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[2]</text><rect x="326" y="82" width="74" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="363" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">31</text><text x="363" y="142" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[3]</text><line x1="150" y1="152" x2="150" y2="190" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk245a)"/><text x="196" y="176" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">a copy is handed to v</text><rect x="96" y="190" width="110" height="42" rx="7" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.15)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="151" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">v = 22</text><line x1="206" y1="211" x2="300" y2="211" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="5 4" marker-end="url(#fk245a)"/><text x="400" y="206" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">v = 0;  writes to the COPY.</text><text x="400" y="224" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">The array never hears about it.</text><text x="300" y="218" fill="#e11d48" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✖</text><rect x="40" y="250" width="620" height="72" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="350" y="278" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">readings[i] = 0;   ← the indexed form writes to the array itself</text><text x="350" y="304" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Use for-each to READ every element in order. Use the indexed loop to WRITE.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the assignment that does nothing</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Both loops look like they zero the array. Only one of them does.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[] readings = {14, 22, 9, 31};

for (int v : readings) v = 0;                 // assigns the COPY
System.out.println("after for-each writes: " + Arrays.toString(readings));

for (int i = 0; i &lt; readings.length; i++) readings[i] = 0;
System.out.println("after indexed writes:  " + Arrays.toString(readings));</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>after for-each writes: [14, 22, 9, 31]
after indexed writes:  [0, 0, 0, 0]</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">On each pass of the first loop, <code>v</code> is created, filled with a copy of the element, assigned 0, and destroyed. The array is never addressed at all — there is no <code>readings[...]</code> anywhere on that line, which is the give-away. It compiles without so much as a warning.</p><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The second loop names the storage: <code>readings[i]</code> on the left of the assignment is the array slot itself. Any exam option claiming a for-each can modify an array of primitives is wrong for this reason.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: unchanged by the for-each; all zeros after the indexed loop</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — empty, null, and an array of objects</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three edge cases the enhanced loop is asked about, and they do not behave the same way.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">int[] empty = new int[0];
int n = 0;
for (int v : empty) n++;
System.out.println("empty array passes: " + n);

int[] missing = null;
try { for (int v : missing) n++; }
catch (NullPointerException e) { System.out.println("null array threw NullPointerException"); }

String[] words = {"alpha", "beta"};
for (String w : words) w = w.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(words));</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>empty array passes: 0
null array threw NullPointerException
[alpha, beta]</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A length-0 array is a perfectly real array with nothing in it. The loop runs zero times, which is correct and needs no guard.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A <code>null</code> array is not an array at all. The enhanced loop has to ask it for its length before it can start, so it throws immediately — <em>before</em> the first pass.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Over an array of objects the loop variable is a copy of the <em>reference</em>. Assigning a new String to <code>w</code> repoints the copy, so the array still holds the originals. What <em>would</em> work is calling a method that changes the object itself — impossible for a String, which is immutable, but the reason a for-each over your own objects can mutate them.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 0 passes; a NullPointerException; and the words unchanged</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">"Empty" and "null" are different states and the exam separates them deliberately. <code>new int[0]</code> is safe to loop over, has a <code>length</code> of 0, and is the right thing to return when there are no results. <code>null</code> throws on the loop, on <code>.length</code>, and on every element access. A guard has to test <code>a == null</code> <em>first</em>, or it throws while checking.</p></div>"},"D286/u5/z5_11":{"body":"<p>Tax brackets are the natural array example because the long <code>else if</code> chain from 3.20 becomes two arrays plus one loop — and adding a new bracket then means editing data, not code.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">double[] limits = {25000, 50000, 100000, Double.MAX_VALUE};
double[] rates  = {0.10,  0.15,  0.22,   0.28};

double rate = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; limits.length; i++) {
    if (salary &lt; limits[i]) { rate = rates[i]; break; }
}
double tax = salary * rate;</pre><p>This is parallel arrays (5.5) doing real work: <code>limits[i]</code> and <code>rates[i]</code> describe one bracket. The <code>break</code> matters — without it, the loop keeps testing and every salary ends up in the top bracket.</p><h3>Why break decides the answer</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">double[] limits = {10000, 40000, 85000, Double.MAX_VALUE};
double[] rates  = {0.10,  0.22,  0.30,  0.37};

double rate = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; limits.length; i++) {
    if (salary &lt;= limits[i]) { rate = rates[i]; break; }   // FIRST match wins
}</pre><p>The brackets are in ascending order, so the first limit the salary fits under is the right one. Without <code>break</code> the loop keeps testing, every later limit also passes, and <code>rate</code> ends up as the last one assigned — so every salary is taxed at the top rate. The bug is silent and uniform, which makes it hard to spot from one test value.</p><p>The final <code>Double.MAX_VALUE</code> entry is a sentinel that guarantees a match, so <code>rate</code> can never be left at 0. Adding a bracket is now editing two array literals rather than rewriting an <code>else if</code> chain — the whole point of the rewrite.</p><i data-d286u5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Table-driven logic:</strong> replacing a branch chain with data in arrays plus one loop</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bracket lookup:</strong> finding the first limit a value falls under and taking the matching rate</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Table-driven code is easier to get right because the ordering rule from 3.4 becomes visible as data. The list is sorted low to high on the page, so you can see the bug rather than trace it.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz511="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — bracket lookup: the break is what makes "first match wins" true</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 340" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk246a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double salary = 18000;   the first limit it fits under wins</text><text x="120" y="62" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">limits[i]</text><text x="330" y="62" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">rates[i]</text><rect x="60" y="74" width="120" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="120" y="98" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">25000</text><rect x="272" y="74" width="116" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="330" y="98" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.10</text><text x="226" y="98" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">i = 0</text><text x="24" y="98" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[0]</text><rect x="60" y="122" width="120" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="120" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">50000</text><rect x="272" y="122" width="116" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="330" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.15</text><text x="226" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">i = 1</text><text x="24" y="146" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[1]</text><rect x="60" y="170" width="120" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="120" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">100000</text><rect x="272" y="170" width="116" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="330" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.22</text><text x="226" y="194" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">i = 2</text><text x="24" y="194" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[2]</text><rect x="60" y="218" width="120" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="120" y="242" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">MAX_VALUE</text><rect x="272" y="218" width="116" height="38" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="330" y="242" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.28</text><text x="226" y="242" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">i = 3</text><text x="24" y="242" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[3]</text><line x1="190" y1="93" x2="262" y2="93" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk246a)"/><text x="400" y="88" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">18000 < 25000  → take 0.10</text><text x="400" y="108" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">break — the loop stops here</text><text x="400" y="150" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">without break the loop keeps going:</text><text x="400" y="172" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">18000 < 50000  → rate = 0.15</text><text x="400" y="192" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">18000 < 100000 → rate = 0.22</text><text x="400" y="212" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">18000 < MAX    → rate = 0.28</text><text x="400" y="248" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">every salary ends up in the top band</text><rect x="24" y="274" width="636" height="52" rx="10" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.10" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="342" y="306" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">The bands are in ascending order, so only the FIRST match is the right one.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — four salaries through one table-driven loop</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">An <code>else if</code> chain becomes two arrays and one loop, and adding a band becomes an edit to data rather than to code.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">double[] limits = {25000, 50000, 100000, Double.MAX_VALUE};
double[] rates  = {0.10, 0.15, 0.22, 0.28};
double[] tests  = {18000, 42000, 61000, 250000};
for (double salary : tests) {
    double rate = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; limits.length; i++) {
        if (salary &lt; limits[i]) { rate = rates[i]; break; }   // FIRST match wins
    }
    System.out.printf("%.0f -&gt; rate %.2f, tax %.2f%n", salary, rate, salary * rate);
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>18000 -&gt; rate 0.10, tax 1800.00
42000 -&gt; rate 0.15, tax 6300.00
61000 -&gt; rate 0.22, tax 13420.00
250000 -&gt; rate 0.28, tax 70000.00</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">18000 fits under the first limit, so the loop takes <code>rates[0]</code> and breaks after one test.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">42000 fails <code>&lt; 25000</code> and passes <code>&lt; 50000</code>, so it takes <code>rates[1]</code> after two tests.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">61000 needs three tests; 250000 falls through to the sentinel <code>Double.MAX_VALUE</code>, which is there precisely so that <em>something</em> always matches and <code>rate</code> is never left at 0.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>limits[i]</code> and <code>rates[i]</code> describe one band — parallel arrays doing real work.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: rates 0.10, 0.15, 0.22 and 0.28</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — deleting the break, one pass at a time</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The same loop with the <code>break</code> removed, printing <code>rate</code> after every test so the damage is visible.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">double[] limits = {25000, 50000, 100000, Double.MAX_VALUE};
double[] rates  = {0.10, 0.15, 0.22, 0.28};
double salary = 18000, rate = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; limits.length; i++) {
    if (salary &lt; limits[i]) { rate = rates[i]; }        // no break
    System.out.printf("  after i=%d rate is %.2f%n", i, rate);
}
System.out.printf("no break: %.0f taxed at %.2f%n", salary, rate);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>  after i=0 rate is 0.10
  after i=1 rate is 0.15
  after i=2 rate is 0.22
  after i=3 rate is 0.28
no break: 18000 taxed at 0.28</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">18000 is under <em>every</em> limit, so every test passes and each one overwrites the rate. The final value is the last band — the top rate, for the lowest earner. Worse, the bug is uniform: <em>every</em> salary comes out at 0.28, so a single test value gives no hint that anything is wrong.</p><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two fixes exist and only one is the topic here. Adding <code>break</code> stops at the first match. Alternatively, reversing the array order and testing <code>salary &gt;= limits[i]</code> also works without a break — but then the data must be sorted descending, and mixing the two conventions is how this loop usually goes wrong.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: 0.28 for an 18000 salary — the highest band, applied to everyone</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Whether the comparison is <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&lt;=</code> decides who sits exactly on a boundary. With <code>salary &lt; 25000</code>, a salary of exactly 25000 falls to the next band. Boundary values are precisely where exam questions are set, so read the operator before you read the numbers.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Table-driven questions are answered by counting <em>tests</em>, not by reading the table. Ask how many comparisons happen before the break fires — that number is both the answer to "how many iterations" and the check that you picked the right band.</p></div>"},"D286/u5/z5_12":{"body":"<p>The final example stores a list of registered domains in an array and checks a candidate against it — the search pattern of 5.4 applied to Strings, with the comparison rules of 3.13.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String[] taken = {"example.com", "wgu.edu", "java.org"};
String candidate = scnr.next().toLowerCase().trim();

boolean available = true;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; taken.length; i++) {
    if (taken[i].equalsIgnoreCase(candidate)) {
        available = false;
        break;
    }
}
System.out.println(candidate + (available ? " is available" : " is taken"));</pre><p>Three earlier ideas are load-bearing here and each one is a place the exam plants an error: <code>.equalsIgnoreCase</code> rather than <code>==</code> (3.2), normalising the input before comparing (3.21), and <code>break</code> so the flag is not overwritten by later non-matches (4.13).</p><h3>Rules as data</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">char[] allowed = {'-', '.'};

boolean legal(char c) {
    if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(c)) return true;
    for (char ok : allowed) if (c == ok) return true;
    return false;
}</pre><p>Putting the permitted characters in an array turns the rule into data: extending it is a one-element edit rather than another clause in a growing boolean expression. It is the same move as the tax brackets in 5.11, applied to validation.</p><p>The loop returns as soon as it finds a match, and returns <code>false</code> only after every possibility has been checked — the shape of every "is this in the set?" search. Returning <code>false</code> inside the loop instead would reject on the first <em>non</em>-match, which rejects everything.</p><i data-d286u5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Membership test:</strong> searching an array for an element equal to a target</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Case-insensitive search:</strong> normalising both sides, or using equalsIgnoreCase, so case does not affect the match</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Using <code>==</code> here would compile and would report every domain as available, because the input String is never the same object as the literals in the array.</p></div><i data-tbd286bz512="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a membership search, and the comparison that decides whether it finds anything</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 332" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk247a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">String[] taken = {"example.com", "wgu.edu", "java.org"};   candidate = "wgu.edu"</text><rect x="60" y="52" width="176" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="148" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"example.com"</text><text x="148" y="112" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[0]</text><rect x="256" y="52" width="176" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.13)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="344" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"wgu.edu"</text><text x="344" y="112" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[1]</text><rect x="452" y="52" width="176" height="44" rx="7" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.13)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="540" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"java.org"</text><text x="540" y="112" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">[2]</text><line x1="148" y1="152" x2="148" y2="122" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk247a)"/><text x="148" y="172" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">equalsIgnoreCase? no</text><line x1="344" y1="152" x2="344" y2="122" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk247a)"/><text x="344" y="172" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">MATCH → available = false; break</text><rect x="480" y="130" width="176" height="40" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="568" y="155" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">never examined</text><rect x="24" y="190" width="316" height="128" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="36" y="208" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">comparing with ==</text><text x="182" y="224" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the candidate came from input,</text><text x="182" y="244" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">so it is a NEW String object</text><text x="182" y="272" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">taken[1] == candidate → false</text><text x="182" y="298" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">every domain reported available</text><rect x="352" y="190" width="308" height="128" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="364" y="208" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">comparing with equals</text><text x="506" y="224" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">compares the characters,</text><text x="506" y="244" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not the address</text><text x="506" y="272" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">taken[1].equals(candidate) → true</text><text x="506" y="298" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the match is found</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — is this domain taken?</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The search pattern of 5.4 with Strings in place of numbers, plus the normalisation that makes the comparison fair.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">String[] taken = {"example.com", "wgu.edu", "java.org"};
String[] tries = {"WGU.edu", "newsite.com"};
for (String raw : tries) {
    String candidate = raw.trim().toLowerCase();
    boolean available = true;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; taken.length; i++) {
        if (taken[i].equalsIgnoreCase(candidate)) { available = false; break; }
    }
    System.out.println(candidate + (available ? " is available" : " is taken"));
}</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>wgu.edu is taken
newsite.com is available</div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>trim().toLowerCase()</code> normalises the input first, so trailing spaces and capitals cannot produce a false "available".</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The flag starts <strong>true</strong> and can only be falsified — the sticky-flag rule from 4.13.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>break</code> stops the scan at the match. Without it the loop keeps testing, and since the remaining entries do not match, nothing resets the flag — so here it is an efficiency point rather than a correctness one. It becomes a correctness point the moment the loop assigns <code>available = ...</code> instead of only assigning <code>false</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">"newsite.com" survives all three comparisons, so the flag stays true.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: wgu.edu is taken; newsite.com is available</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why == compiles and finds nothing</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The candidate arrives as input rather than as a literal, which is what makes this the realistic case.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); padding:12px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0; border:1px solid var(--border);">String[] taken = {"example.com", "wgu.edu"};
Scanner scnr = new Scanner("wgu.edu");
String candidate = scnr.next();          // built at run time — a NEW object
boolean byEquals = false, byIdentity = false;
for (String t : taken) {
    if (t.equals(candidate)) byEquals = true;
    if (t == candidate)      byIdentity = true;
}
System.out.println("equals found it: " + byEquals);
System.out.println("==     found it: " + byIdentity);</pre><div style="margin:8px 0 10px; border-left:3px solid #10b981; background:var(--surface-2); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:8px 12px; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap; line-height:1.6;"><span style="display:block; font-family:inherit; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; opacity:.7; margin-bottom:4px;">OUTPUT</span>equals found it: true
==     found it: false</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The characters are identical and <code>==</code> still says no, because it asks "the same object?" and the Scanner built a fresh String. The array entries are compile-time literals living in the string pool; the input is not. So a registry written with <code>==</code> reports every domain as available, forever, and never throws.</p><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">There is a trap inside the trap: written as <code>String candidate = "wgu" + ".edu";</code> the compiler folds two literals into one pooled literal and <code>==</code> would be <em>true</em>. That is why demonstrations of this rule must use a value built at run time — and why <code>==</code> on Strings can appear to work in testing and fail in production.</p><div style="margin-top:8px; padding:7px 11px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700;">Answer: equals finds it; == does not</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two things make a "rules as data" answer right on this topic: the permitted values live in an array so extending them is a one-element edit, and the search returns as soon as it finds a match but only reports failure <em>after</em> the whole array has been checked. A loop that returns <code>false</code> from inside the body rejects on the first non-match, which rejects everything.</p></div>"},"D286/u6/z6_1":{"body":"<p>So far every program has been one long <code>main</code>. A <strong>method</strong> is a named block of code you can call by name, hand values to, and get a value back from — the first tool in the course for making a program out of parts instead of one continuous script.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int square(int n) {
//     ^      ^   ^      ^
//     |      |   |      parameter list: what it needs
//     |      |   name
//     |      return type: what it gives back  (void = nothing)
//     modifiers

    return n * n;               // hands the value back and exits immediately
}

int result = square(6);         // 36 — the CALL</pre><p>The vocabulary is worth separating now because the exam uses it precisely. The method <em>definition</em> is written once; the <em>call</em> can happen many times; the <em>parameter</em> is the name in the definition; the <em>argument</em> is the value supplied at the call.</p><h3>Reading a method header</h3><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double average(int a, int b) { ... }
  |      |      |        |        |
  |      |      |        |        +-- parameter list (types AND names)
  |      |      |        +----------- method name (camelCase, a verb)
  |      |      +-------------------- return type — what comes back
  |      +--------------------------- static: called on the class, not an object
  +---------------------------------- visibility</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Term</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Parameter</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the variable in the <em>definition</em> — <code>int a</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Argument</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the value at the <em>call</em> — <code>average(3, 5)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Signature</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">name + parameter types; the return type is <em>not</em> part of it</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong><code>void</code></strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">returns nothing — a bare <code>return;</code> is allowed, <code>return x;</code> is not</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A method with a non-<code>void</code> return type must return a value on <em>every</em> path, and the compiler checks it. "Missing return statement" almost always means a branch has no <code>else</code>.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Method:</strong> a named, reusable block of code that may take inputs and return a value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Parameter:</strong> a variable in the method definition that receives an argument</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Argument:</strong> the actual value passed at the call site</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Return type:</strong> the type of value the method hands back; void means none</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Signature:</strong> the method name plus its parameter types — what distinguishes overloads (6.12)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Every remaining unit is built from methods. Classes in Unit 7 are data plus methods; recursion in Unit 11 is a method calling itself; the library you have been using since Unit 1 is nothing but methods.</p></div><i data-d286fx11="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A METHOD CALL IS A ROUND TRIP</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 510 172" style="width:100%; max-width:550px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk78a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="30" width="170" height="110" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="105" y="50" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">main()</text><text x="30" y="76" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int y =</text><text x="30" y="92" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">  square(4);</text><text x="30" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">println(y); // 16</text><rect x="300" y="30" width="180" height="110" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="390" y="50" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int square(int num)</text><text x="310" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">num is 4 (a copy)</text><text x="310" y="108" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return num * num;</text><line x1="190" y1="70" x2="298" y2="70" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk78a)"/><text x="244" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">① call: 4 goes in</text><line x1="298" y1="118" x2="190" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk78a)"/><text x="244" y="134" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">② return: 16 comes back</text><text x="250" y="164" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The call pauses main, jumps in with the argument, and the return value lands where the call was written.</text></svg></div></div><i data-d286_z6_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The four parts of a method</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(applyDiscount(200, 10));
System.out.println(applyDiscount(89, 25));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">180
67</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The RETURN TYPE (int) says what comes back.</li><li>The NAME says what it does.</li><li>The PARAMETERS are the inputs it demands, in order.</li><li>The BODY computes, and <code>return</code> hands the value back.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — void returns nothing, so it cannot be assigned</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">banner("Invoice");
banner("Totals");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">== Invoice ==
== Totals ==</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A <code>void</code> method performs an action instead of producing a value.</li><li>It is called as a statement on its own.</li><li><code>int x = banner("a");</code> would not compile — there is nothing to assign.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Forgetting that a non-void method must return on <em>every</em> path. If an <code>if</code> returns but the <code>else</code> falls off the end, the compiler rejects it with "missing return statement".</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Read the signature before the body. The return type tells you whether the call can be assigned, and the parameter list tells you exactly what the call must supply.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_2":{"body":"<p>The simplest useful methods return nothing and exist purely to avoid repeating output code. Their return type is <code>void</code>, and a <code>void</code> method ends either at its closing brace or at a bare <code>return;</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void printBanner(String title) {
    System.out.println("=================");
    System.out.println("  " + title);
    System.out.println("=================");
}

printBanner("Results");        // a STATEMENT — it produces no value

int x = printBanner("Hi");     // COMPILE ERROR — void gives nothing to assign</pre><p>The distinction between a method that prints and one that returns is a real design decision, not a formality. A method that prints can only ever print; a method that returns can be printed, stored, compared or passed on. Prefer returning — it is also what makes the unit testing in 6.7 possible.</p><p>A <code>void</code> method ends at its closing brace, or early at a bare <code>return;</code> used as a guard. Writing <code>return value;</code> in a <code>void</code> method is a compile error, and so is using its result: <code>int x = printReport();</code> will not compile because there is nothing to assign.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void banner(String title) {
    if (title == null) return;          // early exit, no value
    System.out.println("=== " + title + " ===");
}</pre><p>The choice between printing and returning is a design decision that shows up again in 6.7: a method that <em>prints</em> can only be checked by eye, while a method that <em>returns</em> can be unit tested. Prefer returning the value and letting the caller decide whether to display it.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>void:</strong> a return type meaning the method produces no value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>return;:</strong> in a void method, exits early without a value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Side effect:</strong> anything a method does other than return a value, such as printing</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If a question asks why a method returns a String instead of printing it, the answer is testability and reuse — you cannot assert on something that has already gone to the screen.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — print, println and printf</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk360a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">print(x)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">no line break after</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">println(x)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">adds a line break</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">printf(fmt, ...)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">formats — no break unless %n</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">%.2f</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">two decimal places</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">%n</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a newline</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The three of them side by side</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.print("a");
System.out.print("b");
System.out.println("c");
System.out.println("d");
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", 3.14159);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">abc
d
3.14</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>print</code> leaves the cursor on the same line, so a and b run together.</li><li><code>println</code> ends the line.</li><li><code>printf</code> formats, and needs <code>%n</code> to break the line itself.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A print method of your own</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">printReceipt("Cable", 7.5);
printReceipt("Adapter", 12.25);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Cable        7.50
Adapter     12.25</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>%-10s</code> pads the name to 10 characters, left aligned.</li><li><code>%6.2f</code> right-aligns the number in 6 columns with 2 decimals.</li><li>Wrapping it in a method keeps every line of the receipt identical.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting <code>printf</code> to end the line. It does not — without <code>%n</code> the next output continues on the same line, which is why receipts and tables come out on one long line.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_3":{"body":"<p>Three reasons justify pulling code into a method, and they are the reasons an exam question will list.</p><p><strong>Reuse</strong> removes duplication, so a fix happens in one place rather than five. <strong>Abstraction</strong> lets you use <code>calculateTax(salary)</code> without holding the brackets in your head. <strong>Decomposition</strong> breaks a large problem into pieces small enough to test separately (1.9).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// before: the same six lines in three places
// after:
double tax = calculateTax(salary);</pre><p>The practical rule of thumb: if you have copied a block of code, or if you find yourself writing a comment to explain what the next ten lines do, that block wants to be a method with the comment as its name.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Reason</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">What it buys</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Symptom when ignored</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Reuse</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one copy of the logic</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the same fix applied in five places, four correctly</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Abstraction</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a name instead of the detail</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">code you must re-read to understand</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Testability</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">callable in isolation</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the only way to test is to run the whole program</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Maintainability</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">change in one place</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">edits ripple unpredictably</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The practical rule is one job per method. If describing what it does needs the word "and", it is probably two methods — and each half is then independently reusable and independently testable.</p><p>Method names should be verbs for actions (<code>calculateTax</code>) and questions for booleans (<code>isValid</code>, <code>hasNext</code>). A name that reads like a sentence at the call site is the point of the exercise.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reuse:</strong> calling one definition from many places instead of duplicating code</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Abstraction:</strong> using a method by its name and contract without knowing its implementation</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Decomposition:</strong> splitting a problem into smaller solvable parts</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Cohesion:</strong> a method doing one clearly-named job rather than several</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is the same argument the whole course keeps making at larger scale: methods group statements, classes group methods and data (7.6), packages group classes (7.20).</p></div><i data-d286_z6_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what a method buys you</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk361a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">reuse</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">write once, call anywhere</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">one place to fix</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a bug is corrected once</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a name</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the call says WHAT, not how</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">testable</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one unit can be tested alone</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same logic, repeated versus named</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double a = 100, b = 250;
System.out.println(a * 0.20 + b * 0.20);
System.out.println(vat(a) + vat(b));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">70.0
70.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both lines compute the same total.</li><li>The second says what the number MEANS.</li><li>If the rate changes, the second needs one edit and the first needs every occurrence found.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Decomposition makes the main flow readable</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double[] prices = {19.99, 5.50, 3.75};
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", subtotal(prices));
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", withVat(subtotal(prices)));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">29.24
35.09</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each method does one job with a name that says so.</li><li>The last line reads as a sentence rather than as arithmetic.</li><li>Each piece can also be tested on its own.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Believing methods make a program faster. A call carries a small overhead, so raw speed usually goes very slightly DOWN. The gain is correctness and maintenance — that is the answer the exam wants.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>If a question asks for the PRIMARY benefit of methods, the answer is reuse and maintainability — not performance and not memory.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_4":{"body":"<p>A mathematical method is the cleanest kind: values in, value out, nothing else touched. Every path through it must return, and the compiler enforces that.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double average(int a, int b, int c) {
    return (a + b + c) / 3.0;      // 3.0 not 3 — else integer division (2.5)
}

public static int max(int a, int b) {
    if (a &gt; b) { return a; }
    return b;                       // reached only when a &lt;= b
}</pre><p>The 3.0 in the first method is the same trap as ever, now hidden inside a method where it is harder to see. Declaring the return type as <code>double</code> does not rescue an integer division — the truncation happens first, and the whole number is then widened.</p><p>A <strong>pure</strong> method depends only on its parameters and changes nothing outside itself. Same inputs, same output, every time — which is what makes it trivially testable and safe to call from anywhere.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double circleArea(double r) {
    return Math.PI * r * r;              // pure
}

public static double triangleArea(double b, double h) {
    return (b * h) / 2.0;                // 2.0, not 2
}</pre><p>The <code>2.0</code> is deliberate. With <code>2</code>, if <code>b</code> and <code>h</code> were <code>int</code>s the whole expression would be integer division and lose the half. Writing the literal as a <code>double</code> forces real division regardless of what the operands are.</p><p><code>Math.PI</code> rather than 3.14, and <code>Math.pow(r, 2)</code> is legal but <code>r * r</code> is faster and clearer for a square.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pure method:</strong> one whose result depends only on its arguments and which changes nothing else</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Return path:</strong> every route through the method must reach a return statement</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Math class:</strong> the standard library of static maths methods: abs, pow, sqrt, min, max, round</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>"Missing return statement" means one branch has no return — usually an <code>if</code> with no <code>else</code>. The compiler will not reason about whether that branch can actually happen; it demands a return on every path.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a maths method is just input to output</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk362a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">parameters</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the numbers it needs</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return type</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">double for anything fractional</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">hands the value back</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">no printing</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">so the caller decides the format</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Return the value; let the caller print it</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(circleArea(2));
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", circleArea(2));
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", circleArea(2) * 3);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">12.566370614359172
12.57
37.70</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The method computes and returns; it never prints.</li><li>That lets the caller format it, or use it in further arithmetic.</li><li>A method that printed instead could not be reused in the third line.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Integer division inside a maths method</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(averageBad(7, 8));
System.out.println(averageGood(7, 8));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">7.0
7.5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both parameters are int, so <code>(a + b) / 2</code> is integer division.</li><li>The declared <code>double</code> return type does NOT rescue it — the value is already truncated.</li><li>Dividing by 2.0 forces real division before the return.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Declaring the return type as <code>double</code> does not make the arithmetic inside it double. Check the operands, not the signature.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_5":{"body":"<p>Methods that return <code>boolean</code> turn a condition into a name, and that is worth more than it sounds: <code>if (isLeapYear(y))</code> reads correctly, while the same condition written out does not.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static boolean isLeapYear(int year) {
    if (year % 400 == 0) { return true; }
    if (year % 100 == 0) { return false; }
    return year % 4 == 0;              // return the expression directly
}

public static char grade(int score) {
    if (score &gt;= 90) { return 'A'; }
    if (score &gt;= 80) { return 'B'; }
    return 'F';
}</pre><p>Note the last line of the first method. <code>return year % 4 == 0;</code> is preferred over an <code>if</code> that returns <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> — the comparison already <em>is</em> a boolean (3.12), so wrapping it adds nothing.</p><p>Using <code>return</code> instead of <code>else if</code> also flattens the chain: once a <code>return</code> executes, the method is over, so no <code>else</code> is required to prevent the later checks.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// verbose
public static boolean isEven(int n) {
    if (n % 2 == 0) return true;
    else return false;
}

// the condition IS the answer
public static boolean isEven(int n) {
    return n % 2 == 0;
}</pre><p>Any <code>if (c) return true; else return false;</code> collapses to <code>return c;</code>. The exam asks you to spot it, and the reverse — <code>return !c;</code> for the negated version.</p><p>A <strong>guard clause</strong> handles the exceptional case first and returns, leaving the main path unindented:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double divide(int a, int b) {
    if (b == 0) return 0;                // guard
    return (double) a / b;               // the real work, not nested
}</pre><p>Every path must return. A method whose last statement is inside an <code>if</code> with no <code>else</code> fails to compile even when you can see the condition is always true — the compiler does not reason about values.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Predicate method:</strong> a method returning boolean, named as a question: isValid, hasNext</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Guard clause:</strong> an early return that handles a special case and leaves the rest unindented</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Early return:</strong> returning as soon as the answer is known</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>if (x) return true; else return false;</code> is always reducible to <code>return x;</code>. Exam questions include the long form as a "which is equivalent" distractor.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — every path must return</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 124" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk363a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if returns</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">and else returns -&gt; compiles</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if returns</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">and nothing else -&gt; error</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a final return</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the safe pattern</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Returning from a branch</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(band(95));
System.out.println(band(80));
System.out.println(band(12));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">A
B
F</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>return</code> exits the method immediately.</li><li>So the first matching band wins and nothing below runs.</li><li>The final unconditional return guarantees every path returns something.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Returning a boolean directly</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(isEvenLong(4) + " " + isEven(4));
System.out.println(isEvenLong(7) + " " + isEven(7));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true true
false false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The condition is ALREADY a boolean.</li><li>So the if/else pair is redundant.</li><li>Both versions agree; the second is what a marker expects.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>"Missing return statement" when a method returns inside every <code>if</code> but has nothing after them. The compiler must see a return on every possible path, even one you can argue is unreachable.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_6":{"body":"<p>A method containing a loop is where the accumulate patterns of 4.3 get a name and become reusable. The accumulator is a local variable, so each call starts fresh — which is the fix for the bug in 4.12 by construction.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int sumTo(int n) {
    int sum = 0;                    // local: reset on every call
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) { sum += i; }
    return sum;
}

public static int countVowels(String s) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
        if ("aeiouAEIOU".indexOf(s.charAt(i)) &gt;= 0) { count++; }
    }
    return count;
}</pre><p><code>"aeiouAEIOU".indexOf(c) &gt;= 0</code> is a compact membership test using 3.16: <code>indexOf</code> returns -1 when the character is absent, so a non-negative result means it was found.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int sum(int[] a) {
    int total = 0;                       // LOCAL — fresh every call
    for (int x : a) total += x;
    return total;                        // after the loop
}

public static int indexOf(int[] a, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++)
        if (a[i] == target) return i;    // early return — no break needed
    return -1;                           // reached only if nothing matched
}</pre><p>Putting the accumulator inside the method rather than outside is what fixes the reset bug from 4.12 permanently: each call gets its own, so calls cannot interfere.</p><p>In a search, <code>return</code> replaces <code>break</code> — it leaves the loop <em>and</em> the method at once. The final <code>return -1</code> is not optional; without it the method has a path with no return value and will not compile.</p><p>An empty array is not an error for <code>sum</code> (the loop runs zero times and 0 is right) but it is for an average, which must guard against dividing by <code>a.length == 0</code>.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Local accumulator:</strong> a variable declared inside the method, so each call gets its own</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Loop-and-return:</strong> the pattern of accumulating in a loop and returning the result after it</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Putting a <code>return</code> inside the loop body without a condition ends the method on the first iteration. Return after the loop unless you genuinely want an early exit.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — accumulate inside, return outside</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk364a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">declare total</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">BEFORE the loop</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">total += ...</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">inside the loop</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return total</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">AFTER the loop</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return inside</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">exits on the first pass</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Summing with an accumulator</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(sumTo(5));
System.out.println(sumTo(100));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">15
5050</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>total</code> is declared before the loop so it survives every pass.</li><li>Each pass adds one more term.</li><li>The return happens after the loop, when the total is complete.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Returning too early</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(sumBroken(5));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The return sits INSIDE the loop.</li><li>So the method exits during the first pass.</li><li>It prints 1 rather than 15 — a classic silent wrong answer.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Trace any loop-in-a-method question with a small n, such as 3. The difference between returning inside and after the loop shows up immediately.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_7":{"body":"<p>Unit testing means calling a method with known inputs and checking the outputs — and it is the direct payoff of returning values rather than printing them (6.2).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void main(String[] args) {
    // normal cases
    System.out.println(max(3, 7) == 7);      // expect true
    System.out.println(max(9, 2) == 9);

    // EDGE cases — where the bugs live
    System.out.println(max(5, 5) == 5);      // equal
    System.out.println(max(-3, -8) == -3);   // negatives
}</pre><p>The valuable tests are the edge cases: empty input, a single element, zero, negatives, the exact boundary of a range, the first and last index of an array. A method that passes three ordinary cases and fails on an empty array is the normal state of untested code.</p><p>A unit test calls one method with known inputs and checks the result against what you worked out by hand. It is only possible because the method <em>returns</em> something — which is the concrete reason 6.2 prefers returning to printing.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Test kind</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">For <code>max(int[])</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">typical</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>{3, 9, 4}</code> → 9</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>boundary</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one element <code>{5}</code> → 5; max at the first position; max at the last</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>edge</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">empty array; all values equal; all negative</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">invalid</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>null</code> — does it throw, or return a default?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Boundaries earn their keep because that is where off-by-one errors live: a bound that is wrong by one behaves correctly for every value except at the very edge of the range.</p><p>Tests must be independent — each sets up its own data — so that one failure does not cascade. Re-running the whole set after a change is <strong>regression testing</strong>, and its purpose is to catch the fix that broke something else.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Unit test:</strong> a check that one method produces the expected output for a specific input</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Test case:</strong> one input plus its expected result</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Edge case:</strong> an input at the boundary of what is valid: empty, zero, first, last, maximum</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Regression:</strong> a previously working behaviour broken by a later change</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Edge cases are also where exam questions are set. When you see a trace question with an empty array or a single-element array, that is deliberate — the interesting answer is at the boundary.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what to test</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk365a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a typical case</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the ordinary path</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a boundary</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0, 1, the exact limit</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">an invalid input</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">negative, empty, null</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">expected vs actual</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the assertion itself</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Testing a method by asserting values</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(clamp(5, 1, 10) == 5);
System.out.println(clamp(0, 1, 10) == 1);
System.out.println(clamp(99, 1, 10) == 10);
System.out.println(clamp(1, 1, 10) == 1);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
true
true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each line states an expectation and prints whether it held.</li><li>The boundary values 1 and 10 matter more than the middle ones.</li><li>All true means the method behaves as specified.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A test that catches a real bug</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] xs = {4, 8, 15};
System.out.println(lastIndexBug(xs) == 2);
System.out.println(lastIndex(xs) == 2);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">false
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The buggy version returns 3, one past the end.</li><li>The test prints false, which is the bug being caught.</li><li>The corrected version passes.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Testing only the happy path. The bugs live at the boundaries — an empty array, a zero, the first and last index — which is exactly where a casual test never looks.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>A good test names the expected value explicitly. "It ran without crashing" is not a test; "clamp(99, 1, 10) equals 10" is.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_8":{"body":"<p>Understanding how a call works mechanically explains several behaviours that otherwise look arbitrary. When a method is called, Java pushes a <strong>stack frame</strong> holding that call’s parameters and locals; when it returns, the frame is discarded.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">main            calls  average(a, b)
  |                        |
  | frame: a, b, result    | frame: its OWN a, b  (copies)
  |                        |
  |  &lt;---- returns 86.3 ---+   frame destroyed, locals gone</pre><p>The consequence that matters most is <strong>pass by value</strong>: the parameter is a copy of the argument, so assigning to a parameter cannot change the caller’s variable.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void tryToChange(int x) { x = 99; }

int n = 5;
tryToChange(n);
System.out.println(n);       // still 5</pre><h3>The call stack, and pass-by-value</h3><p>Each call pushes a <strong>stack frame</strong> holding that call's parameters, its local variables and the address to return to. The frame is destroyed when the method returns, which is why locals do not survive and why infinite recursion ends in <code>StackOverflowError</code>.</p><p>Java is <strong>always pass-by-value</strong>. The subtlety is what the value <em>is</em>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void f(int n)   { n = 99; }          // copies the number
static void g(int[] a) { a[0] = 99; }       // copies the REFERENCE
static void h(int[] a) { a = new int[3]; }  // reassigns the copy — caller unaffected

int x = 1;      f(x);   // x is still 1
int[] arr={1};  g(arr); // arr[0] IS now 99  — same array, reached through the copy
                h(arr); // arr still points at the original array</pre><p>For a primitive, the copied value is the number. For an object or array, the copied value is the <em>reference</em> — so the method can reach and modify the same object, but assigning a whole new object to the parameter only rebinds its private copy.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Call stack:</strong> the stack of frames, one per active method call</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stack frame:</strong> the memory holding one call’s parameters and local variables</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pass by value:</strong> the argument’s value is copied into the parameter</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Return address:</strong> where execution resumes in the caller after the method returns</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Java is pass-by-value <em>always</em>, including for objects — what gets copied there is the reference, which is why 6.19 can change an object’s contents but still cannot make the caller’s variable point somewhere new.</p></div><i data-d286fx12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE CALL STACK: ONE FRAME PER ACTIVE CALL</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 170" style="width:100%; max-width:520px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk79a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="110" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">while square(4) runs</text><rect x="40" y="30" width="150" height="44" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="115" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">square</text><text x="115" y="64" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">num = 4</text><rect x="40" y="78" width="150" height="44" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="115" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">main (paused)</text><text x="115" y="112" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">y = ?</text><line x1="210" y1="52" x2="250" y2="52" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk79a)"/><text x="258" y="45" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">top frame =</text><text x="258" y="59" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">currently running</text><text x="300" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">after return</text><rect x="300" y="78" width="150" height="44" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="375" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">main (running)</text><text x="375" y="112" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">y = 16</text><rect x="300" y="30" width="150" height="44" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="375" y="55" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">frame popped, gone</text><text x="245" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Each call pushes a frame holding its OWN parameters and locals; returning pops it. That is why</text><text x="245" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">two methods can both have a variable named num without colliding.</text></svg></div></div><i data-d286_z6_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Parameters are copies — primitives cannot be changed</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int n = 5;
tryToChange(n);
System.out.println(n);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java passes the VALUE, so the method receives a copy.</li><li>Assigning to the parameter changes only that copy.</li><li>The caller's variable is untouched — it still prints 5.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The call stack unwinds in reverse</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("main start");
outer();
System.out.println("main end");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">main start
outer start
inner
outer end
main end</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each call pushes a frame holding its own parameters and locals.</li><li>Control resumes exactly where it left off when the call returns.</li><li>Frames come off in the opposite order they went on.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting a method to change a primitive argument. It cannot. To reflect a change back, <em>return</em> the new value and assign it at the call site.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_9":{"body":"<p>The common method errors are a short list, and all but one are compile errors — which is the good news.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int x = printTotal();          // 1. using the result of a void method
double avg(int a, int b) { }   // 2. missing return statement
max(3);                         // 3. wrong number of arguments
max("a", "b");                  // 4. wrong argument types
int max(int a, int b) { ... }   // 5. defined inside main — methods go beside it</pre><p>The one silent failure is argument order. <code>divide(b, a)</code> compiles perfectly when both parameters are the same type and gives the wrong answer forever — a logic error (1.4) that no compiler can catch, which is what makes named parameters in the call site comment worthwhile.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Error</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">When it bites</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">missing <code>return</code> on some path</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compile</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">wrong number of arguments</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compile</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">argument of the wrong type</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compile</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>return x;</code> in a <code>void</code> method</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compile</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">defining a method inside another method</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compile</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>arguments in the wrong order</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>never — silently wrong answers</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Only the last one is dangerous. <code>divide(b, a)</code> compiles perfectly when both parameters are <code>int</code>, and the result is simply wrong. Naming parameters meaningfully and keeping the list short is the only real defence; a method taking four <code>int</code>s in a row is an accident waiting to happen.</p><p>Ignoring a return value is legal and sometimes a bug: <code>s.toUpperCase();</code> on its own line does nothing, because Strings are immutable and the new String is discarded. It must be <code>s = s.toUpperCase();</code>.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Arity:</strong> the number of parameters a method takes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type mismatch:</strong> an argument whose type does not convert to the parameter type</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Argument order error:</strong> passing correct values in the wrong positions; compiles and is silently wrong</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>In this course methods are <code>static</code> and sit beside <code>main</code> inside the class, never inside <code>main</code> itself. "Illegal start of expression" almost always means a method definition ended up inside another method.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — four that the compiler catches</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk366a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">wrong argument count</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">expects 2, given 1</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">wrong argument type</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">String where int required</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ignoring the return</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">value computed and thrown away</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">missing return</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a path that returns nothing</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Ignoring the returned value</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String msg = "hello";
shout(msg);
System.out.println(msg);
msg = shout(msg);
System.out.println(msg);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">hello
HELLO</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The first call computes the upper-case text and discards it.</li><li>Nothing changes, and the compiler does not complain.</li><li>Assigning the result is what makes it visible.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Arguments are matched by position, not by name</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(range(1, 10));
System.out.println(range(10, 1));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1..10
10..1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The first argument fills the first parameter, whatever it is called.</li><li>Swapping them compiles perfectly and produces nonsense.</li><li>The compiler cannot help here — only reading the signature can.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>If a call "does nothing", check whether the method returns a value you forgot to assign. Nearly every String method is in this category.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_10":{"body":"<p>Passing an array to a method is the first place where pass-by-value stops feeling like pass-by-value — and it is worth being exact, because the exam tests the exact boundary.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void doubleAll(int[] a) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { a[i] *= 2; }   // caller SEES this
}

public static void replace(int[] a) {
    a = new int[]{9, 9, 9};        // caller does NOT see this
}</pre><p>The reference is copied, so the method has a second name for the same array — modifying elements through it modifies the one array everyone shares. But reassigning the parameter only repoints the copy; the caller’s variable still points at the original.</p><p>Because the length travels with the array, an array parameter needs no separate size parameter — <code>a.length</code> is always available inside the method.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void doubleAll(int[] a) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) a[i] *= 2;   // caller SEES this
}

static void replace(int[] a) {
    a = new int[]{9, 9, 9};                        // caller sees NOTHING
}</pre><p>The exact boundary: you may change what the array <em>contains</em>, because the copy of the reference reaches the same array; you may not change which array the caller's variable <em>points at</em>, because that variable was never handed over.</p><p>The array's length travels with it — <code>a.length</code> works inside the method with no extra parameter. That is only true for a perfect size array; an oversize one must pass its count separately, which is the whole of 6.17.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int sum(int... nums) { ... }   // varargs: call as sum(1,2,3) or sum(arr)</pre><p>A <code>null</code> array parameter is legal to pass and throws only when dereferenced, so a method that might receive one should guard with <code>if (a == null || a.length == 0)</code>.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Array parameter:</strong> declared as Type[] name; receives a copy of the reference</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Aliasing:</strong> two variables referring to the same array</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reassignment vs mutation:</strong> changing what a variable points to, versus changing what it points at</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>The rule in one line: a method can change an array’s <em>contents</em> but not which array the caller holds. The same rule governs objects in 7.18.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → 1D Arrays</strong></p><i data-d286_z6_10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — arrays are handed over by reference</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk367a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">primitive parameter</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a COPY of the value</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">array parameter</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a copy of the REFERENCE</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[0] = 9 inside</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the caller sees it</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a = new int[3] inside</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the caller does NOT</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Changing elements is visible to the caller</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] data = {7, 8, 9};
zeroFirst(data);
System.out.println(data[0] + "," + data[1]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0,8</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The method receives a copy of the reference, pointing at the SAME array.</li><li>Writing to an element changes that one array.</li><li>So the caller sees 0.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reassigning the parameter is not</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] data = {7, 8, 9};
replace(data);
System.out.println(data[0] + "," + data[1] + "," + data[2]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">7,8,9</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Assigning to <code>a</code> repoints only the method's own copy of the reference.</li><li>The caller's variable still points at the original array.</li><li>So nothing changed outside — this is the distinction the exam tests.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Concluding that Java is "pass by reference" because array contents change. Java is always pass by value — for an array, the VALUE being copied happens to be a reference.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_11":{"body":"<p>Scope for methods extends the block scope of 4.10 upward. A local variable lives inside its method and is invisible everywhere else, including inside other methods — even ones called from it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Demo {
    static int shared = 10;                 // FIELD: visible to every method

    public static void a() {
        int local = 5;                      // visible only inside a()
        System.out.println(shared);         // fine
    }

    public static void b() {
        System.out.println(local);          // ERROR — local does not exist here
    }
}</pre><p>Two methods can each declare a variable called <code>count</code> with no conflict, because the names never meet. And a local with the same name as a field <strong>shadows</strong> it: inside that method the local wins, and the field is reachable only through <code>this</code> (7.11).</p><p>A local variable is visible only inside its own method — not in methods that method calls, and not in the caller. Methods do not see each other's locals at all; the only channels between them are <em>parameters in</em> and <em>the return value out</em>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void a() {
    int x = 5;
    b();                      // b cannot see x
}
static void b() {
    System.out.println(x);    // compile error: x does not exist here
}</pre><p>A <strong>field</strong> declared in the class is visible to every method of that class, which is how objects hold state across calls (Unit 7). A parameter with the same name as a field <em>shadows</em> it, which is exactly why constructors and setters are written <code>this.name = name;</code> — the bare <code>name</code> refers to the parameter.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Local scope:</strong> a variable visible only within the method that declares it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Class scope (field):</strong> a variable declared in the class, visible to its methods</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Shadowing:</strong> a local variable hiding a field of the same name</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Method scope:</strong> methods in a class can call each other regardless of declaration order</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Methods, unlike variables, do not need to be declared before use. A method defined at the bottom of the class can be called from <code>main</code> at the top.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Encapsulation and Access Modifiers</strong></p><i data-d286_z6_11="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — where a name is visible</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk368a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">inside a method</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">local — that method only</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">inside a loop/if block</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">that block only</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a parameter</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the whole method body</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">outside all methods</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a field — the whole class</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A block-scoped variable dies at the brace</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {
    int doubled = i * 2;
    total += doubled;
}
System.out.println(total);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">12</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>doubled</code> exists only inside the loop body.</li><li>Using it after the closing brace would not compile.</li><li><code>total</code> is declared outside, so it survives and holds the sum.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A local shadows a field of the same name</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">show();
System.out.println(count);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5
100</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Inside <code>show</code> the local <code>count</code> hides the field.</li><li>Printing inside shows 5.</li><li>The field is untouched and still prints 100.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Declaring a variable inside a loop when you need its value afterwards. It is created fresh each pass and destroyed at the closing brace, so the total you wanted never survives.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_12":{"body":"<p>Overloading lets several methods share one name as long as their <strong>parameter lists differ</strong>. The compiler picks the match at compile time from the argument types.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int    add(int a, int b)            { return a + b; }
static double add(double a, double b)      { return a + b; }
static int    add(int a, int b, int c)     { return a + b + c; }

add(2, 3);        // the int version
add(2.0, 3.0);    // the double version
add(1, 2, 3);     // the three-parameter version</pre><p>The rule that catches people: the <strong>return type alone is not enough</strong>. Two methods differing only in what they return will not compile, because at the call site there is nothing for the compiler to choose on.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int    f(int x) { ... }
static double f(int x) { ... }        // COMPILE ERROR — same signature</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int    max(int a, int b)          { ... }   // OK
static double max(double a, double b)    { ... }   // OK — different types
static int    max(int a, int b, int c)   { ... }   // OK — different count

static double max(int a, int b)          { ... }   // ERROR — return type
                                                   // alone is not enough</pre><p>Overloads must differ in the <em>parameter list</em> — the number of parameters, their types, or their order. The return type is not part of the signature, so two methods differing only there cannot coexist: the compiler chooses from the arguments at the call, and the arguments would be identical.</p><p>Parameter <em>names</em> do not matter either. Selection happens at compile time from the static types of the arguments, so <code>max(1, 2)</code> picks the <code>int</code> version and <code>max(1.0, 2)</code> widens the 2 and picks the <code>double</code> one.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Overloading:</strong> multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Signature:</strong> the method name plus the number, types and order of its parameters</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Resolution:</strong> the compile-time choice of which overload a call matches</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Overloading is decided at compile time from the declared types; overriding (10.3) is decided at run time from the actual object. Confusing the two is the most common inheritance mistake in Unit 10.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Static Members and Method Overloading</strong></p><i data-d286_z6_12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what makes an overload legal</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk369a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">different parameter TYPES</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">legal</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">different parameter COUNT</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">legal</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">different parameter ORDER</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">legal (if types differ)</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">different return type only</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">NOT legal</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Java picks the overload from the arguments</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(describe(5));
System.out.println(describe(5.0));
System.out.println(describe(2, 3));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">int 5
double 5.0
two ints 5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The argument types decide which version runs.</li><li>The name is shared; the parameter list is the real identity.</li><li>This is resolved at compile time.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — An int argument will widen if it must</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(only(7));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">double version got 7.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>There is no int overload.</li><li>Java widens the int to a double, which is safe and automatic.</li><li>So the double version runs, printing 7.0.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Trying to overload on the return type alone. <code>int f(int)</code> and <code>double f(int)</code> is a compile error — the call site could not say which one it meant.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_13":{"body":"<p>A method that trusts its arguments will eventually be handed a negative length or a null String. Checking parameters at the top is the cheap version of the exception handling in Unit 12.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double divide(int a, int b) {
    if (b == 0) {
        System.out.println("Error: divide by zero");
        return 0;                    // a sentinel — the caller must check it
    }
    return (double) a / b;
}

// stricter, and usually better: refuse rather than invent a value
public static double divideStrict(int a, int b) {
    if (b == 0) { throw new IllegalArgumentException("b must not be 0"); }
    return (double) a / b;
}</pre><p>The difference between the two is who handles the problem. Returning a sentinel puts the burden on every caller and is silently ignored if they forget; throwing forces the issue at the point of the mistake. Unit 12 formalises the second approach.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double average(int[] a) {
    if (a == null)      return 0;           // guard: null
    if (a.length == 0)  return 0;           // guard: empty
    int sum = 0;
    for (int x : a) sum += x;
    return (double) sum / a.length;         // safe by here
}</pre><p>Guards go at the top and return immediately, so the body below can assume its inputs are sane. The alternative — wrapping the real work in a deep <code>if</code> — indents the important code and hides the normal path.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Answer to a bad argument</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">return a safe default</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">there is an obviously right answer, like 0 for an empty average</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">return a sentinel (<code>-1</code>, <code>null</code>)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the caller can reasonably check it</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">throw <code>IllegalArgumentException</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">continuing would be meaningless — this is Unit 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Validating in the method rather than at every call site means the rule is written once and cannot be forgotten by one caller out of six.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Precondition:</strong> something that must be true of the arguments for the method to work</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Defensive programming:</strong> validating inputs at the boundary rather than assuming them</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>IllegalArgumentException:</strong> the standard exception for an argument that violates a precondition</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is why 12.2 exists. Once you have written a few sentinel returns and watched a caller ignore one, throwing becomes obviously preferable.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_13="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — validate at the boundary</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk370a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">check first</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">before any work</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return a safe value</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">or throw</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">never trust input</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">especially user input</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">document it</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">say what is rejected</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Guarding against a bad argument</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(safeDivide(10, 2));
System.out.println(safeDivide(10, 0));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5.0
cannot divide by zero
0.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The guard runs before any arithmetic.</li><li>The bad case is reported and a safe value returned.</li><li>The good case is unaffected.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Throwing is often better than returning a fake value</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(factorial(5));
try { factorial(-1); }
catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { System.out.println("rejected: " + e.getMessage()); }</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">120
rejected: n must be &gt;= 0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Returning 0 for an invalid input would be indistinguishable from a real 0.</li><li>Throwing makes the failure impossible to ignore.</li><li>The caller decides how to handle it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Checking the argument AFTER using it. A guard placed below the first calculation has already let the division by zero or the negative index happen.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>For the performance assessment, validate at the top of the method and say in a comment what is rejected. Evaluators look for the check, not just for correct output.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_14":{"body":"<p>Passing a <code>Scanner</code> to a method works, and there is one rule: create <strong>one</strong> Scanner in <code>main</code> and pass it around. Creating a second one on <code>System.in</code> inside a method causes input to be lost.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int readValidAge(Scanner scnr) {
    int age = scnr.nextInt();
    while (age &lt; 0 || age &gt; 120) {
        System.out.println("Invalid, try again:");
        age = scnr.nextInt();
    }
    return age;
}

// in main
Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = readValidAge(scnr);</pre><p>The reason is buffering: each Scanner keeps its own buffer over the same stream, so a second one can consume data the first has already pulled in. The same argument reappears in 9.4 for file input.</p><p>Create <strong>one</strong> <code>Scanner</code> on <code>System.in</code> in <code>main</code> and pass it as a parameter to anything that needs input.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
    int age = readAge(scnr);              // pass the SAME scanner
}
static int readAge(Scanner scnr) { return scnr.nextInt(); }</pre><p>A second <code>new Scanner(System.in)</code> inside a method opens a second reader over the same stream. Scanner buffers ahead, so the first one may already hold input the second will never see — and closing either one closes <code>System.in</code> for both, after which every later read throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>.</p><p>The other Scanner trap is the newline: <code>nextInt()</code> consumes the number but leaves the line-ending behind, so an immediately following <code>nextLine()</code> returns an empty String. The fix is an extra <code>scnr.nextLine();</code> to discard it.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Scanner parameter:</strong> passing the existing Scanner so all input shares one buffer</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Buffer:</strong> the internal store of characters read ahead from the stream</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Input method:</strong> a method that encapsulates prompting, reading and validating one value</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Never call <code>new Scanner(System.in)</code> in more than one place in a program. It is the standard cause of input that mysteriously disappears.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → Operators, Strings, and Scanner</strong></p><i data-d286_z6_14="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — pass the Scanner in</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk371a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">one Scanner</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">created once in main</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">pass as a parameter</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">methods share it</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new Scanner each call</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">trouble</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt then nextLine</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">leaves the newline behind</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The newline nextInt leaves behind</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.Scanner;
Scanner sc = new Scanner("42\nAda Lovelace\n");
int id = sc.nextInt();
String leftover = sc.nextLine();
String name = sc.nextLine();
System.out.println(id);
System.out.println("leftover=[" + leftover + "]");
System.out.println("name=[" + name + "]");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">42
leftover=[]
name=[Ada Lovelace]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>nextInt</code> takes the 42 but stops before the newline.</li><li>The first <code>nextLine</code> therefore returns the EMPTY remainder of that line, not the name.</li><li>Only the second <code>nextLine</code> reaches the name.</li><li>Skip that extra <code>nextLine</code> and the name comes back empty — the commonest Scanner bug there is.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Sharing one Scanner between methods</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.Scanner;
Scanner sc = new Scanner("31 27");
System.out.println(askAge(sc));
System.out.println(askAge(sc));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">31
27</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The Scanner is created once and passed in as a parameter.</li><li>Each call continues from where the last one stopped.</li><li>Creating a new Scanner inside the method would restart or fight over the same input.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>After <code>nextInt()</code>, an extra <code>nextLine()</code> is needed to consume the rest of the line before reading text. That single line is the most common Scanner exam question.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_15":{"body":"<p>A <strong>perfect size</strong> array is exactly as long as the data it holds: <code>a.length</code> <em>is</em> the count, so nothing extra needs tracking.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[] scores = new int[n];        // n known before the array is created
for (int i = 0; i &lt; scores.length; i++) { scores[i] = scnr.nextInt(); }

// every loop bound is simply .length
for (int s : scores) { total += s; }</pre><p>It is the simpler design and the one to prefer whenever the count is known up front. The limitation is the fixed length from 5.1: if the count is not known until the data has been read, you cannot size the array in advance — which is the problem 6.16 exists to solve.</p><p>In a <strong>perfect size</strong> array, <code>a.length</code> <em>is</em> the element count, so there is nothing else to keep in step and every loop bound is simply <code>a.length</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int n = scnr.nextInt();          // count first
int[] a = new int[n];            // then the exact array
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) a[i] = scnr.nextInt();</pre><p>It requires knowing the count before creating the array, which is why the count is read first. When the input ends with a sentinel instead of a count, you cannot size it up front — and that is exactly the case oversize arrays exist for.</p><p>Prefer it whenever the count is knowable: it uses no wasted memory and removes an entire class of bug, because there is no separate size variable to fall out of step with the data.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Perfect size array:</strong> an array whose length equals the number of elements stored</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Element count:</strong> in a perfect size array, identical to length</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>In a perfect size array every loop is <code>i &lt; arr.length</code> and the enhanced for loop (5.10) is always safe. Both become wrong for an oversize array.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_15="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a perfect size array</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk372a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">length</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">exactly the number of items</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">every slot</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">holds real data</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.length</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">IS the item count</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">adding an item</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">needs a whole new array</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — length is the count, so loops are simple</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] temps = {18, 21, 19, 24};
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; temps.length; i++) total += temps[i];
System.out.println(temps.length);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", (double) total / temps.length);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4
20.50</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Every slot is real data, so <code>length</code> is the item count.</li><li>The loop runs to <code>length</code> with no separate counter.</li><li>The average divides by <code>length</code> directly.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Growing one means copying</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] bigger = new int[a.length + 1];
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) bigger[i] = a[i];
bigger[a.length] = 4;
System.out.println(bigger.length + " " + bigger[3]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4 4</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>An array's length is fixed once created.</li><li>Adding an item means allocating a new array and copying.</li><li>That cost is the reason oversize arrays exist.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming a perfect size array can grow. <code>a.length</code> is final — there is no add. Either allocate a new array, or use an oversize array with a separate count.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_16":{"body":"<p>An <strong>oversize</strong> array is deliberately longer than the data it holds, with a separate <code>int</code> tracking how many slots are actually in use. It is the standard way to handle a growing collection before <code>ArrayList</code> (7.14).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">final int CAPACITY = 100;
int[] values = new int[CAPACITY];
int numValues = 0;                    // how many are REAL

// adding
if (numValues &lt; values.length) {      // capacity check — required
    values[numValues] = newItem;
    numValues++;
}

// every loop uses numValues, NOT values.length
for (int i = 0; i &lt; numValues; i++) { System.out.println(values[i]); }</pre><p>The count and the array must travel together everywhere. Passing the array to a method without the count leaves the method unable to tell data from the zeros beyond it — which is the argument for a class that holds both (7.6).</p><h3>A note on the size variable's name</h3><p>Different sources name the count differently — <code>numValues</code>, <code>numElements</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>count</code>. They all mean the same thing: how many slots hold real data. What matters is that the loop bound is <em>that variable</em> and never <code>arr.length</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; numElements; i++) { ... }   // correct
for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)   { ... }   // reads the unused slots

arr[numElements] = value;   // append at the first free slot
numElements++;              // then advance the count</pre><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">final int CAPACITY = 100;
int[] a = new int[CAPACITY];     // room for 100
int size = 0;                    // how many are ACTUALLY used

// add
if (size &lt; CAPACITY) { a[size] = value; size++; }   // check capacity FIRST

// traverse — size, never a.length
for (int i = 0; i &lt; size; i++) System.out.println(a[i]);</pre><p>Two numbers now describe the array and they mean different things: <code>a.length</code> is <strong>capacity</strong> (how many it could hold) and <code>size</code> is <strong>count</strong> (how many it does). Looping to <code>a.length</code> walks over the unused slots and prints their defaults — zeros for numbers, <code>null</code> for objects.</p><p>Removing element <em>i</em> means shifting everything after it one place left and decrementing <code>size</code>; the vacated slot at the end still holds its old value, which is harmless precisely because nothing reads past <code>size</code>.</p><p>This is exactly how <code>ArrayList</code> works internally — an oversize array plus a size, reallocated to a bigger one when it fills.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Oversize array:</strong> an array with spare capacity plus a separate count of used elements</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Capacity:</strong> the array’s physical length</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Size / count:</strong> how many elements are actually in use</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Unused slots:</strong> the elements beyond the count, still holding default values</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Looping to <code>values.length</code> instead of <code>numValues</code> processes the unused zeros. It is the defining bug of oversize arrays, and it makes averages wrong rather than crashing.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_16="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — capacity is not size</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk373a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.length</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">CAPACITY — slots allocated</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">numElements</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">SIZE — slots in use</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">loop to numElements</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not to a.length</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">slots beyond size</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">hold stale or default data</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Two numbers, and only one of them is the count</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] scores = new int[10];
int numScores = 0;
scores[numScores++] = 88;
scores[numScores++] = 92;
scores[numScores++] = 75;
System.out.println(scores.length);
System.out.println(numScores);
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; numScores; i++) total += scores[i];
System.out.println(total);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">10
3
255</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>length</code> is 10 — the capacity allocated.</li><li><code>numScores</code> is 3 — how many are real.</li><li>The loop must run to <code>numScores</code>, never to <code>length</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Looping to length instead</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] scores = new int[10];
int numScores = 3;
scores[0] = 88; scores[1] = 92; scores[2] = 75;
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; scores.length; i++) total += scores[i];
System.out.println(total);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", (double) total / scores.length);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">255
25.50</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The seven unused slots hold 0, the default for int.</li><li>The total happens to be right, because adding 0 changes nothing.</li><li>But the AVERAGE is wrong — divided by 10 instead of 3.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>In any oversize-array question, find which variable holds the count and loop to that. <code>length</code> is the capacity and is almost never the right bound.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_17":{"body":"<p>A method taking an oversize array needs both pieces, and the count must be a parameter because there is no way to recover it from the array.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int sum(int[] a, int size) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; size; i++) { total += a[i]; }   // size, not a.length
    return total;
}

// adding an element returns the NEW count, because an int parameter
// cannot be changed for the caller (6.8)
public static int add(int[] a, int size, int value) {
    if (size &lt; a.length) { a[size] = value; size++; }
    return size;
}

numValues = add(values, numValues, 42);       // caller must reassign</pre><p>That return-the-new-count idiom exists precisely because of pass by value. The array contents change through the shared reference (6.10), but the count is an <code>int</code> and cannot be updated in place — so it comes back as the return value and the caller stores it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void print(int[] a, int size) {          // BOTH pieces
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; size; i++) System.out.println(a[i]);
}

static int add(int[] a, int size, int value) {  // returns the NEW size
    if (size &lt; a.length) { a[size] = value; size++; }
    return size;
}

size = add(a, size, 42);                        // caller must reassign</pre><p>The count must be a parameter because it cannot be recovered from the array — the unused slots hold defaults that are indistinguishable from real data.</p><p>And the new count must be <em>returned</em>, because <code>size</code> is an <code>int</code> and Java passes it by value: incrementing it inside the method changes only the copy. The array's contents do change, since the reference is shared — so forgetting <code>size = </code> at the call gives you an array that quietly grows while the count says otherwise.</p><p>Needing two things kept in step, in every signature, is the argument for wrapping them in one object — which is Unit 7, and what <code>ArrayList</code> already does.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Size parameter:</strong> the count of used elements, passed alongside the array</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Return-the-new-size:</strong> the pattern of returning the updated count because an int parameter cannot be modified</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If a question shows an <code>add</code> method whose result is not assigned, the count never advances and every call overwrites the same slot.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_17="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — pass both</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk374a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">method(int[] a)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not enough</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">method(int[] a, int size)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the working pattern</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return the new size</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">when the method adds</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ignore the returned size</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the count drifts</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The size must travel with the array</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] a = new int[8];
a[0] = 5; a[1] = 6; a[2] = 7;
int n = 3;
System.out.println(sum(a, n));
System.out.println(sum(a, a.length));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">18
18</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The method cannot know the count from the array alone.</li><li>Passing it gives the right answer.</li><li>Passing <code>length</code> instead adds the five unused zeros — right here, wrong for an average.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A method that appends returns the new size</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] a = new int[5];
int n = 0;
n = add(a, n, 10);
n = add(a, n, 20);
System.out.println(n + " " + a[0] + " " + a[1]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2 10 20</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The array is modified in place, because it is a reference.</li><li>The size is a primitive, so a change inside would NOT reach the caller.</li><li>Returning it and assigning at the call site is the standard fix.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting a method to update the caller's count variable. The count is an <code>int</code> — passed by value — so it must be returned and reassigned, exactly as in the second example.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_18":{"body":"<p>Choosing between the two array designs comes down to one question: is the number of elements known before the array is created?</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">                  PERFECT SIZE              OVERSIZE
length            equals the data           larger than the data
extra tracking    none                      a separate count
loop bound        arr.length                numValues
enhanced for      safe                      UNSAFE — reads unused slots
add elements      impossible                yes, up to capacity
wasted memory     none                      capacity minus count</pre><p>Perfect size is simpler and should be the default. Oversize buys the ability to grow, and pays for it with a second variable that has to be kept correct in every method it passes through. <code>ArrayList</code> (7.14) is the resolution: it hides the capacity and keeps the count itself.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Perfect size</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Oversize</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Element count</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>a.length</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a separate <code>size</code> variable</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Loop bound</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>a.length</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong><code>size</code></strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Adding</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">not possible — length is fixed</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">write at <code>size</code>, then <code>size++</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Memory</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">exact</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">wastes the unused capacity</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Passing to a method</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the array alone</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the array <em>and</em> the count</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Main risk</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">none of this kind</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the two falling out of step</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The deciding question is whether the count is known before the array is created. Known → perfect size, and prefer it. Not known, or the collection must grow → oversize.</p><p><code>ArrayList</code> is the oversize design with the bookkeeping hidden: it stores an array plus a size, exposes <code>size()</code>, and allocates a larger array and copies when the old one fills.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Trade-off:</strong> simplicity and safety versus the ability to grow</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Wasted capacity:</strong> allocated slots that hold no data</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This comparison is why <code>ArrayList</code> feels like a relief when you meet it. It is an oversize array that manages its own count, which removes the entire class of bugs described in 6.16.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_18="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — choosing between them</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk375a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">perfect size</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">count known up front</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">oversize</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">count grows at run time</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">perfect size loop</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">to a.length</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">oversize loop</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">to numElements</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same total, two shapes</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] perfect = {4, 9, 2};
int t1 = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; perfect.length; i++) t1 += perfect[i];

int[] over = new int[10];
over[0] = 4; over[1] = 9; over[2] = 2;
int n = 3, t2 = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) t2 += over[i];

System.out.println(t1 + " " + t2);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">15 15</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The perfect size array bounds its loop with <code>length</code>.</li><li>The oversize array bounds its loop with the count.</li><li>Both give 15 — the difference is which bound is correct.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Where they diverge</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] over = new int[10];
over[0] = 4; over[1] = 9; over[2] = 2;
int n = 3;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", (double)(4+9+2) / n);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", (double)(4+9+2) / over.length);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5.00
1.50</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Dividing by the count gives the true average.</li><li>Dividing by the capacity silently dilutes it with empty slots.</li><li>Sums hide the mistake; averages expose it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Using <code>a.length</code> on an oversize array. It is the capacity, so every average, percentage and "how many" comes out wrong while the sums still look fine.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Ask one question of any array in an exam: is every slot real data? If yes, use <code>length</code>. If not, there is a count variable and you must use that.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_19":{"body":"<p>Once objects arrive in Unit 7, the rules of 6.10 apply to them unchanged, because an object variable is a reference just as an array variable is.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void giveRaise(Employee e) {
    e.setSalary(e.getSalary() * 1.1);    // caller SEES this — same object
}

public static void replace(Employee e) {
    e = new Employee("New");             // caller does NOT see this
}</pre><p>So a method can change the state of an object it is given, but cannot make the caller’s variable refer to a different object. The one exception in the whole language is immutable types — a method cannot change a String at all (3.14), because nothing can.</p><p>An object variable holds a reference, exactly as an array variable does, so every rule from 6.10 carries over unchanged.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void rename(Student s) { s.setName("Ana"); }   // caller SEES it
static void swap(Student s)   { s = new Student("Bo"); } // caller sees nothing</pre><p>A method can change the <em>state</em> of an object it is handed, but cannot change which object the caller's variable refers to. That is the single sentence that answers most pass-by-value exam questions.</p><p>A reference may also be <code>null</code>, meaning it points at no object. Calling any method on it throws <code>NullPointerException</code> — the most common runtime failure in Java, and the reason a method that may receive <code>null</code> should say so or guard for it.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reference parameter:</strong> a parameter holding a copy of a reference to an object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Mutation:</strong> changing an object’s internal state through its methods</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Immutable object:</strong> one whose state cannot change after construction, e.g. String</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If a method is supposed to hand back a different object, it must <code>return</code> it. Reassigning the parameter is the standard non-working attempt.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Concepts Made Clear → Pass-by-value</strong></p><i data-d286fx13="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHAT A METHOD CAN AND CANNOT CHANGE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 530 148" style="width:100%; max-width:560px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk80a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="120" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">primitive parameter</text><rect x="30" y="32" width="80" height="36" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="70" y="47" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x</text><text x="70" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><line x1="112" y1="50" x2="160" y2="50" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk80a)"/><text x="136" y="42" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">copy</text><rect x="162" y="32" width="80" height="36" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="202" y="47" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">p</text><text x="202" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5 → 99</text><text x="136" y="92" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">p = 99 changes the copy.</text><text x="136" y="106" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">x is STILL 5.</text><text x="370" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">array / object parameter</text><rect x="280" y="32" width="70" height="36" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="315" y="47" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">arr</text><text x="315" y="61" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">●</text><rect x="280" y="80" width="70" height="36" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="315" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nums</text><text x="315" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">●</text><rect x="400" y="50" width="100" height="40" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="450" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the ONE array</text><text x="450" y="81" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">{7, 8, 9}</text><line x1="350" y1="50" x2="398" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk80a)"/><line x1="350" y1="98" x2="398" y2="80" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk80a)"/><text x="400" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">nums[0] = 99 changes what BOTH see.</text><text x="265" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Java always copies the variable. For primitives that copies the value; for arrays/objects it copies the ARROW.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Reassigning the parameter itself (<code>nums = new int[5]</code>) never affects the caller — you bent your copy of the arrow, not theirs. Changing what the arrow points AT (<code>nums[0] = 99</code>) does.</div><i data-d286_z6_19="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One array, two names</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] xs = {1, 2, 3};
int[] alias = xs;
bump(xs);
System.out.println(alias[0] + "," + alias[1] + "," + alias[2]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2,3,4</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>alias</code> and <code>xs</code> refer to the same array.</li><li>The method changes the elements through its own copy of the reference.</li><li>Every name pointing at that array sees the change.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Copying the reference is not copying the array</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] shallow = a;
int[] real = a.clone();
a[0] = 99;
System.out.println(shallow[0]);
System.out.println(real[0]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">99
1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>shallow = a</code> copies only the reference — one array, two names.</li><li><code>a.clone()</code> allocates a second array and copies the contents.</li><li>Changing the original moves the alias and leaves the clone alone.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing <code>int[] copy = original;</code> and expecting an independent copy. It is the same array under a second name; use <code>clone()</code> or <code>Arrays.copyOf</code> for a real copy.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_20":{"body":"<p>A method can return an array, which is the way around the "cannot reassign a parameter" limit: build a new array inside and hand back the reference.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int[] doubled(int[] a) {
    int[] result = new int[a.length];      // a NEW array
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) { result[i] = a[i] * 2; }
    return result;                          // the reference is returned
}

int[] b = doubled(a);      // a is untouched; b is the new array</pre><p>Returning a new array leaves the input untouched, which is usually what a caller expects. The alternative — modifying in place and returning <code>void</code> — is also valid, but the two must not be mixed: a method that both modifies its argument and returns an array is the kind that gets used wrongly.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int[] doubled(int[] a) {
    int[] out = new int[a.length];      // new array, input untouched
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) out[i] = a[i] * 2;
    return out;                          // the reference survives the call
}</pre><p>Returning an array is the way around "you cannot reassign a parameter": build a new one inside and hand back the reference. The local variable <code>out</code> disappears when the frame is destroyed, but the array it pointed at does not — objects live on the heap, and the returned reference keeps it alive.</p><p>For a filter, the length is not known until you have counted, so the usual shape is two passes — count the matches, allocate exactly that many, then fill. The alternative is an oversize array plus <code>Arrays.copyOf(out, n)</code> at the end.</p><p>Return an empty array rather than <code>null</code> for "no results". An empty array loops zero times and needs no special case; <code>null</code> forces every caller to remember a check.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Array return type:</strong> declared as Type[]; returns a reference to an array</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>New array vs in-place:</strong> building a fresh result versus modifying the argument</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Local array lifetime:</strong> an array created in a method survives the return, because the reference is returned; only the local variable disappears</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>The returned array survives the method’s stack frame because arrays live on the heap (8.3). Only the local variable naming it disappears when the frame is popped.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_20="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — returning an array</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk376a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int[] name(...)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the return type is int[]</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return a;</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns the REFERENCE</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a local array</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">survives — the object outlives the frame</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">caller assigns it</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">int[] r = name(...)</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Building and returning a new array</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] in = {1, 2, 3};
int[] result = doubled(in);
System.out.println(result[0] + "," + result[1] + "," + result[2]);
System.out.println(in[0] + "," + in[1] + "," + in[2]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2,4,6
1,2,3</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A fresh array is allocated inside the method.</li><li>Returning it hands back the reference — the object is not destroyed with the frame.</li><li>The input is untouched, because a new array was built rather than edited.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Returning the argument instead of a copy</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] in = {1, 2, 3};
int[] out = doubleInPlace(in);
out[0] = 100;
System.out.println(in[0] + "," + in[1] + "," + in[2]);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">100,4,6</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>This version edits the caller's array and returns the same reference.</li><li>So <code>in</code> and <code>out</code> are one array.</li><li>Changing <code>out</code> changes <code>in</code> — rarely what the caller expects.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Thinking an array created inside a method is destroyed when it returns. The local VARIABLE goes away; the array object survives as long as something still references it.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Decide deliberately whether a method should return a NEW array or modify the one it was given, and make the name say which — <code>doubled</code> against <code>doubleInPlace</code>.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_21":{"body":"<p>Combining methods and arrays produces its own error set, and three of the four are runtime failures rather than compile errors.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">sum(values);                 // 1. forgot the size for an oversize array
                             //    -&gt; processes unused zeros

int[] a = null;
sum(a);                      // 2. NullPointerException on a.length

sum(values, numValues + 1);  // 3. size larger than the real count
                             //    -&gt; ArrayIndexOutOfBounds or garbage

public static int[] f() {
    int[] r = new int[3];
    return;                  // 4. COMPILE ERROR — must return the array
}</pre><p>Null is the one worth guarding: an array parameter can legitimately be <code>null</code>, which is a different state from an array of length 0. <code>if (a == null || a.length == 0)</code> covers both, and the order matters because of short-circuiting (3.19).</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Mistake</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>b = a</code> expecting a copy</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">both names share one array — silent</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>a == b</code> to compare contents</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compares references — silent, usually false</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">using a declared-but-not-created array</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>NullPointerException</code> at run time</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>a.length()</code> on an array</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compile error — arrays use <code>length</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">bound <code>i &lt;= a.length</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">looping to <code>a.length</code> on an oversize array</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">reads unused slots — silent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The two silent ones are the dangerous pair, and they share a cause: an array variable is a reference, so both assignment and <code>==</code> operate on the reference rather than the contents. Use <code>Arrays.copyOf</code> to copy and <code>Arrays.equals</code> to compare.</p><p>An array of objects is doubly null-prone: <code>new Student[5]</code> creates five <code>null</code>s, so the array exists but every element must still be constructed before it can be used.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>NullPointerException:</strong> thrown when a method or field is used on a null reference</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Empty vs null array:</strong> length 0 is a real array with no elements; null is no array at all</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bounds violation:</strong> passing a size larger than the data actually present</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>if (a.length == 0 || a == null)</code> is backwards — the first test throws on a null array before the second is ever reached. Check for null first, always.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_21="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the four that appear most</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk377a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[a.length]</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one past the end</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">loop to length</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">on an oversize array</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">copy = original</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">an alias, not a copy</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">changing a primitive param</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">never reaches the caller</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The off-by-one at the end</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
System.out.println(a[a.length - 1]);
try {
    System.out.println(a[a.length]);
} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("out of bounds: " + e.getMessage());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3
out of bounds: Index 3 out of bounds for length 3</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Indexes run 0 to <code>length - 1</code>.</li><li>So the last element is at <code>length - 1</code>.</li><li>Index <code>length</code> throws at run time, not at compile time.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — An empty array breaks the obvious average</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(avg(new int[]{2, 4}));
System.out.println(avg(new int[0]));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3.0
NaN</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The normal case works.</li><li>An empty array divides 0 by 0, which for doubles gives NaN rather than an exception.</li><li>A guard for <code>a.length == 0</code> is what the marker looks for.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>When an array question offers an answer that is one bigger or one smaller than yours, re-check the loop bound and the last index before changing anything else.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_22":{"body":"<p>Javadoc turns a structured comment into browsable documentation, and it is how every method in the standard library you have used since Unit 1 is described.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">/**
 * Returns the average of three test scores.
 *
 * @param a the first score
 * @param b the second score
 * @param c the third score
 * @return  the arithmetic mean as a double
 */
public static double average(int a, int b, int c) {
    return (a + b + c) / 3.0;
}</pre><p>The tags are the point: <code>@param</code> once per parameter, <code>@return</code> for the result, <code>@throws</code> for each exception it can raise. The habit worth taking from this is the same one 2.19 asked for — read the contract before calling something, and write the contract before someone calls yours.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">/**
 * Returns the average of the values.
 * @param a the values; must not be null
 * @return the mean, or 0 if the array is empty
 */
public static double average(int[] a) { ... }</pre><p>A Javadoc comment starts <code>/**</code> and sits immediately above what it documents. <code>@param</code> describes each argument and <code>@return</code> the result; together they state the method's <em>contract</em> — what it expects and what it guarantees — which is where the guards of 6.13 come from.</p><p>Read the official API the same way: the signature tells you the types, the description tells you the contract, and the "Throws" section tells you what it does with bad input. <code>String.substring(int, int)</code> is the standard example — the second index is <em>exclusive</em>, and only the documentation says so.</p><i data-d286u6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Javadoc:</strong> a /** */ comment processed into API documentation</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>@param:</strong> documents one parameter</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>@return:</strong> documents the returned value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>@throws:</strong> documents an exception the method may throw</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>A Javadoc comment must sit immediately above the method with no blank line between. Placed anywhere else it is just an ordinary comment.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_22="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — reading a Javadoc entry</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk378a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public static int max(int a, int b)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start"></text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">static</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">call on the CLASS</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what you get back</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(int a, int b)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what you must supply</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@param / @return</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the contract in words</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Writing a Javadoc comment</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(larger(3, 9));
System.out.println(larger(-2, -7));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">9
-2</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>/** ... */</code> is a documentation comment, not an ordinary one.</li><li><code>@param</code> describes each input and <code>@return</code> the output.</li><li>Tools and IDEs read these to show help at the call site.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Using the signature to predict the type</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Math.max(3, 7));
System.out.println(Math.max(3.0, 7));
System.out.println(String.valueOf(42).length());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">7
7.0
2</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Two ints select the int overload, so it prints 7.</li><li>One double selects the double overload, so it prints 7.0.</li><li><code>valueOf</code> is static, hence called on the class.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming a documented method modifies its argument. If the docs give a return type other than <code>void</code>, it is handing you a result and leaving the input alone.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_23":{"body":"<p>The salary program returns for the third time, and the difference now is structural: the calculation that was inline in 3.20 and looped in 4.12 becomes two named methods that can be tested on their own.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double taxRate(double salary) {
    if (salary >= 100000) { return 0.28; }
    if (salary >=  50000) { return 0.22; }
    if (salary >=  25000) { return 0.15; }
    return 0.10;
}

public static double netPay(double salary) {
    return salary * (1 - taxRate(salary));
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println(taxRate(60000) == 0.22);      // testable (6.7)
    System.out.printf("%.2f%n", netPay(60000));
}</pre><p>Note the guard-clause style from 6.5: each <code>return</code> ends the method, so no <code>else</code> is needed and the ordering rule of 3.4 still applies unchanged — highest bracket first.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Extracted method:</strong> logic pulled out of a larger routine into its own named method</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Composition:</strong> one method calling another to build a larger result</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is the shape of every well-organised program you will write: <code>main</code> reads input and prints, and the real work happens in small methods that take values and return values.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_23="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — one method per idea</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk379a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">monthly(annual)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one calculation</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">withRaise(salary, pct)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">another</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">main</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reads as a summary</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">format at output</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not inside the maths</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Splitting the salary program into methods</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double annual = 55000;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", monthly(annual));
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", withRaise(annual, 4));
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", monthly(withRaise(annual, 4)));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4583.33
57200.00
4766.67</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each method holds one idea and returns a number.</li><li>Because they return rather than print, they compose.</li><li>The last line reads as "the monthly value of the raised salary".</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Keeping the division honest</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(monthlyBad(55000));
System.out.println(monthlyGood(55000));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4583.0
4583.333333333333</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both parameters are int, so the first divides as integers.</li><li>The <code>double</code> return type cannot recover the lost remainder.</li><li>Dividing by 12.0 keeps the pennies.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Printing inside a calculation method. It cannot then be reused in further arithmetic, which is exactly what the third line of the first example needs.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Compute in one place, format in another. Any method whose name is a noun should return a value, not print one.</div>"},"D286/u6/z6_24":{"body":"<p>Domain validation, done for the third time, becomes a set of small predicates — and the improvement is that each rule can now be tested in isolation.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static boolean hasValidChars(String d) {
    for (int i = 0; i < d.length(); i++) {
        char c = d.charAt(i);
        if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(c) && c != '.' && c != '-') { return false; }
    }
    return true;                        // only if the loop finished
}

public static boolean isValidDomain(String d) {
    return d != null
        && d.length() >= 3
        && d.contains(".")
        && hasValidChars(d);            // short-circuits (3.19) in order
}</pre><p>The <code>return false</code> inside the loop replaces the flag of 4.13 entirely — it is an early exit that cannot be accidentally overwritten. And the null check leads the chain deliberately, so the later calls are safe.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Predicate composition:</strong> building a complex test from small boolean methods joined by &amp;&amp;</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Return-false-early:</strong> exiting the moment a rule is broken, instead of tracking a flag</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Ordering the conditions in <code>isValidDomain</code> is not cosmetic. <code>d != null</code> must come first or every other test can throw, and the cheap checks before the loop avoid scanning strings that were already disqualified.</p></div><i data-d286_z6_24="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — validation returns a boolean</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk380a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isValid(name)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns true / false</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the caller</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">decides the message</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">several small checks</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">combined with &amp;&amp;</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">short-circuit</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">protects later checks</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A validator built from small checks</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(isValidDomain("wgu.edu"));
System.out.println(isValidDomain("nodot"));
System.out.println(isValidDomain(".leading.com"));
System.out.println(isValidDomain(""));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false
false
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each rule is one early return, so the method reads as a checklist.</li><li>The null and empty test comes FIRST, before any method is called on d.</li><li>The caller receives a boolean and decides what to say about it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why the order of the checks matters</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(safeCheck("wgu.edu"));
System.out.println(safeCheck(null));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>d != null</code> is tested first.</li><li>Because && short-circuits, <code>d.length()</code> never runs when d is null.</li><li>Reversing the two would throw a NullPointerException.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Calling a method on the string before checking it for null. <code>d.isEmpty() || d == null</code> throws on null — the null test has to come first, and && short-circuiting is what makes that work.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_1":{"body":"<p>Unit 5 left an unsolved problem: parallel arrays keep a student’s name and score in two places, and nothing stops them drifting apart. An <strong>object</strong> is the fix — one thing that holds its own data and the methods that operate on it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// before: two arrays that must be kept in step
String[] names;  int[] scores;

// after: one object per student, name and score inseparable
Student s = new Student("Ada", 95);
s.getName();  s.getScore();</pre><p>A <strong>class</strong> is the blueprint; an <strong>object</strong> is one thing built from it. The class is written once and describes what every instance will have; each object gets its own copy of the fields, so a thousand Students have a thousand names and one class definition.</p><h3>The four pillars, named</h3><p>Object-oriented programming is conventionally summarised as four principles, and questions ask you to name the one a scenario describes.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Pillar</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Means</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">In this course</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Encapsulation</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">data kept private, reached only through methods</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">7.3, 7.4</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Abstraction</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">expose what it does, hide how</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">7.4, and abstract classes in 10.7</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Inheritance</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a class takes on another's fields and methods</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">10.1</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Polymorphism</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one reference type, many actual behaviours</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">10.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Encapsulation and abstraction are the pair most often confused, because both involve hiding. Encapsulation hides <em>data</em> so the rules can be enforced in one place; abstraction hides <em>complexity</em> so a caller need not understand the internals. A locked bonnet is encapsulation; the steering wheel is abstraction.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><p>A <strong>class</strong> is the description; an <strong>object</strong> is a thing built from it. One class, any number of objects, each with its own copy of the fields — which is the entire reason parallel arrays (5.5) stop being necessary.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Student a = new Student("Ana", 88);
Student b = new Student("Ben", 71);
// one Student class, two objects, two separate name/score pairs</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Instance member</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Static member</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">belongs to</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">each object</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the class itself</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one copy</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">per object</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">shared by all objects</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">called on</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>a.getName()</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Math.max(...)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">can use <code>this</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">yes</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>no</strong> — there is no object</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><code>this</code> is a reference to the object the method was called on. A <code>static</code> method is not called on an object, so <code>this</code> has nothing to refer to and using it is a compile error — which is also why a static method cannot read an instance field directly.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Class:</strong> a blueprint describing the fields and methods its objects will have</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Object / instance:</strong> one concrete thing created from a class</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Field / instance variable:</strong> a variable belonging to each object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Encapsulation:</strong> keeping data private and exposing it only through methods</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Instantiation:</strong> creating an object with new</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Everything after this unit assumes objects. Inheritance (Unit 10), exceptions (Unit 12) and every collection in Unit 14 are classes; even a String has been an object since Unit 1.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a class is the blueprint, an object is the thing</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk381a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">class Part</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">written once — the design</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new Part()</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">builds one object</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fields</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the data each object holds</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">methods</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what each object can do</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">many objects</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one class, independent state</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One class, several independent objects</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Counter a = new Counter();
Counter b = new Counter();
a.bump(); a.bump(); b.bump();
System.out.println(a.count + " " + b.count);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2 1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>new</code> builds a separate object each time.</li><li>Each object carries its own copy of the fields.</li><li>Changing one leaves the other alone — that separation is the point of objects.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Data and the operations on it, together</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Rect r = new Rect(3, 4);
System.out.println(r.area());
r.w = 10;
System.out.println(r.area());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">12.0
40.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The fields hold the state; the method computes from it.</li><li>You never pass the width and height to <code>area()</code> — the object already knows them.</li><li>Change the state and the same call gives a new answer.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Treating a class as if it were an object. <code>Counter.count</code> does not exist — <code>count</code> belongs to each instance, so it is reached through a variable such as <code>a.count</code>.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Class = the blueprint, object = one thing built from it, instance = another word for object. If a question uses all three terms, they are testing exactly that distinction.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_2":{"body":"<p>You have been using classes since Unit 1 without naming it. <code>Scanner</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Math</code> and <code>Random</code> are all classes, and using one always follows the same three-step shape.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import java.util.Scanner;                 // 1. make the name available

Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);    // 2. create an instance
int n = scnr.nextInt();                   // 3. call methods on it</pre><p><code>Math</code> breaks the pattern deliberately, and the reason is worth noticing: its methods are <code>static</code>, so they belong to the class rather than to any instance. <code>Math.sqrt(9)</code> needs no object, and in fact you cannot create a <code>Math</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Math.sqrt(16)      // static — called on the CLASS
scnr.nextInt()     // instance — called on an OBJECT</pre><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
//  |         |       |
//  |         |       +-- constructor call: builds the object
//  |         +---------- the variable name
//  +-------------------- the reference type

scnr.nextInt();          // instance method — needs an object
Math.max(3, 7);          // static method — called on the CLASS</pre><p><code>new</code> does two things: it allocates the object and runs the constructor. The variable does not hold the object, it holds a <em>reference</em> to it — so <code>Scanner s2 = scnr;</code> gives a second name for one Scanner, not a second Scanner.</p><p>Classes outside <code>java.lang</code> need an <code>import</code> — <code>java.util.Scanner</code>, <code>java.util.ArrayList</code>. <code>String</code>, <code>Math</code> and <code>System</code> are in <code>java.lang</code>, which is imported automatically, which is why they never need one.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>import:</strong> makes a class from another package usable by its short name</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>new:</strong> allocates an object and runs its constructor</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Instance method:</strong> called on an object; can see that object’s fields</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Static method:</strong> called on the class; has no object and cannot see instance fields (7.19)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Classes in <code>java.lang</code> — String, Math, System, Integer — need no import. Everything in <code>java.util</code> does. That is why <code>Scanner</code> needs a line at the top and <code>String</code> does not.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the three steps of using an object</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk382a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">declare</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Scanner sc;</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">instantiate</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">new Scanner(...)</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">call</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sc.nextInt()</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the dot</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reaches into THIS object</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Declare, instantiate, then call</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = new String("hello");
System.out.println(s.length());
System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("ab");
sb.append("cd");
System.out.println(sb.toString());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5
HELLO
abcd</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The variable holds a REFERENCE to the object.</li><li>The dot calls a method on that particular object.</li><li><code>StringBuilder</code> is mutable, so <code>append</code> changes it in place.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Calling a method on nothing</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = null;
try {
    System.out.println(s.length());
} catch (NullPointerException e) {
    System.out.println("NullPointerException — no object to call on");
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">NullPointerException — no object to call on</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>null</code> means the variable references no object at all.</li><li>There is nothing to run <code>length()</code> on.</li><li>This is a run-time error, not a compile error.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Declaring a reference and forgetting to instantiate it. <code>Scanner sc;</code> alone gives you a name pointing at nothing, and the first method call throws a NullPointerException.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_3":{"body":"<p>Defining a class means listing what each object holds and what it can do. Fields go at the top, methods below, and the fields are <code>private</code> by default choice rather than by language rule.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private String name;          // FIELDS — one copy per object
    private int score;

    public Student(String n, int s) {      // CONSTRUCTOR — same name, no return type
        name = n;
        score = s;
    }

    public String getName()  { return name; }        // ACCESSOR
    public void setScore(int s) { score = s; }       // MUTATOR
}</pre><p>Fields are private so that outside code cannot reach in and set a score to -400. Everything that changes the object goes through a method, and that method is where the rule lives. That is encapsulation, and it is the whole argument of 7.4.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {          // file MUST be Student.java
    private String name;        // field — one per object
    private int score;          // private: reachable only inside this class

    public Student(String name, int score) {   // constructor
        this.name = name;
        this.score = score;
    }
    public String getName() { return name; }   // instance method
}</pre><p>Fields declared but not assigned get the same defaults as array elements — <code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code> — unlike local variables, which must be assigned before use. That difference catches people: a field silently holds <code>null</code>, a local refuses to compile.</p><p>A public class must live in a file of exactly its own name, and only one class per file may be public. A class needs no <code>main</code> — <code>Student</code> is a type to be used by other code, not a program to run.</p><p>Convention: class names are <code>PascalCase</code> nouns; fields and methods are <code>camelCase</code>.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>public:</strong> accessible from anywhere</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>private:</strong> accessible only from inside this class</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Constructor:</strong> a special method with the class name and no return type, run by new</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Instance variable:</strong> a field; each object has its own, and it gets a type default (2.2)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A constructor has <strong>no return type</strong>, not even <code>void</code>. Writing <code>public void Student(...)</code> makes it an ordinary method that happens to share the class name, so <code>new Student(...)</code> stops finding it — and the error is confusing.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what goes inside a class</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk383a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fields</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the data — usually private</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">constructor</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sets up a new object</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">methods</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the behaviour — usually public</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">private</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reachable only inside the class</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">part of the interface</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A complete small class</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Student s = new Student("Priya", 15);
System.out.println(s.getName());
System.out.println(s.isFullTime());
Student p = new Student("Sam", 6);
System.out.println(p.isFullTime());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Priya
true
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Fields are private, so nothing outside can set them directly.</li><li>The constructor fills them once, at creation.</li><li>The public methods are the only way in — that is encapsulation.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Fields get default values if the constructor does not set them</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Box b = new Box();
System.out.println(b.n);
System.out.println(b.label);
System.out.println(b.ready);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0
null
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Unlike local variables, FIELDS are initialised automatically.</li><li>Numbers default to 0, booleans to false, references to null.</li><li>That null default is where many NullPointerExceptions begin.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>The standard shape is private fields plus public methods. If a question offers a class with public fields, that is usually the wrong answer — it breaks encapsulation.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_4":{"body":"<p>Three kinds of method make up the public face of a class, and naming them the way the exam does saves confusion later.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// ACCESSOR (getter) — reports state, changes nothing
public int getScore() { return score; }

// MUTATOR (setter) — changes state, usually returns void
public void setScore(int s) {
    if (s &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; s &lt;= 100) { score = s; }     // the RULE lives here
}

// PRIVATE HELPER — internal, not part of the public interface
private boolean isValid(int s) { return s &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; s &lt;= 100; }</pre><p>The validation inside the setter is the point of the whole design. Make the field public and there is no place to put that check; make it private and every change in the program’s lifetime passes through this one line.</p><p><strong>Encapsulation</strong> is fields <code>private</code>, access through methods. The reason is not secrecy — it is that a rule enforced in one setter cannot be bypassed.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">private int score;

public int getScore() { return score; }            // accessor / getter
public void setScore(int s) {                      // mutator / setter
    if (s &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; s &lt;= 100) score = s;             // the rule lives HERE
}</pre><p>With <code>score</code> public, any line anywhere can write <code>s.score = -5;</code> and the object is invalid with nothing to blame. With it private, there is exactly one door and the check is on it.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Modifier</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Visible to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>public</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">everywhere</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>protected</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the same package, and subclasses anywhere</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><em>(none)</em> — package-private</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the same package only</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>private</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>the same class only</strong> — not even subclasses</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A <em>private helper</em> is a method the class uses internally and no caller should see — it can be changed or deleted freely, because nothing outside can depend on it.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Accessor / getter:</strong> returns a field value without modifying state</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Mutator / setter:</strong> modifies a field, usually after validating</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Private helper:</strong> a method used internally and not exposed to callers</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Interface (of a class):</strong> the set of public methods — what callers may rely on</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Making every field public and dropping the getters looks simpler and removes the only place a rule can be enforced. This is the difference between a class and a bag of variables.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Encapsulation and Access Modifiers</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — three kinds of method</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk384a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">accessor (getter)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns state, changes nothing</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">mutator (setter)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">changes state, returns void</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">private helper</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">internal, not part of the interface</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">validation</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">belongs in the mutator</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A getter, a setter, and validation between them</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Thermostat t = new Thermostat();
t.setTarget(21);
System.out.println(t.getTarget());
t.setTarget(99);
System.out.println(t.getTarget());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">21
rejected 99
21</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The getter simply reports the state.</li><li>The setter is where the rule lives, so no caller can bypass it.</li><li>The rejected value leaves the object unchanged.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A private helper the outside never sees</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Invoice i = new Invoice(100);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", i.total());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">120.00</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>vat()</code> is an implementation detail, so it is private.</li><li><code>total()</code> is the public interface.</li><li>Calling <code>i.vat()</code> from outside would not compile.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Making fields public "to save writing getters". Anything can then assign an invalid value directly, and the validation in the setter is bypassed entirely.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Accessor returns and does not change; mutator changes and returns void. If a question asks which method is the mutator, look for the one with a <code>void</code> return type and a parameter.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_5":{"body":"<p>A <strong>constructor</strong> runs once, at <code>new</code>, and its job is to leave the object in a valid state. If you write none, Java supplies a no-argument default that leaves every field at its type default (2.2) — and the moment you write any constructor, that free one disappears.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private String name;
    private int score;

    public Student() {                    // no-arg
        name = "unknown";
        score = 0;
    }

    public Student(String n, int s) {     // parameterised
        name = n;
        setScore(s);                      // reuse the validation from the setter
    }
}

Student a = new Student();                // needs the no-arg constructor
Student b = new Student("Ada", 95);</pre><p>Calling <code>setScore</code> from the constructor rather than assigning the field directly means the validation rule exists in exactly one place. That habit is worth adopting as a default.</p><p>A constructor runs once, when <code>new</code> builds the object, and its job is to leave the object in a valid state. It has the class's exact name and <strong>no return type</strong> — not even <code>void</code>. Writing <code>void Student(...)</code> makes it an ordinary method that never runs at construction, which is a favourite exam trap.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private String name;
    public Student() { this("unknown"); }        // must be the FIRST statement
    public Student(String name) { this.name = name; }
}</pre><p>If you write <strong>no</strong> constructor, Java supplies a no-argument default that leaves fields at their defaults. The moment you write <em>any</em> constructor, that free one disappears — so adding <code>Student(String)</code> makes <code>new Student()</code> stop compiling until you write it yourself.</p><p><code>this(...)</code> calls another constructor of the same class and must be the first statement, so the object is fully set up once rather than in overlapping halves.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Default constructor:</strong> the no-argument constructor Java provides only when you define none</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Parameterised constructor:</strong> one taking arguments to initialise fields</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Initialisation:</strong> putting the object into a usable state before anyone can call its methods</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Defining only <code>Student(String, int)</code> and then calling <code>new Student()</code> is a compile error — the free default is gone. If you need both, write both (7.9).</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what a constructor is</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk385a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">same name as the class</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">exactly</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">no return type</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not even void</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">runs on new</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">once per object</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">no constructor written</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Java supplies a no-arg default</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">any constructor written</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the default disappears</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The constructor runs once, at creation</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Account a = new Account("Ada", 250.0);
System.out.println(a.getBalance());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">created for Ada
250.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The constructor is called by <code>new</code>, not by you.</li><li>It has no return type — writing <code>void</code> would make it an ordinary method.</li><li>By the time <code>new</code> finishes, the object is fully set up.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Writing a constructor removes the free one</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Widget w = new Widget(5);
System.out.println(w.size);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java supplies a no-argument constructor only when you write none.</li><li>This class defines one that takes an int, so the free one is gone.</li><li><code>new Widget()</code> would now be a compile error.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>A "constructor" with a return type is not a constructor — it is a method that merely shares the class's name, and <code>new</code> will not call it. That trick appears in exam code.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_6":{"body":"<p>Deciding what should be a class is design rather than syntax, and the reliable test is a grammatical one: nouns in the problem description tend to be classes, verbs tend to be methods, and adjectives tend to be fields.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">"A library lends books to members. Each book has a title and an ISBN.
 A member can borrow up to five books."

nouns      -&gt; Library, Book, Member          (classes)
adjectives -&gt; title, isbn, borrowedCount     (fields)
verbs      -&gt; lend(), borrow(), returnBook() (methods)</pre><p>The signal to watch for is the one from 5.5: whenever two arrays must be kept in step, or a value must always travel with a count (6.16), that pairing is a class waiting to be written. A class is exactly a set of things that belong together.</p><p>Two questions identify a class worth creating: <em>which values always travel together</em>, and <em>what does the program do to them</em>. The first gives the fields, the second gives the methods.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Sign</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">What it suggests</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">parallel arrays kept in step by hand</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">those items are one class</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the same three arguments passed to many methods</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">they belong in one object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a class with data and no behaviour</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">fine as a record, but look for the logic acting on it</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a class that would need "and" to describe</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">split it — one job each</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A noun in the problem statement is a candidate class; a verb is a candidate method. <code>Student</code>, <code>Domain</code>, <code>Employee</code> are classes; <code>calculatePay</code>, <code>isAvailable</code>, <code>register</code> are their methods.</p><p>Not everything deserves a class. A single value with no rules attached is a variable, and a method used once by one caller is a method — creating a class for either adds ceremony without buying anything.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Class identification:</strong> finding the entities in a problem that own their own data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Responsibility:</strong> what one class is accountable for; a class should have one</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Has-a relationship:</strong> a class holding another class as a field — see 10.9</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If a class has one field and one method, it is probably not a class. If it has fifteen fields covering three unrelated ideas, it is probably three classes.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what deserves a class</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk386a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a noun in the problem</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Part, Product, Invoice</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">data + behaviour together</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the test</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">one responsibility each</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not a god class</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a verb</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">usually a METHOD, not a class</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Splitting one blob into two classes</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Product bike = new Product("Bike");
bike.parts.add(new Part("Wheel", 40));
bike.parts.add(new Part("Frame", 120));
System.out.println(bike.parts.size());
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", bike.cost());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2
160.00</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each noun in the problem became a class.</li><li>Each class holds the data that belongs to it.</li><li>The cost calculation lives with Product, because that is whose question it answers.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One responsibility per class</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Temperature t = new Temperature(-4);
System.out.println(t.toFahrenheit());
System.out.println(t.isFreezing());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">24.8
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Everything here is about one temperature.</li><li>Printing, saving to a file or reading input would be someone else's job.</li><li>A class that does one thing is the one you can test.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Creating a class for a verb — a <code>Calculator</code> that only holds methods and no state is usually a sign the behaviour belongs on the data it operates on.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_7":{"body":"<p><code>main</code> is an ordinary static method that the JVM happens to call first, and it can live in any class — including the class it is testing. That is convenient during development and is why the pattern appears throughout this unit.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private String name;
    public Student(String n) { name = n; }
    public String getName() { return name; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {       // test driver
        Student s = new Student("Ada");
        System.out.println(s.getName().equals("Ada"));
    }
}</pre><p>Because <code>main</code> is <code>static</code>, it has no object, so it cannot touch <code>name</code> directly — it must create a <code>Student</code> first. That restriction is not arbitrary: a static method exists before any object does, so there is no <code>this</code> for it to read from (7.19).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Calc {
    public static int square(int n) { return n * n; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(square(4));       // static -&gt; static: fine
        Calc c = new Calc();
        System.out.println(c.hashCode());    // instance member needs an object
    }
}</pre><p><code>main</code> is <code>static</code> because it must run before any object exists — the JVM has nothing to call it on. Everything <code>main</code> touches directly must therefore also be static, or reached through an object it creates first.</p><p>"Non-static method cannot be referenced from a static context" is that rule being enforced: the method needs an object, and <code>main</code> has not made one. From another class, a static method is called on the class — <code>Calc.square(4)</code>.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Test driver:</strong> a main method written to exercise a class</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>static context:</strong> code with no associated object, so instance fields are unreachable</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>"Non-static variable cannot be referenced from a static context" always means the same thing: you used an instance field inside <code>main</code> without creating an object. Create the object, or make the field static.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the signature that starts a program</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk387a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the JVM must reach it</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">static</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">runs with no object</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">void</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">returns nothing</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">main</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the exact name</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">String[] args</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the command-line arguments</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why main must be static</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Greeter g = new Greeter();
g.hello();</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">hello</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>main</code> runs before any object exists.</li><li>A non-static method needs an object to be called on, so main could never start.</li><li>That is why main creates the first objects itself, as here.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reading the command-line arguments</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] args = {"alpha", "beta"};
System.out.println(args.length);
for (String a : args) System.out.println(a);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2
alpha
beta</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>args</code> is an ordinary array of Strings.</li><li>Its length is the number of arguments supplied.</li><li>Running with none gives an array of length 0, not null.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Any change to the signature — dropping <code>static</code>, renaming it <code>Main</code>, returning <code>int</code> — compiles fine and then fails at launch with "main method not found".</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_8":{"body":"<p>Testing a class means testing it through its public methods — create an object, drive it into a state, check the state came out right. The edge cases from 6.7 apply unchanged, plus one new category: <em>invalid</em> input that the setter is supposed to reject.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Student s = new Student("Ada", 95);

System.out.println(s.getScore() == 95);      // constructor worked

s.setScore(80);
System.out.println(s.getScore() == 80);      // setter worked

s.setScore(-5);
System.out.println(s.getScore() == 80);      // rejected, state UNCHANGED</pre><p>That last assertion is the one people forget to write, and it is the only one that actually tests the validation. Checking that a bad value was refused matters more than checking that a good value was accepted.</p><p>Testing a class means building an object, calling methods in a realistic order, and checking the resulting <em>state</em> through its accessors — not just single return values.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Account a = new Account(100);
a.withdraw(30);
// expect getBalance() == 70

Account b = new Account(100);
b.withdraw(500);                 // more than the balance
// expect getBalance() == 100 and the withdrawal refused</pre><p>Each test builds a <strong>fresh object</strong>. Reusing one across tests means test three depends on what tests one and two did, so a failure tells you nothing about which is broken.</p><p>The valuable tests are the ones that attack the rules the setters enforce: the overdraft, the negative score, the empty name. The constructor deserves its own test — build an object and immediately check every field came out as intended.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>State-based testing:</strong> setting state through methods and asserting on what the accessors report</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Invalid input test:</strong> confirming a bad value is rejected and the object is left unchanged</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Object state:</strong> the current values of all an object’s fields</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Test the constructor as carefully as the setters. If a constructor assigns fields directly and skips validation, an invalid object can be created that no setter would ever allow.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — testing an object</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk388a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">construct</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a known starting state</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">act</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">call one method</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">assert</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compare to the expected value</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">test the boundary</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0, empty, the limit</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Arrange, act, assert</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Cart c = new Cart();
System.out.println(c.size() == 0);
c.add(3);
System.out.println(c.size() == 3);
c.add(-5);
System.out.println(c.size() == 3);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Construct the object into a known state.</li><li>Call exactly one method.</li><li>Compare the result against what was specified — including that a bad input changes nothing.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A test that catches a real defect</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">BadCart c = new BadCart();
c.add(3);
c.add(4);
System.out.println(c.size() == 7);
System.out.println(c.size());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">false
4</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>add</code> assigns instead of accumulating.</li><li>One call would have passed; two reveal it.</li><li>The test prints false, which is the bug being caught.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Testing with a single call. Many state bugs — assigning instead of adding, resetting a counter — only appear on the second call.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_9":{"body":"<p>Constructor overloading is method overloading (6.12) applied to constructors: several ways to build an object, distinguished by their parameter lists.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private String name;
    private int score;

    public Student()                    { this("unknown", 0); }
    public Student(String n)            { this(n, 0); }
    public Student(String n, int s)     { name = n; setScore(s); }
    //                                     ^ the ONE that does the real work
}</pre><p><code>this(...)</code> calls another constructor of the same class, and it lets every version funnel into one. It must be the <strong>first statement</strong> in the constructor — that is a hard rule, because the object cannot be half-initialised by two constructors at once.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Rect {
    private int w, h;
    public Rect()               { this(1, 1); }        // delegates
    public Rect(int side)       { this(side, side); }  // delegates
    public Rect(int w, int h) {                        // the real one
        this.w = w; this.h = h;
    }
}</pre><p>Overloaded constructors differ in their parameter lists, exactly like overloaded methods. Chaining them with <code>this(...)</code> puts the validation and assignment in one place — add a check to the two-argument version and all three benefit.</p><p><code>this(...)</code> must be the very first statement, so you cannot validate before delegating. And a constructor has no return type at all, so <code>public void Rect()</code> is not a constructor — it is a method that happens to share the class's name and will never run at construction.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Constructor overloading:</strong> multiple constructors distinguished by parameter list</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>this(...):</strong> calls another constructor of the same class; must be the first statement</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Telescoping constructors:</strong> a chain of constructors each supplying more defaults to the next</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>this(...)</code> chains to a constructor in the same class; <code>super(...)</code> chains to the parent’s (10.1). Both must be first, so a constructor can use one or the other, never both.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — several ways to build one object</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk389a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Pizza()</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sensible defaults</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Pizza(size)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one detail given</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Pizza(size, topping)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fully specified</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">this(...)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one constructor calls another</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Three constructors, one class</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(new Pizza());
System.out.println(new Pizza("large"));
System.out.println(new Pizza("small", "olive"));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">medium/cheese
large/cheese
small/olive</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The argument list decides which constructor runs.</li><li><code>this(...)</code> chains to another constructor so the real work is written once.</li><li>A chained <code>this(...)</code> call must be the FIRST statement.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The default disappears once you write one</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Ticket t = new Ticket(12);
System.out.println(t.row);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">12</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java gives a free no-argument constructor only when a class defines none.</li><li>This class defines one, so the free one is gone.</li><li>Add <code>Ticket() { }</code> explicitly if you still want it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting <code>new Thing()</code> to work after adding a constructor that takes parameters. The compiler-supplied no-argument constructor vanishes the moment you write any constructor of your own.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_10":{"body":"<p>An object variable does not hold the object. It holds a <strong>reference</strong> — the address of an object living on the heap — and every confusing behaviour in this area follows from that one fact.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Student a = new Student("Ada", 95);
Student b = a;                    // b points at the SAME object

b.setScore(50);
a.getScore();                     // 50 — there was only ever one object

Student c = new Student("Ada", 95);
a == c                            // false — different objects
a.equals(c)                       // false too, unless Student overrides equals (10.4)</pre><p>This is the same rule as arrays in 5.7 and Strings in 3.2, and now the reason is visible: the variable is a reference in all three cases. <code>null</code> is the reference that points at nothing, and calling a method on it is the <code>NullPointerException</code> of 6.21.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Student a = new Student("Ana");
Student b = a;               // same object, two names
b.setName("Bo");
a.getName();                 // "Bo"

Student c = new Student("Bo");
a == c                       // false — different objects
a.equals(c)                  // false too, unless Student overrides equals</pre><p><code>==</code> on objects asks "the same object?", never "the same contents?". <code>equals</code> asks about contents — but only if the class defines it. The inherited default compares references, so it behaves identically to <code>==</code> until you override it.</p><p><code>null</code> means the variable refers to no object. Calling anything on it throws <strong><code>NullPointerException</code></strong>, and the message names the method you tried to call, which is usually enough to find it.</p><p><code>toString()</code> is what <code>System.out.println(obj)</code> calls. Without an override you get <code>Student@1b6d</code> — the class name and a hash — which is the giveaway that the class has not defined one.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reference:</strong> a value identifying an object’s location; what an object variable actually stores</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>null:</strong> a reference pointing at no object; the default for uninitialised object fields</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Aliasing:</strong> two references to one object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Shallow copy:</strong> copying the reference rather than the object</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>==</code> on objects asks "same object", not "same contents". Two Students with identical fields are not equal by <code>==</code>, and will not be equal by <code>.equals()</code> either until you write an <code>equals</code> method (10.4).</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286fx14="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — TWO REFERENCES, ONE OBJECT</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 470 178" style="width:100%; max-width:510px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk81a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="40" y="40" width="90" height="36" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="85" y="55" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Car r1</text><text x="85" y="70" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">●</text><rect x="40" y="96" width="90" height="36" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="85" y="111" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Car r2</text><text x="85" y="126" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">●</text><rect x="240" y="52" width="180" height="72" rx="10" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="330" y="74" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one Car object (heap)</text><text x="330" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">speed: 30 → 55</text><line x1="132" y1="58" x2="236" y2="74" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk81a)"/><line x1="132" y1="114" x2="236" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk81a)"/><text x="178" y="50" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">r2 = r1;</text><text x="240" y="156" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Assignment copies the arrow, not the object. r2.setSpeed(55) then "changes r1 too" —</text><text x="240" y="170" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">because there is only one object. r1 == r2 is true: same arrow.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><code>==</code> on objects compares arrows (same object?), <code>.equals()</code> compares contents. Two different String objects with the same letters: <code>equals</code> true, <code>==</code> false.</div><i data-d286_z7_10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Two names, one object</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Dog a = new Dog("Rex");
Dog b = a;
b.name = "Bella";
System.out.println(a.name);
System.out.println(a == b);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Bella
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>b = a</code> copies the REFERENCE, not the object.</li><li>Both names point at one Dog, so changing through b is visible through a.</li><li><code>==</code> is true because they are literally the same object.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — == compares identity, equals compares content</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String a = new String("hi");
String b = new String("hi");
System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a.equals(b));
String c = a;
System.out.println(a == c);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">false
true
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Two separately constructed objects are different objects, so == is false.</li><li><code>equals</code> compares the characters, so it is true.</li><li>Only an assignment of the reference itself makes == true.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>For objects, <code>==</code> asks "the same object?" and <code>.equals()</code> asks "the same value?". Unless a class overrides <code>equals</code>, the two mean the same thing — which is why comparing your own objects usually needs an override.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_11":{"body":"<p>Inside an instance method, <code>this</code> is a reference to the object the method was called on. It is normally invisible — <code>score</code> and <code>this.score</code> mean the same thing — and it becomes necessary in exactly one common situation.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private String name;

    public Student(String name) {       // parameter SHADOWS the field (6.11)
        this.name = name;               // this.name = the field, name = the parameter
    }

    public void copyFrom(Student other) {
        this.name = other.name;         // private is per CLASS, not per object
    }
}</pre><p>Note the second method: a class can read the private fields of another object of the same class, because <code>private</code> restricts access by class, not by instance. That surprises people and it is a legitimate exam question.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public void setName(String name) {
    name = name;              // assigns the parameter to itself — field unchanged
    this.name = name;         // field  =  parameter
}</pre><p>When a parameter shares a field's name it <em>shadows</em> it: the bare name means the parameter for the rest of the method. <code>this.name</code> reaches past the shadow to the field. The self-assignment version compiles and silently does nothing, which is why it is the standard exam question.</p><p><code>this</code> is an implicit parameter every instance method receives — a reference to the object it was called on. <code>a.setName("Bo")</code> passes <code>a</code> as <code>this</code>. That is precisely why a <code>static</code> method cannot use it.</p><p>Its other uses: <code>this(...)</code> to chain constructors, and passing <code>this</code> as an argument when an object needs to hand itself to something else.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>this:</strong> a reference to the object the current instance method was invoked on</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Implicit parameter:</strong> the object before the dot in a call; passed invisibly as this</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Shadowing resolution:</strong> using this.field to reach a field hidden by a parameter of the same name</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Writing <code>name = name;</code> in a constructor whose parameter is also called <code>name</code> assigns the parameter to itself. It compiles, it does nothing, and the field stays null.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Classes, Objects, and Constructors</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_11="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — this = the object the method was called on</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk390a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.bump()</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">inside, this == a</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">this.count</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">THIS object's field</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">this.x = x</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">field = parameter</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">this(...)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">call another constructor</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — this separates the field from the parameter</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(new Point(3, 7));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3,7</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The parameter <code>x</code> hides the field <code>x</code> inside the constructor.</li><li><code>this.x</code> is unambiguous — the field of this object.</li><li>Writing <code>x = x</code> would assign the parameter to itself and leave the field at 0.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Forgetting this</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Broken b = new Broken(42);
System.out.println(b.get());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>size = size</code> assigns the parameter to itself.</li><li>The field is never touched, so it keeps its default of 0.</li><li>It compiles and runs — the output is simply wrong.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Any constructor whose parameters share their names with the fields needs <code>this.</code> on the left of every assignment. Its absence is a silent zero, not an error.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_12":{"body":"<p>Java’s types split cleanly in two, and almost every "why did that happen" in this unit is answered by which side a type is on.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">PRIMITIVE                      REFERENCE
int, double, char, boolean     String, arrays, Scanner, your classes
byte, short, long, float

holds the value itself         holds an address
stored in the stack frame      object lives on the heap (8.3)
== compares values             == compares identity
no methods                     has methods
cannot be null                 can be null
default 0/false                default null</pre><p>There are exactly eight primitives and everything else is a reference type. That is worth memorising as a list, because a question asking "which of these is a primitive" is testing whether <code>String</code> is on the list. It is not.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Primitive</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Examples</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>int, double, char, boolean, long</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>String</code>, arrays, <code>Scanner</code>, your classes</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Holds</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the value itself</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the address of an object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Default</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code>false</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>null</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>==</code> compares</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the values</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the references</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Can be <code>null</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">no</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">yes</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">Has methods</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">no</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Java has exactly eight primitives — <code>byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean</code> — and everything else is a reference type. <code>String</code> looks primitive because of literals and <code>+</code>, but it is a class: it has methods, it can be <code>null</code>, and <code>==</code> on two Strings compares references.</p><p>This one distinction explains pass-by-value (6.8), <code>==</code> versus <code>equals</code>, why arrays copy by reference, and why generics such as <code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code> are illegal.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Primitive type:</strong> one of the eight built-in value types</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reference type:</strong> any class, interface or array type</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Heap:</strong> where objects live; survives the method that created them</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stack:</strong> where locals and parameters live; discarded when the method returns</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Every rule about <code>==</code>, <code>null</code>, defaults and parameter passing in this course follows from this table. When a question puzzles you, work out which column the type is in first.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Concepts Made Clear → Pass-by-value</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_12="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what the variable holds</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk391a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int x = 5</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the VALUE 5</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Dog d = new Dog()</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a REFERENCE to an object</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">primitives</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">8 of them: int, double, char, boolean...</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">everything else</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a reference type</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">default</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0 / false vs null</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Assignment copies different things</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int a = 5;
int b = a;
b = 9;
System.out.println(a);

Bag x = new Bag();
x.n = 5;
Bag y = x;
y.n = 9;
System.out.println(x.n);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">5
9</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>For a primitive, the VALUE is copied — the two are independent.</li><li>For an object, the REFERENCE is copied — both names reach one object.</li><li>That single difference explains most surprises in this unit.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Only reference types can be null</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String s = null;
System.out.println(s);
int i = 0;
System.out.println(i);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">null
0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>null</code> means "references nothing".</li><li>A primitive always holds a value, so it can never be null.</li><li><code>int i = null;</code> would not compile.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Calling <code>.equals()</code> or any method on a reference that is null. The variable exists, but there is no object to run the method on, so it throws at run time.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_13":{"body":"<p>Each primitive has a matching <strong>wrapper class</strong> that turns it into an object — needed because collections such as <code>ArrayList</code> hold objects only, and a primitive is not one.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int    -&gt; Integer      double -&gt; Double      char    -&gt; Character
boolean-&gt; Boolean      long   -&gt; Long        float   -&gt; Float

Integer boxed = 5;              // AUTOBOXING     — int to Integer
int back = boxed;               // UNBOXING       — Integer to int

ArrayList<integer> list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
list.add(5);                    // autoboxed automatically

// parsing text into numbers — the most-used wrapper methods in the course
int n = Integer.parseInt("42");
double d = Double.parseDouble("3.14");</integer></pre><p>Two traps come with wrappers. An <code>Integer</code> can be <code>null</code>, so unboxing one throws <code>NullPointerException</code>. And <code>==</code> on two <code>Integer</code> objects compares references — it appears to work for small values, because Java caches -128 to 127, and stops working above that.</p><h3>valueOf, and how it differs from parse</h3><p>Every wrapper class offers <strong><code>valueOf</code></strong> alongside <code>parseInt</code>, and the difference is what comes back.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int      a = Integer.parseInt("42");   // returns a PRIMITIVE int
Integer  b = Integer.valueOf("42");   // returns an Integer OBJECT
Integer  c = Integer.valueOf(42);     // also works from an int

String   s = String.valueOf(42);      // "42" — the reverse direction</pre><p><code>valueOf</code> also uses the wrapper cache for values from −128 to 127, which is exactly why <code>Integer.valueOf(127) == Integer.valueOf(127)</code> is true and the same comparison at 128 is false. <code>new Integer(42)</code> always allocates and so is never cached — which is one reason it is deprecated.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><p>Every primitive has a <strong>wrapper class</strong> that holds the same value as an object — because collections and generics only accept objects.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Primitive</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Wrapper</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>int</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong><code>Integer</code></strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>char</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong><code>Character</code></strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>double</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Double</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>boolean</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Boolean</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
list.add(5);                 // autoboxing:  int  -&gt; Integer
int x = list.get(0);         // unboxing:    Integer -&gt; int

int n = Integer.parseInt("42");        // String -&gt; primitive int
Integer o = Integer.valueOf("42");     // String -&gt; Integer OBJECT</pre><p>The two irregular names are <code>Integer</code> and <code>Character</code> — not <code>Int</code> or <code>Char</code>. Autoboxing hides the conversion until something is <code>null</code>: unboxing a <code>null</code> <code>Integer</code> into an <code>int</code> throws <code>NullPointerException</code>, and the line looks like plain arithmetic.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Wrapper class:</strong> an object type wrapping a primitive value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Autoboxing:</strong> automatic conversion from primitive to wrapper</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Unboxing:</strong> automatic conversion from wrapper back to primitive</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>parseInt / parseDouble:</strong> static methods converting a String to a number; throw NumberFormatException on bad text</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>Integer a = 200, b = 200; a == b</code> is <strong>false</strong>, while the same code with 100 is true. Always use <code>.equals()</code> — or better, unbox to <code>int</code> and compare that.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Java Basics → Primitive Types and Type Casting</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_13="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — every primitive has a wrapper</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk392a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">int</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Integer</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">double</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Double</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">char</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Character</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">boolean</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Boolean</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">autoboxing</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">int -&gt; Integer automatically</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">unboxing</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Integer -&gt; int automatically</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Boxing, unboxing and parsing</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Integer boxed = 42;
int plain = boxed;
System.out.println(boxed + 1);
System.out.println(Integer.parseInt("123") + 1);
System.out.println(Integer.valueOf("7") + 1);
System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">43
124
8
2147483647</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Autoboxing wraps the int without you asking.</li><li>Unboxing takes it back out again.</li><li><code>parseInt</code> returns a primitive int; <code>valueOf</code> returns an Integer.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why == on Integers is dangerous</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Integer a = 127, b = 127;
System.out.println(a == b);
Integer c = 128, d = 128;
System.out.println(c == d);
System.out.println(c.equals(d));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
false
true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java caches Integer objects from −128 to 127, so a and b are the same object.</li><li>Above that range new objects are created, so == compares two different objects and is false.</li><li><code>equals</code> compares the values and is true either way.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Compare wrapper objects with <code>.equals()</code>, never <code>==</code>. The cache makes == appear to work for small numbers, which is exactly what makes the bug so hard to find.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_14":{"body":"<p><code>ArrayList</code> is the answer to the oversize array of 6.16: a list that grows on demand and keeps its own count. It removes the entire category of bugs that came from tracking a size by hand.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import java.util.ArrayList;

ArrayList<string> names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Ada");            // append
names.add(0, "Grace");       // insert at index 0
names.get(1);                // "Ada"          — NOT names[1]
names.set(1, "Alan");        // replace
names.remove(0);             // remove by index; everything after shifts down
names.size();                // count          — NOT .length or .length()
names.contains("Alan");      // true
names.isEmpty();</string></pre><p>Three names for the same idea now exist and the exam checks you know which is which: arrays use <code>length</code>, Strings use <code>length()</code>, and lists use <code>size()</code>. Indexing also differs — square brackets for arrays, <code>get</code> and <code>set</code> for lists.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
names.add("Ana");            // append
names.add(0, "Zed");         // insert at index 0
names.get(1);                // read      — NOT names[1]
names.set(1, "Bo");          // replace
names.remove(0);             // delete and shift left
names.size();                // count     — NOT length or length()</pre><p>Three different words for "how many" now exist and the exam tests all three: <code>a.length</code> for an array, <code>s.length()</code> for a String, <code>list.size()</code> for an ArrayList.</p><p>An ArrayList grows on demand — it is the oversize array of 6.16 with the bookkeeping hidden. It stores objects only, so <code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code> is illegal and <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is the way, with autoboxing making it look seamless.</p><p><code>remove(int)</code> removes by <em>index</em> while <code>remove(Object)</code> removes by <em>value</em> — so on an <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, <code>remove(1)</code> deletes position 1 and <code>remove(Integer.valueOf(1))</code> deletes the value 1.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ArrayList:</strong> a resizable list backed by an array, from java.util</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type parameter:</strong> the &lt;String&gt; that says what the list holds; see Unit 13</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Diamond operator:</strong> the empty &lt;&gt; on the right-hand side, which infers the type</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>size():</strong> the number of elements currently in the list</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Removing while looping forward skips elements, because everything shifts down into the index you just processed. Loop backwards, or use an iterator, when removing.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → 2D Arrays and ArrayList</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_14="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — ArrayList against a plain array</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk393a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">array</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fixed length</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ArrayList</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">grows and shrinks</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.length</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">vs list.size()</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[0]</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">vs list.get(0)</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">objects only</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;, not &lt;int&gt;</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The operations you actually need</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
names.add("Ada");
names.add("Grace");
names.add(1, "Alan");
System.out.println(names);
System.out.println(names.size());
System.out.println(names.get(0));
names.remove("Alan");
System.out.println(names.contains("Alan"));
System.out.println(names);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">[Ada, Alan, Grace]
3
Ada
false
[Ada, Grace]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>add</code> appends, or inserts at an index.</li><li><code>size()</code> is the count — there is no <code>.length</code>.</li><li><code>remove</code> shifts everything after it down.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — It must hold objects, so ints are boxed</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; nums = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
nums.add(10);
nums.add(20);
int total = 0;
for (int n : nums) total += n;
System.out.println(total);
System.out.println(nums.get(0) + 5);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">30
15</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code> is illegal — generics need a reference type.</li><li><code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code> works, and autoboxing hides the conversion.</li><li>The loop unboxes each Integer back to an int.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Arrays use <code>length</code> and <code>[i]</code>; ArrayLists use <code>size()</code> and <code>get(i)</code>. Mixing them up is the most common compile error in this section.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_15":{"body":"<p>A list of your own objects is where classes and collections finally combine, and it replaces parallel arrays completely.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ArrayList<student> roster = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
roster.add(new Student("Ada", 95));
roster.add(new Student("Alan", 88));

for (Student s : roster) {                    // enhanced for (5.10)
    System.out.println(s.getName() + ": " + s.getScore());
}

// finding the top scorer: track the OBJECT, not an index
Student best = roster.get(0);
for (Student s : roster) {
    if (s.getScore() &gt; best.getScore()) { best = s; }
}</student></pre><p>Compare this with 5.5, where the same job required an index to keep two arrays aligned. Here the name and score cannot separate, because they are one object — which is the payoff the whole unit has been building toward.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ArrayList&lt;Student&gt; roll = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
roll.add(new Student("Ana", 88));

for (Student s : roll)                       // read every one
    System.out.println(s.getName());

for (Student s : roll)                       // MUTATING is fine —
    s.setScore(s.getScore() + 5);            // the reference reaches the object</pre><p>Unlike an array of primitives, a for-each over objects <em>can</em> change them: the loop variable is a copy of the reference, which still points at the same object. What it cannot do is replace an element — that needs <code>set(i, ...)</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = roll.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--)   // remove BACKWARDS
    if (roll.get(i).getScore() &lt; 40) roll.remove(i);</pre><p>Removing while looping forwards skips elements, because everything after the removed item shifts left while the index still advances. Going backwards avoids it entirely; removing inside a for-each throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Collection of objects:</strong> a list whose elements are references to your own class’s instances</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Object accumulator:</strong> tracking a whole object as the current best rather than an index</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>roster.get(0)</code> on an empty list throws <code>IndexOutOfBoundsException</code>. Guard with <code>isEmpty()</code> before assuming a first element exists.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → 2D Arrays and ArrayList</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_15="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a list of your own objects</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk394a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ArrayList&lt;Part&gt;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">holds references</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">list.add(new Part(...))</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one object per entry</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">list.get(i).getName()</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reach in and call</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the list holds references</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">not copies</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Building and searching a list of objects</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;Part&gt; parts = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
parts.add(new Part("Wheel", 40));
parts.add(new Part("Frame", 120));
double total = 0;
for (Part p : parts) total += p.getPrice();
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", total);
for (Part p : parts) if (p.getName().equals("Frame")) System.out.println("found " + p.getPrice());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">160.00
found 120.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each entry is a reference to a Part object.</li><li>The enhanced for loop hands you each one in turn.</li><li>Searching compares with <code>.equals</code>, because the names are Strings.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The list holds references, not copies</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
Item i = new Item(1);
ArrayList&lt;Item&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
list.add(i);
i.qty = 99;
System.out.println(list.get(0).qty);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">99</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Adding an object to a list stores a reference to it.</li><li>The original variable still points at the same object.</li><li>Changing it through either name is visible through both.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting <code>list.add(obj)</code> to store a snapshot. It stores a reference, so later changes to that object show up inside the list.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_16":{"body":"<p>Treating <code>ArrayList</code> as an <strong>abstract data type</strong> means separating what it does from how it does it. The operations — add, get, remove, size — are the contract; the array-with-spare-capacity underneath is an implementation detail you are free to ignore.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ADT operation      what it costs
add(e)             O(1) usually; O(n) when it has to grow
get(i)             O(1)   — direct index into the backing array
remove(i)          O(n)   — everything after i shifts down
contains(e)        O(n)   — a linear search</pre><p>Knowing the costs is what lets you choose. The shifting on <code>remove</code> is exactly what makes <code>LinkedList</code> (14.2) attractive for heavy insertion and deletion, and the linear <code>contains</code> is why <code>HashSet</code> (14.4) exists.</p><p>An <strong>abstract data type</strong> is a type defined by the operations it offers, not by how it stores anything. <code>ArrayList</code> is the ADT; "an oversize array that reallocates when full" is the implementation, and it is deliberately invisible.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Operation</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>add(x)</code> / <code>add(i, x)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">append / insert at a position</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>get(i)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">read by position</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>set(i, x)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">replace at a position — does not grow the list</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>remove(i)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">delete and shift left</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>indexOf(x)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the position of a value, or <code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>contains(x)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">true/false — <code>indexOf(x) &gt;= 0</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>size()</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">how many elements</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><code>get</code> takes a position and returns a value; <code>indexOf</code> takes a value and returns a position — they are inverses, and mixing them up is the usual slip. <code>set</code> overwrites and <code>add(i, x)</code> inserts, so only one of them changes the size.</p><p>Capacity is invisible from outside. <code>size()</code> is the number of elements; the underlying array may be larger, and that is exactly what the ADT is hiding.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Abstract data type (ADT):</strong> a set of operations defined independently of any implementation</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Implementation:</strong> the concrete data structure providing those operations</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Amortised cost:</strong> the average cost per operation over many, allowing for occasional expensive resizes</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This split is what Unit 14 is organised around, and the O-notation attached to it is Unit 17. The same operation name can cost wildly different amounts depending on the structure underneath.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → 2D Arrays and ArrayList</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_16="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the ADT and its implementation</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk395a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the ADT</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what operations exist</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">add / get / remove / size</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the contract</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the implementation</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a resizing array inside</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">get(i)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fast — O(1)</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">remove from the middle</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">shifts everything after</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The interface is what you program against</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
List&lt;String&gt; items = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
items.add("a");
items.add("b");
items.add("c");
System.out.println(items.indexOf("b"));
items.remove(0);
System.out.println(items);
System.out.println(items.isEmpty());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1
[b, c]
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Declaring the variable as <code>List</code> commits you only to the ADT.</li><li>The implementation could be swapped without changing this code.</li><li>That separation is exactly what an abstract data type means.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why removing from the front costs more</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) a.add(i);
System.out.println(a);
a.remove(0);
System.out.println(a);
System.out.println(a.get(0));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
[1, 2, 3, 4]
1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>An ArrayList is a resizing array underneath.</li><li>Removing index 0 shifts every later element down one place.</li><li><code>get(i)</code> stays fast because the position is computed, not searched.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><code>list.remove(1)</code> on an <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code> removes the element at INDEX 1, not the value 1. Use <code>remove(Integer.valueOf(1))</code> to remove by value.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>An ADT specifies the operations and their behaviour, not the storage. If a question asks what an ADT defines, the answer is the interface — never the implementation.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_17":{"body":"<p>Reading the Java API documentation for a class is a skill the exam assumes, and the layout is always the same: constructors first, then methods, each with parameters, return type and exceptions.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public boolean add(E e)
       ^       ^   ^
       |       |   parameter: the element to append
       |       method name
       return type — true if the collection changed

Throws: NullPointerException - if the specified element is null
        and this list does not permit null elements</pre><p>The two lines to read before calling anything unfamiliar are the return type and the throws clause. The return type tells you whether you need to assign the result — the mistake from 3.14 with Strings — and the throws clause tells you what can go wrong.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">/**
 * A student's name and exam score.
 * An instance always has a non-null name and a score in 0..100.
 */
public class Student {
    /** @return the score, always between 0 and 100 */
    public int getScore() { return score; }
}</pre><p>Class documentation states the <strong>invariant</strong> — what is always true of every instance — and that is more useful than describing the fields. "Score is always 0 to 100" tells a reader they need not check it, and tells you the setter must enforce it.</p><p>Document the <em>contract</em>, not the implementation. "Returns the average" survives a rewrite; "loops through the array adding to a total" becomes a lie the moment the storage changes, and stale documentation is worse than none because it is trusted.</p><p>Private helpers need only enough comment for the next maintainer; public members need the full contract, because callers cannot see inside.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>API:</strong> the published set of classes and methods available to you</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Method summary:</strong> the documentation table listing every method with its signature</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Throws clause:</strong> the exceptions a method may raise</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>When the docs show <code>E</code>, <code>T</code> or <code>K, V</code>, those are type parameters (Unit 13), not real classes. <code>E</code> is whatever type you supplied in the angle brackets.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_17="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — reading a class's docs</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk396a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the class summary</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what it is for</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">constructors</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">how to build one</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">method summary</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what it can do</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">returns</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the type you can assign</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">throws</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what can go wrong</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The docs tell you the return type before you assign</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; l = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
l.add("x");
boolean added = l.add("y");
System.out.println(added);
String removed = l.remove(0);
System.out.println(removed);
System.out.println(l);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
x
[y]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>add</code> is documented as returning boolean — always true for a list.</li><li><code>remove(int)</code> is documented as returning the element removed.</li><li>Knowing the return type is what lets you use it rather than discard it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Constructors are documented separately</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; b = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(50);
b.add("one");
System.out.println(a.size() + " " + b.size());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0 1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>One constructor takes nothing; another takes an initial capacity.</li><li>Capacity is not size — the list still contains nothing.</li><li>The docs are where you find which constructors exist.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming a documented method changes the object it is called on. Check the return type: a method that returns a value is usually handing you a result and leaving the receiver alone.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_18":{"body":"<p>Passing an object to a method follows the rule already established for arrays in 6.10, because both are references.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void promote(Student s) {
    s.setScore(100);          // caller SEES this — same object
}

public static void swapOut(Student s) {
    s = new Student("Other"); // caller does NOT see this — copy repointed
}

Student ada = new Student("Ada", 50);
promote(ada);     ada.getScore();   // 100
swapOut(ada);     ada.getName();    // still "Ada"</pre><p>The precise statement — the one to give if the exam asks — is: Java is always pass by value, and for an object the value being copied is the reference. That single sentence explains both lines of behaviour above.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void promote(Student s) { s.setScore(100); }   // caller SEES it
static void swap(Student s)    { s = new Student("X"); } // caller sees nothing</pre><p>Identical to arrays in 6.10, for an identical reason: the reference is copied, so the method reaches the same object but cannot rebind the caller's variable.</p><p>A <strong>defensive copy</strong> is the answer when a class must not let outsiders mutate its internals: return <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(items)</code> rather than <code>items</code>, or the caller can modify your private list through the reference you handed out — encapsulation defeated without a single public field.</p><p>Passing <code>null</code> is legal and only fails when the method dereferences it, so a method taking an object should either guard or document that <code>null</code> is not allowed. <code>String</code> is immutable, so passing one is always safe.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pass by value (references):</strong> the reference is copied, so both names reach one object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Mutation through a parameter:</strong> changing the object’s state, which the caller observes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reassigning a parameter:</strong> repointing the local copy, which the caller never sees</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If a method must give back a different object, return it. And beware of returning a reference to a private field — the caller can then modify the object’s internals, defeating the encapsulation of 7.4.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Concepts Made Clear → Pass-by-value</strong></p><i data-d286_z7_18="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — passing an object to a method</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk397a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the reference is copied</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">always</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">change a FIELD inside</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">caller sees it</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">reassign the PARAMETER</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">caller does not</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">still pass by value</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the value is a reference</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Changing a field is visible; reassigning is not</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Box box = new Box(1);
changeField(box);
System.out.println(box.n);
reassign(box);
System.out.println(box.n);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">99
99</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>changeField</code> follows the reference and edits the one object.</li><li><code>reassign</code> only repoints the method's own copy of the reference.</li><li>The caller's variable still points at the original object.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same rule for a String — but Strings are immutable</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String msg = "hello";
shout(msg);
System.out.println(msg);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">hello</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The method reassigns its own copy of the reference.</li><li>A String cannot be changed in place anyway.</li><li>So the caller's text is unaffected — return the new value instead.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Concluding Java is pass-by-reference because an object's fields can be changed. Java is always pass-by-value; for objects the value copied is the reference, which is why reassigning the parameter changes nothing outside.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_19":{"body":"<p>A <code>static</code> member belongs to the class rather than to any object: there is exactly one copy no matter how many objects exist. That makes it the right home for counters and constants, and the wrong home for anything describing one particular object.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student {
    private static int count = 0;         // ONE copy, shared by all
    private String name;                  // one per object

    public Student(String n) {
        name = n;
        count++;                          // every construction increments the same counter
    }

    public static int getCount() { return count; }   // no object needed
}

Student.getCount();       // called on the CLASS</pre><p>The restriction follows from the definition: a static method has no object, so it cannot read an instance field. The reverse is fine — an instance method can read static members, because the class exists whenever an object does.</p><h3>Static initializer blocks</h3><p>A <code>static</code> field sometimes needs more than one expression to set up. A <strong>static initializer block</strong> runs once, when the class is first loaded, before any object exists and before <code>main</code> runs.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">class Config {
    static Map<string,string> defaults;

    static {                            // runs ONCE, at class load
        defaults = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        defaults.put("theme", "dark");
    }
}</string,string></pre><p>An <em>instance</em> initializer block — the same thing without <code>static</code> — runs before each constructor instead. The full order at object creation is: static blocks once at class load, then instance initializers, then the constructor body.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Counter {
    private static int made = 0;      // ONE copy, shared by every object
    private int id;                   // one per object

    public Counter() { made++; id = made; }
    public static int total() { return made; }   // no object needed
}
Counter.total();                      // called on the CLASS</pre><p>A static field belongs to the class, so every object sees the same one — which is what makes "how many have been created" possible at all. An instance field could not do it, because each object would have its own count of one.</p><p>A static method may not touch instance fields or <code>this</code>, because it runs without an object. The reverse is fine: an instance method can freely use static members, since the class always exists.</p><p><code>static final</code> is the constant idiom — <code>public static final double PI = 3.14159;</code> — one shared copy that cannot change, named in <code>UPPER_SNAKE_CASE</code>.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>static field:</strong> one shared copy belonging to the class</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>static method:</strong> called on the class; cannot access instance fields or this</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>static final constant:</strong> a shared, unchangeable value — the pattern of 2.9</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Instance counter:</strong> a static field incremented in the constructor to count objects created</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A static method cannot use <code>this</code> or any instance field — which is exactly why <code>main</code> has to create an object before it can use one (7.7).</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OOP → Static Members and Method Overloading</strong></p><i data-d286fx15="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — INSTANCE FIELDS VS ONE SHARED STATIC FIELD</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 156" style="width:100%; max-width:550px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk82a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="30" y="50" width="130" height="78" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="95" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">p1 : Player</text><text x="95" y="90" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">name: "Ada"</text><text x="95" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">score: 12</text><rect x="190" y="50" width="130" height="78" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="255" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">p2 : Player</text><text x="255" y="90" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">name: "Ben"</text><text x="255" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">score: 31</text><rect x="360" y="60" width="130" height="58" rx="8" fill="#f59e0b22" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="425" y="80" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Player.count</text><text x="425" y="100" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2  (static — ONE copy)</text><line x1="160" y1="70" x2="358" y2="78" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk82a)"/><line x1="320" y1="100" x2="358" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk82a)"/><text x="95" y="40" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">instance fields: one set PER object</text><text x="260" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">static lives on the class, shared by every instance. Access it as Player.count, not p1.count.</text></svg></div></div><i data-d286_z7_19="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One static field shared by every object</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Visitor a = new Visitor();
Visitor b = new Visitor();
Visitor c = new Visitor();
System.out.println(a.id + " " + b.id + " " + c.id);
System.out.println(Visitor.total);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1 2 3
3</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>id</code> is an instance field, so each object has its own.</li><li><code>total</code> is static, so there is exactly ONE for the whole class.</li><li>A static field is reached through the class name, not an object.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A static method cannot touch instance state</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(MathHelper.twice(21));
MathHelper m = new MathHelper();
System.out.println(m.countedTwice(21) + " calls=" + m.calls);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">42
42 calls=1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>twice</code> is static, so it runs with no object and cannot see <code>calls</code>.</li><li><code>countedTwice</code> is an instance method and can.</li><li>That is why <code>Math.max</code> is static — it needs no state.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>"Non-static variable cannot be referenced from a static context" always means the same thing: a static method tried to use an instance field. Either make the field static, or create an object first.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_20":{"body":"<p>A <strong>package</strong> is a namespace for classes: it prevents name collisions and groups related code. You have been consuming packages since <code>import java.util.Scanner</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">package com.wgu.school;              // FIRST line of the file, if present

import java.util.ArrayList;          // one class
import java.util.*;                  // every class in the package (not sub-packages)

java.lang     — String, Math, System, Integer   (imported automatically)
java.util     — Scanner, ArrayList, HashMap, Random
java.io       — File, FileReader, PrintWriter   (Unit 9)</pre><p>Package membership also affects visibility: a member with no access modifier at all is <em>package-private</em>, visible inside its package and nowhere else. That is a fourth level alongside public, private and protected (10.2).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">package com.example.school;        // FIRST line, matches the folder path
import java.util.ArrayList;        // one class
import java.util.*;                // everything in that package (not subpackages)</pre><p>A package is a namespace and a folder path at once: <code>com.example.school</code> means the file lives in <code>com/example/school/</code>. Two classes may share a name if they are in different packages, which is the problem packages exist to solve.</p><p><code>java.lang</code> is imported automatically — that is why <code>String</code>, <code>Math</code>, <code>System</code> and the wrappers never need one, while <code>java.util.Scanner</code> and <code>java.util.ArrayList</code> do.</p><p>A member with no access modifier is <strong>package-private</strong>: visible to other classes in the same package and nowhere else. It is the default, and it is not the same as <code>public</code>.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Package:</strong> a named grouping of related classes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>import:</strong> lets you use a class by its short name</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Fully qualified name:</strong> the complete name including the package, e.g. java.util.Scanner</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Package-private:</strong> the default access when no modifier is written; visible within the package only</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Two classes with the same short name from different packages require the fully qualified name for at least one of them — <code>java.util.Date</code> versus <code>java.sql.Date</code> is the standard example.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_20="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — packages organise and disambiguate</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk398a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">package com.wgu.parts;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">first line of the file</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">import java.util.ArrayList;</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">brings a name into scope</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">java.lang</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">imported automatically</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fully qualified</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">java.util.List — no import needed</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">import java.util.*;</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the whole package</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Import, or write the full name</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.ArrayList&lt;String&gt; a = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
a.add("x");
System.out.println(a.size());
System.out.println(java.time.LocalDate.of(2026, 3, 14));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1
2026-03-14</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>An import is only a convenience — it saves repeating the package.</li><li>The fully qualified name always works without one.</li><li>Two classes with the same short name are told apart this way.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — java.lang needs no import</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(Math.abs(-4));
System.out.println(String.valueOf(12).length());
System.out.println(Integer.parseInt("9") + 1);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4
2
10</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Math</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Integer</code> and <code>System</code> all live in <code>java.lang</code>.</li><li>That package is imported automatically into every file.</li><li>Everything else — <code>java.util</code>, <code>java.time</code> — must be imported or fully qualified.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Putting the <code>import</code> above the <code>package</code> line. The package declaration must be the first statement in the file; imports come after it.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_21":{"body":"<p>The salary program becomes a class, and the difference from 6.23 is that the data now travels with the behaviour instead of being handed in on every call.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Employee {
    private String name;
    private double salary;

    public Employee(String n, double s) { name = n; salary = s; }

    public double taxRate() {
        if (salary &gt;= 100000) { return 0.28; }
        if (salary &gt;=  50000) { return 0.22; }
        return 0.15;
    }

    public double netPay() { return salary * (1 - taxRate()); }
    public String getName() { return name; }
}

ArrayList<employee> staff = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
staff.add(new Employee("Ada", 95000));
for (Employee e : staff) {
    System.out.printf("%s: %.2f%n", e.getName(), e.netPay());
}</employee></pre><p><code>taxRate()</code> takes no parameter now because it reads its own <code>salary</code> field. That is the shift from Unit 6 to Unit 7 in a single line: methods stop being given the data and start owning it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Employee {
    private String name;
    private double rate;                       // private: the rule is protected
    public Employee(String name, double rate) {
        this.name = name; this.rate = rate;
    }
    public double payFor(double hours) {       // the logic lives WITH the data
        return hours &lt;= 40 ? hours * rate
                           : 40 * rate + (hours - 40) * rate * 1.5;
    }
    public String getName() { return name; }
}

double payroll = 0;
for (Employee e : staff) payroll += e.payFor(hoursFor(e));</pre><p>Compare this with the Unit 6 version. There, the rate lived in a loop variable and the overtime rule was written inline, so every place that needed pay had to repeat it. Here each <code>Employee</code> carries its own rate and knows how to compute its own pay — the rule exists once, and a rate cannot drift away from the person it belongs to.</p><p>The accumulator is still declared before the loop for the same reason as 4.12. What has changed is that the per-employee state moved from a variable that must be reset into a field that cannot be shared by accident.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Behaviour with data:</strong> placing the methods that operate on a value in the class that holds it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Object collection:</strong> an ArrayList of domain objects replacing parallel arrays</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Adding a new field — a bonus, a department — now means editing one class. In the parallel-array version of 5.11 it meant adding an array and updating every loop that touched it.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_21="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the data and its rules together</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk399a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fields</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">name, annual salary</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">constructor</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sets them once</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">monthly()</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a rule about THIS employee</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">raise(pct)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a mutator</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">main</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">reads as a summary</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — An Employee class that owns its own rules</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Employee e = new Employee("Ada", 55000);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", e.monthly());
e.raise(4);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", e.getAnnual());
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", e.monthly());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">4583.33
57200.00
4766.67</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The object holds the salary, so <code>monthly()</code> needs no argument.</li><li><code>raise</code> is a mutator — it changes state and returns nothing.</li><li>After the raise the same call gives a new answer, because the state moved.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Several employees, each with its own state</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Emp[] staff = { new Emp(60000), new Emp(48000) };
double total = 0;
for (Emp e : staff) total += e.monthly();
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", total);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">9000.00</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each object carries its own pay.</li><li>The array holds references to two independent objects.</li><li>The loop asks each one the same question.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Dividing by <code>12</code> rather than <code>12.0</code> when the field is an <code>int</code>. The pennies are lost inside the method and no amount of formatting afterwards recovers them.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>For the performance assessment, keep the rule in the class that owns the data. A controller computing salaries itself is the structural mistake evaluators look for.</div>"},"D286/u7/z7_22":{"body":"<p>The domain example becomes a class too, and the win is that the rules and the registry finally live in one place.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class DomainRegistry {
    private ArrayList<string> taken = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    public boolean isAvailable(String d) {
        String candidate = normalise(d);
        for (String t : taken) {
            if (t.equalsIgnoreCase(candidate)) { return false; }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public boolean register(String d) {
        if (!isAvailable(d)) { return false; }
        taken.add(normalise(d));
        return true;
    }

    private String normalise(String d) { return d.trim().toLowerCase(); }
}</string></pre><p><code>normalise</code> is <code>private</code> because it is an implementation detail — callers should not have to know that names are stored lowercased. And <code>register</code> reuses <code>isAvailable</code> instead of repeating the search, so a change to the matching rule happens once.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Domain {
    private String name;
    private boolean registered;

    public Domain(String name) { this.name = name; this.registered = false; }

    public boolean isValid()     { /* the 4.13 character rules */ }
    public boolean isAvailable() { return isValid() &amp;&amp; !registered; }
    public boolean register() {
        if (!isAvailable()) return false;       // guard: cannot register twice
        registered = true;
        return true;
    }
}</pre><p><code>registered</code> is private and there is no setter, so the only way to change it is <code>register()</code> — which refuses unless the domain is valid and still free. The rule cannot be bypassed because there is no other door, which is encapsulation doing something concrete rather than being a definition.</p><p><code>isAvailable()</code> composes <code>isValid()</code> instead of repeating the character checks, so the validation rules live in exactly one method. <code>register()</code> returning a boolean lets the caller react to a refusal rather than silently assume success.</p><i data-d286u7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Encapsulated state:</strong> the list is private, so the only way to change it is through register()</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Private helper reuse:</strong> sharing internal logic between public methods</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Class invariant:</strong> a property kept true by every method — here, that stored names are always normalised</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Returning <code>boolean</code> from <code>register</code> rather than printing keeps the class usable from a GUI, a test, or a file-driven program — the argument from 6.2, now at class scale.</p></div><i data-d286_z7_22="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a small class with one clear job</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk400a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fields</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the domain name</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isValid()</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a question about itself</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isAvailable(list)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a question about the world</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the caller</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">decides what to print</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A Domain class that validates itself</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(new Domain("wgu.edu").isValid());
System.out.println(new Domain("nodot").isValid());
System.out.println(new Domain("").isValid());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">true
