true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The object knows its own name; the list is passed in.</li><li><code>contains</code> uses <code>equals</code>, which is the correct comparison for Strings.</li><li>Returning a boolean keeps the class free of any printing.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Calling <code>name.isEmpty()</code> before the null check. Ordering the guards the other way round throws a NullPointerException on exactly the input the guard was meant to catch.</div>"},"D286/u8/z8_1":{"body":"<p>Unit 7 said objects live "on the heap" without explaining what that costs. Memory management is that explanation, and in Java the headline is that you never free anything by hand — the garbage collector does it — but you can still keep memory alive by accident.</p><p>Two questions decide everything in this unit: where does this value live, and what is still pointing at it? A local <code>int</code> lives in the stack frame and vanishes when the method returns. An object lives on the heap and survives exactly as long as something can still reach it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">void demo() {
    int n = 5;                    // stack — gone when demo() returns
    Student s = new Student();    // s is on the stack, the OBJECT is on the heap
}                                 // s vanishes; the object becomes unreachable
                                  // and is collected at some later point</pre><p>Memory management is deciding when storage is claimed and when it is given back. In C you do both by hand; in Java <code>new</code> claims and the <strong>garbage collector</strong> gives back, automatically, once nothing can reach the object any more.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Manual (C/C++)</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Automatic (Java)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">claim</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>malloc</code> / <code>new</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>new</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">release</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>free</code> / <code>delete</code> — yours</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the garbage collector</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">forget to release</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">memory leak</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">cannot happen this way</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">release too early</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">dangling pointer, crash</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">cannot happen at all</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Java can still leak, but only in one way: an object you no longer use is still <em>reachable</em> — left in a list, held by a static field — so the collector must keep it. Filling memory that way ends in <code>OutOfMemoryError</code>.</p><p>So the programmer's job is not freeing, it is not holding on. Removing something from a collection when you are done with it is the whole of the discipline.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Memory management:</strong> deciding when memory is allocated and when it is reclaimed</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Allocation:</strong> reserving memory for a new object, done by new</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reachable:</strong> an object is reachable if some live reference chain leads to it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Garbage:</strong> an allocated object that is no longer reachable</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>In C you would call <code>free</code> and risk freeing twice or never. Java removes that entire class of bug and replaces it with a subtler one — holding a reference longer than you meant to, which is the leak described in 8.5.</p></div><i data-tbc81="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHO GIVES THE MEMORY BACK</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 800 300" style="width:100%; max-width:720px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk248a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="14" y="28" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">MANUAL — C / C++</text><rect x="60" y="40" width="118" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="119" y="59" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">malloc</text><text x="119" y="75" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">you claim</text><line x1="178" y1="62" x2="216" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="216" y="40" width="118" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="275" y="59" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">use it</text><text x="275" y="75" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">read / write</text><line x1="334" y1="62" x2="372" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="372" y="40" width="140" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="442" y="59" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">free()</text><text x="442" y="75" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">YOUR job</text><line x1="512" y1="62" x2="550" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="550" y="40" width="190" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="645" y="59" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">memory reclaimed</text><text x="645" y="75" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">exactly when you said</text><line x1="442" y1="84" x2="442" y2="108" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="300" y1="108" x2="620" y2="108" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="300" y1="108" x2="300" y2="122" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><line x1="620" y1="108" x2="620" y2="122" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="214" y="122" width="172" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="300" y="143.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">forget it → LEAK</text><rect x="534" y="122" width="172" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="620" y="143.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">do it twice → CRASH</text><line x1="30" y1="178" x2="780" y2="178" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="14" y="208" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">AUTOMATIC — JAVA</text><rect x="60" y="220" width="118" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="119" y="239" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new</text><text x="119" y="255" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">you claim</text><line x1="178" y1="242" x2="216" y2="242" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="216" y="220" width="118" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="275" y="239" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">use it</text><text x="275" y="255" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">read / write</text><line x1="334" y1="242" x2="372" y2="242" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="372" y="220" width="140" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="442" y="239" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">last reference</text><text x="442" y="255" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">dropped</text><line x1="512" y1="242" x2="550" y2="242" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk248a)"/><rect x="550" y="220" width="190" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="645" y="239" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">collector reclaims</text><text x="645" y="255" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">at some later, unknown time</text><text x="400" y="288" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the only Java leak: the last reference is never dropped — the object stays reachable and stays alive</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — WHERE EACH NAME LIVES, AND WHAT SURVIVES</div><p>Four printed lines answer the two questions this unit is built on: <em>where does this value live</em>, and <em>what is still pointing at it</em>.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Student { String name; Student(String n){ name = n; } }
static void rename(Student s, String n) { s.name = n; }

public static void main(String[] args) {
    int count = 2;
    Student s1 = new Student("Ada");
    Student s2 = s1;
    rename(s1, "Grace");
    System.out.println(s1.name);
    System.out.println(s2.name);
    s1 = null;
    System.out.println(s2.name);
    System.out.println(count);
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>int count = 2;</code> — <strong>the value 2 itself</strong> sits in main's stack frame. There is no heap object anywhere in this line.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new Student("Ada")</code> — one Student object is created <strong>on the heap</strong>. The variable <code>s1</code> is a stack slot holding the address of it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Student s2 = s1;</code> — a second stack slot holding <strong>the same address</strong>. Still one object, now two references to it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>rename(s1, "Grace")</code> — the parameter <code>s</code> is a third reference to that same object, so <code>s.name = n</code> edits <strong>the one object everybody points at</strong>. Both later reads see "Grace".</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>s1 = null;</code> — this overwrites one stack slot. It does <strong>not</strong> touch the heap object, and <code>s2</code> still reaches it, so the next line prints normally.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>count</code> was never involved: primitives are copied, so nothing a method does to a copy can be seen here.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>Grace
Grace
Grace
2</code></pre><p>The whole unit in one trace: three references, one object, and assigning <code>null</code> to a reference removes a path rather than deleting anything.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — AN ACCIDENTAL LEAK, MEASURED</div><p>Java cannot leak the C way. It leaks exactly one way — an object you have finished with is still reachable. Here a logging list is the culprit.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Reading { double value; Reading(double v){ value = v; } }
static ArrayList&lt;Reading&gt; log = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

static void sample(double v) {
    Reading r = new Reading(v);      // local — dies at the closing brace
    log.add(r);                      // but the LIST keeps a reference
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) { sample(i * 1.5); }
    System.out.println("locals gone, list still holds " + log.size());
    log.clear();
    System.out.println("after clear: " + log.size());
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Each call to <code>sample</code> creates one <code>Reading</code> and stores a reference in <code>log</code>. When the method returns, <code>r</code> disappears — but it was never the only reference.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>log</code> is a static field, so it lives for the whole run. Anything inside it is reachable, and therefore <strong>cannot be collected</strong>, no matter how long ago the program stopped caring about it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">After five calls the size is 5 — five objects the collector must keep. Run this loop ten million times instead of five and the program dies with <code>OutOfMemoryError</code>, having never called anything resembling <code>malloc</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>log.clear()</code> releases the list's references. Now nothing reaches the readings and they become collectable.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>locals gone, list still holds 5
after clear: 0</code></pre><p>The fix is never "free the memory" — it is <code>clear()</code>, <code>remove()</code>, or not storing it in the first place. Not holding on <em>is</em> memory management in Java.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><strong>"The object is destroyed when the method returns."</strong> It is not. The <em>variable</em> is destroyed when the frame is popped; the object is destroyed only when nothing anywhere can reach it. In the second worked example the local <code>r</code> vanished five times over and not one object was freed, because the list still pointed at all of them.</p><p>How to notice it: whenever a method hands an object to a collection, a static field, or a caller, ask <em>who else now holds this?</em> That second holder, not the local variable, decides the lifetime.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Two facts answer most memory questions on this exam:</p><p>① <strong>Java cannot have a dangling pointer or a double free</strong> — the collector makes both impossible, so any option offering them is wrong by construction.<br>② <strong>Java can still run out of memory</strong>, by keeping references to objects it no longer uses. If an option says "memory leaks are impossible in Java", it is wrong; the accurate statement is "leaks caused by <em>forgetting to free</em> are impossible".</p><p>When asked "what reclaims this?", the answer is the garbage collector, and the trigger is unreachability — never <code>free</code>, never <code>delete</code>, never the end of a method.</p></div>"},"D286/u8/z8_2":{"body":"<p>A linked list is the clearest demonstration of references doing structural work: each node holds a value and a reference to the next node, and the list is nothing but that chain.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;              // a reference to another Node — null marks the end
    Node(int v) { value = v; }
}

Node a = new Node(1);
Node b = new Node(2);
a.next = b;                 // a -&gt; b -&gt; null

// walking the chain
Node cur = a;
while (cur != null) {
    System.out.println(cur.value);
    cur = cur.next;          // move along
}</pre><p>Compare this with an array (5.1). The array is one contiguous block with instant indexing and a fixed length; the list is scattered nodes with no indexing at all, but it grows by allocating one node. That trade — <code>get(i)</code> versus <code>insert</code> — is exactly the comparison drawn again in 14.2.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;            // a Node holding a reference to another Node
}

head -&gt; [4|*] -&gt; [8|*] -&gt; [15|null]</pre><p>Each node stores a value and a reference to the next; the last node's <code>next</code> is <code>null</code>, and that <code>null</code> is what ends every traversal. The <strong>head</strong> reference is the only way in — lose it and the whole list becomes unreachable and is collected.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (Node cur = head; cur != null; cur = cur.next)
    System.out.println(cur.data);

Node n = new Node();  n.data = 1;
n.next = head;  head = n;          // insert at the FRONT — O(1), no shifting</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Array / ArrayList</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Linked list</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">get element <em>i</em></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">instant — computed address</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">walk from the head — O(n)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">insert at the front</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">shift everything right</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(1)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">memory</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one contiguous block</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">scattered, plus a reference per node</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The trade is direct access against cheap insertion. Assigning <code>head = n.next</code> before saving <code>n</code> is how nodes get lost — nothing refers to them afterwards and the data is gone.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Node:</strong> an object holding a value and a reference to the next node</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Head:</strong> the reference to the first node; losing it loses the whole list</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Traversal:</strong> walking the chain by following next until null</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>null terminator:</strong> the null next reference marking the end of the list</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Reassigning the head before saving it — <code>head = head.next</code> without keeping the old head — makes the earlier nodes unreachable. In C that leaks; in Java the collector takes them, and either way the data is gone.</p></div><i data-tbc82="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A LINKED LIST AS IT ACTUALLY SITS IN MEMORY</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 390" style="width:100%; max-width:740px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk249a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><line x1="196" y1="30" x2="196" y2="196" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="100" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">STACK</text><text x="510" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">HEAP — nodes wherever there was room</text><rect x="46" y="108" width="110" height="40" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="101" y="132.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">head</text><text x="101" y="168" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the only way in</text><rect x="240" y="54" width="116" height="42" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="298" y1="54" x2="298" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="269" y="80" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4</text><circle cx="327" cy="75" r="4" fill="var(--text)"/><text x="298" y="44" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">first node</text><rect x="430" y="140" width="116" height="42" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="488" y1="140" x2="488" y2="182" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="459" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><circle cx="517" cy="161" r="4" fill="var(--text)"/><rect x="626" y="54" width="116" height="42" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="684" y1="54" x2="684" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="655" y="80" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><text x="713" y="80" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">null</text><text x="684" y="44" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">last node</text><line x1="156" y1="128" x2="238" y2="82" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk249a)"/><line x1="327" y1="75" x2="428" y2="152" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk249a)"/><line x1="517" y1="161" x2="624" y2="88" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk249a)"/><text x="694" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">a null next is what</text><text x="694" y="131" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">ends every traversal</text><line x1="30" y1="214" x2="790" y2="214" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="14" y="242" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">INSERT AT THE FRONT — TWO ASSIGNMENTS, NOTHING MOVES</text><rect x="330" y="262" width="76" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="368" y="282.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">head</text><rect x="130" y="306" width="116" height="42" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="188" y1="306" x2="188" y2="348" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="159" y="332" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><circle cx="217" cy="327" r="4" fill="var(--accent)"/><text x="72" y="322" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the new</text><text x="72" y="337" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">node</text><rect x="330" y="306" width="116" height="42" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="388" y1="306" x2="388" y2="348" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="359" y="332" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><circle cx="417" cy="327" r="4" fill="var(--text)"/><rect x="530" y="306" width="116" height="42" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="588" y1="306" x2="588" y2="348" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="559" y="332" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">15</text><text x="617" y="332" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">null</text><line x1="217" y1="327" x2="328" y2="327" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk249a)"/><text x="272" y="368" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">① n.next = head</text><line x1="417" y1="327" x2="528" y2="327" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk249a)"/><line x1="330" y1="278" x2="196" y2="302" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk249a)"/><text x="262" y="268" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">② head = n</text><text x="620" y="358" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">no element is copied or shifted — O(1)</text><text x="620" y="374" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the same insertion in an array is O(n)</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — BUILDING AND WALKING A THREE-NODE LIST</div><p>Nothing here is a list type — just a class holding a value and a reference, plus a loop that follows references until one is null.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;
    Node(int d) { data = d; }
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    Node head = new Node(4);
    head.next = new Node(8);
    head.next.next = new Node(15);

    int count = 0, sum = 0;
    for (Node cur = head; cur != null; cur = cur.next) {
        System.out.print(cur.data + " ");
        count++; sum += cur.data;
    }
    System.out.println();
    System.out.println("nodes=" + count + " sum=" + sum);
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new Node(4)</code> runs the constructor, which sets <code>data = 4</code>. <code>next</code> is never assigned, so it holds its default — <code>null</code>. Every fresh node ends the list until you say otherwise.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>head.next = new Node(8)</code> — the second node is created and its address is stored inside the first node. The chain is now 4 → 8 → null.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>head.next.next = new Node(15)</code> reads two references to get to node 8, then writes into <em>its</em> next field. Chain: 4 → 8 → 15 → null.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The loop starts with <code>cur = head</code>. Test <code>cur != null</code> — true, print 4, then <code>cur = cur.next</code> moves to node 8. Print 8, move to node 15. Print 15, move to <code>null</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The fourth test fails and the loop ends. Three iterations, one per node — <code>count</code> is 3 and <code>sum</code> is 4 + 8 + 15 = 27.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>4 8 15 
nodes=3 sum=27</code></pre><p>Notice there is no index anywhere. The only way to reach node 15 was to walk through nodes 4 and 8, which is the whole cost model of a linked list.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — INSERT AT THE FRONT, THEN UNLINK THE MIDDLE</div><p>Both operations are pure reference rewiring. No element moves and no memory is copied.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static String show(Node head) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("head");
    for (Node c = head; c != null; c = c.next) sb.append(" -&gt; ").append(c.data);
    return sb.append(" -&gt; null").toString();
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    Node head = new Node(8);
    head.next = new Node(15);
    System.out.println(show(head));

    Node n = new Node(4);      // INSERT AT FRONT
    n.next = head;             // ① new node points at the old first
    head = n;                  // ② head now names the new node
    System.out.println(show(head));

    head.next = head.next.next;   // UNLINK the second node
    System.out.println(show(head));
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Start: <code>head</code> → 8 → 15 → null.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Insert.</strong> <code>n.next = head</code> makes the new node point at 8. The order matters: had we written <code>head = n</code> first, <code>head</code> would no longer name node 8 and the rest of the list would be unreachable.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>head = n</code> completes it. Two assignments, list length 3, nothing shifted.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Unlink.</strong> <code>head.next.next</code> is node 15. Assigning it to <code>head.next</code> makes node 4 point straight at 15, so node 8 is skipped in every future traversal.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Node 8 still exists in memory for a moment, but nothing refers to it any more — it is unreachable, and the collector takes it (8.4). You do not free it and you must not try.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>head -&gt; 8 -&gt; 15 -&gt; null
head -&gt; 4 -&gt; 8 -&gt; 15 -&gt; null
head -&gt; 4 -&gt; 15 -&gt; null</code></pre><p>Compare with an array: inserting 4 at the front would shift every element right, and deleting the middle would shift every later element left. Here both are two-line reference edits.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Linked-list questions on this exam are almost always one of three shapes, and all three are answered by drawing boxes and arrows rather than by reading the code twice.</p><p>① <strong>"How many nodes does the loop visit?"</strong> — count nodes, not statements. A <code>while (cur != null)</code> loop runs exactly once per node.<br>② <strong>"Which line loses data?"</strong> — look for a reference being overwritten before it has been saved. <code>head = head.next</code> with nothing else holding the old head is the classic.<br>③ <strong>"Where does the traversal stop?"</strong> — at the first <code>null</code>. If a question sets the last node's <code>next</code> back to <code>head</code>, the loop never ends.</p><p>And know the cost pair cold: <strong>insert at front O(1), access by index O(n)</strong> — exactly the reverse of an array.</p></div>"},"D286/u8/z8_3":{"body":"<p>Java splits memory in two, and knowing which region a value is in answers most questions about lifetime.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">STACK                                HEAP
one frame per active call            all objects, created by new
holds parameters and locals          holds fields
popped automatically on return       reclaimed by garbage collection
fast, small, fixed size              larger, managed
StackOverflowError when too deep     OutOfMemoryError when exhausted</pre><p>A local variable of object type sits on the stack and holds a reference into the heap. So the variable and the object have different lifetimes: when the method returns, the variable disappears immediately, while the object waits until nothing else points at it.</p><p>That gap is exactly why returning an array from a method works (6.20) — the array itself was never on the stack.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Stack</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Heap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">holds</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">stack frames: parameters, local variables</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">every object created with <code>new</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a local <code>int</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the value itself</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">—</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a local <code>Student s</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the <em>reference</em></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the Student object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">fields of an object</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">—</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">inside the object, on the heap</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">freed when</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the method returns — automatic</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">nothing can reach it — garbage collector</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">runs out</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>StackOverflowError</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>OutOfMemoryError</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">void f() {
    int n = 5;                     // n: on the stack
    int[] a = new int[3];          // a: on the stack, the ARRAY: on the heap
}                                  // n and a vanish; the array survives only
                                   // if something else still refers to it</pre><p>This is why a method can return a locally created object safely: the <em>variable</em> died with the frame, but the object was never on the stack, and the returned reference keeps it alive.</p><p>It is also why deep recursion fails before memory does — each call adds a frame, and the stack is small compared with the heap.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stack:</strong> per-call memory for parameters and locals; freed automatically on return</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Heap:</strong> the region where all objects live</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>StackOverflowError:</strong> too many nested calls — the failure mode of runaway recursion (11.8)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>OutOfMemoryError:</strong> the heap is full and nothing can be collected</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Every primitive local is on the stack. Every object, including arrays and Strings, is on the heap — even when the variable naming it is a local.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → Nested, Inner and Anonymous Classes</strong></p><i data-d286fx16="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — STACK HOLDS FRAMES, HEAP HOLDS OBJECTS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 244" style="width:100%; max-width:550px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk83a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="40" y="30" width="180" height="170" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="130" y="50" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">STACK</text><rect x="60" y="62" width="140" height="34" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="130" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">method frame</text><rect x="60" y="102" width="140" height="34" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="130" y="123" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">main frame</text><text x="130" y="158" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">locals + parameters</text><text x="130" y="172" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">freed automatically on return</text><rect x="280" y="30" width="200" height="170" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="380" y="50" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">HEAP</text><rect x="300" y="62" width="70" height="40" rx="8" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="335" y="86" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">obj</text><rect x="390" y="62" width="70" height="40" rx="8" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="425" y="86" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">array</text><rect x="300" y="116" width="70" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="335" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no refs</text><text x="380" y="172" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">everything made with new;</text><text x="380" y="186" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">garbage collector reclaims the unreachable</text><line x1="160" y1="74" x2="298" y2="78" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk83a)"/><text x="260" y="222" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">References live on the stack; the objects they point to live on the heap. When the last</text><text x="260" y="236" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">reference disappears, the object becomes garbage — eligible for collection, not instantly deleted.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbc83="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — RETURNING AN ARRAY FROM A METHOD — WHY IT IS SAFE</div><p>A local array looks like it should die with the method. It does not, because the array was never on the stack — only the reference to it was.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static int[] makeScores() {
    int[] local = new int[3];      // local: STACK.  the array: HEAP
    local[0] = 91; local[1] = 78; local[2] = 84;
    return local;                   // returns the ADDRESS, not the array
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] scores = makeScores();
    System.out.println(scores.length + " " + scores[0] + " " + scores[2]);

    int[] alias = scores;
    alias[0] = 100;
    System.out.println(scores[0]);

    int n = scores[0];
    n = 5;
    System.out.println(scores[0] + " " + n);
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Inside <code>makeScores</code>, the frame holds one stack slot named <code>local</code>. The three-element array itself is heap memory allocated by <code>new</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>return local;</code> copies the <em>address</em> out. When the frame is popped, the slot <code>local</code> is gone — the array is untouched, because it was never in that frame.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>scores</code> in main now holds that address, so the array is still reachable and prints <code>3 91 84</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>int[] alias = scores;</code> copies the address again. <code>alias[0] = 100</code> writes into the one heap array, so reading <code>scores[0]</code> shows 100. One object, two names.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>int n = scores[0];</code> copies a <strong>value</strong> out of the heap into a stack slot. Changing <code>n</code> to 5 cannot possibly affect the array, so the last line prints <code>100 5</code>.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>3 91 84
100
100 5</code></pre><p>Steps 4 and 5 are the same syntax doing two different things. <code>alias = scores</code> copies a reference; <code>n = scores[0]</code> copies an int. Which one you get depends entirely on the type.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — WHAT A METHOD CAN AND CANNOT CHANGE</div><p>Java passes everything by value. For a primitive the value is the number; for an object the value is the reference — and that single sentence predicts all four lines of this output.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static void bump(int prim, int[] arr, StringBuilder sb, String s) {
    prim = prim + 1;          // changes a COPY of the number
    arr[0] = arr[0] + 1;      // follows the reference into the heap
    sb.append("!");           // follows the reference into the heap
    s = s + "!";              // rebinds a COPY of the reference
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    int prim = 10;
    int[] arr = { 10 };
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("hi");
    String s = "hi";
    bump(prim, arr, sb, s);
    System.out.println(prim);
    System.out.println(arr[0]);
    System.out.println(sb);
    System.out.println(s);
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>prim</strong> — the parameter is a fresh stack slot in <code>bump</code>'s frame holding a copy of 10. Incrementing it edits that frame, which is then discarded. main still prints <strong>10</strong>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>arr</strong> — the parameter is a copy of the reference, but it points at main's array. <code>arr[0] = arr[0] + 1</code> writes into the heap object both frames can see, so main prints <strong>11</strong>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>sb</strong> — same story. <code>append</code> mutates the StringBuilder on the heap, so main prints <strong>hi!</strong>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>s</strong> — <code>s + "!"</code> creates a <em>new</em> String on the heap and stores its address in the parameter slot. main's <code>s</code> still points at the original, and Strings are immutable, so main prints <strong>hi</strong>.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>10
11
hi!
hi</code></pre><p>The rule that separates lines 3 and 4: <strong>mutating the object</strong> the reference points at is visible to the caller; <strong>reassigning the parameter</strong> is not, because you only overwrote a stack slot in the callee's own frame.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><strong>"Strings and arrays are small, so they live on the stack."</strong> Size has nothing to do with it. <em>Every</em> object created with <code>new</code> — arrays, Strings, ArrayLists, your own classes — is on the heap. The only things on the stack are stack frames: the parameters and locals of calls that are currently running, which for an object variable means the reference and nothing more.</p><p>How to notice it: ask "did a <code>new</code> make this?" If yes, the object is heap. The name you gave it may be a stack slot, but the name and the object are two different things with two different lifetimes — and confusing them is what makes questions about returning locals, aliasing and pass-by-value look hard.</p><p>The mirror-image error: <em>"a field is on the stack because the method that uses it is."</em> Fields live inside their object, on the heap, for as long as the object does.</p></div>"},"D286/u8/z8_4":{"body":"<p>The garbage collector reclaims objects that can no longer be reached from any live variable. Reachability, not counting, is the criterion — which is why two objects pointing at each other are still collected once nothing outside points to either.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Student s = new Student("Ada");
s = null;                     // the Ada object is now unreachable -&gt; collectable

Student a = new Student("A");
Student b = new Student("B");
a = b;                        // the "A" object is unreachable -&gt; collectable</pre><p>You cannot force collection and you cannot predict when it happens. <code>System.gc()</code> is a suggestion the JVM may ignore, and <code>finalize()</code> is deprecated and must not be relied on. The right way to release a scarce resource such as a file handle is <code>close()</code>, not the collector (9.4).</p><p>The collector reclaims an object when it becomes <strong>unreachable</strong> — no chain of references from a running method, a local variable or a static field leads to it. Not "when it goes out of scope" and not "when nobody is using it".</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Student s = new Student("Ana");
s = new Student("Ben");        // the Ana object is now unreachable -&gt; collectable

Student a = new Student("Cy");
Student b = a;
a = null;                      // still reachable through b — NOT collected</pre><p>Setting a reference to <code>null</code> does not free anything; it removes one path. If any other path remains the object stays. That is why a forgotten entry in a long-lived <code>ArrayList</code> is the classic Java leak.</p><p><code>System.gc()</code> is a <em>request</em> the JVM may ignore, and calling it is almost always a mistake. Collection timing is not something a program should depend on, which is also why finalizers are deprecated — there is no guarantee they run at all.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Garbage collector:</strong> the JVM component that reclaims unreachable objects</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Reachability:</strong> whether a chain of references from a live variable leads to the object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>System.gc():</strong> a request to run collection; not a guarantee</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Memory leak (in Java):</strong> keeping references to objects you no longer need, so they can never be collected</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Setting a variable to <code>null</code> does not delete the object; it removes one reference. If any other reference remains, the object stays alive — which is the aliasing point from 7.10 seen from the other side.</p></div><i data-tbc84="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — COLLECTED WHEN THE LAST REFERENCE DROPS — NOT BEFORE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 430" style="width:100%; max-width:740px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk250a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="200" y="24" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">BEFORE  a = b</text><rect x="26" y="36" width="360" height="150" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="74" y="58" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ROOTS</text><rect x="40" y="70" width="68" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="91.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a</text><rect x="40" y="122" width="68" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="143.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">b</text><rect x="230" y="70" width="130" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="295" y="91.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sensor "S-1"</text><rect x="230" y="122" width="130" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="295" y="143.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sensor "S-2"</text><line x1="108" y1="87" x2="228" y2="87" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><line x1="108" y1="139" x2="228" y2="139" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><text x="618" y="24" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">AFTER  a = b</text><rect x="444" y="36" width="360" height="150" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="492" y="58" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ROOTS</text><rect x="458" y="70" width="68" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="492" y="91.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a</text><rect x="458" y="122" width="68" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="492" y="143.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">b</text><rect x="648" y="70" width="130" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="713" y="91.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sensor "S-1"</text><rect x="648" y="122" width="130" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="713" y="143.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sensor "S-2"</text><line x1="526" y1="87" x2="640" y2="130" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><line x1="526" y1="139" x2="646" y2="139" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><text x="713" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">no arrow in → GARBAGE</text><line x1="26" y1="208" x2="804" y2="208" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="230" y="240" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">A CYCLE IS STILL GARBAGE — REACHABILITY, NOT COUNTING</text><rect x="26" y="252" width="360" height="158" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="44" y="276" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ROOTS:  x = null,  y = null  → nothing points in</text><rect x="44" y="306" width="126" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="107" y="328.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Cart "Ana"</text><rect x="242" y="306" width="126" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="305" y="328.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Cart "Ben"</text><line x1="170" y1="316" x2="240" y2="316" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><line x1="240" y1="332" x2="170" y2="332" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><text x="205" y="310" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">peer</text><text x="205" y="349" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">peer</text><text x="206" y="380" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">each still has one incoming reference —</text><text x="206" y="396" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">and both are collected anyway</text><text x="624" y="240" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">THE ONE JAVA LEAK</text><rect x="444" y="252" width="360" height="158" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="462" y="300" width="118" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="521" y="319" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">static List</text><text x="521" y="335" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">lives for ever</text><rect x="650" y="272" width="130" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="715" y="291.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Reading</text><rect x="650" y="308" width="130" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="715" y="327.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Reading</text><rect x="650" y="344" width="130" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="715" y="363.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Reading</text><line x1="580" y1="316" x2="646" y2="288" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><line x1="580" y1="322" x2="646" y2="322" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><line x1="580" y1="328" x2="646" y2="356" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk250a)"/><text x="624" y="396" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">never used again, always reachable — the collector must keep every one</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — WHICH LINE MAKES WHICH OBJECT COLLECTABLE</div><p>Exam questions phrase this as "after which statement is object X eligible for garbage collection?". Answer it by tracking arrows, one line at a time.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Sensor {
    String id;
    Sensor(String id) { this.id = id; }
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    Sensor a = new Sensor("S-1");   // line 1
    Sensor b = new Sensor("S-2");   // line 2
    Sensor c = a;                    // line 3
    a = b;                           // line 4
    System.out.println(a.id + " " + b.id + " " + c.id);
    c = null;                        // line 5
    System.out.println(a.id + " " + b.id);
    b = null;                        // line 6
    System.out.println(a.id);
}</code></pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">After line</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Points at S-1</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Points at S-2</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Eligible for collection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>a</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>a</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>b</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>a</code>, <code>c</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>b</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>c</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing — <code>c</code> still holds S-1</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">5</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><em>nobody</em></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>S-1</strong> — line 5 dropped the last reference</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">6</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><em>nobody</em></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>a</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">still only S-1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Line 4 is the tempting answer and it is wrong. <code>a = b</code> does remove <em>a</em> path to S-1, but line 3 made a second one through <code>c</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Line 5 removes that last path, so S-1 becomes unreachable exactly there.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Line 6 sets <code>b</code> to null, but <code>a</code> also points at S-2, so S-2 survives. It is only collectable when <code>main</code> ends.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Every print still works, because reassigning references never harms the objects they used to name.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>S-2 S-2 S-1
S-2 S-2
S-2</code></pre><p>The last printed line proves the point: after <code>b = null</code>, S-2 is perfectly usable through <code>a</code>.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — TWO OBJECTS POINTING AT EACH OTHER — BOTH COLLECTED</div><p>Some languages reclaim memory by counting references, and a cycle defeats them: each object has a count of 1 for ever. Java asks a different question, so the cycle is not special.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Cart {
    String owner; Cart peer;
    Cart(String o) { owner = o; }
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    Cart x = new Cart("Ana");
    Cart y = new Cart("Ben");
    x.peer = y;
    y.peer = x;                       // a cycle: x -&gt; y -&gt; x
    System.out.println(x.peer.owner + " " + y.peer.owner);
    System.out.println(x.peer.peer.owner);
    x = null;
    y = null;
    System.out.println("both roots dropped");
    System.gc();
    System.out.println("gc requested, program continues");
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>x.peer = y</code> and <code>y.peer = x</code> build the cycle. Following it twice — <code>x.peer.peer</code> — arrives back at the Ana cart, which is why the second line prints <code>Ana</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">After <code>x = null; y = null;</code> every reference to either object is <em>inside the other object</em>. A reference count would still say 1 apiece.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Java's test is different: <strong>can a chain of references starting from a root reach it?</strong> Roots are the locals of running methods and static fields. No root leads into the pair, so the whole cycle is garbage and both are collected together.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>System.gc()</code> is a request, not a command. The JVM may run a collection, may run a partial one, or may do nothing — which is why nothing about the output can depend on it, and why the program simply carries on to the final line.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>Ben Ana
Ana
both roots dropped
gc requested, program continues</code></pre><p>Nothing observable tells you when — or whether — the two carts were reclaimed. That is by design: collection timing is not part of the language's contract with your program.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><strong>The eligibility question, answered in ten seconds.</strong> Number the lines, then for the object named in the question write down every variable that currently points at it. The answer is the first line after which that list becomes empty.</p><p>Three traps built into these questions:</p><p>① a second reference created earlier (<code>Sensor c = a;</code>) that the question hopes you forgot;<br>② an object still inside an <code>ArrayList</code> or a static field, which counts as reachable;<br>③ a cycle, which does <strong>not</strong> save anything — if no root reaches the cycle, all of it goes.</p><p>Also memorise the exact wording: an object becomes <strong>eligible for</strong> collection. Java never guarantees it <em>is</em> collected, so any option saying "the memory is freed immediately" or "<code>System.gc()</code> forces collection" is wrong.</p></div>"},"D286/u8/z8_5":{"body":"<p>Scope (4.10, 6.11) and garbage collection meet here: when a variable goes out of scope, the reference it held disappears, and if it was the last one the object becomes collectable at that moment.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">void process() {
    {
        Student temp = new Student("X");
        // temp is usable here
    }                        // temp out of scope -&gt; the object is collectable

    ArrayList<student> cache = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    cache.add(new Student("Y"));
    // as long as cache is reachable, EVERY Student in it stays alive
}</student></pre><p>The second half is where real leaks come from. A long-lived collection holds references, so anything added to it is immune to collection until it is removed. A cache nobody ever clears is the standard Java memory leak, and no amount of setting other variables to null helps.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
    Student s = new Student("x");   // 1000 objects created
}                                   // each becomes unreachable at the brace

ArrayList&lt;Student&gt; kept = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) {
    kept.add(new Student("x"));     // the list still refers to all 1000
}                                   // none can be collected</pre><p>Scope ending makes an object <em>eligible</em> only if that variable was the last route to it. The two loops differ in exactly that: the second stores a second reference somewhere that outlives the loop.</p><p>Circular references are not a problem for Java. A refers to B and B refers to A, but if nothing outside reaches either, both are unreachable and both are collected — reachability from a root, not reference counting, is the test.</p><p>Practical guidance is short: let variables fall out of scope naturally, remove things from collections when you are done, and do not sprinkle <code>= null</code> around.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Out of scope:</strong> past the closing brace, where the name no longer exists</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Eligible for collection:</strong> unreachable, and therefore collectable at some unspecified later time</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Retained reference:</strong> a reference held by a long-lived object that keeps another object alive</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>For an exam question asking when an object becomes eligible for collection, look for the line where the <em>last</em> reference to it is overwritten, reassigned or falls out of scope.</p></div><i data-tbc85="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — SCOPE ENDING IS NOT THE SAME AS BEING COLLECTED</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 800 360" style="width:100%; max-width:720px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk251a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="14" y="26" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">LOOP A — the local is the only reference</text><rect x="34" y="38" width="300" height="116" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="184" y="58" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {</text><rect x="60" y="70" width="116" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="118" y="92.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">t</text><line x1="176" y1="88" x2="236" y2="88" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk251a)"/><rect x="236" y="70" width="78" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="275" y="92.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Ticket</text><text x="184" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}   ← t's scope ends here</text><line x1="354" y1="96" x2="400" y2="96" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk251a)"/><rect x="404" y="62" width="190" height="68" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="499" y="93" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">UNREACHABLE</text><text x="499" y="109" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no arrow left → collectable</text><text x="690" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">1000 objects,</text><text x="690" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">all collectable</text><line x1="30" y1="178" x2="770" y2="178" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="14" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">LOOP B — a second reference outlives the scope</text><rect x="34" y="218" width="300" height="116" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="184" y="238" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {</text><rect x="60" y="250" width="116" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="118" y="272.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">t</text><line x1="176" y1="268" x2="236" y2="268" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#fk251a)"/><rect x="236" y="250" width="78" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="275" y="272.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Ticket</text><text x="184" y="310" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}   audit.add(t) ran too</text><rect x="404" y="242" width="132" height="68" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="470" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">audit</text><text x="470" y="289" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">ArrayList, still in scope</text><line x1="404" y1="276" x2="316" y2="270" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk251a)"/><line x1="536" y1="276" x2="600" y2="276" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk251a)"/><rect x="600" y="242" width="176" height="68" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="688" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">STILL REACHABLE</text><text x="688" y="289" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">1000 objects retained</text><text x="400" y="350" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the closing brace destroys the NAME; the object dies only when the last arrow does</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — THE SAME LOOP, TWICE, WITH ONE LINE OF DIFFERENCE</div><p>Both loops create a thousand objects. One loop leaves nothing behind; the other retains every single one — and the only difference is a single <code>add</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Ticket { int id; Ticket(int id){ this.id = id; } }

public static void main(String[] args) {
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 1000; i++) {
        Ticket t = new Ticket(i);          // unreachable at the closing brace
    }
    System.out.println("loop A finished");

    ArrayList&lt;Ticket&gt; audit = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 1000; i++) {
        Ticket t = new Ticket(i);
        audit.add(t);                      // a SECOND reference outlives the loop
    }
    System.out.println("loop B kept " + audit.size() + " objects alive");
    System.out.println("first id still readable: " + audit.get(0).id);
    audit.clear();
    System.out.println("after clear: " + audit.size());
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Loop A.</strong> On each iteration <code>t</code> is created, assigned, and goes out of scope at the closing brace. At that moment nothing else refers to that Ticket, so it becomes eligible immediately — one object born and abandoned a thousand times over.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Loop B.</strong> <code>audit.add(t)</code> stores a copy of the reference inside the list. When <code>t</code> goes out of scope the object still has one arrow pointing at it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>audit</code> itself is a local of <code>main</code>, which runs for the whole program, so every element stays reachable. <code>audit.size()</code> printing 1000 is direct evidence: you cannot have a size of 1000 without holding 1000 references.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>audit.get(0).id</code> reads the very first Ticket, created a thousand iterations earlier — it was never collected because it was never unreachable.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>audit.clear()</code> drops all 1000 references at once, and only then does loop B's worth of objects become collectable.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>loop A finished
loop B kept 1000 objects alive
first id still readable: 1
after clear: 0</code></pre><p>Scope ended identically in both loops. Lifetime did not. Scope governs <em>names</em>; reachability governs <em>objects</em>.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A STATIC FIELD OUTLIVES EVERY BLOCK</div><p>Static fields are garbage-collection roots: they are reachable for the entire run, so anything they point at is too, regardless of where it was created.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static class Session { String user; Session(String u){ user = u; } }
static Session current;                    // a static field = a GC ROOT

public static void main(String[] args) {
    {
        Session temp = new Session("guest");
        System.out.println("inside block: " + temp.user);
    }                                      // temp out of scope
    System.out.println("block ended");

    {
        Session temp = new Session("ada");
        current = temp;                    // stored in the static field
    }                                      // temp out of scope AGAIN
    System.out.println("still alive via static: " + current.user);
    current = null;
    System.out.println("now unreachable");
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The first block creates the "guest" session and lets it go. <code>temp</code> was the only reference, so at the closing brace the object is eligible — and the name <code>temp</code> can be reused afterwards, because it no longer exists.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The second block creates "ada" and copies the reference into <code>current</code>. Now two references exist; one is about to die and one is not.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">At that closing brace <code>temp</code> is gone, yet <code>current.user</code> reads "ada" perfectly. A static field kept an object alive past the end of the scope it was created in.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>current = null</code> is the line that finally makes the ada session collectable — and it is also the only cleanup this program can do, since there is no other way to reach it.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>inside block: guest
block ended
still alive via static: ada
now unreachable</code></pre><p>This is the whole anatomy of a real Java memory leak: a long-lived root (a static field, a cache, a listener list) holding references to short-lived objects. Nothing is broken, nothing is illegal, and memory grows until the program dies.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><strong>"Setting variables to <code>null</code> helps the garbage collector, so do it everywhere."</strong> It does not. A local that is about to go out of scope is already about to lose its reference — the assignment is noise, and scattering <code>= null</code> through a method makes it harder to read while achieving nothing.</p><p><code>= null</code> is worth writing in exactly one situation: a <strong>long-lived</strong> field that is holding an object you have finished with, like <code>current = null</code> above, where nothing else will drop that reference for you.</p><p>The related misconception is the opposite one: <strong>"two objects pointing at each other leak in Java."</strong> They do not. Java tests reachability from roots, not reference counts, so an unreferenced cycle is collected whole. If you remember one sentence from this section, make it: <em>the question is never how many references exist, only whether a root can reach it.</em></p></div>"},"D286/u8/z8_6":{"body":"<p>The employee list ties Unit 7 and Unit 8 together: an <code>ArrayList</code> holds references, so removing from the list is what makes an object collectable.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ArrayList<employee> staff = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
staff.add(new Employee("Ada", 95000));      // the only reference is in the list
staff.add(new Employee("Alan", 88000));

staff.remove(0);        // the Ada object is now unreachable -&gt; collectable
staff.clear();          // every element unreachable

// but this does NOT make it collectable:
Employee held = staff.get(0);
staff.clear();          // held still points at it — object stays alive</employee></pre><p>The last three lines are the whole lesson. Removing from a collection releases the collection’s reference and nothing else; any other variable you handed the object to keeps it alive.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">ArrayList&lt;Employee&gt; staff = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
staff.add(new Employee("Ana", 22.50));

for (int i = staff.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--)          // remove BACKWARDS
    if (staff.get(i).getRate() &lt; 15) staff.remove(i);

double total = 0;
for (Employee e : staff) total += e.payFor(40);</pre><p>Compared with the oversize array of 6.16, the list carries its own count — <code>staff.size()</code> is always right, with no second variable to fall out of step, and adding never needs a capacity check.</p><p>Two things still catch people. <code>remove(i)</code> shifts everything after <em>i</em> left, so a forward loop skips the element that moved into the gap; going backwards avoids it. And the list stores <em>references</em>, so modifying an object through the list changes the same object the rest of the program sees.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Collection as an owner:</strong> the list holds references, so membership determines lifetime</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>clear():</strong> removes every element, releasing the list’s references</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Escaped reference:</strong> a reference handed out to a caller, which then outlives the collection</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is the same escape problem as returning a private field from a getter (7.18). Once a reference leaves the class that owns it, that class no longer controls the object’s lifetime or its state.</p></div><i data-tbc86="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE LIST HOLDS REFERENCES — UNTIL YOU HAND ONE OUT</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 400" style="width:100%; max-width:740px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk252a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="210" y="24" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">BEFORE  staff.clear()</text><rect x="28" y="36" width="372" height="150" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="46" y="76" width="96" height="70" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="94" y="108" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">staff</text><text x="94" y="124" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">ArrayList</text><rect x="158" y="62" width="44" height="30" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="180" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><rect x="158" y="96" width="44" height="30" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="180" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><rect x="158" y="130" width="44" height="30" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="180" y="150" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><line x1="142" y1="111" x2="158" y2="111" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="258" y="60" width="124" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="320" y="80.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Ana</text><rect x="258" y="96" width="124" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="320" y="116.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Ben</text><rect x="258" y="132" width="124" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="320" y="152.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Cy</text><line x1="202" y1="77" x2="256" y2="76" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk252a)"/><line x1="202" y1="111" x2="256" y2="112" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk252a)"/><line x1="202" y1="145" x2="256" y2="148" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk252a)"/><text x="624" y="24" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">AFTER  staff.clear()</text><rect x="430" y="36" width="372" height="150" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="448" y="76" width="96" height="70" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="496" y="108" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">staff</text><text x="496" y="124" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">size() == 0</text><rect x="660" y="60" width="124" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="722" y="80.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Ana</text><rect x="660" y="96" width="124" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="722" y="116.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Ben</text><rect x="660" y="132" width="124" height="32" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="722" y="152.5" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Cy</text><text x="575" y="180" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">no arrows left → all three collectable</text><line x1="28" y1="208" x2="802" y2="208" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="360" y="240" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">UNLESS A REFERENCE ESCAPED FIRST:  Employee held = staff.get(0);</text><rect x="28" y="252" width="774" height="136" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="64" y="266" width="110" height="40" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="119" y="283" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">staff</text><text x="119" y="299" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">cleared</text><rect x="64" y="328" width="110" height="40" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="119" y="345" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">held</text><text x="119" y="361" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a local</text><rect x="348" y="300" width="156" height="38" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="426" y="323.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Employee Ana</text><line x1="174" y1="286" x2="346" y2="306" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><line x1="252" y1="288" x2="268" y2="306" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="268" y1="288" x2="252" y2="306" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="240" y="274" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">reference released by clear()</text><line x1="174" y1="348" x2="346" y2="330" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk252a)"/><text x="250" y="366" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">still points at it</text><line x1="504" y1="319" x2="560" y2="319" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk252a)"/><rect x="564" y="301" width="196" height="36" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/><text x="662" y="323.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">STAYS ALIVE</text><text x="662" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">and stays mutable through held</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — REMOVING WHILE LOOPING — BACKWARDS, AND WHY</div><p>Filtering a list in place is the single most common ArrayList bug in this course. Here is the correct version, traced.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>ArrayList&lt;Employee&gt; staff = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
staff.add(new Employee("Ana", 22.50));
staff.add(new Employee("Ben", 14.00));
staff.add(new Employee("Cy",  13.25));
staff.add(new Employee("Dee", 31.00));

for (int i = staff.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {      // BACKWARDS
    if (staff.get(i).rate &lt; 15) { staff.remove(i); }
}
for (Employee e : staff) { System.out.print(e.name + " "); }
System.out.println();
System.out.println("size=" + staff.size());</code></pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">i</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Element at i</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">rate &lt; 15?</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">List after this step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Dee</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">no (31.00)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Ana, Ben, Cy, Dee</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Cy</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>yes</strong> (13.25)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Ana, Ben, Dee</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Ben</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>yes</strong> (14.00)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Ana, Dee</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">0</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Ana</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">no (22.50)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Ana, Dee</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>remove(i)</code> shifts every element <em>after</em> index <code>i</code> one place left. Going backwards, everything after <code>i</code> has already been examined, so the shift can never move an unchecked element past the cursor.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Starting at <code>staff.size() - 1</code> — index 3, not 4 — is what puts the cursor on the last element rather than one past it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The removals leave Ana and Dee, in their original relative order.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Once removed, the Ben and Cy objects have no reference anywhere and become collectable. That is 8.4 applied: the list was their only owner.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>Ana Dee 
size=2</code></pre><p>Two clean alternatives exist for the same job: <code>staff.removeIf(e -&gt; e.rate &lt; 15);</code>, or an explicit <code>Iterator</code> with <code>it.remove()</code>. Both avoid index arithmetic entirely.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — THE FORWARD LOOP SKIPS — AND A REFERENCE ESCAPES</div><p>The same filter written forwards produces a wrong answer <em>silently</em>, and the second half of this example shows why <code>clear()</code> does not always mean gone.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; staff.size(); i++) {          // FORWARD — buggy
    if (staff.get(i).rate &lt; 15) { staff.remove(i); }
}
for (Employee e : staff) { System.out.print(e.name + " "); }
System.out.println();

Employee held = staff.get(0);
staff.clear();
System.out.println("list size " + staff.size() + ", held still reads " + held.name);
held.rate = 25.00;
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", held.rate);</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>i = 0</code>: Ana, 22.50, kept. List is Ana, Ben, Cy, Dee.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>i = 1</code>: Ben, 14.00, removed. <strong>Cy shifts down into index 1</strong> and the list is Ana, Cy, Dee.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>i = 2</code>: the loop increments past the element it just pulled into position 1, so it reads <strong>Dee</strong>, not Cy. Cy is never tested and survives the filter.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The loop ends with Ana, Cy, Dee printed — one element that should have gone is still there, with no exception and no warning.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Employee held = staff.get(0);</code> copies a reference out of the list. <code>staff.clear()</code> then empties the list, so <code>size()</code> is 0.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The Ana object is still perfectly alive, because <code>held</code> points at it: reading its name works and writing <code>held.rate = 25.00</code> works. The list gave up its reference; the escaped one is still holding on.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>Ana Cy Dee 
list size 0, held still reads Ana
25.00</code></pre><p>The second half is the lifetime lesson: <strong>membership in a collection is one reference among possibly many</strong>. Removing is only the last word if nobody else took a copy.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><strong>Removing inside a forward-counting loop.</strong> Because <code>remove(i)</code> shifts everything left, the element that slides into index <code>i</code> is skipped when <code>i++</code> runs. The result is not a crash — it is a list that quietly kept an element it should have dropped, which is far harder to spot.</p><p>How to notice it: the bug only shows when <strong>two removable elements are adjacent</strong>. In the trace above, Ben and Cy sit next to each other and Cy survives; had Cy been elsewhere in the list, the forward loop would have looked correct. Test with consecutive matches.</p><p>The other half of the same trap: modifying the list inside a <strong>for-each</strong> loop. <code>for (Employee e : staff) staff.remove(e);</code> compiles and then throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> at run time. Use a backwards index loop, <code>removeIf</code>, or an explicit iterator — never a for-each.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Three things this section is tested on, in order of frequency:</p><p>① <strong>What is in the list after a removal loop?</strong> Write the list out and cross elements off as you trace; do not reason about it in your head. A forward loop with <code>remove</code> skips the element after each removal — if the options include a list that is one element too long, that is the distractor for the backwards-loop answer, and vice versa.<br>② <strong>When is an element collectable?</strong> When the list no longer holds it <em>and</em> no variable took a copy. <code>clear()</code> alone is not enough if a <code>get</code> happened first.<br>③ <strong>Does changing an object through the list change it everywhere?</strong> Yes — the list stores references, so <code>staff.get(0).rate = 30</code> and <code>held.rate = 30</code> edit the same object.</p><p>And remember <code>size()</code> is a method call on an ArrayList — <code>staff.size()</code> — while an array uses the field <code>arr.length</code> and a String uses <code>s.length()</code>. Exams mix all three deliberately.</p></div>"},"D286/u9/z9_1":{"body":"<p>Everything you have printed or read so far went through a <strong>stream</strong>: an ordered sequence of data flowing in one direction. Naming the abstraction matters, because file input in 9.4 is the same code as keyboard input with a different source.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">System.out    standard output — the screen
System.err    standard error  — also the screen, but a separate stream
System.in     standard input  — the keyboard

Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);         // read from the keyboard
Scanner file = new Scanner(new File("in.txt")); // read from a file — same methods</pre><p>Because <code>Scanner</code> takes the source as a constructor argument, a method that accepts a <code>Scanner</code> (6.14) works with either without changing a line. That is the practical payoff of the abstraction.</p><h3>When the input is not what you asked for</h3><p><code>nextInt()</code> assumes the next token really is an integer. Hand it <code>"abc"</code> and it throws <strong>InputMismatchException</strong> — and, importantly, it does <em>not</em> consume the bad token, so a naive retry loop reads the same rubbish for ever.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
int age;
while (true) {
    if (scnr.hasNextInt()) { age = scnr.nextInt(); break; }
    scnr.next();                       // DISCARD the bad token — essential
    System.out.println("Numbers only, please:");
}</pre><p>Two safe patterns exist: test first with <code>hasNextInt()</code> as above, or catch <code>InputMismatchException</code> and call <code>scnr.next()</code> inside the catch to clear it. Either way the discard is what stops the infinite loop.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Stream</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Is</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>System.in</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">standard input</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">usually wrapped in a <code>Scanner</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>System.out</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">standard output</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>println</code>, <code>print</code>, <code>printf</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>System.err</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">standard error</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">error messages, kept separate from output</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A <strong>stream</strong> is a one-way sequence of data with a source or a destination. The point of the abstraction is that the same code shape reads from the keyboard, a file or a String — only the object you construct changes.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Scanner kb   = new Scanner(System.in);          // keyboard
Scanner file = new Scanner(new File("in.txt"));  // file
Scanner str  = new Scanner("10 20 30");          // a String</pre><p><code>println</code> adds a line ending and <code>print</code> does not — which is why building output with <code>print</code> in a loop and one <code>println</code> after it is the standard way to put values on one line.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stream:</strong> a sequence of data flowing to or from a source</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Standard output / input / error:</strong> the three streams every program starts with</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>System.out.print vs println:</strong> print leaves the cursor on the line; println ends it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Buffering:</strong> holding output briefly before writing it, for efficiency</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>System.err</code> exists so error messages can be separated from normal output when a program is redirected to a file. They interleave unpredictably on screen because they are buffered differently.</p></div><i data-d286fx17="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — STREAMS: DATA FLOWING OUT AND IN</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 500 186" style="width:100%; max-width:540px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk84a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="40" width="110" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="75" y="58" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">your program</text><text x="75" y="72" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">println / printf</text><line x1="130" y1="60" x2="200" y2="60" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk84a)"/><rect x="200" y="40" width="120" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="260" y="58" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">output stream</text><text x="260" y="72" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">System.out (buffered)</text><line x1="320" y1="60" x2="390" y2="60" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk84a)"/><rect x="390" y="40" width="90" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="435" y="64" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">screen</text><rect x="390" y="110" width="90" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="435" y="134" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">keyboard</text><line x1="390" y1="130" x2="320" y2="130" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk84a)"/><rect x="200" y="110" width="120" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="260" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">input stream</text><text x="260" y="142" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">System.in → Scanner</text><line x1="200" y1="130" x2="130" y2="130" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk84a)"/><rect x="20" y="110" width="110" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="75" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">your program</text><text x="75" y="142" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">next / nextInt</text><text x="250" y="178" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">A stream is a one-way flow of data. Scanner sits on an input stream and carves it into tokens.</text></svg></div></div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">printf specifier</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">means</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">printf("...", 3.14159) gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>%d</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">whole number (int/long)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>3</code> (with an int argument)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>%.2f</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">floating point, 2 decimals</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>3.14</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>%5.1f</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">width 5, 1 decimal, right-aligned</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;3.1</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>%s</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">string</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>text as-is</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>%n</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">newline</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">(line break)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><i data-tbc91="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — ONE METHOD, TWO SOURCES — THE POINT OF THE ABSTRACTION</div><p>Because <code>Scanner</code> takes its source as a constructor argument, a method that accepts a <code>Scanner</code> neither knows nor cares where the data came from.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>static int total(Scanner in) {          // works with ANY source
    int sum = 0;
    while (in.hasNextInt()) { sum += in.nextInt(); }
    return sum;
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner fromString = new Scanner("12 7 31");
    System.out.println(total(fromString));

    Scanner mixed = new Scanner("12 7 oops 31");
    System.out.println(total(mixed));
    System.out.println("stopped at: " + mixed.next());
    System.out.println(total(mixed));
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The first Scanner reads a String; <code>new Scanner(System.in)</code> or <code>new Scanner(new File("in.txt"))</code> would work with <em>the same</em> <code>total</code> method, unchanged. That substitutability is what "stream" buys you.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>hasNextInt()</code> is the loop control. It reports whether the <strong>next token</strong> can be read as an int — it does not consume anything, so the following <code>nextInt()</code> gets that token.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">First call: 12 + 7 + 31 = <strong>50</strong>. The loop ends because the stream is exhausted and <code>hasNextInt()</code> is false.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Second call: 12 + 7 = <strong>19</strong>, then <code>hasNextInt()</code> sees <code>oops</code> and returns false. The loop stops <em>at</em> the bad token, having consumed nothing of it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>mixed.next()</code> reads <code>oops</code> as a plain String, which advances the cursor past it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Calling <code>total</code> again on the same Scanner now resumes from <code>31</code> and returns <strong>31</strong>. A stream is a position as well as a source.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>50
19
stopped at: oops
31</code></pre><p>The last two lines are the practical shape of every "keep reading until the data runs out" loop: test, read, and when the test fails, deal with whatever stopped it.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — THE BAD-TOKEN LOOP — AND WHY IT HANGS WITHOUT THE DISCARD</div><p><code>nextInt()</code> on non-numeric input throws <strong>and leaves the token in the stream</strong>. That single fact is what turns a naive retry loop into an infinite one.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>Scanner scnr = new Scanner("abc 4.5 19 rest");
int age = -1;
while (age &lt; 0) {
    if (scnr.hasNextInt()) {
        age = scnr.nextInt();
    } else {
        String bad = scnr.next();          // DISCARD — essential
        System.out.println("rejected: " + bad);
    }
}
System.out.println("age = " + age);
System.out.println("next token still waiting: " + scnr.next());

Scanner s2 = new Scanner("abc");
try {
    int n = s2.nextInt();
} catch (InputMismatchException e) {
    System.out.println("caught: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
    System.out.println("token NOT consumed: " + s2.next());
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.65;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Token 1 is <code>abc</code>. <code>hasNextInt()</code> is false, so the else branch runs <code>scnr.next()</code>, which consumes and returns it — printing <code>rejected: abc</code> and, crucially, <strong>moving the cursor forward</strong>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Token 2 is <code>4.5</code>. <code>hasNextInt()</code> is <em>also false</em> — a decimal is not an int. It is rejected the same way. (<code>hasNextDouble()</code> would have accepted it.)</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Token 3 is <code>19</code>, so <code>nextInt()</code> reads it, <code>age</code> becomes 19 and the loop condition fails.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>scnr.next()</code> afterwards returns <code>rest</code>, proving the Scanner is sitting exactly where the loop left it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The second half shows the same fact from the exception side. <code>nextInt()</code> on <code>abc</code> throws <code>InputMismatchException</code>, and the very next <code>next()</code> still returns <code>abc</code> — the failed read consumed nothing.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">So a <code>try/catch</code> retry loop <strong>must</strong> call <code>scnr.next()</code> inside the catch. Without it, the loop retries the same unreadable token for ever and the program appears to freeze.</li></ol><div style="margin:9px 0 4px;"><span style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--text-3);">OUTPUT</span></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-3, var(--surface-2)); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; padding:9px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; margin:0 0 10px;"><code>rejected: abc
rejected: 4.5
age = 19
next token still waiting: rest
caught: InputMismatchException
token NOT consumed: abc</code></pre><p>Two safe patterns, then: <em>look before you leap</em> with <code>hasNextInt()</code>, or <em>try it and see</em> with <code>catch (InputMismatchException e) { scnr.next(); }</code>. Both work; only the versions that advance the cursor terminate.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><strong>Mixing <code>nextInt()</code> and <code>nextLine()</code>.</strong> <code>nextInt()</code> reads the number and stops <em>before</em> the newline that follows it. The next <code>nextLine()</code> therefore returns the empty remainder of that line rather than the line you wanted, and the program appears to skip an input.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; margin:10px 0;"><code>Scanner in = new Scanner("7\nAda Lovelace\n");
int n    = in.nextInt();     // reads 7, leaves the newline
String a = in.nextLine();    // returns "" — the rest of line 1
String b = in.nextLine();    // "Ada Lovelace" — what you wanted first time</code></pre><p>How to notice it: a prompt that seems to be ignored, or a String variable that is empty when you know the user typed something. <strong>The fix</strong> is one extra <code>in.nextLine();</code> immediately after the <code>nextInt()</code>, to swallow the leftover newline — or read everything with <code>nextLine()</code> and parse with <code>Integer.parseInt</code>.</p><p>The same trap applies to <code>nextDouble()</code>, <code>next()</code> and any other token-based read followed by a line-based one. Token reads stop at whitespace; line reads stop at newlines, and the two disagree about who owns it.</p></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u9_z9_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The compiler's definite-assignment rule, seen from both sides</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("field default: " + counter);
System.out.println("sum: " + safeSum(new int[] { 1, 2, 3 }));

int x;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1; i++) { x = 9; }
x = 10;
System.out.println(x);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">field default: 0
sum: 6
10</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>total = 0</code> is not decoration — without it the <code>total += x</code> would not compile.</li><li>The loop assigning <code>x</code> is not enough on its own: the compiler cannot prove a loop body runs at all, so it does not count as a definite assignment.</li><li>The explicit <code>x = 10</code> is what makes the print legal here.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Any accumulator you declare must be seeded on the same line — <code>int total = 0;</code>, <code>double sum = 0;</code>, <code>String out = "";</code>. Forgetting it is a compile error rather than a wrong answer, which on a graded coding task costs the whole item.</div>"},"D286/u9/z9_2":{"body":"<p><code>printf</code> gives you control over exactly how a value is rendered, which <code>println</code> cannot do. It takes a format string containing placeholders, followed by one value per placeholder.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">System.out.printf("%s is %d years old%n", name, age);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", 3.14159);          // 3.14   — rounded, not truncated
System.out.printf("%8.2f%n", 3.14159);         // "    3.14" — width 8, right aligned
System.out.printf("%-8s|%n", "hi");            // "hi      |" — left aligned

%d   integer        %f   floating point     %s   String
%c   char           %b   boolean            %%   a literal percent
%n   newline (portable)      \n  newline (Unix-style)</pre><p>Two details are reliably examined. <code>%.2f</code> <strong>rounds</strong> rather than truncating, so 3.567 prints as 3.57. And the number of arguments must match the number of placeholders exactly, or you get a runtime exception rather than a compile error.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">System.out.printf("%s scored %d (%.2f%%)%n", name, score, pct);
//                  |       |      |        |
//                  string  int    2 dp     literal % (doubled)</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Specifier</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">For</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%d</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">integer</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%f</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">floating point — 6 dp by default</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>3.140000</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%.2f</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">fixed to 2 decimals</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>3.14</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%s</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">String, or anything via <code>toString()</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Ana</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%c</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">char</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>A</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%8.2f</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">width 8, right-aligned, 2 dp</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>    3.14</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%-8s</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">width 8, <strong>left</strong>-aligned</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Ana     </code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>%n</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">newline</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Handing a <code>double</code> to <code>%d</code> throws <code>IllegalFormatConversionException</code> at run time — the compiler does not check the pairing, so a mismatch survives to the moment the line executes. The count must match too: too few arguments throws, extra ones are ignored.</p><p><code>String.format</code> takes the identical arguments and returns the String instead of printing it, which is what you want when the text is going somewhere other than the console.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Format specifier:</strong> a %-prefixed placeholder describing how to render one value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Precision:</strong> the .2 in %.2f — digits after the decimal point</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Field width:</strong> the 8 in %8.2f — minimum total characters, padded with spaces</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>String.format:</strong> the same formatting, returning a String instead of printing it</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>%d</code> with a <code>double</code> throws <code>IllegalFormatConversionException</code> at run time. The specifier must match the argument type — and the compiler will not check it for you.</p></div><i data-tbc92="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A FORMAT SPECIFIER, TAKEN APART AND APPLIED</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 820 420" style="width:100%; max-width:760px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk253a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="410" y="24" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ANATOMY OF  %-9.2f</text><rect x="210" y="36" width="72" height="42" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="246" y="61.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">%</text><rect x="290" y="36" width="72" height="42" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="326" y="61.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">-</text><rect x="370" y="36" width="72" height="42" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="406" y="61.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><rect x="450" y="36" width="72" height="42" rx="7" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="486" y="61.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">.2</text><rect x="530" y="36" width="72" height="42" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="566" y="61.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">f</text><line x1="246" y1="78" x2="246" y2="96" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="246" y="110" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">begins a</text><text x="246" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">specifier</text><line x1="326" y1="78" x2="326" y2="132" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="326" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">flag: LEFT-align</text><text x="326" y="158" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">(default is right)</text><line x1="406" y1="78" x2="406" y2="168" s