21891</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A counter in a static field survives across the calls.</li><li>The number of calls grows explosively with n.</li><li>That growth is why naive recursive Fibonacci is a classic example of a bad algorithm.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Leaving the debugging output in. It is fine while you are working and wrong in a submission — the marker sees pages of trace instead of the program's real output.</div>"},"D286/u11/z11_5":{"body":"<p>Creating a recursive method from scratch follows a fixed procedure, and following it beats intuition every time.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">1. State the problem for input n.
2. Find the smallest input you can answer with no work    -&gt; base case
3. Express the answer for n in terms of the answer for a smaller input
4. Check that every recursive call is CLOSER to the base case
5. Check the base case is actually REACHABLE from every path

// count digits
public static int digits(int n) {
    if (n &lt; 10) { return 1; }          // 2: one digit
    return 1 + digits(n / 10);         // 3: one more than the rest (4.3)
}</pre><p>Step 4 is the one people skip and the one that causes infinite recursion. <code>digits(n / 10)</code> shrinks because integer division truncates; <code>digits(n - 1)</code> would also shrink but would take far longer to reach the base case, and <code>digits(n)</code> never would.</p><p>Designing a recursive method is a fixed four-step order, and doing it out of order is what makes recursion feel like guesswork.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">1  What is the smallest input, and what is its answer?      -&gt; base case
2  How does one step make the problem smaller?              -&gt; recursive call
3  How do I build my answer from the smaller answer?        -&gt; combine
4  Does every path reach the base case?                     -&gt; check</pre><p>Step 3 requires <strong>trusting the recursion</strong>: assume the smaller call returns the right answer and use it. Trying to trace all the way down while writing the method is where people lose the thread — the trace is for debugging, not for designing.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static String reverse(String s) {
    if (s.length() &lt;= 1) return s;                     // 1
    return reverse(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0);      // 2 and 3
}</pre><p>A <strong>public wrapper</strong> is common when the recursion needs extra bookkeeping: <code>search(a, target)</code> calls the private <code>search(a, target, lo, hi)</code>, so callers do not have to supply indices they should not have to know about.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Termination argument:</strong> the reason every chain of calls reaches a base case</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Progress:</strong> each call must strictly reduce the problem size</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Infinite recursion:</strong> calls that never reach a base case; ends in StackOverflowError</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Negative or zero inputs are where base cases fail. <code>digits(0)</code> returns 1 correctly here; <code>digits(-5)</code> returns 1 too, which may or may not be what you wanted — decide deliberately.</p></div><i data-d286_z11_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the recipe</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk404a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1. what is the smallest case?</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">that is the base case</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2. how does n relate to n-1?</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">that is the recursive case</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3. does it shrink?</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">otherwise it never ends</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">4. trace a small input</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">by hand</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reversing a string by the recipe</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(reverse("a"));
System.out.println(reverse("abc"));
System.out.println(reverse("recursion"));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">a
cba
noisrucer</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Smallest case: a string of 0 or 1 characters is already reversed.</li><li>Otherwise: reverse everything after the first character, then put that character on the end.</li><li>Each call passes a shorter string, so it must reach the base case.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Counting a character</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(countChar("banana", 'a'));
System.out.println(countChar("banana", 'z'));
System.out.println(countChar("", 'a'));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3
