0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The empty string is the base case and returns 0.</li><li>Each call handles ONE character and delegates the rest.</li><li>The empty case is what makes the whole thing terminate.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Choosing a base case that some inputs skip over. Testing <code>s.length() == 1</code> instead of <code>&lt;= 1</code> means an empty string recurses past the base case and never stops.</div>"},"D286/u11/z11_6":{"body":"<p>The classic recursive maths methods are worth knowing by shape, because they appear in exam questions almost verbatim.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// factorial: n! = n * (n-1)!
static int fact(int n) { return (n &lt;= 1) ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1); }

// Fibonacci: F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2) — TWO recursive calls
static int fib(int n) { return (n &lt;= 1) ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2); }

// power: x^n = x * x^(n-1)
static double pow(double x, int n) { return (n == 0) ? 1 : x * pow(x, n - 1); }

// greatest common divisor — Euclid
static int gcd(int a, int b) { return (b == 0) ? a : gcd(b, a % b); }</pre><p><code>fib</code> is the odd one out and the interesting one: because it calls itself twice, the work grows exponentially and <code>fib(40)</code> recomputes the same values millions of times. It is the standard example of recursion being elegant and slow at once, and the fix — remembering results — is called memoisation.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int fib(int n) {
    if (n &lt;= 1) return n;                    // TWO base cases: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
}</pre><p>This is correct and catastrophically slow. Each call spawns two more, so the work doubles with every increment — O(2ⁿ). <code>fib(40)</code> makes over a billion calls, and <code>fib(5)</code> already computes <code>fib(2)</code> three separate times.</p><p>The fix is <strong>memoization</strong>: keep an array of answers already computed and return the stored one instead of recomputing. That turns O(2ⁿ) into O(n) without changing the shape of the method.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int gcd(int a, int b) { return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b); }
static int sumDigits(int n) { return n &lt; 10 ? n : n % 10 + sumDigits(n / 10); }</pre><p>Both of these shrink fast and reach their base case in a handful of calls, which is what makes recursion genuinely the clearer choice for them.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Factorial:</strong> n! = n × (n-1)!, with 0! = 1</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Fibonacci:</strong> F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2), with F(0)=0 and F(1)=1</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Exponential blow-up:</strong> two recursive calls per level giving roughly 2^n total calls</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Memoisation:</strong> caching computed results so each input is solved once</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Exam questions ask for the <em>number of calls</em> as often as the result. <code>fib(5)</code> makes 15 calls; counting them by drawing the tree is faster than reasoning about it.</p></div><i data-d286_z11_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — definitions that are already recursive</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk405a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">n! = n x (n-1)!</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">base: 0! = 1</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">base: 0 and 1</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">gcd(a,b) = gcd(b, a mod b)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">base: b = 0</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">power(b,e) = b x power(b,e-1)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">base: e = 0</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Three classics, each a direct translation</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(fact(6));
System.out.println(gcd(48, 36));
System.out.println(power(2, 10));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">720
12
1024</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each method is the mathematical definition written in Java.</li><li>The base case is the value the definition gives outright.</li><li>gcd shrinks fastest — the remainder drops quickly towards 0.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Recursive Fibonacci is correct and slow</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(fibSlow(20));
System.out.println(fibFast(20));
System.out.println(fibFast(70));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">6765
