190392490709135</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both agree on the answer.</li><li>The recursive version recomputes the same subproblems again and again.</li><li>The loop keeps only the last two values, so it stays fast even at n = 70.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Using <code>int</code> for factorial or Fibonacci beyond about n = 12 and n = 46. The value overflows silently and comes out negative — use <code>long</code>, and remember the range is the limit, not the algorithm.</div>"},"D286/u11/z11_7":{"body":"<p>Some problems need to try every possibility, and recursion is the natural way to express "choose one, solve the rest, then undo the choice". That undo step is what makes it <strong>backtracking</strong>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void permute(String prefix, String rest) {
    if (rest.isEmpty()) {
        System.out.println(prefix);           // base: nothing left to place
        return;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; rest.length(); i++) {
        permute(prefix + rest.charAt(i),
                rest.substring(0, i) + rest.substring(i + 1));
    }
}

permute("", "abc");     // abc acb bac bca cab cba</pre><p>The loop chooses each remaining character in turn as the next one; the recursion handles everything after it. There are n! permutations of n characters, so this is only usable for small inputs — a real limit, not a caution.</p><p>Exhaustive search is recursion's natural home: at each step, try every choice, recurse, then undo the choice before trying the next. The undo is <strong>backtracking</strong>, and leaving it out is the standard bug — the shared state carries into branches it should not.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void subsets(int[] a, int i, List&lt;Integer&gt; cur) {
    if (i == a.length) { System.out.println(cur); return; }   // base case
    subsets(a, i + 1, cur);                    // choice 1: leave a[i] out
    cur.add(a[i]);
    subsets(a, i + 1, cur);                    // choice 2: put a[i] in
    cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);                // UNDO — backtrack
}</pre><p>Every element is either in or out, so a set of <em>n</em> elements has <strong>2ⁿ</strong> subsets; arrangements of all <em>n</em> give <strong>n!</strong> permutations. Both grow fast enough that exhaustive search is only practical for small <em>n</em> — 20 elements is a million subsets, 20 items is 2.4 × 10¹⁸ permutations.</p><p><strong>Pruning</strong> is abandoning a branch as soon as it cannot lead to a solution. It does not change the worst case but usually makes the difference between usable and not.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Backtracking:</strong> trying an option, recursing, then undoing it and trying the next</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Permutation:</strong> one ordering of a set of items; n items have n! orderings</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Search tree:</strong> the tree of partial solutions the recursion explores</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Combinatorial explosion:</strong> the factorial or exponential growth in the number of possibilities</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is the shape behind maze solving, N-queens and sudoku solvers. The pattern never changes: choose, recurse, undo.</p></div><i data-d286_z11_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — exploring a branching choice</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk406a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">at each step</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">several options</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">choose one</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">recurse</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">undo the choice</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">backtrack</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">base case</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a complete solution</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Every subset of a set</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">subsets(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 0, "");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">{1,2,3}
{1,2}
{1,3}
{1}
