1024</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Two recursive calls per level double the work each time.</li><li>The total is 2ⁿ.</li><li>That is why exhaustive search becomes impossible quite quickly.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Forgetting to undo a choice before trying the next one. If the state is shared rather than passed along, the second branch starts from the first branch's leftovers.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>If a question asks how many possibilities are explored, count the choices per step and raise it to the number of steps — two choices over n items gives 2ⁿ.</div>"},"D286/u11/z11_8":{"body":"<p><code>StackOverflowError</code> is what happens when recursion goes too deep. Each call pushes a frame (8.3) and the stack has a fixed size, so a few thousand levels is typically all you get.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int bad(int n) {
    return bad(n - 1);          // no base case — overflows in milliseconds
}

public static int alsoBad(int n) {
    if (n == 0) { return 0; }
    return alsoBad(n);          // base case exists but is UNREACHABLE
}</pre><p>The two causes are a missing base case and a call that does not shrink the input. A third, legitimate case exists: correct recursion on genuinely deep data — a 100,000-element linked list — where the fix is to rewrite it as a loop rather than to hunt for a bug.</p><p>Each call takes a stack frame, and the stack is a fixed, fairly small region — typically a few thousand frames deep. Exceed it and the JVM throws <strong><code>StackOverflowError</code></strong>.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"><code>StackOverflowError</code></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"><code>OutOfMemoryError</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">region</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the stack</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the heap</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">cause</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">recursion too deep, usually missing a base case</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">too many reachable objects</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">is an</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Error</code>, not an <code>Exception</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Error</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Both are <code>Error</code>s rather than <code>Exception</code>s, which is why you do not catch them: they signal that the program's assumptions have failed, not a condition to be handled.</p><p>The stack trace itself names the cause — the same method repeating for hundreds of lines. If the argument never changes between those lines the recursive case is not shrinking; if it does change but the base case never matches, the base case is testing the wrong thing.</p><p>For a genuinely deep problem the fix is to rewrite it as a loop, since a loop uses one frame no matter how many times it runs.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>StackOverflowError:</strong> an Error thrown when the call stack is exhausted</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Error vs Exception:</strong> an Error signals a condition a program should not try to recover from (12.1)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Tail recursion:</strong> recursion whose recursive call is the last operation; some languages optimise it into a loop, but Java does not</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>StackOverflowError</code> is an <code>Error</code>, not an <code>Exception</code>, so <code>catch (Exception e)</code> will not catch it. That is deliberate — it is not meant to be recovered from.</p></div><i data-d286_z11_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why the stack runs out</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk407a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">each call</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one frame on the stack</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the frame holds</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">parameters and locals</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">frames pop on return</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">if a return ever happens</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">no base case</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">frames pile up until the stack is full</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the result</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">StackOverflowError</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Watching it actually happen</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">depth = 0;
try {
    forever();
} catch (StackOverflowError e) {
    System.out.println("StackOverflowError caught");
    System.out.println("frames before it broke: more than 1000? " + (depth &gt; 1000));
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">StackOverflowError caught
frames before it broke: more than 1000? true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each call adds a frame and never returns, so nothing is ever popped.</li><li>The stack has a fixed size, so it fills.</li><li>Java throws a StackOverflowError — an Error, not an Exception.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same method, with a base case</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(countdown(1000));
System.out.println(countdown(9000));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">0
0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Now each call gets closer to 0.</li><li>The base case returns, and the frames unwind normally.</li><li>Deep but finite recursion is fine; unbounded recursion is not.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>A StackOverflowError almost always means a missing or unreachable base case — check the stopping condition first, not the recursive step. An infinite LOOP by contrast just hangs; it does not throw.</div>"},"D286/u11/z11_9":{"body":"<p>The permutation example from 11.7 written as a complete program, with the pieces named so the structure is visible.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Permutations {

    public static void permute(String prefix, String rest) {
        if (rest.length() == 0) {
            System.out.println(prefix);
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; rest.length(); i++) {
            char chosen  = rest.charAt(i);
            String left  = rest.substring(0, i) + rest.substring(i + 1);
            permute(prefix + chosen, left);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        permute("", "abc");        // 3! = 6 lines of output
    }
}</pre><p>Naming <code>chosen</code> and <code>left</code> instead of nesting the expressions is the difference between code you can trace and code you can only run. The recursion is unchanged; the readability is not.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void permute(char[] a, int k) {
    if (k == a.length) { System.out.println(new String(a)); return; }
    for (int i = k; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        swap(a, k, i);            // choose: put a[i] in position k
        permute(a, k + 1);        // permute the rest
        swap(a, k, i);            // UNDO — restore before the next choice
    }
}</pre><p>Position <em>k</em> is filled in turn by every remaining character, and the rest is permuted recursively. The base case is reaching the end of the array, which is where a completed arrangement is printed — output happens at the <em>leaves</em>, never on the way down.</p><p>The second <code>swap</code> is the whole reason the loop works. Without it the array stays scrambled and the next iteration chooses from data the previous branch left behind, producing duplicates and missing arrangements.</p><p><em>n</em> characters give <strong>n!</strong> permutations — 5 gives 120, 10 gives 3.6 million, 13 exceeds two billion. Repeated characters produce visually duplicate lines, since the algorithm permutes positions rather than distinct values.</p><i data-d286u811="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Prefix accumulator:</strong> the choices made so far, carried down the recursion</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Remaining set:</strong> the items still available at this level</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Output at the base case:</strong> printing when nothing remains, which is one complete solution</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Count the output lines before running it: n characters give n! lines. Four characters give 24, five give 120 — which is how you know at a glance whether an input is reasonable.</p></div><i data-d286_z11_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — permutations by choosing a first character</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 154" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk408a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">pick each character in turn</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">as the first</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">permute what remains</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">recursively</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">base case</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">nothing left — print</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">n characters</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">n! permutations</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — All permutations of a short string</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">permute("", "abc");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">abc
acb
bac
bca
cab
cba</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each loop pass chooses a different character to come next.</li><li>The chosen character moves to the prefix and leaves the remainder.</li><li>When nothing remains, the prefix is one complete permutation.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — How many there are</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(countPerms("abc"));
System.out.println(countPerms("abcd"));
System.out.println(countPerms("abcde"));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">6
24
120</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Three characters give 3! = 6.</li><li>Four give 24, five give 120.</li><li>The count is n!, which is why permutation problems become impossible faster than almost anything else.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Removing the chosen character with <code>replace</code> instead of by index. <code>replace</code> removes EVERY copy, so a string with a repeated letter loses too much and the permutations come out wrong.</div>"},"D286/u12/z12_1":{"body":"<p>6.13 left a question open: when a method is handed a zero divisor, what should it do? Returning a made-up value pushes the problem onto a caller who may ignore it. An <strong>exception</strong> is the alternative — the method refuses, and the failure travels up until someone deals with it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">try {
    int result = 10 / divisor;          // may throw ArithmeticException
    System.out.println(result);
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("cannot divide by zero: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    System.out.println("always runs, thrown or not");
}</pre><p>The flow is worth stating exactly, because trace questions depend on it: if nothing throws, the whole <code>try</code> runs and every <code>catch</code> is skipped. If something throws, the <em>rest of the try is abandoned</em> and the first matching catch runs. <code>finally</code> runs either way — even after a <code>return</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">try {
    int x = a[i] / d;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {      // most SPECIFIC first
    ...
} catch (Exception e) {                // most GENERAL last
    ...
} finally {
    // runs whether or not an exception was thrown, and even after a return
}</pre><p>Catch blocks are tested top to bottom and the first matching one wins, so they must run specific to general. Putting <code>catch (Exception e)</code> first makes every later block unreachable, and that is a <em>compile</em> error rather than a subtle bug.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Checked</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Unchecked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">extends</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Exception</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>RuntimeException</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">compiler forces you to handle it</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>yes</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">no</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">examples</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>IOException</code>, <code>FileNotFoundException</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArithmeticException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBounds</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The distinction is about who can be expected to recover. A missing file is a normal possibility the caller should plan for; a null reference is a programming error, and wrapping every dereference in a try block would be absurd.</p><p><code>finally</code> is for releasing what must be released. It runs after the try block, after any catch, and even if the method returns from inside the try — which is why cleanup goes there rather than at the end of the try.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Exception:</strong> an object describing a runtime failure, thrown up the call stack</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>try:</strong> the block whose failures you want to handle</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>catch:</strong> the handler for one exception type, including its subtypes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>finally:</strong> a block that always runs, used for cleanup such as close()</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stack unwinding:</strong> the search up the call stack for a matching catch</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is the mechanism behind every failure in Units 9 and 13. A missing file, an unparseable number and a bad cast all arrive as exceptions, and one structure handles all of them.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → Exception Handling</strong></p><i data-d286fx21="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — TRY / CATCH / FINALLY: THE TWO PATHS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 500 224" style="width:100%; max-width:540px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk88a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="30" y="30" width="130" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="95" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">try block</text><text x="95" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">runs line by line</text><polygon points="250,26 330,52 250,78 170,52" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="250" y="50" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">exception</text><text x="250" y="64" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">thrown?</text><line x1="160" y1="50" x2="168" y2="50" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk88a)"/><line x1="250" y1="80" x2="250" y2="108" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk88a)"/><text x="272" y="96" fill="#ef4444" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">yes: rest of try is SKIPPED</text><rect x="185" y="110" width="130" height="40" rx="8" fill="#ef444422" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="250" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">matching catch</text><text x="250" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">handles it</text><line x1="332" y1="52" x2="420" y2="52" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk88a)"/><text x="376" y="42" fill="#22c55e" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">no</text><rect x="420" y="32" width="60" height="40" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="450" y="56" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">done</text><line x1="450" y1="72" x2="450" y2="176" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="250" y1="150" x2="250" y2="176" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="250" y1="176" x2="340" y2="176" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="450" y1="176" x2="346" y2="176" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk88a)"/><rect x="285" y="158" width="110" height="36" rx="8" fill="#f59e0b22" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="340" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">finally: ALWAYS</text><text x="255" y="216" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Both paths funnel through finally — it runs whether or not anything was thrown or caught.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>If NO catch matches, finally still runs and THEN the exception keeps propagating up the call stack. And catch blocks are checked top-down — a parent type like <code>Exception</code> listed first swallows everything below it (the compiler rejects unreachable catches).</div><i data-d286_z12_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — try, catch and finally, in the order they actually run</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] a = { 10, 20, 30 };

try {
    System.out.println("before");
    System.out.println(a[5]);
    System.out.println("never reached");
} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("caught: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    System.out.println("finally always runs");
}

System.out.println("execution continues");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">before
caught: Index 5 out of bounds for length 3
finally always runs
execution continues</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The moment <code>a[5]</code> throws, the rest of the <code>try</code> block is abandoned — <code>"never reached"</code> never prints.</li><li>Control jumps straight to the matching <code>catch</code>.</li><li><code>finally</code> runs whether or not anything was thrown, which is what makes it the right place for cleanup.</li><li>After the whole statement, the program carries on normally — a caught exception is not a crash.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Several catch blocks, and why the order matters</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] inputs = { "4", "0", "seven" };
for (String s : inputs) {
    System.out.println("input \"" + s + "\"");
    attempt(s);
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">input "4"
  100 / 4 = 25
input "0"
  arithmetic problem: / by zero
input "seven"
  not a number: seven</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java picks the <strong>first</strong> catch whose type matches, then stops looking — exactly like an <code>else if</code> chain.</li><li>The specific types come first and the general <code>Exception</code> last.</li><li>Reversing that order is a compile error, because a catch after <code>Exception</code> could never be reached.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Know what each of the three blocks is for: <code>try</code> holds the risky code, <code>catch</code> handles one kind of failure, <code>finally</code> cleans up either way. A question asking which lines print after a throw is really asking whether you know the rest of the <code>try</code> block is skipped but <code>finally</code> is not.</div>"},"D286/u12/z12_2":{"body":"<p>Throwing is how a method reports that it cannot do what was asked. The <code>throw</code> statement ends the method immediately and hands an exception object to the caller.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double divide(int a, int b) {
    if (b == 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("divisor must not be zero");
    }
    return (double) a / b;
}

// the two families
UNCHECKED (extends RuntimeException)   CHECKED (extends Exception)
  ArithmeticException                    IOException
  NullPointerException                   FileNotFoundException
  ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException         ClassNotFoundException
  IllegalArgumentException
  NumberFormatException
  -&gt; compiler does not force handling    -&gt; compiler REQUIRES catch or throws</pre><p>The checked/unchecked split is the most-tested idea in the unit. Checked exceptions describe things outside your control — a file that might be missing — and the compiler insists you plan for them. Unchecked exceptions describe programming errors, and forcing a <code>try</code> around every array access would be absurd.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">throw  new IllegalArgumentException("age must not be negative");  // an ACTION
                                                                     // one exception, now

void load(String f) throws IOException { ... }                       // a DECLARATION
                                                                     // this may throw</pre><p><code>throw</code> is a statement that raises an exception immediately; <code>throws</code> is part of a method header announcing what may escape. One letter apart, and the exam relies on that.</p><p>Throwing beats returning an error code because it cannot be ignored: a caller who does nothing about a <code>-1</code> carries on with bad data, while an unhandled exception stops the program at the point of failure with a stack trace.</p><p>Integer division by zero throws <code>ArithmeticException</code>. Floating-point division by zero does <em>not</em> — it produces <code>Infinity</code> or <code>NaN</code>, which then propagates silently through every later calculation. That asymmetry is a favourite question.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>throw:</strong> raises an exception, ending the method immediately</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>throws:</strong> a method declaration saying "I do not handle this; my caller must"</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Checked exception:</strong> must be caught or declared; the compiler enforces it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Unchecked exception:</strong> a RuntimeException or Error; no compiler requirement</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>throw</code> and <code>throws</code> are different keywords. <code>throw</code> is a statement that raises one exception; <code>throws</code> is part of a method signature listing what may escape. Swapping them is a compile error and an exam distractor.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → Exception Handling</strong></p><i data-d286_z12_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — throw, throws, and the two families</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk419a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">throw new X(...)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a statement — raises it now</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">throws X</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">in the signature — declares it may escape</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">checked</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">extends Exception; must be caught or declared</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">unchecked</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">extends RuntimeException; compiler says nothing</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">IllegalArgumentException</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the usual choice for a bad parameter</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Throwing on bad input instead of returning nonsense</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(sqrtOf(9));
System.out.println(Math.sqrt(-9));
try {
    System.out.println(sqrtOf(-9));
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    System.out.println("rejected -&gt; " + e.getMessage());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3.0
NaN
rejected -&gt; negative input: -9.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Math.sqrt(-9)</code> quietly returns <code>NaN</code>, and <code>NaN</code> then poisons every calculation it touches without ever complaining.</li><li>Throwing stops the program at the point the bad value arrived, which is the only place that knows what went wrong.</li><li><code>IllegalArgumentException</code> is unchecked, so the caller is free to catch it or not — no <code>throws</code> clause is needed.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Checked versus unchecked, in one program</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">try {
    checkedOne();
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("1: " + e.getMessage());
}
try {
    uncheckedOne();
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    System.out.println("2: " + e.getMessage());
}
System.out.println(RuntimeException.class.isAssignableFrom(IllegalStateException.class));
System.out.println(RuntimeException.class.isAssignableFrom(java.io.IOException.class));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">1: I am checked - the signature must say so
2: I am unchecked - no throws needed
true
false</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>checkedOne</code> will not compile without <code>throws Exception</code> on its signature — that is what "checked" means.</li><li><code>uncheckedOne</code> compiles with no declaration at all, because it extends <code>RuntimeException</code>.</li><li>The last two lines ask the runtime directly which family each belongs to: <code>IllegalStateException</code> sits under <code>RuntimeException</code>, <code>IOException</code> does not. (Writing this as <code>new IOException("x") instanceof RuntimeException</code> will not even compile — Java rejects an <code>instanceof</code> between types that can never be related.)</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Test yourself on the split, because questions lean on it: <code>IOException</code> and <code>FileNotFoundException</code> are <strong>checked</strong> and force a <code>throws</code> or a <code>catch</code>. <code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArithmeticException</code>, <code>NumberFormatException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> and <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> are <strong>unchecked</strong> and the compiler never mentions them.</div>"},"D286/u12/z12_3":{"body":"<p>File work is where checked exceptions become unavoidable, because 9.4 quietly used <code>throws FileNotFoundException</code> on <code>main</code> to make the compiler stop complaining. Catching it is the version that can actually respond.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Scanner in = null;
try {
    in = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"));
    while (in.hasNextLine()) { System.out.println(in.nextLine()); }
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.out.println("No such file — using defaults instead.");
} finally {
    if (in != null) { in.close(); }        // cleanup happens either way
}

// try-with-resources: closes automatically, no finally needed
try (Scanner in2 = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))) {
    while (in2.hasNextLine()) { System.out.println(in2.nextLine()); }
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.out.println("missing");
}</pre><p>The <code>finally</code> block has to test for null, because if the constructor threw, <code>in</code> was never assigned. Try-with-resources exists precisely to remove that awkwardness — it closes anything declared in its parentheses, in reverse order, whatever happens.</p><h3>What try-with-resources actually requires</h3><p>Try-with-resources closes anything declared in its parentheses, and it can only do that for a type that implements <strong><code>AutoCloseable</code></strong> — the interface whose single method is <code>close()</code>. <code>Scanner</code>, <code>PrintWriter</code>, <code>FileReader</code> and every stream implement it, which is why they all work in that position.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">class Resource implements AutoCloseable {
    public void close() { System.out.println("closed"); }
}

try (Resource r = new Resource()) { ... }   // close() runs automatically</pre><p>Resources are closed in <strong>reverse order</strong> of declaration, and closing happens <em>before</em> any catch or finally block runs — which is the ordering questions ask about.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))) {
    ...
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {        // subclass of IOException
    ...
} catch (IOException e) {                  // more general — must come after
    ...
}</pre><p>File work is where checked exceptions become unavoidable: the compiler will not build code that opens a file without either catching or declaring the failure. That is the language insisting you decide what happens when the file is not there.</p><p><code>try</code>-with-resources closes anything declared in its parentheses whose type implements <strong><code>AutoCloseable</code></strong> — every stream, reader, writer and <code>Scanner</code>. Resources close in reverse order of declaration, and <em>before</em> any catch or finally block runs.</p><p>Without it, the close call has to go in <code>finally</code>, needs its own try block because closing can itself throw, and is skipped entirely if an exception fires before it — which is why the older pattern is no longer taught as the default.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>try-with-resources:</strong> a try whose declared resources are closed automatically</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Resource leak:</strong> an unclosed file handle or connection; not fixed by the garbage collector (8.4)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Recovery:</strong> responding to a failure with a usable alternative rather than crashing</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Catching an exception and doing nothing — an empty catch block — is worse than not catching it. The failure still happened; now nobody will ever know.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → try-with-resources and File I/O</strong></p><i data-d286_z12_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — file work and the exceptions it raises</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk420a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new Scanner(new File(n))</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">throws FileNotFoundException</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">FileNotFoundException</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">checked — catch it or declare it</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">try (Scanner s = ...) { }</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">try-with-resources: auto-close</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt() past the end</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">NoSuchElementException</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt() on text</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">InputMismatchException</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A missing file, handled rather than crashing</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.io.PrintWriter w = new java.io.PrintWriter("notes.txt");
w.println("alpha");
w.println("beta");
w.close();

System.out.println("notes.txt:");
readIt("notes.txt");
System.out.println("gone.txt:");
readIt("gone.txt");
System.out.println("still running");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">notes.txt:
  alpha
  beta
gone.txt:
  could not open gone.txt
still running</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>new Scanner(File)</code> throws a <strong>checked</strong> exception, so the compiler insists this call be wrapped or declared.</li><li>The try-with-resources form closes the Scanner automatically, even if an exception escapes the block.</li><li>The missing file produces one sentence and the program keeps going — nothing about it is fatal.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Reading past the end, and reading the wrong type</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.io.PrintWriter w = new java.io.PrintWriter("mixed.txt");
w.println("7 hello");
w.close();

java.util.Scanner in = new java.util.Scanner(new java.io.File("mixed.txt"));
System.out.println("first int: " + in.nextInt());
try {
    System.out.println(in.nextInt());
} catch (java.util.InputMismatchException e) {
    System.out.println("InputMismatchException - 'hello' is not an int");
}
in.next();
try {
    in.nextInt();
} catch (java.util.NoSuchElementException e) {
    System.out.println("NoSuchElementException - nothing left to read");
}
in.close();</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">first int: 7
InputMismatchException - 'hello' is not an int
NoSuchElementException - nothing left to read</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>nextInt()</code> on the token <code>hello</code> throws <code>InputMismatchException</code> and — importantly — does <em>not</em> consume the token.</li><li><code>next()</code> then consumes it as a String, which is how you recover.</li><li>Asking for another token at end of file throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>; guarding with <code>hasNextInt()</code> avoids both.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Catching <code>FileNotFoundException</code> but never checking that the file was actually read. The catch block prints a warning, execution continues, and the rest of the program then works on empty data as though everything succeeded. Either recover properly or stop — do not carry on with nothing.</div>"},"D286/u12/z12_4":{"body":"<p>A method has two options for an exception it cannot resolve: handle it, or declare it and let the caller decide. Choosing correctly is a design decision about who has enough context to respond.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// PROPAGATE — this method cannot sensibly recover
public static int readAge(String file) throws FileNotFoundException {
    Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(file));
    return in.nextInt();
}

// HANDLE — the caller knows what to do about it
public static void main(String[] args) {
    try {
        System.out.println(readAge("ages.txt"));
    } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
        System.out.println("Using default age 0");
    }
}</pre><p>Multiple catches are searched in order and the <strong>first match wins</strong>, which makes ordering a hard rule: specific types before general ones. <code>catch (Exception e)</code> placed first makes every later catch unreachable, and that is a compile error.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">try { ... }
catch (FileNotFoundException e) { ... }    // specific
catch (IOException e)           { ... }    // its parent
catch (Exception e)             { ... }    // most general — LAST</pre><p>An uncaught exception <strong>propagates</strong>: it leaves the method, then that method's caller, and so on up the call stack until something catches it or the program stops and prints the trace. The trace is that path, innermost call first.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">void a() { b(); }                         // no try — the exception passes through
void b() throws IOException { c(); }      // declared, not handled
void c() throws IOException { throw new IOException(); }</pre><p>Catch where you can actually do something. A method that has no sensible response should declare <code>throws</code> and let a caller with more context decide — catching and logging at every level buries the real failure in noise.</p><p>An empty catch block is the one thing never to write. It converts a failure into silently wrong behaviour, and the reason a program "does nothing" with no error is very often a <code>catch (Exception e) { }</code> several layers down.</p><p>Unchecked exceptions may be declared in a <code>throws</code> clause as documentation, but nothing requires it and nothing enforces it.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Propagation:</strong> declaring an exception with throws so it travels to the caller</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Catch ordering:</strong> subclasses must be caught before their superclasses</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Multi-catch:</strong> catch (IOException | SQLException e) handles several types in one block</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A general catch above a specific one gives "exception has already been caught". The compiler is telling you the second block can never run.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Arrays → Exception Handling</strong></p><i data-d286_z12_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — how an exception travels up the call stack</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk421a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">thrown in c()</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">c has no catch — it propagates</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">b() calls c()</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">no catch here either — keeps rising</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a() calls b()</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">first matching catch wins</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nothing catches it</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the program stops, stack trace printed</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">throws on the signature</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">how a checked one is passed upward</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One catch, three levels up</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">try {
    level1();
} catch (IllegalStateException e) {
    System.out.println("caught in main: " + e.getMessage());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">level1 entered
  level2 entered
    level3 entered
caught in main: broke at level 3</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The exception is thrown three calls deep and no method in between catches it.</li><li>Each unfinished method is abandoned on the way up — neither <code>"level2 finished"</code> nor <code>"level1 finished"</code> prints.</li><li>The first enclosing <code>catch</code> of a matching type handles it, however far up the stack that is.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — finally runs on the way out, even when the exception escapes</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("ok case: " + risky(false));
try {
    System.out.println(risky(true));
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    System.out.println("caught: " + e.getMessage());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">  cleanup ran
ok case: 1
  cleanup ran
caught: boom</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>In the success case <code>finally</code> runs after the <code>return</code> value is computed but before the method actually returns.</li><li>In the failure case there is no <code>catch</code> in <code>risky</code> at all, yet <code>finally</code> still runs as the exception passes through.</li><li>That guarantee is why files and connections are closed in <code>finally</code> — or, better, with try-with-resources.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Trace these questions by walking the call stack downward to the throw, then back up looking for the first matching <code>catch</code>. Any statement after the call that threw, in any method on that path, does not run — but every <code>finally</code> on the path does.</div>"},"D286/u12/z12_5":{"body":"<p>When no built-in type describes your failure, define one. A user-defined exception is just a class extending <code>Exception</code> or <code>RuntimeException</code>, and which one you extend decides whether it is checked.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {   // CHECKED
    private double shortfall;

    public InsufficientFundsException(String msg, double shortfall) {
        super(msg);                       // the message goes to the parent
        this.shortfall = shortfall;
    }

    public double getShortfall() { return shortfall; }
}

// throwing it
if (amount &gt; balance) {
    throw new InsufficientFundsException("Balance too low", amount - balance);
}</pre><p>The advantage over a generic exception is the extra data: the catcher can ask how much was missing and act on it. That is the argument for a custom type — not the name, but the information it can carry.</p><h3>Catching a user-defined exception</h3><p>Once you have defined an exception, it can be caught either by its own type or by <strong>any supertype</strong> it inherits from — because a <code>catch</code> block matches the named type and all of its subclasses.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">catch (InsufficientFundsException e)  // its own type — most specific
catch (Exception e)                    // a supertype — also matches
catch (RuntimeException e)             // matches ONLY if it extends RuntimeException</pre><p>That is the same ordering rule as 12.4: specific before general, or the general block makes the specific one unreachable.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {   // CHECKED
    private final double shortfall;
    public InsufficientFundsException(String msg, double shortfall) {
        super(msg);                      // pass the message up to Exception
        this.shortfall = shortfall;      // carry extra data with the failure
    }
    public double getShortfall() { return shortfall; }
}

if (amount &gt; balance)
    throw new InsufficientFundsException("Balance too low", amount - balance);</pre><p>Extend <code>Exception</code> for a checked one the caller must handle, or <code>RuntimeException</code> for an unchecked one signalling a programming error. That single choice is what the exam asks about.</p><p>A custom exception is worth defining when callers need to distinguish this failure from others — you can catch <code>InsufficientFundsException</code> specifically instead of inspecting the text of a generic message. The convention is a name ending in <code>Exception</code>, and a constructor that passes the message to <code>super</code>.</p><p>Carrying a field, as with <code>shortfall</code> here, lets the handler respond with detail rather than just reporting that something went wrong.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>User-defined exception:</strong> a class extending Exception or RuntimeException</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>super(msg):</strong> passes the message to the parent, where getMessage() reads it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Extends Exception:</strong> makes it checked — callers must handle or declare it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Extends RuntimeException:</strong> makes it unchecked — no compiler requirement</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Name exceptions ending in "Exception" and extend <code>Exception</code> for conditions a caller could reasonably recover from. Extend <code>RuntimeException</code> for programming errors that should be fixed rather than caught.</p></div><i data-d286_z12_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — writing your own exception type</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk422a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">extends Exception</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">checked — callers must handle it</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">extends RuntimeException</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">unchecked — callers may ignore it</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">super(message)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">how getMessage() gets its text</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the class name</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">carries meaning: InsufficientFundsException</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">catch (MyException e)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">catches that type and its subclasses</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A named exception that carries the details with it</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">try {
    withdraw(20);
    System.out.println("balance now " + balance);
    withdraw(80);
    System.out.println("never reached");
} catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
    System.out.println("declined: " + e.getMessage());
    System.out.printf("needs %.2f more%n", e.getShortfall());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">balance now 30.0
declined: short by 50.00
needs 50.00 more</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>super(message)</code> is what makes <code>getMessage()</code> return that text — without it the message is <code>null</code>.</li><li>The class carries an extra field, so the handler gets the number as data instead of having to parse the sentence.</li><li>Because it extends <code>Exception</code>, <code>withdraw</code> must declare <code>throws</code> and callers must handle it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The one decision that changes everything: which class you extend</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">try { mustDeclare(); } catch (CheckedProblem e) { System.out.println(e.getMessage()); }
try { neednt(); } catch (UncheckedProblem e) { System.out.println(e.getMessage()); }

try { neednt(); } catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("a catch of Exception still catches it: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">compiler forced the throws clause
no throws clause anywhere
a catch of Exception still catches it: UncheckedProblem</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both are exception classes you wrote; only the parent differs.</li><li>The checked one propagates through <code>throws</code> declarations the compiler enforces; the unchecked one travels silently.</li><li><code>catch (Exception e)</code> catches both, because <code>RuntimeException</code> is itself a subclass of <code>Exception</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing the constructor as <code>MyException(String m) { }</code> and forgetting <code>super(m)</code>. It compiles, it throws, it catches — and <code>getMessage()</code> returns <code>null</code>, so the error report is blank at exactly the moment you need it.</div>"},"D286/u12/z12_6":{"body":"<p><code>NumberFormatException</code> is the one you will actually meet, because any program reading text and expecting numbers can be handed something else.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String input = scnr.next();

try {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(input);          // "abc" -&gt; NumberFormatException
    System.out.println(n * 2);
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("'" + input + "' is not a whole number");
}

// the alternative: ask before you leap
if (scnr.hasNextInt()) {
    int n = scnr.nextInt();
} else {
    scnr.next();                              // discard the bad token
}</pre><p>Both approaches are valid and they suit different situations. <code>hasNextInt()</code> is cleaner for interactive input where you can re-prompt; <code>try-catch</code> is better when the text arrives in bulk from a file and you want to skip the bad rows and keep going.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">try {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(input);
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("Please enter a whole number");
}</pre><p><code>NumberFormatException</code> is <strong>unchecked</strong> — it extends <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> — so nothing forces you to catch it, and unvalidated user input is exactly where it appears.</p><p>It fires for anything that is not a plain integer: letters, an empty string, <code>"3.5"</code>, or a value beyond <code>int</code> range. Surrounding whitespace also fails, which is why input is usually <code>.trim()</code>ed first. A <code>null</code> argument is different again — that throws <code>NullPointerException</code>.</p><p>Recovering means asking again rather than crashing: catch it inside a loop, print a message, and let the loop repeat until the input parses. The alternative shape is <code>scnr.hasNextInt()</code>, which tests before consuming.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>NumberFormatException:</strong> unchecked; thrown by parseInt or parseDouble on non-numeric text</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>hasNextInt():</strong> tests whether the next token parses as an int, without consuming it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Look before you leap vs try it and see:</strong> the two standard strategies for handling bad input</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p><code>Integer.parseInt("3.5")</code> throws — "3.5" is not an integer. Use <code>Double.parseDouble</code>, or parse as a double and cast (2.12).</p></div><i data-d286_z12_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — parsing text to numbers, and what goes wrong</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk423a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Integer.parseInt(s)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">String → int, or NumberFormatException</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Double.parseDouble(s)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">String → double</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"12.5" to parseInt</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">throws — the dot is not an int</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">" 12 " to parseInt</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">throws — parseInt does not trim</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">NumberFormatException</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">unchecked, extends IllegalArgumentException</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Exactly which strings parseInt refuses</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] tests = { "42", "-7", "+3", " 12 ", "12.5", "", "abc", "1,000" };
for (String s : tests) tryParse(s);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">  "42" -&gt; 42
  "-7" -&gt; -7
  "+3" -&gt; 3
  " 12 " -&gt; NumberFormatException: For input string: " 12 "
  "12.5" -&gt; NumberFormatException: For input string: "12.5"
  "" -&gt; NumberFormatException: For input string: ""
  "abc" -&gt; NumberFormatException: For input string: "abc"
  "1,000" -&gt; NumberFormatException: For input string: "1,000"</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A leading sign is fine; surrounding spaces are not — <code>parseInt</code> does no trimming of its own.</li><li><code>"12.5"</code> fails because it is not an integer; <code>Double.parseDouble("12.5")</code> would succeed.</li><li>The empty string and a thousands separator both fail, and the message always quotes the offending input.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Validating input without letting the exception reach the user</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(parseOr("42", -1));
System.out.println(parseOr(" 42 ", -1));
System.out.println(parseOr("oops", -1));
System.out.println(parseOr(null, -1));
System.out.println(NumberFormatException.class.getSuperclass().getSimpleName());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">42
42
-1
-1
IllegalArgumentException</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>trim()</code> before parsing handles the whitespace that always comes with typed input.</li><li>The fallback turns a thrown exception into an ordinary value the caller can carry on with.</li><li>The last line confirms the hierarchy: <code>NumberFormatException</code> extends <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>, so a catch of either type will catch it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><code>NumberFormatException</code> is <strong>unchecked</strong>, so nothing forces you to handle it and the compiler stays silent. That is precisely why it is the exception that reaches real users — wrap every parse of anything a person typed.</div>"},"D286/u13/z13_1":{"body":"<p>Writing <code>printArray</code> once for <code>int[]</code>, again for <code>String[]</code> and again for <code>double[]</code> is the problem generics solve. A <strong>generic method</strong> is written once with a type placeholder and works for every type.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static <t> void printAll(T[] items) {
//            ^ declares the type parameter, before the return type
    for (T item : items) { System.out.println(item); }
}

printAll(new String[]{"a", "b"});      // T is String
printAll(new Integer[]{1, 2});         // T is Integer — NOT int</t></pre><p>The gain over using <code>Object</code> is compile-time checking. With <code>Object[]</code> the compiler cannot stop you mixing types and every element needs a cast on the way out; with <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> the type is known and the compiler enforces it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static &lt;T&gt; void printAll(List&lt;T&gt; items) {    // &lt;T&gt; declared before the return type
    for (T x : items) System.out.println(x);
}

ArrayList&lt;String&gt; l = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();          // diamond — type inferred
ArrayList raw = new ArrayList();                   // RAW type — no checking</pre><p>A generic method takes the type as a parameter, so one method serves every element type without casting. The <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> before the return type is what declares it — leaving it out makes <code>T</code> an unknown class name and the method will not compile.</p><p>The point is <strong>compile-time</strong> type safety. Before generics, a collection held <code>Object</code>, every read needed a cast, and a wrong type failed at run time with <code>ClassCastException</code>. With generics the mistake is caught while you are typing it.</p><p>A raw type compiles with a warning and gives all that up. And a type argument must be a reference type — <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> is illegal, <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is the way, which is why wrappers exist.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Generic method:</strong> a method with its own type parameter, usable with many types</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type parameter:</strong> the placeholder, conventionally T, E, K or V</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type inference:</strong> the compiler working out T from the arguments so you need not state it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type safety:</strong> errors caught at compile time rather than as a ClassCastException at run time</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Generics do not work with primitives. <code>&lt;int&gt;</code> is a compile error — use <code>Integer</code> and let autoboxing (7.13) handle the conversion.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → Generics</strong></p><i data-d286_z13_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the shape of a generic method</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk424a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">static &lt;T&gt; void f(T x)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">&lt;T&gt; before the return type declares it</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">T</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a placeholder for one real type</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">f("hi")</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">T becomes String, inferred from the argument</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">T[] not int[]</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">generics need objects, not primitives</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Integer, Double, Character</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the wrapper types used instead</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One method, any element type</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] words = { "red", "green", "blue" };
Integer[] nums = { 3, 1, 4 };
Double[] reals = { 1.5, 2.5 };

printAll(words);
printAll(nums);
printAll(reals);

System.out.println(firstOf(words).toUpperCase());
System.out.println(firstOf(nums) + 10);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">red green blue 
3 1 4 
1.5 2.5 
RED
13</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> sits before the return type — that is what declares T, and leaving it out is the usual compile error.</li><li>T is inferred from the argument at each call, so nothing has to be spelled out.</li><li>Because <code>firstOf</code> <em>returns</em> T, the result is already a String or an Integer — no cast is needed, and that is the whole point over using <code>Object</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Why the array is Integer[] and not int[]</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Integer[] boxed = { 1, 2, 3 };
System.out.println(count(boxed));

int primitive = 7;
Integer wrapped = primitive;
int back = wrapped;
System.out.println(wrapped + " " + back);
System.out.println(wrapped.getClass().getSimpleName());

java.util.ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; list = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
list.add(5);
System.out.println(list.get(0) + 1);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3
7 7
Integer
6</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A generic type argument must be a reference type, so <code>&lt;int&gt;</code> is not legal — <code>Integer</code> is used instead.</li><li>Autoboxing converts between the two automatically in both directions, which is why it is easy to forget the distinction exists.</li><li><code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is the same rule in the collection you use most.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Read the signature left to right: <code>public static &lt;T&gt; T pick(T[] a)</code> — <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> introduces the placeholder, the second <code>T</code> is the return type, the third is the parameter type. A question showing <code>public static T pick(T[] a)</code> with no <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> is showing you a compile error.</div>"},"D286/u13/z13_2":{"body":"<p>A plain <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> accepts anything, which means the method can only use the handful of operations every object supports. A <strong>bound</strong> narrows it and unlocks more.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static <t extends="" comparable<t="">&gt; T max(T[] items) {
    T best = items[0];
    for (T item : items) {
        if (item.compareTo(best) &gt; 0) { best = item; }   // legal BECAUSE of the bound
    }
    return best;
}

max(new Integer[]{3, 9, 2});        // 9
max(new String[]{"b", "a"});        // "b" — String is Comparable too</t></pre><p>Without <code>extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;</code>, the call to <code>compareTo</code> would not compile, because <code>T</code> might be any class at all. The bound is a promise about what T can do, and the compiler holds you to it at every call site.</p><h3>Wildcards</h3><p>A type parameter names a type you are choosing. A <strong>wildcard</strong> — written <code>?</code> — stands for a type you are not naming, and it exists because <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is <em>not</em> a subtype of <code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code>, however much it looks like one.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">void printAll(List<!--?--> list)                  // any element type at all
void sum(List<!--? extends Number--> list)        // Number or any subtype — READ from it
void addInts(List<!--? super Integer--> list)     // Integer or any supertype — WRITE to it</pre><p>The memory aid is <strong>PECS</strong> — <em>Producer Extends, Consumer Super</em>. If the collection produces values for you to read, use <code>? extends</code>; if it consumes values you put in, use <code>? super</code>. A plain <code>&lt;?&gt;</code> lets you read only as <code>Object</code> and add nothing but <code>null</code>.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static &lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt; T max(List&lt;T&gt; items) {
    T best = items.get(0);
    for (T x : items) if (x.compareTo(best) &gt; 0) best = x;   // compareTo is now legal
    return best;
}</pre><p>An unbounded <code>T</code> could be anything, so the only methods available are <code>Object</code>'s — no <code>compareTo</code>, no <code>length()</code>. A <strong>bound</strong> narrows what may be substituted and in exchange lets you call that type's methods.</p><p>The keyword is always <code>extends</code>, even when the bound is an interface: <code>&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>, not <code>implements</code>. Multiple bounds are joined with <code>&amp;</code> — <code>&lt;T extends Number &amp; Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> — with the class first if there is one.</p><p>Passing a type that does not satisfy the bound is a compile error at the call site, which is the whole benefit: the restriction is checked once, where the mistake is, rather than failing later inside the method.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bounded type parameter:</strong> &lt;T extends Something&gt; — T must be that type or a subtype</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>extends (in generics):</strong> used for both classes and interfaces here, unlike in a class declaration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Comparable&lt;T&gt;:</strong> the interface providing compareTo, which defines a natural ordering</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Upper bound:</strong> the ceiling type a type argument must satisfy</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>In generics, <code>extends</code> means "is or is below", covering both <code>extends</code> and <code>implements</code>. <code>&lt;T extends Comparable&gt;</code> accepts any class that <em>implements</em> Comparable.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → Generics</strong></p><i data-d286_z13_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — bounding a type parameter</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk425a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;T&gt;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">unbounded — only Object methods available</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;T extends Number&gt;</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">T is Number or a subclass</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">T can be compared to its own kind</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">extends</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">used for classes AND interfaces here</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.compareTo(b)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">negative, zero, positive</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Without a bound there is nothing you can do with T</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(maxOf(new Integer[] { 3, 9, 4 }));
System.out.println(maxOf(new String[] { "pear", "apple", "fig" }));
System.out.println(maxOf(new Double[] { 2.5, 0.5 }));

System.out.println("apple".compareTo("banana"));
System.out.println("banana".compareTo("apple"));
System.out.println("fig".compareTo("fig"));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">9
pear
2.5
-1
1
0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>An unbounded <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> would not compile here: the compiler only knows T is an Object, and Object has no <code>compareTo</code>.</li><li><code>&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> promises every T can be compared to another T, which unlocks the call.</li><li>For Strings <code>compareTo</code> is alphabetical, so the maximum is the last one alphabetically — <code>pear</code>, not the longest.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A numeric bound, and what it does not give you</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(sum(new Integer[] { 1, 2, 3 }));
System.out.println(sum(new Double[] { 1.5, 2.25 }));
System.out.println(sum(new Long[] { 10L, 20L }));

Integer a = 5, b = 3;
System.out.println(a.doubleValue() + " " + a.intValue() + " " + a.compareTo(b));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">6.0
3.75
30.0
5.0 5 1</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>extends Number</code> makes every <code>Number</code> method available on T — <code>doubleValue()</code>, <code>intValue()</code> and the rest.</li><li>It does <strong>not</strong> let you write <code>x + y</code> on two T values; the operator is not part of the Number type, which is why the total is accumulated through <code>doubleValue()</code>.</li><li>The same bound accepts Integer, Double and Long without a single change to the method.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing <code>&lt;T implements Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>. In a type bound the keyword is always <code>extends</code>, even when the bound is an interface — <code>implements</code> there is a syntax error.</div>"},"D286/u13/z13_3":{"body":"<p>A <strong>generic class</strong> takes its type parameter at the class level, so every method can use it. This is exactly what <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code> is — 7.14 was using generics before naming them.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Box<t> {
    private T contents;

    public void put(T item)  { contents = item; }
    public T    get()        { return contents; }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return contents == null; }
}

Box<string> b = new Box&lt;&gt;();      // &lt;&gt; infers String — the diamond operator
b.put("hello");
String s = b.get();               // no cast needed

b.put(42);                        // COMPILE ERROR — caught before it can run</string></t></pre><p>That last line is the whole benefit. The non-generic version would take an <code>Object</code>, accept the 42 silently, and fail with a <code>ClassCastException</code> at run time — the difference between an error you fix now and one your user finds.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Box&lt;T&gt; {                 // T is a placeholder for the whole class
    private T item;                   // field of the parameter type
    public void set(T item) { this.item = item; }
    public T get() { return item; }
}

Box&lt;String&gt; b = new Box&lt;&gt;();
b.set("hello");
String s = b.get();                   // no cast needed</pre><p>The type parameter follows the class name and is then usable throughout — as field types, parameter types and return types. Each instantiation fixes it: a <code>Box&lt;String&gt;</code> and a <code>Box&lt;Integer&gt;</code> are different types and cannot be assigned to each other.</p><p>A <code>static</code> member cannot use the class's type parameter, because <code>T</code> only exists once an object has been created with a specific type. A static method needing a type parameter must declare its own.</p><p>Several parameters are written <code>class Pair&lt;K, V&gt;</code>, which is exactly how <code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code> is declared.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Generic class:</strong> a class parameterised by one or more types</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Diamond operator:</strong> the empty &lt;&gt; that infers the type argument from the declaration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Parameterised type:</strong> Box&lt;String&gt; — the class with its type argument supplied</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Type erasure:</strong> generics exist at compile time only; the runtime class is just Box</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Multiple parameters are ordinary: <code>class Pair&lt;K, V&gt;</code> is how <code>HashMap&lt;K, V&gt;</code> (14.3) is declared.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → Generics</strong></p><i data-d286fx22="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A GENERIC CLASS IS A TEMPLATE WITH A TYPE HOLE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 500 188" style="width:100%; max-width:540px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk89a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="40" y="30" width="170" height="90" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="125" y="52" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">class Box&lt;T&gt;</text><text x="125" y="76" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">private T item;</text><text x="125" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">T get() { ... }</text><line x1="212" y1="56" x2="280" y2="44" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk89a)"/><line x1="212" y1="96" x2="280" y2="122" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk89a)"/><rect x="282" y="24" width="190" height="38" rx="8" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="377" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Box&lt;Integer&gt; — T is Integer</text><text x="377" y="54" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">get() returns Integer</text><rect x="282" y="104" width="190" height="38" rx="8" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="377" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Box&lt;String&gt; — T is String</text><text x="377" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">get() returns String</text><text x="255" y="166" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">One class, many element types — the compiler fills in T at each use and can then catch</text><text x="255" y="180" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">wrong-type mistakes at COMPILE time. Type arguments must be reference types (Integer, not int).</text></svg></div></div><i data-d286_z13_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A class parameterised by the type it holds</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Box&lt;String&gt; s = new Box&lt;&gt;("note");
Box&lt;Integer&gt; n = new Box&lt;&gt;(42);

System.out.println(s + " " + n);
System.out.println(s.get().length());
System.out.println(n.get() + 8);

s.set("replaced");
System.out.println(s.get());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Box(note) Box(42)
4
50
replaced</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The <code>&lt;T&gt;</code> goes on the class name, and every T inside the body then means the same one type per object.</li><li>The diamond <code>&lt;&gt;</code> on the right of <code>new</code> repeats the type the declaration already stated.</li><li><code>s.get()</code> is a String, so <code>.length()</code> works with no cast — the compiler knows the type, which is what a raw <code>Object</code> field could never give you.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A generic pair, and two type parameters</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Pair&lt;String, Integer&gt; score = new Pair&lt;&gt;("Ada", 96);
System.out.println(score);
System.out.println(score.getFirst().toUpperCase());
System.out.println(score.getSecond() / 2);
System.out.println(score.swapped());

java.util.ArrayList&lt;Pair&lt;String, Integer&gt;&gt; all = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
all.add(score);
all.add(new Pair&lt;&gt;("Grace", 91));
for (Pair&lt;String, Integer&gt; p : all) System.out.println(p.getFirst() + " scored " + p.getSecond());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">(Ada, 96)
ADA
48
(96, Ada)
Ada scored 96
Grace scored 91</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A class may declare as many type parameters as it needs, separated by commas.</li><li><code>swapped()</code> returns <code>Pair&lt;B, A&gt;</code> — the parameters can be reordered and reused in the body.</li><li>The types survive into collections: each element of the list is known to be a <code>Pair&lt;String, Integer&gt;</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Using the raw type — <code>Box b = new Box("x");</code> with no <code>&lt;String&gt;</code>. It still compiles, with a warning, but <code>b.get()</code> now returns <code>Object</code>, so <code>b.get().length()</code> fails to compile and any cast you add is unchecked. Always name the type argument.</div>"},"D286/u13/z13_4":{"body":"<p>Bounds work at class level for the same reason as at method level: they let the class use the bounded type’s methods.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class SortedBox<t extends="" comparable<t="">&gt; {
    private ArrayList<t> items = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    public void add(T item) {
        items.add(item);
        Collections.sort(items);       // legal because T is Comparable
    }

    public T max() { return items.get(items.size() - 1); }
}

SortedBox<integer> nums = new SortedBox&lt;&gt;();     // fine
SortedBox<scanner> bad  = new SortedBox&lt;&gt;();     // ERROR — Scanner is not Comparable</scanner></integer></t></t></pre><p>The error appears at the <em>declaration</em>, not on some later call. That is the difference a bound makes: the constraint is checked once, where the type is chosen, rather than being discovered when a method fails.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class SortedBox&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt; {
    private ArrayList&lt;T&gt; items = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    public void add(T x) {
        items.add(x);
        Collections.sort(items);       // legal ONLY because of the bound
    }
}

SortedBox&lt;String&gt; ok  = new SortedBox&lt;&gt;();   // String is Comparable
SortedBox&lt;Scanner&gt; no = new SortedBox&lt;&gt;();   // compile error</pre><p>A bound on a generic class works exactly as on a generic method, and for the same reason: without it the class knows nothing about <code>T</code> beyond it being an <code>Object</code>, so nothing can be sorted or compared.</p><p>The check happens where the type is supplied, so an unsuitable type is rejected at the declaration rather than blowing up later inside <code>add</code>. That is the difference between a compile-time and a run-time guarantee, and it is the standard exam framing of "why bound a type parameter".</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Bounded generic class:</strong> a class whose type parameter must satisfy a constraint</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Multiple bounds:</strong> &lt;T extends A &amp; B&gt; requires both; a class bound must come first</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Collections.sort:</strong> sorts a List whose elements are Comparable</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>This is how the standard library expresses requirements. <code>TreeSet</code> (14.7) demands Comparable elements for the same reason — it cannot keep things in order without a way to compare them.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → Generics</strong></p><i data-d286_z13_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a bound on a class's type parameter</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk426a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">class Stat&lt;T extends Number&gt;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">only numeric types accepted</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">inside the class</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">every Number method is available on T</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Stat&lt;Integer&gt;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">legal — Integer extends Number</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Stat&lt;String&gt;</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">compile error — String is not a Number</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the same idea, for ordering</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The bound decides which type arguments the compiler will accept</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Stat&lt;Integer&gt; ints = new Stat&lt;&gt;();
ints.add(4); ints.add(8); ints.add(3);
System.out.println(ints.total() + " over " + ints.size() + " values");
System.out.printf("mean %.2f%n", ints.mean());

Stat&lt;Double&gt; reals = new Stat&lt;&gt;();
reals.add(1.5); reals.add(2.25);
System.out.println(reals.total());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">15.0 over 3 values
mean 5.00
3.75</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The bound is written once on the class, and every method in the body can then rely on it.</li><li><code>Stat&lt;String&gt;</code> would be rejected at compile time — the bound is checked where the object is created, not where a method is called.</li><li>This is the payoff of a bound: <code>v.doubleValue()</code> is only legal because T is known to be a Number.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A bounded class that sorts what it stores</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Sorted&lt;String&gt; names = new Sorted&lt;&gt;();
names.add("Zoe"); names.add("Ada"); names.add("Kim");
System.out.println(names.min());
System.out.println(names.inOrder());

Sorted&lt;Integer&gt; nums = new Sorted&lt;&gt;();
nums.add(9); nums.add(2); nums.add(5);
System.out.println(nums.min() + " " + nums.inOrder());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Ada
[Ada, Kim, Zoe]
2 [2, 5, 9]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Collections.sort</code> requires its elements to be Comparable, and the class bound is exactly what satisfies that requirement.</li><li><code>inOrder()</code> sorts a copy, so the object's own order is left alone — returning a sorted view without mutating the source.</li><li>One class definition serves Strings and Integers, and each is sorted by its own natural order.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting <code>Stat&lt;Number&gt;</code> to accept a <code>Stat&lt;Integer&gt;</code>. Generics are <strong>invariant</strong>: even though Integer is a Number, <code>Stat&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is not a subtype of <code>Stat&lt;Number&gt;</code> and assigning one to the other will not compile.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Bounds are checked at two different moments, and questions test both: the <em>type argument</em> is checked where you write <code>new Stat&lt;Integer&gt;()</code>, and the <em>method calls inside the class</em> are checked against the bound when the class is compiled.</div>"},"D286/u13/z13_5":{"body":"<p>Implementing <code>Comparable</code> is how your own class becomes sortable, and it is one method: <code>compareTo</code>, returning negative, zero or positive exactly as <code>String.compareTo</code> did in 3.13.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student implements Comparable<student> {
    private String name;
    private int score;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        return Integer.compare(this.score, other.score);   // ascending by score
        // descending: Integer.compare(other.score, this.score)
    }
}

ArrayList<student> roster = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
Collections.sort(roster);              // now works — uses compareTo

// a different order without touching the class
roster.sort(Comparator.comparing(Student::getName));</student></student></pre><p><code>Integer.compare(a, b)</code> is preferred over <code>a - b</code>: the subtraction overflows for large values (2.16) and silently returns the wrong sign. It is a real bug and a common exam distractor.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Student implements Comparable&lt;Student&gt; {
    public int compareTo(Student o) {
        return Integer.compare(this.score, o.score);   // NOT this.score - o.score
    }
}
Collections.sort(list);                                 // uses compareTo

Collections.sort(list, (a, b) -&gt; a.getName().compareTo(b.getName()));  // Comparator
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Student::getName).reversed());</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"><code>Comparable</code></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"><code>Comparator</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">method</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>compareTo(other)</code> — one argument</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>compare(a, b)</code> — two</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">lives</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">inside the class being sorted</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">outside, as a separate object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">gives</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the <em>natural</em> ordering — one per class</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">any number of alternative orderings</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The return value is a <em>sign</em>, not a magnitude: negative if this comes first, zero if equivalent, positive if it comes after. Any negative number will do, which is why <code>a - b</code> looks tempting — and it is wrong, because the subtraction overflows for large or opposite-signed values and flips the sign. <code>Integer.compare</code> is the safe form.</p><p>Strings compare lexicographically by Unicode value, so all uppercase letters sort before all lowercase ones — <code>"Zoe"</code> before <code>"ana"</code>.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Comparable:</strong> the interface defining a class’s natural ordering through compareTo</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>compareTo:</strong> returns negative if this comes first, 0 if equal, positive if this comes after</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Comparator:</strong> a separate object defining an alternative ordering</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Natural ordering:</strong> the single default order a Comparable class defines</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Comparable is the class’s one built-in order; Comparator supplies any number of alternatives from outside. Sort by name and by score at different times, and you need one of each.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Advanced → Comparable and Comparator</strong></p><i data-d286_z13_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — making your own class sortable</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk427a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">implements Comparable&lt;T&gt;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">on the class declaration</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public int compareTo(T o)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the one method it requires</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">negative</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">this comes before o</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">zero</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the two are equal in order</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">positive</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">this comes after o</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Collections.sort(list)</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">uses compareTo, sorts in place</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One class, sorted by its natural order</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.ArrayList&lt;Student&gt; roll = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
roll.add(new Student("Ada", 3.6));
roll.add(new Student("Kim", 3.9));
roll.add(new Student("Zoe", 2.8));

System.out.println("before: " + roll);
java.util.Collections.sort(roll);
System.out.println("after:  " + roll);

java.util.Collections.reverse(roll);
System.out.println("desc:   " + roll);
System.out.println(java.util.Collections.max(roll));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">before: [Ada(3.6), Kim(3.9), Zoe(2.8)]
after:  [Zoe(2.8), Ada(3.6), Kim(3.9)]
desc:   [Kim(3.9), Ada(3.6), Zoe(2.8)]
Kim(3.9)</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Collections.sort</code> sorts the list <strong>in place</strong> and returns nothing — assigning its result is a compile error.</li><li><code>Double.compare(a, b)</code> returns the sign the interface asks for and avoids the rounding problems of subtracting two doubles.</li><li>Once a class is Comparable, <code>max</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>binarySearch</code> all work on it for free.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The sign is the whole contract</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">Student a = new Student("Ada", 3.6);
Student k = new Student("Kim", 3.9);

System.out.println(a.compareTo(k));
System.out.println(k.compareTo(a));
System.out.println(a.compareTo(a));

System.out.println("apple".compareTo("banana"));
System.out.println(Integer.valueOf(7).compareTo(3));

java.util.ArrayList&lt;Student&gt; l = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;(java.util.List.of(k, a));
l.sort(java.util.Comparator.comparing(s -&gt; s.name));
System.out.println("by name: " + l);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">-1
1
0
-1
1
by name: [Ada, Kim]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Only the <strong>sign</strong> matters, not the size: any negative value means "comes first".</li><li>The three calls show the contract in both directions plus the equal case.</li><li>A <code>Comparator</code> sorts by a different key without touching the class — <code>compareTo</code> defines the one natural order, comparators supply the alternatives.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing <code>return (int)(this.gpa - other.gpa);</code>. The cast truncates, so 3.9 and 3.6 both give 0 and the two students are reported as equal — the list comes back looking unsorted. Use <code>Double.compare</code>, and for ints <code>Integer.compare</code> rather than subtraction, which can also overflow.</div>"},"D286/u13/z13_6":{"body":"<p>A generic method that maps values shows the mechanics with two type parameters, and it is the shape behind every transform you will meet later.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static <k, v=""> V lookup(K[] keys, V[] values, K target, V fallback) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; keys.length; i++) {
        if (keys[i].equals(target)) { return values[i]; }   // equals, not == (3.2)
    }
    return fallback;
}

String[] codes = {"US", "GB", "JP"};
String[] names = {"United States", "United Kingdom", "Japan"};

lookup(codes, names, "GB", "Unknown");     // "United Kingdom"
lookup(codes, names, "ZZ", "Unknown");     // "Unknown"</k,></pre><p>Two parallel arrays again (5.5), and the same fragility again — which is exactly what <code>HashMap</code> (14.3) fixes by storing the pairs together and finding them without a loop.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static &lt;T, R&gt; List&lt;R&gt; map(List&lt;T&gt; in, Function&lt;T, R&gt; f) {
    List&lt;R&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    for (T x : in) out.add(f.apply(x));      // input untouched, new list returned
    return out;
}

List&lt;Integer&gt; lengths = map(names, s -&gt; s.length());   // T=String, R=Integer</pre><p>Two type parameters are needed because the input and output element types differ — <code>T</code> in, <code>R</code> out. Writing it with one would force the result to have the same element type as the source, which defeats the purpose.</p><p>Neither is written at the call: the compiler infers both from the arguments. That inference is what makes generic code readable rather than ceremonial.</p><p>Returning a new list rather than modifying the input is the same choice as 6.20 — the caller keeps the original, and the method has no side effect to reason about.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Multiple type parameters:</strong> declared as &lt;K, V&gt;, conventionally key and value</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Default / fallback value:</strong> what to return when the search finds nothing</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Generic lookup:</strong> a search whose key and result types are both parameterised</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Using <code>==</code> instead of <code>.equals()</code> here breaks the method for every reference type — including the Strings above. Inside a generic method you almost always want <code>.equals()</code>.</p></div><i data-d286_z13_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a generic method that transforms a list</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk428a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;T, R&gt;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">two placeholders: input type, result type</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">List&lt;T&gt; in</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what arrives</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">List&lt;R&gt; out</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">what comes back — a new list</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Function&lt;T, R&gt;</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the per-element transformation</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">x -&gt; x * 2</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a lambda supplying that function</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — One mapping method, several different transformations</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.List&lt;Integer&gt; nums = java.util.List.of(1, 2, 3, 4);
java.util.List&lt;String&gt; words = java.util.List.of("red", "green", "blue");

System.out.println(mapAll(nums, x -&gt; x * x));
System.out.println(mapAll(nums, x -&gt; "#" + x));
System.out.println(mapAll(words, String::toUpperCase));
System.out.println(mapAll(words, String::length));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">[1, 4, 9, 16]
[#1, #2, #3, #4]
[RED, GREEN, BLUE]
[3, 5, 4]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>T and R are independent: the second call takes Integers in and gives Strings out.</li><li>The transformation arrives as a parameter, so the loop is written once and never changes.</li><li><code>String::toUpperCase</code> is a method reference — shorthand for the lambda <code>s -&gt; s.toUpperCase()</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Filter and reduce, built the same way</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.List&lt;Integer&gt; nums = java.util.List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6);

System.out.println(keepIf(nums, x -&gt; x % 2 == 0));
System.out.println(reduce(nums, 0, (a, b) -&gt; a + b));
System.out.println(reduce(nums, 1, (a, b) -&gt; a * b));

java.util.List&lt;String&gt; words = java.util.List.of("red", "green", "blue");
System.out.println(keepIf(words, s -&gt; s.length() &gt; 3));
System.out.println(reduce(words, "", (a, b) -&gt; a + b));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">[2, 4, 6]
21
720
[green, blue]
redgreenblue</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code> is the standard name for a test returning boolean; <code>BinaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code> combines two T values into one.</li><li>The starting value matters: 0 for a sum, 1 for a product, <code>""</code> for joining text.</li><li>Every one of these methods is written once and works on any element type — that is what generics buy.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>When a generic method takes two type parameters, write down what each stands for before tracing it. In <code>&lt;T, R&gt; List&lt;R&gt; mapAll(List&lt;T&gt;, Function&lt;T, R&gt;)</code> the answer to "what type is the result?" is always R, whatever T happened to be.</div>"},"D286/u14/z14_1":{"body":"<p>The enhanced <code>for</code> loop from 5.10 works on every collection in this unit, and that is not a coincidence — it works on anything implementing <code>Iterable</code>, which every collection does.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (String s : arrayOfStrings)   { }
for (String s : arrayListOfStrings) { }
for (String s : hashSetOfStrings)   { }

// a Map needs one of its views, because it holds pairs
for (String key : map.keySet())            { }
for (Integer v : map.values())             { }
for (Map.Entry<string,integer> e : map.entrySet()) {
    e.getKey();  e.getValue();
}</string,integer></pre><p>The one hard rule: you may not add to or remove from a collection while a for-each is walking it. Doing so throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Removing requires an explicit <code>Iterator</code> and its <code>remove()</code>, or a plain indexed loop going backwards.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (String s : names) { ... }        // works on arrays AND anything Iterable

for (Map.Entry&lt;String,Integer&gt; e : counts.entrySet())
    System.out.println(e.getKey() + " " + e.getValue());</pre><p>The enhanced <code>for</code> works over any array and any type implementing <code>Iterable</code>, which every collection does. It is read-only in the sense that matters: you cannot replace an element, though you can call mutating methods on the object an element refers to.</p><p>Structurally modifying a collection while a for-each is running throws <strong><code>ConcurrentModificationException</code></strong> — adding or removing during the loop. Removing safely needs an explicit <code>Iterator</code> with <code>it.remove()</code>, or a backwards indexed loop.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Iterable:</strong> the interface that makes a type usable in an enhanced for loop</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Iterator:</strong> the object walking a collection; has hasNext(), next() and remove()</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ConcurrentModificationException:</strong> thrown when a collection is structurally modified during iteration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>entrySet():</strong> a Map view giving key-value pairs together in one pass</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Iterating a <code>HashSet</code> or <code>HashMap</code> gives no guaranteed order — it can differ between runs. If a question shows hash-based output in a specific order, the order is not something you can rely on.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → for Loops, break, and continue</strong></p><i data-d286fx23="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE COLLECTIONS FAMILY AT A GLANCE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 490 244" style="width:100%; max-width:530px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk90a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="160" y="10" width="150" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="235" y="29" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">«Collection»</text><rect x="30" y="76" width="120" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="90" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">«List» ordered</text><rect x="180" y="76" width="120" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="240" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">«Set» no dupes</text><rect x="330" y="76" width="130" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><text x="395" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">«Queue» take-order</text><line x1="90" y1="74" x2="196" y2="42" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk90a)"/><line x1="240" y1="74" x2="235" y2="42" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk90a)"/><line x1="395" y1="74" x2="276" y2="42" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk90a)"/><text x="90" y="130" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ArrayList</text><text x="90" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">LinkedList</text><text x="240" y="130" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">HashSet (fast, unordered)</text><text x="240" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">TreeSet (sorted)</text><text x="395" y="130" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">PriorityQueue</text><text x="395" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ArrayDeque</text><rect x="30" y="170" width="430" height="44" rx="8" fill="#f59e0b18" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="245" y="188" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Map&lt;K,V&gt; — key → value pairs — is SEPARATE: not a Collection</text><text x="245" y="204" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">HashMap (fast, unordered) · TreeMap (keys sorted)</text><text x="245" y="236" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Pick by need: order + index → List; uniqueness → Set; lookup by key → Map; process in order → Queue.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS141="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — walking a Map with entrySet and keeping a running total</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A <code>LinkedHashMap</code> keeps insertion order, so the output below is fixed rather than "whatever the hash decides". Each pass hands you one <code>Map.Entry</code>, and <code>getKey</code> / <code>getValue</code> read the two halves of it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">LinkedHashMap&lt;String,Integer&gt; credits = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();
credits.put("Java Fundamentals", 4);
credits.put("Discrete Math I", 3);
credits.put("Data Management", 4);

int total = 0;
for (Map.Entry&lt;String,Integer&gt; e : credits.entrySet()) {
    total += e.getValue();
    System.out.println(e.getKey() + " -&gt; " + e.getValue()
                       + "  (running total " + total + ")");
}
System.out.println("Total credits: " + total);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">Java Fundamentals -&gt; 4  (running total 4)
Discrete Math I -&gt; 3  (running total 7)
Data Management -&gt; 4  (running total 11)
Total credits: 11</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The loop variable is one <code>Map.Entry&lt;String,Integer&gt;</code> per pass — not a key, not a value, the pair.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>total += e.getValue()</code> unboxes the <code>Integer</code> to an <code>int</code> automatically, so the arithmetic is ordinary.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Doing it with <code>keySet()</code> instead would need <code>credits.get(k)</code> inside the loop: a second lookup per element for information the iterator already had.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>total</code> is declared <em>before</em> the loop. Declared inside, it would be reborn as 0 on every pass — the accumulator rule from Unit 4.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the exception, and the two legal ways to remove during a walk</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Removing from a collection while a for-each is walking it is the error this section exists to prevent. The instructive part is that the removal <em>succeeds</em> — the failure arrives on the next pass, when the iterator notices the collection changed underneath it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">List&lt;String&gt; roster = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of("Ada","Raj","Mia","Sam"));
try {
    for (String s : roster) { if (s.equals("Raj")) roster.remove(s); }
} catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {
    System.out.println("caught " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
}
System.out.println("after the failed attempt: " + roster);

List&lt;String&gt; b = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of("Ada","Raj","Mia","Sam"));
Iterator&lt;String&gt; it = b.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) { if (it.next().equals("Raj")) it.remove(); }
System.out.println("iterator.remove: " + b);

List&lt;String&gt; c = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of("Ada","Raj","Mia","Sam"));
c.removeIf(s -&gt; s.equals("Raj"));
System.out.println("removeIf: " + c);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">caught ConcurrentModificationException
after the failed attempt: [Ada, Mia, Sam]
iterator.remove: [Ada, Mia, Sam]
removeIf: [Ada, Mia, Sam]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Line 2 of the output is the point: <code>Raj</code> really was removed. The list is already modified when the exception is thrown.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The iterator holds a modification count it took at creation. On the next <code>next()</code> it compares that count against the list’s and throws when they disagree.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>it.remove()</code> works because the iterator performs the removal itself and updates its own count to match.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>removeIf</code> is the one-line version of the same thing and is what you should write in new code.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">One quirk worth knowing: removing the <em>second-to-last</em> element happens to leave <code>hasNext()</code> returning false, so that one case exits quietly instead of throwing. Code that "works" for one test input and throws for another is usually this.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Assigning to the loop variable changes nothing. <code>for (String s : names) s = s.toUpperCase();</code> leaves <code>names</code> as <code>[ada, raj]</code> — <code>s</code> is a fresh copy of the reference, and reassigning it drops the copy on the floor. Calling a <em>mutating method</em> on the element does show up: a loop doing <code>sb.append("-01")</code> over a list of <code>StringBuilder</code> objects prints <code>[lab-01]</code>. If you need to replace elements, use an indexed loop with <code>names.set(i, ...)</code>, which turns the same list into <code>[ADA, RAJ]</code>. All three lines were run to confirm it.</p></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_2":{"body":"<p><code>LinkedList</code> is the linked list of 8.2 as a standard collection. It offers the same methods as <code>ArrayList</code> and makes the opposite performance trade, which is the only reason to choose it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">LinkedList<string> list = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
list.add("b");
list.addFirst("a");            // O(1) — ArrayList would shift everything
list.addLast("c");
list.removeFirst();
list.getFirst();

                ArrayList        LinkedList
get(i)          O(1)             O(n)  — must walk from one end
add at end      O(1) amortised   O(1)
add at front    O(n)             O(1)
remove middle   O(n) shift       O(1) once you are there</string></pre><p>In practice <code>ArrayList</code> wins most of the time, because <code>get(i)</code> is common and its memory layout is faster in ways the table does not show. <code>LinkedList</code> earns its place when you work at the ends — which is exactly what makes it the natural backing for a queue or a deque (14.5, 14.6).</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"><code>ArrayList</code></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;"><code>LinkedList</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>get(i)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(n) — walks from an end</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">add / remove at the front</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(n) — shifts everything</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>O(1)</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">add at the end</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(1) amortised</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(1)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">memory</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one array</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">two references per element</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">in practice</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>the default choice</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">only for heavy end-insertion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Both implement <code>List</code>, so declaring the variable as <code>List&lt;String&gt; l = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();</code> keeps the choice swappable — programming to the interface, and the reason exam code so often declares the interface type.</p><p>The trap is an indexed loop over a <code>LinkedList</code>: each <code>get(i)</code> walks from the head, so the loop is O(n²) while looking identical to the array version. Use a for-each, which uses one iterator and stays O(n).</p><p>In practice <code>ArrayList</code> wins most real comparisons even where the theory favours <code>LinkedList</code>, because contiguous memory is far friendlier to the CPU cache.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>LinkedList:</strong> a doubly-linked list implementing both List and Deque</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Random access:</strong> reaching element i directly; ArrayList has it, LinkedList does not</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>addFirst / removeFirst:</strong> constant-time operations at the head</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>List interface:</strong> the common contract both classes implement, so either can be swapped in</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Declare the variable as <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> rather than the concrete class. Then swapping the implementation is a one-line change — the practical use of interfaces from 10.11.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS142="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHERE THE TWO LISTS KEEP THEIR ELEMENTS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 284" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk268a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="24" y="34" width="300" height="116" rx="9" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.1" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="174" y="54" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ArrayList — one contiguous array</text><rect x="44" y="66" width="52" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="70" y="87.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"a"</text><text x="70" y="115" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">0</text><rect x="96" y="66" width="52" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="122" y="87.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"b"</text><text x="122" y="115" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="148" y="66" width="52" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="174" y="87.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"c"</text><text x="174" y="115" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="200" y="66" width="52" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="226" y="87.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"d"</text><text x="226" y="115" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="174" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">address of a[i] = base + i × slot   →  get(i) is one step</text><rect x="340" y="34" width="300" height="116" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.1" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="490" y="54" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LinkedList — nodes joined by references</text><rect x="356" y="68" width="60" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="374" y1="68" x2="374" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="398" y1="68" x2="398" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="386" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"a"</text><line x1="416" y1="78" x2="422" y2="78" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><rect x="424" y="68" width="60" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="442" y1="68" x2="442" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="466" y1="68" x2="466" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="454" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"b"</text><line x1="484" y1="78" x2="490" y2="78" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><line x1="424" y1="90" x2="418" y2="90" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><rect x="492" y="68" width="60" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="510" y1="68" x2="510" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="534" y1="68" x2="534" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="522" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"c"</text><line x1="552" y1="78" x2="558" y2="78" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><line x1="492" y1="90" x2="486" y2="90" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><rect x="560" y="68" width="60" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="578" y1="68" x2="578" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="602" y1="68" x2="602" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="590" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"d"</text><line x1="560" y1="90" x2="554" y2="90" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><text x="490" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">prev  value  next</text><text x="490" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">get(2) must walk a→b→c   →  O(n)</text><text x="332" y="176" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">inserting at the FRONT</text><rect x="24" y="188" width="300" height="82" rx="9" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.07" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="44" y="200" width="52" height="30" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.22)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="70" y="219.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"z"</text><rect x="96" y="200" width="52" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="122" y="219.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"a"</text><rect x="148" y="200" width="52" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="174" y="219.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"b"</text><rect x="200" y="200" width="52" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="226" y="219.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"c"</text><text x="174" y="254" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">every existing element copied one slot right — O(n)</text><rect x="340" y="188" width="300" height="82" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.07" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="356" y="200" width="60" height="30" rx="5" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.18)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="386" y="220" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"z"</text><line x1="416" y1="215" x2="440" y2="215" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk268a)"/><rect x="440" y="200" width="60" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="470" y="220" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"a"</text><text x="560" y="218" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">… unchanged</text><text x="490" y="254" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">two references rewritten, nothing copied — O(1)</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a boarding line built at both ends</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>LinkedList</code> is the only standard <code>List</code> that also implements <code>Deque</code>, so the end operations have their own names and cost nothing.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">LinkedList&lt;String&gt; boarding = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
boarding.add("Raj");         // to the back
boarding.add("Mia");
boarding.addFirst("Ada");    // priority boarding — jumps the line
boarding.addLast("Sam");
System.out.println(boarding);
System.out.println("front: " + boarding.getFirst() + "   back: " + boarding.getLast());
System.out.println("boarded: " + boarding.removeFirst());
System.out.println(boarding);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">[Ada, Raj, Mia, Sam]
front: Ada   back: Sam
boarded: Ada
[Raj, Mia, Sam]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>add</code> and <code>addLast</code> are the same operation; both append.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>addFirst("Ada")</code> puts Ada ahead of Raj by rewriting two references. In an <code>ArrayList</code> the same call is <code>add(0, "Ada")</code> and copies every element right.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>removeFirst()</code> returns the element it removed, which is why the boarded passenger can be printed in the same line.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Read the last line as the queue discipline of 14.5 in list clothing — front out, back in.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — same result, different work — and why the variable is declared as List</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">List&lt;Integer&gt; al = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(20, 30, 40));
al.add(0, 10);
System.out.println("ArrayList after add(0,10): " + al);

LinkedList&lt;Integer&gt; ll = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;(List.of(20, 30, 40));
ll.addFirst(10);
System.out.println("LinkedList after addFirst(10): " + ll);
System.out.println("same contents? " + al.equals(ll));

List&lt;String&gt; l = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;(List.of("a","b","c"));
System.out.println(l.getClass().getSimpleName()
                   + " used through the List interface: " + l);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">ArrayList after add(0,10): [10, 20, 30, 40]
LinkedList after addFirst(10): [10, 20, 30, 40]
same contents? true
LinkedList used through the List interface: [a, b, c]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The contents are identical — <code>equals</code> on two <code>List</code>s compares elements in order, not classes, so an <code>ArrayList</code> can equal a <code>LinkedList</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <em>work</em> differs: three element copies against two reference rewrites. At three elements that is invisible; at a million it is the whole difference.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The last block is the practical point of the interface. The variable is a <code>List</code>, the object is a <code>LinkedList</code>, and swapping in <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()</code> changes one word and nothing else.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Declaring it as <code>LinkedList</code> instead would let calling code use <code>addFirst</code>, and then you could no longer swap the implementation.</li></ol></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_3":{"body":"<p>A <code>HashMap</code> stores key-value pairs and finds a value by its key in roughly constant time. It is the fix for the parallel-array lookup of 13.6, and it is the collection you will reach for most often after <code>ArrayList</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">HashMap<string, integer=""> scores = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

scores.put("Ada", 95);            // add or REPLACE
scores.put("Ada", 97);            // replaces — keys are unique
scores.get("Ada");                // 97
scores.get("Nobody");             // null — NOT an exception
scores.getOrDefault("Nobody", 0); // 0 — usually what you want
scores.containsKey("Ada");        // true
scores.remove("Ada");
scores.size();

for (String k : scores.keySet()) { System.out.println(k + " " + scores.get(k)); }</string,></pre><p>Two behaviours cause most bugs. <code>put</code> with an existing key <em>replaces</em> rather than adding, and <code>get</code> on a missing key returns <code>null</code> rather than throwing — so assigning it straight to an <code>int</code> throws <code>NullPointerException</code> during unboxing (7.13).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Map&lt;String,Integer&gt; ages = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
ages.put("Ana", 30);
ages.put("Ana", 31);            // same key — REPLACES, does not add
ages.get("Zed");                // null — the key is absent
ages.getOrDefault("Zed", 0);    // 0 — no null to handle

// the counting idiom
counts.put(w, counts.getOrDefault(w, 0) + 1);
counts.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);        // the same thing, shorter</pre><p>A map stores key → value pairs with <strong>unique keys</strong>. Re-putting an existing key overwrites and returns the old value; it never creates a second entry. Values may repeat freely.</p><p><code>get</code> on a missing key returns <code>null</code>, which unboxes into a <code>NullPointerException</code> if you assign it to an <code>int</code>. <code>getOrDefault</code> exists to remove that whole class of bug, and <code>containsKey</code> is the right test when <code>null</code> is itself a legitimate stored value.</p><p><code>HashMap</code> gives O(1) average lookup and <strong>no ordering at all</strong> — iteration order is unspecified and may change. Use <code>LinkedHashMap</code> for insertion order or <code>TreeMap</code> for sorted keys. Iterate with <code>entrySet()</code>, which visits keys and values together instead of looking each value up again.</p><p>A custom class used as a key must override both <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code>, or lookups fail even with an identical-looking key.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>HashMap:</strong> an unordered map from unique keys to values, with O(1) average lookup</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Key / value:</strong> the lookup token and the data stored under it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>getOrDefault:</strong> returns a supplied default instead of null when the key is absent</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>hashCode / equals contract:</strong> a key class must override both, or lookups silently fail (10.4)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Using your own class as a key without overriding <code>hashCode()</code> and <code>equals()</code> means an equal-looking key never finds its value. It compiles, it runs, and it returns null forever.</p></div><i data-d286fx24="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — HASHMAP: THE KEY’S HASH PICKS THE BUCKET</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 490 168" style="width:100%; max-width:530px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk91a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="40" width="120" height="36" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="80" y="55" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">key</text><text x="80" y="70" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"apple"</text><line x1="140" y1="58" x2="200" y2="58" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk91a)"/><text x="170" y="48" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">hash</text><rect x="200" y="20" width="110" height="100" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="255" y="14" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">buckets</text><line x1="200" y1="45" x2="310" y2="45" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="200" y1="70" x2="310" y2="70" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="200" y1="95" x2="310" y2="95" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="200" y1="120" x2="310" y2="120" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="201" y="46" width="108" height="23" fill="#6366f133"/><line x1="310" y1="58" x2="370" y2="58" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk91a)"/><rect x="370" y="40" width="100" height="36" rx="7" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="420" y="55" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">value</text><text x="420" y="70" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0.55</text><text x="245" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">put("apple", 0.55) stores the pair; get("apple") re-hashes the key and jumps straight to it.</text><text x="245" y="160" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">One value per key — put with an existing key REPLACES and returns the old value.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS143="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting words — the single most common Map idiom</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A <code>TreeMap</code> is used here rather than a <code>HashMap</code> only so the printed order is fixed and can be shown; every method below behaves identically on a <code>HashMap</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String text = "the rain in spain falls mainly in the plain";
Map&lt;String,Integer&gt; counts = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();

for (String w : text.split(" ")) {
    counts.put(w, counts.getOrDefault(w, 0) + 1);
}
System.out.println(counts);

Map&lt;String,Integer&gt; m2 = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();
for (String w : text.split(" ")) m2.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);
System.out.println("merge gives the same: " + m2.equals(counts));

System.out.println("in -&gt; " + counts.get("in")
    + ", missing -&gt; " + counts.get("fog")
    + ", getOrDefault -&gt; " + counts.getOrDefault("fog", 0));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">{falls=1, in=2, mainly=1, plain=1, rain=1, spain=1, the=2}
merge gives the same: true
in -&gt; 2, missing -&gt; null, getOrDefault -&gt; 0</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">First time a word is seen, <code>getOrDefault(w, 0)</code> returns 0, so <code>put</code> stores 1.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Every later time it returns the stored count, and <code>put</code> <em>replaces</em> the entry — this is exactly the replace-not-add behaviour, used deliberately.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>merge(w, 1, Integer::sum)</code> says: store 1 if absent, otherwise combine the old and new values with <code>+</code>. Same result, one call.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The third line shows the three outcomes for a lookup: a real value, <code>null</code> for a missing key, and a supplied default that avoids the <code>null</code> entirely.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — what put returns, and where the NullPointerException comes from</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Map&lt;String,Integer&gt; scores = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
System.out.println("first put returns: " + scores.put("Ada", 95));
System.out.println("second put returns: " + scores.put("Ada", 97));
System.out.println("size after two puts: " + scores.size());
System.out.println("get(\"Ada\") = " + scores.get("Ada"));

try {
    int missing = scores.get("Nobody");   // Integer null -&gt; int
    System.out.println(missing);
} catch (NullPointerException e) {
    System.out.println("caught NullPointerException while unboxing null");
}
System.out.println("getOrDefault: " + scores.getOrDefault("Nobody", 0));
System.out.println("containsKey(\"Nobody\") = " + scores.containsKey("Nobody"));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">first put returns: null
second put returns: 95
size after two puts: 1
get("Ada") = 97
caught NullPointerException while unboxing null
getOrDefault: 0
containsKey("Nobody") = false</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>put</code> returns the <em>previous</em> value for that key, or <code>null</code> if there was none. That return value is how you find out whether you overwrote something.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Two puts, one entry: <code>size()</code> is 1. Keys are unique, so the second put replaced rather than added.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The crash is not in <code>get</code> — <code>get</code> returned <code>null</code> perfectly happily. It is the assignment to <code>int</code> that calls <code>.intValue()</code> on <code>null</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Read the stack trace of that failure carefully on the exam: it points at the assignment line, not at the map.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three things get asked. (1) <em>Does a repeated key add a second entry?</em> No — it replaces, and <code>size()</code> proves it. (2) <em>What does <code>get</code> return for a missing key?</em> <code>null</code>, never an exception — the exception comes later, on unboxing. (3) <em>What order does iteration produce?</em> For <code>HashMap</code>, unspecified; if an answer option depends on hash order it is wrong on principle. Reach for <code>entrySet()</code> when you need both halves, <code>keySet()</code> when you only need keys, and <code>LinkedHashMap</code> or <code>TreeMap</code> when the question says "in the order added" or "in sorted order".</p></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_4":{"body":"<p>A <code>HashSet</code> is a collection with no duplicates and no order. It answers one question extremely fast — is this already in here — which is the linear <code>contains</code> of 7.16 made constant.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">HashSet<string> seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();

seen.add("a");           // true  — it was added
seen.add("a");           // false — already present, set unchanged
seen.contains("a");      // true  — O(1) average
seen.remove("a");
seen.size();

// the standard use: removing duplicates
ArrayList<string> unique = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(new HashSet&lt;&gt;(withDuplicates));</string></string></pre><p><code>add</code> returning a boolean is genuinely useful: it tells you whether the item was new, which turns "have I seen this before" into a single call. And a set that holds your own objects depends on the same <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> contract as a map key.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Set&lt;String&gt; seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
seen.add("a");                  // true  — it was added
seen.add("a");                  // false — already present, set unchanged
seen.contains("a");             // O(1) average

List&lt;String&gt; unique = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(new HashSet&lt;&gt;(withDuplicates));</pre><p>A set holds no duplicates and has no positions — there is no <code>get(i)</code>, because there is no <em>i</em>. <code>add</code> returning a boolean is genuinely useful: it tells you whether the value was new, which answers "have I seen this before?" in one call.</p><p>Its purpose is membership. Asking "is this in the collection?" is O(1) on a <code>HashSet</code> against O(n) on a list, which is the difference between a fast and a slow duplicate check on large data.</p><p><code>HashSet</code> is unordered, <code>LinkedHashSet</code> keeps insertion order, <code>TreeSet</code> keeps sorted order. Duplicates are decided by <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code>, so custom objects need both — without them, two identical-looking objects are two different elements.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>HashSet:</strong> an unordered collection of unique elements</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Set interface:</strong> the contract for a duplicate-free collection</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>add() return value:</strong> true if the element was not already present</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>LinkedHashSet:</strong> a Set that preserves insertion order</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>If a question needs "unique" or "have I seen this", the answer is a Set. If it needs "how many of each", the answer is a <code>HashMap&lt;T, Integer&gt;</code>.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS144="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — HOW A SET DECIDES SOMETHING IS ALREADY IN IT</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 292" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk269a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">add("BR-104") when "BR-104" is already in the set</text><circle cx="52" cy="66" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="52" y="70" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="74" y="48" width="128" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="138" y="63" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">hashCode()</text><text x="138" y="80" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the object’s own number</text><line x1="202" y1="66" x2="244" y2="66" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk269a)"/><circle cx="258" cy="66" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="258" y="70" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="280" y="48" width="150" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="355" y="63" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">index = hash & (n−1)</text><text x="355" y="80" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">which bucket to look in</text><line x1="430" y1="66" x2="470" y2="66" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk269a)"/><circle cx="484" cy="66" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="484" y="70" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="504" y="48" width="140" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="574" y="63" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">equals()</text><text x="574" y="80" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">compare with what is there</text><text x="120" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the bucket array</text><rect x="52" y="134" width="44" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="150.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">0</text><rect x="52" y="161" width="44" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="177.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="52" y="188" width="44" height="24" rx="4" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="204.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line x1="96" y1="200" x2="128" y2="200" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk269a)"/><rect x="128" y="187" width="92" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="174" y="204.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"BR-104"</text><rect x="52" y="215" width="44" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="231.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="52" y="242" width="44" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="74" y="258.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="74" y="274" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">buckets 0,1,3,4 are never looked at</text><rect x="250" y="128" width="394" height="130" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.09" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="447" y="150" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the two-stage test</text><text x="447" y="172" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">same hash?  no  →  different element, stored</text><text x="447" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">same hash, equals false  →  different element, stored</text><text x="447" y="216" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">same hash, equals true  →  duplicate: add() returns false</text><text x="447" y="242" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">a class that overrides only one of the two breaks this chain</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the boolean from add() answers "have I seen this before?"</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String[] scans = {"BR-104","BR-221","BR-104","BR-880","BR-221","BR-104"};
Set&lt;String&gt; seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
List&lt;String&gt; firstTime = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

for (String s : scans) {
    boolean isNew = seen.add(s);
    System.out.println(s + "  add() -&gt; " + isNew);
    if (isNew) firstTime.add(s);
}
System.out.println("distinct scans: " + seen.size());
System.out.println("in arrival order: " + firstTime);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">BR-104  add() -&gt; true
BR-221  add() -&gt; true
BR-104  add() -&gt; false
BR-880  add() -&gt; true
BR-221  add() -&gt; false
BR-104  add() -&gt; false
distinct scans: 3
in arrival order: [BR-104, BR-221, BR-880]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>add</code> returns <code>true</code> only when the element was not already present, so the boolean <em>is</em> the duplicate test — no separate <code>contains</code> call needed.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Six scans, three distinct values: the set silently absorbed the repeats.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>firstTime</code> list exists because a <code>HashSet</code> has no order. Recording arrival order takes a second structure — or a <code>LinkedHashSet</code>, which does it for you.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">This is the deduplication pattern in full: one set for membership, one list for order.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — your own class in a set: what happens without equals and hashCode</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static class BadgeA {                 // no equals, no hashCode
    final String id;
    BadgeA(String id){ this.id = id; }
}
static class BadgeB {                 // both overridden
    final String id;
    BadgeB(String id){ this.id = id; }
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o){
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof BadgeB)) return false;
        return id.equals(((BadgeB) o).id);
    }
    @Override public int hashCode(){ return id.hashCode(); }
}

Set&lt;BadgeA&gt; s1 = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
s1.add(new BadgeA("E-77")); s1.add(new BadgeA("E-77"));
Set&lt;BadgeB&gt; s2 = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
s2.add(new BadgeB("E-77")); s2.add(new BadgeB("E-77"));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">without equals/hashCode: size=2, contains(new BadgeA("E-77")) = false
with equals/hashCode:    size=1, contains(new BadgeB("E-77")) = true</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>BadgeA</code> inherits <code>Object.hashCode()</code>, which is derived from identity, so two separately-created badges land in different buckets and never meet.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Because they never meet, <code>equals</code> is never even consulted — the set is convinced they are different elements.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>BadgeB</code> derives its hash from the <code>id</code> field, so both badges reach the same bucket, <code>equals</code> runs, and the second add is rejected.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>contains</code> fails for the same reason <code>add</code> did: a freshly built lookup key hashes somewhere else entirely.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The rule the two classes demonstrate: <strong>equal objects must have equal hash codes.</strong> Override the two together or neither.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">It is not enough for two objects to <em>look</em> equal — a set finds an element by its hash first. Override <code>equals</code> alone and the object still hashes by identity, so <code>contains</code> returns <code>false</code> for something that is demonstrably in the set (verified above: size 2 where you expected 1). The symptom is a duplicate check that quietly never fires, and it is silent — nothing warns you, nothing throws.</p></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u14_z14_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the three set operations, and the method that does each</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk432a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.addAll(b)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">union — a becomes everything in either</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.retainAll(b)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">intersection — a keeps only what is in both</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.removeAll(b)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">difference — a drops everything found in b</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">they MUTATE a</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">each changes a in place and returns boolean</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a.containsAll(b)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">subset test — no change to a</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — All three, on the same pair of sets</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; u = a(); u.addAll(b());
java.util.TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; i = a(); i.retainAll(b());
java.util.TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; d = a(); d.removeAll(b());

System.out.println("a = " + a() + "   b = " + b());
System.out.println("union        addAll    : " + u);
System.out.println("intersection retainAll : " + i);
System.out.println("difference   removeAll : " + d);
System.out.println("containsAll            : " + a().containsAll(java.util.List.of(2, 3)));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">a = [1, 2, 3, 4]   b = [3, 4, 5]
union        addAll    : [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
intersection retainAll : [3, 4]
difference   removeAll : [1, 2]
containsAll            : true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each call modifies the set it is invoked on — that is why a fresh copy of <code>a</code> is built for each line.</li><li><code>retainAll</code> keeps the shared elements; <code>removeAll</code> keeps the ones <em>not</em> shared. Mixing the two up is the usual error.</li><li>A <code>TreeSet</code> prints in sorted order, which is why the results read cleanly; a <code>HashSet</code> gives no ordering guarantee.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Expecting these to return a new set. They return <code>boolean</code> (whether the set changed) and mutate the receiver, so <code>Set&lt;Integer&gt; c = a.retainAll(b);</code> does not compile — and <code>a</code> has already been altered.</div>"},"D286/u14/z14_5":{"body":"<p>A <strong>queue</strong> is first-in-first-out — the printer queue, the waiting line. <code>Queue</code> is an interface, so you instantiate something that implements it, usually a <code>LinkedList</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Queue<string> q = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();

q.add("first");        q.offer("second");    // add to the BACK
q.peek();              // "first" — look at the front, do not remove
q.poll();              // "first" — remove and return the front
q.isEmpty();

// add/remove throw when they fail; offer/poll/peek return false or null
q.remove();            // NoSuchElementException on an empty queue
q.poll();              // null on an empty queue</string></pre><p>That pairing is the detail the exam tests: every queue operation comes in a throwing version and a returning version. Which you want depends on whether an empty queue is an error or an ordinary state.</p><h3>Runnable, and where a queue is really used</h3><p>Queues appear most often where work is being handed between parts of a program, and the unit of work is usually a <strong><code>Runnable</code></strong> — the interface with one method, <code>run()</code>, representing a task with no arguments and no result.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Runnable task = () -&gt; System.out.println("working");
Queue<runnable> work = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
work.add(task);
work.poll().run();          // take the next task and execute it</runnable></pre><p>That is the shape of every job queue and thread pool: producers <code>offer</code> tasks at the back, a worker <code>poll</code>s from the front and calls <code>run()</code>. Because <code>Runnable</code> has a single abstract method, a lambda can supply it directly, which is why it appears wherever "a piece of work to do later" is needed.</p><i data-d286merge="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Queue&lt;String&gt; q = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();      // Queue is an INTERFACE
q.offer("a");                  // add at the back  (add() throws when full)
q.peek();                      // look at the front, do not remove
q.poll();                      // remove the front — null if empty

while (!q.isEmpty()) process(q.poll());     // the standard drain loop</pre><p>A queue is <strong>FIFO</strong> — first in, first out, like a checkout line. Work arrives at the back and is taken from the front.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Want</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Safe form (returns null/false)</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Throwing form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">add</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>offer</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>add</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">remove</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>poll</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>remove</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">inspect</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>peek</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>element</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Prefer the left column: <code>poll</code> on an empty queue returns <code>null</code> while <code>remove</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>. The distinction is the most-tested detail in this section.</p><p>Queues model anything processed in arrival order — print jobs, task pipelines, breadth-first search. A stack is the opposite discipline, LIFO, and choosing between them is choosing the order the work comes back out.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Queue:</strong> a FIFO collection — first in, first out</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>offer / poll / peek:</strong> add, remove-front and inspect-front; return special values on failure</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>add / remove / element:</strong> the same three operations, throwing on failure instead</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>FIFO:</strong> first-in-first-out ordering</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>Queue&lt;String&gt; q = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;()</code> is the standard declaration. Declaring it as <code>LinkedList</code> would expose list methods that break the queue discipline.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS145="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — FIFO: THE ORDER WORK COMES BACK OUT</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 336" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk270a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a queue is a pipe: in at the back, out at the front</text><rect x="150" y="44" width="364" height="62" rx="9" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.08" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="166" y="56" width="106" height="38" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="219" y="79.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">report.pdf</text><rect x="278" y="56" width="106" height="38" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="331" y="79.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">labels.png</text><rect x="390" y="56" width="106" height="38" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="443" y="79.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">invoice.pdf</text><text x="219" y="116" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FRONT (head)</text><text x="443" y="116" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">BACK (tail)</text><line x1="596" y1="75" x2="528" y2="75" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk270a)"/><text x="614" y="60" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">offer</text><text x="620" y="92" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">add</text><line x1="140" y1="75" x2="74" y2="75" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk270a)"/><text x="60" y="60" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">poll</text><text x="56" y="92" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">remove</text><text x="332" y="137" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">peek / element look at the front without taking it</text><text x="332" y="168" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the drain loop, one pass at a time</text><text x="148" y="206" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">start</text><rect x="160" y="188" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="209" y="205.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">report.pdf</text><rect x="264" y="188" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="313" y="205.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">labels.png</text><rect x="368" y="188" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="417" y="205.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">invoice.pdf</text><text x="148" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">poll → report.pdf</text><rect x="160" y="222" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="209" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">labels.png</text><rect x="264" y="222" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="313" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">invoice.pdf</text><rect x="368" y="222" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.06)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="148" y="274" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">poll → labels.png</text><rect x="160" y="256" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="209" y="273.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">invoice.pdf</text><rect x="264" y="256" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.06)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="368" y="256" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.06)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="148" y="308" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">poll → invoice.pdf</text><rect x="160" y="290" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.06)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="264" y="290" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.06)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="368" y="290" width="98" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.06)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="486" y="308" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-style="italic">isEmpty() → true, loop ends</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — draining a print queue, watching the size</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Queue&lt;String&gt; jobs = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
jobs.offer("report.pdf");
jobs.offer("labels.png");
jobs.offer("invoice.pdf");
System.out.println("queue: " + jobs + "   size " + jobs.size());
System.out.println("peek: " + jobs.peek() + "   size still " + jobs.size());

while (!jobs.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println("printing " + jobs.poll() + "   remaining " + jobs);
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">queue: [report.pdf, labels.png, invoice.pdf]   size 3
peek: report.pdf   size still 3
printing report.pdf   remaining [labels.png, invoice.pdf]
printing labels.png   remaining [invoice.pdf]
printing invoice.pdf   remaining []</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Printing a queue shows it front-first, so the leftmost element is the next one out.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>peek</code> reads the front and leaves it there — the size is unchanged, which is the whole difference from <code>poll</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>poll</code> removes <em>and</em> returns, so it can be used directly inside the print statement.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>while (!isEmpty())</code> with <code>poll</code> inside is the standard drain. Written as <code>for (String s : jobs)</code> instead, the queue would still be full at the end.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — empty queue: which methods return, and which throw</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Queue&lt;String&gt; q = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
System.out.println("poll on empty:  " + q.poll());
System.out.println("peek on empty:  " + q.peek());
try { q.remove(); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
    System.out.println("remove on empty throws " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
}
try { q.element(); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
    System.out.println("element on empty throws " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">poll on empty:  null
peek on empty:  null
remove on empty throws NoSuchElementException
element on empty throws NoSuchElementException</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>poll</code> and <code>peek</code> report emptiness as a value you can test: <code>if (job == null) return;</code></li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>remove</code> and <code>element</code> report it as an exception, which is right only when an empty queue means something has gone wrong.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Pick by intention. A worker thread finding no work is normal — use <code>poll</code>. A protocol that guarantees a message is waiting is broken if there is none — use <code>remove</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The exception class is <code>NoSuchElementException</code>, not <code>EmptyStackException</code>; that one belongs to the legacy <code>Stack</code> in 14.9.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>remove()</code> and <code>remove(Object)</code> are different methods, and on a <code>LinkedList&lt;Integer&gt;</code> there is a third: <code>remove(int index)</code>. Declared as <code>LinkedList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, <code>list.remove(1)</code> removes the element <em>at index 1</em>, turning <code>[10, 20, 30]</code> into <code>[10, 30]</code>. Declared as <code>Queue&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, the index version is not visible, so <code>q.remove(Integer.valueOf(10))</code> removes the <em>value</em> 10 and gives <code>[20, 30]</code>. Both were run to confirm. Declaring the variable as <code>Queue</code> is what keeps the list methods out of reach.</p></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_6":{"body":"<p>A <strong>deque</strong> — "double-ended queue" — allows adding and removing at both ends, which makes it a queue and a stack at once.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Deque<string> d = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();

d.addFirst("a");   d.addLast("b");
d.peekFirst();     d.peekLast();
d.pollFirst();     d.pollLast();

// as a STACK (LIFO)          // as a QUEUE (FIFO)
d.push("x");                  d.addLast("x");
d.pop();                      d.pollFirst();</string></pre><p><code>ArrayDeque</code> is the recommended implementation for both roles, and it is the reason the legacy <code>Stack</code> class of 14.9 is no longer advised. <code>push</code> and <code>pop</code> on a Deque work at the <em>front</em>, which is what makes it last-in-first-out.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Deque&lt;Integer&gt; d = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();

d.push(1);      d.pop();       // used as a STACK — both at the front
d.offerLast(1); d.pollFirst(); // used as a QUEUE — in at the back, out at the front</pre><p>A <strong>deque</strong> — "double-ended queue" — allows adding and removing at both ends in O(1), so one type covers both stack and queue behaviour. The method names say which end: <code>First</code> or <code>Last</code>.</p><p><code>ArrayDeque</code> is the recommended implementation, and it is the modern replacement for the legacy <code>Stack</code> class, which extends <code>Vector</code> and is synchronised and slower. It rejects <code>null</code> elements outright, precisely so that a <code>null</code> from <code>poll</code> unambiguously means "empty".</p><p>Pick one idiom and keep it. Mixing <code>push</code> with <code>pollLast</code> in the same code is legal and unreadable, because the reader can no longer tell which discipline is intended.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Deque:</strong> a double-ended queue supporting insertion and removal at both ends</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ArrayDeque:</strong> the standard resizable-array implementation; faster than LinkedList for this</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>push / pop:</strong> Deque methods operating on the front, giving stack behaviour</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>LIFO:</strong> last-in-first-out ordering</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>One class covers both disciplines, so the exam asks which <em>end</em> a method touches. <code>push</code> and <code>pop</code> are the front; <code>addLast</code> and <code>pollFirst</code> are the queue pair.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS146="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A DEQUE IS A STACK AND A QUEUE, DEPENDING ON WHICH END YOU USE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 272" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk271a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one structure, two disciplines — the method name says which end</text><rect x="196" y="44" width="68" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="230" y="68.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">cart</text><rect x="270" y="44" width="68" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="304" y="68.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">catalog</text><rect x="344" y="44" width="68" height="40" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="378" y="68.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">home</text><text x="230" y="100" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FIRST / head</text><text x="378" y="100" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LAST / tail</text><line x1="150" y1="52" x2="190" y2="52" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk271a)"/><text x="146" y="46" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">push / addFirst / offerFirst</text><line x1="190" y1="76" x2="150" y2="76" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk271a)"/><text x="146" y="80" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">pop / removeFirst / pollFirst</text><line x1="514" y1="52" x2="474" y2="52" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk271a)"/><text x="518" y="46" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">addLast / offerLast</text><line x1="474" y1="76" x2="514" y2="76" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk271a)"/><text x="518" y="80" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">removeLast / pollLast</text><rect x="30" y="124" width="296" height="132" rx="9" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.1" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="178" y="146" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">used as a STACK  (LIFO)</text><text x="178" y="168" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">push at the front, pop at the front</text><rect x="72" y="182" width="62" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="103" y="200.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="138" y="182" width="62" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="169" y="200.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="204" y="182" width="62" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="235" y="200.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="178" y="232" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">in 1,2,3  →  out 3,2,1</text><text x="178" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the newest is nearest the exit</text><rect x="338" y="124" width="296" height="132" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.1" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="486" y="146" fill="#10b981" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">used as a QUEUE  (FIFO)</text><text x="486" y="168" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">offerLast at the back, pollFirst at the front</text><rect x="380" y="182" width="62" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="411" y="200.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="446" y="182" width="62" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="477" y="200.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="512" y="182" width="62" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="543" y="200.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="486" y="232" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">in 1,2,3  →  out 1,2,3</text><text x="486" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the oldest is nearest the exit</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — browser history as a stack</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Deque&lt;String&gt; history = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
history.push("home");
history.push("catalog");
history.push("cart");
System.out.println("deque printed front-first: " + history);
System.out.println("back button -&gt; " + history.pop());
System.out.println("back button -&gt; " + history.pop());
System.out.println("now showing: " + history.peek());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">deque printed front-first: [cart, catalog, home]
back button -&gt; cart
back button -&gt; catalog
now showing: home</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>push</code> inserts at the <em>front</em>, so the most recent page is printed first. That is why the printed order looks reversed compared with the order the pages were visited.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Each <code>pop</code> takes from the front — the most recent page — which is exactly what a back button does.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>peek</code> reads the front without removing, so "now showing" does not consume a history entry.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Nothing here needed the <code>Stack</code> class. <code>ArrayDeque</code> is the recommended stack in modern Java.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — palindrome check — the operation only a deque can do</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Comparing a word with its reverse needs to consume from both ends at once. A queue cannot; a stack cannot; a deque is exactly the right shape.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static boolean palindrome(String s){
    Deque&lt;Character&gt; d = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) d.addLast(ch);
    while (d.size() &gt; 1) {
        if (d.removeFirst() != d.removeLast()) return false;
    }
    return true;
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">racecar -&gt; true
level   -&gt; true
java    -&gt; false</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Every character goes in at the back, so the deque holds the word left to right.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Each pass removes one character from each end and compares them. Different characters mean it is not a palindrome, and the method returns immediately.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The loop condition is <code>size() &gt; 1</code>, not <code>&gt; 0</code>: an odd-length word ends with one middle character, which has nothing to be compared against.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Written with <code>&gt; 0</code>, the middle character would be removed twice — <code>removeFirst</code> and <code>removeLast</code> would both take it, and the second call would throw on an empty deque.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Mixing the two idioms silently changes the discipline. <code>push(1); push(2); push(3);</code> then <code>pop(), pop()</code> gives <strong>3, 2</strong> — a stack. The same pushes with <code>pollLast(), pollLast()</code> give <strong>1, 2</strong> — a queue, because <code>push</code> loads the front while <code>pollLast</code> drains the back. Both were run. Choose <code>push/pop</code> or <code>offerLast/pollFirst</code> and never mix them in one structure; also note <code>ArrayDeque</code> rejects <code>null</code>, precisely so a <code>null</code> from <code>pollFirst</code> can only mean "empty".</p></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_7":{"body":"<p>A <code>TreeSet</code> is a set that keeps its elements sorted, which is the trade against <code>HashSet</code>: ordered iteration and range queries, in exchange for O(log n) instead of O(1).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">TreeSet<integer> t = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;();
t.add(5); t.add(1); t.add(3);

for (int n : t) { System.out.print(n + " "); }    // 1 3 5 — always sorted

t.first();          // 1
t.last();           // 5
t.higher(3);        // 5  — smallest element strictly greater
t.lower(3);         // 1
t.headSet(3);       // [1]     — everything below
t.tailSet(3);       // [3, 5]  — 3 and above</integer></pre><p>Elements must be <code>Comparable</code> (13.5), or you must supply a <code>Comparator</code> to the constructor — otherwise the set has no way to order them and throws <code>ClassCastException</code> on the first add. The same requirement applies to <code>TreeMap</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; t = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(5, 1, 9, 3));
t.first();          // 1        t.last();        // 9
t.higher(5);        // 9  — strictly greater
t.ceiling(6);       // 9  — greater than or equal
t.headSet(5);       // [1, 3]  — a live view, not a copy
t.subSet(3, 9);     // [3, 5]</pre><p>A <code>TreeSet</code> keeps its elements sorted at all times, which costs O(log n) per operation against <code>HashSet</code>'s O(1). You pay that only when the ordering is worth having — for the range and neighbour queries above, which a hash set cannot answer at all.</p><p>Ordering comes from <code>compareTo</code>, or from a <code>Comparator</code> passed to the constructor — and that same comparison decides duplicates. So a <code>TreeSet</code> uses <code>compareTo == 0</code> rather than <code>equals</code>, and two objects the comparator calls equivalent cannot both be in the set even if <code>equals</code> says they differ.</p><p>It rejects <code>null</code>, because there is no way to compare <code>null</code> with anything.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>TreeSet:</strong> a sorted set backed by a balanced tree; O(log n) operations</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Comparable requirement:</strong> elements must define an ordering, or a Comparator must be supplied</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>first / last / higher / lower:</strong> navigation methods a HashSet cannot offer</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>TreeMap:</strong> the equivalent sorted map, iterating in key order</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>HashSet for speed, TreeSet for order, LinkedHashSet for insertion order. A question mentioning "sorted output" or "next largest" is pointing at TreeSet.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS147="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A TREESET KEEPS ITS ORDER IN THE SHAPE OF THE TREE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 250" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk272a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the elements 89, 45, 120, 67 added in that order</text><circle cx="150" cy="60" r="18" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="150" y="64" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">89</text><circle cx="94" cy="118" r="18" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="94" y="122" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">45</text><circle cx="206" cy="118" r="18" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="206" y="122" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">120</text><circle cx="140" cy="176" r="18" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="140" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">67</text><line x1="138" y1="74" x2="106" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="162" y1="74" x2="194" y2="104" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="106" y1="132" x2="128" y2="162" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="60" y="60" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">smaller ↙</text><text x="258" y="60" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">↘ larger</text><text x="150" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">every left subtree is smaller, every right subtree larger</text><text x="150" y="234" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">so an in-order walk comes out sorted, whatever the insert order</text><line x1="330" y1="120" x2="640" y2="120" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="360" cy="120" r="6" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="360" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">45</text><circle cx="440" cy="120" r="6" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="440" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">67</text><circle cx="520" cy="120" r="6" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="520" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">89</text><circle cx="610" cy="120" r="6" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="610" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">120</text><text x="485" y="52" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the queries a HashSet cannot answer</text><text x="485" y="74" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">first() = 45          last() = 120</text><text x="485" y="92" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">higher(67) = 89   ceiling(67) = 67</text><line x1="440" y1="108" x2="512" y2="108" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk272a)"/><text x="476" y="102" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">higher(67)</text><rect x="336" y="156" width="108" height="22" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="390" y="172" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">headSet(89)</text><rect x="500" y="156" width="140" height="22" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="570" y="172" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">tailSet(89)</text><text x="485" y="202" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">subSet(45, 120) = [45, 67, 89]   — high end excluded</text><text x="485" y="222" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">each query is a walk down the tree: O(log n), not a scan</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — every navigation method on one small set</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; prices = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(89, 45, 120, 45, 67));
System.out.println("set: " + prices);
System.out.println("first=" + prices.first() + "  last=" + prices.last());
System.out.println("higher(67)=" + prices.higher(67) + "  ceiling(67)=" + prices.ceiling(67));
System.out.println("lower(67)=" + prices.lower(67) + "  floor(67)=" + prices.floor(67));
System.out.println("headSet(89)=" + prices.headSet(89) + "  tailSet(89)=" + prices.tailSet(89));
System.out.println("subSet(45,120)=" + prices.subSet(45,120));
System.out.println("descendingSet=" + prices.descendingSet());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">set: [45, 67, 89, 120]
first=45  last=120
higher(67)=89  ceiling(67)=67
lower(67)=45  floor(67)=67
headSet(89)=[45, 67]  tailSet(89)=[89, 120]
subSet(45,120)=[45, 67, 89]
descendingSet=[120, 89, 67, 45]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The duplicate 45 vanished on the way in, and the elements came out sorted although 89 was added first.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>higher</code> is <em>strictly</em> greater, <code>ceiling</code> is greater-or-equal — that is the whole difference, and it shows on 67: 89 against 67.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>lower</code>/<code>floor</code> are the mirror pair going down.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>headSet(89)</code> excludes 89, <code>tailSet(89)</code> includes it. The half-open convention is the same one as <code>substring</code>: low end in, high end out.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>subSet(45, 120)</code> therefore contains 45 but not 120.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the comparator decides what counts as a duplicate</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A <code>HashSet</code> asks <code>equals</code>. A <code>TreeSet</code> never calls <code>equals</code> at all — it asks the ordering whether the comparison is 0. Those can disagree, and here they do.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Set&lt;String&gt; plain = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;();
plain.add("Ada"); plain.add("ADA"); plain.add("ada");
System.out.println("natural order: " + plain + "   size " + plain.size());

Set&lt;String&gt; ci = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER);
ci.add("Ada"); ci.add("ADA"); ci.add("ada");
System.out.println("case-insensitive comparator: " + ci + "   size " + ci.size());
System.out.println("HashSet for comparison: "
    + new HashSet&lt;&gt;(List.of("Ada","ADA","ada")).size());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">natural order: [ADA, Ada, ada]   size 3
case-insensitive comparator: [Ada]   size 1
HashSet for comparison: 3</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">With natural ordering, <code>"ADA".compareTo("Ada")</code> is not 0, so all three survive — and they sort by character code, which puts every capital before every lowercase letter.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">With <code>CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER</code>, the first comparison of "ADA" against "Ada" returns 0, so the set treats it as already present and discards it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Only the <em>first</em> spelling added survives: the set keeps <code>"Ada"</code>, the one that got there first.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>HashSet</code> line is the control: <code>equals</code> says all three differ, so a hash set keeps all three regardless of any comparator.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A <code>TreeSet</code> defines "duplicate" as <code>compare(a, b) == 0</code>, not <code>a.equals(b)</code>. A comparator that ignores case, or one that compares only a price field, will silently discard elements you consider different — one product per price, and no warning. Two related traps come from the same requirement: elements must be orderable, so a class that does not implement <code>Comparable</code> throws <code>ClassCastException</code> on the second <code>add</code> (not the first — one element is never compared with anything), and <code>null</code> is rejected outright because it cannot be compared.</p></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_8":{"body":"<p>A <code>PriorityQueue</code> serves the <em>smallest</em> element first rather than the oldest. It is a queue by interface and a heap by behaviour, which is why the ordering rule surprises people.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">PriorityQueue<integer> pq = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();
pq.add(5); pq.add(1); pq.add(3);

pq.poll();      // 1  — smallest first, NOT insertion order
pq.poll();      // 3
pq.poll();      // 5

// largest first
PriorityQueue<integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Collections.reverseOrder());</integer></integer></pre><p>Only <code>peek</code> and <code>poll</code> respect the priority. Iterating a <code>PriorityQueue</code> with a for-each gives the internal heap order, which is <strong>not</strong> sorted — a genuine trap, because the first element is right and the rest look wrong.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; pq = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();      // min-heap by default
pq.offer(5); pq.offer(1); pq.offer(9);
pq.poll();     // 1 — always the SMALLEST, regardless of insertion order

new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder());          // max-heap
new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.comparing(Task::priority));</pre><p>A priority queue serves by priority rather than arrival: <code>poll</code> always returns the smallest element under the natural ordering. It is a heap, so <code>offer</code> and <code>poll</code> are O(log n) and <code>peek</code> is O(1).</p><p>The catch that catches everyone: <strong>printing it, or iterating it, does not give sorted order.</strong> The internal array is heap-ordered, not sorted — only the sequence of <code>poll</code> calls is ordered. To get every element in order you must drain it.</p><p>Elements must be <code>Comparable</code> or the queue must be given a <code>Comparator</code>; otherwise the first <code>offer</code> of a second element throws <code>ClassCastException</code>. <code>peek</code> and <code>poll</code> on an empty queue return <code>null</code>.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>PriorityQueue:</strong> a queue ordering by priority rather than arrival, backed by a heap</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Min-heap:</strong> the default: the smallest element is served first</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Collections.reverseOrder():</strong> a Comparator that turns it into a max-heap</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Heap order:</strong> the partial internal ordering; only the head is guaranteed correct</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Printing a PriorityQueue directly, or iterating it, does not print sorted output. Only repeated <code>poll()</code> gives the true order.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS148="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A PRIORITY QUEUE IS A HEAP: ONLY THE HEAD IS SORTED</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 258" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk273a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">offer(5), offer(1), offer(9), offer(3) into a min-heap</text><circle cx="150" cy="62" r="17" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="150" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><circle cx="100" cy="128" r="17" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="100" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><circle cx="210" cy="128" r="17" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="210" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">9</text><circle cx="58" cy="190" r="17" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="58" y="194" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">5</text><line x1="140" y1="76" x2="110" y2="114" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="162" y1="76" x2="198" y2="114" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="90" y1="142" x2="68" y2="176" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="150" y="42" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">root = smallest</text><text x="150" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the ONLY guarantee: every parent ≤ its children</text><text x="150" y="242" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">siblings are in no particular order at all</text><text x="470" y="52" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the same heap as an array — what toString shows</text><rect x="352" y="66" width="56" height="32" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.22)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="380" y="86.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="380" y="113" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">0</text><rect x="408" y="66" width="56" height="32" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="436" y="86.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="436" y="113" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="464" y="66" width="56" height="32" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="492" y="86.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">9</text><text x="492" y="113" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="520" y="66" width="56" height="32" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="548" y="86.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="548" y="113" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="470" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">children of index i live at 2i+1 and 2i+2</text><rect x="340" y="140" width="300" height="106" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.09" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="490" y="162" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">printing it  →  [1, 3, 9, 5]</text><text x="490" y="182" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not sorted — only position 0 is meaningful</text><text x="490" y="208" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">poll, poll, poll, poll  →  1  3  5  9</text><text x="490" y="228" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">each poll re-heaps, so the order is exact</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — printing it and draining it give different answers</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; triage = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();
triage.offer(5); triage.offer(1); triage.offer(9); triage.offer(3);
System.out.println("printed straight out: " + triage);
System.out.println("peek: " + triage.peek());

StringBuilder order = new StringBuilder();
while (!triage.isEmpty()) order.append(triage.poll()).append(' ');
System.out.println("poll order: " + order.toString().trim());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">printed straight out: [1, 3, 9, 5]
peek: 1
poll order: 1 3 5 9</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The printed form is the backing array in heap order. 1 is correctly at the front; 9 sitting before 5 is not a bug.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>peek</code> agrees with the first printed element, because both read index 0.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Each <code>poll</code> removes the root, moves the last element into its place and sinks it back down — so the next smallest surfaces.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Only that sequence of polls is sorted. If a question asks "what does printing the queue show", the answer is the heap array, not the sorted list.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — max-heap, and ordering objects by a field</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; max = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder());
max.addAll(List.of(5, 1, 9, 3));
while (!max.isEmpty()) System.out.print(max.poll() + " ");

record Job(String name, int priority) {}
PriorityQueue&lt;Job&gt; jobs = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.comparingInt(Job::priority));
jobs.add(new Job("backup", 5));
jobs.add(new Job("alert", 1));
jobs.add(new Job("report", 3));
while (!jobs.isEmpty()) {
    Job j = jobs.poll();
    System.out.println(j.name() + " (priority " + j.priority() + ")");
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">max-heap drain: 9 5 3 1 
alert (priority 1)
report (priority 3)
backup (priority 5)</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The comparator is supplied to the <em>constructor</em>. Passing it later is impossible — the ordering has to be known before the first element arrives.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Comparator.reverseOrder()</code> inverts every comparison, so the largest element sinks to the root and comes out first.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Comparator.comparingInt(Job::priority)</code> orders jobs by the number in the priority field; the alert, added second, is served first.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Priority 1 being "most urgent" is the usual convention and it catches people: with a min-heap, <em>lower is served sooner</em>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two question shapes recur. <em>Trace one:</em> ignore insertion order entirely, sort the values mentally and read them off smallest-first — that is what repeated <code>poll</code> gives. <em>Choose a structure:</em> anything phrased "always process the most urgent/cheapest/nearest next" is a <code>PriorityQueue</code>; "process in arrival order" is an ordinary <code>Queue</code>; "keep everything sorted and searchable" is a <code>TreeSet</code>. The classic application is the k-largest problem: keep a min-heap of size k, offer each element, and poll whenever the size exceeds k.</p></div>"},"D286/u14/z14_9":{"body":"<p>A <strong>stack</strong> is last-in-first-out, and you have been using one since 6.8 — the call stack is a stack of frames, which is why deep recursion overflows it (11.8).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// legacy class — works, but no longer recommended
Stack<string> s = new Stack&lt;&gt;();
s.push("a"); s.push("b");
s.peek();        // "b" — top, not removed
s.pop();         // "b" — removed
s.isEmpty();

// the preferred modern form
Deque<string> s2 = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
s2.push("a"); s2.pop();</string></string></pre><p>Stacks are the natural structure for anything with nesting: matching brackets, undo history, expression evaluation, and the iterative rewrite of a recursive algorithm. Anywhere you would recurse, a stack is the manual version.</p><h3>What the legacy Stack throws</h3><p>Calling <code>pop()</code> or <code>peek()</code> on an empty <code>Stack</code> throws <strong>EmptyStackException</strong>, which is unchecked. The <code>Deque</code> alternative behaves differently and more usefully: <code>pop()</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>, while <code>pollFirst()</code> returns <code>null</code> instead of throwing. Guarding with <code>isEmpty()</code> before popping avoids all three.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Deque&lt;Character&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();     // preferred over Stack
for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
    if (c == '(') st.push(c);
    else if (c == ')') {
        if (st.isEmpty()) return false;        // a closer with nothing open
        st.pop();
    }
}
return st.isEmpty();                            // leftover openers = unbalanced</pre><p>A stack is <strong>LIFO</strong> — last in, first out. <code>push</code> adds to the top, <code>pop</code> removes and returns it, <code>peek</code> looks without removing. <code>pop</code> on an empty stack throws.</p><p>Bracket matching is the standard example because it needs both checks, and leaving either out passes strings it should reject. An early <code>)</code> must fail on the empty test; a trailing <code>(</code> must fail on the final <code>isEmpty()</code>. Code that only pops and never checks at the end accepts <code>"(("</code>.</p><p>Stacks are everywhere underneath: the call stack of 8.3, undo history, expression evaluation, and depth-first search. Queue is the FIFO counterpart, and swapping one for the other changes a breadth-first traversal into a depth-first one.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stack:</strong> a LIFO collection with push, pop and peek</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>push / pop / peek:</strong> add to the top, remove from the top, inspect the top</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>EmptyStackException:</strong> thrown by pop or peek on an empty Stack</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Call stack:</strong> the JVM’s own stack of method frames (8.3)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Bracket matching is the standard exam use: push every opening bracket, pop on every closing one and check it matches. Balanced input ends with an empty stack.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS149="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — LIFO: A STACK OVER TIME</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 292" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk274a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">push "a", push "b", push "c", pop, push "d"</text><text x="86" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">push "a"</text><rect x="56" y="158" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="86" y="176.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a</text><rect x="56" y="126" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.05)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="56" y="94" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.05)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="52" y1="190" x2="120" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="86" y="206" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">top: "a"</text><text x="212" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">push "b"</text><rect x="182" y="158" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="212" y="176.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a</text><rect x="182" y="126" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="212" y="144.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">b</text><rect x="182" y="94" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.05)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="178" y1="190" x2="246" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="212" y="206" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">top: "b"</text><text x="338" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">push "c"</text><rect x="308" y="158" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="338" y="176.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a</text><rect x="308" y="126" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="338" y="144.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">b</text><rect x="308" y="94" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="338" y="112.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">c</text><line x1="304" y1="190" x2="372" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="338" y="206" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">top: "c"</text><text x="464" y="48" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">pop → "c"</text><rect x="434" y="158" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="464" y="176.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a</text><rect x="434" y="126" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="464" y="144.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">b</text><rect x="434" y="94" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(127,127,127,0.05)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="430" y1="190" x2="498" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="464" y="206" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">top: "b"</text><text x="590" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">push "d"</text><rect x="560" y="158" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="590" y="176.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a</text><rect x="560" y="126" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="590" y="144.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">b</text><rect x="560" y="94" width="60" height="28" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.20)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="590" y="112.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">d</text><line x1="556" y1="190" x2="624" y2="190" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="590" y="206" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">top: "d"</text><line x1="40" y1="66" x2="40" y2="130" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk274a)"/><text x="24" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">time</text><rect x="40" y="222" width="584" height="58" rx="9" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.09" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="332" y="244" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">only the top is reachable — push, pop and peek all act there</text><text x="332" y="266" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the last thing in is the first thing out, which is why "c" left before "b"</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — bracket matching on real-looking code</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The rule needs three checks, and dropping any one of them accepts something it should reject.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static boolean balanced(String s){
    Deque&lt;Character&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
        if (ch == '(' || ch == '[' || ch == '{') st.push(ch);
        else if (ch == ')' || ch == ']' || ch == '}') {
            if (st.isEmpty()) return false;              // a closer with nothing open
            char open = st.pop();
            if (ch == ')' &amp;&amp; open != '(') return false;   // wrong kind of closer
            if (ch == ']' &amp;&amp; open != '[') return false;
            if (ch == '}' &amp;&amp; open != '{') return false;
        }
    }
    return st.isEmpty();                                 // leftover openers
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">true   a[0] = (b+c);
false   if (x) { y[1] = 2;
true   return (a[0]);
false   ([)]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>a[0] = (b+c);</code> — every closer meets its own opener and the stack empties. True.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>if (x) { y[1] = 2;</code> — the <code>{</code> is never closed, so the final <code>isEmpty()</code> fails. Without that final check this string would pass.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>([)]</code> — the <code>)</code> pops a <code>[</code>, and the kind check catches it. Counting brackets instead of stacking them would call this balanced.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>isEmpty()</code> guard before <code>pop</code> is what stops a leading <code>)</code> from throwing instead of returning false.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — evaluating a postfix expression, one push at a time</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Postfix — "3 4 + 2 *" — needs no parentheses because the order is already fixed by position. A stack evaluates it in one left-to-right pass.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String expr = "3 4 + 2 *";
Deque&lt;Integer&gt; st = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
for (String tok : expr.split(" ")) {
    if (tok.equals("+") || tok.equals("*") || tok.equals("-")) {
        int right = st.pop(), left = st.pop();       // RIGHT comes off first
        int r = tok.equals("+") ? left + right
              : tok.equals("-") ? left - right : left * right;
        st.push(r);
    } else {
        st.push(Integer.parseInt(tok));
    }
}
System.out.println("result: " + st.pop());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints (with a trace line per token)</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">push 3        -&gt; stack [3]
push 4        -&gt; stack [4, 3]
apply + to 3 and 4 -&gt; stack [7]
push 2        -&gt; stack [2, 7]
apply * to 7 and 2 -&gt; stack [14]
result: 14</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Numbers are pushed as they arrive; an operator pops the two most recent values.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <em>second</em> pop is the left operand. Getting that backwards computes <code>4 − 3</code> instead of <code>3 − 4</code> — invisible for <code>+</code> and <code>*</code>, wrong for <code>−</code> and <code>/</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The printed stack shows front-first, so <code>[4, 3]</code> means 4 is on top with 3 beneath.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">One value is left at the end. Two values left means the expression was malformed — a useful validity check.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Printing the two kinds of stack gives opposite orders. <code>new Stack&lt;&gt;()</code> extends <code>Vector</code>, so it prints bottom-first: pushing 1, 2, 3 shows <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> with 3 on top. An <code>ArrayDeque</code> used as a stack prints front-first: the same pushes show <code>[3, 2, 1]</code>. Both pop 3. They also fail differently on empty — <code>Stack.pop()</code> throws <code>EmptyStackException</code>, <code>Deque.pop()</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>, and <code>pollFirst()</code> returns <code>null</code> instead of throwing. All four were run to confirm.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_1":{"body":"<p>Graphics in Swing all happen inside one method. You override <code>paintComponent</code>, draw with the <code>Graphics</code> object you are handed, and never call it yourself — the system calls it whenever the component needs redrawing.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class Canvas extends JPanel {
    @Override
    protected void paintComponent(Graphics g) {
        super.paintComponent(g);            // clears the background — do this first

        g.setColor(Color.BLUE);
        g.fillRect(10, 10, 100, 50);        // x, y, width, height
        g.drawOval(10, 70, 80, 80);
        g.drawLine(0, 0, 200, 200);
        g.drawString("hello", 20, 180);
    }
}
repaint();     // ASK for a redraw — never call paintComponent directly</pre><p>The coordinate system is the thing to internalise: (0,0) is the <strong>top left</strong> and y increases <em>downward</em>, which is upside down compared with school graphs. And <code>fill</code> methods draw solid shapes while <code>draw</code> methods draw outlines.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import javax.swing.*;

JFrame frame = new JFrame("Title");                 // 1. the window
frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
frame.add(new JLabel("Hello"));                     // 2. components
frame.pack();                                       // size to fit
frame.setVisible(true);                             // 3. show it — LAST</pre><p>Three pieces make any Swing program: a <strong>container</strong> (the <code>JFrame</code>), <strong>components</strong> inside it, and <strong>listeners</strong> that react to events. Swing lives in <code>javax.swing</code> — note the <code>x</code>, which is not in most other package names.</p><p>Order matters. <code>setVisible(true)</code> goes last, after everything has been added; adding components afterwards may not appear until the frame is revalidated.</p><p>Without <code>setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE)</code> the window closes but the program keeps running, because Swing's event thread is still alive. That is the usual explanation for a program that will not stop.</p><p>A console program runs top to bottom and ends; a GUI is <strong>event-driven</strong> — it sets everything up, then waits, and all remaining work happens inside listeners.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>paintComponent(Graphics g):</strong> the method you override to draw; called by the system</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>repaint():</strong> requests a redraw; the system then calls paintComponent</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Graphics object:</strong> the drawing surface, supplying setColor, fillRect, drawOval and the rest</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Screen coordinates:</strong> origin at the top left, y increasing downward</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Calling <code>paintComponent</code> directly, or forgetting <code>super.paintComponent(g)</code>, produces smeared or stale drawing. Call <code>repaint()</code> and let the system schedule it.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS151="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHO CALLS PAINTCOMPONENT, AND WHERE (0,0) IS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 292" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk275a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="170" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">you never call paintComponent</text><rect x="46" y="40" width="116" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="104" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your code</text><text x="104" y="71" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">level.setLevel(70)</text><line x1="104" y1="74" x2="104" y2="96" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk275a)"/><rect x="46" y="96" width="116" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="104" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">repaint()</text><text x="104" y="127" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a request, not a redraw</text><line x1="104" y1="130" x2="104" y2="152" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk275a)"/><rect x="30" y="152" width="148" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="104" y="167" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">event dispatch thread</text><text x="104" y="184" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">schedules the work</text><line x1="104" y1="188" x2="104" y2="210" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk275a)"/><rect x="30" y="210" width="148" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="104" y="227" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">paintComponent(g)</text><text x="104" y="244" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">called FOR you, with g</text><text x="104" y="266" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">also called on resize, uncover,</text><text x="104" y="280" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">first display — not only by you</text><text x="430" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the coordinate system is upside down</text><rect x="250" y="44" width="380" height="210" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><circle cx="250" cy="44" r="5" fill="#e11d48" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="272" y="38" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">(0, 0) is the TOP LEFT</text><line x1="250" y1="44" x2="250" y2="240" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk275a)"/><text x="238" y="150" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">y grows DOWN</text><line x1="250" y1="44" x2="600" y2="44" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk275a)"/><text x="430" y="60" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">x grows right</text><rect x="310" y="96" width="140" height="60" rx="0" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.20)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><circle cx="310" cy="96" r="4" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="310" y="88" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">(x, y)</text><line x1="310" y1="172" x2="450" y2="172" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="380" y="186" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">width</text><line x1="466" y1="96" x2="466" y2="156" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="492" y="130" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">height</text><text x="430" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">fillRect(310, 96, 140, 60)  →  position THEN size</text><text x="430" y="236" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">not two opposite corners — that is the usual misread</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a level gauge drawn from scratch</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">This panel draws a bar, a border, a percentage and two circles. It compiles as written; the only thing missing is a frame to put it in.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static class Gauge extends JPanel {
    private int level = 40;

    void setLevel(int v){ level = v; repaint(); }   // change state, THEN ask

    @Override protected void paintComponent(Graphics g){
        super.paintComponent(g);            // clears the previous frame — first
        g.setColor(Color.LIGHT_GRAY);
        g.fillRect(20, 20, 200, 30);        // the empty track
        g.setColor(new Color(14, 165, 233));
        g.fillRect(20, 20, 2 * level, 30);  // the filled part: width VARIES
        g.setColor(Color.BLACK);
        g.drawRect(20, 20, 200, 30);        // outline on top of both
        g.drawString(level + "%", 230, 42); // x, y is the text BASELINE
        g.drawOval(20, 70, 60, 60);         // outline circle
        g.fillOval(90, 70, 60, 60);         // solid circle
    }
    @Override public Dimension getPreferredSize(){ return new Dimension(300, 180); }
}</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The bar is two rectangles at the same position: a full-width grey one, then a partial coloured one over it. Drawing order is painting order — later calls cover earlier ones.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>2 * level</code> turns 0–100 into 0–200 pixels. Every varying number in a paint method comes from a field, because the method takes no parameters other than <code>g</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>drawRect</code> after both fills, so the outline is not buried underneath.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>drawString</code>’s y is the baseline of the text, not its top — text drawn at y = 0 is invisible above the panel.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getPreferredSize</code> is what lets <code>frame.pack()</code> give the panel any room at all; without it a custom panel can come out zero-sized.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why the drawing smears, and how repaint fixes it</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two mistakes produce the same symptom — old frames left on screen. Here is what each one does.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// (a) forgetting the super call
@Override protected void paintComponent(Graphics g){
    // super.paintComponent(g);   &lt;-- omitted
    g.setColor(Color.BLUE);
    g.fillOval(x, 40, 30, 30);
}
// the panel background is never repainted, so every past position of the
// circle is still there: one moving dot becomes a blue smear.

// (b) calling the paint method yourself
void moveRight(){
    x += 5;
    paintComponent(getGraphics());   // WRONG — no clearing, no scheduling
}
void moveRightProperly(){
    x += 5;
    repaint();                       // right: state first, then request
}</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>super.paintComponent(g)</code> fills the panel with its background colour. It is the "clear the frame" step, and it must come first or it erases what you just drew.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Calling <code>paintComponent</code> directly hands it a <code>Graphics</code> object outside the normal painting cycle: nothing is cleared, nothing is clipped, and the next real repaint discards your work anyway.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>repaint()</code> returns immediately. It does not draw — it marks the component dirty and lets the event thread call <code>paintComponent</code> when it is ready.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">That also means several <code>repaint()</code> calls in a row can collapse into one paint. Correct behaviour must therefore never depend on how many times painting happened.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The three most-asked facts: (1) <code>paintComponent</code> is <em>overridden</em>, never called — you call <code>repaint()</code>; (2) <code>fillRect(x, y, width, height)</code> is position then size, and <code>fillOval</code> takes the same four numbers as the oval’s <em>bounding box</em>, so a circle of diameter 40 at (30,30) is <code>fillOval(30, 30, 40, 40)</code>; (3) the origin is the top-left with y increasing downward, so "move it up 10 pixels" is <code>y -= 10</code>. Remember which package each class is in: <code>JPanel</code> is <code>javax.swing</code>, <code>Graphics</code> and <code>Color</code> are <code>java.awt</code>.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_2":{"body":"<p>A Swing GUI is a containment hierarchy: a <code>JFrame</code> window holds panels, and panels hold controls. Building one is always the same five steps, in the same order.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import javax.swing.*;

JFrame frame = new JFrame("My App");                    // 1. the window
JPanel panel = new JPanel();                            // 2. a container
JButton button = new JButton("Click me");               // 3. controls
JLabel  label  = new JLabel("Result:");

panel.add(label);                                       // 4. assemble
panel.add(button);
frame.add(panel);

frame.setSize(400, 300);                                // 5. show it
frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
frame.setVisible(true);                                 // LAST — after everything</pre><p><code>setVisible(true)</code> goes last because components added afterwards may not appear until something forces a re-layout. And without <code>setDefaultCloseOperation</code>, closing the window hides it while the program keeps running.</p><h3>The input components</h3><p>Beyond buttons and labels, four input components appear in questions, and each has its own way of being read.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Component</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Purpose</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Read its value with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JTextField</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one line of text</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>getText()</code> — always a String</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JCheckBox</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">an independent on/off choice</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>isSelected()</code> — a boolean</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JRadioButton</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one choice from a group</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>isSelected()</code>, with a <code>ButtonGroup</code> enforcing exclusivity</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>JComboBox</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a drop-down list</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>getSelectedItem()</code> or <code>getSelectedIndex()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The detail that decides questions: a <strong>ButtonGroup</strong> is what makes radio buttons mutually exclusive. Without one they behave as independent checkboxes, since nothing else links them. And <code>getSelectedItem()</code> returns an <code>Object</code>, so it usually needs a cast or <code>toString()</code>.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Component</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">For</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JLabel</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">text or an image the user cannot edit</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JTextField</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one line of typed input</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JTextArea</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">several lines</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JButton</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">an action</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JCheckBox</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">independent on/off choices</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JRadioButton</code> + <code>ButtonGroup</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">mutually exclusive choices</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JComboBox</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a drop-down list</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JPanel</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">grouping other components</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Radio buttons are only mutually exclusive once added to a <code>ButtonGroup</code> — the group does the enforcing, not the class. Radio buttons on their own behave exactly like check boxes, which is the usual bug.</p><p>All of these extend <code>JComponent</code>, so they share <code>setEnabled</code>, <code>setVisible</code>, <code>setToolTipText</code> and the rest — which is polymorphism from Unit 10 doing real work. Read a check box with <code>isSelected()</code> and a text field with <code>getText()</code>.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JFrame:</strong> the top-level window</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JPanel:</strong> a container grouping components, and the usual drawing surface</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Component:</strong> any control — JButton, JLabel, JTextField</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Containment hierarchy:</strong> frame contains panels, panels contain components</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>EXIT_ON_CLOSE:</strong> ends the program when the window is closed</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Swing classes live in <code>javax.swing</code> — note the <strong>x</strong>. AWT classes such as <code>Color</code> and <code>Graphics</code> live in <code>java.awt</code>, so most GUI files import both.</p></div><i data-d286fx25="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A SWING GUI IS A TREE OF COMPONENTS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 238" style="width:100%; max-width:520px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk92a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="150" y="10" width="180" height="32" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="240" y="30" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JFrame (the window)</text><rect x="150" y="74" width="180" height="32" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="240" y="94" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">JPanel (a container)</text><line x1="240" y1="42" x2="240" y2="72" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk92a)"/><rect x="30" y="140" width="120" height="32" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="90" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">JLabel</text><rect x="180" y="140" width="120" height="32" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="240" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">JTextField</text><rect x="330" y="140" width="120" height="32" rx="7" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="390" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">JButton</text><line x1="210" y1="106" x2="96" y2="138" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk92a)"/><line x1="240" y1="106" x2="240" y2="138" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk92a)"/><line x1="270" y1="106" x2="384" y2="138" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk92a)"/><text x="390" y="192" fill="#6366f1" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">click → actionPerformed(...)</text><text x="240" y="216" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Components nest inside containers. Events (clicks, typing) go to listeners you register —</text><text x="240" y="230" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the button does nothing until an ActionListener is added to it.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS152="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a complete window, built in the required order</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Every Swing program is these five steps. The comments mark why each one is where it is.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JFrame frame = new JFrame("Lab bench");                   // 1. the window
frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);    //    or it never exits

JPanel form = new JPanel();                              // 2. a container
JLabel label = new JLabel("Sample id:");                 // 3. the controls
JTextField idField = new JTextField(12);                 //    12 columns wide
JButton submit = new JButton("Record");

form.add(label);                                         // 4. assemble
form.add(idField);
form.add(submit);
frame.add(form);

frame.pack();                                            // 5. size to contents
frame.setVisible(true);                                  //    LAST</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new JFrame("Lab bench")</code> creates the window but shows nothing — a frame is invisible until told otherwise.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Without <code>EXIT_ON_CLOSE</code> the default is <code>HIDE_ON_CLOSE</code>: the window disappears and the process keeps running, because the event thread is still alive. That is the classic "my program will not stop".</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new JTextField(12)</code> asks for room for about 12 characters. It is a sizing hint, not a length limit — the user can type more.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>pack()</code> sizes the window to its contents. <code>setSize(400, 300)</code> is the alternative and ignores what is inside.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setVisible(true)</code> comes last. Components added afterwards may not appear until something forces a re-layout, and "my button is invisible" is usually this.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reading four kinds of input control</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Each control type is read differently, and the return types are what questions turn on.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JTextField name = new JTextField(12);
JCheckBox urgent = new JCheckBox("Urgent");
JRadioButton small = new JRadioButton("Small");
JRadioButton large = new JRadioButton("Large");
ButtonGroup sizes = new ButtonGroup();
sizes.add(small); sizes.add(large);        // THIS is what makes them exclusive
JComboBox&lt;String&gt; lab = new JComboBox&lt;&gt;(new String[]{"North", "South"});

// reading them, typically inside a listener:
String typed   = name.getText();            // always a String, never null
boolean isUrgent = urgent.isSelected();     // boolean
boolean isSmall  = small.isSelected();      // boolean, one per button
String chosen  = (String) lab.getSelectedItem();   // Object -&gt; needs a cast
int index      = lab.getSelectedIndex();    // -1 if nothing is selected</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getText()</code> on an empty field returns <code>""</code>, not <code>null</code> — so <code>isEmpty()</code> is the right test, and <code>parseInt("")</code> throws.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A checkbox is independent: several can be on at once.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Radio buttons are <em>not</em> exclusive by class. The <code>ButtonGroup</code> enforces it, and it is not a visible component — you never add it to a panel, only the buttons.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reading a group means asking each button <code>isSelected()</code>, or keeping the answer in a listener as the user clicks.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getSelectedItem()</code> is declared to return <code>Object</code>, hence the cast; <code>getSelectedIndex()</code> avoids the cast and returns −1 when nothing is chosen.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Radio buttons without a <code>ButtonGroup</code> behave exactly like check boxes — all of them can be selected at once, and nothing warns you, because adding them to a panel looks like it should be enough. The group is a plain object that owns the buttons’ exclusivity; it is never added to the layout. The mirror-image mistake is adding the same button to two groups, or building the group but forgetting one button, which then stays selectable independently of the others.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_3":{"body":"<p>A layout manager decides where components go and how they resize. <code>GridBagLayout</code> is the flexible one: a grid where each component states its own position and how it should stretch.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">panel.setLayout(new GridBagLayout());
GridBagConstraints c = new GridBagConstraints();

c.gridx = 0;  c.gridy = 0;                 // column 0, row 0
c.insets = new Insets(5, 5, 5, 5);         // padding around the component
panel.add(new JLabel("Name:"), c);

c.gridx = 1;
c.fill = GridBagConstraints.HORIZONTAL;    // stretch to fill the cell
c.weightx = 1.0;                           // take the spare horizontal space
panel.add(new JTextField(20), c);</pre><p>The one thing that catches everybody: the constraints object is <em>read</em> when you call <code>add</code>, so its fields persist between calls. Set <code>gridx</code> once and it stays until you change it, which is convenient and is also why leftover settings quietly land on later components.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Layout</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Arranges</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>BorderLayout</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, WEST, CENTER</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>the JFrame default</strong>; one component per region</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>FlowLayout</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">left to right, wrapping</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the <code>JPanel</code> default</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>GridLayout</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">equal-sized cells</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">every cell the same size, always</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>GridBagLayout</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a flexible grid with spans and weights</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the most capable and the most verbose</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A layout manager decides position and size as the window resizes, which is why absolute positioning is avoided: fixed coordinates break the moment the window changes size or the font differs.</p><p>Adding a second component to the same <code>BorderLayout</code> region does not stack them — it <em>replaces</em> the first, which is why the earlier one silently disappears. Put a <code>JPanel</code> in the region and add both to the panel.</p><p>Nesting panels, each with a simple layout, is usually clearer than one <code>GridBagLayout</code> doing everything.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Layout manager:</strong> the object positioning components inside a container</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>GridBagConstraints:</strong> the per-component settings: position, span, fill and weight</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>gridx / gridy:</strong> the column and row</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>weightx / weighty:</strong> how much spare space this cell claims when the window is resized</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>insets:</strong> external padding around a component</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>FlowLayout</code> is the JPanel default and simply flows left to right. <code>BorderLayout</code> is the JFrame default with five regions. Reach for <code>GridBagLayout</code> only when you need real control.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS153="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — GRIDBAGLAYOUT: A GRID WHOSE CELLS STATE THEIR OWN RULES</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 292" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk276a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a two-column form laid out on a grid</text><rect x="60" y="40" width="150" height="44" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.10)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="135" y="66.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JLabel "Sample id:"</text><rect x="210" y="40" width="250" height="44" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="335" y="66.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JTextField</text><rect x="60" y="84" width="150" height="44" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.10)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="135" y="110.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JLabel "Mass (g):"</text><rect x="210" y="84" width="250" height="44" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="335" y="110.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JTextField</text><rect x="60" y="128" width="400" height="44" rx="4" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="260" y="154.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JButton "Record"   gridwidth = 2</text><text x="135" y="32" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">gridx = 0</text><text x="335" y="32" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">gridx = 1</text><text x="48" y="66" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">gridy = 0</text><text x="48" y="110" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">gridy = 1</text><text x="48" y="154" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">gridy = 2</text><line x1="470" y1="62" x2="506" y2="62" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk276a)"/><text x="512" y="66" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">weightx = 1</text><text x="512" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">takes the spare width</text><text x="135" y="176" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">weightx = 0 → stays its natural size</text><rect x="40" y="196" width="584" height="82" rx="9" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.09" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="332" y="218" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ONE GridBagConstraints object, read at each add() call</text><text x="332" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">c.gridx = 0; c.gridy = 0;  add(label, c)   →  c is copied here</text><text x="332" y="258" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">c.gridx = 1;               add(field, c)   →  gridy is STILL 0</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a label-and-field form, line by line</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JPanel form = new JPanel(new GridBagLayout());
GridBagConstraints c = new GridBagConstraints();
c.insets = new Insets(4, 4, 4, 4);          // 4px padding on all sides

c.gridx = 0; c.gridy = 0;
c.anchor = GridBagConstraints.LINE_END;     // right-align the label in its cell
form.add(new JLabel("Sample id:"), c);

c.gridx = 1;                                 // gridy is STILL 0 — same row
c.fill = GridBagConstraints.HORIZONTAL;      // stretch to fill the cell
c.weightx = 1.0;                             // and claim the spare width
JTextField idField = new JTextField(12);
form.add(idField, c);

c.gridx = 0; c.gridy = 1;
c.gridwidth = 2;                             // this one spans both columns
form.add(new JButton("Record"), c);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The constraints object is read <em>when you call add</em>, so its state is copied at that moment. Changing it afterwards affects only later components.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Setting only <code>gridx = 1</code> for the field is deliberate: <code>gridy</code> is still 0, so the field lands beside the label rather than under it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>fill</code> and <code>weightx</code> do different jobs. <code>weightx</code> decides who gets the leftover space in the window; <code>fill</code> decides whether the component stretches to occupy its cell or floats in the middle of it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>gridwidth = 2</code> makes the button span both columns — and it stays 2 for every later add unless reset, which is the leak this section warns about.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — picking the layout manager from the requirement</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// "a row of buttons that wraps when the window narrows"
JPanel toolbar = new JPanel(new FlowLayout());     // JPanel's default anyway

// "a toolbar on top, a status bar at the bottom, the content filling the rest"
frame.setLayout(new BorderLayout());               // JFrame's default anyway
frame.add(toolbar, BorderLayout.NORTH);
frame.add(new JScrollPane(table), BorderLayout.CENTER);   // CENTER grows
frame.add(status, BorderLayout.SOUTH);

// "a 4x3 keypad, every key the same size"
JPanel pad = new JPanel(new GridLayout(4, 3, 6, 6));      // rows, cols, hgap, vgap

// "a form of labels beside fields, with one spanning cell"
JPanel form = new JPanel(new GridBagLayout());</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>FlowLayout</code> keeps components at their natural size and wraps — good for buttons, useless for aligning a form.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>BorderLayout</code> has five named regions and only <code>CENTER</code> absorbs extra space. A component in <code>NORTH</code> keeps its preferred height forever.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>GridLayout(4, 3)</code> forces twelve identical cells. The property that makes it perfect for a keypad is what makes it wrong for a form: the label gets as much width as the field.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Nesting beats configuring. A <code>BorderLayout</code> frame holding a <code>FlowLayout</code> toolbar and a <code>GridBagLayout</code> form is far easier to read than one grid bag doing everything.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two components added to the same <code>BorderLayout</code> region do not stack — the second <em>replaces</em> the first, and the first simply vanishes with no error. The fix is a <code>JPanel</code> in that region holding both. The <code>GridBagLayout</code> version of the same surprise is stale constraints: <code>gridwidth</code>, <code>fill</code> and <code>weightx</code> persist on the constraints object, so a span set for one component quietly applies to every component added after it until you reset it.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_4":{"body":"<p>A GUI does nothing until it responds to events. An <code>ActionListener</code> is a small object with one method, and the system calls it when the control is used.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JButton button = new JButton("Add");

button.addActionListener(new ActionListener() {
    @Override
    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
        String text = field.getText();          // read input
        int n = Integer.parseInt(text);         // may throw (12.6)
        label.setText("Result: " + (n * 2));    // update output
    }
});

// the same thing as a lambda, since Java 8
button.addActionListener(e -&gt; label.setText("clicked"));</pre><p>This inverts the control flow you are used to. Nothing runs top to bottom; you register handlers and the system calls them whenever the user acts. Everything a handler needs must therefore be reachable from it — which is why fields, not locals, usually hold the components.</p><h3>Finding out what was clicked</h3><p>One listener can serve several components, and the <code>ActionEvent</code> carries the information needed to tell them apart.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
    if (e.getSource() == addButton) { ... }        // compare the object
    if ("Add".equals(e.getActionCommand())) { ... } // compare the label/command
}</pre><p><code>getSource()</code> returns the component object that fired the event; <code>getActionCommand()</code> returns its command string, which defaults to the button's text. Comparing by source is safer, because changing a button's label silently breaks a comparison written against the command string.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">button.addActionListener(new ActionListener() {
    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) { ... }    // the one method
});

button.addActionListener(e -&gt; label.setText("clicked"));  // lambda — same thing
button.addActionListener(e -&gt; { if (e.getSource() == button) ... });</pre><p>A button does nothing until a listener is attached. <code>ActionListener</code> has exactly one method, <code>actionPerformed</code>, which is what allows the lambda form — a single-abstract-method interface.</p><p>One listener can serve many buttons: <code>e.getSource()</code> identifies which one fired, and <code>e.getActionCommand()</code> returns its label. That is how a calculator keypad is wired with one handler instead of twelve.</p><p>Listeners run on the event dispatch thread, which is also the thread that repaints. Doing anything slow inside one freezes the whole interface until it finishes — which is why <code>Thread.sleep</code> in a listener is always wrong, and a <code>javax.swing.Timer</code> is the right tool for anything periodic.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Event-driven programming:</strong> code runs in response to user actions rather than in sequence</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ActionListener:</strong> the interface with one method, actionPerformed</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ActionEvent:</strong> the object describing what happened, including the source</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Anonymous inner class:</strong> the new ActionListener(){...} form, defining a class inline</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Lambda:</strong> e -&gt; ... — a shorter form usable for any single-method interface</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Everything from a text field arrives as a <code>String</code>. Parsing it needs <code>Integer.parseInt</code>, and non-numeric input throws <code>NumberFormatException</code> — wrap it in a try-catch or the handler dies silently.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS154="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE PATH FROM A CLICK TO YOUR CODE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 320" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk277a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a click travels a fixed route</text><rect x="22" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="80" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">user clicks</text><text x="80" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">"Record"</text><line x1="138" y1="68" x2="148" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="150" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="208" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">event queue</text><text x="208" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">one ActionEvent</text><line x1="266" y1="68" x2="276" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="278" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="336" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">dispatch</text><text x="336" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">to every listener</text><line x1="394" y1="68" x2="404" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="406" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="464" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">actionPerformed</text><text x="464" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">YOUR code runs</text><line x1="522" y1="68" x2="532" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="534" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="592" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">setText / repaint</text><text x="592" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">window updates</text><rect x="22" y="110" width="620" height="66" rx="9" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.09" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="332" y="132" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">all five happen on ONE thread — the event dispatch thread</text><text x="332" y="154" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">so anything slow inside actionPerformed freezes the entire window until it returns</text><text x="332" y="204" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one listener serving three buttons</text><rect x="150" y="218" width="46" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="173" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">7</text><line x1="173" y1="252" x2="300" y2="276" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="210" y="218" width="46" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="233" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">8</text><line x1="233" y1="252" x2="300" y2="276" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="270" y="218" width="46" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="293" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">÷</text><line x1="293" y1="252" x2="300" y2="276" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk277a)"/><rect x="300" y="268" width="190" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="395" y="285" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">actionPerformed(e)</text><text x="395" y="302" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">e.getSource() says which</text><text x="534" y="282" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">e.getActionCommand()</text><text x="534" y="298" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">is the button’s text</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a handler that reads, validates and reports</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JTextField idField = new JTextField(12);
JLabel status = new JLabel(" ");     // a space, so the row keeps its height
JButton submit = new JButton("Record");

submit.addActionListener(new ActionListener(){
    @Override public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e){
        String text = idField.getText().trim();
        if (text.isEmpty()) { status.setText("Enter a sample id"); return; }
        status.setText("Recorded " + text
            + " (source: " + ((JButton) e.getSource()).getText() + ")");
    }
});

// the same handler as a lambda, since ActionListener has one method
submit.addActionListener(e -&gt; System.out.println(e.getActionCommand()));</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The listener is registered once, at build time. Its body runs later, every time the button is pressed — the inversion that makes GUI code different from console code.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getText().trim()</code> then <code>isEmpty()</code> is the standard input guard: a field holding only spaces must count as empty.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The early <code>return</code> is a guard clause. A handler is a method, so the same structure applies.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>e.getSource()</code> is the component that fired, typed as <code>Object</code> — hence the cast to <code>JButton</code> before calling <code>getText()</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Both listeners are attached to the same button. <code>addActionListener</code> <em>adds</em>, so both run, in the order they were added. (JavaFX’s <code>setOnAction</code> replaces instead — 16.3.)</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — one handler for a whole keypad</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Twelve buttons do not need twelve handlers. Give them all the same listener and ask the event which one fired.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JPanel pad = new JPanel(new GridLayout(4, 3, 4, 4));
JTextField display = new JTextField(10);

ActionListener keys = e -&gt; {
    String k = e.getActionCommand();          // defaults to the button's text
    if (k.equals("C")) { display.setText(""); return; }
    display.setText(display.getText() + k);
};

for (String label : new String[]{"7","8","9","4","5","6","1","2","3","C","0","="}) {
    JButton b = new JButton(label);
    b.addActionListener(keys);                // the SAME listener object
    pad.add(b);
}</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">One <code>ActionListener</code> object is created and handed to twelve buttons. Nothing is duplicated.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getActionCommand()</code> returns the button’s label unless <code>setActionCommand</code> changed it, so the text on the key <em>is</em> the identifier.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">That coupling is also its weakness: rename the button to "Clear" and the <code>equals("C")</code> test silently stops matching. Comparing <code>e.getSource()</code> against the button reference is immune to relabelling.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>GridLayout(4, 3)</code> is chosen here precisely because every key should be the same size — the fill order is left to right, top to bottom, which is why the labels are listed in that order.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Expect one of three: <em>which interface and method?</em> — <code>ActionListener</code>, one method <code>actionPerformed(ActionEvent e)</code>, which is why the lambda form is legal; <em>how does one handler tell buttons apart?</em> — <code>e.getSource()</code> (the object) or <code>e.getActionCommand()</code> (the text); <em>why did the window freeze?</em> — slow work on the event dispatch thread. <code>Thread.sleep</code> inside a listener is always the wrong answer; <code>javax.swing.Timer</code> is the right one for anything periodic. And an exception thrown inside a handler does not stop the program: Swing prints it and carries on, so the button just appears to do nothing.</p></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u15_z15_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — listener interface or adapter class</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk433a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">MouseListener</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">an interface — all 5 methods required</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the 5</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">clicked, pressed, released, entered, exited</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">MouseAdapter</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a class with all 5 already empty</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">extends MouseAdapter</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">override only the one you want</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the cost</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">extends uses your single inheritance slot</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same handler, written both ways</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">// implementing the interface: every method must be present,
// even the four you do not care about
class ClickCounter implements java.awt.event.MouseListener {
    int clicks = 0;
    public void mouseClicked(java.awt.event.MouseEvent e) { clicks++; }
    public void mousePressed(java.awt.event.MouseEvent e)  { }
    public void mouseReleased(java.awt.event.MouseEvent e) { }
    public void mouseEntered(java.awt.event.MouseEvent e)  { }
    public void mouseExited(java.awt.event.MouseEvent e)   { }
}

// extending the adapter: the four empty ones are inherited
class ClickCounter2 extends java.awt.event.MouseAdapter {
    int clicks = 0;
    @Override
    public void mouseClicked(java.awt.event.MouseEvent e) { clicks++; }
}

button.addMouseListener(new ClickCounter2());</pre><ol><li>An interface is all-or-nothing: implement <code>MouseListener</code> and the compiler requires all five methods, so four empty bodies are written purely to satisfy it.</li><li><code>MouseAdapter</code> is a class that already supplies those five as empty methods, so extending it lets you override just the one that matters.</li><li>The trade-off is inheritance: a class may extend only one thing, so if it must already extend something else, implement the interface instead. The same pattern exists for <code>KeyAdapter</code> and <code>WindowAdapter</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Whenever a question offers "implement the listener" against "extend the adapter", the adapter's advantage is always the same sentence: you override only the methods you need. Its only cost is using up the single <code>extends</code> slot.</div>"},"D286/u15/z15_5":{"body":"<p>A <code>JFormattedTextField</code> restricts what can be typed, which removes the parsing problem of 15.4 at the source rather than catching it afterwards.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">NumberFormat fmt = NumberFormat.getIntegerInstance();
JFormattedTextField field = new JFormattedTextField(fmt);
field.setValue(0);

field.addPropertyChangeListener("value", evt -&gt; {
    int n = ((Number) field.getValue()).intValue();     // already a number
    label.setText("doubled: " + (n * 2));
});</pre><p><code>getValue()</code> returns an <code>Object</code> holding a <code>Number</code>, not a String, so the cast replaces the parse. The field also refuses or reverts invalid input on its own, which is validation moved to the earliest possible point.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">try {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(field.getText().trim());
    ...
} catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
    JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(frame, "Please enter a whole number");
}</pre><p><code>getText()</code> always returns a <code>String</code> — never a number, and never <code>null</code>; an empty field gives <code>""</code>, which <code>parseInt</code> rejects. So every numeric field needs the same trim-parse-catch shape from 12.6.</p><p>An uncaught exception inside a listener does not stop the program. Swing catches it, prints a trace to the console and carries on, so the window simply appears not to respond — a silent failure that is much harder to notice than a crash.</p><p>Report problems in the interface, not on the console the user cannot see. <code>JOptionPane.showMessageDialog</code> is the standard way, and <code>field.setText("")</code> clears the field afterwards.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JFormattedTextField:</strong> a text field that enforces a format on its content</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>NumberFormat:</strong> the formatter describing what counts as valid</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>getValue():</strong> returns the parsed Object, typically a Number</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>PropertyChangeListener:</strong> fires when the committed value changes</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Validating at input is better than validating at use. It is the GUI version of the precondition argument in 6.13.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS155="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — VALIDATE AT THE POINT OF INPUT, OR PARSE AND CATCH LATER</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 268" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk278a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">two ways to get a number out of a text box</text><rect x="24" y="40" width="300" height="214" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.08" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="174" y="62" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JTextField</text><rect x="64" y="74" width="220" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="174" y="93.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"  42x "</text><line x1="174" y1="104" x2="174" y2="126" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk278a)"/><rect x="64" y="126" width="220" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="174" y="139" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">getText()</text><text x="174" y="156" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">always a String</text><line x1="174" y1="158" x2="174" y2="178" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk278a)"/><rect x="64" y="178" width="220" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="174" y="191" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Integer.parseInt</text><text x="174" y="208" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">may throw</text><text x="174" y="230" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">NumberFormatException  →  you catch it</text><text x="174" y="246" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">and tell the user in the interface</text><rect x="340" y="40" width="300" height="214" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.08" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="490" y="62" fill="#10b981" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JFormattedTextField</text><rect x="380" y="74" width="220" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="490" y="93.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">typing "42x"</text><line x1="490" y1="104" x2="490" y2="126" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk278a)"/><rect x="380" y="126" width="220" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="490" y="139" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the formatter checks it</text><text x="490" y="156" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">on focus loss / Enter</text><line x1="452" y1="158" x2="420" y2="178" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk278a)"/><line x1="528" y1="158" x2="560" y2="178" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk278a)"/><rect x="348" y="178" width="148" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="422" y="191" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">reverted</text><text x="422" y="208" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">never becomes a value</text><rect x="500" y="178" width="132" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="566" y="191" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">committed</text><text x="566" y="208" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">value changes</text><text x="490" y="230" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">getValue()  →  an Object holding a Number</text><text x="490" y="246" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no parsing, no exception to catch</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the plain field, done correctly</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JTextField massField = new JTextField(8);
JLabel status = new JLabel(" ");

record.addActionListener(e -&gt; {
    String text = massField.getText().trim();     // trim FIRST
    if (text.isEmpty()) { status.setText("Enter a mass in grams"); return; }
    try {
        int grams = Integer.parseInt(text);
        if (grams &lt;= 0) { status.setText("Mass must be positive"); return; }
        status.setText("Recorded " + grams + " g");
    } catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
        status.setText("\"" + text + "\" is not a whole number");
        massField.setText("");                    // clear it so they can retype
    }
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Three distinct failures get three distinct messages: empty, not a number, and out of range. One generic "invalid input" tells the user nothing.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>trim()</code> comes before the emptiness test, so a field of spaces is caught by the first branch rather than by <code>parseInt</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The range check sits <em>after</em> the parse — you cannot compare a <code>String</code> against 0.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reporting goes to a label, not <code>System.out</code>: the user of a window is not watching a console.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Integer.parseInt("42.5")</code> also throws — a decimal point is not a whole number. Use <code>Double.parseDouble</code> when decimals are legal.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the formatted field, which never sees bad input</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">NumberFormat fmt = NumberFormat.getIntegerInstance();
JFormattedTextField massField = new JFormattedTextField(fmt);
massField.setValue(0);                     // seeds the value AND the display

massField.addPropertyChangeListener("value", evt -&gt; {
    int grams = ((Number) massField.getValue()).intValue();
    status.setText("mass " + grams + " g");
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The formatter is supplied at construction; it defines what the field will accept and how it displays what it holds.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setValue(0)</code> is how you initialise it — <code>setText("0")</code> sets the display without setting the value, and the two then disagree.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The listener is a <code>PropertyChangeListener</code> on the <code>"value"</code> property, not an <code>ActionListener</code>. It fires when the value is <em>committed</em> — typically on Enter or when focus leaves — not on every keystroke.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getValue()</code> returns <code>Object</code>. The cast is to <code>Number</code> rather than <code>Integer</code>, because the formatter may produce a <code>Long</code>; <code>Number</code> covers both and <code>intValue()</code> works on either.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">There is no <code>try</code>. Invalid text never becomes a value — the field reverts it — so there is nothing to catch.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>getText()</code> and <code>getValue()</code> are not interchangeable. On a <code>JFormattedTextField</code>, <code>getText()</code> returns whatever characters are currently displayed, including formatting such as the comma in <code>"1,250"</code> — and <code>Integer.parseInt("1,250")</code> throws. <code>getValue()</code> returns the committed <code>Number</code>. The reverse mistake is casting the result of <code>getValue()</code> straight to <code>Integer</code> when the formatter produced a <code>Long</code>, which compiles and throws <code>ClassCastException</code> at run time; casting to <code>Number</code> and calling <code>intValue()</code> is safe either way.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_6":{"body":"<p>A <code>JSpinner</code> constrains input to a range with up and down arrows, so out-of-range values become impossible rather than merely rejected.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">SpinnerNumberModel model = new SpinnerNumberModel(
        50,      // initial
        0,       // minimum
        100,     // maximum
        1);      // step
JSpinner spinner = new JSpinner(model);

spinner.addChangeListener(e -&gt; {
    int value = (Integer) spinner.getValue();
    label.setText("value: " + value);
});</pre><p>Note it is a <code>ChangeListener</code> here rather than an <code>ActionListener</code> — different controls fire different event types, and using the wrong one means the handler is never called. That is the most common Swing debugging story.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">class Canvas extends JPanel {
    @Override
    protected void paintComponent(Graphics g) {
        super.paintComponent(g);          // clears the background — do NOT omit
        g.setColor(Color.BLUE);
        g.fillRect(10, 20, 100, 50);      // x, y, WIDTH, HEIGHT
        g.fillOval(10, 20, 40, 40);       // a circle is an oval in a square box
    }
}
panel.repaint();                          // ASK for a redraw — never call paintComponent</pre><p>Custom drawing means overriding <code>paintComponent</code> and letting Swing call it. Calling it yourself does not work reliably, because the system decides when painting happens; <code>repaint()</code> is the request.</p><p>Omitting <code>super.paintComponent(g)</code> leaves the previous frame on screen, so moving shapes smear — the single most common symptom in this topic.</p><p>The coordinate origin is the <strong>top-left</strong> and y increases <em>downwards</em>, which is upside-down compared with mathematics. Rectangle and oval parameters are position then size, not two corners.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JSpinner:</strong> a control cycling through a bounded sequence of values</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>SpinnerNumberModel:</strong> the model supplying initial value, bounds and step</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ChangeListener:</strong> the listener for value changes, with method stateChanged</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>getValue():</strong> returns the current value as an Object, requiring a cast</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Buttons use ActionListener, spinners and sliders use ChangeListener, text fields can use either. If a handler never fires, check the listener type before anything else.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS156="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — A SPINNER IS A MODEL PLUS TWO ARROWS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 292" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk279a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the model owns the numbers; the spinner only shows them</text><rect x="30" y="42" width="280" height="150" rx="9" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.09" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="170" y="64" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">SpinnerNumberModel(50, 0, 100, 5)</text><text x="64" y="92" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">50</text><text x="96" y="92" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">value — where it starts</text><text x="64" y="116" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">0</text><text x="96" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">minimum — cannot go below</text><text x="64" y="140" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">100</text><text x="96" y="140" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">maximum — cannot go above</text><text x="64" y="164" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">5</text><text x="96" y="164" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">step — one click of an arrow</text><rect x="360" y="66" width="96" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="408" y="87.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">50</text><rect x="456" y="66" width="22" height="17" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="456" y="83" width="22" height="17" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="467" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">▲</text><text x="467" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">▼</text><line x1="490" y1="74" x2="528" y2="74" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk279a)"/><text x="534" y="78" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">55, 60, 65 …</text><line x1="490" y1="92" x2="528" y2="92" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk279a)"/><text x="534" y="96" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">45, 40 … then stops at 0</text><text x="490" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">out-of-range values are not rejected —</text><text x="490" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">they are unreachable in the first place</text><rect x="30" y="206" width="604" height="74" rx="9" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.09" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="332" y="228" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">which listener does this control fire?</text><text x="180" y="252" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">JButton, JMenuItem, JTextField</text><text x="180" y="270" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ActionListener → actionPerformed</text><text x="486" y="252" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">JSpinner, JSlider</text><text x="486" y="270" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ChangeListener → stateChanged</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a bounded numeric spinner and its listener</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">SpinnerNumberModel model = new SpinnerNumberModel(50, 0, 100, 5);
JSpinner spinner = new JSpinner(model);

spinner.addChangeListener(new ChangeListener(){
    @Override public void stateChanged(ChangeEvent e){
        int v = (Integer) ((JSpinner) e.getSource()).getValue();
        status.setText("spinner " + v);
    }
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The four constructor arguments are value, minimum, maximum, step — in that order. Swapping the first two is the usual slip and produces a spinner that starts at its floor.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Clicking up from 50 with a step of 5 gives 55, then 60. At 100 the up arrow does nothing: the model refuses to leave its range.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The event carries no value. You ask the source — or the captured <code>spinner</code> variable — what it now holds.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getValue()</code> returns <code>Object</code>; a <code>SpinnerNumberModel</code> built from <code>int</code> literals yields <code>Integer</code>, so the cast is to <code>Integer</code>. Build the model with <code>double</code> literals and the cast must be to <code>Double</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>stateChanged</code> is the only method of <code>ChangeListener</code>, so this too could be written as a lambda.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a spinner over a list of values, not numbers</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The number model is not the only one. A spinner can cycle through any fixed list, which is how a day-of-week or a shift-code picker is built.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JSpinner dates = new JSpinner(
    new SpinnerListModel(new String[]{"Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri"}));

dates.addChangeListener(e -&gt; {
    String day = (String) dates.getValue();      // the cast follows the model
    status.setText("day " + day);
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>SpinnerListModel</code> replaces <code>SpinnerNumberModel</code>; nothing else about the spinner changes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The type returned by <code>getValue()</code> follows the model — <code>String</code> here, <code>Integer</code> for the numeric model. The cast is not decoration; get it wrong and it is a <code>ClassCastException</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A list model wraps at the ends by default, so pressing up on "Fri" returns to "Mon" — different from the number model, which simply stops.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Compared with a <code>JComboBox</code>: the combo box shows all choices at once and suits long lists; a spinner shows one and suits short, naturally ordered ones.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Attaching an <code>ActionListener</code> to a spinner or a slider compiles — every <code>JComponent</code> inherits <code>addMouseListener</code> and friends, and there is nothing illegal about the call — but the handler never fires, because those controls publish <code>ChangeEvent</code>s, not <code>ActionEvent</code>s. The symptom is a control that visibly moves while your code does nothing. When a handler never runs, check the listener <em>type</em> before you suspect the logic: buttons and menu items use <code>ActionListener</code>, spinners and sliders use <code>ChangeListener</code>.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_7":{"body":"<p>A <code>JTextArea</code> displays many lines, and it is nearly always wrapped in a <code>JScrollPane</code> — a text area alone does not scroll.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JTextArea area = new JTextArea(10, 40);      // rows, columns
area.setEditable(false);
area.setLineWrap(true);
area.setWrapStyleWord(true);

JScrollPane scroll = new JScrollPane(area);   // the AREA goes in the SCROLL PANE
panel.add(scroll);                            // and the SCROLL PANE goes in the panel

area.setText("first line\n");                 // replaces everything
area.append("second line\n");                 // adds to the end</pre><p>The order in the middle is the part people get wrong: adding the text area to the panel and the scroll pane separately gives a text area that grows without ever scrolling. The scroll pane must be the component the panel holds.</p><h3>Menus</h3><p>Menus have a fixed three-level containment that questions ask you to put in order.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JMenuBar   contains   JMenu   contains   JMenuItem

JMenuBar bar = new JMenuBar();
JMenu file = new JMenu("File");
file.add(new JMenuItem("Open"));
bar.add(file);
frame.setJMenuBar(bar);        // NOT frame.add(bar)</pre><p>Two details are tested. The bar is attached with <strong><code>setJMenuBar</code></strong>, not <code>add</code> — it is not an ordinary component in the layout. And a <strong>submenu</strong> is made by adding a <code>JMenu</code> to another <code>JMenu</code> rather than a <code>JMenuItem</code>, which is what nests one menu inside another.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JMenuBar bar = new JMenuBar();
JMenu file = new JMenu("File");
JMenuItem open = new JMenuItem("Open");
open.addActionListener(e -&gt; ...);       // items fire ActionEvents, like buttons
file.add(open);
file.addSeparator();
bar.add(file);
frame.setJMenuBar(bar);                 // NOT frame.add(bar)</pre><p>The hierarchy is fixed: <code>JMenuBar</code> holds <code>JMenu</code>s, which hold <code>JMenuItem</code>s. A <code>JMenu</code> added to another <code>JMenu</code> becomes a submenu.</p><p>The menu bar attaches with <code>setJMenuBar</code>, not <code>add</code> — using <code>add</code> drops it into the layout as an ordinary component, where it looks wrong and often disappears.</p><p>Menu items are <code>AbstractButton</code>s like <code>JButton</code>, so the same <code>ActionListener</code> can serve both a toolbar button and a menu item — one handler, two ways to reach it.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JTextArea:</strong> a multi-line text component</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JScrollPane:</strong> a container adding scrollbars to whatever it wraps</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>setText vs append:</strong> replace all content versus add to the end</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>setLineWrap:</strong> wraps long lines instead of scrolling horizontally</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p><code>setEditable(false)</code> makes a text area an output log rather than an input box — the usual role in a course project.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS157="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WRAPPING FOR SCROLL, AND THE THREE MENU LEVELS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 326" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk280a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the scroll pane must be the thing you add</text><rect x="24" y="38" width="292" height="128" rx="9" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.08" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="170" y="58" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WRONG</text><rect x="50" y="70" width="110" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="105" y="91" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JPanel</text><line x1="160" y1="87" x2="196" y2="76" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><line x1="160" y1="87" x2="196" y2="112" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><rect x="196" y="60" width="104" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="248" y="80" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JTextArea</text><rect x="196" y="96" width="104" height="32" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="248" y="109" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JScrollPane</text><text x="248" y="126" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">empty</text><text x="170" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the area never scrolls; the pane shows nothing</text><rect x="348" y="38" width="292" height="128" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.08" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="494" y="58" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">RIGHT</text><rect x="370" y="78" width="100" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="420" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JPanel</text><line x1="470" y1="95" x2="500" y2="95" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><rect x="500" y="78" width="114" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="557" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JScrollPane</text><line x1="557" y1="112" x2="557" y2="130" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><rect x="505" y="130" width="104" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="557" y="149" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JTextArea</text><text x="494" y="180" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">panel holds the pane, the pane holds the area</text><text x="332" y="210" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the menu hierarchy is fixed</text><rect x="60" y="226" width="150" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="135" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JMenuBar</text><text x="135" y="257" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">setJMenuBar(bar)</text><line x1="210" y1="243" x2="246" y2="243" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><rect x="246" y="226" width="130" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="311" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JMenu</text><text x="311" y="257" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">"File"</text><line x1="376" y1="243" x2="412" y2="243" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><rect x="412" y="226" width="150" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="487" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JMenuItem</text><text x="487" y="257" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">"Open" — fires an</text><text x="596" y="246" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ActionEvent</text><line x1="311" y1="260" x2="311" y2="282" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#fk280a)"/><rect x="246" y="282" width="130" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="311" y="301" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JMenu</text><text x="392" y="302" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-style="italic">a JMenu inside a JMenu is a submenu</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a scrolling, read-only log area</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JTextArea log = new JTextArea(8, 40);       // rows, columns
log.setEditable(false);                     // output, not input
log.setLineWrap(true);                      // wrap instead of scrolling sideways
log.setWrapStyleWord(true);                 // wrap at spaces, not mid-word

JScrollPane logScroll = new JScrollPane(log);   // the AREA goes inside
panel.add(logScroll);                           // the PANE goes in the panel

log.setText("ready\n");        // replaces everything
log.append("job started\n");   // adds to the end — the one you want in a loop</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new JTextArea(8, 40)</code> is a size hint in rows and columns, the same idea as <code>new JTextField(12)</code>. It does not limit how much text the area can hold.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setEditable(false)</code> is what turns an input box into an output log; the user can still select and copy, which is usually wanted.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setLineWrap(true)</code> alone breaks lines mid-word. <code>setWrapStyleWord(true)</code> moves the break to the last space.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setText</code> replaces the entire contents; <code>append</code> adds. Using <code>setText</code> inside a loop leaves only the final line.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Adding <code>log</code> to the panel instead of <code>logScroll</code> gives a text area that grows past the window edge and never scrolls — the mistake this figure exists for.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a File menu with a separator and a submenu</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JMenuBar bar = new JMenuBar();
JMenu file = new JMenu("File");

JMenuItem open = new JMenuItem("Open");
open.addActionListener(e -&gt; loadFile());     // items behave like buttons
file.add(open);
file.addSeparator();                         // the horizontal rule

JMenu export = new JMenu("Export");           // a JMenu inside a JMenu
export.add(new JMenuItem("CSV"));             // = a submenu
file.add(export);

bar.add(file);
frame.setJMenuBar(bar);                       // NOT frame.add(bar)</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Build inside out: items into menus, menus into the bar, the bar onto the frame.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>JMenuItem</code> extends <code>AbstractButton</code>, so it fires an <code>ActionEvent</code> and takes the same <code>ActionListener</code> — one handler can serve a toolbar button and a menu item.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>addSeparator()</code> belongs to the menu, not to the item; it groups the entries visually.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Adding a <code>JMenu</code> where an item would go creates a submenu. Nothing else changes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setJMenuBar</code> attaches the bar to the frame’s own menu slot. <code>frame.add(bar)</code> compiles and drops it into the layout as an ordinary component, where it typically lands in the middle of the window or disappears.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Adding the text component and the scroll pane as siblings is the classic failure: <code>panel.add(area); panel.add(new JScrollPane(area));</code> looks thorough and produces an area that never scrolls, because the pane must be the parent. The same rule catches <code>JList</code> and <code>JTable</code> — and on a table there is a second symptom, since the column headers live in the scroll pane’s header row and simply do not appear without one.</p></div><i data-d286gap_D286_u15_z15_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the two menu shortcuts, which are not the same thing</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk434a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">accelerator</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a direct key combo, e.g. Ctrl+S</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">setAccelerator(KeyStroke…)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">set on the JMenuItem</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">KeyStroke.getKeyStroke(…)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">builds the combination</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">mnemonic</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a letter that navigates an open menu</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">setMnemonic('F')</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">underlines F in the File menu</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the difference</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">accelerator fires it; mnemonic reaches it</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Ctrl+S on a menu item, and Alt-navigation on the menu</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">JMenu fileMenu = new JMenu("File");
fileMenu.setMnemonic('F');            // Alt+F opens this menu

JMenuItem save = new JMenuItem("Save");
save.setMnemonic('S');                // once File is open, S picks Save
save.setAccelerator(
    KeyStroke.getKeyStroke(KeyEvent.VK_S, InputEvent.CTRL_DOWN_MASK));
// Ctrl+S now triggers Save without opening any menu at all

save.addActionListener(e -&gt; saveDocument());
fileMenu.add(save);</pre><ol><li>The <strong>accelerator</strong> is a global shortcut: pressing Ctrl+S fires the item's action directly, with the menu closed.</li><li>The <strong>mnemonic</strong> only works while navigating — Alt+F opens File, then S selects Save.</li><li>Swing displays the accelerator beside the item's label, so the shortcut advertises itself; the mnemonic shows as an underlined letter.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Treating <code>setMnemonic</code> as a keyboard shortcut. It is not — it only selects an item within a menu that is already open. The direct Ctrl+key shortcut is <code>setAccelerator</code> with a <code>KeyStroke</code>.</div>"},"D286/u15/z15_8":{"body":"<p>A <code>JTable</code> displays rows and columns, and it separates the data from the display: the <strong>model</strong> holds the values, the table shows them.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String[] columns = {"Name", "Score"};
Object[][] data = { {"Ada", 95}, {"Alan", 88} };

DefaultTableModel model = new DefaultTableModel(data, columns);
JTable table = new JTable(model);
panel.add(new JScrollPane(table));         // headers only show inside a scroll pane

model.addRow(new Object[]{"Grace", 99});   // change the MODEL, not the table
model.getValueAt(0, 1);                    // 95
model.removeRow(0);</pre><p>Every change goes through the model, which then tells the table to refresh itself. Trying to modify the table directly is the standard mistake, and the column headers vanishing without a scroll pane is the second.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">javax.swing.Timer t = new javax.swing.Timer(1000, e -&gt; {
    x += 5;
    panel.repaint();                    // move, then ask for a redraw
});
t.start();
// t.stop();  t.setDelay(500);</pre><p>Use <strong><code>javax.swing.Timer</code></strong>, not <code>java.util.Timer</code>. The Swing one fires its <code>ActionListener</code> on the event dispatch thread, which is the only thread allowed to touch components; the utility one fires on its own thread and produces intermittent, hard-to-reproduce corruption.</p><p>The delay is in milliseconds, so 1000 is one frame per second. Animation is therefore not a loop — it is a timer that changes state and calls <code>repaint()</code>, letting the interface stay responsive between frames.</p><p><code>Thread.sleep</code> in a listener does the opposite: it blocks the event thread, so nothing repaints and the window appears frozen for exactly as long as the sleep.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JTable:</strong> the component displaying tabular data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>TableModel:</strong> the object holding the data; the table is only a view of it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>DefaultTableModel:</strong> the standard editable implementation with addRow and removeRow</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Model-view separation:</strong> keeping data and display apart so either can change independently</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>This split is the same idea as encapsulation in 7.4 — the data is owned in one place and the display asks for it, so nothing can change it behind the model’s back.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS158="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — MODEL AND VIEW: WHO OWNS THE ROWS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 222" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk281a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the model holds the data; the table is only a window onto it</text><rect x="30" y="40" width="250" height="168" rx="9" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.09" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="155" y="62" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">DefaultTableModel</text><text x="85" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sample</text><text x="200" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Mass</text><rect x="50" y="92" width="90" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="95" y="109.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">S-01</text><rect x="150" y="92" width="90" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="195" y="109.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12</text><text x="258" y="110" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">row 0</text><rect x="50" y="122" width="90" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="95" y="139.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">S-02</text><rect x="150" y="122" width="90" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="195" y="139.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">19</text><text x="258" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">row 1</text><rect x="50" y="152" width="90" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="95" y="169.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">S-03</text><rect x="150" y="152" width="90" height="26" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="195" y="169.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7</text><text x="258" y="170" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">row 2</text><text x="155" y="196" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">getValueAt(row, column) — row first</text><line x1="280" y1="100" x2="360" y2="100" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk281a)"/><text x="320" y="92" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the table asks</text><line x1="360" y1="150" x2="280" y2="150" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk281a)"/><text x="320" y="168" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">addRow / setValueAt</text><text x="320" y="182" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">then the model tells</text><rect x="360" y="40" width="280" height="168" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.09" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="500" y="62" fill="#10b981" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JTable inside a JScrollPane</text><rect x="390" y="74" width="220" height="22" rx="3" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.15)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="445" y="89" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sample</text><text x="555" y="89" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Mass (g)</text><text x="500" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the header row lives in the SCROLL PANE</text><rect x="390" y="116" width="110" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="445" y="132.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">S-01</text><rect x="500" y="116" width="110" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="555" y="132.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12</text><rect x="390" y="142" width="110" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="445" y="158.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">S-02</text><rect x="500" y="142" width="110" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="555" y="158.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">19</text><rect x="390" y="168" width="110" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="445" y="184.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">S-03</text><rect x="500" y="168" width="110" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="555" y="184.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7</text><text x="500" y="200" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">no data of its own — it re-reads the model</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — building a table and changing it through the model</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String[] cols = {"Sample", "Mass (g)"};
Object[][] rows = {{"S-01", 12}, {"S-02", 19}};

DefaultTableModel tm = new DefaultTableModel(rows, cols);
JTable table = new JTable(tm);
panel.add(new JScrollPane(table));       // headers need the scroll pane

tm.addRow(new Object[]{"S-03", 7});      // change the MODEL
Object cell = tm.getValueAt(0, 1);       // -&gt; 12   (row 0, column 1)
tm.setValueAt(14, 0, 1);                 // value FIRST, then row, then column
System.out.println(tm.getRowCount() + " rows, " + tm.getColumnCount() + " columns");</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The data is <code>Object[][]</code>, so a column can hold numbers and another strings. Cells come back as <code>Object</code> and usually need a cast or <code>toString()</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>addRow</code> takes an <code>Object[]</code> whose length must match the column count; a short array leaves trailing cells empty rather than throwing.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getValueAt(row, column)</code> is row first — the 2D-array convention, and the opposite of the JavaFX <code>GridPane</code> order in 16.2.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setValueAt(value, row, column)</code> puts the value first. Two adjacent methods with different argument orders is exactly the kind of detail an exam tests.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Every one of these calls goes to the model. The table repaints itself because the model announces the change.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — animation with a Swing Timer, not a loop</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A loop that redraws cannot work: it occupies the event thread, so nothing repaints until it finishes. A timer fires periodically and returns immediately.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">javax.swing.Timer timer = new javax.swing.Timer(1000, e -&gt; {
    x += 5;              // change the state
    panel.repaint();     // then ask for a redraw
});
timer.start();
timer.setDelay(500);     // twice as fast
timer.stop();</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The first argument is the delay in milliseconds: 1000 is one tick per second.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The second argument is an <code>ActionListener</code> — a timer fires <code>ActionEvent</code>s, exactly like a button.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Use <code>javax.swing.Timer</code>, not <code>java.util.Timer</code>. The Swing one fires on the event dispatch thread, the only thread allowed to touch components; the utility one fires on its own thread and produces intermittent, unreproducible corruption.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The fully qualified name in the declaration is deliberate: with both <code>java.util.*</code> and <code>javax.swing.*</code> imported, the plain name <code>Timer</code> is ambiguous and will not compile.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Thread.sleep</code> in a listener does the opposite of a timer — it blocks the thread that repaints, so the window freezes for exactly the sleep duration.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Calling setters on the <code>JTable</code> instead of the model is the standard dead end — the component is a view, and there is no <code>table.addRow</code> at all, so the code will not even compile. Keep a reference to the <code>DefaultTableModel</code> in a field and go through it. Two smaller ones travel with it: <code>getValueAt</code> returns <code>Object</code> (so <code>(int) tm.getValueAt(0,1)</code> needs the stored value really to be an <code>Integer</code>), and a table added without a <code>JScrollPane</code> shows its rows but no column headers, which looks like the headers were never set.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_9":{"body":"<p>A <code>JSlider</code> chooses a value from a range by dragging, and it fires continuously while the user drags — which is a behaviour you have to design around.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JSlider slider = new JSlider(0, 100, 50);    // min, max, initial
slider.setMajorTickSpacing(25);
slider.setPaintTicks(true);
slider.setPaintLabels(true);

slider.addChangeListener(e -&gt; {
    if (!slider.getValueIsAdjusting()) {     // only when the drag FINISHES
        label.setText("value: " + slider.getValue());
    }
});</pre><p><code>getValueIsAdjusting()</code> is the guard that matters. Without it a handler that does real work — recomputing, redrawing, writing a file — runs dozens of times per drag. Live preview wants it without the guard; anything expensive wants it with.</p><h3>The simpler layout managers</h3><p><code>GridBagLayout</code> from 15.3 is the flexible one; three simpler managers cover most real forms and are asked about by name.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Layout</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Arranges</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Default for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>FlowLayout</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">left to right, wrapping as needed</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JPanel</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>BorderLayout</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">five regions: NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, WEST, CENTER</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JFrame</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>GridLayout</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">equal-sized cells in a fixed grid</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>GridLayout(rows, cols)</strong> forces every cell to the same size, which is why it suits calculators and keypads and not forms with a label beside a field. In <code>BorderLayout</code> only CENTER stretches to fill the leftover space, so a component placed there grows with the window and one in NORTH does not.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JPanel pad = new JPanel(new GridLayout(4, 3));      // equal cells suit a keypad
for (String label : keys) {
    JButton b = new JButton(label);
    b.addActionListener(handler);                   // ONE handler for all of them
    pad.add(b);
}

public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
    String k = e.getActionCommand();                // which key was pressed
    if (k.equals("/") &amp;&amp; current == 0) { show("Cannot divide by zero"); return; }
}</pre><p><code>GridLayout</code> is the right choice here precisely because every cell is the same size — the property that makes it awkward elsewhere is what a keypad wants.</p><p>One listener plus <code>getActionCommand()</code> replaces a dozen near-identical handlers. Digits build the number by appending to the display text rather than doing arithmetic; the operator is stored and applied when the next one or equals arrives.</p><p>The division guard belongs before the calculation, not in a <code>catch</code> — and remember from 12.2 that with <code>double</code> operands dividing by zero does not throw at all, it produces <code>Infinity</code>, so nothing would be caught anyway.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JSlider:</strong> a control selecting a value from a range by dragging</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>ChangeListener:</strong> fires on every value change, including mid-drag</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>getValueIsAdjusting():</strong> true while the user is still dragging</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Tick marks:</strong> the visual scale, enabled with setPaintTicks and setPaintLabels</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Sliders and spinners both use ChangeListener, and both return their value as an <code>int</code> from <code>getValue()</code> — unlike the spinner’s Object-returning version when a model is attached.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS159="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHAT A SLIDER DRAG ACTUALLY SENDS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 306" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk282a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one drag from 40 to 60 fires many events</text><line x1="80" y1="68" x2="584" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2.4"/><line x1="80" y1="62" x2="80" y2="78" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="80" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">0</text><line x1="206" y1="62" x2="206" y2="78" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="206" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">25</text><line x1="332" y1="62" x2="332" y2="78" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="332" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">50</text><line x1="458" y1="62" x2="458" y2="78" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="458" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">75</text><line x1="584" y1="62" x2="584" y2="78" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="584" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">100</text><circle cx="281.6" cy="68" r="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="382.4" cy="68" r="8" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M281.6,58 Q332,28 382.4,58" fill="none" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk282a)"/><text x="332" y="39.4" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one drag</text><text x="332" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the ChangeEvents that arrive</text><rect x="84" y="146" width="72" height="46" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="120" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">41</text><text x="120" y="183" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">adjusting</text><rect x="168" y="146" width="72" height="46" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="204" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">46</text><text x="204" y="183" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">adjusting</text><rect x="252" y="146" width="72" height="46" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="288" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">52</text><text x="288" y="183" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">adjusting</text><rect x="336" y="146" width="72" height="46" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="372" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">58</text><text x="372" y="183" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">adjusting</text><rect x="420" y="146" width="72" height="46" rx="4" fill="rgba(232,121,90,0.14)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="456" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">60</text><text x="456" y="183" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">adjusting</text><rect x="504" y="146" width="72" height="46" rx="4" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.18)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="540" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">60</text><text x="540" y="183" fill="#10b981" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FINAL</text><text x="332" y="218" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">getValueIsAdjusting() is true for the orange ones, false for the green one</text><rect x="60" y="232" width="544" height="62" rx="9" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.09" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="332" y="254" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">no guard  →  the handler runs six times</text><text x="332" y="276" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">if (!s.getValueIsAdjusting())  →  it runs once, at 60</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a slider with ticks, driving a live preview</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JSlider slider = new JSlider(0, 100, 40);    // min, max, initial
slider.setMajorTickSpacing(25);              // labelled ticks every 25
slider.setMinorTickSpacing(5);
slider.setPaintTicks(true);                  // ticks are OFF by default
slider.setPaintLabels(true);                 // and so are the numbers

slider.addChangeListener(e -&gt; {
    JSlider s = (JSlider) e.getSource();
    if (!s.getValueIsAdjusting()) {          // only when the drag finishes
        gauge.setLevel(s.getValue());        // this repaints the gauge
    }
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The three constructor arguments are minimum, maximum, initial value — the same shape as the spinner model, minus the step.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setMajorTickSpacing</code> alone shows nothing: the two <code>setPaint…</code> calls are what make ticks and labels visible.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getValue()</code> returns an <code>int</code> directly, with no cast — unlike <code>JSpinner.getValue()</code>, which returns <code>Object</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The guard means the gauge is redrawn once per drag rather than dozens of times. Drop the guard for a cheap live preview; keep it for anything that recomputes, writes a file, or hits a network.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>gauge.setLevel</code> ends in <code>repaint()</code> — the pattern from 15.1: change state, then request a redraw.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — two controls, one value, kept in step</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A slider and a spinner over the same quantity is a common requirement, and it is where the adjusting flag earns its keep — without care the two listeners call each other in a loop.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JSlider slider = new JSlider(0, 100, 40);
JSpinner spinner = new JSpinner(new SpinnerNumberModel(40, 0, 100, 1));

slider.addChangeListener(e -&gt; {
    int v = slider.getValue();
    if (!spinner.getValue().equals(v)) spinner.setValue(v);   // guard first
});
spinner.addChangeListener(e -&gt; {
    int v = (Integer) spinner.getValue();
    if (slider.getValue() != v) slider.setValue(v);
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Setting a control’s value fires its listener, so the naive version would loop: slider sets spinner, spinner sets slider, forever.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The equality check breaks the cycle. When the second control already holds the value, nothing is set and no further event is generated.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>spinner.getValue()</code> returns <code>Object</code>, so it is compared with <code>equals</code> and cast when an <code>int</code> is needed; <code>slider.getValue()</code> is already an <code>int</code> and uses <code>!=</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Both listeners are <code>ChangeListener</code>s — the shared listener type is exactly why these two controls pair up so often.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A <code>ChangeListener</code> on a slider fires on <em>every</em> intermediate value, not once per drag. A handler that writes a file, re-runs a calculation or repaints a large image will run dozens of times for one gesture and make the interface stutter — and because each call is individually fast, it does not look like a performance bug. Guard with <code>if (!slider.getValueIsAdjusting())</code> for expensive work. The reverse mistake is guarding a cheap live preview, which then updates only when the user lets go and feels broken.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_10":{"body":"<p>The seat reservation project puts the unit together: a table shows the seats, a field takes a seat number, a button reserves it, and a label reports what happened.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JTextField seatField = new JTextField(5);
JButton reserve = new JButton("Reserve");
DefaultTableModel model = new DefaultTableModel(new String[]{"Seat","Status"}, 0);

reserve.addActionListener(e -&gt; {
    try {
        int seat = Integer.parseInt(seatField.getText().trim());
        if (seat &lt; 1 || seat &gt; model.getRowCount()) {
            status.setText("No such seat");
            return;
        }
        if ("taken".equals(model.getValueAt(seat - 1, 1))) {
            status.setText("Seat already taken");
            return;
        }
        model.setValueAt("taken", seat - 1, 1);
        status.setText("Reserved seat " + seat);
    } catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
        status.setText("Enter a number");
    }
});</pre><p>The handler is a stack of guard clauses (6.5), and every one of them corresponds to a way a user can be wrong: not a number, out of range, already taken. A GUI handler that assumes good input is a GUI handler that crashes.</p><h3>Lists and their models</h3><p>A <code>JList</code> follows the same model-view split as <code>JTable</code> in 15.8: the <strong>ListModel</strong> holds the data and the component displays it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">DefaultListModel<string> model = new DefaultListModel&lt;&gt;();
model.addElement("A1");                 // change the MODEL
JList<string> list = new JList&lt;&gt;(model);
panel.add(new JScrollPane(list));       // scroll pane again

list.getSelectedIndex();   // -1 when nothing is selected
list.getSelectedValue();   // the element itself, or null</string></string></pre><p><code>getSelectedIndex()</code> returns <strong>-1</strong> when nothing is selected — the same not-found convention as <code>indexOf</code> in 3.16 — so it must be checked before being used as an index.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">DefaultListModel&lt;String&gt; model = new DefaultListModel&lt;&gt;();
JList&lt;String&gt; list = new JList&lt;&gt;(model);
frame.add(new JScrollPane(list));            // wrap it — JList does not scroll itself

model.addElement(field.getText());           // change the MODEL, not the view
int i = list.getSelectedIndex();
if (i != -1) model.remove(i);                // -1 means nothing selected</pre><p>The component displays a <strong>model</strong>, and you modify the model — the view updates itself. Trying to change the <code>JList</code> directly is the usual dead end; this model-and-view split is the same idea behind <code>JTable</code> with a <code>DefaultTableModel</code>.</p><p>Scrolling is not built in. A <code>JList</code>, <code>JTextArea</code> or <code>JTable</code> must be wrapped in a <code>JScrollPane</code>, and the scroll pane is what gets added to the frame.</p><p><code>getSelectedIndex()</code> returns <strong>−1</strong> when nothing is selected, so removing without that check throws. Guard against empty input too, or the list fills with blank rows.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Guard clauses in a handler:</strong> early returns rejecting each invalid case before the real work</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Seat index offset:</strong> seat 1 is row 0 — the off-by-one from 5.1 in user-facing form</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Status label:</strong> a single label reporting the outcome instead of a dialog per error</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Users count seats from 1 and tables count rows from 0. Converting once, at the boundary, is right; converting in several places is how one of them ends up missing.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS1510="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — EVERY WAY A USER CAN BE WRONG, IN ORDER</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 288" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk283a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the handler is a chain of guards; the last step is the only one that changes anything</text><rect x="20" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="78" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">getText().trim()</text><text x="78" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">" 12 " → "12"</text><line x1="136" y1="68" x2="146" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><rect x="148" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="206" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">parseInt</text><text x="206" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not a number →</text><line x1="264" y1="68" x2="274" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><line x1="206" y1="90" x2="206" y2="116" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><text x="206" y="130" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">status.setText</text><text x="206" y="142" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">return</text><rect x="276" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="334" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">range check</text><text x="334" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">< 1 or > rows →</text><line x1="392" y1="68" x2="402" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><line x1="334" y1="90" x2="334" y2="116" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><text x="334" y="130" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">status.setText</text><text x="334" y="142" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">return</text><rect x="404" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="462" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">already taken?</text><text x="462" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">occupied →</text><line x1="520" y1="68" x2="530" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><line x1="462" y1="90" x2="462" y2="116" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><text x="462" y="130" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">status.setText</text><text x="462" y="142" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">return</text><rect x="532" y="46" width="116" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="590" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">setValueAt</text><text x="590" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">reserved</text><line x1="532" y1="90" x2="532" y2="116" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><text x="532" y="132" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the model changes</text><text x="332" y="184" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the one conversion, done once</text><text x="180" y="210" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what the user says</text><rect x="96" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="117" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="138" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="159" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="180" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="201" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="222" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="243" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="484" y="210" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what the table stores</text><rect x="400" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="421" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">0</text><rect x="442" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="463" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="484" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="505" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="526" y="220" width="42" height="30" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="547" y="239.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line x1="280" y1="235" x2="386" y2="235" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk283a)"/><text x="333" y="226" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">seat − 1</text><text x="333" y="272" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">convert at the boundary, once. Convert in three places and one of them will be missing.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the reserve button, guard by guard</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">reserve.addActionListener(e -&gt; {
    String text = seatField.getText().trim();
    if (text.isEmpty()) { status.setText("Enter a seat number"); return; }
    try {
        int seat = Integer.parseInt(text);
        if (seat &lt; 1 || seat &gt; model.getRowCount()) {
            status.setText("No such seat"); return;
        }
        if ("taken".equals(model.getValueAt(seat - 1, 1))) {
            status.setText("Seat already taken"); return;
        }
        model.setValueAt("taken", seat - 1, 1);      // value, row, column
        status.setText("Reserved seat " + seat);
        seatField.setText("");
    } catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
        status.setText("Enter a number");
    }
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Four guards, four distinct messages, each with its own <code>return</code>. Nothing after a guard has to re-check what the guard already rejected.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The range test uses <code>model.getRowCount()</code> rather than a hard-coded 40, so adding seats to the model cannot leave the check behind.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>"taken".equals(model.getValueAt(...))</code> puts the literal first on purpose: <code>getValueAt</code> returns <code>Object</code> and could be <code>null</code>, and this order cannot throw.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>seat - 1</code> appears twice, in the read and the write, and nowhere else — the single conversion point.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Clearing the field on success is what makes the next reservation quick, and it also signals that something happened.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a JList of reservations, added and removed through its model</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">DefaultListModel&lt;String&gt; listModel = new DefaultListModel&lt;&gt;();
listModel.addElement("S-01");
JList&lt;String&gt; list = new JList&lt;&gt;(listModel);
panel.add(new JScrollPane(list));            // JList does not scroll itself

remove.addActionListener(e -&gt; {
    int i = list.getSelectedIndex();
    if (i == -1) { status.setText("nothing selected"); return; }   // -1 GUARD
    listModel.remove(i);
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Same split as <code>JTable</code>: <code>DefaultListModel</code> owns the strings, <code>JList</code> displays them.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getSelectedIndex()</code> returns −1 when nothing is selected — the same not-found convention as <code>indexOf</code>. Passing −1 straight to <code>remove</code> throws.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getSelectedValue()</code> is the alternative and returns <code>null</code> in the same situation, so it needs a null check instead.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The removal goes to the model; the list redraws itself. There is no <code>list.remove(i)</code> that does what you want.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Project-style questions here reward the boring answer: <em>validate everything the user can type</em>. Expect to be asked which guard is missing from a handler, and check them in this order — empty, unparseable, out of range, already in that state. The off-by-one is the other favourite: seats, rows and menu options are numbered from 1 for humans and from 0 for the model, so if an answer option differs from another only by ±1, the question is about that conversion. Convert once, at the boundary between the interface and the data.</p></div>"},"D286/u15/z15_11":{"body":"<p>Reading a file from a GUI combines Unit 9 and Unit 12 with a file chooser, and adds one Swing-specific obligation.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">JFileChooser chooser = new JFileChooser();
if (chooser.showOpenDialog(frame) == JFileChooser.APPROVE_OPTION) {
    File f = chooser.getSelectedFile();
    try (Scanner in = new Scanner(f)) {              // try-with-resources (12.3)
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (in.hasNextLine()) { sb.append(in.nextLine()).append("\n"); }
        area.setText(sb.toString());
    } catch (FileNotFoundException ex) {
        JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(frame, "Could not open the file");
    }
}</pre><p>Checking <code>APPROVE_OPTION</code> is not optional — the user can cancel, and <code>getSelectedFile()</code> then returns null. The <code>StringBuilder</code> is there for the reason given in 4.6: appending to a String once per line reallocates once per line.</p><p>Converting a console program to a GUI is one rule: <strong>separate the logic from the input and output.</strong> A method that takes values and returns a result works unchanged behind either interface; a method that calls <code>Scanner</code> and <code>println</code> internally has to be rewritten.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Console</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">GUI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>scnr.nextInt()</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Integer.parseInt(field.getText())</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>System.out.println(x)</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>label.setText(x)</code> or <code>area.append(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the main <code>while</code> loop</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the event loop — listeners fire as the user acts</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a validation <code>if</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the same <code>if</code>, unchanged</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The main loop is the piece that disappears entirely. A console program drives the user; a GUI waits to be driven, so what was the body of the loop becomes the body of a listener.</p><p>Validation belongs in the logic, not in the listener, so both interfaces enforce the same rules — and so the logic can still be unit tested without a window, which is the practical payoff of the separation.</p><i data-d286u1215="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JFileChooser:</strong> the standard open and save dialog</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>APPROVE_OPTION:</strong> the return value meaning the user chose a file rather than cancelling</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JOptionPane:</strong> the standard message, confirm and input dialogs</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>try-with-resources:</strong> closes the Scanner automatically, even on failure</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Reporting a failure in a GUI means a dialog or a status label, not <code>System.out.println</code> — the user is not looking at a console.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS1511="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — OPEN, READ, AND REPORT FAILURE WHERE IT CAN BE SEEN</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 308" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk284a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a file chooser has two outcomes, and only one of them gives you a file</text><rect x="24" y="48" width="130" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="89" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">button clicked</text><text x="89" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the handler runs</text><line x1="154" y1="68" x2="186" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><rect x="186" y="48" width="168" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="270" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">showOpenDialog(frame)</text><text x="270" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">blocks until answered</text><line x1="354" y1="60" x2="400" y2="44" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><line x1="354" y1="78" x2="400" y2="112" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><rect x="400" y="26" width="168" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="484" y="41" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">APPROVE_OPTION</text><text x="484" y="58" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">getSelectedFile()</text><rect x="400" y="96" width="168" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="484" y="111" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">CANCEL_OPTION</text><text x="484" y="128" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no file — do nothing</text><line x1="484" y1="62" x2="484" y2="92" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><text x="600" y="46" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">skipping this</text><text x="600" y="60" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">test gives a</text><text x="600" y="74" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">null File</text><text x="332" y="168" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">then it is ordinary file reading — with one Swing obligation</text><rect x="30" y="188" width="140" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="100" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">try (Scanner in …)</text><text x="100" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">closes automatically</text><line x1="170" y1="210" x2="200" y2="210" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><rect x="200" y="188" width="150" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="275" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">StringBuilder</text><text x="275" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">append per line</text><line x1="350" y1="210" x2="380" y2="210" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><rect x="380" y="188" width="130" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="445" y="207" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">area.setText</text><text x="445" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">once, at the end</text><line x1="445" y1="232" x2="445" y2="256" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk284a)"/><rect x="300" y="256" width="290" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="445" y="273" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">catch (FileNotFoundException e)</text><text x="445" y="290" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">JOptionPane — not System.out</text><text x="150" y="262" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the user is looking at a window,</text><text x="150" y="278" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">not at a console</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the whole load handler</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">load.addActionListener(e -&gt; {
    JFileChooser chooser = new JFileChooser();
    if (chooser.showOpenDialog(frame) != JFileChooser.APPROVE_OPTION) {
        status.setText("load cancelled");
        return;                                   // the user pressed Cancel
    }
    File f = chooser.getSelectedFile();
    try (Scanner in = new Scanner(f)) {           // try-with-resources
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int n = 0;
        while (in.hasNextLine()) { sb.append(in.nextLine()).append('\n'); n++; }
        log.setText(sb.toString());               // ONE update, at the end
        status.setText("loaded " + n + " lines from " + f.getName());
    } catch (FileNotFoundException ex) {
        JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(frame,
            "Could not open " + f.getName(), "Error", JOptionPane.ERROR_MESSAGE);
    }
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>showOpenDialog</code> blocks until the user answers and returns an <code>int</code> constant. Testing it is not optional: on Cancel, <code>getSelectedFile()</code> returns <code>null</code> and the next line throws.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Writing the test as "not approve, then return" keeps the successful path unindented — the guard-clause shape again.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>try (Scanner in = new Scanner(f))</code> closes the scanner however the block exits, including on exception.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The text is assembled in a <code>StringBuilder</code> and set once. Calling <code>append</code> on the text area per line works but repaints the component every time.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new Scanner(File)</code> throws the <em>checked</em> <code>FileNotFoundException</code>, so the compiler requires the catch — unlike <code>new Scanner(System.in)</code>, which throws nothing.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — turning a console program into a GUI</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The logic does not change. What changes is where input comes from and where output goes — which is only easy if the logic was in methods to begin with.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// console version
public static double average(int[] scores) { ... }   // reusable — no I/O inside

public static void main(String[] args){
    Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = scnr.nextInt();
    ...
    System.out.printf("Average: %.2f%n", average(scores));
}

// GUI version — SAME average method, different edges
compute.addActionListener(e -&gt; {
    try {
        int n = Integer.parseInt(countField.getText().trim());   // input
        ...
        result.setText(String.format("Average: %.2f", average(scores)));  // output
    } catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
        result.setText("Enter a whole number");
    }
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>scnr.nextInt()</code> becomes <code>Integer.parseInt(field.getText())</code> — and the parse can now fail, because a text field accepts anything.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>System.out.printf</code> becomes <code>setText</code> with <code>String.format</code>, which does the same formatting and returns the string instead of printing it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>while</code> loop that drove the console program disappears entirely. Its body becomes the listener; the event loop replaces it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>average</code> is untouched. A method that takes values and returns a result works behind either interface — and can still be unit tested with no window at all.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A method that called <code>Scanner</code> and <code>println</code> internally would have to be rewritten, which is the practical argument for separating logic from I/O.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Reporting failures with <code>System.out.println</code> in a GUI means nobody sees them: the console is hidden behind the window, or absent when the program is launched by double-clicking. Use <code>JOptionPane.showMessageDialog</code> for something the user must acknowledge, or a status label for something minor. The related trap is silence — an uncaught exception inside a listener does not stop the program; Swing prints the trace to the console and carries on, so a broken button looks like a button that does nothing at all.</p></div>"},"D286/u16/z16_1":{"body":"<p>JavaFX is the newer GUI toolkit, and it does the same job as Swing (Unit 15) with a different vocabulary. Knowing the mapping is most of what you need: <code>Stage</code> is the window, <code>Scene</code> is the contents, and everything inside is a <code>Node</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.stage.Stage;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.control.Button;
import javafx.scene.layout.StackPane;

public class Demo extends Application {
    @Override
    public void start(Stage stage) {           // NOT main — the entry point is start
        Button b = new Button("Click me");
        StackPane root = new StackPane(b);

        stage.setScene(new Scene(root, 300, 200));
        stage.setTitle("Demo");
        stage.show();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) { launch(args); }
}</pre><p>Swing to JavaFX: <code>JFrame</code> becomes <code>Stage</code>, <code>JPanel</code> becomes a layout pane, <code>JButton</code> becomes <code>Button</code>, and <code>setVisible(true)</code> becomes <code>stage.show()</code>. The structure is identical; only the names changed.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public class App extends Application {          // 1. extend Application
    @Override
    public void start(Stage stage) {            // 2. build the UI HERE, not in main
        Label l = new Label("Hello");
        Scene scene = new Scene(l, 300, 200);   // 3. scene holds the node graph
        stage.setScene(scene);
        stage.show();                           // 4. show it last
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) { launch(args); }
}</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">JavaFX</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Swing equivalent</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Stage</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JFrame</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the window</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Scene</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the content pane</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the container for one node graph</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>Node</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>JComponent</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">anything displayable — a control, a shape, a layout</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The nesting is fixed and asked directly: a <strong>Stage</strong> holds a <strong>Scene</strong>, a Scene holds a root <strong>Node</strong>, and nodes hold children. Miss a level and nothing appears.</p><p>The UI is built in <code>start</code>, not <code>main</code>. <code>main</code> only calls <code>launch</code>, which starts the JavaFX runtime and then calls <code>start</code> on the JavaFX application thread — the same event-thread rule as Swing.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Application:</strong> the class a JavaFX program extends; start() replaces main as the entry point</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Stage:</strong> the window</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Scene:</strong> the content attached to a stage, with a root node and a size</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Node:</strong> anything placed in a scene — controls, shapes, layout panes</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>launch(args):</strong> starts the JavaFX runtime, which then calls start()</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Scene graph is the term for the tree of nodes. It is the same containment hierarchy as 15.2, named differently.</p></div><i data-d286fx26="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — JAVAFX: STAGE, SCENE, THEN THE SCENE GRAPH</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 500 244" style="width:100%; max-width:540px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk93a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="40" y="16" width="300" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="190" y="32" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Stage</text><text x="190" y="46" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the window itself</text><text x="360" y="40" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">stage.show()</text><line x1="190" y1="54" x2="190" y2="66" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk93a)"/><rect x="64" y="68" width="252" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="190" y="84" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Scene</text><text x="190" y="98" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">contents of the window</text><text x="360" y="92" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new Scene(root)</text><line x1="190" y1="106" x2="190" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk93a)"/><rect x="88" y="120" width="204" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="190" y="136" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">layout pane</text><text x="190" y="150" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">arranges children (GridPane, VBox...)</text><text x="360" y="144" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">root</text><line x1="190" y1="158" x2="190" y2="170" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk93a)"/><rect x="112" y="172" width="156" height="36" rx="8" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="190" y="188" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">controls</text><text x="190" y="202" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Button, Label, TextField</text><text x="360" y="196" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">leaf nodes</text><text x="250" y="236" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">JavaFX nests Stage → Scene → root pane → controls. The scene graph is this tree.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS161="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the smallest complete JavaFX program, line by line</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.control.Button;
import javafx.scene.layout.StackPane;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

public class Demo extends Application {          // 1. extend Application

    @Override
    public void start(Stage stage) {             // 2. the real entry point
        Button b = new Button("Click me");       // 3. nodes
        StackPane root = new StackPane(b);       // 4. a root node
        Scene scene = new Scene(root, 300, 200); // 5. scene wraps the root
        stage.setScene(scene);                   // 6. stage wears the scene
        stage.setTitle("Demo");
        stage.show();                            // 7. show it — last
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) { launch(args); }
}</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>extends Application</code> is what makes this a JavaFX program; <code>start</code> is abstract there, so the compiler forces you to write it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>main</code> does one thing: <code>launch(args)</code>. That starts the JavaFX runtime, which creates the primary <code>Stage</code> and calls <code>start</code> on the JavaFX application thread.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Building the interface in <code>main</code> instead does not work — the toolkit is not running yet, and touching nodes off the FX thread is exactly the rule Swing has for its event thread.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The nesting is fixed: <code>Stage</code> → <code>Scene</code> → root node → children. Forget the scene and the window opens empty.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>new Scene(root, 300, 200)</code> sets the initial size in the same call. <code>stage.show()</code> is the JavaFX spelling of <code>setVisible(true)</code>, and it goes last for the same reason.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the same window in Swing and in JavaFX, side by side</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// ---------- Swing ----------
JFrame frame = new JFrame("Demo");
frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
JPanel root = new JPanel();
JButton b = new JButton("Click me");
b.addActionListener(e -&gt; System.out.println("clicked"));   // ADDS a listener
root.add(b);
frame.add(root);
frame.setSize(300, 200);
frame.setVisible(true);

// ---------- JavaFX ----------
StackPane root2 = new StackPane();
Button b2 = new Button("Click me");
b2.setOnAction(e -&gt; System.out.println("clicked"));        // SETS the handler
root2.getChildren().add(b2);                               // not root2.add(b2)
stage.setScene(new Scene(root2, 300, 200));
stage.show();</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Swing</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">JavaFX</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>JFrame</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>Stage</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the window</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">content pane</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>Scene</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">JavaFX adds this extra level</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>JComponent</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>Node</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">anything displayable</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>JPanel</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a layout pane</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>VBox</code>, <code>HBox</code>, <code>GridPane</code>, <code>BorderPane</code>, <code>StackPane</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>panel.add(c)</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>pane.getChildren().add(c)</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">children are a live list</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>addActionListener</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>setOnAction</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">adds versus replaces</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>setVisible(true)</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>stage.show()</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">last line either way</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The structure is identical; the vocabulary is not. Most exam questions in this unit are that mapping.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getChildren()</code> returns a real <code>ObservableList</code>, so <code>addAll</code>, <code>remove</code> and <code>clear</code> all work on it — and the display updates as the list changes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setOnAction</code> takes an <code>EventHandler&lt;ActionEvent&gt;</code>, a one-method interface, which is why a lambda fits.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">JavaFX has no <code>EXIT_ON_CLOSE</code> to forget: closing the last stage ends the application by default.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Building the interface in <code>main</code> is the mistake this structure invites, because every other Java program starts there. In JavaFX <code>main</code> only calls <code>launch</code>, which returns after the application <em>exits</em>; anything written after it runs at shutdown, not at startup. The related slip is <code>pane.add(node)</code> — layout panes have no such method; children go through <code>pane.getChildren().add(node)</code>, except on <code>BorderPane</code>, where they go through <code>setTop</code>, <code>setCenter</code> and friends.</p></div>"},"D286/u16/z16_2":{"body":"<p><code>GridPane</code> is the JavaFX equivalent of <code>GridBagLayout</code> (15.3), and it is considerably simpler: position is passed straight to <code>add</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">GridPane grid = new GridPane();
grid.setHgap(10);
grid.setVgap(10);
grid.setPadding(new Insets(20));

grid.add(new Label("Name:"),   0, 0);      // node, COLUMN, ROW
grid.add(new TextField(),      1, 0);
grid.add(new Button("Submit"), 1, 1);
grid.add(new Label("Wide"),    0, 2, 2, 1); // plus colSpan, rowSpan</pre><p>The argument order is the trap: column first, then row — the opposite of the row-then-column convention used for 2D arrays in 5.9. Getting it backwards produces a layout that is transposed rather than broken, which makes it harder to spot.</p><h3>The other JavaFX layout panes</h3><p><code>GridPane</code> is one of several, and the JavaFX names map onto the Swing ones from 15.9.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">JavaFX pane</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Arranges</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Swing equivalent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>BorderPane</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">top, bottom, left, right, center</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">BorderLayout</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>FlowPane</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">left to right, wrapping</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">FlowLayout</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>GridPane</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">rows and columns</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">GridBagLayout</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>VBox / HBox</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a single column / a single row</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">—</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>StackPane</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">children stacked on top of each other, centred</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><code>BorderPane</code> is the usual root for an application window: a menu bar in <code>setTop</code>, content in <code>setCenter</code>, a status bar in <code>setBottom</code>. As with BorderLayout, only the centre region grows.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">GridPane g = new GridPane();
g.setHgap(10); g.setVgap(10);
g.add(node, 1, 0);                    // COLUMN first, then ROW
g.add(wide, 0, 2, 3, 1);              // col, row, colSpan, rowSpan</pre><p><code>add(node, col, row)</code> takes the <strong>column before the row</strong> — the opposite of the (row, column) order used for 2D arrays in 5.9, and the single most common JavaFX slip.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Pane</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Arranges</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>GridPane</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a grid, with spanning</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>VBox</code> / <code>HBox</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a vertical / horizontal stack — spacing via the constructor</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>BorderPane</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">top, bottom, left, right, center</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><code>StackPane</code></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">children on top of one another, centred</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Adding two nodes to the same grid cell overlays them rather than replacing — the opposite of Swing's <code>BorderLayout</code>, where the second add replaces the first. <code>StackPane</code> makes that overlay deliberate.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>GridPane:</strong> a layout arranging nodes in rows and columns</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>add(node, col, row):</strong> column comes first, unlike array indexing</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Hgap / Vgap:</strong> spacing between columns and rows</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>VBox / HBox:</strong> simpler panes stacking nodes vertically or horizontally</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>For a simple form, <code>VBox</code> and <code>HBox</code> nested inside each other are often clearer than a grid, and they need no coordinates at all.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS162="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — COLUMN-THEN-ROW: THE ONE ARGUMENT ORDER TO MEMORISE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 316" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk285a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the same cell, addressed two different ways</text><rect x="120" y="44" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="216" y="44" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="312" y="44" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="408" y="44" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="106" y="71" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">row 0</text><rect x="120" y="88" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="216" y="88" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="312" y="88" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.22)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="408" y="88" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="106" y="115" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">row 1</text><rect x="120" y="132" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="216" y="132" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="312" y="132" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="408" y="132" width="96" height="44" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="106" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">row 2</text><text x="168" y="36" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">col 0</text><text x="264" y="36" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">col 1</text><text x="360" y="36" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">col 2</text><text x="456" y="36" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">col 3</text><text x="360" y="108" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">this cell</text><text x="360" y="124" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">col 2, row 1</text><rect x="40" y="190" width="280" height="88" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.09" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="180" y="212" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JavaFX GridPane</text><text x="180" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">grid.add(node, 2, 1)</text><text x="180" y="258" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">COLUMN first, then row</text><rect x="348" y="190" width="280" height="88" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.09" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="488" y="212" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Java 2D arrays / JTable</text><text x="488" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a[1][2]   getValueAt(1, 2)</text><text x="488" y="258" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ROW first, then column</text><text x="332" y="300" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">get it backwards and the layout is transposed, not broken — which is harder to spot</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a login form on a grid, with a spanning row</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">GridPane grid = new GridPane();
grid.setHgap(10);                       // space BETWEEN columns
grid.setVgap(10);                       // space BETWEEN rows
grid.setPadding(new Insets(20));        // space AROUND the whole grid

Label userLabel = new Label("User:");
TextField userField = new TextField();
Label passLabel = new Label("Password:");
PasswordField passField = new PasswordField();
Button submit = new Button("Sign in");
Label message = new Label();

grid.add(userLabel,  0, 0);             // column 0, row 0
grid.add(userField,  1, 0);             // column 1, row 0 — same row
grid.add(passLabel,  0, 1);
grid.add(passField,  1, 1);
grid.add(submit,     1, 2);
grid.add(message,    0, 3, 2, 1);       // col, row, colSpan, rowSpan</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Rows and columns are created implicitly by the coordinates you use — there is no "new GridPane(4, 2)".</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The label and the field share row 0 because only the first argument differs. That is the shape of every form built this way.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Hgap</code>/<code>Vgap</code> separate cells from one another; <code>padding</code> insets the whole grid from the edge of its parent. Both at 0 is why an untuned grid looks cramped.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The four-argument <code>add</code> extends the two-argument one: column, row, then how many columns and rows to span. <code>message</code> occupies both columns of row 3.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>PasswordField</code> is a real control and behaves like a <code>TextField</code> with masked display; <code>getText()</code> still returns what was typed.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — when a grid is the wrong tool</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Half the layouts that look like grids are simpler as nested boxes, which need no coordinates at all.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// a vertical stack of controls
VBox column = new VBox(10);                 // 10px spacing between children
column.getChildren().addAll(new Label("Filter:"), field, applyButton);

// a row of buttons
HBox buttons = new HBox(8);
buttons.getChildren().addAll(okButton, cancelButton);

// the usual application shell
BorderPane shell = new BorderPane();
shell.setTop(menuBar);
shell.setCenter(table);                      // only the CENTRE grows
shell.setBottom(statusLabel);
shell.setLeft(column);

// deliberate overlay: a label on top of an image
StackPane badge = new StackPane();
badge.getChildren().addAll(imageView, captionLabel);   // later = on top</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>VBox</code>/<code>HBox</code> spacing is a constructor argument, and nesting one inside the other covers most real forms.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>BorderPane</code> is the JavaFX <code>BorderLayout</code>: five regions, set by name, and only the centre absorbs extra space.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Each <code>BorderPane</code> region holds exactly one node, so a region with several controls gets a <code>VBox</code> or <code>HBox</code> in it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>StackPane</code> layers its children in list order — the last added is drawn on top — which is what makes overlays deliberate rather than accidental.</li></ol></div>"},"D286/u16/z16_3":{"body":"<p>JavaFX event handling is the listener pattern of 15.4 with a lambda-friendly interface, and the handler still runs when the user acts rather than in sequence.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Button b = new Button("Add");
TextField field = new TextField();
Label result = new Label();

b.setOnAction(e -&gt; {
    try {
        int n = Integer.parseInt(field.getText());
        result.setText("doubled: " + (n * 2));
    } catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
        result.setText("enter a number");
    }
});

// reacting to every keystroke, not just to the button
field.textProperty().addListener((obs, oldVal, newVal) -&gt; {
    result.setText("typing: " + newVal);
});</pre><p>The second form is what JavaFX adds over Swing: <strong>properties</strong> that can be observed or bound, so a label can track a field without any handler at all. <code>result.textProperty().bind(field.textProperty())</code> keeps them in step permanently.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">button.setOnAction(e -&gt; label.setText("clicked"));    // SETS the handler
button.setOnAction(e -&gt; other());                     // REPLACES the first one

String choice = combo.getValue();                     // typed, not a String cast
field.clear();

slider.valueProperty().addListener((obs, oldV, newV) -&gt; label.setText("" + newV));
label.textProperty().bind(field.textProperty());      // binding — no listener at all</pre><p><code>setOnAction</code> <em>sets</em> rather than adds, so a second call replaces the first — unlike Swing's <code>addActionListener</code>, which stacks them. A handler that has silently stopped working is usually a second <code>setOnAction</code> further down.</p><p><strong>Properties</strong> are JavaFX's addition. A property can be observed with a listener, or <strong>bound</strong> so one value follows another automatically. A bound property cannot then be set directly — the binding owns it, and assigning throws.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>setOnAction:</strong> registers the handler for the control’s primary action</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Lambda handler:</strong> e -&gt; { ... }, the standard form for a single-method interface</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Property:</strong> an observable value that can notify listeners or be bound to another</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Binding:</strong> declaring that one property always follows another</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Text still arrives as a String, so <code>Integer.parseInt</code> and <code>NumberFormatException</code> apply exactly as in 15.4. Nothing about JavaFX makes input validation unnecessary.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS163="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — HANDLERS, LISTENERS AND BINDINGS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 314" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk286a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">set versus add: the difference that loses a handler</text><rect x="24" y="40" width="292" height="122" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.08" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="170" y="62" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Swing — addActionListener</text><rect x="60" y="76" width="220" height="26" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="170" y="93" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">handler A</text><rect x="60" y="108" width="220" height="26" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="170" y="125" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">handler B</text><text x="170" y="150" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">both run, in the order added</text><rect x="348" y="40" width="292" height="122" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.08" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="494" y="62" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">JavaFX — setOnAction</text><rect x="384" y="76" width="220" height="26" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="494" y="93" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">handler A</text><line x1="384" y1="89" x2="604" y2="89" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="384" y="108" width="220" height="26" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="494" y="125" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">handler B</text><text x="494" y="150" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the second call REPLACES the first</text><text x="332" y="196" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a property can be watched, or wired directly to another</text><rect x="70" y="216" width="150" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="145" y="237.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">TextField</text><line x1="220" y1="233" x2="292" y2="233" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk286a)"/><text x="256" y="224" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">addListener</text><rect x="292" y="214" width="150" height="38" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="367" y="230" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your lambda</text><text x="367" y="247" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">runs on each change</text><line x1="442" y1="233" x2="512" y2="233" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk286a)"/><rect x="512" y="216" width="120" height="34" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="572" y="237.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Label</text><path d="M140,274 Q356,230 572,274" fill="none" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk286a)"/><text x="356" y="246.72" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">bind() — no handler at all, the value simply follows</text><text x="332" y="300" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">a bound property cannot then be set by hand: assigning to it throws</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a button, a field, and the failure path</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">TextField field = new TextField();
Label result = new Label();
Button doubleIt = new Button("Double");

doubleIt.setOnAction(e -&gt; {
    String text = field.getText().trim();
    if (text.isEmpty()) { result.setText("Enter a number"); return; }
    try {
        int n = Integer.parseInt(text);
        result.setText("doubled: " + (n * 2));
    } catch (NumberFormatException ex) {
        result.setText("\"" + text + "\" is not a whole number");
    }
    field.clear();
});</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setOnAction</code> takes the handler for the control’s primary action — a click for a button, Enter for a text field.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The body is identical to the Swing version in 15.4. Nothing about JavaFX makes parsing safe: <code>getText()</code> still returns a <code>String</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>field.clear()</code> is JavaFX’s <code>setText("")</code>, and it runs on both paths because it sits after the try-catch.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A second <code>setOnAction</code> on the same button anywhere later in the file would silently delete this handler.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — watching a property, and binding one to another</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// (a) a listener: react to every change, with old and new values
field.textProperty().addListener((obs, oldText, newText) -&gt; {
    charCount.setText(newText.length() + " characters");
});

slider.valueProperty().addListener((obs, oldV, newV) -&gt; {
    // newV is a Number, not a double
    readout.setText(String.format("%.0f%%", newV.doubleValue()));
});

// (b) a binding: no handler at all
mirror.textProperty().bind(field.textProperty());   // mirror always follows
mirror.textProperty().unbind();                     // release it before setting</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A property listener receives three things: the property itself, the previous value and the new one. Most handlers use only the third.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">It fires on every keystroke, which is what makes live character counts and search-as-you-type possible — and what makes expensive work in one a bad idea.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>valueProperty()</code> on a slider hands you a <code>Number</code>, so <code>doubleValue()</code> or <code>intValue()</code> is needed before arithmetic.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>bind</code> replaces the listener entirely: the target property is now computed from the source and updates itself.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A bound property is read-only from then on — calling <code>mirror.setText(...)</code> throws until <code>unbind()</code> is called. That exception is the exam-worthy detail.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The three facts this section is tested on: <code>setOnAction</code> <em>replaces</em> (Swing’s <code>addActionListener</code> <em>adds</em>); a lambda works because <code>EventHandler</code> has a single method; and a bound property cannot be set directly. When a question shows two <code>setOnAction</code> calls on one button, the answer is that only the second one ever runs. Also watch the types: <code>ComboBox.getValue()</code> is generic and needs no cast, unlike Swing’s <code>getSelectedItem()</code>, which returns <code>Object</code>.</p></div>"},"D286/u16/z16_4":{"body":"<p>JavaFX graphics differ from Swing in a fundamental way: instead of overriding a paint method, you either add shape <em>objects</em> to the scene graph or draw onto a <code>Canvas</code>.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// shapes are nodes — they persist and can be changed later
Circle c = new Circle(50, 50, 30);
c.setFill(Color.BLUE);
Rectangle r = new Rectangle(10, 10, 100, 40);
Pane root = new Pane(c, r);

c.setCenterX(80);          // moves it — no repaint call needed

// canvas — immediate-mode drawing, closer to Swing
Canvas canvas = new Canvas(300, 200);
GraphicsContext g = canvas.getGraphicsContext2D();
g.setFill(Color.RED);
g.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);
g.strokeOval(70, 10, 50, 50);</pre><p>With shape nodes there is no <code>repaint()</code> (15.1) — changing a property redraws automatically, because the shape is a live object rather than a set of drawing instructions. The coordinate system is unchanged: origin top left, y increasing downward.</p><h3>Fill versus stroke</h3><p>JavaFX separates the inside of a shape from its outline, and the two are set independently.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">circle.setFill(Color.BLUE);          // the interior colour
circle.setStroke(Color.BLACK);       // the OUTLINE colour
circle.setStrokeWidth(3);            // outline thickness

g.setFill(...)   / g.fillRect(...)   // Canvas: filled shape
g.setStroke(...) / g.strokeRect(...) // Canvas: outline only</pre><p>So <em>fill</em> is the interior and <em>stroke</em> is the outline. The same pair of words applies to shape nodes and to <code>GraphicsContext</code> drawing, and setting only the fill leaves a shape with no visible border.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Circle c = new Circle(50, 50, 20);          // centreX, centreY, RADIUS
Rectangle r = new Rectangle(10, 10, 100, 40);  // x, y, width, height
c.setFill(Color.BLUE);                         // the interior
c.setStroke(Color.BLACK);                      // the outline
pane.getChildren().add(c);

c.setCenterX(120);                             // moves it — no repaint call needed</pre><p>This is <strong>retained mode</strong>: shapes are objects in the scene graph, and changing a property redraws automatically. Swing is <em>immediate</em> mode — you redraw everything inside <code>paintComponent</code> and call <code>repaint()</code> yourself. That difference is the point of the comparison.</p><p>A <code>Circle</code> takes a <strong>radius</strong> while Swing's <code>fillOval</code> takes a bounding box, so the same circle is <code>new Circle(50, 50, 20)</code> here and <code>fillOval(30, 30, 40, 40)</code> there. The origin is still top-left with y increasing downwards.</p><p>Because shapes persist, animation is a <code>Timeline</code> or <code>TranslateTransition</code> changing a property over time rather than a redraw loop.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Shape node:</strong> a Circle, Rectangle or Line that lives in the scene graph and can be modified</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Canvas:</strong> a surface for immediate drawing via a GraphicsContext</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>GraphicsContext:</strong> the JavaFX equivalent of Swing’s Graphics object</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Retained vs immediate mode:</strong> objects the toolkit redraws for you, versus instructions you issue once</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Use shape nodes when things move or change — the toolkit handles redrawing. Use a Canvas for many static elements, where thousands of nodes would be wasteful.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS164="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — RETAINED MODE VERSUS IMMEDIATE MODE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 664 288" style="width:100%; max-width:664px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk287a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="332" y="22" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">two ways to draw, and only one of them remembers what it drew</text><rect x="24" y="40" width="292" height="236" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.08" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="170" y="62" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">RETAINED — shape nodes</text><rect x="58" y="76" width="224" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="170" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Pane (scene graph)</text><line x1="112" y1="106" x2="96" y2="126" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk287a)"/><line x1="228" y1="106" x2="244" y2="126" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk287a)"/><rect x="46" y="126" width="100" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="96" y="145" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Circle</text><rect x="194" y="126" width="100" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="244" y="145" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Rectangle</text><text x="170" y="178" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">c.setCenterX(120)</text><line x1="170" y1="186" x2="170" y2="206" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk287a)"/><text x="170" y="222" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the toolkit redraws it</text><text x="170" y="240" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no repaint() exists, and none is needed</text><text x="170" y="262" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">each shape is a live object you can keep</text><rect x="348" y="40" width="292" height="236" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.08" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="494" y="62" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">IMMEDIATE — Canvas</text><rect x="382" y="76" width="224" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="494" y="95" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Canvas → GraphicsContext</text><line x1="494" y1="106" x2="494" y2="124" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk287a)"/><rect x="382" y="124" width="224" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="494" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">g.fillOval(…)  g.strokeRect(…)</text><text x="494" y="178" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the pixels are painted, then forgotten</text><text x="494" y="200" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">to move the circle you must clear</text><text x="494" y="216" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">and draw the whole scene again</text><text x="494" y="240" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">this is how Swing always works (15.1)</text><text x="494" y="262" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">right choice for thousands of static marks</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — shapes as objects: fill, stroke, and moving one later</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Circle c = new Circle(50, 50, 20);          // centreX, centreY, RADIUS
c.setFill(Color.CORNFLOWERBLUE);            // the interior
c.setStroke(Color.BLACK);                   // the outline
c.setStrokeWidth(3);

Rectangle r = new Rectangle(10, 90, 100, 40);   // x, y, WIDTH, HEIGHT
r.setFill(Color.TRANSPARENT);               // outline only
r.setStroke(Color.web("#e8795a"));

Line axis = new Line(0, 140, 200, 140);

Pane root = new Pane();
root.getChildren().addAll(c, r, axis);

c.setCenterX(120);                          // moves it — nothing else to call
c.setRadius(28);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A <code>Circle</code> takes a <em>radius</em>; Swing’s <code>fillOval</code> takes a bounding box. The same circle is <code>new Circle(50, 50, 20)</code> here and <code>fillOval(30, 30, 40, 40)</code> there — a genuine translation question.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Rectangle</code> keeps the position-then-size convention, so only the circle differs.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Fill is the inside, stroke is the outline. A shape with a fill and no stroke has no border; <code>Color.TRANSPARENT</code> plus a stroke gives an outline-only shape.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Pane</code> is the layout pane that leaves children exactly where their coordinates put them — a <code>VBox</code> or <code>StackPane</code> would reposition them and the coordinates would be ignored.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The last two lines run at any time afterwards and the display updates. That is retained mode: the shape is an object, not a past drawing instruction.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the same picture on a Canvas</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Canvas canvas = new Canvas(300, 200);
GraphicsContext g = canvas.getGraphicsContext2D();

g.setFill(Color.CORNFLOWERBLUE);
g.fillOval(30, 30, 40, 40);          // x, y, w, h — a bounding box again
g.setStroke(Color.BLACK);
g.setLineWidth(3);
g.strokeOval(30, 30, 40, 40);        // outline over the fill
g.strokeRect(10, 90, 100, 40);
g.strokeLine(0, 140, 200, 140);
g.fillText("sample", 10, 170);

// to move the circle: erase and redraw everything
g.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.getWidth(), canvas.getHeight());
g.fillOval(100, 30, 40, 40);</pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>getGraphicsContext2D()</code> is the JavaFX equivalent of Swing’s <code>Graphics</code> object, and the method names line up: <code>fill…</code> for solid, <code>stroke…</code> for outline.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">On a canvas <code>fillOval</code> uses a bounding box, so the same circle is written differently from the <code>Circle</code> node above — the distinction worth carrying out of this section.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>setFill</code> and <code>setStroke</code> are sticky state: they apply to every later call until changed, unlike a shape node where each object carries its own.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">There is no way to move something already drawn. <code>clearRect</code> over the whole canvas and redraw is the only option — the smearing problem of 15.1, in JavaFX.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A canvas is one node no matter how much is painted on it, which is why thousands of marks belong here and a handful of moving shapes belong in the scene graph.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Reaching for <code>repaint()</code> is the reflex Swing leaves behind, and no such method exists on a JavaFX node — changing a property is the redraw. The mirror-image mistake is expecting a <code>Canvas</code> to work the same way: setting a variable does nothing there, because the pixels have no memory of which call produced them. And watch the circle constructors: <code>new Circle(50, 50, 20)</code> is centre-and-radius, while <code>g.fillOval(50, 50, 20, 20)</code> is a corner and a size, so copying coordinates between the two forms draws the circle in the wrong place at half the size.</p></div>"},"D286/u17/z17_1":{"body":"<p>This unit is the first that asks not "does it work" but "how long does it take". Two search algorithms make the contrast, and the difference between them is the whole point of the unit.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// LINEAR SEARCH — works on ANY array
for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] == target) { return i; }
}
return -1;
// worst case: n comparisons  -&gt;  O(n)

// BINARY SEARCH — requires a SORTED array
// worst case: log2(n) comparisons  -&gt;  O(log n)</pre><p>On a million elements, linear search takes up to a million comparisons and binary search takes about twenty. That gap is why sorting is worth doing even when you only want to search: sort once, then search cheaply for ever after.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int linearSearch(int[] a, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++)
        if (a[i] == target) return i;
    return -1;                                   // not found
}</pre><p>Linear search checks every element until it matches. Best case 1 comparison, worst case <em>n</em>, average about <em>n</em>/2 — all O(n). Its one advantage is that it needs <strong>no precondition</strong>: unsorted data, linked lists, anything traversable.</p><p>Return −1 for "not found", never 0, because 0 is a valid index. For objects use <code>.equals</code> rather than <code>==</code>, or you are comparing references and will miss equal-but-distinct objects.</p><p>On a linked list linear search is still O(n) and is the <em>only</em> option — binary search needs indexed access, which a linked list cannot give in constant time.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Linear search:</strong> checking each element in turn; O(n); no precondition</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Binary search:</strong> halving a sorted range; O(log n); requires sorted data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Best / average / worst case:</strong> the fewest, typical and most operations for an input of size n</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Precondition:</strong> what must be true for an algorithm to be correct — sortedness, here</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Every collection choice in Unit 14 was really this question. <code>HashSet.contains</code> is O(1) and <code>ArrayList.contains</code> is O(n), which is the same trade in a different costume.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS171="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — LINEAR SEARCH: ONE ELEMENT AT A TIME UNTIL IT MATCHES</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 262" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk288a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="20" y="18" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">target = 1475  —  the array is NOT sorted, and linear search does not care</text><rect x="20" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="58" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1840</text><text x="58" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">0</text><rect x="96" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="134" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">920</text><text x="134" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="172" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="210" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2310</text><text x="210" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="248" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.22)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="286" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1475</text><text x="286" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="324" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="362" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">880</text><text x="362" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">4</text><rect x="400" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="438" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2050</text><text x="438" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">5</text><rect x="476" y="28" width="76" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="514" y="50.5" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1310</text><text x="514" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">6</text><line x1="58" y1="88" x2="58" y2="104" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk288a)"/><circle cx="58" cy="120" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="58" y="124" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line x1="134" y1="88" x2="134" y2="104" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk288a)"/><circle cx="134" cy="120" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="134" y="124" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line x1="210" y1="88" x2="210" y2="104" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk288a)"/><circle cx="210" cy="120" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="210" y="124" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line x1="286" y1="88" x2="286" y2="104" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk288a)"/><circle cx="286" cy="120" r="11" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="286" y="124" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="286" y="146" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">↑ match — stop here</text><text x="600" y="46" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">return 3</text><text x="600" y="62" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">4 comparisons</text><line x1="20" y1="160" x2="660" y2="160" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><rect x="20" y="174" width="200" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="120" y="197" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">best case: 1</text><text x="120" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the match is at index 0 — O(1)</text><rect x="240" y="174" width="200" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="340" y="197" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">average: n / 2</text><text x="340" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">about half the array — O(n)</text><rect x="460" y="174" width="200" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="560" y="197" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">worst case: n</text><text x="560" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">absent, or last — return −1</text><text x="340" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The only search that works on unsorted data, on a linked list, on anything you can walk.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting the comparisons on real data</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A depot has seven trucks and the loading system searches by weight. The counter is only there so the cost is visible; the search itself is the four lines you already know.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int comparisons;

static int linearSearch(int[] a, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        comparisons++;
        if (a[i] == target) return i;      // leaves the moment it matches
    }
    return -1;                             // fell off the end: not present
}

int[] weights = { 1840, 920, 2310, 1475, 880, 2050, 1310 };

comparisons = 0;
System.out.println("1475 found at index " + linearSearch(weights, 1475)
                   + " after " + comparisons + " comparisons");
comparisons = 0;
System.out.println("1500 found at index " + linearSearch(weights, 1500)
                   + " after " + comparisons + " comparisons");
comparisons = 0;
linearSearch(weights, 1840);
System.out.println("best case (first element): " + comparisons + " comparison");</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">1475 found at index 3 after 4 comparisons
1500 found at index -1 after 7 comparisons
best case (first element): 1 comparison</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">1475 sits at index 3, so the loop tested indices 0, 1, 2, 3 — <strong>four</strong> comparisons, one more than the index. Off-by-one answers on this are almost always that confusion.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The miss costs <strong>seven</strong>: every element had to be ruled out before <code>-1</code> could be returned. A failed linear search is always the worst case.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The best case is one comparison, and it is pure luck — it depends on where the value happens to sit, not on the algorithm.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">All three are O(<em>n</em>). Big-O reports the <em>worst</em> case unless the question says otherwise, and 7, 4 and 1 all come from the same single loop over <em>n</em> elements.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — searching objects — where == quietly fails</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Everything above used <code>int</code>, where <code>==</code> compares values. The moment the elements are objects, <code>==</code> compares <em>references</em>, and a value typed in by a user is a different object from the identical value in the array.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">List&lt;String&gt; codes = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of("D286", "C959", "D287", "D426"));
System.out.println("indexOf(\"D287\") = " + codes.indexOf("D287"));
System.out.println("indexOf(\"D999\") = " + codes.indexOf("D999"));
System.out.println("contains(\"C959\") = " + codes.contains("C959"));

String typed = new String("D287");        // what a Scanner would hand you
System.out.println("typed == codes.get(2) : " + (typed == codes.get(2)));
System.out.println("typed.equals(codes.get(2)) : " + typed.equals(codes.get(2)));

String[] arr = { "D286", "C959", "D287" };
int found = -1;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++) if (arr[i] == typed) { found = i; break; }
System.out.println("hand-written search using == : " + found);
found = -1;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++) if (arr[i].equals(typed)) { found = i; break; }
System.out.println("hand-written search using equals : " + found);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">indexOf("D287") = 2
indexOf("D999") = -1
contains("C959") = true
typed == codes.get(2) : false
typed.equals(codes.get(2)) : true
hand-written search using == : -1
hand-written search using equals : 2</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>indexOf</code> returns the index or <code>-1</code> — the same contract as the loop you would write by hand, which is why it is the right call in real code.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>typed == codes.get(2)</code> is <code>false</code> even though both hold "D287": <code>new String(...)</code> forces a separate object, exactly as reading from a <code>Scanner</code> does.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The two hand-written searches differ in one operator and return <code>-1</code> and <code>2</code>. The <code>==</code> version is not slower or less elegant — it is <em>wrong</em>, and it is wrong only for input that did not come from a literal.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>indexOf</code> and <code>contains</code> use <code>equals</code> internally, which is why they found it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two errors here cost marks reliably. First, returning <code>0</code> for "not found" — 0 is a perfectly good index, so the caller cannot tell a hit at the front from a miss; the convention is <code>-1</code>, and the caller must test <code>if (idx >= 0)</code> rather than <code>if (idx > 0)</code>, which silently rejects a match at index 0. Second, writing <code>if (arr[i] == target)</code> for objects: it compiles, it passes the test where the target is a literal, and it fails the moment the target is typed in.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The question is almost always "how many comparisons" or "which search can you use here". For comparisons: a hit at index <em>k</em> costs <em>k</em>+1, a miss costs <em>n</em>. For applicability: linear search needs nothing at all, binary search needs a <strong>sorted, index-addressable</strong> structure — so on a <code>LinkedList</code>, or on any unsorted array, linear search is not merely an option, it is the only one.</p></div>"},"D286/u17/z17_2":{"body":"<p>Binary search is worth being able to write and to trace, because it is on the exam in both forms. 11.3 gave the recursive version; this is the iterative one.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static int bsearch(int[] a, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;

    while (lo &lt;= hi) {                      // &lt;= not &lt; : a single element must be checked
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;       // avoids overflow (2.16)

        if (a[mid] == target) { return mid; }
        if (a[mid] &lt;  target) { lo = mid + 1; }
        else                  { hi = mid - 1; }
    }
    return -1;
}</pre><p>Two details carry the correctness. <code>lo &lt;= hi</code> rather than <code>&lt;</code>, or a one-element range is never examined; and <code>mid + 1</code> / <code>mid - 1</code> rather than <code>mid</code>, or the range stops shrinking and the loop never ends.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int binarySearch(int[] a, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
    while (lo &lt;= hi) {                       // &lt;= , not &lt;
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;        // overflow-safe
        if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (a[mid] &lt; target) lo = mid + 1;   // +1 / -1 guarantee progress
        else                 hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}</pre><p>Three details carry the marks. <code>lo &lt;= hi</code> rather than <code>&lt;</code>, or a one-element range is never examined. <code>mid + 1</code> and <code>mid - 1</code>, or the range stops shrinking and the loop hangs. And <code>lo + (hi - lo) / 2</code> rather than <code>(lo + hi) / 2</code>, which overflows <code>int</code> for very large arrays.</p><p>Halving gives O(log n): 1,000 elements in about 10 comparisons, a million in about 20, a billion in about 30. Each doubling of the data adds <em>one</em> comparison.</p><p>The precondition is that the array is <strong>sorted</strong>, and violating it fails silently — the search simply misses values that are present.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Search space:</strong> the lo..hi range still under consideration; halves each iteration</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Midpoint calculation:</strong> lo + (hi - lo) / 2 rather than (lo + hi) / 2, to avoid overflow</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Loop invariant:</strong> if the target is present it is always within lo..hi</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Termination:</strong> the range shrinks every pass, so the loop must end</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>To trace one, write lo, mid and hi in three columns and add a row per pass. A 16-element array takes at most 5 rows, so the whole trace fits in the margin.</p></div><i data-d286fx27="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — BINARY SEARCH HALVES THE FIELD EVERY PROBE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 178" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk94a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="30" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">looking for 31 — array MUST already be sorted</text><rect x="30" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="57" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><rect x="84" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="111" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="138" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="165" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12</text><rect x="192" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="#6366f144" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="219" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">19</text><rect x="246" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="273" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">24</text><rect x="300" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="327" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">31</text><rect x="354" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="381" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40</text><rect x="408" y="40" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="435" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">52</text><text x="219" y="34" fill="#6366f1" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">mid</text><text x="478" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">19 < 31 → go right</text><rect x="30" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="57" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><rect x="84" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="111" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="138" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="165" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12</text><rect x="192" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="219" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="12" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">19</text><rect x="246" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="273" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">24</text><rect x="300" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="#6366f144" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="327" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">31</text><rect x="354" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="381" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">40</text><rect x="408" y="96" width="54" height="34" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="435" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">52</text><text x="327" y="90" fill="#6366f1" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">mid</text><text x="478" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">31 = 31 → FOUND</text><text x="300" y="156" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Each test throws away half of what remains: 8 → 4 → 2 → 1. That is why binary search is O(log n)</text><text x="300" y="170" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">while linear search is O(n) — for 1,000,000 items: ~20 probes vs up to 1,000,000.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS172="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — every probe of a real search, with lo, mid and hi written down</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Twelve stock levels, already sorted, and we want 47. The trace prints the three variables before each test so you can see the window closing.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[] stock = { 4, 9, 15, 22, 28, 33, 41, 47, 55, 62, 70, 78 };

int lo = 0, hi = stock.length - 1;
while (lo &lt;= hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    System.out.println("  lo=" + lo + " hi=" + hi + " mid=" + mid + " a[mid]=" + stock[mid]);
    if (stock[mid] == 47) { System.out.println("result index: " + mid); break; }
    if (stock[mid] &lt; 47) lo = mid + 1;
    else                 hi = mid - 1;
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">  lo=0 hi=11 mid=5 a[mid]=33
  lo=6 hi=11 mid=8 a[mid]=55
  lo=6 hi=7 mid=6 a[mid]=41
  lo=7 hi=7 mid=7 a[mid]=47
result index: 7</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Probe 1: <code>mid = 0 + (11−0)/2 = 5</code>. Integer division truncates, so a window of twelve picks the lower middle. 33 &lt; 47, so everything from index 0 to 5 is gone — <code>lo = 6</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Probe 2: <code>mid = 6 + (11−6)/2 = 6 + 2 = 8</code>. 55 &gt; 47, so the top is gone — <code>hi = 7</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Probe 3: <code>mid = 6 + (7−6)/2 = 6</code>. 41 &lt; 47, so <code>lo = 7</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Probe 4: <code>lo == hi == 7</code>, a one-element window. This is the probe that <code>lo &lt;= hi</code> buys you; with <code>&lt;</code> the loop would have exited here and reported "not found" on a value that is present.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Four probes for twelve elements. Linear search would have taken eight.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a value that is absent, and what the library returns instead of −1</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[] stock = { 4, 9, 15, 22, 28, 33, 41, 47, 55, 62, 70, 78 };

// hand-written version, searching for 50
// probes: mid=5 (33), mid=8 (55), mid=6 (41), mid=7 (47) -&gt; lo becomes 8, hi is 7
System.out.println("hand-written result: " + binarySearch(stock, 50));

System.out.println("present  47 -&gt; " + Arrays.binarySearch(stock, 47));
System.out.println("absent   50 -&gt; " + Arrays.binarySearch(stock, 50));
int r = Arrays.binarySearch(stock, 50);
System.out.println("insertion point = " + (-r - 1)
                   + "  (stock[" + (-r - 1) + "] = " + stock[-r - 1] + ")");

int[] messy = { 22, 4, 55, 15, 70, 9, 41 };      // NOT sorted
System.out.println("55 really is at index 2");
System.out.println("binarySearch says: " + binarySearch(messy, 55));
System.out.println("Arrays.binarySearch says: " + Arrays.binarySearch(messy, 55));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">hand-written result: -1
present  47 -&gt; 7
absent   50 -&gt; -9
insertion point = 8  (stock[8] = 55)
55 really is at index 2
binarySearch says: -1
Arrays.binarySearch says: -8</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The absent search runs the same four probes and then <code>lo</code> passes <code>hi</code>. The window is empty, the loop condition fails, and <code>-1</code> comes back.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Arrays.binarySearch</code> agrees on the hit (index 7) but reports a miss as <code>−(insertion point) − 1</code>. Here <code>-9</code> means "not found; it would belong at index 8".</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The formula to recover the insertion point is <code>-r - 1</code>: <code>-(-9) - 1 = 8</code>. That is where 50 would be inserted to keep the array sorted, which is genuinely useful and is why the method does not simply return <code>-1</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The last block is the section’s whole warning made concrete. 55 <em>is</em> in <code>messy</code>, at index 2, and both searches miss it — one says <code>-1</code>, the other says <code>-8</code>. Neither throws, neither complains. Unsorted input does not break binary search; it makes it lie.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The three ways to write this loop wrong all look reasonable. <code>while (lo &lt; hi)</code> skips the final one-element window, so values that happen to land there are reported missing. <code>lo = mid</code> instead of <code>lo = mid + 1</code> makes the window stop shrinking once it is two wide, and the program hangs rather than crashes — an infinite loop, not an exception. And forgetting to sort first is the one with no symptom at all: the search returns <code>-1</code> for a value sitting right there, and nothing in the output says why. If a binary search "sometimes works", check the sorting, not the search.</p></div>"},"D286/u17/z17_3":{"body":"<p>Big-O describes how the work grows as the input grows, ignoring constants and lower-order terms. It answers a question about scaling, not about seconds.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">O(1)         constant     — array access, HashMap.get
O(log n)     logarithmic  — binary search, TreeSet operations
O(n)         linear       — one pass over an array
O(n log n)   linearithmic — merge sort, quicksort average, Collections.sort
O(n^2)       quadratic    — selection sort, insertion sort, nested loops
O(2^n)       exponential  — naive recursive Fibonacci (11.6)

n = 1,000,000:  O(log n) ~ 20    O(n) = 1,000,000    O(n^2) = 10^12</pre><p>Constants are dropped because they do not change the shape of the growth: <code>3n + 100</code> is O(n), and so is <code>n/2</code>. The rule for reading code is to count nested loops — one loop over n is O(n), a loop inside a loop is O(n²), and halving each pass is O(log n).</p><p>Big-O describes how the work <em>grows</em> with the input, not how long it takes. Constants and lower-order terms are dropped because they do not change the shape of that growth: <code>3n + 5</code> is O(n), and <code>n² + 100n</code> is O(n²).</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Notation</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Name</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Typical source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(1)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">constant</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">array index, hash lookup</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(log n)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">logarithmic</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">binary search — halving</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(n)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">linear</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">one loop over the data</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(n log n)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">linearithmic</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">merge sort, quicksort average</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(n²)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">quadratic</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">nested loops over the same data</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">O(2ⁿ)</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">exponential</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">naive Fibonacci, all subsets</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two loops one after the other add — O(n) + O(n) = O(n). Two loops nested multiply — O(n) × O(n) = O(n²). That addition-versus-multiplication rule answers most analysis questions on its own.</p><p>A counter that doubles (<code>i *= 2</code>) runs about log₂n times, which is where the logarithm comes from in code that has no recursion in it.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Big-O:</strong> an upper bound on how the running time grows with input size</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Constant factor:</strong> the multiplier Big-O deliberately ignores</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Dominant term:</strong> the fastest-growing term, which is the only one kept</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Space complexity:</strong> the same analysis applied to extra memory rather than time</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Two sequential loops are O(n) + O(n) = O(n), not O(n²). Only <em>nesting</em> multiplies. That distinction is the most common Big-O error.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS173="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE FIVE CURVES, DRAWN FROM THE ACTUAL FUNCTIONS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 580 314" style="width:100%; max-width:620px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk289a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><line x1="62" y1="250" x2="498" y2="250" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="62" y1="250" x2="62" y2="26" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="52" y="22" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">work</text><text x="274" y="272" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">size of the input  →</text><polyline points="62.0,244.6 67.6,244.6 73.2,244.6 78.7,244.6 84.3,244.6 89.9,244.6 95.5,244.6 101.1,244.6 106.6,244.6 112.2,244.6 117.8,244.6 123.4,244.6 128.9,244.6 134.5,2