font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">44</text><rect x="336" y="166" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="365" y="185" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">55</text><rect x="394" y="166" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.24)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="423" y="185" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">31</text><text x="365" y="162" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">j</text><text x="464" y="185" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">j=5: 19 ≤ 31 — swap into slot 2</text><rect x="46" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.20)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="75" y="227" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12</text><rect x="104" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.20)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="133" y="227" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">27</text><rect x="162" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.20)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="191" y="227" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">19</text><rect x="220" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.24)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="249" y="227" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">31</text><rect x="278" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="307" y="227" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">44</text><rect x="336" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="365" y="227" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">55</text><rect x="394" y="208" width="58" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="423" y="227" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">38</text><text x="464" y="227" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">pivot swaps into slot 3 — its FINAL place</text><rect x="46" y="246" width="174" height="30" rx="9" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.14" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="133" y="265" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">≤ 31</text><rect x="278" y="246" width="174" height="30" rx="9" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.14" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="365" y="265" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">> 31</text><text x="249" y="265" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">31</text><text x="300" y="298" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The pivot is now final and is excluded from both recursive calls — that is what guarantees progress.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — one partition, every comparison shown</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Lomuto partitioning with the last element as pivot. <code>i</code> marks the end of the "small" region; <code>j</code> is the scan.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int partition(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
    int pivot = a[hi];
    int i = lo - 1;
    for (int j = lo; j &lt; hi; j++) {
        if (a[j] &lt;= pivot) { i++; swap(a, i, j); }
    }
    swap(a, i + 1, hi);          // the pivot into its final slot
    return i + 1;
}

int[] a = { 38, 12, 55, 27, 44, 19, 31 };
quicksort(a, 0, a.length - 1);   // trace printed below</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what the trace prints for the first call</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">quicksort on [38, 12, 55, 27, 44, 19, 31]  pivot = 31
   j=0  38 &gt;  31  -&gt; leave it       [38, 12, 55, 27, 44, 19, 31]
   j=1  12 &lt;= 31  -&gt; swap it into slot 0   [12, 38, 55, 27, 44, 19, 31]
   j=2  55 &gt;  31  -&gt; leave it       [12, 38, 55, 27, 44, 19, 31]
   j=3  27 &lt;= 31  -&gt; swap it into slot 1   [12, 27, 55, 38, 44, 19, 31]
   j=4  44 &gt;  31  -&gt; leave it       [12, 27, 55, 38, 44, 19, 31]
   j=5  19 &lt;= 31  -&gt; swap it into slot 2   [12, 27, 19, 38, 44, 55, 31]
   pivot 31 swaps into slot 3  [12, 27, 19, 31, 44, 55, 38]
   -&gt; 31 is now final at index 3; recurse on [12, 27, 19] and [44, 55, 38]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Each "≤ pivot" element is swapped to <code>i+1</code>, immediately after the small region. The value that was sitting there is thrown into the scanned-and-larger area — which is fine, because it is already known to be larger.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Notice the left region is <code>[12, 27, 19]</code>: <strong>not sorted</strong>. Partitioning only promises "all ≤ pivot", never any order within.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The final swap puts 31 at index 3, and 31 is now in its permanent position. It will never be looked at again.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The two recursive calls take <code>lo..p-1</code> and <code>p+1..hi</code>. Excluding <code>p</code> is what shrinks the problem; including it would recurse forever on a one-element range.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Sorting the whole array took 11 comparisons here, against 21 for selection sort on seven elements.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the input that turns O(n log n) into O(n²)</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The bad case for quicksort is the one nobody expects: data that is <em>already sorted</em>, when the pivot is the first or last element.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[][] inputs = {
    { 38, 12, 55, 27, 44, 19, 31, 60 },   // shuffled
    { 12, 19, 27, 31, 38, 44, 55, 60 },   // already sorted
    { 60, 55, 44, 38, 31, 27, 19, 12 }    // reverse sorted
};
String[] labels = { "shuffled      ", "already sorted", "reverse sorted" };
for (int k = 0; k &lt; inputs.length; k++) {
    comps = 0; maxDepth = 0;
    quicksort(inputs[k].clone(), 0, inputs[k].length - 1);
    System.out.println(labels[k] + ": comparisons " + comps
            + ", recursion depth " + maxDepth);
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">shuffled      : comparisons 18, recursion depth 5
already sorted: comparisons 28, recursion depth 8
reverse sorted: comparisons 28, recursion depth 8</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">On sorted input the last element is always the largest, so the partition puts it at the far right and the "smaller" side contains all n−1 remaining elements. Nothing is halved.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The comparison count is 28 — exactly <code>n(n−1)/2</code> for n = 8, the same as selection sort. The recursion depth is 8, one frame per element, which is how quicksort turns into a stack overflow on large sorted arrays.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The shuffled case needs 18 comparisons and depth 5. That is the O(n log n) behaviour quicksort is famous for.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The defence is pivot choice, not a different algorithm: median-of-three, or a random index swapped to the end before partitioning. Java’s <code>Arrays.sort</code> for primitives uses dual-pivot quicksort with exactly this kind of protection.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two questions come up again and again. <strong>"Where does the work happen?"</strong> — quicksort partitions <em>before</em> recursing, merge sort merges <em>after</em>; that single sentence answers most compare-and-contrast items. <strong>"After one partition, which element is definitely in its final position?"</strong> — the pivot, and only the pivot. Nothing else on either side is known to be placed, and an option claiming the left side is sorted is the distractor.</p></div>"},"D286/u17/z17_9":{"body":"<p>Merge sort splits the array in half, sorts each half recursively and merges the two sorted halves. It is the algorithm whose complexity never varies.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">void mergeSort(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo &gt;= hi) { return; }
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    mergeSort(a, lo, mid);
    mergeSort(a, mid + 1, hi);
    merge(a, lo, mid, hi);                // combine two sorted halves
}

// merging {1,5,9} and {2,3,8}: compare the fronts, take the smaller, repeat
// -&gt; 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9</pre><p>It is O(n log n) in every case — best, average and worst — because the splitting is always even. The price is O(n) extra space for the merge, which is the one thing quicksort does not need. It is also stable, which is why <code>Collections.sort</code> uses a variant of it for objects.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static void mergeSort(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo &gt;= hi) return;                     // base case: 0 or 1 element
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    mergeSort(a, lo, mid);
    mergeSort(a, mid + 1, hi);
    merge(a, lo, mid, hi);                    // the WORK happens here, after
}</pre><p>Split in half, sort each half, then merge two sorted halves in one linear pass. The merge is where everything happens, and it is why the recursion has to complete first.</p><p>O(n log n) in <strong>all</strong> cases — the split is always even, so there is no bad input. That guarantee is what you buy with its one real cost: O(n) extra memory for the temporary array, unlike quicksort's in-place partitioning.</p><p>It is <strong>stable</strong>, provided the merge takes from the left half when values are equal, which is exactly why Java uses a merge-based sort for objects where stability is observable.</p><p>Choosing between them: merge sort when you need a guarantee or stability, quicksort when memory is tight and the average case is good enough.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Merge sort:</strong> divide in half, sort each half, merge the sorted halves</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Merge step:</strong> combining two sorted sequences by repeatedly taking the smaller front element</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Guaranteed O(n log n):</strong> the split is always even, so the depth is always log n</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Space O(n):</strong> a temporary array is required for merging</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Merge sort versus quicksort in one line: merge sort guarantees O(n log n) but needs extra memory; quicksort sorts in place but can degrade to O(n²).</p></div><i data-d286fx29="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — MERGE SORT: SPLIT DOWN, MERGE UP</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 500 266" style="width:100%; max-width:540px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk95a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="198" y="8" width="104" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="250" y="25" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">38 27 43 3</text><rect x="120" y="56" width="84" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="162" y="73" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">38 27</text><rect x="300" y="56" width="84" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="342" y="73" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">43 3</text><line x1="230" y1="34" x2="172" y2="54" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="280" y1="34" x2="332" y2="54" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><rect x="80" y="104" width="50" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="105" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">38</text><rect x="172" y="104" width="50" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="197" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">27</text><rect x="282" y="104" width="50" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="307" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">43</text><rect x="374" y="104" width="50" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="399" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3</text><line x1="148" y1="82" x2="112" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="180" y1="82" x2="190" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="326" y1="82" x2="302" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="356" y1="82" x2="392" y2="102" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><rect x="120" y="152" width="84" height="26" rx="6" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="162" y="169" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">27 38</text><rect x="300" y="152" width="84" height="26" rx="6" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="342" y="169" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3 43</text><line x1="105" y1="130" x2="148" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="197" y1="130" x2="172" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="307" y1="130" x2="330" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="399" y1="130" x2="356" y2="150" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><rect x="180" y="200" width="140" height="26" rx="6" fill="#22c55e22" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="250" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3 27 38 43</text><line x1="162" y1="178" x2="220" y2="198" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><line x1="342" y1="178" x2="290" y2="198" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk95a)"/><text x="60" y="70" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">SPLIT</text><text x="60" y="176" fill="#22c55e" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">MERGE</text><text x="250" y="244" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Split until single elements (trivially sorted), then merge pairs back in order.</text><text x="250" y="258" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">log n levels × n work per level = O(n log n) — and it needs extra array space to merge into.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS179="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the merge itself — one comparison decides one element</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Splitting is trivial; everything interesting is in the merge. Two already-sorted halves, one finger on the front of each, and the smaller front element is taken.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// after both halves are sorted:  [8, 14, 26, 33]  and  [5, 19, 30, 41]
int i = lo, j = mid + 1, k = 0;
int[] tmp = new int[hi - lo + 1];
while (i &lt;= mid &amp;&amp; j &lt;= hi) {
    if (a[i] &lt;= a[j]) tmp[k++] = a[i++];      // &lt;= keeps it STABLE
    else              tmp[k++] = a[j++];
}
while (i &lt;= mid) tmp[k++] = a[i++];           // drain whatever is left
while (j &lt;= hi)  tmp[k++] = a[j++];
for (int t = 0; t &lt; tmp.length; t++) a[lo + t] = tmp[t];</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">the trace of that final merge</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">merge [8, 14, 26, 33] with [5, 19, 30, 41]
   8 &gt;  5  take 5 from the right
   8 &lt;= 19  take 8 from the LEFT
   14 &lt;= 19  take 14 from the LEFT
   26 &gt;  19  take 19 from the right
   26 &lt;= 30  take 26 from the LEFT
   33 &gt;  30  take 30 from the right
   33 &lt;= 41  take 33 from the LEFT
   left half empty, take 41 from the right
   merged: [5, 8, 14, 19, 26, 30, 33, 41]</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Eight elements came out of seven comparisons. The last element needs none — once one half is exhausted, the rest of the other half is copied straight across.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Only the two <em>front</em> elements are ever compared. Each half is already sorted, so whichever front is smaller is smaller than everything remaining.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The drain loops at the end are not optional. Leave them out and the tail of the longer half is silently lost.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The merge cannot be done in place with an array, which is where the O(n) extra memory comes from — the one real cost of merge sort.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the same work on every input, and the extra memory it costs</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[][] inputs = {
    { 5, 8, 14, 19, 26, 30, 33, 41 },     // already sorted
    { 41, 33, 30, 26, 19, 14, 8, 5 },     // reverse sorted
    { 26, 14, 33, 8, 41, 19, 5, 30 }      // shuffled
};
String[] labels = { "already sorted", "reverse sorted", "shuffled      " };
for (int k = 0; k &lt; inputs.length; k++) {
    comps = 0; copies = 0;
    mergeSort(inputs[k].clone(), 0, inputs[k].length - 1);
    System.out.println(labels[k] + ": comparisons " + comps
            + ", elements copied into temporary arrays " + copies);
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">already sorted: comparisons 12, elements copied into temporary arrays 24
reverse sorted: comparisons 12, elements copied into temporary arrays 24
shuffled      : comparisons 17, elements copied into temporary arrays 24</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The copy count is <strong>24 on every input</strong>: 8 elements × 3 levels of merging. There is no lucky input and no unlucky one, which is the guarantee merge sort sells.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">8 = 2³, so log₂8 = 3 levels. Each level does n copies. n × log n = 8 × 3 = 24 — the Big-O is not an estimate here, it is the arithmetic.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Comparisons vary a little (12 against 17) because a sorted or reverse-sorted half runs out early and the drain loop finishes the job for free. Sorting is still O(n log n); the variation is a constant factor.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Quicksort on the same sorted input needed 28 comparisons and depth 8. Merge sort has no such input. That is the trade: a guarantee, paid for with an extra array.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two mistakes in the merge, both of which produce output that looks nearly right. Writing <code>if (a[i] &lt; a[j])</code> instead of <code>&lt;=</code> takes the right-hand element on a tie and destroys <strong>stability</strong> — the array is still correctly sorted, so nothing looks broken until a second sort key reveals scrambled ties. And allocating the temporary array <em>inside</em> the merge is normal, but copying it back with the wrong offset — <code>a[t] = tmp[t]</code> rather than <code>a[lo + t] = tmp[t]</code> — writes every merged block to the front of the array, which corrupts the data quietly on every call except the first.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_1":{"body":"<p>This unit is conceptual rather than syntactic, and the exam treats it that way: it asks what a term means and what a technique is for, not for code. Artificial intelligence is the umbrella — systems performing tasks that normally require human intelligence — and the terms nest inside one another.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">AI
 └── Machine Learning         — learns patterns from data instead of being programmed
      └── Deep Learning       — neural networks with many layers
           └── Generative AI  — produces new content
                └── LLMs      — generative models specialised for language</pre><p>The distinction that matters most is rule-based versus learned. A rule-based system does exactly what a programmer wrote; a machine-learning system infers its behaviour from examples, which is why it can handle inputs nobody anticipated and why it can be confidently wrong.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Term</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>AI</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the broad field — machines doing things that need intelligence</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Machine learning</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a subset: behaviour learned from <em>data</em> rather than written as rules</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>Deep learning</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a subset of ML using many-layered neural networks</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The nesting — AI ⊃ ML ⊃ deep learning — is the most-asked fact in this unit. The distinction that matters is <em>where the behaviour comes from</em>: a rule-based system does what a programmer wrote; a learned system infers it from examples.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Learning type</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Data</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>supervised</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">labelled</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">spam / not spam from tagged mail</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>unsupervised</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">unlabelled</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">clustering customers into groups</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>reinforcement</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">reward signal</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">learning a game by playing it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Rules remain better where the logic is known, must be exact and must be explainable — tax calculation, not image recognition. Learned systems are hard to explain, which is a real cost wherever a decision must be justified.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Artificial intelligence:</strong> systems performing tasks normally requiring human intelligence</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Machine learning:</strong> inferring behaviour from data rather than from explicit rules</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Training data:</strong> the examples a model learns from</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Model:</strong> the learned function that maps an input to an output</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Inference:</strong> using a trained model to produce an output for a new input</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Get the nesting right and most questions in this unit answer themselves: every LLM is generative AI, all generative AI is machine learning, and all machine learning is AI — none of it works in reverse.</p></div><i data-d286fx30="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — AI ⊃ MACHINE LEARNING ⊃ DEEP LEARNING</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 460 270" style="width:100%; max-width:500px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk96a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><circle cx="230" cy="120" r="105" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="230" y="34" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Artificial Intelligence</text><text x="230" y="50" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="8.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">any technique that mimics intelligent behavior</text><circle cx="230" cy="140" r="72" fill="none" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="230" y="88" fill="#6366f1" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Machine Learning</text><text x="230" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="8" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">learns patterns from data</text><circle cx="230" cy="160" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="230" y="150" fill="#22c55e" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Deep Learning</text><text x="230" y="163" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="7.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">many-layer neural nets</text><text x="230" y="182" fill="#22c55e" font-size="8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LLMs / generative AI</text><text x="230" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Each ring is a subset of the one outside it. LLMs (like chat assistants) are deep-learning</text><text x="230" y="262" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">models trained on text — so they are ML, and therefore AI, but AI is much bigger than LLMs.</text></svg></div></div><i data-tbD286dS181="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the same decision, once written and once learned</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A property system has to label a listing "large" or "small". Written as rules, a person decides the thresholds. Learned from data, nobody writes a threshold at all — the program computes the centre of each labelled group and asks which centre a new listing is nearer to.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// the rule-based way: a human writes the logic
static String ruleBased(double area, int bedrooms) {
    if (area &gt;= 2200 || bedrooms &gt;= 4) return "large";
    return "small";
}

// the learned way: the logic comes from labelled examples
double[][] largeExamples = { {2400,4}, {2800,5}, {2250,4}, {3100,5} };
double[][] smallExamples = { {900,2}, {1150,2}, {1400,3}, {1050,1} };
double[] cLarge = centroid(largeExamples);      // the centre of each group
double[] cSmall = centroid(smallExamples);
// classify by whichever centre is nearer

double[][] tests = { {2600,4}, {1000,2}, {2300,2}, {1900,3} };</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">centre of the LARGE examples: 2638 sq ft, 4.50 bedrooms
centre of the SMALL examples: 1125 sq ft, 2.00 bedrooms
2600 sq ft, 4 bed -&gt; rules say large | learned says large
1000 sq ft, 2 bed -&gt; rules say small | learned says small
2300 sq ft, 2 bed -&gt; rules say large | learned says small
1900 sq ft, 3 bed -&gt; rules say small | learned says small</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Nobody wrote 2638 or 1125. Those numbers came out of the eight labelled examples — that is the whole of "learned from data" in this small case.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Three of the four test listings get the same label from both. A learned system is not mysterious; on typical input it agrees with the obvious rule.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The fourth disagrees, and that is the interesting one. 2300 sq ft with 2 bedrooms trips the rule’s <code>area &gt;= 2200</code> threshold, but sits much closer to the small group once <em>both</em> features are weighed together.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Which is right? The rules can be read and argued about — the threshold is right there in the source. The learned answer can only be explained by pointing at the training data, which is exactly the trade this section is about.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Add ten more examples and the rule-based method needs a programmer; the learned one needs only the new rows.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — telling the three kinds of learning apart from the data alone</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The exam question is almost never "define supervised learning". It is "here is a situation — which kind is it?" The answer is decided by what the training data contains, not by what the program does.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// SUPERVISED: every training row carries its answer
double[][] largeExamples = ...;   // labelled "large"
double[][] smallExamples = ...;   // labelled "small"
System.out.println("labelled training rows available: "
        + (largeExamples.length + smallExamples.length));

// UNSUPERVISED: no labels at all, group by similarity
double[][] unlabelled = { {920,2}, {2700,5}, {1080,2}, {2450,4}, {1350,3} };
// ... assign each row to the nearer of two centres ...
System.out.println("no labels given; grouped by similarity into "
        + groupA.size() + " and " + groupB.size()
        + " -- nobody said which group is which");

// REINFORCEMENT: no correct answer, only a score after the fact
int[] moves = { 1, -1, 1, 1, -1 };      // +1 good outcome, -1 bad
int reward = 0;
for (int m : moves) reward += m;
System.out.println("five attempts scored by a reward signal; total reward " + reward);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">labelled training rows available: 8
no labels given; grouped by similarity into 3 and 2 -- nobody said which group is which
five attempts scored by a reward signal; total reward 1</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Supervised</strong>: the eight rows arrived already sorted into "large" and "small". Somebody labelled them, and the labels are what the method learns from. Spam filtering trained on tagged mail is the textbook case.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Unsupervised</strong>: the five rows have no labels. The program can still split them into two groups by similarity, but it cannot name the groups — "3 and 2" is all it knows. Customer segmentation works exactly like this.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Reinforcement</strong>: nobody ever supplies the right move. Each attempt gets a score afterwards and the behaviour is adjusted towards higher scores. Learning a game by playing it is the standard example.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The one-line test: <em>are the right answers in the training data?</em> Yes → supervised. No labels at all → unsupervised. Only a score after acting → reinforcement.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The nesting is directional and it is asked in both directions. AI ⊃ machine learning ⊃ deep learning: every deep-learning system is machine learning and every machine-learning system is AI, but <strong>not</strong> the other way round. A rule-based expert system is AI and is not machine learning at all. The second slip is treating "AI" and "learned from data" as synonyms, so the answer "a program that follows rules a programmer wrote is not AI" gets picked — it is AI, it is just not <em>learned</em> AI.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_2":{"body":"<p>A large language model predicts the next token given everything before it. That single mechanism explains both what it is good at and how it fails.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">prompt -&gt; tokens -&gt; model -&gt; probability over next tokens -&gt; sample -&gt; repeat

"The capital of France is"  -&gt;  "Paris" (high probability)</pre><p>Because it is predicting plausible continuations rather than retrieving facts, it can produce text that is fluent and wrong — a <strong>hallucination</strong>. It has no source to check against, which is exactly the gap that retrieval-augmented generation (18.8) exists to fill.</p><p>A large language model predicts the next <strong>token</strong> — a word or word fragment — over and over. Everything it does follows from that: it has no database of facts and no ability to look anything up unless a tool is attached.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Term</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>token</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the unit of text — roughly ¾ of a word</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>context window</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">how much text it can consider at once; beyond it, the earliest text is dropped</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>parameters</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the learned weights — a rough proxy for capacity</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>temperature</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">0 = most predictable, higher = more varied</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>hallucination</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">fluent, confident and wrong</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>training cutoff</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the date after which it knows nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>It is <strong>stateless</strong>. It does not remember previous turns; the application re-sends the conversation each time, which is why history counts against the context window and why a long chat eventually forgets its own beginning.</p><p>Hallucination is not a bug to be patched — it is what next-token prediction does when the pattern is plausible but the fact is absent. That is why every factual output needs verification.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>LLM:</strong> a model trained on large text corpora to predict the next token</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Token:</strong> a chunk of text, roughly a word or word-piece, that the model works in</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Context window:</strong> how much text the model can consider at once</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Hallucination:</strong> fluent output that is factually wrong</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Temperature:</strong> a setting controlling randomness; lower is more predictable</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>An LLM does not "look things up". It generates the most plausible continuation, which is why it must not be trusted for facts without verification.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS182="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — NEXT-TOKEN PREDICTION, AND THE WINDOW IT HAPPENS IN</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 402" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk295a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="20" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">text in — split into tokens, never words</text><rect x="20" y="30" width="41" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="40.5" y="49" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">The</text><rect x="67" y="30" width="76" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="105" y="49" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣quarter</text><rect x="149" y="30" width="34" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="166" y="49" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ly</text><rect x="189" y="30" width="69" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="223.5" y="49" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣report</text><rect x="264" y="30" width="41" height="28" rx="5" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.14)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="284.5" y="49" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣is</text><text x="319" y="49" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">5 tokens, 4 words</text><line x1="200" y1="62" x2="200" y2="82" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk295a)"/><rect x="120" y="84" width="160" height="34" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="200" y="105" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the model</text><line x1="200" y1="118" x2="200" y2="138" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk295a)"/><text x="20" y="136" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">out — a probability for EVERY possible next token</text><text x="150" y="161" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣attached</text><rect x="158" y="148" width="161.2" height="17" rx="3" fill="#0ea5e9" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="327.2" y="161" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0.62</text><text x="150" y="185" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣ready</text><rect x="158" y="172" width="36.400000000000006" height="17" rx="3" fill="var(--text-3)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="202.4" y="185" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0.14</text><text x="150" y="209" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣overdue</text><rect x="158" y="196" width="23.4" height="17" rx="3" fill="var(--text-3)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="189.4" y="209" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0.09</text><text x="150" y="233" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">␣late</text><rect x="158" y="220" width="13" height="17" rx="3" fill="var(--text-3)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="179" y="233" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0.05</text><text x="150" y="257" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">… 50,000 more</text><rect x="158" y="244" width="26" height="17" rx="3" fill="var(--text-3)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="192" y="257" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0.10</text><path d="M460,204 C644,204 644,68 262,68" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="262" y1="68" x2="262" y2="82" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk295a)"/><text x="556" y="152" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">pick one, append it,</text><text x="556" y="166" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">run the whole thing again</text><line x1="20" y1="282" x2="660" y2="282" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="20" y="302" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the context window is a fixed frame, and the conversation slides through it</text><rect x="30" y="314" width="84" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="72" y="334" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">sys</text><rect x="122" y="314" width="84" height="30" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="164" y="334" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">u1</text><rect x="214" y="314" width="84" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="256" y="334" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a1</text><rect x="306" y="314" width="84" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="348" y="334" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">u2</text><rect x="398" y="314" width="84" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="440" y="334" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">a2</text><rect x="490" y="314" width="84" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="532" y="334" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">u3</text><rect x="24" y="306" width="96" height="46" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><rect x="116" y="306" width="96" height="46" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/><rect x="208" y="306" width="374" height="46" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="72" y="368" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">kept</text><text x="164" y="368" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">dropped</text><text x="395" y="368" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">kept — this is all the model ever sees</text><text x="340" y="390" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The model never "remembers" turn u1. It is simply no longer in the text that was sent.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — counting tokens, because that is what you are billed and limited by</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">There is no exact token count without the model’s own tokeniser, but "about four characters per token" is close enough for English prose and is the estimate real code uses for budgeting.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static int estimateTokens(String s) { return (int) Math.ceil(s.length() / 4.0); }

String[] samples = {
    "The quarterly report is attached.",
    "unquestionably",
    "int total = 0;",
    "Reschedule the maintenance window to Tuesday at 02:00 UTC."
};
for (String s : samples) {
    System.out.printf("%-62s chars %3d  words %2d  ~tokens %3d%n",
        "\"" + s + "\"", s.length(), s.trim().split("\\s+").length, estimateTokens(s));
}
String doc = "The quarterly report is attached. ".repeat(200);
System.out.println("a 200-sentence document: " + doc.length()
        + " chars, about " + estimateTokens(doc) + " tokens");</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">"The quarterly report is attached."                            chars  33  words  5  ~tokens   9
"unquestionably"                                               chars  14  words  1  ~tokens   4
"int total = 0;"                                               chars  14  words  4  ~tokens   4
"Reschedule the maintenance window to Tuesday at 02:00 UTC."   chars  58  words  9  ~tokens  15
a 200-sentence document: 6800 chars, about 1700 tokens</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Five words become about nine tokens — tokens are <em>smaller</em> than words, roughly ¾ of one each.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>unquestionably</code> is one word and about four tokens. A long word is split into pieces, which is why the model has no reliable view of the letters in a word.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>int total = 0;</code> is four words and four tokens: punctuation and symbols cost tokens too.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The last sample shows the ratio holding at longer lengths, and the 6,800-character document is about 1,700 tokens. That single number is what decides whether a document fits in one request.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a conversation growing until it no longer fits</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The window is a budget in tokens, not a number of turns. Here it is set to a deliberately tiny 60 so it fills up in six turns; a real one is tens of thousands, and fills up the same way.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int BUDGET = 60;
List&lt;Turn&gt; history = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
// ... six turns are added one at a time ...
for (String[] t : chat) {
    history.add(new Turn(t[0], t[1]));
    int used = 0;
    for (Turn h : history) used += estimateTokens(h.text());
    System.out.println("after " + history.size() + " turns: "
            + used + " tokens used of " + BUDGET);
}

// trimming to fit, always keeping the system turn at index 0
while (used &gt; BUDGET &amp;&amp; kept.size() &gt; 2) {
    Turn dropped = kept.remove(1);
    used -= estimateTokens(dropped.text());
    System.out.println("dropped the oldest turn (" + dropped.role()
            + "), now " + used + " tokens");
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">after 1 turns: 13 tokens used of 60
after 2 turns: 23 tokens used of 60
after 3 turns: 34 tokens used of 60
after 4 turns: 40 tokens used of 60
after 5 turns: 51 tokens used of 60
after 6 turns: 62 tokens used of 60
dropped the oldest turn (user), now 52 tokens
turns sent: 5 of 6
   system: You answer questions about the shipping schedule.
   assistant: It departs on Thursday at 06:00 local time.
   user: And the one after that?
   assistant: The following departure is Sunday at 22:00.
   user: Remind me which port I asked about first.</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The count rises on every turn because the <em>whole</em> history is re-sent each time. Nothing is stored on the other side.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Turn 6 needs 62 tokens against a budget of 60. Without trimming the request is simply rejected.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The trim removes index 1 — the oldest real turn — and keeps index 0, the system message, which carries the instructions and must survive.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">And now the damage: the final question is "which port did I ask about first", and the turn that named Rotterdam has just been deleted. The reply will be confidently wrong, and the cause is a data-structure decision, not the model.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Real applications summarise the dropped turns into one short line rather than deleting them outright — the same trade-off as any fixed-size buffer.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three facts carry most of the marks here. A token is <strong>smaller than a word</strong> (about ¾ of one). The model is <strong>stateless</strong> — memory is the application re-sending the history, which is why history counts against the window. And <strong>hallucination is not a bug</strong>: next-token prediction produces the most plausible continuation, and plausible is not the same as true. Any option that describes the model as "looking things up" or "remembering the last conversation" is the distractor.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_3":{"body":"<p>Generative AI covers more than text, and the applications share one shape: a prompt describing what is wanted, and a generated artefact in response.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">text   -&gt; summarise, translate, draft, explain, answer
code   -&gt; generate, complete, explain, convert between languages
images -&gt; create from a description, edit, restyle
audio  -&gt; speech from text, transcription, music</pre><p>The tasks where it works well have a common property: many acceptable answers, and a human reviewing the output. The tasks where it fails have exactly one correct answer that must be right without review — which is a good rule for deciding when to use it.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Technique</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>specific prompting</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">stating format, length, audience and constraints</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>few-shot</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">giving two or three worked examples of the pattern you want</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>chain of thought</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">asking for the reasoning steps before the answer</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>role prompting</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">setting a perspective — "as a code reviewer"</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>iteration</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">refining the prompt against what came back</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Few-shot works because the model is a pattern completer: examples define the shape of the answer far more reliably than describing it. Chain of thought helps on multi-step problems because each intermediate step becomes context for the next.</p><p>Poor fits are the mirror of how it works. Character counting and arithmetic are unreliable because text is processed as tokens rather than characters or numbers; anything after the training cutoff is unknown; and it cannot tell you what it does not know.</p><p>Good fits are drafting, rephrasing, explaining, summarising and generating variations — tasks where a fluent plausible answer is genuinely the goal.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Prompt:</strong> the input describing what the model should produce</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Prompt engineering:</strong> writing prompts that reliably produce the output you want</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Few-shot prompting:</strong> including examples in the prompt to demonstrate the expected form</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Multimodal:</strong> a model handling more than one kind of data, such as text and images</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Specific prompts beat vague ones. State the role, the task, the format and the constraints — the same clarity a well-written method signature provides (6.22).</p></div><i data-tbD286dS183="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE ANATOMY OF A PROMPT THAT WORKS</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 450" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk296a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="20" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">a specific prompt, one purpose per line</text><rect x="20" y="30" width="116" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="78" y="51" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ROLE</text><rect x="142" y="30" width="348" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="152" y="51" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">You are a technical writer for an operations handbook.</text><rect x="20" y="70" width="116" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="78" y="91" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">TASK</text><rect x="142" y="70" width="348" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="152" y="91" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Summarise the incident report below.</text><rect x="20" y="110" width="116" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="78" y="131" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FORMAT</text><rect x="142" y="110" width="348" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="152" y="131" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Exactly three bullet points, each under 15 words.</text><rect x="20" y="150" width="116" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="78" y="171" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">CONSTRAINTS</text><rect x="142" y="150" width="348" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="152" y="171" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Use only facts stated in the report.</text><rect x="20" y="190" width="116" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="78" y="211" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">INPUT</text><rect x="142" y="190" width="348" height="32" rx="5" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="152" y="211" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">REPORT: At 03:14 the load balancer stopped forwarding…</text><rect x="506" y="30" width="152" height="152" rx="8" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.10)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="582" y="52" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what comes back</text><text x="516" y="76" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">• 2 of 6 web nodes lost traffic</text><text x="516" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">• started 03:14</text><text x="516" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">• cause not stated</text><text x="582" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">three bullets, as asked</text><line x1="20" y1="240" x2="660" y2="240" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="20" y="262" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">few-shot: the examples ARE the specification</text><rect x="20" y="274" width="260" height="26" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="30" y="291" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">3 boxes of A4 paper</text><line x1="286" y1="287" x2="316" y2="287" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk296a)"/><rect x="322" y="274" width="150" height="26" rx="5" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.14)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="397" y="291" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">SUPPLIES|3</text><rect x="20" y="308" width="260" height="26" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="30" y="325" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">one replacement laptop battery</text><line x1="286" y1="321" x2="316" y2="321" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk296a)"/><rect x="322" y="308" width="150" height="26" rx="5" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.14)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="397" y="325" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">HARDWARE|1</text><rect x="20" y="342" width="260" height="26" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="30" y="359" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">12 months of licence renewal</text><line x1="286" y1="355" x2="316" y2="355" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk296a)"/><rect x="322" y="342" width="150" height="26" rx="5" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.14)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="397" y="359" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">SOFTWARE|12</text><rect x="20" y="376" width="260" height="26" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="30" y="393" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2 ergonomic keyboards</text><line x1="286" y1="389" x2="316" y2="389" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk296a)"/><rect x="322" y="376" width="150" height="26" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="397" y="393" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">?</text><text x="490" y="393" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the shape is now unambiguous</text><text x="340" y="424" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Describing a format is a request. Showing three examples of it is a pattern to complete —</text><text x="340" y="438" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">and pattern completion is the one thing the model does.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — building the specific prompt in code, and counting what it costs</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String role   = "You are a technical writer for an operations handbook.";
String task   = "Summarise the incident report below.";
String format = "Output exactly three bullet points, each under 15 words.";
String limits = "Use only facts stated in the report. "
              + "If a cause is not stated, write \"cause not stated\".";
String body   = "At 03:14 the load balancer stopped forwarding traffic "
              + "to two of six web nodes.";

String prompt = String.join("\n", role, task, format, limits, "", "REPORT:", body);
System.out.println(prompt);
System.out.println("--- " + prompt.length() + " characters, about "
        + (int) Math.ceil(prompt.length() / 4.0) + " tokens ---");</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">You are a technical writer for an operations handbook.
Summarise the incident report below.
Output exactly three bullet points, each under 15 words.
Use only facts stated in the report. If a cause is not stated, write "cause not stated".

REPORT:
At 03:14 the load balancer stopped forwarding traffic to two of six web nodes.
--- 325 characters, about 82 tokens ---</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Compare with the whole of the vague version: <code>"Summarise this."</code> — 15 characters, and no way to tell whether the answer that comes back is what was wanted.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>String.join("\n", ...)</code> with an empty string in the middle produces the blank line before <code>REPORT:</code>. Separating instructions from data matters: without it the report text reads as more instructions.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The constraint line does real work. "If a cause is not stated, write 'cause not stated'" gives the model a legitimate way to <em>not</em> answer, which is the practical defence against an invented cause.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Eighty-two tokens of instruction is a fixed cost paid on every request. That is the honest trade for specificity, and it is why the instructions live in a <code>String</code> constant rather than being rebuilt each call.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — few-shot, assembled with a loop</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Few-shot prompting is not a special API. It is string building: a handful of input/output pairs, then the new input with the output line left dangling.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String[][] examples = {
    { "3 boxes of A4 paper",            "SUPPLIES|3" },
    { "one replacement laptop battery", "HARDWARE|1" },
    { "12 months of licence renewal",   "SOFTWARE|12" }
};
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
sb.append("Convert each request line into CATEGORY|QUANTITY.\n\n");
for (String[] ex : examples) {
    sb.append("Input:  ").append(ex[0]).append('\n');
    sb.append("Output: ").append(ex[1]).append("\n\n");
}
sb.append("Input:  2 ergonomic keyboards\n");
sb.append("Output:");
System.out.println(sb.toString());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">Convert each request line into CATEGORY|QUANTITY.

Input:  3 boxes of A4 paper
Output: SUPPLIES|3

Input:  one replacement laptop battery
Output: HARDWARE|1

Input:  12 months of licence renewal
Output: SOFTWARE|12

Input:  2 ergonomic keyboards
Output:</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The examples establish three things at once that prose would struggle with: the vocabulary of categories, the pipe separator, and that "one" and "12 months" both become digits.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The prompt ends on <code>"Output:"</code> with nothing after it. The model is a continuation engine, so the most probable continuation is a line in exactly that shape.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The loop is the point: examples live in an array and the prompt is generated. Adding a fourth category means adding a row, not editing a paragraph.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">This is also why the number of examples is a tuning decision — more examples pin the format down harder and cost more tokens on every single request.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">And when any part of the prompt comes from a user, it has to be escaped before it goes into JSON:</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String userLine = "3 reams of \"heavy\" paper\nand a stapler";
System.out.println("raw:     " + userLine.replace("\n", "\\n"));
System.out.println("escaped: " + jsonEscape(userLine));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">raw:     3 reams of "heavy" paper\nand a stapler
escaped: "3 reams of \"heavy\" paper\nand a stapler"</pre></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The characteristic mistake is asking for work the mechanism cannot do and then trusting the confident answer. Counting characters in a word, arithmetic on long numbers, and anything dated after the training cutoff are all unreliable — not because the model is careless but because it processes tokens rather than letters or numbers, and because the fact was never there. The second mistake is treating a wrong answer as proof the task is impossible: the same request, rewritten with a format and two examples, very often works, and "iterate on the prompt" is a listed technique for exactly that reason.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_4":{"body":"<p>Using AI while coding is now normal practice, and the useful framing is that it is a fast draft rather than a source of truth. Everything it produces has to pass the same review any pull request would.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">good uses                          bad uses
boilerplate and scaffolding        anything you cannot review
explaining unfamiliar code         security-critical logic taken on trust
suggesting test cases              coursework you must be able to defend
translating between languages      relying on it for facts without checking</pre><p>For this course specifically, generated code that you cannot explain is worth nothing on the exam, because the exam asks you to trace and predict rather than to produce. Reading generated code and understanding why it works is the version of this that helps.</p><p>The reliable uses in coding are boilerplate, explaining unfamiliar code, drafting tests, and suggesting approaches. The unreliable ones are anything requiring the API to actually exist.</p><p><strong>Invented APIs are the characteristic failure.</strong> A method that looks exactly like it should exist, with a plausible name and signature, that simply does not. That one at least fails at compile time.</p><p>The dangerous failure is code that compiles and runs and is subtly wrong — an off-by-one bound, a missing edge case, a check omitted. Nothing flags it, which is why generated code must be read and tested rather than accepted because it runs.</p><p>Two practical rules. Never paste secrets, credentials or proprietary code into a tool you do not control. And check your institution's academic policy before using it on coursework — the responsibility for what you submit is yours regardless of what produced it.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Code completion:</strong> suggesting the next lines as you type</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Code review (of generated code):</strong> verifying correctness, security and licensing before use</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Hallucinated API:</strong> a method or class the model invented that does not exist</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Academic integrity:</strong> the requirement that submitted work is your own and defensible</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Invented library methods are the most common failure mode in generated Java. If a method name looks unfamiliar, check it in the API documentation (7.17) before assuming it exists.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS184="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — TWO KINDS OF WRONG — AND ONLY ONE HAS A SAFETY NET</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 700 238" style="width:100%; max-width:720px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk297a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="20" y="40" width="140" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="90" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">generated code</text><line x1="160" y1="62" x2="208" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk297a)"/><rect x="208" y="30" width="122" height="64" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="269" y="55" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">COMPILER</text><text x="269" y="72" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">javac</text><line x1="330" y1="62" x2="378" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk297a)"/><rect x="378" y="30" width="122" height="64" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="439" y="55" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">IT RUNS</text><text x="439" y="72" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no exception</text><line x1="500" y1="62" x2="548" y2="62" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk297a)"/><rect x="548" y="30" width="122" height="64" rx="8" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.10)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="609" y="55" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">SHIPPED</text><text x="609" y="72" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">and wrong</text><line x1="269" y1="94" x2="269" y2="124" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk297a)"/><rect x="186" y="126" width="166" height="46" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.12)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="269" y="144" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">invented method name</text><text x="269" y="160" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">caught in seconds ✓</text><line x1="439" y1="94" x2="439" y2="124" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk297a)"/><rect x="356" y="126" width="166" height="46" rx="7" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.12)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="439" y="144" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">crash on empty input</text><text x="439" y="160" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">caught by one test ✓</text><line x1="609" y1="94" x2="609" y2="124" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk297a)"/><rect x="526" y="126" width="166" height="46" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.10)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="609" y="144" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">sum / count on ints</text><text x="609" y="160" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">caught by nothing ✗</text><text x="20" y="200" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">The only gate left is you: read it, test the edges, check the boundary.</text><text x="350" y="226" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The failures that stop the build are the cheap ones. The expensive failure is the one that compiles.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — code that compiles, runs, and quietly gives the wrong number</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">This is the suggestion. Read it before running it and decide whether you would have accepted it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static double averageScore(int[] scores) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; scores.length; i++) sum += scores[i];
    return sum / scores.length;
}

int[] scores = { 88, 91, 79, 95 };
System.out.println("averageScore      -&gt; " + averageScore(scores));
System.out.println("averageScoreFixed -&gt; " + averageScoreFixed(scores));
System.out.println("the true average is " + (88 + 91 + 79 + 95) / 4.0);

int[] empty = {};
try {
    System.out.println(averageScore(empty));
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("empty array -&gt; " + e.getClass().getSimpleName()
                       + ": " + e.getMessage());
}
System.out.println("empty array, fixed version -&gt; " + averageScoreFixed(empty));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">averageScore      -&gt; 88.0
averageScoreFixed -&gt; 88.25
the true average is 88.25
empty array -&gt; ArithmeticException: / by zero
empty array, fixed version -&gt; 0.0</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The return type is <code>double</code>, so <code>88.0</code> looks like a decimal answer and reads as correct. It is not: <code>sum / scores.length</code> is <code>int / int</code>, truncated to 88 <em>before</em> the widening to <code>88.0</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The error is a quarter of a mark. No exception, no warning, nothing in the output that says "this is wrong" — you only find it by knowing the true answer, which is the whole problem.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The fix is one cast, <code>(double) sum / scores.length</code>, and it is the integer-division rule from Unit 2. Generated code fails on the basics as readily as on the exotic parts.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The empty array is the second omission. It throws <code>ArithmeticException: / by zero</code> — at least this one is loud, and one test with an empty array finds it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the off-by-one that passes the obvious test</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static String longest(String[] words) {
    String best = "";
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; words.length; i++)            // starts at 1
        if (words[i].length() &gt; best.length()) best = words[i];
    return best;
}

String[] a = { "kilo", "hectare", "gram" };
System.out.println("longest(a)      -&gt; " + longest(a));
System.out.println("longestFixed(a) -&gt; " + longestFixed(a));
String[] b = { "kilometre", "gram", "litre" };        // longest is FIRST
System.out.println("longest(b)      -&gt; " + longest(b));
System.out.println("longestFixed(b) -&gt; " + longestFixed(b));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">longest(a)      -&gt; hectare
longestFixed(a) -&gt; hectare
longest(b)      -&gt; litre
longestFixed(b) -&gt; kilometre</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">On the first array the broken version gives the right answer. Test it once, casually, and it passes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The bug is <code>i = 1</code>. Element 0 is never examined, so the method is only wrong when the longest word happens to be first — and in <code>a</code> it was not.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">On <code>b</code> it returns <code>litre</code> (5 letters) while <code>kilometre</code> (9) sat at index 0 the whole time. Still no exception, still a plausible-looking answer.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Three assertions catch both of this section’s bugs: a normal case, a case where the answer is at the boundary, and an empty input. That is the review, and it takes two minutes.</li></ol><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">the three checks</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">check 1 (normal):      true
check 2 (first is longest): true
check 3 (empty input): true</pre></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Expect a question of the form "which failure is the dangerous one". The answer is always the code that <strong>compiles and runs and is subtly wrong</strong> — an invented method name fails at compile time and costs seconds. The other two reliable answers: never paste credentials, keys or proprietary code into a service you do not control; and the responsibility for submitted work is the student’s regardless of what produced it, so check the academic policy first.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_5":{"body":"<p>Calling an AI service from Java is an ordinary HTTP request. Nothing about the mechanics is AI-specific — it is JSON over HTTPS, and every Java networking rule applies.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">import java.net.http.*;
import java.net.URI;

HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();

HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
    .uri(URI.create("https://api.example.com/v1/chat"))
    .header("Content-Type", "application/json")
    .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + System.getenv("API_KEY"))
    .POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(jsonBody))
    .build();

HttpResponse<string> response =
    client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());</string></pre><p><code>System.getenv</code> rather than a literal is the detail that matters. A key written into source ends up in version control and stays in the history even after it is removed — which is a security failure, not a style preference.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String key = System.getenv("OPENAI_API_KEY");   // NEVER hard-code the key
// POST JSON: {"model": ..., "messages": [ {"role":"user","content": ...} ]}
// check the status code BEFORE parsing the body
if (status != 200) { /* handle it — do not parse an error page as a reply */ }</pre><p>An API key belongs in an environment variable or a config file kept out of version control. A key committed to a repository is a key that has been published, and rotating it is the only remedy.</p><p>Because the model is stateless, <strong>conversation memory is the application's job</strong>: keep the message list and re-send it every turn. That list grows without limit, so a real chatbot must trim or summarise old turns before it exceeds the context window.</p><p>Network calls fail, rate limits return 429, and responses can be malformed. Every one of those is an ordinary exception path from Unit 12, and user input placed into JSON must be escaped or it corrupts the request.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>HttpClient:</strong> the standard Java HTTP client, since Java 11</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>API key:</strong> the credential authenticating your requests; never hard-code it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Environment variable:</strong> configuration supplied outside the source, read with System.getenv</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>JSON:</strong> the request and response format used by nearly every web API</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Rate limit:</strong> the cap on requests per period; exceeding it returns an error status</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Network calls fail — timeouts, rate limits, malformed responses. They belong in a try-catch (Unit 12), and <code>IOException</code> is checked, so the compiler will insist.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS185="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — EVERY TURN RE-SENDS THE WHOLE CONVERSATION</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 352" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk298a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="20" y="20" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">turn 1</text><text x="240" y="20" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">turn 2</text><text x="460" y="20" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">turn 3</text><rect x="20" y="30" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="104" y="46" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">system</text><rect x="20" y="58" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.16)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="104" y="74" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">user 1</text><text x="104" y="102" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2 messages sent</text><rect x="240" y="30" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="324" y="46" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">system</text><rect x="240" y="58" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="324" y="74" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">user 1</text><rect x="240" y="86" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="324" y="102" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">assistant 1</text><rect x="240" y="114" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.16)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="324" y="130" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">user 2</text><text x="324" y="158" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4 messages sent</text><rect x="460" y="30" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="544" y="46" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">system</text><rect x="460" y="58" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="544" y="74" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">user 1</text><rect x="460" y="86" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="544" y="102" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">assistant 1</text><rect x="460" y="114" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="544" y="130" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">user 2</text><rect x="460" y="142" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="544" y="158" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">assistant 2</text><rect x="460" y="170" width="168" height="24" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.16)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="544" y="186" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">user 3</text><text x="544" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">6 messages sent</text><line x1="196" y1="60" x2="234" y2="60" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk298a)"/><line x1="416" y1="60" x2="454" y2="60" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk298a)"/><line x1="20" y1="234" x2="660" y2="234" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><rect x="20" y="250" width="180" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="110" y="270" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">environment</text><text x="110" y="287" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">MODEL_API_KEY</text><line x1="200" y1="273" x2="244" y2="273" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk298a)"/><rect x="244" y="250" width="180" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="334" y="270" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your program</text><text x="334" y="287" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">System.getenv(...)</text><line x1="424" y1="273" x2="468" y2="273" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk298a)"/><rect x="468" y="250" width="190" height="46" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="563" y="270" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">request header</text><text x="563" y="287" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">never in the source</text><text x="340" y="316" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">A key written into a .java file and committed is a published key.</text><text x="340" y="340" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The list on the left is the entire memory of the conversation. Lose it and the chat starts over.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — conversation memory is a List your program owns</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">record Message(String role, String content) {}

List&lt;Message&gt; history = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
history.add(new Message("system", "You help staff book meeting rooms."));

String[] userTurns = { "Book room 4B for Tuesday.",
                       "Make it Wednesday instead.",
                       "What time did I ask for?" };
String[] replies   = { "Room 4B is booked for Tuesday at 10:00.",
                       "Moved to Wednesday at 10:00." };

for (int t = 0; t &lt; userTurns.length; t++) {
    history.add(new Message("user", userTurns[t]));
    System.out.println("turn " + (t + 1) + ": sending " + history.size() + " messages");
    if (t &lt; replies.length) history.add(new Message("assistant", replies[t]));
}
for (Message m : history) System.out.println("   " + m.role() + ": " + m.content());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">turn 1: sending 2 messages
turn 2: sending 4 messages
turn 3: sending 6 messages
the whole list goes over the wire every single turn:
   system: You help staff book meeting rooms.
   user: Book room 4B for Tuesday.
   assistant: Room 4B is booked for Tuesday at 10:00.
   user: Make it Wednesday instead.
   assistant: Moved to Wednesday at 10:00.
   user: What time did I ask for?</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">2, 4, 6 — the request grows by two messages a turn, because the reply has to be appended as well as the question. Nothing is remembered on the far side.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>system</code> message goes first and stays first. It is the standing instruction, and it is re-sent every turn along with everything else.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Turn 3 asks "what time did I ask for?" and can only be answered because messages 2 and 3 are still in the list. Delete them and the answer becomes a guess — which is the trimming problem of 18.2 arriving in real code.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A <code>record</code> is exactly the right shape here: two final fields, a constructor, accessors and <code>toString</code> for free, from one line.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — escaping user text, and checking the status before parsing</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two failures happen at the boundary rather than in the model. A quotation mark in user text corrupts the JSON body; and an error response parsed as if it were a reply produces a nonsense answer with no exception thrown.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static String quote(String s) {
    StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder("\"");
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
        char c = s.charAt(i);
        switch (c) {
            case '"'  -&gt; out.append("\\\"");
            case '\\' -&gt; out.append("\\\\");
            case '\n' -&gt; out.append("\\n");
            default   -&gt; out.append(c);
        }
    }
    return out.append('"').toString();
}

hostile.add(new Message("user", "Cancel the \"9am\" booking\nand tell me why"));
System.out.println(toJson(hostile));

for (int status : new int[]{ 200, 401, 429, 503, 400 })
    System.out.println("HTTP " + status + " -&gt; " + describe(status));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">{"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Cancel the \"9am\" booking\nand tell me why"}]}
HTTP 200 -&gt; parse the reply
HTTP 401 -&gt; bad key -- do not retry, fix the credential
HTTP 429 -&gt; rate limited -- wait and retry
HTTP 503 -&gt; server side -- retry with backoff
HTTP 400 -&gt; client error 400 -- fix the request</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Without escaping, the user’s quotation mark around <code>9am</code> would close the JSON string early and the request would be rejected as malformed — a bug that only appears for certain user input.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The newline becomes <code>\n</code>, two characters. A literal newline inside a JSON string is illegal, which is why multi-line input breaks naive string building.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The status codes split into three different responses: 401 must <em>not</em> be retried (retrying a bad key just burns quota), 429 and 5xx should be retried after a wait, and 4xx means your request is wrong.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Every one of these is ordinary exception handling from Unit 12. Nothing about calling a model needs a new technique — it needs the old ones actually applied.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The mistake that costs real money is hard-coding the key: <code>String key = "sk-live-…";</code> committed to a repository is public the moment the repository is, and rotating the key is the only remedy — deleting the commit does not help. Read it from the environment with <code>System.getenv</code> and fail loudly when it is absent. The second mistake is expecting the far side to remember anything: leave the history list out and every turn starts from nothing, which looks like the model "forgetting" and is actually your program never having told it.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_6":{"body":"<p>An image-generation app follows the same request-response shape as 18.5, with two differences: the response is binary or a URL, and generation takes long enough that the user must be told something is happening.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// 1. send the prompt
// 2. receive a URL or base64 data
// 3. decode and save

byte[] imageBytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(base64String);
try (FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream("result.png")) {
    out.write(imageBytes);                          // Unit 9, binary form
}

// in a GUI, do the request OFF the UI thread or the window freezes
new Thread(() -&gt; {
    byte[] data = fetchImage(prompt);
    Platform.runLater(() -&gt; imageView.setImage(new Image(...)));
}).start();</pre><p>The threading note is the practically important one. A network call on the UI thread freezes the whole interface until it returns, and any update to the interface must go back onto that thread — <code>Platform.runLater</code> in JavaFX, <code>SwingUtilities.invokeLater</code> in Swing.</p><p>An image API differs from a text one in what comes back: usually a temporary <strong>URL</strong> that expires within the hour, or a base64-encoded blob. Either way the result must be downloaded and saved to keep it.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">new Thread(() -&gt; {
    Image img = callApi(prompt);                    // slow — OFF the UI thread
    SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -&gt; label.setIcon(new ImageIcon(img)));
}).start();                                          // back ON it to display</pre><p>This is the threading rule of 15.4 with teeth. A network call takes seconds; on the event thread the whole window freezes for the duration. Do the call on a background thread, then return to the UI thread to touch any component.</p><p>Image generation also costs per request and is content-filtered, so a rejected prompt is a normal response to handle rather than an error.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Base64:</strong> a text encoding for binary data, common in JSON responses</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Binary file output:</strong> writing bytes rather than text, using an output stream</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>UI thread:</strong> the single thread that must handle all interface updates</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Background thread:</strong> where slow work belongs so the interface stays responsive</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>"The window went grey and stopped responding" almost always means slow work is running on the UI thread.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS186="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE SLOW CALL BELONGS IN THE OTHER LANE</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 262" style="width:100%; max-width:700px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk299a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="20" y="20" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">WRONG — the call runs on the event thread</text><rect x="20" y="30" width="640" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="150" y="32" width="380" height="30" rx="4" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.16)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="84" y="51" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">click</text><text x="340" y="51" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FROZEN — no repaint, no menus, no cancel</text><text x="596" y="51" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">image</text><text x="20" y="92" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">RIGHT — two lanes</text><text x="14" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">event</text><text x="14" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">thread</text><rect x="64" y="104" width="596" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="110" y="125" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">click</text><rect x="180" y="108" width="100" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.16)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="230" y="125" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">frame 1</text><rect x="290" y="108" width="100" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.16)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="340" y="125" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">frame 2</text><rect x="400" y="108" width="100" height="26" rx="4" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.16)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="450" y="125" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">frame 3</text><text x="576" y="125" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">set the icon</text><text x="14" y="190" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">worker</text><rect x="64" y="172" width="596" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="150" y="174" width="370" height="30" rx="4" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.18)" stroke="none" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="335" y="193" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the slow network call happens here</text><line x1="126" y1="138" x2="150" y2="170" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk299a)"/><text x="96" y="160" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">start()</text><line x1="520" y1="172" x2="560" y2="140" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk299a)"/><text x="566" y="162" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">invokeLater</text><text x="340" y="236" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Slow work on the worker lane, every touch of a component back on the event lane.</text><text x="340" y="250" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Painting from the worker thread is the bug that produces "it works on my machine".</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — watching the two threads interleave</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The sleep stands in for a network call. The point is not the numbers, it is that lines from the two threads are interleaved — the main thread kept working while the other one waited.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">final String[] result = new String[1];
Thread worker = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName() + "] calling the image service...");
    result[0] = slowCall("a lighthouse at dusk");     // Thread.sleep(300) inside
    System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName()
            + "] got " + result[0].length() + " bytes of data");
}, "worker-1");
worker.start();

for (int frame = 1; frame &lt;= 3; frame++) {
    System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName()
            + "] frame " + frame + " painted while waiting");
    Thread.sleep(100);
}
worker.join();
System.out.println("[" + Thread.currentThread().getName()
        + "] back on the UI thread, setting the icon");</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">[main] user clicks Generate
[main] window is still repainting, menus still open
[worker-1] calling the image service...
[main] frame 1 painted while waiting
[main] frame 2 painted while waiting
[main] frame 3 painted while waiting
[worker-1] got 12 bytes of data
[main] back on the UI thread, setting the icon</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Read the thread names down the left. Three <code>[main]</code> lines are printed <em>while</em> <code>[worker-1]</code> is blocked in the call — that is the window staying responsive.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Put the call directly in the button handler instead and every one of those frames would be missing: the same output minus the three middle lines, which on screen is a grey rectangle where the window was.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>worker.join()</code> is the "wait for it to finish" step. In a real Swing program you would not block here at all; the worker would post back with <code>SwingUtilities.invokeLater</code>, and this is what a <code>SwingWorker</code>’s <code>done()</code> method does for you.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The rule is symmetrical and both halves matter: slow work must leave the event thread, and every component update must return to it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — what comes back, and saving it before it expires</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">An image service returns either a temporary URL or base64-encoded bytes. A URL usually expires within the hour, so "keeping" the picture always means writing bytes to disk.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String encoded = "SG93IHRvIHNhdmUgYW4gaW1hZ2U=";
byte[] bytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(encoded);
System.out.println("decoded " + bytes.length + " bytes");

Path out = Path.of("generated.png");
Files.write(out, bytes);
System.out.println("written to " + out + ", size on disk " + Files.size(out));
System.out.println("re-read: " + new String(Files.readAllBytes(out)));

for (String status : new String[]{ "ok", "content_filtered", "rate_limited" }) {
    System.out.println(status + " -&gt; " + switch (status) {
        case "ok" -&gt; "display the image";
        case "content_filtered" -&gt; "show the message, keep the prompt for editing";
        default -&gt; "queue the request and tell the user to wait";
    });
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">decoded 20 bytes
written to generated.png, size on disk 20
re-read: How to save an image
ok -&gt; display the image
content_filtered -&gt; show the message, keep the prompt for editing
rate_limited -&gt; queue the request and tell the user to wait</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Base64.getDecoder().decode</code> is standard <code>java.util</code> — no library required. Base64 exists because JSON is text and image bytes are not.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>Files.write(Path, byte[])</code> writes binary directly, with no <code>Writer</code> and no character encoding to get wrong. Sending image bytes through a <code>PrintWriter</code> corrupts them.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The decoded content here is readable text only because the example encodes a sentence; a real payload would be PNG bytes and the same three lines would handle it unchanged.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The status handling is the part students skip. A filtered prompt is a <em>normal response</em>, not an exception — the app should show the message and keep the prompt so the user can edit it, and a rate limit means wait, not fail.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two threading errors, opposite in shape. Calling the service on the event thread freezes the window for the entire call, and a frozen window is often mistaken for a crash. The reverse — touching a Swing component from the worker thread, <code>label.setIcon(...)</code> straight out of the background code — usually appears to work, which is worse: Swing components are not thread-safe, so it fails intermittently and only under load. Slow work leaves the event thread; component updates come back to it through <code>SwingUtilities.invokeLater</code>. And do not hold onto the returned URL as if it were the picture; it expires.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_7":{"body":"<p>Building a system around a model is mostly engineering rather than modelling, and the pipeline is the same regardless of what the model does.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">input -&gt; validate -&gt; build prompt -&gt; call model -&gt; parse response
      -&gt; validate output -&gt; use it
                 |
                 +-- on failure: retry, fall back, or report</pre><p>Every stage is ordinary Java. Validation is 6.13, failure handling is Unit 12, and the fallback path is what stops the whole feature disappearing when the service is unavailable. The model is one component, not the architecture.</p><p>A production AI feature is mostly ordinary software. The model call is a small part; the work is in what surrounds it.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Piece</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>system prompt</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">sets role, rules and output format once</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>context assembly</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">gathers the right information to include</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>output validation</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">parses and checks the reply before trusting it</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>fallback</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">what happens when the reply is unusable</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>evaluation</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a fixed set of cases with known-good answers</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>logging</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">inputs and outputs, so failures can be diagnosed</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>caching</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">avoids paying twice for the same request</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Output validation is non-negotiable because the reply is text, not a typed object. Asking for JSON does not guarantee JSON, so parsing must be wrapped and a fallback ready — never assume the shape.</p><p>An evaluation set is what makes changes safe. Without one, "the new prompt seems better" is an impression; with one it is a measurement, which is regression testing from 6.7 applied to a non-deterministic component.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Pipeline:</strong> the ordered stages input passes through</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Guardrails:</strong> checks on input and output that constrain what the system will accept or emit</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Fallback:</strong> the behaviour when the model is unavailable or its answer fails validation</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Evaluation:</strong> measuring output quality against known-good examples</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Determinism:</strong> the same input giving the same output; models are not deterministic by default</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>Non-determinism is what makes these systems hard to test with the methods of 6.7. Assertions on exact output do not work, so evaluation moves to properties: is it valid JSON, is it in range, does it cite a real source.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS187="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — THE MODEL CALL IS THE SMALL PART</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 690 270" style="width:100%; max-width:710px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk300a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="16" y="60" width="116" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="74" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">system prompt</text><text x="74" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">role, rules, format</text><rect x="148" y="60" width="116" height="52" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="206" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">context</text><text x="206" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the right data</text><line x1="132" y1="86" x2="146" y2="86" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fk300a)"/><line x1="264" y1="86" x2="292" y2="86" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk300a)"/><rect x="292" y="52" width="104" height="68" rx="9" fill="rgba(14,165,233,0.14)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="2"/><text x="344" y="82" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the model</text><text x="344" y="99" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">call</text><line x1="396" y1="86" x2="424" y2="86" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk300a)"/><rect x="424" y="52" width="116" height="68" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="482" y="76" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">VALIDATE</text><text x="482" y="94" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">parse · range · enum</text><line x1="540" y1="70" x2="574" y2="52" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk300a)"/><rect x="574" y="28" width="100" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="624" y="52" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">use it</text><line x1="540" y1="104" x2="574" y2="122" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk300a)"/><rect x="574" y="104" width="100" height="40" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="624" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">fallback</text><rect x="292" y="8" width="104" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="344" y="29" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">cache hit?</text><line x1="344" y1="40" x2="344" y2="50" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#fk300a)"/><rect x="16" y="156" width="658" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/><text x="345" y="178" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LOG every input and output — otherwise a failure cannot be reproduced</text><rect x="16" y="202" width="658" height="34" rx="7" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/><text x="345" y="224" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">EVALUATION SET — fixed cases with known answers, re-run after every prompt change</text><text x="345" y="258" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">One box in this diagram is the model. Every other box is ordinary software you write and test.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — validating a reply that is only text</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The reply is a <code>String</code>. Asking for JSON does not guarantee JSON, and even valid JSON can contain a category that does not exist. Validation is parse, then range-check, then enum-check — and any failure routes to the fallback.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">record Ticket(String category, int urgency) {}

static Optional&lt;Ticket&gt; parse(String reply) {
    try {
        // ... pull out the {...} and read the two fields ...
        if (category == null || urgency == null) return Optional.empty();
        if (urgency &lt; 1 || urgency &gt; 5) return Optional.empty();
        if (!List.of("BILLING","TECHNICAL","ACCOUNT").contains(category))
            return Optional.empty();
        return Optional.of(new Ticket(category, urgency));
    } catch (RuntimeException e) {
        return Optional.empty();        // malformed is a NORMAL outcome
    }
}</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">the five replies, and what happens to each</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">accepted Ticket[category=BILLING, urgency=3]   &lt;- {"category": "BILLING", "urgency": 3}
accepted Ticket[category=TECHNICAL, urgency=5]   &lt;- Sure! Here is the JSON you asked for: | {"category": "TECHNICAL", "urgency": 5}
REJECTED  -&gt; fall back to the human queue   &lt;- {"category": "URGENT", "urgency": 3}
REJECTED  -&gt; fall back to the human queue   &lt;- {"category": "BILLING", "urgency": 9}
REJECTED  -&gt; fall back to the human queue   &lt;- I'm not able to categorise this ticket.</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reply 1 is the happy path and is the only one most people test.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reply 2 has a chatty preamble before the JSON. Finding the first <code>{</code> and the last <code>}</code> rescues it — a small tolerance that removes a large class of failures.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reply 3 is <em>valid JSON</em> with an invented category. Syntax checking alone would accept it, which is why the enum check exists: the set of allowed values is yours, not the model’s.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reply 4 has urgency 9 on a 1–5 scale. Range checks are not paranoia; out-of-range values are a routine failure mode.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Reply 5 is a refusal in plain English. It parses as nothing, throws nothing useful, and must be caught as a normal outcome rather than an exception that reaches the user.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Every rejection lands on the same fallback: the human queue. A feature with no fallback is a feature that fails on reply 3.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — an evaluation set, and a cache that stops paying twice</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">"The new prompt seems better" is an impression. Five fixed cases with known answers turn it into a number, and the harness is ordinary code you can run on every change.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">String[][] cases = {
    { "card declined twice",           "BILLING" },
    { "cannot reset my password",      "ACCOUNT" },
    { "app crashes on launch",         "TECHNICAL" },
    { "charged twice this month",      "BILLING" },
    { "screen freezes when printing",  "TECHNICAL" }
};
System.out.println("prompt A: " + score(cases, promptA) + " of " + cases.length + " correct");
System.out.println("prompt B: " + score(cases, promptB) + " of " + cases.length + " correct");

Map&lt;String,String&gt; cache = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
int calls = 0;
for (String q : asked) {
    if (cache.containsKey(q)) { System.out.println("cache hit  : " + q); continue; }
    calls++;
    cache.put(q, "answer " + calls);
    System.out.println("model call : " + q);
}
System.out.println(asked.length + " questions asked, " + calls + " calls actually made");</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">prompt A: 4 of 5 correct
prompt B: 5 of 5 correct
without this set, both prompts would just "seem fine"
model call : What is the refund window?
model call : How do I reset my password?
cache hit  : What is the refund window?
cache hit  : What is the refund window?
4 questions asked, 2 calls actually made</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Prompt A gets four of five; prompt B gets five. That is a <em>measurement</em>, and it is the difference between changing a prompt and improving one.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The failing case is "cannot reset my password", which prompt A calls TECHNICAL and the key calls ACCOUNT. Knowing <em>which</em> case failed is what makes the next edit targeted rather than hopeful.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Five cases is a small set and still catches the regression. The point is that it is fixed and re-run, not that it is large — this is the regression testing of Unit 6 applied to a component that is not deterministic.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The cache is a plain <code>HashMap</code>: four questions, two calls. Half the requests never leave the building, which is half the latency and half the cost for one <code>containsKey</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The characteristic beginner system calls the model and uses the reply directly — <code>String category = reply.trim();</code> straight into a database column. It works in the demo and fails on the first chatty preamble, the first invented category, and the first polite refusal. The other half of the same mistake is catching the parse failure and rethrowing it: a malformed reply is an <em>expected</em> outcome, not an exceptional one, so the code path for it must be a real fallback with a defined behaviour — a default value, a retry with a stricter prompt, or a human.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The reliable questions are "what surrounds the model call" and "why is an evaluation set needed". Answer the first with the pipeline: system prompt, context assembly, the call, validation, fallback, logging, caching. Answer the second with non-determinism — the same prompt can give different replies, so improvement has to be measured over a fixed set of cases rather than judged from one look. Anything describing the reply as a typed object is wrong: it is text until you have parsed and checked it.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_8":{"body":"<p>Retrieval-augmented generation is the standard answer to the hallucination problem of 18.2: instead of trusting the model to know something, you find the relevant text first and hand it over with the question.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">1. INDEX      break your documents into chunks, store them
2. RETRIEVE   given a question, find the most relevant chunks
3. AUGMENT    put those chunks into the prompt as context
4. GENERATE   ask the model to answer USING ONLY that context

String prompt = "Answer using only the context below.\n\n"
              + "Context:\n" + String.join("\n", relevantChunks) + "\n\n"
              + "Question: " + question;</pre><p>The retrieval step is ordinary search — the algorithms of 17.1 and the collections of Unit 14, with a <code>HashMap</code> from term to document. Only step 4 involves a model, which is why RAG is more of a data-structures problem than an AI one.</p><h3>How retrieval actually finds the relevant chunks</h3><p>The retrieval step needs a way to measure "relevant". Each chunk is converted into an <strong>embedding</strong> — a vector of numbers representing its meaning — and the query is embedded the same way. The chunks whose vectors point in most nearly the same direction as the query are the ones retrieved.</p><p>The standard measure is <strong>cosine similarity</strong>: the cosine of the angle between two vectors, running from 1 (identical direction) through 0 (unrelated) to −1 (opposite). It compares direction rather than length, which is what makes it work when a long document and a short question are being matched.</p><i data-gapfix="1" style="display:none"></i><p><strong>RAG</strong> — retrieval-augmented generation — answers questions over documents the model was never trained on: retrieve the relevant passages, then include them in the prompt so the answer is grounded in real text.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">indexing:  split documents into CHUNKS -&gt; embed each -&gt; store the vectors
query:     embed the question -&gt; find the top-k most similar chunks
           -&gt; put them in the prompt -&gt; generate the answer</pre><p>An <strong>embedding</strong> turns text into a vector positioned so that similar meanings sit close together; similarity is measured by <strong>cosine similarity</strong>, the angle between two vectors. Chunk size is the tuning knob — too large dilutes relevance, too small loses the surrounding context.</p><p>The weak link is retrieval, not generation. If the right chunk is not retrieved, the model answers from its own training or invents something, and the answer looks exactly as confident either way.</p><p>RAG suits changing or private knowledge; fine-tuning suits changing <em>style</em> or format. Choosing fine-tuning for facts is the standard wrong answer.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>RAG:</strong> retrieval-augmented generation — supplying retrieved context with the question</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Chunking:</strong> splitting documents into pieces small enough to retrieve and to fit the context window</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Embedding:</strong> a numeric vector representing meaning, used to find similar text</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Grounding:</strong> constraining an answer to supplied source material</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Citation:</strong> reporting which retrieved chunk an answer came from</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>RAG reduces hallucination; it does not eliminate it. If retrieval returns nothing relevant, the model will still answer — which is why "say you do not know" belongs in the prompt.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS188="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — RAG: INDEX ONCE, RETRIEVE ON EVERY QUESTION</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 690 308" style="width:100%; max-width:710px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk301a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="16" y="20" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">INDEXING — done once, ahead of any question</text><rect x="16" y="32" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="90" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">documents</text><text x="90" y="71" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the policy PDFs</text><line x1="164" y1="57" x2="184" y2="57" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="184" y="32" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="258" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">chunks</text><text x="258" y="71" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">60 chars, 25 overlap</text><line x1="332" y1="57" x2="352" y2="57" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="352" y="32" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="426" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">vectors</text><text x="426" y="71" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">one per chunk</text><line x1="500" y1="57" x2="520" y2="57" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="520" y="32" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="594" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the store</text><text x="594" y="71" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">text + vector</text><line x1="16" y1="104" x2="674" y2="104" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="16" y="126" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">QUERY — every time somebody asks something</text><rect x="16" y="138" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="90" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">question</text><text x="90" y="177" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">"how long for a refund?"</text><line x1="164" y1="163" x2="184" y2="163" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="184" y="138" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="258" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">vector</text><text x="258" y="177" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">same embedding</text><line x1="332" y1="163" x2="352" y2="163" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="352" y="138" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="426" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">top-k</text><text x="426" y="177" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">cosine similarity</text><line x1="500" y1="163" x2="520" y2="163" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="520" y="138" width="148" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="594" y="160" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">prompt</text><text x="594" y="177" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">context + question</text><line x1="466" y1="82" x2="466" y2="136" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="5 3" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><text x="480" y="118" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the store is searched, not the model</text><line x1="594" y1="188" x2="594" y2="202" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="594" y1="202" x2="174" y2="202" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="174" y1="202" x2="174" y2="212" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="16" y="214" width="316" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="174" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the model sees the question AND the retrieved text</text><line x1="332" y1="236" x2="356" y2="236" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><rect x="356" y="214" width="148" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="430" y="240" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">grounded answer</text><rect x="520" y="214" width="154" height="44" rx="8" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.10)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="597" y="232" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">wrong chunk retrieved?</text><text x="597" y="248" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the answer is invented</text><line x1="504" y1="236" x2="518" y2="236" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#fk301a)"/><text x="345" y="282" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Nothing here retrains anything. The documents are put into the prompt — which is why RAG suits</text><text x="345" y="296" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">knowledge that changes, and fine-tuning suits style that changes.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — chunking, and what the overlap is actually for</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static List&lt;String&gt; chunk(String doc, int size, int overlap) {
    List&lt;String&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    int start = 0;
    while (start &lt; doc.length()) {
        int end = Math.min(start + size, doc.length());
        out.add(doc.substring(start, end));
        if (end == doc.length()) break;
        start = end - overlap;                 // step BACK before the next chunk
    }
    return out;
}

String policy = "Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase. "
              + "Shipping is free on orders over 50 dollars. "
              + "Damaged goods must be reported within 48 hours.";</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">document length: 138 characters
-- 60-character chunks, no overlap --
  chunk 0: [Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase. Shipping is f]
  chunk 1: [ree on orders over 50 dollars. Damaged goods must be reporte]
  chunk 2: [d within 48 hours.]
-- 60-character chunks, 25 of overlap --
  chunk 0: [Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase. Shipping is f]
  chunk 1: [f purchase. Shipping is free on orders over 50 dollars. Dama]
  chunk 2: [ers over 50 dollars. Damaged goods must be reported within 4]
  chunk 3: [must be reported within 48 hours.]
chunks: 3 without overlap, 4 with -- overlap costs storage and saves sentences cut in half</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Look at chunk 1 in the first list: it begins <code>"ree on orders over 50 dollars"</code>. The shipping sentence has been cut across a boundary, so neither chunk states the shipping rule in full.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">With 25 characters of overlap, chunk 1 begins <code>"f purchase. Shipping is free on orders over 50 dollars."</code> — the whole sentence is now inside one chunk and can be retrieved as a unit.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The cost is exact and visible: 4 chunks instead of 3. Overlap trades storage and embedding cost for not slicing meaning in half.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The step is <code>size − overlap</code>. Set overlap ≥ size and the loop never advances — the same "guarantee progress" requirement as binary search in 17.2.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Real systems chunk on sentence or paragraph boundaries rather than fixed characters; the arithmetic is identical and the boundaries are simply chosen better.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — retrieval is a similarity ranking, and its failure is silent</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">An embedding is a vector positioned so that similar meanings sit close together. Three dimensions are used here so the numbers can be read; a real embedding has hundreds or thousands, and the arithmetic is the same.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static double cosine(double[] a, double[] b) {
    double dot = 0, na = 0, nb = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        dot += a[i] * b[i]; na += a[i] * a[i]; nb += b[i] * b[i];
    }
    return dot / (Math.sqrt(na) * Math.sqrt(nb));
}

// [refund-ness, shipping-ness, damage-ness]
new Chunk("Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase.",  {0.92, 0.10, 0.15});
new Chunk("Shipping is free on orders over 50 dollars.",     {0.08, 0.95, 0.05});
new Chunk("Damaged goods must be reported within 48 hours.", {0.20, 0.12, 0.90});
new Chunk("Gift cards are non-refundable.",                  {0.70, 0.05, 0.10});

double[] question = { 0.88, 0.12, 0.20 };   // "how long to get my money back?"
ranked.sort(Map.Entry.&lt;Chunk,Double&gt;comparingByValue().reversed());</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">question vector: [0.88, 0.12, 0.2]
  0.9978  Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase.
  0.9948  Gift cards are non-refundable.
  0.4376  Damaged goods must be reported within 48 hours.
  0.2237  Shipping is free on orders over 50 dollars.
-- prompt actually sent, top-2 chunks only --
Answer using ONLY the context below.

CONTEXT:
- Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase.
- Gift cards are non-refundable.

QUESTION: How long do I have to get my money back?</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Cosine similarity is the angle between two vectors, so it lands between −1 and 1 and ignores length. Two chunks about refunds score 0.99; the shipping chunk scores 0.22.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Only the top <em>k</em> go into the prompt. That is the whole retrieval step — a sort and a slice, with no model involved.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The assembled prompt is the deliverable: the instruction, the retrieved text as CONTEXT, then the question. "Answer using ONLY the context below" is what makes the answer traceable to a document.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Now the failure. An off-topic question — "what is your VAT number?" — still produces a ranking, and the top chunk is still handed over as context:</li></ol><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">the off-topic question</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">  0.5354  Refunds are issued within 14 days of purchase.
  0.6476  Shipping is free on orders over 50 dollars.
  0.8575  Damaged goods must be reported within 48 hours.
  0.5028  Gift cards are non-refundable.
top score is still returned -- nothing in the pipeline says "not in the documents"</pre></div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Nothing in that output flags a problem. A similarity <em>threshold</em> — reject anything below, say, 0.75 — is the missing line, and it is the difference between "I do not have that information" and a fluent invention.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The weak link is retrieval, and it fails without a symptom. If the right chunk is not in the top <em>k</em>, the model answers from its training or invents something, and the answer is worded with exactly the same confidence as a correct one — so the fix is a similarity threshold and citing the chunk, not a better-worded prompt. The second mistake is the standard exam distractor: reaching for <strong>fine-tuning</strong> to teach the system new facts. Fine-tuning changes <em>style and format</em>; facts that change belong in the retrieval store, where updating them means replacing a document rather than retraining anything.</p></div>"},"D286/u18/z18_9":{"body":"<p>The risks are examined as concepts, and each one is worth being able to define and give an example of.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">bias           the model reflects patterns in its training data,
               including unfair ones
privacy        data sent to a service may be stored or used for training
accuracy       fluent output can be confidently wrong (18.2)
attribution    generated work may closely reproduce training material
accountability who is responsible when an automated decision causes harm
transparency   users should know when they are dealing with a model</pre><p>The through-line is that these are human and legal questions the code cannot settle. A model trained on biased hiring data will reproduce that bias no matter how well the Java around it is written — which is precisely why the decision to deploy it is not a technical one.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Risk</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Where it comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>bias</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the training data — the model reproduces patterns in it</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>hallucination</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">plausible prediction with no fact behind it</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>privacy</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">anything sent may be stored or used for training</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>accountability</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">the deployer is responsible, not the model</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>transparency</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">people should know when they are talking to a machine</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;"><strong>environmental cost</strong></td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">training and serving consume significant energy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Bias is not introduced by the algorithm; it is inherited from data reflecting the world as it was recorded. Testing for it means measuring outcomes across groups, not inspecting intentions.</p><p>Human review belongs wherever a decision materially affects someone — hiring, lending, medical, legal. The failure mode to guard against is <strong>automation bias</strong>: trusting a confident output more than it deserves, precisely because it is fluent.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Algorithmic bias:</strong> systematically unfair output arising from the training data</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Data privacy:</strong> the obligation to protect information users supply</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Explainability:</strong> being able to say why a model produced a particular output</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Human in the loop:</strong> requiring a person to review consequential decisions</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Responsible AI:</strong> the practices intended to keep these risks manageable</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Exam questions here are scenario-based: given a situation, name the risk. Hiring and lending scenarios point at bias; anything with personal data points at privacy; anything with a confident wrong answer points at accuracy.</p></div><i data-tbD286dS189="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHERE EACH RISK ENTERS THE CHAIN</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 706 276" style="width:100%; max-width:720px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk302a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="16" y="40" width="152" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="92" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">data collected</text><text x="92" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">from the world as it was</text><line x1="168" y1="65" x2="188" y2="65" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk302a)"/><rect x="188" y="40" width="152" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="264" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">model trained</text><text x="264" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">patterns absorbed</text><line x1="340" y1="65" x2="360" y2="65" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk302a)"/><rect x="360" y="40" width="152" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="436" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">system deployed</text><text x="436" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">decisions at scale</text><line x1="512" y1="65" x2="532" y2="65" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#fk302a)"/><rect x="532" y="40" width="152" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="608" y="62" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a person</text><text x="608" y="79" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">affected by the outcome</text><line x1="92" y1="90" x2="92" y2="112" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="16" y="112" width="152" height="62" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.09)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="92" y="132" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">bias</text><text x="92" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the data recorded the world’s</text><text x="92" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">existing patterns</text><line x1="264" y1="90" x2="264" y2="112" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="188" y="112" width="152" height="62" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.09)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="264" y="132" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">hallucination</text><text x="264" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">plausible prediction with</text><text x="264" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no fact behind it</text><line x1="436" y1="90" x2="436" y2="112" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="360" y="112" width="152" height="62" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.09)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="436" y="132" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">privacy</text><text x="436" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">inputs may be stored</text><text x="436" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">or used for training</text><line x1="608" y1="90" x2="608" y2="112" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><rect x="532" y="112" width="152" height="62" rx="7" fill="rgba(225,29,72,0.09)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="608" y="132" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">accountability</text><text x="608" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the deployer answers for it,</text><text x="608" y="162" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not the model</text><rect x="494" y="196" width="196" height="44" rx="8" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.12)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="592" y="214" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">HUMAN REVIEW</text><text x="592" y="230" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">wherever the decision matters</text><line x1="522" y1="196" x2="522" y2="100" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/><line x1="522" y1="100" x2="522" y2="76" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 3" marker-end="url(#fk302a)"/><text x="16" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">transparency: people should know when the answer came from a machine</text><text x="16" y="232" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">automation bias: a fluent answer is trusted more than it has earned</text><text x="345" y="264" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Each risk enters at a specific stage — which is also where it has to be tested for.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — measuring a disparity instead of arguing about intent</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two hundred lending decisions, split evenly between two regions, every applicant equally qualified. Nothing in the code mentions the region — the model inherited the pattern from data in which one region was under-represented.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Map&lt;String,int[]&gt; tally = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();       // [approved, total]
for (Applicant a : decisions) {
    int[] t = tally.computeIfAbsent(a.group(), k -&gt; new int[2]);
    if (a.approved()) t[0]++;
    t[1]++;
}
double lowest = 1.0, highest = 0.0;
for (Map.Entry&lt;String,int[]&gt; e : tally.entrySet()) {
    double rate = (double) e.getValue()[0] / e.getValue()[1];
    lowest = Math.min(lowest, rate);
    highest = Math.max(highest, rate);
    System.out.printf("%-9s approved %3d of %3d  = %.1f%%%n",
        e.getKey(), e.getValue()[0], e.getValue()[1], rate * 100);
}
System.out.printf("ratio of the lower rate to the higher: %.2f%n", lowest / highest);</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">region A  approved  82 of 100  = 82.0%
region B  approved  61 of 100  = 61.0%
ratio of the lower rate to the higher: 0.74
a ratio below 0.80 is the threshold commonly used to flag a disparity: true
nothing in the code mentions the region -- the pattern came from the data</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Neither rate looks alarming on its own. 82% and 61% are both ordinary numbers; the disparity only appears when they are put next to each other.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The ratio 0.74 is below the 0.80 figure commonly used as a flag. That is a <em>measurement</em>, which is the point: bias testing compares outcomes across groups, it does not inspect intentions or read the source.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The <code>(double)</code> cast on the rate is load-bearing. Without it, <code>82 / 100</code> is integer division, every rate is 0, and the audit silently reports no disparity at all — the Unit 2 rule, in a place where getting it wrong is worse than a wrong number.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><code>computeIfAbsent</code> creates the counter array the first time a group is seen, which is the tallying idiom from 14.3 doing the whole grouping in one line.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The remedy is not in this code. It is more representative training data, or a decision threshold set per group, or a human reviewing the rejections — and choosing between those is a policy question, not a programming one.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — what must never reach a log or a request, and why confidence is not accuracy</div><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">static String redact(String s) {
    return s.replaceAll("\\b\\d{13,16}\\b", "[card]")
            .replaceAll("\\b\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}\\b", "[id]")
            .replaceAll("[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+", "[email]");
}
for (String e : entries) System.out.println("  logged: " + redact(e));</pre><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">what it prints</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">  logged: customer 4471 asked about invoice 90210
  logged: card [card] was declined
  logged: contact me at [email]
  logged: national id [id] on file</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The first line is untouched, and deliberately so — an internal customer number and an invoice number are not personal data, and redacting everything numeric would make the log useless.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The card number, the email address and the national ID are replaced before the line is ever written. Anything sent to a third-party service may be stored or used for training, so redaction belongs <em>before</em> the request, not after the fact.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Regular expressions are a filter, not a guarantee — they catch the common formats and will miss unusual ones, which is why the real control is not collecting the data in the first place.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The second half of the risk is the human reading the output:</p><div style="margin:10px 0; border-left:3px solid #10b981; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; background:var(--surface-2); padding:9px 12px;"><div style="font-size:11.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.05em; opacity:.75; margin-bottom:5px;">confidence against correctness</div><pre style="margin:0; font-size:12.6px; line-height:1.5; overflow-x:auto;">  stated confidence 0.97  actually correct? true
  stated confidence 0.96  actually correct? false
  stated confidence 0.91  actually correct? false
average stated confidence 0.95, actual accuracy 0.33
a human reviewing only the low-confidence answers would have checked none of them</pre></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 20px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">Three outputs, all stated with high confidence, one of them right. A stated confidence is a number the system produced; it is not a measured accuracy.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">A review policy of "check anything below 0.90" would have checked <strong>none</strong> of these and passed two wrong answers straight through. That is automation bias with a process wrapped around it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;">The reliable policy is the opposite shape: review by <em>consequence</em>, not by confidence. Hiring, lending, medical and legal decisions get a human regardless of how sure the output sounds.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The most common wrong answer is that bias is introduced by the algorithm and can be removed by inspecting the code. It cannot: the code above never mentions the region, and the disparity is still 0.74. Bias is inherited from data that recorded the world as it was, so it is found by measuring outcomes across groups and fixed upstream. The second is treating hallucination as a defect awaiting a patch — it is what next-token prediction does when the pattern is plausible and the fact is absent, which is why the control is verification rather than waiting for a fix. And accountability does not move: the organisation that deployed the system answers for its decisions.</p></div>"},"D286/u19/z19_1":{"body":"<p><code>do-while</code> is the third loop form promised in 4.1, and its single distinguishing property is that the body runs <strong>before</strong> the condition is tested — so it always runs at least once.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int input;
do {
    System.out.println("Enter a positive number:");
    input = scnr.nextInt();
} while (input &lt;= 0);            // note the semicolon — it is required

// the while version needs the prompt written twice, or a priming read (4.2)</pre><p>That guarantee is what makes it the right choice for menus and input validation: you must show the prompt before you can possibly know whether the answer is acceptable. Everywhere else, a <code>while</code> loop is clearer.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">do {
    System.out.print("Enter a positive number: ");
    n = scnr.nextInt();
} while (n &lt;= 0);        // the semicolon is REQUIRED</pre><p><code>do-while</code> tests after the body, so it always runs at least once. That is exactly what input validation wants: ask, then decide whether to ask again. A plain <code>while</code> would need the prompt written twice — once before the loop and once inside it.</p><p>The semicolon after the closing <code>while</code> is required and its absence is a compile error, which at least fails loudly.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>do-while:</strong> a post-test loop; the body executes before the condition is checked</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Post-test loop:</strong> a loop guaranteed to run at least once</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Menu loop:</strong> the classic do-while: display options, read a choice, repeat until quit</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Required semicolon:</strong> the while(...) at the end of a do-while must be terminated with ;</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Forgetting the semicolon after <code>while (condition)</code> is a compile error unique to this form. Every other loop has none.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>Control Flow → while and do-while Loops</strong></p><i data-d286fx31="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — WHILE VS DO-WHILE: WHO GOES FIRST</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 470 174" style="width:100%; max-width:510px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk97a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="120" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">while (test first)</text><rect x="60" y="32" width="120" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="120" y="51" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">test</text><line x1="120" y1="62" x2="120" y2="86" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk97a)"/><text x="146" y="76" fill="#22c55e" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">true</text><rect x="60" y="88" width="120" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="120" y="107" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">body</text><line x1="60" y1="103" x2="30" y2="103" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="30" y1="103" x2="30" y2="47" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="30" y1="47" x2="58" y2="47" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk97a)"/><text x="120" y="140" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">body may run 0 times</text><text x="350" y="20" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">do-while (body first)</text><rect x="290" y="32" width="120" height="30" rx="6" fill="#6366f122" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="350" y="51" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">body</text><line x1="350" y1="62" x2="350" y2="86" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk97a)"/><rect x="290" y="88" width="120" height="30" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="350" y="107" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">test</text><line x1="290" y1="103" x2="260" y2="103" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="260" y1="103" x2="260" y2="47" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="260" y1="47" x2="288" y2="47" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk97a)"/><text x="258" y="76" fill="#22c55e" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">true</text><text x="350" y="140" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">body ALWAYS runs at least once</text><text x="235" y="166" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Same loop, different first move. do-while suits input validation: ask once, re-ask while invalid.</text></svg></div></div><i data-d286_z19_1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — while vs do-while: where the test sits</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk409a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">while (c) { B }</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">test first — B may run zero times</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">do { B } while (c);</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">test last — B always runs at least once</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the semicolon</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">required after while (c) in the do form</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">typical use</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">prompt-then-validate, menus, retry loops</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">same body</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">both forms loop while c stays true</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The body runs once even when the condition is false</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int i = 5;
int whileCount = 0;
while (i &lt; 3) { whileCount++; i++; }

int j = 5;
int doCount = 0;
do { doCount++; j++; } while (j &lt; 3);

System.out.println("while ran " + whileCount + " times");
System.out.println("do-while ran " + doCount + " times");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">while ran 0 times
do-while ran 1 times</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Both loops start with the condition already false.</li><li><code>while</code> tests first, sees false, and skips the body entirely.</li><li><code>do-while</code> runs the body, <em>then</em> tests — so it always runs at least once.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Input validation, the pattern do-while was made for</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int age;
do {
    System.out.println("Enter an age above 0:");
    age = nextInput();
    System.out.println("  you typed " + age);
} while (age &lt;= 0);
System.out.println("accepted: " + age);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Enter an age above 0:
  you typed -4
Enter an age above 0:
  you typed 0
Enter an age above 0:
  you typed 17
accepted: 17</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The prompt must appear before there is anything to check, so the body has to run first.</li><li>The loop keeps re-asking while the value is still invalid.</li><li>A plain <code>while</code> here would need the prompt written twice — once before the loop and once inside it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Forgetting the semicolon after <code>while (condition)</code> in the do-while form. It is required, and leaving it off is a compile error — which is the friendly outcome, because the mistake fails loudly instead of quietly changing behaviour.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>If a question shows a loop whose condition is false at the start and asks how many times the body runs, the answer is 0 for <code>while</code> and 1 for <code>do-while</code>. That single difference is what almost every do-while question is testing.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_2":{"body":"<p>The engineering examples apply Units 2 to 5 to physical calculations, and they exist to test the same traps in unfamiliar clothing.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// projectile height over time
final double G = 9.81;
double height = v0 * t - 0.5 * G * t * t;

// unit conversion
double celsius = (fahrenheit - 32) * 5.0 / 9.0;      // 5.0 not 5 (2.5)

// resistors in parallel
double total = 1.0 / (1.0/r1 + 1.0/r2);              // 1.0 not 1</pre><p>Every trap in these problems is the integer-division trap from 2.5, wearing a physics costume. <code>5 / 9</code> is 0, so the temperature conversion returns 0 for every input — and the answer looks like a physics error rather than a type error.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">double current = voltage / resistance;              // guard resistance != 0
double force   = mass * acceleration;
double ke      = 0.5 * mass * Math.pow(velocity, 2);
double avg     = (a + b) / 2.0;                     // 2.0, not 2
double r       = total / (count * factor);          // parenthesise the denominator</pre><p>Engineering formulas concentrate two hazards from earlier units. Use <code>double</code> throughout, because a single <code>int</code> operand makes the whole expression integer division and loses the fraction silently.</p><p>And a written fraction bar is an invisible pair of parentheses: <code>total / count * factor</code> divides then multiplies, which is not what <code>total / (count × factor)</code> means. Any denominator with more than one term needs brackets.</p><p>Division by a measured quantity needs a zero guard before the calculation, and results are formatted with <code>printf("%.2f")</code> rather than printed raw.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Named constant:</strong> final double G = 9.81 — the pattern from 2.9</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Unit conversion:</strong> multiplying by a ratio; a common source of integer-division bugs</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Formula translation:</strong> converting a written equation into an expression with correct precedence (3.10)</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Write at least one operand of every division as a decimal. <code>5.0 / 9</code> is safe; <code>5 / 9</code> is zero.</p></div><i data-d286_z19_2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — turning a formula into Java without losing precision</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk410a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(F − 32) * 5 / 9</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">all ints — division truncates</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(F − 32) * 5.0 / 9.0</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">one double makes it all double</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">final double G = 9.81;</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a named constant, not a magic number</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.pow(b, e)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">exponent — Java has no ** operator</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.sqrt(x)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">square root, returns double</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Integer division silently ruins a physical formula</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int f = 98;
System.out.println((f - 32) * 5 / 9);
System.out.println((f - 32) * 5.0 / 9.0);
System.out.println(5 / 9);
System.out.println(5 / 9 * (f - 32));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">36
36.666666666666664
0
0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Line 2 is all integers: 66 * 5 = 330, and 330 / 9 truncates to 36.</li><li>Line 3 promotes the whole expression to double and gives the real answer.</li><li>Line 5 shows the worst version — <code>5 / 9</code> is evaluated first and is 0, so the entire formula collapses to 0.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Constants and Math for a projectile</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">final double G = 9.81;
double v0 = 20.0;
double angleDeg = 45.0;

double rad = Math.toRadians(angleDeg);
double range = v0 * v0 * Math.sin(2 * rad) / G;
double maxHeight = Math.pow(v0 * Math.sin(rad), 2) / (2 * G);

System.out.printf("range %.2f m%n", range);
System.out.printf("height %.2f m%n", maxHeight);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">range 40.77 m
height 10.19 m</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Java's trig functions take <strong>radians</strong>, so the degrees are converted first.</li><li><code>final</code> says G is a constant — the compiler rejects any later assignment to it.</li><li><code>printf</code> with <code>%.2f</code> rounds for display only; the stored value keeps full precision.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Passing degrees straight into <code>Math.sin</code>. It expects radians, so <code>Math.sin(45)</code> is the sine of 45 radians (about 0.851), not of 45°. Nothing errors — the number is just wrong.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>When a formula mixes literals and int variables, force one operand to double the moment you write it: <code>5.0 / 9</code>, not <code>5 / 9</code>. Position matters too — <code>(a) * 5.0 / 9</code> is safe, <code>5 / 9 * (a)</code> is not.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_3":{"body":"<p>The same calculations become methods, which is where the argument of 6.3 pays off: a formula written once and tested once is a formula that is right everywhere it is used.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double celsiusFromF(double f) { return (f - 32) * 5.0 / 9.0; }

public static double parallelResistance(double r1, double r2) {
    if (r1 &lt;= 0 || r2 &lt;= 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("resistance must be positive");
    }
    return 1.0 / (1.0/r1 + 1.0/r2);
}

// testable in one line each (6.7)
System.out.println(Math.abs(celsiusFromF(212) - 100) &lt; 0.0001);   // 3.18</pre><p>Note the comparison in the test. Floating-point results must be compared with a tolerance, never with <code>==</code> — the rule from 3.18, which is easy to forget precisely when you are writing tests.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static double current(double v, double r) {
    if (r == 0) return 0;                 // guard INSIDE the method
    return v / r;
}</pre><p>One method per formula, each <em>returning</em> rather than printing. That is 6.2 and 6.7 applied: a returned value can be unit tested against a hand calculation, a printed one can only be checked by eye.</p><p>Guards belong inside the method so the rule is written once rather than at every call site. Adding a formula is then adding a method, with nothing else to change — which is the property the exercise is demonstrating.</p><p>These are pure functions of their arguments, so they are <code>static</code>: there is no object state involved.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Formula method:</strong> one calculation, named, taking its inputs as parameters</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Precondition check:</strong> rejecting physically impossible inputs (6.13)</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Epsilon comparison:</strong> comparing doubles against a tolerance rather than for equality</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">💡 WHY IT MATTERS</div><p>A formula in a method can be unit-tested against known values — 212°F is 100°C — which is not possible when it is buried inline in <code>main</code>.</p></div><i data-d286_z19_3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — why a formula belongs in its own method</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk411a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">one formula, one method</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">named, testable, reusable</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">parameters in</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the inputs the formula needs</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">return out</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the single computed value</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">precondition check</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">throw early on impossible input</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Math.abs(a − b) &lt; eps</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">how you compare doubles in a test</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The formula extracted, then checked</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(celsiusFromF(212));
System.out.println(celsiusFromF(32));
System.out.println(parallelResistance(4, 4));
try {
    parallelResistance(0, 4);
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    System.out.println("rejected: " + e.getMessage());
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">100.0
0.0
2.0
rejected: resistance must be positive</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Each formula now has a name that says what it computes.</li><li>The precondition check fails fast, at the point the bad value arrives, rather than returning <code>Infinity</code> for the caller to puzzle over.</li><li>Two equal resistors in parallel give half the resistance — an answer you can sanity-check by hand.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Testing a double-returning method the right way</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double got = celsiusFromF(212);
System.out.println("exact == check: " + (got == 100.0));

double tricky = 0.1 + 0.2;
System.out.println(tricky);
System.out.println("0.3 == check:   " + (tricky == 0.3));
System.out.println("epsilon check: " + (Math.abs(tricky - 0.3) &lt; 0.0001));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">exact == check: true
0.30000000000000004
0.3 == check:   false
epsilon check: true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>This particular conversion lands exactly on 100.0, so <code>==</code> happens to work.</li><li><code>0.1 + 0.2</code> shows why you cannot rely on that — binary floating point cannot store those values exactly.</li><li>The epsilon comparison is the habit to build: ask whether the answer is <em>close enough</em>, not whether it is bit-identical.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Comparing two doubles with <code>==</code> in a test. It passes on the examples you tried and fails on the one the grader picked. Use <code>Math.abs(actual − expected) &lt; 0.0001</code>.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>A method that computes something should <em>return</em> it, not print it. A method that prints cannot be tested, reused inside a bigger formula, or have its result formatted differently later.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_4":{"body":"<p><code>args</code> in <code>main</code> has been sitting there unexplained since 1.2. It holds the <strong>command-line arguments</strong>: whatever was typed after the class name when the program was run.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">java MyProgram hello 42 3.14

args[0]        // "hello"
args[1]        // "42"     — a String, ALWAYS
args.length    // 3        — no program name, unlike C

int n = Integer.parseInt(args[1]);       // parsing is required (7.13)

if (args.length &lt; 2) {                    // check FIRST
    System.out.println("usage: java MyProgram <name> <count>");
    return;
}</count></name></pre><p>Two things trip people up. Every argument is a <code>String</code>, so numbers must be parsed. And <code>args.length</code> does <em>not</em> include the program name — unlike C, where <code>argv[0]</code> is the program itself.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void main(String[] args) {
    if (args.length &lt; 1) {                       // ALWAYS check first
        System.out.println("Usage: java Prog &lt;count&gt;");
        return;
    }
    int n = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);           // args are Strings
}</pre><p><code>args[0]</code> is the <strong>first argument</strong>, not the program name — unlike C, where the name occupies slot 0. With no arguments <code>args</code> is an empty array, never <code>null</code>, so <code>args.length</code> is always safe to read.</p><p>Everything arrives as a <code>String</code> and must be parsed, with the <code>NumberFormatException</code> of 12.6 waiting for anything that is not a number. Reading <code>args[0]</code> without checking the length throws <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> — hence the usage message.</p><p>Arguments are split on spaces unless quoted, so a filename containing a space must be quoted on the command line.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Command-line argument:</strong> a value supplied after the class name when the program is launched</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>args array:</strong> the String[] parameter of main, holding those values in order</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Usage message:</strong> the message printed when the arguments are missing or wrong</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Argument parsing:</strong> converting the String arguments to the types the program needs</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Reading <code>args[0]</code> without checking <code>args.length</code> throws <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> when the program is run with no arguments — which is exactly how a grader will run it.</p></div><i data-d286_z19_4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what arrives in args[]</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk412a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">java Sum 5 8</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">args = {"5", "8"}</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">args.length</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">2 — the class name is NOT included</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">args[0]</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the String "5", not the number 5</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Integer.parseInt(args[0])</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">String → int</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Double.parseDouble(args[0])</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">String → double</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Everything in args is a String until you parse it</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">String[] args = { "5", "8" };

System.out.println(args[0] + args[1]);
System.out.println(Integer.parseInt(args[0]) + Integer.parseInt(args[1]));
System.out.println("count: " + args.length);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">58
13
count: 2</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>+</code> between two Strings concatenates, so the first line prints 58, not 13.</li><li>Parsing converts the text to numbers, and only then does <code>+</code> add.</li><li><code>args.length</code> counts the arguments only — running <code>java Sum 5 8</code> gives 2, not 3.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Checking before you index, and reporting what went wrong</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">run(new String[] { "5", "8" });
run(new String[] { "5" });
run(new String[] { "5", "eight" });</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">sum = 13
usage: java Sum &lt;a&gt; &lt;b&gt;  (got 1 args)
not a number: For input string: "eight"</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The length check runs before any indexing, so a missing argument gives a usage message instead of an <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>.</li><li><code>parseInt</code> throws <code>NumberFormatException</code> on text that is not a number — catching it turns a stack trace into a sentence.</li><li>Notice the exception's message quotes the offending input, which is exactly what the user needs to see.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Assuming <code>args[0]</code> is the program name, the way it works in C. In Java it is the <em>first argument</em> — the class name never appears in <code>args</code>, so <code>java Prog a b c</code> gives <code>args.length == 3</code>.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Two failures to guard on every command-line program: too few arguments (check <code>args.length</code> first) and unparsable text (catch <code>NumberFormatException</code>). Questions on this unit nearly always target one of the two.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_5":{"body":"<p>Passing file names as arguments is what makes a program usable from a script, and it combines 19.4 with Unit 9 and Unit 12.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">public static void main(String[] args) {
    if (args.length &lt; 2) {
        System.out.println("usage: java Copy <input> <output>");
        return;
    }

    try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(args[0]));
         PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(args[1])) {

        while (in.hasNextLine()) { out.println(in.nextLine()); }

    } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
        System.out.println("Cannot open: " + e.getMessage());
    }
}</output></pre><p>Both resources are declared in one try-with-resources and both are closed automatically, in reverse order. That matters here: the output file must be flushed and closed or the copy is truncated (9.5).</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if (args.length &lt; 1) { System.out.println("Usage: java Prog &lt;file&gt;"); return; }
try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(args[0]))) {
    while (in.hasNextLine()) process(in.nextLine());
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot open " + args[0]);
}</pre><p>This combines the whole of Units 9, 12 and 19: check the argument count, open with try-with-resources so the file closes automatically, and catch the checked <code>FileNotFoundException</code> the compiler insists on.</p><p>A relative path resolves against the directory the program was launched from, not the location of the source file — which is why a file that "is right there" is often not found. Report the actual name from <code>args[0]</code> in the error so the mismatch is visible.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>File name argument:</strong> a path supplied on the command line rather than hard-coded</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Multiple resources:</strong> several declarations in one try, separated by semicolons</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Relative path:</strong> resolved against the working directory the program was started from</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>A path that works in the IDE and fails from a terminal is almost always a relative path resolved against a different working directory.</p></div><i data-d286_z19_5="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — opening a file named on the command line</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk413a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new File(args[0])</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the name arrives as text</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">f.exists()</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">check before you open</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new Scanner(f)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">throws FileNotFoundException</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">hasNextLine() / nextLine()</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">read until the file runs out</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">close()</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">release the handle when done</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Write a file, then read it back the way a real program would</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.io.PrintWriter out = new java.io.PrintWriter("scores.txt");
out.println("88");
out.println("92");
out.println("79");
out.close();

report("scores.txt");
report("missing.txt");</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">scores.txt: 3 lines
cannot open 'missing.txt' - no such file in the working directory</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>PrintWriter</code> creates the file; without <code>close()</code> the text may still be sitting in a buffer when the program ends.</li><li><code>exists()</code> is checked first so a missing file produces a sentence, not a stack trace.</li><li>The message names the file it could not find, and says <em>where</em> it looked — the working directory, not the folder holding the source. That one distinction solves most 'it works in the IDE' mysteries.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Summing the numbers a file contains</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.io.PrintWriter out = new java.io.PrintWriter("nums.txt");
out.println("10 20");
out.println("30");
out.close();

java.util.Scanner in = new java.util.Scanner(new java.io.File("nums.txt"));
int sum = 0, count = 0;
while (in.hasNextInt()) {
    sum += in.nextInt();
    count++;
}
in.close();
System.out.println(count + " numbers, sum " + sum);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">3 numbers, sum 60</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>hasNextInt()</code> is the loop guard: it stops cleanly at end of file instead of throwing <code>NoSuchElementException</code>.</li><li><code>nextInt()</code> skips whitespace, so it does not care that the numbers are split across lines.</li><li>Reading with <code>hasNext…</code>/<code>next…</code> in that pairing is the safe file-reading shape for every question in this unit.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>A relative filename like <code>data.txt</code> is resolved against the <strong>working directory</strong>, not the folder holding the .java file. That is why a program opens the file from the IDE and fails from a terminal — same code, different starting directory.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>When a file cannot be opened, print the filename you were given <em>and</em> the absolute path you tried. Guessing at a path problem is slow; printing it ends the question in one run.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_6":{"body":"<p>Output art is nested-loop practice (4.7) with a visible answer — which is what makes it good practice, because a wrong loop bound is immediately obvious.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// right triangle
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; i; j++) { System.out.print("*"); }
    System.out.println();                       // OUTER loop
}

// centred pyramid: spaces then stars
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
    for (int s = 0; s &lt; n - i; s++) { System.out.print(" "); }
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 2*i - 1; j++) { System.out.print("*"); }
    System.out.println();
}</pre><p>The technique is to write the relationship between the row number and the counts before writing any code. Row <code>i</code> has <code>n - i</code> spaces and <code>2i - 1</code> stars; once that is on paper, the loops write themselves.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">for (int r = 1; r &lt;= n; r++) {
    for (int s = 0; s &lt; n - r; s++) System.out.print(" ");   // leading spaces
    for (int s = 0; s &lt; r; s++)     System.out.print("*");   // then stars
    System.out.println();                                    // OUTER loop
}</pre><p>Pattern printing is nested loops from 4.7 with the inner bound depending on the outer variable. The row loop is outer; each inner loop prints one part of the row; the <code>println</code> belongs to the outer loop, and moving it inside puts every character on its own line.</p><p>Right alignment is spaces before stars, with the two counts summing to <em>n</em> — which is why the space loop runs <code>n - r</code> times and the star loop <code>r</code>. Use <code>print</code> within a row and <code>println</code> to end it; automated checks compare output exactly, so a stray trailing space fails.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Nested output loop:</strong> the inner loop prints one row, the outer loop advances rows</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>print vs println:</strong> print stays on the line; println ends it</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Row formula:</strong> the expression relating the row index to how many characters it holds</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Putting <code>println()</code> inside the inner loop puts every character on its own line. It belongs to the outer loop — the same brace question as 4.7.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OA Exam Prep → Coding Drills 1</strong></p><i data-d286_z19_6="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — row formulas for the four standard triangles</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk414a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">left-aligned, row r</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">r stars</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">inverted, row r of n</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">n − r + 1 stars</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">right-aligned, row r of n</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">n − r spaces, then r stars</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">pyramid, row r of n</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">n − r spaces, then 2r − 1 stars</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">println vs print</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">print stays on the line; println ends it</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The same nested loop, four ways</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">leftTri(4);
System.out.println("--");
rightTri(4);
System.out.println("--");
pyramid(4);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">*
**
***
****
--
   *
  **
 ***
****
--
   *
  ***
 *****
*******</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The outer loop counts rows; the inner loops count characters within a row.</li><li>The only thing that changes between the three shapes is the arithmetic in the inner loop bounds.</li><li><code>System.out.println()</code> with no argument is what ends each row — it is the line break, nothing else.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A multiplication table, aligned with printf</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">for (int r = 1; r &lt;= 4; r++) {
    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= 4; c++) {
        System.out.printf("%4d", r * c);
    }
    System.out.println();
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">   1   2   3   4
   2   4   6   8
   3   6   9  12
   4   8  12  16</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>%4d</code> pads each number to four characters wide, so the columns line up whatever the digit count.</li><li>The inner loop writes one row with <code>printf</code> and never breaks the line.</li><li>The <code>println()</code> after the inner loop — not inside it — is what moves to the next row.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Calling <code>println</code> inside the inner loop. Every character then lands on its own line and the shape disappears. Use <code>print</code> inside, <code>println</code> once after.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Do not guess these loop bounds — derive them. Write out rows 1 and n by hand, count spaces and stars in each, and find the formula that produces both. Pyramid rows are always an odd number of stars: 1, 3, 5, 7.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_7":{"body":"<p>Grade calculation is the array patterns of 5.4 with a branch chain (3.4) on the end, and it is the most common exam program in the whole course.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">int[] scores = {88, 92, 79, 95, 61};

int sum = 0;
for (int s : scores) { sum += s; }
double avg = (double) sum / scores.length;      // CAST — else integer division

char grade;
if      (avg &gt;= 90) { grade = 'A'; }
else if (avg &gt;= 80) { grade = 'B'; }
else if (avg &gt;= 70) { grade = 'C'; }
else                { grade = 'F'; }

System.out.printf("Average: %.2f  Grade: %c%n", avg, grade);</pre><p>Three earlier traps are all present in nine lines: the cast before the division (2.5), the chain ordered highest first (3.4), and <code>%c</code> matching a <code>char</code> rather than <code>%s</code> (9.2). Exam versions of this program plant errors in exactly those three places.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">if      (avg &gt;= 90) grade = 'A';
else if (avg &gt;= 80) grade = 'B';        // descending — the first true wins
else if (avg &gt;= 70) grade = 'C';
else                grade = 'F';

double avg = count == 0 ? 0 : (double) total / count;   // guard, and cast</pre><p>The chain must run descending, because the first matching branch wins and no upper bound is needed — everything higher was already caught. Ascending order gives every passing score the lowest grade, which is 1.3's nested-conditional point in its most testable form.</p><p>Boundaries are where marks are lost: exactly 90 must be an A, so the test is <code>&gt;=</code> and not <code>&gt;</code>. And the average needs both the empty-class guard and the <code>double</code> cast, or an integer division quietly truncates it.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Average of an int array:</strong> sum as int, then cast before dividing by length</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Grade chain:</strong> an ordered else-if chain, highest threshold first</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Formatted report:</strong> printf with %.2f for the average and %c for the grade</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Rounding matters at the boundary. An average of 89.5 printed as "89.50" is still a B unless the program rounds before grading — read whether the question grades the raw value or the rounded one.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OA Exam Prep → Coding Drills 2</strong></p><i data-d286_z19_7="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the pieces of a grading program</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk415a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">weighted average</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Σ (score × weight), weights summing to 1.0</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">100.0 * part / total</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">double division, not integer</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">if / else if chain</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">first true branch wins — order matters</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ordering</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">test the highest cut-off first</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">drop-lowest</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">subtract the min before averaging</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Letter grades, and why the chain order decides the answer</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double[] marks = { 95, 83, 70, 59.9 };
for (double m : marks) {
    System.out.println(m + " -&gt; " + letter(m) + "   (reversed chain: " + broken(m) + ")");
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">95.0 -&gt; A   (reversed chain: D)
83.0 -&gt; B   (reversed chain: D)
70.0 -&gt; C   (reversed chain: D)
59.9 -&gt; F   (reversed chain: F)</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>An <code>else if</code> chain stops at the first true test, so the cut-offs must be checked from the top down.</li><li>The reversed chain gives everyone a D, because 95 &ge; 60 is true and nothing after it is ever reached.</li><li>59.9 falls through every test and lands on the <code>else</code> — which is exactly what the final <code>else</code> is for.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A weighted average with the lowest quiz dropped</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] quizzes = { 80, 95, 40, 90 };
System.out.printf("quiz avg          %.2f%n", average(quizzes, false));
System.out.printf("quiz avg, dropped %.2f%n", average(quizzes, true));

double finalPct = 0.30 * average(quizzes, true) + 0.30 * 88 + 0.40 * 91;
System.out.printf("course total      %.2f%n", finalPct);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">quiz avg          76.25
quiz avg, dropped 88.33
course total      89.30</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The single pass finds the total and the minimum together — there is no need to sort.</li><li>Dropping the lowest means removing it from the total <em>and</em> reducing the divisor; forgetting the second is the usual bug.</li><li>The weights add to 1.00, so the weighted sum is already a percentage and needs no further division.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Writing the average as <code>total / scores.length</code> with both operands <code>int</code>. That truncates — 3 scores totalling 250 gives 83, not 83.33. Multiply by <code>100.0</code> or cast one side to <code>double</code>.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Check a weighting scheme by adding the weights: if they do not sum to 1.0 (or 100), the program is wrong before it computes anything. It is the fastest sanity check on this whole topic.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_8":{"body":"<p>An abbreviation decoder is a lookup problem, and it shows the same job done with the tools of three different units.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">// parallel arrays (5.5) — works, fragile
String[] abbr = {"BTW", "IMO", "FYI"};
String[] full = {"by the way", "in my opinion", "for your information"};
for (int i = 0; i &lt; abbr.length; i++) {
    if (abbr[i].equalsIgnoreCase(input)) { System.out.println(full[i]); }
}

// HashMap (14.3) — the right tool
HashMap<string, string=""> map = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
map.put("BTW", "by the way");
System.out.println(map.getOrDefault(input.toUpperCase(), "unknown"));</string,></pre><p>The map version is shorter, faster and cannot fall out of step, which is the whole argument of Unit 14 in six lines. Normalising the key with <code>toUpperCase()</code> is what replaces <code>equalsIgnoreCase</code> — a <code>HashMap</code> lookup is always case-sensitive.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Map&lt;String,String&gt; codes = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
codes.put("ASAP", "as soon as possible");

String full = codes.getOrDefault(input.toUpperCase().trim(), "unknown code");</pre><p>A map is the natural structure: lookup is O(1) and adding an entry is one line, against a parallel-array scan that is O(n) and must be kept in step. This is 14.3 replacing 5.5 for exactly the reason 5.5 warned about.</p><p>Normalise before looking up — <code>toUpperCase()</code> and <code>trim()</code> — or <code>"asap "</code> misses a key that is present. <code>getOrDefault</code> handles the unknown code without a null check, which is the whole reason it exists.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Lookup table:</strong> a mapping from key to value, by parallel arrays or by a Map</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>getOrDefault:</strong> returns a fallback instead of null for a missing key</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Key normalisation:</strong> converting keys to one case so lookups match consistently</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>A <code>HashMap</code> has no case-insensitive lookup. Normalise both the stored keys and the query, or every lowercase input misses.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OA Exam Prep → Coding Drills 2</strong></p><i data-d286_z19_8="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a lookup table, and the calls that matter</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk416a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">HashMap&lt;String,String&gt;</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">key → value lookup</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">put(k, v)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">insert, or overwrite an existing key</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">get(k)</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the value, or null if absent</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">getOrDefault(k, d)</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the value, or d — no null to handle</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">containsKey(k)</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">membership test without fetching</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Building the table and looking things up</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.HashMap&lt;String, String&gt; codes = new java.util.HashMap&lt;&gt;();
codes.put("ID", "Idaho");
codes.put("UT", "Utah");
codes.put("NV", "Nevada");

System.out.println(codes.get("UT"));
System.out.println(codes.get("XX"));
System.out.println(codes.getOrDefault("XX", "unknown code"));
System.out.println(codes.containsKey("ID"));
System.out.println(codes.size());</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">Utah
null
unknown code
true
3</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>get</code> on a missing key returns <code>null</code> — it does not throw, which is why the null slips downstream unnoticed.</li><li><code>getOrDefault</code> removes the null entirely and is the right call for a decoder.</li><li><code>size()</code> counts keys, so overwriting a key never grows the map.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Case-insensitive decoding, driven by a loop</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.HashMap&lt;String, String&gt; m = table();
String[] input = { "ut", " ID ", "Ut", "zz" };
for (String s : input) {
    System.out.println("'" + s + "' -&gt; " + decode(m, s));
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">'ut' -&gt; Utah
' ID ' -&gt; Idaho
'Ut' -&gt; Utah
'zz' -&gt; unknown: zz</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Keys are compared with <code>equals</code>, so <code>"ut"</code> and <code>"UT"</code> are different keys.</li><li>Normalising the input once, on the way in, means the map only ever needs one spelling of each key.</li><li><code>trim()</code> handles the stray spaces that always come with typed input.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Calling <code>get</code> and using the result straight away. A missing key gives <code>null</code>, and the failure surfaces later as a <code>NullPointerException</code> somewhere unrelated. Use <code>getOrDefault</code>, or test <code>containsKey</code> first.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><code>put</code> on an existing key <strong>replaces</strong> the value rather than adding a second entry — maps hold unique keys. If a question puts the same key twice and asks for <code>size()</code>, the answer counts it once.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_9":{"body":"<p>Dice statistics combine random numbers (2.18) with array counting (5.4), and it is the standard demonstration that a histogram is just an array indexed by outcome.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Random rand = new Random();
int[] counts = new int[13];                     // indexes 0-12; 0 and 1 unused

for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++) {
    int roll = rand.nextInt(6) + 1 + rand.nextInt(6) + 1;    // TWO dice
    counts[roll]++;                              // the value IS the index
}

for (int total = 2; total &lt;= 12; total++) {
    System.out.printf("%2d: %d%n", total, counts[total]);
}</pre><p><code>rand.nextInt(6) + 1</code> twice is not the same as <code>rand.nextInt(11) + 2</code>. Two dice give a triangular distribution peaking at 7; one roll of 2 to 12 is uniform. That is the whole point of the exercise, and it is a favourite exam question.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">Random rnd = new Random();
int die = rnd.nextInt(6) + 1;            // 0..5 shifted to 1..6
int sum = d1 + d2;                       // 2..12

int[] counts = new int[13];              // index BY the sum — 0 and 1 unused
counts[sum]++;
System.out.printf("%d: %.2f%%%n", s, 100.0 * counts[s] / trials);</pre><p><code>nextInt(6)</code> returns 0 to 5, so <code>+ 1</code> is required. Sizing the array 13 rather than 11 lets the sum index it directly — two wasted slots in exchange for no arithmetic on every access, which is the usual trade.</p><p>Two dice are not uniform: 7 has six ways to occur and 2 has one, so the distribution peaks in the middle and approaches the expected 16.7% for 7 as trials increase. Multiplying by <code>100.0</code> rather than 100 keeps the percentage from being integer-divided to zero.</p><p><code>new Random(42)</code> with a fixed seed repeats the same sequence, which is what makes a simulation testable.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Random.nextInt(n):</strong> a value from 0 to n-1 inclusive</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Histogram array:</strong> an array where the index is the outcome and the value is its count</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Seeding:</strong> new Random(42) gives a repeatable sequence, which is useful for testing</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Uniform vs triangular:</strong> one die is uniform; the sum of two is not</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="oa" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p>Sizing the array to 13 and ignoring indexes 0 and 1 wastes two slots and removes all the index arithmetic. Trading a little memory for clarity is usually right.</p></div><p data-xref="1" style="margin:26px 0 0; padding-top:12px; border-top:1px solid var(--border);font-size:12.5px; opacity:0.72;">✅ Also covered in depth: <strong>OA Exam Prep → Coding Drills 2</strong></p><i data-d286_z19_9="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — simulating dice, repeatably</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk417a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">new Random(42)</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fixed seed — same sequence every run</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt(6)</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">0..5, the upper bound is excluded</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">nextInt(6) + 1</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">1..6, one actual die</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">counts[sum]++</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">tally into an array indexed by the outcome</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">array of size 13</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sums run 2..12, so index up to 12</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Two dice, tallied into an array</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.Random rng = new java.util.Random(42);
int[] counts = new int[13];
int rolls = 60000;

for (int i = 0; i &lt; rolls; i++) {
    int sum = (rng.nextInt(6) + 1) + (rng.nextInt(6) + 1);
    counts[sum]++;
}

for (int s = 2; s &lt;= 12; s++) {
    System.out.printf("%2d: %5d  (%.1f%%)%n", s, counts[s], 100.0 * counts[s] / rolls);
}</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;"> 2:  1675  (2.8%)
 3:  3275  (5.5%)
 4:  5014  (8.4%)
 5:  6783  (11.3%)
 6:  8329  (13.9%)
 7: 10022  (16.7%)
 8:  8288  (13.8%)
 9:  6579  (11.0%)
10:  4944  (8.2%)
11:  3339  (5.6%)
12:  1752  (2.9%)</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The array is sized 13 so that index 12 exists — a size of 12 would throw on the first double six.</li><li>Two separate <code>nextInt(6) + 1</code> calls model two dice; <code>nextInt(11) + 2</code> would give a flat distribution and be wrong.</li><li>7 comes out most often because six of the 36 combinations produce it — the simulation lands near 16.7%.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The seed is what makes a simulation testable</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">java.util.Random a = new java.util.Random(42);
java.util.Random b = new java.util.Random(42);
java.util.Random c = new java.util.Random(7);

System.out.println(a.nextInt(100) + " " + a.nextInt(100) + " " + a.nextInt(100));
System.out.println(b.nextInt(100) + " " + b.nextInt(100) + " " + b.nextInt(100));
System.out.println(c.nextInt(100) + " " + c.nextInt(100) + " " + c.nextInt(100));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">30 63 48
30 63 48
36 64 85</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Same seed, same sequence — the two generators agree exactly.</li><li>A different seed gives a different sequence.</li><li><code>new Random()</code> with no seed picks one from the clock, which is what you want in production and never want in a test.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div><code>nextInt(6)</code> returns 0 through 5, not 1 through 6 — the bound is exclusive. Forgetting the <code>+ 1</code> lets a die roll a zero, and two of them can then sum to 0, which falls outside the 2..12 range your tally assumes.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Sizing a tally array: index by the outcome and make the array <code>maxOutcome + 1</code> long. Index 0 and 1 simply stay unused for dice sums — wasting two slots is far cheaper than the arithmetic to shift every index by 2.</div>"},"D286/u19/z19_10":{"body":"<p>The zyBooks programming window compiles and runs your code against automated tests, and the way it does so changes how you should write.</p><pre style="background:var(--bg-2, var(--surface-2)); padding:12px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px; overflow-x:auto; line-height:1.55;">- input comes from a supplied file, not from typing
- output is compared to expected output EXACTLY
- a trailing space or a missing newline fails the test
- System.out.println adds the newline; print does not</pre><p>Because matching is exact, most failures are formatting rather than logic. Check the expected output character by character before re-reading your algorithm — a <code>printf</code> with <code>%.2f</code> where the test wants <code>%.1f</code> fails just as hard as a wrong answer.</p><p>Automated checks compare your output against expected output <strong>character by character</strong>. A correct calculation with different spacing, capitalisation or an extra blank line still fails.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Symptom</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:.03em; text-transform:uppercase; opacity:.75;">Usual cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">everything looks right but fails</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a trailing space, or a missing final newline</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">fails only on the hidden test</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">an untested edge case — empty input, zero, one element</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">output interleaved oddly</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">a prompt printed in a test that supplies input silently</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">first test passes, later ones fail</td><td style="padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); vertical-align:top;">state left over between runs, or an unconsumed newline</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Where tests supply input from a file rather than a person, printing prompts adds text the comparison did not expect — so read without prompting when the specification says so.</p><p>Debug prints must be removed before submitting, since they are output too. Develop against the smallest failing case, and check the boundaries first: empty input, a single element, and the largest allowed value.</p><i data-d286u1619="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="why" style="border-left-color:#4f46e5;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Auto-grader:</strong> a system running your program against fixed inputs and comparing output</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Exact match:</strong> comparison including whitespace and line breaks</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Test input file:</strong> the supplied stdin the program is run against</p><p style="margin:0 0 7px;"><strong>Whitespace error:</strong> a failure caused only by spacing or newlines</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="warn" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON MISTAKE</div><p>Prompts you print for the user become part of the output being compared. If a test supplies input from a file and expects only the answer, an extra "Enter a number:" fails it.</p></div><i data-d286_z19_10="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what an automated grader is really comparing</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 184" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk418a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">stdout, exactly</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">text, whitespace, line breaks, all of it</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">extra prompt text</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">fails a test that expected only the answer</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">print vs println</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a missing newline is a mismatch</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">%.2f</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">2.5 and 2.50 are different strings</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">hidden test cases</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">edge inputs you were never shown</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Formatting differences a grader treats as wrong answers</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">double v = 2.5;
System.out.println(v);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", v);
System.out.println(1.0 / 3);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", 1.0 / 3);
System.out.println(10 / 4);
System.out.println(10 / 4.0);</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2.5
2.50
0.3333333333333333
0.33
2
2.5</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>println(2.5)</code> writes <code>2.5</code>; a test expecting <code>2.50</code> fails on a value that is numerically correct.</li><li><code>%.2f</code> rounds for display — the underlying double is untouched.</li><li>The last pair is the integer-division trap once more: 2 versus 2.5, from the same numbers.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Edge cases the hidden tests will reach for</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println(average(new int[] { 1, 2, 3 }));
System.out.println(average(new int[] { 5 }));
System.out.println(average(new int[] { }));
System.out.println(average(new int[] { -4, 4 }));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">2.0
5.0
0.0
0.0</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The empty array is the case that crashes an unguarded version with a divide-by-zero — the visible test almost never covers it.</li><li>A single element and a result of exactly zero are the other two boundaries worth trying by hand.</li><li>The cast to <code>double</code> is on the <em>total</em>, before the division, which is what keeps the fraction.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Leaving a prompt such as <code>System.out.print("Enter a number: ")</code> in a program an automated checker grades. The checker compares the whole of stdout, so the prompt is counted as unexpected output and the test fails even though the computed answer is right.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Before submitting, run the three inputs a grader always tries: empty, one element, and zero or negative values. Then read your output character by character against the expected output — a trailing space or a missing newline fails just as hard as a wrong number.</div>"},"D286/concepts/s1":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-classobject">
<h3>🏗️ Class vs Object</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE BLUEPRINT MODEL</div>A <b>class</b> is a blueprint — describes what something is and can do, but isn't a real thing yet. An <b>object</b> is an actual thing built from it. One "House" blueprint; many actual houses. One Car class; many car objects, each with its own color and mileage.</div>
<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Fields</b></td><td>The data each object holds (instance variables / attributes)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Methods</b></td><td>The actions / behaviors</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Instantiation</b></td><td class="num">Car myCar = new Car(); — the new keyword builds one object</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Constructor</b></td><td>Special method named like the class — sets a new object's starting values</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">EACH OBJECT IS SEPARATE</div>Each object is its own <b>instance</b> with its own copy of the fields — changing myCar's color doesn't touch yourCar.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE CLASS ISN'T AN OBJECT</div>The class is written ONCE; objects are made MANY times at runtime. The class is the recipe, not the dish.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>An object is best described as:
<ol type="A"><li>An instance of a class</li><li>A blueprint</li><li>A method</li><li>A data type keyword</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Class = blueprint/template; object = an instance created from it via new.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Class vs object</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>This is the mental leap that makes all of OOP click. A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint — it describes what something is and can do, but isn't a real thing yet. An <strong>object</strong> is an actual thing built from that blueprint. One “House” blueprint; many actual houses. One <code>Car</code> class; many individual car objects, each with its own color and mileage.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>A class defines <strong>fields</strong> (the data each object holds — color, speed) and <strong>methods</strong> (the actions — accelerate). Writing <code>Car myCar = new Car();</code> uses the <strong>new</strong> keyword to build (“instantiate”) one object.</p><p>Each object is a separate <strong>instance</strong> with its own copy of the fields. Changing <code>myCar</code>'s color doesn't touch <code>yourCar</code>. The <strong>constructor</strong> (a special method named like the class) sets a new object's starting values.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p>A class is a <span class="key-term">blueprint/template</span>; an object is an <span class="key-term">instance</span> of a class. Creating one is <span class="key-term">instantiation</span> (via <code>new</code>). Fields = <strong>instance variables / attributes</strong>; methods = the <strong>behaviors</strong>. The <strong>constructor</strong> initializes a new object.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>The class is written once; objects are made many times at runtime. Don't say a class “is” an object — it's the <strong>recipe</strong> for objects.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s2":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-pillars">
<h3>🏛️ The Four Pillars of OOP</h3>
<table><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>One-line definition</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Encapsulation</b></td><td>Keep data PRIVATE, control access — data hiding / bundling data with methods</td><td>private fields + public getters/setters</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Inheritance</b></td><td>A subclass acquires a superclass's members — "IS-A"</td><td>Dog extends Animal</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Polymorphism</b></td><td>One interface, MANY behaviors</td><td>animal.makeSound() barks for Dog, meows for Cat</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Abstraction</b></td><td>Expose the WHAT, hide the HOW</td><td>Call .start() without knowing the engine — via abstract classes and interfaces</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE TWO CLASSIC MIX-UPS</div>Encapsulation = "hide the DATA" (access control); abstraction = "hide the COMPLEXITY" (simplified interface). And inheritance is "IS-A"; a field you simply contain is "HAS-A" (composition).</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Making fields private and exposing getters/setters is:
<ol type="A"><li>Encapsulation</li><li>Abstraction</li><li>Inheritance</li><li>Polymorphism</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> The object guards its own data — the "capsule."</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>The four pillars of OOP</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>OOP rests on four big ideas. In one breath: <strong>Encapsulation</strong> = keep data private and control access. <strong>Inheritance</strong> = build new classes on existing ones. <strong>Polymorphism</strong> = one interface, many behaviors. <strong>Abstraction</strong> = expose the what, hide the how.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>Encapsulation:</strong> make fields <code>private</code> and expose <code>public</code> getters/setters. The object guards its own data. It's the “capsule” bundling data with the methods that protect it.</p><p><strong>Inheritance:</strong> a <code>Dog</code> class <code>extends</code> an <code>Animal</code> class, reusing its members and adding its own. “Is-a”: a Dog IS AN Animal.</p><p><strong>Polymorphism:</strong> the same call behaves differently by object — <code>animal.makeSound()</code> barks for a Dog, meows for a Cat.</p><p><strong>Abstraction:</strong> hide complex internals behind a simple interface (call <code>.start()</code> without knowing the engine). Done with abstract classes and interfaces.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p>Memorize by definition: <span class="key-term">Encapsulation</span> = data hiding / bundling data with methods; <span class="key-term">Inheritance</span> = a subclass acquiring a superclass's members (“is-a”); <span class="key-term">Polymorphism</span> = one interface / many implementations; <span class="key-term">Abstraction</span> = hiding implementation, showing essentials.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>Encapsulation vs abstraction trips people up: encapsulation is “<strong>hide the data</strong>” (access control), abstraction is “<strong>hide the complexity</strong>” (simplified interface). Inheritance is “is-a”; a field you simply contain is “has-a” (composition).</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s3":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-passbyvalue">
<h3>📮 Pass-by-Value</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE RULE</div>Java ALWAYS passes a COPY — never the original variable. Java is strictly <b>pass-by-value</b>; for objects, "the value of the reference is passed."</div>
<table><tr><th>Passing…</th><th>What's copied</th><th>Can the method change your original?</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>A primitive</b> (int, double, boolean, char)</td><td>The VALUE</td><td>NO — changes inside do nothing to the caller</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>An object</b></td><td>The REFERENCE (the arrow)</td><td>The method points at the SAME object, so mutating its contents IS visible</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">THE TWO OUTCOMES, SIDE BY SIDE</div>
<span class="num">list.add(x)</span> inside a method → visible outside (same object). <span class="num">list = new ArrayList()</span> inside → only repoints the COPY; the caller's list is unchanged. References point to objects on the <b>heap</b>; primitives live on the <b>stack</b>; <b>null</b> is a reference pointing to nothing.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE EXAM TRAP</div>Java is NEVER pass-by-reference, even for objects. Reassigning a parameter (=) inside a method never affects the caller — only MUTATING the object's contents does.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Java passes method arguments:
<ol type="A"><li>Always by value (copies, including reference copies)</li><li>By reference for objects</li><li>By reference always</li><li>It depends on a keyword</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Strictly pass-by-value — the classic trap says otherwise.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Pass-by-value</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Java always passes a <strong>copy</strong> to a method — never the original variable. For a plain number, it copies the number, so the method can't change your original. For an object, it copies the <strong>reference</strong> (the arrow pointing to the object) — so the method points at the SAME object and CAN change its contents. That subtle difference explains a lot of “why did/didn't my value change?” moments.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>For <strong>primitives</strong> (<code>int, double, boolean, char</code>): the method gets a copy of the value. Changing it inside does nothing to the caller.</p><p>For <strong>objects</strong>: the method gets a copy of the reference — both arrows point to the same object. So <code>list.add(x)</code> inside a method IS visible outside (same object). But <code>list = new ArrayList()</code> inside only repoints the copy — the caller's list is unchanged.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p>Java is strictly <span class="key-term">pass-by-value</span> — for objects, “the value of the reference is passed.” A <strong>reference</strong> points to an object on the <strong>heap</strong>; primitives live on the <strong>stack</strong>. <code>null</code> is a reference pointing to nothing.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>Java is <strong>never</strong> pass-by-reference, even for objects — a common exam trap says it is. Reassigning a parameter (=) inside a method never affects the caller; only <strong>mutating the object's contents</strong> does.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s4":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-static">
<h3>🏢 static vs instance</h3>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Instance member</th><th>Static member</th></tr>
<tr><th>Belongs to</th><td>EACH individual object (every car has its own mileage)</td><td>The CLASS itself — ONE shared copy (a company-wide car counter)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Exists</th><td>Once an object is made</td><td>Even before any object exists</td></tr>
<tr><th>Called via</th><td class="num">myCar.drive()</td><td class="num">Math.sqrt(9) · Car.getCount() — the class name</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE</div><span class="num">static int carCount</span> has ONE value across all instances — increment it in the constructor and it counts every car ever made. <b>main is static</b> so it runs without an object; Math methods/constants are static.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE ONE-WAY STREET</div>A static method CANNOT directly access instance fields, call instance methods, or use <b>this</b> (there's no specific object). But an instance method CAN access static members.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Why is main declared static?
<ol type="A"><li>So it can run without creating an object first</li><li>To make it faster</li><li>To make it private</li><li>Java requires all methods to be static</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> The JVM calls main on the CLASS before any object exists.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>static vs instance</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>An <strong>instance</strong> member belongs to each individual object — every car has its own mileage. A <strong>static</strong> member belongs to the CLASS itself, shared by all objects — like a company-wide counter of how many cars exist. Static things exist even before you make a single object.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>An instance field (<code>this.mileage</code>) has a separate value per object. A static field (<code>static int carCount</code>) has ONE value shared across all instances — increment it in the constructor and it counts every car ever made.</p><p>Call an instance method on an object (<code>myCar.drive()</code>); call a static method on the class (<code>Math.sqrt(9)</code>, <code>Car.getCount()</code>). Static methods can't use <code>this</code> or touch instance fields — there's no specific object.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">static</span> = belongs to the class (one shared copy); <span class="key-term">instance</span> (non-static) = belongs to each object. <code>main</code> is static so it runs without an object. <strong>Math</strong> methods/constants are static. A static member is accessed via the <strong>class name</strong>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>A static method <strong>cannot</strong> directly access instance fields or call instance methods (no object to belong to), and can't use <code>this</code>. But an instance method CAN access static members.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s5":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-override">
<h3>🔁 Overriding vs Overloading</h3>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Overriding</th><th>Overloading</th></tr>
<tr><th>What</th><td>A subclass REPLACES an inherited method with its own version</td><td>SAME name, DIFFERENT parameter lists, in the same class</td></tr>
<tr><th>Signature</th><td>IDENTICAL (name + parameters) to the parent's</td><td>Parameter list differs (count or types) — print(int) vs print(String)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Resolved</th><td><b>Runtime</b> (dynamic dispatch, by the actual object) — powers polymorphism</td><td><b>Compile time</b> (static, by the arguments you pass)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Marker</th><td class="num">@Override</td><td>—</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ RETURN TYPE ALONE DOES NOTHING</div>Changing ONLY the return type is neither — it won't compile as an overload. Overloading needs a different parameter list; overriding needs an identical signature in a subclass.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Two methods in one class named add, one taking (int, int) and one taking (double, double). This is:
<ol type="A"><li>Overloading</li><li>Overriding</li><li>Polymorphism only</li><li>An error</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Same name + different parameters in the same class = overloading, resolved at compile time.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Overriding vs overloading</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Two similar-sounding words, two different ideas. <strong>Overriding</strong> = a subclass REPLACES a method it inherited, giving its own version (a Dog's <code>makeSound()</code> replaces Animal's). <strong>Overloading</strong> = same method name, DIFFERENT parameter lists, in the same class (several <code>add(...)</code> methods taking different arguments).</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>Overriding:</strong> same method signature (name + parameters) as the parent's, but new code in the child. Resolved at <strong>runtime</strong> by the actual object — this powers polymorphism. Marked with <code>@Override</code>.</p><p><strong>Overloading:</strong> same name, but the parameter list differs (count or types). <code>print(int)</code> vs <code>print(String)</code>. Resolved at <strong>compile time</strong> by which arguments you pass. Return type alone does NOT overload.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">Override</span> = redefine an inherited method (same signature, subclass); <span class="key-term">overload</span> = same name, different parameters (same class). Overriding is <strong>runtime</strong> (dynamic) dispatch; overloading is <strong>compile-time</strong> (static). <code>@Override</code> documents an override.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>Changing ONLY the return type is <strong>neither</strong> — it won't compile as an overload. Overloading needs a different parameter list; overriding needs an <strong>identical</strong> signature in a subclass.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s6":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-exceptions">
<h3>🛟 Exceptions: try / catch / finally</h3>
<table><tr><th>Block / keyword</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">try</td><td>Holds the RISKY code that might fail</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">catch</td><td>Handles a thrown exception of a matching type</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">finally</td><td>Runs afterward ALWAYS — success or failure — used for cleanup (closing files/connections)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">throw</td><td>RAISES an exception</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">throws</td><td>DECLARES one in a method signature</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">CHECKED vs UNCHECKED</div>
<table><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Rule</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Checked</b></td><td>MUST be caught or declared with throws — the compiler enforces it</td><td class="num">IOException</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Unchecked / runtime</b></td><td>Not enforced — usually signal bugs</td><td class="num">NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ TWO SUBTLETIES</div>finally runs EVEN IF the try or catch returns or throws — that's the point. And catch a more SPECIFIC exception before a general one: catch(Exception e) placed first swallows everything below it.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Which block runs whether or not an exception was thrown?
<ol type="A"><li>finally</li><li>catch</li><li>try</li><li>throws</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> finally always executes — which is why cleanup code lives there.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Exceptions: try / catch / finally</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Things go wrong at runtime — a file's missing, a divide-by-zero, a user types letters where you wanted digits. Exceptions are Java's way to handle those without crashing. You “try” risky code, “catch” the problem if it happens, and optionally “finally” run cleanup no matter what.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><code>try</code> holds code that might fail. If it throws, control jumps to a matching <code>catch</code> block for that exception type. <code>finally</code> runs afterward <strong>always</strong> — success or failure — which is why it's used to close files/connections.</p><p><strong>Checked exceptions</strong> (e.g. <code>IOException</code>) must be caught or declared with <code>throws</code> — the compiler enforces it. <strong>Unchecked / runtime</strong> exceptions (<code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>) don't have to be, usually signaling bugs.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">try</span> = risky code, <span class="key-term">catch</span> = handle a thrown exception, <span class="key-term">finally</span> = always runs (cleanup). <strong>Checked</strong> = must handle/declare (compile-time); <strong>unchecked / runtime</strong> = not enforced. <code>throw</code> raises one; <code>throws</code> declares one.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>finally runs <strong>even if</strong> the try or catch returns or throws — that's the point. Catch a more <strong>specific</strong> exception before a general one; a <code>catch(Exception e)</code> placed first swallows everything below it.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s7":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-arrays">
<h3>📦 Arrays & Zero-Based Indexing</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE RULE THAT CAUSES HALF OF ALL ARRAY BUGS</div>Arrays are numbered starting at <b>0</b>. Five items → valid indices 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — there is NO box 5. Last valid index = <b>length − 1</b>.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">THE ESSENTIALS</div>
<table><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">int[] a = new int[5];</td><td>5 slots (indices 0–4), auto-filled with 0</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">a[0]</td><td>Access by index</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">a.length</td><td>Size — a FIELD, no parentheses</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++)</td><td>The standard loop (or for-each: for (int x : a))</td></tr></table>
Arrays are FIXED size and one type. Need to grow? Use an <b>ArrayList</b>, which resizes automatically. Out of range throws <span class="num">ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</span>.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ TWO EXAM FAVORITES</div>a[n] on a length-n array is the classic off-by-one crash. And it's <b>a.length</b> for arrays (field) but <b>s.length()</b> for Strings (method) — mixed on purpose.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>For int[] a = new int[8], the last valid index is:
<ol type="A"><li>7</li><li>8</li><li>9</li><li>0</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 7.</b> Indices run 0 to length−1.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Arrays & zero-based indexing</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>An array is a fixed-size row of boxes, all the same type, numbered starting at <strong>0</strong>. If it has 5 items, the valid box numbers are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — there is no box 5. Half of all array bugs come from forgetting it starts at 0 and ends at length−1.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><code>int[] a = new int[5];</code> makes 5 slots (indices 0–4), auto-filled with 0. Access with <code>a[0]</code>; the size is <code>a.length</code> (a field, no parentheses — unlike String's <code>.length()</code> method).</p><p>Arrays are <strong>fixed size</strong> once created. Need to grow? Use an <code>ArrayList</code>, which resizes automatically. Loop with <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++)</code> or a for-each <code>for (int x : a)</code>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p>Arrays are <span class="key-term">zero-indexed</span>; the last valid index is <strong>length − 1</strong>. <code>array.length</code> is a field; <code>string.length()</code> is a method. Out of range throws <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>. Arrays hold <strong>one type</strong> and a <strong>fixed size</strong>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>An array of length n has indices <strong>0 to n−1</strong> — accessing <code>a[n]</code> is the classic “off-by-one” crash. And it's <code>a.length</code> for arrays but <code>s.length()</code> for Strings — the exam mixes these on purpose.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s8":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-controlflow">
<h3>🚦 Control Flow: if, switch & Loops</h3>
<table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Use</th><th>Key rule</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>if / else if / else</b></td><td>Branch on CONDITIONS</td><td>Runs the first true block</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>switch</b></td><td>Branch on a VALUE's cases</td><td>Each case needs <b>break</b> or it FALLS THROUGH</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>for</b></td><td>KNOWN count</td><td>Counted loop</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>while</b></td><td>Repeat while a condition holds</td><td>May run ZERO times</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>do-while</b></td><td>Must run at least once</td><td>Condition checked AFTER the body</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">break vs continue</div><b>break</b> exits the loop (or switch case) immediately; <b>continue</b> skips to the next iteration.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE FALL-THROUGH BUG</div>A missing break in a switch causes execution to fall through into the next case — a classic the exam tests. And remember: while can run zero times; do-while always runs at least once.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Which loop is guaranteed to execute its body at least once?
<ol type="A"><li>do-while</li><li>while</li><li>for</li><li>None of them</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> The do-while checks its condition AFTER the body runs.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Control flow: if, switch & loops</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>By default code runs top to bottom. Control flow lets it branch (do this OR that) and repeat (do this many times). Master the handful of structures and you can express any logic. The bugs live in the boundaries — off-by-one loops and missing breaks.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>if / else if / else:</strong> run a block when a condition is true. <strong>switch:</strong> pick a branch by a value's cases — each case usually needs a <code>break</code> or execution “falls through” to the next.</p><p><strong>Loops:</strong> <code>for</code> (known count), <code>while</code> (repeat while a condition holds), <code>do-while</code> (runs at least once). <code>break</code> exits a loop early; <code>continue</code> skips to the next iteration.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">if/else</span> branches on conditions; <span class="key-term">switch</span> branches on a value (cases need <span class="key-term">break</span> or they fall through). <code>for</code> = counted loops, <code>while</code>/<code>do-while</code> = condition loops (do-while runs ≥ once). <code>break</code> exits; <code>continue</code> skips to next iteration.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>A missing <code>break</code> in a switch causes <strong>fall-through</strong> to the next case — a classic bug the exam tests. And a <code>while</code> may run zero times, but a <code>do-while</code> always runs at least once.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s9":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-inheritance">
<h3>🧬 Inheritance, super & the IS-A Chain</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE IDEA</div><span class="num">class Dog extends Animal</span> means Dog IS-A Animal — it inherits Animal's public/protected members for free, then adds or overrides its own.</div>
<table><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Does</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">extends</td><td>Creates the IS-A relationship</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">super()</td><td>Calls the PARENT's constructor — must be the FIRST line if used</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">super.method()</td><td>Calls the parent's version of a method</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">THE FAMILY FACTS</div>Java has <b>single inheritance</b> for classes — one direct parent only (multiple inheritance comes only via interfaces). And every class ultimately extends <b>Object</b>.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ TWO RULES THE EXAM CHECKS</div>A class can extend only ONE class. And super(), if written, must be the very first line of the constructor.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>How many classes can a Java class directly extend?
<ol type="A"><li>Exactly one</li><li>Any number</li><li>Two</li><li>Zero — extends isn't allowed</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Single inheritance for classes; multiple capabilities come from implementing interfaces.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Inheritance, super & the IS-A chain</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Inheritance lets a new class reuse an existing one and add or change behavior. A <code>Dog</code> extends <code>Animal</code>, getting all of Animal's fields and methods for free, then adding its own. The keyword <code>super</code> lets the child reach up to the parent.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><code>class Dog extends Animal</code> means Dog IS-A Animal and inherits its <code>public</code>/<code>protected</code> members. Dog can add new members and <strong>override</strong> inherited methods.</p><p><code>super</code> refers to the parent: <code>super()</code> calls the parent's constructor (and must be the first line if used), and <code>super.method()</code> calls the parent's version of a method. Every class ultimately extends <code>Object</code>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">extends</span> creates an <span class="key-term">IS-A</span> relationship and inherits accessible members. <span class="key-term">super</span> accesses the parent: <code>super()</code> (parent constructor, first statement) and <code>super.method()</code>. Java has <strong>single inheritance</strong> for classes (one direct parent); all classes extend <code>Object</code>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>Java allows a class to extend only ONE class (single inheritance) — multiple inheritance comes only via interfaces. And <code>super()</code>, if written, must be the very first line of the constructor.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s10":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-interfaces">
<h3>📋 Interfaces vs Abstract Classes</h3>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Interface</th><th>Abstract class</th></tr>
<tr><th>What</th><td>A pure CONTRACT — a list of method promises (no implementation, traditionally)</td><td>A HALF-BUILT class — some methods done, some abstract, plus fields and constructors</td></tr>
<tr><th>Keyword</th><td class="num">implements</td><td class="num">extends</td></tr>
<tr><th>How many</th><td><b>Many</b> — a class can implement multiple</td><td><b>One</b> — single inheritance</td></tr>
<tr><th>Use for</th><td>A capability</td><td>A partial base</td></tr>
<tr><th>Instantiable?</th><td colspan="2">NEITHER can be instantiated directly</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE KEY DISTINCTION THE EXAM PROBES</div>You IMPLEMENT an interface but EXTEND an abstract class — and a class can implement MANY interfaces but extend only ONE class.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>A class needs three separate capabilities defined externally. It should:
<ol type="A"><li>Implement three interfaces</li><li>Extend three abstract classes</li><li>Extend one class three times</li><li>It's impossible</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Multiple interfaces are allowed; multiple class inheritance is not.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Interfaces vs abstract classes</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Both let you define “what a class must be able to do” without fully building it. An <strong>interface</strong> is a pure contract — a list of method promises. An <strong>abstract class</strong> is a half-built class — some methods done, some left for children. Use an interface for a capability; an abstract class for a partial base.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>An <strong>interface</strong> declares method signatures (a contract) with no implementation (traditionally); a class <code>implements</code> it and must provide all the methods. A class can implement <strong>many</strong> interfaces.</p><p>An <strong>abstract class</strong> can have both finished methods and <code>abstract</code> (unfinished) ones, plus fields and constructors; a subclass <code>extends</code> it and completes the abstract methods. A class can extend only <strong>one</strong> abstract class. Neither an interface nor an abstract class can be instantiated directly.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">Interface</span> = a contract of methods, <code>implements</code>, and a class can implement <strong>multiple</strong>. <span class="key-term">Abstract class</span> = a partial base with some implemented + some <code>abstract</code> methods, <code>extends</code>, only <strong>one</strong> allowed. Neither can be <span class="key-term">instantiated</span>. Use interfaces for multiple capabilities.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>You <code>implement</code> an interface but <code>extend</code> an abstract class. A class can implement many interfaces but extend only one (abstract) class — that's the key difference the exam probes.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s11":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-constructors">
<h3>🔨 Constructors & the 'this' Keyword</h3>
<table><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Name & return type</b></td><td>Same name as the class, NO return type (not even void)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Default constructor</b></td><td>Write none → Java gives a free no-arg one. Write ANY → the free one DISAPPEARS</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Overloading</b></td><td>Several constructors with different parameters = different ways to build the object</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">this.field = field;</td><td>Assigns the parameter to the object's OWN field when names clash</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">this()</td><td>Calls another constructor in the same class</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE "WHY WON'T new Foo() COMPILE?" TRAP</div>Once you write ANY constructor, the free default no-arg constructor is gone — new Foo() fails unless you add a no-arg one back yourself.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>A constructor's return type is:
<ol type="A"><li>None — it has no return type at all</li><li>void</li><li>The class type</li><li>int</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Not even void — writing one turns it into an ordinary method.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Constructors & the 'this' keyword</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>A constructor is the special method that runs when you make an object — its job is to set the starting values. <code>this</code> is a word meaning “the current object,” used to tell an object's own field apart from a parameter with the same name.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p>A <strong>constructor</strong> has the same name as the class and no return type. If you write none, Java gives a default no-arg one. You can <strong>overload</strong> constructors (several with different parameters) to allow different ways to build an object.</p><p><code>this.field = field;</code> assigns the parameter to the object's own field when they share a name. <code>this()</code> can call another constructor in the same class.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p>A <span class="key-term">constructor</span> shares the class name, has no return type, and initializes a new object. The default no-arg constructor disappears once you define any constructor. <span class="key-term">this</span> = the current object (disambiguates fields from parameters). Constructors can be <span class="key-term">overloaded</span>.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>Once you write ANY constructor, Java stops giving you the free default no-arg one — a common “why won't <code>new Foo()</code> compile?” trap. A constructor has no return type (not even void).</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s12":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-access">
<h3>🔒 Access Modifiers & Encapsulation</h3>
<table><tr><th>Modifier</th><th>Reachable from</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">private</td><td>Only INSIDE this class (most restrictive)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">default (no modifier)</td><td>Same package only</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">protected</td><td>Same package + SUBCLASSES</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">public</td><td>Everywhere (least restrictive)</td></tr></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">ENCAPSULATION IN PRACTICE</div>Make fields <b>private</b>, expose <b>public getters/setters</b>. The setter can VALIDATE (reject a negative age) so the object's data stays valid. Getters read; setters write.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE POINT IS VALIDATION</div>protected is BROADER than default (it also reaches subclasses). And encapsulation isn't just hiding — the value is the validation the setter can enforce.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Which modifier allows access from subclasses AND the same package?
<ol type="A"><li>protected</li><li>private</li><li>default</li><li>final</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> protected = package + subclasses; default stops at the package boundary.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Access modifiers & encapsulation</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Access modifiers control which code can reach a field or method. The whole point of encapsulation is to keep an object's data private and force outsiders to go through controlled methods (getters/setters). Think of it as the object protecting itself from bad input.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>private</strong> = only inside this class. <strong>public</strong> = anyone. <strong>protected</strong> = this class, subclasses, and same package. <strong>default</strong> (no modifier) = same package only.</p><p><strong>Encapsulation in practice:</strong> make fields <code>private</code>, expose <code>public</code> getters/setters. The setter can validate (reject a negative age), so the object's data stays valid.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">private</span> (this class only) < <span class="key-term">default</span> (package) < <span class="key-term">protected</span> (package + subclasses) < <span class="key-term">public</span> (everywhere). <span class="key-term">Encapsulation</span> = private fields + public getters/setters, letting the object validate its own data. Getters read; setters write (with validation).</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p>private is the most restrictive, public the least — “protected” is broader than default because it also reaches subclasses. Encapsulation isn't just hiding; the value is the VALIDATION the setter can enforce.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/concepts/s13":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cc-strings">
<h3>🧵 Strings & Immutability</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE SURPRISING RULE</div>Strings are <b>immutable</b> — once created, contents can't change. Every "changing" operation actually returns a NEW String: <span class="num">s.toUpperCase() · s.replace(...) · s + "x"</span> all leave the original untouched.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">THE ASSIGNMENT PATTERN</div>
<span class="num">s.toUpperCase();</span> alone does NOTHING. You must assign: <span class="num">s = s.toUpperCase();</span>. Same for s.trim().</div>
<table><tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Compares</th><th>Use for text?</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">.equals()</td><td>The CONTENTS</td><td><b>YES</b></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">==</td><td>Whether they're the SAME OBJECT in memory (identity)</td><td>NO — can be false for identical text</td></tr></table>
For heavy string-building, use <b>StringBuilder</b> (mutable) to avoid creating many temporary Strings. Strings are zero-indexed like arrays.
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE TWO STRING TRAPS</div>Forgetting to assign the result of a String method, and using == instead of .equals() for text comparison.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>To compare two Strings' text content, use:
<ol type="A"><li>.equals()</li><li>==</li><li>.compare()</li><li>=</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> == checks identity (same object), which can be false even for identical characters.</div></details></div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><h3>Strings & immutability</h3>
<div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>Strings are everywhere, and they have one surprising rule: they're <strong>immutable</strong> — once created, a String's contents can't change. Every operation that seems to “change” a String actually makes a NEW String. That explains a lot of subtle bugs.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><code>s.toUpperCase()</code>, <code>s.replace(...)</code>, <code>s + "x"</code> all return NEW Strings; the original is untouched. So <code>s.toUpperCase();</code> alone does nothing unless you assign it: <code>s = s.toUpperCase();</code>.</p><p>Compare String CONTENTS with <code>.equals()</code>, not <code>==</code> — <code>==</code> compares whether they're the same object in memory, which can be false even for equal text. For heavy string-building, use <code>StringBuilder</code> (mutable) to avoid creating many temporary Strings.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p>Strings are <span class="key-term">immutable</span> — methods return NEW Strings, they don't modify in place. Compare content with <span class="key-term">.equals()</span>; <code>==</code> compares references (identity), not text. <code>StringBuilder</code> is the <span class="key-term">mutable</span> alternative for efficient concatenation. Strings are zero-indexed like arrays.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ WATCH OUT</div><p><code>s.trim()</code> or <code>s.toUpperCase()</code> on its own does nothing — you must assign the result. And use <code>.equals()</code> for text comparison; <code>==</code> can be false for two Strings with identical characters.</p></div></div></details>"},"D286/cheat/s1":{"body":"
