<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full cheat sheet — every reference table (tap to collapse)</summary><div>
<h3>Java Cheat Sheet — Unit 3: OOP &amp; Methods</h3>
<p>Classes, objects, constructors, encapsulation, access modifiers, static, and overloading.</p>

<h4>① Class, object, constructor</h4>
<ul>
<li><span class="key-term">Class</span> = blueprint; <span class="key-term">object</span> = an instance made with <code>new</code>.</li>
<li><span class="key-term">Constructor</span> = same name as the class, <strong>no return type</strong>; runs on <code>new</code>. Write none and Java supplies a default no-arg constructor.</li>
<li><code>this</code> refers to the current object (often used to set fields from same-named parameters).</li>
</ul>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px 12px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0">public class Dog {
    private String name;                       // field (encapsulated)
    public Dog(String name) {                  // constructor (no return type)
        this.name = name;
    }
    public String getName() { return name; }   // getter
    public void setName(String n) { name = n; } // setter
}
Dog d = new Dog("Rex");                        // create an object</pre>

<h4>② Methods</h4>
<ul>
<li>A method has a name, a parameter list, and a <strong>return type</strong> (or <code>void</code>).</li>
<li><span class="key-term">Overloading</span> = same method name with <strong>different parameter lists</strong> (resolved at compile time).</li>
<li>Java is <strong>pass-by-value</strong> — a copy of each argument is passed (object references are copied too).</li>
</ul>

<h4>③ Encapsulation &amp; access modifiers</h4>
<p><span class="key-term">Encapsulation</span> = make fields <code>private</code> and expose <code>public</code> getters/setters.</p>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0"><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Modifier</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Visible from</th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">public</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">everywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">protected</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">same package + subclasses</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">default (no keyword)</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">same package only</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">private</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">same class only</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>④ static &amp; final</h4>
<ul>
<li><span class="key-term">static</span> — belongs to the <strong>class</strong>, not an instance (shared). A static method can't directly use instance fields or <code>this</code>.</li>
<li><span class="key-term">final</span> — variable = a constant; method = can't be overridden; class = can't be extended.</li>
</ul>
<div class='callout callout-pitfall'><div class='callout-label'>⚠ TRAPS</div><ul>
<li>Constructors have <strong>no return type</strong> (not even void) and match the class name exactly.</li>
<li>Overloading = different parameters (compile time); <em>overriding</em> = same signature in a subclass (runtime).</li>
<li>A <code>static</code> method can't call instance methods/fields without an object.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class='callout callout-recall'><div class='callout-label'>✓ UNIT 3 MUST-KNOWS</div><ul>
<li>Constructor = class name, no return type; <code>this</code> = the current object.</li>
<li>Encapsulation = private fields + public getters/setters.</li>
<li>static = class-level/shared; final = constant / no-override / no-extend.</li>
<li>Overloading = same name, different parameters.</li>
</ul></div>

<h3>➕ Expanded reference — overloading, pass-by-value &amp; static (JVM-verified)</h3>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>Overload resolution — which method runs?</strong><br>
Given pick(int) and pick(double):
<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>Runs</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>pick(5)</td><td class='num'>pick(int)</td><td>5 is an int literal — exact match wins</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>pick(5.0)</td><td class='num'>pick(double)</td><td>5.0 is double</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>pick('x' + 0.0)</td><td class='num'>pick(double)</td><td>char + double promotes to double</td></tr></table>
Overloading = same name, DIFFERENT parameter lists, chosen at compile time. (Return type alone can't overload.)</div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>Pass-by-value — the #1 exam concept, proven</strong><br>
<table><tr><th>Experiment</th><th>After the call</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>swap(p, q) with p=1, q=2</td><td class='num'>still p=1, q=2</td><td>the method got COPIES of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>fill(arr) sets arr[0]=99</td><td class='num'>arr[0] IS 99</td><td>the copy was of the REFERENCE — same array object</td></tr></table>
Java is always pass-by-value. For objects, the value passed is the reference — so contents can change, but reassigning the parameter does nothing outside.</div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>static vs instance</strong><br>
<table><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>static field</td><td class='num'>ONE copy shared by the whole class — 3 calls to a static counter → 3</td></tr>
<tr><td>instance field</td><td class='num'>one copy PER object</td></tr>
<tr><td>static method</td><td class='num'>no this; cannot touch instance fields directly (main is static — that's why)</td></tr>
<tr><td>this</td><td class='num'>the current object; this.x = x; assigns parameter x to field x</td></tr></table></div>
<div class='callout callout-recall'><div class='callout-label'>✓ METHOD SIGNATURES</div><ul>
<li>Signature = name + parameter types. void f(int a) and int f(int b) COLLIDE — return type doesn't count.</li>
<li>Local variables have NO default value — using one before assignment is a compile error (fields DO default: 0/false/null).</li>
<li>void returns nothing; a non-void method must return on every path.</li></ul></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/cheat/s4":{"body":"
