<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full cheat sheet — every reference table (tap to collapse)</summary><div>
<h3>Java Cheat Sheet — Unit 4: Arrays, ArrayList &amp; Exceptions</h3>

<h4>① Arrays (fixed size)</h4>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px 12px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0">int[] nums = new int[5];        // 5 ints, all default 0
int[] vals = {10, 20, 30};      // array literal
nums[0] = 99;                   // index starts at 0
int len = nums.length;          // FIELD, no parentheses
int[][] grid = new int[3][4];   // 2D: 3 rows, 4 cols
grid[1][2] = 7;</pre>
<p>Valid indexes run <strong>0 to length − 1</strong>. For 2D: <code>grid.length</code> = rows, <code>grid[0].length</code> = columns.</p>

<h4>② ArrayList (resizable)</h4>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px 12px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
names.add("Ana");          // add
names.get(0);              // read
names.set(0, "Bo");        // replace
names.remove(0);           // delete
int count = names.size();  // METHOD, with parentheses
names.contains("Bo");</pre>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0"><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">&nbsp;</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Array</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">ArrayList</th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Size</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">fixed</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">grows / shrinks</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Count</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><code>.length</code> (field)</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><code>.size()</code> (method)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Holds</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">primitives or objects</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">objects only (Integer, not int) — autoboxing</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>③ Exceptions</h4>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px 12px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0">try {
    int x = 10 / divisor;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Always runs");
}</pre>
<ul>
<li><code>finally</code> <strong>always</strong> runs (cleanup) — exception or not.</li>
<li><span class="key-term">Checked</span> exceptions must be handled or declared with <code>throws</code> (e.g. IOException). <span class="key-term">Unchecked</span> ones (RuntimeException) need not be.</li>
<li>Throw your own with <code>throw new Exception("msg");</code></li>
</ul>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0"><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Exception</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Cause</th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">ArithmeticException</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">integer divide by 0</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">NullPointerException</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">use a null reference</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">index &lt; 0 or ≥ length</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><span class="key-term">NumberFormatException</span></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">parse non-numeric text as a number</td></tr>
</table>
<div class='callout callout-pitfall'><div class='callout-label'>⚠ TRAPS</div><ul>
<li><code>array.length</code> (field) vs <code>list.size()</code> (method) vs <code>string.length()</code> (method).</li>
<li>Highest valid index = <strong>length − 1</strong>; one past the end throws ArrayIndexOutOfBounds.</li>
<li>Integer <code>10 / 0</code> throws ArithmeticException; <code>10.0 / 0</code> gives Infinity (no exception).</li>
<li><code>finally</code> runs even after a <code>return</code> inside try.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class='callout callout-recall'><div class='callout-label'>✓ UNIT 4 MUST-KNOWS</div><ul>
<li>Array = fixed, <code>.length</code>, index 0..len−1. ArrayList = dynamic, <code>.size()</code>, objects only.</li>
<li>try / catch / finally — finally always runs.</li>
<li>Know the four common exceptions and what triggers each.</li>
</ul></div>

<h3>➕ Expanded reference — ArrayList trace, boxing trap &amp; exception order (JVM-verified)</h3>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>One ArrayList, five operations, traced</strong><br>
Start empty, then:
<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>List afterward</th></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>add("a"), add("b"), add("c")</td><td class='num'>[a, b, c]</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>add(1, "X") — insert at index 1</td><td class='num'>[a, X, b, c] (b and c shift right)</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>remove("b") — by VALUE</td><td class='num'>[a, X, c]</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>set(0, "Z") — replace</td><td class='num'>[Z, X, c]</td></tr></table>
Final: size() = 3 · get(1) = "X" · contains("c") = true · indexOf("c") = 2.<br>
Trap: for an ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;, remove(2) removes the element AT INDEX 2, remove(Integer.valueOf(2)) removes the VALUE 2.</div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>Array vs ArrayList defaults &amp; sizing</strong><br>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Array</th><th>ArrayList</th></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td class='num'>fixed at creation (.length — a field)</td><td class='num'>grows (.size() — a method)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Defaults</td><td class='num'>int→0, boolean→false, objects→null</td><td class='num'>starts empty</td></tr>
<tr><td>Primitives?</td><td class='num'>yes</td><td class='num'>no — needs wrappers (Integer, Double)</td></tr></table></div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>The Integer == trap (real output)</strong><br>
Integer a = 127, b = 127 → a == b is <strong>true</strong> · Integer c = 128, d = 128 → c == d is <strong>false</strong> (cache covers −128..127 only) · c.equals(d) is always true. Compare wrappers with .equals().</div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>try / catch / finally — verified order</strong><br>
<table><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Printed order</th></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>exception thrown at z[5] on a length-2 array</td><td class='num'>catch("Index 5 out of bounds for length 2") → finally</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>no exception</td><td class='num'>try → (catch skipped) → finally</td></tr></table>
finally runs in BOTH cases — even after a return. Code right after the throwing line never runs.</div>
<div class='callout callout-recall'><div class='callout-label'>✓ EXCEPTION SORTING</div><ul>
<li>Unchecked (RuntimeException family): NullPointer, ArrayIndexOutOfBounds, Arithmetic (÷ by zero), NumberFormat — compiler doesn't force handling.</li>
<li>Checked: IOException, FileNotFoundException — must catch or declare with throws.</li>
<li>Catch subclasses FIRST: a catch (Exception e) above a catch (IOException e) won't compile — unreachable.</li></ul></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/cheat/s5":{"body":"
<div class="vl" data-visual="d286-cheat5">
<h3>🗂️ Java Cheat Sheet — Unit 5: Inheritance & Polymorphism</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHAT'S INSIDE</div>
<table><tr><th>Part</th><th>Covers</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">① Inheritance</td><td>extends, single inheritance, super() first line, super.method()</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">② Overriding vs overloading</td><td>The full comparison table</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">③ Polymorphism</td><td>Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak() → "Woof"</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">④ Abstract classes vs interfaces</td><td>extends vs implements, one vs many</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">MOST-TESTED FACTS</div>
<table><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Polymorphism in one line</td><td class="num">Parent-type reference, child object — the CHILD's override runs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overriding vs overloading</td><td class="num">Runtime vs compile time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Class inheritance</td><td class="num">Single (extends one); interfaces many (implements)</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ HOW TO USE</div>The full reference follows, nothing removed.</div>
</div>
<details class="vl-orig" open><summary>📖 Full cheat sheet — every reference table (tap to collapse)</summary><div>
<h3>Java Cheat Sheet — Unit 5: Inheritance &amp; Polymorphism</h3>

<h4>① Inheritance</h4>
<ul>
<li><span class="key-term">extends</span> — a subclass inherits a superclass. Java allows <strong>single</strong> class inheritance only.</li>
<li><code>super()</code> calls the parent constructor (must be the <strong>first</strong> line); <code>super.method()</code> calls a parent method.</li>
</ul>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px 12px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0">class Animal {
    void speak() { System.out.println("..."); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void speak() { System.out.println("Woof"); }   // overriding
}</pre>

<h4>② Overriding vs overloading</h4>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0"><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">&nbsp;</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Overriding</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">Overloading</th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Where</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">subclass redefines a parent method</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">same class, same name</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Signature</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><strong>same</strong> name + parameters</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><strong>different</strong> parameters</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Decided</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">at runtime (dynamic)</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">at compile time (static)</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>③ Polymorphism</h4>
<p>A parent-type reference can point to a child object; the <strong>overridden</strong> method that runs is the child's.</p>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px 12px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;margin:8px 0">Animal a = new Dog();   // parent reference, child object
a.speak();              // prints "Woof"</pre>

<h4>④ Abstract classes vs interfaces</h4>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0"><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700">&nbsp;</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700"><span class="key-term">abstract class</span></th><th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--surface-2);font-weight:700"><span class="key-term">interface</span></th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Keyword</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><code>extends</code></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top"><code>implements</code></td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Methods</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">abstract + concrete</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">abstract (Java 8+: default/static allowed)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">How many</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">extend <strong>one</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">implement <strong>many</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">Instantiate?</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">No</td><td style="padding:6px 9px;border:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top">No</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Use <code>instanceof</code> to test an object's type. Every class inherits <code>toString()</code>, <code>equals()</code>, and <code>hashCode()</code> from <code>Object</code>.</p>
<div class='callout callout-pitfall'><div class='callout-label'>⚠ TRAPS</div><ul>
<li>Overriding = same signature (runtime); overloading = different parameters (compile time).</li>
<li>A class can <code>extends</code> only <strong>one</strong> parent but <code>implements</code> <strong>many</strong> interfaces.</li>
<li>You <strong>cannot</strong> instantiate an abstract class or an interface with <code>new</code>.</li>
<li><code>super()</code> must be the first statement in a constructor.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class='callout callout-recall'><div class='callout-label'>✓ UNIT 5 MUST-KNOWS</div><ul>
<li>extends = single inheritance; super() calls the parent constructor first.</li>
<li>Override = same signature in a subclass (runtime polymorphism); overload = different params.</li>
<li>Abstract class: can't instantiate, mixes abstract + concrete. Interface: implement many.</li>
<li>Parent reference + child object → the child's overridden method runs.</li>
</ul></div>

<h3>➕ Expanded reference — constructor order &amp; dynamic dispatch, proven</h3>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>What actually printed for <span class='num'>Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak();</span></strong><br>
<table><tr><th>Line</th><th>Output</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>new Dog()</td><td class='num'>"Animal ctor" then "Dog ctor"</td><td>parent constructor ALWAYS runs first (implicit super())</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>a.speak()</td><td class='num'>"woof"</td><td>dispatch by the OBJECT's type (Dog), not the variable's type (Animal)</td></tr>
<tr><td class='num'>a instanceof Dog / Animal</td><td class='num'>true / true</td><td>a Dog is both</td></tr></table>
Variable type decides what you may CALL (compile time) · object type decides what RUNS (runtime).</div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>Override vs overload — one letter apart on the test</strong><br>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Override</th><th>Overload</th></tr>
<tr><td>Where</td><td class='num'>subclass, SAME signature</td><td class='num'>same class, DIFFERENT parameters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chosen</td><td class='num'>at runtime (object type)</td><td class='num'>at compile time (argument types)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marker</td><td class='num'>@Override catches typos</td><td class='num'>—</td></tr></table></div>
<div class='formula-box'><strong>abstract class vs interface</strong><br>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Abstract class</th><th>Interface</th></tr>
<tr><td>Inherit how many</td><td class='num'>extends ONE</td><td class='num'>implements MANY</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fields</td><td class='num'>any</td><td class='num'>public static final constants only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Constructors</td><td class='num'>yes (called via super)</td><td class='num'>no</td></tr>
<tr><td>New instance?</td><td class='num'>never directly</td><td class='num'>never directly</td></tr></table></div>
<div class='callout callout-recall'><div class='callout-label'>✓ CASTING &amp; Object METHODS</div><ul>
<li>Upcast (Dog → Animal) is automatic. Downcast needs (Dog) and throws ClassCastException if the object isn't really a Dog — guard with instanceof.</li>
<li>Without a toString() override you inherit the parent's (verified: our Dog printed "Animal" via Animal's toString).</li>
<li>super.method() calls the parent version; super(args) must be the FIRST line of a constructor.</li>
<li>private members are inherited-but-inaccessible; protected opens to subclasses.</li></ul></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch1/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-jvm"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — JVM, JRE, JDK (three nested boxes)</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHO IS WHO</div>• <b>JVM</b> (Java Virtual Machine) — RUNS bytecode. The reason Java is “write once, run anywhere.”<br>• <b>JRE</b> (Runtime Environment) — JVM + core libraries. Enough to RUN Java programs.<br>• <b>JDK</b> (Development Kit) — JRE + compiler (javac) + tools. Needed to WRITE Java.<br>Nesting: <b>JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM</b>.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — follow Hello.java to the screen</div><pre>javac Hello.java   →  Hello.class   (compiler produces BYTECODE)
java Hello         →  JVM loads Hello.class and executes it</pre>Bytecode is not machine code for your CPU — it’s a universal format any JVM understands, so the same .class runs on Windows, Mac, Linux. Users installing your app need the JRE; you developing it need the JDK.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ COMPILED *AND* INTERPRETED</div>Java compiles source → bytecode (compile step), then the JVM executes bytecode (with JIT compiling hot code as it runs). If asked “what does javac produce?” — bytecode (.class), never machine code.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Which do you need to COMPILE Java source code?<ol type="A"><li>JDK</li><li>JRE only</li><li>JVM only</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — the JDK</b>; it contains javac. JRE/JVM can only run already-compiled bytecode.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>"Write once, run anywhere" is Java's promise — made possible by compiling to bytecode that the JVM executes on any platform. Understanding JVM/JRE/JDK determines what you install, what you ship, and why Java can run on billions of devices from smartphones to servers.</p></div><h3>The Java Platform</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Component</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Contains</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Use case</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>JVM</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Bytecode interpreter + JIT compiler</td><td style="padding:8px">Executes .class files on any OS</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>JRE</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">JVM + standard class libraries</td><td style="padding:8px">Run Java programs (not develop)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>JDK</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">JRE + compiler (javac) + tools (debugger, javadoc)</td><td style="padding:8px">Write, compile, and run Java programs</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="formula-box" style="background:var(--surface-2);border-left:4px solid var(--accent);padding:12px;margin:12px 0;border-radius:4px">JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM<br>To develop: install JDK. To only run: JRE suffices.<br>Java execution flow: source.java → [javac] → bytecode.class → [JVM] → runs</div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>JVM: executes bytecode. JRE = JVM + libraries. JDK = JRE + compiler.</li><li>JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM: each contains the one before it</li><li>Bytecode is platform-independent; JVM is platform-specific</li></ul></div>
<h3>The compile-run pipeline, traced</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// 1. You write:        Hello.java   (source code)
// 2. javac compiles:    javac Hello.java  →  Hello.class  (bytecode)
// 3. JVM executes:      java Hello   (note: no .class extension!)

public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, WGU!");
    }
}</pre>
<h3>Anatomy of main — every word matters</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>public</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">JVM must be able to call it from outside the class</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>static</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">JVM calls it without creating an object first</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>void</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Returns nothing to the JVM</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>main</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">The exact name the JVM looks for</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>String[] args</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Command-line arguments (java Hello a b → args = {"a","b"})</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ OA TRAPS</div><p>(1) File name must MATCH the public class name: public class Hello must live in Hello.java — else compile error. (2) <code>java Hello.class</code> fails — run with <code>java Hello</code>. (3) Misspell main (Main, miain) → compiles fine but "Error: Main method not found" at runtime.</p></div>
<h3>Compile-time vs runtime errors — know the difference</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int x = "hello";        // COMPILE error — caught by javac, no .class produced
int[] a = new int[3];
a[5] = 1;               // RUNTIME error — compiles fine, crashes when run
                        // (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException)</pre>
<p>The OA loves asking "does this fail at compile time or runtime?" Rule of thumb: type mismatches and syntax → compile time; bad values discovered during execution (array bounds, null, divide by zero) → runtime.</p>
<h3>Why bytecode = portability</h3>
<p>C++ compiles to machine code for ONE cpu/OS. Java compiles to bytecode — a universal intermediate language. Each platform has its own JVM that translates bytecode to local machine code (with JIT compiling hot paths for speed). Same .class file runs on Windows, Mac, Linux, Android. That's "write once, run anywhere."</p>
<div style="margin:16px 0;text-align:center"><svg viewBox="0 0 420 210" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="JDK contains JRE contains JVM" style="max-width:420px;width:100%"><rect x="8" y="8" width="404" height="194" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"></rect><text x="20" y="28" class="svg-label-bold">JDK — Java Development Kit</text><text x="20" y="45" class="svg-sub">= JRE + development tools (javac, jdb, javadoc)</text><rect x="30" y="56" width="360" height="140" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><text x="44" y="76" class="svg-label-bold">JRE — Java Runtime Environment</text><text x="44" y="92" class="svg-sub">= JVM + core class libraries</text><rect x="62" y="104" width="296" height="84" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><text x="210" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">JVM — Java Virtual Machine</text><text x="210" y="154" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-sub">executes platform-independent bytecode</text><text x="210" y="172" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-sub">(the JVM itself is platform-specific)</text></svg><div style="font-size:13px;opacity:.75;margin-top:6px">The JDK contains the JRE, which contains the JVM. You need the <strong>JDK to write</strong> Java and the <strong>JRE to run</strong> it.</div></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>javac: .java → .class (bytecode); java: runs the class (no extension)</li><li>public static void main(String[] args) — every word has a job</li><li>File name must equal the public class name</li><li>Type/syntax errors = compile time; bad runtime values = exceptions</li></ul></div>
</div></details><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">🧠 MEMORY AID</div><p><strong>JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM (“Developers Run Virtually”):</strong> the <strong>JDK</strong> (Development Kit, includes the compiler <code>javac</code>) contains the <strong>JRE</strong> (Runtime Environment, libraries), which contains the <strong>JVM</strong> (the Virtual Machine that runs bytecode). Source <code>.java</code> → <code>javac</code> → <code>.class</code> bytecode → JVM executes it — that is what makes Java “write once, run anywhere.”</p></div><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>The JVM/JRE/JDK nesting: <strong>JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM</strong>. The <strong>JDK</strong> is for <em>developing</em> (it adds the compiler <code>javac</code>); the <strong>JRE</strong> only <em>runs</em> programs; the <strong>JVM</strong> executes the bytecode. Remember: <strong>bytecode is platform-independent, the JVM is platform-specific</strong> — that is what “write once, run anywhere” means.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>MyApp.java  --javac--&gt;  MyApp.class (bytecode)  --JVM--&gt;  runs</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>source → bytecode → execution</code></p><p><code>javac</code> (in the JDK) compiles <code>.java</code> source into platform-independent <code>.class</code> bytecode; the JVM then executes that bytecode on any platform.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — how Java actually runs — and why it is portable</div><h3>The two-step translation</h3><p>Most languages compile straight to machine code for one processor. Java does something different, and every "write once, run anywhere" question depends on it. <code>javac</code> compiles your <code>.java</code> source into <strong>bytecode</strong> (a <code>.class</code> file) — instructions for an imaginary machine, not for any real CPU. At run time the <strong>JVM</strong> reads that bytecode and executes it on the actual hardware.</p><p>So the <em>bytecode is portable</em> and the <em>JVM is platform-specific</em>. You ship one <code>.class</code> file; Windows, macOS and Linux each supply their own JVM. Java is therefore <strong>both compiled and interpreted</strong> — a statement that sounds contradictory until you see the two stages.</p><h3>JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM</h3><p>These nest, which is the whole exam question:</p><ul><li><strong>JVM</strong> — executes bytecode. Nothing else.</li><li><strong>JRE</strong> = JVM + the standard class libraries. Enough to <em>run</em> a Java program.</li><li><strong>JDK</strong> = JRE + development tools (<code>javac</code>, debugger, docs). Enough to <em>write</em> one.</li></ul><p>Practical test: a user only running an app needs the JRE; you, writing code, need the JDK. If <code>javac</code> is missing, you have a JRE, not a JDK.</p><h3>What the JVM does while running</h3><p>It does not merely interpret. The <strong>JIT compiler</strong> notices hot code paths and compiles them to native machine code on the fly, which is why long-running Java approaches native speed. The JVM also performs <strong>garbage collection</strong> — reclaiming objects nothing references any more — so you never free memory manually. You cannot force collection; <code>System.gc()</code> is only a suggestion.</p><h3>Decoding the main method</h3><p><code>public static void main(String[] args)</code> — every word is required and each is asked about:</p><ul><li><strong>public</strong> — the JVM, outside your class, must be able to call it.</li><li><strong>static</strong> — it runs before any object exists, so it cannot belong to an instance.</li><li><strong>void</strong> — nothing is returned to the JVM.</li><li><strong>String[] args</strong> — command-line arguments.</li></ul><h3>How this connects</h3><p>The compile-vs-interpret distinction is the same one from Intro to CS, applied concretely. <strong>static</strong> here previews static members in Unit 3, and garbage collection explains why Java has no destructors and why memory leaks take the form of forgotten references.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-jvm">
<h3>☕ JVM, JRE, JDK — who does what</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>You need it to…</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>JVM</b> (Java Virtual Machine)</td><td>Runs compiled bytecode on any device</td><td>execute Java programs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>JRE</b> (Runtime Environment)</td><td>JVM + standard libraries</td><td><b>RUN</b> Java apps</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>JDK</b> (Development Kit)</td><td>JRE + compiler (javac) + dev tools</td><td><b>WRITE &amp; compile</b> Java</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE COMPILE-RUN PIPELINE</div><span class="bignum">Hello.java → javac → Hello.class (bytecode) → JVM runs it</span><br>Bytecode runs on ANY device with a JVM — "write once, run anywhere."</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — anatomy of Hello World</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Line</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">public class Hello {</td><td>class name MUST match the filename Hello.java</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">public static void main(String[] args) {</td><td>the exact entry-point signature Java looks for</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">System.out.println("Hi!");</td><td>print with a newline (print = no newline)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">} }</td><td>close method, close class</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ NESTING DOLLS</div>JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM. Developers install the JDK; users only need a JRE. "Which do you need to COMPILE?" → JDK.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Which component converts .java source into bytecode?
<ol type="A"><li>The compiler (javac, part of the JDK)</li><li>The JVM</li><li>The JRE</li><li>System.out</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> javac compiles source → .class bytecode; the JVM then RUNS that bytecode.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch1/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-primitives"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Primitive Types &amp; Casting</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE 8 PRIMITIVES (the 4 you’ll actually use in bold)</div><b>int</b> (whole numbers), <b>double</b> (decimals), <b>boolean</b> (true/false), <b>char</b> (one character in single quotes) — plus byte, short, long, float. String is NOT a primitive (it’s a class).</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — widening is free, narrowing needs a cast</div><pre>int i = 7;
double d = i;          // widening: int → double, automatic. d = 7.0

double price = 9.99;
int whole = (int) price;   // narrowing: MUST cast. whole = 9 (truncated!)</pre>Small-into-big happens silently; big-into-small demands the (int) cast and <b>truncates toward zero</b> — 9.99 becomes 9, not 10.<br><br>And the famous division line: <code>int a = 7 / 2;</code> → <b>3</b> (int ÷ int stays int). <code>double b = 7 / 2;</code> is STILL 3.0 — the division happened in int-land before the assignment. You need <code>7 / 2.0</code> or a cast to get 3.5.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ CASTING ≠ ROUNDING</div>(int) 9.99 = 9. Casting chops the decimals; Math.round(9.99) = 10 rounds. Exams test exactly this difference.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>What does (int) 7.8 evaluate to?<ol type="A"><li>7</li><li>8</li><li>7.8</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 7.</b> A narrowing cast truncates (drops the fraction); it never rounds up.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Java's strict type system catches errors at compile time. Understanding type sizes prevents overflow bugs. Casting rules determine when data loss occurs. Integer division truncating is one of the most common D286 OA question topics.</p></div><h3>Primitive Data Types</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Type</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Size</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Range / Notes</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Default</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">byte</td><td style="padding:8px">8-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">−128 to 127</td><td style="padding:8px">0</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">short</td><td style="padding:8px">16-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">−32,768 to 32,767</td><td style="padding:8px">0</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">int</td><td style="padding:8px">32-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">−2.1B to 2.1B — most-used integer</td><td style="padding:8px">0</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">long</td><td style="padding:8px">64-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">Very large integers; suffix L</td><td style="padding:8px">0L</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">float</td><td style="padding:8px">32-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">~7 decimal digits; suffix f</td><td style="padding:8px">0.0f</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">double</td><td style="padding:8px">64-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">~15 decimal digits; default for decimals</td><td style="padding:8px">0.0</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">char</td><td style="padding:8px">16-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">Single Unicode character</td><td style="padding:8px">'�'</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">boolean</td><td style="padding:8px">1-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">true or false</td><td style="padding:8px">false</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Type Casting</h3><div class="formula-box" style="background:var(--surface-2);border-left:4px solid var(--accent);padding:12px;margin:12px 0;border-radius:4px">Widening (implicit, safe): byte → short → int → long → float → double<br>int i = 100;  double d = i;    // automatic, no data loss<br><br>Narrowing (explicit cast, may lose data):<br>double d = 9.99;  int i = (int) d;   // i = 9 (TRUNCATED, not rounded)<br><br>Integer division: 7 / 2 = 3  (not 3.5)  7.0 / 2 = 3.5</div><div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ TOP OA TRAPS</div><ul><li>String is NOT a primitive — it's a class</li><li>7 / 2 = 3 (integer division truncates, not rounds)</li><li>(int)9.99 = 9, not 10</li></ul></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>8 primitives: byte(8), short(16), int(32), long(64), float(32), double(64), char(16), boolean</li><li>Widening: automatic. Narrowing: explicit cast required, truncates.</li><li>Integer division truncates the decimal portion</li></ul></div>
<h3>The 8 primitives — the full table</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Type</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Size</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Range / values</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Default</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">byte</td><td style="padding:8px">8-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">-128 to 127</td><td style="padding:8px">0</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">short</td><td style="padding:8px">16-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">-32,768 to 32,767</td><td style="padding:8px">0</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">int</td><td style="padding:8px">32-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">±2.1 billion</td><td style="padding:8px">0</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">long</td><td style="padding:8px">64-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">huge; literal needs L: 10000000000L</td><td style="padding:8px">0L</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">float</td><td style="padding:8px">32-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">~7 digits; literal needs f: 3.14f</td><td style="padding:8px">0.0f</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">double</td><td style="padding:8px">64-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">~15 digits; DEFAULT for decimals</td><td style="padding:8px">0.0</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">char</td><td style="padding:8px">16-bit</td><td style="padding:8px">single character: 'A' (single quotes!)</td><td style="padding:8px">'�'</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">boolean</td><td style="padding:8px">1-bit*</td><td style="padding:8px">true / false only (no 0/1 like C)</td><td style="padding:8px">false</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Casting — widening is free, narrowing needs a cast</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// WIDENING (small → big): automatic, safe
int i = 100;
long l = i;          // fine
double d = i;        // fine — 100.0

// NARROWING (big → small): explicit cast REQUIRED, may lose data
double price = 9.99;
int dollars = (int) price;     // 9 — TRUNCATES toward zero, no rounding!
long big = 130;
byte b = (byte) big;           // -126 — OVERFLOW wraps around!

// The order: byte → short → int → long → float → double
//                     char ↗</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT (OA style)</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int a = 7, b = 2;
System.out.println(a / b);          // 3   (int ÷ int = int — truncated!)
System.out.println(a / 2.0);        // 3.5 (one double → double math)
System.out.println((double) a / b); // 3.5 (cast BEFORE divide)
System.out.println((double)(a / b));// 3.0 (divide first as ints, THEN cast — trap!)
System.out.println((int) 9.99);     // 9   (truncate, not round)
byte x = (byte) 200;
System.out.println(x);              // -56 (200 wraps past 127)</pre></div>
<h3>char is secretly a number</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">char c = 'A';
System.out.println(c + 1);       // 66  (int math — 'A' is 65)
System.out.println((char)(c+1)); // B   (cast back to char)
char d = 65;                     // legal! d is 'A'</pre>
<h3>Overflow — silent and deadly</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int max = Integer.MAX_VALUE;     // 2147483647
System.out.println(max + 1);     // -2147483648  — wraps, NO error!
// Money/large counts → use long. Java never warns you about overflow.</pre>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Decimals default to double (3.14f for float); big literals need L</li><li>Widening auto; narrowing needs (cast) and truncates/wraps</li><li>(double)a/b ≠ (double)(a/b) — cast position is the #1 OA trap</li><li>char does int math; overflow wraps silently</li></ul></div>
<h3>Identifiers &amp; Scientific Notation (zyBooks 2.3, 2.8)</h3><p><strong>Identifier rules:</strong> a legal Java identifier starts with a letter, underscore _, or dollar sign $ — <em>never a digit</em> — and may then contain letters, digits, _ and $. Identifiers are case-sensitive (count and Count differ) and cannot be a reserved keyword like class, int, or public. Convention: variables and methods use camelCase; constants use UPPER_CASE.</p><p><strong>Scientific notation literals:</strong> a floating-point literal can use e-notation: 6.02e23 means 6.02 times 10 to the 23rd, and 1.5e-3 means 0.0015. These literals have type double.</p><h3>💡 Exam power-ups: types &amp; casting</h3><p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “Small fits in big for free” — widening (int→double) is automatic; narrowing (double→int) needs an explicit cast and TRUNCATES (no rounding): (int)9.99 is 9. int/int stays int: 5/2 is 2, but 5/2.0 is 2.5. <strong>Strategy:</strong> on every output question, first scan for integer division and mixed types — they are the two most-planted traps.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><strong>Integer division truncates</strong> (drops the fraction): <code>7 / 2</code> is <code>3</code>, not 3.5. Mixing an int with a double <strong>promotes the result to double</strong>. <strong>Widening</strong> (int→double) is automatic; <strong>narrowing</strong> (double→int) needs an explicit cast and truncates. A <code>char</code> is a numeric (Unicode) type, so <code>(int)'A'</code> is <code>65</code>.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>int x = 5, y = 2;
System.out.println(x / y);              // int / int
System.out.println((double) x / y);     // promoted</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>2
2.5</code></p><p><code>x / y</code> is int division → 2. Casting one operand to double forces floating-point division → 2.5. The cast binds to <code>x</code> before the division.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — primitives, memory and the rules of casting</div><h3>Eight primitives, and why the size matters</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:10px 0;font-size:13px;"><tbody><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Type</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Size</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Holds</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">byte / short / int / long</td><td style="padding:5px;">1 / 2 / 4 / 8 bytes</td><td style="padding:5px;">Whole numbers, exact</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">float / double</td><td style="padding:5px;">4 / 8 bytes</td><td style="padding:5px;">Decimals, approximate</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">char</td><td style="padding:5px;">2 bytes</td><td style="padding:5px;">One Unicode character — and it is numeric</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">boolean</td><td style="padding:5px;">JVM-dependent</td><td style="padding:5px;">true / false only</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>int</code> spans about ±2.1 billion; exceed it and the value <strong>wraps</strong> silently rather than erroring. <code>long</code> literals need an <code>L</code> suffix (<code>10000000000L</code>) and <code>float</code> literals an <code>f</code>, because Java defaults whole numbers to <code>int</code> and decimals to <code>double</code>.</p><h3>Widening vs narrowing</h3><p><strong>Widening</strong> (int → long → float → double) happens automatically: the target holds everything the source could. <strong>Narrowing</strong> loses information, so the compiler refuses unless you write an explicit cast — and the cast <strong>truncates toward zero</strong> rather than rounding: <code>(int) 9.99</code> is 9. That cast is you telling the compiler "I accept the loss."</p><h3>The two arithmetic traps</h3><p><strong>Integer division:</strong> if both operands are integers the result is an integer — <code>5 / 2</code> is 2, not 2.5. Fix by making one side floating point: <code>5 / 2.0</code> or <code>(double) a / b</code>. This silently ruins averages and percentages and appears on nearly every Java exam.</p><p><strong>Floating-point precision:</strong> <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is not exactly <code>0.3</code>, because those fractions have no exact binary form. Never compare doubles with <code>==</code>, and never store money in a <code>double</code> — use <code>BigDecimal</code> or whole cents in an <code>int</code>.</p><h3>char is a number in disguise</h3><p><code>char c = 'A'</code> stores 65. So <code>c + 1</code> is 66, and <code>(char)(c + 1)</code> is <code>'B'</code>. This is what lets you loop through the alphabet or convert case by arithmetic.</p><h3>Primitives vs wrappers</h3><p>Each primitive has an object wrapper (<code>int</code> → <code>Integer</code>). Collections hold objects only, so Java <strong>autoboxes</strong> between them. Two consequences: an uninitialized <code>Integer</code> is <code>null</code> (unboxing it throws a NullPointerException), and <code>==</code> on wrappers compares references — use <code>.equals()</code>.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-primitives">
<h3>🧮 The 8 primitive types</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Type</th><th>Holds</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">byte / short / int / long</td><td>whole numbers (increasing size; <b>int is the default</b>)</td><td class="num">int x = 42;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">float / double</td><td>decimals (<b>double is the default</b>)</td><td class="num">double d = 3.14;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">char</td><td>ONE character, in 'single quotes'</td><td class="num">char c = 'A';</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">boolean</td><td>true or false only</td><td class="num">boolean ok = true;</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ String IS NOT A PRIMITIVE</div>String is a class (that's why it's capitalized). "Which of these is NOT a primitive?" → String.</div>
<h3>🔄 Type casting — widening vs narrowing</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th></th><th>Widening (small → big)</th><th>Narrowing (big → small)</th></tr>
<tr><th>Example</th><td class="num">int → double</td><td class="num">double → int</td></tr>
<tr><th>Cast needed?</th><td><b>No</b> — automatic, safe</td><td><b>YES</b> — (int) required, data may be lost</td></tr>
<tr><th>Result</th><td class="num">int 5 → 5.0</td><td class="num">(int) 3.9 → 3 (TRUNCATES, no rounding!)</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — integer division vs mixed division</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Result</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">7 / 2</td><td class="num">3</td><td>int ÷ int = int — decimal DROPPED</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">7 / 2.0</td><td class="num">3.5</td><td>one double promotes the whole thing</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">(int) 7.8</td><td class="num">7</td><td>cast truncates toward zero, never rounds</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">(double) 7 / 2</td><td class="num">3.5</td><td>cast 7 first, then divide</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
What does System.out.println(9 / 4); print?
<ol type="A"><li>2</li><li>2.25</li><li>2.0</li><li>Error</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 2.</b> Both operands are ints → integer division, decimal part thrown away. 9/4.0 would give 2.25.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch1/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-operators"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Operators, Strings &amp; Scanner</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — the + operator’s double life</div><pre>System.out.println(1 + 2 + "a");   // 3a   (left to right: 1+2=3, then "3"+"a")
System.out.println("a" + 1 + 2);   // a12  (string first → everything concatenates)</pre>+ is addition between numbers but CONCATENATION once a String enters — and evaluation runs left to right. This one line of knowledge answers several OA questions.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — Scanner input, the standard recipe</div><pre>Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = in.nextInt();        // reads a number
String name = in.nextLine();   // reads a whole line</pre>Trap: after nextInt(), the Enter key’s newline is still waiting — an immediate nextLine() grabs that EMPTY leftover. Fix: call an extra in.nextLine() to consume it first.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">STRING ESSENTIALS</div>length(), charAt(0), substring(1,3) (start inclusive, end EXCLUSIVE), equals() for comparison. <code>"cat".substring(1,3)</code> = "at". Strings are immutable — methods return NEW strings.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ == vs .equals()</div>For Strings, <b>==</b> compares references (same object?), <b>.equals()</b> compares CONTENT. Comparing user input with == is the classic bug: always .equals().</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>What prints: System.out.println(2 + 3 + "x");<ol type="A"><li>5x</li><li>23x</li><li>x5</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 5x.</b> Left to right: 2+3 adds (5), then "5" + "x" concatenates.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Operators are used in every line of code. String manipulation methods are called in virtually every Java program. Scanner is how console programs accept input. These are foundational skills tested throughout D286 and used in every subsequent course.</p></div><h3>Operators</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Category</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Operators</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Key notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">Arithmetic</td><td style="padding:8px">+ − * / %</td><td style="padding:8px">% is modulo: 10%3=1. / on integers truncates.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Relational</td><td style="padding:8px">== != &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</td><td style="padding:8px">Return boolean. Don't use == for Strings.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Logical</td><td style="padding:8px">&amp;&amp; ‖ !</td><td style="padding:8px">&amp;&amp; and ‖ short-circuit. ! negates.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Increment</td><td style="padding:8px">++ ——</td><td style="padding:8px">Pre (++x) increments before use. Post (x++) after.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>String Methods</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">String s = "Hello";
s.length()          // 5  (method, with parentheses)
s.charAt(0)         // 'H'
s.toUpperCase()     // "HELLO"
s.substring(1, 3)   // "el"  (1 inclusive, 3 exclusive)
s.equals("Hello")   // true  (use this, NOT ==)
s.contains("ell")   // true
"Hi" + " there"     // "Hi there" (concatenation)</pre><h3>Scanner Input</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">import java.util.Scanner;
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
int n     = sc.nextInt();
double d  = sc.nextDouble();
String w  = sc.next();       // one token (stops at whitespace)
String ln = sc.nextLine();   // full line
sc.close();</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Use .equals() to compare Strings, never ==</li><li>array.length (no parens); String.length() (with parens)</li><li>Short-circuit: &amp;&amp; stops on first false; ‖ stops on first true</li></ul></div>
<h3>Integer division and modulo — drilled until automatic</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">System.out.println(17 / 5);    // 3   — quotient, truncated
System.out.println(17 % 5);    // 2   — remainder
System.out.println(5 / 17);    // 0   — smaller/bigger = 0!
System.out.println(5 % 17);    // 5   — remainder is the whole number
System.out.println(-7 % 3);    // -1  — sign follows the LEFT operand
// % uses: even/odd (n % 2 == 0), last digit (n % 10),
//         drop last digit (n / 10), wrap-around indexing (i % len)</pre>
<h3>Pre vs post increment — the classic</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int i = 5;
System.out.println(i++);   // 5  — print THEN increment
System.out.println(i);     // 6
int j = 5;
System.out.println(++j);   // 6  — increment THEN print

int k = 5;
int result = k++ + ++k;    // 5 + 7 = 12  (k is 7 after)
// Left-to-right: k++ yields 5 (k→6), ++k makes k 7 and yields 7</pre>
<h3>== vs .equals() — THE String question</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">String a = "hello";
String b = "hello";
String c = new String("hello");

a == b           // true  — string literals share one pooled object
a == c           // false — 'new' forces a different object; == compares REFERENCES
a.equals(c)      // true  — compares CONTENTS
// RULE: Strings (and all objects) → ALWAYS .equals(). == only for primitives.</pre>
<h3>String immutability + the methods you need</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">String s = "Java";
s.toUpperCase();             // returns "JAVA" but s is UNCHANGED
s = s.toUpperCase();         // must reassign to keep it

String t = "Hello, World";
t.length()                   // 12 — method WITH parens (arrays use .length, no parens)
t.charAt(0)                  // 'H'        (index from 0)
t.indexOf("World")           // 7          (-1 if absent)
t.substring(7)               // "World"    (from index to end)
t.substring(0, 5)            // "Hello"    (end index EXCLUSIVE)
t.contains("lo")             // true
t.replace("l", "L")          // "HeLLo, WorLd" (returns new string)
t.trim()                     // strips leading/trailing spaces
t.split(",")                 // {"Hello", " World"}
"a" + 1 + 2                  // "a12"  — left-to-right, + becomes concat
1 + 2 + "a"                  // "3a"   — math first while both are ints!</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">String s = "WGU";
s.concat(" rocks");
System.out.println(s);            // WGU   — immutable! result was discarded
System.out.println("5" + 3 + 2);  // 532
System.out.println(5 + 3 + "2");  // 82
System.out.println("abc".substring(1, 2)); // b  (end exclusive)</pre></div>
<h3>Scanner — and its infamous newline bug</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">import java.util.Scanner;
Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);

int age = in.nextInt();          // reads the NUMBER, leaves "\n" in the buffer!
String name = in.nextLine();     // instantly consumes that leftover "\n" → name = ""

// THE FIX — flush the leftover newline:
int age2 = in.nextInt();
in.nextLine();                   // throw away the rest of that line
String name2 = in.nextLine();    // now reads real input ✓

// next() reads ONE word; nextLine() reads to end of line
// hasNextInt() checks before reading — avoids InputMismatchException</pre>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>int/int truncates; % sign follows left operand; n%10 = last digit</li><li>i++ uses then bumps; ++i bumps then uses</li><li>Strings: .equals() for content, never ==; immutable — reassign results</li><li>substring end is EXCLUSIVE; String length() has parens, array length doesn't</li><li>nextInt() then nextLine() needs a flushing nextLine() between</li></ul></div>
<h3>Math Methods (zyBooks 2.10)</h3><p>The Math class provides static methods: <strong>Math.pow(base, exp)</strong> returns base raised to exp as a double (Math.pow(2, 3) is 8.0), <strong>Math.sqrt(x)</strong> returns the square root as a double (Math.sqrt(16) is 4.0), <strong>Math.abs(x)</strong> gives absolute value, <strong>Math.max(a, b)</strong> / <strong>Math.min(a, b)</strong> pick the larger/smaller, and <strong>Math.round(x)</strong> rounds to the nearest whole number. Remember: pow and sqrt always return double even for whole-number results.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><strong>Strings are immutable.</strong> Methods like <code>concat</code>, <code>toUpperCase</code>, <code>substring</code>, and <code>replace</code> return a <em>new</em> String — they never change the original. Compare <strong>contents with <code>.equals()</code></strong>; <code>==</code> compares object <em>references</em>. <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> <strong>short-circuit</strong> (the right side may never run). <code>%</code> is remainder; <code>++</code>/<code>--</code> pre vs post matters.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>String s = "ab";
s.concat("c");            // result discarded
System.out.println(s);
String a = "hi", b = new String("hi");
System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a.equals(b));</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>ab
false
true</code></p><p><code>concat</code> returns a new String that is thrown away, so <code>s</code> stays "ab". <code>a == b</code> is false (different objects); <code>a.equals(b)</code> is true (same characters).</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — operators, String immutability and Scanner</div><h3>Precedence and the compound-assignment shortcut</h3><p>Multiplication and division bind before addition and subtraction; parentheses override. One genuinely useful oddity: compound assignment performs an <strong>implicit cast</strong>. <code>byte b = 10; b += 5;</code> compiles, but <code>b = b + 5;</code> does not — because <code>b + 5</code> promotes to <code>int</code>, and assigning an int to a byte is narrowing. <code>+=</code> quietly inserts the cast for you.</p><h3>Why String immutability matters</h3><p>A <code>String</code> can never be changed after creation. Every operation that appears to modify one actually <strong>creates a new object</strong>: <code>s.concat("!")</code> and <code>s.toUpperCase()</code> return new Strings and leave <code>s</code> untouched. If you write <code>s.concat("!");</code> without assigning the result, nothing happens — a classic trick question.</p><p>Consequences worth knowing: string literals are cached in the <strong>string pool</strong> and reused, which is why <code>==</code> sometimes appears to work; and building a string in a loop with <code>+</code> creates a new object every iteration, so use <strong>StringBuilder</strong> (which IS mutable) for that.</p><h3>== versus .equals() — the single most-tested Java trap</h3><p><code>==</code> compares <strong>references</strong> (are these the same object?). <code>.equals()</code> compares <strong>contents</strong>. Two Strings built at run time can hold identical text yet fail <code>==</code>. <strong>Always compare Strings with <code>.equals()</code></strong> (or <code>.equalsIgnoreCase()</code>).</p><h3>String methods, with their gotchas</h3><ul><li><code>length()</code> — a method on String, but <code>length</code> is a <em>field</em> on arrays. Both appear on exams.</li><li><code>charAt(i)</code> — zero-based; <code>charAt(length())</code> throws.</li><li><code>substring(begin, end)</code> — <strong>end is exclusive</strong>, so <code>"Hello".substring(1,3)</code> is <code>"el"</code>.</li><li><code>indexOf(x)</code> — returns <strong>−1</strong> when not found, not 0.</li></ul><h3>The Scanner newline problem</h3><p><code>nextInt()</code> consumes the number but leaves the newline in the buffer, so the following <code>nextLine()</code> returns an empty string instead of waiting for input. The fix is an extra <code>nextLine()</code> to swallow the leftover newline. This is the most common Scanner bug and a favourite exam scenario.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-operators">
<h3>➕ Operators &amp; precedence</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">+ − * /</td><td>arithmetic</td><td class="num">17 / 5 → 3 (ints!)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">%</td><td>remainder (modulus)</td><td class="num">17 % 5 → 2</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">++ / −−</td><td>add/subtract 1</td><td class="num">i++ → i = i + 1</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">+= −= *= /=</td><td>compound assignment</td><td class="num">x += 3 → x = x + 3</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">PRECEDENCE</div>* / % happen BEFORE + −. So 2 + 3 * 4 = 2 + 12 = <b>14</b>, not 20. Parentheses override everything.</div>
<h3>🔤 String essentials</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Method</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example on "Java"</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">.length()</td><td>character count</td><td class="num">4</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">.charAt(i)</td><td>character at index i (starts at 0)</td><td class="num">charAt(0) → 'J'</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">.substring(b, e)</td><td>from b up to but NOT including e</td><td class="num">substring(1, 3) → "av"</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">.equals(s)</td><td>compares CONTENTS</td><td class="num">the RIGHT way to compare</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ == vs .equals() — THE #1 JAVA TRAP</div><b>==</b> asks "same object in memory?" · <b>.equals()</b> asks "same characters?" Always use .equals() for String comparison.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — concatenation order matters</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Result</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">"1" + 2 + 3</td><td class="num">"123"</td><td>left to right: "1"+2 = "12", then +3 = "123"</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">1 + 2 + "3"</td><td class="num">"33"</td><td>1+2 = 3 (math first!), then 3+"3" = "33"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>⌨️ Scanner (keyboard input)</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Step</th><th>Code</th></tr>
<tr><td>Create it</td><td class="num">Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);</td></tr>
<tr><td>Read an int / double / word / line</td><td class="num">nextInt() · nextDouble() · next() · nextLine()</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
What does "Hello".substring(1, 4) return?
<ol type="A"><li>"ell"</li><li>"ello"</li><li>"Hel"</li><li>"llo"</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — "ell".</b> Start at index 1 ('e'), stop BEFORE index 4: indexes 1, 2, 3 → e, l, l.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch2/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-ifswitch"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — if/else &amp; switch</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — the if/else-if ladder, traced</div><pre>int score = 85;
if (score &gt;= 90)      System.out.println("A");
else if (score &gt;= 80) System.out.println("B");
else if (score &gt;= 70) System.out.println("C");
else                  System.out.println("F");</pre>85 ≥ 90? no. 85 ≥ 80? YES → prints <b>B</b> and the rest of the ladder is skipped. Only ONE branch of a ladder ever runs — order matters (put the strictest test first).</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — switch and the missing break</div><pre>int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Mon"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("Tue");        // no break!
    case 3: System.out.println("Wed"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Other");
}</pre>day = 2 matches case 2 → prints Tue — but with no break, execution <b>falls through</b> into case 3 → also prints Wed. Output: <b>Tue Wed</b>. The missing-break fall-through is the #1 switch question.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ = vs == IN CONDITIONS</div><code>if (x == 5)</code> compares. <code>if (x = 5)</code> tries to assign (compile error for ints in Java, but the exam still asks). And chained ladders: once a branch fires, everything after is skipped.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>In a switch, omitting break at the end of a matched case causes:<ol type="A"><li>Fall-through into the next case</li><li>A compile error</li><li>The switch to restart</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — fall-through:</b> execution continues into the following case until a break (or the end).</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>if/else and switch are in virtually every non-trivial program. Switch fall-through is the most tested OA scenario — forgetting break is a classic bug that produces output that surprises even experienced developers.</p></div><h3>if / else-if / else</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">if (score &gt;= 90) {
    grade = "A";
} else if (score &gt;= 80) {
    grade = "B";
} else if (score &gt;= 70) {
    grade = "C";
} else {
    grade = "F";
}
// Only the FIRST matching branch runs. Others are skipped.</pre><h3>Ternary Operator</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">String label = (age &gt;= 18) ? "adult" : "minor";</pre><h3>switch Statement</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Mon"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("Tue"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Other");
}</pre><div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ FALL-THROUGH — THE OA FAVOURITE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:4px;">switch (x) {   // x = 1
    case 1: System.out.print("one ");   // no break!
    case 2: System.out.print("two ");   // no break!
    case 3: System.out.print("three "); break;
}
// Output: one two three    (falls through cases 2 and 3)</pre><p>Valid switch types: int, char, String, byte, short, enum. NOT double, float, or boolean.</p></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>First matching if/else-if branch runs; rest are skipped</li><li>Missing break causes fall-through in switch</li><li>switch supports int/char/String/byte/short/enum. NOT double/float/boolean.</li></ul></div>
<h3>Dangling else and missing braces — silent logic bugs</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// Without braces, only ONE statement belongs to the if:
if (score &gt; 90)
    System.out.println("Great!");
    System.out.println("A grade");   // ALWAYS runs — indentation lies!

// else binds to the NEAREST if:
if (a &gt; 0)
    if (b &gt; 0) System.out.println("both");
    else System.out.println("?");    // belongs to if(b&gt;0), not if(a&gt;0)!
// Lesson: ALWAYS use braces. The OA tests this with cruel indentation.</pre>
<h3>Conditions: &amp;&amp; || ! and short-circuit</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// &amp;&amp; stops at first false; || stops at first true — "short-circuit"
String s = null;
if (s != null &amp;&amp; s.length() &gt; 0)  { }  // SAFE — length() never runs when s is null
if (s.length() &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; s != null)  { }  // CRASHES — wrong order, NPE first

int x = 5;
if (x = 3)  { }     // COMPILE ERROR in Java (assignment isn't boolean) — unlike C!
boolean ok = true;
if (ok = false) { } // compiles (assigns false, evaluates false) — sneaky OA trap</pre>
<h3>switch on String and the modern arrow form</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// Classic (know fall-through cold):
switch (command) {                 // works on int, char, String, enum — NOT long/double
    case "start": run(); break;
    case "stop":
    case "halt":  end(); break;    // stacked cases share a body
    default:      help();
}

// Modern switch expression (Java 14+) — no fall-through, returns a value:
String size = switch (code) {
    case 1 -&gt; "small";
    case 2, 3 -&gt; "medium";
    default -&gt; "large";
};</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.print("Mon ");
    case 2: System.out.print("Tue ");
    case 3: System.out.print("Wed ");
    default: System.out.print("End");
}
// Output: Tue Wed End   ← enters at case 2, FALLS THROUGH everything below

int n = 7;
if (n % 2 == 0) System.out.println("even");
else if (n % 3 == 0) System.out.println("div3");
else if (n &gt; 5) System.out.println("big");      // ← prints "big"
else System.out.println("none");
// Only the FIRST true branch runs — 7 isn't even, isn't div-by-3, IS &gt; 5</pre></div>
<h3>Ternary chains — readable or regrettable</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">String grade = score &gt;= 90 ? "A"
             : score &gt;= 80 ? "B"
             : score &gt;= 70 ? "C" : "F";
// Fine for grading ladders; if you nest deeper, use if/else.</pre>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Braces always — indentation means nothing to Java</li><li>&amp;&amp;/|| short-circuit: null-check BEFORE use, order matters</li><li>switch fall-through without break is the most-tested OA pattern</li><li>switch takes int/char/String/enum — never long or double</li></ul></div>
<h3>Comparing Floating-Point Numbers (zyBooks 3.16)</h3><p>Never compare doubles with == — tiny rounding errors make mathematically-equal values differ (0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3). Instead compare with a tolerance called <strong>epsilon</strong>: <code>Math.abs(a - b) &lt; 0.0001</code>. If the absolute difference is under epsilon, treat the values as equal. This epsilon technique is the standard zyBooks pattern for floating-point comparison.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>A <code>switch</code> case <strong>falls through</strong> to the next case unless you end it with <code>break</code>. Java can switch on <strong>int, char, String, and enum</strong> — <em>not</em> <code>double</code> or <code>boolean</code>. Watch the <code>=</code> vs <code>==</code> trap: <code>=</code> assigns, <code>==</code> compares.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>int n = 1;
switch (n) {
  case 1: System.out.print("one");
  case 2: System.out.print("two"); break;
  default: System.out.print("other");
}</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>onetwo</code></p><p>case 1 has no <code>break</code>, so execution falls through into case 2 and prints both, stopping at the break before <code>default</code>.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — branching — conditions, switch and the fall-through rule</div><h3>Conditions must be boolean</h3><p>Java will not accept a number where a boolean belongs — <code>if (x)</code> is a compile error unless <code>x</code> is a boolean. This deliberately blocks the C-style bug where <code>if (x = 5)</code> assigns instead of comparing. In Java that mistake only compiles when the variable is boolean, so the language catches most of it for you.</p><h3>if / else-if chains</h3><p>Conditions are tested top to bottom and <strong>the first true branch wins</strong>; everything after it is skipped. Grade-boundary questions rely on this: with <code>score = 85</code>, testing <code>&gt;= 90</code> then <code>&gt;= 80</code> prints the second branch and never evaluates the rest. Order your conditions from most to least restrictive, or a broad condition will swallow the narrow ones.</p><p>Without braces, an <code>else</code> binds to the <strong>nearest unmatched if</strong> — which is why the "dangling else" produces behavior that contradicts the indentation. Always use braces.</p><h3>switch and the break you must not forget</h3><p><code>switch</code> compares one variable against discrete constant values. It works with <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>String</code> and enums — <strong>not</strong> with ranges or boolean expressions, so <code>case x &gt; 5:</code> is invalid.</p><p>The defining behavior is <strong>fall-through</strong>: once a case matches, execution continues into the following cases until it hits a <code>break</code>. Omit the break and you run several cases — usually a bug, but occasionally deliberate when several values share an action (stacked cases with one body).</p><h3>Short-circuit evaluation as a tool</h3><p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> stops at the first false, <code>||</code> at the first true. Use this to guard: <code>if (s != null &amp;&amp; s.length() &gt; 0)</code> can never throw a NullPointerException, because the right side is skipped when the left is false. Reversing the order breaks it. Order is a <em>correctness</em> concern here, not style.</p><h3>The ternary</h3><p><code>result = condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;</code> is an expression, so it produces a value — unlike an if statement. Good for a single simple choice, unreadable when nested.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-ifswitch">
<h3>🔀 if / else if / else</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">HOW THE CHAIN WORKS</div>Java checks top to bottom and runs the FIRST true branch only — everything after is skipped. The final else is the catch-all.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — trace with score = 85</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Check</th><th>score = 85</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">if (score &gt;= 90)</td><td>false</td><td>skip</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">else if (score &gt;= 80)</td><td><b>true</b></td><td>print "B" — and STOP checking</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">else if (score &gt;= 70)</td><td>never reached</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3>🎚️ switch</h3>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — with and without break</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code (day = 2)</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">switch(day){<br>&nbsp;case 1: print("Mon"); break;<br>&nbsp;case 2: print("Tue"); break;<br>&nbsp;case 3: print("Wed"); break;<br>&nbsp;default: print("?");<br>}</td><td class="num">Tue</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">same code with NO breaks</td><td class="num">TueWed? — falls through every case below!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ FALL-THROUGH</div>Without <b>break</b>, execution continues into the NEXT case even though it didn't match. The OA loves asking what prints when a break is missing.</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Use if/else when…</th><th>Use switch when…</th></tr>
<tr><td>Testing RANGES (score &gt;= 80) or complex conditions</td><td>Matching one variable against specific VALUES (1, 2, 3 or "red", "blue")</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
In a switch, what happens if a matching case has no break?
<ol type="A"><li>Execution falls through into the next case's code</li><li>A compile error</li><li>The switch exits immediately</li><li>Only the default runs</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Fall-through: it keeps running case after case until a break or the end of the switch.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch2/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-while"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — while &amp; do-while</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — same loop, two flavors</div><pre>int n = 10;
while (n &lt; 5) {            // test FIRST: 10 &lt; 5 is false
    System.out.println(n);  // never runs
}

do {
    System.out.println(n);  // runs ONCE: prints 10
} while (n &lt; 5);            // then tests: false → stop</pre>while = pretest (can run zero times). do-while = posttest (always runs at least once). Same condition, different output — this exact contrast is the exam question.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — a sentinel loop, traced</div><pre>int sum = 0, x = in.nextInt();
while (x != -1) {         // -1 is the sentinel: "stop" signal
    sum += x;
    x = in.nextInt();
}</pre>Input 5, 3, −1 → sum: 5, then 8; reading −1 ends the loop. sum = <b>8</b> (the sentinel itself is never added). Forgetting to read the next value INSIDE the loop = infinite loop.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE UPDATE LIVES INSIDE</div>An event-controlled loop must change its condition variable inside the body. No update → <b>infinite loop</b>. That’s the first thing to check in any “why does this loop never end” question.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Which loop is guaranteed to execute its body at least once?<ol type="A"><li>do-while</li><li>while</li><li>for</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — do-while</b>: the condition is tested AFTER the body (posttest).</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>while handles unknown iteration counts; do-while guarantees at least one run (perfect for menus and input validation). The D286 OA tests loop tracing — tracking variable values through each iteration to predict output.</p></div><h3>while Loop</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int i = 0;
while (i &lt; 5) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
    i++;
}
// Output: 0 1 2 3 4
// Condition checked BEFORE each iteration. May run 0 times if false initially.</pre><h3>do-while Loop</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int j = 10;
do {
    System.out.print(j + " ");
    j++;
} while (j &lt; 5);
// Output: 10    (runs ONCE even though 10 &lt; 5 is immediately false)
// Condition checked AFTER each iteration. Guaranteed at least one run.</pre><h3>Infinite Loop</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int count = 5;
while (count &gt; 0) {
    System.out.println(count);
    // count never changes -- INFINITE LOOP
}</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>while: condition checked first; may run 0 times</li><li>do-while: body runs first; guaranteed at least once</li><li>Use do-while for menus and "try again" input validation</li><li>Trace loops by building a variable-state table for each iteration</li></ul></div>
<h3>Tracing loops like the OA expects</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int i = 1, sum = 0;
while (i &lt;= 4) {
    sum += i;
    i++;
}
System.out.println(sum + " " + i);   // 10 5  ← i is 5 AFTER the loop ends!

// TRACE TABLE — write this on scratch paper every time:
// check i&lt;=4 | sum after | i after
//   1 ✓      |    1      |   2
//   2 ✓      |    3      |   3
//   3 ✓      |    6      |   4
//   4 ✓      |   10      |   5
//   5 ✗ exit — final: sum=10, i=5</pre>
<h3>do-while: the "at least once" loop</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int x = 100;
do {
    System.out.println("runs!");     // prints ONCE even though condition is false
    x++;
} while (x &lt; 10);

// while  → check FIRST, may run zero times
// do-while → run FIRST, check after — guaranteed one pass
// Classic use: menus and input validation (must ask at least once):
int choice;
do {
    System.out.print("Enter 1-3: ");
    choice = in.nextInt();
} while (choice &lt; 1 || choice &gt; 3);   // re-ask until valid</pre>
<h3>Sentinel loops — read until a stop value</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int total = 0;
int val = in.nextInt();
while (val != -1) {          // -1 is the sentinel
    total += val;
    val = in.nextInt();       // read the NEXT value at the loop's end
}
// Forgetting the inner read = infinite loop on the first value</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ INFINITE LOOP PATTERNS (find the bug)</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int i = 0;
while (i &lt; 10);              // ① semicolon = empty body, i never changes
    i++;

int j = 10;
while (j &gt; 0) {
    System.out.println(j);    // ② forgot j-- → prints 10 forever
}

int k = 0;
while (k != 7) k += 2;       // ③ k goes 0,2,4,6,8... skips 7 — never equals it
// Safer condition: k &lt; 7 or k &lt;= 7 instead of !=</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int n = 3;
do {
    System.out.print(n + " ");
    n--;
} while (n &gt; 1);
// Output: 3 2     (n=1 fails the check AFTER printing 2)

int a = 0, count = 0;
while (a &lt; 5) {
    a += 2;
    count++;
}
System.out.println(a + " " + count);   // 6 3   (a: 2,4,6 — overshoots past 5)</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Trace tables on paper — the loop variable's FINAL value is always asked</li><li>do-while always runs once — the menu/validation loop</li><li>Sentinel loops: read once before, read again at loop bottom</li><li>Infinite loop causes: stray semicolon, no update, != that gets skipped over</li></ul></div>
<h3>📘 Deep dive: while vs do-while, traced</h3><p><strong>The difference that matters:</strong> while checks BEFORE running (may run zero times); do-while checks AFTER (always runs at least once). Mnemonic: <em>do it first, ask later.</em></p><p><strong>Worked trace:</strong> int n = 0; while (n &gt; 0) { n--; } — the body never runs, n stays 0. But int n = 0; do { n--; } while (n &gt; 0); runs once — n becomes −1, then the check fails.</p><p><strong>Sentinel loop:</strong> keep reading until a special value: while (!input.equals("quit")) { … } — the classic event-controlled pattern.</p><p><strong>Infinite-loop traps the OA plants:</strong> never updating the loop variable in the body, or moving it the wrong direction. If nothing pushes the condition toward false, the loop never ends.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>A <code>while</code> checks the condition <em>before</em> the body (may run 0 times); a <strong><code>do-while</code> checks after, so it always runs at least once.</strong> If the condition never becomes false you get an <strong>infinite loop</strong>. The loop variable's scope is limited to the loop when declared in it.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>int i = 5;
while (i &lt; 5) { System.out.print("W"); }
do   { System.out.print("D"); } while (i &lt; 5);</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>D</code></p><p>The <code>while</code> body never runs (5 &lt; 5 is false). The <code>do-while</code> runs once <em>before</em> testing, printing one "D".</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — while and do-while — loops that run an unknown number of times</div><h3>The distinction that decides which to use</h3><p><code>while</code> tests <em>before</em> the body, so it can run <strong>zero</strong> times. <code>do-while</code> tests <em>after</em>, so it always runs <strong>at least once</strong>. Match this to the scenario: validating input you already have → <code>while</code>; showing a menu then asking whether to repeat → <code>do-while</code>. Note the syntax detail — a do-while ends with a semicolon after the condition.</p><h3>Sentinel-controlled loops</h3><p>When you cannot know the iteration count up front you loop until a signal value arrives: read until the user types <code>quit</code> or a reader returns <code>null</code>. The pattern is: read once before the loop, test, then read again at the <em>end</em> of the body. Getting that order wrong either skips the first value or processes the sentinel itself.</p><h3>Why loops hang</h3><p>An infinite loop almost always means the condition variable is never updated inside the body, or is updated in a way that can never satisfy the exit test (decrementing when the condition waits for an increase). When tracing, always ask: <em>what changes each pass, and does it move toward the exit?</em></p><h3>break and continue</h3><p><code>break</code> leaves the loop entirely; <code>continue</code> skips the rest of this pass and jumps to the next test. A frequent bug: using <code>continue</code> in a <code>while</code> loop <em>before</em> the update statement, which skips the update and hangs the program. In a <code>for</code> loop the update still runs, so <code>continue</code> is safer there.</p><h3>Loop variable scope</h3><p>A variable declared inside the loop body is destroyed each pass and cannot be read afterwards. Any accumulator — a running total, a counter, a best-so-far — must be declared <strong>before</strong> the loop, or it resets every iteration.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-while">
<h3>🔁 while vs do-while</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th></th><th>while (pretest)</th><th>do-while (posttest)</th></tr>
<tr><th>Checks condition</th><td>BEFORE each pass</td><td>AFTER each pass</td></tr>
<tr><th>Minimum runs</th><td class="num">0 — can skip entirely</td><td class="num"><b>1 — always runs once</b></td></tr>
<tr><th>Syntax note</th><td class="num">while (cond) { }</td><td class="num">do { } while (cond); ← semicolon!</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — trace the loop</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Trace</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">int i = 0;<br>while (i &lt; 3) {<br>&nbsp;System.out.print(i + " ");<br>&nbsp;i++;<br>}</td><td class="num">i=0: print 0 → i=1<br>i=1: print 1 → i=2<br>i=2: print 2 → i=3<br>i=3: 3&lt;3 false → exit</td><td class="num"><b>0 1 2</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — do-while with a FALSE condition</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Output</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">int i = 10;<br>do {<br>&nbsp;System.out.print(i);<br>&nbsp;i++;<br>} while (i &lt; 5);</td><td class="num">10</td><td>body runs ONCE before the check; 11 &lt; 5 is false → exit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ INFINITE LOOPS</div>Forget the update (i++) and the condition never changes → the loop never ends. Every loop needs something inside that moves it toward stopping.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Which loop is guaranteed to execute its body at least once?
<ol type="A"><li>do-while</li><li>while</li><li>for</li><li>None</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — do-while.</b> The body runs BEFORE the first condition check.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch2/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-for"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — for Loops, break &amp; continue</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — the for anatomy, traced</div><pre>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}   // output: 0 1 2</pre>Order of events: init (i=0) → test (0&lt;3 ✓) → body → update (i++) → test → … Runs while the test holds: prints 0 1 2 and stops when i reaches 3. “i &lt;= 3” would print 0 1 2 3 — off-by-one lives in that one character.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — break vs continue</div><pre>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;   // skip JUST this pass
    if (i == 5) break;      // exit the loop entirely
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}</pre>i=1 → 1; i=2 → 2; i=3 → continue (nothing); i=4 → 4; i=5 → break. Output: <b>1 2 4</b>. continue skips one iteration; break abandons the loop.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ NESTED LOOPS MULTIPLY</div>A loop of n passes inside a loop of m passes runs the inner body m·n times: two nested 1-to-10 loops → 100 executions. break only exits the INNER loop it lives in.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>How many times does the body of for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) run?<ol type="A"><li>4</li><li>5</li><li>3</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 4</b> (i = 0, 1, 2, 3). The test fails when i hits 4.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>The for loop is the workhorse of array traversal and counted iteration. Enhanced for (for-each) simplifies collection iteration. break and continue give precise control over loop flow — and their difference is a common OA question.</p></div><h3>Standard for Loop</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
// Output: 0 1 2 3 4
// Parts: init (int i=0); condition (i&lt;5); update (i++)</pre><h3>Enhanced for (for-each)</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[] nums = {10, 20, 30, 40};
for (int n : nums) {
    System.out.print(n + " ");
}
// Output: 10 20 30 40
// Can't modify index; read-only traversal</pre><h3>break and continue</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 8; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;  // skip i=3, go to next iteration
    if (i == 6) break;     // exit loop entirely at i=6
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
// Output: 0 1 2 4 5</pre><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Keyword</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">break</td><td style="padding:8px">Exit the nearest enclosing loop (or switch) immediately</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">continue</td><td style="padding:8px">Skip rest of current iteration; move to next</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>for: init; condition; update — condition checked before each iteration</li><li>for-each: clean syntax for arrays and collections; no index access</li><li>break: exits loop. continue: skips current iteration.</li></ul></div>
<h3>for-loop anatomy — exact execution order</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}
// ORDER: init once → CHECK → body → update → CHECK → body → update → CHECK(fail) → exit
// Output: 0 1 2     i dies with the loop (declared in the header = loop scope)

// Count down, step by 2, multiple variables — all legal:
for (int i = 10; i &gt; 0; i -= 2)      // 10 8 6 4 2
for (int i = 0, j = 9; i &lt; j; i++, j--)  // converging pointers</pre>
<h3>Off-by-one — the OA's favorite food</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int i = 0; i &lt;  5; i++)   // 0,1,2,3,4   — 5 iterations
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 5; i++)   // 0..5        — 6 iterations!
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++)   // 1..5        — 5 iterations
// Array safety: i &lt; arr.length (NEVER &lt;=, that's an instant crash)</pre>
<h3>break vs continue — drilled</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) break;             // KILL the loop entirely
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}                                  // 1 2

for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;          // SKIP rest of THIS pass, go to update
    System.out.print(i + " ");
}                                  // 1 2 4 5</pre>
<h3>Nested loops + labeled break</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int r = 1; r &lt;= 3; r++) {
    for (int c = 1; c &lt;= 3; c++) {
        System.out.print(r * c + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();           // newline per ROW — placement matters
}
// 1 2 3
// 2 4 6
// 3 6 9

outer:                              // a label
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 5; j++) {
        if (i * j &gt; 6) break outer; // breaks BOTH loops (plain break = inner only)
    }
}</pre>
<h3>Enhanced for (for-each)</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[] nums = {4, 8, 15};
for (int n : nums) {               // "for each n in nums"
    System.out.print(n + " ");      // no index, no off-by-one possible
}
// Limits: no index access, can't modify the array slots,
// can't iterate two arrays in lockstep → use classic for then.</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++);        // ← semicolon! empty loop body
    System.out.println("hi");        // prints ONCE, after the silent loop
// Output: hi

int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 4; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue;
    total += i;
}
System.out.println(total);           // 4  (1 + 3; evens skipped)

String s = "";
for (int i = 3; i &gt; 0; i--) s += i;
System.out.println(s);               // 321</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Order: init → check → body → update; header-declared i is loop-scoped</li><li>&lt; vs &lt;= changes the count by one; arrays always i &lt; length</li><li>break exits the loop; continue skips to the update; labels reach outer loops</li><li>for-each = clean read-only traversal; classic for when you need the index</li></ul></div>
<h3>📘 Deep dive: nested loop tracing</h3><p><strong>Count the prints:</strong> for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) for (int j = 1; j &lt;= 4; j++) print("*"); → the inner loop runs fully for EACH outer pass: 3 × 4 = 12 stars. Multiply the trip counts.</p><p><strong>Dependent bounds:</strong> for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) for (int j = 1; j &lt;= i; j++) print(i); → prints 1 once, 2 twice, 3 three times: 122333 (1+2+3 = 6 outputs).</p><p><strong>break vs continue:</strong> break exits the ENTIRE current loop; continue skips to the next iteration check. In nested loops each affects only the INNERMOST loop containing it.</p><p><strong>OA strategy:</strong> write the (i, j) pairs as a small grid before answering — 30 seconds of grid beats a guess.</p><h3>Exam focus: for-loop mechanics Java tests</h3><p><strong>When a loop terminates:</strong> <code>for(int i=10; i&gt;=1; i--)</code> runs while the condition is true and <strong>terminates when the condition becomes false</strong> — here when i reaches 0. <strong>Loop-variable scope:</strong> a variable declared in the header, like <code>int i</code>, is <strong>local to the loop</strong>; it does not exist after the loop ends. <strong>Off-by-one:</strong> <code>for(int i=0; i&lt;=arr.length; i++) arr[i]</code> uses <code>&lt;=</code>, so the last access is arr[arr.length] — one past the end — which throws <strong>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</strong> at runtime (it compiles fine). The safe condition is <code>i &lt; arr.length</code>.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><code>for (init; condition; update)</code> — count the iterations carefully. <strong><code>break</code> exits the whole loop; <code>continue</code> skips the rest of the current iteration</strong> and jumps to the update. The enhanced for (<code>for (int x : arr)</code>) reads elements but can’t modify the array’s structure.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
  if (i == 2) continue;
  if (i == 3) break;
  System.out.print(i);
}</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>01</code></p><p>i=0,1 print; i=2 hits <code>continue</code> (skips print); i=3 hits <code>break</code> (exits). So "01".</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — for loops, for-each and nesting</div><h3>The four parts, and their scope</h3><p><code>for (init; condition; update)</code> packages the three loop controls on one line, which is why it fits any <em>counted</em> repetition. A variable declared in the init section exists only inside the loop — if you need the final value afterwards, declare it outside.</p><p>The standard array traversal is <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)</code>. Note <code>&lt;</code>, not <code>&lt;=</code>: valid indexes stop at <code>length − 1</code>, so <code>&lt;=</code> throws <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> on the last pass. Note also <code>length</code> with no parentheses — arrays expose a field, Strings a method.</p><h3>The enhanced for (for-each)</h3><p><code>for (String name : names)</code> reads far better when you simply need every element. Its limits are exactly what exams probe:</p><ul><li>There is <strong>no index</strong> — if you need the position, use a classic for loop.</li><li>The loop variable is a <strong>copy</strong> of the element for primitives, so assigning to it does not change the array.</li><li>You cannot iterate backwards or skip elements.</li><li><strong>Structurally modifying</strong> a collection while for-eaching it (adding or removing) throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Use an explicit iterator, or collect changes and apply them afterwards.</li></ul><h3>Nested loops</h3><p>Nesting <strong>multiplies</strong>: an outer loop of 3 around an inner loop of 4 executes the inner body 12 times. This is how you walk a 2D array (outer = rows, inner = columns) and it is where <strong>O(n²)</strong> comes from — the practical reason a routine that is fine with 100 items crawls with 10,000.</p><p>A plain <code>break</code> exits only the <em>innermost</em> loop. To leave both you need a labeled break or a flag variable — another favourite trace question.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-for">
<h3>🔂 The for loop — three parts, exact order</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">EXECUTION ORDER</div><span class="bignum">for (init; test; update)</span><br>1) init ONCE → 2) test → 3) body → 4) update → back to 2. The update runs AFTER the body each time.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — summing 1 to 5</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Pass</th><th>i</th><th>sum after body</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">1</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">3</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">6</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="num">5</td><td class="num">15</td></tr></tbody></table>
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) { sum += i; } → sum = <b>15</b></div>
<h3>⏭️ break vs continue</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Definition</th><th>Picture it</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>break</b></td><td>EXIT the loop entirely, right now</td><td>Leave the building</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>continue</b></td><td>Skip the REST of this pass, jump to the next iteration</td><td>Skip this floor, keep climbing</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — same loop, different keyword (i from 1 to 5, trigger at i == 3)</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Inside the loop</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">if (i == 3) break; print(i);</td><td class="num">1 2 — loop DIES at 3</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">if (i == 3) continue; print(i);</td><td class="num">1 2 4 5 — only 3 is skipped</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ NESTED LOOPS</div>break/continue affect only the INNERMOST loop they sit in. An inner break does not stop the outer loop.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++){ if (i == 2) continue; System.out.print(i); } prints:
<ol type="A"><li>013</li><li>01</li><li>0123</li><li>013 then error</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 013.</b> continue skips only the i==2 pass; the loop finishes 0, 1, (skip), 3.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch3/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-classes"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Classes, Objects &amp; Constructors</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a complete class, used</div><pre>public class Dog {
    private String name;              // field (instance variable)

    public Dog(String name) {         // constructor: same name as class,
        this.name = name;             // NO return type, runs at new
    }

    public void speak() {             // method
        System.out.println(name + " says woof");
    }
}

Dog d = new Dog("Rex");   // constructor runs, name = "Rex"
d.speak();                // Rex says woof</pre><code>new</code> allocates the object and calls the constructor. Each object gets its OWN fields: new Dog("Bella") has its own name, untouched by Rex.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">CONSTRUCTOR FACTS</div>• Same name as the class, no return type (not even void).<br>• If you write NO constructor, Java supplies a default no-arg one; write ANY constructor and the freebie disappears.<br>• Constructors can be overloaded: Dog() and Dog(String name) can coexist.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE VANISHING DEFAULT CONSTRUCTOR</div>Add Dog(String name) and suddenly <code>new Dog()</code> stops compiling — the automatic no-arg constructor is only provided when you define none at all. A precise, favorite exam fact.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A constructor differs from a normal method because it:<ol type="A"><li>Has no return type and shares the class’s name</li><li>Must return void</li><li>Cannot take parameters</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Constructors are named after the class, declare no return type, and run automatically at new.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Classes and objects are the fundamental units of Java programs. Every Spring controller, JPA entity, and Android activity is a class. Understanding constructors, the this keyword, and object creation with new is the foundation for every OOP course that follows D286.</p></div><h3>Class and Object</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Car {
    // Fields (instance variables)
    private String make;
    private int year;

    // Constructor
    public Car(String make, int year) {
        this.make = make;    // 'this.make' = field; 'make' = parameter
        this.year = year;
    }

    // Instance method
    public String describe() {
        return year + " " + make;
    }
}

// Creating objects:
Car c1 = new Car("Toyota", 2022);  // new calls the constructor
System.out.println(c1.describe()); // "2022 Toyota"</pre><h3>The this Keyword</h3><p><code>this</code> refers to the current object. Required when a constructor/method parameter has the same name as a field:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// Without this: BOTH refer to the parameter (field never set!)
name = name;           // BUG: assigns parameter to itself
// With this: unambiguous
this.name = name;      // CORRECT: field = parameter</pre><h3>Constructor Chaining</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public Car() {
    this("Unknown", 2000);  // calls Car(String, int) — must be first line
}</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Class = blueprint. Object = instance created with new.</li><li>Constructor: same name as class, no return type</li><li>this = current object; use to disambiguate fields from parameters</li><li>this() calls another constructor in same class (must be first line)</li></ul></div>
<h3>Objects are references — the mental model that fixes everything</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">Dog d1 = new Dog("Rex");
Dog d2 = d1;                 // copies the REFERENCE, not the dog!
d2.name = "Buddy";
System.out.println(d1.name); // Buddy — both variables point at ONE object

Dog d3 = null;               // reference to nothing
d3.bark();                   // NullPointerException at runtime</pre>
<p>Primitives copy VALUES; objects copy REFERENCES (arrows to the same heap object). Half of all OA object questions reduce to "how many actual objects exist, and who points where?" — draw arrows on scratch paper.</p>
<h3>Constructors — the rules that get tested</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    public Student() {                 // no-arg constructor
        this("Unknown", 0);             // constructor chaining — MUST be line 1
    }
    public Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;               // this.name = field, name = parameter
        this.age = age;
    }
}

Student s = new Student("Natasha", 30);</pre>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 1</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Constructor name = class name exactly, NO return type (not even void)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 2</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Write NO constructor → Java gives a free default no-arg one</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 3</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Write ANY constructor → the free default DISAPPEARS (new Student() breaks unless you add it back)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 4</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">this(...) calls a sibling constructor; must be the FIRST statement</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Counter {
    int count;                          // instance fields default to 0
    public Counter() { count++; }
}
Counter a = new Counter();
Counter b = new Counter();
b.count += 5;
System.out.println(a.count + " " + b.count);   // 1 6 — separate objects!

public class Box {
    int size;
    public void setSize(int size) { size = size; }  // BUG: param to itself
}
Box bx = new Box();
bx.setSize(10);
System.out.println(bx.size);            // 0 — needed this.size = size</pre></div>
<h3>Methods: pass-by-value, always</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">void bump(int x)      { x++; }            // copies the VALUE — caller unaffected
void rename(Dog d)    { d.name = "Max"; } // copies the REFERENCE — same object,
                                            // so the change IS visible to caller
void replace(Dog d)   { d = new Dog("Z");}// reassigning the COPY — caller unaffected
// Java is ALWAYS pass-by-value; for objects the value passed is the reference.</pre>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Object variables hold arrows; = copies the arrow, not the object</li><li>Defining any constructor deletes the free default one</li><li>this.field = param fixes shadowing; this(...) chains and goes first</li><li>Pass-by-value: mutating a passed object shows; reassigning it doesn't</li></ul></div>
</div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>A <strong>class is a blueprint; an object is an instance</strong> created with <code>new</code>. A <strong>constructor has the same name as the class and no return type</strong> (not even <code>void</code>). If you write no constructor, Java supplies a default no-arg one — but adding any constructor removes that default. <code>this</code> refers to the current object.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>class Dog {
  String name;
  Dog(String n) { this.name = n; }
}
Dog d = new Dog("Rex");
System.out.println(d.name);</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>Rex</code></p><p><code>new Dog("Rex")</code> calls the constructor, which assigns the parameter to the object’s field via <code>this.name</code>.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — classes, objects and the rules of constructors</div><h3>Reference variables and the heap</h3><p><code>Dog d = new Dog();</code> does three things: <code>new</code> allocates the object on the <strong>heap</strong>, the constructor initializes it, and <code>d</code> stores a <strong>reference</strong> to it — not the object itself. That distinction explains the rest of Java:</p><ul><li>Two variables can reference one object, so a change through either is visible to both.</li><li><code>==</code> on objects compares references, not contents.</li><li>An uninitialized reference is <code>null</code>, and calling a method on it throws a <strong>NullPointerException</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Constructor rules</h3><p>A constructor has <strong>the same name as the class</strong> and <strong>no return type</strong> — not even <code>void</code>. Writing <code>void Dog()</code> silently creates an ordinary method, and the compiler then supplies a default constructor; code that looked fine fails mysteriously.</p><p>The critical rule: Java gives you a <strong>no-argument default constructor only if you define no constructor at all</strong>. The moment you write <code>Dog(String name)</code>, the free default disappears and <code>new Dog()</code> stops compiling. If you need both, declare both.</p><h3>Overloading and this()</h3><p>Several constructors may differ by parameter list. To avoid duplicating setup, one can call another with <code>this(...)</code> — which must be the <strong>first statement</strong> in the constructor. Inside an instance method or constructor, <code>this</code> refers to the current object, which is what disambiguates <code>this.name = name</code> when a parameter shadows a field. Without <code>this.</code>, that line assigns the parameter to itself and the field stays null — a classic silent bug.</p><h3>Initialization order</h3><p>Fields receive their <strong>default values first</strong> (0, 0.0, false, null), then explicit initializers run, then the constructor body. This is why an object is never in a garbage state, and why forgetting to set a field leaves it null rather than undefined.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-classes">
<h3>🏗️ Classes, Objects &amp; Constructors</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Class</b></td><td>The BLUEPRINT — defines fields + methods</td><td class="num">class Dog { ... }</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Object</b></td><td>An INSTANCE built from the blueprint with <b>new</b></td><td class="num">Dog d = new Dog("Rex");</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Constructor</b></td><td>Special method that runs when the object is created</td><td class="num">public Dog(String n) { ... }</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>this</b></td><td>Refers to THE CURRENT object — distinguishes field from parameter</td><td class="num">this.name = name;</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">CONSTRUCTOR RULES (memorize both)</div>1) Name EXACTLY matches the class name. 2) NO return type — not even void. If you write none, Java supplies an empty <b>default constructor</b>.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — one blueprint, two different objects</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">class Dog {<br>&nbsp;String name;<br>&nbsp;public Dog(String name) {<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;this.name = name;<br>&nbsp;}<br>}</td><td>blueprint: every Dog has a name; constructor stores the argument into the field</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">Dog a = new Dog("Rex");<br>Dog b = new Dog("Bella");</td><td>TWO separate objects, each with its OWN name — a.name is "Rex", b.name is "Bella"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ new IS WHAT CREATES</div>Dog d; declares a reference only — no object exists until <b>new Dog(...)</b> runs (and runs the constructor).</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Which is true of a Java constructor?
<ol type="A"><li>It has the same name as the class and no return type</li><li>It must return void</li><li>It can have any name</li><li>It runs only when you call it manually</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Same name, no return type (void would make it a regular method), and it runs automatically at new.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch3/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-encap"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Encapsulation &amp; Access Modifiers</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE MODIFIERS</div>• <b>private</b> — visible only inside the class (the default choice for fields).<br>• <b>public</b> — visible everywhere (the usual choice for methods).<br>• <b>protected</b> — class + subclasses + same package.<br>• (no modifier) — package-private: same package only.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a getter/setter that actually protects</div><pre>public class Account {
    private double balance;                 // hidden from outside

    public double getBalance() { return balance; }

    public void deposit(double amt) {
        if (amt &gt; 0) balance += amt;      // the setter can VALIDATE
    }
}</pre>Outside code cannot write <code>account.balance = -1000;</code> — it doesn’t compile (private). It must go through deposit(), which refuses bad amounts. THAT is the point of encapsulation: the class guards its own data.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ “WHY PRIVATE FIELDS?” — THE EXPECTED ANSWER</div>Not secrecy for its own sake: private fields + public methods let the class enforce rules, change internals without breaking callers, and keep invalid states impossible. If an option says “control access / protect data integrity,” that’s the one.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Fields should typically be declared:<ol type="A"><li>private, with public getters/setters as needed</li><li>public, for convenience</li><li>protected, always</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Hide the data, expose controlled methods — the encapsulation pattern.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Encapsulation is why your bank account balance can't be set to arbitrary values by outside code. Private fields with public getters/setters enforce data validation, enable change of internal representation without breaking callers, and are the D286 OA's most-tested OOP concept.</p></div><h3>Access Modifiers</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Modifier</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Accessible from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">private</td><td style="padding:8px">Same class only</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">(none)</td><td style="padding:8px">Same package</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">protected</td><td style="padding:8px">Same class, same package, subclasses</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">public</td><td style="padding:8px">Anywhere</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Encapsulation Pattern</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;  // private: can't access directly from outside

    public double getBalance() {      // getter
        return balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {  // setter with validation
        if (amount &gt; 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
        // negative deposit silently rejected
    }
}</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>private: only the class itself can access the field</li><li>Getter: return the field value. Setter: validate then set.</li><li>Boolean fields use is prefix: isActive(), not getActive()</li><li>Encapsulation benefits: validation, internal change without breaking API</li></ul></div>
<h3>The four access levels — memorize the table</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Modifier</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Same class</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Same package</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Subclass</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Everywhere</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">private</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✗</td><td style="padding:8px">✗</td><td style="padding:8px">✗</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">(default/package)</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✗</td><td style="padding:8px">✗</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">protected</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✗</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">public</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td><td style="padding:8px">✓</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>The encapsulation pattern — private fields, public methods</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;                  // hidden from outside

    public double getBalance() {            // read access — controlled
        return balance;
    }
    public void deposit(double amount) {     // write access — WITH RULES
        if (amount &gt; 0) balance += amount;   // validation lives here
    }
    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; amount &lt;= balance) balance -= amount;
    }
}

BankAccount acct = new BankAccount();
acct.balance = -999;        // COMPILE ERROR — private ✓ that's the point
acct.deposit(-50);          // silently rejected by validation ✓</pre>
<p><strong>Why bother?</strong> Without encapsulation, every part of a program can corrupt every field — a negative balance, an age of -5. With it, the class GUARDS its own data: one place to validate, one place to change later (switch balance to cents? only this class changes). This "data hiding" is the first pillar of OOP and a guaranteed OA topic.</p>
<h3>Getter/setter naming conventions (tested!)</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">private String name;        →  public String getName()
                               public void setName(String name)
private boolean active;     →  public boolean isActive()    // boolean uses "is"
                               public void setActive(boolean active)</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE BEHAVIOR</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Temp {
    private int celsius;
    public void setCelsius(int c) {
        if (c &gt;= -273) celsius = c;          // physics floor
    }
    public int getCelsius() { return celsius; }
}
Temp t = new Temp();
t.setCelsius(-500);
System.out.println(t.getCelsius());   // 0 — rejected, field kept its default
t.setCelsius(25);
System.out.println(t.getCelsius());   // 25</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>private → class only; default → package; protected adds subclasses; public → all</li><li>Pattern: private fields + public getters/setters with validation</li><li>boolean getters use isX(); everything else getX()/setX()</li><li>Encapsulation = the class guards its own data — one place to validate and change</li></ul></div>
<h3>💡 Exam power-ups: encapsulation</h3><p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “private fields, public gates” — data private, access through getters/setters. Access order from open to closed: public → protected → (default/package) → private. <strong>Real world:</strong> an ATM shows buttons, not the vault — that interface/implementation split is encapsulation. <strong>Strategy:</strong> if an answer choice exposes a public field, it is almost never the “best practice” answer.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">🧠 MEMORY AID</div><p><strong>Access modifiers, widest → narrowest:</strong> <code>public</code> (everywhere) &gt; <code>protected</code> (package + subclasses) &gt; <em>default/package-private</em> (same package) &gt; <code>private</code> (same class only). Encapsulation = keep fields <code>private</code> and expose them through public getters/setters.</p></div><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><strong>Encapsulation = private fields + public getters/setters.</strong> Access, widest→narrowest: <code>public</code> → <code>protected</code> → <em>default/package</em> → <code>private</code>. A <code>private</code> member is visible only inside its own class; setters let you validate before assigning.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>class Account {
  private double balance;
  public double getBalance() { return balance; }
  public void deposit(double a) { if (a &gt; 0) balance += a; }
}</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>balance is protected from invalid writes</code></p><p>The field is <code>private</code>, so outside code must go through <code>deposit</code>/<code>getBalance</code>; <code>deposit</code> rejects non-positive amounts, protecting the invariant.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — encapsulation and what each access modifier actually allows</div><h3>The four levels</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:10px 0;font-size:13px;"><tbody><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Modifier</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Same class</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Same package</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Subclass elsewhere</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Anywhere</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;"><code>private</code></td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✘</td><td style="padding:5px;">✘</td><td style="padding:5px;">✘</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">(default/package)</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✘</td><td style="padding:5px;">✘</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;"><code>protected</code></td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✘</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;"><code>public</code></td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td><td style="padding:5px;">✔</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two details that get tested: <strong>default has no keyword</strong> — you get it by writing nothing — and <strong>protected also grants package access</strong>, so it is broader than "subclasses only."</p><h3>What encapsulation actually buys</h3><p>Private fields plus public methods is not ceremony. A <strong>setter is a gate</strong>: <code>setAge()</code> can reject a negative value, so the object can never hold an impossible state. A public field accepts anything, and every caller becomes a place the invariant can break.</p><p>The second payoff is freedom to change. If a class stores a full name internally but exposes <code>getFirstName()</code>, the team can later split it into two fields and <strong>no calling code changes</strong>. Public fields would have frozen the internal design permanently.</p><h3>Encapsulation vs abstraction</h3><p>Constantly confused, so hold the distinction: <strong>abstraction</strong> is deciding <em>what</em> to expose (the public interface); <strong>encapsulation</strong> is the <em>enforcement</em> — private fields and accessor methods — that keeps the rest hidden. Abstraction is the design decision; encapsulation is the mechanism that makes it stick.</p><h3>A subtle leak</h3><p>Returning a mutable object from a getter hands the caller a reference to your internals, so they can modify your state without going through your setter. Returning a copy is the defensive fix — a favourite "why is this still broken?" question.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 2 alternate explanations &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-encapsulation">
<h3>🔒 Encapsulation &amp; Access Modifiers</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE ENCAPSULATION RECIPE</div>Make fields <b>private</b> + provide <b>public getters/setters</b>. Outsiders go through your methods — you control and validate every change.</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Modifier</th><th>Who can access</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>public</b></td><td>Everyone, everywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>protected</b></td><td>Same package + subclasses</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>(default — no keyword)</b></td><td>Same package only</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>private</b></td><td>Inside this class ONLY — most restrictive</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — proper encapsulation with validation</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">private double balance;</td><td>field hidden from outside</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">public double getBalance() {<br>&nbsp;return balance;<br>}</td><td>getter — read access</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">public void deposit(double amt) {<br>&nbsp;if (amt &gt; 0) balance += amt;<br>}</td><td>setter-style method — REJECTS bad input (negative deposits)</td></tr></tbody></table>
Direct access like account.balance = −500; won't even compile — that's the protection.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ ORDER OF RESTRICTIVENESS</div>private &gt; default &gt; protected &gt; public. "Most restrictive" = <b>private</b>; "least" = public.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Encapsulation in Java is typically achieved by:
<ol type="A"><li>Private fields with public getters and setters</li><li>Public fields</li><li>Static methods only</li><li>Using inheritance</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Hide the data (private), expose controlled access (public methods).</div></details></div>
</div><div class="callout callout-tip" data-patch="gapfix"><div class="callout-label">📝 THE final KEYWORD</div><p>Declaring a variable <span class="key-term">final</span> means its value cannot change after initialization — Java’s named constant. Convention: ALL_CAPS names, e.g. <code>final double TAX_RATE = 0.08;</code>. Reassigning a final variable is a compile error.</p></div></details>"},"D286/ch3/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-static"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — static Members &amp; Method Overloading</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — static = shared by the CLASS</div><pre>public class Counter {
    static int total = 0;    // ONE copy for the whole class
    int mine = 0;            // one copy PER OBJECT

    Counter() { total++; mine++; }
}
new Counter(); new Counter(); new Counter();
System.out.println(Counter.total);   // 3  (shared count)</pre>Each new object bumped the SAME total. Meanwhile every object’s mine is just 1. static belongs to the class (access via Counter.total); instance fields belong to each object.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — overloading = same name, different parameters</div><pre>int    max(int a, int b)       { ... }
double max(double a, double b) { ... }
int    max(int a, int b, int c){ ... }</pre>Three methods named max, distinguished by their <b>parameter lists</b>. The compiler picks by the arguments: max(2, 9) → the int version. Return type ALONE cannot distinguish overloads.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ STATIC METHODS CAN’T TOUCH INSTANCE FIELDS</div>main() is static — it runs with NO object existing, so it cannot directly use instance variables (“non-static variable referenced from static context”). That compiler error is quoted verbatim on exams.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Two methods with the same name and DIFFERENT parameter lists demonstrate:<ol type="A"><li>Overloading</li><li>Overriding</li><li>Inheritance</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — overloading</b> (same class, same name, different parameters). Overriding is a SUBCLASS redefining an inherited method with the SAME signature.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Static members belong to the class, not to any object. Confusing static and instance context is a very common compile error in D286 and beyond. Method overloading lets one method name handle different input types — it's how Java knows whether you're calling Math.max(int, int) or Math.max(double, double).</p></div><h3>Static vs Instance</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Type</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Belongs to</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Access via</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Shared?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">Instance field/method</td><td style="padding:8px">Each object</td><td style="padding:8px">objectRef.member</td><td style="padding:8px">No — each object has its own</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Static field/method</td><td style="padding:8px">The class</td><td style="padding:8px">ClassName.member</td><td style="padding:8px">Yes — one copy for all</td></tr></tbody></table><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Counter {
    private static int count = 0;  // shared by all Counter objects
    private int id;

    public Counter() { count++; this.id = count; }
    public static int getCount() { return count; }
    // static method CAN'T use 'this' or access 'id' directly
}
Counter a = new Counter();  // count = 1
Counter b = new Counter();  // count = 2
System.out.println(Counter.getCount());  // 2</pre><h3>Method Overloading</h3><p>Same method name, different parameter list (type, count, or order). Resolved at compile time.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public int add(int a, int b)          { return a + b; }
public double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
// Return type alone is NOT enough to distinguish overloads</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Static: belongs to class, shared. Instance: each object has its own copy.</li><li>Static methods can't access instance fields (no this).</li><li>Overloading: same name, different parameters. Resolved at compile time.</li></ul></div>
<h3>static = belongs to the CLASS, not objects</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Counter {
    static int total = 0;        // ONE copy shared by all instances
    int id;                      // each object gets its own

    public Counter() {
        total++;                 // bumps the shared counter
        id = total;
    }
}
new Counter(); new Counter(); Counter c = new Counter();
System.out.println(Counter.total);   // 3 — access via CLASS name
System.out.println(c.id);            // 3</pre>
<h3>The static access rules (compile-error territory)</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Demo {
    int instanceVar = 5;
    static int staticVar = 10;

    static void staticMethod() {
        System.out.println(staticVar);     // ✓ static → static fine
        System.out.println(instanceVar);   // ✗ COMPILE ERROR — whose instanceVar?
        helper();                          // ✗ COMPILE ERROR — instance method
    }
    void instanceMethod() {
        System.out.println(staticVar);     // ✓ instance → static fine
        System.out.println(instanceVar);   // ✓
    }
    void helper() { }
}
// This is why main (static) can't call your instance methods without
// creating an object first: new Demo().instanceMethod();</pre>
<h3>Method overloading — same name, different parameters</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public static int    max(int a, int b)        { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }
public static double max(double a, double b)  { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }
public static int    max(int a, int b, int c) { return max(max(a,b), c); }

// LEGAL overloads differ in: parameter COUNT, TYPES, or ORDER
// ILLEGAL: differing ONLY by return type →
public static int    parse(String s) {...}
public static double parse(String s) {...}   // ✗ COMPILE ERROR</pre>
<h3>Which overload runs? Resolution rules</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">void show(int x)    { System.out.println("int"); }
void show(double x) { System.out.println("double"); }
void show(Object x) { System.out.println("Object"); }

show(5);        // int      — exact match wins
show(5.0);      // double
show(5L);       // double   — long widens to double (no long version)
show("hi");     // Object   — String IS-A Object
show('A');      // int      — char widens to int first!</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class App {
    static int count = 0;
    int local = 0;
    void bump() { count++; local++; }
}
App a = new App();
App b = new App();
a.bump(); a.bump(); b.bump();
System.out.println(App.count + " " + a.local + " " + b.local);
// 3 2 1 — static shared across all; local per-object</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>static = one shared copy, access via ClassName; instance = per object</li><li>static methods CANNOT touch instance members (no object to look at)</li><li>Overload by count/types/order — return type alone is illegal</li><li>Resolution: exact match → widening → boxing/Object; char widens to int</li></ul></div>
<h3>Unit Testing Your Methods (zyBooks 6.x)</h3><p><strong>Unit testing</strong> means verifying one method at a time with test cases: call the method with known inputs, compare the <strong>actual</strong> returned value against the <strong>expected</strong> value, and report a failure when they differ. A simple test harness is a main() that runs several assertions. Choose normal cases plus <strong>border cases</strong> (0, negative numbers, empty strings, the largest value) — border cases are where bugs hide. Testing methods in isolation catches errors before they spread into the larger program.</p><h3>Exam focus: scope of a variable declared inside a loop body</h3><p>A variable declared <em>inside</em> a for-loop body is accessible <strong>only within that loop body</strong>, and it is re-created on every iteration. It is not visible after the loop, in the enclosing method, or anywhere else. Java scope rule: a local variable lives only inside the innermost braces { } where it is declared.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><strong>static</strong> members belong to the <em>class</em>, not an instance — call them via the class name and note they <strong>cannot directly access instance (non-static) fields</strong>. <strong>Overloading</strong> = same method name, <em>different parameter lists</em>, resolved at <strong>compile time</strong> by the argument types.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>static void f(int x)    { System.out.print("int"); }
static void f(double x) { System.out.print("double"); }
f(5);
f(5.0);</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>intdouble</code></p><p><code>f(5)</code> matches the <code>int</code> overload exactly; <code>f(5.0)</code> matches <code>double</code>. The compiler picks the best match from the literal’s type.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — static members and overloading resolution</div><h3>static means "belongs to the class"</h3><p>A <strong>static</strong> field exists once, shared by every instance and even when no instance exists. An instance field exists once <em>per object</em>. So a static counter incremented in a constructor counts how many objects were ever created — the standard demonstration.</p><p>Static members are accessed through the class: <code>Math.max()</code>, <code>ClassName.method()</code>. This is why <code>main</code> is static — the JVM must call it before any object exists.</p><h3>The rule that causes the compile error</h3><p>A <strong>static method cannot use <code>this</code>, nor call instance methods or read instance fields directly</strong> — there is no particular object for them to belong to. The reverse is fine: an instance method may freely use static members. "Non-static variable cannot be referenced from a static context" always means you tried to use instance state from a static method.</p><h3>Constants</h3><p><code>static final</code> is the idiom for a constant: one shared copy (static) that cannot be reassigned (final), conventionally named in capitals. It documents intent and centralizes change.</p><h3>Overloading — and how Java picks</h3><p>Overloaded methods share a name but differ in <strong>parameter list</strong> (count, types or order). The return type alone is <strong>not</strong> enough — two methods differing only in return type will not compile, because a call like <code>doThing(5)</code> would be ambiguous.</p><p>Resolution happens at <strong>compile time</strong> from the declared argument types. Contrast this with <strong>overriding</strong>, resolved at <strong>run time</strong> from the object's actual type. That single sentence separates the two concepts the exam most likes to confuse:</p><ul><li><strong>Overloading</strong> — same class, same name, <em>different</em> parameters, compile-time.</li><li><strong>Overriding</strong> — subclass, same name, <em>same</em> parameters, run-time.</li></ul></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-static">
<h3>⚡ static vs instance</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th></th><th>static (class member)</th><th>instance member</th></tr>
<tr><th>Belongs to</th><td>The CLASS itself — ONE shared copy</td><td>Each OBJECT — its own copy</td></tr>
<tr><th>Call it via</th><td class="num">ClassName.method() — e.g. Math.max(3, 7)</td><td class="num">object.method() — e.g. d.bark()</td></tr>
<tr><th>Example field</th><td class="num">static int dogCount; (shared counter)</td><td class="num">String name; (each dog's own)</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE STATIC WALL</div>A static method CANNOT directly use instance fields or <b>this</b> — it doesn't belong to any object. That's why main (which is static) must CREATE objects to use them.</div>
<h3>🎭 Method Overloading</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">DEFINITION</div>Same method name, DIFFERENT parameter lists (different number OR types of parameters) in the same class. Java picks the match by the arguments you pass.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — three overloads of add</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Definition</th><th>Called by</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">int add(int a, int b)</td><td class="num">add(2, 3) → 5</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">double add(double a, double b)</td><td class="num">add(2.5, 3.5) → 6.0</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">int add(int a, int b, int c)</td><td class="num">add(1, 2, 3) → 6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ RETURN TYPE ALONE ≠ OVERLOAD</div>int f(int x) and double f(int x) do NOT compile together — the parameter lists are identical. Only the parameters distinguish overloads.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Which pair is a valid overload?
<ol type="A"><li>void f(int a) and void f(int a, int b)</li><li>int f(int a) and double f(int a)</li><li>void f(int a) and void f(int b)</li><li>Any two methods with the same name</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Different parameter COUNT = valid. B differs only by return type (invalid); C renames the parameter but same type list (invalid).</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch4/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-arrays"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — 1D Arrays</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — create, fill, and walk an array</div><pre>int[] scores = new int[4];      // [0, 0, 0, 0] — defaults
scores[0] = 90;  scores[3] = 75;

int[] quick = {90, 82, 68, 75};  // literal initialization

int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; quick.length; i++) {
    sum += quick[i];
}
// sum = 315; average = sum / (double) quick.length = 78.75</pre>Indices run 0 to length−1 (here 0..3). <code>quick.length</code> is a FIELD for arrays (no parentheses) — vs .length() the method on Strings and .size() on ArrayLists.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE BOUNDARY IS length − 1</div><code>quick[4]</code> throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at runtime. The classic causes: using &lt;= in the loop condition, or indexing with .length itself. Arrays are also FIXED size forever — growing means creating a new array (or using ArrayList).</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>For int[] a = new int[10], the valid indices are:<ol type="A"><li>0 through 9</li><li>1 through 10</li><li>0 through 10</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — 0 through 9</b> (length − 1). a[10] is out of bounds.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Arrays are the foundational data structure. Every algorithm involving collections of data uses arrays or builds on them. The OA tests zero-indexing, the length property (no parentheses!), and detecting ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException scenarios.</p></div><h3>Array Declaration and Initialization</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[] nums = new int[5];              // [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]  (default 0)
int[] primes = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11};     // initializer list  (length=5)
String[] names = new String[3];       // [null, null, null]</pre><h3>Accessing Elements</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">primes[0]        // 2  (first element — zero-indexed)
primes[4]        // 11 (last element = length - 1)
primes.length    // 5  (NO parentheses! array property, not method)
// primes[5] would throw ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</pre><h3>Traversal</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// Index-based for loop
for (int i = 0; i &lt; primes.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(primes[i]);
}

// Enhanced for (read-only)
for (int p : primes) {
    System.out.println(p);
}</pre><div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ ARRAY TRAPS</div><ul><li>Last valid index = length - 1. Accessing [length] throws exception.</li><li>array.length (no parens). String.length() (with parens).</li><li>Arrays are fixed size after creation — can't add or remove elements.</li></ul></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Zero-indexed: first=0, last=length-1</li><li>array.length (no parentheses)</li><li>Fixed size: declare size at creation, can't resize</li></ul></div>
<h3>Array fundamentals — defaults, length, bounds</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[] nums = new int[5];          // {0,0,0,0,0}    — int defaults 0
double[] d = new double[3];        // 0.0s
boolean[] b = new boolean[2];      // falses
String[] s = new String[3];        // {null,null,null} — objects default NULL!

int[] scores = {90, 85, 77};      // literal init
scores.length                      // 3 — FIELD, no parens (Strings use length())
scores[0]                          // 90 — first index is 0
scores[scores.length - 1]          // 77 — last element idiom
scores[3]                          // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException — runtime!</pre>
<h3>The four algorithms you must write cold</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// SUM and AVERAGE
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; scores.length; i++) sum += scores[i];
double avg = (double) sum / scores.length;     // cast or integer division!

// MAX (min is symmetric)
int max = scores[0];                            // start with FIRST element, not 0!
for (int i = 1; i &lt; scores.length; i++)
    if (scores[i] &gt; max) max = scores[i];
// Why not max=0? All-negative arrays would wrongly return 0.

// COUNT matching
int evens = 0;
for (int n : scores) if (n % 2 == 0) evens++;

// REVERSE in place
for (int i = 0; i &lt; scores.length / 2; i++) {
    int tmp = scores[i];
    scores[i] = scores[scores.length - 1 - i];
    scores[scores.length - 1 - i] = tmp;
}</pre>
<h3>Arrays are objects — reference semantics apply</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = a;                  // same array, two names
b[0] = 99;
System.out.println(a[0]);     // 99!

int[] copy = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length);   // a REAL copy
System.out.println(a == copy);              // false — different objects
System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.equals(a, copy)); // true — same contents
// Arrays.toString(a) → "[99, 2, 3]" for printing (plain println(a) prints gibberish)</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[] x = new int[4];
x[1] = 7;
System.out.println(x[0] + " " + x[1]);     // 0 7

int[] y = {3, 6, 9};
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= y.length; i++)         // &lt;= is the bug
    System.out.print(y[i] + " ");
// 3 6 9 then CRASH: ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 3

String[] names = new String[2];
System.out.println(names[0].length());      // NullPointerException — default null</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Defaults: 0 / 0.0 / false / null — null elements NPE on use</li><li>length (no parens) for arrays; last index = length-1; i &lt; length always</li><li>max starts at element [0], never at 0</li><li>= aliases the same array; Arrays.copyOf for real copies; Arrays.equals for content</li></ul></div>
<h3>Arrays as Method Parameters (zyBooks 6.x)</h3><p>An array parameter is declared like <code>void doubleAll(int[] nums)</code> and passed by giving just the array name: <code>doubleAll(scores)</code>. Java passes the <strong>reference</strong> value, so the method's parameter refers to the <em>same</em> array object — changes the method makes to elements are visible to the caller after the call. Passing an array does NOT copy it. (Reassigning the parameter itself to a new array, however, does not affect the caller's variable.)</p><h3>Exam focus: array default values</h3><p><code>int[] nums = new int[3];</code> creates an array whose elements are automatically initialized to <strong>0</strong> — never garbage or undefined. Java default values: numeric types → 0 (or 0.0), boolean → false, char → '�', object references → null. So a new String[3] holds three nulls, while a new int[3] holds three zeros.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>Arrays have a <strong>fixed size</strong> and are <strong>0-indexed</strong>, so valid indices are <code>0 .. length-1</code>. Reading out of range throws <strong><code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code> at run time</strong> (it compiles fine). A <code>new</code> array is auto-initialized to <strong>0 / 0.0 / false / null</strong>.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>int[] a = new int[3];
System.out.println(a[0]);   // default value
System.out.println(a[3]);   // out of range</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>0  then ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code></p><p>Elements default to 0, so <code>a[0]</code> prints 0. Index 3 is past the last valid index (2), throwing <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — arrays — fixed size, zero-based, and passed by reference</div><h3>What an array actually is</h3><p>An array is a <strong>fixed-size, contiguous block</strong> of same-typed slots. Two consequences follow from "contiguous": access by index is instant (the machine computes <code>base + index × size</code>), and the size can <strong>never change</strong> after creation. "Resizing" an array really means allocating a new one and copying — which is exactly what <code>ArrayList</code> does for you internally.</p><h3>Declaration and defaults</h3><p><code>int[] nums = new int[5];</code> creates five slots <em>already initialized</em> to the type default — 0 for numbers, <code>false</code> for boolean, <code>null</code> for objects. That last one matters: <code>String[] names = new String[3];</code> holds three nulls, so calling a method on <code>names[0]</code> throws a NullPointerException even though the array itself exists.</p><h3>The index rules that generate exam questions</h3><ul><li>Indexes run <strong>0 to length − 1</strong>. An array of length 5 has no index 5 — <code>arr[5]</code> throws <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>.</li><li><code>arr.length</code> is a <strong>field, no parentheses</strong>; <code>str.length()</code> is a method. Mixing them is a compile error and a deliberate exam trap.</li><li>The last element is always <code>arr[arr.length - 1]</code>.</li><li>Loop with <code>i &lt; arr.length</code>, never <code>&lt;=</code>.</li></ul><h3>Arrays are objects</h3><p>An array variable holds a <strong>reference</strong>. So passing an array to a method passes a copy of the reference — both names point at the same array, and <strong>changes made inside the method are visible to the caller</strong>. This is the clearest illustration of Java's pass-by-value-of-a-reference rule: mutating the contents is visible, but reassigning the parameter to a new array is not.</p><p>Likewise <code>arr2 = arr1</code> does <em>not</em> copy the array — it creates a second reference to the same one. Use <code>Arrays.copyOf()</code> or <code>clone()</code> for a real copy, and compare contents with <code>Arrays.equals()</code>, since <code>==</code> only compares references.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-arrays1d">
<h3>📦 1D Arrays</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Task</th><th>Code</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Declare with size</td><td class="num">int[] nums = new int[5];</td><td>5 slots, all start at 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Declare with values</td><td class="num">int[] nums = {10, 20, 30};</td><td>size inferred = 3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access / assign</td><td class="num">nums[0] · nums[2] = 99;</td><td><b>zero-based</b>: first index is 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td class="num">nums.length</td><td><b>NO parentheses</b> for arrays!</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE INDEX RULE</div>An array of length n has valid indices <span class="bignum">0 … n − 1</span>. The LAST element is nums[nums.length − 1].</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — traverse and sum {10, 20, 30}</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Pass</th><th>i</th><th>nums[i]</th><th>sum</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">10</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">20</td><td class="num">30</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">30</td><td class="num">60</td></tr></tbody></table>
for (int i = 0; i &lt; nums.length; i++) { sum += nums[i]; } → sum = <b>60</b></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ OFF-BY-ONE = CRASH</div>nums[nums.length] does NOT exist — for length 5, index 5 throws <b>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</b>. Loops use i &lt; length, never i &lt;= length.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
For int[] a = new int[6];, which index causes an exception?
<ol type="A"><li>a[6]</li><li>a[0]</li><li>a[5]</li><li>a[1]</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — a[6].</b> Valid indices are 0–5. Index 6 is one past the end → ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch4/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-2darr"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — 2D Arrays &amp; ArrayList</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 1 — a 2D array is [row][column]</div><pre>int[][] grid = {
    {1, 2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6}
};
System.out.println(grid[1][2]);   // row 1, column 2 → 6
// grid.length = 2 (rows); grid[0].length = 3 (columns)</pre>Always row first, then column. The nested for-loop walks it: outer loop rows, inner loop columns.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — ArrayList, the growable list</div><pre>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
names.add("Ana");        // [Ana]
names.add("Ben");        // [Ana, Ben]
names.add(1, "Cy");      // [Ana, Cy, Ben]   (insert shifts the rest)
names.remove("Ana");     // [Cy, Ben]
System.out.println(names.get(0) + ", size " + names.size()); // Cy, size 2</pre>ArrayList grows and shrinks automatically, uses methods — get(i), size(), add, remove — and holds OBJECTS only (Integer, not int; autoboxing converts for you).</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ length vs length() vs size()</div>Array → <code>.length</code> (field). String → <code>.length()</code> (method). ArrayList → <code>.size()</code> (method). Mixing these is a compile error and a beloved exam distractor.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>In grid[2][5], the 2 refers to the:<ol type="A"><li>Row</li><li>Column</li><li>Diagonal</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — row.</b> Java 2D arrays index [row][column].</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>2D arrays model grids, matrices, and tables. ArrayList solves the fixed-size limitation of arrays for dynamic collections. The OA tests ArrayList method names and when to choose ArrayList over an array.</p></div><h3>2D Arrays</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[][] grid = new int[3][4];  // 3 rows, 4 columns, 12 total elements
grid[0][0] = 1;                // row 0, column 0
grid[2][3] = 9;                // row 2, column 3

// Traverse with nested loops:
for (int r = 0; r &lt; grid.length; r++) {        // grid.length = rows
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; grid[r].length; c++) { // grid[r].length = cols
        System.out.print(grid[r][c] + " ");
    }
}</pre><h3>ArrayList</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Alice");           // add to end: ["Alice"]
names.add(0, "Zara");         // insert at index 0: ["Zara", "Alice"]
names.get(1);                 // "Alice"
names.size();                 // 2
names.remove(0);              // remove by index: ["Alice"]
names.remove("Alice");        // remove by value: []
names.set(0, "Bob");          // replace at index
names.contains("Bob");        // true/false</pre><p>ArrayList stores Objects only — use wrapper types: Integer, Double, Boolean for primitives.</p><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>2D array: grid[row][col]. grid.length = rows. grid[r].length = cols.</li><li>ArrayList: dynamic size. Key methods: add, get, set, remove, size, contains.</li><li>ArrayList requires Object types — int becomes Integer (autoboxing).</li></ul></div>
<h3>2D arrays — arrays of arrays</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">int[][] grid = new int[3][4];          // 3 rows × 4 cols, all zeros
int[][] m = { {1,2,3}, {4,5,6} };     // 2×3 literal

m.length        // 2  — number of ROWS
m[0].length     // 3  — columns in row 0
m[1][2]         // 6  — row 1, col 2

// THE standard traversal — burn into memory:
for (int r = 0; r &lt; m.length; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c &lt; m[r].length; c++) {
        System.out.print(m[r][c] + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();               // newline after each ROW
}

// Ragged rows are legal:
int[][] tri = new int[3][];
tri[0] = new int[1]; tri[1] = new int[2]; tri[2] = new int[3];
// That's why the inner loop uses m[r].length, not a fixed number</pre>
<h3>ArrayList — the resizable array</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();   // only OBJECT types in &lt;&gt;
list.add("apple");                  // append
list.add("cherry");
list.add(1, "banana");              // insert AT index, shifts the rest right
list.get(0)                         // "apple"
list.set(0, "apricot");             // replace at index
list.size()                         // 3 — method! (array.length, list.size())
list.remove(0);                     // remove by INDEX, shifts left
list.remove("cherry");              // remove by VALUE (first match)
list.contains("banana")             // true
list.indexOf("banana")              // 0 (after the removals above)

for (String fruit : list) System.out.println(fruit);   // for-each works</pre>
<h3>Autoboxing + the Integer remove trap</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; nums = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();   // int → Integer wrapper
nums.add(5);                        // autoboxing: int 5 → Integer 5
int v = nums.get(0);                // unboxing back

nums.add(10); nums.add(20);         // [5, 10, 20]
nums.remove(1);                     // by INDEX → removes 10 → [5, 20]
nums.remove(Integer.valueOf(20));   // by VALUE → removes 20 → [5]
// remove(int) and remove(Object) are different overloads — classic exam trap</pre>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left"></th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Array</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">ArrayList</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Size</td><td style="padding:8px">fixed at creation</td><td style="padding:8px">grows/shrinks</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Length</td><td style="padding:8px">.length (field)</td><td style="padding:8px">.size() (method)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Access</td><td style="padding:8px">a[i]</td><td style="padding:8px">list.get(i) / set(i, v)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Types</td><td style="padding:8px">primitives + objects</td><td style="padding:8px">objects only (Integer, not int)</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">ArrayList&lt;String&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
a.add("x"); a.add("y"); a.add("z");
a.remove(1);
a.add(1, "w");
System.out.println(a);              // [x, w, z]

int[][] g = new int[2][3];
g[1][2] = 9;
System.out.println(g.length + " " + g[0].length + " " + g[1][2]);  // 2 3 9</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>m.length = rows; m[r].length = that row's cols (handles ragged)</li><li>ArrayList: add/get/set/remove/size(); array: [i]/.length</li><li>Generics take object types: ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;, autoboxing bridges int</li><li>remove(1) = index; remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) = value</li></ul></div>
</div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>A <strong>2D array is an array of arrays</strong> — <code>m[row][col]</code>, and rows can have different lengths (jagged). An <strong><code>ArrayList</code> (java.util) grows dynamically</strong>: <code>add</code>, <code>get(i)</code>, <code>size()</code>, <code>remove(i)</code>. It stores objects, so primitives are autoboxed (<code>int</code>→<code>Integer</code>).</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
list.add(10);
list.add(20);
System.out.println(list.get(1) + " " + list.size());</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>20 2</code></p><p><code>get(1)</code> returns the second element (20); <code>size()</code> is 2. Indices are 0-based just like arrays.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — 2D arrays and choosing ArrayList over an array</div><h3>2D arrays are arrays of arrays</h3><p><code>int[][] grid = new int[3][4];</code> is three references, each pointing to a four-element array. So <code>grid.length</code> is the <strong>row count (3)</strong> and <code>grid[0].length</code> is the <strong>column count (4)</strong> — mixing those up is the standard 2D bug. Traverse with nested loops: outer over rows, inner over columns.</p><p>Because rows are separate arrays they need not be the same length — a <strong>jagged array</strong>. That is why you must use <code>grid[i].length</code> for the inner bound rather than assuming a single width.</p><h3>ArrayList — what it adds and what it costs</h3><p><code>ArrayList</code> wraps a resizable array and hides the copying. Use it when the size is unknown or changes; use a plain array when the size is fixed and known.</p><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:10px 0;font-size:13px;"><tbody><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;"></th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Array</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">ArrayList</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">Size</td><td style="padding:5px;">Fixed at creation</td><td style="padding:5px;">Grows and shrinks</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">Length</td><td style="padding:5px;"><code>arr.length</code> (field)</td><td style="padding:5px;"><code>list.size()</code> (method)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">Access</td><td style="padding:5px;"><code>arr[i]</code></td><td style="padding:5px;"><code>list.get(i)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">Holds</td><td style="padding:5px;">Primitives or objects</td><td style="padding:5px;">Objects only (autoboxing)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Why ArrayList cannot hold int</h3><p>Generics work with objects, so you write <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, not <code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code>. Java autoboxes on the way in and unboxes on the way out, which is invisible until a <code>null</code> element gets unboxed and throws a NullPointerException.</p><h3>The remove() ambiguity</h3><p>For an <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, <code>remove(2)</code> removes the element at <strong>index</strong> 2, while <code>remove(Integer.valueOf(2))</code> removes the <strong>value</strong> 2 — overload resolution picks the <code>int</code> version for a bare literal. Also remember that removing while looping forward shifts later elements down and makes the loop skip one; iterate backwards or use an iterator.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-arrays2d">
<h3>🗺️ 2D Arrays</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Task</th><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Declare</td><td class="num">int[][] grid = new int[3][4];</td><td>3 ROWS × 4 COLUMNS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access</td><td class="num">grid[1][2]</td><td>row 1, column 2 (both zero-based)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Row count</td><td class="num">grid.length</td><td class="num">3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Column count</td><td class="num">grid[0].length</td><td class="num">4</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — which cell is grid[1][2]?</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th></th><th>col 0</th><th>col 1</th><th>col 2</th><th>col 3</th></tr>
<tr><th>row 0</th><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td></tr>
<tr><th>row 1</th><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">★ HERE</td><td class="num">·</td></tr>
<tr><th>row 2</th><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td><td class="num">·</td></tr></tbody></table>
Always ROW first, COLUMN second: grid[row][col].</div>
<h3>📋 ArrayList — the resizable array</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Task</th><th>Code</th></tr>
<tr><td>Create</td><td class="num">ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add / read / change / delete</td><td class="num">list.add(5) · list.get(0) · list.set(0, 9) · list.remove(0)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td class="num">list.size()</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE THREE LENGTHS (classic OA trap)</div>
<b>Array</b> → .length (no parens) · <b>String</b> → .length() (parens) · <b>ArrayList</b> → .size(). Mixing them up = compile error.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
How do you get the number of elements in ArrayList&lt;String&gt; names?
<ol type="A"><li>names.size()</li><li>names.length</li><li>names.length()</li><li>names.count()</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — size().</b> .length is for arrays, .length() for Strings.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch4/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-except"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Exception Handling (try / catch / finally)</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — trace the flow when things break</div><pre>try {
    int[] a = new int[3];
    System.out.println("before");
    a[5] = 1;                       // throws! jump to catch
    System.out.println("never");    // skipped
} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("bad index");
} finally {
    System.out.println("always");
}</pre>Output: <b>before, bad index, always</b>. The moment the exception fires, the rest of try is abandoned; the MATCHING catch runs; <b>finally runs no matter what</b> — exception or not.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">CHECKED vs UNCHECKED</div>• <b>Unchecked</b> (RuntimeException family): NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBounds, ArithmeticException (÷ by zero), NumberFormatException — compiler doesn’t force handling.<br>• <b>Checked</b> (IOException, FileNotFoundException): must be caught or declared with <code>throws</code>, or the code won’t compile.<br>• Multiple catches: order them <b>specific first</b>, general (Exception) last.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ CATCH ORDER COMPILES OR DOESN’T</div>catch (Exception e) placed BEFORE catch (IOException e) is a compile error — the specific catch is unreachable. Specific first, always.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>The finally block executes:<ol type="A"><li>Whether or not an exception was thrown</li><li>Only after an exception</li><li>Only when no exception occurs</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — always</b> (barring program exit). That’s why cleanup code lives there.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Every real Java program must handle errors gracefully. Understanding when to catch vs declare exceptions, and the difference between throws and throw, is essential for writing production code and passing the D286 OA.</p></div><h3>try / catch / finally</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">try {
    int result = 10 / 0;             // throws ArithmeticException
    System.out.println("unreachable");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Division by zero: " + e.getMessage());
} catch (Exception e) {              // catches any Exception
    System.out.println("General: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    System.out.println("Always runs");  // cleanup code
}
// finally runs whether exception thrown or not</pre><h3>Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Type</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Examples</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Compiler requires handling?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">Checked</td><td style="padding:8px">IOException, FileNotFoundException, SQLException</td><td style="padding:8px"><strong>YES</strong> — must catch or declare throws</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Unchecked (RuntimeException)</td><td style="padding:8px">NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, ArithmeticException</td><td style="padding:8px">No — optional to handle</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>throws vs throw</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// throws: method DECLARATION — says this method may throw this exception
public void readFile(String path) throws IOException {
    // file reading code
}

// throw: STATEMENT — actually raises an exception right now
public void setAge(int age) {
    if (age &lt; 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative");
    this.age = age;
}</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>finally: always runs (cleanup code)</li><li>Checked exceptions: compiler forces you to handle (catch or throws)</li><li>Unchecked (RuntimeException): optional to handle</li><li>throws = declaration on method signature. throw = statement that raises exception.</li></ul></div>
<h3>Checked vs unchecked — the dividing line</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left"></th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Unchecked (RuntimeException)</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Compiler forces handling?</td><td style="padding:8px">No</td><td style="padding:8px">YES — catch or declare throws</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Usually means</td><td style="padding:8px">programming bug</td><td style="padding:8px">external problem (file, network)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Examples</td><td style="padding:8px">NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBounds, ArithmeticException, NumberFormatException, InputMismatchException</td><td style="padding:8px">IOException, FileNotFoundException, SQLException</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>try / catch / finally — full mechanics</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">try {
    int[] a = new int[3];
    a[5] = 1;                              // throws here
    System.out.println("never reached");   // skipped after the throw
} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("bad index: " + e.getMessage());
} catch (Exception e) {                    // broader catches must come LAST
    System.out.println("something else");
} finally {
    System.out.println("ALWAYS runs");     // cleanup home — even after return!
}

// Catch order rule: subclass before superclass.
// catch (Exception e) before catch (ArithmeticException e) = COMPILE ERROR
// ("already caught" — the broad one swallows everything)

// Multi-catch (one body, several types):
catch (NumberFormatException | ArithmeticException e) { ... }</pre>
<h3>throw and throws — one letter, different jobs</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// throw — YOU raise an exception right here:
public void setAge(int age) {
    if (age &lt; 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("age can't be negative");
    this.age = age;
}

// throws — declares "this method might pass a CHECKED exception to its caller":
public String readFile(String path) throws IOException {
    // caller must now try/catch this or declare throws itself
}</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT (finally edition)</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">static int test() {
    try {
        return 1;
    } finally {
        System.out.println("finally!");   // runs BEFORE the return completes
    }
}
System.out.println(test());
// Output: finally!  then  1

try {
    System.out.print("A");
    int x = 5 / 0;                        // ArithmeticException
    System.out.print("B");                // skipped
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.print("C");
} finally {
    System.out.print("D");
}
System.out.print("E");
// Output: ACDE

Integer.parseInt("12a");                  // NumberFormatException (unchecked)
new java.util.Scanner(System.in).nextInt(); // typing "abc" → InputMismatchException</pre></div>
<h3>Custom exceptions in 4 lines</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {   // checked
    public InsufficientFundsException(String msg) { super(msg); }
}
// extends RuntimeException instead → unchecked version
if (amount &gt; balance) throw new InsufficientFundsException("Short by $" + (amount-balance));</pre>
<div style="margin:16px 0;text-align:center"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 270" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Java exception class hierarchy" style="max-width:600px;width:100%"><defs><marker id="ar2" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z" fill="var(--text-3)"></path></marker></defs><rect x="225" y="8" width="150" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><text x="300" y="30" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">Throwable</text><rect x="40" y="80" width="150" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><text x="115" y="102" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">Error</text><text x="115" y="130" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-sub">serious JVM problems — don’t catch</text><rect x="400" y="80" width="150" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><text x="475" y="102" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">Exception</text><rect x="400" y="152" width="150" height="34" rx="6" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><text x="475" y="174" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">RuntimeException</text><text x="475" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-sub">UNCHECKED</text><rect x="300" y="224" width="140" height="30" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"></rect><text x="370" y="243" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-sub">NullPointerException</text><rect x="452" y="224" width="138" height="30" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"></rect><text x="521" y="243" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-sub">ArithmeticException</text><line x1="280" y1="42" x2="130" y2="80" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar2)"></line><line x1="320" y1="42" x2="465" y2="80" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar2)"></line><line x1="475" y1="114" x2="475" y2="152" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar2)"></line><line x1="455" y1="186" x2="380" y2="224" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar2)"></line><line x1="495" y1="186" x2="521" y2="224" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar2)"></line></svg><div style="font-size:13px;opacity:.75;margin-top:6px">Catch <strong>Exception</strong> subtypes, never <strong>Error</strong>. RuntimeExceptions are <em>unchecked</em> (optional to catch); all other Exceptions (e.g. IOException) are <em>checked</em> — the compiler forces you to handle them.</div></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Checked = compiler-enforced (IO/SQL); unchecked = RuntimeException bugs</li><li>Catch subclasses first; finally ALWAYS runs (even past return)</li><li>throw raises one; throws declares the possibility up the chain</li><li>Custom exception = extends Exception (+ constructor calling super)</li></ul></div>
<h3>💡 Exam power-ups: exceptions</h3><p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “the Compiler Checks Checked” — checked exceptions (IOException) must be handled or declared; unchecked (RuntimeException: NullPointer, ArrayIndexOutOfBounds, ClassCast, Arithmetic) surface at runtime. Catch subclass BEFORE superclass or the later block is unreachable (compile error). finally always runs. <strong>Strategy:</strong> memorize the five unchecked names above — D286 loves “which is unchecked?”</p><h3>Exam focus: multiple catch blocks</h3><p>A try with multiple catch blocks is checked <strong>top-down; the first matching block runs and the rest are skipped</strong>. Order matters: a subclass exception (like FileNotFoundException) must appear <em>before</em> its superclass (IOException) — putting the superclass first makes the later block unreachable, which is a <strong>compile error</strong>.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">🧠 MEMORY AID</div><p><strong>Checked vs Unchecked exceptions:</strong> <strong>Checked</strong> = the <em>Compiler</em> forces you to handle or declare it (<code>IOException</code>, <code>SQLException</code>). <strong>Unchecked</strong> = <code>RuntimeException</code> subclasses (<code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>). Flow: <strong>try → catch → finally</strong>, and <code>finally</code> runs whether or not an exception was thrown.</p></div><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><strong>Checked</strong> exceptions are compiler-enforced (you must catch or declare them): <code>IOException</code>, <code>SQLException</code>, <code>FileNotFoundException</code>. <strong>Unchecked</strong> = <code>RuntimeException</code> subclasses: <code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>, <code>ArithmeticException</code>. Order matters: <strong>catch specific before general</strong>. <strong><code>finally</code> always runs.</strong></p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>try {
  int x = 1 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
  System.out.print("caught");
} finally {
  System.out.print("done");
}</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>caughtdone</code></p><p><code>1/0</code> throws <code>ArithmeticException</code> → the catch prints "caught"; then <code>finally</code> runs no matter what, printing "done".</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — exceptions — checked, unchecked and the order of catch blocks</div><h3>What an exception is</h3><p>An exception is an object describing a failure. When one is thrown, normal flow stops and the JVM unwinds the call stack looking for a matching <code>catch</code>; if none exists the program terminates and prints a stack trace. The stack trace is a debugging gift: it names the exception, the message, and the exact line and call chain.</p><h3>Checked vs unchecked — the distinction that decides your code</h3><ul><li><strong>Checked</strong> (IOException, FileNotFoundException, SQLException) — conditions outside your control. The <strong>compiler forces</strong> you to either catch them or declare <code>throws</code>. Refusing is a compile error.</li><li><strong>Unchecked</strong> (NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, ArithmeticException, NumberFormatException) — programming bugs, subclasses of RuntimeException. The compiler does not force handling, because the right fix is usually to correct the logic, not to catch it.</li></ul><p>Scenario test: "the compiler will not let this compile until the exception is handled" ⟹ checked.</p><h3>try / catch / finally</h3><p><code>try</code> holds the risky code; <code>catch</code> handles a matching type; <code>finally</code> runs <strong>whether or not</strong> an exception occurred — even after a <code>return</code> — which makes it the place for cleanup such as closing resources. (Better still, <code>try-with-resources</code> closes them automatically.)</p><h3>Catch order is a compile-time rule</h3><p>Catch blocks are tested top to bottom, so they must run <strong>most specific first</strong>. Putting <code>catch (Exception e)</code> above <code>catch (IOException e)</code> makes the second unreachable, and Java rejects it as a compile error rather than a warning. This ordering question appears on nearly every Java exam.</p><h3>throw vs throws</h3><p><code>throw</code> is a statement that raises an exception right now (<code>throw new IllegalArgumentException("...")</code>). <code>throws</code> is a clause in a method signature declaring what that method might raise, passing responsibility to the caller. One letter, entirely different jobs.</p><h3>What not to do</h3><p>An empty catch block swallows the failure and leaves the program running on bad state — the hardest kind of bug to trace. Catch what you can genuinely handle; otherwise let it propagate to someone who can.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-exceptions">
<h3>🚨 Exception Handling — try / catch / finally</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Block</th><th>Definition</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>try</b></td><td>The risky code being watched</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>catch</b></td><td>Runs ONLY if a matching exception is thrown</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>finally</b></td><td>Runs <b>ALWAYS</b> — exception or not (cleanup: closing files, etc.)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>throw</b></td><td>Raise an exception yourself: throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad");</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>throws</b></td><td>In a method header: declares the method might pass one up</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — trace with a divide by zero</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">try {<br>&nbsp;int x = 10 / 0;<br>&nbsp;System.out.println("A");<br>} catch (ArithmeticException e) {<br>&nbsp;System.out.println("B");<br>} finally {<br>&nbsp;System.out.println("C");<br>}</td><td class="num">B<br>C<br><br>10/0 throws → "A" is SKIPPED → catch prints B → finally always prints C</td></tr></tbody></table>
With 10 / 2 instead: output is <b>A then C</b> — catch skipped, finally still runs.</div>
<h3>🧾 Common exceptions — recognize on sight</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Exception</th><th>Triggered by</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">ArithmeticException</td><td>integer division by zero: 10 / 0</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">NullPointerException</td><td>using a reference that is null: s.length() when s = null</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</td><td>a[6] on a length-6 array</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">NumberFormatException</td><td>Integer.parseInt("abc")</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">InputMismatchException</td><td>Scanner nextInt() receives "hello"</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ CATCH ORDER: SPECIFIC BEFORE GENERAL</div>catch (Exception e) before catch (ArithmeticException e) won't compile — the general one would swallow everything, making the specific catch unreachable.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
When does the finally block execute?
<ol type="A"><li>Always — whether or not an exception occurred</li><li>Only when an exception is thrown</li><li>Only when NO exception is thrown</li><li>Only if catch runs first</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> finally is the guaranteed-cleanup block — it runs on success AND on failure.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch5/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-inherit"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Inheritance with extends &amp; super</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a subclass, built and traced</div><pre>public class Animal {
    protected String name;
    public Animal(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public void eat() { System.out.println(name + " eats"); }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);              // MUST run the parent constructor first
    }
    public void fetch() { System.out.println(name + " fetches"); }
}

Dog d = new Dog("Rex");
d.eat();     // inherited from Animal → "Rex eats"
d.fetch();   // Dog's own → "Rex fetches"</pre>Dog IS-An Animal: it inherits eat() and name for free and adds fetch(). super(name) hands the name up to Animal’s constructor — and it must be the FIRST line of Dog’s constructor.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE RULES</div>• Java is single inheritance: one parent per class (interfaces fill the gap).<br>• private members are inherited but NOT directly accessible — use the parent’s getters (or protected).<br>• super.method() calls the parent’s version; super(...) calls the parent constructor.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ CONSTRUCTORS AREN’T INHERITED</div>Dog does not get Animal’s constructor — it must define its own and chain with super(...). If the parent lacks a no-arg constructor, the child MUST call super(args) explicitly or it won’t compile.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Inside a subclass constructor, super(...) must appear:<ol type="A"><li>As the first statement</li><li>As the last statement</li><li>Anywhere</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — first.</b> The parent must be fully constructed before the child adds its part.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Inheritance enables code reuse and hierarchical design. super() is how subclasses initialize parent state — forgetting it when the parent has no default constructor is a common D286 compile error. Every framework (Spring, JPA, Android) relies heavily on inheritance.</p></div><h3>extends</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Animal {
    protected String name;
    public Animal(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public void speak() { System.out.println(name + " makes a sound"); }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);  // MUST be first line; calls parent constructor
    }
    @Override
    public void speak() {
        System.out.println(name + " barks!");
    }
    public void fetch() { System.out.println("Fetching!"); }
}</pre><h3>super keyword</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Usage</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Meaning</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Constraint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">super(args)</td><td style="padding:8px">Calls parent constructor</td><td style="padding:8px">Must be first line in child constructor</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">super.method()</td><td style="padding:8px">Calls parent's version of overridden method</td><td style="padding:8px">Can be called anywhere in child method</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ MISSING super() COMPILE ERROR</div><p>If parent has no no-arg constructor (only parameterized), child constructor MUST call super(args) explicitly. Java won't auto-insert it if it can't find a matching no-arg constructor.</p></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>extends: child class inherits all non-private members of parent</li><li>super(): call parent constructor — must be first line</li><li>super.method(): call parent's version of a method</li></ul></div>
<h3>What inheritance actually gives you</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Animal {
    protected String name;                 // protected → subclasses can touch
    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("Animal ctor");
    }
    public void eat() { System.out.println(name + " eats"); }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {          // Dog IS-A Animal
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);                       // MUST be first line — parent builds first
        System.out.println("Dog ctor");
    }
    public void fetch() { System.out.println(name + " fetches"); }
}

Dog d = new Dog("Rex");
// Output: Animal ctor
//         Dog ctor          ← construction runs TOP-DOWN, always
d.eat();      // inherited ✓
d.fetch();    // own method ✓</pre>
<h3>The super() rules (compile-error territory)</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 1</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">super(...) must be the FIRST statement of the constructor</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 2</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">No explicit super(...) → Java silently inserts super() (no-arg)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 3</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">If the parent has NO no-arg constructor, Rule 2 fails → you MUST call super(args) yourself or it won't compile</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px"><strong>Rule 4</strong></td><td style="padding:8px">Constructors are NOT inherited — each class declares its own</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>What is and isn't inherited</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">// INHERITED: public + protected fields and methods, static members (shared)
// NOT inherited: constructors, private members
//   (private fields EXIST in the child object but aren't directly accessible —
//    use the parent's public getters)

public class Cat extends Animal {
    void test() {
        System.out.println(name);       // ✓ protected
        // System.out.println(secret); // ✗ if secret is private in Animal
    }
}</pre>
<h3>super.method() — extend, don't replace</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public class Puppy extends Dog {
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        super.eat();                      // run the parent version first...
        System.out.println("...messily"); // ...then add behavior
    }
}</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">class A { A() { System.out.print("A"); } }
class B extends A { B() { System.out.print("B"); } }
class C extends B { C() { System.out.print("C"); } }
new C();
// Output: ABC — grandparent → parent → child, every time

class Base {
    Base(int x) { }                       // ONLY constructor takes an int
}
class Kid extends Base {
    Kid() { }                             // ✗ COMPILE ERROR
    // implicit super() finds no Base() — must write super(someInt)
}</pre></div>
<div style="margin:16px 0;text-align:center"><svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Dog and Cat inherit from Animal" style="max-width:460px;width:100%"><defs><marker id="inh3" markerWidth="16" markerHeight="14" refX="13" refY="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L13,6 L0,12 Z" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1"></path></marker></defs><rect x="160" y="10" width="140" height="70" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><line x1="160" y1="34" x2="300" y2="34" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="230" y="27" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">Animal</text><text x="170" y="50" class="svg-sub">+ eat()</text><text x="170" y="63" class="svg-sub">+ sleep()</text><text x="170" y="76" class="svg-sub">+ makeSound()</text><rect x="22" y="162" width="170" height="76" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><line x1="22" y1="186" x2="192" y2="186" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="107" y="179" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">Dog</text><text x="32" y="202" class="svg-sub">+ makeSound()  «override»</text><text x="32" y="216" class="svg-sub">+ fetch()</text><rect x="268" y="162" width="170" height="76" rx="4" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"></rect><line x1="268" y1="186" x2="438" y2="186" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1"></line><text x="353" y="179" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-label-bold">Cat</text><text x="278" y="202" class="svg-sub">+ makeSound()  «override»</text><text x="278" y="216" class="svg-sub">+ purr()</text><line x1="107" y1="162" x2="220" y2="82" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#inh3)"></line><line x1="353" y1="162" x2="240" y2="82" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#inh3)"></line></svg><div style="font-size:13px;opacity:.75;margin-top:6px">Dog and Cat <strong>extend</strong> Animal: they inherit eat() and sleep(), and each <strong>overrides</strong> makeSound() — the basis of polymorphism.</div></div><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>extends = IS-A; child gets public/protected members, never constructors</li><li>super(...) first line; implicit super() breaks if parent lacks a no-arg ctor</li><li>Construction order: top of the family tree down to the child</li><li>super.method() inside an override = parent behavior + extras</li></ul></div>
<h3>UML Class Diagrams (zyBooks 8.x)</h3><p><strong>UML</strong> (Unified Modeling Language) draws each class as a box with three parts: the class name, the fields, and the methods. A <strong>+</strong> marks public members and a <strong>-</strong> marks private members. Inheritance (the is-a relationship) is drawn as a solid line with a hollow triangle arrow pointing from the derived class to the base class. UML lets you design and communicate class relationships before writing code.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>A subclass uses <strong><code>extends</code></strong> to inherit (Java has <strong>single</strong> class inheritance). A subclass constructor <strong>implicitly calls the parent’s no-arg constructor (<code>super()</code>) first</strong>; use <code>super(args)</code> to pick a specific parent constructor and <code>super.method()</code> to call the parent’s version.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>class Animal { Animal() { System.out.print("A"); } }
class Dog extends Animal { Dog() { System.out.print("D"); } }
new Dog();</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>AD</code></p><p>Creating a <code>Dog</code> runs <code>Animal()</code> first (implicit <code>super()</code>), printing "A", then the <code>Dog</code> body prints "D".</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — inheritance — what is inherited, and constructor chaining</div><h3>IS-A, and what actually gets inherited</h3><p><code>class Dog extends Animal</code> declares <strong>Dog IS-A Animal</strong>. Dog inherits Animal's accessible fields and methods and may add its own. It does <strong>not</strong> inherit constructors, and it cannot see Animal's <code>private</code> members directly — that is what <code>protected</code> or a public getter is for.</p><p>Java has <strong>single inheritance</strong> for classes: one direct superclass, no more. Multiple inheritance of <em>type</em> comes from interfaces, which sidesteps the ambiguity of inheriting two competing implementations. Every class ultimately extends <code>Object</code>, which is where <code>toString()</code>, <code>equals()</code> and <code>hashCode()</code> come from.</p><h3>Constructor chaining — the rule behind confusing errors</h3><p>A subclass constructor <strong>always</strong> runs its superclass constructor first, so the inherited part is initialized before the subclass touches it. If you do not call <code>super(...)</code> explicitly, Java silently inserts <code>super()</code> — the <em>no-argument</em> version.</p><p>That implicit call is the source of a classic compile error: if the parent defines only <code>Animal(String name)</code> and no no-arg constructor, the invisible <code>super()</code> has nothing to call and compilation fails. The fix is an explicit <code>super(name)</code> — which, like <code>this(...)</code>, must be the <strong>first statement</strong> in the constructor.</p><h3>super as a keyword</h3><p>Beyond constructors, <code>super.method()</code> calls the parent's version — used when an override wants to <em>extend</em> rather than replace behavior: do the parent's work, then add your own. <code>super.field</code> reaches a shadowed parent field.</p><h3>When NOT to inherit</h3><p>Inheritance is for IS-A. A Car <em>has</em> an Engine, so Engine belongs as a field (<strong>composition</strong>), not a superclass. Deep hierarchies are brittle: a change near the top ripples through every descendant, and subclasses depend on parent internals. Modern practice favors composition unless the IS-A relationship is genuine and stable.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 2 alternate explanations &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-inheritance">
<h3>🧬 Inheritance — extends &amp; super</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>extends</b></td><td>Declares a subclass (child) of a superclass (parent)</td><td class="num">class Dog extends Animal</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>IS-A</b></td><td>The relationship inheritance creates</td><td>A Dog IS-An Animal</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>super(...)</b></td><td>Calls the PARENT constructor — must be the FIRST line of the child constructor</td><td class="num">super(name);</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>super.method()</b></td><td>Calls the parent's version of a method</td><td class="num">super.speak();</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHAT GETS INHERITED</div>public and protected members come down to the child. <b>private fields exist in the child object but are NOT directly accessible</b> — reach them through the parent's public getters. Java allows only ONE parent class (single inheritance).</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — constructor chain order</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Output of new Dog()</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">class Animal {<br>&nbsp;Animal() { System.out.println("Animal born"); }<br>}<br>class Dog extends Animal {<br>&nbsp;Dog() { System.out.println("Dog born"); }<br>}</td><td class="num">Animal born<br>Dog born<br><br><b>Parent constructor runs FIRST</b> (Java inserts super() automatically), then the child's.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ super() PLACEMENT</div>If you call super(...) yourself, it must be line ONE of the constructor — anything before it is a compile error.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
class Cat extends Animal — when new Cat() runs, which constructor executes first?
<ol type="A"><li>Animal's (the superclass)</li><li>Cat's</li><li>They run simultaneously</li><li>Neither — constructors aren't inherited</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> The parent is always constructed first (implicit super()), building the foundation before the child adds its part.</div></details></div>
</div><div class="callout callout-tip" data-patch="gapfix"><div class="callout-label">📝 final IN INHERITANCE</div><p>A <span class="key-term">final class</span> cannot be extended (no subclasses — e.g. String). A <span class="key-term">final method</span> cannot be overridden by subclasses. Use them to lock behavior you don’t want changed.</p></div></details>"},"D286/ch5/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-poly"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Overriding &amp; Polymorphism</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — dynamic dispatch, the whole idea in 10 lines</div><pre>class Animal { public void speak() { System.out.println("..."); } }
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override public void speak() { System.out.println("Woof"); }
}
class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override public void speak() { System.out.println("Meow"); }
}

Animal a = new Dog();    // parent REFERENCE, child OBJECT
a.speak();               // Woof — the OBJECT's type decides!

Animal[] zoo = { new Dog(), new Cat() };
for (Animal x : zoo) x.speak();   // Woof, Meow</pre>The variable’s declared type (Animal) controls what you MAY call; the actual object’s type (Dog) controls WHICH version runs at runtime. That runtime choice is polymorphism (dynamic dispatch).</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ OVERRIDE = SAME SIGNATURE, SUBCLASS</div>Overriding replaces an inherited method: same name, same parameters, in the child. Change the parameters and you’ve accidentally OVERLOADED instead — @Override makes the compiler catch that mistake, which is exactly why you should write it.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Animal a = new Cat(); a.speak(); runs whose speak()?<ol type="A"><li>Cat’s — the actual object’s version</li><li>Animal’s — the reference type’s version</li><li>Neither — compile error</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — Cat’s.</b> Method choice happens at runtime based on the real object (dynamic dispatch).</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Polymorphism is what makes frameworks work. Spring doesn't know your specific controller class — it knows it implements an interface or extends a base class, and calls the right method at runtime. The OA tests which method runs when a parent reference holds a child object.</p></div><h3>Method Overriding</h3><p>A subclass redefines a parent method with the same signature. Use <code>@Override</code> — the compiler catches typos that would silently create a new method instead of overriding.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">@Override           // compiler verifies this actually overrides
public void speak() {
    System.out.println(name + " barks!");
}</pre><h3>Polymorphism and Runtime Dispatch</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">Animal a = new Dog("Rex");   // Animal reference, Dog object
a.speak();   // "Rex barks!" -- Dog's version runs (runtime dispatch)
// a.fetch();  // COMPILE ERROR -- Animal reference doesn't know fetch()

// Polymorphic array:
Animal[] zoo = { new Dog("Rex"), new Dog("Buddy") };
for (Animal x : zoo) {
    x.speak();   // correct method for each actual type
}</pre><h3>instanceof and Casting</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">if (a instanceof Dog) {         // check before casting
    Dog d = (Dog) a;            // safe downcast
    d.fetch();                  // can now call Dog-specific methods
}</pre><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>@Override: compile-time verification + documentation of intent</li><li>Polymorphism: parent reference, child method runs at runtime</li><li>Parent reference can't call child-only methods (compile error)</li><li>instanceof before casting prevents ClassCastException</li></ul></div>
<h3>Polymorphism — one variable type, many behaviors</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">Animal a = new Dog("Rex");      // UPCAST: parent reference → child object. Always legal.
a.eat();                        // calls DOG's override (if any) — runtime decides
// a.fetch();                   // ✗ COMPILE ERROR — the REFERENCE type (Animal)
                                //   controls what you're ALLOWED to call

// The two-question framework for every polymorphism problem:
// 1. COMPILE: does the REFERENCE type have this method? No → compile error.
// 2. RUNTIME: which version runs? The OBJECT's actual type's version.</pre>
<h3>Override rules — and @Override</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">class Animal { public void speak() { System.out.println("..."); } }
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override                              // annotation = compiler checks the match
    public void speak() { System.out.println("Woof"); }
}
// Same name, SAME parameters, same (or covariant) return type
// Can't REDUCE visibility (public parent → private child = compile error)
// static methods don't override — they HIDE (resolved by reference type!)
// @Override on a typo (speek) → compile error instead of a silent new method ✓</pre>
<h3>Overriding vs Overloading — the comparison they always test</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left"></th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Overloading</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Overriding</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Where</td><td style="padding:8px">same class (or inherited set)</td><td style="padding:8px">subclass redefines parent's</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Signature</td><td style="padding:8px">same name, DIFFERENT params</td><td style="padding:8px">same name, SAME params</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Decided at</td><td style="padding:8px">COMPILE time (static binding)</td><td style="padding:8px">RUNTIME (dynamic binding)</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Downcasting and instanceof</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">Animal a = new Dog("Rex");
// a.fetch() forbidden — so downcast when you KNOW it's a Dog:
if (a instanceof Dog) {
    Dog d = (Dog) a;                // explicit downcast
    d.fetch();                      // ✓
}
Animal cat = new Cat("Mia");
Dog wrong = (Dog) cat;              // compiles... ClassCastException at RUNTIME
// instanceof guard prevents the crash. (Modern: if (a instanceof Dog d) { d.fetch(); })</pre>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE OUTPUT</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">class Shape {
    public double area() { return 0; }
    public String toString() { return "Shape:" + area(); }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    public double area() { return 3.14; }
}
Shape s = new Circle();
System.out.println(s.area());      // 3.14 — object type wins at runtime
System.out.println(s);             // Shape:3.14 — toString calls the OVERRIDDEN area!

class P { static String who() { return "P"; } }
class Q extends P { static String who() { return "Q"; } }
P p = new Q();
System.out.println(p.who());       // P — static methods bind to the REFERENCE type</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Compile question: reference type has the method? Runtime question: object type's version runs</li><li>Override = same signature, runtime dispatch; overload = different params, compile-time</li><li>Downcast needs (Cast) + instanceof guard or risk ClassCastException</li><li>static methods hide, not override — they follow the reference type</li></ul></div>
<h3>💡 Exam power-ups: overloading vs overriding</h3><p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> over<strong>L</strong>oading = same c<strong>L</strong>ass, different parameter lists (compile time). over<strong>R</strong>iding = inhe<strong>R</strong>itance, same signature (runtime). Dynamic dispatch: Parent p = new Child(); p.m() runs Child’s override. <strong>Strategy:</strong> ask “which TYPE decides?” — reference type for overloads and fields, object type for overridden methods.</p></div></details><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">🧠 MEMORY AID</div><p><strong>Overload vs Override:</strong> <strong>Overload</strong> = same method name, <em>different parameter list</em>, decided at <strong>compile time</strong> (same class). <strong>Override</strong> = <em>same signature</em>, a subclass replaces the parent’s version, decided at <strong>run time</strong> (polymorphism). Memory hook: over<strong>LOAD</strong> changes the <strong>LOAD</strong> (the parameters); over<strong>RIDE</strong> rides over the parent.</p></div><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p><strong>Overriding</strong> = a subclass redefines a method with the <em>same signature</em>; the version that runs is chosen at <strong>run time by the object’s actual type</strong> (dynamic dispatch / polymorphism), even through a superclass reference. Use <code>@Override</code> so the compiler verifies the signature. Contrast with overloading (compile-time, by parameters).</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>class A { String m() { return "A"; } }
class B extends A { @Override String m() { return "B"; } }
A obj = new B();
System.out.println(obj.m());</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>B</code></p><p>Although the reference type is <code>A</code>, the object is a <code>B</code>, so <code>B.m()</code> runs. The actual object type — not the reference type — decides the overridden method.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — overriding and polymorphism — resolved at run time</div><h3>The rules of a valid override</h3><p>An override must have the <strong>same name, same parameter list and compatible return type</strong> as the parent method. Change the parameters and you have <em>overloaded</em>, not overridden — the parent version still runs for the original signature, which is a silent and very common bug. Add <code>@Override</code> and the compiler will catch that mistake for you; that is its entire purpose.</p><p>Additional constraints: you may not <strong>reduce visibility</strong> (a public method cannot become protected), and <code>static</code>, <code>final</code> and <code>private</code> methods cannot be overridden — <code>final</code> exists precisely to forbid it.</p><h3>Polymorphism, and the two types in play</h3><p><code>Animal a = new Dog();</code> is legal because Dog IS-A Animal. Now two types matter:</p><ul><li>The <strong>reference type</strong> (Animal) decides <em>which methods you may call</em> — checked at compile time. Calling a Dog-only method through an Animal reference will not compile without a cast.</li><li>The <strong>object type</strong> (Dog) decides <em>which version actually runs</em> — chosen at run time. This is <strong>dynamic dispatch</strong>, and it is what polymorphism means.</li></ul><p>So <code>a.speak()</code> compiles because Animal declares <code>speak()</code>, but executes Dog's override. Trace questions hinge on exactly this split.</p><h3>Why it matters practically</h3><p>A list of <code>Animal</code> can hold Dogs, Cats and Birds, and one loop calling <code>speak()</code> produces the right behavior for each. Adding a new subclass requires <strong>no change to that loop</strong> — the extensibility argument for OOP in one sentence.</p><h3>Casting and instanceof</h3><p>Upcasting (Dog → Animal) is always safe and implicit. Downcasting (Animal → Dog) is checked at run time and throws <code>ClassCastException</code> if the object is not really a Dog — guard it with <code>instanceof</code> first.</p><h3>The summary line worth memorizing</h3><p><strong>Overloading is compile-time and depends on the declared argument types; overriding is run-time and depends on the actual object.</strong></p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-polymorphism">
<h3>🎭 Method Overriding &amp; Polymorphism</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th></th><th>Overriding</th><th>Overloading</th></tr>
<tr><th>Where</th><td>Subclass REPLACES a parent method</td><td>SAME class, same name</td></tr>
<tr><th>Signature</th><td><b>IDENTICAL</b> (same name + parameters)</td><td><b>DIFFERENT</b> parameter lists</td></tr>
<tr><th>Decided</th><td>At RUNTIME (by the actual object)</td><td>At COMPILE time (by the arguments)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Marker</th><td class="num">@Override (catches typos)</td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">POLYMORPHISM — THE ONE RULE</div>When a parent-type variable holds a child object, an overridden method runs the <b>CHILD's version</b>. The ACTUAL OBJECT decides, not the variable type. (Dynamic dispatch.)</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — what prints?</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">class Animal { void speak() { System.out.println("..."); } }<br>class Dog extends Animal {<br>&nbsp;@Override void speak() { System.out.println("Woof"); }<br>}<br><br>Animal a = new Dog();<br>a.speak();</td><td class="num"><b>Woof</b><br><br>Variable type says Animal, but the OBJECT is a Dog — the Dog version wins.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE VARIABLE TYPE STILL GATES WHAT YOU CAN CALL</div>a.fetch() won't compile if fetch() exists only in Dog — the Animal-typed variable only exposes Animal's methods, even though the object is a Dog.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Shape s = new Circle(); s.draw(); — Circle overrides draw(). Which version runs?
<ol type="A"><li>Circle's — the actual object's type decides</li><li>Shape's — the variable's type decides</li><li>Both run</li><li>Compile error</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Overridden methods dispatch on the runtime object (Circle), not the reference type (Shape).</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch5/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-abstract"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Abstract Classes &amp; Interfaces</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE TWO CONTRACTS</div>• <b>Abstract class</b> — a partial class: may mix implemented methods, fields, constructors, and <b>abstract methods</b> (no body). Cannot be instantiated. One per child (extends).<br>• <b>Interface</b> — a pure contract: method signatures (+ default methods). A class can implement <b>many</b>. No instance state.<br>Choose: shared code + is-a hierarchy → abstract class. Capability that cuts across unrelated classes (Comparable, Runnable) → interface.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — one of each</div><pre>abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();                 // children MUST provide
    void describe() {                        // shared, already written
        System.out.println("Area: " + area());
    }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    double r;
    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
    double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }   // fulfills the contract
}

interface Printable { void print(); }
class Report implements Printable {
    public void print() { System.out.println("report"); }
}</pre><code>new Shape()</code> is a compile error (abstract). new Circle(2).describe() works — the shared describe() calls the child’s area(). Report implements Printable and could ALSO implement more interfaces at once.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ FORGET ONE ABSTRACT METHOD, BECOME ABSTRACT</div>A concrete subclass must implement EVERY abstract/interface method — miss one and the compiler demands the class itself be declared abstract. Also: you can’t new an interface or abstract class, but you CAN use them as reference types.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A class needs to promise behavior from TWO unrelated contracts. It should:<ol type="A"><li>Implement two interfaces</li><li>Extend two abstract classes</li><li>Neither is possible in Java</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Java allows implementing many interfaces but extending only ONE class.</div></details></div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div><div class="callout callout-why"><div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div><p>Abstract classes and interfaces are the tools for designing extensible APIs. Java's entire standard library is built on interfaces (List, Map, Runnable, Comparable). The D286 OA tests the abstract vs interface distinction, especially on what each can contain and how many you can extend/implement.</p></div><h3>Abstract Classes</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public abstract class Shape {
    public abstract double area();   // no body -- subclass MUST implement
    public void describe() {         // concrete method -- subclass inherits
        System.out.println("Area: " + area());
    }
}
// Shape s = new Shape();  // COMPILE ERROR -- can't instantiate abstract
Shape s = new Circle(5);   // OK -- Circle is concrete</pre><h3>Interfaces</h3><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public interface Flyable {
    void fly();                        // implicitly public abstract
    default void land() {             // default method (Java 8+) -- has body
        System.out.println("Landing...");
    }
}

public class Bird extends Animal implements Flyable, Swimmable {
    public void fly() { System.out.println(name + " is flying!"); }
}</pre><h3>Abstract Class vs Interface</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--surface-3)"><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Feature</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Abstract Class</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Interface</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:8px">Instantiate?</td><td style="padding:8px">No</td><td style="padding:8px">No</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Instance fields?</td><td style="padding:8px">Yes</td><td style="padding:8px">No (only static final)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Constructors?</td><td style="padding:8px">Yes</td><td style="padding:8px">No</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px">Multiple inheritance?</td><td style="padding:8px">No — extends one</td><td style="padding:8px">Yes — implements many</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Abstract class: can't instantiate; may have instance fields and concrete methods</li><li>Interface: no instance fields; all methods implicitly public; implement multiple</li><li>Abstract class for shared state/partial implementation. Interface for contracts/multiple types.</li></ul></div>
<h3>Abstract classes — the rules table</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public abstract class Shape {                 // CANNOT be instantiated
    protected String name;
    public Shape(String name) { this.name = name; }   // ctors allowed (for subclasses)

    public abstract double area();             // no body — subclasses MUST implement

    public void describe() {                   // concrete methods allowed too
        System.out.println(name + " area=" + area());
    }
}

public class Circle extends Shape {
    private double r;
    public Circle(double r) { super("circle"); this.r = r; }
    @Override
    public double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }   // forced by abstract
}

Shape s = new Shape("x");      // ✗ COMPILE ERROR — abstract
Shape c = new Circle(2);       // ✓ polymorphic reference is fine
// A subclass that does NOT implement area() must itself be declared abstract.</pre>
<h3>Interfaces — pure contract</h3>
<pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">public interface Drawable {
    void draw();                               // implicitly public abstract
    int MAX_SIZE = 100;                        // implicitly public static final
    default void preview() {                   // default methods (Java 8+) have bodies
        System.out.println("previewing...");
    }
    static boolean isValid(int s) { return s &lt;= MAX_SIZE; }
}

public class Icon implements Drawable {        // implements, not extends
    @Override
    public void draw() { System.out.println("drawing icon"); }  // MUST be public
}

// THE killer feature — multiple interfaces (single inheritance only for classes):
public class Sprite extends GameObject implements Drawable, Movable, Serializable { }</pre>
<h3>Abstract class vs interface — the decision</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0"><thead><tr><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left"></th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Abstract class</th><th style="padding:8px;text-align:left">Interface</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">How many can a class take</td><td style="padding:8px">extends ONE</td><td style="padding:8px">implements MANY</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Fields</td><td style="padding:8px">any kind, any visibility</td><td style="padding:8px">public static final constants only</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Constructors</td><td style="padding:8px">yes</td><td style="padding:8px">no</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Method bodies</td><td style="padding:8px">abstract + concrete freely</td><td style="padding:8px">abstract + default/static</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px">Relationship</td><td style="padding:8px">IS-A with shared state/code</td><td style="padding:8px">CAN-DO capability</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Mnemonic: <strong>abstract class = family inheritance with shared belongings; interface = a certification anyone can earn (and you can earn several).</strong></p>
<div class="callout callout-pitfall"><div class="callout-label">⚠ PREDICT THE BEHAVIOR</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:6px;">interface Flyer { void fly(); }
abstract class Bird { abstract void sing(); void sleep() { System.out.print("zzz"); } }

class Robin extends Bird implements Flyer {
    public void fly()  { System.out.print("fly "); }
    void sing()        { System.out.print("tweet "); }
}
Bird b = new Robin();
b.sing(); b.sleep();              // tweet zzz
// b.fly();                       // ✗ compile error — Bird reference lacks fly()
((Flyer) b).fly();                // fly  — cast to the interface ✓

abstract class A { abstract void m(); }
class B extends A { }             // ✗ COMPILE ERROR — must implement m() or be abstract</pre></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>abstract class: no instantiation, mixes abstract + concrete, has ctors/state</li><li>interface: contract; methods implicitly public abstract, fields public static final</li><li>One extends, many implements — interfaces are Java's multiple inheritance</li><li>Skip implementing an abstract method → the subclass must be abstract too</li></ul></div>
</div></details><div class="callout callout-tip" data-exam-focus="d286"><div class="callout-label">★ ON THE OA — WATCH FOR</div><p>An <strong>abstract class cannot be instantiated</strong> and may mix abstract (unimplemented) and concrete methods; a subclass must implement the abstract ones. An <strong>interface</strong> is implemented with <code>implements</code>, a class can implement <strong>many</strong> interfaces, and interfaces hold constants and (modern Java) default methods — but <strong>no instance state</strong>.</p></div><div class="callout callout-worked"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;overflow-x:auto"><code>abstract class Shape { abstract double area(); }
// Shape s = new Shape();   // COMPILE ERROR
class Circle extends Shape { double area() { return 3.14; } }
Shape s = new Circle();</code></pre><p><strong>Output:</strong> <code>Circle works; new Shape() is illegal</code></p><p>You cannot write <code>new Shape()</code> — abstract classes have no instances. You instantiate a concrete subclass (<code>Circle</code>) that implements the abstract <code>area()</code>.</p></div><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — abstract classes vs interfaces — how to choose</div><h3>What each one is</h3><p>An <strong>abstract class</strong> is a partially built class: it may hold fields, constructors, implemented methods and <code>abstract</code> methods with no body. It cannot be instantiated — <code>new Shape()</code> fails — because it is incomplete. A subclass must implement every abstract method or itself be declared abstract.</p><p>An <strong>interface</strong> is a contract of behavior. Its methods are implicitly public and abstract, its fields implicitly <code>public static final</code>. Modern Java also allows <code>default</code> methods with a body, so an interface can supply shared behavior without breaking existing implementers.</p><h3>The decision</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:10px 0;font-size:13px;"><tbody><tr><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Use an abstract class when…</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:5px;">Use an interface when…</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">Classes share state (fields) and code</td><td style="padding:5px;">Unrelated classes share a capability</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">There is a genuine IS-A hierarchy</td><td style="padding:5px;">You need multiple, independent contracts</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:5px;">You want a constructor and protected helpers</td><td style="padding:5px;">You want maximum flexibility for implementers</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The decisive constraint: a class may <code>extend</code> <strong>one</strong> class but <code>implement</code> <strong>many</strong> interfaces. That is why capabilities such as Comparable, Serializable or Flyable are interfaces — a Bird can extend Animal <em>and</em> implement Flyable, which single inheritance alone could never express.</p><h3>Programming to the interface</h3><p>Declaring <code>List&lt;String&gt; items = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</code> rather than <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; items</code> means you can swap in a <code>LinkedList</code> later without touching the rest of the code. Depending on the contract instead of the implementation is the practical payoff of abstraction — and the reason interfaces dominate professional Java.</p><h3>Traps</h3><p>An abstract class may contain <em>zero</em> abstract methods and still be uninstantiable. A class implementing an interface must implement <strong>every</strong> method or be abstract itself. And an abstract method has no body — writing <code>{}</code> makes it concrete and empty, which is a different thing entirely.</p></div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-abstract">
<h3>👻 Abstract Classes &amp; Interfaces</h3>
<table><tbody><tr><th></th><th>Abstract class</th><th>Interface</th></tr>
<tr><th>Instantiate it?</th><td><b>NO</b> — new Shape() won't compile</td><td><b>NO</b></td></tr>
<tr><th>Methods</th><td>MIX of abstract (no body) + concrete (with body)</td><td>Traditionally all abstract (a pure contract)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Fields</th><td>Regular fields allowed</td><td>Only constants (public static final)</td></tr>
<tr><th>A class can take</th><td><b>ONE</b> (extends)</td><td><b>MANY</b> (implements A, B, C)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Choose it when</th><td>Related classes share code + state</td><td>Unrelated classes share a capability ("can do X")</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — abstract Shape, concrete Circle</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">abstract class Shape {<br>&nbsp;abstract double area();<br>&nbsp;void describe() { System.out.println("I am a shape"); }<br>}</td><td>area() has NO body — children MUST provide it; describe() is shared as-is</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">class Circle extends Shape {<br>&nbsp;double r;<br>&nbsp;double area() { return 3.14159 * r * r; }<br>}</td><td>Circle fills in the blank → now it CAN be instantiated</td></tr></tbody></table>
new Shape(); → compile error. Shape s = new Circle(); → ✓ (polymorphism again).</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE CONTRACT IS BINDING</div>A concrete (non-abstract) subclass MUST implement every abstract method it inherits — skip one and it won't compile (unless the subclass is declared abstract too).</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>
Which statement about abstract classes is true?
<ol type="A"><li>They cannot be instantiated directly</li><li>All their methods must be abstract</li><li>A class can extend several of them</li><li>They cannot have constructors</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> No new for abstract classes. They MAY mix concrete methods (B false), single inheritance applies (C false), and they can have constructors for subclasses to call (D false).</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch6/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-oaprep"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Attacking the D286 OA</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE GAME PLAN</div>• The OA leans hard on <b>reading code</b>: “what is printed?” questions dominate. Your weapon is the <b>trace table</b> — write variable values per step, never simulate in your head.<br>• Bank the fast facts: casting truncates, int division drops fractions, == vs equals, length/length()/size(), fall-through, off-by-one bounds, super() first line, override vs overload.<br>• Time: quick definitional questions first, code traces second pass — flag and return rather than stall.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a typical OA trace, done the reliable way</div><pre>int x = 3, y = 0;
while (x &gt; 0) {
    y += x;
    x -= 2;
}
System.out.println(y);</pre>Table: x=3,y=0 → pass 1: y=3, x=1 → pass 2 (1&gt;0): y=4, x=−1 → test fails. Prints <b>4</b>. Thirty seconds with a table beats three minutes of mental replay — and catches the x=−1 endpoint you’d otherwise miss.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>The most reliable way to answer “what is printed?” questions is:<ol type="A"><li>A written trace table of every variable per pass</li><li>Running the loop mentally once</li><li>Choosing the middle option</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Trace tables turn code questions into bookkeeping — slow is smooth, smooth is fast.</div></details></div></div>

