<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The D286 OA is NOT multiple choice — you write real Java code in a browser-based environment, and an automated grader runs it. Knowing the format and having a task strategy is worth as much as knowing Java.</p>
</div>
<h3>What the assessment looks like</h3>
<p>You'll face a series of coding tasks (typically around 8-12) in a locked-down browser IDE. Each task gives you a prompt — usually "write a program/method that reads X and outputs Y" — starter code in some cases, and you write Java that must compile and produce exactly the expected output. Partial credit exists per task, but output formatting matters: an extra space or missing newline can fail a test case.</p>
<h3>The golden rules of coding OAs</h3><div data-marker="dgf_golden"><ol style="margin:10px 0 14px 20px; line-height:1.7;"><li><strong>Read the whole prompt twice</strong> before typing — half of lost marks are answering a different question.</li><li><strong>Trace with a concrete value.</strong> Pick a small input and walk the code line by line on paper.</li><li><strong>Check boundaries first:</strong> 0, 1, empty, the last index — loops live or die at the edges (<code>&lt;</code> vs <code>&lt;=</code>).</li><li><strong>Mind integer division and types:</strong> <code>7 / 2</code> is 3 in int land; <code>==</code> on Strings compares references.</li><li><strong>Eliminate before you choose:</strong> two options usually die fast; decide between the survivors by tracing.</li><li><strong>Never leave blanks.</strong> No penalty for guessing — an eliminated-to-two guess is a coin you should always flip.</li></ol></div>

<h3>The complete topic checklist</h3>
<p>Go into the OA only when you can code each of these cold:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input/Output:</strong> Scanner (nextInt, nextDouble, nextLine, next), System.out.print/println/printf</li>
<li><strong>Types:</strong> int, double, boolean, char, String; casting; integer division and modulo</li>
<li><strong>Strings:</strong> length(), charAt(), substring(), equals(), equalsIgnoreCase(), compareTo(), indexOf(), toUpperCase/LowerCase(), trim(), contains()</li>
<li><strong>Conditionals:</strong> if/else-if/else, switch, ternary, logical operators &amp;&amp;, ||, !</li>
<li><strong>Loops:</strong> for, while, do-while, for-each, break, continue, nested loops</li>
<li><strong>Arrays:</strong> declare, initialize, traverse, find max/min/sum/average, search</li>
<li><strong>ArrayList:</strong> add, get, set, remove, size, contains, traversal</li>
<li><strong>Methods:</strong> signatures, parameters, return types, overloading, static vs instance</li>
<li><strong>Classes:</strong> fields, constructors, getters/setters, toString(), creating and using objects</li>
<li><strong>Exceptions:</strong> try/catch, multiple catches, finally, throwing exceptions</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Scanner trap that fails more students than any Java concept</h3>
<pre><code>Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = sc.nextInt();      // reads the NUMBER but leaves "\n" in the buffer
String name = sc.nextLine(); // reads that leftover "\n" → name is EMPTY!

// THE FIX: consume the leftover newline
int age2 = sc.nextInt();
sc.nextLine();               // eat the newline
String name2 = sc.nextLine(); // now reads the actual next line ✓</code></pre>
<h3>Point-losing mistakes catalog</h3>
<pre><code>// 1. == on Strings (compares references, not contents)
if (word == "yes")          // ✗ unreliable
if (word.equals("yes"))     // ✓

// 2. Integer division surprise
double avg = sum / count;            // ✗ if both int → truncates BEFORE assigning
double avg2 = (double) sum / count;  // ✓ cast first

// 3. Off-by-one in loops
for (int i = 0; i &lt;= arr.length; i++)  // ✗ ArrayIndexOutOfBounds at i = length
for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++)   // ✓

// 4. println when they asked for print (or vice versa)
// "output the numbers separated by spaces on one line" → print(x + " "), not println

// 5. Forgetting to close/handle the last case in switch (missing break = fall-through)</code></pre>
<h3>Your exam-day pacing plan</h3>
<p>1. <strong>Minutes 0-5:</strong> skim every task; mark each Easy / Medium / Hard.<br>
2. <strong>First pass:</strong> all Easy tasks — compile and verify each before moving on.<br>
3. <strong>Second pass:</strong> Mediums. If stuck 10+ minutes, write the partial solution (read input, set up structure) and move on.<br>
4. <strong>Final pass:</strong> Hards + return to stuck tasks with fresh eyes.<br>
5. <strong>Last 10 minutes:</strong> verify every task at least compiles and prints something — a compile error scores zero even if the logic is 95% there.</p>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>It's a coding exam — practice WRITING programs, not just reading them</li><li>Exact output format; compile early; never leave blanks</li><li>The nextInt-then-nextLine Scanner trap and == on Strings are the classic killers</li><li>Bank easy tasks first; a compiling partial beats an ambitious blank</li></ul></div>
<p><strong>Good OA habits:</strong> during the exam, flag hard questions and move on rather than fixing every doubt immediately — never ignore the timer. Read compiler-error questions carefully: identify what kind of errors the code produces before choosing. Return to flagged items with your remaining time.</p><h3>💡 Sure-fire OA strategies</h3><p><strong>1.</strong> Trace every code question on your scratch space — write the variable table; never eyeball increments. <strong>2.</strong> Bank the traps: integer division, x++ vs ++x, == vs .equals, fall-through switch, off-by-one at arr.length, subclass-catch-first. <strong>3.</strong> Two passes: answer sure ones first, flag the rest — momentum beats perfection. <strong>4.</strong> If two answers look identical, re-read the TYPES in the question; the difference is usually a type. <strong>5.</strong> The night before: redo only your flagged/weak questions, then stop — rested beats crammed.</p><h3>Exam focus: edge cases — the testing habit that catches bugs</h3><p>Good tests go beyond one “happy path” sample. Always check <strong>boundary and unusual inputs</strong>: 0, negative numbers, the empty string or empty array, the largest/smallest allowed value, and duplicates. Most real bugs — and most exam answers — live at these edges, not in the middle of the range.</p></div></details><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — reading the D286 OA like an examiner</div><h3>What the exam is testing</h3><p>The OA is not a vocabulary quiz. Most questions show you code and ask what it <strong>outputs</strong>, whether it <strong>compiles</strong>, or which construct you should <strong>choose</strong>. So your preparation should be: trace code on paper, and be able to say why each wrong option is wrong.</p><h3>The highest-yield trap list</h3><ul><li><code>==</code> vs <code>.equals()</code> on Strings and wrappers.</li><li>Integer division: <code>5 / 2</code> is 2.</li><li><code>arr.length</code> (field) vs <code>str.length()</code> (method) vs <code>list.size()</code>.</li><li>Index bounds: last index is <code>length − 1</code>; loop with <code>&lt;</code>.</li><li><code>substring(a, b)</code> — <strong>b is exclusive</strong>; <code>indexOf</code> returns <strong>−1</strong> when absent.</li><li>Missing <code>break</code> in a switch causes fall-through.</li><li>Defining any constructor removes the free default one.</li><li>Overloading (compile-time, different parameters) vs overriding (run-time, same parameters).</li><li>Catch blocks must be ordered most-specific first, or it will not compile.</li><li>Checked exceptions must be caught or declared; unchecked need not be.</li><li>Strings are immutable — methods return a new String, they do not modify.</li><li>Overriding <code>equals</code> without <code>hashCode</code> breaks HashMap and HashSet.</li></ul><h3>A method for code questions</h3><p>1) Scan for a <em>compile</em> error first — wrong catch order, missing constructor, calling an instance member from static, mismatched types. If it will not compile, that is the answer. 2) Otherwise trace with a variable table, writing each value as it changes. 3) Watch the boundaries — first and last iteration are where the errors are planted. 4) Eliminate options you can disprove rather than hunting for the one that feels right.</p></div><i data-d286_s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the exam-day decision rules, in one place</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk429a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"></path></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">read the prompt twice</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">half of lost tasks solve the wrong problem</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">compile early, compile often</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a compile error scores zero</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">match the output exactly</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">print vs println, spaces, newlines</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">easy tasks first</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">bank the sure points, then return</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">stuck 10+ minutes</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">write the partial, move on</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">last 10 minutes</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">make every task at least compile</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The Scanner trap, actually run</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("without the fix:");
broken();
System.out.println("with sc.nextLine() to eat the newline:");
fixed();</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">without the fix:
  age=27  name=[]
with sc.nextLine() to eat the newline:
  age=27  name=[Ada Lovelace]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li><code>nextInt()</code> reads the digits and stops — it leaves the newline sitting in the buffer.</li><li>The next <code>nextLine()</code> reads from there to the end of that line, which is nothing at all, so the name comes back empty.</li><li>The fix is one extra <code>sc.nextLine()</code> whose only job is to consume the leftover newline. The brackets in the output are there so you can see the empty string rather than guess at it.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — "Correct" output that an automated grader still fails</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] nums = { 3, 1, 4 };

System.out.println("asked for: one line, values separated by spaces");
System.out.print("got A: ");
for (int n : nums) System.out.print(n + " ");
System.out.println();

System.out.println("got B:");
for (int n : nums) System.out.println(n);

System.out.printf("average as asked  : %.2f%n", 8.0 / 3);
System.out.println("average unformatted: " + (8.0 / 3));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">asked for: one line, values separated by spaces
got A: 3 1 4 
got B:
3
1
4
average as asked  : 2.67
average unformatted: 2.6666666666666665</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A and B compute exactly the same numbers; only the printing differs, and only A satisfies the prompt.</li><li>Swapping <code>print</code> for <code>println</code> is the single most common way to fail a task whose logic is perfect.</li><li>The last two lines are the same value shown two ways — a prompt asking for two decimals wants the <code>printf</code>, not the raw <code>double</code>.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Treat the output format as part of the problem, not as presentation. Before you submit a task, re-read the prompt's sample output and compare it to yours character by character — separators, capitalisation, decimal places and the final newline included. The grader does a literal string comparison and gives no credit for nearly.</div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-oa-format">
<h3>💻 The D286 OA: Format, Strategy &amp; Topic Checklist</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE BIG DIFFERENCE</div>This OA is <b>NOT multiple choice</b> — you write REAL Java code in a locked-down browser IDE, and an automated grader runs it. Around <b>8–12 coding tasks</b>: a prompt ("write a program that reads X and outputs Y"), sometimes starter code, and your Java must compile and produce EXACTLY the expected output. Partial credit exists per task — but an extra space or missing newline can fail a test case.</div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">THE GOLDEN RULES OF CODING OAs</div>
<table><tbody><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Read the FULL prompt twice before typing</b></td><td>Half of failed tasks come from solving the wrong problem</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Match output format EXACTLY</b></td><td>println vs print, spaces, punctuation — the grader is literal</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Compile early, compile often</b></td><td>Fix one error at a time; don't write 40 lines before first compile</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Bank the easy tasks first</b></td><td>Skim all tasks, do the sure ones, return to hard ones</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Test edge cases mentally</b></td><td>Empty input, zero, negative numbers, single-element arrays</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Never leave a task blank</b></td><td>Even reading input + printing something can earn partial credit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE TOPIC CHECKLIST — code each of these cold before test day</div>
<b>Input/Output:</b> Scanner (nextInt, nextDouble, nextLine, next) · print/println/printf. <b>Types:</b> int, double, boolean, char, String; casting; integer division and modulo. <b>Strings:</b> length(), charAt(), substring(), equals(), equalsIgnoreCase(), compareTo(), indexOf(), toUpperCase/LowerCase(), trim(), contains(). <b>Conditionals, loops, arrays, ArrayList, methods, classes, exceptions</b> — the full list continues in the original below.</div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Your output is correct except for a missing newline. The automated grader will:
<ol type="A"><li>Fail that test case — formatting is literal</li><li>Accept it anyway</li><li>Give a warning</li><li>Ask you to fix it</li></ol>
<details><summary>Show answer + steps</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Match the expected output EXACTLY — println vs print matters.</div></details></div>
</div></details>"},"D286/ch6/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-drills1"><h3>📖 Worked drill — basics, strings &amp; loops in one problem</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — count the vowels (full solution, explained)</div><pre>String s = "banana";
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
    char c = s.charAt(i);
    if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') {
        count++;
    }
}
System.out.println(count);   // 3</pre>The pattern stack: loop over indices (0 to length()−1) → charAt to inspect → condition → accumulate. b-a-n-a-n-a has a at indices 1, 3, 5 → <b>3</b>. Most drill problems are this same skeleton with a different condition — recognize the skeleton and the problem writes itself.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ chars USE SINGLE QUOTES</div>'a' is a char; "a" is a String — c == "a" doesn’t compile. And the loop bound is s.length() with parentheses (String method), i &lt; length, not &lt;=.</div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div>

<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>These ten drills mirror the style and difficulty of real D286 tasks. Write each one yourself FIRST — in an actual editor (use jdoodle.com or replit if you're away from your IDE) — then compare against the solution. Reading solutions without writing code first teaches you nothing.</p>
</div>
<h3>Drill 1 — Input and arithmetic</h3>
<p><em>Read two integers and print their sum, difference, product, and the result of dividing the first by the second as a double with 2 decimals.</em></p>
<pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int a = sc.nextInt();
    int b = sc.nextInt();
    System.out.println(a + b);
    System.out.println(a - b);
    System.out.println(a * b);
    System.out.printf("%.2f%n", (double) a / b);   // cast BEFORE dividing
  }
}</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 2 — Even/odd with edge case</h3>
<p><em>Read an integer; print "EVEN", "ODD", or "ZERO".</em></p>
<pre><code>int n = sc.nextInt();
if (n == 0)            System.out.println("ZERO");
else if (n % 2 == 0)   System.out.println("EVEN");
else                   System.out.println("ODD");
// Watch: negative odd numbers → n % 2 is -1, not 1. (n % 2 != 0) catches both.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 3 — String reversal (no built-in reverse)</h3>
<pre><code>String s = sc.nextLine();
String rev = "";
for (int i = s.length() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {
    rev += s.charAt(i);
}
System.out.println(rev);
// Alternative they also accept: new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString()</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 4 — Vowel counter</h3>
<p><em>Count vowels in a line, case-insensitive.</em></p>
<pre><code>String line = sc.nextLine().toLowerCase();
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; line.length(); i++) {
    char c = line.charAt(i);
    if (c=='a' || c=='e' || c=='i' || c=='o' || c=='u') count++;
}
System.out.println(count);
// Or: if ("aeiou".indexOf(c) &gt;= 0) count++;</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 5 — Grade calculator (if/else chain)</h3>
<pre><code>int score = sc.nextInt();
String grade;
if      (score &gt;= 90) grade = "A";
else if (score &gt;= 80) grade = "B";
else if (score &gt;= 70) grade = "C";
else if (score &gt;= 60) grade = "D";
else                  grade = "F";
System.out.println(grade);
// Order matters: check from highest down, no need for "score &lt; 90 &amp;&amp;"</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 6 — Sum until sentinel</h3>
<p><em>Read integers until -1 is entered; print their sum (excluding the -1).</em></p>
<pre><code>int sum = 0;
int n = sc.nextInt();
while (n != -1) {
    sum += n;
    n = sc.nextInt();   // read the NEXT value at the END of the loop
}
System.out.println(sum);
// Classic bug: reading inside the loop FIRST adds the sentinel. Pattern: read → test → process → read.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 7 — Multiplication table (nested-ish loop)</h3>
<pre><code>int n = sc.nextInt();
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {
    System.out.println(n + " x " + i + " = " + (n * i));
}
// Note the parentheses around (n * i): without them, + concatenates strings.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 8 — FizzBuzz (the eternal classic)</h3>
<pre><code>for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 100; i++) {
    if (i % 15 == 0)      System.out.println("FizzBuzz");  // test 15 FIRST
    else if (i % 3 == 0)  System.out.println("Fizz");
    else if (i % 5 == 0)  System.out.println("Buzz");
    else                  System.out.println(i);
}
// The order trap: testing %3 before %15 means "FizzBuzz" never prints.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 9 — Password validator</h3>
<p><em>Valid if: length ≥ 8, contains a digit, contains an uppercase letter. Print VALID or INVALID.</em></p>
<pre><code>String pw = sc.nextLine();
boolean hasDigit = false, hasUpper = false;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; pw.length(); i++) {
    char c = pw.charAt(i);
    if (Character.isDigit(c))     hasDigit = true;
    if (Character.isUpperCase(c)) hasUpper = true;
}
if (pw.length() &gt;= 8 &amp;&amp; hasDigit &amp;&amp; hasUpper) System.out.println("VALID");
else                                          System.out.println("INVALID");</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 10 — do-while menu loop</h3>
<pre><code>int choice;
do {
    System.out.println("1. Deposit  2. Withdraw  3. Exit");
    choice = sc.nextInt();
    if (choice == 1)      System.out.println("Depositing...");
    else if (choice == 2) System.out.println("Withdrawing...");
} while (choice != 3);
System.out.println("Goodbye");
// do-while = body guaranteed to run at least once — the menu pattern.</code></pre>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">💡 PRACTICE PROTOCOL</div><p>For each drill: (1) write it without looking, (2) compile and run with 2-3 test inputs including an edge case, (3) only then compare with the solution. Re-do any drill you couldn't complete from memory two days later.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Sentinel loops: read → test → process → read-again pattern</li><li>FizzBuzz teaches condition ORDER; grade chains teach threshold order</li><li>(double) cast before division; parentheses around arithmetic inside concatenation</li><li>Character.isDigit/isUpperCase beat manual ASCII checks</li></ul></div>
<h3>Exam focus: safe odd test and printf newline</h3><p><strong>Odd test:</strong> in Java, <code>x % 2 == 1</code> FAILS for negative numbers (−3 % 2 is −1). The safe odd test that also handles negatives is <strong><code>x % 2 != 0</code></strong>. <strong>printf newline:</strong> in format strings, <strong><code>%n</code></strong> writes the platform-appropriate line separator (\n is a literal linefeed and is not platform-aware).</p></div></details><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — drilling basics, strings, conditionals and loops</div><h3>What to practise, and why</h3><p>These drills exist to make the mechanical parts automatic so that exam time goes to reasoning. Concretely, you should be able to do each of these without hesitation:</p><ul><li>Trace a loop and state the value of every variable after each pass.</li><li>Predict integer-division and modulo results, including with negatives.</li><li>Walk a String character by character with <code>charAt</code> and <code>length()</code>.</li><li>Say whether a given <code>if</code>/<code>switch</code> falls through, and which branch fires.</li><li>Spot the off-by-one in a loop bound at a glance.</li></ul><h3>The patterns that recur</h3><p><strong>Accumulator:</strong> declare a total <em>before</em> the loop, add inside. <strong>Counter with a condition:</strong> count items matching a test. <strong>Max/min so far:</strong> initialize from the first element (not 0, which breaks with negative data). <strong>Search with a flag:</strong> set found = true and break. <strong>Building a string:</strong> use StringBuilder inside loops. <strong>Swap:</strong> always via a temporary variable.</p><p>Recognizing which pattern a question wants is faster than reasoning from scratch, and these five cover most exam code.</p><h3>How to practise properly</h3><p>Predict the output <em>before</em> you check. If you were wrong, do not just read the answer — find the exact line where your mental model diverged. That gap is the thing being tested, and it is what will otherwise repeat on the exam.</p></div><i data-d286_s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the loop patterns nearly every drill is built from</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk430a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"></path></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">accumulator</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">total declared before, added inside</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">counter</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">count items passing a test</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">max/min so far</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">seed from the first element, not 0</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">search with a flag</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">set found, then break</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">sentinel loop</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">read → test → process → read again</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">build a string</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">StringBuilder inside the loop</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — The sentinel loop, right and wrong</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] typed = { 10, 20, 30, -1 };
System.out.println("read-test-process-read: " + rightWay(typed));
System.out.println("read inside the loop:   " + wrongWay(typed));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">read-test-process-read: 60
read inside the loop:   59</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>The correct shape reads one value <em>before</em> the loop, then reads the next at the very end of the body.</li><li>The wrong shape reads at the top of the body, so the sentinel is added to the total before the test can stop it.</li><li>The difference is exactly the sentinel's value: 60 against 59. It is a quiet bug — the program runs, prints a number, and is simply wrong.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — FizzBuzz: why the order of the tests decides the answer</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">for (int i = 13; i &lt;= 16; i++) {
