</div></details>"},"D286/ch6/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d286-uni-drills2"><h3>📖 Worked drill — arrays, methods &amp; classes in one problem</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — find the max with a method (full solution)</div><pre>public static int max(int[] arr) {
    int best = arr[0];                    // start with the FIRST element
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] &gt; best) {
            best = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return best;
}

int[] data = {4, 9, 2, 9, 5};
System.out.println(max(data));   // 9</pre>Trace: best=4 → 9&gt;4, best=9 → 2 no → 9 no (not &gt;) → 5 no → returns <b>9</b>. Seeds: max starts at arr[0] (NOT 0 — fails on all-negative arrays); min starts at arr[0] too. This find-the-extreme skeleton, plus sum/average and count-matching, covers most array drills on the OA.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ START best AT arr[0], NOT 0</div>Initializing max to 0 breaks on {−5, −2} (returns 0, which isn’t even in the array). Start from the first element and loop from index 1 — the version graders expect.</div></div>

<details class="vl-orig" open=""><summary>📖 Full lesson — every definition &amp; detail (tap to collapse)</summary><div>

<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Round two covers the heavier half of the OA: arrays, ArrayLists, methods, classes, and exceptions. These tasks earn more points and separate passing from failing. Same protocol: write first, compare after.</p>
</div>
<h3>Drill 11 — Array statistics</h3>
<p><em>Read n, then n integers into an array; print max, min, and average (2 decimals).</em></p>
<pre><code>int n = sc.nextInt();
int[] arr = new int[n];
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();

int max = arr[0], min = arr[0], sum = 0;
for (int x : arr) {
    if (x &gt; max) max = x;
    if (x &lt; min) min = x;
    sum += x;
}
System.out.println(max);
System.out.println(min);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", (double) sum / n);
// Initialize max/min from arr[0], NOT from 0 — all-negative arrays break the 0 version.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 12 — Linear search with position</h3>
<pre><code>int target = sc.nextInt();
int foundAt = -1;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] == target) { foundAt = i; break; }
}
System.out.println(foundAt &gt;= 0 ? "Found at index " + foundAt : "Not found");</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 13 — Reverse an array in place</h3>
<pre><code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.length / 2; i++) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[arr.length - 1 - i];
    arr[arr.length - 1 - i] = temp;
}
// Loop only to the MIDDLE — going further swaps everything back.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 14 — ArrayList filtering</h3>
<p><em>Read words until "stop"; print only words longer than 4 characters.</em></p>
<pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;
ArrayList&lt;String&gt; words = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
String w = sc.next();
while (!w.equals("stop")) {     // .equals, never ==
    words.add(w);
    w = sc.next();
}
for (String word : words) {
    if (word.length() &gt; 4) System.out.println(word);
}</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 15 — Remove from ArrayList safely</h3>
<pre><code>// Remove all even numbers from an ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;
for (int i = list.size() - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) {   // iterate BACKWARD when removing
    if (list.get(i) % 2 == 0) list.remove(i);
}
// Forward iteration skips elements after each removal — the classic ArrayList trap.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 16 — Method with return value</h3>
<p><em>Write a method countOccurrences(int[] arr, int target) returning how many times target appears, and a main that uses it.</em></p>
<pre><code>public class Main {
  public static int countOccurrences(int[] arr, int target) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int x : arr) {
        if (x == target) count++;
    }
    return count;
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] data = {3, 7, 3, 1, 3};
    System.out.println(countOccurrences(data, 3));   // 3
  }
}
// Methods called from main without an object must be STATIC.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 17 — Method overloading</h3>
<pre><code>public static double area(double radius) {            // circle
    return Math.PI * radius * radius;
}
public static double area(double w, double h) {       // rectangle
    return w * h;
}
// Same name, different parameter lists = overloading. Return type alone is NOT enough.</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 18 — A complete class</h3>
<p><em>Create a Student class: name, score fields; constructor; getters; a passed() method (score ≥ 60); toString. Main creates two students and prints them.</em></p>
<pre><code>class Student {
    private String name;
    private int score;

    public Student(String name, int score) {
        this.name = name;          // this. resolves field vs parameter shadowing
        this.score = score;
    }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public int getScore()   { return score; }
    public boolean passed() { return score &gt;= 60; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + ": " + score + (passed() ? " (PASS)" : " (FAIL)");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student("Ana", 84);
        Student s2 = new Student("Raj", 51);
        System.out.println(s1);    // println calls toString automatically
        System.out.println(s2);
    }
}</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 19 — try/catch for robust input</h3>
<pre><code>import java.util.InputMismatchException;
try {
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    System.out.println(100 / n);
} catch (InputMismatchException e) {
    System.out.println("Not a number");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {          // division by zero
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Done");            // runs ALWAYS — exception or not
}
// Catch order: specific exceptions before general (Exception last if at all).</code></pre>
<h3>Drill 20 — Putting it together</h3>
<p><em>Read n scores into an ArrayList; use a method to compute the average; handle the n=0 case with an exception or guard; print "No scores" or the average.</em></p>
<pre><code>public static double average(ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; scores) {
    if (scores.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("No scores");
    int sum = 0;
    for (int s : scores) sum += s;
    return (double) sum / scores.size();
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; scores = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) scores.add(sc.nextInt());
    try {
        System.out.printf("%.2f%n", average(scores));
    } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
        System.out.println(e.getMessage());
    }
}</code></pre>
<div class="callout callout-warning"><div class="callout-label">⚠ THE FIVE HEAVYWEIGHT TRAPS</div><p>(1) max/min initialized to 0 instead of arr[0]; (2) forward iteration while removing from ArrayList; (3) non-static method called from main; (4) == on Strings/Integers (autoboxing makes small ints "work" then big ints fail!); (5) array.length vs list.size() vs string.length() — three different spellings.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><ul><li>Reverse in place: swap to the middle only; remove from lists: iterate backward</li><li>this. in constructors; static for main-called methods; toString for printing objects</li><li>Catch specific exceptions first; finally always runs</li><li>length (array) / length() (String) / size() (ArrayList) — memorize the trio</li></ul></div>
</div></details><div class="vl" data-meat="1" style="margin:18px 0; padding:16px 16px 10px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); border-radius:14px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:0.06em; color:var(--accent); margin-bottom:8px;">🔬 GOING DEEPER — drilling arrays, methods, classes and exceptions</div><h3>What the harder half of the exam looks like</h3><p>Later questions combine topics: an array traversal inside a method that throws, or a subclass overriding a method that uses a field the parent initialized. Handle these by identifying <em>each</em> mechanism in play rather than reading the code as one lump.</p><h3>Reference semantics — the recurring theme</h3><p>Most "why did this change / not change?" questions reduce to one rule: Java passes a <strong>copy of the reference</strong>. Therefore mutating the object (<code>arr[0] = 9</code>, <code>list.add(x)</code>, <code>sb.append()</code>) is visible to the caller, while reassigning the parameter (<code>arr = new int[5]</code>) is not. Apply that test and the answer follows every time.</p><h3>Object questions</h3><p>Check the constructor chain (does an implicit <code>super()</code> exist to call?), whether a method is overridden or merely overloaded, and whether the <em>reference</em> type permits the call while the <em>object</em> type supplies the behavior. For static members, ask whether the code is reaching instance state from a static context — the standard planted compile error.</p><h3>Exception questions</h3><p>Trace the flow explicitly: which line throws, which catch matches first, and remember that <code>finally</code> runs regardless — including when the try block returns. Then check the ordering rule: a broader catch above a narrower one is a compile error, not a runtime surprise.</p><h3>Before the exam</h3><p>Rehearse the failure modes rather than the successes. Most exam code is deliberately near a boundary — an empty array, a null reference, the last index, a zero divisor. If you can name what breaks, you can pick the answer.</p></div><i data-d286_s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the three spellings, and what a method can change</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk431a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"></path></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">arr.length</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">array — a field, no parentheses</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">str.length()</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">String — a method</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">list.size()</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">ArrayList — a different method again</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">arr[0] = 9 inside a method</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">caller SEES it — the object was mutated</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">arr = new int[5] inside</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">caller does NOT — only the copy was reassigned</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"></rect><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">last valid index</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">length − 1, so loop with &lt;</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Removing while iterating forward really does skip elements</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">System.out.println("start:    " + make());
System.out.println("forward:  " + forward(make()));
System.out.println("backward: " + backward(make()));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">start:    [1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8]
forward:  [1, 4, 7]
backward: [1, 7]</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Removing element <code>i</code> shifts everything after it one place left, and the loop's <code>i++</code> then steps over the element that moved into that slot.</li><li>Trace it: removing the 2 at index 1 slides the 4 down into index 1, but <code>i</code> has already become 2 — so the 4 is never examined and survives. The 6 and 8 happen to line up and are removed.</li><li>Counting downward means each removal only shifts positions the loop has already passed, so nothing is skipped and every even number goes.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — Two claims about arrays and == worth seeing fail</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">int[] negatives = { -5, -2, -9 };
System.out.println("seeded with 0:    " + maxFromZero(negatives));
System.out.println("seeded with a[0]: " + maxFromFirst(negatives));

Integer a = 127, b = 127;
Integer c = 128, d = 128;
System.out.println("127 == 127 : " + (a == b));
System.out.println("128 == 128 : " + (c == d));
System.out.println("equals     : " + c.equals(d));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">seeded with 0:    0
seeded with a[0]: -2
127 == 127 : true
128 == 128 : false
equals     : true</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>Seeding the maximum with 0 returns 0 for an all-negative array — a value that is not even in the data, from code that never errors.</li><li>The Integer comparison is worse: <code>==</code> gives <strong>true</strong> at 127 and <strong>false</strong> at 128, because Java caches small Integer objects and hands back the same one.</li><li>That is why a test with small numbers passes and the graded one with larger numbers fails. <code>equals</code> is right at every value.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>For any "why did this change / not change?" question, apply one rule: Java passes a <strong>copy of the reference</strong>. Mutating the object through it — <code>arr[0] = 9</code>, <code>list.add(x)</code>, <code>sb.append(…)</code> — is visible to the caller. Reassigning the parameter itself — <code>arr = new int[5]</code> — is not. That single test answers the whole family.</div><details data-tidy="v1" style="margin-top: 18px; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--surface-2);"><summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-2);">📚 Extra review — 1 alternate explanation &amp; recall cards (tap to open)</summary><div class="vl" data-visual="d286-drills2">
