{"D286/pa_sim/sim":[{"id":"fresh_D286_1","topic":"Java Basics > JVM/JRE/JDK","text":"Which component of the Java platform contains the javac compiler needed to turn .java source files into .class bytecode?","options":["JVM","JIT compiler","JDK","JRE"],"correct":2,"explain":"The JDK (Java Development Kit) includes development tools such as the javac compiler; the JRE and JVM only run bytecode."},{"id":"fresh_D286_2","topic":"Java Basics > Primitive Types and Type Casting","text":"What is printed?

byte b = (byte) 200;
System.out.println(b);","options":["-56","72","200","-72"],"correct":0,"explain":"200 exceeds the signed byte range, so it wraps to 200 - 256 = -56."},{"id":"fresh_D286_3","topic":"Java Basics > Operators","text":"What is the value of the expression 2 + 3 * 4 - 8 / 2 in Java?","options":["9","6","10","12"],"correct":2,"explain":"Multiplication and division bind first: 2 + 12 - 4 = 10."},{"id":"fresh_D286_4","topic":"Java Basics > Operators","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println(7 / 2 + 7 % 2);","options":["4","3","4.5","5"],"correct":0,"explain":"Integer division 7/2 is 3 and 7%2 is 1, so the sum is 4."},{"id":"fresh_D286_5","topic":"Java Basics > Type Casting and char arithmetic","text":"What is printed?

char c = 'A';
c += 4;
System.out.println(c);","options":["D","compile error","E","69"],"correct":2,"explain":"'A' is code 65; adding 4 gives 69, and the compound assignment keeps char type, printing 'E'."},{"id":"fresh_D286_6","topic":"Java Basics > Strings and operators","text":"What is printed?

System.out.println(1 + 2 + "3" + 4 + 5);","options":["3345","1245","33345","15"],"correct":0,"explain":"Left operands 1+2 are added numerically to 3, then everything after the String becomes concatenation: "3345"."},{"id":"fresh_D286_7","topic":"Java Basics > Strings (== vs equals)","text":"What is printed?

String a = "cat";
String b = new String("cat");
System.out.println(a == b);","options":["true","cat","false","compile error"],"correct":2,"explain":"new String creates a distinct object, so the == reference comparison is false even though contents match."},{"id":"fresh_D286_8","topic":"Java Basics > Integer caching","text":"What is printed?

Integer a = 127, b = 127, c = 128, d = 128;
System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (c == d));","options":["true false","true true","false false","false true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Autoboxed Integers from -128 to 127 are cached (a==b true), but 128 creates new objects, so c==d is false."},{"id":"fresh_D286_9","topic":"Control Flow > switch Statements","text":"What is printed?

int n = 2;
switch(n) {
  case 1: System.out.print("A");
  case 2: System.out.print("B");
  case 3: System.out.print("C"); break;
  case 4: System.out.print("D");
}","options":["ABC","BCD","BC","B"],"correct":2,"explain":"Execution starts at case 2 and falls through case 3 (no break there yet) until the break, printing "BC"."},{"id":"fresh_D286_10","topic":"Control Flow > Ternary operator","text":"What is printed?

int x = 5;
String r = x &gt; 3 ? (x &gt; 10 ? "big" : "mid") : "small";
System.out.println(r);","options":["small","mid","big","compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"x>3 is true, then the nested x>10 is false, so "mid" is selected."},{"id":"fresh_D286_11","topic":"Control Flow > while Loops","text":"What is printed?

int i = 0, sum = 0;
while(i &lt; 5) { sum += i; i++; }
System.out.println(sum);","options":["15","5","10","20"],"correct":2,"explain":"It adds 0+1+2+3+4, which is 10 (i=5 stops the loop before being added)."},{"id":"fresh_D286_12","topic":"Control Flow > do-while Loops","text":"What does this code do?

int i = 10;
do { System.out.print(i + " "); i++; } while(i &lt; 5);","options":["It loops infinitely","It prints 10 11 12 13 14","It prints 10 once","It prints nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"A do-while always runs its body once; the condition 11<5 is then false, so only "10 " is printed."},{"id":"fresh_D286_13","topic":"Control Flow > for Loops and continue","text":"What is printed?

int sum = 0;
for(int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
  if(i % 2 == 0) continue;
  sum += i;
}
System.out.println(sum);","options":["15","8","9","6"],"correct":2,"explain":"continue skips even values, so only 1+3+5 = 9 is summed."},{"id":"fresh_D286_14","topic":"Control Flow > for Loops and break","text":"What is printed?

int i;
for(i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
  if(i * i &gt; 20) break;
}
System.out.println(i);","options":["10","4","5","6"],"correct":2,"explain":"At i=5, 25 > 20 triggers break while i is still 5, so 5 is printed."},{"id":"fresh_D286_15","topic":"OOP > Classes, Objects, and default field values","text":"What is printed?

class Box { int w; }
// ...
Box b = new Box();
System.out.println(b.w);","options":["null","compile error","0","a garbage value"],"correct":2,"explain":"An uninitialized int instance field defaults to 0."},{"id":"fresh_D286_16","topic":"OOP > Constructors and this()","text":"What is printed?

class Point {
  int x, y;
  Point() { this(1, 1); }
  Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
}
// ...
Point p = new Point();
System.out.println(p.x + "," + p.y);","options":["compile error","1,0","1,1","0,0"],"correct":2,"explain":"The no-arg constructor delegates via this(1,1) to the two-arg constructor, setting both fields to 1."},{"id":"fresh_D286_17","topic":"OOP > Encapsulation and Access Modifiers","text":"Which access modifier makes a class member accessible only from within its own class?","options":["public","default (package-private)","private","protected"],"correct":2,"explain":"private restricts access to the declaring class itself."},{"id":"fresh_D286_18","topic":"OOP > Static Members","text":"What is printed?

class Widget { static int count = 0; Widget() { count++; } }
// ...
new Widget(); new Widget(); new Widget();
System.out.println(Widget.count);","options":["compile error","1","3","0"],"correct":2,"explain":"The static field is shared across all instances, so three constructions increment it to 3."},{"id":"fresh_D286_19","topic":"OOP > Method Overloading","text":"What is printed?

static void f(int x) { System.out.print("int"); }
static void f(double x) { System.out.print("double"); }
// ...
f(5);","options":["double","compile error (ambiguous)","int","prints nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"The literal 5 is an int, so the exact-match f(int) overload is chosen over the widening double version."},{"id":"fresh_D286_20","topic":"OOP > Static vs Instance Members","text":"Which statement about a static method is correct?","options":["It belongs to the JVM and cannot be called by user code","It belongs to the class itself and can be called without creating an object","It belongs to a specific object and needs an instance to be called","It can only exist inside inner classes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Static methods are associated with the class and are invoked via the class name without an instance."},{"id":"fresh_D286_21","topic":"Arrays > 1D Arrays","text":"What is printed?

int[] a = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
int max = a[0];
for(int x : a) if(x &gt; max) max = x;
System.out.println(max);","options":["5","4","3","14"],"correct":0,"explain":"The loop tracks the largest element, which is 5."},{"id":"fresh_D286_22","topic":"Arrays > Exception Handling with arrays","text":"What is printed?

int[] a = new int[3];
try { a[3] = 10; System.out.print("set "); }
catch(ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) { System.out.print("caught"); }","options":["caught","set ","set caught","compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Index 3 is out of bounds for a length-3 array, so the assignment throws before printing and the catch runs."},{"id":"fresh_D286_23","topic":"Arrays > 2D Arrays","text":"What is printed?

int[][] m = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};
System.out.println(m[1][2]);","options":["6","3","5","compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Row index 1 is {4,5,6} and column index 2 within it is 6."},{"id":"fresh_D286_24","topic":"Arrays > ArrayList","text":"What is printed?

ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
list.add(10); list.add(20); list.add(30);
list.remove(1);
System.out.println(list);","options":["[10, 30]","[20, 30]","[10, 20, 30]","[10, 20]"],"correct":0,"explain":"remove(1) takes an int index, removing the element at index 1 (the value 20), leaving [10, 30]."},{"id":"fresh_D286_25","topic":"Exception Handling > try/catch/finally","text":"What is printed?

try { System.out.print("A"); throw new RuntimeException(); }
catch(Exception e) { System.out.print("B"); }
finally { System.out.print("C"); }","options":["ABC","A","AC","AB"],"correct":0,"explain":"A prints, the exception is caught printing B, and finally always runs printing C: "ABC"."},{"id":"fresh_D286_26","topic":"Exception Handling > ArithmeticException","text":"What is printed?

try { int x = 5 / 0; System.out.print("A"); }
catch(ArithmeticException e) { System.out.print("B"); }","options":["B","A","AB","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Integer division by zero throws ArithmeticException before A prints, so only the catch runs."},{"id":"fresh_D286_27","topic":"Inheritance > super and constructor order","text":"What is printed?

class A { A() { System.out.print("A"); } }
class B extends A { B() { System.out.print("B"); } }
// ...
new B();","options":["AB","B","BA","nothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"A subclass constructor implicitly calls the superclass constructor first, so A prints before B."},{"id":"fresh_D286_28","topic":"Polymorphism > Method Overriding","text":"What is printed?

class Animal { String sound() { return "..."; } }
class Dog extends Animal { String sound() { return "Woof"; } }
// ...
Animal a = new Dog();
System.out.println(a.sound());","options":["Woof","compile error","Animal","..."],"correct":0,"explain":"Overridden instance methods dispatch on the runtime type (Dog), so "Woof" is printed."},{"id":"fresh_D286_29","topic":"Polymorphism > Field hiding vs overriding","text":"What is printed?

class A { int x = 1; int get() { return x; } }
class B extends A { int x = 2; int get() { return x; } }
// ...
A obj = new B();
System.out.println(obj.x + " " + obj.get());","options":["1 2","2 2","1 1","2 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fields are resolved by the reference's compile-time type (A gives 1), but the overridden method uses the runtime type B and returns 2."},{"id":"fresh_D286_30","topic":"Abstract Classes and Interfaces > Abstract classes","text":"What happens if you attempt to instantiate an abstract class directly with new?","options":["It works normally","It causes a compile-time error","It throws a runtime exception","It runs but returns null"],"correct":1,"explain":"Abstract classes cannot be instantiated; attempting new on one is a compile-time error."},{"id":"fresh_D286_31","topic":"Inheritance > super method call","text":"What is printed?

class A { String who() { return "A"; } }
class B extends A { String who() { return "B+" + super.who(); } }
// ...
System.out.println(new B().who());","options":["B+","B+B","B+A","A"],"correct":2,"explain":"super.who() calls the parent version returning "A", concatenated after "B+" to give "B+A"."},{"id":"fresh_D286_32","topic":"Abstract Classes and Interfaces > Interface fields","text":"Fields declared in an interface without explicit modifiers are implicitly which of the following?","options":["public and non-final","private and non-static","public, static, and final","protected and final"],"correct":2,"explain":"All interface fields are implicitly public, static, and final constants."},{"id":"fresh_D286_33","topic":"Generics > Type-Safe Collections","text":"What is the main benefit of using a generic collection such as a List parameterized to hold String?","options":["Compile-time type safety that prevents adding the wrong type","Faster element access than a raw untyped collection","Automatic resizing that a raw collection cannot do","Runtime type checks that throw on the wrong type"],"correct":0,"explain":"Generics enforce element types at compile time, avoiding unsafe casts and runtime ClassCastExceptions."},{"id":"fresh_D286_34","topic":"Object Contract > toString()","text":"When you pass an object reference to System.out.println, which method is invoked to produce its text representation?","options":["equals()","hashCode()","toString()","compareTo()"],"correct":2,"explain":"println calls the object's toString() to obtain a printable String."},{"id":"fresh_D286_35","topic":"Object Contract > equals() and hashCode()","text":"According to the Object contract, if a.equals(b) returns true, which must also be true?","options":["a and b must be the same reference","a.hashCode() must equal b.hashCode()","their hash codes must differ","b.equals(a) may return false"],"correct":1,"explain":"Equal objects are required to have equal hash codes to preserve correct behavior in hash-based collections."},{"id":"fresh_D286_36","topic":"Comparable & Comparator > compareTo","text":"The call "apple".compareTo("apricot") returns a value that is which of the following?","options":["a positive number","undefined","a negative number","zero"],"correct":2,"explain":"The strings match until the third letter where 'p' (112) is less than 'r' (114), yielding a negative result."},{"id":"fresh_D286_37","topic":"Comparable & Comparator > Comparator","text":"To sort a list of your own objects by a rule other than their natural ordering, you supply which of the following?","options":["an overridden finalize() method","a static initializer block","a Comparator implementation","a second constructor"],"correct":2,"explain":"A Comparator defines an external ordering that can be passed to sorting methods."},{"id":"fresh_D286_38","topic":"try-with-resources & File I/O","text":"For an object to be used in a try-with-resources statement so it closes automatically, its class must implement which interface?","options":["Serializable","Runnable","AutoCloseable","Cloneable"],"correct":2,"explain":"try-with-resources manages any resource whose type implements AutoCloseable (or its subtype Closeable)."},{"id":"fresh_D286_39","topic":"Nested, Inner & Anonymous Classes","text":"An anonymous class is most commonly used to do which of the following?","options":["Replace the main method","Declare a top-level public class","Provide a one-off inline implementation of an interface or abstract class","Define constants shared across packages"],"correct":2,"explain":"Anonymous classes create a single unnamed instance implementing an interface or extending a class inline."},{"id":"fresh_D286_40","topic":"OA Prep > Scanner input","text":"Given Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in), which call reads the next whitespace-delimited integer token from input?","options":["sc.readInt()","sc.nextInt()","sc.nextLine()","sc.next()"],"correct":1,"explain":"Scanner.nextInt() parses and returns the next token as an int; readInt() does not exist."},{"id":"lg_D286_0_0","topic":"Variables","text":"Which best describes a variable in Java?","options":["A fixed value that never changes","A method that returns data","A type of loop","A named storage location that holds a value"],"correct":3,"explain":"A variable is a named location in memory whose value can change.","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_1","topic":"Primitive types","text":"Which list contains only Java primitive types?","options":["Integer, Double, Boolean","Array, List, Map","String, int, double","int, double, boolean, char"],"correct":3,"explain":"The eight primitives include int, double, boolean, and char; String and the wrapper classes are objects.","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_2","topic":"int size","text":"How many bits does a Java int occupy?","options":["64 bits","16 bits","8 bits","32 bits"],"correct":3,"explain":"A Java int is a 32-bit signed integer.","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_3","topic":"boolean","text":"What values can a Java boolean hold?","options":["the strings "yes"/"no"","any integer","0 or 1","true or false"],"correct":3,"explain":"A boolean holds only true or false (not 0/1 as in some languages).","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_4","topic":"String type","text":"In Java, a String is:","options":["a primitive type","an int array","a char primitive","an object (reference type)"],"correct":3,"explain":"String is a reference type (an object), not a primitive.","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_5","topic":"Console output","text":"Which statement prints a line to the console in Java?","options":["echo(...)","print(...)","console.log(...)","System.out.println(...)"],"correct":3,"explain":"System.out.println outputs a line; console.log and echo are from other languages.","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_6","topic":"Reading input","text":"Which class reads keyboard input in Java?","options":["Reader","Printer","BufferedImage","Scanner"],"correct":3,"explain":"Scanner (often new Scanner(System.in)) reads user input.","__rebal":1},{"id":"lg_D286_0_7","topic":"Modulo","text":"What does the % operator return?","optio