System.out.println(n);","options":["16","16.0","8","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Math.pow always returns a double (16.0 here), so storing it in an int needs the cast, which yields 16. Without the cast the line would not compile."},{"id":"fs_z2_10_2","topic":"round returns long","text":"When given a double argument, Math.round returns which type?","options":["long","int","double","float"],"correct":0,"explain":"Math.round(double) returns a long. Reading return types in the signature matters more than what the method computes - it is why int x = Math.round(2.6); does not compile without a cast. Assigning the result to an int requires a cast."},{"id":"fs_z2_10_3","topic":"Rounding vs truncating","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println(Math.round(5.6) + " " + (int) 5.6);","options":["6 5","6 6","5 5","5 6"],"correct":0,"explain":"Math.round rounds to the nearest whole number (6), while a cast truncates the fraction toward zero (5). The two operations disagree whenever the fraction is .5 or more."},{"id":"fs_z2_10_4","topic":"Static methods","text":"Why is Math.sqrt(x) called on the class Math rather than on an object?","options":["sqrt is a static method","Math objects are too expensive to create","All Java methods are called on class names","sqrt is a constructor"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every Math method is static: you call it on the class, never on an instance. Math also lives in java.lang, so no import is needed. It belongs to the class itself and needs no object."},{"id":"zf235","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.pow(2, 3)); prints:","options":["8","6.0","8.0","9.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Math.pow returns a DOUBLE, so the whole result prints as 8.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf236","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.sqrt(25)); prints:","options":["5","25.0","12.5","5.0"],"correct":3,"explain":"Square root of 25 is 5, returned as the double 5.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf237","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.abs(-6)); prints:","options":["6","-6","6.0","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Absolute value strips the sign; with an int argument the result is an int.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf238","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.max(4, 9)); prints:","options":["4","9","13","9.0"],"correct":1,"explain":"max returns the larger of its two arguments.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf239","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.min(12, 5)); prints:","options":["12","7","5","5.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"min returns the smaller argument.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf240","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.round(2.6)); prints:","options":["2","2.6","3","3.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"round goes to the NEAREST whole number and returns a long: 3.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf241","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.floor(3.8)); prints:","options":["3","4.0","3.8","3.0"],"correct":3,"explain":"floor always goes DOWN, and returns a double: 3.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf242","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.ceil(3.2)); prints:","options":["4.0","3.0","3","4"],"correct":0,"explain":"ceil always goes UP, returning the double 4.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf243","topic":"","text":"Math methods are called as Math.sqrt(x) rather than on an object because:","options":["Math is a variable","they are static methods of Math","sqrt is a keyword","the JVM injects them"],"correct":1,"explain":"Static methods belong to the class itself - no object needed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf244","topic":"","text":"Using the Math class requires:","options":["import java.util.Math;","import java.math.*; always","no import - it lives in java.lang","installing a library"],"correct":2,"explain":"java.lang classes (Math, String, System) are available automatically.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf245","topic":"","text":"Math.pow(5, 2) returns 25.0 rather than 25 because:","options":["5 squared is not exact","the JVM rounds up","pow returns a String","pow always returns a double"],"correct":3,"explain":"Even whole-number powers come back as doubles - cast if you need an int.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_11":[{"id":"zya2_11_0","topic":"Integer division result","text":"What is 7 / 2 in Java when both are ints?","options":["4","3.0","3.5","3"],"correct":3,"explain":"Integer division discards the fractional part — it truncates toward zero rather than rounding."},{"id":"zya2_11_1","topic":"Modulo basics","text":"What is 17 % 5?","options":["3.4","12","3","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"17 = 3*5 + 2, so the remainder is 2."},{"id":"zya2_11_2","topic":"Even test","text":"Which expression tests whether int n is even?","options":["n / 2 == 0","n % 0 == 2","n % 2 == 1","n % 2 == 0"],"correct":3,"explain":"A remainder of 0 when divided by 2 means even. Note that n % 2 == 1 fails for negative odd numbers, where the result is -1."},{"id":"zya2_11_3","topic":"Last digit","text":"n % 10 gives:","options":["n divided by 10","The first digit","The number of digits","The last digit of n"],"correct":3,"explain":"Dividing by 10 shifts digits right; the remainder is what falls off — the last digit. Pairing n % 10 with n / 10 in a loop walks every digit."},{"id":"zya2_11_4","topic":"Modulo by zero","text":"What happens at runtime for 5 % 0 with ints?","options":["Returns NaN","Returns 0","Returns 5","Throws ArithmeticException"],"correct":3,"explain":"Integer division and modulo by zero both throw ArithmeticException. Doubles behave differently, giving NaN or Infinity."},{"id":"zy2_11_7_h4_286_9","topic":"negative modulo","_dom":"basics","difficulty":"hard","text":"System.out.println(-7 % 3); prints:","options":["-2","2","-1","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"In Java the result of % takes the sign of the dividend, so -7 % 3 = -1.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_11_8_lg_D286_0_7","topic":"Modulo","text":"What does the % operator return?","options":["A percentage","The quotient","The remainder of a division","The product"],"correct":2,"explain":"% yields the remainder, e.g., 7 % 2 = 1.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_11_9_bp_D286_t11_10","topic":"Negative modulo","text":"For n = -3, n % 2 equals:","options":["0","1","3","-1"],"correct":3,"explain":"In Java the remainder’s sign follows the dividend → -1.","__rebal":1},{"id":"n_n2_11_a","topic":"Output","text":"System.out.println(-7 / 2); prints:","options":["-4","-3.5","3","-3"],"correct":3,"explain":"Integer division truncates TOWARD ZERO in Java, so -3.5 becomes -3, not -4. Languages that floor instead would give -4, which is the usual confusion."},{"id":"n_n2_11_b","topic":"Output","text":"System.out.println(-7 % 2); prints:","options":["0","-1","1","-3"],"correct":1,"explain":"In Java the remainder takes the sign of the LEFT operand, so -7 % 2 is -1. Several other languages return 1 here."},{"id":"n_n2_11_c","topic":"Modulo","text":"n % 2 == 0 tests whether n is:","options":["Positive","Divisible by 10","Even","Odd"],"correct":2,"explain":"A remainder of zero on division by two means no leftover, which is the definition of even. n % 2 != 0 tests odd."},{"id":"fs_z2_11_1","topic":"Digit extraction","text":"What does this print?

