2"],"correct":2,"explain":"n % 10 gives the last digit (2), and n / 10 removes it (47, by integer division). Together they are the standard pattern for walking the digits of a number."},{"id":"fs_z2_11_2","topic":"Division-remainder identity","text":"For any int values a and b (with b not 0), the expression (a / b) * b + (a % b) always equals:","options":["a","b","a * b","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Integer division answers how many whole groups fit, and % answers what is left over. They are one calculation split in two, so groups times size plus remainder rebuilds a exactly."},{"id":"fs_z2_11_3","topic":"Wrap-around","text":"Which expression makes an increasing counter i cycle through the values 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, ...?","options":["3 % i","i - 3","i % 3","i / 3"],"correct":2,"explain":"i % 3 always yields 0 to 2 and repeats as i grows - clock arithmetic. This wrap-around pattern is how alternating rows, circular indices, and cycles are implemented."},{"id":"zf246","topic":"","text":"What does System.out.println(273 % 10); output?","options":["3","27","73","30"],"correct":0,"explain":"% 10 extracts the LAST digit: 273 ends in 3.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf247","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(273 / 10); prints:","options":["3","27","2.73","274"],"correct":1,"explain":"/ 10 drops the last digit: 27.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf248","topic":"","text":"int totalSec = 130; System.out.println(totalSec / 60 + " min " + totalSec % 60 + " sec"); prints:","options":["2 min 16 sec","1 min 70 sec","2 min 10 sec","2.16 min"],"correct":2,"explain":"130 / 60 = 2 whole minutes; 130 % 60 = 10 seconds left over.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf249","topic":"","text":"int cents = 68; System.out.println(cents / 25 + " quarters, " + cents % 25 + " cents left"); prints:","options":["2 quarters, 43 cents left","3 quarters, 7 cents left","2.72 quarters","2 quarters, 18 cents left"],"correct":3,"explain":"68 / 25 = 2 quarters (50 cents); the remainder 68 % 25 = 18.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf250","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(-9 / 2); prints:","options":["-5","-4.5","4","-4"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java truncates TOWARD ZERO: -9 / 2 = -4 (not -5).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf251","topic":"","text":"What is the output of System.out.println(-9 % 2);?","options":["-1","1","-4","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"The remainder takes the sign of the DIVIDEND: -9 % 2 = -1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf252","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(3 % 7); prints:","options":["0","3","7","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"When the divisor is larger, the quotient is 0 and the remainder is the whole value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf253","topic":"","text":"For any non-negative x, the expression x % 5 can produce which values?","options":["1 through 5","0 through 5","0 through 4","only 0 or 5"],"correct":2,"explain":"A remainder is always smaller than the divisor.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf254","topic":"","text":"The standard test for an odd non-negative number n is:","options":["n / 2 == 1","n % 2 == 2","n * 2 == 1","n % 2 == 1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Odd numbers leave remainder 1 when divided by 2.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf255","topic":"","text":"int eggs = 50; System.out.println(eggs / 12 + " cartons, " + eggs % 12 + " loose"); prints:","options":["4 cartons, 2 loose","4 cartons, 4 loose","5 cartons, 0 loose","4.17 cartons"],"correct":0,"explain":"50 / 12 = 4 full dozens; 50 % 12 = 2 eggs left over.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf256","topic":"","text":"Which expression extracts the TENS digit of a 3-digit int n (e.g., 7 from 473)?","options":["n % 10","n / 10 % 10","n / 100","n % 100 / 100"],"correct":1,"explain":"n / 10 drops the ones digit (47), then % 10 keeps its last digit (7).","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_12":[{"id":"zya2_12_0","topic":"Widening conversion","text":"int n = 5; double d = n; This conversion is:","options":["A runtime error","Illegal without a cast","Automatic widening","A narrowing conversion"],"correct":2,"explain":"Widening (int to double, int to long) is automatic because no information is lost. No cast needed."},{"id":"zya2_12_1","topic":"Narrowing requires a cast","text":"double d = 5.9; int n = d; produces:","options":["n == 5","n == 6","A compile error","A runtime exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java refuses implicit narrowing. You must write int n = (int) d; to state that the loss is intentional. Possible lossy conversion."},{"id":"zya2_12_2","topic":"Cast truncates","text":"int n = (int) 5.9; What is n?","options":["0","6","5","5.9"],"correct":2,"explain":"Casting a double to an int truncates toward zero — it does not round. Math.round(5.9) gives 6."},{"id":"zya2_12_3","topic":"Casting negatives","text":"int n = (int) -2.7; What is n?","options":["2","-3","-2","-2.7"],"correct":2,"explain":"Truncation drops the fraction, moving toward zero — so -2.7 becomes -2, not -3."},{"id":"zya2_12_4","topic":"Mixed-type arithmetic","text":"In 5 / 2.0, the result is:","options":["A compile error","2 (int)","2.5 (double)","2.0 (double)"],"correct":2,"explain":"When operand types differ, the int is promoted to double, so floating-point division runs and gives 2.5."},{"id":"zy2_12_5_q2","topic":"Widening","text":"int i = 500; double d = i; Is this valid?","options":["Yes — widening conversion is automatic","No — incompatible types","No — requires explicit cast","Yes — but wrong type"],"correct":0,"explain":"int to double is widening — Java does it automatically with no data loss.","distractors":{"0":"Widening is implicit.","1":"d will correctly be 500.0.","2":"Compatible via widening."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_12_8_b18","topic":"Narrowing","text":"double d = 9.99; int i = (int) d; gives i =","options":["9.99","10","0","9"],"correct":3,"explain":"Casting double to int truncates toward zero → 9 (not rounded).","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_12_9_z286_ba3","topic":"Type promotion","_dom":"basics","text":"In Java, the expression 5 + 2.0 evaluates to a value of 