r.","__rebal":1},{"id":"h286_8","topic":"char to int","_dom":"basics","difficulty":"hard","text":"char c = 'A'; System.out.println((int) c); Given 'A' is 65, this prints:","options":["0","65","'A'","A"],"correct":1,"explain":"Casting a char to int yields its code point: 65.","__rebal":1},{"id":"h2_286_7","topic":"final","_dom":"basics","difficulty":"hard","text":"final int MAX = 10; MAX = 20; This code:","options":["prints 20","reassigns MAX","causes a compile error","prints 10"],"correct":2,"explain":"A final variable cannot be reassigned; MAX = 20 is a compile-time error.","__rebal":1}],"D286/u2/z2_13":[{"id":"zya2_13_0","topic":"Binary base","text":"The binary number system uses base:","options":["16","2","8","10"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two digits, 0 and 1. Each position is a power of 2."},{"id":"zya2_13_1","topic":"Binary to decimal","text":"What is binary 1011 in decimal?","options":["9","11","13","23"],"correct":1,"explain":"8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11. Positions from the right are 1, 2, 4, 8."},{"id":"zya2_13_2","topic":"Decimal to binary","text":"What is decimal 10 in binary?","options":["0110","1010","1100","1001"],"correct":1,"explain":"8 + 2 = 10, so the 8 and 2 positions are set: 1010."},{"id":"zya2_13_3","topic":"Bits and values","text":"How many distinct values can 4 bits represent?","options":["8","16","32","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"2^4 = 16, covering 0 through 15."},{"id":"zya2_13_4","topic":"Byte range unsigned","text":"An 8-bit pattern can represent how many values?","options":["128","256","512","8"],"correct":1,"explain":"2^8 = 256. As a signed Java byte that range is -128 to 127."},{"id":"zya2_13_5","topic":"Why binary","text":"Computers use binary because:","options":["Binary numbers take less space than decimal ones","Humans find base two easier to reason about","Electronic circuits reliably distinguish two states","Binary is the only base a CPU can add in"],"correct":2,"explain":"On or off, high or low voltage — two states are far easier to make reliable than ten."},{"id":"zya2_13_6","topic":"Hexadecimal purpose","text":"Hexadecimal is commonly used because:","options":["Hexadecimal values are stored more efficiently in RAM","Each hex digit maps to exactly 4 bits, compacting binary","The CPU performs arithmetic directly in base sixteen","Hexadecimal avoids the rounding errors of binary"],"correct":1,"explain":"FF is 11111111 — much easier to read and write than the binary form."},{"id":"zya2_13_7","topic":"Hex literal in Java","text":"How is the hex value 1F written as a Java literal?","options":["1F","0x1F","#1F","h1F"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 0x prefix marks a hexadecimal literal. 0b marks binary and a leading 0 marks octal."},{"id":"zya2_13_8","topic":"Leftmost bit value","text":"In an 8-bit unsigned number, the leftmost bit represents:","options":["64","128","255","1"],"correct":1,"explain":"Positions double from the right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128."},{"id":"zya2_13_9","topic":"Binary of 255","text":"Decimal 255 in 8-bit binary is:","options":["11111110","11111111","10000000","01111111"],"correct":1,"explain":"All eight bits set: 128+64+32+16+8+4+2+1 = 255, the maximum for 8 unsigned bits."},{"id":"n_n2_13_a","topic":"Binary","text":"The decimal number 13 in binary is:","options":["1110","1001","1101","1011"],"correct":2,"explain":"8 + 4 + 1 = 13, so the bits at values 8, 4 and 1 are set."},{"id":"n_n2_13_b","topic":"Binary","text":"How many distinct values can 8 bits hold?","options":["128","64","256","255"],"correct":2,"explain":"2^8 = 256 patterns, which for an unsigned byte means 0 through 255. Confusing the count with the maximum is the usual slip."},{"id":"n_n2_13_c","topic":"Output","text":"System.out.println(Integer.toBinaryString(10)); prints:","options":["10","1110","1010","01010"],"correct":2,"explain":"8 + 2 = 10. toBinaryString omits leading zeros."},{"id":"zf257","topic":"","text":"The binary number 1011 equals which decimal value?","options":["12","10","11","1011"],"correct":2,"explain