,"2147484000","a runtime exception","2147483647"],"correct":0,"explain":"2,147,484,000 exceeds MAX_VALUE, so the sum wraps negative.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf287","topic":"","text":"Multiplying two large ints safely into a long requires:","options":["(long)(a * b) after the fact","casting an operand long BEFORE multiplying","nothing - Java promotes automatically","using double instead"],"correct":1,"explain":"(long)(a*b) casts a value that ALREADY overflowed; cast an operand first.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_17":[{"id":"zya2_17_0","topic":"int size","text":"How many bits does a Java int use?","options":["16","32","64","8"],"correct":1,"explain":"int is always 32 bits in Java, on every platform — unlike C, where it varies."},{"id":"zya2_17_1","topic":"Choosing a whole-number type","text":"A variable must hold values up to 10 billion. The right type is:","options":["int","long","short","byte"],"correct":1,"explain":"int tops out near 2.1 billion, so long (64-bit) is required."},{"id":"zya2_17_2","topic":"long literal suffix","text":"long big = 10000000000; produces:","options":["A valid long","A compile error","A runtime error","A double"],"correct":1,"explain":"Integer literals default to int. Append L: long big = 10000000000L; The literal is treated as an int."},{"id":"zy2_17_5_b09","topic":"Primitive count","text":"How many primitive types does Java have?","options":["10","7","8","6"],"correct":2,"explain":"The 8 primitives: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_17_6_b14","topic":"long literal","text":"The literal for ten billion must be written:","options":["10000000000L","(long)10000000000","10000000000","10000000000f"],"correct":0,"explain":"A literal too big for int needs the L suffix.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_17_7_w286_ba3","topic":"Primitive types","_dom":"basics","text":"Which of the following are primitive types in Java? (Select all that apply.)","options":["int","String","boolean","double","ArrayList"],"multi":true,"correctSet":[0,2,3],"explain":"int, boolean, and double are primitives; String and ArrayList are reference types."},{"id":"zy2_17_8_fresh_D286_2","topic":"Java Basics > Primitive Types and Type Casting","text":"What is printed?

byte b = (byte) 200;
System.out.println(b);","options":["-72","-56","72","200"],"correct":1,"explain":"200 exceeds the signed byte range, so it wraps to 200 - 256 = -56."},{"id":"n_n2_17_a","topic":"Types","text":"Which type holds a whole number with the widest range?","options":["byte","long","int","short"],"correct":1,"explain":"byte is 8 bits, short 16, int 32 and long 64. Each doubles the bits and vastly widens the range."},{"id":"n_n2_17_b","topic":"Types","text":"A double literal such as 3.5 has which type by default?","options":["float","int","BigDecimal","double"],"correct":3,"explain":"Floating-point literals default to double. Writing float f = 3.5; fails; it needs 3.5f."},{"id":"n_n2_17_c","topic":"Types","text":"Widening conversion (int to double) happens:","options":["Automatically","Only with a cast","Never","Only for constants"],"correct":0,"explain":"Java widens silently and narrows only with an explicit cast, because narrowing can discard data. No information is lost."},{"id":"fs_z2_17_1","topic":"Storing money","text":"A program must track an account balance exactly. What is the best representation?","options":["Whole cents stored in an int or long","A double holding the amount in dollars","A float holding the amount in dollars","A String formatted to two decimal places"],"correct":0,"explain":"Neither float nor double should hold money, because binary floating point cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly. Whole cents in an integer type keep every value exact."},{"id":"fs_z2_17_2","topic":"Primitives vs references","text":"Why does String comparison use .equals() while int comparison uses ==?","options":["== does not compile when used with Strings","int values are objects that happen to lack an equals method","String is a reference type","String is a primitive with special comparison rules"],"correct":2,"explain":"String is an object, not one of the eight primitives, so a String variable holds a reference. Comparing content therefore needs .equals(); primitives hold their value directly, so == compares the values. == would compare references; int is a primitive that holds its value directly."},{"id":"fs_z2_17_3","topic":"Type sizes","text":"How many bits does a Java long occupy?","options":["64","32","16","128"],"correct":0,"explain":"A long is 64 bits, giving a range up to about 9.2 quintillion - the type to reach for when values can exceed int's 2.1 billion ceiling. Java fixes these sizes on every platform."},{"id":"zf288","topic":"","text":"An int occupies how many bytes?","options":["2","8","4","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"32 bits = 4 bytes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf289","topic":"","text":"A double occupies how many bytes?","options":["4","2","16","8"],"correct":3,"explain":"64 bits of IEEE floating point.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf290","topic":"","text":"The range of the byte type is:","options":["0 to 255","-256 to 255","-127 to 128","-128 to 127"],"correct":3,"explain":"8 signed bits: -2^7 through 2^7 - 1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf291","topic":"","text":"The type of the literal 3.5 (no suffix) is:","options":["double","float","int","decimal"],"correct":0,"explain":"Floating literals default to double; a float needs the f suffix.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf292","topic":"","text":"To write a float literal you must add:","options":["an F is illegal","an f suffix, like 3.5f","a d suffix","quotes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without f, assigning 3.5 to a float is a compile error (possible lossy conversion).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf293","topic":"","text":"Which lists integer types from smallest to largest?","options":["short, byte, int, long","byte, int, short, long","byte, short, int, long","int, byte, short, long"],"correct":2,"explain":"1, 2, 4, and 8 bytes respectively.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf294","topic":"","text":"The best type for a count of students is:","options":["double","float","char","int"],"correct":3,"explain":"Whole-number counts fit comfortably in int - the default integer choice.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf295","topic":"","text":"The best type for a price with cents, like 19.99, in beginner code is:","options":["double","int","char","boolean"],"correct":0,"explain":"It needs a fractional part; double is the default floating-point type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf296","topic":"","text":"The range of short is:","options":["-128 to 127","-32,768 to 32,767","0 to 65,535","-32,767 to 32,768"],"correct":1,"explain":"16 signed bits: -2^15 through 2^15 - 1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf297","topic":"","text":"A double is precise to roughly how many significant decimal digits?","options":["exactly 2","about 7","about 15","unlimited"],"correct":2,"explain":"64-bit doubles carry ~15-16 significant digits; float only ~7.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf298","topic":"","text":"float differs from double mainly in:","options":["float stores text","float cannot be negative","float is an integer type","size and precision"],"correct":3,"explain":"double is the higher-precision default; float saves space when precision can be lower.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf299","topic":"","text":"Assigning an int value to a double variable, like double d = 42;:","options":["works - 42 widens to 42.0","is a compile error","truncates the value","requires a cast"],"correct":0,"explain":"Widening (int to double) is safe and automatic; the reverse needs a cast.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_18":[{"id":"zya2_18_0","topic":"Random object","text":"Which creates a random number generator?","options":["Random r = new Random();","Random r = Random();","new Math.Random();","Random.create();"],"correct":0,"explain":"Random is a class in java.util and needs new. Math.random() is the alternative, but it is a static method returning a double."},{"id":"zya2_18_1","topic":"nextInt range","text":"r.nextInt(6) returns values in the range:","options":["0 to 5","1 to 5","1 to 6","0 to 6"],"correct":0,"explain":"The bound is exclusive. For a die roll you need r.nextInt(6) + 1."},{"id":"zya2_18_2","topic":"Math.random range","text":"Math.random() returns a double in:","options":["[0.0, 1.0)","(0.0, 1.0)","[1.0, 10.0)","[0.0, 1.0]"],"correct":0,"explain":"Zero is possible, 1.0 is not — the upper bound is exclusive."},{"id":"zya2_18_3","topic":"Random in a range","text":"Which produces a random int from 10 to 20 inclusive?","options":["r.nextInt(11) + 10","r.nextInt(20) - 10","r.nextInt(20)","r.nextInt(10) + 20"],"correct":0,"explain":"The count of values is 20 - 10 + 1 = 11, then shift up by the minimum."},{"id":"zya2_18_4","topic":"Seeding","text":"new Random(42) with a fixed seed produces:","options":["The same sequence every run","An exception","Only the number 42","Truly random numbers"],"correct":0,"explain":"A fixed seed makes runs reproducible — essential when debugging code that uses randomness. Is useful for testing."},{"id":"zya2_18_5","topic":"Pseudorandom meaning","text":"Java random numbers are called pseudorandom because:","options":["They are drawn from measurements of physical noise","They repeat only after the program is restarted","They come from a deterministic algorithm driven by a seed","They are uniformly spread across the full range"],"correct":2,"explain":"The sequence is computed, not physically random. It passes statistical tests but is fully determined by the seed."},{"id":"zy2_18_8_bp_D286_q2_1","topic":"Scanner trap","text":"After sc.nextInt(), calling sc.nextLine() immediately returns:","options":["The next full line the user types at the prompt","The integer just read, converted into a string","An empty string — the newline left behind in the buffer","null, because the input buffer has been emptied"],"correct":2,"explain":"nextInt() leaves the newline in the buffer; nextLine() consumes it and returns essentially empty. Fix: add an extra sc.nextLine() to eat the newline.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy2_18_9_bp_D286_r15_14","topic":"Scanner fix","text":"To fix the nextInt/nextLine bug you:","options":["add an extra nextLine() to flush","use print","use nextInt twice","close the Scanner"],"correct":0,"explain":"A throwaway nextLine() after nextInt() clears the buffer.","__rebal":1},{"id":"n_n2_18_a","topic":"Random","text":"new Random().nextInt(10) returns a value in:","options":["0 to 9","1 to 10","0 to 10","1 to 9"],"correct":0,"explain":"The bound is exclusive, so nextInt(10) never returns 10. Getting 1..10 needs nextInt(10) + 1."},{"id":"n_n2_18_b","topic":"Random","text":"Seeding a Random with a fixed number is useful because:","options":["It runs faster","It avoids negative values","The same sequence is produced every run","It makes the numbers more random"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reproducibility is the point. A test that fails only on some runs is nearly impossible to debug. Makes tests repeatable."},{"id":"fs_z2_18_1","topic":"Range of nextInt plus offset","text":"Random r = new Random();
int v = r.nextInt(5) + 3;

The possible values of v are:","options":["3 through 7 inclusive","3 through 8 inclusive","0 through 5 inclusive","4 through 8 inclusive"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextInt(5) returns 0 through 4 - the bound is exclusive. Adding 3 shifts the range to 3 through 7. Checking both ends by hand catches this kind of off-by-one."},{"id":"fs_z2_18_2","topic":"Exclusive bound","text":"How many distinct values can r.nextInt(6) return?","options":["6","7","5","Infinitely many"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextInt(6) returns 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5 - six values. The bound 6 itself never appears, which is why a die roll needs the extra + 1."},{"id":"fs_z2_18_3","topic":"Inclusive range formula","text":"To produce a random int from min to max inclusive, the argument passed to nextInt must be:","options":["max - min + 1","max - min","max + min","max"],"correct":0,"explain":"There are max - min + 1 values in an inclusive range, so the call is r.nextInt(max - min + 1) + min. Dropping the + 1 makes the top of the range unreachable."},{"id":"fs_z2_18_4","topic":"nextDouble bounds","text":"Which value can r.nextDouble() return?","options":["0.0 exactly, but never 1.0","1.0 exactly, but never 0.0","Both 0.0 and 1.0","Neither 0.0 nor 1.0"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextDouble() returns a value in [0.0, 1.0): zero is a possible result, but 1.0 is excluded - the same half-open convention as nextInt's bound."},{"id":"zf300","topic":"","text":"Math.random() returns a double in the range:","options":["0.0 through 1.0 inclusive","1.0 through 10.0","-1.0 to 1.0","0.0 (inclusive) up to 1.0 (exclusive)"],"correct":3,"explain":"It can return 0.0 but never exactly 1.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf301","topic":"","text":"(int)(Math.random() * 6) produces which values?","options":["0 through 5","1 through 6","0 through 6","1 through 5"],"correct":0,"explain":"The double in [0,1) scales to [0,6) and the cast truncates: 0..5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf302","topic":"","text":"(int)(Math.random() * 6) + 1 simulates:","options":["a coin flip","a standard die roll, 1 through 6","values 0 through 6","values 2 through 7"],"correct":1,"explain":"Shift the 0..5 range up by one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf303","topic":"","text":"(int)(Math.random() * 10) produces:","options":["1 through 10","0 through 10","0 through 9","1 through 9"],"correct":2,"explain":"Scaling by 10 and truncating gives ten values starting at 0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf304","topic":"","text":"new Random().nextInt(20) returns:","options":["1 through 20","0 through 20","-20 to 20","0 through 19"],"correct":3,"explain":"nextInt(n) spans 0 up to n-1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf305","topic":"","text":"rand.nextInt(10) + 5 returns:","options":["5 through 14","5 through 15","0 through 15","10 through 15"],"correct":0,"explain":"Ten possible values (0..9), shifted up by 5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf306","topic":"","text":"To get random ints from min through max inclusive, use:","options":["rand.nextInt(max) + min","rand.nextInt(max - min + 1) + min","rand.nextInt(max - min) + min","rand.nextInt(min) + max"],"correct":1,"explain":"The count of values is max-min+1; then shift by min.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf307","topic":"","text":"Using the Random class requires:","options":["no import - it is in java.lang","import java.math.Random;","import java.util.Random;","installing a library"],"correct":2,"explain":"Random lives in java.util, unlike Math.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf308","topic":"","text":"Two Random objects created with the SAME seed:","options":["produce opposite numbers","throw an exception","share one sequence between them","produce the same sequence of numbers"],"correct":3,"explain":"Seeded generators are deterministic - handy for repeatable tests.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf309","topic":"","text":"Random numbers from Math.random() are really:","options":["pseudo-random","measured from atmospheric noise","truly unpredictable","all equal over time"],"correct":0,"explain":"They look random but follow an algorithm from a starting seed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf310","topic":"","text":"A fair coin flip can be coded as:","options":["rand.nextInt(2), with 0 and 1 as sides","rand.nextInt(1)","Math.random() cast to int alone","rand.nextInt(2) + 1 giving 0 or 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextInt(2) yields exactly two equally likely values, 0 and 1.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_19":[{"id":"zya2_19_0","topic":"Purpose of the API docs","text":"The Java API documentation tells you:","options":["How the JVM executes bytecode on each platform","Which coding conventions your team has adopted","How long each library method takes to run","What classes and methods exist, with parameters and returns"],"correct":3,"explain":"It is the reference for the contract of every library class — what to pass, what comes back, what can be thrown."},{"id":"zya2_19_1","topic":"Method signature reading","text":"public int indexOf(String str) tells you the method:","options":["Takes nothing","Returns nothing","Takes an int and returns a String","Takes a String and returns an int"],"correct":3,"explain":"Read it as: return type first, then name, then parameter types in parentheses."},{"id":"zya2_19_2","topic":"Static in the docs","text":"A method listed as static is called:","options":["Only inside constructors","Only from main","On an object instance","On the class name, e.g. Math.abs(x)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Static members belong to the class, so no object is needed."},{"id":"zya2_19_3","topic":"Return type void","text":"A method with return type void:","options":["Returns null","Cannot be called","Returns 0","Returns nothing to the caller"],"correct":3,"explain":"It performs an action instead — println is the obvious example."},{"id":"zya2_19_4","topic":"Overloaded entries","text":"Seeing substring(int) and substring(int, int) in the docs means:","options":["A documentation error","You must call both","One is deprecated","The method is overloaded"],"correct":3,"explain":"Overloading lets one name serve related purposes. The compiler picks by argument count and types. Two versions with different parameter lists."},{"id":"zya2_19_5","topic":"Throws clause","text":"A documented "Throws: NumberFormatException" means:","options":["You must catch it or the code will not compile","The method returns an exception","The method always fails","The method may throw that exception for invalid input"],"correct":3,"explain":"NumberFormatException is unchecked, so catching is optional — but the docs are telling you the failure mode exists."},{"id":"zya2_19_6","topic":"Package location","text":"The docs list String in package:","options":["java.io","java.text","java.util","java.lang"],"correct":3,"explain":"java.lang is imported automatically, which is why String, Math and System need no import."},{"id":"zya2_19_7","topic":"Finding the right class","text":"You need to sort an ArrayList. The docs class to consult is:","options":["Math","Scanner","System","Collections"],"correct":3,"explain":"Collections.sort(list) is the utility. Arrays.sort handles plain arrays."},{"id":"zya2_19_8","topic":"Parameter meaning","text":"substring(int beginIndex, int endIndex) — endIndex is:","options":["The length to take","Optional","Inclusive","Exclusive"],"correct":3,"explain":"The docs state it explicitly, which is why "Hello".substring(1,3) gives "el" and not "ell"."},{"id":"zya2_19_9","topic":"Why read docs over guessing","text":"Consulting the API rather than guessing a method name prevents:","options":["Runtime exceptions caused by invalid user input","The need to test the finished program at all","Compile errors from methods that do not exist, and misuse","Style-guide violations flagged during code review"],"correct":2,"explain":"Guessed names fail to compile — the harmless case. The dangerous case is a real method that behaves slightly differently than assumed."},{"id":"fs_z2_19_1","topic":"Return values must be used","text":"The statement s.trim(); on a line by itself accomplishes nothing. Which fact from the documentation explains why?","options":["Methods with a return type cannot be called as statements","trim returns a new String","trim is a static method","trim throws a checked exception that was not handled"],"correct":1,"explain":"The return type in the signature tells you the result must be assigned: trim hands back a new String and leaves the original untouched. s = s.trim(); is the working version. The return value must be assigned, because the original is not changed in place."},{"id":"fs_z2_19_2","topic":"Reading signatures","text":"In the signature public int indexOf(String str, int fromIndex), the int written before the method name tells you:","options":["That the method is static","The type of value the method returns","The number of parameters the method takes","The position where searching starts"],"correct":1,"explain":"The type before the name is the return type - what you get back from the call. The int fromIndex inside the parentheses is the parameter that controls where searching starts."},{"id":"fs_z2_19_3","topic":"Special failure values","text":"indexOf returns -1 when the text is not found. In API terms, -1 here is:","options":["The index of the last character","A documented special value that signals failure","An exception in disguise","A compile-time constant error"],"correct":1,"explain":"APIs document special values that signal failure - -1 for indexOf, null for a missing map key. Knowing them is one of the four things to extract before using an unfamiliar method."},{"id":"fs_z2_19_4","topic":"Exclusive end index","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println("hello".substring(1, 3));","options":["he","el","ell","hel"],"correct":1,"explain":"The documentation says the end index is exclusive: substring(1, 3) takes characters at indexes 1 and 2, giving "el". Assuming it is inclusive is the classic mistake only the docs prevent."},{"id":"zf311","topic":"","text":"API documentation for a method tells you:","options":["its complete source code line by line","its purpose, parameters, and return type","who wrote it","how fast your computer is"],"correct":1,"explain":"The doc is the contract: what goes in, what comes out, what it does.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf312","topic":"","text":"String and Math need no import because:","options":["they are keywords","the IDE inlines them","they live in the auto-imported java.lang","they are part of your class"],"correct":2,"explain":"java.lang is the one package Java always imports for you.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf314","topic":"","text":"In the signature double sqrt(double a), the FIRST double is:","options":["the return type","the parameter type","a keyword","the class name"],"correct":0,"explain":"Signatures read: returnType name(parameterTypes).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf315","topic":"","text":"The docs show several abs methods: abs(int), abs(double), abs(long). This is called:","options":["overriding","overloading","inheritance","recursion"],"correct":1,"explain":"The compiler picks the version matching your argument type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf316","topic":"","text":"A static method in the docs is called:","options":["on an object you must create first","only from main","on the class name, like Math.sqrt(9)","with the new keyword"],"correct":2,"explain":"No instance needed - the class itself owns the method.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf317","topic":"","text":"A method marked deprecated in the docs:","options":["no longer compiles","runs slower on purpose","is faster","works but should be avoided"],"correct":3,"explain":"Deprecation is a warning that better replacements exist and removal may come.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf318","topic":"","text":"The parameter names in documentation (like "double a"):","options":["describe the inputs","must match your variable names","are required keywords","are the return values"],"correct":0,"explain":"Docs name parameters for explanation only.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf319","topic":"","text":"If the docs say a method "returns the larger of a and b", calling Math.max(3, 8) gives:","options":["3","8","11","true"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reading the described behavior tells you the result without guessing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf320","topic":"","text":"Checking documentation BEFORE using an unfamiliar method matters because:","options":["the compiler requires proof you read it","assumptions about edge cases are often wrong","docs change the bytecode","methods without docs cannot run"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two minutes of reading beats an hour of debugging a misunderstood method.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf321","topic":"","text":"The Java API documentation is organized by:","options":["alphabetical keywords only","year of release","packages of classes with their methods","file size"],"correct":2,"explain":"Navigate package -> class -> method summary/detail.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr2","topic":"Reading API documentation","text":"In the Java API documentation, the "Method Summary" table of a class tells you:","options":["only the methods you have already imported","the source code of every method in the class","each method's name, parameters and return type","which methods the compiler will inline"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Method Summary lists the signature of every public method — its return type, name and parameter list — which is what you need to call it correctly.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_20":[{"id":"zya2_20_0","topic":"Debugging definition","text":"Debugging is:","options":["Removing every compiler warning from the source","Rewriting code so that it runs more quickly","Adding comments that explain what the code does","Finding and fixing the cause of incorrect behaviour"],"correct":3,"explain":"The emphasis is on cause. Changing code until symptoms disappear is not debugging."},{"id":"zya2_20_1","topic":"Print debugging","text":"Adding System.out.println of variable values helps because:","options":["It makes the program run more slowly and safely","It forces the compiler to re-check the types","It reveals actual values at specific points","It prevents exceptions from being thrown"],"correct":2,"explain":"Most logic bugs come from a variable holding something other than what you assumed. Can be compared with expectations."},{"id":"zya2_20_2","topic":"Where to print","text":"To find why a loop produces the wrong total, print:","options":["The final total once the loop has finished","The running total and loop counter on each iteration","The array's length before the loop begins","The method's parameters when it is first called"],"correct":1,"explain":"Watching values change per iteration shows exactly which step diverges from expectation."},{"id":"zya2_20_3","topic":"Reading the stack trace","text":"An exception stack trace is most useful for:","options":["The values held by every variable at the time","The reason the compiler rejected the program","The amount of memory the program had allocated","The exception's line number and the call path that led there"],"correct":3,"explain":"Read the top line for what and where, then scan downward for how execution arrived."},{"id":"zya2_20_4","topic":"Reproducing a bug","text":"Before fixing a bug you should first:","options":["Rewrite the method from scratch to be safe","Add a try-catch so it no longer crashes","Ask a colleague to review the whole file","Reproduce it reliably with a specific input"],"correct":3,"explain":"If you cannot reproduce it, you cannot confirm the fix worked."},{"id":"zya2_20_5","topic":"Binary search debugging","text":"With a bug somewhere in 200 lines, an efficient tactic is:","options":["Read the file from the first line to the last","Add a try-catch around every single statement","Check state at the midpoint to halve the suspect region","Rewrite the section you understand least well"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each check halves the search space — the same idea as binary search."},{"id":"zya2_20_6","topic":"Rubber duck","text":"Explaining code line by line to someone (or something) helps because:","options":["The listener always spots the defect immediately","Verbalizing forces you to check assumptions you skipped","Speaking slows you down enough to avoid typos","It satisfies the code-review requirement for a bug"],"correct":1,"explain":"The bug is usually in the step you keep glossing over."},{"id":"zya2_20_7","topic":"Change one thing","text":"When debugging you should change:","options":["Only comments","The compiler settings","Many things at once to save time","One thing at a time, retesting after each"],"correct":3,"explain":"Multiple simultaneous changes make it impossible to attribute the result."},{"id":"zya2_20_8","topic":"Off-by-one symptom","text":"A loop that processes all but the last element most likely has:","options":["An accumulator variable that was never initialised","A missing break statement somewhere inside the body","An array declared with the wrong element type","A condition using < where <= was needed, or a wrong starting index"],"correct":3,"explain":"Off-by-one errors show up exactly at the boundaries, so check the first and last iterations first."},{"id":"zya2_20_9","topic":"After the fix","text":"Once a bug is fixed you should:","options":["Delete the tests that were passing before the fix","Move on, since the fix was verified by hand","Re-run the earlier tests to confirm nothing else broke","Add a comment recording who introduced the bug"],"correct":2,"explain":"Fixes commonly break something else. Re-running previous tests is regression testing."},{"id":"fs_z2_20_1","topic":"Diagnosis by symptom","text":"After a loop finishes, the total equals only the last value processed instead of the sum. What is the most likely cause?","options":["println was called inside the loop","= was used where += was meant","The loop ran one extra time","The total overflowed"],"correct":1,"explain":"total = a[i] replaces the accumulator every pass, leaving only the final value. total += a[i] is what accumulates. Diagnosing from the symptom pattern is faster than re-reading every line. Each pass overwrote the total."},{"id":"fs_z2_20_2","topic":"Diagnosis by symptom","text":"A loop's total prints as 0 even though the array clearly contains nonzero values. What is the likely cause?","options":["println rounds small totals down to 0","The accumulator was declared inside the loop","Arrays always sum to 0 unless sorted","The += operator subtracts for negative indexes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Declaring int total = 0; inside the loop body recreates and rezeroes it each iteration. The accumulator must be declared once, before the loop begins. It was reset to 0 on every pass."},{"id":"fs_z2_20_3","topic":"Regressions","text":"A regression is:","options":["A bug introduced by a change to code that previously worked","A bug that appears only under a heavy workload","A statistical technique for predicting run time","A bug the compiler reports but does not block"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fixes routinely break something else, which is why re-running earlier tests after a fix is part of the debugging procedure - that is what catches regressions."},{"id":"fs_z2_20_4","topic":"Cause vs symptom","text":"Changing code until the symptom disappears is poor debugging practice because:","options":["The compiler flags every guessed change as an error","Without finding the cause, the underlying bug may remain hidden or resurface elsewhere","Code may never be changed while debugging","Symptoms cannot disappear without a recompile"],"correct":1,"explain":"Debugging is finding the cause of wrong behavior, not making the symptom vanish. A change that masks the symptom without explaining it leaves the real bug in place."},{"id":"zf322","topic":"","text":"The FIRST step when a bug is reported is to:","options":["rewrite the whole program","blame the compiler","add more features","reproduce the problem reliably"],"correct":3,"explain":"You cannot verify a fix for a bug you cannot make happen.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf323","topic":"","text":"Adding temporary System.out.println statements helps debugging by:","options":["showing actual values at key points","fixing the bug automatically","speeding up the program","suppressing exceptions"],"correct":0,"explain":"Comparing actual values to expected values localizes the fault.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf324","topic":"","text":"In a stack trace, the topmost "at" line shows:","options":["the last line of main","where the exception was thrown","a random line","the import section"],"correct":1,"explain":"Read top-down: the first frame is the throwing line, callers follow.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf325","topic":"","text":"A stack trace line like at Pay.main(Pay.java:14) tells you:","options":["the program has 14 lines","14 errors exist","the exception hit line 14 of Pay.java","line 14 is a comment"],"correct":2,"explain":"File and line numbers aim you straight at the code involved.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf326","topic":"","text":"Explaining your code line-by-line to someone (or something) else:","options":["is a waste of time","only works with managers","requires special software","often reveals the bug as you speak"],"correct":3,"explain":"Rubber-duck debugging works because saying it out loud exposes gaps.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf327","topic":"","text":"If output is wrong, checking the INPUT values first matters because:","options":["wrong input makes correct logic look wrong","inputs are usually corrupted by the JVM","logic errors are impossible","inputs cannot be printed"],"correct":0,"explain":"Garbage in, garbage out - verify the data before rewriting the code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf328","topic":"","text":"To narrow a bug in a long calculation, a good tactic is to:","options":["delete half the program permanently","print intermediates; find the first wrong one","re-run it and hope","increase the font size"],"correct":1,"explain":"The first incorrect intermediate value marks where the fault lives.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf329","topic":"","text":"A "fix" made without understanding WHY it works:","options":["is always fine if tests pass once","is the professional standard","is risky - the real bug may remain","makes the code faster"],"correct":2,"explain":"Symptom-patching leaves the underlying cause free to strike elsewhere.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf330","topic":"","text":"A debugger improves on println debugging by:","options":["writing the fix for you","removing the need to think","pausing to inspect variables with no code edits","running the program backwards by default"],"correct":2,"explain":"Breakpoints and stepping examine live state with zero code changes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf331","topic":"","text":"After fixing a bug you should:","options":["delete the tests","assume everything else still works","ship immediately without running it","rerun the tests"],"correct":3,"explain":"Regression testing catches fixes that break something else.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf864","topic":"","text":"A NullPointerException stack trace most directly tells you:","options":["which variable to delete","that the JVM is broken","the line where a null was dereferenced","the total number of bugs"],"correct":2,"explain":"The top frame points at the dereference; work backwards to find where the null came from.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_21":[{"id":"zya2_21_0","topic":"Class naming","text":"Java convention names classes using:","options":["PascalCase (StudentRecord)","ALL_CAPS","lowercase with underscores","camelCase"],"correct":0,"explain":"Classes start with a capital; variables and methods start lowercase."},{"id":"zya2_21_1","topic":"Method naming","text":"A method that returns a student name is conventionally named:","options":["getName","NAME","GetName","get_name"],"correct":0,"explain":"camelCase with a verb — methods do things, so their names start with verbs."},{"id":"zya2_21_2","topic":"Meaningful names","text":"Which variable name is best for a loop counter over students?","options":["studentIndex","a1","x","temp"],"correct":0,"explain":"Descriptive names remove the need for a comment explaining them. Short names like i are acceptable only for tight, obvious loops."},{"id":"zya2_21_3","topic":"Brace style","text":"Java convention places the opening brace:","options":["At the end of the line that opens the block","Indented four spaces below","Anywhere — it is required to be consistent only","On its own line"],"correct":0,"explain":"The compiler accepts any placement; convention is end-of-line, and consistency matters more than the choice."},{"id":"zya2_21_4","topic":"Indentation amount","text":"Standard Java indentation per nesting level is:","options":["4 spaces (or one tab)","8 spaces","No indentation","1 space"],"correct":0,"explain":"Four spaces is the common convention. What matters most is applying it consistently."},{"id":"zya2_21_5","topic":"Line length","text":"Very long lines are discouraged because:","options":["They are hard to read and review","Java limits line length","They use more memory","They compile slowly"],"correct":0,"explain":"Around 80-120 characters is typical guidance; wrap long expressions at operators."},{"id":"zya2_21_6","topic":"One statement per line","text":"Writing int a=1; int b=2; on one line is:","options":["A compile error; one declaration per line","Legal but discouraged for readability","Legal and preferred, since it is more compact","Legal only when both variables share a type"],"correct":1,"explain":"It compiles, but it hides statements and makes debugging by line number harder."},{"id":"zya2_21_7","topic":"Constant naming","text":"Which follows convention for a constant?","options":["MAX_SIZE","max_size","maxSize","MaxSize"],"correct":0,"explain":"ALL_CAPS with underscores signals a value that never changes."},{"id":"zya2_21_8","topic":"Why conventions matter","text":"Coding conventions primarily exist to:","options":["Make the compiled program run more quickly","Reduce the number of source files in a project","Allow the compiler to infer missing type names","Make code readable and consistent across a team"],"correct":3,"explain":"The compiler is indifferent. Humans reading the code are not."},{"id":"zya2_21_9","topic":"Whitespace around operators","text":"Which is conventionally formatted?","options":["int sum = a + b;","int sum =a+ b;","intsum = a+b;","int sum=a+b;"],"correct":0,"explain":"Spaces around binary operators aid readability. The last option would not compile at all."},{"id":"fs_z2_21_1","topic":"Conventions carry information","text":"A reader who sees the name MAX_SIZE written in ALL_CAPS immediately knows:","options":["It is a constant whose value will not change","It is a class name","It is a loop counter","It is a method that returns a maximum"],"correct":0,"explain":"Naming conventions carry information: ALL_CAPS signals a constant, PascalCase signals a class, camelCase signals a variable or method. The name documents the code."},{"id":"fs_z2_21_2","topic":"Boolean naming","text":"Which is the conventional name for a boolean method that checks whether input is valid?","options":["isValid","validate","checkTheValidityOfTheInputCarefully","VALID"],"correct":0,"explain":"Booleans read best with an is or has prefix because the call site becomes a sentence. Verb names like validate suit methods that act; value-returning methods are named for what they return. The call site reads like a sentence: if (isValid(domain))."},{"id":"fs_z2_21_3","topic":"Style is not enforced","text":"Declaring a class as class bankAccount (lowercase b) results in:","options":["Code that compiles and runs but breaks the PascalCase convention for class names","A compile error","A runtime exception when the class is instantiated","A warning that halts execution"],"correct":0,"explain":"None of the style rules are enforced by the compiler - the code works fine. Convention says classes are PascalCase (BankAccount), and every reader will expect it."},{"id":"fs_z2_21_4","topic":"The naming test","text":"Which name best passes the test that a reader can tell what the variable holds without tracing the code?","options":["daysRemaining","d","temp","var1"],"correct":0,"explain":"int d fails the naming test; int daysRemaining passes it. Single letters like i are acceptable only as tight, obvious loop counters."},{"id":"zf332","topic":"","text":"Java variable and method names conventionally use:","options":["camelCase, like totalPay","ALL_CAPS","PascalCase, like TotalPay","snake_case only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Lowercase start, capitals at word boundaries - grossPay, computeTax.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf333","topic":"","text":"Class names conventionally use:","options":["camelCase like payrollReport","PascalCase, starting with a capital","all lowercase","ALL_CAPS"],"correct":1,"explain":"Classes are capitalized; that instantly distinguishes them from variables.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf334","topic":"","text":"The main reason to follow style conventions is:","options":["the compiler enforces them","styled code runs faster","code is read more often than written","they are legally required"],"correct":2,"explain":"Consistent style lets any reader (including future you) parse the code quickly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf335","topic":"","text":"Which name best fits a variable holding hours worked?","options":["hw","x2","TheNumberOfHoursThatWereWorkedThisWeekByTheEmployee","hoursWorked"],"correct":3,"explain":"Descriptive but not absurd - meaningful and readable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf336","topic":"","text":"Magic numbers like 0.0765 sprinkled in code should be:","options":["replaced with named constants","left alone - they are exact","converted to strings","rounded off"],"correct":0,"explain":"final double SS_TAX_RATE = 0.0765; explains itself and centralizes change.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf337","topic":"","text":"Good practice for statements per line is:","options":["as many as fit","one statement per line","two, always","statements have no line rules"],"correct":1,"explain":"int a = 1; int b = 2; on one line compiles but reads poorly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf338","topic":"","text":"Indentation inside braces should:","options":["be random","decrease as nesting deepens","increase one level per nested block","be avoided"],"correct":2,"explain":"The eye uses indentation to see structure at a glance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf339","topic":"","text":"Writing x=y+z versus x = y + z:","options":["only the first compiles","only the second compiles","the spacing changes the math","both compile"],"correct":3,"explain":"Spaces around operators cost nothing and improve readability.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf340","topic":"","text":"A variable named data used for a tax rate is poor style because:","options":["data is a reserved word","tax rates cannot be stored","it is too long","the name says nothing about the value"],"correct":3,"explain":"Names should carry meaning; taxRate documents itself.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf341","topic":"","text":"Consistent brace placement (same pattern everywhere) matters because:","options":["inconsistency makes readers re-parse blocks","the compiler warns otherwise","braces affect speed","some braces are optional in Java"],"correct":0,"explain":"Pick the project’s convention and stick to it everywhere.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf342","topic":"","text":"Comments, naming, and indentation together are aimed at ONE audience:","options":["the JVM","human readers of the code","the operating system","the compiler’s optimizer"],"correct":1,"explain":"The machine ignores all three - style exists entirely for people.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_22":[{"id":"zya2_22_0","topic":"var meaning","text":"var name = "Ana"; declares name as:","options":["A variable with no type","A String","An Object","A dynamically typed variable"],"correct":1,"explain":"Java stays statically typed. var only asks the compiler to work out the type, and it is fixed from then on. The type is inferred from the initializer."},{"id":"zya2_22_1","topic":"var requires initializer","text":"var x; is:","options":["Legal, x is null","A compile error","Legal, x is an Object","Legal, x is an int"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without an initializer the compiler has no information, so var is rejected. There is nothing to infer the type from."},{"id":"zya2_22_2","topic":"var and reassignment","text":"var n = 5; n = "hello"; is:","options":["Legal, because var makes the variable dynamic","A compile error, because n was inferred as int","Legal, but n silently becomes a String at runtime","A runtime exception thrown at the assignment"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is the key distinction from JavaScript or Python: the inferred type is permanent."},{"id":"zya2_22_3","topic":"Where var is allowed","text":"var can be used for:","options":["Return types","Local variables inside methods","Class fields","Method parameters"],"correct":1,"explain":"Local variables only — including loop variables. Fields, parameters and return types still need explicit types."},{"id":"zya2_22_4","topic":"var in a for loop","text":"for (var i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) is:","options":["Illegal","Legal","Legal but i is a double","A runtime error"],"correct":1,"explain":"The literal 0 is an int, so i is int. I is inferred as int."},{"id":"zya2_22_5","topic":"var with new","text":"var list = new ArrayList&lt;String>(); infers:","options":["ArrayList<String>","List<String>","Object"],"correct":0,"explain":"Inference takes the exact type of the initializer, not an interface it implements."},{"id":"zya2_22_6","topic":"var readability caution","text":"var total = compute(); is criticized because:","options":["var cannot be used with a method call at all","The reader cannot see the type without checking compute()","The compiler cannot infer a type from a method","It forces the variable to be treated as an Object"],"correct":1,"explain":"var helps when the type is obvious from the right-hand side and hurts when it is not."},{"id":"zya2_22_7","topic":"var is not a keyword","text":"var is:","options":["A reserved keyword","A reserved type name","Illegal as an identifier anywhere","A class in java.lang"],"correct":1,"explain":"Existing code that used var as a variable name still compiles — a deliberate compatibility decision. A variable may still be called var."},{"id":"zya2_22_8","topic":"var with null","text":"var x = null; is:","options":["Legal, x is Object","A compile error","Legal, x is String","Legal, x is a special null type"],"correct":1,"explain":"null could be any reference type, so there is nothing to infer. Null has no inferable type."},{"id":"zya2_22_9","topic":"Type safety with var","text":"Using var means the program is:","options":["Dynamically typed, with checks deferred to runtime","Slower, because types are resolved while running","Still fully statically type-checked at compile time","Unable to use generics anywhere in that method"],"correct":2,"explain":"var is purely a source-level convenience. The generated bytecode is identical to writing the type out."},{"id":"n_n2_22_a","topic":"var","text":"var x = 5; gives x the type:","options":["Object","double","int","var"],"correct":2,"explain":"var is inference, not a dynamic type. The compiler fixes x as int from the initialiser and it can never hold anything else."},{"id":"n_n2_22_b","topic":"var","text":"Which is illegal?","options":["var s = "hi";","var list = new ArrayList<String>();","var x;","var x = 5;"],"correct":2,"explain":"There is no initialiser, so the compiler has nothing to infer from. var always requires a value on the same line."},{"id":"n_n2_22_c","topic":"var","text":"var may be used for:","options":["Method parameters","Return types","Local variables inside a method","Fields of a class"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is restricted to local variables (and some loop variables). Fields, parameters and return types must still be declared explicitly."},{"id":"zf343","topic":"","text":"var count = 5; gives count the type:","options":["var","double","int","Integer only"],"correct":2,"explain":"The compiler infers the type from the initializer - a plain 5 is an int.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf344","topic":"","text":"var name = "Ada"; gives name the type:","options":["var","char","Object only","String"],"correct":3,"explain":"A string literal initializer infers String.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf345","topic":"","text":"var price = 2.5; gives price the type:","options":["double","float","int","BigDecimal"],"correct":0,"explain":"Floating literals default to double, so that is what var infers.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf346","topic":"","text":"var flag = true; gives flag the type:","options":["int","boolean","Boolean text","bit"],"correct":1,"explain":"true/false literals infer boolean.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf347","topic":"","text":"var x; (no initializer) is:","options":["legal, defaulting to int","legal, defaulting to Object","a compile error","a warning"],"correct":2,"explain":"No right-hand side means no type to infer.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf348","topic":"","text":"var n = 5; n = "text"; causes:","options":["n to become a String","a runtime exception","n to hold both","a compile error"],"correct":3,"explain":"var is NOT dynamic typing; the inferred type is fixed at declaration.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf349","topic":"","text":"A good use of var is:","options":["when the type is obvious from the right side","hiding what type things are on purpose","every declaration without exception","constants only"],"correct":0,"explain":"var shines when it removes redundancy without hiding information.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf350","topic":"","text":"var total = 3 + 4.0; gives total the type:","options":["double","int","float","String"],"correct":0,"explain":"The expression mixes int and double, so its value (7.0) is a double.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf351","topic":"","text":"The var feature keeps Java:","options":["dynamically typed like Python","statically typed","untyped","interpreted"],"correct":1,"explain":"Inference chooses the type early and locks it in; nothing becomes dynamic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf352","topic":"","text":"Which declaration is ILLEGAL?","options":["var count = 10;","var msg = "hi";","var scores = null;","var half = 0.5;"],"correct":2,"explain":"null carries no type information, so there is nothing to infer.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf353","topic":"","text":"Compared with int total = 0;, writing var total = 0;:","options":["creates a different, slower variable","requires an import","makes total constant","behaves identically - both declare an int"],"correct":3,"explain":"var is purely a syntax convenience; the compiled result is the same.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf865","topic":"","text":"var nums = new int[5]; gives nums the type:","options":["var[]","Object","ArrayList","int[]"],"correct":3,"explain":"The initializer is an int array, so that is exactly what the compiler infers.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_23":[{"id":"zya2_23_0","topic":"Gross pay formula","text":"An employee earns rate per hour for hours worked. Gross pay for 40 hours at 20.0 is computed as:","options":["hours - rate","rate + hours","rate * hours","rate / hours"],"correct":2,"explain":"Rate multiplied by hours: 20.0 * 40 = 800.0."},{"id":"zya2_23_1","topic":"Type for pay","text":"Pay amounts such as 812.50 should be stored in a:","options":["boolean","int","double","char"],"correct":2,"explain":"Money has a fractional part, so int would truncate the cents away."},{"id":"zya2_23_2","topic":"Overtime condition","text":"Overtime applies above 40 hours. The correct test is:","options":["if (hours < 40)","if (hours == 40)","if (hours > 40)","if (hours >= 40)"],"correct":2,"explain":"At exactly 40 hours there is no overtime yet, so the comparison must be strictly greater."},{"id":"zya2_23_3","topic":"Overtime pay","text":"With time-and-a-half above 40 hours, pay for 45 hours at rate r is:","options":["r * 45","r * 40 + r * 5","r * 40 + r * 1.5 * 5","r * 1.5 * 45"],"correct":2,"explain":"The first 40 hours pay normal rate; only the 5 extra hours get the 1.5 multiplier."},{"id":"zya2_23_4","topic":"Annual from weekly","text":"Annual salary from weekly pay w is:","options":["w / 52","w * 12","w * 52","w * 365"],"correct":2,"explain":"There are 52 weeks in a year. Multiplying by 12 would confuse weekly with monthly."},{"id":"zya2_23_5","topic":"Monthly from annual","text":"Monthly pay from annual salary a is:","options":["a / 365","a / 52","a / 12","a * 12"],"correct":2,"explain":"Twelve months in a year."},{"id":"zya2_23_6","topic":"Net pay","text":"If tax rate is 0.20, net pay from gross g is:","options":["g * 0.20","g - 0.20","g * (1 - 0.20)","g / 0.20"],"correct":2,"explain":"Keeping 80% is g * 0.8, equivalently g - g * 0.20."},{"id":"zya2_23_7","topic":"Formatting currency","text":"Which prints pay with two decimals?","options":["System.out.print(pay + ".00");","System.out.println(pay);","System.out.printf("%.2f%n", pay);","System.out.printf("%d%n", pay);"],"correct":2,"explain":"%.2f rounds to two decimal places. Appending ".00" as text would be wrong whenever there are real cents."},{"id":"zya2_23_8","topic":"Constant for tax","text":"Storing the tax rate as final double TAX_RATE = 0.20; is preferred because:","options":["The multiplication runs faster at execution time","final guarantees the arithmetic is exact","The value can be edited while the program runs","The value has a name and changes in exactly one place"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same reasoning as 2.9 — readability and single-point-of-change."},{"id":"zya2_23_9","topic":"Integer division bug","text":"int gross = 1000; int weeks = 3; double avg = gross / weeks; gives:","options":["A compile error","333.333...","333.0","333"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both operands are int, so integer division truncates to 333 and only then widens to 333.0. Cast one operand to fix it."},{"id":"fs_z2_23_1","topic":"Overtime calculation","text":"What does this print?

final double OVERTIME_THRESHOLD = 40.0;
double rate = 20.0;
double hours = 45.0;
double gross;
if (hours > OVERTIME_THRESHOLD) {
    gross = rate * OVERTIME_THRESHOLD + rate * 1.5 * (hours - OVERTIME_THRESHOLD);
} else {
    gross = rate * hours;
}
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", gross);","options":["900.00","800.00","950.00","1350.00"],"correct":2,"explain":"Check by hand: 40 hours at 20.00 is 800, plus 5 overtime hours at 30.00 is 150, giving 950. The wrong version that applies 1.5 to all 45 hours produces 1350."},{"id":"fs_z2_23_2","topic":"The wrong overtime formula","text":"A student computes pay for 45 hours at rate 20.0 by applying time-and-a-half to every hour. What does this print?

System.out.println(20.0 * 1.5 * 45);","options":["1350","900.0","1350.0","950.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"This prints 1350.0 - but it overpays, because the 1.5 multiplier should apply only to the 5 hours beyond the 40-hour threshold. The correct gross is 950."},{"id":"fs_z2_23_3","topic":"Percent vs amount","text":"With gross pay g and tax rate 0.20, the expression g - 0.20 is wrong because:","options":["0.20 must be written as the int 20","Tax should be added to gross pay","It subtracts twenty cents rather than twenty percent","Subtraction is not defined for doubles"],"correct":2,"explain":"g - 0.20 compiles and runs but subtracts a fixed 0.20 (twenty cents), not a fraction of gross. Keeping 80% is g * (1 - TAX_RATE), or equivalently g - g * TAX_RATE. Net pay is g * (1 - 0.20)."},{"id":"fs_z2_23_4","topic":"Net pay","text":"What does this print?

double gross = 950.0;
double net = gross * (1 - 0.20);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", net);","options":["949.80","190.00","760.00","950.20"],"correct":2,"explain":"(1 - 0.20) is 0.80, so the expression keeps 80% of gross: 950 times 0.80 is 760. The distractor 949.80 is what the mistaken gross - 0.20 would give."},{"id":"zf354","topic":"","text":"int rate = 18; int hours = 40; System.out.println(rate * hours); prints:","options":["720","58","738","702"],"correct":0,"explain":"Weekly pay = rate x hours = 18 x 40 = 720.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf355","topic":"","text":"int rate = 22; int hours = 35; System.out.println(rate * hours); prints:","options":["57","770","792","748"],"correct":1,"explain":"Weekly pay = rate x hours = 22 x 35 = 770.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf356","topic":"","text":"int rate = 15; int hours = 38; System.out.println(rate * hours); prints:","options":["53","585","570","555"],"correct":2,"explain":"Weekly pay = rate x hours = 15 x 38 = 570.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf357","topic":"","text":"int weekly = 900; System.out.println(weekly * 52); prints:","options":["45000","52900","46852","46800"],"correct":3,"explain":"Annual pay multiplies the weekly amount by 52 weeks: 46,800.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf358","topic":"","text":"int pay = 800; int bonus = 150; System.out.println(pay + bonus); prints:","options":["950","800150","650","815"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both operands are ints, so + means addition: 950.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf359","topic":"","text":"System.out.println("Pay: " + 700 + 50); prints:","options":["Pay: 750","Pay: 70050","750","Pay: 700"],"correct":1,"explain":"Left to right: "Pay: " + 700 makes a STRING, so the 50 concatenates too.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf360","topic":"","text":"What is the output? System.out.println("Pay: " + (700 + 50));","options":["Pay: 70050","Pay: 750","Pay: 700 + 50","750"],"correct":1,"explain":"Parentheses force the addition first, then the sum joins the label.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf361","topic":"","text":"int gross = 1000; int net = gross - gross / 10; System.out.println(net); prints:","options":["990","1000","900","100"],"correct":2,"explain":"gross / 10 = 100 (the deduction), so net = 1000 - 100 = 900.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf362","topic":"","text":"int overtimeHours = 6; int otRate = 27; int base = 720; System.out.println(base + overtimeHours * otRate); prints:","options":["753","882 + 27","19602","882"],"correct":3,"explain":"Multiplication first: 6 x 27 = 162 overtime; 720 + 162 = 882.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf363","topic":"","text":"Two employees earn 640 and 580. System.out.println(640 + 580); prints:","options":["1220","640580","1120","1210"],"correct":0,"explain":"Plain int addition: 1,220 combined.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf364","topic":"","text":"int hours = 40 + 5; int pay = hours * 20; System.out.println(pay); prints:","options":["800","900","805","8100"],"correct":1,"explain":"45 hours at 20 per hour is 900.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u2/z2_25":[{"id":"zya2_25_0","topic":"Storing names in variables","text":"Given String first = "Ann", last = "Lee"; which prints "Ann Lee"?","options":["System.out.println(first + " " + last);","System.out.println("first last");","System.out.println(first, last);","System.out.println(first + last);"],"correct":0,"explain":"The space must be an explicit literal, and println takes only one argument."},{"id":"zya2_25_1","topic":"Why use variables","text":"Storing the names in variables rather than literals means:","options":["The compiler can then check the spelling of names","String literals are not permitted inside methods","The names can be changed or read from input in one place","It reduces the memory the program needs at runtime"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is the step from a fixed program to one that works for any input."},{"id":"zya2_25_2","topic":"Reading a name","text":"Which Scanner call reads a single word such as a first name?","options":["in.next()","in.nextDouble()","in.nextChar()","in.nextInt()"],"correct":0,"explain":"next() reads one whitespace-delimited token. nextLine() would read the whole line including any surname."},{"id":"zya2_25_3","topic":"Reading a full line","text":"To read "Ann Lee" as one value you use:","options":["in.nextLine()","in.nextWord()","in.nextString()","in.next()"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextLine() reads to the end of the line, so it keeps the space and both names."},{"id":"zya2_25_4","topic":"Reversed order","text":"To print "Lee, Ann" from the same variables:","options":["last + ", " + first","first + ", " + last","last + " " + first","last + first"],"correct":0,"explain":"The comma and space are part of the literal between the two variables."},{"id":"zya2_25_5","topic":"String concatenation with a number","text":"String s = "Age: " + 30; produces:","options":[""Age: 30"","30",""Age: " only","A compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"When either operand of + is a String, the other is converted to its String form."},{"id":"zya2_25_6","topic":"Numeric addition before concatenation","text":"System.out.println("Sum: " + 2 + 3); prints:","options":["Sum: 23","5","A compile error","Sum: 5"],"correct":0,"explain":"Evaluation is left to right: "Sum: " + 2 makes a String, then 3 is appended. Use ("Sum: " + (2 + 3)) to get 5."},{"id":"zya2_25_7","topic":"Uppercase transform","text":"first.toUpperCase() with first = "Ann" returns:","options":["ANN, leaving first unchanged","Ann","A compile error","ANN and changes first"],"correct":0,"explain":"Strings are immutable — the method returns a new String. You must assign the result to keep it."},{"id":"zya2_25_8","topic":"Length of a name","text":""Ann".length() returns:","options":["3","4","A compile error","2"],"correct":0,"explain":"length() counts characters. Note it is a method with parentheses for Strings, but a field without them for arrays."},{"id":"zya2_25_9","topic":"First initial","text":"To get the first character of String first, use:","options":["first.charAt(0)","first.charAt(1)","first[0]","first.substring(1)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Indexing starts at 0, and Strings use charAt rather than bracket notation."},{"id":"fs_z2_25_1","topic":"next() leaves the rest of the line","text":"What does this print?

Scanner in = new Scanner("Ann Lee");
String a = in.next();
String rest = in.nextLine();
System.out.println("[" + rest + "]");","options":["[ Lee]","[Lee]","[]","[Ann Lee]"],"correct":0,"explain":"next() consumes only the token Ann and stops at the whitespace. nextLine() then returns everything left on the line - the space and Lee - so the brackets show [ Lee]."},{"id":"fs_z2_25_2","topic":"Variables add flexibility","text":"Compared with a version that hard-codes the two names as literals, reading them with Scanner changes what?","options":["The program now works for any names typed at run time, while the joining logic stays identical","The + operator changes from concatenation to addition","The program can no longer print string literals","The output loses its spaces"],"correct":0,"explain":"Variables do not add capability, they add flexibility: the logic is unchanged, but the values can now come from input instead of from the source code."},{"id":"fs_z2_25_3","topic":"Concatenation adds no spaces","text":"What does this print?

Scanner in = new Scanner("Ann Lee");
String first = in.next();
String last = in.next();
System.out.println("Welcome " + first + last);","options":["Welcome AnnLee","Welcome Ann Lee","Welcome Ann","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The two next() calls read Ann and Lee, and + concatenates exactly what it is given - it never adds a space of its own. Every space in the output must exist as a literal in the code."},{"id":"fs_z2_25_4","topic":"System.in","text":"In the expression new Scanner(System.in), what is System.in?","options":["The standard input stream, typically the keyboard","A file on disk named in","The program's command-line arguments","A source of random numbers"],"correct":0,"explain":"System.in is the standard input stream - normally the keyboard. Wrapping it in a Scanner is how Java programs read typed input as words, lines, and numbers."},{"id":"zf379","topic":"","text":"String first = "Rosa"; String last = "Diaz"; System.out.println(last + ", " + first); prints:","options":["Rosa Diaz","Diaz Rosa","last, first","Diaz, Rosa"],"correct":3,"explain":"The VALUES print in the order written: last, comma-space, first.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf380","topic":"","text":"String a = "Kai"; String b = "Lee"; a = b; System.out.println(a); prints:","options":["Kai","KaiLee","b","Lee"],"correct":3,"explain":"Assignment copies the reference value; a now refers to "Lee".","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf381","topic":"","text":"String name = "Ann"; name = name + " Cho"; System.out.println(name); prints:","options":["Ann Cho","Ann","Cho Ann"," Cho"],"correct":0,"explain":"Concatenation builds a NEW string, which is stored back into name.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf382","topic":"","text":"String w = "Joy"; String h = "Ben"; System.out.println(h + " & " + w); prints:","options":["Joy & Ben","Ben & Joy","Ben&Joy","h & w"],"correct":1,"explain":"h prints first because it is written first; " & " supplies the spacing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf383","topic":"","text":"String couple = "Lin" + "-" + "Park"; System.out.println(couple); prints:","options":["Lin - Park","LinPark","Lin-Park","Lin_Park"],"correct":2,"explain":"The hyphen literal joins the two names with no extra spaces.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf384","topic":"","text":"String s = "Ana"; String t = s; s = "Eva"; System.out.println(t); prints:","options":["Eva","AnaEva","s","Ana"],"correct":3,"explain":"t captured the reference to "Ana"; reassigning s later does not change t.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf385","topic":"","text":"String greeting = "Dear " + "Mr." + " " + "Ito"; System.out.println(greeting); prints:","options":["Dear Mr. Ito","DearMr.Ito","Dear Mr.Ito","Dear Mr Ito"],"correct":0,"explain":"Concatenation keeps exactly the spaces present in the literals.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf386","topic":"","text":"String full = "Mia" + " " + "Ray"; System.out.println(full.length()); prints:","options":["6","7","8","MiaRay"],"correct":1,"explain":""Mia Ray" has 3 + 1 + 3 = 7 characters, counting the space.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf387","topic":"","text":"Storing names in variables (rather than literals everywhere) helps because:","options":["literals cannot contain capital letters","variables print faster","changing one assignment renames every use at once","Java requires it for Strings"],"correct":2,"explain":"One source of truth - the same reason numeric values get variables.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf388","topic":"","text":"String x1 = "Sky"; String y1 = "Lark"; System.out.println(x1 + y1); prints:","options":["Sky Lark","x1y1","Lark Sky","SkyLark"],"correct":3,"explain":"No space was concatenated, so the names run together.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf389","topic":"","text":"String title = "Dr."; String person = "Wu"; System.out.println(title + " " + person + "!"); prints:","options":["Dr. Wu!","Dr.Wu!","Dr. Wu !","title Wu!"],"correct":0,"explain":"The pieces join in order with the single quoted space: Dr. Wu!","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_2":[{"id":"zya3_2_0","topic":"Equality operator","text":"Which operator tests whether two ints are equal?","options":["==","===",".equals()","="],"correct":0,"explain":"A single = assigns. == compares primitives. Java has no === operator."},{"id":"zya3_2_1","topic":"Assignment in a condition","text":"if (x = 5) with int x produces:","options":["A compile error","x becomes 5 and the branch runs","A runtime exception","A test for equality"],"correct":0,"explain":"In Java this is caught at compile time because if requires a boolean. In C it would silently be true — Java closed that hole. The expression is an int, not a boolean."},{"id":"zya3_2_2","topic":"Comparing Strings","text":"String a = "hi", b = new String("hi"); a == b evaluates to:","options":["false","A compile error","Depends on length","true"],"correct":0,"explain":"== compares references. new String makes a distinct object, so the references differ even though the text matches. Use a.equals(b)."},{"id":"zya3_2_3","topic":"equals for Strings","text":"The correct way to test whether two Strings hold the same text is:","options":["s1.equals(s2)","s1.compare(s2)","s1 = s2","s1 == s2"],"correct":0,"explain":"equals compares character by character. compareTo also works but returns an int, not a boolean."},{"id":"zya3_2_4","topic":"Not equal","text":"Which tests that x is NOT equal to 5?","options":["x != 5","!x = 5","x <> 5","x =! 5"],"correct":0,"explain":"!= is the inequality operator. =! would parse as assigning the negation."},{"id":"zya3_2_5","topic":"Case-insensitive comparison","text":"To compare two Strings ignoring case, use:","options":["equalsIgnoreCase","==","compareTo","equals"],"correct":0,"explain":"equalsIgnoreCase handles "YES" matching "yes" without converting either first."},{"id":"zya3_2_6","topic":"Comparing chars","text":"char g = 'A'; if (g == 'A') is:","options":["Valid","Always false","Requires equals()","A compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"char is a primitive, so == is correct. Only reference types need equals. Chars are primitives, so == compares values."},{"id":"zya3_2_7","topic":"Boolean comparison","text":"if (isDone == true) can be simplified to:","options":["if (isDone)","if (isDone = true)","if (!isDone)","if (isDone == 1)"],"correct":0,"explain":"A boolean already is the condition. Comparing it to true adds nothing, and the second option is an assignment bug."},{"id":"zya3_2_8","topic":"Equality with doubles","text":"if (0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3) evaluates to:","options":["false","A compile error","Sometimes true","true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Binary floating-point rounding makes the sum slightly off. Compare with a tolerance instead."},{"id":"zya3_2_9","topic":"Null comparison","text":"To check whether String s is null, write:","options":["s == null","s = null","s.isNull()","s.equals(null)"],"correct":0,"explain":"s.equals(null) would throw NullPointerException when s is actually null. == null is the safe test."},{"id":"fs_z3_2_1","topic":"equals() on Strings","text":"What does this print?

String p = "yes";
String q = new String("yes");
System.out.println(p.equals(q));","options":["true","false","Compiler error","yes"],"correct":0,"explain":"equals() compares contents, and both Strings hold the characters "yes", so it returns true. == would return false here because new String creates a separate object, but equals ignores identity and looks only at the characters."},{"id":"fs_z3_2_2","topic":"== vs equals with runtime concatenation","text":"What does this print?

String s1 = "cat";
String part = "ca";
String s2 = part + "t";
System.out.println((s1 == s2) + " " + s1.equals(s2));","options":["false true","true true","true false","false false"],"correct":0,"explain":"Concatenation performed at run time builds a new String object outside the literal pool, so s1 == s2 compares two different references and is false. equals() compares the characters, which match, so it is true."},{"id":"fs_z3_2_3","topic":"String pool","text":"Two String variables are both assigned the literal "cat", and comparing them with == returns true. Why does == give the right answer in this case?","options":["Java keeps identical string literals in a shared pool, so both variables refer to the same object","== automatically calls equals() when both operands are Strings","The compiler converts == to a character-by-character comparison for literals","Strings are primitives in Java, so == always compares their characters"],"correct":0,"explain":"Java stores identical string literals in a shared pool, so two literals with the same characters are usually the same object and the reference comparison happens to succeed. The moment a String comes from input, run-time concatenation, or new, it is a different object and == silently returns false — which is why .equals() is the rule."},{"id":"fs_z3_2_4","topic":"== fails on input Strings","text":"A program reads a String from a Scanner and compares it to a literal with ==. The comparison returns false even though the user typed exactly the same characters. What is the reason?","options":["A String read from input is a different object from the literal, and == compares references, not characters","Scanner adds a hidden newline character to the end of every String it reads","String literals are stored in uppercase internally, so the cases never match","== only works on Strings shorter than 16 characters"],"correct":0,"explain":"== on objects asks "are these the same object in memory". A String built from input is never the pooled literal object, so the reference comparison is false regardless of the characters. Content comparison requires equals()."},{"id":"zf403","topic":"","text":"int a = 5; int b = 5; if (a == b) System.out.println("match"); else System.out.println("differ"); prints:","options":["differ","a == b","true","match"],"correct":3,"explain":"5 == 5 is true.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf404","topic":"","text":"int a = 8; int b = 3; if (a == b) System.out.println("match"); else System.out.println("differ"); prints:","options":["differ","match","a == b","true"],"correct":0,"explain":"8 == 3 is false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf405","topic":"","text":"The equality comparison operator in Java is:","options":["=","==","===","equals for ints"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two equals signs compare; one assigns.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf406","topic":"","text":"if (x = 5) instead of if (x == 5) causes:","options":["a comparison anyway","an infinite loop","a compile error for an int x","a runtime warning"],"correct":2,"explain":"x = 5 has value 5 (an int), and if() demands a boolean - Java catches this classic typo.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf407","topic":"","text":"The operator != means:","options":["equal to","assignment","greater than","not equal to"],"correct":3,"explain":"a != b is true exactly when the values differ.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf408","topic":"","text":"int code = 7; System.out.println(code != 7); prints:","options":["false","true","7","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"code IS 7, so "not equal to 7" is false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf409","topic":"","text":"Comparing two doubles with == is risky because:","options":["doubles cannot use ==","rounding can make near-equals unequal","== rounds the values first","it throws an exception"],"correct":1,"explain":"Use a tolerance: Math.abs(a - b) < epsilon.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf410","topic":"","text":"For two int variables, x == y evaluates to:","options":["an int - 0 or 1","a boolean - true or false","a String","the larger value"],"correct":1,"explain":"Comparisons produce booleans, which is what branches consume.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf411","topic":"","text":"int pin = 4321; int entry = 4312; System.out.println(pin == entry); prints:","options":["true","4321","false","0"],"correct":2,"explain":"The digits are transposed - the values differ.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf412","topic":"","text":"Checking that BOTH a equals b AND b equals c requires:","options":["a == b == c","a && b == c","a = b = c","a == b && b == c"],"correct":3,"explain":"Chained == like a == b == c does not compile for ints; combine two comparisons with &&.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf413","topic":"","text":"char ch = 'y'; if (ch == 'y') System.out.println("yes"); prints:","options":["yes","nothing","y","error - chars need equals()"],"correct":0,"explain":"Primitive chars compare correctly with ==.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_3":[{"id":"zya3_3_0","topic":"Range test","text":"Which tests that x is between 10 and 20 inclusive?","options":["if (x >= 10 || x <= 20)","if (x > 10 & x < 20)","if (10 <= x <= 20)","if (x >= 10 && x <= 20)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java cannot chain comparisons; each side needs its own operator, joined by &&. Using || would be true for every number."},{"id":"zya3_3_1","topic":"Inclusive vs exclusive","text":"To include the boundary value 10, use:","options":["x != 10","x < 10","x > 10","x >= 10"],"correct":3,"explain":">= includes the endpoint; > excludes it. This distinction is where most range bugs live."},{"id":"zya3_3_2","topic":"Wrong operator effect","text":"if (x >= 10 || x &lt;= 20) is true for:","options":["No values","Only negatives","Only 10 to 20","Every possible value of x"],"correct":3,"explain":"Every number is either at least 10 or at most 20, so the OR is always true — a very common slip."},{"id":"zya3_3_3","topic":"Outside a range","text":"Which is true when x is outside 10 to 20?","options":["!(x < 10 || x > 20)","x <= 10 || x >= 20","x < 10 && x > 20","x < 10 || x > 20"],"correct":3,"explain":"Nothing is simultaneously below 10 and above 20, so && can never be true here."},{"id":"zya3_3_4","topic":"Grade boundary","text":"A score of exactly 90 should be an A when the rule is "90 and above". The test is:","options":["score < 90","score == 90","score > 90","score >= 90"],"correct":3,"explain":"Using > would give 90 a B — the classic off-by-one at a boundary."},{"id":"zya3_3_5","topic":"Negative range","text":"Which tests that t is between -5 and 5 inclusive?","options":["t >= 5 && t <= -5","t > -5 || t < 5","Math.abs(t) > 5","t >= -5 && t <= 5"],"correct":3,"explain":"Alternatively Math.abs(t) <= 5 expresses the same thing."},{"id":"zya3_3_6","topic":"Order of bounds","text":"if (x >= 20 && x &lt;= 10) is:","options":["Always true","Valid for x = 15","A compile error","Always false"],"correct":3,"explain":"It compiles fine, which makes reversed bounds a silent logic error rather than a caught one. The bounds are reversed."},{"id":"zya3_3_7","topic":"Half-open range","text":"Which matches "10 up to but not including 20"?","options":["x > 10 && x < 20","x > 10 && x <= 20","x >= 10 && x <= 20","x >= 10 && x < 20"],"correct":3,"explain":"Include the lower bound, exclude the upper — the same convention as substring and array indices."},{"id":"zya3_3_8","topic":"Range with a variable bound","text":"To test that x is within tolerance tol of target, use:","options":["x - target <= tol","x > target - tol","x == target","Math.abs(x - target) <= tol"],"correct":3,"explain":"Absolute difference covers both directions in one test."},{"id":"fs_z3_3_1","topic":"Boundary values","text":"What does this print?

int age = 18;
if (age > 18) {
    System.out.println("adult");
} else {
    System.out.println("not yet");
}","options":["adult","adult
not yet","No output","not yet"],"correct":3,"explain":"The operator is >, which excludes the boundary: 18 > 18 is false, so the else branch runs. "Over 18" is > 18, while "at least 18" would be >= 18 — the boundary value itself is where the two differ."},{"id":"fs_z3_3_2","topic":"Chained comparison error","text":"Why does the expression 0 <= x <= 100 fail to compile in Java?","options":["<= cannot appear twice in the same statement","It compiles, but always evaluates to false","It parses as (0 <= x) <= 100","Java requires the variable to appear first, as in x >= 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java has no mathematical chained comparison. The expression groups as (0 <= x) <= 100, and the left side produces a boolean, which cannot be compared to the int 100. The correct form is two comparisons joined by &&: 0 <= x && x <= 100. Compares a boolean with an int."},{"id":"fs_z3_3_3","topic":"Inclusive range check","text":"What does this print?

int score = 70;
System.out.println(score >= 70 && score <= 100);","options":["true","false","70","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both bounds use inclusive operators, so the boundary value 70 satisfies score >= 70, and 70 <= 100 is also true. && of two true comparisons is true."},{"id":"fs_z3_3_4","topic":"Wording to operator","text":"Museum entry is free for children under 5 years old. Which condition correctly tests for free entry?","options":["age >= 5","age > 5","age < 5","age <= 5"],"correct":2,"explain":""Under 5" excludes the boundary: a child who is exactly 5 pays. That is age < 5. Testing the boundary value is the reliable check — at exactly age 5, < gives false (pays) which matches the wording, while <= would wrongly give free entry."},{"id":"fs_z3_3_5","topic":"Half-open range boundary","text":"What does this print?

int t = 20;
if (t >= 10 && t < 20) {
    System.out.println("in range");
} else {
    System.out.println("out of range");
}","options":["out of range","in range","in range
out of range","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The upper bound uses <, an exclusive bound, so 20 < 20 is false and the whole && is false. The value 20 sits exactly on the excluded boundary, so the else branch runs."},{"id":"zf414","topic":"","text":"int v = 15; if (v >= 10 && v <= 20) System.out.println("in"); else System.out.println("out"); prints:","options":["out","in","in out","v"],"correct":1,"explain":"15 is between 10 and 20 inclusive.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf415","topic":"","text":"int v = 25; if (v >= 10 && v <= 20) System.out.println("in"); else System.out.println("out"); prints:","options":["in","in out","out","v"],"correct":2,"explain":"25 is not between 10 and 20 inclusive.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf416","topic":"","text":"int v = 10; if (v >= 10 && v <= 20) System.out.println("in"); else System.out.println("out"); prints:","options":["out","in out","v","in"],"correct":3,"explain":"10 is between 10 and 20 inclusive.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf417","topic":"","text":"The mathematical range 10 <= x <= 20 is written in Java as:","options":["x >= 10 && x <= 20","10 <= x <= 20","x >= 10 || x <= 20","x <= 10 && x >= 20"],"correct":0,"explain":"Java has no chained comparisons; combine two tests with &&.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf418","topic":"","text":"Why does 10 <= x <= 20 fail to compile in Java?","options":["20 is too large","a boolean cannot be compared to 20","<= is not a Java operator","x must be a double"],"correct":1,"explain":"boolean <= int is meaningless to the compiler.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf419","topic":"","text":"x >= 10 || x <= 20 as a "between" test is wrong because:","options":["it is false for every value","|| is not allowed with >=","it is true for EVERY value of x","it only works for doubles"],"correct":2,"explain":"Any number is >= 10 or <= 20 (or both) - the OR makes the test useless.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf420","topic":"","text":"A test for x OUTSIDE the range 1..100 is:","options":["x < 1 && x > 100","x >= 1 || x <= 100","x < 1 || x > 100","x != 100"],"correct":2,"explain":"Outside means below the bottom OR above the top; both at once is impossible, so && would always be false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf421","topic":"","text":"An inclusive range check uses >= and <=; an EXCLUSIVE one (endpoints not allowed) uses:","options":["== and !=",">= and <=","only ==","> and <"],"correct":3,"explain":"Strict inequalities shut out the endpoints themselves.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf422","topic":"","text":"int temp = 99; if (temp >= 97 && temp <= 99) System.out.println("normal"); prints:","options":["normal","nothing","fever","99"],"correct":0,"explain":"99 meets both tests - the top endpoint is included by <=.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf423","topic":"","text":"Overlapping if-else-if ranges (like x > 50 then x > 90) should be ordered:","options":["widest condition first always","narrowest condition first","randomly","alphabetically"],"correct":1,"explain":"If x > 50 comes first, x = 95 never reaches the x > 90 branch.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf424","topic":"","text":"int grade = 85; if (grade >= 90) System.out.println("A"); else if (grade >= 80) System.out.println("B"); else System.out.println("C"); prints:","options":["A","C","B","AB"],"correct":2,"explain":"85 fails the A test but passes >= 80, so B prints and the chain ends.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_4":[{"id":"zya3_4_0","topic":"Grade chain order","text":"For a grading chain, if-else-if conditions should be ordered:","options":["From the least restrictive boundary upward","In alphabetical order of the grade letters","From the most restrictive boundary downward","In any order, since only one branch can match"],"correct":2,"explain":"Checking >= 60 first would catch every passing score, so 90 would never reach the A branch. Test the highest threshold first."},{"id":"zya3_4_1","topic":"Chain evaluation","text":"In an if-else-if chain, how many branches execute?","options":["All of them","All that are true","At most one","Exactly one always"],"correct":2,"explain":"Once a branch is taken the rest are skipped, which is what makes ordering matter. The first true condition."},{"id":"zya3_4_2","topic":"Redundant condition","text":"After if (s >= 90), the next branch can simply test:","options":["s > 80","s >= 80 && s < 90","s >= 80","s < 90"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reaching the second branch already proves s < 90, so restating it is redundant."},{"id":"zya3_4_3","topic":"else placement","text":"The final else in a chain handles:","options":["The first condition, re-checked as a safeguard","Only the cases where the input is invalid","Nothing; it exists purely for readability","Every case not matched by any earlier condition"],"correct":3,"explain":"It is the catch-all, and including one prevents silent no-ops when input falls outside expectations."},{"id":"zya3_4_4","topic":"Missing else","text":"With no final else and no condition matching, the program:","options":["Throws an exception at the end of the chain","Repeats the chain until a condition matches","Does nothing and continues after the chain","Fails to compile without a final else branch"],"correct":2,"explain":"Silently doing nothing is often the bug — the value simply never gets set."},{"id":"zya3_4_5","topic":"Independent ifs vs chain","text":"Replacing else if with separate if statements means:","options":["A compile error","Identical behaviour always","Every condition is tested","Faster execution"],"correct":2,"explain":"For mutually exclusive ranges the result may look the same, but each condition is still evaluated — and if the branches modify the tested variable, behaviour changes. More than one branch can run."},{"id":"zya3_4_6","topic":"Nested vs chained","text":"Deeply nested ifs are usually rewritten as an else-if chain because:","options":["Nesting causes runtime errors","They run faster","They are flatter and easier to read","Nesting is illegal"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reducing nesting depth is one of the cheapest readability wins."},{"id":"zya3_4_7","topic":"Boundary check","text":"With chain s>=90 A, s>=80 B, s>=70 C, a score of 80 gets:","options":["Nothing","A","B","C"],"correct":2,"explain":"80 fails >= 90 and passes >= 80."},{"id":"zya3_4_8","topic":"Chain with equal thresholds","text":"If two branches have the same condition, the second:","options":["Runs first","Overrides the first","Is unreachable","Causes a compile error"],"correct":2,"explain":"The first true branch wins, so the duplicate never executes — logically dead code."},{"id":"zya3_4_9","topic":"Category assignment","text":"Assigning exactly one category from ranges is best expressed with:","options":["Nested ternaries only","Multiple independent if statements","An if-else-if chain","A while loop"],"correct":2,"explain":"A chain makes mutual exclusivity structural rather than something you must maintain by hand."},{"id":"fs_z3_4_1","topic":"Misordered else-if chain","text":"What does this print?

int score = 95;
if (score >= 70) {
    System.out.println("C");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else {
    System.out.println("F");
}","options":["A
B
C","F","C","A"],"correct":2,"explain":"The first true condition wins and the rest are never tested. Since 95 >= 70 is true, C prints — even though 95 also satisfies the later conditions. The chain is ordered least restrictive first, which is the classic silent logic error; it must run from most restrictive (>= 90) down."},{"id":"fs_z3_4_2","topic":"Tracing a correct chain","text":"What does this print?

int w = 12;
if (w >= 20) {
    System.out.println("heavy");
} else if (w >= 10) {
    System.out.println("medium");
} else {
    System.out.println("light");
}","options":["light","medium
light","medium","heavy"],"correct":2,"explain":"12 >= 20 is false, so the chain moves on; 12 >= 10 is true, so "medium" prints and the remaining branches are skipped. Exactly one branch of an if-else-if chain executes."},{"id":"fs_z3_4_3","topic":"Implied upper bound","text":"In the chain below, why does the second condition not need to test score < 90?

if (score >= 90) { grade = 'A'; }
else if (score >= 80) { grade = 'B'; }","options":["The condition score >= 80 implies score < 90 mathematically","It does need the test; without it, scores of 95 get a B","The else if is only reached when score >= 90 was false","Java automatically adds the missing upper bound to every else if"],"correct":2,"explain":"In an else-if chain each later condition carries hidden context: it runs only when every earlier condition was false. Reaching score >= 80 means score >= 90 already failed, so the upper bound is implied and restating it is redundant. Score < 90 is already known."},{"id":"fs_z3_4_4","topic":"Chain order as a correctness issue","text":"A grading chain is written lowest bound first: it tests score >= 70 first, then score >= 80, then score >= 90. What is the consequence?","options":["It compiles and runs, but every score of 70 or above gets the grade for the >= 70 branch","It fails to compile because the conditions overlap","It throws a runtime exception when a score above 90 is entered","It behaves identically to the correctly ordered chain"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first true condition wins, and score >= 70 is true for every passing score, so the later branches become unreachable in practice. Nothing crashes and nothing is reported — it is a pure logic error, which is why chains must be ordered from most restrictive to least."},{"id":"zf425","topic":"","text":"int amt = 120; if (amt >= 100) System.out.println("high"); else if (amt >= 50) System.out.println("mid"); else System.out.println("low"); prints:","options":["mid","low","nothing is printed","high"],"correct":3,"explain":"120 lands in the high bracket; earlier tests decide before later ones are reached.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf426","topic":"","text":"int amt = 75; if (amt >= 100) System.out.println("high"); else if (amt >= 50) System.out.println("mid"); else System.out.println("low"); prints:","options":["mid","high","low","nothing is printed"],"correct":0,"explain":"75 lands in the mid bracket; earlier tests decide before later ones are reached.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf427","topic":"","text":"int amt = 30; if (amt >= 100) System.out.println("high"); else if (amt >= 50) System.out.println("mid"); else System.out.println("low"); prints:","options":["high","low","mid","nothing is printed"],"correct":1,"explain":"30 lands in the low bracket; earlier tests decide before later ones are reached.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf428","topic":"","text":"In an if-else-if ladder testing amt >= 100 then amt >= 50, the second test runs only when:","options":["amt is at least 100","amt is exactly 50","amt is less than 100","never"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reaching else-if means the first condition already failed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf429","topic":"","text":"int speed = 65; if (speed > 65) System.out.println("ticket"); else System.out.println("ok"); prints:","options":["ticket","65","nothing","ok"],"correct":3,"explain":"speed is not GREATER than 65 - the boundary case falls to else.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf430","topic":"","text":"To make exactly 65 also earn a ticket, the test becomes:","options":["speed > 65","speed == 65 only","speed != 65","speed >= 65"],"correct":3,"explain":">= includes the boundary value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf431","topic":"","text":"A shipping fee is 0 for orders of 50 or more, else 8. Which code is right?","options":["if (order >= 50) fee = 0; else fee = 8;","if (order > 50) fee = 0; else fee = 8;","if (order >= 50) fee = 8; else fee = 0;","fee = 8;"],"correct":0,"explain":""50 or more" needs >=, and the branches must assign the right way around.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf432","topic":"","text":"int bmiClass = 27; if (bmiClass >= 30) System.out.println("obese"); else if (bmiClass >= 25) System.out.println("overweight"); else System.out.println("normal or below"); prints:","options":["obese","overweight","normal or below","27"],"correct":1,"explain":"27 fails the first test and passes the second.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf433","topic":"","text":"Ranges in a well-built ladder should be:","options":["overlapping for safety","defined only for expected values","non-overlapping, covering every value","in random order"],"correct":2,"explain":"Gaps drop values into the wrong branch; the final else catches everything left.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf434","topic":"","text":"The final bare else in a ladder acts as:","options":["a syntax decoration","a repeat of the first branch","an error handler","the catch-all for unmatched values"],"correct":3,"explain":"It guarantees exactly one branch always runs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf435","topic":"","text":"int score2 = 59; if (score2 >= 60) System.out.println("pass"); else System.out.println("fail"); prints:","options":["fail","pass","59","pass fail"],"correct":0,"explain":"One point short: 59 >= 60 is false.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_5":[{"id":"zya3_5_0","topic":"AND operator","text":"The Java logical AND operator is:","options":["and","&&","&|","||"],"correct":1,"explain":"&& is logical AND for booleans. A single & is the bitwise/non-short-circuit form."},{"id":"zya3_5_1","topic":"OR truth","text":"true || false evaluates to:","options":["null","true","false","A compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"OR is true when at least one operand is true."},{"id":"zya3_5_2","topic":"NOT operator","text":"!(x > 5) is equivalent to:","options":["x > 5","x <= 5","x < 5","x >= 5"],"correct":1,"explain":"Negating "greater than" gives "less than or equal to" — the boundary flips sides."},{"id":"zya3_5_3","topic":"AND truth table","text":"For && to be true:","options":["At least one operand is true","Both operands must be true","Neither is true","Exactly one is true"],"correct":1,"explain":"Both. Exactly-one is XOR, written ^ for booleans."},{"id":"zya3_5_4","topic":"De Morgan","text":"!(a && b) is equivalent to:","options":["!a && !b","!a || !b","a || b","!(a || b)"],"correct":1,"explain":"De Morgan's law: negating an AND turns it into an OR of the negations."},{"id":"zya3_5_5","topic":"Combining conditions","text":""Age at least 18 AND has a licence" is written:","options":["age >= 18 || hasLicence","age >= 18 && hasLicence","age >= 18 && hasLicence == true","!(age < 18)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Both requirements must hold. Comparing a boolean to true is redundant."},{"id":"zya3_5_6","topic":"Precedence of && and ||","text":"In a || b && c, which operator is evaluated first?","options":["||","&&","Left to right regardless","They have equal precedence"],"correct":1,"explain":"&& binds tighter than ||, so it reads as a || (b && c). Parentheses make intent explicit."},{"id":"fs_z3_5_1","topic":"&& binds tighter than ||","text":"What does this print?

boolean r = true || false && false;
System.out.println(r);","options":["Compiler error","true false","true","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"&& binds tighter than ||, so the expression is true || (false && false). The && part is false, but true || false is true. Reading it left to right in English order — (true || false) && false — would wrongly give false, which is exactly why mixed conditions deserve parentheses."},{"id":"fs_z3_5_2","topic":"! operator","text":"What does this print?

int a = 5;
int b = 10;
System.out.println(!(a < b));","options":["false","true","5","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"a < b is 5 < 10, which is true, and ! flips it to false. The comparison inside the parentheses evaluates first, then NOT inverts the resulting boolean."},{"id":"fs_z3_5_3","topic":"De Morgan with relational operators","text":"Which condition is equivalent to !(x >= 0 && x <= 9)?","options":["!(x < 0) || !(x > 9)","x < 0 || x > 9","x < 0 && x > 9","x <= 0 || x >= 9"],"correct":1,"explain":"De Morgan's law turns !(a && b) into !a || !b: the operator flips along with each side. Negating x >= 0 gives x < 0 and negating x <= 9 gives x > 9, joined by ||. Keeping && while negating the sides is the standard wrong answer."},{"id":"fs_z3_5_4","topic":"Combining boolean variables","text":"What does this print?

int age = 15;
boolean isTeen = age >= 13 && age <= 19;
boolean isWeekend = false;
System.out.println(isTeen && !isWeekend);","options":["15","true","false","Compiler error"],"correct":1,"explain":"15 is between 13 and 19, so isTeen is true. isWeekend is false, so !isWeekend is true, and true && true is true. ! binds tightest, so it applies only to isWeekend before the && combines the two values."},{"id":"fs_z3_5_5","topic":"Logical operator precedence","text":"Which list gives the logical operators from highest precedence (binds first) to lowest?","options":["!, ||, &&","!, &&, ||","||, &&, !","&&, ||, !"],"correct":1,"explain":"NOT binds tightest, then AND, then OR. That is why a || b && c means a || (b && c) and why !flag && ready negates only flag. When && and || mix, parenthesize anyway — a reader parsing in English order will get it wrong."},{"id":"fs_z3_5_6","topic":"Negating a compound OR","text":"The condition !(x == 0 || y == 0) is true exactly when:","options":["exactly one of x and y is zero","both x and y are nonzero","either x or y is nonzero","both x and y are zero"],"correct":1,"explain":"By De Morgan, !(a || b) is !a && !b, so the condition becomes x != 0 && y != 0: neither value may be zero. Choosing "either is nonzero" comes from forgetting to flip || to && during the negation."},{"id":"zf436","topic":"","text":"boolean p = true; boolean q = false; System.out.println(p && q); prints:","options":["true","false","p && q","1"],"correct":1,"explain":"AND needs both true; here it is false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf437","topic":"","text":"boolean p = false; boolean q = false; System.out.println(p && q); prints:","options":["error","p && q","false","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"AND needs both true; here it is false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf438","topic":"","text":"The && operator yields true when:","options":["either operand is true","exactly one operand is true","both are false","both operands are true"],"correct":3,"explain":"AND is strict: one false operand sinks it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf439","topic":"","text":"The || operator yields false ONLY when:","options":["both operands are false","either operand is false","both are true","the left is false"],"correct":0,"explain":"OR succeeds on any true; it fails only with two falses.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf440","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(true || false); prints:","options":["true","false","1","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"One true operand is enough for OR.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf441","topic":"","text":"The ! operator:","options":["compares two values","flips a boolean - !true is false","assigns false","ends the program"],"correct":1,"explain":"NOT inverts its single operand.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf442","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(!(4 > 2)); prints:","options":["true","4","false","2"],"correct":2,"explain":"4 > 2 is true; ! flips it to false.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf443","topic":"","text":"int m = 8; System.out.println(m > 5 && m < 12); prints:","options":["false","8","5 && 12","true"],"correct":3,"explain":"Both comparisons hold: 8 is above 5 and below 12.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf444","topic":"","text":"int n = 15; System.out.println(n < 5 || n > 12); prints:","options":["true","false","15","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The left test fails but the right succeeds - OR needs just one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf445","topic":"","text":"A theme-park ride requires height 48 to 76 inches. The admission test is:","options":["h >= 48 || h <= 76","h >= 48 && h <= 76","h > 48 || h < 76","h == 48 && h == 76"],"correct":1,"explain":"Inside-a-range means BOTH ends must hold: AND.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf446","topic":"","text":"!(a && b) is logically the same as:","options":["!a && !b","a || b","!a || !b","a && b"],"correct":2,"explain":"De Morgan's law: negating an AND flips it to an OR of negations.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf447","topic":"","text":"boolean ok = 5 > 3 && 2 > 7; System.out.println(ok); prints:","options":["true","5 > 3","2","false"],"correct":3,"explain":"The second comparison (2 > 7) is false, so the AND is false.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_6":[{"id":"zya3_6_0","topic":"Range with a gap","text":"To match x in 1-10 or 20-30, use:","options":["(x>=1 && x<=10) || (x>=20 && x<=30)","x>=1 && x<=30","x>=1 || x<=30","(x>=1 && x<=10) && (x>=20 && x<=30)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each interval is an AND; the alternatives are joined with OR. Nothing can be in both intervals at once, so && gives always-false."},{"id":"zya3_6_1","topic":"Excluding a middle range","text":"To match everything except 10-20, use:","options":["x < 10 || x > 20","!(x < 10 || x > 20)","x <= 10 && x >= 20","x < 10 && x > 20"],"correct":0,"explain":"Below the range or above it. The AND version is impossible to satisfy."},{"id":"zya3_6_2","topic":"Parenthesizing gaps","text":"Why parenthesize each interval?","options":["Because && binds tighter than ||","It runs faster","To avoid runtime errors","Java requires it"],"correct":0,"explain":"Without parentheses the expression still parses, but reading it correctly requires knowing the precedence rules by heart. Grouping makes intent explicit and safe."},{"id":"zya3_6_3","topic":"Three ranges","text":"Matching 1-5, 10-15 or 20-25 requires:","options":["Two && operators joining three OR groups","Three || operators joining three single tests","Two || operators joining three AND groups","One || operator joining two AND groups"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each interval is an AND-pair, and n intervals need n-1 ORs."},{"id":"zya3_6_4","topic":"Gap boundary","text":"With ranges 1-10 and 20-30, the value 15 should:","options":["Match neither range","Match both","Cause an error","Match the first range"],"correct":0,"explain":"15 sits in the gap, which is exactly what a gap test must handle correctly."},{"id":"zya3_6_5","topic":"Off-by-one at a gap","text":"Ranges 1-10 and 11-20 written as (x>=1&&x&lt;=10)||(x>=11&&x&lt;=20) leave out:","options":["Nothing","The value 10","The value 11","Values above 20 only"],"correct":0,"explain":"The intervals meet exactly, so no integer falls between them. Using x<10 in the first would have dropped 10. They are contiguous."},{"id":"zya3_6_6","topic":"Simplifying with a helper","text":"Testing several disjoint ranges repeatedly is best handled by:","options":["A boolean method such as inRange(x, lo, hi)","A longer if chain","Nested loops","Copying the condition everywhere"],"correct":0,"explain":"A named boolean method makes the intent readable and fixes a bug in one place."},{"id":"zya3_6_7","topic":"Character ranges","text":"To test whether char c is a digit without a library call:","options":["c >= '0' && c <= '9'","c == "0-9"","Character.isDigit(c) only","c >= 0 && c <= 9"],"correct":0,"explain":"Comparing to char literals works because chars compare by their code values. Character.isDigit is the clearer choice in practice."},{"id":"zya3_6_8","topic":"Leap-year style gaps","text":"Conditions with several ORs and ANDs are clearest when:","options":["Written on one line so the whole test is visible","Rewritten to remove every pair of parentheses","Broken across lines with parentheses grouping each idea","Replaced by a switch statement on the same value"],"correct":2,"explain":"Formatting and grouping are how complex boolean logic stays reviewable."},{"id":"zya3_6_9","topic":"Testing gap logic","text":"The most important test inputs for a gap condition are:","options":["Only values well inside each accepted range","The boundary values and one value inside the gap","A large number of random values across the range","Only the smallest and largest possible values"],"correct":1,"explain":"Boundaries and the gap itself are where the logic errors are."},{"id":"fs_z3_6_1","topic":"Two accepted bands","text":"What does this print?

int n = 25;
if ((n >= 1 && n <= 10) || (n >= 20 && n <= 30)) {
    System.out.println("accepted");
} else {
    System.out.println("rejected");
}","options":["accepted","rejected","accepted
rejected","No output"],"correct":0,"explain":"25 fails the first band (25 <= 10 is false) but satisfies the second: 25 >= 20 and 25 <= 30 are both true. One true side is enough for ||, so "accepted" prints."},{"id":"fs_z3_6_2","topic":"What the else covers","text":"A program accepts values in 1-10 or 20-30 with one compound if, and everything else falls to the else branch. Which values does that else handle?","options":["The gap 11-19, plus everything below 1 and everything above 30","Only the gap between the bands, 11-19","Only values below 1 and above 30","Nothing; a value in the gap matches neither branch and is skipped"],"correct":0,"explain":"The else is the complement of the accepted set, so it catches the gap and both outer regions at once. That is why expressing the accepted bands and letting else handle the remainder is complete by construction, while enumerating rejected values invites holes."},{"id":"fs_z3_6_3","topic":"Negating a band","text":"What does this print?

int n = 15;
System.out.println(!(n >= 11 && n <= 19));","options":["false","true","15","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"15 is inside 11-19, so n >= 11 && n <= 19 is true, and ! flips it to false. The expression as a whole matches everything except 11-19, and 15 is exactly what it excludes."},{"id":"fs_z3_6_4","topic":"Accepted set vs rejected set","text":"When valid input is two bands with a gap between them, why is it safer to write the condition for the accepted bands rather than enumerating the rejected values?","options":[""Not accepted" needs no enumeration","Conditions describing rejected values do not compile in Java","Enumerating rejected values is slower at runtime","The accepted-band form avoids needing any logical operators"],"correct":0,"explain":"Describing the smaller, well-defined accepted set makes the rejection complete by construction — the else is its exact complement. Enumerating rejections is where holes appear: it is easy to cover 11-19 and forget everything above 30. A single else covers the gap and both outer regions, so no values can be missed."},{"id":"zf448","topic":"","text":"int ch = 4; if ((ch >= 1 && ch <= 5) || (ch >= 8 && ch <= 10)) System.out.println("valid"); else System.out.println("invalid"); prints:","options":["valid","invalid","4","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"4 falls inside one of the two windows (1-5 and 8-10).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf449","topic":"","text":"int ch = 6; if ((ch >= 1 && ch <= 5) || (ch >= 8 && ch <= 10)) System.out.println("valid"); else System.out.println("invalid"); prints:","options":["valid","invalid","6","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"6 sits in the gap between the windows (1-5 and 8-10).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf450","topic":"","text":"int ch = 9; if ((ch >= 1 && ch <= 5) || (ch >= 8 && ch <= 10)) System.out.println("valid"); else System.out.println("invalid"); prints:","options":["invalid","valid","9","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"9 falls inside one of the two windows (1-5 and 8-10).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf451","topic":"","text":"Accepting values in TWO separate ranges (like 1-5 or 8-10) requires:","options":["two range tests joined with &&","a single >= test","two range tests joined with ||","the switch keyword only"],"correct":2,"explain":"A value belongs to the union if it is in the first range OR the second.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf452","topic":"","text":"Joining two disjoint range tests with && instead of || makes the condition:","options":["always true","identical to ||","a compile error","always false"],"correct":3,"explain":"Disjoint ranges share no values, so the AND can never hold.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf453","topic":"","text":"Free shipping applies to orders under 10 dollars or over 100. The test is:","options":["cost < 10 || cost > 100","cost < 10 && cost > 100","cost >= 10 || cost <= 100","cost != 50"],"correct":0,"explain":"Two disconnected intervals - OR them together.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf454","topic":"","text":"int day = 6; if (day == 6 || day == 7) System.out.println("weekend"); else System.out.println("weekday"); prints:","options":["weekday","weekend","6","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"Day 6 matches the first alternative.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf455","topic":"","text":"Parenthesizing each range, as in (x >= 1 && x <= 5) || (x >= 8 && x <= 10):","options":["is required or it will not compile","changes the result","groups the tests clearly","slows the check"],"correct":2,"explain":"The parentheses match how && and || bind anyway; they make the two windows visible.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf456","topic":"","text":"A gap check for teens EXCLUDING exactly 15: age >= 13 && age <= 19 && age != 15 accepts:","options":["every teen including 15","only 15","13 through 19 and 15","13, 14, 16, 17, 18, 19"],"correct":3,"explain":"The extra != carves one value out of the range.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf457","topic":"","text":"int floor2 = 13; if (floor2 >= 1 && floor2 <= 12 || floor2 >= 14 && floor2 <= 20) System.out.println("served"); else System.out.println("skipped"); prints:","options":["skipped","served","13","1420"],"correct":0,"explain":"A building without a 13th floor: 13 is in neither window.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf458","topic":"","text":"Which value makes (x >= 10 && x <= 20) || x == 25 true?","options":["22","25","9","21"],"correct":1,"explain":"25 misses the range but hits the extra allowed value.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_7":[{"id":"zya3_7_0","topic":"Multiple independent features","text":"Checking three independent optional features is best done with:","options":["A single switch","A ternary","An if-else-if chain","Three separate if statements"],"correct":3,"explain":"A chain stops at the first match, so it cannot report more than one feature. Independent conditions need independent ifs."},{"id":"zya3_7_1","topic":"Chain vs separate ifs","text":"Using else if when features are independent causes:","options":["All features to be handled twice","Slower code","A compile error","Only the first matching feature to be handled"],"correct":3,"explain":"This is the core distinction the section tests: chain means "choose one", separate ifs mean "check each"."},{"id":"zya3_7_2","topic":"Counting matched features","text":"To count how many of several conditions hold:","options":["Use separate ifs, each incrementing a counter","Use an if-else-if chain, incrementing once","Use a switch on the combined boolean value","Use nested ifs so each depends on the last"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each condition must be evaluated for the count to be right."},{"id":"zya3_7_3","topic":"Combining requirements","text":""Must have A and B but not C" is written:","options":["a && b || !c","!(a && b && c)","a || b || !c","a && b && !c"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each requirement is joined with &&, and the exclusion is negated."},{"id":"zya3_7_4","topic":"Optional feature default","text":"When no feature condition matches and there is no else:","options":["The first branch runs","The program stops","An exception is thrown","Nothing happens for that feature"],"correct":3,"explain":"Which is correct behaviour here — no feature means nothing to do."},{"id":"zya3_7_5","topic":"Accumulating a description","text":"Building a description like "large red round" from three flags uses:","options":["An if-else-if chain appending only one word","Separate ifs each appending to a String","A switch statement on the three flags at once","Nested ifs so each word requires the previous"],"correct":1,"explain":"Each attribute is independent, so each gets its own test and its own append."},{"id":"zya3_7_6","topic":"Order independence","text":"With separate independent ifs, changing their order:","options":["Does not change behaviour, unless they share mutable state","Always changes behaviour, since order is significant","Causes a compile error if the order is altered","Changes behaviour only when the conditions overlap"],"correct":0,"explain":"That independence is exactly what distinguishes them from a chain — but it fails if one branch alters a variable another tests."},{"id":"zya3_7_7","topic":"Nested feature check","text":"Checking a sub-option only when a main option is on is expressed by:","options":["An else","A while loop","Two separate ifs","A nested if inside the main if"],"correct":3,"explain":"Nesting encodes the dependency: the inner test only matters when the outer one holds."},{"id":"zya3_7_8","topic":"Boolean flags","text":"Storing feature states in boolean variables rather than re-testing conditions:","options":["Uses less memory than testing the condition twice","Is required, since conditions cannot be reused","Makes the logic readable and avoids recomputing","Forces the compiler to evaluate them in order"],"correct":2,"explain":"A well-named boolean such as isEligible documents the condition and can be reused."},{"id":"fs_z3_7_1","topic":"Independent ifs stack","text":"What does this print?

int price = 100;
boolean member = true;
boolean coupon = true;
if (member) {
    price -= 10;
}
if (coupon) {
    price -= 5;
}
System.out.println(price);","options":["90","95","100","85"],"correct":3,"explain":"These are separate if statements, so each condition is tested and both fire: 100 - 10 = 90, then 90 - 5 = 85. That stacking is exactly why independent adjustments use separate ifs rather than a chain."},{"id":"fs_z3_7_2","topic":"Chain fires at most once","text":"What does this print?

int price = 100;
boolean member = true;
boolean coupon = true;
if (member) {
    price -= 10;
} else if (coupon) {
    price -= 5;
}
System.out.println(price);","options":["95","100","90","85"],"correct":2,"explain":"In a chain at most one branch runs. member is true, so price becomes 90 and the else if is never tested even though coupon is also true. Using a chain where discounts should stack applies only the first one — a silent logic error."},{"id":"fs_z3_7_3","topic":"Nested if equivalence","text":"Which single condition is equivalent to this nested structure?

if (a) {
    if (b) {
        doWork();
    }
}","options":["if (a && b) { doWork(); }","if (a || b) { doWork(); }","if (a) { doWork(); } if (b) { doWork(); }","if (!a && b) { doWork(); }"],"correct":0,"explain":"The inner body runs only when the outer test passed AND the inner test passed, which is precisely a && b. The || version would run doWork when either alone is true, and the separate-ifs version could run it twice."},{"id":"fs_z3_7_4","topic":"Choosing chain vs separate ifs","text":"What is the deciding question when choosing between an else-if chain and separate if statements?","options":["Are the conditions comparing numbers or Strings?","Does the program need a final else branch?","Can two of the conditions be true at once, and if so should both take effect?","Which version uses fewer lines of code?"],"correct":2,"explain":"The choice is not stylistic. If several conditions can hold simultaneously and each should act (stacking discounts), use separate ifs; if at most one outcome should apply (grade bands), use a chain. Getting it wrong produces silent bugs in opposite directions."},{"id":"fs_z3_7_5","topic":"Separate ifs re-test updated values","text":"What does this print?

int price = 100;
if (price > 50) {
    price -= 30;
}
if (price > 50) {
    price -= 30;
}
System.out.println(price);","options":["40","70","100","10"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each separate if is evaluated in turn against the current value. The first test sees 100 > 50 and drops price to 70; the second sees 70 > 50 and drops it to 40. The second condition is tested against the updated value, not the original."},{"id":"zf459","topic":"","text":"int age = 70; boolean student = false; if (age >= 65 || student) System.out.println("discount"); else System.out.println("full price"); prints:","options":["full price","65","discount","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"The senior test passes; OR needs only one reason.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf460","topic":"","text":"int age = 30; boolean student = true; if (age >= 65 || student) System.out.println("reduced"); else System.out.println("standard"); prints:","options":["standard","30","reduced","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"Not a senior, but a student - the OR still succeeds.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf461","topic":"","text":"A loan requires income >= 30000 AND creditScore >= 700. income = 45000, creditScore = 650 gives:","options":["approved - income is high enough","approved - one test suffices","a compile error","denied - the credit test fails"],"correct":3,"explain":"AND requires every requirement; one failure denies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf462","topic":"","text":"boolean member = true; boolean coupon = true; if (member && coupon) System.out.println("20% off"); else if (member || coupon) System.out.println("10% off"); else System.out.println("no deal"); prints:","options":["20% off","10% off","no deal","30% off"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true, so the first (strictest) branch wins.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf463","topic":"","text":"boolean member2 = false; boolean coupon2 = true; if (member2 && coupon2) System.out.println("best"); else if (member2 || coupon2) System.out.println("some"); else System.out.println("none"); prints:","options":["best","some","none","true"],"correct":1,"explain":"AND fails with one false, but OR passes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf464","topic":"","text":"Nesting an if inside another if means the inner body runs when:","options":["either condition is true","the outer is false","outer and inner conditions are both true","conditions are ignored"],"correct":2,"explain":"Nested ifs multiply requirements, like an &&.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf465","topic":"","text":"if (hasTicket) { if (age >= 17) System.out.println("admitted"); } is equivalent to:","options":["if (hasTicket || age >= 17) ...","if (!hasTicket) ...","an infinite loop","if (hasTicket && age >= 17) ..."],"correct":3,"explain":"Nesting two ifs with single bodies is exactly an AND.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf466","topic":"","text":"int temp2 = 72; boolean sunny = true; if (temp2 >= 70 && sunny) System.out.println("beach"); else System.out.println("indoors"); prints:","options":["beach","indoors","72","sunny"],"correct":0,"explain":"Warm AND sunny - both conditions hold.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf467","topic":"","text":"A password is accepted if it is long enough AND contains a digit. This combination is:","options":["two alternatives - test with ||","two required features - test with &&","one feature negated","impossible to code"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every listed requirement must hold, so AND chains them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf468","topic":"","text":"boolean weekday = true; boolean holiday = false; if (weekday && !holiday) System.out.println("work"); else System.out.println("rest"); prints:","options":["rest","holiday","work","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is a weekday and NOT a holiday - both parts of the AND are true.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf469","topic":"","text":"Three conditions all required at once are written:","options":["a || b || c","a && b || c","a, b, c","a && b && c"],"correct":3,"explain":"Chain every requirement with && - the whole thing is true only if all are.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_8":[{"id":"zya3_8_0","topic":"Semicolon after if","text":"if (x > 5); { System.out.println("big"); } prints "big":","options":["Only when x <= 5","Only when x > 5","Always","Never"],"correct":2,"explain":"The stray semicolon becomes the entire if body. The braces that follow are then just an ordinary block that always runs. The semicolon ends the if with an empty body."},{"id":"zya3_8_1","topic":"Missing braces","text":"if (x > 5) a++; b++; — when x is 3, b++:","options":["Runs twice","Does not run","Runs","Causes a compile error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Without braces the if governs exactly one statement. Misleading indentation makes this hard to spot, which is why braces are recommended always. Only the first statement belongs to the if."},{"id":"zya3_8_2","topic":"Assignment vs equality","text":"if (x = 5) with int x gives:","options":["A runtime error","A warning only","A compile error","true when x is 5"],"correct":2,"explain":"The if requires a boolean and gets an int, so Java rejects it — one of the few places Java is stricter than C for good reason."},{"id":"zya3_8_3","topic":"Comparing Strings with ==","text":"if (name == "Ana") may be false even when name holds "Ana" because:","options":["== only works on ints","Strings cannot be compared","== compares references, not contents","name must be final"],"correct":2,"explain":"Literals are often interned so it can appear to work, which makes the bug intermittent. Always use equals."},{"id":"zya3_8_4","topic":"Dangling else","text":"In if (a) if (b) x(); else y(); the else belongs to:","options":["Neither — it is a syntax error","The outer if","The inner if","Both"],"correct":2,"explain":"An else binds to the nearest unmatched if. Braces are the only reliable way to say otherwise."},{"id":"zya3_8_5","topic":"Unreachable branch","text":"if (x > 10) {...} else if (x > 20) {...} — the second branch:","options":["Runs first","Runs for x = 25","Never runs","Causes a compile error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Anything above 20 is caught by the first condition. Ordering from the most restrictive threshold downward avoids this. X > 20 implies x > 10."},{"id":"zya3_8_6","topic":"Empty if body","text":"Writing if (cond) { } with an empty body:","options":["Skips the else","Is a compile error","Compiles and does nothing","Throws an exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"It often means the logic belongs in the else, or the condition should have been negated. Usually a mistake or an inverted condition."},{"id":"zy3_8_8_d17","topic":"Missing braces","text":"if(score>90) print("Great!"); print("A"); with score=50 prints:","options":["Great!","A","nothing","Great! A"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without braces only the first statement is the if-body; print("A") always runs.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_8_1","topic":"Missing braces","text":"What does this print?

int a = 0;
int b = 0;
int x = 3;
if (x > 5)
    a = 1;
    b = 2;
System.out.println(a + " " + b);","options":["1 0","0 2","0 0","1 2"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without braces only the first statement belongs to the if. Java reads braces, not indentation, so b = 2 runs unconditionally even though x > 5 is false. a stays 0 and b becomes 2 — one of the silent errors the compiler happily accepts."},{"id":"fs_z3_8_2","topic":"Dangling else","text":"What does this print?

int x = 10;
if (x > 5)
    if (x > 20)
        System.out.println("huge");
    else
        System.out.println("medium");","options":["huge","No output","Compiler error","medium"],"correct":3,"explain":"An else binds to the nearest unmatched if, not to the one indentation suggests. Here it belongs to if (x > 20). Since x > 5 is true, the inner if runs; x > 20 is false, so its else prints "medium"."},{"id":"fs_z3_8_3","topic":"Assignment in a condition","text":"With int x and boolean flag, why does if (x = 5) fail to compile while if (flag = true) compiles?","options":["An assignment produces the assigned value","Java forbids assignment inside if only for numeric types as a special safety rule","flag = true is automatically corrected to flag == true by the compiler","Neither compiles; assignment is never allowed inside a condition"],"correct":0,"explain":"An assignment expression yields the value assigned. An if requires a boolean, so the int produced by x = 5 is rejected, while the boolean produced by flag = true slips through — making the boolean version a legal but nasty bug rather than a compile error. Flag = true produces a boolean, which if accepts, but x = 5 produces an int."},{"id":"fs_z3_8_4","topic":"Stray semicolon","text":"What does this print?

int n = 1;
if (n == 2);
System.out.println("two");
System.out.println("end");","options":["two
end","end","No output","Compiler error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The semicolon right after the condition is an empty statement — it IS the branch body. The if therefore controls nothing, and both println lines run unconditionally even though n == 2 is false."},{"id":"fs_z3_8_5","topic":"Which error the compiler catches","text":"Of the four classic branching errors — a stray semicolon after the condition, missing braces around a two-statement body, == on Strings, and = in place of == on an int — which one does the compiler report?","options":["= in place of == on an int","The stray semicolon after the condition","Missing braces around a two-statement body","== on Strings"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only the assignment is caught, and only because x = 5 produces an int where the if requires a boolean. The other three are legal Java — an empty body, a one-statement branch followed by an unrelated statement, and a reference comparison — so they compile cleanly and fail silently."},{"id":"fs_z3_8_6","topic":"Why always use braces","text":"Always writing braces around every if body, even for a single statement, permanently prevents which pair of bugs?","options":["The empty-body stray semicolon and the unbraced second statement that runs unconditionally","Reference comparison of Strings and the dangling else","Using = instead of == and integer division","Misordered else-if chains and off-by-one range bounds"],"correct":0,"explain":"With braces, a stray semicolon cannot silently become the whole body, and every statement you indent into the branch really is in the branch. Braces do nothing for String comparison or chain ordering — those are separate errors."},{"id":"zf470","topic":"","text":"if (x == 5); { System.out.println("five"); } always prints five because:","options":["== always succeeds","braces run twice","println ignores conditions","the stray semicolon is an empty body"],"correct":3,"explain":"The ; ends the if with an empty statement; the { } block then runs unconditionally.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf471","topic":"","text":"Writing if (score = 100) instead of if (score == 100) with an int score:","options":["fails to compile","compares them anyway","sets score to 0","compiles and warns"],"correct":0,"explain":"Java demands a boolean condition, so this classic typo is caught.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf472","topic":"","text":"if (a > 5) System.out.println("big"); System.out.println("done"); When a is 2, the output is:","options":["big done","done","nothing","big"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without braces the if guards ONLY the first println; "done" runs regardless.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf473","topic":"","text":"The "dangling else" rule in Java attaches an else to:","options":["the first if in the method","the furthest if","the nearest unmatched if","whichever is indented to match"],"correct":2,"explain":"Indentation is ignored - braces are the only way to change the pairing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf474","topic":"","text":"Testing if (0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3) prints false because:","options":["== is broken for numbers","0.3 is negative","the compiler reorders the sum","0.1 and 0.2 are stored approximately"],"correct":3,"explain":"Compare doubles with a tolerance instead.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf475","topic":"","text":"Using if (str == "yes") to compare STRING CONTENT is wrong because:","options":["== compares references","strings cannot be compared at all","== only works for doubles","equals is slower and wrong"],"correct":0,"explain":"Two equal-content strings can live at different addresses.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf476","topic":"","text":"An if-else-if ladder where every branch tests x > 0 first:","options":["tests all branches anyway","never reaches later branches when x > 0","is a compile error","reorders itself"],"correct":1,"explain":"The first true condition consumes the case - order from specific to general.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf477","topic":"","text":"Forgetting the final else in a validation ladder means:","options":["the code will not compile","the first branch runs by default","unmatched values silently do nothing","an exception is thrown"],"correct":2,"explain":"Often fine - but if every value needs handling, the catch-all else is the net.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf478","topic":"","text":"if (n > 10) if (n > 100) System.out.println("huge"); else System.out.println("medium"); With n = 5, this prints:","options":["medium","huge","5","nothing"],"correct":3,"explain":"The else belongs to the INNER if; with n = 5 the outer if fails and skips everything.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf479","topic":"","text":"Copy-pasting a branch and forgetting to change the condition creates:","options":["two branches with the same test","a compile error","faster code","an automatic merge"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first identical condition always wins; the clone is dead code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf480","topic":"","text":"Which boundary bug does if (age > 18) have for "18 and over may vote"?","options":["exactly 18 is wrongly rejected","19 is wrongly rejected","17 is wrongly accepted","none - it is correct"],"correct":0,"explain":""18 and over" needs >=; the strict > excludes the boundary.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_9":[{"id":"zya3_9_0","topic":"Toll by vehicle type","text":"Choosing one toll from several vehicle categories is best written as:","options":["Separate if statements","An if-else-if chain","A while loop","Nested loops"],"correct":1,"explain":"The categories are mutually exclusive — exactly one toll applies — which is what a chain expresses."},{"id":"zya3_9_1","topic":"Peak surcharge","text":"Adding a peak-hour surcharge on top of the base toll uses:","options":["Another branch of the same chain","A separate if after the chain","An else on the chain","A switch"],"correct":1,"explain":"The surcharge is independent of vehicle type, so it must be tested separately or it will be skipped whenever a chain branch already matched."},{"id":"zya3_9_2","topic":"Accumulating charges","text":"double toll = base; if (peak) toll += 2.0; This pattern is:","options":["A correct way to layer an adjustment onto a base value","A compile error, since toll is already assigned","A pattern that discards the base value entirely","Equivalent to writing toll = 2.0 in every case"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compute the base, then apply each independent adjustment in turn."},{"id":"zya3_9_3","topic":"Discount condition","text":"A 50% discount for pass holders applies as:","options":["toll = 0.5;","toll = toll * 0.5;","toll = toll - 0.5;","toll / 0.5;"],"correct":1,"explain":"Multiplying by 0.5 halves it. Subtracting 0.5 removes fifty cents, and the last option discards its result."},{"id":"zya3_9_4","topic":"Order of adjustments","text":"Applying a surcharge before or after a percentage discount:","options":["Only matters for ints","Always gives the same total","Can give different totals, so the order must match the stated rules","Is a compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"Careful: (base + 2) * 0.5 differs from base * 0.5 + 2. The rules dictate the order — read the specification."},{"id":"zya3_9_5","topic":"Axle-count pricing","text":"Toll rising with axle count, charged per axle, is:","options":["A chain of if statements per count","ratePerAxle * axles","A switch on the total","A loop over axles"],"correct":1,"explain":"When the rule is linear, arithmetic replaces branching entirely — simpler and correct for any axle count."},{"id":"zya3_9_6","topic":"Default category","text":"A vehicle type not matching any branch should:","options":["Fall to a final else that reports an error or a default","Silently skip the calculation with no message","Repeat the chain until some branch matches","Throw an exception the caller must always catch"],"correct":0,"explain":"A catch-all else is how unexpected input becomes visible instead of silently producing zero."},{"id":"zya3_9_7","topic":"Weekend free","text":"Free passage on weekends is expressed as:","options":["if (!isWeekend) toll = 0;","if (isWeekend) toll = 0;","if (isWeekend) toll == 0;","if (isWeekend = true) toll = 0;"],"correct":1,"explain":"The second uses comparison instead of assignment; the third assigns inside the condition; the fourth inverts the rule."},{"id":"zya3_9_8","topic":"Combining free and discount","text":"If weekends are free, the discount branch afterwards:","options":["Doubles the toll","Should be skipped or guarded","Must always run","Causes a compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"It happens to give the right number here, but relying on that is fragile — express the exclusivity so the code states the rule. 50% of 0 is still 0 but the logic reads wrong."},{"id":"zya3_9_9","topic":"Currency output","text":"Printing the final toll to two decimals uses:","options":["System.out.println(toll);","System.out.printf("%.2f%n", toll);","System.out.printf("%d%n", toll);","Math.round(toll)"],"correct":1,"explain":"%.2f gives currency formatting; %d with a double throws an exception."},{"id":"fs_z3_9_1","topic":"Toll trace: weekday rush hour","text":"What does this print?

int day = 3;
int hour = 17;
boolean weekday = day >= 1 && day <= 5;
boolean rushHour = (hour >= 6 && hour <= 9) || (hour >= 16 && hour <= 19);
double toll;
if (weekday && rushHour) {
    toll = 5.00;
} else if (weekday) {
    toll = 2.50;
} else {
    toll = 1.00;
}
System.out.println(toll);","options":["2.5","1.0","5.00","5.0"],"correct":3,"explain":"Day 3 makes weekday true; hour 17 falls in 16-19, making rushHour true. The first branch therefore assigns 5.00, and println shows a double as 5.0 (trailing zeros are not kept). Working out the two named booleans first resolves the chain in one pass."},{"id":"fs_z3_9_2","topic":"Toll trace: weekday off-peak","text":"What does this print?

int day = 2;
int hour = 12;
boolean weekday = day >= 1 && day <= 5;
boolean rushHour = (hour >= 6 && hour <= 9) || (hour >= 16 && hour <= 19);
double toll;
if (weekday && rushHour) {
    toll = 5.00;
} else if (weekday) {
    toll = 2.50;
} else {
    toll = 1.00;
}
System.out.println(toll);","options":["5.0","1.0","0.0","2.5"],"correct":3,"explain":"Day 2 makes weekday true, but hour 12 is in neither rush band (6-9 or 16-19), so rushHour is false. The first condition fails, the second — just weekday — succeeds, and the off-peak weekday rate 2.50 is assigned, printing as 2.5."},{"id":"fs_z3_9_3","topic":"Naming conditions","text":"In the toll example, the compound tests are first assigned to booleans named weekday and rushHour instead of being written directly in the if conditions. What is the main benefit?","options":["Boolean variables are evaluated faster than inline conditions at runtime","Java requires compound conditions with || to be stored in variables first","It prevents short-circuit evaluation from skipping part of the condition","The chain reads as named ideas"],"correct":3,"explain":"Naming the conditions turns a four-clause compound test into two named ideas, so the chain reads like English and a trace only needs each sub-condition computed once. It is a readability and correctness technique, not a performance or language requirement. Its logic and ordering can be checked and traced far more easily."},{"id":"fs_z3_9_4","topic":"Tracing technique","text":"An exam question gives specific input values and a long if-else-if chain whose conditions share compound sub-expressions. What is the fastest reliable way to trace it?","options":["Read the branches bottom-up, since the else usually matches","Assume the first branch runs, since chains are ordered most likely first","Re-evaluate the full compound condition character by character at every branch","Evaluate each boolean sub-condition once for the given input, then walk the chain using those values"],"correct":3,"explain":"Work out the sub-conditions (like weekday and rushHour) once for the given input, then the chain resolves in a single pass to the first true condition. Re-deriving each long condition at every step is slow and error-prone, and guessing branches is not tracing."},{"id":"zf481","topic":"","text":"Toll rules: 2 dollars for vehicles with axles <= 2, else 5; add 1 during rush hour. axles = 2, rushHour = true. The toll is:","options":["2","3","5","6"],"correct":1,"explain":"Base is 2; rush adds 1: total 3.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf482","topic":"","text":"Toll rules: 2 dollars for vehicles with axles <= 2, else 5; add 1 during rush hour. axles = 4, rushHour = false. The toll is:","options":["2","6","5","7"],"correct":2,"explain":"Base is 5; rush adds 0: total 5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf483","topic":"","text":"Toll rules: 2 dollars for vehicles with axles <= 2, else 5; add 1 during rush hour. axles = 3, rushHour = true. The toll is:","options":["3","5","8","6"],"correct":3,"explain":"Base is 5; rush adds 1: total 6.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf484","topic":"","text":"In the toll program, the base rate and the rush surcharge are computed with:","options":["a base if-else plus a separate if","one giant condition","a loop","string concatenation"],"correct":0,"explain":"Independent decisions belong in independent branches - cleaner than enumerating every combination.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf485","topic":"","text":"int axles = 2; int fee = 0; if (axles <= 2) fee = 2; else fee = 5; System.out.println(fee); prints:","options":["5","2","0","7"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two axles meets the <= 2 test, so the base fee is 2.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf486","topic":"","text":"If the surcharge if is written as else-if attached to the base if-else, the bug is:","options":["the surcharge doubles","nothing changes","the surcharge can never apply","it will not compile"],"correct":2,"explain":"else-if chains are exclusive; the independent surcharge needs its own if.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf487","topic":"","text":"A motorcycle (1 axle) at rush hour, with base 2 for axles <= 2 and +1 for rush, pays:","options":["2","5","6","3"],"correct":3,"explain":"Base 2 plus the 1-dollar surcharge.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf488","topic":"","text":"The toll example returns different fees from the same code because:","options":["the branch taken depends on the inputs","the code rewrites itself","Java randomizes branches","tolls are stored in an array"],"correct":0,"explain":"Branching is exactly how one program handles many cases.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf489","topic":"","text":"A truck with 6 axles outside rush hour, base 5 for axles > 2, pays:","options":["6","5","2","11"],"correct":1,"explain":"The heavy-vehicle base applies, and no surcharge is added.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf490","topic":"","text":"Testing the toll code well means trying:","options":["one typical car only","both sides of 2 axles, rush on and off","only rush hour cases","random values without checking results"],"correct":1,"explain":"Cover each branch and each boundary - four combinations minimum.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf491","topic":"","text":"If the rules add a 1-dollar DISCOUNT for carpools, the cleanest change is:","options":["rewriting all branches into one condition","a new program","another independent if subtracting when carpool is true","changing the base rates"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each independent rule maps to its own small branch.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_10":[{"id":"zya3_10_0","topic":"Arithmetic precedence","text":"What is the value of 10 - 4 / 2?","options":["3","8","2","5"],"correct":1,"explain":"Division binds tighter, so 4 / 2 gives 2 and 10 - 2 is 8."},{"id":"zya3_10_1","topic":"Left-to-right associativity","text":"What is 20 / 5 * 2?","options":["2","8","10","0.5"],"correct":1,"explain":"Equal precedence means left to right: 20 / 5 is 4, then 4 * 2 is 8. Not 20 / 10."},{"id":"zya3_10_2","topic":"Comparison vs arithmetic","text":"In x + 1 > y * 2, which runs first?","options":["The comparison first, then the arithmetic","The arithmetic on both sides, then the comparison","Strictly left to right, one operator at a time","The right side fully, then the left side"],"correct":1,"explain":"Arithmetic operators bind tighter than relational ones, which is why this needs no parentheses."},{"id":"zya3_10_3","topic":"Relational vs logical","text":"In a > b && c > d, which is evaluated first?","options":["&&","Both comparisons, then &&","Left to right, so a > b && c first","It is a compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"Relational operators bind tighter than &&, so the comparisons resolve first."},{"id":"zya3_10_4","topic":"Parentheses override","text":"To force addition before multiplication, write:","options":["a + b * c","(a + b) * c","a + (b * c)","a * b + c"],"correct":1,"explain":"Parentheses have the highest precedence and are the readable way to state intent."},{"id":"zya3_10_5","topic":"Unary minus","text":"What is -3 + 5?","options":["-8","2","8","-2"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unary minus applies to 3 first, then addition runs."},{"id":"zya3_10_6","topic":"Assignment precedence","text":"int x = 2 + 3 * 4; gives x =","options":["20","14","24","9"],"correct":1,"explain":"The full right-hand expression is evaluated before assignment, which has the lowest precedence."},{"id":"zya3_10_7","topic":"Increment in an expression","text":"int i = 5; int j = i++ + 2; leaves j and i as:","options":["j=8, i=6","j=7, i=6","j=7, i=5","j=8, i=5"],"correct":1,"explain":"Post-increment yields the old value 5 for the expression, then increments i to 6."},{"id":"zya3_10_8","topic":"Modulo precedence","text":"What is 10 + 7 % 3?","options":["5","11","1","2"],"correct":1,"explain":"% has the same precedence as * and /, so 7 % 3 gives 1 and 10 + 1 is 11."},{"id":"zy3_10_9_bp_D286_t22_21","topic":"Precedence","text":"In "n x " + i + " = " + n * i, n * i computes first because:","options":["of parentheses","* has higher precedence than +","println does it","it reads right to left"],"correct":1,"explain":"Multiplication binds tighter than the + used for concatenation.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_10_1","topic":"Arithmetic before comparison","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println(3 + 4 * 2 > 10);","options":["false","11","Compiler error","true"],"correct":3,"explain":"Multiplication binds first (4 * 2 = 8), then addition (3 + 8 = 11), and only then the relational operator: 11 > 10 is true. Arithmetic always resolves before comparison."},{"id":"fs_z3_10_2","topic":"Right-associative assignment","text":"What does this print?

int a;
int b;
a = b = 7;
System.out.println(a + " " + b);","options":["0 7","7 0","Compiler error","7 7"],"correct":3,"explain":"Assignment is right-associative: b = 7 happens first and the assignment expression produces the value 7, which is then assigned to a. Both variables end up holding 7."},{"id":"fs_z3_10_3","topic":"Full-expression grouping","text":"Which fully parenthesized version shows how Java groups the expression !ok && x > 0 || y > 0?","options":["(!ok) && ((x > 0) || (y > 0))","!(ok && (x > 0)) || (y > 0)","!((ok && x > 0) || y > 0)","((!ok) && (x > 0)) || (y > 0)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Unary ! binds tightest (to ok only), relational > resolves next, then && groups before ||. So the && combines !ok with x > 0, and that result is ORed with y > 0."},{"id":"fs_z3_10_4","topic":"% before == in a condition","text":"What does this print?

int x = 5;
if (x % 2 == 1) {
    System.out.println("odd");
} else {
    System.out.println("even");
}","options":["even","1","Compiler error","odd"],"correct":3,"explain":"% is multiplicative, so it evaluates before the equality operator: 5 % 2 is 1, and 1 == 1 is true, so the if branch prints "odd". No parentheses are needed around x % 2 because arithmetic finishes before comparison."},{"id":"zf492","topic":"","text":"In 3 + 4 > 5 && 2 < 1, which happens FIRST?","options":["the && test","2 < 1","left-to-right regardless of operator","the arithmetic 3 + 4"],"correct":3,"explain":"Arithmetic outranks relational, which outranks logical.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf493","topic":"","text":"Among !, &&, and ||, the FIRST to bind is:","options":["!","&&","||","they are equal"],"correct":0,"explain":"NOT binds tightest, then AND, then OR.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf494","topic":"","text":"true || false && false evaluates to:","options":["false - left to right gives (true || false) && false","true - && binds before ||, so it is true || (false && false)","a compile error","undefined"],"correct":1,"explain":"Precedence groups the && first; the || then sees true on the left.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf495","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(!false && true); prints:","options":["false","!false","true","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"! applies first: true && true = true.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf496","topic":"","text":"The safest way to make a complex condition readable is:","options":["remove all spaces","split it into 10 nested ifs always","memorize the full precedence table","add parentheses showing the grouping"],"correct":3,"explain":"Parentheses cost nothing and remove every doubt.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf497","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(10 - 2 * 3 > 3); prints:","options":["true","false","24","4"],"correct":0,"explain":"2*3 = 6 first, 10 - 6 = 4, then 4 > 3 is true.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf498","topic":"","text":"In x > 5 && y > 5 || z > 5, the grouping Java uses is:","options":["x > 5 && (y > 5 || z > 5)","(x > 5 && y > 5) || z > 5","(x > 5 && y > 5 || z) > 5","left to right strictly"],"correct":1,"explain":"&& binds before ||.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf499","topic":"","text":"Relational operators like < and >= bind:","options":["tighter than arithmetic","looser than &&","between arithmetic and && / ||","equally with everything"],"correct":2,"explain":"That ordering is why a + b > c && d < e needs no parentheses to mean the usual thing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf500","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(2 + 3 == 5); prints:","options":["false","true","5","23"],"correct":1,"explain":"Addition first: 5 == 5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf501","topic":"","text":"A condition like a && b || c that others must read should be written:","options":["a && (b || c) to be safe","with no parentheses ever","(a && b) || c","as three separate programs"],"correct":2,"explain":"Match the parentheses to the true meaning; readers should never have to consult the table.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf867","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(1 + 2 * 3 == 7); prints:","options":["false","true","7","9"],"correct":1,"explain":"Arithmetic first: 1 + 6 = 7, then 7 == 7 is true.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_15":[{"id":"zya3_15_0","topic":"Character.isDigit","text":"Character.isDigit('7') returns:","options":["'7'","7","true","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Character utility methods return booleans that classify a char."},{"id":"zya3_15_1","topic":"isLetter on a digit","text":"Character.isLetter('5') returns:","options":["A compile error","true","false","5"],"correct":2,"explain":"5 is a digit, not a letter."},{"id":"zya3_15_2","topic":"toUpperCase for char","text":"Character.toUpperCase('a') returns:","options":["A compile error",""A" as a String","'A' as a char","97"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Character version takes and returns a char; the String version returns a String."},{"id":"zya3_15_3","topic":"char arithmetic","text":"What is the value of 'a' + 1 in Java?","options":["A compile error","'b'","98 as an int",""a1""],"correct":2,"explain":"char promotes to int in arithmetic, so the result is the numeric code 98. Cast back with (char) to get 'b'."},{"id":"zya3_15_4","topic":"Comparing chars","text":"'a' &lt; 'b' evaluates to:","options":["A compile error","Depends on locale","true","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"Chars compare by their Unicode code values, and letters are in order."},{"id":"zya3_15_5","topic":"Digit char to int","text":"To convert char c = '7' into the int 7:","options":["c * 1","(int) c","c - '0'","Integer.parseInt(c)"],"correct":2,"explain":"(int) c gives 55, the code for '7'. Subtracting the code for '0' gives the digit value."},{"id":"zya3_15_6","topic":"isWhitespace","text":"Character.isWhitespace(' ') returns:","options":["1","A compile error","true","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"It also returns true for tabs and newlines, which makes it useful for parsing."},{"id":"zya3_15_7","topic":"Char literal quotes","text":"Which is a valid char literal?","options":["a",""a"","'a'","'ab'"],"correct":2,"explain":"Single quotes and exactly one character. Double quotes make a String."},{"id":"zya3_15_8","topic":"isLetterOrDigit","text":"Character.isLetterOrDigit('-') returns:","options":["null","true","false","A compile error"],"correct":2,"explain":"A hyphen is punctuation, so it is neither — which is exactly the test a validation routine needs."},{"id":"zy3_15_9_bp_D286_t15_14","topic":"isDigit","text":"The cleanest check that char c is a digit is:","options":["c % 10","c >= 0","Character.isDigit(c)","c == "0""],"correct":2,"explain":"Character.isDigit(c) returns true for ‘0’..‘9’.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_15_1","topic":"char arithmetic with cast","text":"What does this print?

char c = 'A';
char next = (char)(c + 1);
System.out.println(next);","options":["A1","Compiler error","B","66"],"correct":2,"explain":"A char is a 16-bit number, so c + 1 is the int 66 — the code for 'B'. The cast back to char is required (int does not narrow automatically), and printing a char shows the character, not the number."},{"id":"fs_z3_15_2","topic":"Digit character to value","text":"What does this print?

char d = '5';
System.out.println(d - '0');","options":["'5'","0","5","53"],"correct":2,"explain":"'5' has code 53 and '0' has code 48; subtracting gives the int 5, the digit's numeric value. Subtracting '0' is the standard way to convert a digit character to its int value."},{"id":"fs_z3_15_3","topic":"Ordering of character codes","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println('Z' < 'a');","options":["Compiler error","Z","true","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"Character comparison uses the numeric codes, and the entire uppercase block comes before the lowercase block: 'Z' is 90 while 'a' is 97. So 'Z' < 'a' is true even though Z is later in the alphabet than a as letters."},{"id":"fs_z3_15_4","topic":"char vs String literals","text":"Why does the declaration char c = "A"; fail to compile?","options":["char variables can only be assigned numbers, never letters","It compiles; the compiler converts one-character Strings to char automatically","Double quotes create a String, and a String cannot be assigned to a char",""A" is too long to fit in a char, but "" would work"],"correct":2,"explain":"Single quotes make a char literal and double quotes make a String — different types with no automatic conversion between them. The correct declaration is char c = 'A'; with single quotes."},{"id":"zf536","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.isDigit('7')); prints:","options":["false","7","true","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"'7' is a digit character.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf537","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.isDigit('x')); prints:","options":["true","x","0","false"],"correct":3,"explain":"x is a letter, not a digit.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf538","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.isLetter('Q')); prints:","options":["true","false","Q","81"],"correct":0,"explain":"Uppercase and lowercase letters both count as letters.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf539","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.isUpperCase('h')); prints:","options":["true","false","H","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"h is lowercase.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf540","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.toLowerCase('T')); prints:","options":["'T' unchanged","t","84","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"toLowerCase converts a single char.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf541","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.isWhitespace(' ')); prints:","options":["false","space","true","32"],"correct":2,"explain":"The space character is whitespace (as are tab and newline).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf542","topic":"","text":"char c1 = 'b'; char c2 = 'p'; System.out.println(c1 < c2); prints:","options":["false","bp","error","true"],"correct":3,"explain":"Chars compare by code: b (98) is less than p (112).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf543","topic":"","text":"A loop validating that every character of an ID is a letter or digit uses:","options":["Character.isLetterOrDigit(ch)","ch.isValid()","Character.isSymbol(ch)","ch == letter"],"correct":0,"explain":"The Character class bundles exactly this test.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf544","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Character.toUpperCase('a') == 'A'); prints:","options":["false","true","A","97"],"correct":1,"explain":"The converted char is exactly A, so the comparison holds.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf545","topic":"","text":"Character.isLetter('4') returns:","options":["true","4","false","an exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"A digit is not a letter.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf546","topic":"","text":"To count the digits in a string s, the loop body tests:","options":["if (s.charAt(i) == "digit") count++;","if (s.isDigit(i)) count++;","if (i.isDigit()) count++;","if (Character.isDigit(s.charAt(i))) count++;"],"correct":3,"explain":"Pull the char out with charAt, then ask the Character class about it.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_16":[{"id":"zya3_16_0","topic":"replace returns","text":"String s = "cat"; s.replace('c', 'b'); System.out.println(s); prints:","options":["b","A compile error","bat","cat"],"correct":3,"explain":"Strings are immutable, so replace returns a new String. Without assigning the result, s is unchanged."},{"id":"zy3_16_2_a09","topic":"Containment","text":"Which one contains the other two?","options":["JRE","JVM","JDK","bytecode"],"correct":2,"explain":"JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM: the JDK contains the JRE, which contains the JVM.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_16_3_k22","topic":"contains","text":"list.contains("Bob") returns:","options":["the index","true or false","the element","the size"],"correct":1,"explain":"contains returns a boolean.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_16_5_q8","topic":"Old vs new","text":"try-with-resources replaces which older pattern?","options":["Catching the exception in a catch block","Closing the resource in a finally block","Declaring the exception with a throws clause","Wrapping the resource in a synchronized block"],"correct":1,"explain":"It replaces the verbose finally { resource.close(); } idiom with automatic closing.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_16_8_lg_D286_0_1","topic":"Primitive types","text":"Which list contains only Java primitive types?","options":["String, int, double","int, double, boolean, char","Integer, Double, Boolean","Array, List, Map"],"correct":1,"explain":"The eight primitives include int, double, boolean, and char; String and the wrapper classes are objects.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_16_9_lg_D286_0_18","topic":"for loop","text":"A standard for-loop header contains:","options":["Initialization, condition, and update","Condition, body, and update","Initialization, condition, and body","Initialization, update, and return type"],"correct":0,"explain":"for(init; condition; update) combines all three loop-control parts.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_16_1","topic":"indexOf and lastIndexOf","text":"What does this print?

String s = "hello world";
System.out.println(s.indexOf("o") + " " + s.lastIndexOf("o"));","options":["7 4","4 7","5 8","4 4"],"correct":1,"explain":"indexOf finds the first occurrence and lastIndexOf the last. Counting from 0, the 'o' in "hello" is at index 4 and the 'o' in "world" is at index 7."},{"id":"fs_z3_16_2","topic":"indexOf with a start position","text":"What does this print?

String s = "abcabc";
System.out.println(s.indexOf("b", 2));","options":["-1","4","1","2"],"correct":1,"explain":"The two-argument indexOf searches starting at the given index. The 'b' at index 1 is before position 2, so it is skipped; the first match at or after index 2 is the 'b' at index 4."},{"id":"fs_z3_16_3","topic":"The -1 sentinel","text":"What does this print?

String s = "hello";
System.out.println(s.indexOf("z"));","options":["Nothing; it throws an exception","-1","0","5"],"correct":1,"explain":"The indexOf family never throws for a missing substring — it returns the sentinel value -1, which the caller must check before using the result as an index."},{"id":"fs_z3_16_4","topic":"replace replaces every occurrence","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println("mississippi".replace("ss", "S"));","options":["miSiSippi","miSissippi","mississippi","miSiSiSi"],"correct":0,"explain":"replace substitutes EVERY occurrence, not just the first: both "ss" pairs become "S", giving "miSiSippi". An answer showing only the first occurrence changed is the classic wrong option."},{"id":"fs_z3_16_5","topic":"Unchecked -1 fails later","text":"String s = "abc"; int i = s.indexOf("x"); String t = s.substring(i); — what happens?","options":["t becomes the empty string","t becomes "abc" because -1 means "from the start"","indexOf quietly returns -1, and substring then throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException","indexOf throws an exception as soon as "x" is not found"],"correct":2,"explain":"indexOf signals "not found" with the sentinel -1 rather than throwing, so the failure is delayed: the -1 is handed to substring, which throws on the invalid index. The exception appears on a different line from the actual mistake — which is why the result must be checked before use."},{"id":"fs_z3_16_6","topic":"Testing presence","text":"A program only needs to know whether the text "cat" appears anywhere in String s — the position is irrelevant. Which call expresses that most directly?","options":["s.contains("cat")","s.indexOf("cat")","s.charAt("cat")","s.replace("cat", "")"],"correct":0,"explain":"contains returns a boolean — exactly the yes/no answer needed. indexOf works but returns a position that must then be compared to -1, charAt takes an int index, and replace builds a new String rather than answering a question."},{"id":"zf547","topic":"","text":"String a = "seesaw"; System.out.println(a.replace('s', 't')); prints:","options":["teetaw","teesaw","seetaw","seesaw"],"correct":0,"explain":"replace changes EVERY matching character: both s's become t's.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf548","topic":"","text":"String a = "seesaw"; a.replace('s', 't'); System.out.println(a); prints:","options":["teetaw","seesaw","teesaw","an error"],"correct":1,"explain":"Strings are immutable - replace RETURNS a new string, and the result was discarded.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf549","topic":"","text":"String b = "hotdog"; System.out.println(b.replace("dog", "cake")); prints:","options":["hotdog","cakedog","hotcake","hotcakedog"],"correct":2,"explain":"The substring "dog" is replaced by "cake" in the returned string.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf550","topic":"","text":"String m = "mississippi"; System.out.println(m.indexOf("ss")); prints:","options":["3","5","2","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"m-i-s: the first "ss" begins at index 2.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf551","topic":"","text":"String m = "mississippi"; System.out.println(m.indexOf("ss", 3)); prints:","options":["2","4","-1","5"],"correct":3,"explain":"Starting the search at index 3 skips the first match and finds the one at 5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf552","topic":"","text":"String c = "notebook"; System.out.println(c.contains("book")); prints:","options":["true","false","4","book"],"correct":0,"explain":"contains simply reports whether the text appears.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf553","topic":"","text":"String c2 = "notebook"; System.out.println(c2.contains("Book")); prints:","options":["true","false","book","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"contains is case-sensitive; the capital B does not match.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf554","topic":"","text":"String d = "a-b-c"; System.out.println(d.replace("-", "")); prints:","options":["a-b-c","a b c","abc","-"],"correct":2,"explain":"Replacing with the empty string deletes every hyphen.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf555","topic":"","text":"To find where "@" sits in an email string e, use:","options":["e.find("@")","e.search("@")","e.charAt("@")","e.indexOf("@")"],"correct":3,"explain":"indexOf returns the position (or -1 if missing).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf556","topic":"","text":"String h2 = "hi"; h2.concat(" there"); System.out.println(h2); prints:","options":["hi","hi there","there","an empty string"],"correct":0,"explain":"concat RETURNS a new string that was discarded; strings are immutable, so h2 still holds hi.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf557","topic":"","text":"Checking whether a file name ends with ".txt" uses:","options":["name.contains("txt") alone","name.endsWith(".txt")","name.startsWith(".txt")","name == ".txt""],"correct":1,"explain":"endsWith anchors the match to the end - contains would also match mid-name.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf558","topic":"","text":"The startsWith method, as in url.startsWith("https"), returns:","options":["the index of the prefix","the prefix itself","true when the string starts with it","a new string"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is a boolean test anchored at position 0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr3","topic":"Finding and replacing text in a string","text":"String s = "banana"; What does s.replace('a', 'o') return?","options":[""bonana"",""banana"",""bonono" with the original also changed",""bonono""],"correct":3,"explain":"replace swaps EVERY matching character and returns a new String. Strings are immutable, so the original "banana" is unchanged unless you assign the result back.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_17":[{"id":"zya3_17_0","topic":"Ternary syntax","text":"The conditional operator has the form:","options":["condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse","condition : valueIfTrue ? valueIfFalse","if condition then a else b","condition ?: a, b"],"correct":0,"explain":"Question mark after the condition, colon between the two results."},{"id":"zya3_17_1","topic":"Ternary evaluation","text":"int max = (a > b) ? a : b; with a=3, b=7 gives max =","options":["7","true","A compile error","3"],"correct":0,"explain":"The condition is false, so the value after the colon is chosen."},{"id":"zya3_17_2","topic":"Ternary produces a value","text":"Unlike if-else, a ternary:","options":["Evaluates to a value that can be assigned or printed","Cannot be nested","Runs faster always","Can contain multiple statements"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is an expression, not a statement — which is why it can appear inside a println argument."},{"id":"zya3_17_3","topic":"Ternary readability","text":"Deeply nested ternaries are discouraged because:","options":["They quickly become harder to read than an if-else chain","They are not permitted by the Java compiler","They evaluate every branch, wasting execution time","They cannot return a value of a reference type"],"correct":0,"explain":"One level is often clearer than an if-else; three levels rarely is."},{"id":"zy3_17_6_d23","topic":"Ternary purpose","text":"The ternary ?: is a shorthand for:","options":["a method","a small if/else that yields a value","a loop","a switch"],"correct":1,"explain":"condition ? a : b chooses between two values — a compact if/else.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_17_7_h2_286_2","topic":"Ternary","_dom":"control","difficulty":"hard","text":"int a = 5; System.out.println(a > 3 ? "big" : "small"); prints:","options":["big","true","5","small"],"correct":0,"explain":"a > 3 is true, so the ternary yields the first value, "big".","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_17_8_fresh_D286_10","topic":"Control Flow > Ternary operator","text":"What is printed?

