int r = flag ? 1 : "one";","options":["It compiles, but throws ClassCastException when flag is false","r becomes the String "one" converted to its length","Compile error","r becomes 1 when flag is true and 0 otherwise"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both branches of a ternary must produce compatible types, and the result must fit the target: an int cannot hold "one", so the compiler rejects it. Nothing is deferred to runtime — type checking happens at compile time. The two branches have incompatible types for an int result."},{"id":"fs_z3_17_6","topic":"Parentheses around a ternary in concatenation","text":"In "Count: " + (n == 0 ? "none" : "some"), why are the parentheses around the ternary required?","options":["Ternaries may not appear inside String concatenation without a cast","They force the false branch to be evaluated first","Concatenation binds tighter than ?:","They are purely stylistic; the expression works identically without them"],"correct":2,"explain":"The + operator has higher precedence than the conditional operator, so without parentheses Java would try to evaluate "Count: " + n first and then compare the resulting String to 0 with ==, which is a type error. The parentheses make the ternary evaluate as one value before concatenation. Without them the condition would become "Count: " + n == 0, which does not compile."},{"id":"zf559","topic":"","text":"int x = 7; int y = 12; int m = (x > y) ? x : y; System.out.println(m); prints:","options":["7","19","x","12"],"correct":3,"explain":"The ternary picks x when x > y is true, else y - here the larger is 12.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf560","topic":"","text":"int x = 20; int y = 9; int m = (x > y) ? x : y; System.out.println(m); prints:","options":["9","29","x","20"],"correct":3,"explain":"The ternary picks x when x > y is true, else y - here the larger is 20.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf561","topic":"","text":"The three parts of cond ? a : b are:","options":["condition, true-value, false-value","three values of any kind","two conditions and a value","a loop, a condition, a value"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is an EXPRESSION form of if-else that produces a value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf562","topic":"","text":"String msg = (age >= 18) ? "adult" : "minor"; is equivalent to:","options":["a loop over ages","an if-else assigning one of the two","two independent assignments","a switch on strings only"],"correct":1,"explain":"The ternary condenses assign-one-of-two into a single expression.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf563","topic":"","text":"int n = 4; System.out.println(n % 2 == 0 ? "even" : "odd"); prints:","options":["odd","true","even","4"],"correct":2,"explain":"4 % 2 is 0, so the condition holds and the first value is chosen.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf564","topic":"","text":"int n2 = 9; String r = (n2 % 2 == 0) ? "even" : "odd"; System.out.println(r); prints:","options":["even","9","false","odd"],"correct":3,"explain":"9 is odd; the false-branch value is selected.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf565","topic":"","text":"A ternary is a poor choice when:","options":["the outcomes are multi-statement actions","assigning one of two values","the condition is simple","a default value is needed"],"correct":0,"explain":"Ternaries shine for small value choices; real branching logic belongs in if-else.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf566","topic":"","text":"int abs = (v < 0) ? -v : v; computes:","options":["the negative of v always","the absolute value of v","zero","v squared"],"correct":1,"explain":"Negative values are flipped; non-negatives pass through.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf567","topic":"","text":"int fee = (member) ? 0 : 25; When member is true, fee is:","options":["25","true","0","-25"],"correct":2,"explain":"The true-branch value comes first after the ?.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf568","topic":"","text":"Nesting ternaries, like a ? 1 : b ? 2 : 3:","options":["does not compile","is required for 3 cases","runs all branches","legal but quickly hard to read"],"correct":3,"explain":"One level is fine; beyond that an if-else-if ladder is clearer.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf569","topic":"","text":"The type of the whole expression (x > 0) ? 1 : -1 is:","options":["int","boolean","String","void"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both result values are ints, so the expression is an int.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_18":[{"id":"zya3_18_0","topic":"Why not ==","text":"Comparing two doubles with == is unreliable because:","options":["== compares references rather than values for doubles","Doubles must be cast to float before comparison","== is undefined for floating-point primitives","Tiny binary rounding errors make equal values differ"],"correct":3,"explain":"Values like 0.1 have no exact binary representation, so arithmetic accumulates small errors."},{"id":"zya3_18_1","topic":"Epsilon comparison","text":"The standard way to compare doubles a and b is:","options":["a == b","Math.abs(a","a.equals(b)","(int) a == (int) b"],"correct":1,"explain":"Check that the difference is smaller than a chosen tolerance. B) < 0.0001."},{"id":"zya3_18_2","topic":"Choosing epsilon","text":"The tolerance value should be:","options":["Always exactly 0.0001, whatever the data","Chosen to suit the magnitude and precision of the data","As small as the double type can represent","Set to zero, so the comparison stays exact"],"correct":1,"explain":"Too large and unequal values compare equal; too small and the test fails for values that are equal in practice."},{"id":"zya3_18_3","topic":"Comparing to zero","text":"To test whether double d is effectively zero:","options":["d == 0","Math.abs(d) < 1e-9","d = 0","d.isZero()"],"correct":1,"explain":"Same principle — the tolerance replaces exact equality."},{"id":"zya3_18_4","topic":"Ordering comparisons","text":"Using &lt; and > with doubles is:","options":["Equally unreliable as ==","Generally fine","Not permitted","Slower than =="],"correct":1,"explain":"A tiny error rarely flips an ordering unless the values are nearly identical, which is exactly the case == cannot handle. Only exact equality is the problem."},{"id":"zy3_18_5_zb1","topic":"Epsilon","text":"The correct way to compare doubles a and b for equality is:","options":["a.equals(b)","a == b","Math.abs(a","a = b"],"correct":2,"explain":"Compare within an epsilon tolerance; == fails due to rounding error. B) < 0.0001.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_18_7_zb3","topic":"Epsilon term","text":"The small tolerance used when comparing floating-point values is called:","options":["sigma","delta force","modulo","epsilon"],"correct":3,"explain":"The epsilon tolerance defines how close counts as equal.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_18_8_lg_D286_1_53","topic":"Epsilon","text":"Why avoid == when comparing doubles?","options":["rounding errors make equal values differ slightly","== compares references rather than values for doubles","doubles cannot be compared without a cast to float","== is reserved for objects and illegal on primitives"],"correct":0,"explain":"Floating-point rounding makes exact equality unreliable.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_18_9_lg_D286_2_11","topic":"Epsilon","text":"To test if doubles x and y are effectively equal, use:","options":["Math.abs(x","x.equals(y)","x == y","x >= y"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compare within an epsilon tolerance. Y) < 0.0001.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_18_1","topic":"Accumulated representation error","text":"What does this print?

