System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);","options":["0.30","0.299999999999999","0.30000000000000004","0.3"],"correct":2,"explain":"Neither 0.1 nor 0.2 is exactly representable in binary, and their sum lands on a double slightly above 0.3. Java prints the shortest decimal that identifies that double: 0.30000000000000004 — the visible form of representation error."},{"id":"zf570","topic":"","text":"The recommended way to compare doubles a and b for "equality" is:","options":["Math.abs(a - b) < someSmallEpsilon","a == b","a.equals(b)","(int) a == (int) b"],"correct":0,"explain":"Test closeness, not exactness - rounding error makes == unreliable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf571","topic":"","text":"System.out.println(Math.abs(0.3 - 0.3) < 0.0001); prints:","options":["false","true","0.0","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"The difference is exactly 0.0, comfortably inside the tolerance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf572","topic":"","text":"An epsilon of 0.0001 in a comparison means:","options":["values must differ by 0.0001","the numbers are rounded to 4 digits first","values within 0.0001 of each other count as equal","a compile-time setting"],"correct":2,"explain":"Epsilon is the closeness threshold you accept.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf573","topic":"","text":"double third = 1.0 / 3.0; System.out.println(third * 3 == 1.0); The safe expectation is:","options":["always true by algebra","a compile error","always an exception","it may print false"],"correct":3,"explain":"1/3 has no exact binary form, so tripling it may miss 1.0 by a tiny amount.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf574","topic":"","text":"Why do 0.1 and 0.2 not sum to exactly 0.3 in a double?","options":["none of the three is exact in binary","the + operator is approximate","doubles only hold 3 digits","Java rounds down always"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each is stored as the nearest binary fraction; the tiny errors show up in the sum.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf575","topic":"","text":"Choosing epsilon appropriately depends on:","options":["a single universal constant","the application’s scale and precision","the phase of the JVM","the variable name"],"correct":1,"explain":"Comparing planet masses and comparing pennies need very different tolerances.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf576","topic":"","text":"double x2 = 2.0; System.out.println(Math.abs(x2 - 2.0000001) < 0.001); prints:","options":["false","2.0","true","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"The difference (about 1e-7) is far below the 1e-3 tolerance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf577","topic":"","text":"Comparing doubles with < or > (rather than ==):","options":["is equally broken","requires epsilon too, always","never works","is generally fine"],"correct":3,"explain":"Ordering comparisons tolerate tiny errors unless values are nearly equal.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf578","topic":"","text":"A price computed as 19.9999999 when 20.0 was expected indicates:","options":["normal accumulated rounding","a broken CPU","a Java bug to report","memory corruption"],"correct":0,"explain":"Normal double behavior - compare with epsilon or store whole cents.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf579","topic":"","text":"if (total == expected) for doubles in a grader would be better written:","options":["if (total.equals(expected))","if (Math.abs(total - expected) < 1e-6)","if ((float) total == expected)","if (total - expected == 0.0)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The tolerance form passes when the values agree to within a millionth.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf580","topic":"","text":"Float and double equality surprises exist in:","options":["Java only","nearly every programming language","old computers only","interpreted languages only"],"correct":1,"explain":"IEEE 754 binary floating point is universal - and so is this pitfall.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_19":[{"id":"zya3_19_0","topic":"Short-circuit AND","text":"In a && b, when is b evaluated?","options":["Never","Always","Only when a is true","Only when a is false"],"correct":2,"explain":"If a is false the result is already false, so Java skips b entirely."},{"id":"zya3_19_1","topic":"Short-circuit OR","text":"In a || b, when is b evaluated?","options":["Never","Always","Only when a is false","Only when a is true"],"correct":2,"explain":"If a is true the result is already true, so b is not needed."},{"id":"zya3_19_3","topic":"Non-short-circuit operator","text":"Using & instead of && means:","options":["Faster execution","A syntax error","Both sides are always evaluated","The result is inverted"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which matters when the right side has a side effect, or would throw — & would call s.length() on a null and crash."},{"id":"zy3_19_4_q3","topic":"Short-circuit AND","text":"In (x != 0 && 10/x > 5), if x = 0, does the division execute?","options":["Division throws exception first","Yes — in Java both always evaluate","No — && short-circuits: left is false, right never evaluated","Yes — both always evaluate"],"correct":2,"explain":"&& short-circuits: if left is false, right is skipped entirely.","distractors":{"0":"&& short-circuits.","2":"Short-circuit prevents the exception.","3":"&& is specifically defined to short-circuit."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_19_6_h2_286_5","topic":"Short-circuit safety","_dom":"control","difficulty":"hard","text":"if (5 > 3 || 10 / 0 > 1) System.out.print("ok"); The result is:","options":["compile error","ArithmeticException","prints ok (10/0 never runs)","prints nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"|| short-circuits: the left side is true, so 10/0 is never evaluated and no exception occurs.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_19_8_lg_D286_0_66","topic":"Short-circuit","text":"In (true || compute()), the call compute():","options":["Runs twice","Throws","Never runs","Runs first"],"correct":2,"explain":"|| short-circuits: a true left side decides the result, so the right side is skipped.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zyf3_19_0","topic":"Order matters","text":"if (count != 0 && total / count > 5) is safe because:","options":["The && evaluates both sides and combines the results","The && converts a divide-by-zero into infinity","The && stops before dividing when count is 0","The && catches the ArithmeticException internally"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reversing the operands would throw ArithmeticException."},{"id":"fs_z3_19_1","topic":"|| short-circuit guard","text":"What does this print?

