    System.out.print(tag);
    return false;
}","options":["adone","abdone","abindone","done"],"correct":0,"explain":"f("a") prints "a" and returns false, which settles the && — f("b") is never called, so "b" never prints, and the branch body is skipped. Only "a" then "done" appear. Work left to right and stop the moment the result is determined."},{"id":"fs_z3_19_6","topic":"Guard condition terminology","text":"In if (s != null && s.isEmpty() == false), the s != null test placed first specifically to make the following method call safe is called a:","options":["guard condition","sentinel value","flag variable","loop invariant"],"correct":0,"explain":"A guard condition is a test placed first so that short-circuiting makes the next test safe to evaluate. A sentinel is a special value like indexOf's -1, and a flag is a boolean recording whether something happened — different ideas."},{"id":"zf581","topic":"","text":"In false && expensiveCheck(), the call expensiveCheck():","options":["always runs","runs twice","never runs - && already knows","decides the result"],"correct":2,"explain":"The AND result is already known; Java skips the right side.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf582","topic":"","text":"In true || riskyCall(), the call riskyCall():","options":["always runs","throws first","must be a boolean variable","never runs - || is already true"],"correct":3,"explain":"One true operand settles an OR.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf583","topic":"","text":"int[] arr = {}; if (arr.length > 0 && arr[0] == 5) ... is safe because:","options":["a false left side skips arr[0] entirely","empty arrays return 0 for arr[0]","&& catches exceptions","the JVM checks bounds first"],"correct":0,"explain":"Short-circuiting is the standard guard pattern for risky right-hand tests.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf584","topic":"","text":"Reversing the guard to arr[0] == 5 && arr.length > 0 on an empty array:","options":["still safe","throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","is faster","prints false"],"correct":1,"explain":"Order matters: the guard must come FIRST.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf585","topic":"","text":"The operators & and | (single symbols) on booleans:","options":["do not compile","are identical to && and ||","evaluate BOTH sides always","work only on ints"],"correct":2,"explain":"Single & and | are the non-short-circuit versions (also bitwise on ints).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf586","topic":"","text":"int calls = 0; boolean r = (3 > 5) && (++calls > 0); System.out.println(calls); prints:","options":["1","2","true","0"],"correct":3,"explain":"The left side is false, so the increment on the right never executes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf587","topic":"","text":"int calls2 = 0; boolean r2 = (5 > 3) || (++calls2 > 0); System.out.println(calls2); prints:","options":["0","1","true","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The OR is settled by the true left side; the right side is skipped.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf588","topic":"","text":"int calls3 = 0; boolean r3 = (5 > 3) && (++calls3 > 0); System.out.println(calls3); prints:","options":["0","1","2","false"],"correct":1,"explain":"A true left side means && MUST evaluate the right side - the increment runs once.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf589","topic":"","text":"Short-circuiting improves performance when:","options":["both tests are always needed","tests are ordered randomly","the cheap, decisive test goes first","conditions have side effects"],"correct":2,"explain":"Put the fast, most-often-decisive check on the left.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf590","topic":"","text":"A null guard like if (s != null && s.length() > 3) works because:","options":["null strings have length 0","&& swallows the exception","s.length() runs only when s is non-null","length checks for null itself"],"correct":2,"explain":"Left-to-right with early exit is precisely what makes the idiom safe.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf591","topic":"","text":"Putting a side effect (like i++) inside a short-circuited condition is risky because:","options":["side effects are illegal there","it always runs twice","the compiler removes it","its execution depends on the other side"],"correct":3,"explain":"Skipped right-hand sides mean skipped side effects - subtle bugs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf868","topic":"","text":"boolean v19 = (2 > 1) || (1 / 0 > 0); This code:","options":["throws ArithmeticException","prints Infinity","never divides","makes v19 false"],"correct":2,"explain":"The || is settled by 2 > 1, so the divide-by-zero on the right is never evaluated.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"h286_11","topic":"Short-circuit","_dom":"control","difficulty":"hard","text":"int i = 0; if (false && (i++ > 0)) { } System.out.println(i); prints:","options":["1","0","2","true"],"correct":1,"explain":"&& short-circuits: because the left side is false, i++ is never evaluated, so i stays 0.","__rebal":1}],"D286/u3/z3_20":[{"id":"zya3_20_0","topic":"Discount branch","text":"A 10% discount applies when total exceeds 100. The test is:","options":["if (total >= 100) total -= 0.9;","if (total > 100) total * 0.9;","if (total > 100) total *= 0.9;","if (total > 100) total = 0.9;"],"correct":2,"explain":"Multiplying by 0.9 keeps 90%. The last option computes a value and discards it."},{"id":"zya3_20_1","topic":"Tiered discount order","text":"With 20% above 500 and 10% above 100, the chain must test:","options":["Both with separate ifs","100 first","500 first, then 100","Either order"],"correct":2,"explain":"Testing 100 first would catch a 600 total and apply only 10%. Most restrictive threshold first."},{"id":"zya3_20_2","topic":"Applying two discounts","text":"Using separate ifs for the 500 and 100 tiers causes:","options":["Neither discount to apply to a large total","Both discounts to apply to a large total","A compile error, since the tests overlap","Only the smaller discount ever to apply"],"correct":1,"explain":"The tiers are meant to be exclusive, so they belong in one chain."},{"id":"zya3_20_3","topic":"Commission on sales","text":"A 5% commission on sales s is:","options":["s - 0.05","s + 0.05","s * 0.05","s / 0.05"],"correct":2,"explain":"Percentage of a value is multiplication by the decimal rate."},{"id":"zya3_20_4","topic":"Bonus threshold","text":"A bonus paid only at 1000 or more uses:","options":["if (sales == 1000)","if (sales > 1000)","if (sales >= 1000)","if (sales < 1000)"],"correct":2,"explain":""1000 or more" includes exactly 1000, so >= is required."},{"id":"zya3_20_5","topic":"Salary after raise","text":"A 3% raise on salary is:","options":["salary / 1.03","salary * 0.03","salary * 1.03","salary + 0.03"],"correct":2,"explain":"Multiplying by 1.03 keeps the original and adds 3%. Multiplying by 0.03 gives only the raise amount."},{"id":"zya3_20_6","topic":"Combining discount and tax","text":"Applying a discount then 8% tax to total is:","options":["total * (0.9 + 1.08)","total / 0.9 * 1.08","total * 0.9 * 1.08","total * 0.9 + 0.08"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each multiplier applies in sequence to the running value."},{"id":"zya3_20_7","topic":"Negative guard","text":"Before computing a discount you should check that total is:","options":["Exactly 100","Greater than 1000","Not negative","An int"],"correct":2,"explain":"Validating input keeps a nonsensical negative from producing a nonsensical result."},{"id":"zya3_20_8","topic":"Rounding money","text":"To round a double total to the nearest cent:","options":["total % 100","(int) total","Math.round(total * 100) / 100.0","Math.round(total)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Scale up, round, scale back down. Dividing by 100.0 rather than 100 keeps it a double."},{"id":"zya3_20_9","topic":"Reporting the result","text":"Printing "Total: 95.40" from a double uses:","options":["print(total + "0")","println(total)","printf("Total: %.2f%n", total)","printf("Total: %d%n", total)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Only %.2f guarantees exactly two decimal places."},{"id":"fs_z3_20_1","topic":"Tiered rate chain trace","text":"What does this print?

double salary = 60000;
double rate;
if (salary >= 100000) {
    rate = 0.28;
} else if (salary >= 50000) {
    rate = 0.22;
} else if (salary >= 25000) {
    rate = 0.15;
} else {
    rate = 0.10;
}
System.out.println(rate);","options":["0.28","0.1","0.22","0.15"],"correct":2,"explain":"The chain is ordered highest band first. 60000 >= 100000 is false, then 60000 >= 50000 is true, so 0.22 is assigned and the remaining branches are skipped — exactly one band applies."},{"id":"fs_z3_20_2","topic":"Compound discount condition trace","text":"What does this print?

double purchase = 200.0;
boolean isMember = true;
if (isMember && purchase > 100) {
    purchase *= 0.90;
}
System.out.println(purchase);","options":["20.0","190.0","180.0","200.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both requirements hold — isMember is true and 200.0 > 100 — so the discount fires: 200.0 * 0.90 is 180.0. Had either condition failed, the && would be false and the price would stay 200.0."},{"id":"fs_z3_20_3","topic":"Chain vs separate if in one program","text":"In the salary example, the tax rate is chosen by an else-if chain, but the member discount is a separate if statement. Why the two different structures?","options":["The discount could not appear in a chain because it uses &&","It is purely stylistic; either structure would behave the same in both places","The rate bands are mutually exclusive so exactly one must apply, while the discount is an independent adjustment that applies on top","Chains are required for doubles, and separate ifs are required for booleans"],"correct":2,"explain":"A chain picks exactly one of several mutually exclusive outcomes — the tiered rate. The discount is independent and should take effect regardless of which band matched, so it must be a separate statement. Swapping either structure produces a program that runs and is silently wrong."},{"id":"fs_z3_20_4","topic":"|| where the rule says "and"","text":"The rule says the discount applies to members AND purchases over $100. A programmer writes if (isMember || purchase > 100). What is the effect?","options":["The code fails to compile, since || cannot join those operand types","The behavior is identical, because && and || agree when both sides are true","The discount applies when either condition alone holds — every member and every large purchase gets it","The discount never applies, because || requires both sides to be false"],"correct":2,"explain":"|| is true when either side is true, so a non-member with a $150 purchase and a member with a $20 purchase both get the discount the wording denies them. The code compiles and runs — it is a silent requirements bug. Read the requirement sentence once per operator."},{"id":"zf592","topic":"","text":"int total = 120; if (total >= 100) total = total - 20; System.out.println(total); prints:","options":["100","120","80","20"],"correct":0,"explain":"The order qualifies for the 20-off discount: 120 - 20 = 100.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf593","topic":"","text":"int total2 = 90; if (total2 >= 100) total2 = total2 - 20; System.out.println(total2); prints:","options":["70","90","100","110"],"correct":1,"explain":"Below the threshold, the discount branch is skipped entirely.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf594","topic":"","text":"int pay3 = 1000; boolean manager = true; if (manager) pay3 = pay3 + 250; System.out.println(pay3); prints:","options":["1000","250","1250","750"],"correct":2,"explain":"The manager bonus applies: 1000 + 250.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf595","topic":"","text":"A 10% discount on an int total uses total - total / 10. For total = 250 the result is:","options":["240","25","245","225"],"correct":3,"explain":"250 / 10 = 25 off, leaving 225.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf596","topic":"","text":"Two discounts that must NOT stack are safest coded as:","options":["an if-else if chain - one branch only","two separate ifs","one combined subtraction","a loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Exclusive branches structurally prevent double-dipping.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf597","topic":"","text":"int price2 = 80; int qty = 3; int bill = price2 * qty; if (bill > 200) bill = bill - 15; System.out.println(bill); prints:","options":["240","225","185","200"],"correct":1,"explain":"80 x 3 = 240, which is over 200, so 15 comes off: 225.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf598","topic":"","text":"int hours2 = 45; int rate3 = 10; int pay4 = 40 * rate3 + (hours2 - 40) * rate3 * 2; System.out.println(pay4); prints:","options":["450","400","500","900"],"correct":2,"explain":"40 regular hours (400) plus 5 overtime hours at double rate (100) = 500.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf599","topic":"","text":"Why compute the subtotal BEFORE testing for a discount?","options":["Java requires assignments before ifs","subtotals cannot follow branches","discounts change the prices","the rule depends on the full amount"],"correct":3,"explain":"Decision inputs must exist before the decision.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf600","topic":"","text":"int sale = 60; if (sale >= 50 && sale < 100) System.out.println("tier 1"); else System.out.println("other"); prints:","options":["other","60","tier 1","tier 2"],"correct":2,"explain":"60 sits inside the 50-99 tier window.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf601","topic":"","text":"A negative total slipping through discount code suggests adding:","options":["a bigger discount","more variables","a longer receipt","input validation up front"],"correct":3,"explain":"Guard clauses keep nonsense data out of the money math.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf602","topic":"","text":"int base2 = 900; int years = 6; if (years >= 5) base2 = base2 + base2 / 10; System.out.println(base2); prints:","options":["990","900","1000","90"],"correct":0,"explain":"Five-plus years earns the 10% loyalty raise: 900 + 90.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_21":[{"id":"zya3_21_0","topic":"Comparing a name","text":"To test whether String name equals "Ana":","options":["name == "Ana"","name.equals("Ana")","name = "Ana"","name.compare("Ana")"],"correct":1,"explain":"equals compares contents; == compares references and may be false for equal text."},{"id":"zya3_21_1","topic":"Safe comparison order","text":"Which avoids NullPointerException when name might be null?","options":["name.equals("Ana")",""Ana".equals(name)","name == "Ana"","name.equalsIgnoreCase("Ana")"],"correct":1,"explain":"Calling equals on the literal is safe because the literal is never null. This idiom is common in production code."},{"id":"zya3_21_2","topic":"Case-insensitive search","text":"To match "ana", "Ana" and "ANA" alike:","options":["equals","equalsIgnoreCase","==","contains"],"correct":1,"explain":"equalsIgnoreCase performs the comparison without regard to case."},{"id":"zya3_21_3","topic":"Partial match","text":"To test whether name contains "an":","options":["name.equals("an")","name.contains("an")","name == "an"","name.charAt("an")"],"correct":1,"explain":"contains returns true when the substring appears anywhere."},{"id":"zya3_21_4","topic":"Prefix test","text":"To test whether name begins with "A":","options":["name.substring("A")","name.startsWith("A")","name.charAt(0) == "A"","name.equals("A")"],"correct":1,"explain":"startsWith is the direct method. Note that charAt returns a char, so comparing it to the String "A" would not compile."},{"id":"zya3_21_5","topic":"Index of a name","text":"names.indexOf("Ana") returns -1 when:","options":[""Ana" is first",""Ana" is not present","The list is sorted",""Ana" is last"],"correct":1,"explain":"-1 is the conventional not-found result across Java search methods."},{"id":"zya3_21_6","topic":"Trimming input","text":"Comparing user input safely often requires:","options":["Calling length() to confirm the input is not empty","Calling trim() to remove stray leading and trailing spaces","Using == so the exact characters must match","Casting the input to a char array before comparing"],"correct":1,"explain":""Ana " does not equal "Ana", which is a very common source of failed matches on typed input."},{"id":"zya3_21_7","topic":"Empty name check","text":"To test whether name is empty:","options":["name == ""","name.isEmpty()","name.length == 0","name.equals(null)"],"correct":1,"explain":"isEmpty() is clearest. length() == 0 also works — but note length is a method for Strings, not a field."},{"id":"zya3_21_8","topic":"Search result reporting","text":"After a failed search the program should:","options":["Return the first element as a safe default","Throw an exception the caller must handle","Silently continue with an empty result","Report clearly that the name was not found"],"correct":3,"explain":"A silent no-op leaves the user unable to distinguish "not found" from "the program did not run"."},{"id":"zya3_21_9","topic":"Comparing for order","text":"name1.compareTo(name2) returns a negative number when:","options":["name1 comes after name2 alphabetically","the two names are exactly equal","name1 comes before name2 alphabetically","name1 is shorter than name2"],"correct":2,"explain":"Negative means before, zero means equal, positive means after — the contract Collections.sort relies on."},{"id":"fs_z3_21_1","topic":"trim then compare","text":"What does this print?

String input = "  Ada  ";
input = input.trim();
System.out.println(input.equalsIgnoreCase("ADA"));","options":["false","Ada","Compiler error","true"],"correct":3,"explain":"trim strips the leading and trailing spaces, leaving "Ada", and equalsIgnoreCase matches it against "ADA" regardless of case. Without the trim, the surrounding spaces would defeat the comparison — normalise the input, then test it."},{"id":"fs_z3_21_2","topic":"Lowercase then search","text":"What does this print?

String name = "MADdie";
System.out.println(name.toLowerCase().indexOf("ad"));","options":["-1","0","2","1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Lowercasing first gives "maddie", where "ad" appears starting at index 1. Searching the original String instead would miss, because indexOf is case-sensitive and "MADdie" contains "AD", not "ad" — lowercase first, then search."},{"id":"fs_z3_21_3","topic":"Case-insensitive prefix test","text":"startsWith is case-sensitive. Which expression tests whether String s begins with the letter a in either case?","options":["s.startsWith("a" ) || s.startsWith("a".toUpperCase().toLowerCase())","s.startsWithIgnoreCase("a")","s.startsWith("a").toLowerCase()","s.toLowerCase().startsWith("a")"],"correct":3,"explain":"There is no startsWithIgnoreCase in the String API, so the technique is to normalise first: lowercase the whole String, then test the prefix. The second option lowercases an uppercased "a" back to "a" — both its tests are identical, so it still misses "A". The last does not compile: startsWith returns a boolean."},{"id":"fs_z3_21_4","topic":"isEmpty vs isBlank","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println("   ".isEmpty() + " " + "   ".isBlank());","options":["true true","false false","true false","false true"],"correct":3,"explain":"isEmpty is true only when the length is 0, and three spaces have length 3, so it returns false. isBlank is true when the String is empty or contains only whitespace, so it returns true. The pair distinguishes "no characters" from "nothing visible"."},{"id":"zf603","topic":"","text":"String target = "Kim"; String guest = "Kim"; if (guest.equals(target)) System.out.println("found"); else System.out.println("absent"); prints:","options":["absent","found","Kim","true"],"correct":1,"explain":"equals matches the contents exactly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf604","topic":"","text":"String target2 = "Kim"; String guest2 = "kim"; if (guest2.equals(target2)) System.out.println("yes"); else System.out.println("no"); prints:","options":["yes","kim","no","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Lowercase k fails the case-sensitive comparison.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf605","topic":"","text":"To accept any capitalization of a name, use:","options":["==","compareTo alone","toUpperCase with ==","equalsIgnoreCase"],"correct":3,"explain":"equalsIgnoreCase is built for exactly this.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf606","topic":"","text":"String n1 = "Ada"; String n2 = "Ben"; if (n1.compareTo(n2) < 0) System.out.println(n1 + " first"); else System.out.println(n2 + " first"); prints:","options":["Ada first","Ben first","Ada Ben","negative"],"correct":0,"explain":"Ada sorts before Ben, so compareTo is negative and the first branch runs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf607","topic":"","text":"A name search that also reports WHERE the name is in a comma list uses:","options":["equals","indexOf","length","charAt only"],"correct":1,"explain":"indexOf returns the position of the match, or -1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf608","topic":"","text":"String list = "Ana,Raj,Ed"; System.out.println(list.contains("Raj")); prints:","options":["false","1","true","Raj"],"correct":2,"explain":"The substring appears, so contains reports true.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf609","topic":"","text":"The bug in if (name == "Ada") System.out.println("hello Ada"); is:","options":["a missing loop","Ada is a keyword","println cannot take strings","reference comparison"],"correct":3,"explain":"Content checks need equals; == may be false for a runtime-built "Ada".","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf610","topic":"","text":"String entry = " Lee "; if (entry.trim().equals("Lee")) System.out.println("match"); else System.out.println("mismatch"); prints:","options":["mismatch","Lee","error","match"],"correct":3,"explain":"trim strips the surrounding spaces, letting the content match.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf611","topic":"","text":"Checking a name is non-empty BEFORE searching, as in !name.isEmpty() && ...:","options":["guards against meaningless empty input","is required by the compiler","slows the search","changes the alphabet"],"correct":0,"explain":"Validation-then-work is the reliable ordering.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf612","topic":"","text":"String v = "Vega"; if (v.startsWith("V")) System.out.println("V-section"); else System.out.println("elsewhere"); prints:","options":["elsewhere","V-section","Vega","V"],"correct":1,"explain":"The name starts with V, so the prefix test passes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf613","topic":"","text":"If BOTH first AND last name must match a record, the test is:","options":["first.equals(f) || last.equals(l)","first == f && last == l","first.equals(f) && last.equals(l)","first + last"],"correct":2,"explain":"Two required matches join with &&, each using equals.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u1/z1_2":[{"id":"n_n1_2_a","topic":"Output","text":"int a = 7, b = 2; System.out.println(a / b); prints:","options":["3","3.5","3.0","4"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both operands are int, so Java does integer division and throws away the fraction. 7/2 is 3, not 3.5 — the decimal is discarded, not rounded."},{"id":"n_n1_2_b","topic":"Output","text":"System.out.println(7 / 2.0); prints:","options":["3.0","4.0","3.5","3"],"correct":2,"explain":"One operand is a double, so the int is promoted and real division happens. Mixing in a single 2.0 is the usual way to avoid accidental integer division."},{"id":"n_n1_2_c","topic":"Output","text":"int n = 9; System.out.println(n % 4); prints:","options":["2","2.25","0","1"],"correct":3,"explain":"% is the remainder operator. 9 = 4*2 + 1, so the remainder is 1."},{"id":"n_n1_2_d","topic":"Output","text":"int i = 3; System.out.println(i++ + " " + i); prints:","options":["4 4","3 3","4 3","3 4"],"correct":3,"explain":"i++ is post-increment: it yields the OLD value (3) and then makes i 4. By the time the second part is evaluated i is already 4."},{"id":"n_n1_2_e","topic":"Output","text":"int i = 3; System.out.println(++i + " " + i); prints:","options":["3 4","4 3","3 3","4 4"],"correct":3,"explain":"++i is pre-increment: it increments first and yields the NEW value. Both parts therefore see 4."},{"id":"n_n1_2_f","topic":"Types","text":"Which statement does NOT compile?","options":["double d = 3;","int y = (int) 3.5;","double e = 3.5;","int x = 3.5;"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java will widen int to double automatically (double d = 3 is fine) but never narrows double to int silently, because that loses data. It needs the explicit cast (int)."},{"id":"n_n1_2_g","topic":"Output","text":"System.out.println(10 / 4 * 4); prints:","options":["10","10.0","9","8"],"correct":3,"explain":"Left to right with equal precedence: 10/4 is 2 by integer division, then 2*4 is 8. Integer division early in an expression is where the 2 is lost."},{"id":"n_n1_2_h","topic":"Output","text":"int x = 5; System.out.println("Value: " + x); prints:","options":["Value: + 5","5Value:","Compile error","Value: 5"],"correct":3,"explain":"When either side of + is a String, + means concatenation, and the int is converted to its text form."},{"id":"zf1","topic":"","text":"Execution of a Java program begins in:","options":["the class constructor","the main method","the first import statement","the first method defined in the file"],"correct":1,"explain":"The JVM looks for public static void main(String[] args) and starts there.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2","topic":"","text":"The difference between System.out.println and System.out.print is:","options":["print is for numbers, println is for text","println prints faster than print","println moves to a new line after printing","print can only be used once per program"],"correct":2,"explain":"println appends a newline; print leaves the cursor on the same line.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf3","topic":"","text":"Every Java statement must end with:","options":["a period","a colon","a closing brace","a semicolon"],"correct":3,"explain":"The semicolon terminates a statement; forgetting it is a compile error.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4","topic":"","text":"Java source code files use the file extension:","options":[".java",".class",".exe",".txt"],"correct":0,"explain":"Source is .java; the compiler produces .class bytecode files.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf5","topic":"","text":"The Java compiler translates source code into:","options":["machine code for one specific processor","bytecode for the JVM","plain English pseudocode","HTML for the browser"],"correct":1,"explain":"javac produces .class bytecode, which any JVM can execute.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf6","topic":"","text":"The job of the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) is to:","options":["translate Java into Python","edit source code","execute compiled bytecode","manage the source-code files on disk"],"correct":2,"explain":"The JVM runs .class bytecode, which is what makes Java portable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf7","topic":"","text":"What is the output? System.out.print("Go"); System.out.print("Team");","options":["Go Team","Go then Team on two lines","Team Go","GoTeam"],"correct":3,"explain":"print does not add a newline or a space, so the two outputs run together.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf8","topic":"","text":"What is the output? System.out.println("Hi"); System.out.println("There");","options":["Hi and There on two separate lines","HiThere on one line","Hi There on one line","There then Hi"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each println ends its line, so the second string starts on a new line.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf9","topic":"","text":"A variable declaration such as int count; tells the compiler to:","options":["print the word count","set aside a named spot in memory","create a new class named count","run the program from that line"],"correct":1,"explain":"Declaring gives the variable a name and a type so memory can be set aside.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf10","topic":"","text":"Programs must be compiled before running because:","options":["source code takes too much disk space","the processor cannot execute source text directly","the compiler adds comments automatically","running uncompiled code would skip the main method"],"correct":1,"explain":"Hardware executes low-level instructions; the compiler bridges the gap.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf11","topic":"","text":"In public static void main(String[] args), the word void means:","options":["main takes no arguments","main is empty","main returns no value","main cannot use variables"],"correct":2,"explain":"void is the return type: main performs work but returns nothing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf12","topic":"","text":"A statement like x = 5; is best described as:","options":["a declaration","a comparison","a method definition","an assignment statement"],"correct":3,"explain":"It stores the value 5 into the already-declared variable x.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf13","topic":"","text":"Which line prints the number 42 (not the text "42 ")?","options":["System.out.println(42);","System.out.println("42 ");","print(42)","System.println(42);"],"correct":0,"explain":"prin