"nothing - ! needs numbers","whether passed is 1"],"correct":1,"explain":"! flips the stored result: not passed = failed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf509","topic":"","text":"A good boolean variable name looks like:","options":["flag1","x","isEmpty or hasLicense","data"],"correct":2,"explain":"is/has names read like the question the value answers.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf510","topic":"","text":"boolean a2 = true; a2 = !a2; a2 = !a2; System.out.println(a2); prints:","options":["false","!true","true","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Two flips return to the original value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf511","topic":"","text":"Storing a comparison in a named boolean, like boolean eligible = age >= 18 && hasId;:","options":["slows the program down","is required by the compiler","changes the logic","names the meaning and allows reuse"],"correct":3,"explain":"The name explains WHAT the condition means - self-documenting code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf512","topic":"","text":"The default value printed for an uninitialized boolean FIELD of a class is:","options":["false","true","null","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fields get defaults (false for boolean); local variables get none.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_13":[{"id":"zy3_13_4_tuw1_d286_u2_1","topic":"equals contract","text":"The equals()/hashCode() contract requires that if two objects are equal, then their hash codes must be:","options":["Ignored","Different","Equal","Both zero"],"correct":2,"explain":"Equal objects must return equal hash codes; otherwise hash-based collections like HashMap/HashSet behave incorrectly."},{"id":"zy3_13_5_tuw1_d286_u2_2","topic":"Default equals","text":"The default Object.equals() compares objects by:","options":["Field-by-field comparison of the contents","Reference identity (same object in memory)","The value returned by hashCode() for each","The order the objects were created in"],"correct":1,"explain":"Object.equals() returns true only for the same instance (==); to compare by value you must override it."},{"id":"zy3_13_7_tuw1_d286_u2_6","topic":"Default hashCode","text":"If you override equals() but not hashCode(), two "equal" objects may:","options":["Be treated as equal everywhere, including in a HashMap","Land in different buckets and be treated as distinct","Cause a compile error when added to a collection","Throw an exception the first time they are hashed"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unequal hash codes for equal objects scatter them across buckets, so a HashSet/HashMap fails to recognize them as the same key."},{"id":"zy3_13_8_tuw1_d286_u2_7","topic":"Reflexive","text":"The equals() contract requires it to be reflexive, meaning:","options":["x.equals(null) is true","x.equals(x) is always true","equals ignores type","equals is random"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reflexivity: an object must equal itself. The contract also requires symmetry, transitivity, and consistency."},{"id":"zy3_13_9_tuw1_d286_u2_8","topic":"Null handling","text":"A correct equals() implementation, for a non-null x, must make x.equals(null) return:","options":["false","null","an exception","true"],"correct":0,"explain":"By contract, x.equals(null) must be false and must not throw; well-written equals checks for null and type first."},{"id":"zy3_13_10_q3","topic":"Return value","text":"In compareTo, returning a NEGATIVE value means:","options":["this comes before other","this comes after other","invalid","they are equal"],"correct":0,"explain":"Negative return = this object should be ordered before the argument.","distractors":{"0":"Negative means before, not after.","2":"Correct.","3":"Zero means equal, not negative."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_13_11_q10","topic":"Equal elements","text":"If compareTo returns 0, the two objects are:","options":["Always equal by equals()","Definitely the same object","Errors","Equal in sort order"],"correct":3,"explain":"Zero means equal for ordering purposes. It's recommended (but not required) that this be consistent with equals().","distractors":{"0":"Same order doesn't mean same reference.","2":"Correct.","3":"Consistency with equals is advised, not guaranteed automatically."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_13_1","topic":"compareTo sign","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println("apple".compareTo("banana") < 0);","options":["-1","Compiler error","true","false"],"correct":2,"explain":""apple" comes before "banana" in dictionary order, so compareTo returns a negative int, and negative < 0 is true. Only the sign of the result is meaningful, which is why the test is written < 0 rather than == -1."},{"id":"fs_z3_13_2","topic":"equals vs equalsIgnoreCase","text":"What does this print?

String a = "Hello";
String b = "hello";
System.out.println(a.equals(b) + " " + a.equalsIgnoreCase(b));","options":["false false","true false","false true","true true"],"correct":2,"explain":"equals is case-sensitive, and 'H' differs from 'h', so it returns false. equalsIgnoreCase compares the contents ignoring letter case, so it returns true. The two methods answer different questions."},{"id":"fs_z3_13_3","topic":"Only the sign is meaningful","text":"Why is the test s1.compareTo(s2) == -1 considered fragile, while s1.compareTo(s2) < 0 is correct?","options":["== cannot be used to compare two int values in Java","compareTo returns a boolean, so comparing it to -1 does not compile","compareTo only promises a negative, zero, or positive result; the exact magnitude is an implementation detail","compareTo can never return exactly -1"],"correct":2,"explain":"The contract of compareTo specifies only the sign: negative means the receiver comes first. The specific number returned may vary, so code must test < 0, > 0, or == 0 rather than expecting -1 or 1."},{"id":"fs_z3_13_4","topic":"Lexicographic order and case","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println("Zebra".compareTo("apple") < 0);","options":["Compiler error","true","false","0"],"correct":1,"explain":"Lexicographic order uses character codes, and every uppercase letter sorts before every lowercase letter — 'Z' is 90 while 'a' is 97. So "Zebra" compares as coming before "apple", and the result is negative, making the expression true."},{"id":"fs_z3_13_5","topic":"Literal-first equals","text":"A program tests user input with "quit".equals(command) instead of command.equals("quit"). What does the literal-first form gain?","options":["It runs faster because literals are pooled","It also matches "QUIT" and "Quit" automatically","Nothing; the two forms behave identically in every case","It cannot throw a NullPointerException when command is null; it simply returns false"],"correct":3,"explain":"Calling a method on a null reference throws NullPointerException, so command.equals("quit") crashes when command is null. The literal "quit" is never null, so "quit".equals(command) safely returns false instead. Case handling is unchanged — that would need equalsIgnoreCase."},{"id":"fs_z3_13_6","topic":"Choosing the comparison method","text":"A program must decide whether one String comes before another in dictionary order, for sorting. Which method answers that question?","options":["contains, which checks the relative ordering","compareTo, using the sign of its int result","equals, which returns true when the first comes earlier","equalsIgnoreCase, which returns the alphabetical distance"],"correct":1,"explain":"Only compareTo reports ordering: a negative result means the receiver comes first, zero means equal, positive means it comes later. equals and equalsIgnoreCase return only true or false for content matches, and contains tests substring presence — none of them can order two Strings."},{"id":"zf513","topic":"","text":"The correct way to test whether two strings have the same characters is:","options":["s1 == s2","s1.equals(s2)","s1 = s2","s1.compare(s2)"],"correct":1,"explain":"equals checks content; == checks whether both references point at the same object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf514","topic":"","text":""apple".equals("Apple") evaluates to:","options":["true","0","false","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"equals is case-sensitive; the capital A differs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf515","topic":"","text":""apple".equalsIgnoreCase("Apple") evaluates to:","options":["false","APPLE","error","true"],"correct":3,"explain":"equalsIgnoreCase treats the two cases as matching.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf516","topic":"","text":""cat".compareTo("dog") returns:","options":["a negative number","a positive number","zero","the string "catdog""],"correct":0,"explain":"c comes before d, so the receiver is 'smaller': negative.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf517","topic":"","text":""pear".compareTo("pear") returns:","options":["a negative number","0","a positive number","true"],"correct":1,"explain":"Identical strings compare as zero.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf518","topic":"","text":""zoo".compareTo("apple") returns:","options":["a negative number","zero","a positive number","false"],"correct":2,"explain":"z sorts after a, so the receiver is greater: positive.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf519","topic":"","text":"In compareTo ordering, "Zebra" compared to "apple" is:","options":["positive - z beats a","zero","an exception","negative - uppercase sorts first"],"correct":3,"explain":"'Z' is code 90 and 'a' is 97, so Zebra sorts first. Case matters.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf520","topic":"","text":"Why can s1 == s2 sometimes print true for equal strings and false other times?","options":["== flips a coin","equals changes ==","strings compare randomly","sharing one object is not guaranteed"],"correct":3,"explain":"Literals may share a pooled object; strings built at runtime usually do not.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf521","topic":"","text":"String pw = "aBc1"; if (pw.equals("abc1")) System.out.println("ok"); else System.out.println("denied"); prints:","options":["denied","ok","abc1","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The capital B breaks the case-sensitive match.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf522","topic":"","text":"Sorting names alphabetically in code relies on which method?","options":["equals","compareTo","length","charAt"],"correct":1,"explain":"compareTo supplies the less/equal/greater answer that ordering needs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf523","topic":"","text":"A safe way to compare a possibly-null variable s against a literal is:","options":["s.equals("yes")","s == "yes"",""yes".equals(s)","s.compareTo("yes") alone"],"correct":2,"explain":"Calling equals ON the literal cannot throw a NullPointerException; s.equals(...) would if s is null.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf524","topic":"","text":""bat".compareTo("ball") is positive because:","options":["bat is shorter","b beats b","a beats a","t vs l decides, and t comes after l"],"correct":3,"explain":"compareTo scans left to right and decides at the FIRST differing character.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u3/z3_14":[{"id":"zy3_14_2_c20","topic":"substring from","text":""Hello, World".substring(7) returns:","options":[""World"",""Hello"","", World"",""orld""],"correct":0,"explain":"One-arg substring runs from index 7 to the end → "World".","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_3_px286_op_12","topic":"What is printed","text":"What is printed?  String s = "hello";  System.out.println(s.length());","options":["5","4","6","hello"],"correct":0,"explain":""hello" has 5 characters, so length() returns 5.","distractors":{"0":"4 is an off-by-one error; length() counts every character, not the last index.","2":"4 is an off-by-one error; length() counts every character, not the last index.","3":"4 is an off-by-one error; length() counts every character, not the last index."},"_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_4_px286_op_14","topic":"What does  "HelloWorld"","text":"What does  "HelloWorld".substring(5)  return?","options":[""Hello"",""HelloW"",""World"",""oWorld""],"correct":2,"explain":"substring(5) returns everything from index 5 to the end: "World".","distractors":{"0":""Hello" is the part BEFORE index 5; substring(5) keeps the tail, not the head.","2":""Hello" is the part BEFORE index 5; substring(5) keeps the tail, not the head.","3":""Hello" is the part BEFORE index 5; substring(5) keeps the tail, not the head."},"_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_5_d18","topic":"Short-circuit","text":"if(s != null && s.length() > 0) is safe because:","options":["&& evaluates both sides and then combines them","&& converts null to an empty string automatically","&& skips the right side when the left is false","&& catches the NullPointerException internally"],"correct":2,"explain":"&& short-circuits: if s != null is false, length() never runs — no NPE.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_6_j13","topic":"length vs length()","text":"array.length differs from String.length() because:","options":["arrays use a method","arrays use a field (no parentheses)","they are identical","arrays have none"],"correct":1,"explain":"array.length is a field; String.length() is a method.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_7_h2_286_6","topic":"Downcast","_dom":"inherit","difficulty":"hard","text":"Object o = "hi"; String s = (String) o; System.out.println(s.length()); prints:","options":["hi","0","error","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"o actually references a String, so the cast succeeds and length() returns 2.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_8_lg_D286_0_38","topic":"substring","text":"str.substring(start, end) returns:","options":["The substring from start up to (not including) end","The substring from start up to and including end","The substring of length end starting at start","The substring from index end back to start"],"correct":0,"explain":"substring includes start and excludes end.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy3_14_9_lg_D286_2_7","topic":"Strings","text":"&quot;Java&quot;.substring(1, 3) returns:","options":["va","Jav","av","Ja"],"correct":2,"explain":"substring(1,3) takes indices 1 and 2: av.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z3_14_1","topic":"charAt indexing","text":"What does this print?

String s = "Hello";
System.out.println(s.charAt(1));","options":["He","l","e","H"],"correct":2,"explain":"Indexes start at 0, so charAt(0) is 'H' and charAt(1) is 'e'. Reading charAt(1) as "the first character" is the classic off-by-one."},{"id":"fs_z3_14_2","topic":"Two-argument substring","text":"What does this print?

String s = "programming";
System.out.println(s.substring(3, 7));","options":["gram","gramm","rogr","ramm"],"correct":0,"explain":"substring(3, 7) takes characters from index 3 up to but NOT including index 7: 'g','r','a','m'. The half-open range means the result always has length 7 - 3 = 4."},{"id":"fs_z3_14_3","topic":"Immutability: unassigned result","text":"What does this print?

String s = "java";
s.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(s);","options":["Compiler error","java","JAVA","Java"],"correct":1,"explain":"Strings are immutable: toUpperCase returns a NEW String and leaves s untouched. Because the result is never assigned, the call does nothing observable — the number one String bug. Keeping the result requires s = s.toUpperCase();"},{"id":"fs_z3_14_4","topic":"Index out of bounds","text":"String s = "cat"; — what happens when the program evaluates s.charAt(3)?","options":["It fails to compile because 3 exceeds the length","It throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException at runtime","It returns 't', the last character","It returns a space character"],"correct":1,"explain":""cat" has length 3, so the valid indexes are 0 through 2 — the last valid index is length() - 1. An index equal to length() is out of bounds, which is a runtime exception, not a compile error."},{"id":"fs_z3_14_5","topic":"Length of a substring","text":"For any String s long enough, how many characters does s.substring(2, 6) return?","options":["It depends on the contents of s","4","5","3"],"correct":1,"explain":"substring includes the start index and excludes the end index, so the length is always end - start: 6 - 2 = 4. That no-adjustment arithmetic is the practical payoff of the half-open convention."},{"id":"fs_z3_14_6","topic":"One-argument substring","text":"What does this print?

String s = "notebook";
System.out.println(s.substring(4));","options":["ook","book","ebook","note"],"correct":1,"explain":"The one-argument form takes everything from the given index to the end. Index 4 of "notebook" is 'b' (n=0, o=1, t=2, e=3, b=4), so the result is "book"."},{"id":"zf525","topic":"","text":"String s = "compute"; System.out.println(s.length()); prints:","options":["7","6","8","compute"],"correct":0,"explain":""compute" has 7 characters.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf526","topic":"","text":"String s = "compute"; System.out.println(s.charAt(0)); prints:","options":["o","c","0","e"],"correct":1,"explain":"Indexing starts at 0, so charAt(0) is the first character.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf527","topic":"","text":"String s = "compute"; System.out.println(s.charAt(3)); prints:","options":["m","u","p","4"],"correct":2,"explain":"Counting from 0: positions 0,1,2,3 hold c, o, m, p - charAt(3) is p.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf528","topic":"","text":"The LAST character of a string s is:","options":["s.charAt(s.length())","s.charAt(-1)","s.last()","s.charAt(s.length() - 1)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Indexes run 0 to length-1; s.charAt(s.length()) throws.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf529","topic":"","text":"String w = "planet"; System.out.println(w.substring(1, 4)); prints:","options":["lan","lane","pla","ane"],"correct":0,"explain":"substring(1,4) takes indexes 1,2,3 - the end index is EXCLUDED.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf530","topic":"","text":"String w = "planet"; System.out.println(w.substring(3)); prints:","options":["net","pla","anet","t"],"correct":0,"explain":"One-argument substring runs from index 3 to the end.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf531","topic":"","text":"String t = "banana"; System.out.println(t.indexOf("an")); prints:","options":["2","1","3","0"],"correct":1,"explain":"The first "an" starts at index 1 (b-a-n).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf532","topic":"","text":"String t = "banana"; System.out.println(t.indexOf("z")); prints:","options":["0","6","-1","an exception is thrown"],"correct":2,"explain":"indexOf returns -1 when the text is absent - no exception.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf533","topic":"","text":"Calling s.charAt(10) on the 6-character string "planet" causes:","options":["a compile error","the last character","an empty char","a StringIndexOutOfBoundsException"],"correct":3,"explain":"Out-of-range indexes are a runtime exception, not caught by the compiler.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf534","topic":"","text":"String u = "code"; System.out.println(u.isEmpty()); prints:","options":["false","true","0","4"],"correct":0,"explain":"isEmpty is true only for the zero-length string "".","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf535","topic":"","text":""streams".substring(2, 5).length() equals:","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":1,"explain":"substring(2,5) covers indexes 2,3,4 - always end minus start characters.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u4/z4_1":[{"id":"zya4_1_0","topic":"Loop purpose","text":"A loop is used to:","options":["Repeat a block of statements","Define a method","Declare a variable","Choose between two paths"],"correct":0,"explain":"Branching chooses; looping repeats. Both are control flow, but they answer different questions."},{"id":"zya4_1_1","topic":"Loop body","text":"The statements a loop repeats are called:","options":["The loop body","The iterator","The header","The condition"],"correct":0,"explain":"The body is what runs each pass; the condition decides whether another pass happens."},{"id":"zya4_1_2","topic":"Iteration","text":"One execution of the loop body is called:","options":["An iteration","A recursion","A branch","A cycle"],"correct":0,"explain":"Ten iterations means the body ran ten times."},{"id":"zya4_1_3","topic":"Condition checked when","text":"A while loop checks its condition:","options":["After each iteration, including the first","Only once, before the loop begins","Only when a continue statement is reached","Before each iteration, including the first"],"correct":3,"explain":"This is why a while loop can run zero times, unlike a do-while."},{"id":"zya4_1_4","topic":"Infinite loop cause","text":"A loop runs forever most often because:","options":["The condition was written with < instead of <=","The loop variable was declared outside the loop","Nothing in the body changes the value the condition tests","The body contains a continue statement"],"correct":2,"explain":"The condition must eventually become false, which requires the body to move it toward that."},{"id":"zy4_1_3_q5","topic":"while trace","text":"int i=0; while(i&lt;4){ i+=2; } How many iterations?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"correct":2,"explain":"i=0: 0<4 true, i=2. i=2: 2<4 true, i=4. i=4: false. 2 iterations.","distractors":{"0":"1 would stop after i becomes 2.","1":"Initial condition is true.","2":"4 would require i+=1."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy4_1_5_px286_for_11","topic":"Which statement immediately ends the CURRENT ite","text":"Which statement immediately ends the CURRENT iteration and moves to the next?","options":["exit","return","continue","break"],"correct":2,"explain":"continue skips the rest of the current iteration and proceeds to the next.","distractors":{"0":"break ends the ENTIRE loop, not just the current iteration.","2":"break ends the ENTIRE loop, not just the current iteration.","3":"break ends the ENTIRE loop, not just the current iteration."},"_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z4_1_1","topic":"While loop trace","text":"What does this print?

int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.print(i);
    i++;
}","options":["12345","012345","Nothing — it loops forever","01234"],"correct":3,"explain":"The condition is tested before each pass, so the body runs with i = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4; when i reaches 5 the test i < 5 fails and the loop ends. The i++ update is what makes the condition eventually false."},{"id":"fs_z4_1_2","topic":"Loop parts","text":"Every loop, regardless of its syntax, is built from which three moving parts?","options":["Header, body, footer","Initialisation, condition, update","Declaration, body, return","Condition, break, continue"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every loop initialises a variable, tests a condition before each pass, and updates something the condition depends on. Missing any one of the three produces a loop that never starts, never stops, or starts in the wrong place."},{"id":"fs_z4_1_3","topic":"Zero-iteration while","text":"What does this print?

int i = 10;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.print(i);
    i++;
}
System.out.print("done");","options":["1011121314done","Nothing — infinite loop","done","10done"],"correct":2,"explain":"A while loop checks its condition before the first pass. 10 < 5 is already false, so the body never runs at all and only "done" prints. A loop that runs zero times is often correct behaviour, not a bug."},{"id":"fs_z4_1_4","topic":"do-while guarantee","text":"Which loop form guarantees its body executes at least once?","options":["for","The enhanced for loop","do-while","while"],"correct":2,"explain":"A do-while tests its condition after the body, so the body always runs at least once. while and for test before the first pass and may run zero times."},{"id":"fs_z4_1_5","topic":"do-while trace","text":"What does this print?

int i = 0;
do {
    System.out.print(i);
