intln(max10); prints:","options":["-9","-5","0","-2"],"correct":3,"explain":"The champion scan works with for-each; the least-negative value wins.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf830","topic":"","text":"Break inside a for-each, as in for (int x : arr) { if (x == 7) break; }:","options":["does not compile","skips one element","restarts","is allowed and exits the loop early"],"correct":3,"explain":"break and continue work the same as in any loop.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf831","topic":"","text":"boolean any10 = false; for (int x : new int[]{1, 3, 5}) if (x % 2 == 0) any10 = true; System.out.println(any10); prints:","options":["false","true","0","error"],"correct":0,"explain":"No even values exist, so the flag never flips.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf832","topic":"","text":"Choosing between for-each and the counted for comes down to:","options":["which is newer","whether the task needs indexes or writes","random preference","for-each being always faster"],"correct":1,"explain":"Read-only value scans: for-each. Position-aware or mutating passes: indexes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf833","topic":"","text":"for (char ch10 : "java".toCharArray()) if (ch10 == 'a') count10++; With count10 starting at 0, it ends at:","options":["1","3","2","4"],"correct":2,"explain":"toCharArray lets for-each walk a string; the two a-characters count.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_1":[{"id":"zya6_1_0","topic":"Method definition parts","text":"A method definition consists of:","options":["Return type, name, and body only","Modifiers, name, and parameter list only","Modifiers, return type, name, parameter list, and body","Name, parameter list, and a return statement"],"correct":2,"explain":"public static int add(int a, int b) { ... } shows every part in order."},{"id":"zya6_1_1","topic":"void meaning","text":"A method declared void:","options":["Returns null","Returns 0","Returns nothing","Cannot be called"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is called for its effect — printing, modifying state — not for a value."},{"id":"zya6_1_2","topic":"Calling a method","text":"A static method add in the same class is called as:","options":["this.add(2, 3);","new add(2, 3);","add(2, 3);","call add(2, 3);"],"correct":2,"explain":"Just the name and arguments. this. is for instance methods and new is for constructors."},{"id":"zya6_1_3","topic":"Return statement","text":"A non-void method must:","options":["Return a value on at least one path","Return a value only when no exception occurs","Declare its type as void if it may not return","Return a value on every path through the method"],"correct":3,"explain":"A path that reaches the end without returning is a compile error: missing return statement."},{"id":"zya6_1_4","topic":"Method name convention","text":"Methods are conventionally named:","options":["With underscores","With a noun in PascalCase","With a verb in camelCase","In ALL_CAPS"],"correct":2,"explain":"calculateTotal, getName, isValid — methods do things, so they read as actions."},{"id":"zy6_1_4_bp_D286_u19_20","topic":"Return type alone","text":"Two area methods differing only by return type would be:","options":["valid overloading (the return type distinguishes them)","valid overriding (the child narrows the return type)","valid, provided one of them is declared static","invalid (return type alone is not enough)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Overloads must differ in parameters, not just return type.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z6_1_1","topic":"Method call and return value","text":"What does this print?

