ts negatives shows the benefit of:","options":["polymorphism","encapsulation","inheritance","static members"],"correct":1,"explain":"Guarding writes through a setter lets the class validate its data. Controlled access enables validation.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy6_3_3_n25","topic":"Benefit","text":"The main benefit of inheritance is:","options":["slower code","removing methods","code reuse and hierarchical design","hiding all data"],"correct":2,"explain":"Inheritance enables reuse and a class hierarchy.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy6_3_6_q1","topic":"Generic purpose","text":"What is the main benefit of generics like ArrayList&lt;String>?","options":["Faster execution because casts are removed at runtime","Automatic conversion between any two element types","Compile-time type safety and no casting","Runtime checks that reject the wrong element type"],"correct":2,"explain":"Generics let the compiler enforce the element type at compile time and return already-typed elements, so no cast is needed.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy6_3_7_tuw1_d286_u1_8","topic":"Type safety benefit","text":"A key benefit of List&lt;String&gt; over a raw List is that:","options":["Adding a non-String is caught when the program runs","Adding a non-String is caught by the compiler","Elements are stored more compactly in memory","The list can hold only one element at a time"],"correct":1,"explain":"A typed list rejects incompatible elements at compile time, so bugs surface early rather than as runtime cast failures."},{"id":"zy6_3_9_fresh_D286_33","topic":"Generics > Type-Safe Collections","text":"What is the main benefit of using a generic collection such as a List parameterized to hold String?","options":["Compile-time type safety that prevents adding the wrong type","Faster element access than a raw untyped collection","Automatic resizing that a raw collection cannot do","Runtime type checks that throw on the wrong type"],"correct":0,"explain":"Generics enforce element types at compile time, avoiding unsafe casts and runtime ClassCastExceptions."},{"id":"fs_z6_3_1","topic":"Reuse","text":"The same six-line tax calculation appears in three different places in a program. Moving it into a single calculateTax(salary) method primarily achieves:","options":["Faster execution, because method calls are optimised away","Automatic validation of the salary argument","A smaller memory footprint for the JVM","Reuse - one definition, so a later fix is made in one place"],"correct":3,"explain":"Reuse means one copy of the logic called from many places. Duplicated code means a fix must be applied five times, and typically four of them are correct."},{"id":"fs_z6_3_2","topic":"Abstraction","text":"Being able to call calculateTax(salary) without knowing how the tax brackets are implemented is an example of:","options":["Regression","Abstraction","Overloading","Decomposition"],"correct":1,"explain":"Abstraction is using a method through its name and contract rather than its implementation. Decomposition is the related but different idea of splitting a problem into smaller parts."},{"id":"fs_z6_3_3","topic":"Decomposition","text":"Splitting a large program into several small methods so that each piece can be written and checked on its own is called:","options":["Shadowing","Concatenation","Decomposition","Aliasing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Decomposition breaks a problem into parts small enough to solve and test separately, which is also what makes unit testing possible."},{"id":"fs_z6_3_4","topic":"Cohesion","text":"A method's job is described as "validates the input and writes it to the report file". According to the one-job-per-method rule, this description suggests that the method:","options":["should be overloaded with a second parameter list","should be moved inside main","should be split into two methods, one for each job","should be declared void"],"correct":2,"explain":"If describing what a method does needs the word "and", it is probably two methods. Each half is then independently reusable and independently testable."},{"id":"fs_z6_3_5","topic":"Naming methods","text":"Which name best follows the convention for a method that returns a boolean?","options":["NextCheck","next_check","hasNext","getNextOrFalse"],"correct":2,"explain":"Boolean methods are named as questions - isValid, hasNext - so that the call site reads like a sentence. Java also uses camelCase starting with a lowercase letter."},{"id":"fs_z6_3_6","topic":"Reuse in practice","text":"What does this print?

public static double areaOfRect(double w, double h) {
    return w * h;
}

double total = areaOfRect(2, 3) + areaOfRect(4, 5);
System.out.println(total);","options":["120.0","14.0","26.0","26"],"correct":2,"explain":"One definition is called twice: 2*3 is 6.0 and 4*5 is 20.0, giving 26.0. The result is a double, so println shows the decimal point."},{"id":"zf2023","topic":"","text":"The main reasons to define methods are:","options":["faster execution only","longer programs","fewer variables","reuse, organization, and easier testing"],"correct":3,"explain":"Write once call many, name the steps, test pieces in isolation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2024","topic":"","text":"Decomposing a program into methods means:","options":["splitting work into named pieces","deleting half the code","avoiding loops","merging everything into main"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each method does one job; main reads like an outline.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2025","topic":"","text":"A bug in duplicated code must be fixed:","options":["once anywhere","in every copy - which methods avoid","never","by the compiler"],"correct":1,"explain":"One method body means one fix; copy-paste means a hunt.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2026","topic":"","text":"A well-named method like computeMonthlyPay():","options":["hides the code forever","slows the program","documents WHAT happens at the call","confuses readers"],"correct":2,"explain":"The name is abstraction: callers read intent, not mechanics.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2027","topic":"","text":"Methods make testing easier because:","options":["tests become unnecessary","main gets longer","output disappears","each piece runs alone on known inputs"],"correct":3,"explain":"Feed the method sample arguments; compare returns to expected values.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2028","topic":"","text":""Abstraction" in the context of methods means:","options":["using name and contract, not internals","making code vague","removing all names","avoiding parameters"],"correct":0,"explain":"You call Math.sqrt without reading its source - same idea for your own methods.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2029","topic":"","text":"A 200-line main with repeated blocks is best improved by:","options":["adding comments only","extracting the repeats into methods","longer variable names","deleting the blocks"],"correct":1,"explain":"Extract, name, and call - the standard refactoring.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2030","topic":"","text":"Methods aid teamwork because:","options":["only one person may code","work can proceed in parallel","methods prevent merging","teams need one file"],"correct":1,"explain":"Clean boundaries let work proceed in parallel.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2031","topic":"","text":"If a calculation changes (say a new tax formula), method callers:","options":["must all be rewritten","must be deleted","are unaffected if the contract holds","stop compiling"],"correct":2,"explain":"Implementation can change behind a stable name and signature.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2032","topic":"","text":"A method should ideally:","options":["do as many tasks as possible","be at least 100 lines","avoid parameters","do one focused task"],"correct":3,"explain":"Single responsibility keeps methods nameable, testable, and reusable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2033","topic":"","text":"Turning the pseudocode steps of a plan into method names produces:","options":["a main that reads like the plan","slower code","a compile error","fewer tests"],"correct":0,"explain":"readInput(); computePay(); printReport(); - the outline IS the code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr7","topic":"Reasons for defining methods","text":"The same 12 lines of validation appear in four places in a program. Turning them into one method mainly:","options":["makes the program run four times faster","reduces the number of variables the program needs","removes the need to test that logic","means a fix has to be made in only one place"],"correct":3,"explain":"The main reason to define a method is removing duplication: one definition means one place to correct, and no risk of fixing three copies and missing the fourth.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr8","topic":"Reasons for defining methods","text":"Which is the best reason to split a long method into several smaller ones?","options":["Each piece can be named, understood and tested on its own","Smaller methods always execute faster than long ones","Java sets a hard limit on how long a method may be","It reduces the size of the compiled class file"],"correct":0,"explain":"Decomposition is about comprehension and testing, not speed. A named method also documents what that block of code is for.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_7":[{"id":"zya6_7_0","topic":"Unit test purpose","text":"A unit test verifies:","options":["That the whole program runs without crashing","That the code follows the team's style guide","That a method returns the expected result for a given input","That the program is fast enough for real data"],"correct":2,"explain":"It tests one unit — usually one method — in isolation."},{"id":"zya6_7_1","topic":"Test case content","text":"A good test case specifies:","options":["The input and the expected output","Only the output","The implementation","Only the input"],"correct":0,"explain":"Without a known expected value there is nothing to compare against."},{"id":"zya6_7_2","topic":"Testing boundaries","text":"The most valuable test inputs are:","options":["Boundaries","Random large values","Only positives","Typical middle values only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Bugs concentrate at boundaries; the middle of the range usually works by construction. Empty, zero, one element, maximum, negative."},{"id":"zya6_7_3","topic":"Test independence","text":"Each test should:","options":["Be able to run on its own","Share state","Run only once","Depend on the previous test"],"correct":0,"explain":"Order-dependent tests fail confusingly when one is changed or skipped."},{"id":"zya6_7_4","topic":"A failing test","text":"A test that fails means:","options":["The method is always the part that is wrong","The test should be deleted and rewritten","Either the method or the test expectation is wrong","The compiler rejected one of the two files"],"correct":2,"explain":"Assuming the code is right and "fixing" the test is how bugs get locked in. Investigate before changing code."},{"id":"zya6_7_5","topic":"Regression testing","text":"Re-running old tests after a change:","options":["Wastes time, since they passed once already","Catches changes that broke previously working behaviour","Confirms the new feature works as intended","Is only needed before the final submission"],"correct":1,"explain":"Most bugs introduced during a fix are caught this way."},{"id":"zy6_7_1_zb2","topic":"Border cases","text":"Good unit tests include border cases such as:","options":["only large values","0, negatives, and empty strings","no inputs","only typical values"],"correct":1,"explain":"Bugs hide at borders: 0, negative numbers, empty strings, extremes.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy6_7_2_lg_D286_1_56","topic":"Unit testing","text":"Testing one method at a time with expected-vs-actual comparisons is called:","options":["compiling","unit testing","debugging","integration testing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unit testing verifies individual methods in isolation.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy6_7_3_lg_D286_2_16","topic":"Unit test","text":"Your unit test expected 42 but the method returned 41. The test should:","options":["be deleted","recompile","report a failure","pass"],"correct":2,"explain":"Actual differs from expected, so the test fails.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z6_7_1","topic":"Boundary test cases","text":"What does this print?

public static boolean inRange(int n) {
    return n > 0 && n < 100;
}

System.out.println(inRange(0));
System.out.println(inRange(1));
System.out.println(inRange(100));","options":["false
true
false","true
true
true","false
false
false","true
true
false"],"correct":0,"explain":"These are the boundary tests: 0 and 100 are excluded by the strict comparisons and 1 is the first accepted value. Testing only 50 would never reveal an off-by-one bound."},{"id":"fs_z6_7_2","topic":"Choosing test inputs","text":"Tests of max(int[] a) already pass for {3, 9, 4}. Which additional inputs are the most valuable?","options":["three more arrays of three positive numbers","the same array {3, 9, 4} passed a second time","one array of a thousand random positive numbers","an empty array, a one-element array, and an all-negative array"],"correct":3,"explain":"Bugs live at the edges: empty input, a single element, and negatives each exercise a case the ordinary array never touches. More ordinary cases add almost no new information."},{"id":"fs_z6_7_3","topic":"Regression","text":"A test that passed yesterday starts failing after an unrelated change is made elsewhere in the program. This situation is called a:","options":["stub","regression","syntax error","boundary case"],"correct":1,"explain":"A regression is previously working behaviour that a later change has broken. Re-running the whole test set after every change is what catches it."},{"id":"fs_z6_7_4","topic":"Independent tests","text":"Why should each test case build its own input data rather than sharing one array across all of the tests?","options":["Because an array may only be passed to a method once","Because static methods cannot see arrays declared in main","Because separate arrays make the tests run faster","So that a failure or a mutation in one test cannot affect the others"],"correct":3,"explain":"Tests must be independent so one failure does not cascade into misleading failures. A method that modifies its array argument would otherwise corrupt every test that follows."},{"id":"fs_z6_7_5","topic":"A test that exposes a bug","text":"What does this print?

public static double average(int[] a) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        sum += x;
    }
    return sum / a.length;
}

int[] t = {1, 2};
System.out.println(average(t));","options":["0.5","1.0","1.5","1"],"correct":1,"explain":"sum and a.length are both int, so sum / a.length is integer division: 3 / 2 truncates to 1 and is then widened to 1.0. The test comparing against 1.5 is what reveals the missing cast."},{"id":"zf2067","topic":"","text":"A unit test of a method compares:","options":["two methods’ source code","execution speed","variable names","actual return vs expected value"],"correct":3,"explain":"Known input, known expected output, assert they match.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2068","topic":"","text":"A simple test harness is:","options":["a main calling it and checking results","a hardware device","a faster compiler","a loop with no body"],"correct":0,"explain":"Even a few if-checks in a test main catch regressions.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2069","topic":"","text":"Testing computeArea with width 0 checks:","options":["a typical case","a boundary case","nothing useful","the compiler"],"correct":1,"explain":"Zero, negatives, and maximums are where formulas and validation break.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2070","topic":"","text":"Each unit test should be:","options":["dependent on test order","independent of the other tests","run only once ever","combined into one giant test"],"correct":1,"explain":"Order-dependent tests hide failures and break when rearranged.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2071","topic":"","text":"If maxOf({3, 3, 3}) is untested, the risk is:","options":["nothing - ties are impossible","the compiler catches it","ties may silently be handled wrongly","arrays reject duplicates"],"correct":2,"explain":"Equal-value edge cases are classic silent-failure territory.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2072","topic":"","text":"A test that PASSES proves:","options":["the method is bug-free for all inputs","the code is fast","no more tests are needed","the method works for that specific input"],"correct":3,"explain":"Tests sample the input space; good tests sample its dangerous corners.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2073","topic":"","text":"After changing a method’s implementation, you should:","options":["rerun its existing tests","delete the tests","skip testing - it worked before","only test other methods"],"correct":0,"explain":"That is regression testing: confirming the change broke nothing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2074","topic":"","text":"Testing methods separately from the full program helps because:","options":["whole-program tests are illegal","a failure points at the one method","methods change randomly","it avoids compiling"],"correct":1,"explain":"Small test scope means small search space for the bug.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2075","topic":"","text":"Good test inputs for isEven include:","options":["one even number only","huge numbers only","even, odd, zero, and negative values","no inputs"],"correct":2,"explain":"Cover each behavior class and the boundaries between them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2076","topic":"","text":"Expected values in tests should come from:","options":["running the method itself","guessing","the compiler","hand computation or a trusted source"],"correct":3,"explain":"Deriving expectations FROM the code under test makes the test circular.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2077","topic":"","text":"A test for toF(100) expecting 212.0 that gets 212.00001 suggests using:","options":["a tolerance comparison for doubles","exact == comparison","integer casts","no tests for doubles"],"correct":0,"explain":"Floating-point outputs are asserted within an epsilon.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_8":[{"id":"zya6_8_0","topic":"Call stack","text":"When a method is called, Java:","options":["Copies the whole calling method onto the heap","Replaces the caller's frame with the callee's","Pushes a new stack frame holding its parameters and locals","Allocates the method's locals as static fields"],"correct":2,"explain":"The frame is popped on return, which is why locals do not survive the call."},{"id":"zya6_8_1","topic":"Argument passing","text":"Java passes arguments:","options":["By reference always","By value always","By name","By pointer"],"correct":1,"explain":"The distinction that matters: reassigning a parameter never affects the caller, but mutating the object it points to does. For objects, the reference value is copied."},{"id":"zya6_8_2","topic":"Primitive parameter","text":"void f(int x){ x = 99; } called with a variable a = 5 leaves a as:","options":["99","5","0","Undefined"],"correct":1,"explain":"x is a copy. Assigning to it changes nothing outside."},{"id":"zya6_8_3","topic":"Array parameter","text":"void f(int[] a){ a[0] = 99; } modifies the caller's array:","options":["No, because the array is copied when passed","No, because primitives are passed by value","Only if the array is declared final by the caller","Yes, because the reference points to the same array"],"correct":3,"explain":"The reference is copied, but both copies point to one array object."},{"id":"zya6_8_4","topic":"Reassigning an array parameter","text":"void f(int[] a){ a = new int[3]; } affects the caller:","options":["Yes, the caller's variable now points to the new array","Yes, but only the first three elements change","No — only the local copy of the reference is changed","It is a compile error to reassign a parameter"],"correct":2,"explain":"This is the precise line between "pass by value of a reference" and "pass by reference"."},{"id":"zya6_8_5","topic":"Return address","text":"After a method finishes, execution resumes:","options":["At the start of main","At the statement after the call","At the method start","At the program end"],"correct":1,"explain":"The frame records where to return to."},{"id":"zya6_8_6","topic":"Nested calls","text":"If a calls b and b calls c, the stack:","options":["Holds one frame","Holds three frames until c returns","Is empty","Overflows"],"correct":1,"explain":"Frames unwind in reverse order — the basis of a stack trace."},{"id":"zy6_8_2_bp_D286_u15_16","topic":"Static method","text":"A method called from main without an object must be:","options":["abstract","public","final","static"],"correct":3,"explain":"main is static, so it can only call static methods directly.","__rebal":1},{"id":"px286_static_6","topic":"Java passes arguments to methods using","text":"Java passes arguments to methods using:","options":["Pass by reference for objects, by value for primitives","Pass by reference for everything","Pass by value (including copies of references)","Pass by name, resolved when the parameter is used"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java is strictly pass-by-value; for objects, the reference value (address) is copied.","_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"cg_d3_2","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"How does Java pass an OBJECT to a method?","options":["It depends on whether the class is final","It passes a copy of the reference (still by value)","It passes the object itself by reference","It clones the whole object"],"correct":1,"explain":"Java is always pass-by-value; for objects, the VALUE copied is the reference. Both names then point at one object."},{"id":"cg_d3_3","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"void f(int[] arr) { arr[0] = 99; } — after passing {1, 2, 3}, what is element 0?","options":["0","A compile error","99","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"The copied reference points at the SAME array, so mutating its contents is visible to the caller."},{"id":"cg_d3_4","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"void f(String s) { s = "bye"; } — after String t = "hi"; f(t); what is t?","options":["null","""",""hi"",""bye""],"correct":2,"explain":"Reassigning the parameter only repoints the local copy of the reference. (Strings are immutable, so it could not be changed in place anyway.)"},{"id":"cg_d3_6","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"void f(StringBuilder sb) { sb.append("!"); } — after passing a StringBuilder holding "hi", what does it hold?","options":[""!"","null",""hi!"",""hi""],"correct":2,"explain":"append MUTATES the shared object rather than reassigning the reference, so the caller sees the change."},{"id":"cg_d3_7","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"Reassigning a parameter inside a method changes the caller’s variable.","options":["True, but only for objects","True, but only for primitives","False — never","True — always"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reassignment only rebinds the method’s own copy. Only MUTATING a shared object is visible outside."},{"id":"cg_d3_9","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"Java’s argument-passing mechanism is described as:","options":["Pass-by-reference for everything","Pass-by-name","Strictly pass-by-value","Pass-by-reference for objects"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java is strictly pass-by-value. Objects only FEEL like references because the value being copied is itself a reference."},{"id":"zf2078","topic":"","text":"When a method is called, control:","options":["restarts main","enters, then resumes after the call","moves to the next file","ends the program"],"correct":1,"explain":"Call, execute body, resume at the call site - every time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2079","topic":"","text":"Each method call gets its own:","options":["copy of the whole program","output window","local variables (a fresh stack frame)","processor"],"correct":2,"explain":"Locals live per-call; two active calls never share them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2080","topic":"","text":"The call stack records:","options":["every variable ever created","the program’s output","the in-progress calls and return points","the source code"],"correct":2,"explain":"Frames push on call and pop on return.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2081","topic":"","text":"If main calls a(), and a() calls b(), then b() returns first to:","options":["main directly","the JVM","b() again","a(), which later returns to main"],"correct":3,"explain":"Returns unwind in reverse call order - last in, first out.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2082","topic":"","text":"Arguments are passed to Java methods:","options":["by value - the method receives copies","by name","by reference for ints","by global variables"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reassigning a parameter never touches the caller’s variable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2083","topic":"","text":"static void bump(int n) { n = n + 1; } int x = 5; bump(x); System.out.println(x); prints:","options":["6","5","0","error"],"correct":1,"explain":"bump changed its own copy; x is untouched - pass-by-value in action.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2084","topic":"","text":"A method’s local variables exist:","options":["for the whole program","until main ends","only while that call is running","forever in memory"],"correct":2,"explain":"The frame (and its locals) disappears at return.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2085","topic":"","text":"Two simultaneous active calls of the same method (via nesting) have:","options":["shared locals","a compile error","one frozen copy","separate copies of the local variables"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each frame is independent - the basis of recursion.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2086","topic":"","text":"The value of a non-void call like square(4):","options":["replaces the call in the expression","prints automatically","is stored in a global","is lost always"],"correct":0,"explain":"Expressions treat the call as its returned value: 3 + square(4) is 19.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2087","topic":"","text":"A stack overflow error most commonly comes from:","options":["too many comments","calls nesting too deep","large println output","slow hardware"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every un-returned call holds a frame; infinite nesting exhausts the stack.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zy2_2_9_cg_d3_5","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"Can a Java method swap two int variables belonging to its caller?","options":["Only for values under 128","No — it only receives copies of their values","Yes, if the parameters are marked final","Yes, using the this keyword"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is the classic consequence of pass-by-value: a swap method cannot affect the caller’s primitives."},{"id":"zy6_11_5_cg_d3_8","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"Which action inside a method IS visible to the caller?","options":["Mutating the object a reference parameter points to","Reassigning the parameter to a new object","Reassigning an int parameter","Declaring a new local variable"],"correct":0,"explain":"Mutation travels through the shared reference; reassignment does not."},{"id":"zy6_19_11_cg_d3_1","topic":"Pass-by-value","text":"void change(int x) { x = 99; } — after int a = 5; change(a); what is a?","options":["0","undefined","5","99"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java copies the value into the parameter. Reassigning the copy never touches the caller’s variable."}],"D286/u6/z6_10":[{"id":"zya6_10_0","topic":"Array parameter syntax","text":"A method taking an int array declares it as:","options":["int[] a","int a","Array a","int a[]"],"correct":0,"explain":"int a[] also compiles but int[] a is the conventional form."},{"id":"zya6_10_1","topic":"Passing an array","text":"Calling sum(scores) passes:","options":["A full copy of the array's contents","Only the first element of the array","A copy of the reference to the same array","The array's length rather than its data"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which is why the method can modify the caller's data."},{"id":"zya6_10_2","topic":"Array length inside a method","text":"The method knows the array size from:","options":["The array's own length field","The caller printing it","A global","A separate parameter always"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arrays carry their length, unlike C where it must be passed separately."},{"id":"zya6_10_3","topic":"Modifying inside a method","text":"void doubleAll(int[] a){ for(...) a[i] *= 2; } affects the caller:","options":["Yes — same array object","Only for the first element","Only if returned","No"],"correct":0,"explain":"A common intentional design: the method mutates in place and returns void."},{"id":"zya6_10_4","topic":"null array parameter","text":"Passing null to a method that loops over the array:","options":["Silently skips the loop and returns zero","Fails to compile, since null has no length","Treats null as an empty array of length zero","Throws NullPointerException on a.length"],"correct":3,"explain":"Guard with if (a == null || a.length == 0) when null is possible."},{"id":"zya6_10_5","topic":"Empty array parameter","text":"Passing a zero-length array:","options":["Runs the loop zero times","Is a compile error","Returns null","Throws an exception"],"correct":0,"explain":"Empty and null are different cases and often need different handling. Is usually correct."},{"id":"zya6_10_6","topic":"Multiple array parameters","text":"A method comparing two arrays:","options":["Can take two array parameters","Must use a 2D array","Must be void","Can take only one array"],"correct":0,"explain":"boolean equal(int[] a, int[] b) is entirely normal."},{"id":"zya6_10_7","topic":"Varargs","text":"int sum(int... nums) allows:","options":["Calling with any number of int arguments","Calling with exactly one int argument only","Calling with an array, but never loose values","Calling with arguments of any type at all"],"correct":0,"explain":"Inside the method nums behaves as an int array."},{"id":"zya6_10_8","topic":"2D array parameter","text":"A method taking a 2D int array declares:","options":["int[][] a","int a[2]","int[2][2] a","int[] a"],"correct":0,"explain":"Dimensions are declared without sizes in a parameter."},{"id":"zy6_10_0_zb3","topic":"Reassign param","text":"If a method reassigns its array parameter to a new array, the caller variable:","options":["also changes","throws an exception","is unaffected","becomes null"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reassigning the parameter only changes the local reference copy.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z6_10_1","topic":"Mutation vs reassignment","text":"What does this print?

public static void doubleAll(int[] a) {
    for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
        a[i] *= 2;
    }
}

public static void replace(int[] a) {
    a = new int[]{9, 9, 9};
}

int[] nums = {1, 2, 3};
doubleAll(nums);
replace(nums);
