"},{"id":"zf2307","topic":"","text":"Two classes in a program each with a main method:","options":["is legal - you choose which class to RUN","is a compile error","runs both mains","merges them"],"correct":0,"explain":"The launched class’s main is the entry point; the other is ignored (or used for tests).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2308","topic":"","text":"String[] args in main receives:","options":["the class fields","the command-line arguments","random data","the compiler flags"],"correct":1,"explain":"Values typed after the program name arrive in that array.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2309","topic":"","text":"A helper method called directly from main (no object) must be:","options":["private","void","static as well","final"],"correct":2,"explain":"Static may call static freely; instance members need an instance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2310","topic":"","text":"Keeping main SHORT (create objects, delegate work) is good because:","options":["main cannot exceed 10 lines","short mains run faster","the real logic lives in testable methods","Java requires it"],"correct":2,"explain":"main as a thin launcher is the mature program shape.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2311","topic":"","text":"public static void main(String[] args) must be spelled exactly because:","options":["the compiler suggests it","any name works too","static is optional","the JVM starts at exactly that signature"],"correct":3,"explain":"Main, main(int), or non-static main will compile but not launch.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_8":[{"id":"zya7_8_0","topic":"Testing a class","text":"Testing an object involves:","options":["Reading the class file to confirm its fields","Compiling the class and confirming no warnings","Creating one, calling its methods, and checking the state it reports","Printing the object and reading its default toString"],"correct":2,"explain":"Behaviour is verified through the public interface."},{"id":"zya7_8_1","topic":"Most valuable test","text":"The most valuable test for an encapsulated class checks that it:","options":["Runs faster than the unencapsulated version","Refuses invalid input and keeps its invariant","Exposes every field through a public getter","Compiles without any warnings from the compiler"],"correct":1,"explain":"The happy path proves it works when nothing goes wrong; the refusal is what encapsulation exists to guarantee."},{"id":"zya7_8_2","topic":"Checking state","text":"After a.deposit(50) on a balance of 100, the test asserts:","options":["Nothing","a is not null","a.getBalance() equals 150","deposit returned true"],"correct":2,"explain":"Observe the resulting state through the accessor."},{"id":"zya7_8_3","topic":"Overdraft test","text":"Testing withdraw(9999) on a balance of 120 should confirm:","options":["The object is null","An exception is always thrown","The balance is still 120","The balance is negative"],"correct":2,"explain":"The refusal is the behaviour under test."},{"id":"zya7_8_4","topic":"Fresh object per test","text":"Each test should ideally:","options":["Reuse one shared object to save on memory","Run only after every other test has passed","Create a fresh object so earlier tests do not affect it","Check every field of the object at once"],"correct":2,"explain":"Shared state makes failures depend on test order."},{"id":"zya7_8_5","topic":"Testing the constructor","text":"A constructor test verifies:","options":["That the constructor runs faster than the setters","That the initial field values match what the arguments specified","That the object can be created without arguments","That every method is callable after construction"],"correct":1,"explain":"A constructor that silently ignores an argument is a real and common bug."},{"id":"fs_z7_8_1","topic":"Asserting that a bad value was refused","text":"setScore stores its argument only when it is between 0 and 100 inclusive, and the constructor routes through setScore. What does this print?

Student s = new Student("Ada", 95);
s.setScore(80);
s.setScore(-5);
System.out.println(s.getScore());","options":["95","80","-5","0"],"correct":1,"explain":"The valid value 80 is stored, and the invalid -5 is refused, leaving the object in its previous state. Asserting that the score is still 80 is the only assertion here that actually tests the validation."},{"id":"fs_z7_8_2","topic":"Testing an overdraft","text":"withdraw(amt) subtracts and returns true when amt is at most the balance, and otherwise changes nothing and returns false. What does this print?

Account a = new Account(100);
System.out.println(a.withdraw(30));
System.out.println(a.withdraw(500));
System.out.println(a.getBalance());","options":["true
true
-430","false
false
100","true
false
100","true
false
70"],"correct":3,"explain":"The first withdrawal succeeds and leaves 70. The second asks for more than the balance, so it is refused and the balance is unchanged at 70 rather than reverting to 100."},{"id":"fs_z7_8_3","topic":"The test that exercises validation","text":"A setScore(int) method is specified to accept only 0 through 100. Which single test actually verifies that validation?","options":["Call setScore(-5) on an object whose score is 80, then assert getScore() is still 80","Create the object and assert getScore() is not negative","Call setScore(80), then assert getScore() is 80","Call setScore(100), then assert getScore() is 100"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only a rejected value proves the guard exists, and the assertion must check that the object was left unchanged. Tests that pass valid values would still succeed if the validation were deleted."},{"id":"fs_z7_8_4","topic":"Independent tests","text":"Three tests share one Account object in sequence, and the third one fails. What is the main flaw in that arrangement?","options":["The failure may have been caused by an earlier test","Reusing an object causes a NullPointerException","A test may only ever call one method","The shared object cannot be garbage collected"],"correct":0,"explain":"When state carries over, a failure tells you only that something in the chain went wrong. Building a fresh object for each test makes each result point at exactly one behaviour. It does not identify which method is broken."},{"id":"fs_z7_8_5","topic":"State-based testing","text":"State-based testing of a class means:","options":["Driving the object with method calls and asserting on what its accessors then report","Reading the object's private fields directly from the test code","Comparing the source code line by line with the specification","Measuring how long each method takes to run"],"correct":0,"explain":"You test a class through its public interface: call the methods in a realistic order, then check the resulting state with the accessors. Private fields are deliberately unreachable from outside."},{"id":"fs_z7_8_6","topic":"Testing the constructor","text":"The Student constructor assigns the name and then passes the score to setScore, which stores it only when it is between 0 and 100 inclusive. What does this print?

Student a = new Student("Ada", 200);
Student b = new Student("Bo", 55);
System.out.println(a.getScore() + " " + b.getScore());","options":["0 55","Compiler error: 200 is out of range","100 55","200 55"],"correct":0,"explain":"Routing the constructor through setScore means an out-of-range 200 is refused, so score keeps its default 0. This is why the constructor deserves its own test: a constructor that skipped setScore would have stored 200 silently."},{"id":"zf2312","topic":"","text":"Unit testing a class means:","options":["checking methods on known objects","reading the source aloud","only compiling it","testing main alone"],"correct":0,"explain":"Construct, call, compare against expected - per method.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2313","topic":"","text":"A test of setInventory(5) should verify:","options":["the program prints 5","getInventory() then returns 5","the field is public","main was called"],"correct":1,"explain":"Set-then-get is the basic mutator/accessor test.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2314","topic":"","text":"Testing a validating setter means also testing:","options":["only good values","nothing - validation is automatic","that BAD values are rejected as designed","the compiler"],"correct":2,"explain":"The rejection path is half the behavior; test it explicitly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2315","topic":"","text":"Each test should build a FRESH object because:","options":["objects wear out","constructors need exercise","Java requires it","leftover state can hide or fake failures"],"correct":3,"explain":"Independent tests, independent fixtures.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2316","topic":"","text":"A test bench (or test main) for class Part is:","options":["code exercising Parts and checking","a hardware rig","the Part class itself","the JVM"],"correct":0,"explain":"The zyBooks term for the driver that exercises the class.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2317","topic":"","text":"Testing getters immediately after the constructor verifies:","options":["setters work","the promised initial state","printing works","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Born-valid is a testable claim.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2318","topic":"","text":"If tests only ever call methods in one fixed order:","options":["all bugs are found","tests run faster","order-dependent bugs stay invisible","objects break"],"correct":2,"explain":"Vary call sequences: set-get-set, get-before-set, repeated sets.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2319","topic":"","text":"A boundary test for setInventory with valid range 0-100 uses:","options":["50 only","0 only","1000 only","0, 100, -1, and 101"],"correct":3,"explain":"Both edges plus one step outside each - the boundary quartet.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2320","topic":"","text":"System.out.println-based checks vs assert-style checks differ in that:","options":["prints are illegal in tests","asserts are slower","there is no difference","prints need a human to notice"],"correct":3,"explain":"Automated pass/fail scales; eyeballing does not.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2321","topic":"","text":"When a class’s test fails after an edit elsewhere in the class:","options":["the edit broke an interaction","the test is wrong by definition","ignore it","delete the class"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fields couple methods; tests watch the couplings.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2322","topic":"","text":"Testing a class WITHOUT its user interface (no Scanner, no menus):","options":["is impossible","is the point of unit testing","tests nothing","requires a robot"],"correct":1,"explain":"Construct-and-call replaces typing; results are compared in code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2323","topic":"","text":"A regression suite for a class is:","options":["the first test only","deleted after passing","all the tests, rerun after each change","the compiler’s job"],"correct":2,"explain":"Passing today’s edit against yesterday’s expectations.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2324","topic":"","text":"Which method of an Item class is the HIGHEST priority to test?","options":["the plain getter","toString only","none","the one with the most logic"],"correct":3,"explain":"Test effort follows logic density - trivial getters rarely break.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_10":[{"id":"zy7_10_3_px286_exc_4","topic":"Accessing a method on a null reference throws","text":"Accessing a method on a null reference throws:","options":["ClassCastException","ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","ArithmeticException","NullPointerException"],"correct":3,"explain":"Calling a method on null throws NullPointerException.","distractors":{"0":"ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is for bad array indices, not null references.","2":"ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is for bad array indices, not null references.","3":"ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is for bad array indices, not null references."},"_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z7_10_1","topic":"Assignment copies the reference","text":"Student stores a name and a score with ordinary accessors and mutators. What does this print?

