0","null
0","null
null","A NullPointerException is thrown"],"correct":1,"explain":"An uninitialised reference field defaults to null, and println prints the text null rather than throwing. The exception would come only from calling a method on that null reference."},{"id":"fs_z7_10_5","topic":"Aliasing","text":"Two variables that refer to the same object are called:","options":["clones","aliases","a deep copy","overloads"],"correct":1,"explain":"Aliasing is two references naming one object, so a change made through either is visible through both. A copy would require creating a second object."},{"id":"fs_z7_10_6","topic":"What == asks about objects","text":"Applied to two object references, the == operator asks:","options":["Whether the two objects hold equal field values","Whether both variables refer to the same object","Whether either reference is null","Whether the two objects are instances of the same class"],"correct":1,"explain":"== on references compares identity, never contents. Comparing contents is the job of equals, and only if the class overrides it."},{"id":"zf2336","topic":"","text":"Part a = new Part(); Part b = a; How many objects exist?","options":["two","zero","one, with two references to it","three"],"correct":2,"explain":"Assignment copies the reference, never the object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2337","topic":"","text":"With b = a, calling b.setQty(9) then a.getQty() returns:","options":["the old value","0","null","9"],"correct":3,"explain":"Both names reach the same object - the change is visible through either.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2338","topic":"","text":"Part c = null; means:","options":["c refers to no object","c is a new empty object","c is zero","c is deleted"],"correct":0,"explain":"null is the deliberate absence of a referent.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2339","topic":"","text":"c.getName() when c is null throws:","options":["nothing","NullPointerException","a compile error","ClassCastException"],"correct":1,"explain":"Member access through null is the most famous runtime error in Java.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2340","topic":"","text":"a == b for references tests:","options":["whether contents match","whether they point at the SAME object","whether types match","string equality"],"correct":1,"explain":"Identity, not equality of state.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2341","topic":"","text":"Two Parts with identical field values compared with ==:","options":["true always","a compile error","false when they are separate objects","null"],"correct":2,"explain":"Same-looking state in two objects is still two objects.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2342","topic":"","text":"Comparing object CONTENTS is done with:","options":["==","!=","the > operator","an equals method"],"correct":3,"explain":"Classes define equals to say what "same value" means for them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2343","topic":"","text":"Part d = new Part(); d = new Part(); The FIRST object is now:","options":["unreachable - eligible for garbage collection","still referenced by d","deleted instantly by the assignment","renamed"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reassignment dropped the only reference; the collector may reclaim it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2344","topic":"","text":"Passing a Part to a method and calling setName inside:","options":["changes a copy","changes the caller’s object","does nothing","throws"],"correct":1,"explain":"The method’s parameter references the same instance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2345","topic":"","text":"void m(Part p) { p = new Part(); p.setName("X"); } After m(mine), mine’s name is:","options":["X","null","unchanged","empty"],"correct":2,"explain":"The method re-aimed its LOCAL reference before writing - the caller’s object never saw it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2346","topic":"","text":"A reference variable’s declared type determines:","options":["the object’s size","its field values","nothing","which methods may be called through it"],"correct":3,"explain":"The compiler checks calls against the reference type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2347","topic":"","text":"Storing the same Part in an array twice, arr[0] = p; arr[1] = p;:","options":["both slots alias one object","creates two objects","is a compile error","clones p"],"correct":0,"explain":"Collections hold references; duplicates are aliases.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2348","topic":"","text":"Setting p = null when the object is still referenced elsewhere:","options":["destroys the object","only clears THIS reference","clears all references","throws"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reachability through any reference keeps an object alive.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2349","topic":"","text":"The safe pattern before optional-object use is:","options":["p.doWork() always","p == null && p.doWork()","if (p != null) p.doWork();","try p twice"],"correct":2,"explain":"Null-guarding turns a crash into a controlled skip.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2350","topic":"","text":"Printing a reference variable directly shows something like Part@1a2b3c because:","options":["memory is broken","printing is illegal","the default toString is not contents","the object is empty"],"correct":2,"explain":"Override toString to print meaningful state.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2351","topic":"","text":"Part x = new Part(); Part y = new Part(); x = y; Objects now:","options":["both are alive with two names each","both destroyed","swap contents","one is shared by x and y"],"correct":3,"explain":"The assignment abandoned x’s first object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2352","topic":"","text":"References exist (rather than variables holding objects directly) because:","options":["objects vary in size and are shared","Java cannot copy data","memory is infinite","primitives are obsolete"],"correct":0,"explain":"Cheap to pass, natural to share - the design rationale.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2353","topic":"","text":""Aliasing bugs" are surprises caused by:","options":["too many classes","edits via one name appearing via another","slow loops","private fields"],"correct":1,"explain":"Forgetting that two names share one object - the classic reference pitfall.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_11":[{"id":"zya7_11_0","topic":"this meaning","text":"Inside an instance method, this refers to:","options":["The superclass","The class","The object the method was called on","The parameter"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is the implicit parameter every instance method receives."},{"id":"zy7_11_0_bp_D286_q6_5","topic":"this keyword","text":"In a constructor with parameter 'name' and field 'name', this.name = name; means:","options":["Assign the parameter's value to the object's field","Assign the field to the parameter","Create a new variable","Compare the two names"],"correct":0,"explain":"this.name = the object's field; bare name = the parameter (closest scope wins). this. resolves the shadowing.","distractors":{"1":"No new variable; both already exist.","2":"Reversed — right side assigns INTO left side.","3":"= assigns; == compares."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy7_11_1_q2","topic":"this keyword","text":"Constructor has parameter 'name' and field also called 'name'. Without this, name=name:","options":["Compile error","Assigns field correctly","Assigns parameter to itself (bug!)","Works due to scoping"],"correct":2,"explain":"Without 'this.', both sides refer to parameter. Use this.name to refer to field.","distractors":{"1":"Local scope shadows the field.","2":"Compiles but is a logic bug.","3":"Both refer to parameter."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy7_11_3_lg_D286_0_46","topic":"this keyword","text":"The keyword this refers to:","options":["The current object","The parent class","The main method","A static field"],"correct":0,"explain":"this references the instance on which the method/constructor runs.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy7_11_4_g15","topic":"this disambiguation","text":"When a parameter and field share a name, this.name = name:","options":["does nothing","sets the field to the parameter","assigns the parameter to itself","is a compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"this.name is the field; name is the parameter — the field gets set.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy7_11_7_fresh_D286_16","topic":"OOP > Constructors and this()","text":"What is printed?

class Point {
  int x, y;
  Point() { this(1, 1); }
  Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
}
// ...
Point p = new Point();
System.out.println(p.x + "," + p.y);","options":["1,1","0,0","compile error","1,0"],"correct":0,"explain":"The no-arg constructor delegates via this(1,1) to the two-arg constructor, setting both fields to 1."},{"id":"zyf7_11_0","topic":"this for disambiguation","text":"In a constructor, this.name = name; is needed because:","options":["name is static","Style","The parameter shadows the field","Java requires this"],"correct":2,"explain":"Without this, the assignment would set the parameter to itself. This.name distinguishes them."},{"id":"q4_d2","topic":"Constructor chaining","text":"this('default', 0) as first line of constructor calls:","options":["Another constructor in same class","Superclass method","Static factory method","Parent class constructor"],"correct":0,"explain":"this() calls another constructor in the same class. Must be the first statement.","distractors":{"1":"this() is constructor invocation.","2":"super() calls parent.","3":"this() is for constructors."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"g19","topic":"this() placement","text":"this(...) (constructor chaining) must be:","options":["the last statement in the constructor","placed anywhere inside the constructor body","outside the constructor, at class level","the first statement in the constructor"],"correct":3,"explain":"A this(...) call must be the first statement of the constructor.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q8","topic":"this() placement","text":"this() (constructor chain call) must be:","options":["First line","Last line","Anywhere","From static method"],"correct":0,"explain":"this() must be the first statement in a constructor — Java enforces at compile time.","distractors":{"1":"Java requires it first.","2":"this() can only be called from another constructor.","3":"Must be first."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"g16","topic":"Self-assign bug","text":"Inside a constructor, writing name = name (no this):","options":["sets it to null","sets the field","is illegal","assigns the parameter to itself"],"correct":3,"explain":"Both 