list","a stack employee","nothing","a copy in main"],"correct":0,"explain":"The list stores the reference to the fresh object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2533","topic":"","text":"staff.size() after adding Ana and Ben is:","options":["1","2","0","40"],"correct":1,"explain":"One entry per add.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2534","topic":"","text":"staff.get(1) with [Ana, Ben] returns:","options":["a copy of Ben","Ana","the reference to Ben","the name "Ben""],"correct":2,"explain":"Index 1 is the second entry; you get the object handle.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2535","topic":"","text":"Employee e = staff.get(0); e.setRate(25); changes:","options":["only a copy","nothing","the whole list","the Ana object inside the list too"],"correct":3,"explain":"e aliases the stored object - there is only one Ana.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2536","topic":"","text":"staff.remove(0) with [Ana, Ben] leaves:","options":["[Ben] - Ana becomes collectable","[Ana]","[Ana, Ben]","an empty list"],"correct":0,"explain":"List removal drops the reference; GC handles the rest.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2537","topic":"","text":"Total payroll over the list uses:","options":["staff.total()","a loop summing e.weeklyPay()","one multiplication","the list size"],"correct":1,"explain":"for (Employee e : staff) total += e.weeklyPay();","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2538","topic":"","text":"Finding the highest-paid employee tracks:","options":["only the best pay number when the NAME is needed too","the list length","the best employee reference (or index) while scanning","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Keep the champion object so name AND pay are available at the end.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2539","topic":"","text":"Hiring means:","options":["growing an array by hand","editing Ben","recompiling","new Employee, then add to the list"],"correct":3,"explain":"The dynamic list absorbs growth naturally.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2540","topic":"","text":"Two entries both referencing the SAME Employee object mean:","options":["independent employees","a compile error","double memory","one raise shows through both"],"correct":3,"explain":"Aliasing inside collections - visible immediately via the other entry.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2541","topic":"","text":"The example’s memory story: list on the heap, entries are:","options":["references to heap Employee objects","inline object copies","stack frames","primitives"],"correct":0,"explain":"Objects all the way down, connected by references.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2542","topic":"","text":"Clearing staff at shutdown, staff.clear();:","options":["deletes the JVM","unpins every Employee for collection","saves to disk","fires everyone in real life"],"correct":1,"explain":"Container emptied, objects unpinned.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2543","topic":"","text":"This example previews why ArrayLists of objects are the default shape for:","options":["single numbers","loop counters","rosters, inventories, and registries","constants"],"correct":2,"explain":"A growable collection of self-contained objects models almost everything.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u9/z9_1":[{"id":"zy9_1_6_h3_286_4","topic":"double truncation cast","_dom":"basics","difficulty":"hard","text":"int n = (int) 3.99; System.out.println(n); prints:","options":["4","3.99","3","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Casting a double to int truncates toward zero (it does NOT round), so 3.99 becomes 3.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy9_1_7_h3_286_12","topic":"uninitialized local","_dom":"basics","difficulty":"hard","text":"void m(){ int x; System.out.println(x); } This code:","options":["prints 0","fails to compile","throws","prints null"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unlike fields, LOCAL variables get no default value; using x before assignment is a compile error.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy9_1_8_q2","topic":"Modulo","text":"System.out.println(17 % 5); Output?","options":["3","2","3.4","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"17 = 3×5 + 2. Remainder is 2.","distractors":{"0":"% gives integer remainder.","1":"4 would be 19 % 5.","2":"Not 3."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy9_1_9_q8","topic":"Concatenation","text":"int x = 5; System.out.println('Value: ' + x); Output?","options":["5Value:","Compile error — can't + int and String","Value: + 5","Value: 5"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java's + operator concatenates when one operand is String. 'Value: ' + 5 → 'Value: 5'.","distractors":{"0":"+ performs concatenation.","1":"String + int is valid.","3":"String comes first."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy9_1_10_c15","topic":"Post-increment","text":"int i = 5; System.out.println(i++); prints:","options":["error","5","6","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"Post-increment prints the old value (5) then bumps i to 6.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy9_1_11_c16","topic":"Pre-increment","text":"int j = 5; System.out.println(++j); prints:","options":["6","error","5","7"],"correct":0,"explain":"Pre-increment bumps j to 6 first, then prints 6.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z9_1_1","topic":"Standard streams","text":"How does System.err differ from System.out?","options":["It is a separate output stream, used by convention for error messages so they can be handled apart from normal output","It writes to a log file instead of the screen","It is an input stream, so it is read rather than written","It prints the same text as System.out but stops the program afterwards"],"correct":0,"explain":"System.out and System.err are two independent output streams that usually both reach the screen. Keeping errors on their own stream lets them be redirected separately."},{"id":"fs_z9_1_2","topic":"Scanner and its source","text":"A method is declared as void process(Scanner in). Why can the same method read from the keyboard in one program and from a file in another?","options":["The method must be overloaded, once for System.in and once for File","Scanner always reads the keyboard; a file must be read with a different class","The source is supplied to the Scanner constructor, and the reading methods are identical whatever the source is","Scanner detects at run time whether a file exists and switches source automatically"],"correct":2,"explain":"new Scanner(System.in) and new Scanner(new File("in.txt")) differ only in the constructor argument. next(), nextInt() and hasNextLine() work the same either way, which is the point of the stream abstraction."},{"id":"fs_z9_1_3","topic":"InputMismatchException","text":"A program calls scnr.nextInt() and the user types abc. What happens?","options":["nextInt() returns 0 and the token abc is discarded","nextInt() returns the numeric codes of the characters","The program will not compile, because nextInt() may fail","An InputMismatchException is thrown, and the token abc is left unread in the stream"],"correct":3,"explain":"nextInt() only consumes the token if it really is an integer. Because the bad token stays in the stream, a retry that does not discard it reads the same rubbish for ever."},{"id":"fs_z9_1_4","topic":"Clearing a bad token","text":"A loop catches InputMismatchException and then asks the user to type the number again, but it repeats the prompt for ever without waiting for new input. What is missing from the catch block?","options":["A call to scnr.close() before reading again","A call to System.gc() to reset the stream","A break statement, since the loop can never succeed","A call to scnr.next() to discard the token that could not be read"],"correct":3,"explain":"The offending token was never consumed, so each pass reads it again. Discarding it with next() (or testing first with hasNextInt()) is what breaks the cycle."},{"id":"fs_z9_1_5","topic":"hasNextInt as a guard","text":"What does this program print?

Scanner s = new Scanner("42 abc");
