ay.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4081","topic":"","text":"Making a class abstract with NO abstract methods:","options":["is a compile error","is legal - it just blocks instantiation","makes methods vanish","is required"],"correct":1,"explain":"Sometimes you only want to force subclassing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4082","topic":"","text":"Fields declared in an abstract class:","options":["vanish","must be static","are inherited by subclasses normally","are abstract too"],"correct":2,"explain":"State machinery lives in the base like any inheritance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4083","topic":"","text":"A polymorphic payroll: for (Employee e : staff) total += e.pay(); relies on:","options":["one shared pay","abstract bodies","statics","each concrete class’s pay override"],"correct":3,"explain":"The abstract method guarantees pay() exists; overrides supply the math.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4084","topic":"","text":"Salaried and Hourly both extending abstract Employee is an example of:","options":["one contract, multiple implementations","overloading","encapsulation only","recursion"],"correct":0,"explain":"The abstract base defines WHAT; children define HOW.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4085","topic":"","text":"Calling super.pay() inside a subclass when pay() is abstract in the base:","options":["runs the contract","does not compile","returns 0","recurses"],"correct":1,"explain":"super reaches implementations; abstract methods have none.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4086","topic":"","text":"Hourly’s pay() { return rate * hours; } with rate 12, hours 10 returns:","options":["22.0","1210.0","120.0","120 as an int"],"correct":2,"explain":"12 x 10 via the double-returning contract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4087","topic":"","text":"Choosing WHICH methods to make abstract follows:","options":["alphabetical order","method length","field count","what varies versus what is shared"],"correct":3,"explain":"Varying behavior = abstract; common behavior = concrete in the base.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4088","topic":"","text":"An abstract getName() when every employee stores name identically is:","options":["poor design - share it in the base","ideal","required","impossible"],"correct":0,"explain":"Do not force subclasses to repeat identical code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4089","topic":"","text":"Instantiating "through" a concrete subclass is the ONLY way to get:","options":["any object at all","objects with that class in ancestry","static access","a null"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every Employee object is really a Salaried, Hourly, etc.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4090","topic":"","text":"The compile error for new AbstractThing() names the cause:","options":["missing semicolon","abstract class - cannot instantiate","wrong package","private constructor"],"correct":1,"explain":"A message worth recognizing on sight.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4091","topic":"","text":"Abstract classes primarily buy the design:","options":["speed","smaller files","guaranteed interface plus shared code","fewer classes"],"correct":2,"explain":"Contract + reuse in one construct.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4092","topic":"","text":"A mid-hierarchy abstract class (concrete above and below):","options":["is forbidden","collapses","must be top-level","legal - abstractness is per class"],"correct":3,"explain":"Hierarchies can alternate concrete and abstract levels as design demands.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr11","topic":"Abstract classes","text":"Why can an abstract class not be instantiated with new?","options":["It has no constructor at all","Its fields cannot be given initial values","It may declare methods that have no body yet","It is always declared final as well"],"correct":2,"explain":"An abstract class can leave methods unimplemented, so an object of it would have methods with nothing to run. It still has a constructor, which its subclasses call via super(...).","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u10/z10_10":[{"id":"zy10_10_0_lg_D286_1_54","topic":"UML","text":"In a UML class diagram, + and - indicate:","options":["input and output","public and private visibility","addition and subtraction","static and final"],"correct":1,"explain":"+ is public, - is private.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy10_10_1_zb3","topic":"UML inheritance","text":"UML draws inheritance as a hollow triangle arrow pointing:","options":["from derived class to base class","from base to derived","both ways","nowhere"],"correct":0,"explain":"The arrow points at the base class the derived class extends.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy10_10_2_lg_D286_1_55","topic":"UML","text":"The hollow-triangle arrow in UML points from:","options":["main to class","base to derived","derived class to its base class","field to method"],"correct":2,"explain":"The derived class points at the class it extends (is-a).","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy10_10_5_o10","topic":"@Override","text":"The @Override annotation:","options":["forces the JVM to call the child version at runtime","makes the compiler verify the method really overrides","creates the parent method if it does not exist","is required, or the override is silently ignored"],"correct":1,"explain":"@Override catches typos that would silently create a new method.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zyf10_10_0","topic":"Inheritance arrow","text":"In UML, inheritance is shown as:","options":["A dashed line with a hollow triangle at one end","A solid line with a filled diamond at the owner end","A dashed line with a plain open arrowhead at one end","A solid line with a hollow triangle pointing at the superclass"],"correct":3,"explain":"The triangle points toward the more general class."},{"id":"zyf10_10_1","topic":"Composition symbol","text":"A filled diamond in UML indicates:","options":["Composition","An interface","A method","Inheritance"],"correct":0,"explain":"A hollow diamond indicates aggregation. A strong has-a relationship."},{"id":"zyf10_10_2","topic":"Visibility markers","text":"In UML, a minus sign before a member means:","options":["private","protected","static","public"],"correct":0,"explain":"Plus is public and hash is protected."},{"id":"zyf10_10_3","topic":"Interface notation","text":"An implemented interface is drawn with:","options":["A solid line with a hollow triangle","A dashed line with a hollow triangle","A dashed line with a filled diamond","A solid line with an open arrowhead"],"correct":1,"explain":"Dashed distinguishes realization from class inheritance."},{"id":"fs_z10_10_1","topic":"Compartments of a UML class box","text":"A UML class box is drawn with three stacked compartments. Reading from the top down, what do they contain?","options":["The class name, then the fields (attributes), then the methods (operations)","The class name, then the methods, then the fields","The package name, then the class name, then everything else","The constructors, then the fields, then the inherited members"],"correct":0,"explain":"The standard order is name, attributes, operations. Visibility markers such as + and - sit in front of the members in the lower two compartments."},{"id":"fs_z10_10_2","topic":"Italic class names in UML","text":"In a UML class diagram, what does it mean when a class name is written in italics?","options":["The class is static","The class is in a different package","The class is abstract","The class is final"],"correct":2,"explain":"Italics mark an abstract class, and italicised operations mark abstract methods. An interface is instead labelled with the <<interface>> stereotype above its name."},{"id":"fs_z10_10_3","topic":"Aggregation vs composition","text":"A UML association is drawn with a HOLLOW diamond at the container end. What does that notation state about the relationship?","options":["Composition: the part is owned by the whole and is destroyed with it","Inheritance: the part is a subclass of the whole","Implementation: the part is an interface the whole implements","Aggregation: the whole holds the part, but the part can exist independently and outlive the whole"],"correct":3,"explain":"A hollow diamond is aggregation, a weaker has-a in which the part survives the whole; a filled diamond is composition, where the part dies with the whole. Neither is inheritance, which uses a hollow triangle."},{"id":"fs_z10_10_4","topic":"Interface stereotype","text":"How is an interface identified in a UML class diagram?","options":["By the stereotype <<interface>> written above the type's name","By a filled diamond drawn on its box","By underlining every one of its operations","By a plus sign written in front of the type's name"],"correct":0,"explain":"The <<interface>> stereotype labels the box as an interface, and classes that implement it connect with a dashed line ending in a hollow triangle."},{"id":"fs_z10_10_5","topic":"Multiplicity","text":"An association line between Order and LineItem is labelled 1 at the Order end and 1..* at the LineItem end. What does that say?","options":["Each Order has at most one LineItem, which is optional","LineItem is a subclass of Order that may be instantiated many times","Order and LineItem are the same class drawn twice","Each Order is linked to one or more LineItems, and each LineItem belongs to exactly one Order"],"correct":3,"explain":"Multiplicity markers count the objects at each end: 1 means exactly one, and 1..* means one or more. They describe an association, not inheritance."},{"id":"fs_z10_10_6","topic":"Reading a UML diagram into code","text":"A diagram shows a box named Employee and, below it, a box named Manager. A solid line with a hollow triangle runs from Manager and the triangle touches Employee. Which Java declaration does the diagram describe?","options":["class Employee extends Manager","class Manager implements Employee","class Employee { private Manager manager; }","class Manager extends Employee"],"correct":3,"explain":"A solid line with a hollow triangle is inheritance, and the triangle always sits on the parent, so Manager extends Employee. A dashed line with the same triangle would mean implements instead."},{"id":"zf4100","topic":"","text":"A UML class box’s three sections are:","options":["name, imports, code","name, attributes, operations","fields only","methods only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Top to bottom: who, what it holds, what it does.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4101","topic":"","text":"The + sign before a UML member means:","options":["private","protected","public","static"],"correct":2,"explain":"+ public, - private, # protected.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4102","topic":"","text":"Inheritance in UML is drawn as:","options":["a dashed circle","a filled diamond at the child","a plain line","a hollow triangle aimed at the base"],"correct":3,"explain":"The open triangle on the parent end is the is-a arrow.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4103","topic":"","text":"Composition in UML uses:","options":["a filled diamond at the containing class","a hollow triangle","a dotted arrow","no notation"],"correct":0,"explain":"The diamond sits on the owner of the part.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4104","topic":"","text":"An attribute written level : int means:","options":["a method","a field named level of type int","an int named type","a constant"],"correct":1,"explain":"UML writes name : type, reversing Java’s order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4105","topic":"","text":"An operation written +getPay() : double means:","options":["a private field","a constructor","a public method returning double","an abstract class"],"correct":2,"explain":"Visibility, name, parameters, colon, return type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4106","topic":"","text":"Static members in UML are shown:","options":["italicized","bold","starred","underlined"],"correct":3,"explain":"Underline = class-level; italics = abstract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4107","topic":"","text":"UML’s value in design discussions is:","options":["showing structure, not implementation","generating tests",