"],"correct":0,"explain":"The problem is solved in terms of a smaller version of itself."},{"id":"zya11_1_1","topic":"Two required parts","text":"Every correct recursive method has:","options":["A base case and a recursive case","Two parameters","A try block","A loop and a return"],"correct":0,"explain":"Without a base case it never stops; without a recursive case it never progresses."},{"id":"zya11_1_2","topic":"Base case purpose","text":"The base case:","options":["Stops the recursion by returning without calling again","Makes the first recursive call to begin the process","Increases the argument so the problem grows","Catches the StackOverflowError when it occurs"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is the smallest problem you can answer directly."},{"id":"zya11_1_3","topic":"Progress requirement","text":"Each recursive call must:","options":["Move toward the base case","Be the last statement","Return void","Use the same argument"],"correct":0,"explain":"Calling with an unchanged argument recurses forever."},{"id":"zya11_1_4","topic":"Missing base case","text":"Recursion with no reachable base case causes:","options":["StackOverflowError","An infinite loop with no error","Nothing","A compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each call consumes a stack frame until the stack is exhausted."},{"id":"zya11_1_5","topic":"Recursion vs iteration","text":"Any recursive solution:","options":["Can in principle be rewritten with a loop","Is always faster","Uses less memory","Cannot be written iteratively"],"correct":0,"explain":"Recursion is often clearer for tree and divide-and-conquer problems; loops are cheaper."},{"id":"zya11_1_6","topic":"Memory cost","text":"Recursion uses more memory than a loop because:","options":["Each pending call keeps a stack frame","Each call allocates a copy of the whole program","The compiler duplicates the method body per call","Recursive methods are stored on the heap"],"correct":0,"explain":"Depth n means n frames alive at once."},{"id":"zy11_1_0_lg_D