s more memory","It cannot be iterated"],"correct":0,"explain":"A raw type turns off generic checking: any type can be added, and retrievals return Object requiring a cast that can fail at runtime. You can add anything and must cast on retrieval.","distractors":{"0":"Speed is unaffected.","2":"Memory is unaffected.","3":"It can still be iterated."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"q8","topic":"Type erasure","text":"At runtime, what does the JVM know about the &lt;String> in ArrayList&lt;String>?","options":["The full type is stored per element","Nothing","It converts to Object[]","It is stored once in the list header"],"correct":1,"explain":"Java uses type erasure: generics are enforced by the compiler, then the type argument is removed, so it isn't available at runtime. Generic type info is erased at compile time.","distractors":{"0":"Type info is erased, not stored per element.","2":"There is no runtime type header for the generic argument.","3":"Erasure is why new T[] is illegal, but the list isn't literally retyped for you."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"q9","topic":"K,V convention","text":"In `Map&lt;K,V>`, what do K and V conventionally represent?","options":["Class and Variable","Kind and Version","Key and Value","Nothing specific"],"correct":2,"explain":"By convention K is the key type and V the value type in a Map, e.g., Map<String,Integer>.","distractors":{"0":"K/V mean Key/Value.","2":"K/V mean Key/Value.","3":"They are conventional, meaningful names."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u1_2","topic":"Diamond operator","text":"In "List&lt;String&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();", the &lt;&gt; is called the:","options":["Wildcard operator (accepts any type argument)","Diamond operator (infers the type argument)","Raw type marker (disables generic checking)","Array notation (declares a list of lists)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The diamond operator <> lets the compiler infer the generic type argument from the declaration, avoiding repetition."},{"id":"zf4285","topic":"","text":"A generic method declares:","options":["a wildcard import","an Object cast","a macro","<T> before the return type"],"correct":3,"explain":"static <T> void show(T item) - T is decided per call.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4286","topic":"","text":"The point of generics is:","options":["one implementation","faster loops","runtime typing","fewer classes only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Type-safe reuse without copy-pasting per type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4287","topic":"","text":"static <T> T first(T[] arr) { return arr[0]; } first(new String[]{"a","b"}) returns type:","options":["Object","String","T","int"],"correct":1,"explain":"T binds to String from the argument - no cast needed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4288","topic":"","text":"Before generics, general containers used Object, which required:","options":["nothing","more memory only","casts at every retrieval","new syntax"],"correct":2,"explain":"Generics move those failures to compile time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4289","topic":"","text":"Type arguments must be:","options":["primitives allowed","any keyword","arrays only","reference types"],"correct":3,"explain":"<int> is illegal; <Integer> with autoboxing covers it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4290","topic":"","text":"The compiler INFERS T at the call site from:","options":["the return use only","a config file","runtime values","the argument types"],"correct":3,"explain":"first(stringArray) - T = String, no explicit annotation needed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4291","topic":"","text":"static <T> void swap(T[] a, int i, int j) works on:","options":["any object array - one method for all","int[] directly","only Strings","nothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"The generic signature is the entire point. (int[] needs Integer[] or its own version.)","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4292","topic":"","text":"Two type parameters are written:","options":["<T; T>","<T1, T2> (or <K, V>)","T and T","<T><T>"],"correct":1,"explain":"static <K, V> void 