","topic":"","text":"<T extends Comparable<T>> means:","options":["T must compare to its own type","T is a Comparable object","T extends a class named T","T is abstract"],"correct":0,"explain":"The bound admits only types with compareTo.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4300","topic":"","text":"The bound exists so the method body can call:","options":["println","charAt","sqrt","compareTo on T values"],"correct":3,"explain":"Bounds unlock the bounded type’s methods safely.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4301","topic":"","text":"A generic max method without a Comparable bound:","options":["has no legal way to compare elements","uses < anyway","guesses","sorts first"],"correct":0,"explain":"< is not defined for objects; compareTo requires the bound.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4302","topic":"","text":"min(Integer[]) with a Comparable bound works because:","options":["ints are primitive","Integer implements Comparable<Integer>","the JVM converts","luck"],"correct":1,"explain":"The wrappers (and String) all carry compareTo.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4303","topic":"","text":"a.compareTo(b) < 0 means:","options":["a equals b","a orders after b","a orders before b","an error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Negative-zero-positive is the compareTo contract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4304","topic":"","text":"Calling a Comparable-bounded max with a class LACKING compareTo:","options":["fails at runtime","returns null","works","is a compile error at the call site"],"correct":3,"explain":"The bound filters the callers - the safety is compile-time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4305","topic":"","text":"extends in a type bound covers:","options":["class AND interface conformance","classes only","interfaces only","neither"],"correct":0,"explain":"Bound syntax uses extends for both kinds of conformance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4306","topic":"","text":"<T extends Number> permits which argument types?","options":["String","Integer, Double, and other Numbers","any type","only Number itself"],"correct":1,"explain":"Numeric-only generic methods use the Number bound.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4307","topic":"","text":"Inside <T extends Number>, calling t.doubleValue():","options":["fails","needs a cast","compiles - Number declares it","is runtime-checked"],"correct":2,"explain":"The bound’s interface becomes the usable surface.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4308","topic":"","text":"The standard generic max signature is:","options":["static T max<Comparable>(T, T)","static max(Comparable a, b)","static <T> Comparable max(T, T)","bound in <...>, T as params and return"],"correct":3,"explain":"Bound in the parameter section, T as parameter and return types.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4309","topic":"","text":"max("apple", "pear") with that method returns:","options":["pear","apple","applepear","an error"],"correct":0,"explain":"String’s compareTo orders alphabetically; p follows a.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u13/z13_3":[{"id":"zy13_3_0_q12","topic":"Generic class use","text":"Given `class Pair&lt;A,B>`, how do you make a pair of String and Integer?","options":["new Pair(String,Integer);","new Pair<String,Integer>();"],"correct":1,"explain":"Supply both type arguments in order: Pair<String,Integer>. The class declares <A,B>; the caller fills both.","distractors":{"0":"Raw type loses safety and doesn't specify the two types.","2":"Pair needs two type arguments.","3":"Type arguments go in angle brackets, not parentheses."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy13_3_2_tuw1_d286_u1_7","topic":"Raw type","text":"Using a generic class without a type argument (a raw type) is discouraged because it:","options":["Fails to compile in any recent version of Java","Loses compile-time type checking and risks ClassCastException","Uses noticeably more memory than a generic type","Prevents the collection from being iterated at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"Raw types bypass generic type checks, reintroducing the unchecked-cast errors generics were designed to prevent."},{"id":"zyf13_3_0","topic":"Generic class syntax","text":"A generic class is declared as:","options":["class Box extends T","class Box[T]","class Box(T)","class Box<T>"],"correct":3,"explain":"The type parameter follows the class name in angle brackets."},{"id":"zyf13_3_1","topic":"Using it","text":"Box&lt;String> b = new Box&lt;>(); the empty diamond:","options":["Declares the box as a raw type with no argument","Infers the type argument from the declaration","Allows any type to be stored, checked at runtime","Is required, or the constructor cannot be called"],"correct":1,"explain":"The diamond operator avoids repeating the type."},{"id":"zyf13_3_2","topic":"Field of type T","text":"A field declared T value:","options":["Is always of type Object at compile time","Must be assigned in a static initialiser block","Can hold any type, regardless of the instance","Takes whatever type the instance was created with"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each instantiation fixes T."},{"id":"zyf13_3_3","topic":"Static and T","text":"A static method cannot use the class's type parameter because:","options":["Static methods cannot declare parameters at all","T belongs to an instance, not the class","T is erased before any static method is compiled","Static methods run before the class is loaded"],"correct":1,"explain":"A static generic method declares its own type parameter instead."},{"id":"zyf13_3_4","topic":"Primitives","text":"Box&lt;int> is:","options":["Faster","Automatic","Valid","A compile error"],"correct":3,"explain":"Type arguments must be reference types. Use Box<Integer>."},{"id":"zyf13_3_5","topic":"Type safety","text":"A generic class prevents:","options":["Storing more elements than the declared capacity","Storing the wrong type, caught at compile time","The object from being used in more than one class","Any subclass of the type argument being stored"],"correct":1,"explain":"And removes the casts a raw Object version would need."},{"id":"zyf13_3_6",