nstantiation binds its own type argument.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4312","topic":"","text":"Inside Box<T>, a field of type T:","options":["holds Objects loosely","must be static","whatever type this Box was made for","is illegal"],"correct":2,"explain":"private T contents; - the typed payload.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4313","topic":"","text":"b.get() on a Box<String> returns:","options":["Object","T","a copy","String, no cast needed"],"correct":3,"explain":"The bound type flows through the interface - generics’ everyday payoff.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4314","topic":"","text":"Box<Integer> and Box<String>:","options":["are incompatible types to the compiler","interchange freely","share contents","compare equal"],"correct":0,"explain":"The type argument is part of the compile-time type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4315","topic":"","text":"ArrayList itself is:","options":["a special keyword","a generic class - ArrayList<E>","non-generic","an interface"],"correct":1,"explain":"You have used generic classes all along.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4316","topic":"","text":"The diamond, new Box<>();:","options":["makes a raw Box","is an error","infers the argument from the left side","erases types"],"correct":2,"explain":"Box<String> b = new Box<>(); - say it once.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4317","topic":"","text":"Adding an Integer to a Box<String>:","options":["autoboxes","runs then throws","warns only","is a compile error"],"correct":3,"explain":"The mismatch is caught before the program ever runs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4318","topic":"","text":"A Pair<K, V> class holds:","options":["two fields of independently chosen types","two identical types","keys only","primitives"],"correct":0,"explain":"Pair<String, Integer> name-and-count - two parameters, two roles.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4319","topic":"","text":"Raw types, Box b = new Box();:","options":["are errors","compile with warnings","are the norm","are faster"],"correct":1,"explain":"Legacy compatibility only - modern code always supplies the argument.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4320","topic":"","text":"Writing your own Stack<T> class demonstrates:","options":["GUIs","the pattern generics were built for","recursion","file I/O"],"correct":1,"explain":"push(T), pop() returning T - one class, every element type.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u13/z13_5":[{"id":"zy13_5_1_fresh_D286_36","topic":"Comparable & Comparator > compareTo","text":"The call "apple".compareTo("apricot") returns a value that is which of the following?","options":["a positive number","undefined","a negative number","zero"],"correct":2,"explain":"The strings match until the third letter where 'p' (112) is less than 'r' (114), yielding a negative result."},{"id":"zy13_5_3_fresh_D286_37","topic":"Comparable & Comparator > Comparator","text":"To sort a list of your own objects by a rule other than their natural ordering, you supply which of the following?","options":["a Comparator implementation","a second constructor","an overridden finalize() method","a static initializer block"],"correct":0,"explain":"A Comparator defines an external ordering that can be passed to sorting methods."},{"id":"zy13_5_4_q1","topic":"Comparable method","text":"Which method does the Comparable interface require?","options":["equals(o)","sort()","compareTo(other)","compare(a, b)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Comparable<T> requires public int compareTo(T other), defining the object's natural ordering.","distractors":{"0":"compare(a,b) belongs to Comparator.","2":"equals tests equality, not ordering.","3":"sort is a Collections method."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy13_5_5_q2","topic":"Comparator method","text":"Which method does the Comparator interface require?","options":["order(a,b)","hashCode()","compareTo(other)","compare(a, b)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Comparator<T> requires public int compare(T a, T b) — an external ordering rule.","distractors":{"0":"compareTo belongs to Comparable.","2":"Correct.","3":"hashCode is unrelated to ordering."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy13_5_7_q7","topic":"compare vs subtraction","text":"Why prefer Integer.compare(a,b) over a - b in a comparator?","options":["a - b can overflow for large ints and give the wrong sign","a - b is slower because it allocates an Integer","a - b cannot be used inside a lambda expression","a - b returns a boolean rather than an int"],"correct":0,"explain":"Subtracting ints can overflow (e.g., large positive minus large negative), flipping the sign; Integer.compare is safe.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy13_5_8_q8","topic":"Passing a Comparator","text":"How do you sort a list with a custom Comparator cmp?","options":["cmp.sort(list)","Collections.sort(list, cmp)","Collections.sort(list)","list.compareTo(cmp)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pass the comparator as the second argument: Collections.sort(list, cmp) (or list.sort(cmp)).","distractors":{"0":"That uses natural ordering, ignoring cmp.","2":"Correct.","3":"compareTo isn't how you sort a list."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy13_5_9_q9","topic":"Reverse order","text":"Which yields descending order with a Comparator?","options":["-cmp","cmp.negate()","cmp.reversed()","cmp.ascending()"],"correct":2,"explain":"Comparator.reversed() returns a comparator with the opposite ordering.","distractors":{"0":"There is no ascending() needed; natural is ascending.","2":"Correct.","3":"negate() is for Predicates, not Comparators."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"zy13_5_11_q11","topic":"String natural order","text":"What is String's natural ordering (its compareTo)?","options":["Lexicographic (dictionary/Unicode) order","Case-insensitive alphabetical order","By length, then alphabetically within a length","The order the strings were created in"],"correct":0,"explain":"String.compareTo compares character by character using Unicode values — lexicographic order.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q4","topic":"Natural ordering","text":"Where is an object's 'natural ordering' defined?","options":["In a separate Comparator passed to the sort","In the class via Comparable.compareTo","By the order the objects were created in","By the object's hashCode value"],"correct":1,"explain":"Natural ordering is the class's own compareTo from implementing Comparable.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q5","topic":"Sort requires Comparable","text":"To sort your own objects with Collections.sort(list) (no Comparator argument), the element class must:","options":["Override equals() and hashCode() on the class","Override toString() so the values can be compared","Implement Comparable and define compareTo()","Mark the class Serializable so it can be ordered"],"correct":2,"explain":"Collections.sort with no Comparator uses natural ordering, so the element type must implement Comparable (compareTo). Otherwise, pass a Comparator as the second argument.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q6","topic":"Two orderings","text":"You need to sort Employees by name AND (elsewhere) by salary. Best approach?","options":["Two separate classes, each ordered by one of the fields","Two compareTo methods, overloaded by their parameter","A single Comparator that sorts by both fields at once","Comparable for the natural order, plus a Comparator for the other"],"correct":3,"explain":"A class has one natural ordering (Comparable); provide additional orderings as separate Comparators.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q12","topic":"Mnemonic","text":"Which describes Comparator (not Comparable)?","options":["Requires compareTo","A separate object providing an alternate ordering","Cannot be passed to sort","Implemented by the class itself for its natural order"],"correct":1,"explain":"Comparator is an external object (compare(a,b)) supplying an alternate ordering, passed to sort.","distractors":{"0":"That describes Comparable.","2":"Correct.","3":"That's Comparable's method; and Comparators ARE passed to sort."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_1","topic":"Comparable method","text":"The Comparable interface requires implementing which method?","options":["equals(other)","sort()","compare(a, b)","compareTo(other)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Comparable<T> defines int compareTo(T o), giving a class its natural ordering."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_2","topic":"Comparator method","text":"The Comparator interface requires implementing:","options":["order(a, b)","rank(x)","compareTo(other)","compare(a, b)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Comparator<T> defines int compare(T a, T b), letting you define orderings separate from the class itself."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_3","topic":"Return value","text":"compareTo/compare should return a negative number when the first argument is:","options":["Equal to the second","Null","Greater than the second","Less than the second"],"correct":3,"explain":"Negative means first < second, zero means equal, positive means first > second."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_4","topic":"Natural ordering","text":"Implementing Comparable gives a class its:","options":["An alternative ordering, used only when passed in","Its natural default ordering, used by Collections.sort","A guarantee that no two instances compare equal","The ability to be stored in a HashSet correctly"],"correct":1,"explain":"A Comparable class defines one natural order, which Collections.sort and TreeSet/TreeMap use by default."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_5","topic":"Multiple orderings","text":"To sort the same objects by different fields at different times, use:","options":["Only equals","Only hashCode","Multiple Comparable implementations","Several Comparator objects"],"correct":3,"explain":"A class has one natural order (Comparable), but you can supply many Comparators for alternative orderings."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_6","topic":"Sort with comparator","text":"Which call sorts a List of Person by name using a comparator?","options":["list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Person::getName))","list.sort(Person::getName)","list.sort(Comparable.comparing(Person::getName))","Collections.sort(list, Person::compareTo)"],"correct":0,"explain":"List.sort accepts a Comparator; Comparator.comparing(Person::getName) builds one keyed on the name field."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_7","topic":"Consistency with equals","text":"It is recommended (though not required) that compareTo be consistent with:","options":["equals","hashCode only","finalize"],"correct":0,"explain":"When compareTo returns 0 for objects that are also equal(), sorted collections behave predictably; inconsistency can cause surprising results in TreeSet/TreeMap."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u3_8","topic":"Reverse order","text":"To sort in descending natural order, you can use:","options":["A raw type","hashCode()","Comparator.reverseOrder()","compareTo() twice"],"correct":2,"explain":"Comparator.reverseOrder() (or reversed() on an existing comparator) flips the ordering to descending."},{"id":"zf4334","topic":"","text":"For Collections.sort(listOfParts) to compile, Part must:","options":["extend ArrayList","be final","implement Comparable<Part>","have a main"],"correct":2,"explain":"The sort needs compareTo to know the order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4335","topic":"","text":"compareTo returns:","options":["true or false","a String","always 1","negative, zero, or positive"],"correct":3,"explain":"The three-way contract that all ordering builds on.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4336","topic":"","text":"public int compareTo(Part o) { return this.price - o.price; } orders parts by:","options":["ascending price","descending price","name","insertion"],"correct":0,"explain":"Cheaper parts return negative against pricier ones, sorting first. (Safe here when prices are small non-negative ints.)","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4337","topic":"","text":"Sorting by name instead uses:","options":["name subtraction","return this.name.compareTo(o.name);","== on names","length comparison"],"correct":1,"explain":"Delegate to String’s own ordering.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4338","topic":"","text":"After implementing Comparable and calling Collections.sort(list), the list is:","options":["copied sorted","unchanged","reordered in place by compareTo","reversed"],"correct":2,"explain":"sort mutates the list using your ordering rule.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u13/z13_6":[{"id":"zy13_6_1_tuw1_d286_u1_4","topic":"Bounded type","text":"What does "&lt;T extends Number&gt;" mean for a generic method?","options":["T must be exactly Number, never a subclass","T must be Number or a subclass of Number","T must be a superclass of Number","T may be any type; the bound is only advisory"],"correct":1,"explain":"An upper-bounded type parameter restricts T to Number or its subclasses, so Number’s methods (like doubleValue) are usable."},{"id":"zyf13_6_0","topic":"Generic map method","text":"A generic map method transforms:","options":["Elements in place, changing the original list","One list into a single combined value","Keys into values within a single map","Elements of one type into another, preserving the structure"],"correct":3,"explain":"Its signature typically uses two type parameters."},{"id":"zyf13_6_1","topic":"Two type parameters","text":"Mapping from T to R requires:","options":["<T, R>","No parameters"],"correct":0,"explain":"One for the input element type, one for the result."},{"id":"zyf13_6_2","topic":"Function parameter","text":"The transformation is supplied as:","options":["A subclass overriding an abstract transform method","A String naming the method to call by reflection","An array of the results, computed in advance","A functional interface such as Function<T,R>, often a lambda"],"correct":3,"explain":"Which makes the method reusable for any transformation."},{"id":"zyf13_6_3","topic":"Return type","text":"A map over a List&lt;T> with a T-to-R function returns:","options":["List<R>","T","void"],"correct":0,"explain":"The element type changes; the container does not."},{"id":"zyf13_6_4","topic":"Why generic","text":"Writing map generically rather than for one type:","options":["Makes the method run faster for each type","Removes the need to test the transformation","Allows primitives to be used as the element type","Avoids duplicating the method for every type combination"],"correct":3,"explain":"Type safety is preserved by the parameters."},{"id":"zyf13_6_5","topic":"Type inference at the call","text":"Calling map(list, x -> x.length()) infers:","options":["Both T and R from the list's declared type","T from the list and R from the lambda's return type","Both T and R from the lambda's parameter type","Neither; both must be written out explicitly"],"correct":1,"explain":"Inference is what keeps generic calls readable."},{"id":"zyf13_6_6","topic":"Original list","text":"A well-behaved map:","options":["Modifies the input list and returns it","Returns null when the input list is empty","Sorts the result before returning it","Returns a new list, leaving the input unchanged"],"correct":3,"explain":"Avoiding surprising side effects on the caller's data."},{"id":"fs_z13_6_1","topic":"Generic lookup with a fallback","text":"What does this print?

static <K, V> V lookup(K[] keys, V[] values, K target, V fallback) {
    for (int i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
        if (keys[i].equals(target)) {
            return values[i];
        }
    }
    return fallback;
}

