e","text":"How does do-while differ from while?","options":["its body never runs if the condition starts false","it repeats a fixed number of times set in advance","it evaluates the condition after every statement","its body runs once before the condition is checked"],"correct":3,"explain":"do-while is posttest — the body always runs at least once.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy19_1_5_e11","topic":"do-while once","text":"int x = 100; do { print(x); x++; } while(x &lt; 5); prints:","options":["forever","100","5","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"The body runs once before the false check → prints 100.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy19_1_6_w286_co2","topic":"do-while","_dom":"control","text":"Which loop is guaranteed to execute its body at least once?","options":["do-while","enhanced for","while","for"],"correct":0,"explain":"A do-while checks its condition after the body, so the body always runs at least once.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy19_1_7_fresh_D286_12","topic":"Control Flow > do-while Loops","text":"What does this code do?

