(␣ marks a space, ⏎ a line break.)","options":["␣␣* ⏎ ␣** ⏎ ***","␣␣* ⏎ ␣*** ⏎ *****","* ⏎ *** ⏎ *****","***** ⏎ ␣*** ⏎ ␣␣*"],"correct":1,"explain":"Row i prints n - i spaces then 2i - 1 stars: 2 spaces + 1 star, 1 space + 3 stars, 0 spaces + 5 stars - a centred pyramid. Writing the row formulas down first is what makes the loops easy."},{"id":"fs_z19_6_5","topic":"Right-aligned row formula","text":"In a right-aligned triangle of height n (stars flush to the right edge), row r (counting from 1) needs:","options":["r stars followed by n - r spaces","n - r spaces followed by r stars","r spaces followed by n - r stars","n spaces followed by n stars"],"correct":1,"explain":"The two counts must sum to n so every row is the same width, with stars growing as rows descend: spaces shrink (n - r) while stars grow (r). Working out this relationship first makes the loops write themselves."},{"id":"fs_z19_6_6","topic":"Inverted triangle formula","text":"To print an INVERTED right triangle - first row n stars, last row 1 star - row r (counting from 1) should contain:","options":["2r - 1 stars","n - r + 1 stars","r stars","n - r stars"],"correct":1,"explain":"Row 1 needs n stars and row n needs 1, so the count falls by one per row: n - r + 1. The n - r formula is off by one (row 1 would get n - 1 stars), and r stars gives the upright triangle instead."},{"id":"zf4866","topic":"","text":"ASCII art programs are built from:","options":["println/print calls and loops","graphics contexts","files","GUIs"],"correct":0,"explain":"Text as pixels - loops draw the patterns.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4867","topic":"","text":"A 4-row right triangle of *s uses:","options":["one println","nested loops","recursion required","fillRect"],"correct":1,"explain":"Inner bound tied to the outer index makes the diagonal.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4868","topic":"","text":"Printing a backslash in art requires:","options":["a plain backslash","quotes","the escape \\","%s"],"correct":2,"explain":"One escaped backslash per visible backslash.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4869","topic":"","text":"A centered pyramid needs per row:","options":["only stars","tabs only","random padding","leading spaces then stars"],"correct":3,"explain":"Two inner loops (or two computed strings) per row.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4870","topic":"","text":"The 3-row pyramid’s star counts are:","options":["1, 2, 3","2, 4, 6","3, 3, 3","1, 3, 5"],"correct":3,"explain":"Odd counts (2r+1) keep the pyramid symmetric.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4871","topic":"","text":"Art tasks in graders fail most often on:","options":["trailing spaces or missing newlines","logic","loops","stars"],"correct":0,"explain":"Exact-match comparison - the invisible characters count (19.10).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4872","topic":"","text":"A bordered box of width w uses:","options":["one loop total","top, edged middles, bottom","fillRect","recursion"],"correct":1,"explain":"Three print phases - the structure mirrors the picture.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4873","topic":"","text":"Repeating a string, like "-".repeat(10):","options":["errors","prints 10 lines","produces ---------- concisely","loops visibly"],"correct":2,"explain":"The repeat method (Java 11+) shortens art code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4874","topic":"","text":"Parameterizing art size (triangle of height n) turns art into:","options":["a constant program","graphics","file I/O","a loop-bounds exercise in one variable"],"correct":3,"explain":"Generalizing from 4 to n is the actual lesson.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4875","topic":"","text":"Printing "* " (star space) per star and trimming the last space:","options":["matters for exact-match graders","never matters","is automatic","breaks loops"],"correct":0,"explain":"Separator discipline - print between, not after.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4876","topic":"","text":"Art exercises train precisely:","options":["drawing skill","loop boundaries and output exactness","file formats","recursion"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pretty pictures, serious off-by-one practice.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u4/z4_3":[{"id":"zya4_3_0","topic":"Sentinel loop","text":"A sentinel-controlled loop stops when:","options":["An exception occurs","A counter reaches a limit","A special marker value is read","The array ends"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reading until -1 or "quit" is sentinel control; the count is not known in advance."},{"id":"zya4_3_1","topic":"Reading before the loop","text":"A sentinel loop needs a read before the while because:","options":["The compiler requires every variable to be read once","The condition must have a value to test on the first pass","The sentinel value must be printed before the loop","A while loop cannot read input inside its body"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is the priming read. Without it the first test examines an uninitialized or stale value."},{"id":"zya4_3_2","topic":"Accumulator","text":"int sum = 0; while(...) { sum += x; } — sum is:","options":["An accumulator that builds a running total","A counter that records how many times the loop ran","A sentinel that signals when the loop should stop","An invariant that stays unchanged each iteration"],"correct":0,"explain":"Accumulators must be initialized before the loop, normally to 0 for sums and 1 for products."},{"id":"zya4_3_3","topic":"Counting matches","text":"To count how many inputs exceed 100:","options":["count += x;","sum += x;","if (x > 100) count++;","count = x;"],"correct":2,"explain":"A counter increments by 1 per match, regardless of the value."},{"id":"zya4_3_4","topic":"Averaging in a loop","text":"After summing n values, the average is:","options":["sum % n","sum / n with both ints","(double) sum / n","sum / (double) 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Cast before dividing or integer division truncates the answer."},{"id":"zya4_3_5","topic":"Empty input guard","text":"Before dividing by a count, you must check:","options":["Nothing","That the loop ran twice","That the count is positive","That the sum is positive"],"correct":2,"explain":"Zero inputs would divide by zero — ArithmeticException for ints, NaN for doubles."},{"id":"zy4_3_4_bp_D286_t18_17","topic":"Sentinel bug","text":"Reading inside the loop FIRST in a sentinel loop:","options":["wrongly includes the sentinel value","is faster","skips everything","is correct"],"correct":0,"explain":"Processing before re-testing adds the -1 to the result.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy4_3_5_bp_D286_t24_23","topic":"Read pattern","text":"The correct sentinel pattern is:","options":["read → test → process → read","process → read","read once","test → read → process"],"correct":0,"explain":"Read first, test the sentinel, process, then read again.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z4_3_1","topic":"Digit counting","text":"What does this print?

int n = 4372;
int count = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    n = n / 10;
    count++;
}
System.out.println(count);","options":["3","4","5","0"],"correct":1,"explain":"Integer division chops one digit per pass: 4372 -> 437 -> 43 -> 4 -> 0, which is four passes, so count is 4. The loop stops when n reaches 0."},{"id":"fs_z4_3_2","topic":"Digit extraction","text":"What does this print?

int n = 123;
int sum = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    sum += n % 10;
    n = n / 10;
}
System.out.println(sum);","options":["321","123","3","6"],"correct":3,"explain":"n % 10 extracts the last digit and n / 10 removes it: the passes add 3, then 2, then 1, so sum is 6. This digit-extraction pattern depends entirely on integer division and modulo."},{"id":"fs_z4_3_3","topic":"Accumulator pattern","text":"What does this print?

int sum = 0;
int i = 1;
while (i <= 4) {
    sum += i;
    i++;
}
System.out.println(sum);","options":["10","15","6","4"],"correct":0,"explain":"The accumulator gathers 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10. The condition i <= 4 includes 4 itself; the tempting answer 6 comes from stopping at 3 as if the condition were i < 4."},{"id":"fs_z4_3_4","topic":"Accumulator placement","text":"What goes wrong if a running-total variable is declared inside the loop body instead of before the loop?","options":["It is reset to its initial value on every pass, so no total ever accumulates","The code will not compile","The total accumulates twice as fast","Nothing — the placement makes no difference"],"correct":0,"explain":"A variable declared inside the loop is a brand-new variable each pass, so it starts over from its initial value every time. An accumulator must be declared before the loop so its value survives across passes — this is the single most common accumulator bug."},{"id":"fs_z4_3_5","topic":"Sentinel values","text":"In a sentinel-controlled loop, how is the sentinel value itself treated?","options":["It is a stop signal and is never processed as data","It is added to the total like any other value","It is processed first, then the loop ends","It is stored at the end of the results"],"correct":0,"explain":"The sentinel is a signal, not data — which is why the read happens before the test: the while condition rejects the sentinel before the body can process it."},{"id":"fs_z4_3_6","topic":"Average and integer division","text":"What does this print?

int sum = 7;
int count = 2;
double avg = sum / count;
System.out.println(avg);","options":["3.0","3","Compiler error","3.5"],"correct":0,"explain":"sum / count is int / int, so integer division happens first, giving 3; only then is 3 widened to the double 3.0 for storage. To get 3.5 you must cast before dividing: (double) sum / count."},{"id":"zf626","topic":"","text":"int total = 0; int v = 16; while (v > 0) { total = total + v; v = v / 2; } System.out.println(total); prints:","options":["30","16","31","32"],"correct":2,"explain":"16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 31; then v becomes 0 and the loop ends.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf627","topic":"","text":"int count = 0; int n = 45; while (n > 0) { n = n / 10; count = count + 1; } System.out.println(count); prints:","options":["1","3","45","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each pass drops a digit: 45 -> 4 -> 0. Two passes = the digit count.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf628","topic":"","text":"while (!done) { ... } keeps running while:","options":["done is false","done is true","done is null","forever regardless"],"correct":0,"explain":"The ! flips it: NOT done means keep going.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf629","topic":"","text":"int steps = 0; int x = 1; while (x < 100) { x = x * 3; steps = steps + 1; } System.out.println(steps); prints:","options":["4","5","6","100"],"correct":1,"explain":"x: 1->3->9->27->81->243. Five multiplications until x reaches 243 >= 100.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf630","topic":"","text":"int balance = 100; while (balance >= 30) { balance = balance - 30; } System.out.println(balance); prints:","options":["0","10","30","-20"],"correct":1,"explain":"100 -> 70 -> 40 -> 10. At 10 the condition fails - like repeated withdrawals.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf631","topic":"","text":"Reading user input until it is valid is naturally:","options":["a single if","a constant","a while loop re-prompting until valid","an array"],"correct":2,"explain":"Ask, check, re-ask - repetition with an unknown count.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf632","topic":"","text":"int digitsSum = 0; int num = 234; while (num > 0) { digitsSum = digitsSum + num % 10; num = num / 10; } System.out.println(digitsSum); prints:","options":["234","6","23","9"],"correct":3,"explain":"4 + 3 + 2 = 9. % 10 peels the last digit; / 10 discards it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf633","topic":"","text":"int p = 1; while (p < 50) { p = p * 2; } System.out.println(p); prints:","options":["64","32","50","128"],"correct":0,"explain":"Doubling: 1,2,4,8,16,32,64. The first power of two at or past 50 is 64.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf634","topic":"","text":"The condition of a while loop is checked:","options":["after every iteration","before every iteration, even the first","once at program start","only when a break runs"],"correct":1,"explain":"That is why a false-from-the-start while body never executes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf635","topic":"","text":"int gcdA = 24; int gcdB = 18; while (gcdB != 0) { int t = gcdB; gcdB = gcdA % gcdB; gcdA = t; } System.out.println(gcdA); prints:","options":["24","18","6","2"],"correct":2,"explain":"Euclid's algorithm: (24,18) -> (18,6) -> (6,0). The GCD of 24 and 18 is 6.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf869","topic":"","text":"int w = 3; while (w != 0) { w = w - 1; } System.out.println(w); prints:","options":["3","-1","it loops forever","0"],"correct":3,"explain":"w counts 3, 2, 1, 0; at 0 the != test fails and 0 prints.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u4/z4_8":[{"id":"zya4_8_1","topic":"Stub","text":"A stub is:","options":["A method that has been commented out entirely","A placeholder method that compiles so the rest can be tested","A method that throws an exception when called","A comment describing a method not yet written"],"correct":1,"explain":"Returning a fixed value lets you build and test the caller before the real logic exists."},{"id":"zya4_8_2","topic":"Test after each step","text":"Compiling and running after each small change:","options":["Localizes errors to the change just made","Makes the final program run more quickly","Removes the need to test the finished program","Guarantees the change introduced no new defect"],"correct":0,"explain":"The alternative is a wall of errors with no idea which change caused which."},{"id":"zya4_8_3","topic":"Hard-coded input first","text":"Starting with a fixed test value instead of Scanner input:","options":["Is required, since Scanner cannot be used in tests","Makes the finished program run without any input","Prevents the compiler checking the input type","Removes one source of error while the logic is developed"],"correct":3,"explain":"It also makes each run reproducible without retyping input."},{"id":"zya4_8_4","topic":"Order of features","text":"With several features to build, you should:","options":["Write all of them, then compile once at the end","Start with the hardest while you are freshest","Get the simplest one fully working first","Write every method signature before any logic"],"correct":2,"explain":"An early working version is something to compare against when the next feature breaks things."},{"id":"zya4_8_5","topic":"Version safety","text":"Before a large risky change you should:","options":["Delete the old code so it cannot confuse you","Keep a working copy you can return to","Comment out the tests until the change is done","Make every related change in one single edit"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is precisely the problem version control solves."},{"id":"fs_z4_8_1","topic":"Stubs","text":"A developer writes this so the rest of the program can compile and run before the tax logic exists:

public static double taxFor(double pay) {
    return 0.0;
}

This method is an example of:","options":["A stub","A recursive method","An overloaded method","A trace print"],"correct":0,"explain":"A stub has the right name and signature but a trivial body, so the surrounding program can be compiled and exercised before that part is really written. It is what keeps a program runnable at every step."},{"id":"fs_z4_8_2","topic":"Why increments help debugging","text":"The main debugging advantage of compiling and running after every small addition is:","options":["The finished program runs faster","It removes the need for test data","When something breaks, the cause is almost certainly in the few lines just added","The compiler produces fewer warnings"],"correct":2,"explain":"Incremental development means you always have a running program, so a new failure points straight at the newest few lines. Writing sixty lines and then compiling gives a pile of errors with no ordering and no way to tell which are consequences of which."},{"id":"fs_z4_8_3","topic":"Trace output","text":"A temporary debugging print placed inside a loop is most useful when it shows:","options":["Only the final result after the loop","A fixed message with no values in it","The total number of lines in the program","The loop variable together with the value the pass changed"],"correct":3,"explain":"A value alone rarely tells you which pass produced it. Printing the loop variable alongside whatever the body changed lets you see exactly where the computation first goes wrong."},{"id":"fs_z4_8_4","topic":"Hard-coding input first","text":"Why hard-code a fixed input value before wiring the program up to read user input?","options":["Scanner input is too slow for testing","Fixed values make the program compile faster","User input is not allowed until a program is finished","The calculation can be verified against a value you can check by hand"],"correct":3,"explain":"Hard-coding the input applies incremental development to data: get the calculation right with a known value first, and only then add the input-reading step. Each piece is verified on its own."},{"id":"fs_z4_8_5","topic":"Trace print output","text":"What does this print?

int sum = 0;
