}","options":["1 2 3","6","1 1
2 2
3 3","1 1
2 3
3 6"],"correct":3,"explain":"The trace print shows the loop variable and the running total together each pass: after adding 1 sum is 1, after adding 2 it is 3, after adding 3 it is 6. This pairing is what makes trace output useful."},{"id":"fs_z4_8_6","topic":"Stub behaviour","text":"taxFor is currently a stub:

static double taxFor(double pay) {
    return 0.0;
}

What does this print?

System.out.println(taxFor(50000.0));","options":["Compiler error — the method has no real body","11000.0","0","0.0"],"correct":3,"explain":"A stub compiles and runs like any method; it just gives a trivial answer. taxFor returns the double 0.0, and println shows a double with its decimal point."},{"id":"zf681","topic":"","text":"The core habit of incremental development is:","options":["write everything, then compile once at the end","never testing until the deadline","deleting working code often","write a small piece, compile, test, then extend"],"correct":3,"explain":"Small verified steps keep every bug recent and local.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf682","topic":"","text":"The first "increment" of a program that will read, validate, and total scores should be:","options":["reading and echoing input, verified","the full validation logic","the final report formatting","all three at once"],"correct":0,"explain":"Get data flowing first; each later feature builds on proof, not hope.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf683","topic":"","text":"Compiling after every small change means:","options":["the compiler memorizes your code","new errors come from the new lines","fewer features get built","tests become unnecessary"],"correct":1,"explain":"A tiny diff makes the search space for any new error tiny too.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf684","topic":"","text":"A "stub" method used during incremental building:","options":["is a finished, optimized method","is a compile error kept on purpose","a real signature with a placeholder body","is a comment block"],"correct":2,"explain":"Stubs let the skeleton stand while organs are added one by one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf685","topic":"","text":"Testing each increment with KNOWN expected outputs matters because:","options":["known outputs are prettier","the JVM requires it","it removes the need for a compiler","wrong results are caught one step old"],"correct":3,"explain":"Compare actual vs expected at every stage - drift is caught immediately.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf686","topic":"","text":"When an increment breaks something that worked before, the FIRST suspect is:","options":["the change just made","the operating system","last month’s code","the keyboard"],"correct":0,"explain":"That is the debugging payoff of small steps: the culprit is fresh.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf687","topic":"","text":"Adding print statements that show a loop’s counter and total each pass is:","options":["forbidden in Java","a legitimate verification technique","a syntax error","only for experts"],"correct":1,"explain":"Temporary tracing output verifies loop behavior against hand-computed values.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf688","topic":"","text":"The wrong way to build a 200-line program as a beginner is:","options":["20 verified steps of 10 lines","stubbing the hard parts","typing all 200 lines, then compiling","testing along the way"],"correct":2,"explain":"A first compile with dozens of tangled errors is exactly what increments avoid.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf689","topic":"","text":"Version-controlling (or copying) each working increment lets you:","options":["skip compiling","avoid all bugs forever","write without thinking","roll back to the last good state"],"correct":3,"explain":"A known-good checkpoint turns disasters into small rollbacks.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf690","topic":"","text":"In the salary-program example, a sensible increment order is:","options":["format output first","overtime before reading input","all at once","input -> base pay -> overtime -> output"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each stage consumes the verified result of the one before.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf691","topic":"","text":"An increment should be sized so that:","options":["it is written and tested in one sitting","it takes a week","it touches every file","it cannot be tested"],"correct":0,"explain":"If you cannot test it promptly, the increment is too big.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf692","topic":"","text":"The main risk incremental development guards against is:","options":["using too few variables","a pile of errors found all at once","writing too many comments","programs ending too early"],"correct":1,"explain":"Debugging one fresh error is easy; debugging fifty stale ones is misery.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zj9","topic":"Incremental development","text":"You add a loop, run the program, then add the calculation inside it and run again. The benefit is:","options":["the compiler produces faster bytecode this way","a new fault can only be in the piece you just added","you no longer need to test the finished program","variables can be declared after they are used"],"correct":1,"explain":"Building in small tested steps localises every new bug to the small change you just made, instead of leaving you to search the whole program.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zya1_9_5","topic":"Incremental development","text":"Developing incrementally means:","options":["Writing code without comments","Using only one method","Writing the entire program then compiling once","Building and testing a small piece at a time"],"correct":3,"explain":"When you add one small piece and it breaks, you know exactly what caused it. Writing 300 lines then compiling gives you a wall of errors."}],"D286/u4/z4_10":[{"id":"zya4_10_0","topic":"Block scope","text":"A variable declared inside a loop body:","options":["Is static","Is visible after the loop","Exists only within that block","Becomes a field"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is created and destroyed each iteration, which is why it cannot accumulate across passes."},{"id":"zya4_10_1","topic":"Accumulator placement","text":"An accumulator must be declared:","options":["After the loop","Inside the loop body","Before the loop","In the loop header"],"correct":2,"explain":"Declaring it inside resets it every iteration, so the total is always the last value."},{"id":"zya4_10_2","topic":"Redeclaration","text":"Declaring int x inside a loop when x already exists outside is:","options":["A runtime error","Allowed and shadows the outer one","A compile error in Java","Allowed and merges them"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java forbids a local variable shadowing another local. Fields, by contrast, can be shadowed by locals."},{"id":"zya4_10_3","topic":"Loop variable after the loop","text":"for (int i=0;...){} System.out.println(i); gives:","options":["A runtime error","The final value of i","A compile error","0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Declare i before the loop if you need its value afterwards. I is out of scope."},{"id":"zya4_10_4","topic":"Scope and lifetime","text":"A local variable's memory is released when:","options":["Garbage collection runs","The program ends","Its enclosing block finishes","It is set to null"],"correct":2,"explain":"Locals live on the stack and disappear with their frame or block."},{"id":"zya4_10_5","topic":"Narrow scope benefit","text":"Declaring variables in the smallest scope that works:","options":["Makes the compiled program measurably faster","Is required by the compiler inside every loop","Reduces accidental reuse and makes the code easier to follow","Allows the variable to be shared between methods"],"correct":2,"explain":"It also lets the reader see the variable's whole life at once."},{"id":"zyf4_10_0","topic":"Loop counter reuse","text":"Reusing one counter variable for two sequential loops:","options":["Is a compile error, since the name is duplicated","Always fails, because the second loop starts high","Works if it is declared before both and reset appropriately","Works only when the loops have the same bounds"],"correct":2,"explain":"Forgetting to reset it is the usual bug."},{"id":"fs_z4_10_1","topic":"Fresh variable each pass","text":"What does this print?

for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    int sum = 0;
    sum += i;
    System.out.print(sum + " ");
}","options":["Compiler error","1 3 6","1 2 3","0 0 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"sum is declared inside the loop, so it is a genuinely new variable each pass, recreated at 0 before adding i. It never accumulates — the running-total answer 1 3 6 would require sum to be declared before the loop."},{"id":"fs_z4_10_2","topic":"Surviving the loop","text":"In the linear-search pattern, why is int found = -1; declared before the loop rather than inside it?","options":["It makes the loop run faster","It must still exist after the loop ends so the result can be used","Java forbids declaring int variables inside loops","-1 can only be assigned outside a loop body"],"correct":1,"explain":"A variable exists only from its declaration to the closing brace of its block. Declared inside the loop, found would vanish at the loop's closing brace; declared before it, the found index survives to be tested afterwards."},{"id":"fs_z4_10_3","topic":"Scope definition","text":"The scope of a local variable extends from:","options":["Its declaration to the end of the source file","The opening brace of main to the end of the program","Its first use to its last use","Its declaration to the closing brace of the block containing it"],"correct":3,"explain":"The rule is simple: a variable exists from its declaration to the closing brace of the enclosing block, and nowhere else. Everything about loop-variable visibility follows from that one rule."},{"id":"fs_z4_10_4","topic":"Scope versus lifetime","text":"For a local variable, what is the difference between scope and lifetime?","options":["They are unrelated concepts that never overlap","Scope is where the name can be seen; lifetime is when the value exists","Scope applies to primitives, lifetime to objects","Lifetime is always longer than scope"],"correct":1,"explain":"Scope answers "where can this name be seen" and lifetime answers "when does the value exist". For a local variable they coincide — created at the declaration, destroyed at the closing brace — which is why a variable declared in a loop body is a new variable every pass. And for locals the two coincide."},{"id":"fs_z4_10_5","topic":"Sequential loop headers","text":"Two consecutive loops each declare their own counter:

for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { }
for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) { }

What happens?","options":["Compile error: i is already defined","The second loop continues counting from 3","A runtime error occurs","It compiles and runs fine — each i is scoped to its own loop, so the names never clash"],"correct":3,"explain":"A variable declared in a for header exists only within that loop. The first i is gone by the time the second loop declares its own, so there is no conflict and the second loop starts fresh at 0."},{"id":"zf693","topic":"","text":"for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { } System.out.println(i); causes:","options":["the output 3","the output 2","a compile error","the output 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"The header variable belongs to the loop; it does not exist afterwards.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf694","topic":"","text":"To use the loop counter AFTER the loop ends, declare it:","options":["inside the body","twice","as a String","before the loop, as in int i = 0"],"correct":3,"explain":"Declaring outside widens the scope to the enclosing block.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf695","topic":"","text":"The scope of a variable is:","options":["the code region where its name works","its numeric range","how long the program runs","its position in memory"],"correct":0,"explain":"Scope is about visibility of the NAME, decided at compile time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf696","topic":"","text":"Declaring int x inside a block when an int x already exists in the same method:","options":["shadows it silently","is a compile error","merges the two","renames the outer one"],"correct":1,"explain":"Java forbids redeclaring a local name inside an overlapping local scope.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf697","topic":"","text":"A method parameter’s scope is:","options":["the whole class","one statement","the entire body of that method","the calling method too"],"correct":2,"explain":"Parameters are locals initialized by the caller’s arguments.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf698","topic":"","text":"if (ok) { int t = 5; } System.out.println(t); causes:","options":["the output 5","the output 0","a runtime exception","a compile error"],"correct":3,"explain":"Braces bound the variable’s life; outside them the name is unknown.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf699","topic":"","text":"Two SEPARATE (non-nested) blocks can each declare their own int temp because:","options":["their scopes do not overlap","temp is a keyword","Java merges them into one","only one will compile"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sibling blocks are independent; the same name can be reused cleanly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf700","topic":"","text":"Keeping variable scope as SMALL as possible is good practice because:","options":["small scopes run faster","the compiler requires it","names become shorter","less code can see (and break) it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Narrow scope shrinks the search space when something goes wrong.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf701","topic":"","text":"int total10 = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { int add10 = 2; total10 += add10; } System.out.println(total10); prints:","options":["6","2","0","a compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"add10 is recreated each pass, but total10 (outer scope) accumulates: 2+2+2.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf702","topic":"","text":"The loop for (int i = 0; ...) followed later by for (int i = 5; ...) is:","options":["a compile error","fine - each i is scoped to its own loop","one shared i","undefined behavior"],"correct":1,"explain":"The first i dies with its loop; the second is a brand-new variable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf703","topic":"","text":"A variable declared at the top of main is visible:","options":["before its declaration too","in every method","from its declaration to the end of main","only on its own line"],"correct":2,"explain":"Visibility starts AT the declaration line - not above it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf704","topic":"","text":"Which declaration placement is best for a value used only inside one if block?","options":["at the top of the class","before main","outside all braces","inside that if block"],"correct":3,"explain":"Declare closest to use - the narrowest scope that works.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf870","topic":"","text":"A local variable named total in main and another named total in a different method:","options":["are the same variable","conflict at compile time","share their value","are two unrelated variables"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each method has its own scope; the shared name is a coincidence, not a connection.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u4/z4_11":[{"id":"zya4_11_0","topic":"Enum purpose","text":"An enum defines:","options":["A class with a single instance created lazily","An interface whose methods have no bodies","A set of integers with automatic conversion","A fixed set of named constant values"],"correct":3,"explain":"enum Day { MON, TUE, WED } gives a type whose values are exactly those constants."},{"id":"zya4_11_1","topic":"Enum type safety","text":"The advantage of an enum over int constants is:","options":["Enums occupy less memory than int constants","Enums can be compared with < and > operators","The compiler rejects values outside the set","Enums are converted to ints automatically"],"correct":2,"explain":"An int parameter accepts 47; a Day parameter accepts only real days."},{"id":"zya4_11_2","topic":"Enum in a switch","text":"An enum can be used in a switch:","options":["Yes, but each case must repeat the enum type","Yes, and the case labels omit the type name","No, switch works only on int and String","No, unless the enum defines an ordinal method"],"correct":1,"explain":"You write case MON:, not case Day.MON:."},{"id":"zya4_11_3","topic":"Enum values()","text":"Day.values() returns:","options":["The number of constants","An array of all the constants","A String","The first constant"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which makes for (Day d : Day.values()) the standard way to iterate them."},{"id":"zya4_11_4","topic":"Enum comparison","text":"Two enum constants are compared with:","options":["== is unsafe; equals() must always be used","compareTo() is required for any comparison","== works only after calling name() on each","== is safe because constants are singletons"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each constant exists once, so reference comparison is both correct and idiomatic."},{"id":"zya4_11_5","topic":"Enum ordinal","text":"The ordinal() of the first constant is:","options":["1","0","-1","Undefined"],"correct":1,"explain":"Positions are zero-based, though relying on ordinal values is fragile if the order changes."},{"id":"zya4_11_6","topic":"Enum naming","text":"Enum constants conventionally use:","options":["camelCase","ALL_CAPS","PascalCase","lowercase"],"correct":1,"explain":"They are constants, so they follow constant naming."},{"id":"zya4_11_7","topic":"Enum with fields","text":"An enum can have fields and methods:","options":["Yes — it is a class with a fixed set of instances","No — an enum may contain only constant names","Yes, but only static fields are permitted","No — fields require a class rather than an enum"],"correct":0,"explain":"This is how each constant can carry data, such as a display label or a numeric weight."},{"id":"zya4_11_8","topic":"Enum vs String","text":"Using an enum instead of String constants prevents:","options":["All bugs","Typos","Null values entirely","Exceptions"],"correct":1,"explain":""Mondey" compiles; Day.MONDEY does not. The compiler checks the name."},{"id":"zya4_11_9","topic":"Where enums are declared","text":"An enum can be declared:","options":["Only inside a method","At class level or in its own file","Only in main","Only in interfaces"],"correct":1,"explain":"A local enum inside a method is not allowed in older Java versions."},{"id":"fs_z4_11_1","topic":"Iterating enum values","text":"Given:

enum Day { MON, TUE, WED }

what does this print?

for (Day d : Day.values()) {
    System.out.print(d + " ");
}","options":["TUE WED MON","Day.MON Day.TUE Day.WED","Compiler error","MON TUE WED"],"correct":3,"explain":"values() returns every constant in declaration order, and printing a constant shows its name without the type prefix. So the loop prints MON TUE WED."},{"id":"fs_z4_11_2","topic":"ordinal()","text":"Given:

enum Size { SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE }

what does this print?

System.out.println(Size.LARGE.ordinal());","options":["LARGE","3","1","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"ordinal() is the declaration position starting at 0: SMALL is 0, MEDIUM is 1, LARGE is 2. Counting from 1 gives the tempting wrong answer 3."},{"id":"fs_z4_11_3","topic":"valueOf()","text":"For enum Day { MON, TUE, WED }, what does Day.valueOf("TUE") do?","options":["Returns null whenever the name is not found","Creates a brand-new Day constant named TUE","Returns the ordinal position of TUE","Returns Day.TUE if the name matches exactly; otherwise it throws an exception"],"correct":3,"explain":"valueOf converts a String to the matching constant, requiring an exact name match — "tue" or "Tuesday" would throw an IllegalArgumentException rather than return null."},{"id":"fs_z4_11_4","topic":"ordinal() fragility","text":"Why is storing d.ordinal() (for example in a file or database) considered risky?","options":["ordinal() returns a different value on every run","ordinal() is very slow","Ordinals start at 1, which wastes the value 0","Reordering the enum's constants silently changes every stored ordinal's meaning"],"correct":3,"explain":"ordinal() returns the declaration position, so reordering or inserting constants silently shifts every value. If a number genuinely matters, give the enum a field carrying it instead of relying on position."},{"id":"zf705","topic":"","text":"An enum like enum Season { WINTER, SPRING, SUMMER, FALL } defines:","options":["a type limited to the listed constants","four int variables","four Strings","a loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Variables of type Season can hold exactly those four values - nothing else.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf706","topic":"","text":"Season s = Season.SUMMER; shows that enum values are accessed:","options":["with quotes","through the enum type name","with new","by index number"],"correct":1,"explain":"EnumName.CONSTANT is the standard reference.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf707","topic":"","text":"Enum constants are conventionally named:","options":["in camelCase","in quotes","in ALL_CAPS","with digits first"],"correct":2,"explain":"They are constants, so they follow constant naming.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf708","topic":"","text":"The chief advantage of an enum over int codes (0 = winter, 1 = spring...):","options":["enums use less memory always","ints cannot be compared","enums are faster to type","the compiler rejects invalid values"],"correct":3,"explain":"Season s = 7 is impossible; int season = 7 compiles and lurks as a bug.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf709","topic":"","text":"Comparing enum values, as in s == Season.WINTER:","options":["is safe and idiomatic","requires equals always","is a compile error","needs a cast"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each constant exists once, so reference comparison is reliable for enums.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf710","topic":"","text":"An enum can be used in a switch statement:","options":["directly, with constants as case labels","never","only after casting to int","only with strings"],"correct":0,"explain":"switch (s) { case WINTER: ... } reads exactly like the domain.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf711","topic":"","text":"Season.values() returns:","options":["the number of constants","an array of all the constants in order","a random constant","the names as one string"],"correct":1,"explain":"Handy for looping over every value: for (Season x : Season.values()).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf712","topic":"","text":"Trying to assign Season s = "WINTER"; causes:","options":["automatic conversion","s to be null","a compile error","a runtime exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"Enums are their own type; strings do not convert implicitly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf713","topic":"","text":"Creating an enum value with new, as in new Season():","options":["makes a fifth season","copies SUMMER","is required once","is a compile error"],"correct":3,"explain":"The fixed set of constants is created by the JVM; no instantiation allowed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf714","topic":"","text":"Printing an enum constant, System.out.println(Season.FALL);, shows:","options":["FALL","3","Season","autumn"],"correct":0,"explain":"The default toString of an enum constant is its name.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf715","topic":"","text":"A good candidate for an enum is:","options":["arbitrary user-typed names","the days of the week","a running total","file contents"],"correct":1,"explain":"Small fixed sets of named alternatives are exactly what enums model.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u4/z4_12":[{"id":"zya4_12_0","topic":"Summing weekly pay","text":"To total pay over n weeks, the loop should:","options":["Assign each week to the total inside the loop","Initialise the total inside the loop each pass","Multiply the first week's pay by the week count","Add each week to a running total initialised before the loop"],"correct":3,"explain":"total += weekPay; with total = 0 declared outside the loop."},{"id":"zya4_12_1","topic":"Loop count for a year","text":"To iterate over 52 weeks:","options":["for (int w = 1; w < 53; w++)","for (int w = 1; w < 52; w++)","for (int w = 0; w <= 52; w++)","for (int w = 0; w < 53; w++)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both w=1..<=52 and w=1..<53 give 52 iterations. Option 1 gives 51 and option 3 gives 53 — the classic off-by-one pair."},{"id":"zya4_12_2","topic":"Highest pay so far","text":"Tracking the maximum weekly pay uses:","options":["if (pay > max) max = pay;","max += pay;","max = pay / n;","max = pay;"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compare then replace. Initialize max to the first value or to a very small number."},{"id":"zya4_12_3","topic":"Counting overtime weeks","text":"To count weeks with over 40 hours:","options":["if (hours > 40) count++;","count = hours;","count++ always","count += hours;"],"correct":0,"explain":"A counter increments once per qualifying week regardless of by how much."},{"id":"zya4_12_4","topic":"Average weekly pay","text":"After the loop, average pay is:","options":["total / (double) weeks","total % weeks","total * weeks","total / weeks with both ints"],"correct":0,"explain":"Cast so the division is floating-point."},{"id":"zya4_12_5","topic":"Raise applied each year","text":"Applying a 3% annual raise over 5 years uses:","options":["salary = salary + 1.03 inside a 5-iteration loop","salary *= 0.03 inside a 5-iteration loop","salary *= 1.03 inside a 5-iteration loop","salary *= 1.03 once, outside any loop"],"correct":2,"explain":"Compounding requires reapplying the multiplier to the updated value each year."},{"id":"zya4_12_6","topic":"Accumulator initialization","text":"A running total should start at:","options":["The first value or 0","Any value","1","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"0 for a sum. Starting at the first value works too, provided the loop then begins at the second."},{"id":"zya4_12_7","topic":"Printing per-week detail","text":"Printing each week inside the loop and the total after it:","options":["Is the correct placement","Prints the total 52 times","Causes an error","Is impossible"],"correct":0,"explain":"Placement relative to the loop is what decides how many times something prints. Detail inside, summary after."},{"id":"zya4_12_8","topic":"Loop guard for input","text":"Reading pay values until a sentinel of -1 uses:","options":["A while loop testing against the sentinel","A do-while with no condition","Nested loops","A for loop with a fixed count"],"correct":0,"explain":"The count is not known in advance, which is exactly when while beats for."},{"id":"zya4_12_9","topic":"Formatting the total","text":"Printing the annual total to two decimals uses:","options":["printf("%.2f%n", total)","printf("%d%n", total)","print(total)","println(total)"],"correct":0,"explain":"%.2f formats a double; %d would throw for a double argument."},{"id":"fs_z4_12_1","topic":"Accumulator placed inside","text":"A developer accidentally declares the payroll total inside the loop. What does this print?

for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    double total = 0;
    total += 100 * i;
    if (i == 3) {
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}","options":["300.0","0.0","100.0","600.0"],"correct":0,"explain":"total is recreated at 0 on every pass, so it only ever holds the current pass's amount. On the final pass it holds 100 * 3 = 300.0 — the last employee's pay, not the 600.0 grand total that declaring it before the loop would give."},{"id":"fs_z4_12_2","topic":"Declaration placement","text":"In a payroll loop over many employees, why is rate declared inside the loop body while totalPayroll is declared before the loop?","options":["Each employee needs a fresh rate, while the total must accumulate across all employees","Java requires doubles to be declared inside loops","rate would not compile if declared outside","The order of declarations never matters"],"correct":0,"explain":"totalPayroll outside the loop accumulates across passes; rate inside the loop is recreated per employee so one employee's rate can never leak into the next. Swapping the placements produces two different silent bugs."},{"id":"fs_z4_12_3","topic":"Tiered rate selection","text":"What does this print?

double salary = 60000;
double rate;
if (salary >= 100000) {
    rate = 0.28;
} else if (salary >= 50000) {
    rate = 0.22;
} else {
    rate = 0.15;
}
System.out.println(rate);","options":["0.22","0.37","0.15","0.28"],"correct":0,"explain":"60000 fails the first test (>= 100000) but passes the second (>= 50000), so rate is 0.22 and the else never runs. The chain assigns exactly one rate because else-if branches are mutually exclusive."},{"id":"fs_z4_12_4","topic":"printf formatting","text":"What does this print?

System.out.printf("%.2f", 3.14159);","options":["3.14","3.14159","3.142","3"],"correct":0,"explain":"%.2f formats a floating-point value with exactly two digits after the decimal point, rounding as needed, so 3.14159 prints as 3.14."},{"id":"zf716","topic":"","text":"Weekly hours are 40, 35, and 45 at a rate of 10 per hour. A loop adds hours * 10 for each week into total12. The final total is:","options":["120","1000","1200","400"],"correct":2,"explain":"(40 + 35 + 45) x 10 = 1200. The loop accumulates one week per pass.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf717","topic":"","text":"int pay12 = 1000; for (int y = 0; y < 3; y++) pay12 = pay12 + 100; System.out.println(pay12); prints:","options":["1100","1000","3000","1300"],"correct":3,"explain":"Three annual raises of 100: 1000 -> 1100 -> 1200 -> 1300.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf718","topic":"","text":"double sal12 = 1000.0; for (int y = 0; y < 2; y++) sal12 = sal12 * 1.1; The result is closest to:","options":["1210","1200","1100","1331"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compound 10%: 1000 -> 1100 -> 1210. Multiplying beats adding for growth.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf719","topic":"","text":"A payroll loop over 4 employees printing each pay runs its body:","options":["once","4 times","5 times","40 times"],"correct":1,"explain":"One pass per employee - the loop count mirrors the data count.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf720","topic":"","text":"int sum12 = 0; int c12 = 1; while (c12 <= 5) { sum12 += c12 * 100; c12++; } System.out.println(sum12); prints:","options":["500","1500","1000","1400"],"correct":1,"explain":"100+200+300+400+500 = 1500.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf721","topic":"","text":"Summing salaries but forgetting to initialize total12 = 0 before the loop:","options":["starts from garbage values","works fine","is a compile error for a local variable","throws at runtime"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java requires locals to be definitely assigned before use - the compiler blocks it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf722","topic":"","text":"To find the HIGHEST salary in a list with a loop, each pass does:","options":["max = salary; unconditionally","max = max + salary;","if (salary < max) max = salary;","if (salary > max) max = salary;"],"correct":3,"explain":"Keep the current champion; replace it only when beaten.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf723","topic":"","text":"Initializing that running max safely uses:","options":["the first salary in the list","0 - even if salaries could be negative","100","the last salary"],"correct":0,"explain":"Starting from a real data value works for any range; 0 fails if all values are below zero.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf724","topic":"","text":"int overtime12 = 0; int[] hrs are 38, 42, 45. For each over 40, overtime12 += (h - 40). The total is:","options":["5","7","9","2"],"correct":1,"explain":"42 gives 2, 45 gives 5, 38 gives none: 7 overtime hours.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf725","topic":"","text":"Why does a loop beat copy-pasting the pay calculation 12 times for a year?","options":["loops always compute faster","copy-paste will not compile","one body serves every month","12 is too many lines"],"correct":2,"explain":"Single source of truth - the loop body IS the calculation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf726","topic":"","text":"int net12 = 2000; while (net12 > 1500) { net12 = net12 - 300; } System.out.println(net12); prints:","options":["1500","1700","2000","1400"],"correct":3,"explain":"2000 -> 1700 -> 1400; at 1400 the condition fails.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u4/z4_13":[{"id":"zya4_13_0","topic":"Validating each character","text":"To confirm every character of a domain is allowed:","options":["Check only the first and last characters","Compare the whole string with a list of valid names","Count the characters and compare with a maximum","Loop over all characters, rejecting on the first invalid one"],"correct":3,"explain":"Returning false as soon as one fails avoids pointless work and is the standard validation shape."},{"id":"zya4_13_1","topic":"Early exit","text":"Returning false immediately on an invalid character is:","options":["Slower","Only valid in loops","A logic error","Correct and efficient"],"correct":3,"explain":"The overall answer cannot change once a violation is found. One failure is enough."},{"id":"zya4_13_2","topic":"Requiring a dot","text":"Ensuring a domain contains a dot uses:","options":["name.equals(".")","name.length() > 0","name.charAt(0) == '.'","name.contains(".")"],"correct":3,"explain":"contains checks anywhere in the String; the others test the wrong thing."},{"id":"zya4_13_3","topic":"Rejecting empty input","text":"A domain of "" should be:","options":["Rejected by the loop","Padded","Valid","Rejected before the loop"],"correct":3,"explain":"An empty String passes a loop-only check vacuously — a guard clause is needed for the empty case. The loop body never runs."},{"id":"zya4_13_4","topic":"Allowed characters","text":"Letters, digits, dots and hyphens are permitted. The test is:","options":["Character.isLetter(c) || c == '.' || c == '-'","Character.isDigit(c) || c == '.' || c == '-'","c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' || c == '.' || c == '-'","Character.isLetterOrDigit(c) || c == '.' || c == '-'"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each additional allowance must be ORed in explicitly."},{"id":"zya4_13_5","topic":"Loop bound","text":"Iterating a String's characters uses the bound:","options":["i < name.length","i <= name.length","i <= name.length()","i < name.length()"],"correct":3,"explain":"Strict less-than, and length() is a method for Strings."},{"id":"zy4_13_2_h20","topic":"Validation result","text":"setCelsius rejects c &lt; -273. After setCelsius(-500), getCelsius() returns:","options":["error","0 (rejected; field kept default)","-500","-273"],"correct":1,"explain":"The invalid value is rejected, so the field keeps its default 0.","__rebal":1},{"id":"fs_z4_13_1","topic":"Validation flag trace","text":"What does this print?

String s = "ab#cd";
boolean valid = true;
for (int i = 0; i < s.length() && valid; i++) {
    char c = s.charAt(i);
    if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(c) && c != '.') {
        valid = false;
    }
}
System.out.println(valid);","options":["false","valid","Compiler error","true"],"correct":0,"explain":"'#' is neither a letter, a digit, nor a dot, so the flag flips to false at index 2 — and because valid is part of the loop condition, the loop stops at the next check instead of scanning on."},{"id":"fs_z4_13_2","topic":"Flag in the loop condition","text":"Writing the loop condition as i < s.length() && valid achieves what?","options":["A compile error, because conditions cannot mix comparisons and booleans","The loop restarts from index 0 when valid changes","Early exit as soon as the flag turns false — the same effect as break, with the exit rule visible at the top","Nothing; boolean variables are ignored in loop conditions"],"correct":2,"explain":"Once the body falsifies valid, the next condition check fails and the loop stops — early termination without a break statement. Both styles are fine; this one keeps the exit rule in the header."},{"id":"fs_z4_13_3","topic":"Early exit versus full scan","text":"Checking "every character is legal" can stop at the first bad character, but checking "the string contains at least one dot" cannot stop early. Why?","options":["Dot comparisons are slower than letter comparisons","charAt cannot detect dots directly","The dot check requires a nested loop","A rule about every character fails on the first counter-example, but an existence rule is undecided until the whole string has been read"],"correct":3,"explain":"The two rules behave differently: one counter-example settles a universal rule immediately, while "at least one" can only be answered no after everything has been examined. That is why the dot check is a flag that survives the whole loop."},{"id":"fs_z4_13_4","topic":"Overwriting the flag","text":"A loop sets valid (all characters legal) and hasDot (a dot was seen). The final line is mistakenly written as valid = hasDot; instead of valid = valid && hasDot;. What is the effect?","options":["The program no longer compiles","Nothing — the two lines are equivalent","valid becomes false for every possible input","A string of illegal characters that happens to contain a dot is wrongly reported valid"],"correct":3,"explain":"valid = hasDot; throws away the character-legality result and answers only the dot question. Combining both rules with && preserves the earlier check; overwriting an answer that was already known is the genuinely wrong version."},{"id":"fs_z4_13_5","topic":"Counting characters","text":"What does this print?

String s = "a.b.c";
int dots = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
    if (s.charAt(i) == '.') {
        dots++;
    }
}
System.out.println(dots);","options":["5","3","1","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"The loop examines all five characters and counts the dots at indexes 1 and 3, so it prints 2. Counting occurrences is a full-scan job — there is no early exit."},{"id":"fs_z4_13_6","topic":"Why flags are never reset","text":"A validation flag is set to false on the first rule violation and never set back to true inside the loop. Why must it never be reset?","options":["Java does not allow assigning a boolean twice","Resetting it would make the loop infinite","Because the flag is declared final","A later legal character must not erase an earlier recorded failure"],"correct":3,"explain":"One bad character makes the whole string invalid, permanently. If the loop assigned the flag on every character, a legal character after the bad one would overwrite the recorded failure and the string would wrongly pass."},{"id":"zf727","topic":"","text":"A loop that checks each character of "my site" for spaces, rejecting if found, will:","options":["reject it - index 2 holds a space","accept it","crash","skip the space"],"correct":0,"explain":"The scan finds the space and the validation fails, as it should.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf728","topic":"","text":"String d13 = "shop.com"; System.out.println(d13.endsWith(".com")); prints:","options":["false","true",".com","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"The suffix matches exactly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf729","topic":"","text":"Validating that a name has ONLY letters and digits uses a loop with:","options":["equals on the whole string","one contains call","Character.isLetterOrDigit on each char","length alone"],"correct":2,"explain":"Per-character rules need a per-character scan.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf730","topic":"","text":"boolean ok13 = true; String n13 = "web-1"; for (int i = 0; i < n13.length(); i++) if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(n13.charAt(i))) ok13 = false; System.out.println(ok13); prints:","options":["true","web1","false","error"],"correct":2,"explain":"The hyphen at index 3 fails the letter-or-digit test and flips the flag.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf731","topic":"","text":"Rejecting empty input BEFORE the character loop, via if (name.isEmpty()) ...:","options":["is redundant - loops reject empties","causes an exception","is a compile error","catches what the loop would silently accept"],"correct":3,"explain":"A zero-length string makes the loop body run zero times - no check ever fails, so the empty must be caught explicitly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf732","topic":"","text":"int dots13 = 0; String u13 = "a.b.c"; for (int i = 0; i < u13.length(); i++) if (u13.charAt(i) == '.') dots13++; System.out.println(dots13); prints:","options":["2","1","3","5"],"correct":0,"explain":"Dots sit at indexes 1 and 3.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf733","topic":"","text":"A validation loop that should stop at the FIRST bad character uses:","options":["continue","break once the failure is found","a second loop","System.exit"],"correct":1,"explain":"No reason to scan on - the answer is already no.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf734","topic":"","text":"Checking a domain label length of 1 to 63 characters is:","options":["done by the character loop automatically","impossible in Java","a length range test before the char scan","a compiler setting"],"correct":2,"explain":"Length rules and character rules are separate checks; do the cheap one first.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf735","topic":"","text":"String host13 = "Mail.Example.COM"; host13.toLowerCase() is used before validation because:","options":["uppercase letters are illegal in Java","toLowerCase removes dots","it shortens the string","domains are case-insensitive; normalize once"],"correct":3,"explain":"Normalize first, then validate - fewer cases to reason about.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf736","topic":"","text":"boolean has13 = false; String s13 = "example.org"; for (int i = 0; i < s13.length(); i++) if (s13.charAt(i) == '.') has13 = true; This flag pattern computes:","options":["whether at least one dot exists anywhere","the number of dots","the position of the last dot","whether every character is a dot"],"correct":0,"explain":"Set-once flags answer existence questions; counters answer how-many.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf737","topic":"","text":"A loop validating "abc..com" against consecutive dots compares:","options":["charAt(i) twice","charAt(i) and charAt(i + 1)","the first and last characters","lengths of the halves"],"correct":1,"explain":"Adjacent-pair checks need the lookahead bound pulled in by one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf738","topic":"","text":"The overall shape of input validation code is:","options":["one giant expression nobody can read","acceptance first, checks later","checks that reject early; accept at the end","random spot checks"],"correct":2,"explain":"Guard-clause style: every rule gets its turn to say no.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_3":[{"id":"zya5_3_0","topic":"Standard traversal","text":"The standard loop over array a is:","options":["for (int i=0; i<=a.length; i++)","for (int i=0; i<a.length; i++)","for (int i=1; i<a.length; i++)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Indices are 0 to length-1, and length is a field for arrays, not a method."},{"id":"zya5_3_1","topic":"Last index","text":"For an array of size 5, the last valid index is:","options":["5","4","6","0"],"correct":1,"explain":"a[a.length - 1] is the general form for the last element."},{"id":"zya5_3_2","topic":"Out of bounds","text":"Accessing a[a.length] throws:","options":["NullPointerException","ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","A compile error","Nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"It is a runtime exception because the index is only known when the line executes."},{"id":"zya5_3_3","topic":"Summing an array","text":"int sum = 0; for (int i=0;i&lt;a.length;i++) sum += a[i]; computes:","options":["The array length","The total of all elements","The largest element","The average"],"correct":1,"explain":"The accumulator pattern applied to an array."},{"id":"zya5_3_4","topic":"Finding the maximum","text":"To find the largest value, initialize max to:","options":["0","a[0], then compare from index 1","The array length","Integer.MAX_VALUE"],"correct":1,"explain":"Starting at 0 gives the wrong answer for an array of all-negative numbers."},{"id":"zya5_3_5","topic":"Counting matches","text":"To count elements greater than 10:","options":["sum += a[i];","if (a[i] > 10) count++;","count = a[i];","count += a[i];"],"correct":1,"explain":"A counter, not an accumulator — it increments by one per match."},{"id":"zya5_3_6","topic":"Reverse traversal","text":"To walk an array backwards:","options":["for (int i=a.length; i>0; i--)","for (int i=a.length-1; i>=0; i--)","for (int i=0; i>a.length; i--)","for (int i=a.length-1; i>0; i--)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Start at the last index and continue through 0 inclusive."},{"id":"fs_z5_3_1","topic":"Element as index","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16, 23};
System.out.println(a[a[0]]);","options":["4","16","23","An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown"],"correct":2,"explain":"Evaluate inside out: a[0] is 4, so a[a[0]] is a[4], which is 23. An element used as an index is legal as long as the resulting index is in bounds."},{"id":"fs_z5_3_2","topic":"Last element idiom","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16, 23};
System.out.println(a[2] + a[a.length - 1]);","options":["38","39","27","31"],"correct":0,"explain":"a[2] is 15 and a[a.length - 1] is the last element, 23, so the sum is 38. a[a.length - 1] is the standard idiom for "the last one"."},{"id":"fs_z5_3_3","topic":"Modifying in a loop","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {3, 1, 4};
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    a[i] = a[i] + i;
}
System.out.println(a[1] + "," + a[2]);","options":["1,4","2,6","1,5","3,6"],"correct":1,"explain":"Each element gains its own index: a becomes {3+0, 1+1, 4+2} = {3, 2, 6}, so a[1] is 2 and a[2] is 6. A trace table with one row per pass settles it quickly."},{"id":"fs_z5_3_4","topic":"Compile-time versus runtime","text":"int[] a = new int[5]; and later a[10] = 1; — what kind of error is this?","options":["A syntax error","A runtime exception; it compiles fine because Java checks indexes only while running","A compile error — the compiler can see 10 is out of range","No error at all; the array grows to fit"],"correct":1,"explain":"Bounds are checked only at runtime, so a[10] on a 5-element array compiles without complaint and then throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException when executed — and the exception names the offending index."},{"id":"fs_z5_3_5","topic":"Loop start off-by-one","text":"What does this print?

