int sum = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    sum += a[i];
}
System.out.println(sum);","options":["43","39","27","16"],"correct":1,"explain":"The loop starts at index 1, so a[0] = 4 is never added: 8 + 15 + 16 = 39. The full sum 43 is the answer only if the loop starts at 0 — the first pass is one of the two boundary rows always worth double-checking."},{"id":"fs_z5_3_6","topic":"Negative indexes","text":"For int[] a = new int[5];, what does accessing a[-1] cause?","options":["It returns 0","An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at runtime","A compile error","It returns the last element, counting from the end"],"correct":1,"explain":"Valid indexes run from 0 through length - 1. Any index that is negative or >= length throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException while the program runs — Java arrays have no count-from-the-end shorthand."},{"id":"zf751","topic":"","text":"int[] a = {3, 7, 2, 8}; int s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) s += a[i]; System.out.println(s); prints:","options":["12","20","8","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"The loop adds every element: 3 + 7 + 2 + 8 = 20.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf752","topic":"","text":"int[] b = {5, 1, 6}; System.out.println(b.length); prints:","options":["4","6","3","5"],"correct":2,"explain":"length reports the element count, not any element value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf753","topic":"","text":"int[] c = {9, 4, 11, 6}; int m = c[0]; for (int i = 1; i < c.length; i++) if (c[i] > m) m = c[i]; System.out.println(m); prints:","options":["9","6","4","11"],"correct":3,"explain":"The champion pattern keeps the largest seen: 9 -> 9 -> 11 -> 11.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf754","topic":"","text":"int[] d = {9, 4, 11, 6}; int mn = d[0]; for (int i = 1; i < d.length; i++) if (d[i] < mn) mn = d[i]; System.out.println(mn); prints:","options":["4","9","11","6"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same pattern flipped for minimum: 9 -> 4 stays champion.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf755","topic":"","text":"int[] e = {2, 5, 5, 8, 5}; int cnt = 0; for (int i = 0; i < e.length; i++) if (e[i] == 5) cnt++; System.out.println(cnt); prints:","options":["2","3","1","5"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fives sit at indexes 1, 2, and 4.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf756","topic":"","text":"int[] f = {4, 6, 1}; for (int i = 0; i < f.length; i++) System.out.print(f[i] + " "); prints:","options":["1 6 4","4 6","4 6 1","0 1 2"],"correct":2,"explain":"Forward iteration prints elements in index order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf757","topic":"","text":"int[] g = {4, 6, 1}; for (int i = g.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) System.out.print(g[i]); prints:","options":["461","146","641","164"],"correct":3,"explain":"Backward iteration starts at the last index.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf758","topic":"","text":"int[] h = {10, 20, 30}; System.out.println(h[1] + h[2]); prints:","options":["50","30","1020","60"],"correct":0,"explain":"h[1] is 20 and h[2] is 30 - remember, indexing starts at 0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf759","topic":"","text":"The loop bound i < arr.length (rather than <=) exists because:","options":["arrays start at 1","the last valid index is length - 1","length lies by one","<= is not valid syntax"],"correct":1,"explain":"Using <= walks one slot past the end and throws.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf760","topic":"","text":"int[] odds = {3, 8, 5, 12}; int t = 0; for (int i = 0; i < odds.length; i++) if (odds[i] % 2 == 1) t += odds[i]; System.out.println(t); prints:","options":["20","8","28","15"],"correct":1,"explain":"The odd values are 3 and 5; their sum is 8.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf761","topic":"","text":"double[] w = {1.5, 2.5}; double s2 = 0; for (int i = 0; i < w.length; i++) s2 += w[i]; System.out.println(s2); prints:","options":["4","3.5","4.0","1.52.5"],"correct":2,"explain":"1.5 + 2.5 = 4.0, printed with the decimal since s2 is a double.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr4","topic":"Array iteration drill","text":"int[] a = {4, 8, 15}; int t = 0; for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) t += a[i]; What is t?","options":["27","15","12","3"],"correct":0,"explain":"The loop runs for i = 0, 1, 2 and adds every element: 4 + 8 + 15 = 27. Using a.length rather than a fixed number is what keeps the loop correct if the array changes.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_5":[{"id":"zya5_5_0","topic":"Parallel arrays","text":"Parallel arrays store related data by:","options":["Storing every attribute inside one combined array","Linking each element to the next with a pointer","Using the same index across several arrays","Keeping one array sorted and searching the others"],"correct":2,"explain":"names[i] and scores[i] describe the same person."},{"id":"zya5_5_1","topic":"Parallel array risk","text":"The main danger of parallel arrays is:","options":["They cannot hold more than one type of data","They can fall out of sync when one is modified alone","They must all be sorted before any can be read","They use far more memory than a single array"],"correct":1,"explain":"Nothing in the language enforces the correspondence — it exists only in the programmer's head."},{"id":"zya5_5_2","topic":"Better alternative","text":"Parallel arrays are usually better replaced by:","options":["A longer array","Two loops","A 2D array","An array of objects, one per entity"],"correct":3,"explain":"A Student object keeps name and score together so they cannot desynchronize. This is the argument for Unit 7."},{"id":"zya5_5_3","topic":"Sorting parallel arrays","text":"Sorting one parallel array alone:","options":["Sorts every parallel array to match automatically","Has no effect, since the arrays are independent","Is a compile error, as the lengths must match","Breaks the correspondence with the others"],"correct":3,"explain":"Sorting names without moving scores silently assigns the wrong score to everyone."},{"id":"zya5_5_4","topic":"Same length requirement","text":"Parallel arrays must:","options":["Hold values of exactly the same element type","Be declared inside the same method to work","Have matching lengths for every valid index","Be sorted in ascending order before any lookup"],"correct":2,"explain":"Different lengths guarantee an out-of-bounds error or missing data."},{"id":"zya5_5_5","topic":"Looking up by name","text":"To find a score given a name in parallel arrays:","options":["Search names for the index, then read scores at that index","Search scores for the value, then read names there","Sort both arrays, then read the matching position","Compare the arrays element by element for equality"],"correct":0,"explain":"The index is the link between the two arrays."},{"id":"zya5_5_6","topic":"Adding an entry","text":"Adding a new person to parallel arrays requires:","options":["Creating a new array","Sorting","Updating only the names array","Updating every array at the same index"],"correct":3,"explain":"Forgetting one array is exactly how they drift apart."},{"id":"zya5_5_7","topic":"Type flexibility","text":"Parallel arrays can hold different types because:","options":["Java arrays may mix element types within one array","The shared index converts between the two types","They are separate arrays, each with its own type","Autoboxing lets one array hold every kind of value"],"correct":2,"explain":"That flexibility is their one genuine advantage over a single 2D array, which must be uniform."},{"id":"zya5_5_8","topic":"Counting with a condition","text":"To count people scoring over 90 in parallel arrays:","options":["Loop over names, incrementing when the name is present","Loop over scores, incrementing when the value exceeds 90","Sort scores, then subtract the index of the first over 90","Compare the lengths of the two parallel arrays"],"correct":1,"explain":"Use names[i] only if you also need to report who."},{"id":"zya5_5_9","topic":"Maintenance","text":"As entities gain more attributes, parallel arrays:","options":["Combine automatically into a single wider array","Multiply in number and get harder to keep consistent","Become faster, since each array stays small","Require the arrays to be sorted after each change"],"correct":1,"explain":"Five attributes means five arrays to update together — the practical breaking point."},{"id":"fs_z5_5_1","topic":"Index of maximum","text":"What does this print?

String[] names = {"Ada", "Alan", "Grace"};
int[] scores = {95, 88, 99};
int best = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < scores.length; i++) {
    if (scores[i] > scores[best]) {
        best = i;
    }
}
System.out.println(names[best]);","options":["99","Grace","Ada","Alan"],"correct":1,"explain":"The loop tracks the INDEX of the largest score: 88 does not beat 95, but 99 does, so best ends as 2 and names[2] is "Grace". Tracking the index is what lets the matching name be looked up afterwards."},{"id":"fs_z5_5_2","topic":"Index versus value","text":"Using parallel arrays names and scores, printing the NAME of the top scorer requires the loop to track:","options":["The alphabetically last name","The index of the largest score","Only the largest score value itself","The lengths of both arrays"],"correct":1,"explain":"Knowing the winning value alone tells you nothing about who earned it. The index is the link between the parallel arrays, so it is the index that must be remembered. Names[best] and scores[best] describe the same person."},{"id":"fs_z5_5_3","topic":"Nothing links parallel arrays","text":"What keeps names[i] and scores[i] referring to the same student?","options":["A hidden index object created at declaration","Nothing in the language","The JVM automatically links arrays of equal length","The compiler verifies parallel arrays stay matched"],"correct":1,"explain":"Parallel arrays are a pure convention: every insert, delete, or sort must touch all of them together, and nothing warns you if the correspondence is broken. That fragility is the argument for combining the fields into one class. Only programmer discipline keeps the arrays in step."},{"id":"fs_z5_5_4","topic":"Search then cross-reference","text":"What does this print?

String[] names = {"Ana", "Ben", "Cy"};
int[] scores = {88, 71, 95};
int idx = -1;
