ain":"If lengths could differ, the longer array’s bound overruns the shorter.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf779","topic":"","text":"String[] days5 = {"Mon", "Tue"}; int[] steps5 = {4200, 6100}; for (int i = 0; i < days5.length; i++) System.out.println(days5[i] + ": " + steps5[i]); The second line printed is:","options":["Mon: 4200","Tue: 4200","6100: Tue","Tue: 6100"],"correct":3,"explain":"Index 1 pairs Tue with 6100.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf780","topic":"","text":"The index of the highest score also identifies the top scorer because:","options":["scores contain names","indexes are alphabetical","the max is always first","the same position in the names array"],"correct":3,"explain":"Track the INDEX of the max, not just its value, to answer "who".","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf781","topic":"","text":"int[] price5 = {5, 3}; int[] qty5 = {2, 4}; int bill5 = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) bill5 += price5[i] * qty5[i]; System.out.println(bill5); prints:","options":["22","14","24","9"],"correct":0,"explain":"5x2 + 3x4 = 10 + 12 = 22 - a dot product of prices and quantities.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf782","topic":"","text":"A cleaner alternative to many parallel arrays in larger programs is:","options":["more arrays","a class holding the fields together","longer names","global variables"],"correct":1,"explain":"A Student object with name and score travels as one unit - unit 7 territory.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf783","topic":"","text":"char[] grade5 = {'A', 'B'}; int[] cut5 = {90, 80}; A score of 85 maps to which grade using these tables?","options":["A","C","B - 85 fails the 90 cutoff but meets 80","no grade"],"correct":2,"explain":"Walk the cutoffs until one is met; the matching index gives the letter.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_6":[{"id":"zya5_6_0","topic":"Swap requires a temp","text":"To swap int a and int b you need:","options":["An array","a = b; b = a;","A third variable to hold one value","No extra variable ever"],"correct":2,"explain":"a = b; overwrites a, so the original value is gone before b can receive it."},{"id":"zya5_6_1","topic":"Correct swap","text":"Which correctly swaps a and b?","options":["a=t; t=b; b=a;","a=b; b=a;","int t=a; a=b; b=t;","int t=a; b=a; a=t;"],"correct":2,"explain":"Save one value, overwrite it, then restore from the saved copy."},{"id":"zya5_6_2","topic":"Result of the naive swap","text":"int a=1,b=2; a=b; b=a; leaves:","options":["a=1, b=2","a=2, b=1","a=2, b=2","a=1, b=1"],"correct":2,"explain":"After a=b both are 2, so b=a assigns 2 to itself. The value 1 was lost."},{"id":"zya5_6_3","topic":"Swapping array elements","text":"To swap a[i] and a[j]:","options":["a[i]+=a[j];","a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=a[i];","int t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;","swap(a[i], a[j]);"],"correct":2,"explain":"Same three-step pattern with array slots — the core operation inside most sorts."},{"id":"zya5_6_4","topic":"Swap in a method","text":"A method void swap(int x, int y) that swaps its parameters:","options":["Throws an exception","Works on the caller's variables","Has no effect on the caller","Requires final"],"correct":2,"explain":"Java copies primitive arguments. Swapping array elements works only because the array reference is shared. Primitives are passed by value."},{"id":"zya5_6_5","topic":"Swapping via arithmetic","text":"a = a + b; b = a - b; a = a - b; is:","options":["An invalid swap that leaves both values equal","A valid swap without a temp, though it risks overflow","A valid swap only when both values are positive","A compile error, since a is used before reassignment"],"correct":1,"explain":"It works arithmetically but the temp variable version is clearer and safe — prefer it."},{"id":"zya5_6_6","topic":"Swapping Strings","text":"Swapping two String variables:","options":["Modifies the text","Is impossible because Strings are immutable","Uses the same temp pattern","Requires new String"],"correct":2,"explain":"Immutability restricts changing the characters, not reassigning which String a variable points to. Only the references move."},{"id":"zya5_6_7","topic":"Reversing with swaps","text":"Reversing an array in place swaps:","options":["Nothing","Every pair of adjacent elements","Element i with element length-1-i, for the first half only","All elements with a[0]"],"correct":2,"explain":"Looping over the whole length would swap everything back to the original order."},{"id":"zya5_6_8","topic":"Number of swaps to reverse","text":"Reversing an array of 8 elements needs:","options":["16 swaps","8 swaps","4 swaps","7 swaps"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each swap places two elements, so n/2 swaps suffice."},{"id":"zya5_6_9","topic":"Swap and sorting","text":"Swapping is fundamental to sorting because:","options":["Every sort must copy the array before reordering","Most in-place sorts rearrange by exchanging pairs","Comparisons are impossible without a swap first","Swapping is what makes a sort stable"],"correct":1,"explain":"Selection sort, insertion sort and quicksort all move data by swapping."},{"id":"fs_z5_6_1","topic":"Swap trace","text":"What does this print?

int a = 3;
int b = 7;
int temp = a;
a = b;
b = temp;
System.out.println(a + " " + b);","options":["7 7","3 3","7 3","3 7"],"correct":2,"explain":"temp saves a's 3 before it is overwritten; a takes b's 7, and b takes the saved 3. The order save, overwrite, restore is what makes the swap work."},{"id":"fs_z5_6_2","topic":"Array element swap","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4};
int t = a[0];
a[0] = a[3];
a[3] = t;
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));","options":["[1, 2, 3, 4]","[4, 3, 2, 1]","[4, 2, 3, 1]","[4, 2, 3, 4]"],"correct":2,"explain":"This is the standard three-step swap applied to indexes 0 and 3: the ends exchange while the middle elements stay put, giving [4, 2, 3, 1]. Swapping one pair does not reverse the whole array."},{"id":"fs_z5_6_3","topic":"Why a temp is needed","text":"Why does swapping two variables require a third, temporary variable?","options":["Two consecutive assignments would deadlock the JVM","The temporary prevents integer overflow","Assignment overwrites","Java forbids assigning one variable directly to another"],"correct":2,"explain":"After a = b; the original value of a is gone, so b = a; just copies b back into itself. The temporary exists purely to hold the value the first assignment is about to destroy. The first assignment destroys a value that is still needed unless it is saved first."},{"id":"fs_z5_6_4","topic":"Swapping through a method","text":"A method can successfully swap arr[i] and arr[j], yet a method void swap(int x, int y) cannot swap its two int arguments. Why?","options":["The compiler rewrites array swaps into special instructions","int parameters are implicitly final","The method receives a copy of the array reference","Arrays are passed by value while ints are passed by reference"],"correct":2,"explain":"Primitives are passed by value, so the method shuffles its own copies and the caller's ints never change. An array parameter is a copy of the reference, so arr[i] inside the method touches the very same array the caller sees. Which still reaches the original array — but only copies of the int values."},{"id":"zf784","topic":"","text":"int x = 3; int y = 9; int tmp = x; x = y; y = tmp; Now x and y are:","options":["x is 3, y is 9","both 9","both 3","x is 9, y is 3"],"correct":3,"explain":"The temp saves the original x so nothing is lost.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf785","topic":"","text":"int x2 = 3; int y2 = 9; x2 = y2; y2 = x2; (no temp) leaves:","options":["both variables at 9","a correct swap","both at 3","a compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first assignment destroys x2’s old value; the second copies 9 back.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf786","topic":"","text":"The three-step swap order is:","options":["overwrite both, then save","save one value, overwrite, restore","save both values twice","any order works"],"correct":1,"explain":"temp = a; a = b; b = temp; - the save must come FIRST.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf787","topic":"","text":"Swapping arr[0] and arr[2] uses:","options":["arr[0] = arr[2]; arr[2] = arr[0];","arr.swap(0, 2);","int t = arr[0]; arr[0] = arr[2]; arr[2] = t;","t = arr; arr = t;"],"correct":2,"explain":"Array slots swap exactly like variables - through a temporary.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf788","topic":"","text":"After int a6 = 4; int b6 = 7; int t6 = b6; b6 = a6; a6 = t6; the values are:","options":["a6 is 4, b6 is 7","both 7","both 4","a6 is 7, b6 is 4"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same dance starting from the other side - still a correct swap.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf789","topic":"","text":"A swap is the core building block of:","options":["in-place sorting algorithms","printing","compiling","string concatenation"],"correct":0,"explain":"Bubble, selection, and insertion sort all move data by swapping.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf790","topic":"","text":"Swapping two DOUBLE variables requires the temp to be:","options":["a double as well","an int","a String","any type"],"correct":0,"explain":"The temporary must hold the same type it saves.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf791","topic":"","text":"int p6 = 5; int q6 = 5; A swap between them:","options":["crashes","harmless - nothing changes","makes them differ","is a compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"Swapping equal values changes nothing, and the code neither knows nor cares.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf792","topic":"","text":"The number of temporary variables a single swap needs is:","options":["zero, always","two","one","three"],"correct":2,"explain":"One saved value bridges the overwrite. (Arithmetic tricks exist but are poor style.)","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf793","topic":"","text":"In Java, a method void swap(int a, int b) that swaps its parameters:","options":["swaps the caller’s variables","only works for fields","throws an exception","does NOT affect the caller’s variables"],"correct":3,"explain":"Pass-by-value means the method swaps its own local copies only.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf794","topic":"","text":"To let a method swap two values a caller can see, the values should live:","options":["in an array the method can modify","in two separate ints","in final variables","in string literals"],"correct":0,"explain":"The reference is copied, but both copies point at the SAME array slots.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_7":[{"id":"zya5_7_0","topic":"Array assignment","text":"int[] b = a; makes b:","options":["A copy of a","Another reference to the same array","An empty array","A compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"Arrays are objects. Assigning copies the reference, so changing b[0] also changes a[0]."},{"id":"zya5_7_1","topic":"Proper copy","text":"To make an independent copy of array a:","options":["int[] b = a;","int[] b = Arrays.copyOf(a, a.length);","int[] b = new int[a.length];","int[] b = a.clone;"],"correct":1,"explain":"copyOf allocates and fills a new array. A bare new array is empty, and clone needs parentheses."},{"id":"zya5_7_2","topic":"Comparing arrays with ==","text":"a == b for two arrays with identical contents is:","options":["false unless they are the same object","true, because the contents are compared element by element","true, because arrays override equals for you","a compile error; arrays cannot be compared with =="],"correct":0,"explain":"== compares references, so it answers "same array?" not "same contents?"."},{"id":"zya5_7_3","topic":"Comparing contents","text":"To compare array contents:","options":["a.equals(b)","Arrays.equals(a, b)","a == b","a.compareTo(b)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Arrays do not override equals, so a.equals(b) behaves exactly like ==."},{"id":"zya5_7_4","topic":"Modifying while looping","text":"Changing a[i] inside a for loop over a:","options":["Is not allowed","Is fine","Throws an exception","Skips elements"],"correct":1,"explain":"Arrays are fixed size, so the traversal is unaffected. Modifying a collection during a for-each is the case that throws. The array length does not change."},{"id":"zya5_7_5","topic":"Shifting elements","text":"Removing an element from the middle of an array requires:","options":["Setting the slot to null and leaving the rest alone","Shifting later elements left and tracking a smaller size","Creating a new array one element shorter every time","Calling remove() on the array with that index"],"correct":1,"explain":"Array length is fixed, which is exactly why ArrayList exists."},{"id":"zya5_7_6","topic":"Filling an array","text":"Arrays.fill(a, 7) does what?","options":["Sets a[0] to 7","Sets every element to 7","Adds 7 to the array","Resizes it to 7"],"correct":1,"explain":"A convenient way to initialize an array to a non-zero default."},{"id":"zya5_7_7","topic":"2D array copy","text":"Arrays.copyOf on a 2D array produces:","options":["A deep copy with independent inner row arrays","A compile error; copyOf works only on 1D arrays","A single flattened array of all the elements","A shallow copy sharing the inner row arrays"],"correct":3,"explain":"The outer array is new but the rows are shared references — modifying a row affects both."},{"id":"zya5_7_8","topic":"Default values","text":"int[] a = new int[3]; contains:","options":["null values","0, 0, 0","Random values","Nothing until assigned"],"correct":1,"explain":"Numeric arrays default to 0, boolean to false, and object arrays to null."},{"id":"fs_z5_7_1","topic":"Reference assignment","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = a;
b[0] = 99;
System.out.println(a[0]);","options":["1","99","0","Compiler error"],"correct":1,"explain":"int[] b = a; copies the reference, not the array — two names, one array. Writing through b is visible through a, so a[0] is 99. A real copy needs a loop or Arrays.copyOf."},{"id":"fs_z5_7_2","topic":"== versus Arrays.equals","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 2};
int[] b = {1, 2};
System.out.println((a == b) + " " + Arrays.equals(a, b));","options":["false true","true true","false false","true false"],"correct":0,"explain":"a and b are two separate array objects, so == (which compares references) is false, while Arrays.equals compares element by element and reports true. It is the same distinction as == versus .equals on Strings."},{"id":"fs_z5_7_3","topic":"What == asks","text":"For two arrays a and b, the expression a == b answers which question?","options":["Do a and b refer to the very same array object?","Do a and b hold the same elements in the same order?","Are a and b the same length?","Are both arrays non-null?"],"correct":0,"explain":"An array variable holds a reference, so == compares references: it asks "the same array?" and never "the same contents?". Content comparison is Arrays.equals."},{"id":"fs_z5_7_4","topic":"copyOf with a larger length","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = Arrays.copyOf(a, 5);
System.out.println(b[4]);","options":["0","null","3","An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arrays.copyOf makes a genuinely new array of the requested length, copying what fits and filling the extra slots with the element type's default — 0 for int. b has length 5, so b[4] is a valid index holding 0."},{"id":"fs_z5_7_5","topic":"Deep copying 2D arrays","text":"To make a fully independent copy of a 2D array, you must:","options":["Compare the arrays with == first","Assign it with int[][] b = a;","Copy each row individually in a loop","Call clone() once on the outer array"],"correct":2,"explain":"For a 2D array, clone() and copyOf are shallow: the new outer array still holds references to the same row arrays, so element changes show through both. A deep copy loops over the rows and copies each one. Clone() and Arrays.copyOf duplicate only the outer array."},{"id":"zf795","topic":"","text":"int[] a7 = {1, 2, 3}; int[] b7 = a7; b7[0] = 99; System.out.println(a7[0]); prints:","options":["1","99","0","an exception"],"correct":1,"explain":"b7 = a7 copies the REFERENCE - both names point at the one array.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf796","topic":"","text":"A true independent copy of int[] a7 requires:","options":["b7 = a7;","casting","a new array plus an element-copy loop","renaming"],"correct":2,"explain":"Copy the CONTENTS, not the reference.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf797","topic":"","text":"int[] x7 = {1, 2}; int[] y7 = {1, 2}; System.out.println(x7 == y7); prints:","options":["true","1 2","error","false"],"correct":3,"explain":"== on arrays compares references; these are two separate arrays.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf798","topic":"","text":"Comparing array CONTENTS correctly uses:","options":["a loop over elements (or Arrays.equals)","==",".equals() on the array, which is == in disguise","length comparison alone"],"correct":0,"explain":"arr1.equals(arr2) is inherited identity comparison - loop or Arrays.equals instead.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf799","topic":"","text":"int[] m7 = {4, 9, 1}; for (int i = 0; i < m7.length; i++) m7[i] = m7[i] * 10; m7 now holds:","options":["4, 9, 1","40, 90, 10","40, 9, 1","10, 10, 10"],"correct":1,"explain":"In-place modification scales every element.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf800","topic":"","text":"int[] src7 = {5, 6, 7}; int[] dst7 = new int[3]; for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) dst7[i] = src7[i]; dst7[1] = 0; src7[1] is now:","options":["6 - the copy is independent","0","null","7"],"correct":0,"explain":"Element-by-element copying created a second array; edits do not travel.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf801","topic":"","text":"Passing an array to a method that modifies its elements:","options":["copies everything first","changes the caller’s array","is forbidden","only changes lengths"],"correct":1,"explain":"The reference is passed by value, but it still points at the one array.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf802","topic":"","text":"boolean same7 = arr1.length == arr2.length; then a loop checks each pair. The early exit on a mismatch is:","options":["continue","return the index","set same7 false and break","restart the loop"],"correct":2,"explain":"One differing pair settles the question - no need to keep scanning.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf803","topic":"","text":"int[] n7 = {2, 4, 6}; int[] o7 = n7; o7 = new int[]{9, 9, 9}; System.out.println(n7[0]); prints:","options":["9","0","error","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Reassigning o7 to a NEW array breaks the sharing; n7 still holds the original.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf804","topic":"","text":"Doubling only the even-index elements of int[] {1,2,3,4} in place yields:","options":["2, 2, 6, 4","2, 4, 6, 8","1, 4, 3, 8","2, 2, 3, 4"],"correct":0,"explain":"Indexes 0 and 2 double (1->2, 3->6); indexes 1 and 3 stay.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf805","topic":"","text":"Why is Arrays.toString(arr) handy while debugging?","options":["arrays cannot be printed at all","plain println(arr) shows a reference code","it sorts the array","it converts to JSON"],"correct":1,"explain":"System.out.println(arr) gives something like [I@1b6d3586 - toString shows [1, 2, 3].","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_8":[{"id":"zya5_8_0","topic":"Reverse loop bound","text":"Reversing in place with for (int i=0; i&lt;a.length; i++) swap(i, a.length-1-i) produces:","options":["The original array","An exception","An empty array","A reversed array"],"correct":0,"explain":"The classic bug. The loop must stop at a.length / 2. Every pair is swapped twice."},{"id":"zya5_8_1","topic":"Correct reverse bound","text":"The correct loop for an in-place reverse is:","options":["i < a.length / 2","i <= a.length / 2","i < a.length - 1","i < a.length"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each iteration handles two elements, so half the length covers all of them."},{"id":"zya5_8_2","topic":"Odd-length middle","text":"Reversing an odd-length array leaves the middle element:","options":["In place","Duplicated","Null","Removed"],"correct":0,"explain":"The middle element is its own mirror, so integer division naturally skips it. Is correct."},{"id":"zya5_8_3","topic":"Finding the bug","text":"The fastest way to find this bug is:","options":["Read the whole method again from the top","Add a try-catch around the swap statement","Rewrite the sort using a different algorithm","Print the array after each swap for a small input"],"correct":3,"explain":"Watching a 4-element array get reversed then un-reversed makes the cause obvious immediately."},{"id":"zya5_8_4","topic":"Reverse into a new array","text":"Building a reversed copy uses:","options":["b[i] = a[a.length-1-i]","b[i] = a[i+1]","b[i] = a[0]","b[i] = a[i]"],"correct":0,"explain":"Index i of the copy takes the mirrored index from the source."},{"id":"zya5_8_5","topic":"Off-by-one in the mirror","text":"Using a[a.length - i] instead of a[a.length - 1 - i] causes:","options":["The last element being silently skipped","ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException on the first iteration","The loop running one iteration too few","A compile error caught before the program runs"],"correct":1,"explain":"At i = 0 that index equals a.length, which is one past the end."},{"id":"zya5_8_6","topic":"Verifying a reverse","text":"A good test for a reverse method includes:","options":["Empty, one-element, two-element and odd-length arrays","Only a long array of random values","Only an array already in reverse order","Only arrays whose length is a power of two"],"correct":0,"explain":"Boundary sizes are where the loop bound errors show up."},{"id":"zya5_8_7","topic":"Two-pointer reverse","text":"An alternative reverse walks:","options":["One index from 0 to length, copying as it goes","One index from the end, appending to a new array","Two indices from 0 upward in lock step","Two indices toward each other until they meet"],"correct":3,"explain":"while (lo < hi) { swap; lo++; hi--; } — the meeting condition replaces the length/2 bound."},{"id":"zya5_8_8","topic":"In place meaning","text":"Reversing in place means:","options":["Building a new reversed array and returning it","Sorting the array into descending order instead","Rearranging the original array with O(1) extra memory","Copying to a list, reversing it, and copying back"],"correct":2,"explain":"No proportional extra storage — just a temp variable for the swap."},{"id":"zya5_8_9","topic":"Debugging print placement","text":"To see the reverse go wrong, print the array:","options":["Inside the loop after each swap","Before the loop only","Never","Only at the end"],"correct":0,"explain":"Final-only output shows that it is wrong but not where it went wrong."},{"id":"fs_z5_8_1","topic":"Correct in-place reverse","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (int i = 0; i < a.length / 2; i++) {
    int t = a[i];
    a[i] = a[a.length - 1 - i];
    a[a.length - 1 - i] = t;
}
System.out.println(a[0] + " " + a[2] + " " + a[4]);","options":["5 3 1","1 3 5","5 4 3","1 2 3"],"correct":0,"explain":"The half-length bound swaps each pair exactly once: the array becomes {5, 4, 3, 2, 1}, so positions 0, 2, 4 hold 5, 3, 1. With length 5, a.length / 2 is 2, and the middle element correctly stays put."},{"id":"fs_z5_8_2","topic":"Why the bound is half","text":"Why does the correct in-place reverse loop run only while i < a.length / 2?","options":["Each swap places two elements at once; continuing past the middle re-swaps every pair and undoes the work","The second half of the array never needs to change position","It prevents an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","Integer division requires the bound to be even"],"correct":0,"explain":"One swap handles both i and its mirror, so half the length is exactly one pass per pair. Running the full length processes every pair a second time from the other end, restoring the original order — a bug that produces no error at all."},{"id":"fs_z5_8_3","topic":"Counting the swaps","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
int swaps = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length / 2; i++) {
    int t = a[i];
    a[i] = a[6 - i];
    a[6 - i] = t;
    swaps++;
}
System.out.println(swaps + " " + a[0]);","options":["3 7","7 1","3 1","6 7"],"correct":0,"explain":"For length 7, a.length / 2 is 3 (integer division), so i takes 0, 1, 2 — three swaps — and the middle element at index 3 stays put on its own. After reversing, a[0] holds the old last element, 7."},{"id":"fs_z5_8_4","topic":"Two-pointer reverse","text":"Consider this alternative reverse:

int i = 0;
int j = a.length - 1;
while (i < j) {
    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
    i++;
