This version is:","options":["A correct reverse — the two pointers meet in the middle, so no pair is ever swapped twice","Wrong — it reverses the array and then un-reverses it","Wrong — it fails to handle the middle element of an odd-length array","A compile error, because two variables cannot be updated in one loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"The condition i < j stops the loop exactly when the pointers meet, so each pair is swapped once. For an odd length the middle element is its own mirror and correctly stays where it is — the same behaviour as the half-length for loop."},{"id":"zf806","topic":"","text":"Reversing {1, 2, 3, 4} by swapping arr[i] with arr[length-1-i] for i up to length/2 gives:","options":["1, 2, 3, 4","2, 1, 4, 3","4, 3, 2, 1","4, 3, 2, 4"],"correct":2,"explain":"Swapping (0,3) and (1,2) reverses correctly: 4, 3, 2, 1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf807","topic":"","text":"The classic bug: running that swap loop for ALL i from 0 to length-1 produces:","options":["a reversed array","an exception","all zeros","the ORIGINAL order"],"correct":3,"explain":"The second half undoes the first half’s work. Stop at the middle.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf808","topic":"","text":"The correct loop bound for an in-place reversal is:","options":["i < arr.length / 2","i < arr.length","i <= arr.length / 2 always","i < arr.length - 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each pass fixes TWO elements, so half the passes suffice.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf809","topic":"","text":"For an ODD-length array, the middle element during reversal:","options":["is lost","stays where it is - it needs no swap","swaps with itself and breaks","must be removed first"],"correct":1,"explain":"length/2 with int division naturally skips the center.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf810","topic":"","text":"The partner of index i in a reversal of length n is:","options":["n - i","n - 1 - i","i - 1","n + i"],"correct":1,"explain":"First pairs with last: 0 with n-1, 1 with n-2, and so on.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf811","topic":"","text":"Reversing {7, 8} swaps indexes:","options":["nothing - too short","1 and 2","0 and 1, giving 8, 7","0 and 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"length/2 = 1 pass: the single swap flips the pair.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf812","topic":"","text":"A reversal PRINTED backwards (loop from the end) differs from an in-place reversal because:","options":["both rewrite the array","printing is a compile error","they are identical","printing leaves the array unchanged"],"correct":3,"explain":"Choose by whether later code needs the reversed ORDER stored.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf813","topic":"","text":"Verifying the reversal fix means testing:","options":["even, odd, and 1-element lengths","one big array only","the empty string","only length 4"],"correct":0,"explain":"Boundary sizes are where index arithmetic breaks.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf814","topic":"","text":"Reversing an EMPTY array with the half-length loop:","options":["throws","safely does nothing - zero passes","fills with zeros","loops forever"],"correct":1,"explain":"0 / 2 = 0 iterations; robust code handles the degenerate case for free.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf815","topic":"","text":"While debugging the reversal, printing the array after EVERY swap shows:","options":["nothing useful","the compiled bytecode","which swap first damages the order","memory addresses only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Step-by-step state is how the double-swap bug becomes visible.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf816","topic":"","text":"The reversal bug (looping too far) produced no exception because:","options":["exceptions were disabled","the array grew","Java hides errors","every index touched was still legal"],"correct":3,"explain":"Legal-but-wrong is precisely what makes logic errors sneaky.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_11":[{"id":"zya5_11_0","topic":"Tax bracket lookup","text":"Storing bracket limits and rates in parallel arrays lets you:","options":["Sort the incomes and read the rate at that index","Add the two arrays together to get the tax owed","Store every taxpayer's rate in a single array","Find the rate by scanning for the first limit income falls under"],"correct":3,"explain":"A loop over the limits replaces a long if-else chain and makes adding a bracket a data change, not a code change."},{"id":"zya5_11_1","topic":"Loop for brackets","text":"Scanning brackets from lowest to highest, you stop at:","options":["The first limit that the income exceeds","The last limit in the array, always","The first limit that the income does not exceed","The limit closest in value to the income"],"correct":2,"explain":"Once the income fits a bracket, later brackets do not apply."},{"id":"zya5_11_2","topic":"Array of rates type","text":"Tax rates such as 0.12 must be stored in:","options":["double[]","String[]","boolean[]","int[]"],"correct":0,"explain":"int would truncate every rate to 0."},{"id":"zya5_11_3","topic":"Applying the rate","text":"Tax owed on income at rate r is:","options":["income * r","income / r","income - r","income + r"],"correct":0,"explain":"A percentage of a value is multiplication."},{"id":"zya5_11_4","topic":"Top bracket","text":"Income above every listed limit should:","options":["Return zero, since no bracket matched","Use the lowest rate as a safe default","Throw an exception the caller must handle","Use the highest rate as a default after the loop"],"correct":3,"explain":"A default after the loop is what handles the open-ended top bracket."},{"id":"zya5_11_5","topic":"Parallel array sync","text":"The limits and rates arrays must:","options":["Stay index-aligned","Be the same type","Be final","Be sorted independently"],"correct":0,"explain":"The pairing is the whole mechanism, and nothing enforces it automatically. Limits[i] pairs with rates[i]."},{"id":"zya5_11_6","topic":"Adding a bracket","text":"With this design, adding a bracket means:","options":["Adding one entry to each array","Adding an if statement","Nothing","Rewriting the loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"That separation of data from logic is the point of the array approach."},{"id":"zya5_11_7","topic":"Effective rate","text":"Total tax divided by income gives:","options":["The effective (average) rate","The bracket limit","The taxable income","The marginal rate"],"correct":0,"explain":"The marginal rate is the rate on the last dollar; the effective rate is the overall proportion."},{"id":"zya5_11_8","topic":"Formatting the result","text":"Printing tax owed to two decimals uses:","options":["printf("%.2f%n", tax)","printf("%d%n", tax)","Math.round(tax)","println(tax)"],"correct":0,"explain":"%.2f is the currency-style format."},{"id":"fs_z5_11_1","topic":"Bracket lookup","text":"What does this print?

