air with rates {0%, 10%, 20%} (highest cutoff met applies). A salary of 30000 pays:","options":["0%","20%","25%","10%"],"correct":3,"explain":"30000 meets the cutoffs up to 20000, so the rate is 10%.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf835","topic":"","text":"Cutoffs {0, 20000, 50000} pair with rates {0%, 10%, 20%} (highest cutoff met applies). A salary of 60000 pays:","options":["20%","0%","10%","25%"],"correct":0,"explain":"60000 meets the cutoffs up to 50000, so the rate is 20%.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf836","topic":"","text":"Cutoffs {0, 20000, 50000} pair with rates {0%, 10%, 20%} (highest cutoff met applies). A salary of 15000 pays:","options":["10%","0%","20%","25%"],"correct":1,"explain":"15000 meets the cutoffs up to 0, so the rate is 0%.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf837","topic":"","text":"Storing tax cutoffs and rates in two parallel arrays (rather than an if-chain) helps because:","options":["arrays compute taxes automatically","if-chains cannot compare numbers","table changes are data, not logic, edits","arrays are required by law"],"correct":2,"explain":"New bracket? Add one element to each array - the lookup loop is untouched.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf838","topic":"","text":"The lookup loop walks the cutoff array and keeps:","options":["the first rate always","the average rate","the largest rate in the table","the rate of the LAST cutoff met"],"correct":3,"explain":"Later (higher) cutoffs override earlier ones as long as the salary qualifies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf839","topic":"","text":"int[] cut11 = {0, 20000, 50000}; double[] rate11 = {0.0, 0.10, 0.20}; The arrays must:","options":["equal lengths, matching positions","be sorted descending","hold the same type","start at 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"Position i pairs a cutoff with its rate - the parallel-array contract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf840","topic":"","text":"A tax of 20% on a 60000 salary computed as (int)(60000 * 0.20) is:","options":["12000","1200","48000","300"],"correct":0,"explain":"60000 x 0.20 = 12000.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf841","topic":"","text":"If the cutoffs array is accidentally UNSORTED, the keep-the-last-met loop:","options":["still works","can return a wrong rate","throws an exception","sorts it first"],"correct":1,"explain":"Data assumptions are real requirements; violating them breaks silently.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf842","topic":"","text":"Adding a new 30% bracket at 100000 requires:","options":["rewriting the loop","a new program","appending one element to each array","changing all other rates"],"correct":2,"explain":"That one-line-of-data change is the whole point of table-driven design.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf843","topic":"","text":"A salary of exactly 20000 with cutoff >= tests pays:","options":["0%","20%","15%","10% - the boundary is included"],"correct":3,"explain":">= includes the exact cutoff value in the higher bracket.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf844","topic":"","text":"Testing the tax lookup should include salaries:","options":["below, at, and above each boundary","only round millions","one middle value","negative only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Boundary values are exactly where bracket logic goes wrong.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u5/z5_12":[{"id":"zya5_12_0","topic":"Array of domains","text":"Validating many domains is best done by:","options":["Copying the code for each","Storing them in an array and looping","Using 2D arrays","Using one long String"],"correct":1,"explain":"The validation logic is written once and applied to every element."},{"id":"zya5_12_1","topic":"Counting valid entries","text":"To count how many domains are valid:","options":["count += domain.length()","if (isValid(d)) count++;","count = i;","count += i;"],"correct":1,"explain":"A counter increments once per valid entry."},{"id":"zya5_12_2","topic":"Collecting results","text":"To record which domains are valid, use:","options":["A single boolean holding whether all were valid","A parallel boolean array indexed the same as the domains","A count of how many domains passed the check","A second copy of the domains array, filtered"],"correct":1,"explain":"valid[i] describes domains[i] — parallel arrays again."},{"id":"zya5_12_3","topic":"Nested loops here","text":"Validating characters of every domain requires:","options":["A loop over domains containing a loop over characters","A single loop over the domains array only","A single loop over the characters of one domain","Two sequential loops, one after the other"],"correct":0,"explain":"Outer over the array, inner over each String — the standard nested pattern."},{"id":"zya5_12_4","topic":"Empty slot handling","text":"An array entry that is null must be:","options":["Replaced with an empty string when the array is built","Removed by shifting the later elements left","Ignored, since Java skips null entries automatically","Checked before calling methods on it, or it throws NPE"],"correct":3,"explain":"Calling length() on null throws, so the guard has to come first."},{"id":"zya5_12_5","topic":"Reporting results","text":"After validating, printing each domain with its verdict uses:","options":["One println after the loop","A println inside the loop","No output","printf only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Per-item output belongs inside the loop; the summary belongs after it."},{"id":"fs_z5_12_1","topic":"Case-insensitive membership","text":"What does this print?

