le logic: any approved suffix is enough.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf850","topic":"","text":"Requiring the name to match EVERY rule in a rules array instead flips the pattern to:","options":["start false and set true on any pass","start true and set false on any failed rule","count to length/2","no flag at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"All-match starts optimistic and falls on the first failure - the mirror image.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf851","topic":"","text":"String[] banned12 = {"spam", "fake"}; Checking a name against the ban list rejects when:","options":["the loop finishes","name equals the array","contains(banned12[i]) is true for ANY i","contains is false once"],"correct":2,"explain":"Ban lists are any-match too - one hit is fatal.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf852","topic":"","text":"The loop bound when testing name against String[] tlds is:","options":["i < name.length()","i <= tlds.length","i < tlds.length - 1","i < tlds.length"],"correct":3,"explain":"Iterate the RULE table, not the string being tested.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf853","topic":"","text":"Adding "edu" to the approved String[] {"com", "org", "net"} makes its length:","options":["4","3","5","edu"],"correct":0,"explain":"Initializer arrays size themselves from the element count.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf854","topic":"","text":"After the any-match loop, printing "valid" or "invalid" branches on:","options":["the loop counter","the boolean flag","the array length","the last suffix tried"],"correct":1,"explain":"The flag carries the loop’s verdict to the code that acts on it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf855","topic":"","text":"An empty approved-endings array makes the any-match loop:","options":["accept everything","throw","reject everything","loop forever"],"correct":2,"explain":"Zero rules means zero chances to match - check that the table is sensible.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf856","topic":"","text":"Case differences ("Shop.COM" vs "shop.com") in domain checks are handled by:","options":["uppercase rule tables and lowercase inputs","ignoring case is automatic","regex only","lowercasing the input once before the loop"],"correct":3,"explain":"Normalize, then compare - one canonical form beats case-juggling in every test.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf857","topic":"","text":"The overall lesson of the domain example is:","options":["rules become data plus one loop","validation needs no arrays","loops are only for math","domains cannot be validated"],"correct":0,"explain":"Rules-as-data is a design idea that scales far beyond domains.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_4":[{"id":"zya6_4_0","topic":"Math method shape","text":"A method computing the area of a circle should:","options":["Print the area","Take the radius and return the area","Take no parameters","Be void"],"correct":1,"explain":"Returning rather than printing lets the value be reused, formatted, or tested."},{"id":"zya6_4_1","topic":"Return type for area","text":"public ??? area(double r) — the return type is:","options":["int","double","void","String"],"correct":1,"explain":"The result has a fractional part, so double."},{"id":"zya6_4_2","topic":"Using Math.PI","text":"The area formula in Java is:","options":["Math.pow(Math.PI, r)","Math.PI * r * r","Math.PI * r ^ 2","Math.PI + r * r"],"correct":1,"explain":"Java has no exponent operator; ^ is bitwise XOR. Math.pow(r, 2) also works."},{"id":"zya6_4_3","topic":"Multiple parameters","text":"A method computing hypotenuse from two legs:","options":["Takes no parameters and prints the result","Takes two parameters and returns void","Takes one parameter and returns an int","Takes two parameters and returns a double"],"correct":3,"explain":"Math.sqrt(a*a + b*b) returns the result."},{"id":"zya6_4_4","topic":"Pure method benefit","text":"A method that only computes from its parameters and returns a value:","options":["It never needs a return statement at all","It runs faster than a method that prints","The same inputs always give the same output","The compiler verifies its result automatically"],"correct":2,"explain":"No hidden state means it can be verified in isolation — the reason mathematical methods are the easiest to unit test."},{"id":"zya6_4_5","topic":"Returning early","text":"A method can contain:","options":["Only one return statement, at the very end","Multiple returns, the first one reached ends the method","Multiple returns, all of which are executed","No return at all, even when it declares a type"],"correct":1,"explain":"Guard clauses that return early often read better than deep nesting."},{"id":"zya6_4_6","topic":"Integer division in a method","text":"public double half(int n) { return n / 2; } for n = 7 returns:","options":["3.5","3.0","3","A compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"The division is int/int, so it truncates to 3 before widening to 3.0. Use n / 2.0."},{"id":"zya6_4_7","topic":"Method with no side effects","text":"Which is preferable for a calculation?","options":["void printArea(double r) that prints","double area(double r) that returns","Both are identical","A method that does both"],"correct":1,"explain":"Returning is more reusable: the caller decides whether to print, sum, or store it."},{"id":"zyf6_4_0","topic":"Math method purity","text":"A well-written math method should depend on:","options":["The clock","Global state","Only its parameters","User input"],"correct":2,"explain":"That is what makes it reusable and testable."},{"id":"zyf6_4_1","topic":"Rounding","text":"To round a double to the nearest whole number use:","options":["Math.floor(x)","(int) x","Math.round(x)","x % 1"],"correct":2,"explain":"Casting truncates instead of rounding."},{"id":"fs_z6_4_1","topic":"Integer division inside a method","text":"What does this print?

