ad reference where the fix is obvious, not three calls later.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2138","topic":"","text":"If a method both validates AND its callers validate:","options":["the program crashes","validation cancels out","that is defense in depth","the compiler complains"],"correct":2,"explain":"Redundant checks cost little; unvalidated paths cost debugging nights.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2139","topic":"","text":"Returning a special value like -1 for invalid input requires:","options":["nothing - it is automatic","a bigger int type","an array","callers to actually CHECK for it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Sentinel returns only work when the call site tests them - else garbage flows on.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2140","topic":"","text":"static double divide(double a, double b) { if (b == 0) ... } The guard prevents:","options":["slow division","negative results","compile errors","a meaningless Infinity result"],"correct":3,"explain":"Floating division by zero does not throw - it silently produces Infinity, which is worse.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2141","topic":"","text":"Which parameter check belongs in isLeapYear(int year) for a program covering 1583 onward?","options":["reject years before 1583 (pre-Gregorian)","reject even years","reject years over 2000","no check ever"],"correct":0,"explain":"Domain knowledge defines validity - the calendar itself changed in 1582.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2142","topic":"","text":"Error messages in guards should say:","options":[""error" alone","what was wrong and what is acceptable","nothing - silence is fine","the method’s source code"],"correct":1,"explain":""hours must be 0-80, got -4" turns a mystery into a fix.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_15":[{"id":"zya6_15_0","topic":"Perfect size array","text":"A perfect size array is one where:","options":["Its length is larger than the elements it holds","Its length is a power of two for fast indexing","Its elements are all initialised to the same value","Its length exactly equals the number of elements stored"],"correct":3,"explain":"Every slot is meaningful, so a.length is the true count."},{"id":"zya6_15_1","topic":"Advantage","text":"The advantage of a perfect size array is:","options":["It uses more memory","It is faster to sort","Elements can be added easily","a.length is the element count"],"correct":3,"explain":"No bookkeeping variable to keep in sync. Loops need no separate size variable."},{"id":"zya6_15_2","topic":"Disadvantage","text":"The main drawback is:","options":["Elements cannot be read once the array is full","Adding an element requires creating a new, larger array","Its length cannot be queried after creation","It wastes memory on slots that stay unused"],"correct":1,"explain":"Array length is fixed at creation, so growth means allocate-and-copy."},{"id":"zya6_15_3","topic":"Knowing the count first","text":"A perfect size array requires:","options":["Knowing how many elements there will be before creating it","Keeping a separate count of the elements in use","Allocating twice the expected number of slots","Sorting the values before they are stored"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is the practical constraint — you often do not know until you have read the data."},{"id":"zya6_15_4","topic":"Creating one from input","text":"Reading n values into a perfect size array:","options":["Requires creating the array first, then reading n","Requires an oversize array, since n is unknown","Requires reading n first, then creating the array","Requires reading every value before knowing n"],"correct":2,"explain":"The count must precede the allocation."},{"id":"zya6_15_5","topic":"Traversal","text":"Looping a perfect size array uses:","options":["i <= a.length","i < a.count","i < size where size is separate","i < a.length"],"correct":3,"explain":"The array length is authoritative — that is the whole point."},{"id":"zya6_15_6","topic":"Memory efficiency","text":"A perfect size array:","options":["Doubles in size","Requires null slots","Wastes space","Uses exactly the space needed"],"correct":3,"explain":"No unused slots, unlike an oversize array."},{"id":"zya6_15_7","topic":"When to prefer it","text":"A perfect size array suits data that is:","options":["Sorted","Numeric only","Constantly changing","Fixed once created"],"correct":3,"explain":"For changing data an ArrayList is the practical answer."},{"id":"zyf6_15_0","topic":"Creating from a count","text":"A perfect size array is created after:","options":["Nothing","Sorting","Determining the exact element count","The loop"],"correct":2,"explain":"The size is fixed at allocation."},{"id":"zyf6_15_1","topic":"Growing one","text":"Adding an element to a full perfect size array requires:","options":["Allocating a larger array and copying","Overwriting the element at the last index","Incrementing a separate count variable","Calling add() on the array with the new value"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arrays cannot be resized in place."},{"id":"fs_z6_15_1","topic":"length is the element count","text":"What does this print?

