arr[length] = value;","arr[size] = value; size++;","arr.add(value)","size--; arr[size] = value;"],"correct":1,"explain":"Write at the current size, then bump the count.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2169","topic":"","text":"int[] data = new int[100]; int size = 0; data.length and size mean:","options":["100 items stored","0 capacity","capacity 100, currently 0 items","the same thing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Length is potential; size is actual.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2170","topic":"","text":"Loops over an oversize array must bound with:","options":["i < data.length","i <= size","i < size","i < 100 always"],"correct":2,"explain":"Looping to length would process the junk in the unused slots.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2171","topic":"","text":"The unused slots of a new oversize int array contain:","options":["random garbage that crashes","nulls","the value size","0s that are NOT real data"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java zero-fills, but those zeros are meaningless - only size separates data from filler.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2172","topic":"","text":"When size == data.length, the oversize array is:","options":["full - no room for another add","empty","broken","automatically doubled"],"correct":0,"explain":"Capacity reached; adding needs a bigger array or a rejection.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2173","topic":"","text":"Removing the LAST element of an oversize array is simply:","options":["data[size] = 0; alone","size--;","a new array","impossible"],"correct":1,"explain":"Shrinking the count logically removes it; the slot value no longer matters.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2174","topic":"","text":"Oversize arrays fit situations where:","options":["the count is fixed forever","no maximum exists at all","items arrive over time, max known","data never arrives"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reserve for the max, track the actual - a guest list with a room capacity.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2175","topic":"","text":"Compared to reallocating a perfect size array on every add, the oversize pattern:","options":["copies on every add","uses less total memory always","cannot add","adds cheaply until capacity is hit"],"correct":3,"explain":"Pre-reserved space turns adds into two quick statements.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2176","topic":"","text":"Printing an oversize array’s contents correctly prints:","options":["the first size elements","all length slots","the last size slots","length - size elements"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only the counted prefix is real data.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_17":[{"id":"zya6_17_0","topic":"Passing an oversize array","text":"A method processing an oversize array needs:","options":["Only the array","The array and the element count","The capacity only","Nothing extra"],"correct":1,"explain":"The array alone cannot tell the method where the real data ends."},{"id":"zya6_17_1","topic":"Signature","text":"A correct signature is:","options":["int sum(int[] a)","int sum(int[] a, int count)","int sum(int count)","void sum(int[] a)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Both pieces are required to interpret the data."},{"id":"zya6_17_2","topic":"Loop inside the method","text":"The loop bound must be:","options":["a.length","the count parameter","count + 1","a.length - 1"],"correct":1,"explain":"Otherwise unused slots are included in the result."},{"id":"zya6_17_3","topic":"Adding inside a method","text":"A method appending to an oversize array:","options":["Can update the count in the caller directly","Must return the new count","Cannot append","Must be void"],"correct":1,"explain":"The array contents change through the shared reference, but the count is a primitive copy — this asymmetry is the key point. Ints are passed by value."},{"id":"zya6_17_4","topic":"Return value","text":"Such a method typically returns:","options":["void","The updated element count","The array","A boolean"],"correct":1,"explain":"numElements = add(a, numElements, value); is the standard call shape."},{"id":"zya6_17_5","topic":"Capacity check location","text":"The full-array check belongs:","options":["In the caller only","Inside the method, before writing","Nowhere","After writing"],"correct":1,"explain":"The method owns the write, so it must own the guard."},{"id":"zya6_17_6","topic":"Returning the array","text":"Returning the array itself is:","options":["Necessary","Unnecessary","A compile error","Faster"],"correct":1,"explain":"Only reallocation would require returning a new reference. The caller already holds the same reference."},{"id":"zya6_17_7","topic":"Two pieces of state","text":"The core awkwardness of oversize arrays in methods is:","options":["Two related values must be passed and kept in sync by hand","The array cannot be passed to a method at all","The count must be declared as a static field","Methods cannot return an oversize array"],"correct":0,"explain":"A class that holds both — which is what ArrayList is — removes the problem entirely."},{"id":"zyf6_17_0","topic":"Why the count is returned","text":"An append method returns the new count because:","options":["int parameters are passed by value","It is faster","Java requires it","Style"],"correct":0,"explain":"The array contents change through the shared reference; the count does not. The caller's count cannot be updated directly."},{"id":"zyf6_17_1","topic":"Guarding the write","text":"Before writing at index count the method must check:","options":["count < a.length","a is sorted","Nothing","count > 0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Otherwise ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException."},{"id":"fs_z6_17_1","topic":"Returning the new count","text":"What does this print?

public static int add(int[] a, int size, int value) {
    if (size < a.length) {
        a[size] = value;
        size++;
    }
    return size;
}

public static int sum(int[] a, int size) {
    int t = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        t += a[i];
    }
    return t;
}

int[] a = new int[5];
int size = 0;
size = add(a, size, 3);
size = add(a, size, 4);
System.out.println(size + " " + sum(a, size));","options":["0 0","2 0","0 7","2 7"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each add writes at the current count and returns the incremented one, which the caller stores back into size. sum then totals only the first 2 slots: 3 + 4 is 7."},{"id":"fs_z6_17_2","topic":"Forgetting to reassign the count","text":"What does this print?

