call blindly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2182","topic":"","text":"Why can a method WRITE a[size] = v and the caller sees it, but size++ inside is lost?","options":["arrays are global","the array is a shared object","size is final","Java bug"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reference-to-object vs by-value primitive - the heart of the pattern.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2183","topic":"","text":"static int find(int[] a, int size, int target) returns the index or -1. find({4, 8, 6, 0}, 3, 6) returns:","options":["3","-1","2","6"],"correct":2,"explain":"The scan over the 3 real elements finds 6 at index 2.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2184","topic":"","text":"find({4, 8, 6, 0}, 3, 0) with that method returns:","options":["3","0","4","-1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Bounding by size correctly refuses to "find" the unused slot.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2185","topic":"","text":"A remove-at-index method for oversize arrays must:","options":["shift left and return size - 1","just zero the slot","swap with slot 0","grow the array"],"correct":0,"explain":"Contiguity is maintained by shifting; the count shrinks by one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2186","topic":"","text":"The parameter order (int[] a, int size) versus (int size, int[] a):","options":["is enforced by Java","is a convention","changes behavior","only one compiles"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pick one order project-wide so call sites never guess.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2187","topic":"","text":"Printing method: static void printUsed(int[] a, int size) prints:","options":["the whole length","capacity - size items","elements 0 to size - 1","the filler"],"correct":2,"explain":"Exactly the data, none of the padding.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_18":[{"id":"zya6_18_0","topic":"Element count","text":"With a perfect size array the count is:","options":["a.length","Always 0","Unknown","A separate variable"],"correct":0,"explain":"With an oversize array it must be tracked separately."},{"id":"zya6_18_1","topic":"Adding elements","text":"Appending is cheap with:","options":["A perfect size array of exactly the right length","A parallel array holding the same indices","An oversize array with spare capacity","An array declared final at the class level"],"correct":2,"explain":"A perfect size array must be reallocated and copied to grow."},{"id":"zya6_18_2","topic":"Memory use","text":"Memory is used most efficiently by:","options":["A perfect size array","Both equally","Neither","An oversize array"],"correct":0,"explain":"No unused slots."},{"id":"zya6_18_3","topic":"Loop bound risk","text":"The extra bug risk with oversize arrays is:","options":["Using a.length instead of the count","Passing by reference","Null elements","Sorting incorrectly"],"correct":0,"explain":"Processing unused slots yields wrong sums, wrong averages, and phantom entries."},{"id":"zya6_18_4","topic":"Method parameters","text":"Passing to a method is simpler with:","options":["A perfect size array","Neither","Both the same","An oversize array"],"correct":0,"explain":"Oversize requires the array plus the count. One parameter suffices."},{"id":"zya6_18_5","topic":"Best of both","text":"The structure combining cheap appends with an accurate count is:","options":["ArrayList","A String","A perfect size array","A 2D array"],"correct":0,"explain":"It maintains capacity and size internally and grows automatically."},{"id":"zya6_18_6","topic":"When perfect size wins","text":"Choose a perfect size array when:","options":["Elements are added one at a time over the run","The number of elements is known up front and fixed","The upper bound is known but the count is not","The array will be resized many times"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fixed data with a known count has no reason to carry spare capacity."},{"id":"zya6_18_7","topic":"When oversize wins","text":"Choose an oversize array when:","options":["Elements are added over time and an upper bound is known","The exact count is known before the array is made","Memory is scarce and no slot may be wasted","The elements must stay sorted at all times"],"correct":0,"explain":"Without a known bound, ArrayList is the better answer."},{"id":"zyf6_18_0","topic":"Traversal difference","text":"The loop bound differs because:","options":["An oversize array uses a.length; a perfect one the count","Both use a.length, but the oversize loop starts at 1","A perfect size array uses a.length; an oversize one the count","Neither uses a.length; both need an explicit capacity"],"correct":2,"explain":"Using a.length on an oversize array processes unused slots."},{"id":"zyf6_18_1","topic":"Which ArrayList resembles","text":"ArrayList internally behaves most like:","options":["An oversize array, with capacity and size tracked separately","A perfect size array recreated on every change","A linked chain of nodes joined by references","A hash table keyed on the element's index"],"correct":0,"explain":"It manages both values for you."},{"id":"fs_z6_18_1","topic":"What length means in each design","text":"Which statement is true for a perfect size array but false for an oversize array?","options":["arr.length is the number of elements actually stored","the array can be passed to a method","the first index is 0","the elements are all of the same type"],"correct":0,"explain":"In a perfect size array length and count are the same number. In an oversize array length is the capacity and the count is a separate variable that must be kept in step."},{"id":"fs_z6_18_2","topic":"Choosing the design","text":"A program must collect an unknown number of readings until a sentinel value is entered, using arrays only. The appropriate design is:","options":["an oversize array with a separate count variable","a perfect size array, created once the loop has finished","a perfect size array of length 1","an array variable that is declared but never created"],"correct":0,"explain":"An array's length is fixed at creation, so with no count known in advance the only array option is to allocate generous capacity and track how many slots are in use."},{"id":"fs_z6_18_3","topic":"ArrayList and the two designs","text":"How does ArrayList relate to the perfect size and oversize array designs?","options":["It is the oversize design with the capacity and the count managed internally","It is a perfect size array that is recreated at exactly the right length after each change","It is a fixed array of ten elements that cannot grow","It abandons indexing entirely in favour of a linked structure"],"correct":0,"explain":"ArrayList holds an array plus a size, exposes size(), and allocates a bigger array and copies when the current one fills. Hiding that bookkeeping removes the class of bugs oversize arrays invite."},{"id":"fs_z6_18_4","topic":"Comparing the two","text":"What does this print?

