torage","A new array created inside the method","A static array shared by every call"],"correct":2,"explain":"Returning a newly built result leaves the caller's data untouched."},{"id":"zya6_20_2","topic":"Returning a local array","text":"Returning an array declared inside the method is:","options":["A compile error","Only allowed if static","Unsafe — it is destroyed on return","Safe, because the array lives on the heap"],"correct":3,"explain":"Only the local reference variable disappears; the object survives as long as something references it."},{"id":"zya6_20_3","topic":"Empty result","text":"A filter method that matches nothing should return:","options":["A one-element array","An exception","null","A zero-length array"],"correct":3,"explain":"Returning an empty array lets the caller loop without a null check — return null and every caller must guard."},{"id":"zya6_20_4","topic":"Sizing the result","text":"To return only elements matching a condition you must:","options":["Allocate the full length and leave gaps as zero","Return the original array with non-matches nulled","Append to the array as matches are found","Count the matches first, then allocate and fill"],"correct":3,"explain":"Two passes, because array size is fixed at creation. An ArrayList avoids the first pass."},{"id":"zya6_20_5","topic":"Returning multiple values","text":"An array return is one way to:","options":["Avoid declaring a return type on the method","Return several related values from a method","Pass several values into a method at once","Guarantee the caller cannot modify the result"],"correct":1,"explain":"Though a small class with named fields is usually clearer than a two-element array."},{"id":"zya6_20_6","topic":"Modifying vs returning","text":"A method that modifies the parameter array in place:","options":["Cannot compile","Returns null","Must also return it","Can be void"],"correct":3,"explain":"Doing both is legal and sometimes convenient for chaining, but it can mislead a reader into thinking the original is unchanged. The caller sees the changes."},{"id":"zya6_20_7","topic":"Caller usage","text":"int[] r = doubled(a); means r references:","options":["null","A copy of a always","The same array as a","Whatever array the method returned"],"correct":3,"explain":"Whether that is a new array or a itself depends entirely on the method."},{"id":"zyf6_20_0","topic":"Filter sizing","text":"Returning only matching elements requires knowing the match count because:","options":["Elements must be sorted","Methods return one value","Arrays have fixed size","Java forbids loops"],"correct":2,"explain":"Hence the count-then-allocate two-pass approach."},{"id":"zyf6_20_1","topic":"null versus empty","text":"Returning an empty array rather than null means callers:","options":["Can loop immediately without guarding","Forces callers to check for null before looping","Uses less memory than returning null does","Is a compile error when the array type is known"],"correct":0,"explain":"It removes a whole class of NullPointerException."},{"id":"fs_z6_20_1","topic":"Returning a new array","text":"What does this print?

public static int[] doubled(int[] a) {
    int[] out = new int[a.length];
    for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
        out[i] = a[i] * 2;
    }
    return out;
}

