public static int[] evens(int[] a) {
    int n = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        if (x % 2 == 0) {
            n++;
        }
    }
    int[] out = new int[n];
    int j = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        if (x % 2 == 0) {
            out[j++] = x;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

int[] r = evens(new int[]{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6});
System.out.println(r.length + " " + r[0] + " " + r[r.length - 1]);","options":["0 0 0","3 2 6","6 2 6","3 1 5"],"correct":1,"explain":"The first pass counts the matches so the result array can be allocated at exactly the right length; the second pass fills it. The evens of 1..6 are 2, 4 and 6."},{"id":"fs_z6_20_3","topic":"Lifetime of a returned array","text":"A method creates int[] out = new int[3]; fills it, and ends with return out;. Why is the array still usable after the method has finished?","options":["It is not usable - the returned reference becomes null","The array lives on the heap; only the local variable out disappears","Java copies the array into the caller's stack frame as it returns","Arrays created inside a method are automatically static"],"correct":1,"explain":"The local variable is destroyed with the method's frame, but the object it pointed at is on the heap and the returned reference keeps it alive."},{"id":"fs_z6_20_4","topic":"Empty array rather than null","text":"What does this print?

public static int[] matches(int[] a, int target) {
    int n = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        if (x == target) {
            n++;
        }
    }
    int[] out = new int[n];
    int j = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        if (x == target) {
            out[j++] = x;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

