 sized array","return the count","print them instead"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two-pass count-then-fill is the standard perfect-size production pattern.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2215","topic":"","text":"static int[] copyOf(int[] src) { int[] c = new int[src.length]; for (int i = 0; i < src.length; i++) c[i] = src[i]; return c; } After c[0] changes, src[0]:","options":["changes too","becomes 0","is unchanged - the copy is independent","is deleted"],"correct":2,"explain":"A fresh array plus element copying breaks the aliasing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2216","topic":"","text":"int[] result = merge(a, b); The variable result:","options":["is a copy of a","is a copy of b","must be pre-allocated","refers to the array merge built"],"correct":3,"explain":"The method builds and returns; the caller just catches the reference.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2217","topic":"","text":"Returning null instead of an empty array from a search method:","options":["forces every caller to null-check","is required","saves memory meaningfully","is identical"],"correct":0,"explain":"Returning new int[0] lets callers loop safely with zero special cases.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2218","topic":"","text":"The length of a returned array is discovered by the caller via:","options":["a second returned int","result.length","guessing","the method name"],"correct":1,"explain":"Arrays self-describe their length - one more reason to return them.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_22":[{"id":"zya6_22_0","topic":"Javadoc placement","text":"Method documentation goes:","options":["Inside the method body, on the first line","At the top of the file, above the imports","Directly below the method's closing brace","Directly above the method signature, in /** ... */"],"correct":3,"explain":"Keeping it attached means it travels with the method."},{"id":"zya6_22_1","topic":"@param tag","text":"@param documents:","options":["The value the method hands back to its caller","The exceptions the method is able to throw","One parameter and its meaning or valid range","The other methods this one calls internally"],"correct":2,"explain":"One @param per parameter, naming it first."},{"id":"zya6_22_2","topic":"@return tag","text":"@return documents:","options":["Each parameter and the range it accepts","What the method returns, including special values","The conditions under which it throws","The author and date the method was written"],"correct":1,"explain":""@return the index, or -1 if not found" is exactly the detail a caller needs."},{"id":"zya6_22_3","topic":"@throws tag","text":"@throws documents:","options":["The conditions under which an exception is raised","Each parameter and its meaning or valid range","The value the method hands back to its caller","The try-catch blocks used inside the method"],"correct":0,"explain":"Especially valuable for the input that triggers it."},{"id":"zya6_22_4","topic":"void and @return","text":"A void method should:","options":["Still include @return","Omit @return","Return null","Not be documented"],"correct":1,"explain":"There is no value to describe."},{"id":"zya6_22_5","topic":"Documenting the contract","text":"Good method documentation states:","options":["Which lines of the body do the real work","Who wrote it and when it was last changed","What it does, what it needs, and what it guarantees","How long it takes to run on typical input"],"correct":2,"explain":"Describing implementation ties the docs to code that may change; describing the contract does not."},{"id":"zya6_22_6","topic":"Generated documentation","text":"The javadoc tool produces:","options":["A .class file","Browsable HTML API documentation","An executable","A test report"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is exactly how the official Java API docs are produced."},{"id":"zya6_22_7","topic":"When to document","text":"Documentation is most valuable for:","options":["Methods whose behaviour or valid inputs are not obvious","Methods that are only two or three lines long","Methods whose names already describe them fully","Methods that are declared private to the class"],"correct":0,"explain":"A comment restating an obvious name adds noise; one explaining a boundary rule adds value."},{"id":"zyf6_22_0","topic":"Tag for parameters","text":"Each parameter is documented with:","options":["@param","@throws","@author","@return"],"correct":0,"explain":"One @param per parameter, naming it first."},{"id":"zyf6_22_1","topic":"Documenting -1","text":"A method returning -1 for not-found should record that in:","options":["The @return description","The method name","Nowhere","A comment inside the body"],"correct":0,"explain":"The caller cannot handle what is not documented."},{"id":"fs_z6_22_1","topic":"Javadoc comment form","text":"Which comment is processed by the javadoc tool into API documentation for a method?","options":["a /** ... */ comment placed immediately above the method","a // ... comment placed immediately above the method","a /* ... */ comment placed inside the method body","a /** ... */ comment placed as the first statement inside the method body"],"correct":0,"explain":"Javadoc comments start with /** and must sit immediately above the item they document. Ordinary // and /* */ comments are ignored by the tool."},{"id":"fs_z6_22_2","topic":"Documenting a sentinel result","text":"public static int indexOf(int[] a, int target) returns -1 when the target is not present. Which tag should record that fact?","options":["@param","@throws","@author","@return"],"correct":3,"explain":"@return documents the value handed back, including special results such as -1 for not found. That is part of the contract a caller must know before using the method."},{"id":"fs_z6_22_3","topic":"How many @param tags","text":"How many @param tags belong in the Javadoc for public static double average(int a, int b, int c)?","options":["One, listing all three parameters together","None, because all three parameters have the same type","Four - one per parameter plus one for the returned value","Three - one per parameter"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each parameter gets its own @param tag naming it and describing it. The returned value is documented separately with @return."},{"id":"fs_z6_22_4","topic":"Placement of a Javadoc comment","text":"A /** ... */ comment is written several lines above a method, with an unrelated statement in between. The effect is that the comment:","options":["causes the program to fail to compile","is attached to the previous method instead","is moved into place automatically by the compiler","is not attached to that method and is treated as an ordinary comment"],"correct":3,"explain":"A Javadoc comment documents whatever immediately follows it. Placed anywhere else it is just a comment and the method ends up undocumented."},{"id":"zf2230","topic":"","text":"A Javadoc comment for a method begins with:","options":["//","/*","///","/**"],"correct":3,"explain":"The double-star opener marks documentation comments.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2231","topic":"","text":"The @param tag documents:","options":["one parameter’s meaning","the return value","the author","the version"],"correct":0,"explain":"@param hours the hours worked this week - one tag per parameter.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2232","topic":"","text":"The @return tag documents:","options":["each parameter","what the method hands back","exceptions only","the file name"],"correct":1,"explain":"It describes the value and its meaning/units.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2233","topic":"","text":"Javadoc comments are placed:","options":["inside the body","after the method","right BEFORE the described method","at the file’s end"],"correct":2,"explain":"The tool associates the comment with the declaration that follows it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2234","topic":"","text":"The javadoc tool turns these comments into:","options":["faster bytecode","unit tests","compile errors","linked HTML documentation pages"],"correct":3,"explain":"The official Java API docs are generated exactly this way.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2235","topic":"","text":"The FIRST sentence of a method’s Javadoc should:","options":["summarize what the method does","list the local variables","apologize for bugs","name the author"],"correct":0,"explain":"That sentence becomes the summary-table entry - make it stand alone.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2236","topic":"","text":"/** Computes weekly pay. @param rate hourly rate @param hours hours worked @return the pay in dollars */ documents a method with:","options":["one parameter","two parameters and a return value","no return","three parameters"],"correct":1,"explain":"One @param per parameter, plus the @return.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2237","topic":"","text":"Good Javadoc describes:","options":["every statement inside","the compiler version","the WHAT and the contract, not the HOW","keyboard shortcuts"],"correct":2,"explain":"Callers need the contract; the body explains itself to maintainers.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2238","topic":"","text":"Javadoc on a method documents valid inputs, like "hours must be 0-168", because:","options":["the JVM enforces comments","it changes execution","javadoc requires numbers","callers need the contract to call right"],"correct":3,"explain":"Preconditions belong in the documented contract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2239","topic":"","text":"IDE tooltips that pop up while calling your own method come from:","options":["your Javadoc comments","the JVM","random text","the internet"],"correct":0,"explain":"Write the doc once; every future call site benefits.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2240","topic":"","text":"Documenting units, as in "@return the temperature in Celsius":","options":["prevents unit-confusion bugs","slows the method","is forbidden","converts the units"],"correct":0,"explain":"Mars orbiters have been lost to undocumented units - say them.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u6/z6_23":[{"id":"zya6_23_0","topic":"Decomposing pay","text":"Splitting a salary program into methods means:","options":["One long method with clearly labelled sections","One method per input value the program reads","Separate classes rather than separate methods","Separate methods such as grossPay, tax, and netPay"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each computation becomes independently testable and reusable."},{"id":"zya6_23_1","topic":"grossPay signature","text":"A gross pay method takes:","options":["No parameters, reading the values from fields","The rate only, returning the hours worked","The rate and the hours, returning a double","The rate and hours, printing rather than returning"],"correct":2,"explain":"Passing both keeps it usable for any employee."},{"id":"zya6_23_2","topic":"Overtime inside the method","text":"Where does the over-40-hours rule belong?","options":["Nowhere","In main","Inside grossPay","In the tax method"],"correct":2,"explain":"Putting the rule in one place means it cannot be applied inconsistently. Every caller gets it."},{"id":"zya6_23_3","topic":"Tax method","text":"A tax method should take:","options":["Nothing","The hours","The gross pay and the rate","The employee name"],"correct":2,"explain":"It computes from the gross, so that is what it needs."},{"id":"zya6_23_4","topic":"Composing methods","text":"Net pay is computed by:","options":["Calling tax first, then passing the result to grossPay","Calling grossPay and tax and adding the two results","Calling a single method that does all three steps","Calling grossPay, then passing the result to tax"],"correct":3,"explain":"Small methods compose into the whole calculation."},{"id":"zya6_23_5","topic":"Returning vs printing","text":"Each calculation method should:","options":["Print it, so the caller need not handle output","Store it in a static field for the caller to read","Return it, leaving printing to the caller","Both print and return it, for flexibility"],"correct":2,"explain":"A method that prints cannot be reused in a total, a test, or a GUI."},{"id":"zya6_23_6","topic":"Testing the pieces","text":"Splitting into methods makes testing easier because:","options":["The compiler tests each method as it is compiled","Each method can be checked against known values on its own","Shorter methods contain fewer defects by nature","Only the final method needs to be tested at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"You can verify grossPay(20, 45) == 950 without running the whole program."},{"id":"zya6_23_7","topic":"Duplicate logic","text":"If the overtime formula appears in three places:","options":["It should be extracted into one method","It should be copied carefully so each stays correct","It should be turned into three named constants","It should be moved into main so it appears once"],"correct":0,"explain":"Three copies means three places to fix and two chances to miss one."},{"id":"zya6_23_8","topic":"Main's role","text":"With good decomposition, main should:","options":["Contain every calculation the program performs","Be empty, with a helper method called from it","Handle only the output, with input read elsewhere","Read input, call the methods, and display results"],"correct":3,"explain":"main becomes the coordinator, and the program reads as a summary of what it does."},{"id":"zya6_23_9","topic":"Adding a feature","text":"Adding a bonus calculation to a decomposed program means:","options":["Nothing","Rewriting main","Adding one method and one call","Changing every method"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which is the practical payoff of decomposition."},{"id":"fs_z6_23_1","topic":"Composed calculation methods","text":"What does this print?

public static double taxRate(double salary) {
    if (salary >= 100000) {
        return 0.28;
    }
    if (salary >= 50000) {
        return 0.22;
    }
    if (salary >= 25000) {
        return 0.15;
    }
    return 0.10;
}

public static double netPay(double salary) {
    return salary * (1 - taxRate(salary));
}

