false"],"correct":1,"explain":"The method exits the moment a non-digit is found, so "12a4" is rejected at index 2. The empty string enters no iteration at all and reaches return true - an edge case worth a deliberate decision."},{"id":"fs_z6_24_4","topic":"Where return true belongs","text":"In hasValidChars the statement return true; is placed after the loop rather than inside it. Why?","options":["Returning from inside a loop causes a runtime error","The string is only known to be valid once every character has passed the test","A loop body is not allowed to contain a return statement","The compiler requires the last statement of a method to be return true;"],"correct":1,"explain":"return false can fire as soon as one character breaks the rule, but success is only certain when the loop has finished. return true inside the loop would accept a string after checking one character."},{"id":"zf2252","topic":"","text":"static boolean isValidLength(String n) { return n.length() >= 1 && n.length() <= 63; } isValidLength("") returns:","options":["true","0","error","false"],"correct":3,"explain":"The empty string fails the minimum-length rule.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2253","topic":"","text":"static boolean hasOnlyLettersDigits(String n) { for (int i = 0; i < n.length(); i++) if (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(n.charAt(i))) return false; return true; } hasOnlyLettersDigits("shop24") returns:","options":["true","false","24","shop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every character is a letter or digit.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2254","topic":"","text":"hasOnlyLettersDigits("my_site") returns:","options":["true","false","_","7"],"correct":1,"explain":"The underscore fails the test at index 2.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2255","topic":"","text":"A master method isValidDomain calling the small checks in sequence:","options":["runs all rules regardless","picks a random rule","returns false at the first failed rule","always returns true"],"correct":2,"explain":"if (!isValidLength(n)) return false; ... - guard-clause chaining.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2256","topic":"","text":"Each validation rule as its own boolean method means:","options":["rules cannot change","tests are impossible","main gets longer","each rule tests individually"],"correct":3,"explain":"isValidLength gets its own tiny test set, independent of the rest.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2257","topic":"","text":"Boolean validation methods are conventionally named:","options":["isSomething or hasSomething","checkStuff1","validate","doIt"],"correct":0,"explain":"The name reads as the question the return answers.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2258","topic":"","text":"static boolean startsOk(String n) { return !n.isEmpty() && Character.isLetter(n.charAt(0)); } startsOk("9lives") returns:","options":["true","false","9","lives"],"correct":1,"explain":"The first character is a digit, not a letter.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2259","topic":"","text":"The !n.isEmpty() check in startsOk exists because:","options":["empty strings are valid","isLetter needs it","charAt(0) on an empty string would throw","style only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Short-circuit guard before the risky access.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2260","topic":"","text":"Reusing isValidDomain in three different programs requires:","options":["three rewrites","three languages","calling the same method - no copies","recompiling Java"],"correct":2,"explain":"Write-once validation is the reuse payoff.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2261","topic":"","text":"A test list for isValidDomain should include:","options":["only valid names","one random string","no empty string","one case per rule plus a valid name"],"correct":3,"explain":"One test per rule, plus the happy path.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2262","topic":"","text":"Combining results, return isValidLength(n) && startsOk(n) && hasOnlyLettersDigits(n);:","options":["a compact all-rules-must-pass check","passes if ANY rule passes","always false","a compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"&& chains the requirements; short-circuiting skips later checks after a failure.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_3":[{"id":"zya7_3_0","topic":"Class definition","text":"A class definition specifies:","options":["The specific values a single instance will hold","The order in which its objects must be created","The fields and methods its objects will have","The memory address each object will occupy"],"correct":2,"explain":"The class is the blueprint; objects are instances built from it."},{"id":"zya7_3_1","topic":"Field declaration","text":"A field is declared:","options":["In main only","In the constructor only","Inside a method","At class level, outside any method"],"correct":3,"explain":"Fields belong to the object and live as long as it does."},{"id":"zya7_3_2","topic":"Field default value","text":"private int count; in a class defaults to:","options":["Uninitialized — a compile error to read","1","null","0"],"correct":3,"explain":"Fields get defaults (0, false, null); local variables do not."},{"id":"zya7_3_3","topic":"Access modifier for fields","text":"Fields are conventionally declared:","options":["static","final","public","private"],"correct":3,"explain":"Private fields with public methods is encapsulation — the object controls its own state."},{"id":"zya7_3_4","topic":"Instance vs class","text":"Each object created from a class has:","options":["No fields","Only static fields","One shared copy of the fields","Its own copy of each instance field"],"correct":3,"explain":"Static fields are the shared ones."},{"id":"zya7_3_5","topic":"Method accessing a field","text":"An instance method can use a field:","options":["Only with static","Never","Only via a parameter","Directly by name"],"correct":3,"explain":"The field is in scope throughout the class."},{"id":"zya7_3_6","topic":"Class naming","text":"A class holding bank account data is named:","options":["BANK_ACCOUNT","bank_account","bankAccount","BankAccount"],"correct":3,"explain":"PascalCase for class names."},{"id":"zya7_3_7","topic":"File naming","text":"A public class BankAccount must be in a file named:","options":["Main.java","Any name","bankaccount.java","BankAccount.java"],"correct":3,"explain":"Public class name and file name must match exactly, including case."},{"id":"zya7_3_8","topic":"Class with no main","text":"A class without a main method:","options":["Fails to compile; every class needs a main","Compiles but can never be used by any program","Compiles fine and is used by other classes","Compiles and runs on its own, doing nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Only the class you launch needs main."},{"id":"zya7_3_9","topic":"Purpose of a class","text":"Grouping related data and behaviour into a class:","options":["Makes the program run measurably faster","Keeps data with the operations that maintain it","Removes the need for methods to take parameters","Guarantees the fields can never become invalid"],"correct":1,"explain":"A balance and the rules for changing it belong together — that is the argument against parallel arrays."},{"id":"q2","topic":"Create static nested","text":"How do you instantiate a static nested class Nested inside Outer?","options":["outer.new Nested()","new Nested()","new Outer.Nested()","Outer.Nested.create()"],"correct":2,"explain":"A static nested class is created with new Outer.Nested() — no outer instance required.","distractors":{"0":"That syntax is for inner (non-static) classes.","2":"You must qualify with Outer.","3":"There's no such factory by default."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"q3","topic":"Create inner","text":"How do you instantiate a non-static inner class Inner given an Outer object `o`?","options":["Inner.of(o)","new Inner(o)","new Outer.Inner()","o.new Inner()"],"correct":3,"explain":"A non-static inner class needs an enclosing instance: o.new Inner().","distractors":{"0":"Static syntax doesn't apply to inner classes.","2":"That's not the inner-class syntax.","3":"No such default factory."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"q4","topic":"Inner access","text":"A non-static inner class can access what of its outer instance?","options":["Even the outer object's private fields","Only the outer class's public fields","Only the outer class's static members","Nothing of the outer object at all"],"correct":0,"explain":"Inner classes can directly access the enclosing instance's private members — a key reason to use them.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q5","topic":"Anonymous class","text":"What is an anonymous class?","options":["A class whose methods all have completely empty bodies","A class with no name, declared and created in one expression","A class declared inside a method and given a name","A class that cannot be instantiated at all, ever"],"correct":1,"explain":"An anonymous class is defined and instantiated at once with no name, usually to implement an interface/extend a class inline.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q7","topic":"Anonymous use","text":"Anonymous classes are most often used to:","options":["Group several helper types under one class","Hide a class from every other package","Share one instance across the whole program","Provide a quick one-off implementation of an interface"],"correct":3,"explain":"They give a compact inline implementation of an interface or abstract class, e.g., an event listener or Runnable.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q9","topic":"Static nested access","text":"A static nested class can directly access which outer members?","options":["Every member of the outer class, including private","Only the outer class's static members","Only the outer class's public instance fields","Nothing; it is entirely independent of the outer"],"correct":1,"explain":"Without an outer instance, a static nested class can access only the outer class's static members directly.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q10","topic":"Why inner class","text":"A good use case for a non-static inner class is:","options":["A utility holding only static helper methods","A constant table shared by the whole program","An iterator that needs the outer collection's private data","A class that must be usable without the outer one"],"correct":2,"explain":"Inner classes shine when the helper is tightly coupled to one outer instance's state, like an iterator over its private array.","__rebal":1},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u5_1","topic":"Static nested","text":"A static nested class differs from an inner class in that it:","options":["Holds a reference to the enclosing instance","Does not hold a reference to an enclosing instance","Cannot be declared private within the outer class","Must be declared before any field of the outer class"],"correct":1,"explain":"A static nested class has no implicit reference to an outer instance, so it can exist without one; an inner (non-static) class does."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u5_2","topic":"Inner class access","text":"A (non-static) inner class can access the enclosing object’s:","options":["Nothing","Only public methods","Only static members","Private instance fields and methods"],"correct":3,"explain":"An inner class is tied to an outer instance and can access all its members, including private ones."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u5_3","topic":"Anonymous class","text":"An anonymous class is most commonly used to:","options":["Group a helper type together with the class that uses it","Provide a one-off implementation of an interface or abstract type","Give a nested class access to the outer's private data","Declare a type that no other class is allowed to extend"],"correct":1,"explain":"Anonymous classes give a concise, one-use implementation (e.g., a Comparator or event handler) at the point of use."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u5_6","topic":"Local class","text":"A local class is a class declared:","options":["At the top level","In another package","Inside a method body","As an interface"],"correct":2,"explain":"A local class is defined within a method and is visible only inside that method."},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u5_9","topic":"Nested purpose","text":"A common reason to use a nested class is to:","options":["Reduce the number of methods the outer class must declare itself","Logically group a helper type with the class that uses it and improve encapsulation","Allow the outer class to be instantiated more than one time","Make the helper type accessible from every other package"],"correct":1,"explain":"Nesting a helper class that is only used by its enclosing class improves encapsulation and keeps related code together."},{"id":"zf2281","topic":"","text":"public class Part { private String name; public void setName(String n) { name = n; } } The field name is:","options":["public","private - only Part’s methods touch it","static","a local variable"],"correct":1,"explain":"private fields plus public methods is the standard encapsulation shape.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2282","topic":"","text":"Fields are declared:","options":["inside main","inside each method","in the class, outside every method","outside the class"],"correct":2,"explain":"That placement is what makes them per-object state.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2283","topic":"","text":"Making fields private and access public (through methods) is called:","options":["inheritance","overloading","iteration","encapsulation"],"correct":3,"explain":"The class controls its own data; outsiders go through the interface.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_9":[{"id":"zya7_9_0","topic":"Constructor overloading","text":"Multiple constructors in one class must differ in:","options":["Return type","Their parameter lists","Name","Access modifier"],"correct":1,"explain":"Constructors all share the class name, so the parameter list is the only distinguishing feature."},{"id":"zya7_9_1","topic":"Default constructor","text":"If a class defines no constructor, Java:","options":["Refuses to compile until one is written","Provides a no-argument constructor leaving fields at defaults","Provides a constructor matching every field","Creates objects without initialising memory at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"That free constructor disappears the moment you write any constructor of your own."},{"id":"zya7_9_2","topic":"Losing the default","text":"After defining Dog(String name), calling new Dog() is:","options":["A compile error, unless a no-arg constructor is also defined","Valid, since Java always supplies the no-arg form","Valid, with name set to the empty string","A runtime error thrown when the object is built"],"correct":0,"explain":"A very common surprise — adding one constructor removes the implicit one."},{"id":"zya7_9_3","topic":"this() call","text":"this("Rex"); inside a constructor:","options":["Calls the parent class's constructor","Creates a second Dog object named Rex","Assigns Rex to the name field directly","Calls another constructor of the same class"],"correct":3,"explain":"Constructor chaining puts shared initialization in one place."},{"id":"zya7_9_4","topic":"this() placement","text":"A this(...) call must be:","options":["Anywhere in the constructor","The very first statement","The last statement","Outside the constructor"],"correct":1,"explain":"The compiler enforces this so initialization happens exactly once, in order."},{"id":"zya7_9_5","topic":"Constructor return type","text":"A constructor declares:","options":["void","No return type at all","The class type","int"],"correct":1,"explain":"Writing void Dog() makes it an ordinary method that happens to share the class name — and then new Dog() will not find a constructor."},{"id":"zya7_9_6","topic":"Overload resolution","text":"new Dog("Rex", 3) selects the constructor:","options":["Whose parameter list matches (String, int)","The first constructor declared in the class","The constructor with the most parameters","Whichever constructor was defined last"],"correct":0,"explain":"Matching is by argument count and types."},{"id":"zya7_9_7","topic":"Reducing duplication","text":"Three constructors sharing setup code should:","options":["Chain with super(...) to the parent constructor","Copy the shared code into each of the three","Move the shared code into a static initialiser","Chain with this(...) to a single primary constructor"],"correct":3,"explain":"One place to fix a bug rather than three."},{"id":"zya7_9_8","topic":"Constructor with validation","text":"Rejecting an invalid argument in a constructor:","options":["Is not allowed","Is good practice","Requires void","Must be silent"],"correct":1,"explain":"An object should be valid from the moment it exists. It prevents an invalid object from ever existing."},{"id":"zya7_9_9","topic":"Field initialization order","text":"Fields with initializers are set:","options":["After the constructor body","Before the constructor body runs","Never","Only if static"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is why a constructor assignment overrides a field initializer."},{"id":"fs_z7_9_1","topic":"Chained constructors","text":"Rect() delegates with this(1, 1), Rect(int side) delegates with this(side, side), and Rect(int w, int h) assigns both fields. area() returns w * h. What does this print?

Rect a = new Rect();
Rect b = new Rect(4);
Rect c = new Rect(2, 5);
System.out.println(a.area() + " " + b.area() + " " + c.area());","options":["1 4 10","1 16 7","0 16 10","1 16 10"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each delegating constructor funnels into the two-argument one, so the no-argument version builds 1 by 1 (area 1), Rect(4) builds 4 by 4 (area 16) and Rect(2, 5) has area 10."},{"id":"fs_z7_9_2","topic":"Return types and constructors","text":"Inside class Rect, a member written as public void Rect() { w = 1; h = 1; } is:","options":["A constructor that must be called explicitly as Rect()","A compile error, because a method may not share the class name","A valid no-argument constructor","An ordinary method named Rect that new will never run"],"correct":3,"explain":"A constructor has no return type at all, so adding void turns the member into a plain method that merely happens to be spelled like the class. Because no constructor is then declared, new Rect() calls the compiler-supplied default and the fields stay 0."},{"id":"fs_z7_9_3","topic":"this(...) and super(...)","text":"Which statement about this(...) and super(...) inside a constructor is true?","options":["Every constructor must contain an explicit this(...) call","super(...) may follow this(...) as long as both come before any field assignment","this(...) may appear anywhere before the last statement","A constructor may use one or the other, never both"],"correct":3,"explain":"Both delegation calls are required to be the very first statement, so only one of them can occupy that position. A constructor that chains with this(...) reaches the parent indirectly, through the constructor it delegates to. Each must be the first statement."},{"id":"fs_z7_9_4","topic":"Overload selection and shared validation","text":"Student() delegates with this("unknown", 0), Student(String n) delegates with this(n, 0), and Student(String n, int s) stores the score only when it is 0 through 100 and otherwise stores 0. toString() returns name + ":" + score. What does this print?

System.out.println(new Student());
System.out.println(new Student("Ada"));
System.out.println(new Student("Bo", 88));
System.out.println(new Student("Cy", 150));","options":["unknown:0
Ada:0
Bo:88
Cy:150","unknown:0
Ada:unknown
Bo:88
Cy:0","null:0
Ada:0
Bo:88
Cy:0","unknown:0
Ada:0
Bo:88
Cy:0"],"correct":3,"explain":"The argument list picks the constructor, and the two delegating versions funnel into the one that does the work. Because the validation lives in that single constructor, the out-of-range 150 is rejected for every path into the class."},{"id":"zf2325","topic":"","text":"Constructor overloading means a class has:","options":["constructors with differing parameters","two names","no constructors","static constructors"],"correct":0,"explain":"new Part() and new Part("Bolt", 5) can coexist.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2326","topic":"","text":"public Part() { } and public Part(String n) { name = n; } Calling new Part("Nut") runs:","options":["the no-arg version","the String version","both","neither"],"correct":1,"explain":"The argument list selects the constructor, exactly like method overloading.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2327","topic":"","text":"The no-argument constructor typically:","options":["throws","reads the keyboard","sets fields to sensible defaults","calls main"],"correct":2,"explain":"A blank Part might default to name "unknown", quantity 0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2328","topic":"","text":"Defining ONLY Part(String n) and calling new Part() causes:","options":["the String version with null","an empty object","a warning","a compile error"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java supplies the invisible default ONLY when you define no constructor at all.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2329","topic":"","text":"One constructor can invoke another with:","options":["this(...) as its FIRST statement","super.main()","new inside itself freely","constructor.call()"],"correct":0,"explain":"public Part() { this("unknown", 0); } - chaining avoids duplicated init code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2330","topic":"","text":"The benefit of constructor chaining via this(...) is:","options":["init logic lives exactly once","faster objects","fewer fields","no benefit"],"correct":0,"explain":"The short constructors just fill defaults and delegate.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2331","topic":"","text":"Two constructors Part(String n) and Part(String n, int q):","options":["collide","legal overloads picked by count","merge","pick randomly"],"correct":1,"explain":"Different parameter lists = different signatures.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2332","topic":"","text":"new Part("Gear", 12) with matching constructor sets:","options":["only the name","only the quantity","both fields from the arguments in order","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Arguments map to parameters positionally.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2333","topic":"","text":"A constructor taking all fields exists so that:","options":["setters become illegal","fields become public","testing stops","objects arrive fully-formed in one step"],"correct":3,"explain":"new Part("Bolt", 0.25, 40) beats new-then-three-setters.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2334","topic":"","text":"Constructors cannot be called like p.Part("X") later because:","options":["they run only at object creation via new","they are private always","the name is wrong","they return void"],"correct":0,"explain":"Re-initializing uses ordinary methods, not the constructor.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2335","topic":"","text":"Choosing WHICH constructors to provide should follow:","options":["always exactly five","the creation paths callers really need","one per field","random choice"],"correct":1,"explain":"Common creation paths get constructors; rare ones can use setters.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zy6_1_5_g13","topic":"Return type","text":"A Java constructor:","options":["has no return type at all","returns int","returns void","returns the class"],"correct":0,"explain":"Constructors have no return type — not even void.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zy6_1_6_lg_D286_0_44","topic":"Constructor return","text":"Regarding return types, a Java constructor:","options":["Returns an int","Has no return type","Returns the class type","Returns void"],"correct":1,"explain":"Constructors declare no return type, not even void.","__rebal":1}],"D286/u7/z7_15":[{"id":"zya7_15_0","topic":"ArrayList of objects","text":"ArrayList&lt;Student> holds:","options":["Primitives","Copies of Student objects","References to Student objects","Only Strings"],"correct":2,"explain":"Modifying an object retrieved from the list changes the object in the list — they are the same object."},{"id":"zya7_15_1","topic":"Adding an object","text":"Adding a new student uses:","options":["list[0] = new Student("Ana");","list.put(new Student("Ana"));","list.add(new Student("Ana"));","list.new Student("Ana");"],"correct":2,"explain":"add appends; put is a Map method and brackets are for arrays."},{"id":"zya7_15_2","topic":"Accessing a field","text":"To print the first student's name:","options":["list.getName(0)","list[0].getName()","list.get(0).getName()","list.get(0).name"],"correct":2,"explain":"get returns the object, then you call its method. Direct field access fails when the field is private."},{"id":"zya7_15_3","topic":"Looping objects","text":"The cleanest loop over ArrayList&lt;Student> is:","options":["while (list.hasNext())","for (int i=0; i<list.size(); i++)","for (Student s","for (Student s in list)"],"correct":2,"explain":"The enhanced for works on any Iterable, including ArrayList. List)."},{"id":"zya7_15_4","topic":"Modifying through the loop","text":"Calling s.setGrade(90) inside a for-each:","options":["Is a compile error","Has no effect","Modifies the object in the list","Throws an exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"Mutating the object is fine; replacing the list slot during iteration is what throws. S is a reference."},{"id":"zya7_15_5","topic":"Searching for an object","text":"Finding a student by name requires:","options":["Looping and comparing with == on the name field","Calling indexOf with the name on the array","Looping and comparing with equals on the name field","Sorting the array and reading the first element"],"correct":2,"explain":"indexOf compares whole objects using equals, which by default is reference identity."},{"id":"zya7_15_6","topic":"Removing during iteration","text":"Calling list.remove(s) inside a for-each throws:","options":["Nothing","NullPointerException","ConcurrentModificationException","ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException"],"correct":2,"explain":"Use an explicit Iterator with it.remove(), or removeIf."},{"id":"zya7_15_7","topic":"Size of the list","text":"The number of students is:","options":["list.length()","list.length","list.size()","list.count"],"correct":2,"explain":"Arrays use length, Strings use length(), collections use size()."},{"id":"zya7_15_8","topic":"Printing the list","text":"System.out.println(list) prints readable text only if:","options":["Elements are Strings","The list is small","Student overrides toString()","The list is sorted"],"correct":2,"explain":"Otherwise you get the class name and a hash code."},{"id":"zya7_15_9","topic":"Empty list","text":"A new ArrayList&lt;Student>() has size:","options":["Undefined","null","0","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"Empty but not null — list.isEmpty() returns true."},{"id":"fs_z7_15_1","topic":"Mutating objects in a for-each","text":"Student stores a name and a score with accessors and setScore. What does this print?

ArrayList<Student> roll = new ArrayList<>();
roll.add(new Student("Ana", 88));
roll.add(new Student("Bo", 70));
for (Student s : roll) {
    s.setScore(s.getScore() + 5);
}
System.out.println(roll.get(0).getScore() + " " + roll.get(1).getScore());","options":["88 70","93 70","93 75","Compiler error: a for-each variable cannot be modified"],"correct":2,"explain":"The loop variable is a copy of the reference, which still points at the object in the list, so setScore changes the stored object. What the loop could not do is replace an element, which needs set(i, ...)."},{"id":"fs_z7_15_2","topic":"Tracking the best object","text":"Student stores a name and a score with accessors. What does this print?

ArrayList<Student> roster = new ArrayList<>();
roster.add(new Student("Ada", 95));
roster.add(new Student("Alan", 88));
roster.add(new Student("Grace", 99));
Student best = roster.get(0);
for (Student s : roster) {
    if (s.getScore() > best.getScore()) { best = s; }
}
System.out.println(best.getName() + " " + best.getScore());","options":["Ada 95","Grace 95","Grace 99","Alan 88"],"correct":2,"explain":"Holding the whole object as the running best keeps the name and the score together, so no index is needed to line them up. Grace has the highest score, and her name travels with it."},{"id":"fs_z7_15_3","topic":"Removing while looping forwards","text":"A loop runs i from 0 upward and calls list.remove(i) whenever an element should go. Why does it skip elements?","options":["remove(i) removes two elements whenever i is even","remove(i) deletes the element after position i","Removing shifts every later element one place left while i still advances","The loop reads size() only once, before the first iteration"],"correct":2,"explain":"The element that slides into position i is never examined, because i has already moved past it. Counting down from size() - 1 avoids the problem entirely, and removing inside a for-each throws ConcurrentModificationException instead."},{"id":"fs_z7_15_4","topic":"What a for-each variable can and cannot do","text":"Iterating an ArrayList<Student> with for (Student s : roll), what can the loop variable s do?","options":["It copies the whole object, so any field change is discarded at the end of the iteration","Assigning a new Student to s replaces that element in the list","It can change the object's fields through its methods, but assigning to s does not replace the element","It can neither change fields nor replace elements, because it is a copy of the object"],"correct":2,"explain":"s is a copy of the reference, not a copy of the object, so method calls reach the very object stored in the list. Assigning to s only repoints the local copy; replacing an element requires roll.set(i, ...)."},{"id":"zf2402","topic":"","text":"ArrayList<Part> parts = new ArrayList<Part>(); parts.add(new Part("Bolt")); The list stores:","options":["a reference to the Part object","a copy of the Part","the String "Bolt"","a primitive"],"correct":0,"explain":"Collections of objects hold references, same as everywhere else.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2403","topic":"","text":"parts.get(0).getName() does what?","options":["names the list","first Part fetched, then its name","creates a Part","fails to compile"],"correct":1,"explain":"Chained access: element first, then its method.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2404","topic":"","text":"Editing an object AFTER adding it to the list, p.setQty(9):","options":["does not affect the list copy","throws","is visible through the list","duplicates p"],"correct":2,"explain":"The list holds a reference to p, not a snapshot.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2405","topic":"","text":"Looping to total quantities: for (Part p : parts) total += p.getQty(); computes:","options":["the largest quantity","the list size","nothing","the sum across every stored Part"],"correct":3,"explain":"For-each over object lists is the everyday inventory-total pattern.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2406","topic":"","text":"Finding a Part by name in the list uses:","options":["a loop with getName().equals(target)","parts.get(name)","parts.find(name) built-in","== on names"],"correct":0,"explain":"Search by field is a manual scan (with equals for the strings).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2407","topic":"","text":"parts.remove(0) removes:","options":["the object from all of memory instantly","the first reference from the list","the last Part","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"List membership and object existence are separate things.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2408","topic":"","text":"A list of 3 Bolt Parts created with THREE new calls holds:","options":["one object thrice","zero objects","three distinct objects","a compile error"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each new is a fresh instance, however similar the state.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2409","topic":"","text":"Adding the SAME Part object twice, parts.add(p); parts.add(p);:","options":["is a compile error","copies p once","merges to one entry","two entries aliasing one object"],"correct":3,"explain":"Editing p changes "both" entries - they are the same object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2410","topic":"","text":"ArrayList<Part> beats Part[] when:","options":["the count is fixed forever","parts are primitives","never","the part count changes at runtime"],"correct":3,"explain":"Dynamic membership is exactly the ArrayList use case.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2411","topic":"","text":"Printing every part: for (Part p : parts) System.out.println(p.getName() + ": " + p.getQty()); The loop variable p is:","options":["each stored reference in turn","a copy of each object","an index","the list itself"],"correct":0,"explain":"p aliases the current element - method calls act on the real object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2412","topic":"","text":"An inventory report method should take the list as:","options":["a global","a parameter, like any reference","a String","it cannot be passed"],"correct":1,"explain":"Lists pass like any reference - the report method works on the caller’s data.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_16":[{"id":"zya7_16_0","topic":"ADT meaning","text":"An abstract data type is defined by:","options":["The exact memory layout that its elements occupy","The specific class that implements it in Java","The primitive types the language happens to provide","Its operations and their behaviour, apart from implementation"],"correct":3,"explain":"You use add, get and remove without knowing an array sits underneath."},{"id":"zya7_16_1","topic":"ArrayList as an ADT","text":"ArrayList implements the List ADT, which guarantees:","options":["Unique elements","Sorted order","Constant-time insertion anywhere","Ordered elements accessible by index, with duplicates allowed"],"correct":3,"explain":"Sets forbid duplicates; lists do not."},{"id":"zya7_16_2","topic":"Implementation hiding","text":"Because ArrayList is an ADT you can:","options":["Modify its capacity field","Skip using methods","Access its internal array directly","Switch to LinkedList by changing one declaration, if you coded to List"],"correct":3,"explain":"List<String> x = new ArrayList<>(); makes the swap a one-line change."},{"id":"zya7_16_3","topic":"Core list operations","text":"The essential List operations are:","options":["add, get, set, remove, size, and contains","push, pop, peek, and isEmpty","put, get, keySet, and containsKey","open, read, write, and close"],"correct":0,"explain":"Together these define what a list can do."},{"id":"zya7_16_4","topic":"get versus indexOf","text":"list.get(2) returns:","options":["2","The size","The index of 2","The element at index 2"],"correct":3,"explain":"indexOf goes the other direction — element to index."},{"id":"zya7_16_5","topic":"set versus add","text":"list.set(1, "X") does what?","options":["Appends X","Removes index 1","Inserts X at index 1, shifting others","Replaces the element at index 1"],"correct":3,"explain":"add(1, "X") is the inserting version."},{"id":"zya7_16_6","topic":"remove ambiguity","text":"On an ArrayList&lt;Integer>, remove(1) removes:","options":["Both","Nothing","The value 1","The element at index 1"],"correct":3,"explain":"The int overload means index. To remove the value, call remove(Integer.valueOf(1)) — a genuine API trap."},{"id":"zya7_16_7","topic":"Capacity vs size","text":"An ArrayList's size is:","options":["The number of elements it currently holds","The capacity it was allocated with","The index of its last element","The number of bytes it occupies"],"correct":0,"explain":"Capacity is internal bookkeeping and grows automatically."},{"id":"zya7_16_8","topic":"Why an ADT matters","text":"Programming against the ADT rather than the implementation:","options":["Makes the program run measurably faster","Removes the need to test the implementation","Guarantees only one implementation can exist","Makes code easier to change and reason about"],"correct":3,"explain":"The same reasoning behind private fields, one level up."},{"id":"zya7_16_9","topic":"contains","text":"list.contains(x) is:","options":["Always false","Only for Strings","O(1)","O(n)"],"correct":3,"explain":"It also relies on equals, so custom classes must override it for contains to work as expected. It scans."},{"id":"fs_z7_16_1","topic":"Inverse operations","text":"Which List operation takes a VALUE and returns a POSITION, giving -1 when the value is absent?","options":["set(i, x)","indexOf(x)","get(i)","contains(x)"],"correct":1,"explain":"indexOf and get are inverses: indexOf takes a value and returns a position, while get takes a position and returns a value. contains answers the same search with true or false rather than an index."},{"id":"fs_z7_16_2","topic":"set replaces, add inserts","text":"What does this print?

ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("a");
list.add("b");
list.set(1, "X");
System.out.println(list + " " + list.size());
list.add(1, "Y");
System.out.println(list + " " + list.size());","options":["[a, X] 2
[a, Y] 2","[a, X] 2
[a, Y, X] 3","[a, b, X] 3
[a, Y, b, X] 4","[a, X] 2
[a, X, Y] 3"],"correct":1,"explain":"set(i, x) overwrites the element already at i and leaves the size alone, while add(i, x) inserts at i and shifts the rest right, so the size grows by one. Only one of the two ever changes size()."},{"id":"fs_z7_16_3","topic":"Costs behind the ADT","text":"Why is remove(i) on an ArrayList O(n) while get(i) is O(1)?","options":["get results are cached by the list and remove results are not","get indexes straight into the backing array, but remove must shift every later element down one place","remove has to search the list for the value before deleting it","remove always allocates a brand new backing array"],"correct":1,"explain":"Direct indexing into the backing array is a constant-time calculation, but closing the gap left by a removal touches everything after it. That shifting cost is exactly why LinkedList is attractive for heavy insertion and deletion."},{"id":"fs_z7_16_4","topic":"Size versus capacity","text":"An ArrayList's backing array is often larger than the number of elements stored. What does size() report?","options":["The total number of add() calls ever made","The number of elements currently in the list","The length of the backing array","The index of the last element"],"correct":1,"explain":"Capacity is an implementation detail the ADT deliberately hides; size() reports only how many elements the list holds. The index of the last element is size() - 1, and removals mean the count of add() calls proves nothing."},{"id":"zf2413","topic":"","text":"An ADT (abstract data type) is defined by:","options":["its source code","its memory layout","its operations, not its implementation","its speed alone"],"correct":2,"explain":"A list is add/get/remove/size behaving list-ishly - however it is built inside.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2414","topic":"","text":"ArrayList’s name reveals its implementation:","options":["a linked chain","a tree","a file","a resizable ARRAY underneath"],"correct":3,"explain":"Array-backed list: contiguous storage with automatic reallocation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2415","topic":"","text":"When an ArrayList’s internal array fills, adding another element:","options":["allocates a bigger array and copies","fails","overwrites slot 0","throws"],"correct":0,"explain":"The oversize-array grow dance, automated.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2416","topic":"","text":"list.indexOf(9) on [4, 9, 9] returns:","options":["2","1 - the FIRST match","both indexes","-1"],"correct":1,"explain":"indexOf scans from the front and stops at the first hit.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2417","topic":"","text":"list.indexOf(99) when absent returns:","options":["0","null","-1","an exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"Same not-found convention as String.indexOf.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2418","topic":"","text":"list.isEmpty() is equivalent to:","options":["list == null","list.get(0) == null","list.length == 0","list.size() == 0"],"correct":3,"explain":"Empty means zero elements - null means no list at all.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2419","topic":"","text":"list.clear() does what?","options":["removes every element, leaving size 0","deletes the list variable","frees the JVM","removes only the first"],"correct":0,"explain":"The list survives, emptied.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2420","topic":"","text":"Inserting mid-list, list.add(1, 42), does what to later elements?","options":["shifts them right by one","overwrites index 1","drops them","nothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Positional add makes room; size grows by one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2421","topic":"","text":"Removing from the FRONT of a large ArrayList is relatively costly because:","options":["the list re-sorts","every later element shifts left one slot","memory is freed slowly","it is not costly"],"correct":1,"explain":"Array-backed lists pay for front edits - an implementation fact peeking through the ADT.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2422","topic":"","text":"Coding against the list ADT (add/get/size) rather than array details means:","options":["slower thinking","more bugs","your logic outlives the implementation","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Program to the interface - the ADT idea in one sentence.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2423","topic":"","text":"Which is an ADT-level question (not implementation)?","options":["how big is the spare capacity?","where is the array in memory?","when does reallocation trigger?","does remove(0) keep the rest in order?"],"correct":3,"explain":"Behavior questions are ADT questions; layout questions are implementation.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_17":[{"id":"zya7_17_0","topic":"Class documentation","text":"A class-level Javadoc comment states:","options":["Each parameter of every method in the class","The exceptions each method is able to throw","The order the fields are stored in memory","What the object represents and any rule it guarantees"],"correct":3,"explain":""Represents an account whose balance can never fall below zero" tells a reader the invariant."},{"id":"zya7_17_1","topic":"@author tag","text":"@author documents:","options":["Who wrote the class","The parameters","The return value","The class purpose"],"correct":0,"explain":"Along with @version, it is class-level metadata."},{"id":"zya7_17_2","topic":"Documenting an invariant","text":"Recording that a field is never negative:","options":["Makes the compiler reject any negative assignment","Tells users what the class guarantees so they need not re-check","Converts negative values to zero automatically","Is required before the field can be made private"],"correct":1,"explain":"The documentation states the promise; the code must enforce it."},{"id":"zya7_17_3","topic":"Abstraction through docs","text":"Good class documentation lets someone:","options":["Use the class without reading its implementation","Modify the implementation without reading it","Skip writing any tests for the class","Access the private fields from outside"],"correct":0,"explain":"That is the practical meaning of abstraction."},{"id":"zya7_17_4","topic":"Documenting private helpers","text":"Private methods:","options":["Must carry full Javadoc, exactly like public ones","Need no comment of any kind, ever","Are documented in the class comment instead","Benefit from a brief comment but are not part of the contract"],"correct":3,"explain":"They serve maintainers rather than users."},{"id":"zya7_17_5","topic":"Keeping docs current","text":"Documentation that contradicts the code:","options":["Is worse than none","Prevents compiling","Is auto-corrected","Is harmless"],"correct":0,"explain":"Stale docs cause bugs by being believed. It misleads."},{"id":"zya7_17_6","topic":"Where the docs live","text":"Class documentation goes:","options":["Immediately below the class declaration","Immediately above the class declaration","At the very bottom of the source file","Inside the constructor, on the first line"],"correct":1,"explain":"Above the declaration, below the package and import statements."},{"id":"zya7_17_7","topic":"What not to document","text":"Documentation should avoid:","options":["Restating obvious syntax line by line","Describing what the method guarantees","Naming the valid range of each parameter","Explaining why an unusual choice was made"],"correct":0,"explain":"Explain intent and constraints, not mechanics the reader can see."},{"id":"zyf7_17_0","topic":"Contract versus implementation","text":"Class documentation should describe:","options":["The algorithm used","What the class guarantees","The line count","Private fields"],"correct":1,"explain":"Documenting internals ties you to them. Internals can change freely."},{"id":"zyf7_17_1","topic":"Stale documentation","text":"Documentation contradicting the code is:","options":["Harmless","Worse than none","A compile error","Auto-updated"],"correct":1,"explain":"It causes bugs by being believed. Readers act on it."},{"id":"fs_z7_17_1","topic":"Reading a method signature","text":"The API documentation shows public boolean add(E e). What does the boolean result tell the caller?","options":["Whether the collection changed as a result of the call","Whether the backing array still had spare capacity","Whether the argument was null","The index the new element ended up at"],"correct":0,"explain":"The documented meaning of the return value is that the collection changed, which matters for collections such as sets that refuse duplicates. An index would need an int return type, not a boolean."},{"id":"fs_z7_17_2","topic":"The throws clause","text":"Which part of a method's documentation tells you what can go wrong at run time?","options":["The Throws clause","The @author tag","The return type","The parameter list"],"correct":0,"explain":"The throws clause lists the exceptions the method may raise, such as NullPointerException for an element a list will not accept. The return type tells you what comes back, not what can fail."},{"id":"fs_z7_17_3","topic":"Type parameters in the docs","text":"In documentation such as public boolean add(E e), what does E stand for?","options":["A type parameter standing for whatever element type the collection was declared to hold","An enumeration constant defined by the class","A placeholder that accepts primitive types only","The exception the method throws"],"correct":0,"explain":"E, T and the pair K, V are type parameters, filled in when you declare the collection: for ArrayList<String>, E is String. They are why a list declared to hold Strings will not accept an Integer."},{"id":"fs_z7_17_4","topic":"Documenting the contract","text":"A public method is best documented by describing:","options":["The contract","The loop structure and local variables inside it","How long the method takes to run on the developer's machine","The order in which the fields were declared"],"correct":0,"explain":""Returns the average" survives a rewrite, while "loops through the array adding to a total" becomes a lie the moment the storage changes. Documentation that contradicts the code is worse than none, because it is trusted. What it guarantees to callers, including the invariants that always hold."},{"id":"zf2424","topic":"","text":"A class’s Javadoc page lists:","options":["constructors, methods, and summaries","its private source","its bytecode","its authorship contract"],"correct":0,"explain":"The generated page is the class’s public manual.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2425","topic":"","text":"Class-level Javadoc is written:","options":["inside the constructor","immediately above the class declaration","in a separate file","below the class"],"correct":1,"explain":"/** Represents one inventory part... */ public class Part { }","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2426","topic":"","text":"The constructor summary of a documented class tells users:","options":["how to delete objects","the field layout","the available ways to create instances","the JVM flags"],"correct":2,"explain":"Creation options are the first thing a user needs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2427","topic":"","text":"@author and @version tags:","options":["change behavior","are required to compile","set permissions","record the writer and revision"],"correct":3,"explain":"Metadata tags for the generated page.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2428","topic":"","text":"Reading the docs for an unfamiliar class, start with:","options":["the class description and method summary","the longest method","private members","the imports"],"correct":0,"explain":"Purpose first, capabilities second, details on demand.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2429","topic":"","text":"Documenting a class invariant like "inventory always 0..maxInventory":","options":["is impossible","states what the class always guarantees","changes the fields","is only for tests"],"correct":1,"explain":"Invariants are the strongest, most useful facts a doc can state.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2430","topic":"","text":"Javadoc for a getter should state:","options":["the loop inside","what the value means (and units if any)","nothing - getters are obvious","the field’s memory size"],"correct":1,"explain":""Returns the current inventory count" beats "gets inventory".","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2431","topic":"","text":"Generating docs for YOUR classes uses the same javadoc tool as:","options":["a different paid tool","the compiler only","the official Java API documentation","no tool exists"],"correct":2,"explain":"Your Part page can look exactly like the String page.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2432","topic":"","text":"A user of your documented class should NOT need:","options":["the method summaries","the constructor list","the class description","to read the class’s source code"],"correct":3,"explain":"Docs succeed when the interface story is complete.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2433","topic":"","text":"{@code new Part("Bolt")} inside Javadoc renders:","options":["as code-formatted text in the page","as an executed example","as an error","invisibly"],"correct":0,"explain":"Inline code markup keeps examples readable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2434","topic":"","text":"Keeping Javadoc UP TO DATE matters because:","options":["the compiler checks them","wrong docs beat no docs at misleading","old docs delete themselves","dates expire"],"correct":1,"explain":"A doc promising validation that was removed is a trap - update docs with the code.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_18":[{"id":"zya7_18_0","topic":"Passing an object","text":"Passing an object to a method copies:","options":["The whole object","The reference","Nothing","Only the fields"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is why the method can change the object's state. Both point to one object."},{"id":"zya7_18_1","topic":"Modifying through a parameter","text":"void raise(Employee e){ e.setPay(100); } affects the caller's object:","options":["No","Yes — same object","Only if returned","Only if final"],"correct":1,"explain":"Field changes are visible to everyone holding that reference."},{"id":"zya7_18_2","topic":"Reassigning the parameter","text":"void f(Employee e){ e = new Employee(); } affects the caller:","options":["Yes, the caller's variable points to the new object","No — only the local reference is repointed","Yes, but only the fields set in the constructor","It is a compile error to reassign a parameter"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is the exact boundary between pass-by-value-of-reference and true pass-by-reference."},{"id":"zya7_18_3","topic":"Passing null","text":"Passing null and then calling a method on the parameter:","options":["Returns null","Throws NullPointerException","Is a compile error","Does nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Guard when null is a legitimate possibility."},{"id":"zya7_18_4","topic":"Immutable parameter","text":"Passing a String to a method that "changes" it:","options":["Modifies the caller's String","Cannot modify it","Throws","Requires final"],"correct":1,"explain":"The method can only build and return a new String. Strings are immutable."},{"id":"zya7_18_5","topic":"Defensive copy","text":"Returning a reference to a private mutable field:","options":["Is safe, since the field is declared private","Returns a copy automatically for private fields","Lets callers modify internal state, defeating encapsulation","Fails to compile unless the field is public"],"correct":2,"explain":"Returning a copy preserves the guarantee the class is supposed to enforce."},{"id":"zya7_18_6","topic":"Returning objects","text":"A method can return an object reference:","options":["No, methods may return only primitive values","Yes, and the caller then shares that object","Yes, but the caller receives an independent copy","Only when the object is declared final"],"correct":1,"explain":"Whether that is safe depends on whether the object is mutable and shared."},{"id":"zya7_18_7","topic":"Comparing object parameters","text":"Comparing two object parameters with == tests:","options":["Their contents","Whether they are the same object","Their types","Their sizes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Use equals for content comparison, and override it in your class for that to be meaningful."},{"id":"zya7_18_8","topic":"Array of objects parameter","text":"Passing Employee[] to a method allows:","options":["Reading the elements but never modifying them","Modifying the objects but not the array slots","Neither, since the array is copied when passed","Reading and modifying both the array slots and the objects"],"correct":3,"explain":"The array and its objects are all shared."},{"id":"zya7_18_9","topic":"this as an argument","text":"Passing this to another method:","options":["Passes a copy of the current object","Passes only the object's public fields","Passes a reference to the current object","Is a compile error outside a constructor"],"correct":2,"explain":"Common when an object registers itself with something else."},{"id":"fs_z7_18_1","topic":"Mutating versus reassigning a parameter","text":"The class also declares:

static void promote(Student s) { s.setScore(100); }
static void swapOut(Student s) { s = new Student("Other", 0); }

What does this print?

Student ada = new Student("Ada", 50);
promote(ada);
System.out.println(ada.getScore());
swapOut(ada);
System.out.println(ada.getName());","options":["50
Other","50
Ada","100
Other","100
Ada"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java copies the reference, so promote reaches the caller's object and its change is visible. swapOut only repoints its own local copy, which cannot rebind the caller's variable."},{"id":"fs_z7_18_2","topic":"Passing a list to a method","text":"The class also declares:

static void addOne(ArrayList<String> list) { list.add("new"); }
static void replace(ArrayList<String> list) { list = new ArrayList<>(); }

What does this print?

ArrayList<String> items = new ArrayList<>();
items.add("a");
addOne(items);
replace(items);
System.out.println(items);","options":["[a]","[]","[new]","[a, new]"],"correct":3,"explain":"addOne mutates the very list the caller passed, so the addition survives. replace assigns a fresh list to its local parameter, which leaves the caller's variable pointing at the original."},{"id":"fs_z7_18_3","topic":"Defensive copies","text":"A class stores a private ArrayList<String> items, and its getter returns items directly. What is the problem, and the fix?","options":["The getter must be static so that the field cannot be reached","The list is copied on return, so the caller's changes are lost; return the field itself","A public method may not return a collection; return an array instead","Callers can modify the private list through the returned reference; return new ArrayList<>(items) instead"],"correct":3,"explain":"Handing out the reference defeats encapsulation just as thoroughly as a public field would, because the caller can add and remove at will. Returning a copy gives the caller the data without giving away control of the field."},{"id":"fs_z7_18_4","topic":"Giving the caller a different object","text":"A method must give its caller a different object rather than change the one passed in. What must it do?","options":["Declare the parameter final so the caller sees the replacement","Declare the method static so it can reach the caller's variable","Assign the new object to the parameter, which rebinds the caller's variable","Return the new object, because assigning to the parameter changes only the method's local copy"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java is always pass by value, and for an object the value copied is the reference, so reassigning a parameter is invisible outside the method. Returning the object is the only way to hand back a different one."},{"id":"zf2435","topic":"","text":"static void restock(Part p) { p.setQty(p.getQty() + 10); } After restock(bolt), bolt’s quantity:","options":["is unchanged","is 10 exactly","increased by 10","is null"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reference parameter, shared object, visible mutation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2436","topic":"","text":"Object parameters make methods like transfer(Account from, Account to):","options":["read-only","copy both accounts","illegal","able to modify both passed objects"],"correct":3,"explain":"Two references in, two updated objects out - no returns needed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2437","topic":"","text":"static void reset(Part p) { p = new Part(); } After reset(bolt), bolt:","options":["is exactly as before","is a fresh Part","is null","is deleted"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reassigning the parameter cannot rebind the caller’s variable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2438","topic":"","text":"The rule "Java is pass-by-value" applies to references how?","options":["objects are copied fully","the reference itself is the value copied","it does not apply","references pass by name"],"correct":1,"explain":"A copy of the arrow, both aiming at one object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2439","topic":"","text":"A method receiving a Part it must NOT modify relies on:","options":["automatic protection","the final keyword blocking setters","discipline or a defensive copy","encryption"],"correct":2,"explain":"final on the parameter blocks reassignment only, not mutation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2440","topic":"","text":"Passing a String and "changing" it inside a method:","options":["alters it","depends on length","cannot alter the caller’s string","throws"],"correct":2,"explain":"Every String "change" creates a new object; the caller’s reference still sees the original.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2441","topic":"","text":"static boolean sameName(Part a, Part b) { return a.getName().equals(b.getName()); } compares:","options":["identity of a and b","list membership","quantities","the two objects’ name contents"],"correct":3,"explain":"Field-level comparison through the references.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2442","topic":"","text":"Passing null to restock(Part p):","options":["throws NullPointerException at p.","creates a Part","skips silently","is a compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Methods that accept objects should document or check their null policy.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2443","topic":"","text":"A method taking (Part p, int qty) receives:","options":["two copies","a shared reference AND a private int","two references","two objects"],"correct":1,"explain":"Mixed parameter kinds follow their own rules side by side.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2444","topic":"","text":"Returning the SAME reference it received, return p;:","options":["copies the object","is illegal","gives the caller another alias","nulls p"],"correct":2,"explain":"Now caller-variable and returned-value both name one instance.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2445","topic":"","text":"Why do object parameters make Java programs efficient at scale?","options":["objects shrink when passed","the JVM parallelizes","they do not","giant objects pass as small references"],"correct":3,"explain":"A million-element list passes as cheaply as an int.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u7/z7_21":[{"id":"zya7_21_0","topic":"Rate as a field","text":"Storing hourlyRate as a field rather than a parameter means:","options":["It must be passed to every method that uses it","It is shared by every employee in the program","It can only be read inside the constructor","It belongs to the employee and is stored once"],"correct":3,"explain":"weeklyPay(hours) then needs only the hours — the improvement over the Unit 6 version."},{"id":"zya7_21_1","topic":"Array of employees","text":"An array of Employee objects replaces:","options":["A single array holding every attribute in turn","A HashMap keyed on each employee's name","Several parallel arrays that could fall out of sync","One method per employee in the program"],"correct":2,"explain":"Name and rate travel together inside the object, so they cannot desynchronize."},{"id":"zya7_21_2","topic":"Constructor use","text":"new Employee("Ana", 20.0) does what?","options":["Declares a reference without creating anything","Creates an object with those field values","Copies an existing Employee into a new variable","Sets the fields of an Employee created earlier"],"correct":1,"explain":"The constructor initializes the new object's state."},{"id":"zya7_21_3","topic":"Private rate","text":"Declaring hourlyRate private means:","options":["Outside code must go through a method to read or change it","The field cannot be changed after construction","The field is shared by every Employee object","Subclasses inherit it and may read it directly"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which lets the class reject a negative rate."},{"id":"zya7_21_4","topic":"Overtime location","text":"The over-40-hours rule belongs:","options":["In main","In the Employee class's pay method","In a separate array","In the constructor"],"correct":1,"explain":"The rule lives with the data it applies to, so every caller gets it right."},{"id":"zya7_21_5","topic":"Totalling payroll","text":"Summing pay for all employees uses:","options":["A single call to weeklyPay on the array itself","A loop over the array reading the rate field","A loop over the array calling weeklyPay on each object","Sorting the array and reading the last total"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each object computes its own pay; the loop accumulates the total."},{"id":"zya7_21_6","topic":"Accessor for the name","text":"Reading the private name field requires:","options":["e.name","e.getName()","Employee.name","A cast"],"correct":1,"explain":"The accessor is the public read path."},{"id":"zya7_21_7","topic":"Adding a bonus","text":"Adding bonus calculation means:","options":["Rewriting every array loop","Adding a method to Employee","Changing main only","Adding another array"],"correct":1,"explain":"New behaviour attaches to the class that owns the data."},{"id":"zya7_21_8","topic":"Formatting output","text":"Printing name and pay aligned uses:","options":["println only","printf with %s and %.2f","print with commas","String concatenation only"],"correct":1,"explain":"printf("%s: %.2f%n", e.getName(), e.weeklyPay(45))."},{"id":"zya7_21_9","topic":"Comparing to Unit 6","text":"The classes version improves on the methods version by:","options":["Reducing the number of source files the project needs","Making the calculations run measurably faster at runtime","Keeping each employee's data together and attaching behaviour to it","Removing the need to test the calculations separately"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is the same computation with the state properly owned."},{"id":"fs_z7_21_1","topic":"Overtime logic inside the class","text":"Employee stores a name and an hourly rate, and payFor(hours) returns hours * rate up to 40 hours, or 40 * rate plus time and a half on the excess beyond 40. What does this print?

