 stored flag into the question users ask.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2470","topic":"","text":"ArrayList<Domain> registry with a search method finds a domain by:","options":["registry.get(query)","a loop using getName().equals(query)","== on names","indexOf(query) directly"],"correct":1,"explain":"Object lists are searched by field, with equals for the strings.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2471","topic":"","text":"If the search finds no match, a common return is:","options":["a random domain","the whole list","null (documented!) or an "empty" result","an int"],"correct":2,"explain":"Callers must know the not-found convention - document it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2472","topic":"","text":"public void claim() { taken = true; } after d.claim(), d.isAvailable() returns:","options":["true","null","error","false"],"correct":3,"explain":"The mutator flipped the state the accessor reports.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2473","topic":"","text":"Checking then claiming, if (d.isAvailable()) d.claim();:","options":["the check-then-act pattern","is illegal","bypasses the class","loops forever"],"correct":0,"explain":"All state changes flow through the class’s methods.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2474","topic":"","text":"The Domain class validating its name in the constructor means:","options":["names are optional","illegal-name Domains can never exist","validation runs weekly","main validates instead"],"correct":1,"explain":"Constructor validation makes invalid instances unrepresentable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2475","topic":"","text":"Compared with parallel arrays (names[], taken[]), the Domain list:","options":["is misaligned more often","uses no memory","cannot get its fields misaligned","loses data"],"correct":2,"explain":"The class fuses what parallel arrays merely juxtapose.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2476","topic":"","text":"A method suggestAlternatives(String base) on the registry class would:","options":["delete taken domains","rename the registry","crash","generate and check name variants"],"correct":3,"explain":"Behavior over the collection lives naturally with the collection.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2477","topic":"","text":"Unit tests for Domain would cover:","options":["every behavior and state change","printing only","the internet","main"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every public behavior, each state transition.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2606","topic":"","text":"After Domain d = new Domain("shop.com"); the call d.getName() returns:","options":["Domain","d","shop.com","null"],"correct":2,"explain":"The constructor stored the name; the getter reads it back.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u8/z8_1":[{"id":"zya8_1_0","topic":"Memory management meaning","text":"Memory management refers to:","options":["Choosing which variables to declare as static","Deciding how many source files a program needs","Allocating memory for data and reclaiming it when unneeded","Compressing the program so it occupies less space"],"correct":2,"explain":"In Java allocation happens on new and reclamation is automatic via garbage collection."},{"id":"zya8_1_1","topic":"Java vs manual management","text":"Compared with C, Java:","options":["Reclaims unused objects automatically","Never allocates memory","Uses no heap","Requires explicit free() calls"],"correct":0,"explain":"No free or delete exists, which eliminates a whole class of bugs — but not all memory problems."},{"id":"zya8_1_2","topic":"Memory leak in Java","text":"A memory leak can still occur when:","options":["Unneeded objects remain reachable from a live reference","The garbage collector runs less often than usual","An object is set to null before it is used","A method allocates more locals than it returns"],"correct":0,"explain":"A collection that keeps growing is the classic case — the objects are unused but not unreachable."},{"id":"zya8_1_3","topic":"Allocation trigger","text":"Memory for an object is allocated when:","options":["new is executed","The class is compiled","The method returns","The variable is declared"],"correct":0,"explain":"Declaration only creates a reference variable, which starts as null."},{"id":"zya8_1_4","topic":"Two memory regions","text":"Java divides runtime memory mainly into:","options":["Stack and heap","Registers and RAM","Code and data","Cache and disk"],"correct":0,"explain":"Locals and frames on the stack, objects on the heap."},{"id":"zya8_1_5","topic":"Object lifetime","text":"An object is eligible for reclamation when:","options":["No live reference to it remains","It is set to 0","The program ends","Its method finishes"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reachability, not scope, is the criterion."},{"id":"zya8_1_6","topic":"Cost of allocation","text":"Creating objects in a tight loop:","options":["Has a real cost in allocation and later collection","Is free, since the collector reclaims them instantly","Fails to compile if the loop count is large","Reuses the same object on every iteration"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why StringBuilder beats repeated String concatenation in a loop."},{"id":"zya8_1_7","topic":"null and memory","text":"Setting a reference to null:","options":["Immediately frees the memory that the object occupied","Deletes every other reference pointing at that object","Has no effect at all on how garbage collection works","Removes one reference; the object is collectable if it was the last"],"correct":3,"explain":"It only breaks a link; the collector decides when to reclaim."},{"id":"zya8_1_8","topic":"OutOfMemoryError","text":"Running out of heap space causes:","options":["OutOfMemoryError at runtime","Silent truncation","A warning","A compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is an Error rather than an Exception, and is usually not something to catch."},{"id":"zya8_1_9","topic":"Programmer responsibility","text":"In Java the programmer is responsible for:","options":["Not holding references longer than needed","Allocating the heap","Running the collector","Calling free on every object"],"correct":0,"explain":"Dropping references you no longer need is the practical form of memory management in Java."},{"id":"fs_z8_1_1","topic":"Stack values vs heap objects","text":"What does this program print?

static void bump(int n) { n++; }
static void bump(int[] a) { a[0]++; }

int n = 1;
int[] a = { 1 };
bump(n);
bump(a);
System.out.println(n + " " + a[0]);","options":["1 2","2 2","1 1","2 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"The int parameter is a copy of the value held in the caller's stack frame, so n++ changes only the copy. The array parameter is a copy of the reference, but it still points at the same array on the heap, so a[0]++ is visible to the caller."},{"id":"fs_z8_1_2","topic":"Allocation","text":"In Java, which operation reserves storage on the heap for a brand new object?","options":["The new operator","A call to malloc","A call to free","The garbage collector"],"correct":0,"explain":"new performs the allocation; the garbage collector only reclaims. malloc and free belong to C, where release is the programmer's job."},{"id":"fs_z8_1_3","topic":"Retained references","text":"A long-running program adds an object to a static List on every request and never removes anything. What is the most likely eventual result?","options":["An OutOfMemoryError, because every added object stays reachable through the static List","Nothing unusual, because the collector reclaims objects the program has stopped using","A StackOverflowError, because the List grows deeper with every call","A compiler error, because a static field may not hold a growing collection"],"correct":0,"explain":"The collector reclaims only unreachable objects. A static field is a root, so everything the List holds stays reachable and the heap fills up."},{"id":"fs_z8_1_4","topic":"Manual vs automatic memory","text":"Which memory bug found in C and C++ cannot happen in Java?","options":["Using a pointer to memory that has already been freed","Holding on to data that is no longer needed","Allocating more memory than the machine has","Reading past the end of an array"],"correct":0,"explain":"The collector never reclaims an object that is still reachable, so a reference can never dangle. Java can still exhaust memory by keeping unneeded objects reachable, and array bounds are checked at run time."},{"id":"zf2478","topic":"","text":"Objects created with new are stored in:","options":["the stack","the heap","the source file","the CPU registers"],"correct":1,"explain":"The heap is the region for dynamically allocated objects.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2479","topic":"","text":"The reference variable pointing at a heap object typically lives:","options":["on the heap beside the object","in the class file","on the stack, pointing into the heap","nowhere"],"correct":2,"explain":"Small handle on the stack, big object on the heap.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2480","topic":"","text":"Memory for an object is allocated:","options":["at declaration","at compile time","when new runs","when main ends"],"correct":2,"explain":"Part p; reserves nothing for an object; new Part() does.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2481","topic":"","text":"Java programmers do NOT free memory manually because:","options":["memory is infinite","the OS forbids it","objects never die","the GC reclaims unreachable objects"],"correct":3,"explain":"No free()/delete - reachability decides lifetime.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2482","topic":"","text":"The lifetime of a local int variable is bounded by:","options":["its method call (its stack frame)","the whole program","the heap","the class"],"correct":0,"explain":"Frame pops, local dies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2483","topic":"","text":"The lifetime of a heap object is bounded by:","options":["one method call","reachability","one statement","the constructor"],"correct":1,"explain":"Objects outlive the calls that created them, as long as references survive.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2484","topic":"","text":"new int[1000000] allocates:","options":["a million stack slots","disk space","one large block on the heap","nothing until used"],"correct":2,"explain":"Arrays are heap objects regardless of element type.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2485","topic":"","text":"Running out of heap memory produces:","options":["a compile error","silent truncation","StackOverflowError","OutOfMemoryError"],"correct":3,"explain":"Heap exhaustion and stack exhaustion are different errors.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2486","topic":"","text":"A memory "leak" in Java typically means:","options":["unused objects kept reachable","the GC is broken","freed memory reused","hardware failure"],"correct":0,"explain":"The collector cannot reclaim what your data structures still reference.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2487","topic":"","text":"Memory management matters even with GC because:","options":["GC handles everything perfectly","held references bloat the program","memory is unlimited","it does not matter"],"correct":1,"explain":"Clearing references (or scoping them tightly) lets the collector work.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2488","topic":"","text":"The two key memory regions discussed for Java programs are:","options":["the cloud and the disk","RAM and ROM","the stack and the heap","cache and register"],"correct":2,"explain":"Frames on the stack, objects on the heap - the core model.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u8/z8_2":[{"id":"zya8_2_0","topic":"Linked list node","text":"A linked list node typically holds:","options":["Data and its position index in the list","Data and a copy of the whole remaining list","Data and a reference to the next node","A reference to the next node only, with no data"],"correct":2,"explain":"That next reference is what chains the nodes together."},{"id":"zya8_2_1","topic":"Last node","text":"The final node's next reference is:","options":["A copy of the first","null","Itself","Undefined"],"correct":1,"explain":"null marks the end and is what loop conditions test for."},{"id":"zya8_2_2","topic":"Head reference","text":"The list is accessed through:","options":["An index","A reference to the first node","The last node","An array"],"correct":1,"explain":"Lose the head and the whole list becomes unreachable — and collectable."},{"id":"zya8_2_3","topic":"Traversal","text":"Walking a linked list uses:","options":["A loop from index 0 to the list's length","A recursive call on node.data until it is null","A binary search halving the list each pass","A loop following node = node.next until null"],"correct":3,"explain":"There is no index arithmetic; you must follow the chain."},{"id":"zya8_2_4","topic":"Access cost","text":"Reaching the nth element of a linked list is:","options":["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","Free"],"correct":1,"explain":"Nodes are scattered in memory, so there is no address to compute."},{"id":"zya8_2_5","topic":"Insertion at the front","text":"Adding a node at the head is:","options":["O(n)","O(1)","Impossible","Requires copying"],"correct":1,"explain":"No shifting, which is the linked list's advantage over an array. Create the node and repoint the head."},{"id":"zya8_2_6","topic":"Memory layout","text":"Linked list nodes are:","options":["Separate objects scattered on the heap","One contiguous block, exactly like an array","Stored on the stack with the method's locals","Copies of one shared node object"],"correct":0,"explain":"This is why arrays are more cache-friendly despite the same big-O for traversal."},{"id":"zya8_2_7","topic":"Node as a class","text":"A node is naturally represented as:","options":["An array of exactly two elements: the data then the next","A primitive that holds both values packed in together","An interface that every list element must implement","A small class with a data field and a next field of its own type"],"correct":3,"explain":"A class referring to itself is exactly what makes the chain possible."},{"id":"zya8_2_8","topic":"Losing a node","text":"Repointing a node's next without saving the old value:","options":["Reverses the remainder of the list automatically","Is a compile error; next cannot be reassigned","Can make the rest of the list unreachable","Has no effect, since next is copied on read"],"correct":2,"explain":"Order of pointer updates is where linked-list bugs live."},{"id":"zya8_2_9","topic":"Versus ArrayList","text":"A linked list is preferable when:","options":["Random access by index is frequent","Insertions and removals at the ends dominate","Data is sorted","Memory is scarce"],"correct":1,"explain":"For index-heavy work an array-backed list wins clearly."},{"id":"fs_z8_2_1","topic":"Traversing a linked list","text":"What does this program print?

static class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;
    Node(int v) { value = v; }
}

Node a = new Node(4);
a.next = new Node(8);
a.next.next = new Node(15);
int sum = 0;
for (Node cur = a; cur != null; cur = cur.next) {
    sum += cur.value;
}
System.out.println(sum);","options":["15","12","4","27"],"correct":3,"explain":"The loop starts at the head and follows next until it reaches null, visiting 4, 8 and 15, so the sum is 27. The null in the last node's next is what ends the traversal."},{"id":"fs_z8_2_2","topic":"Inserting at the head","text":"What does this program print?

static class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;
    Node(int v) { value = v; }
}

Node head = null;
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    Node n = new Node(i);
    n.next = head;
    head = n;
}
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (Node cur = head; cur != null; cur = cur.next) {
    sb.append(cur.value).append(" ");
}
System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());","options":["1 2 3","1 1 1","3 3 3","3 2 1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each new node points at the old head and then becomes the head, so inserting at the front reverses the order in which the values were created."},{"id":"fs_z8_2_3","topic":"Unlinking a node","text":"What does this program print?

static class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;
    Node(int v) { value = v; }
}

Node a = new Node(1);
a.next = new Node(2);
a.next.next = new Node(3);
a.next = a.next.next;
int count = 0;
for (Node cur = a; cur != null; cur = cur.next) {
    count++;
}
System.out.println(count);","options":["3","1","0","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Assigning a.next = a.next.next skips over the middle node, so the chain is 1 -> 3 -> null and the loop counts 2 nodes. The skipped node is now unreachable and becomes collectable."},{"id":"fs_z8_2_4","topic":"Losing the head","text":"A 100-node linked list is reachable only through the variable head. The program then executes head = null. What happens to the 100 nodes?","options":["Only the first node becomes unreachable; the other 99 still refer to each other","Nothing, because each node still stores a reference to the next one","The program throws a NullPointerException at the assignment","All 100 become unreachable and are eligible for garbage collection"],"correct":3,"explain":"The nodes refer to one another, but reachability is measured from live roots. With head gone there is no way in, so the whole chain is collectable. References between the nodes themselves do not keep them alive."},{"id":"zf2489","topic":"","text":"A basic linked-list node class contains:","options":["an array of nodes","only data","only a next reference","data plus a next-node reference"],"correct":3,"explain":"class Node { int data; Node next; } - the two-part cell.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2490","topic":"","text":"The END of a linked list is marked by:","options":["a special -1 value","the largest node","an exception","a next reference of null"],"correct":3,"explain":"null-next means nothing follows.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2491","topic":"","text":"Traversing a list uses the pattern:","options":["Node cur = head","for (int i = 0; i < list.length; i++)","head++","recursion only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Walk the arrows until null.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2492","topic":"","text":"head -> [3] -> [7] -> null. head.next.data is:","options":["3","7","null","an error"],"correct":1,"explain":"head.next is the second node; its data is 7.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2493","topic":"","text":"Inserting at the FRONT of a linked list:","options":["shifts every node","copies the list","newNode.next = head; head = newNode;","requires an index"],"correct":2,"explain":"No shifting - just two reference updates. Contrast with array front-insertion.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2494","topic":"","text":"A node object with NO references left pointing to it:","options":["stays forever","moves to the stack","re-links itself","becomes garbage-collectable"],"correct":3,"explain":"Unlinking is how list removal frees memory (eventually).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2495","topic":"","text":"Linked lists trade away arrays’ strength of:","options":["instant access by index","storing data","growing","being loopable"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reaching element 500 means walking 500 links.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2496","topic":"","text":"Linked lists WIN over arrays at:","options":["random access","cheap insert/remove without shifting","memory per element","simplicity"],"correct":1,"explain":"Re-aim two references vs shift half the array.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2497","topic":"","text":"head = null; for a whole list makes:","options":["only the first node garbage","the list reverse","every node unreachable","an exception"],"correct":2,"explain":"Cut the head reference and the chain follows it into collectability.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2498","topic":"","text":"Forgetting cur = cur.next in a traversal loop causes:","options":["a skip","an exception","end of list","an infinite loop on the same node"],"correct":3,"explain":"No progress along the chain - the linked-list equivalent of a stuck counter.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2607","topic":"","text":"head -> [5] -> [2] -> null. The value of head.data is:","options":["2","null","7","5"],"correct":3,"explain":"head references the first node, whose data field holds 5.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u8/z8_3":[{"id":"zya8_3_0","topic":"Stack contents","text":"The call stack holds:","options":["Every object the program has created","The class definitions loaded by the JVM","Method frames with parameters and local variables","The static fields shared across the program"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each call pushes a frame; each return pops one."},{"id":"zya8_3_1","topic":"Heap contents","text":"The heap holds:","options":["Return addresses","Local variables","Objects created with new","Method frames"],"correct":2,"explain":"Every object lives on the heap regardless of where the reference is stored."},{"id":"zya8_3_2","topic":"Reference vs object","text":"Employee e = new Employee(); places:","options":["The reference on the stack and the object on the heap","Both the reference and the object on the stack","Both the reference and the object on the heap","The object on the stack and the reference on the heap"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why passing e copies only the small reference, not the whole object."},{"id":"zya8_3_3","topic":"Local primitive","text":"int x = 5; inside a method stores x:","options":["On the heap, alongside the objects","In the class's static field area","In the constant pool of the class file","On the stack, in that method's frame"],"correct":3,"explain":"It disappears when the method returns."},{"id":"zya8_3_4","topic":"Field storage","text":"An instance field of an object is stored:","options":["In a register","On the stack","On the heap, inside the object","In the class file"],"correct":2,"explain":"It lives as long as the object does."},{"id":"zya8_3_5","topic":"Stack lifetime","text":"Stack memory is reclaimed:","options":["By the garbage collector, at times it chooses","Automatically when the method returns","Only when the program finally exits","When the reference is explicitly set to null"],"correct":1,"explain":"It is strictly last-in-first-out, so no collector is needed."},{"id":"zya8_3_6","topic":"Heap lifetime","text":"Heap memory is reclaimed:","options":["By the garbage collector once the object is unreachable","Automatically when the enclosing method returns","Immediately when a reference is set to null","Only when the program finally exits"],"correct":0,"explain":"Objects outlive the method that created them if a reference escapes."},{"id":"zya8_3_7","topic":"StackOverflowError","text":"Deep recursion exhausts:","options":["The cache","The heap","The stack","Disk space"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each call adds a frame; too many frames overflows the stack."},{"id":"zya8_3_8","topic":"Array storage","text":"int[] a = new int[1000]; stores the elements:","options":["In registers","On the stack","On the heap","In the class file"],"correct":2,"explain":"Arrays are objects, so a large array does not risk a stack overflow."},{"id":"zya8_3_9","topic":"Returning a local object","text":"Returning an object created inside a method is safe because:","options":["The object is copied into the caller's frame","The object is on the heap and survives the frame being popped","The compiler moves the object onto the stack","The collector is paused until the caller finishes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Only the local reference variable disappears."},{"id":"fs_z8_3_1","topic":"Reference on the stack, object on the heap","text":"What does this program print?

static class Box {
    int v;
}

Box b1 = new Box();
b1.v = 5;
Box b2 = b1;
b2.v = 9;
System.out.println(b1.v);","options":["0","14","9","5"],"correct":2,"explain":"b1 and b2 are two stack variables holding the same reference, so there is only one Box on the heap. Writing through b2 changes the object b1 also points at."},{"id":"fs_z8_3_2","topic":"Returning a locally created object","text":"What does this program print?

static int[] make() {
    int[] a = new int[3];
    a[0] = 7;
    return a;
}

int[] r = make();
System.out.println(r.length + " " + r[0]);","options":["3 0","It compiles but fails at run time because the array was destroyed when make() returned","3 7","0 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"The local variable a lived in make()'s stack frame and disappeared when the method returned, but the array itself was always on the heap. The returned reference keeps it alive."},{"id":"fs_z8_3_3","topic":"Frames and local variables","text":"While a recursive method has five calls in progress at once, where do the local variables of those five calls live?","options":["The locals are moved to the heap so the calls can share them","Only the deepest call has locals; the others are discarded until it returns","Each call has its own set of locals in its own stack frame","All five calls share one copy of each local variable"],"correct":2,"explain":"A new stack frame is pushed for every call, holding that call's parameters and locals. That per-call storage is exactly why deep recursion exhausts the stack."},{"id":"fs_z8_3_4","topic":"Comparing the two regions","text":"Compared with the heap, the call stack is:","options":["The same size, but reserved for objects rather than for primitives","Unlimited, which is why recursion can go as deep as it likes","Smaller and fixed in size, and reclaimed automatically when each method returns","Larger, and reclaimed only when the garbage collector runs"],"correct":2,"explain":"The stack is a small fixed region popped automatically on return; the heap is the larger region managed by the collector. That size difference is why StackOverflowError usually arrives long before OutOfMemoryError."},{"id":"zf2499","topic":"","text":"Each method call pushes onto the stack:","options":["a frame of locals and return point","a heap object","the whole class","the output"],"correct":0,"explain":"Frames stack up with nesting and pop on return.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2500","topic":"","text":"int n = 5; inside a method stores 5:","options":["on the heap","in the class file","on disk","in the method’s stack frame"],"correct":3,"explain":"Primitive locals are frame-resident.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2501","topic":"","text":"Part p = new Part(); splits across regions how?","options":["p (the reference) on the stack","both on the stack","both on the heap","p on heap, object on stack"],"correct":0,"explain":"The canonical picture of Java memory.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2502","topic":"","text":"When a method returns, its frame:","options":["moves to the heap","pops - all its locals vanish","persists","is garbage collected later"],"correct":1,"explain":"Stack cleanup is immediate and automatic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2503","topic":"","text":"An object created in a method can outlive the method if:","options":["it cannot","it is large","its reference escaped (returned/stored)","it is printed"],"correct":2,"explain":"Heap lifetime follows reachability, not the creating frame.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2504","topic":"","text":"Deep recursion overflows THE STACK because:","options":["objects fill the heap","loops allocate frames","println is slow","unfinished calls keep frames pushed"],"correct":3,"explain":"Frames accumulate until the stack limit - StackOverflowError.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2505","topic":"","text":"Which is on the HEAP: the array or the array reference variable arr?","options":["the array; arr is a stack local","both","neither","arr; the array is stacked"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same split as every object/reference pair.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2506","topic":"","text":"Two concurrent calls of the same method have:","options":["one shared frame","separate frames with separate locals","no frames","merged locals"],"correct":1,"explain":"Per-call frames are why recursion and reentrancy work.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2507","topic":"","text":"Stack allocation is fast because:","options":["the heap is slow on purpose","stacks are in cache always","it just moves the stack pointer","it is not fast"],"correct":2,"explain":"Push/pop discipline makes stack management nearly free.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2508","topic":"","text":"The garbage collector manages:","options":["both regions","the stack only","the CPU","the heap only"],"correct":3,"explain":"GC hunts unreachable heap objects; frames never need it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2608","topic":"","text":"A local double declared inside main lives on:","options":["the stack, in main’s frame","the heap","the disk","the garbage collector"],"correct":0,"explain":"Primitive locals are frame-resident; only objects go on the heap.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u8/z8_5":[{"id":"zya8_5_0","topic":"Scope ending","text":"When a local reference goes out of scope, the object it pointed to:","options":["Is freed immediately, before the method returns","Remains alive until the program terminates","Becomes eligible for collection if nothing else references it","Is copied into the caller's frame automatically"],"correct":2,"explain":"Eligible, not collected — timing is the collector's decision."},{"id":"zya8_5_1","topic":"Still referenced","text":"An object created in a method but stored in a field:","options":["Survives","Throws","Is copied","Is collected on return"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reachability, not the creating scope, determines lifetime. The field still references it."},{"id":"zya8_5_2","topic":"Reassigning a reference","text":"a = new Thing(); when a already pointed to an object:","options":["Makes the old object collectable if that was the last reference","Frees the old object's memory immediately","Is a compile error; a is already assigned","Copies the new object's fields into the old one"],"correct":0,"explain":"The old object is simply abandoned."},{"id":"zya8_5_3","topic":"Two references","text":"If two variables reference one object and one is set to null:","options":["The object survives","Both become null","A copy is made","The object is collected"],"correct":0,"explain":"All references must be gone. The other reference keeps it reachable."},{"id":"zya8_5_4","topic":"Collection in a list","text":"Objects added to a long-lived ArrayList:","options":["Stay reachable through the list","Are copied","Are freed","Are collected when the method returns"],"correct":0,"explain":"This is the standard accidental-retention pattern."},{"id":"zya8_5_5","topic":"Scope vs lifetime","text":"Scope and lifetime are:","options":["Different","Both compile-time concepts","Both runtime concepts","The same thing"],"correct":0,"explain":"For locals they usually coincide; for objects they routinely do not. Scope is where a name is visible, lifetime is how long an object exists."},{"id":"zya8_5_6","topic":"Loop-created objects","text":"Objects created inside a loop and not stored:","options":["Become collectable each iteration","Cause an error","Are reused","Accumulate forever"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why the allocation cost, not a leak, is the concern."},{"id":"zya8_5_7","topic":"Explicit collection","text":"System.gc() does what?","options":["Suggests collection; the JVM may ignore it","Is required","Deletes references","Immediately frees all unused objects"],"correct":0,"explain":"You cannot force collection, and calling it is almost never appropriate."},{"id":"zya8_5_8","topic":"Circular references","text":"Two objects referencing each other but nothing else:","options":["Are still collectable","Cause an error","Are merged","Leak forever"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reference counting would leak here; Java does not use reference counting. Java tracks reachability from roots."},{"id":"zya8_5_9","topic":"Practical guidance","text":"To help the collector you should:","options":["Call the collector explicitly after every loop","Avoid keeping references longer than needed","Allocate every object as a static field","Set every local variable to null before returning"],"correct":1,"explain":"Clearing a collection you are done with is usually enough."},{"id":"fs_z8_5_1","topic":"Scope versus a retained reference","text":"What does this program print, and how many of the five Item objects can be collected once the loop ends?

static class Item {
}

ArrayList<Item> kept = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    Item x = new Item();
    if (i % 2 == 0) {
        kept.add(x);
    }
}
System.out.println(kept.size());","options":["3, and the other 2 are collectable","5, and none are collectable","3, and all 5 are collectable","2, and the other 3 are collectable"],"correct":0,"explain":"x goes out of scope at the end of every iteration, but the three Items added to kept have a second reference that outlives the loop. Only the two never stored become unreachable."},{"id":"fs_z8_5_2","topic":"Helping the collector","text":"Which practice actually helps the garbage collector in a long-running program?","options":["Removing entries from long-lived collections once they are no longer needed","Assigning null to every local variable just before a method returns","Calling System.gc() at the end of each method","Avoiding object creation inside loops"],"correct":0,"explain":"Objects held by a long-lived collection cannot be collected, so removing them is the fix that matters. Locals disappear with the frame anyway, and forcing collection is not something a program can do."},{"id":"fs_z8_5_3","topic":"Out of scope is not gone","text":"What does this program print?

ArrayList<StringBuilder> log = new ArrayList<>();
{
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("in");
    log.add(sb);
}
log.get(0).append("-out");
System.out.println(log.get(0));","options":["in-out","in","-out","Nothing; sb went out of scope so the object was already collected"],"correct":0,"explain":"The name sb stops existing at the closing brace, but the list still refers to the StringBuilder, so the object is alive and can still be modified."},{"id":"fs_z8_5_4","topic":"Nulling a parameter","text":"What does this program print?

static class Tag {
    String n;
    Tag(String n) { this.n = n; }
}
static void clear(Tag t) { t = null; }

Tag t = new Tag("A");
clear(t);
System.out.println(t == null ? "null" : t.n);","options":["A","null","Nothing; it throws a NullPointerException","It does not compile"],"correct":0,"explain":"The parameter t is a copy of the caller's reference, so assigning null to it changes only the copy inside clear(). The caller's variable, and the object, are untouched."},{"id":"zf2520","topic":"","text":"A local reference going OUT OF SCOPE:","options":["deletes the object instantly","no longer keeps its object reachable","extends the object’s life","throws"],"correct":1,"explain":"Frame pops, reference dies, object may become collectable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2521","topic":"","text":"An object created inside a loop iteration, with its reference reassigned next pass:","options":["accumulates all objects as live","is illegal","abandons the previous object each pass","reuses one object"],"correct":2,"explain":"for (...) { Part p = new Part(); } - each pass abandons the last.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2522","topic":"","text":"Storing every loop-created object into a list instead:","options":["changes nothing","collects them anyway","shrinks them","keeps them ALL reachable"],"correct":3,"explain":"The list is the reference that pins them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2523","topic":"","text":"A method-local object whose reference is RETURNED:","options":["lives on via the caller’s reference","dies at return","is copied to the caller","is static now"],"correct":0,"explain":"Escaping the scope by return extends reachability.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2524","topic":"","text":"Narrow scoping, declaring references in the smallest block needed:","options":["slows the program","releases objects as early as possible","confuses the GC","is only style"],"correct":1,"explain":"Shorter reference life = earlier eligibility.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2525","topic":"","text":"A static field referencing an object keeps it alive:","options":["one method","one loop","for the class’s whole lifetime","never"],"correct":2,"explain":"Statics are GC roots that essentially never die - use with care.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2526","topic":"","text":"list.clear() on a huge list of objects:","options":["frees nothing","deletes the list variable","moves them to the stack","drops its references, freeing them"],"correct":3,"explain":"Emptying the container releases what it pinned (absent other references).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2527","topic":"","text":"Part a = new Part(); { Part b = a; } After the block, the object is:","options":["still reachable through a","collectable","collected","null"],"correct":0,"explain":"b died with its block, but a survives - reachability needs only one path.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2528","topic":"","text":"The connection between scope and GC is:","options":["scope controls the CPU","scope ends reference lives","they are unrelated","GC sets scope"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two links in one chain: block -> reference -> object.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2529","topic":"","text":"An object passed to a method during a call is:","options":["collectable mid-call","copied to be safe","reachable at least until the call ends","frozen"],"correct":2,"explain":"The parameter reference in the active frame is a root.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2530","topic":"","text":"Why can’t the GC collect an object you WILL never use but COULD reach?","options":["it can read intent","it collects it anyway","GC proves reachability, not intent","objects report usage"],"correct":2,"explain":"That gap is exactly why forgotten references leak.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u9/z9_2":[{"id":"zya9_2_0","topic":"printf purpose","text":"System.out.printf is used to:","options":["Read formatted input from the keyboard","Write the output to a file rather than the screen","Produce formatted output using format specifiers","Convert a String into a number for calculation"],"correct":2,"explain":"It lets you control decimals, width and alignment."},{"id":"zya9_2_1","topic":"Integer specifier","text":"The specifier for an int is:","options":["%c","%s","%d","%f"],"correct":2,"explain":"%d for integers, %f for floating point."},{"id":"zya9_2_2","topic":"Two decimals","text":"To print a double with exactly two decimals:","options":["%2f","%f","%.2f","%d"],"correct":2,"explain":"The .2 sets the precision."},{"id":"zya9_2_3","topic":"String specifier","text":"The specifier for a String is:","options":["%t","%d","%s","%f"],"correct":2,"explain":"%s also works for any object, calling its toString."},{"id":"zya9_2_4","topic":"Newline","text":"In printf, a portable newline is written as:","options":["\r","\n only","%n","endl"],"correct":2,"explain":"%n produces the platform-appropriate line separator; println adds one automatically."},{"id":"zya9_2_5","topic":"Wrong specifier","text":"printf("%d", 3.5) causes:","options":["A compile error before the program runs","Prints 3, silently truncating the value","IllegalFormatConversionException at runtime","Prints 3.5, since %d accepts any number"],"correct":2,"explain":"The mismatch is not caught at compile time, which makes it a runtime surprise."},{"id":"zya9_2_6","topic":"Field width","text":"%10.2f means:","options":["Multiply by 10","10 decimal places","Minimum width 10, two decimals","10 characters exactly"],"correct":2,"explain":"Useful for aligning numbers into columns."},{"id":"zya9_2_7","topic":"Left alignment","text":"To left-align within a field:","options":["%.10s","%10s","%-10s","%+10s"],"correct":2,"explain":"The minus sign flips the default right alignment."},{"id":"zya9_2_8","topic":"String.format","text":"String.format uses the same specifiers but:","options":["Prints the result and returns nothing","Uses a completely different set of specifiers","Returns the formatted String instead of printing","Formats only numbers, never text"],"correct":2,"explain":"Handy when the text is needed for a label or a file rather than the console."},{"id":"zya9_2_9","topic":"Multiple arguments","text":"printf("%s is %d%n", name, age) matches:","options":["Nothing","Arguments in reverse order","Arguments in order to the specifiers","Only the first argument"],"correct":2,"explain":"A count or order mismatch throws at runtime."},{"id":"fs_z9_2_1","topic":"printf specifiers","text":"What does this statement print?

System.out.printf("%s scored %d (%.1f%%)%n", "Ada", 95, 87.25);","options":["Ada scored 95 (87.25%)","Ada scored 95 (87.3%%)","Ada scored 95 (87.3%)","Ada scored 95 (87.2%)"],"correct":2,"explain":"%.1f rounds rather than truncating, so 87.25 becomes 87.3, and %% prints a single literal percent sign."},{"id":"fs_z9_2_2","topic":"Width and alignment","text":"What does this program print?

System.out.printf("[%8.2f]%n", 3.14159);
System.out.printf("[%-8s]%n", "hi");","options":["[3.14]
[hi]","[ 3.14159]
[hi      ]","[    3.14]
[hi      ]","[3.14    ]
[      hi]"],"correct":2,"explain":"The 8 is a minimum field width: numbers are right-aligned by default, and the minus sign in %-8s asks for left alignment. The .2 fixes the number at two decimals."},{"id":"fs_z9_2_3","topic":"Too few arguments","text":"A printf call supplies fewer values than its format string has placeholders, for example System.out.printf("%s is %d%n", name). What happens?","options":["The missing placeholder is printed as the text null","The missing placeholder is silently skipped","An exception is thrown at run time when the statement executes","The compiler rejects the call because the counts do not match"],"correct":2,"explain":"The compiler does not check the pairing of placeholders and arguments, so the mismatch survives to run time and throws (MissingFormatArgumentException). Extra arguments, by contrast, are simply ignored."},{"id":"fs_z9_2_4","topic":"String.format","text":"Which expression produces the String "Total: 12.50" without printing anything?","options":["System.out.println("Total: %.2f", 12.5)","String.printf("Total: %.2f", 12.5)","String.format("Total: %.2f", 12.5)","System.out.printf("Total: %.2f", 12.5)"],"correct":2,"explain":"String.format takes exactly the same format string and arguments as printf but returns the formatted String instead of writing it to the console."},{"id":"zf2557","topic":"","text":"System.out.printf("%.2f", 3.14159); prints:","options":["3.14159","3.1","3.142","3.14"],"correct":3,"explain":"%.2f rounds to two decimal places.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2558","topic":"","text":"System.out.printf("%.2f", 5.0); prints:","options":["5.00","5.0","5","5.000"],"correct":0,"explain":"The format guarantees exactly two decimals, padding with zeros.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2559","topic":"","text":"In "%d", the d stands for:","options":["a double","a decimal integer value","a date","a char"],"correct":1,"explain":"%d formats ints; %f handles floating point.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2560","topic":"","text":"System.out.printf("%5d", 42); prints:","options":["42 then spaces","three spaces then 42","00042","42"],"correct":1,"explain":"Width pads on the left by default.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2561","topic":"","text":"printf("%s costs $%.2f", "Bolt", 0.25); prints:","options":["%s costs $%.2f","Bolt costs $0.3","Bolt costs $0.25","Bolt costs $.25"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each specifier consumes its matching argument in order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2562","topic":"","text":"The %n specifier in printf:","options":["prints the letter n","numbers lines","does nothing","outputs the platform’s line separator"],"correct":3,"explain":"Prefer %n inside format strings over hard-coded \n for portability.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2563","topic":"","text":"String.format("%.1f", 2.35 + 0.05) returns:","options":["the formatted STRING, not a printout","nothing","a printout","a double"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same formatting engine, result handed back for storage or later use.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2564","topic":"","text":"Formatting money in columns uses:","options":["%d","width plus precision, like %8.2f","plain println","string addition"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fixed width + fixed decimals = aligned currency columns.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2565","topic":"","text":"printf("%d items", 3, 7); (extra argument):","options":["prints both","throws always","prints "3 items"","prints 7 items"],"correct":2,"explain":"Extra arguments are permitted and skipped; MISSING ones throw.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2566","topic":"","text":"The main reason for formatted output is:","options":["faster printing","smaller files","required syntax","readable, aligned, rounded results"],"correct":3,"explain":"Tables and money demand more control than plain println gives.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2609","topic":"","text":"System.out.printf("%d%%", 50); prints:","options":["%d%%","50%","50","%50"],"correct":1,"explain":"%% is the escape for a literal percent sign.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u9/z9_5":[{"id":"zya9_5_0","topic":"Writing a file","text":"Writing text to a file commonly uses:","options":["System.in","File only","Scanner","PrintWriter"],"correct":3,"explain":"PrintWriter offers the familiar println and printf methods."},{"id":"zya9_5_1","topic":"Creating the file","text":"new PrintWriter("out.txt") when the file does not exist:","options":["Returns null","Is a compile error","Throws","Creates it"],"correct":3,"explain":"And if it does exist, it is truncated — existing contents are lost."},{"id":"zya9_5_2","topic":"Overwrite risk","text":"Opening an existing file for writing without append mode:","options":["Throws","Renames it","Appends","Erases the previous contents"],"correct":3,"explain":"Use new FileWriter(path, true) to append instead."},{"id":"zya9_5_3","topic":"Flushing","text":"Data may not reach disk until:","options":["The file is read","Garbage collection","The program crashes","The writer is flushed or closed"],"correct":3,"explain":"Forgetting to close is the classic cause of an unexpectedly empty output file."},{"id":"zya9_5_4","topic":"Closing the writer","text":"Not closing a PrintWriter typically causes:","options":["The file to be deleted","Nothing","A compile error","Buffered output to be lost"],"correct":3,"explain":"try-with-resources closes it on every path."},{"id":"zya9_5_5","topic":"Checked exception on write","text":"Creating a PrintWriter can throw:","options":["ArithmeticException","NullPointerException","Nothing","FileNotFoundException"],"correct":3,"explain":"The directory may not exist or may not be writable. Must be handled."},{"id":"zya9_5_6","topic":"Writing formatted output","text":"PrintWriter supports:","options":["Only write","Only append","Only print","print, println and printf"],"correct":3,"explain":"The same formatting API as System.out."},{"id":"zya9_5_7","topic":"Reading vs writing","text":"The key difference in failure modes is:","options":["Both fail only when the file is already open elsewhere","Reading fails on permissions; writing when file missing","Neither can fail once the file has been opened successfully","Reading fails if the file is missing; writing on permissions or space"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each needs its own error handling."},{"id":"zyf9_5_0","topic":"Append mode","text":"To add to a file without erasing it use:","options":["new FileWriter(path, true)","new Scanner(path)","new File(path)","new PrintWriter(path)"],"correct":0,"explain":"The boolean second argument enables appending."},{"id":"zyf9_5_1","topic":"Empty output file","text":"An output file that ends up empty usually means:","options":["The writer was never flushed or closed","The file was opened in read-only mode","The data was written to standard output instead","The file name contained an illegal character"],"correct":0,"explain":"Buffered output is lost if the writer is not closed."},{"id":"fs_z9_5_1","topic":"PrintWriter formatting","text":"What does this program print?

PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(System.out);
out.println("alpha");
out.printf("%-6s|%n", "hi");
out.flush();
(␣ marks a space, ⏎ a line break.)","options":["Nothing, because PrintWriter has no printf method","alpha ⏎ hi␣␣␣␣|","alpha ⏎ ␣␣␣␣hi|","alpha␣hi␣␣␣␣|"],"correct":1,"explain":"PrintWriter offers the same print, println and printf as System.out, so %-6s pads hi on the right to six characters. flush() pushes the buffered text out."},{"id":"fs_z9_5_2","topic":"Unflushed output is lost","text":"What does this program print?

System.out.println("start");
PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(System.out);
out.println("buffered");","options":["Nothing at all","start","start
buffered","buffered
start"],"correct":1,"explain":"A PrintWriter buffers what it is given and does not flush automatically here, so the text never reaches the destination before the program ends. The same mistake with a file writer is the usual reason an output file ends up empty."},{"id":"fs_z9_5_3","topic":"try-with-resources for output","text":"Why is try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter("report.txt")) { ... } preferred to calling out.close() as the last statement of the writing code?","options":["It makes the writes go straight to disk without buffering","The writer is closed, and therefore flushed, even if an exception is thrown part way through","It stops the file being truncated when it is opened","It removes the need to handle FileNotFoundException at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"A close() written as the last statement is skipped when an exception jumps out of the block, leaving buffered text unwritten. The try-with-resources form closes the writer on every exit path."},{"id":"fs_z9_5_4","topic":"Moving formatting to a file","text":"A program's console report is built with System.out.printf. What is the smallest change that writes the same text to report.txt instead?","options":["Nothing can be reused; file output supports only println","Call printf with the same format string and arguments on a PrintWriter opened on report.txt","Redirect System.out by assigning a File to it","Replace each printf with String.format and write the result with File.write"],"correct":1,"explain":"PrintWriter provides print, println and printf with the same signatures as System.out, so formatting code moves between console and file unchanged."},{"id":"zf2590","topic":"","text":"PrintWriter provides which familiar methods?","options":["print, println, and printf","nextInt and next","charAt","only write bytes"],"correct":0,"explain":"The console habits transfer to files directly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2591","topic":"","text":"Opening an EXISTING file for output (default mode):","options":["appends","overwrites its previous contents","fails","merges"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fresh start unless append mode is requested.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2592","topic":"","text":"Appending instead of overwriting uses:","options":["a second file","append() on PrintWriter","new FileOutputStream("log.txt", true)","nothing - impossible"],"correct":2,"explain":"The boolean true selects append mode.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2593","topic":"","text":"Forgetting pw.close() risks:","options":["file deletion","double writing","nothing at all","buffered output never reaching the file"],"correct":3,"explain":"close() flushes the buffer; without it the file can end up empty or truncated.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2594","topic":"","text":"pw.println("Total: " + total); writes:","options":["the line into the file, not the console","to the screen","to both","nowhere"],"correct":0,"explain":"The PrintWriter’s destination is the file it wraps.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2595","topic":"","text":"Creating a PrintWriter to a NEW filename:","options":["throws always","creates the file on disk","requires the file to exist","opens a console"],"correct":1,"explain":"Output streams create their target when needed (permissions allowing).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2596","topic":"","text":"A program that reads data.txt and writes report.txt uses:","options":["one object for both","only System.out","a Scanner in, a PrintWriter out","memory only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Two independent streams, one per file, each closed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2597","topic":"","text":"pw.printf("%-10s %8.2f%n", name, price); into a file produces:","options":["unformatted text","an error - printf is console-only","binary data","aligned columns, like the console"],"correct":3,"explain":"Formatting is destination-independent.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2598","topic":"","text":"Writing a header line before the loop of data lines gives the file:","options":["a labeled, readable structure","errors","slower writes","nothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Files are interfaces too - design their layout.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2599","topic":"","text":"The read-process-write pattern (input file -> compute -> output file):","options":["is rare","is the shape of real data processing","needs databases","is console-only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Batch processing in its simplest form - this section’s big picture.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2610","topic":"","text":"pw.print("A"); pw.print("B"); pw.close(); The file contains:","options":["A B","AB","BA","nothing - print is console-only"],"correct":1,"explain":"print appends without separators, and close flushes it all to disk.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u10/z10_9":[{"id":"zya10_9_0","topic":"Is-a relationship","text":"Inheritance models which relationship?","options":["uses-a","part-of","has-a","is-a"],"correct":3,"explain":"A Dog is-a Animal, so Dog extends Animal."},{"id":"zya10_9_1","topic":"Has-a relationship","text":"A Car containing an Engine object is:","options":["Polymorphism","An interface","Inheritance","Composition"],"correct":3,"explain":"A Car is not a kind of Engine, so it holds one as a field. A has-a relationship."},{"id":"zya10_9_2","topic":"Choosing between them","text":"A Library and its Books are related by:","options":["Book extends Library","An abstract class","Library extends Book","Composition"],"correct":3,"explain":"A library is not a kind of book. Library has Books."},{"id":"zya10_9_3","topic":"Wrong inheritance","text":"Making Employee extend Company is wrong because:","options":["Java forbids it","It is too slow","Companies have no methods","An employee is not a kind of company"],"correct":3,"explain":"The is-a test catches most inheritance design mistakes. It works for one."},{"id":"zya10_9_4","topic":"Composition flexibility","text":"Composition is often preferred because:","options":["It always uses less memory than inheritance does","It removes the need for interfaces in the design","The contained object can be swapped and the coupling is looser","It lets a class inherit from several parents at once"],"correct":2,"explain":""Favour composition over inheritance" is standard design guidance."},{"id":"zya10_9_5","topic":"Inheritance coupling","text":"A subclass is tightly coupled to its superclass because:","options":["The subclass must be in the same package","A change to the superclass can break every subclass","The subclass cannot add fields of its own","The superclass must know each of its subclasses"],"correct":1,"explain":"This fragility is why inheritance is used deliberately rather than casually."},{"id":"zya10_9_6","topic":"Both relationships","text":"A Manager who is an Employee and has an Office:","options":["Extends Employee and holds an Office field","Extends both Employee and Office","Holds an Employee field and an Office field","Implements Employee and extends Office"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each relationship gets the mechanism that matches it."},{"id":"zya10_9_7","topic":"Testing the relationship","text":"The practical test for inheritance is:","options":["Does the subclass reuse any of the parent's code","Do the two classes share at least one field name","Are the two classes declared in the same package","Can the subclass be used anywhere the superclass is expected"],"correct":3,"explain":"That substitutability is what polymorphism depends on."},{"id":"zya10_9_8","topic":"Square and Rectangle","text":"Making Square extend Rectangle is a classic problem because:","options":["Rectangle has no fields for a Square to inherit","Java forbids extending a class with two dimensions","Setting width and height independently breaks the square's rule","Square would need two parents, which is not allowed"],"correct":2,"explain":"Passing the is-a test in English is not enough — the behaviour must substitute cleanly too."},{"id":"px286_inh_4","topic":"Which is TRUE about constructors and inheritance","text":"Which is TRUE about constructors and inheritance?","options":["A subclass constructor implicitly calls super() first","Parent constructors never run","super() must be last in the constructor","Constructors are inherited"],"correct":0,"explain":"A subclass constructor implicitly calls the parent's no-arg super() as its first action.","distractors":{"0":"Constructors are NOT inherited, and super() must be FIRST, not last.","2":"Constructors are NOT inherited, and super() must be FIRST, not last.","3":"Constructors are NOT inherited, and super() must be FIRST, not last."},"_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"z2_in2","topic":"super() call","_dom":"inherit","text":"When a subclass object is created, before its own constructor body runs Java first:","options":["Runs the subclass field initialisers before anything else","Calls the subclass's own no-arg constructor first","Implicitly calls the superclass constructor (super())","Allocates the parent object as a separate instance"],"correct":2,"explain":"A subclass constructor implicitly invokes super() before its own body.","__rebal":1},{"id":"x286_oo1","topic":"Inheritance IS-A","_dom":"oop","text":"The relationship 'a Dog IS-A Animal' is BEST modeled in Java using:","options":["Inheritance (extends)","Composition","A static method","An enum"],"correct":0,"explain":"An IS-A relationship is expressed with inheritance via extends.","__rebal":1},{"id":"h6_286_1","topic":"inheritance/super","_dom":"oop","difficulty":"hard","text":"class A{ int f(){return 1;} } class B extends A{ int f(){return super.f()+1;} } new B().f() returns:","options":["3","compile error","2","1"],"correct":2,"explain":"B.f() calls super.f() (A's version, returns 1) and adds 1, giving 2.","__rebal":1},{"id":"px286_inh_2","topic":"What does  super()  do in a subclass constructor","text":"What does  super()  do in a subclass constructor?","options":["Creates a new subclass object","Overrides a method","Calls the parent class's constructor","Makes the class abstract"],"correct":2,"explain":"super() invokes the superclass constructor to initialize inherited state.","distractors":{"0":"super() does not override methods — it calls the parent constructor.","2":"super() does not override methods — it calls the parent constructor.","3":"super() does not override methods — it calls the parent constructor."},"_practice":true,"__rebal":1},{"id":"x286_in1","topic":"super","_dom":"inherit","text":"In a subclass, the super keyword is used to:","options":["Call another constructor in the same subclass","Create a new instance of the parent class","Declare that the subclass replaces the parent","Call the parent class's constructor or an overridden method"],"correct":3,"explain":"super invokes the superclass constructor or an overridden parent method.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q7","topic":"Inheritance transitive","text":"C extends B extends A. C inherits from:","options":["Only B","Only A","Both A and B (transitively)","Neither unless declared"],"correct":2,"explain":"Inheritance is transitive: C inherits B's members, and B inherits A's.","distractors":{"0":"C inherits from both.","1":"A also inherited transitively.","3":"Transitive inheritance is automatic."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"n19","topic":"IS-A","text":"Dog extends Animal expresses:","options":["Dog HAS-A Animal","Animal IS-A Dog","no relationship","Dog IS-A Animal"],"correct":3,"explain":"Inheritance models an IS-A relationship.","__rebal":1},{"id":"zf4093","topic":"","text":"Is-a relationships are modeled with:","options":["inheritance (extends)","fields","imports","methods"],"correct":0,"explain":"A Truck IS A Vehicle - so Truck extends Vehicle.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4094","topic":"","text":"Has-a relationships are modeled with:","options":["extends","composition - a field of that type","static","abstract"],"correct":1,"explain":"A Car HAS AN Engine - so Car holds an Engine field.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4095","topic":"","text":"Car extends Engine is wrong because:","options":["Engine is final","extends is rare","a car is not a kind of engine","engines are abstract"],"correct":2,"explain":"Misusing inheritance for has-a creates nonsense hierarchies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4096","topic":"","text":"A Library and its Books:","options":["is-a","neither","both","has-a - the library contains books"],"correct":3,"explain":"Containment is composition, not kinship.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4097","topic":"","text":"A SavingsAccount and an Account:","options":["is-a - a kind of account","has-a","neither","impossible to say"],"correct":0,"explain":"The "kind of" test settles it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4098","topic":"","text":"The quick test for choosing extends is asking:","options":["does X use Y?","is X a kind of Y?","is X bigger?","are both classes?"],"correct":1,"explain":"If "X is a Y" sounds wrong aloud, do not inherit.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4099","topic":"","text":"Preferring composition when in doubt is advised because:","options":["fields are faster","extends is deprecated","has-a couples classes far more loosely","inheritance is rare"],"correct":2,"explain":"You can swap a contained Engine; you cannot easily un-extend a base.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zr10","topic":"Is-a versus has-a relationships","text":"A Library holds many Book objects. Which relationship is that?","options":["is-a, so Library should extend Book","has-a, so Library should hold Books as a field","is-a, so Book should extend Library","has-a, so Book should extend Library"],"correct":1,"explain":"If you can say "a Library HAS a Book" rather than "a Library IS a Book", model it with a field (composition), not with inheritance.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u11/z11_2":[{"id":"zya11_2_0","topic":"Factorial base case","text":"For factorial, the base case is:","options":["There is none","n == 0 (or 1), returning 1","n == 100","n < 0"],"correct":1,"explain":"0! is defined as 1, which stops the recursion."},{"id":"zya11_2_1","topic":"Factorial recursive case","text":"The recursive case returns:","options":["n * factorial(n)","n * factorial(n - 1)","factorial(n - 1)","n + factorial(n - 1)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Using n rather than n-1 never shrinks the problem."},{"id":"zya11_2_2","topic":"Trace factorial(4)","text":"factorial(4) returns:","options":["12","24","16","4"],"correct":1,"explain":"4*3*2*1 = 24. The multiplications happen as the calls return."},{"id":"zya11_2_3","topic":"Number of calls","text":"factorial(4) makes how many calls in total, including the first?","options":["4","5","3","24"],"correct":1,"explain":"factorial(4), (3), (2), (1), (0) — five frames if the base case is n == 0."},{"id":"zya11_2_4","topic":"Negative input","text":"factorial(-1) with a base case of n == 0:","options":["Returns 1, since the base case still applies","Fails to compile, since n cannot be negative","Recurses forever until StackOverflowError","Returns immediately with a negative result"],"correct":2,"explain":"The argument moves away from the base case, so a guard for negatives is needed."},{"id":"zya11_2_5","topic":"Order of multiplication","text":"In n * factorial(n-1), the multiplication happens:","options":["Before the recursive call","After the recursive call returns","Never","In the base case only"],"correct":1,"explain":"The call must produce a value before it can be multiplied — which is why the stack unwinds to compute."},{"id":"zya11_2_6","topic":"Return type","text":"Factorial of 20 requires:","options":["int","long","double","String"],"correct":1,"explain":"20! is about 2.4 x 10^18, beyond int but within long. 20! exceeds int range."},{"id":"zya11_2_7","topic":"Sum recursion","text":"A recursive sum of 1..n has the base case:","options":["None","n == 0 returning 0","n == 1 returning n only","n == 0 returning 1"],"correct":1,"explain":"The identity for addition is 0, not 1."},{"id":"zyf11_2_0","topic":"Reduction requirement","text":"A recursive call using the same argument