":"One per character: c-o-d-e.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4152","topic":"","text":"The return value of a recursive call:","options":["is used like any method result","is discarded","prints itself","is stored globally"],"correct":0,"explain":"n * f(n - 1) - the sub-answer feeds the current computation.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u11/z11_3":[{"id":"zya11_3_0","topic":"Recursive binary search","text":"Recursive binary search compares the target with:","options":["Every element","The first element","The middle element","The last element"],"correct":2,"explain":"Then it recurses into whichever half can still contain the target."},{"id":"zya11_3_1","topic":"Base case for search","text":"Binary search stops when:","options":["The middle matches, or low equals high","The middle matches, or low exceeds high","The first or last element matches the target","Every element has been compared once"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two base cases: found, and range exhausted meaning not found."},{"id":"zya11_3_2","topic":"Narrowing the range","text":"If the middle is less than the target, the next call uses:","options":["0 to mid","low to mid-1","mid+1 to high","low to high"],"correct":2,"explain":"Everything at or below mid is too small."},{"id":"zya11_3_3","topic":"Not found result","text":"When the range empties, the method returns:","options":["The middle","0","-1","null"],"correct":2,"explain":"-1 is the standard not-found convention."},{"id":"zya11_3_4","topic":"Precondition","text":"Binary search requires the array to be:","options":["Small","Non-empty","Sorted","All positive"],"correct":2,"explain":"On unsorted data it silently returns wrong answers rather than an error."},{"id":"zya11_3_5","topic":"Complexity","text":"Recursive binary search on n elements is:","options":["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each call halves the range, so the depth is log2 n."},{"id":"zya11_3_6","topic":"Recursion depth","text":"Searching 1,000,000 elements reaches a depth of about:","options":["100","1,000,000","20","1,000"],"correct":2,"explain":"log2 of a million is roughly 20 — nowhere near a stack overflow."},{"id":"zya11_3_7","topic":"Linear search recursion","text":"A recursive linear search base case is:","options":["The array is sorted","Never","index equals length, returning -1","index == 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Running off the end means the value is absent."},{"id":"zyf11_3_0","topic":"Two base cases","text":"Recursive binary search terminates on:","options":["Three conditions","Never","One condition only","A match, or an empty range"],"correct":3,"explain":"Both must be present or it recurses forever."},{"id":"zyf11_3_1","topic":"Passing the range","text":"The recursive call must pass:","options":["Only the array","The target only","The whole array bounds again","The narrowed low and high values"],"correct":3,"explain":"Otherwise the problem never shrinks."},{"id":"fs_z11_3_1","topic":"Recursive binary search","text":"What does this program print?

static int bsearch(int[] a, int target, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo > hi) {
        return -1;
    }
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) {
        return mid;
    }
    if (a[mid] < target) {
        return bsearch(a, target, mid + 1, hi);
    }
    return bsearch(a, target, lo, mid - 1);
}

int[] a = { 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 };
System.out.println(bsearch(a, 12, 0, a.length - 1) + " " + bsearch(a, 5, 0, a.length - 1));","options":["5 0","6 -1","5 -1","12 -1"],"correct":2,"explain":"The search for 12 halves the range twice and finds it at index 5; the search for 5 empties its range, so lo > hi and the method returns -1. Binary search returns an index, not the value."},{"id":"fs_z11_3_2","topic":"Halving the search space","text":"What does this program print?

static int calls = 0;

static int bsearch(int[] a, int target, int lo, int hi) {
    calls++;
    if (lo > hi) {
        return -1;
    }
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) {
        return mid;
    }
    if (a[mid] < target) {
        return bsearch(a, target, mid + 1, hi);
    }
    return bsearch(a, target, lo, mid - 1);
}

int[] a = { 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 };
int idx = bsearch(a, 2, 0, a.length - 1);
System.out.println(idx + " " + calls);","options":["0 7","2 3","0 3","0 1"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each call halves the range: 0..6 then 0..2 then 0..0, where 2 is found at index 0. Three calls for seven elements is the log n behaviour, not the seven a linear scan would need."},{"id":"fs_z11_3_3","topic":"Integer division in the midpoint","text":"In int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;, what is mid when lo is 3 and hi is 4?","options":["3.5, because the division produces a double","The statement does not compile for those values","3, because integer division discards the fractional part","4, because the result is rounded up"],"correct":2,"explain":"7 / 2 is int division, so the result truncates to 3. The midpoint therefore lands on the lower of the two elements, and mid + 1 or mid - 1 still shrinks the range."},{"id":"fs_z11_3_4","topic":"Divide and conquer","text":"Binary search is described as a divide-and-conquer algorithm because each call:","options":["Compares the target with two elements at once","Sorts the half of the array it is about to search","Solves a strictly smaller instance of the very same problem, on half the range","Divides the array into two new arrays that are searched separately"],"correct":2,"explain":"The recursive call is the same search applied to one half of the range, which is what makes recursion the natural expression of it. No copying or sorting takes place; only lo and hi change."},{"id":"zf4153","topic":"","text":"Recursive binary search each call:","options":["checks every element","halves the range, recursing into one","sorts first","doubles the range"],"correct":1,"explain":"Compare the middle; go left or right with a smaller range.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4154","topic":"","text":"Binary search’s base cases are:","options":["the array ending only","one comparison","the value found","the first element"],"correct":2,"explain":"Success and exhausted-range both stop the recursion.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4155","topic":"","text":"Recursive binary search on a range uses parameters:","options":["the array alone","just the target","a loop counter","the array, the target, low, and high"],"correct":3,"explain":"The shrinking low..high window IS the smaller subproblem.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4156","topic":"","text":"Searching a sorted 16-element array recursively needs at most about:","options":["5 comparisons","16","8","2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each halving: 16 -> 8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1 (log2 16 = 4, plus one).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4157","topic":"","text":"If mid’s value is LESS than the target, recurse into:","options":["the lower half","the upper half (mid + 1 to high)","both halves","the whole array"],"correct":1,"explain":"The target can only be where values are larger.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4158","topic":"","text":"The empty-range base case returns:","options":["0","the last index","-1 (or another not-found signal)","an exception always"],"correct":2,"explain":"low > high means the window closed with no match.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4159","topic":"","text":"Binary search demands the data be:","options":["unique","small","positive","sorted"],"correct":3,"explain":"Discarding half the range is only valid when order is guaranteed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4160","topic":"","text":"Recursive LINEAR search on a range checks:","options":["the middle","both ends","a random element","the first element"],"correct":3,"explain":"Base: empty range or match; step: shrink by one.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4161","topic":"","text":"Compared to its loop version, recursive binary search:","options":["the same halving, via calls","is asymptotically slower","searches differently","needs no base case"],"correct":0,"explain":"Identical logic, different control mechanism.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4162","topic":"","text":"search(arr, t, 0, arr.length - 1) - the initial call covers:","options":["half the array","the whole array","one element","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Callers start the recursion with the full window.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4163","topic":"","text":"Each recursive search call trusts that:","options":["the array shrinks","the target moves","the sub-search handles its window","loops back it up"],"correct":2,"explain":"The recursive leap of faith, applied to search.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u11/z11_4":[{"id":"zya11_4_0","topic":"Debug output value","text":"Printing on entry to a recursive method shows:","options":["The value each call finally returns","The total number of frames on the stack","The sequence of calls and their arguments","The memory each call has allocated"],"correct":2,"explain":"Seeing the argument sequence immediately reveals a call that fails to shrink."},{"id":"zya11_4_1","topic":"Indenting output","text":"Indenting by depth helps because:","options":["It makes the program run measurably faster overall","It makes the call tree visible, showing nesting and repetition","It is required for the recursion to terminate correctly","It reduces the number of stack frames that are used"],"correct":1,"explain":"Passing a depth parameter and printing that many spaces is the standard trick."},{"id":"zya11_4_2","topic":"What to print","text":"The most useful trace prints:","options":["The arguments on entry and the value on return","Only the arguments, since returns are predictable","Only the final answer the top-level call gives","The memory address of each stack frame"],"correct":0,"explain":"Together they show what each level was asked and what it answered."},{"id":"zya11_4_3","topic":"Spotting infinite recursion","text":"A trace showing the same argument repeatedly means:","options":["The base case is being reached too early","The method is being called from two places","The compiler has optimised away the recursion","The recursive call is not reducing the problem"],"correct":3,"explain":"That is the direct cause of StackOverflowError."},{"id":"zya11_4_4","topic":"Trace 