lty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4168","topic":"","text":"Return-value prints for fact(3) appear in the order:","options":["6, 2, 1","3, 2, 1","1, 1, 1","1, 2, 6"],"correct":3,"explain":"Deepest returns first - results assemble on the way UP.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4169","topic":"","text":"A debug trace showing arguments that never shrink reveals:","options":["missing progress toward the base case","a fast method","correct recursion","a compiler bug"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same-size sub-calls = the infinite recursion signature.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4170","topic":"","text":"A trace showing n reaching -1, -2, ... reveals:","options":["a base case some inputs jump over","success","extra speed","sorted data"],"correct":0,"explain":"The n == 0 check missed a path (e.g., negative or skipped values).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4171","topic":"","text":"Debug output in recursion is best removed or disabled:","options":["never","after the bug is fixed","before debugging","during the bug"],"correct":1,"explain":"Traces are scaffolding, not product.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4172","topic":"","text":"Watching DUPLICATE arguments in a fib trace (fib(2) three times) reveals:","options":["a crash","wrong answers","repeated work","sorted input"],"correct":2,"explain":"Seeing the redundancy motivates memoization later.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4173","topic":"","text":"Compared to a debugger, println tracing of recursion:","options":["is always worse","changes the algorithm","only works once","shows the whole call history at once"],"correct":3,"explain":"A full printed tree can beat stepping call-by-call.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4174","topic":"","text":"The parameter to add for indentation, void f(int n, int depth):","options":["increments in each recursive call","stays fixed","decrements","randomizes"],"correct":0,"explain":"f(n - 1, depth + 1) - depth mirrors the call level.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u11/z11_5":[{"id":"zya11_5_0","topic":"First design step","text":"When designing a recursive method, first identify:","options":["The base case","The return type","The parameters","The loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Without it there is nothing to stop at. The smallest input you can answer directly."},{"id":"zya11_5_1","topic":"Second step","text":"Next you determine:","options":["How many times the method will call itself","How to express the problem in terms of a smaller instance","Which loop construct will replace the recursion","How much stack space each call will need"],"correct":1,"explain":"For sum(n) that is n + sum(n-1)."},{"id":"zya11_5_2","topic":"Trusting the recursion","text":"When writing the recursive case you should:","options":["Assume the recursive call correctly solves the smaller problem","Trace every level of the recursion by hand first","Write the base case only after the recursion works","Ensure each call receives the same argument value"],"correct":0,"explain":"The "recursive leap of faith" — verify the base case and the reduction, and correctness follows."},{"id":"zya11_5_3","topic":"Parameter choice","text":"The parameter should be something that:","options":["Stays the same so the logic is consistent","Grows with each call until the limit is reached","Alternates between two values on each call","Gets measurably smaller with each call"],"correct":3,"explain":"A shrinking measure is what guarantees termination."},{"id":"zya11_5_4","topic":"String recursion","text":"Reversing a String recursively uses:","options":["s.charAt(0) + reverse(s.substring(1))","reverse(s.substring(0)) + s.charAt(1)","reverse(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0)","reverse(s) + s.charAt(s.length() - 1)"],"correct":2,"explain":"The base case is an empty or one-character String."},{"id":"zya11_5_5","topic":"Helper method","text":"A recursive method needing extra state often uses:","options":["A static field that is shared by every one of the calls","A helper with extra parameters, called from a simple public method","A global array declared outside of the class body entirely","An extra return value packed into one String result"],"correct":1,"explain":"search(a, target) calls search(a, target, low, high) — the public API stays clean."},{"id":"zya11_5_6","topic":"Verifying correctness","text":"To check a recursive method, verify:","options":["The base case is right and each call reduces toward it","The base case is right; the rest follows","Each call reduces the input; the base case is optional","The method returns the same type on every path"],"correct":0,"explain":"Those two conditions together are what make it correct."},{"id":"zya11_5_7","topic":"When recursion fits","text":"Recursion is most natural for:","options":["Problems that simply iterate over a fixed-size array of values","Problems that read input until a sentinel value is entered","Problems that are required to run in strictly constant memory","Problems with self-similar substructure, such as trees and divide-and-conquer"],"correct":3,"explain":"Summing an array is more naturally a loop."},{"id":"zyf11_5_0","topic":"Design order","text":"When designing recursion you identify first:","options":["The return type","The base case","The parameters","The complexity"],"correct":1,"explain":"It is what stops the recursion."},{"id":"zyf11_5_1","topic":"Public wrapper","text":"A recursive helper with extra parameters is usually:","options":["Public, so callers can supply the extra state","Private, called from a simpler public method","Static, so it needs no enclosing object","Abstract, so subclasses can supply the logic"],"correct":1,"explain":"It keeps the caller-facing API clean."},{"id":"fs_z11_5_1","topic":"Designing a recursive method","text":"What does this program print?

