System.out.println(pal("racecar") + " " + pal("java"));","options":["true false","false false","true true","false true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each call compares the outer pair and recurses on the inside, shrinking the String by two characters. racecar reduces to a single character and returns true; java fails on the first comparison."},{"id":"fs_z11_5_4","topic":"Choosing the reduction step","text":"A method that counts the digits of n could recurse on n / 10 or on n - 1, and both eventually reach a value below 10. Why is n / 10 the right choice?","options":["It reaches the base case in a handful of calls, while n - 1 would need a stack frame for every value down to 9","n - 1 does not actually make the argument smaller","n / 10 is the only expression allowed in a recursive call","n - 1 would count each digit twice"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both shrink the argument, so both terminate, but only n / 10 removes a digit per call. Subtracting one would take millions of frames for a large n and overflow the stack."},{"id":"zf4175","topic":"","text":"Designing a recursion starts by asking:","options":["how fast is it?","which input can I answer directly?","which loop replaces it?","what prints first?"],"correct":1,"explain":"Base case first; the step comes second.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4176","topic":"","text":"The recursive step should express the answer for n:","options":["in terms of itself unchanged","as a printout","using the answer for a smaller input","as a constant"],"correct":2,"explain":"sum(n) = n + sum(n-1) - the self-referential formula.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4177","topic":"","text":"Writing sumDigits(1234) recursively:","options":["loop over a string","return 1234","cast to double","return 1234 % 10 + sumDigits(1234 / 10);"],"correct":3,"explain":"Last digit plus the sum of the rest; base: n == 0 returns 0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4178","topic":"","text":"sumDigits(1234) with that design returns:","options":["10","1234","4","24"],"correct":0,"explain":"4 + 3 + 2 + 1.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4179","topic":"","text":"A recursive countdown’s base case is naturally:","options":["the largest number","reaching zero (or the stopping value)","any even number","the first print"],"correct":1,"explain":"Direct answer: nothing left to count.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4180","topic":"","text":"static boolean isPal(String s) { if (s.length() <= 1) return true; if (s.charAt(0) != s.charAt(s.length()-1)) return false; return isPal(s.substring(1, s.length()-1)); } isPal("level") returns:","options":["false","true","level","an error"],"correct":1,"explain":"l=l, e=e, then "v" hits the length-1 base case.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4181","topic":"","text":"isPal("code") returns:","options":["true","code","false","null"],"correct":2,"explain":"c vs e fails immediately.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4182","topic":"","text":"Choosing recursion over a loop is most justified when:","options":["always","the input is tiny","loops are broken","the problem is naturally self-similar"],"correct":3,"explain":"Straight counting favors loops; structural descent favors recursion.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4183","topic":"","text":"A helper method with extra parameters (like low/high) exists because:","options":["a clean public signature","Java demands it","helpers are faster","parameters are free"],"correct":0,"explain":"search(arr, t) delegates to search(arr, t, 0, len-1).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4184","topic":"","text":"Testing a new recursive method starts with:","options":["the biggest input","the base case inputs themselves","random inputs only","no tests"],"correct":1,"explain":"If the foundation answers wrong, every level above it is wrong.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4185","topic":"","text":"The two failure modes to design against are:","options":["slow typing and bad names","imports and packages","no base case reached, and wrong sub-problem","overflow and underflow of ints only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Termination and correctness - verify both explicitly.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u11/z11_6":[{"id":"zya11_6_0","topic":"Fibonacci base cases","text":"Naive Fibonacci needs base cases for:","options":["n == 0 only","n == 0 and n == 1","n == 1 only","n == 2"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two base cases, because the recursive case refers to the two preceding values."},{"id":"zya11_6_1","topic":"Fibonacci recursive case","text":"fib(n) returns:","options":["fib(n-1)","fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)","n * fib(n-1)","fib(n) + 1"],"correct":1,"explain":"Each term is the sum of the previous two."},{"id":"zya11_6_2","topic":"Naive Fibonacci cost","text":"Naive recursive Fibonacci is:","options":["O(n)","O(2^n)","O(log n)","O(1)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The same subproblems are recomputed enormously often, which is why fib(50) is impractical. Exponential."},{"id":"zya11_6_3","topic":"Fixing the cost","text":"The exponential blow-up is avoided by:","options":["More recursion","Memoization or an iterative loop","A larger stack","Using double"],"correct":1,"explain":"Caching computed values reduces it to O(n)."},{"id":"zya11_6_4","topic":"Power recursion","text":"A recursive power method x^n has base case:","options":["None","n == 0 returning 1","n == 1 returning 0","n == 0 returning 0"],"correct":1,"explain":"Anything to the power 0 is 1 — the identity for multiplication."},{"id":"zya11_6_5","topic":"GCD recursion","text":"Euclid's algorithm computes gcd(a, b) as:","options":["gcd(a-b, b)","gcd(b, a % b) with base case b == 0","a * b","a / b"],"correct":1,"explain":"When b reaches 0 the answer is a."},{"id":"zya11_6_6","topic":"Sum of digits","text":"Recursively summing digits of n uses:","options":["n * 10","n % 10 + sumDigits(n / 10)","n / 10 + sumDigits(n % 10)","n + sumDigits(n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"% 10 extracts the last digit and / 10 removes it — base case n == 0."},{"id":"zya11_6_7","topic":"Recursion vs loop cost","text":"For simple math methods, a loop is usually:","options":["Slower but easier for the compiler to check","Impossible; some math needs recursion","Identical in speed and memory to recursion","Faster and uses constant stack space"],"correct":3,"explain":"Recursion earns its cost on branching problems, not linear ones."},{"id":"zyf11_6_0","topic":"Naive Fibonacci cost","text":"Recomputing the same subproblems makes naive fib:","options":["O(2^n)","O(log n)","O(1)","O(n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"fib(50) is impractical without memoization."},{"id":"zyf11_6_1","topic":"Memoization","text":"Caching computed results reduces Fibonacci to:","options":["O(n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)","O(2^n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each value is computed once."},{"id":"fs_z11_6_1","topic":"Fibonacci","text":"What does this program print?

