,"correct":1,"explain":"Every unit-11 idea appears in one program.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u12/z12_4":[{"id":"zya12_4_0","topic":"Propagating exceptions","text":"If a method does not catch an exception it throws:","options":["It becomes a warning","The program continues","It propagates up to the caller","It is ignored"],"correct":2,"explain":"It keeps unwinding until something catches it or the program terminates."},{"id":"zya12_4_1","topic":"throws clause","text":"void read() throws IOException declares that:","options":["The method catches IOException internally","Callers must handle or declare IOException","IOException is thrown every time it is called","IOException becomes unchecked for the caller"],"correct":1,"explain":"It is part of the method contract for checked exceptions."},{"id":"zya12_4_2","topic":"Checked vs unchecked","text":"A checked exception:","options":["Must be caught or declared, enforced by the compiler","May be ignored, since the compiler does not check","Is raised only by the JVM, never by user code","Is reported before the program is ever run"],"correct":0,"explain":"IOException is checked; NullPointerException is not."},{"id":"zya12_4_3","topic":"Where to catch","text":"An exception is best caught:","options":["As close to the throw as possible, always","In main, so every failure lands in one place","Inside the method that declared it as thrown","At the level that can actually do something about it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Catching and ignoring at a low level hides real failures."},{"id":"zya12_4_4","topic":"Rethrowing","text":"Catching an exception, logging it, and rethrowing:","options":["Is always wrong; an exception must be handled once","Fails to compile, since the exception is consumed","Is valid when the current level cannot fully handle it","Doubles the exception, so callers see it twice"],"correct":2,"explain":"It records context while letting a higher level decide."},{"id":"zya12_4_5","topic":"Empty catch block","text":"catch (Exception e) { } is dangerous because:","options":["It catches only checked exceptions, missing others","It silently swallows every failure, including bugs","It is a compile error to leave a catch empty","It rethrows the exception after a short delay"],"correct":1,"explain":"The program continues in an unknown state with no evidence anything went wrong."},{"id":"zya12_4_6","topic":"finally with methods","text":"A finally block runs:","options":["Whether or not an exception occurred, even after a return","Only when an exception was actually thrown inside","Only when no exception was thrown at all in the try","Only if the try block completed entirely normally"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why it is used for cleanup."},{"id":"zya12_4_7","topic":"Multiple catch order","text":"Catch blocks must be ordered:","options":["Any order","Most general first","Most specific first, general last","Alphabetically"],"correct":2,"explain":"Catching Exception first makes every later block unreachable — a compile error."},{"id":"zya12_4_8","topic":"Exception in a called method","text":"If method b throws and a calls b without handling it:","options":["The exception is silently discarded by a","a fails to compile until it catches it","The exception propagates out of a as well","The JVM converts it into a runtime warning"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each frame is unwound in turn."},{"id":"zya12_4_9","topic":"Declaring unchecked exceptions","text":"Declaring throws for a RuntimeException is:","options":["Always an error","Required","Optional","Illegal"],"correct":2,"explain":"It can serve as documentation, but nothing forces callers to respond. The compiler does not enforce it."},{"id":"fs_z12_4_1","topic":"First matching catch wins","text":"What does this print?

