static class SortedBox<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private ArrayList<T> items = new ArrayList<>();
    public void add(T item) {
        items.add(item);
        Collections.sort(items);
    }
    public T min() { return items.get(0); }
    public T max() { return items.get(items.size() - 1); }
}

SortedBox<String> box = new SortedBox<>();
box.add("pear");
box.add("apple");
box.add("mango");
System.out.println(box.min());
System.out.println(box.max());","options":["pear
mango","apple
mango","pear
apple","apple
pear"],"correct":3,"explain":"Collections.sort is legal only because the bound guarantees every T is Comparable. Strings sort lexicographically, so after each insertion the list is apple, mango, pear, making min "apple" and max "pear"."},{"id":"fs_z13_4_2","topic":"Bounding a class to Number","text":"What does this print?

static class Stat<T extends Number> {
    private ArrayList<T> values = new ArrayList<>();
    public void add(T v) { values.add(v); }
    public double total() {
        double t = 0;
        for (T v : values) {
            t += v.doubleValue();
        }
        return t;
    }
}

Stat<Integer> s = new Stat<>();
s.add(3);
s.add(4);
System.out.println(s.total());
Stat<Double> d = new Stat<>();
d.add(1.5);
System.out.println(d.total());","options":["7
