"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unit-2 constants discipline in an engineering coat.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4828","topic":"","text":"Formatted output %8.2f for engineering tables provides:","options":["random spacing","color","aligned columns with fixed decimals","sorting"],"correct":2,"explain":"Readable numeric tables - unit 9’s printf earning its keep.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4829","topic":"","text":"The projectile range formula uses Math methods like:","options":["charAt","substring","equals","Math.sin and Math.pow"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Math library is the engineering toolbox.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4830","topic":"","text":"Checking results against hand-computed cases matters because:","options":["physics changes","compilers verify math","it does not","transcription errors fail silently"],"correct":3,"explain":"A misplaced parenthesis yields plausible wrong numbers - test with knowns.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4831","topic":"","text":"These examples show programming serving engineering as:","options":["repeatable calculation at scale","a replacement for physics","decoration","hardware design"],"correct":0,"explain":"One formula, thousands of inputs, formatted output - the value proposition.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u19/z19_3":[{"id":"zya19_3_0","topic":"Method per formula","text":"Turning each formula into a method gives:","options":["Fewer method parameters than the formula has variables","Faster arithmetic than writing the formula inline","A named, independently testable unit with its own guard","Automatic validation without writing any guard code"],"correct":2,"explain":"The program then reads as a list of the physics it knows."},{"id":"zya19_3_1","topic":"Return vs print","text":"A formula method should:","options":["Use a field","Print the result","Return it so the caller can use it","Be void"],"correct":2,"explain":"A returned value can be reused, formatted, summed, or tested."},{"id":"zya19_3_2","topic":"Guard placement","text":"The zero-resistance check belongs:","options":["Inside the current method, before dividing","In main, where all validation is supposed to live","After the division, where the bad result can be spotted","Nowhere - dividing by zero just returns zero for doubles"],"correct":0,"explain":"The method owns the division, so it owns the guard."},{"id":"zya19_3_3","topic":"Parameters","text":"A method computing force takes:","options":["An array","No parameters","Mass and acceleration","Only mass"],"correct":2,"explain":"Everything it needs comes in as parameters, making it pure and testable."},{"id":"zya19_3_4","topic":"Adding a formula","text":"Adding a new formula to a decomposed program:","options":["Is impossible","Requires rewriting main","Means adding one method","Changes every method"],"correct":2,"explain":"Existing methods are untouched, which is the point of decomposition."},{"id":"zya19_3_5","topic":"Testing formulas","text":"A formula method is easy to test because:","options":["It prints its result, so correctness is visible on screen","The same inputs always produce the same output, with no hidden state","It is static, and static methods cannot contain bugs","It returns void, which simplifies every assertion"],"correct":1,"explain":"Check current(12, 4) == 3.0 without running the whole program."},{"id":"zya19_3_6","topic":"Static or instance","text":"A pure formula method is naturally:","options":["private","An instance method","static","abstract"],"correct":2,"explain":"Math.sqrt follows exactly this pattern. It needs no object state."},{"id":"zya19_3_7","topic":"Naming","text":"A method computing current is best named:","options":["method1","calc","current or calculateCurrent","doIt"],"correct":2,"explain":"The name should say what the returned value is."},{"id":"zyf19_3_0","topic":"Guard location","text":"A divide-by-zero guard belongs:","options":["In the caller, which knows the value's business meaning best","After the division, paired with a rollback of the result","Nowhere - the JVM raises a clear error on its own","Inside the method that performs the division"],"correct":3,"explain":"The method that divides owns the guard."},{"id":"zyf19_3_1","topic":"Return not print","text":"A formula method should return its value because:","options":["Printing inside the method keeps the caller's code shorter","The caller can then reuse, format or test it","Java requires every static method to return something","Returning skips the stack frame and runs faster"],"correct":1,"explain":"A printing method cannot be reused."},{"id":"fs_z19_3_1","topic":"Formula method trace","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println(celsiusFromF(212));

where:

public static double celsiusFromF(double f) {
  return (f - 32) * 5.0 / 9.0;
}","options":["82.22222222222223","0.0","100.0","100"],"correct":2,"explain":"The int argument 212 widens to double 212.0; (212.0 - 32) * 5.0 / 9.0 = 100.0, and println for a double always shows a decimal point. 212 F is the boiling point, 100 C - a good known value to test against."},{"id":"fs_z19_3_2","topic":"Epsilon comparison","text":"A test for a formula method is written as Math.abs(celsiusFromF(212) - 100) < 0.0001 rather than celsiusFromF(212) == 100. Why?","options":["Math.abs runs faster than ==","The tolerance makes the test pass even when the formula is wrong","Floating-point results must be compared within a tolerance, never with ==","== does not compile for doubles"],"correct":2,"explain":"Doubles carry rounding error, so exact == comparison can fail on values that are mathematically equal. Comparing the difference against a small epsilon is the standard, correct pattern for floating-point tests."},{"id":"fs_z19_3_3","topic":"Guard inside the method","text":"What does this print?

System.out.println(current(12, 4));
System.out.println(current(12, 0));

where:

public static double current(double v, double r) {
  if (r == 0) return 0;
  return v / r;
}","options":["3.0 then an exception","3 then 0","3.0 then 0.0","3.0 then Infinity"],"correct":2,"explain":"current(12, 4) is 3.0. For r == 0 the guard returns 0, which as a double prints 0.0. Without the guard, 12.0 / 0.0 would print Infinity - double division by zero does not throw."},{"id":"fs_z19_3_4","topic":"Precondition checks","text":"parallelResistance throws IllegalArgumentException when either resistance is zero or negative. This pattern is called:","options":["A checked exception declaration","Overloading","A precondition check","An epsilon comparison"],"correct":2,"explain":"Validating inputs before computing is a precondition check, and putting it inside the method means the rule is written once rather than at every call site. IllegalArgumentException is the conventional signal for a bad argument. Rejecting physically impossible inputs at the method boundary."},{"id":"zf4832","topic":"","text":"Wrapping toCelsius(double f) as a method gives:","options":["faster math","a named, testable, reusable conversion","new physics","GUI support"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unit 6 discipline applied to formulas.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4833","topic":"","text":"toCelsius(212) with (f - 32) * 5 / 9 returns:","options":["180.0","212.0","100.0","0.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"Boiling point: (212-32) x 5/9.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4834","topic":"","text":"toCelsius(32) returns:","options":["32.0","-32.0","17.8","0.0"],"correct":3,"explain":"Freezing point maps to zero.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4835","topic":"","text":"A method per formula lets a table generator:","options":["loop over inputs calling the method","inline every formula","skip loops","avoid printing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Composition: loop + method + printf.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4836","topic":"","text":"Testing the conversion method uses:","options":["random values only","anchors like 32 -> 0 and 212 -> 100","no tests","GUI clicks"],"correct":1,"explain":"Physical constants provide free expected values.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4837","topic":"","text":"static double bmi(double kg, double m) { return kg / (m * m); } bmi(80, 2.0) returns:","options":["40.0","160.0","20.0","20"],"correct":2,"explain":"80 / 4 = 20.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4838","topic":"","text":"Parameter ORDER mistakes in bmi(m, kg) produce:","options":["compile errors","crashes","warnings","plausible but wrong numbers"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same-type swaps sail through - unit 6’s warning, engineering stakes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4839","topic":"","text":"A pipeline of methods, pressure(depth(reading)):","options":["composes verified stages","is illegal","confuses Java","loses precision always"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each stage tested alone; the chain inherits the trust.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4840","topic":"","text":"Units in NAMES, like metersToFeet:","options":["prevent unit-mixup bugs at calls","are decoration","slow code","are enforced by Java"],"correct":0,"explain":"The Mars-orbiter lesson: say the units everywhere.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4841","topic":"","text":"Returning double from formula methods (not printing inside) enables:","options":["nothing","reuse in tables, GUIs, and files alike","printing twice","faster math"],"correct":1,"explain":"Compute-return-let-callers-present - separation of concerns.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4842","topic":"","text":"The engineering-methods lesson mirrors the course’s arc:","options":["physics is special","methods are optional","decompose, name, validate, test","formulas resist code"],"correct":2,"explain":"The same software discipline, portable across fields.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u19/z19_5":[{"id":"zya19_5_0","topic":"Filename from arguments","text":"Taking a filename from the command line uses:","options":["args[0]","args.length","System.in","args[1]"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first argument is at index 0 — the program name is not included."},{"id":"zya19_5_1","topic":"Checking arguments","text":"Before using args[0] you must verify:","options":["args.length is at least 1","args[0] is an int","Nothing","args is sorted"],"correct":0,"explain":"Otherwise ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException."},{"id":"zya19_5_2","topic":"Opening the file","text":"Creating a Scanner on a file requires:","options":["new Scanner(new File(args[0]))","new File(args[0]).read()","System.in","new Scanner(args[0])"],"correct":0,"explain":"Passing the String directly makes a Scanner that parses the filename text itself — a subtle and common bug."},{"id":"zya19_5_3","topic":"Checked exception","text":"Opening the file can throw:","options":["FileNotFoundException","ArithmeticException","Nothing","NullPointerException"],"correct":0,"explain":"The compiler enforces handling. Must be caught or declared."},{"id":"zya19_5_4","topic":"try-with-resources","text":"Wrapping the Scanner in try-with-resources:","options":["Is required before any Scanner method can be called","Guarantees the file exists before opening begins","Closes it automatically on every exit path","Reopens the file automatically if reading fails midway"],"correct":2,"explain":"Including when an exception is thrown mid-read."},{"id":"zya19_5_5","topic":"Relative path","text":"A relative filename resolves against:","options":["The folder containing the compiled .class files always","The working directory of the running program","The user's home directory on every operating system","The directory where the JDK itself is installed"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which explains why it can work in an IDE and fail from a terminal."},{"id":"zya19_5_7","topic":"Multiple arguments","text":"Reading an input and an output filename uses:","options":["args[1] and args[2], since args[0] holds the program name","A Scanner prompt, since filenames cannot be arguments","args[0] alone, with the output name derived from it","args[0] and args[1], after checking length >= 2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Indices are zero-based and the length check must cover both."},{"id":"zyf19_5_0","topic":"Opening the file","text":"Creating a Scanner from a filename argument uses:","options":["new Scanner(args[0])","new Scanner(new File(args[0]))","new File(args[0])","System.in"],"correct":1,"explain":"Passing the String directly parses the filename text itself."},{"id":"zyf19_5_1","topic":"Resource closing","text":"try-with-resources guarantees the Scanner is:","options":["Flushed to disk but left open for later reuse","Closed on every exit path, including exceptions","Reopened automatically the next time it is used","Exempt from closing because Scanners are lightweight"],"correct":1,"explain":"The path that is easy to forget manually."},{"id":"fs_z19_5_1","topic":"Multiple resources","text":"A copy program declares both files in one statement:

try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(args[0])); PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(args[1])) { ... }

When the block ends:","options":["Both resources are closed automatically, in reverse order of declaration","Only the first resource is closed","The programmer must call close() on each","The files stay open until the JVM exits"],"correct":0,"explain":"try-with-resources closes every declared resource automatically, last-declared first. Closing the PrintWriter matters especially: it flushes buffered output, without which the copied file would be truncated."},{"id":"fs_z19_5_2","topic":"Relative path mystery","text":"A program opens data.txt successfully when run from the IDE but reports 'Cannot open' when run from a terminal. The most likely cause is:","options":["The relative path resolves against a different working directory in each case","The terminal cannot read text files","The file was deleted between runs","Scanner only works inside an IDE"],"correct":0,"explain":"A relative path resolves against the directory the program was LAUNCHED from, not where the source lives. The IDE and the terminal launch from different directories, so the same relative name finds different places."},{"id":"fs_z19_5_3","topic":"Helpful error reporting","text":"When the file named on the command line cannot be opened, a good error message should include:","options":["The actual filename from args[0]","Only the words 'an error occurred'","The full stack trace and nothing else","The contents of the working directory"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reporting the name the program actually tried - args[0] - lets the user see typos and path mistakes immediately. A vague message hides exactly the information needed to fix the problem. The mismatch is visible."},{"id":"fs_z19_5_4","topic":"Write then read trace","text":"What does this print?

try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter("demo_copy.txt")) {
  out.println("line one");
  out.println("line two");
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
  System.out.println("cannot open");
}
int count = 0;
try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File("demo_copy.txt"))) {
  while (in.hasNextLine()) {
    in.nextLine();
    count++;
  }
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
  System.out.println("cannot open");
}
System.out.println(count + " lines");","options":["2 lines","0 lines","cannot open
0 lines","1 lines"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first try-with-resources closes (and therefore flushes) the PrintWriter when its block ends, so both lines are on disk before the Scanner opens the file and counts them. Without that close, the read could see a truncated file."},{"id":"zf4854","topic":"","text":"A program taking input and output filenames as args opens:","options":["two Scanners","a reader on args[0], writer on args[1]","two PrintWriters","GUI dialogs"],"correct":1,"explain":"The read-process-write pipeline, parameterized by the command line.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4855","topic":"","text":"The benefit over hard-coded filenames is:","options":["speed","smaller class files","any files served without recompiling","none"],"correct":2,"explain":"Generality via parameters - unit 6’s lesson at program scale.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4856","topic":"","text":"java Stats data1.txt with a missing data1.txt:","options":["creates it","reads zeros","loops","throws FileNotFoundException to handle"],"correct":3,"explain":"Argument validity does not guarantee file existence - two separate checks.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4857","topic":"","text":"Checking args.length BEFORE opening files:","options":["orders the failure modes helpfully","is redundant","replaces try/catch","slows startup"],"correct":0,"explain":"Usage errors and I/O errors get distinct, accurate messages.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4858","topic":"","text":"A batch grader java Grade scores.txt report.txt demonstrates:","options":["GUIs","automation with no human interaction","recursion","threads"],"correct":1,"explain":"Scriptable programs - the command-line’s enduring power.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4859","topic":"","text":"Reading filename1 while accidentally writing to it as output:","options":["is harmless","cannot happen","destroys the input","warns"],"correct":2,"explain":"Document and validate the expected order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4860","topic":"","text":"An optional flag like -v among args is detected by:","options":["the JVM","Scanner","scanning args for the flag string","file headers"],"correct":2,"explain":"Simple flag parsing: loop and compare.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4861","topic":"","text":"Programs composed via files (one’s output, another’s input):","options":["are forbidden","need sockets","share memory","form processing pipelines"],"correct":3,"explain":"Small tools chained by files - a durable architecture.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4862","topic":"","text":"Appending vs overwriting the output file is chosen by:","options":["the FileOutputStream append flag","the OS randomly","file size","luck"],"correct":0,"explain":"The same output-mode decision, now argument-driven.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4863","topic":"","text":"Testing such a program uses:","options":["typing per run","prepared inputs and expected outputs","GUIs","no tests"],"correct":1,"explain":"Deterministic files in, comparable files out - ideal test harness material.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4864","topic":"","text":"The wc-like exercise (count lines/words of args[0]) combines:","options":["GUIs and threads","recursion only","args, file reading, loops, and counters","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"A classic small tool touching half the course.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4865","topic":"","text":"Reporting results to System.out while errors go to System.err:","options":["is impossible","confuses users","doubles output","keeps data and diagnostics separable"],"correct":3,"explain":"The stream separation of unit 9, motivated at last.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u19/z19_8":[{"id":"zya19_8_0","topic":"Lookup approach","text":"Decoding abbreviations to full words is naturally done with:","options":["A recursive scan comparing one character per call","A chain of nested loops over the alphabet","Parallel arrays or a HashMap of abbreviation to word","Character arithmetic on the abbreviation's letters"],"correct":2,"explain":"A map makes adding an entry a data change rather than a code change."},{"id":"zya19_8_1","topic":"Parallel array lookup","text":"With parallel arrays, you find the word by:","options":["Binary-searching the abbreviation array even though it is unsorted","Scanning the abbreviation array for a match, then reading the same index","Hashing the abbreviation itself directly into an array index","Sorting both arrays first so that matching indexes line up again"],"correct":1,"explain":"The index is the link between the two arrays."},{"id":"zya19_8_2","topic":"Case handling","text":"To accept both "ny" and "NY":","options":["Use equalsIgnoreCase, or normalize the input case first","Compare with ==, which ignores case for short strings","Store every possible casing of each abbreviation","Sort the input so case differences cancel out"],"correct":0,"explain":"Normalizing once at the boundary is usually cleaner than repeating it."},{"id":"zya19_8_3","topic":"Not found","text":"An unknown abbreviation should:","options":["Return the abbreviation silently","Return null","Crash","Produce a clear "unknown" message"],"correct":3,"explain":"Silent failure leaves the user unsure whether the program worked."},{"id":"zya19_8_4","topic":"HashMap lookup","text":"map.get("NY") for an absent key returns:","options":["A default","An exception","An empty String","null"],"correct":3,"explain":"Use getOrDefault to supply a fallback safely."},{"id":"zya19_8_5","topic":"Loop over input","text":"Decoding several abbreviations in one line uses:","options":["charAt in a loop, splitting wherever a capital appears","split on whitespace, then a loop over the tokens","One equals comparison against the whole line","A recursive call per character of the line"],"correct":1,"explain":"Each token is looked up in turn."},{"id":"zya19_8_6","topic":"Adding an entry","text":"With a map-based decoder, adding an abbreviation means:","options":["Adding one put call or one data entry","Adding an else-if branch to the lookup method","Rebuilding and re-sorting the whole structure","Editing every call site that performs a lookup"],"correct":0,"explain":"Separating data from logic is the design goal here."},{"id":"zya19_8_7","topic":"Efficiency","text":"HashMap lookup is:","options":["O(log n)","O(n^2)","O(n)","O(1) on average"],"correct":3,"explain":"Versus O(n) for scanning parallel arrays."},{"id":"zyf19_8_0","topic":"Lookup structure","text":"Mapping abbreviations to full names is best held in:","options":["A boolean","A single String","A HashMap or parallel arrays","An int array"],"correct":2,"explain":"A map makes adding an entry a data change."},{"id":"zyf19_8_1","topic":"Unknown code","text":"An abbreviation not present should produce:","options":["The abbreviation itself","A crash","A clear "unknown" message","Silence"],"correct":2,"explain":"Silent failure hides the problem from the user."},{"id":"fs_z19_8_1","topic":"Normalised lookup trace","text":"What does this print?

HashMap<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("BTW", "by the way");
map.put("FYI", "for your information");
String input = " btw ";
System.out.println(map.getOrDefault(input.trim().toUpperCase(), "unknown"));","options":["It throws an exception","by the way","unknown","null"],"correct":1,"explain":"trim() strips the spaces and toUpperCase() gives "BTW", which matches the stored key, so the value prints. Without normalisation, " btw " would miss the key and getOrDefault would return "unknown"."},{"id":"fs_z19_8_2","topic":"Case-sensitive keys","text":"What does this print?

HashMap<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("BTW", "by the way");
System.out.println(map.get("btw"));","options":["It throws an exception","null","by the way","unknown"],"correct":1,"explain":"HashMap lookup is case-sensitive: "btw" and "BTW" are different keys, so get returns null (not an exception). This is why decoders normalise both the stored keys and the query to one case."},{"id":"fs_z19_8_3","topic":"Parallel array fragility","text":"The main structural risk of the parallel-arrays version of a decoder (String[] abbr and String[] full) is:","options":["The arrays sort themselves alphabetically","An edit to one array but not the other silently misaligns every later lookup","The arrays cannot hold Strings","Lookups throw exceptions for missing keys"],"correct":1,"explain":"The two arrays are linked only by index: insert or remove in one without the other and abbr[i] no longer corresponds to full[i] - with no error to tell you. A map keeps each key physically paired with its value, so it cannot fall out of step."},{"id":"fs_z19_8_4","topic":"getOrDefault vs get","text":"map.getOrDefault(key, "unknown") differs from map.get(key) in that it:","options":["Ignores the case of the key","Returns "unknown" instead of null when the key is absent","Adds the key to the map when absent","Throws an exception when the key is absent"],"correct":1,"explain":"getOrDefault supplies a fallback value for missing keys, removing the need for a null check at the call site. It does not insert anything and it is still case-sensitive."},{"id":"zf4888","topic":"","text":"A decoder mapping "brb" to "be right back" is naturally stored in:","options":["a HashMap<String, String>","two ints","an if per entry forever","a file only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Key-value lookup - unit 14’s map earning its keep.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4889","topic":"","text":"map.get("brb") returns:","options":["always text","the expansion, or null if unknown","an index","a boolean"],"correct":1,"explain":"The null case IS the unknown-abbreviation case - handle it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4890","topic":"","text":"Handling unknown abbreviations well means:","options":["crashing","echoing the original","skipping output","guessing"],"correct":1,"explain":"getOrDefault(word, word) - graceful degradation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4891","topic":"","text":"Decoding a whole sentence uses:","options":["one giant replace","charAt loops only","split on spaces, map each word, rejoin","recursion"],"correct":2,"explain":"The split-transform-join pipeline over words.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4892","topic":"","text":""lol brb" through the decoder with both mapped becomes:","options":["unchanged","one word","an error","the two expansions joined with a space"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each token translates; the join restores the sentence shape.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4893","topic":"","text":"Case-insensitive decoding ("BRB") is achieved by:","options":["lowercasing tokens before lookup","duplicate entries for every casing","luck","uppercase maps"],"correct":0,"explain":"Normalize-then-lookup - the canonical-form trick again.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4894","topic":"","text":"Loading the abbreviation table from a file makes the decoder:","options":["slower and worse","data-driven - edit entries, not code","impossible","GUI-based"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unit 9 reading + unit 14 storage = maintainable tools.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4895","topic":"","text":"Each file line "brb=be right back" is parsed by:","options":["charAt math","substring guesses","split on the = into key and value","JSON"],"correct":2,"explain":"One-line records with a delimiter - the simple config format.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4896","topic":"","text":"Punctuation ("brb!") complicates lookup because:","options":["maps ban symbols","it does not","files reject it","the token no longer matches the key"],"correct":3,"explain":"Strip or separate punctuation before lookup - real text is messy.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4897","topic":"","text":"The reverse operation (encode long phrases to abbreviations) needs:","options":["a second map from expansion to abbreviation","the same map used backwards directly","no map","sorting"],"correct":0,"explain":"HashMaps are one-directional; invert into a sibling map.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4898","topic":"","text":"The exercise’s lesson is:","options":["strings are hard","translation problems become lookups","loops are old","maps are rare"],"correct":1,"explain":"Dictionary problems want dictionary structures.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u19/z19_9":[{"id":"zya19_9_0","topic":"Simulating a die","text":"A fair six-sided die roll is:","options":["r.nextInt(6) + 1","r.nextInt(7)","Math.random() * 6","r.nextInt(6)"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextInt(6) gives 0-5, so add 1 for 1-6."},{"id":"zya19_9_1","topic":"Two dice sum","text":"Rolling two dice should be:","options":["(r.nextInt(6)+1) + (r.nextInt(6)+1)","r.nextInt(12) + 1","r.nextInt(6) * 2","r.nextInt(11) + 2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Summing two independent rolls — the distribution is not uniform, which the first option wrongly assumes."},{"id":"zya19_9_2","topic":"Counting outcomes","text":"Tallying how often each total appears uses:","options":["An array indexed by the total","A String","Recursion","One variable"],"correct":0,"explain":"counts[total]++ is the standard frequency-counting idiom."},{"id":"zya19_9_3","topic":"Array sizing","text":"For two-dice totals of 2 to 12, the counts array needs:","options":["size 12 exactly, since the largest total is twelve","size 6, one slot for each face of a single die","size 11 with an offset, or size 13 ignoring 0 and 1","size 36, one slot for every ordered dice pair"],"correct":2,"explain":"Either subtract 2 from the index or allocate a slightly larger array and ignore the unused slots."},{"id":"zya19_9_4","topic":"Computing the average","text":"The mean roll is:","options":["(double) sum / count","sum % count","sum * count","sum / count with both ints"],"correct":0,"explain":"Cast before dividing."},{"id":"zya19_9_5","topic":"Expected value","text":"The expected average of one fair die is:","options":["3.5","4.0","6.0","3.0"],"correct":0,"explain":"(1+2+3+4+5+6)/6 = 3.5, which is a useful sanity check on the simulation."},{"id":"zya19_9_6","topic":"More trials","text":"Increasing the number of rolls makes the observed average:","options":["Converge toward the expected value","Stay constant","Become exact","Diverge"],"correct":0,"explain":"The law of large numbers — and a good way to verify the simulation is fair."},{"id":"zya19_9_7","topic":"Reproducible runs","text":"To repeat the exact same sequence while debugging:","options":["Seed the Random with a fixed value","Sort the results","Use a loop","Use Math.random"],"correct":0,"explain":"new Random(42) makes the run reproducible."},{"id":"zya19_9_8","topic":"Percentage output","text":"Reporting each total as a percentage uses:","options":["100.0 * count / total","count * total","count % total","count / total"],"correct":0,"explain":"Starting with 100.0 forces floating-point division."},{"id":"zyf19_9_0","topic":"Die roll","text":"A fair six-sided die is simulated with:","options":["Math.random() * 6","r.nextInt(6)","r.nextInt(6) + 1","r.nextInt(7)"],"correct":2,"explain":"nextInt(6) yields 0-5, so add 1."},{"id":"fs_z19_9_1","topic":"Die roll range","text":"What does this print?

Random rand = new Random();
int min = 99, max = -99;
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
  int roll = rand.nextInt(6) + 1;
  if (roll < min) min = roll;
  if (roll > max) max = roll;
}
System.out.println(min + " " + max);","options":["1 6","0 5","0 6","1 5"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextInt(6) returns 0 to 5, and + 1 shifts the range to 1 to 6. Over 100,000 rolls every face appears, so the minimum seen is 1 and the maximum 6. Forgetting the + 1 gives the 0-5 range - the classic mistake."},{"id":"fs_z19_9_2","topic":"Histogram indexing","text":"What does this print?

int[] counts = new int[13];
int[] rolls = {7, 7, 11, 7};
for (int roll : rolls) {
  counts[roll]++;
}
System.out.println(counts[7] + " " + counts[11] + " " + counts[2]);","options":["3 1 0","7 11 2","3 1","It throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException"],"correct":0,"explain":"The outcome IS the index: three 7s make counts[7] = 3, one 11 makes counts[11] = 1, and counts[2] was never incremented so it holds its default 0. Sizing the array 13 lets totals 2-12 index directly, wasting slots 0 and 1."},{"id":"fs_z19_9_3","topic":"Two dice vs one range","text":"Why is rand.nextInt(11) + 2 NOT a correct simulation of rolling two six-sided dice?","options":["It makes every total 2-12 equally likely, but real two-dice totals peak at 7","Its range is 2 to 13, one too high","nextInt cannot take 11 as an argument","It always returns even numbers"],"correct":0,"explain":"nextInt(11) + 2 does give 2-12, but uniformly. Summing two dice gives a triangular distribution: 7 can occur six ways while 2 has only one, so 7 is six times as likely. The distribution, not the range, is what is wrong."},{"id":"fs_z19_9_4","topic":"Seeded repeatability","text":"What does this print?

Random a = new Random(42);
Random b = new Random(42);
System.out.println(a.nextInt(100) == b.nextInt(100));","options":["true","false","Sometimes true, sometimes false","It does not compile"],"correct":0,"explain":"Two Random objects built from the same seed produce the same sequence, so their first values are equal - always. Fixed seeding is what makes a simulation repeatable and therefore testable."},{"id":"zf4899","topic":"","text":"Rolling one die in Java is:","options":["rand.nextInt(6)","Math.random() * 7","rand.nextInt(6) + 1","nextInt(1, 6)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Six values shifted to 1..6 - unit 2’s random recipe.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4900","topic":"","text":"The sum of TWO dice ranges over:","options":["1 through 12","2 through 12","2 through 6","0 through 12"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two minimums to two maximums.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4901","topic":"","text":"Counting sum frequencies over many rolls uses:","options":["a string","one counter","an int array indexed by sum","a GUI"],"correct":2,"explain":"counts[sum]++ - unit 5 arrays as histogram bins.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4902","topic":"","text":"The most frequent two-dice sum in a long simulation is:","options":["2","12","6","7"],"correct":3,"explain":"Six of 36 combinations make 7 - the peak of the distribution.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4903","topic":"","text":"Rolling 2 dice 3,600 times, sevens appear roughly:","options":["600 times","100","3,600","36"],"correct":0,"explain":"6/36 of the rolls - probability made visible by simulation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4904","topic":"","text":"Percentages per sum are computed as:","options":["count / totalRolls with ints","count * 100.0 / totalRolls","count * 100 / 0","count alone"],"correct":1,"explain":"The double arithmetic avoids the int-division-zero classic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4905","topic":"","text":"A text histogram prints per sum:","options":["random stars","the count only","one * per counted unit (or scale)","colors"],"correct":2,"explain":"Loops drawing proportional bars - 19.6’s art serving 19.9’s data.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4906","topic":"","text":"Seeding the Random generator makes runs:","options":["more random","slower","identical to real dice","reproducible for testing"],"correct":3,"explain":"Fixed seed, fixed sequence - deterministic tests of stochastic code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4907","topic":"","text":"More rolls make the observed frequencies:","options":["approach the theoretical probabilities","diverge","equal exactly always","random"],"correct":0,"explain":"The law of large numbers, watched from a for loop.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4908","topic":"","text":"The bins array for two-dice sums is comfortably sized:","options":["6","13 (index 0-12, using 2-12)","12","36"],"correct":1,"explain":"Index-by-sum with a couple of unused low slots - simple beats clever.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4909","topic":"","text":"This exercise integrates:","options":["GUIs and files","recursion","randomness, arrays, loops, formatting","generics"],"correct":2,"explain":"Simulation as a capstone of the core units.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u19/z19_10":[{"id":"zya19_10_0","topic":"Output comparison","text":"The zyBooks auto-grader compares your output:","options":["Not at all","Loosely, ignoring spacing","Exactly, character for character","Only the numbers"],"correct":2,"explain":"A missing space or extra blank line fails a test even when the logic is right."},{"id":"zya19_10_1","topic":"Prompts in tests","text":"When input comes from a test rather than a person you should:","options":["Print the prompts anyway - graders strip them automatically","Write prompts to System.err so they stay out of stdout","Omit prompts unless the expected output includes them","Print extra debug lines to show the grader your work"],"correct":2,"explain":"An unexpected prompt line makes the output mismatch."},{"id":"zya19_10_2","topic":"Reading test input","text":"Test input is supplied:","options":["Typed live by the grader while your program waits","Automatically to standard input, exactly as the test defines","Passed as command-line arguments in every case","Pasted into your source file before compilation"],"correct":1,"explain":"Scanner reads it the same way it would read typed input."},{"id":"zya19_10_3","topic":"Diagnosing a failure","text":"When a test fails, first compare:","options":["The expected output against your actual output","Your source code against the reference solution's code","The compiler version against the grader's version","The file name against the assignment's requirements"],"correct":0,"explain":"A large share of failures are formatting rather than logic."},{"id":"zya19_10_4","topic":"Trailing newline","text":"An extra blank line at the end:","options":["Is required","Is ignored","Can fail an exact-match test","Improves readability"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which is why println versus print matters on the last line."},{"id":"zya19_10_5","topic":"Developing in the window","text":"The recommended approach is:","options":["Write the entire program first, then run all tests once","Submit early drafts repeatedly and patch whatever fails","Build incrementally, getting one test passing at a time","Skip the visible tests and target the hidden ones"],"correct":2,"explain":"The same incremental development principle from 4.8."},{"id":"zya19_10_6","topic":"Debug output","text":"Leaving debug prints in submitted code:","options":["Is required","Is fine","Will fail exact-output tests","Improves the grade"],"correct":2,"explain":"Remove them before submitting."},{"id":"zya19_10_7","topic":"Multiple test cases","text":"Passing one test but failing others usually means:","options":["Your logic handles the common case but not an edge case","The grader is comparing against the wrong expected file","A compile error that only affects some test runs","Random variation in how the tests supply their input"],"correct":0,"explain":"Look at which input differs, since that is where the missing branch is."},{"id":"zyf19_10_0","topic":"Exact comparison","text":"Auto-graded output is compared:","options":["Loosely","Character for character","Numerically only","Not at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"A single missing space fails the test."},{"id":"zyf19_10_1","topic":"Prompts","text":"When input comes from a test harness you should:","options":["Prompt anyway, since harnesses ignore extra output","Omit prompts unless the expected output includes them","Route all prompts to System.err instead of removing them","Add debug output so failures are easier to diagnose"],"correct":1,"explain":"An unexpected prompt breaks the match."},{"id":"fs_z19_10_1","topic":"Hidden test failures","text":"A submission passes every visible test but fails only the hidden one. The usual cause is:","options":["A syntax error the compiler missed","The hidden test uses a different programming language","An untested edge case","The auto-grader compares hidden tests differently"],"correct":2,"explain":"Hidden tests exist to probe the boundaries you did not try: empty input, zero, one element, the largest allowed value. Checking those cases yourself before submitting is the reliable defence. Empty input, zero, or a single element."},{"id":"fs_z19_10_2","topic":"Formatting counts as correctness","text":"A program computes exactly the right value but prints it with %.2f where the expected output shows one decimal place. The auto-grader:","options":["Passes it with a formatting warning","Rounds both outputs before comparing","Fails the test - the comparison is exact, and formatting differences fail like wrong answers","Passes it, since the value is numerically correct"],"correct":2,"explain":"Output is compared character by character, so "84.67" against an expected "84.7" is simply a mismatch. Most auto-grader failures are formatting, not logic - compare the expected output character by character first."},{"id":"fs_z19_10_3","topic":"print vs println","text":"The difference between System.out.print and System.out.println is:","options":["println flushes to a file; print writes to the screen","print only accepts Strings","println appends a newline after the output; print does not","print is faster but otherwise identical"],"correct":2,"explain":"println ends the line; print leaves the cursor where it is. Under exact-match grading, a missing final newline - or an extra one - is precisely the kind of invisible difference that fails a test."},{"id":"fs_z19_10_4","topic":"Prompts under test input","text":"An auto-graded run shows the program's prompt text tangled into the output difference. The cause is:","options":["Scanner cannot read from files","The program printed too few newlines","Printing a prompt while the test supplies input silently from a file","The test file was corrupted"],"correct":2,"explain":"When tests feed input from a file, nobody needs a prompt - but a printed prompt still counts as output and appears in the comparison. When the specification says input is supplied, read without prompting."},{"id":"zf4910","topic":"","text":"The zyBooks auto-grader compares output:","options":["by meaning","by keyword","character by character vs expected","by length"],"correct":2,"explain":"Exact match - the section’s central fact.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4911","topic":"","text":"A correct calculation can still FAIL the grader because:","options":["graders err often","Java differs there","points are random","spacing, casing, or newlines differ"],"correct":3,"explain":"Format failures dominate - check output before logic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4912","topic":"","text":"println vs print differ, for the grader, by:","options":["the newline println appends","speed","fonts","nothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"A missing final newline is a classic invisible failure.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4913","topic":"","text":"Input in graded tests comes from:","options":["the keyboard always","a supplied file/stream, not live typing","the GUI","args only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is why unprompted reading is sometimes specified.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4914","topic":"","text":"Printing a prompt when the test supplies input silently:","options":["is required","is ignored","adds text the comparison rejects","helps"],"correct":2,"explain":"Follow the specification about prompts exactly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4915","topic":"","text":""Fails only on the hidden test" usually means:","options":["grader bugs","bad luck","slow code","an untested edge case"],"correct":3,"explain":"The visible tests are samples; edge cases lurk in the hidden ones.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4916","topic":"","text":"A trailing space at line end:","options":["fails the exact comparison","is trimmed","is invisible to graders","helps alignment"],"correct":0,"explain":"Print separators BETWEEN items, not after the last.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4917","topic":"","text":"%.2f when the expected shows one decimal:","options":["rounds fine","fails - match the specified precision","is closer","warns"],"correct":1,"explain":"Format specifiers must mirror the expected output exactly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4918","topic":"","text":"Before rereading your algorithm on a failure, first:","options":["rewrite everything","add prompts","diff actual vs expected, char by char","change languages"],"correct":2,"explain":"Most failures are formatting - look there first.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4919","topic":"","text":"State left from a previous run causing later test failures suggests:","options":["graders leak","Java caches","skip tests","reinitialize everything per run"],"correct":3,"explain":"Fresh state per execution - the harness runs your main repeatedly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4920","topic":"","text":"The transferable lesson beyond zyBooks is:","options":["graders are unfair","formatting is trivia","only logic matters","output is an interface"],"correct":3,"explain":"Real systems parse each other’s output; exactness is not pedantry.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u14/z14_2":[{"id":"zya14_2_0","topic":"LinkedList interface","text":"LinkedList implements the same interface as ArrayList, which is:","options":["Queue only","Set","List","Map"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are Lists, so the method names are identical — what differs is the internal storage."},{"id":"zya14_2_1","topic":"get cost","text":"Calling list.get(i) on a LinkedList is:","options":["Free","O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"There is no address arithmetic — the nodes are scattered on the heap. It must walk the chain."},{"id":"zya14_2_2","topic":"addFirst cost","text":"Adding at the front of a LinkedList is:","options":["Impossible","O(n)","O(1)","O(log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"An ArrayList would have to shift every element right. Relink two nodes."},{"id":"zya14_2_3","topic":"When to choose LinkedList","text":"LinkedList is preferable when the code:","options":["Uses binary search","Calls get(i) in a loop","Adds and removes mostly at the ends","Sorts frequently"],"correct":2,"explain":"Queue and undo-stack workloads suit it; index-heavy work does not."},{"id":"zya14_2_4","topic":"Indexed loop trap","text":"for (int i=0;i&lt;list.size();i++) list.get(i) on a LinkedList is:","options":["O(1)","O(n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Use a for-each loop instead, which walks the chain exactly once. Each get restarts the walk."},{"id":"zya14_2_5","topic":"Memory overhead","text":"A LinkedList uses more memory than an ArrayList because:","options":["It reserves spare capacity for future growth","Each node carries extra references alongside the data","It stores a copy of each element for safety","It keeps the elements sorted at all times"],"correct":1,"explain":"Two references per node in a doubly linked list."},{"id":"zya14_2_6","topic":"LinkedList-only methods","text":"Which method exists on LinkedList but not on ArrayList?","options":["get","add","addFirst","size"],"correct":2,"explain":"addFirst, addLast, getFirst and removeFirst come from the Deque interface."},{"id":"zya14_2_7","topic":"Adding at the end","text":"Appending to a LinkedList is:","options":["Impossible","O(n)","O(1)","O(log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both list types append cheaply; the difference shows at the front and middle. It keeps a tail reference."},{"id":"zya14_2_8","topic":"Declaring to the interface","text":"List&lt;String> x = new LinkedList&lt;>(); allows:","options":["Storing elements of any type in the list","Calling LinkedList-specific methods directly","Swapping the implementation by changing one line","Automatic conversion between the two types"],"correct":2,"explain":"The trade-off is that LinkedList-specific methods are then unavailable."},{"id":"zya14_2_9","topic":"Cache behaviour","text":"ArrayList often outperforms LinkedList in practice because:","options":["It stores fewer elements per unit of memory","Contiguous memory is far more cache-friendly","Its insertions at the front are constant time","It avoids the cost of resizing entirely"],"correct":1,"explain":"Big-O hides constant factors, and here they are large."},{"id":"fs_z14_2_1","topic":"LinkedList end operations","text":"What does this print?

LinkedList<String> list = new LinkedList<>();
list.add("b");
list.addFirst("a");
list.addLast("c");
System.out.println(list);
System.out.println(list.removeFirst() + " " + list.getFirst());","options":["[a, b, c]
a a","[a, b, c]
b c","[a, b, c]
a b","[b, a, c]
b a"],"correct":2,"explain":"add appends, addFirst puts a at the head and addLast puts c at the tail, giving [a, b, c]. removeFirst returns and deletes a, so the later getFirst sees b."},{"id":"fs_z14_2_2","topic":"push on a LinkedList","text":"What does this print?

LinkedList<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
q.add(1);
q.add(2);
q.push(3);
