 vs shifting every element.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4364","topic":"","text":"Both classes implement the interface:","options":["Set","List","Map","Queue only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Same List operations, different performance profiles.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4365","topic":"","text":"List<String> names = new LinkedList<String>(); The declared type List:","options":["slows the code","hides all methods","lets the implementation swap freely","is required"],"correct":2,"explain":"Program to the interface - change new LinkedList to new ArrayList in one spot.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4366","topic":"","text":"LinkedList additionally offers:","options":["sort methods","key lookup","nothing extra","addFirst/addLast, removeFirst/removeLast"],"correct":3,"explain":"The deque-flavored ends operations are its specialty.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4367","topic":"","text":"list.removeFirst() on a LinkedList holding [a, b, c] leaves:","options":["[b, c] and returns a","[a, b]","[c]","an error"],"correct":0,"explain":"Ends operations return the removed element.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4368","topic":"","text":"Memory per element, LinkedList vs ArrayList:","options":["identical","LinkedList pays extra for node links","ArrayList pays more","both zero"],"correct":1,"explain":"Each node object carries reference overhead beyond the data.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4369","topic":"","text":"Choosing LinkedList is justified when:","options":["random access dominates","always","front and middle edits dominate","memory is scarce"],"correct":2,"explain":"Match the structure to the operation mix.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4370","topic":"","text":"Iterating a LinkedList with for-each is:","options":["slow like repeated get(i)","impossible","efficient - it follows the links","unordered"],"correct":2,"explain":"get(i) in a loop would re-walk from the head each time; iteration does not.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u14/z14_4":[{"id":"zya14_4_0