= new LinkedList<>();"},{"id":"zya14_5_2","topic":"peek behaviour","text":"q.peek() does what?","options":["Adds an element","Returns the size","Removes and returns the head","Returns the head without removing it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Inspect without consuming."},{"id":"zya14_5_3","topic":"poll on empty","text":"q.poll() on an empty queue returns:","options":["An exception","The last element","0","null"],"correct":3,"explain":"Whereas remove() throws NoSuchElementException — the key distinction in this section."},{"id":"zya14_5_4","topic":"remove on empty","text":"q.remove() on an empty queue:","options":["Returns 0","Does nothing","Returns null","Throws NoSuchElementException"],"correct":3,"explain":"The two method families differ precisely in how they report failure."},{"id":"zya14_5_5","topic":"Adding to a queue","text":"Both add and offer insert, but offer:","options":["Inserts at the front rather than at the back","Returns false rather than throwing when capacity is exceeded","Blocks until space becomes available in the queue","Adds the element only if it is not already present"],"correct":1,"explain":"It matters for bounded queues; for a LinkedList queue they behave the same."},{"id":"zya14_5_6","topic":"Standard drain loop","text":"Processing every element uses:","options":["A for-each with remove","A do-while","for (int i=0;i<q.size();i++) q.get(i)","while (!q.isEmpty()) { x = q.poll(); ... }"],"correct":3,"explain":"Queues have no get(i), and removing during a for-each throws."},{"id":"zya14_5_7","topic":"Typical use","text":"Queues naturally model:","options":["Undo history and the browser's back button behaviour","Recursive descent through a program's call stack","Sorted lookup tables searched by binary search","Print jobs, task scheduling, and breadth-first traversal"],"correct":3,"explain":"Anything where fairness by arrival order matters."},{"id":"zya14_5_8","topic":"Queue versus Stack","text":"The difference between a Queue and a Stack is:","option