1"],"correct":1,"explain":"The legacy Stack extends Vector, so its toString lists elements bottom to top: the last item printed is the top of the stack, which is what peek returns."},{"id":"fs_z14_9_4","topic":"Empty-stack behaviour of ArrayDeque","text":"A Deque<String> created as new ArrayDeque<>() is being used as a stack. What does pop() do when the deque is empty?","options":["It returns an empty string","It throws NoSuchElementException","It throws EmptyStackException","It returns null"],"correct":1,"explain":"EmptyStackException belongs to the legacy java.util.Stack. On a Deque, pop() throws NoSuchElementException, while pollFirst() is the version that returns null instead of throwing. Guarding with isEmpty() avoids both."},{"id":"zf4437","topic":"","text":"A stack’s discipline is:","options":["FIFO","sorted","LIFO - last in, first out","random"],"correct":2,"explain":"Like a plate stack: the top plate leaves first.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4438","topic":"","text":"push and pop operate on:","options":["the bottom","both ends","the middle","the top of the stack"],"correct":3,"explain":"One busy end is the whole design.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4439","topic":"","text":"push(1); push(2); push(3); pop() returns:","options":["3","1","2","null"],"correct":0,"explain":"Most recent first.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4440","topic":"","text":"After that pop, pop() again returns:","options":["2","1","3","empty"],"correct":0,"explain":"The stack unwinds newest-to-oldest.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4441","topic":"","text":"peek() on a stack:","options":["removes the top","shows the top without removing it","shows the bottom","reverses"],"correct":1,"explain":"Inspect before committing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4442","topic":"","text":"Undo functionality naturally uses a stack because:","options":["undo is sorted","actions are keys","the LAST action is the first undone","it does not"],"correct":2,"explain":"Each action pushes; each undo pops.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4443","topic":"","text":"Matching brackets in code uses a stack by:","options":["counting only","sorting brackets","hashing","push openers; pop to match closers"],"correct":3,"explain":"The most recent unmatched opener must match the next closer - LIFO exactly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4444","topic":"","text":"The method-call stack (unit 6/8) and this data structure:","options":["share the same LIFO principle","are unrelated","are both FIFO","conflict"],"correct":0,"explain":"Frames push on call, pop on return - the language runs on a stack.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4445","topic":"","text":"Reversing a sequence with a stack works because:","options":["stacks sort","push all, pop all - order inverts","pop is random","it does not"],"correct":1,"explain":"In: a b c. Out: c b a.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4446","topic":"","text":"In modern Java, prefer creating stacks as:","options":["new Stack<T>() (legacy)","a HashMap","Deque<T> s = new ArrayDeque<T>();","an array only"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Deque interface’s push/pop/peek supersede the old Stack class.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4447","topic":"","text":"Evaluating postfix expressions (3 4 +) uses a stack by:","options":["queueing operators","sorting tokens","recursion only","push operands; pop two per operator"],"correct":3,"explain":"See +: pop 4 and 3, push 7 - the classic stack algorithm.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_1":[{"id":"zya15_1_0","topic":"GUI vs console","text":"A GUI program differs from a console program because:","options":["The program decides the order, as in a console","Output must be formatted before it is displayed","The user decides what happens next, via events","Input can only be read once the window closes"],"correct":2,"explain":"Control is inverted: you build the window, then wait."},{"id":"zya15_1_1","topic":"Window class","text":"In Swing, the window is a:","options":["JFrame","JLabel","JButton","JPanel"],"correct":0,"explain":"A JPanel is a container inside it, not a window."},{"id":"zya15_1_2","topic":"Making it visible","text":"A frame appears only after calling:","options":["frame.setVisible(true)","frame.open()","frame.start()","frame.show()"],"correct":0,"explain":"Omitting it produces a program that runs with no window and no error."},{"id":"zya15_1_3","topic":"Closing behaviour","text":"Without setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE):","options":["Clicking X has no effect at all on the window","The window cannot be closed without the keyboard","The program exits, but without saving any state","Clicking X hides the window but the program keeps running"],"correct":3,"explain":"A very common cause of processes that never terminate."},{"id":"zya15_1_4","topic":"Adding components","text":"Components should be added:","options":["Before setVisible(true)","In any order","Never","After setVisible(true)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Adding afterwards may leave them unlaid-out until something forces a revalidate."},{"id":"zya15_1_5","topic":"Three GUI pieces","text":"Every Java GUI consists of:","options":["Components, containers and listeners","Loops, arrays and methods","Classes only","Frames, files and fonts"],"correct":0,"explain":"The visible parts, the things that hold them, and the code that reacts."},{"id":"zya15_1_6","topic":"Swing package","text":"Swing classes are imported from:","options":["javax.swing","java.util","java.io","java.awt only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Note the x in javax — event classes come from java.awt.event."},{"id":"zya15_1_7","topic":"Event-driven meaning","text":"Event-driven means:","options":["Code runs in response to user actions","There are no methods","Input is read with Scanner","The program runs top to bottom"],"correct":0,"explain":"The user's clicks replace the driving loop."},{"id":"zyf15_1_0","topic":"Package","text":"Swing components come from:","options":["java.awt","javax.swing","java.util","java.io"],"correct":1,"explain":"Note the x; events come from java.awt.event."},{"id":"zyf15_1_1","topic":"Order of operations","text":"setVisible(true) should be called:","options":["First","After components are added","Never","Twice"],"correct":1,"explain":"Otherwise components may not be laid out."},{"id":"fs_z15_1_1","topic":"draw versus fill","text":"Inside paintComponent, what is the difference between g.drawOval(10, 70, 80, 80) and g.fillOval(10, 70, 80, 80)?","options":["drawOval paints only the outline, while fillOval paints a solid shape","drawOval paints a solid shape, while fillOval paints only the outline","drawOval uses the current colour, while fillOval always uses black","They are identical; fillOval is simply the older name"],"correct":0,"explain":"The draw family produces outlines and the fill family produces solid shapes; both use whatever colour was last set with g.setColor."},{"id":"fs_z15_1_2","topic":"Rectangle arguments","text":"In the call g.fillRect(10, 20, 100, 50), what do the last two numbers mean?","options":["The width and the height of the rectangle","The x and y coordinates of the bottom-right corner","The horizontal and vertical padding around the rectangle","The corner radius in the x and y directions"],"correct":0,"explain":"The arguments are x, y, width, height: a position followed by a size, not two corners. The rectangle therefore spans x from 10 to 110 and y from 20 to 70."},{"id":"fs_z15_1_3","topic":"Who calls paintComponent","text":"Which statement about paintComponent is correct?","options":["You override it but never call it; you call repaint() to request a redraw","You call it directly whenever the drawing needs to change","You override it and call it from the constructor to draw the first frame","It is a static method, so it is called on the class rather than the panel"],"correct":0,"explain":"Swing decides when painting happens and passes in a valid Graphics object. Your code asks for a redraw with repaint(); calling paintComponent yourself is unreliable."},{"id":"fs_z15_1_4","topic":"Screen coordinate system","text":"A shape drawn at y = 10 is redrawn at y = 60 on the same panel. Where does it appear to move?","options":["50 pixels down the screen","50 pixels up the screen","50 pixels to the right","It does not move; y is only a drawing order"],"correct":0,"explain":"The origin (0,0) is the top-left corner and y increases downward, which is upside down compared with a mathematics graph, so a larger y is lower on the screen."},{"id":"zf4448","topic":"","text":"Custom Swing drawing goes in an override of:","options":["paintComponent(Graphics g)","main","draw()","render()"],"correct":0,"explain":"The framework calls paintComponent whenever the component needs repainting.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4449","topic":"","text":"The Graphics parameter g provides:","options":["file access","drawing methods (drawLine, fillRect)","user input","threads"],"correct":1,"explain":"g is the pen handed to you by the toolkit.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4450","topic":"","text":"Swing’s drawing coordinate origin (0, 0) is:","options":["the center","the top-left corner","bottom-left","bottom-right"],"correct":1,"explain":"x grows rightward, y grows DOWNWARD - unlike math class.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4451","topic":"","text":"g.drawRect(10, 20, 100, 50) draws:","options":["a filled rectangle","a line to (100,50)","a rectangle outline at","a circle"],"correct":2,"explain":"Position then size; draw = outline, fill = solid.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4452","topic":"","text":"g.fillOval(0, 0, 60, 60) produces:","options":["an outline oval","a square","an arc","a filled circle 60 across"],"correct":3,"explain":"Equal width and height make the oval a circle.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4453","topic":"","text":"Colors are set via:","options":["g.setColor(Color.RED) before drawing","per-pixel loops","the constructor","file settings"],"correct":0,"explain":"The pen keeps its color until changed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4454","topic":"","text":"g.drawString("Score: 10", 50, 40) puts text:","options":["centered on screen","with its baseline near (50, 40)","in the console","in a text field"],"correct":1,"explain":"Text drawing is positioned like the shapes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4455","topic":"","text":"The FIRST line of a paintComponent override should be:","options":["repaint();","g.clear();","super.paintComponent(g);","return;"],"correct":2,"explain":"Letting the base class clear the background prevents smearing artifacts.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4456","topic":"","text":"Programs never call paintComponent directly; they request drawing by:","options":["calling paintComponent()","sleeping","looping","calling repaint()"],"correct":3,"explain":"repaint schedules; the toolkit calls paint when ready.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4457","topic":"","text":"Drawing a bar chart from an int array uses:","options":["fillRect in a loop, one bar per value","drawString only","one big oval","no loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Heights map from data; x advances per bar - graphics meets arrays.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4458","topic":"","text":"Increasing y in Swing moves a shape:","options":["up","down the screen","left","nowhere"],"correct":1,"explain":"The inverted y-axis is the classic beginner surprise.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_2":[{"id":"zya15_2_0","topic":"Label component","text":"A JLabel is used for:","options":["Editable single-line text entry from the user","A clickable control that fires an action event","Non-editable display text or an icon","An invisible container for grouping components"],"correct":2,"explain":"Use JTextField when the user should be able to type."},{"id":"zya15_2_1","topic":"Single-line input","text":"One line of typed input uses:","options":["JLabel","JTextField","JTextArea","JButton"],"correct":1,"explain":"JTextArea is the multi-line version."},{"id":"zya15_2_2","topic":"Grouping components","text":"A JPanel is:","options":["An invisible container used to group and lay out components","A top-level window with a title bar and border","A non-editable area that displays text or an icon","A manager that decides where components are placed"],"correct":0,"explain":"Nesting panels is how complex layouts are built."},{"id":"zya15_2_3","topic":"Mutually exclusive choice","text":"Radio buttons behave exclusively only when:","options":["They are in a JPanel","They are added to a ButtonGroup","They are named alike","There are exactly two"],"correct":1,"explain":"The ButtonGroup is a logical grouping and is not added to the panel itself."},{"id":"zya15_2_4","topic":"Independent toggles","text":"For several independent on/off options use:","options":["JRadioButton","JCheckBox","JComboBox","JLabel"],"correct":1,"explain":"Checkboxes do not exclude one another."},{"id":"zya15_2_5","topic":"Drop-down list","text":"A drop-down selection uses:","options":["JList","JComboBox","JSpinner","JSlider"],"correct":1,"explain":"JList shows several items at once instead."},{"id":"zya15_2_6","topic":"Shared base class","text":"Swing components share methods such as setEnabled because:","options":["They are copied","They all extend JComponent","They are static","Java requires it"],"correct":1,"explain":"Inheritance from Unit 10, visible in the library you use."},{"id":"zya15_2_7","topic":"Reading a checkbox","text":"To test whether a checkbox is ticked:","options":["box.getText()","box.isSelected()","box.getValue()","box.isChecked()"],"correct":1,"explain":"It returns a boolean."},{"id":"zyf15_2_0","topic":"Editable text","text":"For text the user types, use:","options":["JTextField","JPanel","JFrame","JLabel"],"correct":0,"explain":"JLabel is display-only."},{"id":"zyf15_2_1","topic":"Shared methods","text":"Swing components share setEnabled and setFont because:","options":["They inherit from JComponent","They are static","Java requires it","They are copied"],"correct":0,"explain":"Inheritance in the library you use daily."},{"id":"fs_z15_2_1","topic":"JComboBox selection type","text":"What type does a JComboBox's getSelectedItem() return?","options":["String, so it can be used directly as text","int, the index of the chosen row","boolean, indicating whether anything is selected","Object, so it usually needs a cast or toString()"],"correct":3,"explain":"getSelectedItem() is declared to return Object because a combo box can hold any type of item; getSelectedIndex() is the method that returns an int position."},{"id":"fs_z15_2_2","topic":"Multi-line text component","text":"Which Swing component is intended for several lines of text the user can edit?","options":["JTextField","JLabel","JPanel","JTextArea"],"correct":3,"explain":"JTextField is a single line and JLabel is display-only text the user cannot edit. JTextArea is the multi-line editable component."},{"id":"fs_z15_2_3","topic":"Containment hierarchy","text":"Which describes the normal Swing containment hierarchy for a button on a window?","options":["The JButton holds a JPanel, and the JPanel holds the JFrame","The JPanel holds a JFrame, and the JFrame holds the JButton","The JButton and the JFrame are added to each other in either order","The JFrame holds a JPanel, and the JPanel holds the JButton"],"correct":3,"explain":"The window is the outermost container; panels group controls inside it, and the controls sit inside the panels. Building the hierarchy the other way round is not possible."},{"id":"fs_z15_2_4","topic":"frame.pack()","text":"What does frame.pack() do?","options":["Removes all components from the window so it can be rebuilt","Compresses the window's memory usage before it is shown","Arranges the components into a grid, replacing the layout manager","Resizes the window to the preferred size of the components it contains"],"correct":3,"explain":"pack() sizes the window to fit its contents, which is the alternative to choosing pixel dimensions with setSize. Either way, setVisible(true) still comes last."},{"id":"zf4459","topic":"","text":"A JFrame is:","options":["a button","a text field","the top-level application window","a layout"],"correct":2,"explain":"The outer window that contains everything else.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4460","topic":"","text":"A JLabel displays:","options":["editable input","a window","non-editable text (or an icon)","a menu"],"correct":2,"explain":"Labels present; fields accept.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4461","topic":"","text":"A JTextField provides:","options":["multi-line text","a static caption","a picture","a single-line editable text box"],"correct":3,"explain":"The basic typed-input widget.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4462","topic":"","text":"A JButton exists to:","options":["trigger an action when clicked","show images only","hold layouts","store data"],"correct":0,"explain":"Click = event = your code runs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4463","topic":"","text":"Components appear in the window after:","options":["being declared","being added to the frame’s content area","compiling","importing"],"correct":1,"explain":"frame.add(component) - constructing is not showing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4464","topic":"","text":"frame.setVisible(true) does what?","options":["starts main","adds components","makes the window appear on screen","compiles the GUI"],"correct":2,"explain":"The final reveal, after building the contents.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4465","topic":"","text":"setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE) makes:","options":["the window unclosable","a save dialog","a restart","the app terminate when the window closes"],"correct":3,"explain":"Without it, closing hides the window while the JVM lives on.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4466","topic":"","text":"GUI programs differ from console programs by being:","options":["event-driven","sequential top-to-bottom","faster","file-based"],"correct":0,"explain":"The program waits; events fire handlers.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4467","topic":"","text":"frame.setSize(400, 300); sets:","options":["the font size","the window’s width and height in pixels","component count","screen resolution"],"correct":1,"explain":"Or use pack() to size from the contents.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4468","topic":"","text":"Swing classes live in the package:","options":["java.awt only","java.util","javax.swing","javax.gui"],"correct":2,"explain":"import javax.swing.*; (with java.awt for graphics/layout helpers).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4469","topic":"","text":"The J prefix (JButton, JLabel) marks:","options":["Java keywords","JSON classes","test classes","Swing components, vs older AWT ones"],"correct":3,"explain":"Swing re-implemented the widget set; the J distinguishes it.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_3":[{"id":"zya15_3_0","topic":"Layout manager purpose","text":"A layout manager decides:","options":["Which listener responds to each component","What text each component displays initially","Where components go and how they resize","Whether the window can be resized by the user"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which is why absolute pixel positioning is discouraged."},{"id":"zya15_3_1","topic":"JFrame default","text":"The default layout of a JFrame content pane is:","options":["None","FlowLayout","BorderLayout","GridLayout"],"correct":2,"explain":"A JPanel defaults to FlowLayout instead."},{"id":"zya15_3_2","topic":"BorderLayout regions","text":"BorderLayout provides:","options":["One region","Three regions","Five regions","A grid"],"correct":2,"explain":"CENTER absorbs the leftover space. North, south, east, west and center."},{"id":"zya15_3_3","topic":"One per region","text":"Adding a second component to BorderLayout.NORTH:","options":["Splits the region","Stacks them","Replaces the first","Throws"],"correct":2,"explain":"Put a JPanel in the region and add both to that panel."},{"id":"zya15_3_4","topic":"GridLayout behaviour","text":"GridLayout arranges components:","options":["In one row or column, at their preferred sizes","With one component in each of five regions","In equally sized cells, filled row by row","At absolute coordinates the programmer supplies"],"correct":2,"explain":"Every cell is the same size, which is what makes it suit keypads."},{"id":"zya15_3_5","topic":"FlowLayout behaviour","text":"FlowLayout places components:","options":["Stacked","In a grid","Left to right, wrapping like text","In five regions"],"correct":2,"explain":"The default for JPanel."},{"id":"zya15_3_6","topic":"Complex layouts","text":"Complex screens are built by:","options":["Nesting simple layouts inside each other","Setting absolute coordinates for every component","Using one very large GridLayout for the window","Placing each component in its own separate window"],"correct":0,"explain":"A GridLayout form inside the CENTER of a BorderLayout, for example."},{"id":"zya15_3_7","topic":"Absolute positioning","text":"setLayout(null) with explicit coordinates:","options":["Is the recommended approach for portable layouts","Fails to compile in any recent version of Java","Automatically scales the components on resize","Works until the window resizes or the platform changes"],"correct":3,"explain":"Which is exactly why layout managers exist."},{"id":"zyf15_3_0","topic":"Default frame layout","text":"A JFrame content pane defaults to:","options":["GridLayout","null","FlowLayout","BorderLayout"],"correct":3,"explain":"JPanel defaults to FlowLayout."},{"id":"zyf15_3_1","topic":"Two in one region","text":"Adding two components to BorderLayout.SOUTH:","options":["Splits the region","Throws","Stacks them","Replaces the first"],"correct":3,"explain":"Wrap them in a JPanel first."},{"id":"fs_z15_3_1","topic":"gridx and gridy","text":"In a GridBagConstraints object, what do the fields gridx and gridy specify?","options":["The amount of spare space the component should absorb","The number of columns and rows the component spans","The column and the row of the cell the component occupies","The width and the height of the component in pixels"],"correct":2,"explain":"gridx is the column and gridy is the row. The number of cells spanned is gridwidth and gridheight, and the share of spare space is weightx and weighty."},{"id":"fs_z15_3_2","topic":"Constraints objects persist","text":"One GridBagConstraints object is reused for several add calls. Why can a setting made for one component quietly affect a later one?","options":["GridBagLayout copies the last constraints object over all previous ones","Constraints are applied in reverse order, so the final settings win everywhere","The constraints are read when add is called, and the object's fields keep their values until changed","add stores a reference, so changing the object later moves every component already added"],"correct":2,"explain":"The layout copies the constraint values at the moment of the add call, so each component keeps the settings in force at that time. Fields you forget to reset simply carry over to the next add."},{"id":"fs_z15_3_3","topic":"weightx","text":"What is the effect of setting c.weightx = 1.0 for a component in a GridBagLayout?","options":["The component is drawn with a one-pixel border","The component is moved to the first column","Its column claims a share of any spare horizontal space when the container grows","The component is made exactly one cell wide"],"correct":2,"explain":"weightx and weighty decide how extra space is distributed as the window is resized; with all weights left at 0 the components stay clustered in the middle."},{"id":"fs_z15_3_4","topic":"fill constraint","text":"A JTextField should stretch across the full width of its GridBagLayout cell. Which constraint achieves that?","options":["c.gridwidth = 0","c.anchor = GridBagConstraints.CENTER","c.fill = GridBagConstraints.HORIZONTAL","c.insets = new Insets(0, 0, 0, 0)"],"correct":2,"explain":"fill says whether a component expands to occupy its cell, horizontally, vertically or both. insets add padding, anchor positions a component that is not filling, and neither stretches it."},{"id":"zf4470","topic":"","text":"A layout manager’s job is:","options":["drawing shapes","handling clicks","storing data","placing and sizing components for you"],"correct":3,"explain":"You describe intent; the manager computes pixels.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4471","topic":"","text":"GridBagLayout arranges components:","options":["in a flexible grid of rows and columns","in one row only","absolutely by pixel","randomly"],"correct":0,"explain":"The most capable (and most verbose) standard layout.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4472","topic":"","text":"GridBagConstraints gridx and gridy specify:","options":["pixel position","the component’s column and row","size in pixels","color"],"correct":1,"explain":"Cell coordinates in the conceptual grid.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4473","topic":"","text":"constraints.gridwidth = 2 makes a component:","options":["twice as tall","two pixels wide","span two columns","appear twice"],"correct":2,"explain":"Spanning is how wide controls sit over multiple cells.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4474","topic":"","text":"The insets field of the constraints controls:","options":["text margins inside text fields","color","font","padding around the component"],"correct":3,"explain":"new Insets(top, left, bottom, right) - breathing room per side.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4475","topic":"","text":"Each add call with GridBagLayout passes:","options":["the component AND its constraints object","the component alone","coordinates in pixels","a string"],"correct":0,"explain":"frame.add(button, constraints); - the constraints place it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4476","topic":"","text":"Reusing one GridBagConstraints object for several components:","options":["is forbidden","works - set the fields before each add","shares position","crashes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Update gridx/gridy per component; the values are read at add time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4477","topic":"","text":"Why layouts beat setting exact pixel positions?","options":["pixels are illegal","layouts are faster","the GUI adapts to resizing","no reason"],"correct":2,"explain":"Absolute positioning breaks the moment anything changes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4478","topic":"","text":"A label in column 0 and its text field in column 1, same row, uses:","options":["two rows","two frames","spans","gridx 0 and 1 with the same gridy"],"correct":3,"explain":"The standard form-row arrangement.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4479","topic":"","text":"constraints.fill = GridBagConstraints.HORIZONTAL makes a component:","options":["stretch to fill its cell’s width","fill the screen","center only","repaint"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fill behavior controls stretching within the allotted cell.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4480","topic":"","text":"Simpler alternatives to GridBagLayout include:","options":["FlowLayout and BorderLayout","PixelLayout","MainLayout","NoLayout"],"correct":0,"explain":"Choose the simplest manager that achieves the design.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_4":[{"id":"zya15_4_0","topic":"Making a button work","text":"A button does nothing unless:","options":["It is added to a panel rather than the frame","Its text is set before the window is displayed","A listener is registered with addActionListener","The frame's layout manager is set explicitly"],"correct":2,"explain":"The single most common beginner GUI bug."},{"id":"zya15_4_1","topic":"ActionListener method","text":"ActionListener requires implementing:","options":["onClick","run","handle","actionPerformed"],"correct":3,"explain":"It takes an ActionEvent parameter."},{"id":"zya15_4_2","topic":"Lambda form","text":"button.addActionListener(e -> {...}) works because:","options":["ActionListener has exactly one abstract method","Lambdas may be used in place of any interface","ActionListener is a class rather than an interface","The compiler generates the missing methods for you"],"correct":0,"explain":"Any single-method interface can be written as a lambda."},{"id":"zya15_4_3","topic":"Identifying the source","text":"With one listener for several buttons, you distinguish them using:","options":["e.getClass() or e.toString()","The order the listeners were registered in","A separate listener is required for each button","e.getSource() or e.getActionCommand()"],"correct":3,"explain":"getActionCommand returns the button label by default."},{"id":"zya15_4_4","topic":"MouseListener burden","text":"Implementing MouseListener directly requires:","options":["Only the one method you actually care about","A single method named mouseEvent to handle all","All five methods, even the unused ones","No methods; the interface has default bodies"],"correct":2,"explain":"Extend MouseAdapter and override only what you need."},{"id":"zya15_4_5","topic":"Multiple listeners","text":"Calling addActionListener twice on one button:","options":["Throws","Is a compile error","Replaces the first","Registers both, and both run"],"correct":3,"explain":"JavaFX setOnAction behaves the opposite way — it replaces."},{"id":"zya15_4_6","topic":"Updating the display","text":"Inside a listener, changing a label uses:","options":["System.out.println","label.update()","label.print()","label.setText(...)"],"correct":3,"explain":"println goes to the console, which the user is not looking at."},{"id":"zya15_4_7","topic":"Long work in a listener","text":"Doing a slow computation inside a listener:","options":["Speeds it up","Throws","Is fine","Freezes the interface"],"correct":3,"explain":"Move it to a background thread such as SwingWorker. It blocks the event thread."},{"id":"zyf15_4_0","topic":"MouseAdapter","text":"Extending MouseAdapter instead of implementing MouseListener lets you:","options":["Avoid events","Handle no events","Override only the methods you need","Skip registration"],"correct":2,"explain":"MouseListener would force all five."},{"id":"zyf15_4_1","topic":"Blocking the thread","text":"Slow work inside a listener:","options":["Throws","Runs in the background","Freezes the interface","Is faster"],"correct":2,"explain":"Use SwingWorker for long tasks."},{"id":"fs_z15_4_1","topic":"ActionListener method","text":"What is the one method an ActionListener must supply?","options":["public void run(ActionEvent e)","public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e)","public void onClick(ActionEvent e)","public boolean actionPerformed()"],"correct":1,"explain":"ActionListener declares exactly one method, actionPerformed, taking the ActionEvent and returning void. Having a single abstract method is also what lets a lambda stand in for it."},{"id":"fs_z15_4_2","topic":"getActionCommand","text":"For a JButton whose label was set to "Add", what does e.getActionCommand() return inside actionPerformed by default?","options":["null, unless setActionCommand has been called","The string "Add"","The JButton object itself","The name of the listener class"],"correct":1,"explain":"A button's action command defaults to its text. getSource() is the method that returns the component object itself."},{"id":"fs_z15_4_3","topic":"getSource versus getActionCommand","text":"One listener serves several buttons. Why is comparing e.getSource() == addButton safer than comparing e.getActionCommand() with "Add"?","options":["getActionCommand can only be called once per event","Renaming the button's label changes the command string and silently breaks the comparison","getActionCommand always returns null for buttons","getSource is checked by the compiler, while getActionCommand is not"],"correct":1,"explain":"The command string defaults to the visible label, so a wording change breaks a string comparison with no compile error and no exception. Comparing the source object compares identity, which the label cannot affect."},{"id":"fs_z15_4_4","topic":"Reaching components from a listener","text":"In an event-driven Swing program, why are components such as the result JLabel usually stored in fields rather than local variables of a setup method?","options":["So the layout manager can find them when the window is resized","So the listeners","Because local variables cannot be added to a JPanel","Because Swing requires every component to be static"],"correct":1,"explain":"Nothing runs top to bottom in a GUI: the setup method finishes and the handlers fire later, so anything a handler must read or update has to remain reachable after setup returns. Run later, can still reach and update them."},{"id":"zy3_16_6_q8","topic":"Lambda relation","text":"Modern Java often replaces small anonymous classes with:","options":["Lambdas","Static blocks","Enums","Generics"],"correct":0,"explain":"For single-method (functional) interfaces, a lambda expression is a concise replacement for an anonymous class.","distractors":{"0":"Static blocks are for init.","2":"Enums are unrelated.","3":"Generics are unrelated."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"tuw1_d286_u5_7","topic":"Lambda vs anonymous","text":"For a functional interface, a lambda is often preferred over an anonymous class because it is:","options":["More concise with less boilerplate","The only legal option","Thread-safe","Slower but clearer"],"correct":0,"explain":"Lambdas provide the same single-method implementation far more concisely than an anonymous class for functional interfaces."},{"id":"zf4481","topic":"","text":"Reacting to a button click requires:","options":["a loop polling the button","registering an ActionListener on it","a new thread","repaint"],"correct":1,"explain":"button.addActionListener(handler); - the event wiring.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4482","topic":"","text":"The ActionListener interface declares:","options":["onClick()","run()","actionPerformed(ActionEvent e)","handle()"],"correct":2,"explain":"One method: what to do when the action fires.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4483","topic":"","text":"The code inside actionPerformed runs:","options":["once at startup","continuously","never","each time the user triggers it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Event-driven: user act, handler run.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4484","topic":"","text":"Reading the text field inside the handler uses:","options":["field.getText()","field.read()","System.in","args"],"correct":0,"explain":"Then parse it (with the unit-12 defenses) if a number is expected.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4485","topic":"","text":"Displaying a result back on a label uses:","options":["println","label.setText(result)","label.print","drawString"],"correct":1,"explain":"GUI output = updating components.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4486","topic":"","text":"A class implementing ActionListener must:","options":["extend JFrame","be abstract","provide the actionPerformed method","have no fields"],"correct":2,"explain":"The interface contract - the framework calls your implementation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4487","topic":"","text":"One listener attached to two buttons distinguishes them via:","options":["guessing","thread ids","timing","e.getSource() (or action commands)"],"correct":3,"explain":"The event object identifies its origin.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4488","topic":"","text":"Long-running work directly inside actionPerformed:","options":["freezes the interface until it finishes","runs in parallel automatically","is impossible","is best practice"],"correct":0,"explain":"Handlers run on the UI thread - keep them quick (unit 18 revisits this).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4489","topic":"","text":"The unregistered-button symptom - clicks do nothing - means:","options":["Swing is broken","addActionListener was never called","the button is final","main ended"],"correct":1,"explain":"No listener, no reaction: the most common wiring bug.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_5":[{"id":"zya15_5_0","topic":"Reading a text field","text":"Getting the typed text uses:","options":["field.getText()","field.read()","field.value()","field.text()"],"correct":0,"explain":"It always returns a String."},{"id":"zya15_5_1","topic":"Empty field","text":"getText() on an empty field returns:","options":["An empty String","A space","An exception","null"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why parseInt on it throws rather than returning null."},{"id":"zya15_5_2","topic":"Converting to a number","text":"Turning the text into an int uses:","options":["Integer.parseInt(text)","text.toInt()","Double.parse(text)","(int) text"],"correct":0,"explain":"And it must be wrapped in try/catch."},{"id":"zya15_5_3","topic":"Invalid input","text":"Integer.parseInt("abc") throws:","options":["NumberFormatException","NullPointerException","Nothing","IOException"],"correct":0,"explain":"GUI input is user input, so every parse needs a guard."},{"id":"zya15_5_4","topic":"Reporting an error","text":"An invalid entry should be reported by:","options":["Setting a label or showing a dialog","Throwing","Ignoring it","System.out.println"],"correct":0,"explain":"Console output is invisible to a GUI user."},{"id":"zya15_5_5","topic":"Uncaught exception in a listener","text":"An exception escaping a listener:","options":["Closes the window immediately with a stack trace","Leaves the window looking fine while the action silently fails","Is caught automatically and shown in a dialog","Prevents any further events from being delivered"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which makes silent failure the default unless you handle it."},{"id":"zya15_5_6","topic":"Clearing a field","text":"After handling input, clearing it uses:","options":["field.setText("")","field.reset()","field.setText(null)","field.clear()"],"correct":0,"explain":"JavaFX offers clear(); Swing does not."},{"id":"zya15_5_7","topic":"Trimming input","text":"Calling trim() on typed input:","options":["Converts the whole of the input to lowercase before comparing","Removes every space, including those inside the text","Rejects the input whenever it contains any space","Removes stray spaces that would otherwise break comparisons and parsing"],"correct":3,"explain":"" 42 " parses fine but " abc " and " Ana" comparisons do not."},{"id":"zyf15_5_0","topic":"Return type of getText","text":"getText always returns:","options":["An int","A String, even for numeric input","A double","An Object"],"correct":1,"explain":"Conversion and validation are your responsibility."},{"id":"zyf15_5_1","topic":"Silent failure","text":"An exception thrown inside a listener and not caught:","options":["Terminates the whole program immediately and visibly","Leaves the window looking normal while the action silently failed","Is displayed to the user in a pop-up error dialog","Is retried automatically when the next event arrives"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which makes explicit handling essential."},{"id":"fs_z15_5_1","topic":"getValue on a formatted field","text":"A JFormattedTextField was built with NumberFormat.getIntegerInstance(). What does its getValue() return?","options":["An Object holding a Number, so it is cast and then read with intValue()","A String, which still has to be passed to Integer.parseInt","An int, ready to use in arithmetic","null until the user presses Enter for the first time"],"correct":0,"explain":"The field parses the text for you, so getValue() hands back the parsed object rather than raw text; the usual idiom is ((Number) field.getValue()).intValue()."},{"id":"fs_z15_5_2","topic":"Listening for a committed value","text":"Which listener is registered to react when a JFormattedTextField's committed value changes?","options":["A PropertyChangeListener on the "value" property","An ActionListener, the same as on a JButton","A ChangeListener, the same as on a JSpinner","A MouseListener on the field"],"correct":0,"explain":"The field exposes its parsed content as a bound property named "value", so field.addPropertyChangeListener("value", evt -> ...) is what fires when a new valid value is committed."},{"id":"fs_z15_5_3","topic":"Why validate at input","text":"What is the main advantage of a JFormattedTextField over parsing the text of a plain JTextField?","options":["Invalid input is refused or reverted as it is entered, instead of being caught later","It removes the need to add the field to a container","It converts any text into a number, so parsing can never fail","It automatically shows an error dialog for every keystroke"],"correct":0,"explain":"The formatter constrains what the field will hold, moving validation to the earliest possible point rather than leaving a parse to fail somewhere downstream."},{"id":"fs_z15_5_4","topic":"Reading a number from a plain field","text":"Which sequence correctly reads a whole number from a plain JTextField and reports bad input?","options":["Call getText(), trim it, parse it inside a try block, and show a message dialog from the catch of NumberFormatException","Call getValue(), cast it to Integer, and catch ClassCastException","Call getText(), compare it with null, and parse only if it is not null","Parse first and call trim() on the result if the parse fails"],"correct":0,"explain":"getText() always returns a String, and an empty field gives "" rather than null, so the null check proves nothing. Trim, parse in a try, and report the NumberFormatException in the interface where the user can see it."},{"id":"zf4490","topic":"","text":"JFormattedTextField improves on JTextField by:","options":["being larger","constraining input to a format","auto-saving","coloring text"],"correct":1,"explain":"The field itself polices what can be entered/committed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4491","topic":"","text":"A NumberFormat-based formatted field returns values via:","options":["getText() only","getInt()","getValue(), as an Object to convert","args"],"correct":2,"explain":"((Number) field.getValue()).intValue() - the retrieval idiom.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4492","topic":"","text":"The formatter prevents:","options":["all typing","clicks","layout changes","committing text that breaks the format"],"correct":3,"explain":"Bad input never becomes the field’s value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4493","topic":"","text":"Formatted fields reduce the need for:","options":["manual parseInt try/catch on that input","layouts","listeners","labels"],"correct":0,"explain":"Validation moves from your handler into the component.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4494","topic":"","text":"field.setValue(42) does what?","options":["locks the field","sets the field’s value from code","renames it","adds 42 listeners"],"correct":1,"explain":"Initialization and reset go through the same value interface.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4495","topic":"","text":"A currency-formatted field displays 1234.5 as:","options":["1234.5 always","an error","something like $1,234.50","1,234"],"correct":2,"explain":"NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance supplies the locale format.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4496","topic":"","text":"When the user types junk and focus leaves the field, the field:","options":["keeps the junk as value","crashes","clears the window","reverts or refuses, per its policy"],"correct":3,"explain":"Commit-or-revert behavior is configurable; junk does not become the value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4497","topic":"","text":"Formatted fields still benefit from range CHECKS because:","options":["format-valid can be range-invalid","formats check ranges","ranges are automatic","they do not"],"correct":0,"explain":"-5 is a perfectly formatted integer - business rules stay your job.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4498","topic":"","text":"The percent formatter treats an entered 50 as:","options":["50.0","50% (0.5 as a value)","0.05","5000"],"correct":1,"explain":"Format-specific interpretation - know your formatter.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4499","topic":"","text":"Compared to validating in the handler, field-level formatting:","options":["is identical","is later","catches errors earlier, at entry time","is invisible"],"correct":2,"explain":"The earliest possible rejection is the best user experience.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4500","topic":"","text":"Formatted fields fire their listeners most usefully on:","options":["every keystroke by default","value commit (Enter or focus change)","mouse movement","repaint"],"correct":1,"explain":"React to committed values, not half-typed text.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_6":[{"id":"zya15_6_0","topic":"Custom drawing","text":"Custom drawing is done by overriding:","options":["paint(Graphics g) in a JFrame subclass","draw(Graphics g) in any Swing component","paintComponent(Graphics g) in a JPanel subclass","repaint() in the class that owns the panel"],"correct":2,"explain":"Swing calls it whenever the panel needs redrawing."},{"id":"zya15_6_1","topic":"Calling it yourself","text":"You should trigger a redraw with:","options":["paintComponent(g)","repaint()","update()","setVisible(true)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Never call paintComponent directly — let Swing schedule it."},{"id":"zya15_6_2","topic":"super call","text":"Omitting super.paintComponent(g):","options":["Leaves the previous frame on screen, causing smears","Prevents the component from being drawn at all","Draws the component twice, doubling the work","Has no visible effect on the rendered output"],"correct":0,"explain":"The super call clears the background."},{"id":"zya15_6_3","topic":"Coordinate origin","text":"In Java 2D graphics, (0,0) is:","options":["The bottom-left corner, with y increasing upward","The centre of the component, with y upward","The top-left corner, with y increasing upward","The top-left corner, with y increasing downward"],"correct":3,"explain":"The opposite of a mathematical graph."},{"id":"zya15_6_4","topic":"Rectangle parameters","text":"fillRect(20, 20, 100, 60) specifies:","options":["Two corners","x, y, width and height","Centre and size","Four corners"],"correct":1,"explain":"Top-left corner plus dimensions."},{"id":"zya15_6_5","topic":"Drawing a circle","text":"drawOval(x, y, d, d) draws:","options":["An oval always","A circle of diameter d","A square","A line"],"correct":1,"explain":"The oval is defined by its bounding box."},{"id":"zya15_6_6","topic":"Setting colour","text":"Changing the drawing colour uses:","options":["g.color = Color.RED","g.setColor(Color.RED)","g.paint(Color.RED)","Color.RED.apply()"],"correct":1,"explain":"It applies to subsequent drawing operations."},{"id":"zya15_6_7","topic":"Panel size","text":"A custom drawing panel should be given:","options":["An explicit background colour, or it stays black","A layout manager, or nothing will be painted","A registered listener, or paint is never called","A preferred size, or it may collapse to zero"],"correct":3,"explain":"setPreferredSize(new Dimension(w, h))."},{"id":"zyf15_6_0","topic":"Trigger a redraw","text":"After changing drawing state you call:","options":["repaint()","setVisible","revalidate","paintComponent"],"correct":0,"explain":"Swing then schedules the paint."},{"id":"zyf15_6_1","topic":"Missing super call","text":"Omitting super.paintComponent(g) causes:","options":["The component never to be painted at all","Previous frames to remain, producing smears","The background to be painted twice each frame","An exception to be thrown on the first repaint"],"correct":1,"explain":"The super call clears the background."},{"id":"fs_z15_6_1","topic":"JSpinner listener type","text":"Which listener does a JSpinner notify when its value changes?","options":["An ActionListener, whose method is actionPerformed","A PropertyChangeListener, whose method is propertyChange","An ItemListener, whose method is itemStateChanged","A ChangeListener, whose method is stateChanged"],"correct":3,"explain":"Different controls fire different event types: buttons fire ActionEvents, while a spinner fires ChangeEvents to a ChangeListener. Attaching the wrong listener type simply means the handler is never called."},{"id":"fs_z15_6_2","topic":"SpinnerNumberModel arguments","text":"What do the four arguments of new SpinnerNumberModel(50, 0, 100, 1) mean, in order?","options":["Minimum, maximum, initial value, step size","Step size, initial value, minimum, maximum","Initial value, step size, minimum, maximum","Initial value, minimum, maximum, step size"],"correct":3,"explain":"The model is constructed as (value, minimum, maximum, stepSize), so this spinner starts at 50 and moves by 1 between 0 and 100."},{"id":"fs_z15_6_3","topic":"Reading a spinner","text":"What does spinner.getValue() return for a spinner backed by a SpinnerNumberModel of ints?","options":["An int, which can be used in arithmetic directly","A String showing the number as displayed","A SpinnerNumberModel describing the current state","An Object, which must be cast, for example (Integer) spinner.getValue()"],"correct":3,"explain":"getValue() is declared to return Object because a spinner can cycle through any kind of value, so numeric use needs a cast such as (Integer)."},{"id":"fs_z15_6_4","topic":"Why choose a spinner","text":"Why is a JSpinner with a SpinnerNumberModel(50, 0, 100, 1) preferable to a JTextField for choosing a value between 0 and 100?","options":["It is the only Swing component that can fire events","It removes the need to add a listener at all","It stores its value as an int, so no cast is ever required","Out-of-range and non-numeric entries become impossible rather than merely rejected afterwards"],"correct":3,"explain":"The model bounds the value, so the control cannot produce an invalid one. A text field accepts anything and leaves you to parse and range-check it after the fact."},{"id":"zf4501","topic":"","text":"A JSpinner provides:","options":["free text","a slider","a value with up/down arrows to step it","a menu"],"correct":2,"explain":"Click-to-step numeric (or list) input.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4502","topic":"","text":"SpinnerNumberModel(5, 0, 10, 1) creates a spinner with:","options":["five spinners","10 steps of 5","range 1-5","start 5, range 0-10, step 1"],"correct":3,"explain":"initial, min, max, step - the four constructor arguments.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4503","topic":"","text":"The spinner’s guarantee is:","options":["its value stays within the model’s range","any value","text allowed","no listeners"],"correct":0,"explain":"Clamped input by construction - no out-of-range possible.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4504","topic":"","text":"spinner.getValue() returns:","options":["an int directly","an Object needing a cast/convert","a String always","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"(Integer) spinner.getValue() - same Object-retrieval pattern as formatted fields.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4505","topic":"","text":"Reacting to spinner changes uses:","options":["an ActionListener","a WindowListener","a ChangeListener","polling"],"correct":2,"explain":"stateChanged fires on each value change.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4506","topic":"","text":"Spinners suit inputs like:","options":["free-form names","essays","file paths","quantity 1-99 in a shopping cart"],"correct":3,"explain":"Small bounded numeric ranges are the sweet spot.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4507","topic":"","text":"A spinner over the strings {"S", "M", "L"} uses:","options":["SpinnerListModel","SpinnerNumberModel","a text field","three buttons"],"correct":0,"explain":"Spinners can step through lists, not just numbers.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4508","topic":"","text":"spinner.setValue(3) does what?","options":["adds 3","sets the current value programmatically","sets the max","spins thrice"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reset and initialize through the model’s value.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4509","topic":"","text":"Compared with a text field for quantity, a spinner:","options":["allows anything","is slower to code and unsafe","prevents junk input entirely","needs parsing"],"correct":2,"explain":"No parsing, no catch blocks - invalid values are unreachable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4510","topic":"","text":"The step size 5 in a 0-100 spinner yields values:","options":["every integer","only 5","0, 5, 10, ..., 100","random fives"],"correct":2,"explain":"Steps quantize the range.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4511","topic":"","text":"Spinner models separate:","options":["colors from fonts","GUIs from Java","nothing","the value logic from the widget"],"correct":3,"explain":"Model-view separation - the widget renders what the model permits.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_7":[{"id":"zya15_7_0","topic":"Menu hierarchy","text":"The correct nesting is:","options":["JMenu contains JMenuBar contains JMenuItem","JMenuItem contains JMenu contains JMenuBar","JMenuBar contains JMenu contains JMenuItem","JMenuBar contains JMenuItem contains JMenu"],"correct":2,"explain":"Bar at the top, menus inside it, items inside those."},{"id":"zya15_7_1","topic":"Attaching the bar","text":"A menu bar is attached with:","options":["bar.attach(frame)","frame.add(bar)","frame.setJMenuBar(bar)","frame.addMenu(bar)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Using add puts it in the content area where the layout treats it as an ordinary component."},{"id":"zya15_7_2","topic":"Menu item events","text":"A JMenuItem fires:","options":["An ActionEvent, exactly like a button","A MouseEvent, unlike a button","A ChangeEvent when it is highlighted","No event; menus are handled by the frame"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why a toolbar button and a menu item can share one handler."},{"id":"zya15_7_3","topic":"Separator","text":"file.addSeparator() adds:","options":["A submenu holding the following items","A keyboard shortcut for the previous item","A blank menu item that can still be clicked","A dividing line between groups of items"],"correct":3,"explain":"Purely visual grouping."},{"id":"zya15_7_4","topic":"Submenu","text":"Adding a JMenu to another JMenu creates:","options":["A dialog","An error","A submenu","A menu bar"],"correct":2,"explain":"Menus nest arbitrarily deep."},{"id":"zya15_7_5","topic":"Keyboard shortcut","text":"A menu accelerator is set with:","options":["addKeyListener","setText","setAccelerator(KeyStroke...)","setShortcut"],"correct":2,"explain":"It shows the shortcut in the menu and activates the item."},{"id":"zya15_7_6","topic":"Exiting from a menu","text":"A working Exit item calls:","options":["frame.dispose() only","frame.setVisible(false)","System.exit(0)","return"],"correct":2,"explain":"setVisible(false) merely hides the window while the program continues."},{"id":"zya15_7_7","topic":"Sharing handlers","text":"A menu item and a toolbar button doing the same thing should:","options":["Each have their own copy of the logic","Be placed in the same container to stay in sync","Share a single component added in two places","Register the same listener or call the same method"],"correct":3,"explain":"One implementation, two entry points."},{"id":"zyf15_7_1","topic":"Item events","text":"JMenuItem fires the same event type as:","options":["A frame","A panel","A label","A button"],"correct":3,"explain":"So one handler can serve both. ActionEvent."},{"id":"fs_z15_7_1","topic":"JTextArea constructor","text":"In new JTextArea(10, 40), what do 10 and 40 specify?","options":["The minimum and maximum number of characters allowed","The x and y position of the area in its container","The number of visible rows and columns of text","The width and height of the area in pixels"],"correct":2,"explain":"The constructor takes rows then columns, expressed in characters, which the component turns into a preferred size using the current font."},{"id":"fs_z15_7_2","topic":"Scrolling a text area","text":"A JTextArea is added straight to a JPanel and its text grows past the visible area, but no scroll bar ever appears. What is wrong?","options":["A JTextArea cannot scroll; a JTextField must be used instead","setLineWrap(true) is required before a scroll bar can be shown","The text area must be wrapped in a JScrollPane, and the scroll pane is what gets added to the panel","setEditable(false) must be called before scrolling is enabled"],"correct":2,"explain":"A JTextArea does not scroll on its own. Construct new JScrollPane(area) and add the scroll pane to the container; adding the area itself leaves it growing with no scroll bars."},{"id":"fs_z15_7_3","topic":"setText versus append","text":"How do area.setText("line\n") and area.append("line\n") differ on a JTextArea?","options":["setText works only on an editable area, while append works on any area","They are identical; append is a convenience name for setText","setText replaces all existing content, while append adds to the end of it","setText adds to the end, while append replaces all existing content"],"correct":2,"explain":"setText discards whatever was there, which is why a log-style display that should accumulate lines uses append."},{"id":"fs_z15_7_4","topic":"Line wrapping","text":"What is the effect of calling both setLineWrap(true) and setWrapStyleWord(true) on a JTextArea?","options":["Each line is right-justified within the visible width","The area becomes read-only so the wrapping cannot be disturbed","Long lines wrap to the next line, and the break falls between words rather than inside one","Long lines are truncated at the right edge instead of wrapping"],"correct":2,"explain":"setLineWrap turns wrapping on; without setWrapStyleWord the wrap can happen mid-word at whatever character reaches the edge."},{"id":"zf4512","topic":"","text":"A JTextArea differs from a JTextField by:","options":["holding multiple lines of text","being read-only","being numeric","being a window"],"correct":0,"explain":"Field = one line; area = a page.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4513","topic":"","text":"Appending a line to a text area uses:","options":["setText only","area.append(text + "\n")","println","add"],"correct":1,"explain":"append grows the content; the newline separates entries.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4514","topic":"","text":"setText("") on a text area:","options":["deletes the component","adds a blank line","clears it","disables it"],"correct":2,"explain":"Replacing all content with nothing empties the log.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4515","topic":"","text":"area.setEditable(false) creates:","options":["a hidden area","an error","a label","a display-only log, no typing"],"correct":3,"explain":"Output areas are typically locked against edits.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4516","topic":"","text":"Long content becomes scrollable by:","options":["wrapping the area in a JScrollPane","a bigger window only","setScroll(true)","nothing - automatic"],"correct":0,"explain":"new JScrollPane(area) - then add the scroll pane, not the bare area.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4517","topic":"","text":"Adding the bare JTextArea instead of its scroll pane causes:","options":["auto-scrolling","overflow content becomes unreachable","an exception","a beep"],"correct":1,"explain":"The classic mistake: the scrollbars live on the JScrollPane.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4518","topic":"","text":"Line wrap for prose uses:","options":["a wider window","substring calls","setLineWrap(true)","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Wrap at word boundaries for readable paragraphs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4519","topic":"","text":"A multi-line report built in a loop is best:","options":["setText per line, replacing","printed to console","drawn with graphics","accumulated (e.g."],"correct":3,"explain":"Either append rows or build-then-set - not repeated full replacement.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4520","topic":"","text":"area.getText() returns:","options":["one line","the last append","nothing","the entire contents as one String"],"correct":3,"explain":"Save-to-file features read the area this way (unit 9 writes it out).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4521","topic":"","text":"Displaying an ArrayList of orders in an area uses:","options":["a loop appending each order’s line","one toString of the list only","a table always","drag and drop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Collections meet GUIs: iterate, format, append.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4522","topic":"","text":"The \n in appended text:","options":["is shown literally","starts a new line in the area","beeps","tabs"],"correct":1,"explain":"Same escape as console output, same effect.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4523","topic":"","text":"A text area’s natural role in this course’s apps is:","options":["the main input","the window itself","the multi-line output/log panel","a button"],"correct":2,"explain":"Results, histories, and reports land in the text area.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_8":[{"id":"zya15_8_0","topic":"Timer purpose","text":"javax.swing.Timer fires:","options":["A single ActionEvent after the delay elapses","A MouseEvent whenever the window is redrawn","An ActionEvent repeatedly at a fixed interval","No event; it simply blocks for the given time"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which is what drives animation."},{"id":"zya15_8_1","topic":"Which Timer","text":"For updating Swing components you must use:","options":["Thread.sleep","System.currentTimeMillis","java.util.Timer","javax.swing.Timer"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Swing timer fires on the event dispatch thread, so component updates are safe."},{"id":"zya15_8_2","topic":"Starting it","text":"A timer begins firing when you call:","options":["timer.run()","timer.fire()","new Timer(...)","timer.start()"],"correct":3,"explain":"Constructing it is not enough."},{"id":"zya15_8_3","topic":"Thread.sleep in a listener","text":"Calling Thread.sleep inside a listener:","options":["Is required","Starts a thread","Animates smoothly","Freezes the entire interface"],"correct":3,"explain":"It blocks the thread that also handles clicks and repaints."},{"id":"zya15_8_4","topic":"Animation loop","text":"A while(true) loop with sleep in a GUI:","options":["Runs on a new thread","Is faster","Is the correct approach","Hangs the window"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Timer breaks the work into short callbacks. Is why a Timer is used."},{"id":"zya15_8_5","topic":"Changing speed","text":"To make the animation faster:","options":["Call timer.setDelay with a larger value","Call timer.setDelay with a smaller value","Call repaint() more often from the listener","Start a second timer alongside the first"],"correct":1,"explain":"The delay is in milliseconds."},{"id":"zya15_8_6","topic":"Stopping","text":"Halting animation uses:","options":["timer.end()","timer = null","timer.pause()","timer.stop()"],"correct":3,"explain":"It can be restarted later with start()."},{"id":"zya15_8_7","topic":"Redrawing","text":"After moving a shape, the panel is updated by calling:","options":["revalidate()","setVisible","paintComponent","repaint()"],"correct":3,"explain":"Which schedules a paint on the event thread."},{"id":"zyf15_8_0","topic":"Correct Timer","text":"For Swing animation, import:","options":["No timer","java.util.Timer","javax.swing.Timer","java.time.Timer"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Swing timer fires on the event dispatch thread."},{"id":"zyf15_8_1","topic":"Sleep in a listener","text":"Thread.sleep inside a listener:","options":["Starts a thread","Animates","Hangs the whole interface","Is required"],"correct":2,"explain":"It blocks the thread that handles all events."},{"id":"fs_z15_8_1","topic":"Adding a row","text":"A JTable is displaying a DefaultTableModel. What is the correct way to add a row at run time?","options":["Call table.add(new JLabel(...)) for each cell in the new row","Call model.addRow(new Object[]{...}); the table refreshes itself","Call table.addRow(new Object[]{...}) directly on the JTable","Rebuild the JTable from a new array and add it to the panel again"],"correct":1,"explain":"The model owns the data and the table is only a view of it. Changing the model notifies the table, which repaints; JTable itself has no addRow method."},{"id":"fs_z15_8_2","topic":"Missing column headers","text":"A JTable shows its rows but no column headers at all. What is the usual cause?","options":["setVisible(true) was called before the table was created","The table was added directly to the container instead of being placed in a JScrollPane","The column names array was passed after the data array","The model was created with DefaultTableModel instead of TableModel"],"correct":1,"explain":"The header is a separate component that a JScrollPane positions above the table's viewport. Added on its own, a JTable shows only its rows."},{"id":"fs_z15_8_3","topic":"getValueAt","text":"What does model.getValueAt(0, 1) return?","options":["A String rendering of every cell in row 0","The value in row 0, column 1, typed as Object","The value in column 0, row 1, typed as Object","The whole first row as an Object array"],"correct":1,"explain":"TableModel accessors take row first, then column, and return Object because a column may hold any type. Both indexes are zero-based."},{"id":"fs_z15_8_4","topic":"setValueAt argument order","text":"Which call writes the text "taken" into row 3, column 1 of a DefaultTableModel named model?","options":["model.setValueAt("taken", 1, 3)","model.setValueAt("taken", 3, 1)","model.setValueAt(3, 1, "taken")","model.setValueAt(1, 3, "taken")"],"correct":1,"explain":"setValueAt takes the new value first, then the row and the column, which is the reverse shape of getValueAt(row, column) and a common slip."},{"id":"zf4524","topic":"","text":"A JTable displays data as:","options":["a single line","a tree","free text","rows and columns"],"correct":3,"explain":"The spreadsheet-style component.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4525","topic":"","text":"The simple JTable constructor takes:","options":["a 2D data array and a column-name array","a string","one list","a file"],"correct":0,"explain":"new JTable(rowData, columnNames);","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4526","topic":"","text":"In the data array Object[][] rows, each inner array is:","options":["one column","one table row","a header","a cell"],"correct":1,"explain":"Row-major data: rows[2][0] is row 2, first column.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4527","topic":"","text":"Column headers appear when the table is:","options":["clicked","resized","placed in a JScrollPane","colored"],"correct":2,"explain":"The scroll pane hosts the header component - same lesson as text areas.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4528","topic":"","text":"table.getValueAt(1, 2) reads:","options":["column 1, row 2","cell 12","the header","row 1, column 2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Row first, then column - zero-based both.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4529","topic":"","text":"table.setValueAt("Sold", 0, 3) does what?","options":["writes into row 0, column 3","adds a row","renames a column","sorts"],"correct":0,"explain":"Programmatic cell updates for editable models.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4530","topic":"","text":"Filling a table from an ArrayList of objects requires:","options":["building the 2D array","nothing - automatic","toString","a file"],"correct":0,"explain":"One row per object, one column per displayed field.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4531","topic":"","text":"The selected row index comes from:","options":["getValueAt alone","table.getSelectedRow()","the header","a loop"],"correct":1,"explain":"Returns -1 when nothing is selected - check before using.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4532","topic":"","text":"A "details" panel updating when the user picks a row uses:","options":["polling","repaint","a selection listener on the table","a second table"],"correct":2,"explain":"Selection events drive master-detail interfaces.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4533","topic":"","text":"Tables beat text areas for data because:","options":["tables are prettier only","areas cannot show numbers","no reason","columns align and cells are addressable"],"correct":3,"explain":"Structured data deserves a structured component.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4534","topic":"","text":"The seat-reservation example (next sections) uses a table as:","options":["the live grid of seats/bookings","a menu","a splash screen","storage"],"correct":0,"explain":"The table is both display and interaction surface for the grid.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_9":[{"id":"zya15_9_0","topic":"Keypad layout","text":"A calculator keypad of equal buttons is best laid out with:","options":["GridLayout","BorderLayout","null layout","FlowLayout"],"correct":0,"explain":"Equal cells filled row by row is exactly a keypad."},{"id":"zya15_9_1","topic":"Display field","text":"A read-only display uses:","options":["A JTextField with setEnabled(false)","A JTextField with setEditable(false)","A JButton with its listener removed","A JPanel with no components added"],"correct":1,"explain":"It keeps the text-field appearance while preventing typing."},{"id":"zya15_9_2","topic":"One listener for many buttons","text":"Attaching the same handler to every key works because:","options":["e.getSource() identifies which button fired","Each key registers a separate inner class","The listener is called once per key press only","Swing passes the key's text as the event type"],"correct":0,"explain":"The alternative is sixteen nearly identical listeners."},{"id":"zya15_9_3","topic":"Reading the button label","text":"Getting the character a key represents uses:","options":["((JButton) e.getSource()).getText()","e.getKey()","e.getActionCommand() only","e.getText()"],"correct":0,"explain":"getActionCommand also works, since it defaults to the label."},{"id":"zya15_9_4","topic":"Choosing the operation","text":"Dispatching on the operator is naturally done with:","options":["A switch on the operator String","Recursion","An array","A loop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each case applies the corresponding arithmetic."},{"id":"zya15_9_5","topic":"Division guard","text":"Before dividing, the calculator must check:","options":["The divisor is not zero","The result is an int","Nothing","The display is empty"],"correct":0,"explain":"Otherwise ArithmeticException for ints, or Infinity for doubles."},{"id":"zya15_9_6","topic":"Building the number","text":"Pressing 4 then 2 should:","options":["Append","Replace, showing 2","Throw","Show 6"],"correct":0,"explain":"Digits concatenate onto the display text. The display reads 42."},{"id":"zya15_9_7","topic":"Clear behaviour","text":"The C key should reset:","options":["The display only, leaving the stored operand","The stored operand only, leaving the display","The whole window, by disposing and rebuilding it","The display, plus any stored operand and pending operation"],"correct":3,"explain":"Clearing only the display leaves stale state that corrupts the next calculation."},{"id":"zyf15_9_0","topic":"Keypad layout choice","text":"Equal-sized calculator keys suit:","options":["FlowLayout","GridLayout","BorderLayout","null"],"correct":1,"explain":"Equal cells filled row by row."},{"id":"zyf15_9_1","topic":"Divide guard","text":"Before dividing, the calculator must check the divisor is:","options":["Positive","Not zero","An int","Even"],"correct":1,"explain":"Otherwise ArithmeticException or Infinity."},{"id":"fs_z15_9_1","topic":"JSlider constructor","text":"What do the arguments of new JSlider(0, 100, 50) specify, in order?","options":["Minimum, maximum, and initial value","Initial value, minimum, and maximum","Minimum, maximum, and step size","Width in pixels, height in pixels, and initial value"],"correct":0,"explain":"The three-argument constructor is (min, max, value), so this slider ranges from 0 to 100 and starts halfway."},{"id":"fs_z15_9_2","topic":"getValueIsAdjusting","text":"A slider's ChangeListener recomputes an expensive image. The body is wrapped in if (!slider.getValueIsAdjusting()) { ... }. When does the recomputation run?","options":["Only once the user finishes dragging and releases the knob","On every pixel of movement while the knob is dragged","Only the first time the slider is moved after the window opens","Never, because the guard is always false"],"correct":0,"explain":"A slider fires continuously during a drag, and getValueIsAdjusting() is true throughout it. Negating it lets the expensive work happen once, at the end of the gesture; a cheap live preview would omit the guard."},{"id":"fs_z15_9_3","topic":"Slider ticks and labels","text":"Which combination makes numbered tick marks appear every 25 units on a JSlider?","options":["setMajorTickSpacing(25), setPaintTicks(true) and setPaintLabels(true)","setMajorTickSpacing(25) alone, since ticks are painted by default","setPaintTicks(25) and setPaintLabels(25)","setValue(25) and setPaintLabels(true)"],"correct":0,"explain":"The spacing method only says where ticks would go; the two paint flags decide whether the ticks and their numeric labels are actually drawn."},{"id":"fs_z15_9_4","topic":"BorderLayout resizing","text":"A window uses BorderLayout with a control panel in NORTH and a drawing panel in CENTER. What happens when the user enlarges the window?","options":["The CENTER panel absorbs the extra space while NORTH keeps its preferred height","Both panels grow by the same number of pixels","The NORTH panel absorbs the extra space while CENTER stays fixed","Neither grows; the components stay at their original size and are centred"],"correct":0,"explain":"In BorderLayout only CENTER stretches to take the leftover space; the edge regions keep the preferred size of what they hold."},{"id":"zf4535","topic":"","text":"A JSlider lets the user choose:","options":["text","a value by dragging along a range","files","colors only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Continuous-feeling numeric input.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4536","topic":"","text":"new JSlider(0, 100, 50) creates:","options":["50 sliders","range 50-100","range 0-100 starting at 50","three sliders"],"correct":2,"explain":"min, max, initial.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4537","topic":"","text":"Slider changes are heard via:","options":["ActionListener","KeyListener","polling","a ChangeListener’s stateChanged"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same listener family as spinners.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4538","topic":"","text":"slider.getValue() returns:","options":["an int - no casting dance","an Object","a double","a String"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sliders are natively integer-valued.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4539","topic":"","text":"Tick marks appear via:","options":["automatic always","tick spacing plus setPaintTicks","the constructor only","a label"],"correct":1,"explain":"Spacing defines them; painting shows them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4540","topic":"","text":"setPaintLabels(true) adds:","options":["a title","numeric labels at the major ticks","tooltips","colors"],"correct":1,"explain":"Ticks plus labels make the scale readable.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4541","topic":"","text":"A volume control from 0-10 fits a slider because:","options":["volumes are text","ranges are rare","the range is bounded, precision loose","sliders store audio"],"correct":2,"explain":"Fast, fuzzy adjustment - the slider’s strength.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4542","topic":"","text":"During a drag, stateChanged fires:","options":["once at the end only, always","never","per pixel exactly once","repeatedly as the value moves"],"correct":3,"explain":"Use getValueIsAdjusting() to act only on release when needed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4543","topic":"","text":"slider.getValueIsAdjusting() returns true when:","options":["the user is still mid-drag","the slider is broken","the value is max","labels show"],"correct":0,"explain":"Skip expensive updates until the drag settles.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4544","topic":"","text":"Pairing a slider WITH a live label showing its number:","options":["is redundant","precise feedback for a fuzzy control","is impossible","slows the GUI"],"correct":1,"explain":"stateChanged -> label.setText(String.valueOf(slider.getValue())).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4545","topic":"","text":"A slider bound 60-100 for a heart-rate zone GUARANTEES:","options":["nothing","sorted input","no out-of-zone value can ever be chosen","fitness"],"correct":2,"explain":"Like spinners: constrained input by construction.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_10":[{"id":"zya15_10_0","topic":"List model","text":"A JList displays data held in:","options":["An ArrayList passed to its constructor","A plain array that it copies internally","A String with the items separated by newlines","A ListModel such as DefaultListModel"],"correct":3,"explain":"This is the model/view split."},{"id":"zya15_10_1","topic":"Adding an item","text":"Adding to a displayed list uses:","options":["list.setText(...)","list.add(...)","model.addElement(...)","list.append(...)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Changing the JList directly does nothing visible."},{"id":"zya15_10_2","topic":"Automatic update","text":"After model.addElement, the JList:","options":["Does not change","Must be manually refreshed","Updates itself","Must be recreated"],"correct":2,"explain":"That notification is what the model provides. It observes the model."},{"id":"zya15_10_3","topic":"Scrolling","text":"A long list scrolls only if:","options":["It is a JTextArea","It is small","It is wrapped in a JScrollPane","It uses GridLayout"],"correct":2,"explain":"And the scroll pane, not the list, is added to the frame."},{"id":"zya15_10_4","topic":"Selected item","text":"Finding which row is selected uses:","options":["model.getSelected()","list.getSelected()","list.getSelectedIndex()","list.getIndex()"],"correct":2,"explain":"It returns -1 when nothing is selected."},{"id":"zya15_10_5","topic":"Removing safely","text":"Removing the selected item requires first checking:","options":["Nothing","The list is sorted","The selected index is not -1","The model is empty"],"correct":2,"explain":"Calling remove(-1) throws."},{"id":"zya15_10_6","topic":"Empty input","text":"Adding an empty task should be:","options":["Added, then removed on the next refresh","Prevented with a trim-and-isEmpty guard","Replaced with a placeholder task name","Allowed, since the model rejects it anyway"],"correct":1,"explain":"Otherwise blank rows accumulate."},{"id":"zya15_10_7","topic":"Same pattern elsewhere","text":"The model/view split also drives:","options":["JFrame","JLabel","JTable with a TableModel","JButton"],"correct":2,"explain":"The same idea scales to tables and trees."},{"id":"zyf15_10_0","topic":"What to modify","text":"To change what a JList shows you modify:","options":["The JList","The ListModel","The frame","The layout"],"correct":1,"explain":"The view observes the model."},{"id":"zyf15_10_1","topic":"Scrolling","text":"A long JList scrolls only when:","options":["It is sorted","It is wrapped in a JScrollPane","It is small","It uses GridLayout"],"correct":1,"explain":"And the scroll pane is what gets added to the frame."},{"id":"fs_z15_10_1","topic":"Seat numbers versus row indexes","text":"The reservation handler writes model.setValueAt("taken", seat - 1, 1) after the user types a seat number. Why is 1 subtracted?","options":["setValueAt counts rows from the bottom of the table upward","The seat number includes the column, which must be removed","Seats are numbered from 1 but table rows are indexed from 0","The first row of a DefaultTableModel always holds the column headers"],"correct":2,"explain":"The user-facing numbering starts at 1 while the model is zero-based, so seat 1 lives in row 0. Forgetting the conversion reserves the wrong seat, or throws on the last one."},{"id":"fs_z15_10_2","topic":"Guard clauses in a handler","text":"The reserve handler is a series of if checks that each set a status message and return early. What is the benefit of that shape?","options":["It makes the handler run on a background thread automatically","It guarantees the table model cannot be modified by mistake","Each way the input can be wrong is handled once, leaving the success path unnested at the end","It avoids the need for a try-catch anywhere in the handler"],"correct":2,"explain":"Early returns keep the failure cases flat and separate, so the remaining code runs only when every precondition already holds."},{"id":"fs_z15_10_3","topic":"Range checking user input","text":"After the seat number parses successfully, which check must still happen before the model is touched?","options":["That the text field is not currently focused","That the table is inside a JScrollPane","That the number lies between 1 and model.getRowCount()","That the number is even, so it maps to a valid row"],"correct":2,"explain":"A parseable number can still be 0 or 999. Without the range test, setValueAt or getValueAt is called with an index outside the model and throws."},{"id":"fs_z15_10_4","topic":"JList with nothing selected","text":"What does list.getSelectedValue() return when no item in the JList is selected?","options":["An empty string","The first element of the model","null","-1"],"correct":2,"explain":"getSelectedValue() returns the element itself, so it reports null when there is no selection; getSelectedIndex() is the one that uses -1 for the same situation."},{"id":"zf4546","topic":"","text":"The seat-reservation GUI combines:","options":["only labels","a console","one slider","a table, fields, and action buttons"],"correct":3,"explain":"The capstone wiring of the unit’s components.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4547","topic":"","text":"The table’s role is showing:","options":["each seat’s row and current status","the code","user passwords","colors"],"correct":0,"explain":"The live state of the model, rendered as rows.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4548","topic":"","text":"Clicking Reserve triggers:","options":["a repaint only","the button’s actionPerformed","a file dialog","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Event -> validate -> update data -> refresh display.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4549","topic":"","text":"Reserving an already-taken seat should:","options":["overwrite silently","crash","be rejected with a clear message","duplicate the seat"],"correct":2,"explain":"The business rule guards live in the handler (or the model).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4550","topic":"","text":"The underlying data for the table is best kept in:","options":["the table cells alone","the window title","the program’s own structures","a global string"],"correct":2,"explain":"Model drives view - do not use the widget as the database.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4551","topic":"","text":"After a successful reservation, the table updates via:","options":["restarting","re-adding the table","nothing","setValueAt (or refreshing the model)"],"correct":3,"explain":"The visible grid must reflect the changed state immediately.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4552","topic":"","text":"Validating the name field before reserving demonstrates:","options":["input checks living in event handlers","GUIs skip validation","fields self-validate","luck"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same unit-3/6 discipline, event-driven edition.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4553","topic":"","text":"A status label ("Seat 2B reserved for Ana") provides:","options":["decoration","feedback confirming the action’s result","a second table","errors only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every action deserves a visible consequence.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4554","topic":"","text":"The Cancel/Release button’s handler must:","options":["free anything","close the app","verify the seat IS reserved first","sort seats"],"correct":2,"explain":"Symmetric validation for the inverse operation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4555","topic":"","text":"This example’s architecture lesson is:","options":["widgets store truth","main loops forever","GUIs are one-way","events update data"],"correct":3,"explain":"The unidirectional loop that scales to real apps.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4556","topic":"","text":"Persisting reservations between runs would add:","options":["file save/load with the unit 9 streams","nothing","a bigger table","more buttons only"],"correct":0,"explain":"The units connect: GUI state meets file I/O.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u15/z15_11":[{"id":"zya15_11_0","topic":"Converting to a GUI","text":"A console program converts cleanly to a GUI when:","options":["Its calculations are all written inline inside main","It prints each of its results as it computes them","It reads all of its input before doing any calculating","Its calculations sit in methods that take parameters and return"],"correct":3,"explain":"Logic tangled with I/O cannot be reused."},{"id":"zya15_11_1","topic":"The key rule","text":"A method that prints its own result:","options":["Works equally well in a console and a GUI","Must be declared static before it can be reused","Cannot be reused in a GUI, where the console is invisible","Is preferred, since the caller needs no return"],"correct":2,"explain":"Return the value and let the caller decide how to present it."},{"id":"zya15_11_2","topic":"Replacing Scanner","text":"Each nextLine() call becomes:","options":["A label that displays the prompt text","A component to read from, such as a text field","A listener registered on the frame","A dialog that blocks until it is dismissed"],"correct":1,"explain":"The input source changes; the logic does not."},{"id":"zya15_11_3","topic":"Replacing println","text":"Each println becomes:","options":["setText on a label, or an append to a text area","A new Scanner reading from the component","A repaint call on the drawing panel","A separate window opened for each line"],"correct":0,"explain":"Output goes to a visible component."},{"id":"zya15_11_4","topic":"Replacing the main loop","text":"The driving while loop becomes:","options":["A for loop","Event listeners","Recursion","A Timer always"],"correct":1,"explain":"That is the inversion of control at the heart of GUI programming. The user's clicks are the loop."},{"id":"zya15_11_5","topic":"Reusable logic","text":"A method such as toCelsius(double f):","options":["Works only in console programs","Works unchanged in console, GUI, and tests","Must be rewritten","Must print"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is the practical proof that the separation was done correctly."},{"id":"zya15_11_6","topic":"Where validation goes","text":"Input validation in the GUI version belongs:","options":["Inside the calculation method itself, always","In the listener, before calling the calculation","In the constructor of the frame, at start-up","In the paintComponent method, on each redraw"],"correct":1,"explain":"The calculation should receive values it can trust."},{"id":"zya15_11_7","topic":"Benefit of separation","text":"Separating logic from I/O primarily makes code:","options":["Testable and reusable across interfaces","Faster, since fewer methods are called","Smaller, since the source file is shorter","Safer, since exceptions cannot escape"],"correct":0,"explain":"The same argument as returning rather than printing, applied to whole programs."},{"id":"zyf15_11_0","topic":"Reusable logic","text":"A calculation method that returns rather than prints:","options":["Must be void","Works only in console apps","Works in console, GUI and tests alike","Is slower"],"correct":2,"explain":"The single rule that makes conversion possible."},{"id":"zyf15_11_1","topic":"Loop replacement","text":"In a GUI, the console driving loop is replaced by:","options":["A Timer always","A for loop","Event listeners","Recursion"],"correct":2,"explain":"User actions drive execution."},{"id":"fs_z15_11_1","topic":"APPROVE_OPTION","text":"Why must the result of chooser.showOpenDialog(frame) be compared with JFileChooser.APPROVE_OPTION before the file is used?","options":["The comparison is what actually displays the dialog on screen","It converts the chosen File into a Scanner","Without it the dialog stays open and blocks the event thread","The user may cancel the dialog, in which case no file was chosen"],"correct":3,"explain":"showOpenDialog returns a code saying how the dialog was dismissed. Only APPROVE_OPTION means a file was picked; proceeding after a cancel leads to working with no file at all."},{"id":"fs_z15_11_2","topic":"try-with-resources","text":"The Scanner is opened as try (Scanner in = new Scanner(f)) { ... }. What does that form guarantee?","options":["Any exception thrown inside the block is silently ignored","The file is created if it does not already exist","The whole file is read into memory before the block starts","The Scanner is closed automatically when the block ends, even if an exception is thrown"],"correct":3,"explain":"try-with-resources closes anything declared in its header on the way out, so the file handle is released whether the read succeeded or failed."},{"id":"fs_z15_11_3","topic":"StringBuilder while reading","text":"Lines read from the file are collected with sb.append(line).append("\n") rather than text = text + line + "\n". Why?","options":["A String cannot hold a newline character","Only a StringBuilder can be passed to area.setText","Concatenation would read the lines in reverse order","Strings are immutable"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each + on Strings allocates and copies a fresh object, so a long file costs one reallocation per line; a StringBuilder appends into one growing buffer and is converted once at the end. The concatenation version builds a new String on every line."},{"id":"fs_z15_11_4","topic":"Checked exception from Scanner","text":"Which checked exception must be handled when constructing new Scanner(file) for a File object?","options":["NumberFormatException","NoSuchElementException","IllegalArgumentException","FileNotFoundException"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Scanner(File) constructor is declared to throw FileNotFoundException, a checked exception, so the call must sit in a try-catch or the method must declare it. The others are unchecked and need no handler."},{"id":"zf4557","topic":"","text":"A JFileChooser provides:","options":["file reading itself","the standard open/save file dialog","a text area","a menu bar"],"correct":1,"explain":"It picks the file; reading remains your code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4558","topic":"","text":"chooser.showOpenDialog(frame) returns:","options":["the file contents","a boolean","a status (APPROVE_OPTION or cancel)","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Check approval BEFORE using the selection.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4559","topic":"","text":"The chosen file comes from:","options":["getText","args","a scanner","chooser.getSelectedFile()"],"correct":3,"explain":"A File object to feed into your unit-9 reading code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4560","topic":"","text":"If the user cancels the dialog:","options":["read anyway","throw","exit the app","skip the reading gracefully"],"correct":3,"explain":"Cancellation is normal flow, not an error.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4561","topic":"","text":"Reading the chosen file uses:","options":["the same unit-9 Scanner code","special GUI readers","the table","magic"],"correct":0,"explain":"File I/O is I/O; the GUI only supplied the path.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4562","topic":"","text":"FileNotFoundException in a GUI app is best surfaced via:","options":["a console print the user never sees","a dialog (JOptionPane) explaining the problem","silence","a crash"],"correct":1,"explain":"GUI users need GUI-visible errors.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4563","topic":"","text":"JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(frame, msg) displays:","options":["a file dialog","a new frame","a popup message box","a print"],"correct":2,"explain":"The quick modal notification tool.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4564","topic":"","text":"Loaded file lines typically land in:","options":["the title bar","a slider","the button","a text area or table for display"],"correct":3,"explain":"Read, then render - the components from earlier sections.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4565","topic":"","text":"Filtering the chooser to .txt files uses:","options":["a FileNameExtensionFilter","string checks after crash","renaming files","nothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"chooser.setFileFilter(new FileNameExtensionFilter("Text", "txt"));","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4566","topic":"","text":"Reading a large file inside the click handler risks:","options":["nothing","freezing the UI during the read","faster reads","auto-threading"],"correct":1,"explain":"The UI-thread rule again - the recurring theme before unit 18.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4567","topic":"","text":"This section closes the loop between:","options":["sorting and searching","recursion and loops","GUI events and file input","classes and packages"],"correct":2,"explain":"Choose (dialog) -> read (streams) -> show (components).","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u16/z16_1":[{"id":"zya16_1_0","topic":"JavaFX window","text":"In JavaFX, the window is a:","options":["JFrame","Stage","Scene","Pane"],"correct":1,"explain":"Stage is the window, Scene is its content, Panes hold nodes."},{"id":"zya16_1_1","topic":"Application class","text":"A JavaFX app class must:","options":["Extend Stage and override show()","Extend Application and override start(Stage)","Implement Runnable and override run()","Extend Scene and override launch()"],"correct":1,"explain":"The UI is built in start, not in main."},{"id":"zya16_1_2","topic":"Launching","text":"main in a JavaFX app typically calls:","options":["start()","launch(args)","new Application()","run()"],"correct":1,"explain":"launch starts the JavaFX runtime, which then calls start."},{"id":"zya16_1_3","topic":"Adding a node","text":"Adding a component to a pane uses:","options":["pane.add(node)","pane.getChildren().add(node)","pane.append(node)","pane.setNode(node)"],"correct":1,"explain":"There is no direct add method — a frequent early error."},{"id":"zya16_1_4","topic":"Showing the window","text":"A JavaFX window appears when you call:","options":["stage.setVisible(true)","stage.show()","stage.open()","scene.show()"],"correct":1,"explain":"The counterpart of Swing's setVisible(true)."},{"id":"zya16_1_5","topic":"Scene role","text":"A Scene is:","options":["The top-level window with its title bar","The container of the node tree displayed on a stage","A single visual element such as a rectangle","The manager that positions the child nodes"],"correct":1,"explain":"You can swap scenes on one stage to change screens."},{"id":"zya16_1_6","topic":"Node meaning","text":"In JavaFX, every visible thing is a:","options":["Component","Node","Panel","Widget"],"correct":1,"explain":"Buttons, shapes, layouts and images are all Nodes."},{"id":"zyf16_1_0","topic":"Adding children","text":"Nodes are added to a pane with:","options":["pane.add(node)","pane.getChildren().add(node)","pane.append(node)","pane.set(node)"],"correct":1,"explain":"There is no direct add method."},{"id":"zyf16_1_1","topic":"Where the UI is built","text":"A JavaFX UI is constructed in:","options":["the constructor only","a listener","main","start(Stage)"],"correct":3,"explain":"main normally just calls launch."},{"id":"fs_z16_1_1","topic":"launch and start","text":"In a JavaFX application, main contains only launch(args). What does that call do?","options":["It creates the Stage object and returns it to main for further setup","It starts the JavaFX runtime","It immediately calls start on the same thread that ran main and returns its result","It compiles the scene graph so that show() can display it later"],"correct":1,"explain":"launch initialises the toolkit and hands control to the framework, which constructs the primary Stage and calls start with it. That is why the interface is built in start rather than in main. Then calls start(Stage) on the JavaFX application thread."},{"id":"fs_z16_1_2","topic":"Containment order","text":"Which describes the required nesting in a JavaFX application?","options":["A Stage holds a Scene, the Scene holds a root node, and the root node holds the other nodes","A Scene holds a Stage, the Stage holds a root node, and the root node holds the other nodes","A Stage holds nodes directly, and a Scene is only needed for styling","A root node holds a Scene, which holds one Stage per window"],"correct":0,"explain":"Stage is the window, Scene is its contents, and the Scene has exactly one root node whose children make up the rest of the interface. Skipping a level means nothing appears."},{"id":"fs_z16_1_3","topic":"Scene constructor","text":"What do the numbers mean in new Scene(root, 300, 200)?","options":["The number of columns and rows available to the root pane","The minimum and maximum width the window may take","The width and the height of the scene, in pixels","The x and y position of the window on the desktop"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Scene constructor takes the root node followed by a width and a height; the Stage then sizes itself around that scene."},{"id":"fs_z16_1_4","topic":"Nothing appears","text":"A JavaFX program creates a Button, sets a Scene on the Stage and calls stage.show(), but the window comes up empty. What was most likely omitted?","options":["The button was never added to the layout pane used as the scene's root","The call to stage.setTitle, without which controls stay hidden","The call to button.setVisible(true), which is false by default","The call to launch inside start, which draws the scene graph"],"correct":0,"explain":"A node is displayed only if it is part of the scene graph, reached from the scene's root. Constructing a control and never adding it to a pane leaves it invisible."},{"id":"fs_z16_1_5","topic":"JavaFX packages","text":"Which package contains the JavaFX controls Button, Label and TextField?","options":["javafx.application","javax.swing","javafx.scene.control","javafx.stage"],"correct":2,"explain":"Controls live in javafx.scene.control; javafx.stage holds Stage, javafx.application holds Application, and javax.swing is the older toolkit entirely."},{"id":"zf4568","topic":"","text":"A JavaFX application extends:","options":["JFrame","Applet","Main","Application, overriding start(Stage)"],"correct":3,"explain":"The framework instantiates your class and calls start.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4569","topic":"","text":"In JavaFX terms, the Stage is:","options":["the window","the layout","a button","the theme"],"correct":0,"explain":"Stage = window; Scene = its contents.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4570","topic":"","text":"The Scene holds:","options":["the node tree shown on the stage","files","threads","the JVM"],"correct":0,"explain":"scene = new Scene(rootNode, width, height);","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4571","topic":"","text":"JavaFX UI elements are called:","options":["widgets in the Swing sense","nodes (controls like Button, Label)","frames","beans"],"correct":1,"explain":"Everything on screen is a node in the scene graph.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4572","topic":"","text":"stage.setScene(scene); stage.show(); does what?","options":["compiles","hides the app","attaches content and shows the window","saves state"],"correct":2,"explain":"The JavaFX reveal, parallel to setVisible(true).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4573","topic":"","text":"launch(args) inside main:","options":["calls start directly","loops","is optional always","boots the JavaFX runtime"],"correct":3,"explain":"The framework controls the lifecycle around your code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4574","topic":"","text":"JavaFX relative to Swing is:","options":["the newer Java UI toolkit","the same library","deprecated first","console-only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Modern styling, scene graph, properties - the successor generation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4575","topic":"","text":"JavaFX styling can use:","options":["only code","CSS applied to nodes","HTML","paint overrides"],"correct":1,"explain":"Web-like separation of look from structure.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4576","topic":"","text":"A Label in JavaFX is created:","options":["through XML only","via JLabel","new Label("text") like other nodes","by the stage"],"correct":2,"explain":"Same construct-and-add rhythm, new class names.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4577","topic":"","text":"The scene graph organizes nodes as:","options":["a flat list","a map","a table","a tree: root layout holding children"],"correct":3,"explain":"Layout panes nest - structure mirrors the visual hierarchy.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4578","topic":"","text":"Swing knowledge transfers to JavaFX because:","options":["the concepts (components","the APIs are identical","it does not","JavaFX has no events"],"correct":0,"explain":"Learn the ideas once; map the vocabulary.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u16/z16_2":[{"id":"zya16_2_0","topic":"GridPane coordinates","text":"grid.add(node, 2, 1) places the node at:","options":["Column 2, row 1","Index 21","The centre","Row 2, column 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"Column first, then row — the reverse of array[row][col], and the classic JavaFX transposition bug."},{"id":"zya16_2_1","topic":"Spacing","text":"Space between GridPane columns is set with:","options":["setHgap","setPadding","setMargin","setSpacing"],"correct":0,"explain":"setVgap handles rows and setPadding handles the outer edge."},{"id":"zya16_2_2","topic":"Spanning cells","text":"grid.add(node, 0, 3, 2, 1) means the node spans:","options":["Two columns and one row","Four cells","Nothing","Two rows"],"correct":0,"explain":"The last two arguments are colSpan and rowSpan."},{"id":"zya16_2_3","topic":"Vertical stacking","text":"Stacking children vertically uses:","options":["VBox","GridPane","StackPane","HBox"],"correct":0,"explain":"HBox lays them out in a row."},{"id":"zya16_2_4","topic":"Overlapping children","text":"StackPane places children:","options":["On top of each other, centred","In a grid","In five regions","Side by side"],"correct":0,"explain":"Useful for overlaying text on an image."},{"id":"zya16_2_5","topic":"Five regions","text":"The JavaFX analogue of BorderLayout is:","options":["BorderPane","TilePane","AnchorPane","FlowPane"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same five-region idea."},{"id":"zya16_2_6","topic":"Constructor spacing","text":"new VBox(10) sets:","options":["Ten pixels of spacing between children","A vertical box exactly ten pixels wide","A vertical box holding at most ten children","Ten pixels of padding around the whole box"],"correct":0,"explain":"A convenient shorthand for setSpacing(10)."},{"id":"zyf16_2_0","topic":"Argument order","text":"grid.add(node, col, row) takes:","options":["Column then row","Index only","Width then height","Row then column"],"correct":0,"explain":"The reverse of array[row][col] — the classic transposition bug."},{"id":"zyf16_2_1","topic":"Horizontal spacing","text":"Space between columns is set with:","options":["setSpacing","setVgap","setHgap","setPadding"],"correct":2,"explain":"setVgap handles rows."},{"id":"zyf16_2_2","topic":"Overlaying nodes","text":"Placing children on top of one another uses:","options":["StackPane","HBox","GridPane","VBox"],"correct":0,"explain":"They are centred by default."},{"id":"fs_z16_2_1","topic":"Padding versus gaps","text":"What does grid.setPadding(new Insets(20)) do, compared with setHgap and setVgap?","options":["It adds space between the pane's outer edge and its contents, while the gaps space the cells from each other","It adds space between the cells, while the gaps affect the outer edge","It sets the pixel size of every cell, overriding the gaps","It has no effect unless a gap of the same size is also set"],"correct":0,"explain":"Padding is the margin inside the pane around everything it holds; hgap and vgap are the horizontal and vertical space between neighbouring cells."},{"id":"fs_z16_2_2","topic":"Two nodes in one cell","text":"Two nodes are added to a GridPane with the same column and row. What happens?","options":["Both stay in the grid and are drawn on top of one another in the same cell","The second replaces the first, which is removed from the pane","The second is pushed into the next free cell to the right","An IllegalArgumentException is thrown when the second is added"],"correct":0,"explain":"A GridPane cell is not limited to one child; the nodes simply overlap, which usually looks like the earlier one has vanished behind the later one."},{"id":"fs_z16_2_3","topic":"BorderPane regions","text":"An application uses a BorderPane with a menu bar in setTop, a table in setCenter and a status bar in setBottom. Which part grows when the window is enlarged?","options":["The centre region only","All three regions, in proportion to their contents","The top and bottom regions only","None of them, unless a GridPane is used instead"],"correct":0,"explain":"As with Swing's BorderLayout, only the centre takes the leftover space; the edge regions keep the preferred size of their contents."},{"id":"fs_z16_2_4","topic":"FlowPane","text":"Which JavaFX pane arranges its children left to right and wraps to a new line when the row is full, like Swing's FlowLayout?","options":["FlowPane","StackPane","GridPane","VBox"],"correct":0,"explain":"FlowPane is the direct counterpart of FlowLayout. StackPane centres children on top of each other, GridPane uses explicit cells, and VBox is a single column."},{"id":"zf4579","topic":"","text":"A GridPane positions children:","options":["absolutely","in rows and columns","in a circle","one per window"],"correct":1,"explain":"JavaFX’s grid layout - the GridBag counterpart.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4580","topic":"","text":"grid.add(nameField, 1, 0) places the field at:","options":["row 1, column 0","column 1, row 0","pixel (1,0)","cell 10"],"correct":1,"explain":"GridPane.add takes (node, columnIndex, rowIndex) - column FIRST.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4581","topic":"","text":"The column-first argument order vs Swing’s habits:","options":["does not matter","is the same","is a classic misplacement trap","only affects labels"],"correct":2,"explain":"Swapped coordinates put controls in surprising cells.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4582","topic":"","text":"grid.setHgap(10) and setVgap(8) control:","options":["cell sizes","font sizes","borders","spacing between columns and rows"],"correct":3,"explain":"Gap properties replace per-component inset juggling.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4583","topic":"","text":"grid.setPadding(new Insets(10)) adds:","options":["space around the whole grid","cell borders","a title","margins per node only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Outer breathing room for the layout.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4584","topic":"","text":"A label-and-field form row in GridPane uses:","options":["two rows","the label at (0","one cell","absolute pixels"],"correct":1,"explain":"The same two-column form pattern as Swing, cleaner API.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4585","topic":"","text":"GridPane.setColumnSpan(node, 2) (or add with spans):","options":["duplicates it","widens pixels","lets a node cover two columns","adds columns"],"correct":2,"explain":"Buttons and headers often span the form width.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4586","topic":"","text":"The GridPane usually serves as:","options":["the stage","a control","a thread","the scene’s root (or a nested pane)"],"correct":3,"explain":"new Scene(grid, w, h) - layout as the content root.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4587","topic":"","text":"Resizing the window with a GridPane:","options":["reflows/stretches per the constraints","clips fixed pixels","crashes","closes"],"correct":0,"explain":"Managed layout adapts - the perennial layout-manager payoff.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4588","topic":"","text":"Other JavaFX panes include:","options":["GridBagLayout","VBox and HBox for simple stacking","BorderFactory","JPanel"],"correct":1,"explain":"Simple linear layouts for simple arrangements.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4589","topic":"","text":"Nesting an HBox of buttons inside a GridPane cell shows:","options":["an error","flattening","layout composition - panes within panes","one layout per app"],"correct":2,"explain":"Complex screens compose from simple layout pieces.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u16/z16_3":[{"id":"zya16_3_0","topic":"Button handler","text":"A JavaFX button handler is registered with:","options":["addEventHandler only","onClick","addActionListener","setOnAction"],"correct":3,"explain":"It takes an EventHandler<ActionEvent>, so a lambda works."},{"id":"zya16_3_1","topic":"Replacing handlers","text":"Calling setOnAction twice on one button:","options":["Throws","Is a compile error","Registers both","Replaces the first handler"],"correct":3,"explain":"The opposite of Swing's addActionListener, which accumulates."},{"id":"zya16_3_2","topic":"Reading a combo box","text":"Getting the selected value uses:","options":["getText()","getSelected()","getSelectedItem()","getValue()"],"correct":3,"explain":"A small but frequently tested difference from Swing."},{"id":"zya16_3_3","topic":"Clearing a field","text":"Emptying a JavaFX TextField uses:","options":["reset(), which restores the initial value","delete(), which removes the field itself","setText(null), which is the only valid way","clear(), though setText("") also works"],"correct":3,"explain":"clear() is the idiomatic form."},{"id":"zya16_3_4","topic":"Property listener","text":"textProperty().addListener(...) fires:","options":["Only when the field loses keyboard focus","Only when Enter is pressed in the field","On every change to the text, such as each keystroke","Once, when the listener is first registered"],"correct":2,"explain":"It enables live reaction without a button."},{"id":"zya16_3_5","topic":"Binding","text":"output.textProperty().bind(input.textProperty()) means:","options":["The output is copied once, at bind time","The output always mirrors the input automatically","The two fields swap their values each change","The input becomes read-only from then on"],"correct":1,"explain":"Declarative synchronization — the feature Swing lacks."},{"id":"zya16_3_6","topic":"Setting a bound property","text":"Calling setText on a bound label:","options":["Unbinds it","Is ignored","Works normally","Throws"],"correct":3,"explain":"You must unbind first. The property is bound."},{"id":"zya16_3_7","topic":"Lambda equivalence","text":"setOnAction(e -> ...) is shorthand for:","options":["A subclass of Button overriding its action","A separate thread that waits for the click","A binding between the button and the property","An anonymous EventHandler with one handle method"],"correct":3,"explain":"Single-abstract-method interfaces accept lambdas."},{"id":"zyf16_3_0","topic":"Handler replacement","text":"Calling setOnAction twice:","options":["Is illegal","Registers both","Replaces the first","Throws"],"correct":2,"explain":"The opposite of Swing addActionListener."},{"id":"zyf16_3_1","topic":"Combo box value","text":"Reading the selection uses:","options":["getItem()","getSelectedItem()","getValue()","getText()"],"correct":2,"explain":"A small but frequently tested API difference."},{"id":"fs_z16_3_1","topic":"EventHandler","text":"In button.setOnAction(e -> label.setText("clicked")), which interface does the lambda implement?","options":["Runnable","EventHandler<ActionEvent>","ActionListener","ChangeListener<String>"],"correct":1,"explain":"JavaFX uses EventHandler, whose single method handle(T event) receives the event. Because it has just one abstract method, a lambda can supply it; ActionListener is the Swing equivalent."},{"id":"fs_z16_3_2","topic":"Property change listener","text":"In field.textProperty().addListener((obs, oldVal, newVal) -> ...), what does newVal hold?","options":["The TextField object whose property changed","The field's text after the change that triggered the listener","The text the field held before the change","The single character the user just typed"],"correct":1,"explain":"A ChangeListener receives the observable value followed by the old value and the new value, so newVal is the current contents and the listener fires on every edit rather than only on Enter."},{"id":"fs_z16_3_3","topic":"The event object","text":"The handler registered with setOnAction receives one parameter. What is it, and what is it typically used for?","options":["A boolean saying whether the control is still enabled","An ActionEvent, whose getSource() tells you which control fired it","A Stage, so the handler can close the window","A String holding the control's text"],"correct":1,"explain":"The handler is passed the ActionEvent describing what happened; getSource() identifies the control, which lets one handler serve several buttons."},{"id":"fs_z16_3_4","topic":"The application thread","text":"Why should a JavaFX event handler avoid a long-running computation?","options":["Any code taking more than one frame throws IllegalStateException","It runs on the JavaFX application thread","Handlers are limited to one second and are cancelled after that","Long computations cause the handler to be replaced by the next setOnAction"],"correct":1,"explain":"Handlers execute on the single thread that also draws the interface and delivers events, exactly as in Swing, so slow work inside one freezes the window until it returns. The interface stops responding until it finishes."},{"id":"zf4590","topic":"","text":"JavaFX button clicks are handled via:","options":["addActionListener","onClick =","button.setOnAction(handler)","polling"],"correct":2,"explain":"The set-on-event style replaces Swing’s add-listener.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4591","topic":"","text":"The handler is typically written as:","options":["a subclass of Stage","XML","a loop","a lambda: e -> { ... }"],"correct":3,"explain":"setOnAction(e -> label.setText("Hi")); - concise event code.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4592","topic":"","text":"The event parameter e in the lambda is:","options":["an ActionEvent describing the trigger","the button itself always","a String","the scene"],"correct":0,"explain":"Query it (e.getSource()) when one handler serves several nodes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4593","topic":"","text":"Reading a TextField in a handler uses:","options":["getValue","field.getText()","read()","args"],"correct":1,"explain":"Same accessor name as Swing - some vocabulary carries straight over.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4594","topic":"","text":"Updating a Label from the handler uses:","options":["println","drawString","label.setText(newText)","setLabel"],"correct":2,"explain":"Output-by-component-update, the GUI constant.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4595","topic":"","text":"The lambda handler runs on:","options":["its own thread","the main thread after start returns","the GC thread","the JavaFX Application Thread"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same single-UI-thread model - keep handlers brief.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4596","topic":"","text":"Handling text-change-as-you-type uses:","options":["a listener on the field’s textProperty()","setOnAction only","polling","key codes only"],"correct":0,"explain":"JavaFX properties are observable - change listeners attach to them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4597","topic":"","text":"The parse-and-validate discipline inside JavaFX handlers is:","options":["unnecessary","identical to Swing and console code","automatic","different math"],"correct":1,"explain":"try/catch around Integer.parseInt lives wherever input does.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4598","topic":"","text":"setOnAction replacing a listener INTERFACE implementation shows:","options":["interfaces are gone","events changed meaning","lambdas as one-method implementations","magic"],"correct":2,"explain":"EventHandler<ActionEvent> has one method - lambda-ready.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4599","topic":"","text":"Two buttons sharing one named handler method use:","options":["copy-pasted logic","one button","threads","a shared method both lambdas call"],"correct":3,"explain":"Shared behavior still factors into methods - unit 6 forever applies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4600","topic":"","text":"An Enter-key submit on a TextField uses:","options":["a button only","key polling","the field’s own setOnAction","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Text fields fire action events on Enter - free keyboard ergonomics.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u16/z16_4":[{"id":"zya16_4_0","topic":"Shape as an object","text":"JavaFX shapes differ from Swing drawing because they are:","options":["Images","Drawn each frame in a paint method","Objects added to the scene graph once, then mutated","Text only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Retained mode versus immediate mode."},{"id":"zya16_4_1","topic":"Circle parameters","text":"new Circle(50, 50, 30) specifies:","options":["Diameter only","Top-left corner and radius","Centre x, centre y and radius","Two corners"],"correct":2,"explain":"Unlike Rectangle, which takes its top-left corner."},{"id":"zya16_4_2","topic":"Rectangle parameters","text":"new Rectangle(120, 20, 100, 60) specifies:","options":["Top-left x, y, then width and height","Width and height, then top-left x and y","Centre x, y, then width and height","Top-left x, y, then bottom-right x and y"],"correct":0,"explain":"Mixing these up with Circle shifts the shape by one radius."},{"id":"zya16_4_3","topic":"Redrawing","text":"After changing a shape's position you must:","options":["Call show()","Call repaint()","Do nothing","Recreate the shape"],"correct":2,"explain":"A genuine simplification over Swing. The scene graph redraws automatically."},{"id":"zya16_4_4","topic":"Fill versus stroke","text":"setFill sets the interior colour and setStroke sets:","options":["The opacity","The shadow","The outline colour","The size"],"correct":2,"explain":"setStrokeWidth controls the outline thickness."},{"id":"zya16_4_5","topic":"Animation","text":"Continuous animation in JavaFX commonly uses:","options":["repaint()","Thread.sleep","AnimationTimer or Timeline","A while loop"],"correct":2,"explain":"Its handle method is called once per frame."},{"id":"zya16_4_6","topic":"Coordinate origin","text":"JavaFX graphics place (0,0) at:","options":["The top-left, with y increasing downward","The bottom-left, with y increasing upward","The centre of the scene, with y upward","The top-left, with y increasing upward"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same convention as Swing."},{"id":"zya16_4_7","topic":"Immediate mode option","text":"For Swing-style direct drawing, JavaFX offers:","options":["JPanel","No option","Canvas with a GraphicsContext","Only shapes"],"correct":2,"explain":"Useful when there are too many elements for individual nodes."},{"id":"zyf16_4_0","topic":"Circle arguments","text":"new Circle(x, y, r) specifies:","options":["Two corners","Diameter","Top-left and radius","Centre and radius"],"correct":3,"explain":"Rectangle takes its top-left corner instead."},{"id":"zyf16_4_1","topic":"Redraw","text":"After moving a JavaFX shape you must:","options":["Recreate it","Call show","Call repaint","Do nothing"],"correct":3,"explain":"A genuine simplification over Swing. The scene graph redraws itself."},{"id":"fs_z16_4_1","topic":"GraphicsContext","text":"You have created a Canvas. Which call gives you the object whose fillRect and strokeOval methods draw on it?","options":["new GraphicsContext(canvas)","canvas.getChildren()","canvas.getGraphicsContext2D()","canvas.getGraphics()"],"correct":2,"explain":"A JavaFX Canvas hands out a GraphicsContext through getGraphicsContext2D(); getGraphics() belongs to the Swing world, and GraphicsContext is not constructed directly."},{"id":"fs_z16_4_2","topic":"Canvas versus shape nodes","text":"How does drawing on a Canvas differ from adding Shape nodes to a pane?","options":["Canvas drawing ignores the current fill colour and always uses black","Canvas drawing can only be done from within a Shape subclass","Canvas drawing is immediate: the pixels are not kept as objects, so changing something means drawing again","Canvas drawing is retained: each call creates a node that can be moved later"],"correct":2,"explain":"Shape nodes live in the scene graph and redraw themselves when a property changes. A Canvas keeps only the resulting picture, so an update means issuing the drawing calls again, much as Swing's paintComponent does."},{"id":"fs_z16_4_3","topic":"Stroke width","text":"What does circle.setStrokeWidth(3) change?","options":["The number of pixels between the circle and its neighbours","The transparency of the circle's interior","The thickness of the circle's outline","The radius of the circle, in pixels"],"correct":2,"explain":"Fill is the interior and stroke is the outline; setStrokeWidth sets how thick that outline is drawn. The radius is a constructor argument or setRadius."},{"id":"fs_z16_4_4","topic":"Color class","text":"Which package holds the Color class used by circle.setFill(Color.BLUE) in JavaFX?","options":["javafx.scene.shape","javafx.scene.canvas","javafx.scene.paint","java.awt"],"correct":2,"explain":"JavaFX has its own Color in javafx.scene.paint; java.awt.Color is the Swing one, and importing the wrong one is a common source of type errors. Shapes such as Circle come from javafx.scene.shape."},{"id":"zf4601","topic":"","text":"JavaFX drawing surfaces are provided by:","options":["paintComponent","JPanel","the Stage","the Canvas node and its GraphicsContext"],"correct":3,"explain":"canvas.getGraphicsContext2D() hands you the pen.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4602","topic":"","text":"gc.fillRect(10, 10, 80, 40) draws:","options":["a filled rectangle at (10,10), 80 by 40","an outline","a line","text"],"correct":0,"explain":"Same position-then-size convention as Swing’s Graphics.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4603","topic":"","text":"Colors are set with:","options":["g.setColor","gc.setFill(...) and gc.setStroke(...)","CSS only","pixels"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fill for solid shapes, stroke for outlines - split explicitly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4604","topic":"","text":"strokeOval vs fillOval:","options":["big versus small","slow versus fast","outline versus solid","identical"],"correct":2,"explain":"The stroke/fill split runs through the whole API.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4605","topic":"","text":"The JavaFX coordinate origin is:","options":["center","bottom-left","configurable only","top-left, y increasing downward"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same screen-space convention as Swing - the y surprise persists.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4606","topic":"","text":"An alternative to Canvas for simple pictures is:","options":["shape NODES (Circle","ASCII art","tables","labels"],"correct":0,"explain":"new Circle(40) as a node - shapes as first-class scene citizens.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4607","topic":"","text":"Shape nodes differ from canvas drawing because they:","options":["render once","stay live, movable, restylable objects","are faster always","cannot be styled"],"correct":1,"explain":"circle.setCenterX(...) animates; canvas pixels are fire-and-forget.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4608","topic":"","text":"gc.strokeLine(0, 0, 100, 100) draws:","options":["a rectangle","an arc","a diagonal line between those points","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"From (0,0) to (100,100) - down-right across the canvas.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4609","topic":"","text":"gc.fillText("Hi", 20, 30) renders:","options":["a tooltip","a label node","console output","text onto the canvas at that position"],"correct":3,"explain":"Canvas text joins the shapes on the drawing surface.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4610","topic":"","text":"A bar chart on Canvas from an array uses:","options":["one rect","a table","no loop","a loop of data-scaled fillRect calls"],"correct":3,"explain":"The unit-15 exercise, JavaFX edition - data drives geometry.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4611","topic":"","text":"Clearing the canvas for a redraw uses:","options":["gc.clearRect(0, 0, width, height)","a new window","deleting nodes","repaint()"],"correct":0,"explain":"Wipe, then redraw - the manual canvas cycle.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_2":[{"id":"zya17_2_1","topic":"Loop condition","text":"The correct binary search loop condition is:","options":["low <= high","low != high","low > high","low < high"],"correct":0,"explain":"Using < misses the case where the range narrows to a single element."},{"id":"zya17_2_2","topic":"Updating low","text":"When a[mid] is less than the target, you set:","options":["high = mid - 1","low = mid","low = mid + 1","high = mid"],"correct":2,"explain":"Assigning low = mid loops forever, since mid can repeat."},{"id":"zya17_2_3","topic":"Complexity","text":"Binary search on n elements is:","options":["O(log n)","O(n log n)","O(1)","O(n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each comparison halves the remaining range."},{"id":"zya17_2_4","topic":"Comparisons on a million","text":"Searching 1,000,000 sorted elements takes about:","options":["100 comparisons","1,000,000 comparisons","20 comparisons","1,000 comparisons"],"correct":2,"explain":"log2(1,000,000) is roughly 20."},{"id":"zya17_2_5","topic":"Overflow-safe midpoint","text":"mid is best computed as:","options":["low + (high","high / 2","(low + high) * 2","(low + high) / 2"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first form can overflow int for very large indices. Low) / 2."},{"id":"zya17_2_6","topic":"Not found","text":"When the range empties, binary search returns:","options":["The midpoint","0","-1","null"],"correct":2,"explain":"The standard not-found convention."},{"id":"zya17_2_7","topic":"Trace","text":"Finding 23 in [2,5,8,12,16,23,38,56,72,91] takes how many comparisons?","options":["3","6","10","1"],"correct":0,"explain":"mid=16 (too small), mid=56 (too big), mid=23 found."},{"id":"zyf17_2_0","topic":"Loop condition","text":"Binary search uses the condition:","options":["low != high","low > high","low < high","low <= high"],"correct":3,"explain":"Using < misses single-element ranges."},{"id":"zyf17_2_1","topic":"Updating high","text":"When a[mid] exceeds the target, you set:","options":["low = mid + 1","high = mid - 1","high = mid","low = mid"],"correct":1,"explain":"Assigning high = mid can loop forever."},{"id":"fs_z17_2_1","topic":"Binary search trace","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11};
System.out.println(bsearch(a, 9));

where bsearch is:

static int bsearch(int[] a, int target) {
  int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}","options":["9","3","-1","4"],"correct":3,"explain":"First pass: lo=0, hi=5, mid=2, a[2]=5 < 9, so lo becomes 3. Second pass: mid=4, a[4]=9 matches, so index 4 is returned. Binary search returns the index, not the value."},{"id":"fs_z17_2_2","topic":"Midpoints visited","text":"This version prints a[mid] each pass before comparing. What does it print for target 8?

int[] a = {2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72, 91};
