5"],"correct":2,"explain":"lo=0, hi=9 gives mid=4 (16); 16 > 8 sets hi=3. Then mid=1 (5); 5 < 8 sets lo=2. Then mid=2 (8), a match. The midpoints visited are 16, 5, 8 - three comparisons."},{"id":"fs_z17_2_3","topic":"Violated precondition","text":"Binary search is run on an array that is NOT sorted. The most likely result is:","options":["It silently returns -1 for values that are actually present","It throws an IllegalStateException","It still finds every value, just more slowly","It sorts the array automatically before searching"],"correct":0,"explain":"Violating the sorted precondition fails silently: the halving logic discards the half that actually contains the target, so present values are simply missed. Nothing checks sortedness and no exception is thrown."},{"id":"fs_z17_2_4","topic":"Loop condition bug","text":"A student writes the loop as while (lo < hi) instead of while (lo <= hi). The consequence is:","options":["The search examines twice as many elements","Nothing changes; the two conditions are equivalent","A range containing exactly one element is never examined","The loop never terminates"],"correct":2,"explain":"When lo == hi the range still holds one unexamined element. With lo < hi the loop exits at that point, so a target sitting in that last slot is missed and -1 is returned. lo <= hi is required. A present target can be reported missing."},{"id":"fs_z17_2_5","topic":"Absent target trace","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {10, 20, 30, 40};
System.out.println(bsearch(a, 25));

where bsearch is:

static int bsearch(int[] a, int target) {
  int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}","options":["-1","2","1","It loops forever"],"correct":0,"explain":"mid=1 (20 < 25) sets lo=2; mid=2 (30 > 25) sets hi=1. Now lo > hi, the range is empty, and -1 is returned. The +1/-1 updates guarantee the range shrinks, so it cannot loop forever."},{"id":"zf4623","topic":"","text":"Binary search requires:","options":["unique data","numeric data","small data","sorted data"],"correct":3,"explain":"The discard-half logic depends entirely on order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4624","topic":"","text":"Each binary search step:","options":["halves the remaining range","removes one element","doubles the range","sorts a half"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compare the middle, keep the possible half.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4625","topic":"","text":"Worst-case comparisons on 1,000,000 sorted items: about","options":["1,000,000","20","500,000","1000"],"correct":1,"explain":"log2 of a million is ~20 - the headline number.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4626","topic":"","text":"mid for the range low..high is computed:","options":["low + high","high - low","(low + high) / 2","low * 2"],"correct":2,"explain":"The midpoint index of the current window.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4627","topic":"","text":"If arr[mid] < target, the search continues in:","options":["low .. mid - 1","the same range","a new array","mid + 1 .. high"],"correct":3,"explain":"Everything at mid and below is too small.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4628","topic":"","text":"The not-found condition is:","options":["low exceeding high","mid == 0","arr[mid] == -1","one comparison left"],"correct":0,"explain":"An empty window means the value cannot exist.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4629","topic":"","text":"Binary search on {2, 5, 8, 12, 16} for 12: the FIRST index examined is:","options":["0","2 (value 8)","4","3"],"correct":1,"explain":"mid of 0..4 is 2; 8 < 12 sends the search right.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4630","topic":"","text":"That search finds 12 after how many comparisons?","options":["1","2","3","5"],"correct":1,"explain":"Compare 8 (go right), then mid of 3..4 is 3 - value 12. Found.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4631","topic":"","text":"Binary search on UNSORTED data:","options":["is just slower","still works","returns wrong answers","sorts as it goes"],"correct":2,"explain":"Not slow - WRONG. The precondition is not optional.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4632","topic":"","text":"Doubling n from 1M to 2M adds roughly how many comparisons?","options":["1,000,000","20","double","1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Logarithms: each doubling costs one more halving.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4633","topic":"","text":"Sort-once-then-binary-search-many beats repeated linear search when:","options":["searches are frequent","data is tiny and searched once","never","data changes per search"],"correct":0,"explain":"Amortize the sort across many cheap searches.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_3":[{"id":"zya17_3_0","topic":"Big-O meaning","text":"Big-O describes:","options":["The exact runtime in seconds on given hardware","The number of lines the algorithm contains","How runtime grows as the input grows","The memory the compiled program occupies"],"correct":2,"explain":"It deliberately drops constants and lower-order terms."},{"id":"zya17_3_1","topic":"Simplifying","text":"3n^2 + 5n + 100 is:","options":["O(n^2)","O(3n^2)","O(100)","O(n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Keep only the dominant term, without its coefficient."},{"id":"zya17_3_2","topic":"Constant time","text":"O(1) means the operation:","options":["Takes the same time regardless of input size","Takes exactly one step, whatever the input","Takes time proportional to the input size","Takes one second on any modern machine"],"correct":0,"explain":"Array indexing and HashMap get are the standard examples."},{"id":"zya17_3_3","topic":"Correct notation","text":"The right way to state linear growth is:","options":["O(n)","O(n + 5)","O(n/2)","O(2n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Constants are dropped by definition."},{"id":"zya17_3_4","topic":"Sequential loops","text":"Two loops one after another over n items are:","options":["O(n)","O(2n) which is different from O(n)","O(log n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Addition, not multiplication — n + n = 2n, which is O(n)."},{"id":"zya17_3_5","topic":"Nested loops","text":"One loop inside another over n items is:","options":["O(n^2)","O(2n)","O(log n)","O(n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Multiplication, because the inner loop runs fully per outer iteration."},{"id":"zya17_3_6","topic":"Doubling counter","text":"A loop with i *= 2 runs:","options":["log n times","n^2 times","Once","n times"],"correct":0,"explain":"The counter reaches n after about log2 n doublings."},{"id":"zya17_3_7","topic":"Ordering the ladder","text":"Which is fastest-growing?","options":["O(2^n)","O(log n)","O(n log n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Exponential growth outpaces every polynomial, and factorial outpaces exponential."},{"id":"zya17_3_8","topic":"Why constants drop","text":"Constants are ignored because:","options":["Growth rate dominates for large n","They are unknown","Java optimizes them","They are always 1"],"correct":0,"explain":"At n = 1,000,000 an O(n) algorithm with a large constant still beats O(n^2)."},{"id":"zyf17_3_0","topic":"Simplification rule","text":"2n + 7 simplifies to:","options":["O(n + 7)","O(2n)","O(n)","O(7)"],"correct":2,"explain":"Constants and lower-order terms are dropped."},{"id":"fs_z17_3_1","topic":"Growth-rate ordering","text":"Which list orders complexities from slowest-growing to fastest-growing?","options":["O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n^2)","O(n), O(log n), O(n^2), O(n log n)","O(n log n), O(n), O(log n), O(n^2)","O(log n), O(n^2), O(n), O(n log n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Logarithmic grows slowest, then linear, then linearithmic, then quadratic. For n = 1,000,000 that is roughly 20 vs 10^6 vs 2x10^7 vs 10^12 operations."},{"id":"fs_z17_3_2","topic":"O(1) operations","text":"Which operation on a million-element structure is O(1)?","options":["Reading a[500] from an array","Binary search of a sorted array","Linear search of an array","Merge sorting the array"],"correct":0,"explain":"Array indexing is constant time - the element's address is computed directly, no matter how large the array is. Binary search is O(log n), linear search O(n), merge sort O(n log n)."},{"id":"fs_z17_3_3","topic":"Scaling a quadratic","text":"An O(n^2) algorithm takes about 1 second on 1,000 items. On 2,000 items it should take about:","options":["4 seconds","2 seconds","1 second","8 seconds"],"correct":0,"explain":"Quadratic work grows with the square of the input: doubling n multiplies n^2 by four. Big-O is exactly this kind of scaling statement - it predicts ratios, not absolute times."},{"id":"fs_z17_3_4","topic":"Doubling loop count","text":"What does this print?

int count = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < 16; i *= 2) {
  count++;
}
System.out.println(count);","options":["4","16","5","8"],"correct":0,"explain":"i takes the values 1, 2, 4, 8 - four iterations - and stops when i reaches 16. A counter that doubles runs about log2(n) times, which is where O(log n) comes from in loop code."},{"id":"zf4634","topic":"","text":"3n + 20 simplifies to:","options":["O(3n)","O(n)","O(n + 20)","O(1)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Constants and lower terms vanish in O notation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4635","topic":"","text":"n2/2 + 5n simplifies to:","options":["O(n)","O(n^2/2)","O(n^2)","O(5n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"The dominant term rules; its coefficient drops.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4636","topic":"","text":"Array access arr[i] is:","options":["O(n)","O(log n)","O(i)","O(1)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Constant time - independent of array size.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4637","topic":"","text":"Linear search is O(n); binary search is:","options":["O(log n)","O(n log n)","O(1)","O(n^2)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Halving per step is the logarithm’s signature.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4638","topic":"","text":"A doubly nested loop over n items is typically:","options":["O(2n)","O(n^2)","O(n)","O(log n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"n iterations of n iterations multiply.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4639","topic":"","text":"Ranked fastest-growing last:","options":["O(n), O(1), O(n^2), O(log n), O(n log n)","O(n^2) first","O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n^2)","random"],"correct":2,"explain":"The ladder every algorithm sits on.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4640","topic":"","text":"At n = 1000, an O(n^2) algorithm does roughly:","options":["a thousand","a billion","a million steps","ten"],"correct":2,"explain":"n squared: 1000 x 1000.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4641","topic":"","text":"Two sequential (non-nested) O(n) loops total:","options":["O(n^2)","O(2^n)","O(n log n)","O(n)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Addition of same-order terms stays that order - only nesting multiplies.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4642","topic":"","text":"O notation ignores constants because:","options":["growth rate dominates for large inputs","constants are secret","hardware is fixed","it is lazy"],"correct":0,"explain":"A 2x constant loses to an n-fold growth difference every time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4643","topic":"","text":"An algorithm whose steps double per element added is:","options":["O(n^2)","O(2^n)","O(n)","O(log n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Exponential - the practical wall (unit 11’s subsets live here).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4945","topic":"","text":"for (int i = 1; i < n; i = i * 2) count++; This loop is:","options":["O(n)","O(1)","O(log n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":2,"explain":"i doubles each pass, so about log2(n) passes occur.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_4":[{"id":"zya17_4_0","topic":"Analyzing a single loop","text":"for (int i=0;i&lt;n;i++) with constant work inside is:","options":["O(1)","O(n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The body runs n times."},{"id":"zya17_4_1","topic":"Triangular nesting","text":"for (i=0;i&lt;n;i++) for (j=i+1;j&lt;n;j++) is:","options":["O(n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)","O(n log n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"It runs n(n-1)/2 times, which is still quadratic."},{"id":"zya17_4_2","topic":"Worst versus average","text":"An unqualified complexity statement usually refers to:","options":["The best case","The worst case","The average case","Memory"],"correct":1,"explain":"Quicksort is O(n log n) average but O(n^2) worst."},{"id":"zya17_4_3","topic":"Best case of linear search","text":"Linear search has best case:","options":["O(n)","O(1), when the target is first","O(log n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The worst case is O(n)."},{"id":"zya17_4_4","topic":"Space complexity","text":"Sorting in place uses extra space of:","options":["O(n)","O(1)","O(log n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Merge sort, by contrast, needs O(n) for its temporary array."},{"id":"zya17_4_5","topic":"Recursion space","text":"Recursion costs stack space proportional to:","options":["The input size always","The recursion depth","O(1)","The output size"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is why depth, not just time, matters."},{"id":"zya17_4_6","topic":"Hidden cost","text":"A loop calling list.contains(x) each iteration is:","options":["O(n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)","O(1)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Library calls have costs that must be counted. Contains is itself O(n)."},{"id":"zya17_4_7","topic":"String concatenation in a loop","text":"Building a String with += in a loop is:","options":["O(n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)","O(1)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Exactly why StringBuilder exists. Each concatenation copies the whole String."},{"id":"zya17_4_8","topic":"Mixed nesting","text":"A log-time loop inside a linear loop is:","options":["O(n)","O(n log n)","O(log n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Multiply the two."},{"id":"zyf17_4_0","topic":"Sequential versus nested","text":"Two loops in sequence over n items are:","options":["O(log n)","O(n log n)","O(n^2)","O(n)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Add, do not multiply — nesting is what multiplies."},{"id":"fs_z17_4_1","topic":"Triangular loop count","text":"What does this print?

