    count++;
  }
}
System.out.println(count);","options":["16","12","4","6"],"correct":3,"explain":"The inner loop runs 3, 2, 1, then 0 times: 6 total, which is n(n-1)/2. A triangular nesting does about half the work of a square one but is still O(n^2) - the 1/2 constant drops out."},{"id":"fs_z17_4_2","topic":"Space complexity of recursion","text":"Iterative binary search uses O(1) extra space. The recursive version uses:","options":["O(1), the same as the loop","O(n), one frame per element","O(n log n)","O(log n), one stack frame per halving"],"correct":3,"explain":"Recursion costs stack space proportional to its depth. Binary search halves the range each call, so the depth - and the stack usage - is O(log n), while the loop version needs only a few variables."},{"id":"fs_z17_4_3","topic":"Worst-case input","text":"The worst case for linear search on an array of n elements occurs when the target is:","options":["In the first position","In the middle position","Equal to more than one element","Absent, or in the last position"],"correct":3,"explain":"Worst case means the input that maximizes the work. Linear search must scan the whole array when the target is last or missing: n comparisons, which is what the O(n) claim reports. Both cost n comparisons."},{"id":"fs_z17_4_4","topic":"n log n loop count","text":"What does this print?

