System.out.println(count);","options":["64","11","16","24"],"correct":3,"explain":"The inner loop runs with j = 1, 2, 4: three times (log2 of 8). The outer loop repeats that 8 times, giving 24. A log-time loop nested inside a linear loop is the shape of O(n log n)."},{"id":"zf4644","topic":"","text":"Worst-case analysis asks:","options":["the fastest input","the average luck","the most steps any size-n input forces","the compiler"],"correct":2,"explain":"The guarantee: never worse than this.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4645","topic":"","text":"Best case for linear search:","options":["the target is absent","n/2","sorted input","the target sits first - 1 comparison"],"correct":3,"explain":"Cases describe INPUTS, not moods.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4646","topic":"","text":"Counting "basic operations" (comparisons, assignments) abstracts away:","options":["hardware and language speed differences","the algorithm","loops","inputs"],"correct":0,"explain":"Machine-independent measurement is the analytic point.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4647","topic":"","text":"int count = 0; for (i = 0; i < n; i++) for (j = 0; j < n; j++) count++; The final count is:","options":["2n","n * n","n","n + n"],"correct":1,"explain":"The measurable footprint of nested iteration.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4648","topic":"","text":"for (i = 0; i < n; i++) for (j = i + 1; j < n; j++) runs the inner body:","options":["n^2 exactly","n","n(n-1)/2 times - still O(n^2)","log n"],"correct":2,"explain":"The triangular count: half the square, same order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4649","topic":"","text":"An O(n) algorithm processing 1,000 items in 2 ms should handle 10,000 in roughly:","options":["2 ms","200 ms","2 s","20 ms"],"correct":3,"explain":"Linear scaling: 10x data, 10x time.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4650","topic":"","text":"An O(n^2) algorithm at 2 ms for 1,000 items needs roughly what for 10,000?","options":["20 ms","2 s","4 ms","200 ms"],"correct":3,"explain":"10x data means 100x time under quadratic growth.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4651","topic":"","text":"Space complexity measures:","options":["extra memory growth with input size","disk brands","time again","code bytes"],"correct":0,"explain":"The same O-language applied to memory.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4652","topic":"","text":"An in-place algorithm uses:","options":["no memory at all","O(1) extra space","O(n) extra","the disk"],"correct":1,"explain":"Constant scratch space beyond the input itself.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4653","topic":"","text":"Analysis guides engineering by:","options":["naming variables","replacing testing","predicting what survives data growth","styling code"],"correct":2,"explain":"The 100x-data question is answered before the rewrite, not after.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4654","topic":"","text":"Measuring runtime empirically COMPLEMENTS O analysis because:","options":["analysis is wrong","timing is exact","they conflict","constants and caches matter in practice"],"correct":3,"explain":"Theory bounds growth; the stopwatch grounds it.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_6":[{"id":"zya17_6_0","topic":"Selection sort method","text":"Selection sort works by:","options":["Partitioning around a pivot","Merging halves","Inserting each element into a sorted prefix","Repeatedly finding the smallest remaining element and swapping it into place"],"correct":3,"explain":"Each pass places one element in its final position."},{"id":"zya17_6_1","topic":"Complexity","text":"Selection sort’s running time is:","options":["O(n log n)","O(log n)","O(n) best case","O(n^2) in all cases"],"correct":3,"explain":"The inner loop always scans the whole remaining tail, even on sorted input."},{"id":"zya17_6_2","topic":"Swap count","text":"Selection sort performs at most:","options":["n log n swaps","No swaps","n^2 swaps","n - 1 swaps"],"correct":3,"explain":"The fewest of any simple sort, which matters when writes are expensive."},{"id":"zya17_6_3","topic":"Tracking the minimum","text":"The inner loop should track:","options":["The maximum","The count","The minimum value","The index of the minimum"],"correct":3,"explain":"You need the index in order to swap."},{"id":"zya17_6_4","topic":"After k passes","text":"After k passes, the first k positions hold:","options":["The k largest values in final sorted order","k values sorted among themselves but not final","The k smallest values in final sorted order","The original first k values, still unmoved"],"correct":2,"explain":"A correct prefix — which is how you identify selection sort from a partial trace."},{"id":"zya17_6_6","topic":"Adaptive behaviour","text":"On an already-sorted array, selection sort:","options":["Skips the loop","Throws","Finishes in O(n)","Still takes O(n^2)"],"correct":3,"explain":"It is not adaptive, unlike insertion sort."},{"id":"zya17_6_7","topic":"Trace","text":"Sorting [29,10,14,37,13], after the first pass the array is:","options":["[10,13,14,37,29]","[13,10,14,37,29]","[29,10,13,37,14]","[10,29,14,37,13]"],"correct":3,"explain":"The minimum 10 swaps with the first element 29."},{"id":"zyf17_6_0","topic":"Always quadratic","text":"Selection sort on already-sorted data is:","options":["O(1)","O(n)","Still O(n^2)","O(log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is not adaptive — the inner scan always runs fully."},{"id":"fs_z17_6_1","topic":"Selection sort two passes","text":"What does this print? (Only the first TWO passes of selection sort run.)

int[] a = {5, 2, 9, 1, 6};
for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
  int min = i;
  for (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++) {
    if (a[j] < a[min]) min = j;
  }
  int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[min]; a[min] = tmp;
}
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));","options":["[2, 5, 1, 9, 6]","[1, 2, 9, 5, 6]","[1, 2, 5, 9, 6]","[1, 2, 5, 6, 9]"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pass 1 swaps the minimum 1 into position 0: {1, 2, 9, 5, 6}. Pass 2 finds 2 already at position 1 (it swaps with itself). After two passes the first two slots hold the two smallest values; the rest is untouched."},{"id":"fs_z17_6_2","topic":"Why selection sort is unstable","text":"Selection sort is not stable because:","options":["It makes too many swaps","Its long-distance swap can jump an equal element over another","It compares elements with == instead of equals","It uses recursion"],"correct":1,"explain":"The swap moves the minimum from far away into position i, and whatever was at position i lands far to the right - possibly leapfrogging an element equal to it, which destroys the original relative order of equals."},{"id":"fs_z17_6_3","topic":"Selection sort pass-by-pass","text":"What does this print? (The array is shown after each pass.)

