for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
  int min = i;
  for (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++) {
    if (a[j] < a[min]) min = j;
  }
  int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[min]; a[min] = tmp;
  System.out.println(a[0] + " " + a[1] + " " + a[2]);
}","options":["2 1 3
1 2 3","1 3 2
1 2 3","1 2 3
1 2 3","3 1 2
1 3 2"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pass 1 swaps the minimum 1 with 3, giving 1 3 2. Pass 2 finds 2 as the minimum of the remaining {3, 2} and swaps, giving 1 2 3. Two passes suffice for three elements: the outer loop runs n-1 times."},{"id":"fs_z17_6_4","topic":"Comparison count","text":"Sorting 5 elements, selection sort performs how many comparisons in total?","options":["25, in the worst case only","10, regardless of the initial order","5, regardless of the initial order","4, if the array is already sorted"],"correct":1,"explain":"The inner loop always scans the whole remainder: 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10 comparisons, the n(n-1)/2 pattern. The count is identical for sorted, reversed, or random input - selection sort is not adaptive."},{"id":"zf4666","topic":"","text":"Selection sort’s strategy per pass is:","options":["bubble neighbors","insert into a sorted prefix","select the min and swap it into place","split and merge"],"correct":2,"explain":"Select the minimum; fix it at the front; repeat on the rest.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4667","topic":"","text":"After pass 1 on {5, 2, 8, 1}, the array is:","options":["{2, 5, 8, 1}","{1, 5, 8, 2}","{5, 2, 8, 1}","{1, 2, 8, 5}"],"correct":3,"explain":"The min (1) swaps with position 0’s 5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4668","topic":"","text":"After pass 2 of that sort, the array is:","options":["{1, 2, 8, 5}","{1, 2, 5, 8}","{2, 1, 8, 5}","{1, 8, 2, 5}"],"correct":0,"explain":"The min of the rest (2) is already at position 1 - the pass swaps it with itself.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4669","topic":"","text":"Selection sort on n elements makes how many passes?","options":["n","n - 1","n/2","log n"],"correct":1,"explain":"Each pass fixes one position; the last element lands free.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4670","topic":"","text":"Its comparison count is:","options":["n","about n^2/2 regardless of input order","n log n","input-dependent"],"correct":1,"explain":"Every pass scans the whole remainder - sorted input saves nothing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4671","topic":"","text":"Its swap count is at most:","options":["n^2","n log n","n - 1","unbounded"],"correct":2,"explain":"One swap per pass - the frugal-swap property.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4672","topic":"","text":"That few-swaps property matters when:","options":["comparisons are free","never","arrays are sorted","moving elements is expensive"],"correct":3,"explain":"Minimum data movement is selection sort’s one professional niche.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4673","topic":"","text":"The inner loop of pass i scans:","options":["i+1 through the end, seeking the min","0 through i","the whole array always","random picks"],"correct":0,"explain":"The unsorted suffix is the search space.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4674","topic":"","text":"Selection sort’s sorted region grows:","options":["from the back","from the front, one slot per pass","from the middle","all at once"],"correct":1,"explain":"Left of i: finished; right: still competing.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4675","topic":"","text":"Tracking minIndex rather than swapping during the scan:","options":["is wrong","sorts descending","delays the one swap to pass end","doubles swaps"],"correct":2,"explain":"Remember WHERE the min is; swap once at pass end.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4676","topic":"","text":"Descending selection sort simply:","options":["reverses the array after","cannot exist","uses recursion","selects the MAXIMUM each pass"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same skeleton, flipped comparison.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4677","topic":"","text":"Selection sort’s best case is:","options":["O(n^2) - the same as its worst","O(n)","O(log n)","O(1)"],"correct":0,"explain":"The scans happen regardless; only swaps vary.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4678","topic":"","text":"For 10 elements, selection sort performs how many comparisons?","options":["100","45","10","90"],"correct":1,"explain":"9+8+...+1 = 45 - the triangular number.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_7":[{"id":"zya17_7_0","topic":"Insertion sort method","text":"Insertion sort works by:","options":["Selecting the smallest remaining and moving it first","Comparing and swapping each adjacent out-of-order pair","Sliding each element back into place among the sorted ones","Splitting the list in half and merging the halves"],"correct":2,"explain":"The way people sort a hand of cards."},{"id":"zya17_7_1","topic":"Best case","text":"On already-sorted data, insertion sort is:","options":["O(n)","O(n log n)","O(1)","O(n^2)"],"correct":0,"explain":"The inner while loop never executes — it is the only adaptive simple sort."},{"id":"zya17_7_2","topic":"Worst case","text":"On reverse-sorted data it is:","options":["O(n^2)","O(n log n)","O(log n)","O(n)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every element shifts all the way back."},{"id":"zya17_7_3","topic":"Starting index","text":"The outer loop starts at index:","options":["1","2","n-1","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"A single element is already sorted by definition."},{"id":"zya17_7_4","topic":"Short-circuit necessity","text":"The inner condition must be j >= 0 && a[j] > key because:","options":["The order makes no difference to the result","It ensures the sort remains stable for equal keys","Short-circuiting prevents an out-of-bounds read when j hits -1","It stops the loop before the array is fully scanned"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reversing the two tests throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException."},{"id":"zya17_7_5","topic":"Stability","text":"Insertion sort is:","options":["Stable","Stable only for Strings","Unpredictable","Not stable"],"correct":0,"explain":"Equal elements never jump past one another."},{"id":"zya17_7_6","topic":"Practical use","text":"Real library sorts use insertion sort for:","options":["Small partitions, where its low overhead wins","Large partitions, where its O(n^2) is acceptable","Every partition, since it is always fastest","The final merge step of a merge sort"],"correct":0,"explain":"Java's TimSort does exactly this."},{"id":"zya17_7_7","topic":"After k passes","text":"After k passes, the first k+1 elements are:","options":["The k+1 smallest values in their final positions","Untouched, since insertion sort works backwards","In reverse order, awaiting the final pass","Sorted among themselves but not necessarily in final position"],"correct":3,"explain":"The distinction from selection sort that partial-trace questions test."},{"id":"zyf17_7_0","topic":"Adaptive behaviour","text":"On nearly sorted data, insertion sort approaches:","options":["O(n^2)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n log n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The only adaptive simple sort, which is why libraries use it for small runs."},{"id":"zyf17_7_1","topic":"Guard order","text":"The inner condition must test j >= 0 before a[j] > key because:","options":["The comparison is faster when j is tested first","Short-circuiting prevents an out-of-bounds read","It keeps the sort stable for equal keys","The compiler requires conditions in that order"],"correct":1,"explain":"Reversing them throws when the key belongs at index 0."},{"id":"fs_z17_7_1","topic":"Insertion sort pass-by-pass","text":"What does this print? (The array is shown after each outer-loop pass.)

