","recursion"],"correct":1,"explain":"Shift-shift-shift, then drop the saved value in.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4684","topic":"","text":"Insertion sort is preferred for:","options":["huge random data","all cases","small or nearly-sorted arrays","linked structures only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Real libraries use it as the small-subarray finisher inside fast sorts.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4685","topic":"","text":"Nearly-sorted data (each element near its spot) sorts in:","options":["quadratic still","log time","constant","near-linear time"],"correct":3,"explain":"Short slides = little work - adaptivity in action.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4686","topic":"","text":"The sorted prefix after k outer steps contains:","options":["the first k+1 elements","the k smallest overall","random items","the last k"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sorted, but not final - later arrivals may still insert among them (unlike selection sort’s prefix).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4687","topic":"","text":"Sorting {3, 1, 2}: total shifts performed are:","options":["0","2","3","6"],"correct":1,"explain":"1 shifts past 3 (one shift); 2 shifts past 3 (one shift).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4688","topic":"","text":"Insertion vs selection on the SAME reversed array:","options":["insertion wins big","selection is linear","both quadratic","identical work"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reverse order is insertion’s nightmare and selection’s indifference.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4946","topic":"","text":"Insertion sort on {2, 3, 1}: the total element shifts are:","options":["1","3","0","2"],"correct":3,"explain":"The 3 needs no shift; the 1 shifts past 3 and past 2.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_8":[{"id":"zya17_8_0","topic":"Quicksort method","text":"Quicksort works by:","options":["Splitting in half, sorting each half, then merging","Repeatedly selecting the minimum of the rest","Partitioning around a pivot, then sorting each side","Comparing and swapping each adjacent pair"],"correct":2,"explain":"After partitioning, the pivot is already in its final position."},{"id":"zya17_8_1","topic":"Average complexity","text":"Quicksort averages:","options":["O(n)","O(n log n)","O(n^2)","O(log n)"],"correct":1,"explain":"A balanced pivot halves the problem, giving log n levels of O(n) work."},{"id":"zya17_8_2","topic":"Worst case","text":"Quicksort degrades to O(n^2) when:","options":["The pivot is consistently the minimum or maximum","The pivot is consistently the median value","The array is already in random order","The array contains many distinct values"],"correct":0,"explain":"With last-element pivoting, already-sorted input is the worst case."},{"id":"zya17_8_3","topic":"Recursive calls","text":"After partitioning at index p, the recursive calls cover:","options":["low..p and p..high","low..p-1 and p+1..high","low..high twice","p only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Including the pivot makes the subproblem never shrink, causing StackOverflowError."},{"id":"zya17_8_4","topic":"Pivot position","text":"After partition returns p:","options":["The pivot must still be moved once more","Both halves are already fully sorted","The pivot is in its final sorted position","The array is sorted except for the pivot"],"correct":2,"explain":"Which is why it is excluded from both recursive calls."},{"id":"zya17_8_5","topic":"Avoiding the worst case","text":"A common mitigation is:","options":["Always choosing the first element as the pivot","Choosing the pivot as a random element or median-of-three","Sorting the array before running quicksort","Switching to bubble sort for large partitions"],"correct":1,"explain":"It makes the pathological case vanishingly unlikely."},{"id":"zya17_8_6","topic":"Space","text":"Quicksort sorts in place using extra space of:","options":["O(log n) on average, for the recursion stack","O(n) always, for a second copy of the array","O(1) always, since it sorts fully in place","O(n log n), matching its running time"],"correct":0,"explain":"No auxiliary array is needed for the data itself."},{"id":"zya17_8_7","topic":"Stability","text":"Quicksort is:","options":["Stable","Not stable","Always stable","Stable with a comparator"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is why Arrays.sort uses TimSort for objects. Partitioning swaps distant elements."},{"id":"zya17_8_8","topic":"Work placement","text":"Quicksort does its main work:","options":["After the recursive calls","Before recursing, during partitioning","Only at the base case","In the merge"],"correct":1,"explain":"Merge sort is the opposite — split first, work on the way back up."},{"id":"zyf17_8_0","topic":"Recursive bounds","text":"The recursive calls must exclude the pivot by using:","options":["p only","low and high","p and p","p - 1 and p + 1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Including it prevents the subproblem from shrinking."},{"id":"fs_z17_8_1","topic":"Partition trace","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {3, 8, 2, 5};
int p = partition(a, 0, a.length - 1);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a) + " p=" + p);

where partition uses the last element as pivot:

static int partition(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
  int pivot = a[hi];
  int i = lo - 1;
  for (int j = lo; j < hi; j++) {
    if (a[j] < pivot) { i++; swap(a, i, j); }
  }
  swap(a, i + 1, hi);
  return i + 1;
}","options":["[2, 3, 5, 8] p=2","[3, 2, 5, 8] p=3","[2, 3, 8, 5] p=1","[3, 2, 5, 8] p=2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Pivot is 5. Elements 3 and 2 (each < 5) are gathered at the front, 8 stays right, and the final swap puts 5 between them at index 2. Everything left of the pivot is smaller and everything right is larger - but neither side is sorted yet."},{"id":"fs_z17_8_2","topic":"Worst-case trigger","text":"With the last element always chosen as pivot, which input drives quicksort to O(n^2)?","options":["A randomly shuffled array","An array of all-distinct values","Any array longer than 1,000 elements","An already-sorted array"],"correct":3,"explain":"On sorted input the last-element pivot is the maximum every time, so each partition puts everything on one side and only one element is placed per call - n levels of O(n) work. Random or median-of-three pivots are the standard defence."},{"id":"fs_z17_8_3","topic":"Quicksort base case","text":"Quicksort's recursion stops when lo >= hi. That condition means:","options":["The array contains duplicate values","The pivot was the median","The recursion depth limit was reached","The range holds zero or one elements"],"correct":3,"explain":"lo > hi is an empty range and lo == hi is a single element; neither needs sorting, so the call simply returns. Every recursive algorithm needs such a base case to terminate. Is already sorted."},{"id":"fs_z17_8_4","topic":"Full quicksort trace","text":"What does this print?

