,"sorted input"],"correct":0,"explain":"Tiny ranges are sorted by definition.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4696","topic":"","text":"Partitioning {3, 8, 2, 5} around pivot 5 (others compared to it) groups:","options":["{8} left, {3,2} right","{3, 2} left and {8} right of 5","all left","all right"],"correct":1,"explain":"3 and 2 are smaller; 8 is larger; 5 lands between them (final position 2).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4697","topic":"","text":"Quicksort vs merge sort trade-offs:","options":["identical","quick is always better","quick is in-place","merge is in-place"],"correct":2,"explain":"Speed-in-practice vs guarantees - the classic engineering pick.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4698","topic":"","text":"The "quick" reputation comes from:","options":["a better O class than merge","marketing only","skipping comparisons","small constants, cache-friendly work"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same average order as merge sort, faster in real memory.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4699","topic":"","text":"Recursion depth on balanced quicksort of 1,024 items is about:","options":["10","1024","512","2"],"correct":0,"explain":"log2(1024) levels of halving.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_9":[{"id":"zya17_9_0","topic":"Merge sort method","text":"Merge sort works by:","options":["Partitioning around a pivot and recursing","Repeatedly selecting the minimum of the rest","Splitting in half, sorting each half, then merging them","Sliding each element into a sorted prefix"],"correct":2,"explain":"The work happens during the merge, on the way back up."},{"id":"zya17_9_1","topic":"Complexity guarantee","text":"Merge sort is O(n log n):","options":["Never","Only on average","In every case, including the worst","Only when sorted"],"correct":2,"explain":"The split is always even, so the depth is always log n."},{"id":"zya17_9_2","topic":"Space cost","text":"Merge sort requires extra space of:","options":["None","O(1)","O(n) for the temporary array","O(log n)"],"correct":2,"explain":"The main trade-off against quicksort."},{"id":"zya17_9_3","topic":"Stability","text":"Merge sort is:","options":["Unstable, since the halves are sorted separately","Stable only when the input is already sorted","Stable, but only for arrays of primitive types","Stable, provided the merge uses <= when taking from the left"],"correct":3,"explain":"That single comparison operator is what preserves the relative order."},{"id":"zya17_9_4","topic":"Merging cost","text":"Merging two sorted halves of total length n takes:","options":["O(1)","O(n log n)","O(n)","O(n^2)"],"correct":2,"explain":"One pass, comparing the fronts of each half."},{"id":"zya17_9_5","topic":"Levels of recursion","text":"Splitting n elements down to singletons gives:","options":["2 levels","n levels","log n levels","n^2 levels"],"correct":2,"explain":"log n levels times O(n) merging per level."},{"id":"zya17_9_6","topic":"Base case","text":"Merge sort stops splitting when:","options":["Never","low > high","The range has one element or fewer","n is 2"],"correct":2,"explain":"A single element is already sorted."},{"id":"zya17_9_7","topic":"Choosing between sorts","text":"Merge sort is preferred over quicksort when:","options":["Its average case beats quicksort's average case on random data","The data is already nearly sorted, which quicksort handles best","Memory is scarce, since merge sort works entirely in place","A guaranteed O(n log n) worst case or stability is required"],"correct":3,"explain":"Quicksort wins on memory and typical speed."},{"id":"zya17_9_8","topic":"Work placement","text":"Merge sort does its main work:","options":["During splitting","Before recursing","After the recursive calls return","At the base case"],"correct":2,"explain":"The opposite of quicksort — a distinction exam questions rely on."},{"id":"zyf17_9_0","topic":"Worst case guarantee","text":"Merge sort's worst case is:","options":["O(n log n)","O(n)","Unbounded","O(n^2)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Guaranteed, unlike quicksort — at the cost of O(n) extra space."},{"id":"fs_z17_9_1","topic":"Merge step trace","text":"Two sorted halves are merged by repeatedly taking the smaller front element. What does this print?

