where mergeSort splits at the midpoint, recursively sorts both halves, then merges them.","options":["[2, 6, 4, 8]","[6, 2, 8, 4]","[2, 4, 6, 8]","[8, 6, 4, 2]"],"correct":2,"explain":"Merge sort always produces the fully sorted ascending array: {6,2} and {8,4} are each sorted to {2,6} and {4,8}, then merged to {2,4,6,8}. Its O(n log n) cost is the same for every input."},{"id":"fs_z17_9_4","topic":"Recursive call ranges","text":"Merge sort recurses on (lo..mid) and (mid+1..hi). Unlike quicksort's recursive calls, these ranges:","options":["Overlap by one element","Cover only the unsorted half","Together include every element, because no element is in its final position before the merge","Exclude the middle element, which is already placed"],"correct":2,"explain":"Quicksort excludes its pivot because partitioning already placed it. Merge sort places nothing until the merge, so both halves - all elements - must be recursively sorted first. The work happens after the recursion, not before."},{"id":"zf4700","topic":"","text":"Merge sort’s strategy is:","options":["partition around pivots","select minimums","shift elements","split, sort halves, merge them"],"correct":3,"explain":"The canonical divide-and-conquer sort.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4701","topic":"","text":"The merge step combines two sorted halves by:","options":["always taking the smaller front item","concatenating","re-sorting from scratch","hashing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Two fingers walking two sorted lists - linear work.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4702","topic":"","text":"Merging {2, 8} and {3, 5} produces:","options":["{2, 8, 3, 5}","{2, 3, 5, 8}","{8, 5, 3, 2}","{3, 2, 5, 8}"],"correct":1,"explain":"Take 2, then 3, then 5, then 8.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4703","topic":"","text":"Merge sort’s time in ALL cases is:","options":["O(n^2) worst","O(n) best","O(n log n)","varies wildly"],"correct":2,"explain":"The guarantee is its calling card - no bad inputs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4704","topic":"","text":"Its main cost is:","options":["quadratic time","instability","pivot choice","O(n) extra space for merging"],"correct":3,"explain":"The temporary arrays are the price of the guarantee.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4705","topic":"","text":"The recursion splits until:","options":["ranges hold one element","ranges hold half","pivots repeat","memory fills"],"correct":0,"explain":"Single elements are trivially sorted; merging builds up from there.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4706","topic":"","text":"The number of split levels for 8 elements is:","options":["8","3","4","1"],"correct":1,"explain":"8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1: log2(8) levels.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4707","topic":"","text":"Each level of merging processes:","options":["one element","half the elements","all n elements once","log n elements"],"correct":2,"explain":"n work per level x log n levels = n log n.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4708","topic":"","text":"Merge sort is stable because:","options":["it is not","stability is random","pivots preserve order","ties take the LEFT half’s element first"],"correct":3,"explain":"The <= in the merge keeps equal keys in original order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4709","topic":"","text":"Merge sort suits linked lists and external (disk) sorting because:","options":["it consumes data sequentially","it is in-place","it needs random access","it does not"],"correct":0,"explain":"Sequential merging matches streams and lists; quicksort’s jumping does not.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4710","topic":"","text":"Sorting 1,000,000 items: merge sort’s ~n log n operation count is near:","options":["20 million","1 trillion","1 million","20,000"],"correct":0,"explain":"n x log2(n) = 1M x 20 - versus a simple sort’s 10^12.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_1":[{"id":"zya18_1_0","topic":"AI definition","text":"Artificial intelligence refers to:","options":["Programs that store their rules in a large fact database","Any software that uses probability or statistics internally","Systems performing tasks that normally require human intelligence","Systems that must run on specialized neural hardware"],"correct":2,"explain":"It is a broad field, wider than what "AI" currently connotes in conversation."},{"id":"zya18_1_1","topic":"Machine learning distinction","text":"Machine learning differs from rule-based AI because:","options":["It follows hand-written rules more precisely than a human coder","The system derives rules from data rather than being given them","It stores every training example and looks answers up verbatim","It removes the need for any training data at all"],"correct":1,"explain":"Traditional programming is rules + data to answers; ML is data + answers to rules."},{"id":"zya18_1_2","topic":"Deep learning","text":"Deep learning is:","options":["Machine learning using many-layered neural networks","Any program that reasons over a deep tree of if-else rules","A search technique that explores possibilities many levels deep","A database design with many nested layers of tables"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is what made modern image recognition and language models work."},{"id":"zya18_1_3","topic":"Supervised learning","text":"Supervised learning trains on:","options":["Trial-and-error interaction where good moves earn rewards","Raw unlabelled text scraped without any human annotation","A fixed set of hand-written rules provided by experts","Labelled examples, such as photo to "cat""],"correct":3,"explain":"Used for classification and prediction."},{"id":"zya18_1_4","topic":"Unsupervised learning","text":"Unsupervised learning is used to:","options":["Predict a known label for each new example it is shown","Learn from rewards earned by acting in an environment","Find structure in unlabelled data, such as clustering","Memorize labelled pairs for later exact-match lookup"],"correct":2,"explain":"Customer segmentation is a typical application."},{"id":"zya18_1_5","topic":"Reinforcement learning","text":"Reinforcement learning learns from:","options":["Clustering","Fixed rules","Labelled examples","Trial and error guided by reward"],"correct":3,"explain":"Game playing and robotics are the classic domains."},{"id":"zya18_1_6","topic":"Rule-based strengths","text":"Symbolic, rule-based AI is:","options":["Predictable and explainable, but brittle outside anticipated cases","Adaptive to novel situations, but impossible to explain afterwards","Identical to machine learning once enough rules are written","More accurate than learned models on messy real-world input"],"correct":0,"explain":"Explainability is its genuine advantage."},{"id":"zya18_1_7","topic":"Programming contrast","text":"The core contrast with traditional programming is:","options":["Traditional code learns from data while ML follows fixed rules","ML programs require no source code, only a trained database","Traditional programs cannot process large datasets at all","ML infers the rules from data instead of having them written"],"correct":3,"explain":"That inversion is the defining idea."},{"id":"zyf18_1_0","topic":"Learning from data","text":"Machine learning differs from rule-based AI in that it:","options":["Executes its hand-written rules faster than rule engines can","Skips the training phase that rule-based systems require","Guarantees correct answers where rule systems only estimate","Derives rules from data instead of receiving them"],"correct":3,"explain":"Data plus answers produce rules."},{"id":"zyf18_1_1","topic":"Explainability","text":"Rule-based systems are generally:","options":["Slower","Less explainable","More explainable but more brittle","Always better"],"correct":2,"explain":"The trade-off against learned models."},{"id":"fs_z18_1_1","topic":"Term nesting","text":"Which statement correctly nests the terms from broadest to narrowest?","options":["The three terms name separate, non-overlapping fields","Artificial intelligence contains machine learning, which contains deep learning","Machine learning contains artificial intelligence, which contains deep learning","Deep learning contains machine learning, which contains artificial intelligence"],"correct":1,"explain":"AI is the umbrella field; machine learning is the subset that learns behaviour from data; deep learning is the subset of ML that uses many-layered neural networks. The nesting AI > ML > deep learning is the most-asked fact in this material."},{"id":"fs_z18_1_2","topic":"Unsupervised learning","text":"A company groups its customers into segments using purchase data that carries no labels or categories. This is an example of:","options":["Rule-based AI","Unsupervised learning","Supervised learning","Reinforcement learning"],"correct":1,"explain":"Clustering unlabelled data into groups is the signature unsupervised task - there are no correct answers provided to learn from. Supervised learning would need labelled examples, and reinforcement learning needs a reward signal."},{"id":"fs_z18_1_3","topic":"Supervised learning","text":"A spam filter is trained on a large set of emails, each one already tagged spam or not-spam. This is:","options":["Symbolic rule-based AI","Supervised learning","Unsupervised learning","Reinforcement learning"],"correct":1,"explain":"The tags are labels: the system learns from example inputs paired with correct answers, which is the definition of supervised learning. Spam-from-tagged-mail is the textbook example."},{"id":"fs_z18_1_4","topic":"When rules beat learning","text":"For which task does a rule-based system remain the better choice over machine learning?","options":["Recommending films from viewing history","Tax calculation, where the logic is known, must be exact, and must be explainable","Recognizing faces in photos","Transcribing recorded speech"],"correct":1,"explain":"Rules win where the logic is known, must be exact, and must be justified - tax law is written down. Learned systems shine on fuzzy perception tasks but are hard to explain, a real cost wherever decisions must be defended."},{"id":"zf4711","topic":"","text":"AI, broadly, is software that:","options":["is always conscious","does tasks linked to human intelligence","only plays games","runs without hardware"],"correct":1,"explain":"Recognition, language, decision-making - capability, not consciousness.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4712","topic":"","text":"Machine learning differs from classic programming by:","options":["using no computers","being random","learning the rules from data","avoiding math"],"correct":2,"explain":"Rules are inferred from examples, not authored line-by-line.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4713","topic":"","text":"Training data is:","options":["the model’s output","source code","test users","the examples the model learns from"],"correct":3,"explain":"Data quality bounds model quality.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4714","topic":"","text":"A model, once trained, is:","options":["the learned artifact that predicts","the dataset","the GPU","a spreadsheet"],"correct":0,"explain":"Training produces it; inference uses it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4715","topic":"","text":"Spam filtering is a classic ML task because:","options":["spam is rare","rules are easier learned than penned","email is old","filters are hardware"],"correct":1,"explain":"Ever-shifting adversarial patterns favor learning over rule lists.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4716","topic":"","text":"AI systems are still PROGRAMS, meaning:","options":["normal code is obsolete","only Python exists","Java skills (I/O","no engineering remains"],"correct":2,"explain":"The unit’s framing: AI services are components your code orchestrates.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4717","topic":"","text":"Narrow AI refers to:","options":["general human-level systems","robots","old software","systems good at specific tasks only"],"correct":3,"explain":"Chess engines and chatbots excel narrowly - the current reality.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4718","topic":"","text":""Garbage in, garbage out" applies to ML as:","options":["biased or bad data yields bad models","models fix data","data is irrelevant","a hardware rule"],"correct":0,"explain":"The data pipeline is the model’s ceiling.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4719","topic":"","text":"Inference means:","options":["training","running the trained model on new inputs","labeling data","compiling"],"correct":1,"explain":"The prediction phase your applications call.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4720","topic":"","text":"A Java app most commonly uses AI today by:","options":["training in main()","calling model services over APIs","avoiding it","rewriting the JVM"],"correct":1,"explain":"Integration-by-HTTP - setting up 18.5.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4721","topic":"","text":"Classification vs generation:","options":["identical","both are search","labeling inputs vs producing new content","hardware terms"],"correct":2,"explain":"Spam-or-not vs write-me-a-paragraph - two AI task families.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_2":[{"id":"zya18_2_0","topic":"LLM basis","text":"A large language model is trained to:","options":["Retrieve the most relevant stored document for each question","Translate natural language into database queries internally","Predict the next token given the preceding context","Verify each generated sentence against a fact database"],"correct":2,"explain":"Everything else it appears to do emerges from that objective."},{"id":"zya18_2_1","topic":"Token","text":"A token is:","options":["A single character, since models read text one letter at a time","A chunk of text, roughly three quarters of a word","A complete sentence, the smallest unit models can process","A fixed eight-byte block of the input text"],"correct":1,"explain":"Cost and limits are measured in tokens."},{"id":"zya18_2_2","topic":"Context window","text":"The context window is:","options":["How many tokens the model can consider at once","The maximum number of responses the model can give per session","The amount of text the model memorized during training","The number of users who can share one conversation"],"correct":0,"explain":"Anything beyond it is invisible to the model."},{"id":"zya18_2_3","topic":"Hallucination","text":"A hallucination is:","options":["A response the model refuses to complete for safety reasons","Output that repeats the prompt instead of answering it","A degraded answer caused by servers being overloaded","Fluent output that is factually wrong"],"correct":3,"explain":"The model has no internal signal distinguishing it from correct output."},{"id":"zya18_2_4","topic":"Temperature","text":"Temperature controls:","options":["Cost","Speed","Randomness","Length"],"correct":2,"explain":"Near 0 is best when determinism matters. Low values give repeatable, likely output."},{"id":"zya18_2_5","topic":"Statelessness","text":"LLM APIs are stateless, which means:","options":["The server keeps your conversation, so requests stay tiny","Each request must carry whatever conversation history matters","Requests are queued and answered strictly one at a time","The model resets its training weights between requests"],"correct":1,"explain":"Chat applications resend the history every turn."},{"id":"zya18_2_6","topic":"Training cutoff","text":"A training cutoff means the model:","options":["Has no knowledge of events after that date","Is retrained automatically each night on that day's news","Refuses to answer any question dated before that year","Deletes older conversations once the date passes"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is one motivation for retrieval augmentation."},{"id":"zya18_2_7","topic":"Parameters","text":"Model parameters are:","options":["The command-line options passed when the model is launched","The tokens the user supplies in each individual prompt","The list of topics the model is permitted to discuss","The learned weights, indicating model size"],"correct":3,"explain":"Larger is not automatically better for every task."},{"id":"zya18_2_8","topic":"Reliability implication","text":"Because it predicts plausible text, an LLM:","options":["Always refuses to answer once its confidence drops too low","Attaches a verified citation to every factual sentence","Can state an invented fact as confidently as a real one","Flags invented content with a special warning marker"],"correct":2,"explain":"Confidence in the output carries no information about correctness."},{"id":"zyf18_2_0","topic":"Context limit","text":"Content beyond the context window is:","options":["Invisible to the model","Stored permanently","Compressed","Summarized automatically"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why long conversations must be trimmed."},{"id":"fs_z18_2_1","topic":"Next-token prediction","text":"In a large language model's generation loop, the model's raw output at each step is:","options":["A record retrieved from a database of facts","An exact sentence copied from its training data","A probability distribution over possible next tokens, from which one is sampled","The complete answer, produced in a single step"],"correct":2,"explain":"The loop is prompt -> tokens -> model -> probabilities over the next token -> sample -> repeat. The model assigns probabilities and one token is chosen; there is no fact database and no wholesale copying step."},{"id":"fs_z18_2_2","topic":"LLM limitations","text":"Without an attached tool, a plain large language model cannot:","options":["Translate between human languages","Continue a conversation whose history is re-sent to it","Look up current information from a live source","Produce fluent paragraphs of text"],"correct":2,"explain":"An LLM has no database and no ability to look anything up - it only predicts plausible next tokens from its training. Fluent text, translation, and continuing re-sent history are all within next-token prediction."},{"id":"fs_z18_2_3","topic":"Training cutoff in practice","text":"A model whose training cutoff was last year is asked about an event that happened yesterday. The most likely outcome is:","options":["A correct answer, because models update themselves continuously","A crash in the generation loop","A fluent, confident answer that may be entirely fabricated","A guaranteed refusal to answer"],"correct":2,"explain":"The model knows nothing after its cutoff, but next-token prediction still produces a plausible continuation - which here means fluent, confident, and possibly wrong. That is precisely why factual output needs verification."},{"id":"fs_z18_2_4","topic":"Nature of hallucination","text":"Hallucination in a language model is best understood as:","options":["A network transmission error corrupting the response","A symptom of setting the temperature to zero","A natural consequence of next-token prediction when the pattern is plausible but the fact is absent","A rare software bug that vendors are steadily patching out"],"correct":2,"explain":"Hallucination is what predicting plausible continuations does when there is no fact to draw on - it is inherent to the mechanism, not a defect to patch. That is why verification, and techniques like retrieval, exist."},{"id":"zf4722","topic":"","text":"A large language model fundamentally:","options":["looks up answers in a database","browses live by default","stores your files","predicts likely next tokens in context"],"correct":3,"explain":"Statistical next-token prediction, scaled enormously.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4723","topic":"","text":"A token is roughly:","options":["a word piece the model reads and writes","a password","a pixel","a thread"],"correct":0,"explain":"Text is chopped into tokens; costs and limits count them.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4724","topic":"","text":"The context window limits:","options":["training data size","how much text fits in one request","the internet","users"],"correct":1,"explain":"Long inputs beyond the window are forgotten or must be trimmed.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4725","topic":"","text":"Hallucination means:","options":["model crashes","slow replies","confident output that is factually wrong","copyright"],"correct":2,"explain":"Fluent falsehood - the failure mode to design around (RAG in 18.8).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4726","topic":"","text":"A prompt is:","options":["the model’s code","a bug","a GPU setting","the input text steering the output"],"correct":3,"explain":"Instructions, context, and question - the interface to generative models.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4727","topic":"","text":"Model outputs vary between runs because:","options":["sampling adds controlled randomness","bugs","the moon","caching"],"correct":0,"explain":"Higher temperature = more variety; lower = more deterministic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4728","topic":"","text":"Generative models produce:","options":["only copies","new content in learned patterns","proofs","random noise"],"correct":1,"explain":"Text, images, code - fresh sequences, learned style.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4729","topic":"","text":"LLMs handle code because:","options":["they run compilers","code is stored","code is text with strong patterns","they cannot"],"correct":2,"explain":"The same next-token machinery, applied to programming languages.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4730","topic":"","text":"Model answers should be verified when:","options":["never","only for math","facts or correctness matter","only offline"],"correct":2,"explain":"Trust-but-verify is the professional default (18.9 expands).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4731","topic":"","text":"The "large" in LLM refers to:","options":["response length","company size","file size only","parameter count and training scale"],"correct":3,"explain":"Billions of learned weights - scale is the capability driver.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4732","topic":"","text":"An LLM’s knowledge has a cutoff because:","options":["its data ends at training time","it forgets daily","laws require it","tokens expire"],"correct":0,"explain":"Post-cutoff events need retrieval or tools - not the frozen weights.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_3":[{"id":"zya18_3_0","topic":"Specific prompts","text":"Prompt quality improves most from:","options":["Keeping the prompt to a single short sentence at most","Repeating the key requirement several times for emphasis","Stating the task, format and constraints precisely","Writing the request entirely in capital letters"],"correct":2,"explain":"Specifying the edge-case behaviour is often what fixes a bad answer."},{"id":"zya18_3_1","topic":"Few-shot prompting","text":"Providing an example input and output:","options":["Teaches the model permanently, improving later sessions too","Pins down the desired format better than describing it","Doubles the cost while producing an identical response","Prevents the model from generalizing beyond that example"],"correct":1,"explain":"One good example often outperforms a paragraph of description."},{"id":"zya18_3_2","topic":"Reasoning steps","text":"Asking for intermediate steps helps because:","options":["The steps can be checked, not just the conclusion","The model skips the reasoning and answers twice as fast","Intermediate steps are guaranteed correct even when the answer is not","It permanently improves the model's arithmetic ability"],"correct":0,"explain":"Verifiability is the benefit, not guaranteed correctness."},{"id":"zya18_3_3","topic":"Iteration","text":"The most value usually comes from:","options":["Writing one perfect prompt before ever sending anything","Always accepting the first answer to avoid extra token cost","Re-sending the identical prompt until the answer changes","Refining the prompt after seeing the first answer"],"correct":3,"explain":"Treat it as a conversation rather than a single query."},{"id":"zya18_3_4","topic":"Poor fit","text":"Generative AI is a poor fit for:","options":["Rewording an awkward paragraph into a friendlier tone","Producing a first draft that a human will then revise","Tasks requiring guaranteed factual accuracy or exact arithmetic","Condensing a long report into a short summary"],"correct":2,"explain":"Verification cost is what should drive the decision."},{"id":"zya18_3_5","topic":"Good fit","text":"Generative AI fits well when:","options":["Every output ships to users without any human review","A wrong answer is cheap to detect and fix","The task has exactly one acceptable numeric answer","No one downstream is able to judge the result's quality"],"correct":1,"explain":"Drafting and boilerplate meet that test; medical advice does not."},{"id":"zya18_3_6","topic":"Role prompting","text":"Specifying an audience or role:","options":["Shapes the vocabulary and depth of the response","Locks the response length to a fixed word count","Switches the request to a differently trained model","Only works when the role is a real licensed profession"],"correct":0,"explain":""Explain to someone who has never used recursion" produces a different answer than "explain"."},{"id":"zya18_3_7","topic":"Counting tasks","text":"Exact counting and arithmetic are:","options":["A strength of LLMs","A known weakness","Impossible to do","Always correct"],"correct":1,"explain":"Which is why tool use and code execution are common workarounds. They predict tokens rather than compute."},{"id":"zyf18_3_0","topic":"Few-shot","text":"Including an example input and output:","options":["Counts against quality since models penalize long prompts","Overrides the model's safety rules for that request","Makes the model copy the example verbatim every time","Specifies the format more reliably than describing it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Often the fastest fix for badly formatted output."},{"id":"zyf18_3_1","topic":"Arithmetic","text":"Exact arithmetic is:","options":["A known weakness","Always correct","Impossible to check","A model strength"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why calculators and code execution are delegated to tools. Tokens are predicted rather than computed."},{"id":"fs_z18_3_1","topic":"Role prompting","text":"Beginning a prompt with "You are an experienced code reviewer" is an example of:","options":["Few-shot prompting","Chain-of-thought prompting","Temperature adjustment","Role prompting"],"correct":3,"explain":"Role prompting sets a perspective for the model to answer from. Few-shot supplies worked examples, and chain of thought asks for reasoning steps - different techniques with different mechanisms."},{"id":"fs_z18_3_2","topic":"Few-shot prompting","text":"Including two or three worked input-output examples in the prompt to demonstrate the expected pattern is called:","options":["Role prompting","Retrieval-augmented generation","Fine-tuning","Few-shot prompting"],"correct":3,"explain":"That is few-shot prompting, and it works because the model is a pattern completer: examples define the shape of the desired answer more reliably than describing it. Fine-tuning changes the model itself, not the prompt."},{"id":"fs_z18_3_3","topic":"Chain of thought","text":"Asking the model to lay out its reasoning steps before giving the final answer is called:","options":["Few-shot prompting","Iteration","Grounding","Chain-of-thought prompting"],"correct":3,"explain":"Chain of thought asks for the reasoning first, which helps on multi-step problems because each intermediate step becomes context for the next. Iteration means refining the prompt across attempts."},{"id":"fs_z18_3_4","topic":"Why counting fails","text":"Counting the exact number of letters in a word is unreliable for a language model because:","options":["Its context window is too small to hold a word","Letters are removed during training","It refuses all numeric questions","It processes text as tokens, not as individual characters"],"correct":3,"explain":"Text reaches the model as tokens - chunks of roughly three-quarters of a word - so individual characters are not what it operates on. That is why character counting and exact arithmetic are characteristic weak spots."},{"id":"zf4733","topic":"","text":"Effective prompts typically include:","options":["as few words as possible always","a clear task, context, and format","secrets","code only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Specificity in, quality out.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4734","topic":"","text":"Asking for output "as a JSON object with fields x and y":","options":["is impossible","slows the model","makes the reply parseable by your code","encrypts it"],"correct":2,"explain":"Structured output requests are how programs consume model text.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4735","topic":"","text":"Iterating on a prompt after weak output is:","options":["cheating","forbidden","a bug report","normal practice - refine and retry"],"correct":3,"explain":"Prompting is an edit-run-inspect loop, like debugging.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4736","topic":"","text":"Giving the model an EXAMPLE of the desired output is called:","options":["few-shot prompting","tokenization","fine-tuning","caching"],"correct":0,"explain":"Demonstrations steer format and style powerfully.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4737","topic":"","text":"A system/role instruction like "You are a strict JSON API":","options":["is decoration","frames every later response","crashes models","ends the chat"],"correct":1,"explain":"Role framing is a durable steering tool.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4738","topic":"","text":"Long documents exceeding the context window are handled by:","options":["larger fonts","zip files","chunking, summarizing, or retrieval","hoping"],"correct":2,"explain":"Split-and-select - previewing the RAG pipeline.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4739","topic":"","text":"Temperature 0 (or low) suits:","options":["poetry only","nothing","GPU savings","repeatable outputs (data extraction)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Consistency for pipelines; creativity dials up for brainstorming.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4740","topic":"","text":"Model output pasted into your codebase should be:","options":["trusted as-is","minified","committed instantly","reviewed and tested like any code"],"correct":3,"explain":"Generated code is a draft, not a verdict.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4741","topic":"","text":"Asking the model to explain its answer helps because:","options":["visible steps expose weak logic","explanations are proofs","it cannot","tokens are free"],"correct":0,"explain":"Visible reasoning is checkable reasoning (though still verify).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4742","topic":"","text":"The practical prompt template taught is:","options":["just the question","role, task, context, constraints, format","emoji","keywords only"],"correct":1,"explain":"A checklist that turns vague asks into usable specifications.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4743","topic":"","text":"Using generative AI for boilerplate (getters, tests, docs):","options":["is forbidden","replaces design","is a productivity win when reviewed","removes bugs by itself"],"correct":2,"explain":"Automate the mechanical; keep judgment human.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_4":[{"id":"zya18_4_0","topic":"Best use in coding","text":"AI coding assistants are most reliable for:","options":["Authoritative security reviews that need no human check","Precise floating-point edge cases and overflow arithmetic","Boilerplate, unfamiliar APIs and explaining error messages","Committing directly to production without running tests"],"correct":2,"explain":"Low-risk, easily verified work."},{"id":"zya18_4_1","topic":"Invented APIs","text":"A generated call to a non-existent method:","options":["Fails to compile","Is undetectable","Corrupts data","Runs incorrectly"],"correct":0,"explain":"The compiler catches it immediately. Makes it the harmless failure mode."},{"id":"zya18_4_2","topic":"Dangerous failure","text":"The more dangerous AI coding error is:","options":["Code that compiles and runs but is wrong at the boundaries","A misspelled keyword that stops the file from compiling","An import statement referencing a package that is absent","A call to a library method that was never defined"],"correct":0,"explain":"Off-by-one and edge-case errors pass casual inspection."},{"id":"zya18_4_3","topic":"Verification duty","text":"Before keeping generated code you should:","options":["Merge it immediately while the suggestion is still fresh","Delete your existing tests so they cannot conflict with it","Assume the tool validated it more thoroughly than you could","Read every line and be able to explain it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Code you cannot explain is a liability in an exam and on the job."},{"id":"zya18_4_4","topic":"Testing generated code","text":"Generated code should be tested with:","options":["Boundaries","No tests","Random input only","The happy path only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Exactly where the likely errors are. Empty, single element, maximum, negative, null."},{"id":"zya18_4_5","topic":"Privacy","text":"Pasting into an AI tool should never include:","options":["Credentials, keys or private data","Open-source code","Error messages","Public documentation"],"correct":0,"explain":"Prompts may be stored or reviewed."},{"id":"zya18_4_6","topic":"Explanations","text":"An AI explanation of a bug:","options":["May be plausible but wrong","Cannot be produced","Replaces debugging","Is always correct"],"correct":0,"explain":"Confident wrong diagnoses are common. It must be verified."},{"id":"zya18_4_7","topic":"Academic policy","text":"For coursework, undisclosed AI-generated submissions are:","options":["Encouraged, since tools are part of professional practice everywhere","Impossible for an instructor to ever notice or question","Fine as long as the generated code compiles and passes","Commonly treated as an integrity issue"],"correct":3,"explain":"You remain responsible for what you submit. Check the specific policy."},{"id":"zyf18_4_0","topic":"Compile-time catch","text":"A hallucinated method name is:","options":["A silent wrong result that only careful testing reveals","A crash that appears only when users run the deployed app","Invisible, because the compiler assumes the method exists","Caught by the compiler, making it the safe failure mode"],"correct":3,"explain":"The dangerous errors are the ones that compile."},{"id":"zyf18_4_1","topic":"Responsibility","text":"For submitted coursework, responsibility for generated code lies with:","options":["The tool","You","The instructor","Nobody"],"correct":1,"explain":"Code you cannot explain is a liability."},{"id":"fs_z18_4_1","topic":"Generated code and exams","text":"For an exam that asks you to trace and predict code, the useful way to work with generated code while studying is:","options":["Read it and understand why it works","Memorize it verbatim without analysis","Submit it directly whenever it compiles","Avoid reading any code you did not write"],"correct":0,"explain":"The exam tests tracing and prediction, not production. Generated code helps only as reading material you fully understand - understanding why it works is the version of AI assistance that transfers to the test. Code you cannot explain earns nothing."},{"id":"fs_z18_4_2","topic":"Good and bad uses","text":"Which use of an AI coding assistant is specifically a POOR practice?","options":["Accepting security-critical logic on trust, without review","Generating boilerplate and scaffolding to edit","Asking it to explain an unfamiliar piece of code","Asking it to suggest test cases for your method"],"correct":0,"explain":"Boilerplate, explanation, and test suggestions are the reliable uses. Security-critical logic taken on trust is the listed bad use: subtle flaws compile and run silently, so such code demands careful human review most of all."},{"id":"fs_z18_4_3","topic":"Draft, not truth","text":"Treating AI output as "a fast draft rather than a source of truth" means:","options":["Everything it produces must pass the same review any pull request would","Its output can be merged without reading if tests exist","Only its comments need checking, not its code","It should only ever be used for documentation"],"correct":0,"explain":"A draft speeds you up but carries no authority: generated code gets read, questioned, and tested exactly like a colleague's pull request. Running is not proof of correctness - the dangerous failure is code that runs and is subtly wrong."},{"id":"fs_z18_4_4","topic":"Coursework policy","text":"Before using an AI assistant on graded coursework, the first thing to do is:","options":["Check the institution's academic policy on AI use","Raise the assistant's temperature setting","Remove code comments so the source is unclear","Nothing - generated work carries no responsibility"],"correct":0,"explain":"Academic policy varies by institution and violating it has real consequences. Whatever the policy allows, responsibility for what you submit remains yours regardless of what produced it."},{"id":"zf4744","topic":"","text":"AI coding assistants are best treated as:","options":["an oracle","a compiler","a replacement for testing","a fast junior partner needing review"],"correct":3,"explain":"Speed plus fallibility - review is the contract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4745","topic":"","text":"Generated code must still be:","options":["read, understood, and tested by you","shipped directly","minified","trusted by length"],"correct":0,"explain":"You own what you merge - the unit’s ethics-meets-practice rule.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4746","topic":"","text":"Good uses of AI while learning Java include:","options":["submitting its answers as yours","explaining errors, comparing approaches","skipping fundamentals","avoiding docs forever"],"correct":1,"explain":"A tutor that answers instantly - if you stay the one doing the learning.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4747","topic":"","text":"Pasting a stack trace into an assistant is useful because:","options":["traces are secrets","it fixes the JVM","it decodes the trace, suggesting causes","it reruns code"],"correct":2,"explain":"Error-message translation is a high-value assist.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4748","topic":"","text":"The risk of accepting suggestions you do not understand is:","options":["nothing","faster shipping","more RAM","unmaintainable code with hidden bugs"],"correct":3,"explain":"Understanding is the maintenance prerequisite.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4749","topic":"","text":"AI suggestions may be subtly wrong because:","options":["models pattern-match, not verify","keyboards differ","they compile first","they cannot be"],"correct":0,"explain":"Plausible-looking is not correct - tests decide.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4750","topic":"","text":"Describing the desired method in a comment before generation:","options":["hands the assistant a specification","is wasted typing","confuses it","compiles faster"],"correct":0,"explain":"Spec-first prompting - the clearer the intent, the better the draft.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4751","topic":"","text":"School policies on AI use should be:","options":["ignored","known and followed for each assignment","identical everywhere","irrelevant"],"correct":1,"explain":"Allowed-tool boundaries differ; integrity is contextual.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4752","topic":"","text":"Unit tests around AI-written code:","options":["are redundant","insult the model","convert plausible into verified","slow learning"],"correct":2,"explain":"The same regression net as for human code - more necessary, if anything.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4753","topic":"","text":"Asking the assistant for TWO approaches and comparing:","options":["wastes tokens","confuses git","is impossible","builds judgment while harvesting speed"],"correct":3,"explain":"Compare-and-choose keeps the engineering muscle active.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4754","topic":"","text":"The skill that AI assistance does NOT replace is:","options":["specifying, decomposing, verifying","typing","syntax memory","boilerplate"],"correct":0,"explain":"The thinking framework of units 1-17 is exactly what remains yours.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_5":[{"id":"zya18_5_0","topic":"API call basics","text":"Calling an LLM API from Java is fundamentally:","options":["A file read","A local method call","A database query","An HTTP POST with a JSON body"],"correct":3,"explain":"Java 11+ includes HttpClient for exactly this."},{"id":"zya18_5_1","topic":"API key storage","text":"An API key should be stored:","options":["Directly in the source file so the code is self-contained","In an environment variable or config file outside version control","In a code comment near the call so reviewers can find it","Inside the request JSON so it travels with each call"],"correct":1,"explain":"Committed keys are a common and costly security incident."},{"id":"zya18_5_2","topic":"Checking the response","text":"After the call you must check:","options":["The status code, handling non-200 responses","Only that the response body is not the empty string","That your API key still appears inside the response","Nothing - a returned response always means success"],"correct":0,"explain":"Rate limits and server errors are normal operating conditions."},{"id":"zya18_5_3","topic":"Conversation memory","text":"Because the API is stateless, chat history is:","options":["Reconstructed by the server from your API key automatically","Impossible, since each request must stand entirely alone","Kept by the model in its weights between your calls","Maintained by your program and resent each turn"],"correct":3,"explain":"Usually a List of messages."},{"id":"zya18_5_4","topic":"Growing history","text":"As the conversation grows you eventually must:","options":["Purchase a longer API key that unlocks a bigger window","Restart the JVM so the model's memory is cleared","Trim or summarize older turns to stay within the context window","Switch every message to uppercase to save tokens"],"correct":2,"explain":"Otherwise the request is rejected."},{"id":"zya18_5_5","topic":"Error handling","text":"Network calls require handling:","options":["Only the success path, since HTTP retries automatically","Timeouts, rate limits and malformed responses","Compile-time errors that the network stack raises","Nothing, because HttpClient silently fixes failures"],"correct":1,"explain":"They fail in ways local method calls do not."},{"id":"zya18_5_6","topic":"Response format","text":"The raw response body is typically:","options":["JSON that must be parsed to extract the generated text","A ready-to-use Java String containing just the answer text","A serialized Java object you can cast to a response class","Raw image bytes even for text-generation endpoints"],"correct":0,"explain":"A JSON library or manual parsing is needed."},{"id":"zya18_5_7","topic":"Escaping input","text":"User text placed into a JSON body must be:","options":["Truncated to one line so the JSON stays on a single row","Converted to uppercase so the server can parse it reliably","Pasted in directly, since JSON accepts any raw text","Escaped, or quotes in the input will corrupt the JSON"],"correct":3,"explain":"The same class of problem as SQL injection."},{"id":"zyf18_5_0","topic":"Key handling","text":"An API key belongs in:","options":["The first line of main, where it is easiest to find","A shared team document everyone can copy it from","The repository README so collaborators can reuse it","An environment variable or config outside version control"],"correct":3,"explain":"Committed keys are a common security incident."},{"id":"zyf18_5_1","topic":"Conversation state","text":"Because the API is stateless, history must be:","options":["Compressed","Stored server-side","Resent by your program each turn","Discarded"],"correct":2,"explain":"Usually a List of messages."},{"id":"fs_z18_5_1","topic":"Leaked API keys","text":"An API key was committed to a repository, then deleted in a later commit. The correct response is:","options":["Rename the repository","Rotate (revoke and replace) the key","Nothing further; the deletion removed it","Move the key to a different branch"],"correct":1,"explain":"Version control keeps history: the key remains retrievable from earlier commits even after deletion. A committed key is a published key, and rotating it is the only remedy - which is why keys belong in environment variables, never source. It is still in the repository history."},{"id":"fs_z18_5_2","topic":"Java HTTP client","text":"The standard library class used to send the HTTPS request to an AI service from Java is:","options":["java.net.ServerSocket","java.net.http.HttpClient","java.util.Scanner","java.io.FileReader"],"correct":1,"explain":"HttpClient (with HttpRequest and HttpResponse) is the modern standard API for HTTP calls. Scanner and FileReader read input, and ServerSocket listens for incoming connections - none of them make web requests."},{"id":"fs_z18_5_3","topic":"Rate limiting","text":"The API answers a request with HTTP status 429. That means:","options":["The response body is guaranteed valid JSON","The rate limit was exceeded","The request succeeded","The API key has been permanently revoked"],"correct":1,"explain":"429 is the rate-limit status: the client sent requests too quickly. It is an expected, ordinary condition a robust client handles by backing off and retrying - one of the exception paths every network program needs. Too many requests; wait and retry."},{"id":"fs_z18_5_4","topic":"Where the key travels","text":"In the HTTP request to the AI service, the API key is typically sent in:","options":["A cookie the server previously set","The Authorization header, as a Bearer token","The URL path","The messages array of the JSON body"],"correct":1,"explain":"The pattern is .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + key). Headers keep credentials out of URLs (which get logged) and out of the message payload, which carries the conversation content."},{"id":"zf4755","topic":"","text":"Calling an AI service from Java is fundamentally:","options":["a JVM feature","an HTTP request with a JSON body","magic","a socket-free operation"],"correct":1,"explain":"JSON over HTTPS - ordinary networking, AI payload.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4756","topic":"","text":"Java’s modern built-in HTTP tooling is:","options":["Scanner","FileReader","java.net.http.HttpClient","Sockets only"],"correct":2,"explain":"HttpClient/HttpRequest/HttpResponse - the standard trio.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4757","topic":"","text":"The API key belongs in:","options":["a String literal","the repo README","the prompt","an environment variable"],"correct":3,"explain":"Committed keys are published keys - rotation is the only cure.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4758","topic":"","text":"System.getenv("OPENAI_API_KEY") reads:","options":["the key from the process environment","a file named OPENAI","the internet","the JVM flags"],"correct":0,"explain":"Configuration stays outside the codebase.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4759","topic":"","text":"The Authorization header carries:","options":["the prompt",""Bearer " plus the API key","the model weights","your password"],"correct":1,"explain":"Standard bearer-token authentication.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4760","topic":"","text":"Before parsing the response body, check:","options":["the body length","the HTTP status code is 200","the time","nothing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Parsing an error page as a chat reply corrupts everything downstream.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4761","topic":"","text":"The request body for a chat API contains:","options":["raw text only","Java source","JSON with the model and messages","a file path"],"correct":2,"explain":"{"model": ..., "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": ...}]}","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4762","topic":"","text":"A conversational bot maintains context by:","options":["server memory by default","cookies","the JVM","resending prior messages in each request"],"correct":3,"explain":"The messages array IS the memory - the client curates it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4763","topic":"","text":"Network failures around the AI call are handled with:","options":["the unit-12 exception machinery","ignoring them","print and continue blindly","GUI colors"],"correct":0,"explain":"try/catch around send - services fail; programs should not.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4764","topic":"","text":"The POST(...ofString(jsonBody)) part of the builder sets:","options":["the URL","the request’s method and body","the headers","the response"],"correct":1,"explain":"POST with a payload - the shape of API calls.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4765","topic":"","text":"This section proves the course arc by showing:","options":["AI replaces Java","chatbots are hardware","prior units composing one AI app","JSON is optional"],"correct":2,"explain":"Every prior unit is load-bearing in 40 lines of integration code.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_6":[{"id":"zya18_6_0","topic":"Image API difference","text":"Compared with text generation, image generation:","options":["Returns plain text that renders as an image in the browser","Completes faster because pictures are smaller than text","Takes seconds and returns binary data or a URL","Requires no authentication since images are public"],"correct":2,"explain":"The latency changes how the program must be structured."},{"id":"zya18_6_1","topic":"GUI threading","text":"Calling a slow image API from a button listener:","options":["Runs smoothly because Swing threads network calls itself","Freezes the window unless it runs on a background thread","Is impossible - listeners cannot make network requests","Speeds the call up by giving it the UI thread's priority"],"correct":1,"explain":"SwingWorker or a separate Thread keeps the UI responsive."},{"id":"zya18_6_2","topic":"Expiring URLs","text":"A returned image URL should be:","options":["Printed only","Stored as a permanent link","Downloaded promptly","Ignored"],"correct":2,"explain":"Saving the link rather than the bytes leads to broken images later. Such URLs usually expire."},{"id":"zya18_6_3","topic":"Base64 response","text":"Base64 image data is turned into a file by:","options":["Writing the base64 String straight into a .png file","Casting the String to a byte array with (byte[])","Printing it to the console and redirecting to a file","Decoding it to bytes, then writing those bytes"],"correct":3,"explain":"Base64.getDecoder().decode(data)."},{"id":"zya18_6_4","topic":"Displaying the result","text":"Showing a saved PNG in Swing uses:","options":["System.out","JTextArea","An ImageIcon in a JLabel","JButton"],"correct":2,"explain":"Or a Graphics drawImage call."},{"id":"zya18_6_5","topic":"Cost awareness","text":"Image endpoints typically:","options":["Run locally","Are free","Cost money per call","Are unlimited"],"correct":2,"explain":"Regenerating identical images wastes money. Results should be cached."},{"id":"zya18_6_6","topic":"Content filtering","text":"A prompt may be refused, which the program should:","options":["Retry forever","Treat as a crash","Handle as a normal response path","Ignore"],"correct":2,"explain":"A refusal is a valid outcome, not an exception."},{"id":"zya18_6_7","topic":"File writing","text":"Saving the downloaded image can throw:","options":["NumberFormatException","Nothing","IOException","ArithmeticException"],"correct":2,"explain":"Disk and permission failures are real. Must be handled."},{"id":"zyf18_6_0","topic":"Threading","text":"A slow image call from a GUI listener must run:","options":["Twice","Synchronously","On the event thread","On a background thread"],"correct":3,"explain":"Otherwise the window freezes."},{"id":"fs_z18_6_1","topic":"Why base64","text":"Image APIs often return the picture as base64 text inside the JSON response because:","options":["Base64 encrypts the image for security","Java cannot process raw bytes","JSON is a text format","Base64 compresses the image smaller than PNG"],"correct":2,"explain":"Base64 is a text encoding for binary data - it exists so bytes can ride inside text formats like JSON. It neither compresses (it grows the data by about a third) nor encrypts anything. Binary bytes must be encoded as text to travel in it."},{"id":"fs_z18_6_2","topic":"Returning to the UI thread","text":"A background thread has finished downloading the generated image. In a JavaFX app, the ImageView must be updated by:","options":["Starting another background thread for the update","Calling Thread.sleep on the UI thread until done","Wrapping the update in Platform.runLater","Calling setImage directly from the background thread"],"correct":2,"explain":"UI components may only be touched on the UI thread. Platform.runLater hands the update back to that thread (SwingUtilities.invokeLater is the Swing equivalent). Touching components from a background thread is the classic threading error."},{"id":"fs_z18_6_3","topic":"Binary output stream","text":"Saving the decoded PNG bytes to disk should use:","options":["FileWriter, which writes characters","Scanner, which reads input","FileOutputStream, which writes raw bytes","PrintWriter, which writes text"],"correct":2,"explain":"An image is binary data, so it needs a byte-oriented stream: FileOutputStream.write(imageBytes). Character-oriented writers like PrintWriter and FileWriter can corrupt binary data by applying text encoding."},{"id":"fs_z18_6_4","topic":"Slow calls and the GUI","text":"Because image generation takes several seconds, a well-behaved GUI application should:","options":["Call Thread.sleep on the event thread while polling","Silently drop the request if it takes over a second","Run the request on a background thread and show an in-progress indication meanwhile","Freeze the window until the response arrives"],"correct":2,"explain":"A network call on the UI thread freezes the whole interface for its duration. The pattern is: background thread for the slow work, visible progress feedback for the user, then back to the UI thread to display the result."},{"id":"zf4766","topic":"","text":"An image API’s response typically contains:","options":["a JPEG object","a Java Image directly","nothing","a temporary URL or base64 data"],"correct":3,"explain":"Either fetch the URL or decode the base64 - then save.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4767","topic":"","text":"Base64 image data becomes bytes via:","options":["Base64.getDecoder().decode(base64String)","parseInt","charAt","casting"],"correct":0,"explain":"The standard-library decoder yields the raw image bytes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4768","topic":"","text":"Saving those bytes uses:","options":["println","a FileOutputStream and the bytes","a JLabel","a String"],"correct":1,"explain":"Binary file output - unit 9’s streams, binary flavor.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4769","topic":"","text":"A slow generation call made ON the UI thread:","options":["shows progress","threads itself","freezes the window until it finishes","is required"],"correct":2,"explain":"The event thread must stay free - the section’s central warning.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4770","topic":"","text":"The fix is running the request:","options":["twice","never","on a background thread","after closing the GUI"],"correct":2,"explain":"new Thread(() -> { ... }).start(); - work off the UI thread.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4771","topic":"","text":"Updating the GUI from that background thread requires:","options":["nothing special","file locks","a second window","handing the update back to the UI thread"],"correct":3,"explain":"SwingUtilities.invokeLater / Platform.runLater - UI updates on the UI thread only.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4772","topic":"","text":"In JavaFX, the hand-back call is:","options":["Platform.runLater(() -> ...)","invokeLater","runNow","uiUpdate"],"correct":0,"explain":"The FX-specific version of the same rule.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4773","topic":"","text":"While generating, the app should:","options":["appear frozen","show a progress/status indication","close","beep"],"correct":1,"explain":"Long operations owe the user visible feedback.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4774","topic":"","text":"A temporary result URL from the API:","options":["lasts forever","is the image","expires - download it promptly","is a file path"],"correct":2,"explain":"Fetch and save; do not bookmark ephemeral URLs.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4775","topic":"","text":"try (FileOutputStream out = ...) around the save gives:","options":["speed","compression","nothing","automatic close even on exceptions"],"correct":3,"explain":"Try-with-resources - unit 12 guarding unit 9.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4776","topic":"","text":"The user’s prompt travels to the API as:","options":["part of the JSON request body","a filename","a header only","pixels"],"correct":0,"explain":"Text in, image out - the generative exchange.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4777","topic":"","text":"This app combines which units?","options":["only unit 1","events, threads, HTTP, file output","none","graphics only"],"correct":1,"explain":"The whole toolkit converges again, now with binary data.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_7":[{"id":"zya18_7_0","topic":"System prompt","text":"A system prompt provides:","options":["The user's current question, repeated for reliability","The credentials the server needs to authorize the call","Persistent instructions defining role, tone and boundaries","The example output the model must copy exactly"],"correct":2,"explain":"It applies across the whole conversation."},{"id":"zya18_7_1","topic":"Output validation","text":"If you ask the model for JSON you must:","options":["Trust the structure, since the model never breaks a promise","Parse defensively and handle malformed output","Print it directly to users without inspecting it first","Cast the raw String to a Java object and use it"],"correct":1,"explain":"The model can produce almost-JSON."},{"id":"zya18_7_2","topic":"Fallback behaviour","text":"When the API is down, a well-built feature should:","options":["Degrade gracefully with a clear message","Retry in a tight loop until the service finally answers","Show the stack trace so users can diagnose the outage","Return cached text from a different user's session"],"correct":0,"explain":"Availability is not guaranteed."},{"id":"zya18_7_3","topic":"Evaluation","text":"A fixed set of test inputs with known-good answers lets you:","options":["Skip API calls entirely by memorizing the answers","Guarantee the model never hallucinates on other inputs","Reduce the per-token price the provider charges","Tell whether a prompt change actually helped"],"correct":3,"explain":"Without it, prompt tuning is guesswork."},{"id":"zya18_7_4","topic":"Context assembly","text":"Deciding what to include in the prompt matters because:","options":["Prompts are free, so including everything is always safest","The model reads only the first sentence anyway","The context window is finite and irrelevant content dilutes the task","Extra context always improves answers, never dilutes them"],"correct":2,"explain":"This is where retrieval belongs."},{"id":"zya18_7_5","topic":"Caching","text":"Caching responses to repeated queries:","options":["Is impossible","Reduces cost and latency","Reduces quality","Is required"],"correct":1,"explain":"Especially valuable for common questions."},{"id":"zya18_7_6","topic":"Most of the work","text":"In a production AI feature, most of the code is:","options":["The model itself","Ordinary software around the call","The prompt only","The UI only"],"correct":1,"explain":"The model call is often a small fraction. Validation, error handling, parsing."},{"id":"zya18_7_7","topic":"Logging","text":"Logging inputs and outputs supports:","options":["Making each response arrive noticeably faster","Lowering the per-request price the provider bills","Training the model further with every logged line","Evaluation and debugging of quality over time"],"correct":3,"explain":"Subject to the privacy constraints on what may be stored."},{"id":"zyf18_7_0","topic":"Output parsing","text":"Requested JSON output should be:","options":["Trusted fully, since requested formats are guaranteed","Displayed raw to the user, who can read JSON directly","Discarded, because models cannot produce valid JSON","Parsed defensively, handling malformed responses"],"correct":3,"explain":"Models can emit almost-JSON."},{"id":"zyf18_7_1","topic":"Evaluation set","text":"A fixed set of inputs with known answers lets you:","options":["Tell whether a prompt change helped","Reduce cost","Avoid the API","Skip testing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Without it, prompt tuning is guesswork."},{"id":"fs_z18_7_1","topic":"Pipeline order","text":"Which is the correct order of stages in an AI feature's pipeline?","options":["Call model -> build prompt -> validate input -> use it -> parse response","Build prompt -> validate output -> call model -> validate input -> use it","Parse response -> call model -> build prompt -> use it -> validate input","Validate input -> build prompt -> call model -> parse response -> validate output -> use it"],"correct":3,"explain":"Input is validated before money is spent on a model call, and the reply is parsed and validated before anything trusts it. Every stage except the model call is ordinary Java - validation, error handling, fallbacks."},{"id":"fs_z18_7_2","topic":"Non-determinism","text":"Saying that a model is NOT deterministic means:","options":["It always returns malformed JSON","It cannot be called twice in one session","Its output length is unbounded","The same input can produce different outputs on different runs"],"correct":3,"explain":"Determinism is the same input giving the same output; models do not guarantee that. This is why evaluation uses a fixed set of cases with known-good answers - it turns 'the new prompt seems better' from an impression into a measurement."},{"id":"fs_z18_7_3","topic":"Guardrails","text":"In an AI system, "guardrails" are:","options":["The physical racks the servers are mounted in","A visual border drawn around the chat window","The model's internal neural network layers","Checks on input and output that constrain what the system will accept or emit"],"correct":3,"explain":"Guardrails are validation applied on both sides of the model call: rejecting unacceptable input before it goes in, and checking the reply before it is used. They are ordinary code, not part of the model."},{"id":"fs_z18_7_4","topic":"The model's place","text":"In a production AI feature, the model itself is best regarded as:","options":["The whole architecture, with a thin wrapper","A replacement for input validation","A guaranteed source of well-formed JSON","One component in a larger pipeline, not the architecture"],"correct":3,"explain":"The model call is a small part; the engineering lives in validation, context assembly, output checking, fallbacks, logging, caching, and evaluation around it. Treating the model as the architecture leaves all of that missing."},{"id":"zf4778","topic":"","text":"An AI SYSTEM around a model includes:","options":["just the model","only the GUI","input, prompts, validation, output","hardware"],"correct":2,"explain":"The model is one component; engineering surrounds it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4779","topic":"","text":"Validating model output before acting on it matters because:","options":["models are certain","JSON is guaranteed","it does not","responses may be malformed or wrong"],"correct":3,"explain":"Parse defensively; check fields exist; verify ranges - unit-12 thinking.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4780","topic":"","text":"Retrying a failed API call with backoff means:","options":["hammering instantly","giving up","changing keys","progressively longer waits"],"correct":3,"explain":"Robust integrations assume transient failures.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4781","topic":"","text":"Caching identical requests’ responses:","options":["saves cost and time on repeats","is forbidden","breaks models","uses no memory"],"correct":0,"explain":"A HashMap from prompt to reply - unit 14 saving money.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4782","topic":"","text":"Rate limits from providers mean your code should:","options":["ignore 429s","throttle and queue requests gracefully","multithread harder","crash"],"correct":1,"explain":"Respecting limits is part of the API contract.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4783","topic":"","text":"Logging prompts and responses (minus secrets) helps:","options":["nothing","the model learn","debugging and quality tracking over time","GDPR alone"],"correct":2,"explain":"Observability for a nondeterministic component is essential.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4784","topic":"","text":"Separating the AI-calling code into its own class:","options":["slows calls","is over-engineering always","hides bugs","lets you swap providers or mock it"],"correct":3,"explain":"An AiClient interface - unit 7/10 design serving unit 18.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4785","topic":"","text":"Mocking the AI client in unit tests:","options":["makes tests fast","is cheating","tests nothing","breaks builds"],"correct":0,"explain":"Test your logic against canned replies; hit the real API sparingly.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4786","topic":"","text":"Fallback behavior when the AI is down should be:","options":["silence","a designed degraded mode, not a crash","infinite retries","reboot"],"correct":1,"explain":""Service unavailable, try later" beats a stack trace.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4787","topic":"","text":"Cost awareness in AI systems means:","options":["ignoring bills","free tiers forever","designing with token/call costs in mind","hardware budgets"],"correct":2,"explain":"Long prompts and chatty loops have literal prices.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4788","topic":"","text":"The engineering lesson of AI systems is:","options":["models are reliable alone","engineering is obsolete","GUIs matter most","reliability lives AROUND the model"],"correct":3,"explain":"Same software discipline, new unreliable-but-powerful component.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_8":[{"id":"zya18_8_0","topic":"RAG purpose","text":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation solves the problem that:","options":["Java has no libraries capable of sending HTTP requests","Models answer too slowly without a retrieval cache","The model does not know your documents and they will not fit in the context","API providers charge extra for questions about documents"],"correct":2,"explain":"Retrieve the relevant pieces and send only those."},{"id":"zya18_8_1","topic":"Embedding","text":"An embedding is:","options":["A compressed copy of the text that can be decompressed later","A vector of numbers capturing the meaning of text","The token that authenticates your retrieval requests","A shortened summary the model writes for each document"],"correct":1,"explain":"Similar meanings produce nearby vectors."},{"id":"zya18_8_2","topic":"Similarity measure","text":"Relevance between vectors is commonly measured with:","options":["Cosine similarity","Array length","Hashing","String equality"],"correct":0,"explain":"It compares direction rather than magnitude."},{"id":"zya18_8_3","topic":"Chunking","text":"Documents are split into chunks because:","options":["Whole documents embed more accurately than passages do","Java arrays cannot hold strings longer than a page","The model bills less for many small requests than one large","Retrieval works better on focused passages that fit the context"],"correct":3,"explain":"Whole documents are too coarse and too large."},{"id":"zya18_8_4","topic":"Query-time steps","text":"At query time, RAG:","options":["Sends the entire document set with every single question","Retrains the model overnight on your document folder","Embeds the question, finds the nearest chunks, and includes them in the prompt","Answers from its training data and cites your files afterwards"],"correct":2,"explain":"The model then answers from supplied context."},{"id":"zya18_8_5","topic":"Main benefit","text":"Grounding answers in retrieved text:","options":["Makes every generated sentence provably true","Sharply reduces hallucination and allows citing the source","Removes the need for a language model entirely","Works only for documents shorter than one page"],"correct":1,"explain":"You can point at which chunk supported the answer."},{"id":"zya18_8_6","topic":"Main failure mode","text":"RAG most often fails when:","options":["Retrieval returns the wrong chunks","The prompt is short","Java is used","The model is small"],"correct":0,"explain":"Retrieval is usually the weak link, not generation. The model answers confidently from irrelevant text."},{"id":"zya18_8_7","topic":"RAG versus fine-tuning","text":"For making a bot know your company handbook, the right approach is:","options":["RAG","Neither","Both required","Fine-tuning"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fine-tuning suits style and format more than facts. It updates as the documents update and needs no retraining."},{"id":"zya18_8_8","topic":"Top-k selection","text":"Selecting the k most similar chunks is naturally done with:","options":["A HashSet, since similarity scores are always unique","A Stack, so the most recent chunk is always chosen","A PriorityQueue ordered by similarity","A LinkedList, because chunks arrive in insertion order"],"correct":2,"explain":"The same structure from Unit 14, applied here."},{"id":"zyf18_8_0","topic":"Weak link","text":"RAG most often fails because of:","options":["The model being too small to read the retrieved text","Java collections being too slow for similarity search","Generation taking longer than the retrieval step","Retrieval returning the wrong chunks"],"correct":3,"explain":"The model then answers confidently from irrelevant text."},{"id":"fs_z18_8_1","topic":"RAG pipeline order","text":"Which is the correct order of the four RAG steps?","options":["Index -> retrieve -> augment -> generate","Generate -> retrieve -> index -> augment","Retrieve -> generate -> index -> augment","Augment -> index -> generate -> retrieve"],"correct":0,"explain":"First the documents are chunked and indexed; at question time the relevant chunks are retrieved, placed into the prompt as context (augmentation), and only then does the model generate an answer from that context."},{"id":"fs_z18_8_2","topic":"Cosine similarity","text":"Cosine similarity compares vector DIRECTION rather than length. For retrieval this matters because:","options":["A short question can still match a long document about the same topic","Longer vectors always score higher","It makes every similarity exactly 1","It only works on sorted vectors"],"correct":0,"explain":"Cosine similarity measures the angle between embeddings, running from 1 (same direction) through 0 (unrelated) to -1 (opposite). Ignoring magnitude is what lets a brief query match a lengthy chunk with the same meaning."},{"id":"fs_z18_8_3","topic":"Where the model sits","text":"In the four-step RAG pipeline (index, retrieve, augment, generate), the language model produces the answer in which step?","options":["Generate - the earlier steps are data-structure and search work","Index - the model organizes the documents","Retrieve - the model searches the chunks","Augment - the model rewrites the prompt"],"correct":0,"explain":"Indexing, retrieving, and assembling the prompt are ordinary search and collections work. Only the final generation step asks the language model to answer, using the retrieved chunks as its context - which is why RAG is largely a data-structures problem."},{"id":"fs_z18_8_4","topic":"The RAG prompt","text":"The prompt a RAG system builds typically instructs the model to:","options":["Answer using only the supplied context chunks","Ignore the context and answer from memory","Refuse to answer any factual question","Summarize its own training data"],"correct":0,"explain":"The prompt pairs the retrieved chunks with the question and says to answer using only that context. Constraining the model to real retrieved text is exactly how RAG counters hallucination."},{"id":"zf4789","topic":"","text":"RAG stands for:","options":["retrieval-augmented generation","random answer generation","rapid AI graphics","recursive algorithm guide"],"correct":0,"explain":"Retrieve relevant text; generate answers from it.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4790","topic":"","text":"RAG primarily combats:","options":["hallucination, via grounding","slow networks","GUI freezes","token limits only"],"correct":0,"explain":"The model answers FROM your documents, not from vibes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4791","topic":"","text":"The four RAG steps are:","options":["compile, run, test, ship","index, retrieve, augment, generate","read, sort, print, exit","none"],"correct":1,"explain":"Chunk-store, find-relevant, prompt-with-context, ask-model.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4792","topic":"","text":"Indexing means:","options":["alphabetizing","compressing","chunk-and-store for later lookup","deleting"],"correct":2,"explain":"The searchable knowledge base is built before any question arrives.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4793","topic":"","text":"Retrieval, in the simple Java version, is:","options":["a model call","a GUI","random choice","ordinary search over the chunks"],"correct":3,"explain":"Term matching with data structures - "more data structures than AI".","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4794","topic":"","text":"Cosine similarity measures:","options":["how closely two vectors align","string length","file size","token cost"],"correct":0,"explain":"1 = same direction, 0 = unrelated - the standard relevance score.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4795","topic":"","text":"The augment step places the retrieved chunks:","options":["on disk","into the prompt as context","into training","in the GUI"],"correct":1,"explain":""Answer using only the context below" + chunks + question.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4796","topic":"","text":"Instructing "answer ONLY from the context":","options":["is decoration","breaks models","confines answers to retrieved facts","speeds tokens"],"correct":2,"explain":"The grounding instruction that makes RAG trustworthy.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4797","topic":"","text":"If retrieval returns nothing relevant, the system should:","options":["guess anyway","crash","retry forever","admit not-knowing instead of guessing"],"correct":3,"explain":"Honest absence beats grounded-sounding fabrication.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4798","topic":"","text":"RAG suits which use case in this course’s spirit?","options":["Q&A over your OWN notes and documents","general trivia","image editing","sorting"],"correct":0,"explain":"Private-knowledge Q&A - the practical payoff of the pipeline.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4947","topic":"","text":"A RAG answer’s quality depends most directly on:","options":["the relevance of the retrieved chunks","GPU model","file names","JSON formatting"],"correct":0,"explain":"Garbage retrieval, garbage grounding - the search step carries the system.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u18/z18_9":[{"id":"zya18_9_0","topic":"Bias origin","text":"Bias in AI systems arises because:","options":["Code has bugs","Data is too small","Computers prefer certain answers","Models learn from human-produced data and reproduce its patterns"],"correct":3,"explain":"It must be measured and mitigated deliberately, not patched once."},{"id":"zya18_9_1","topic":"Hallucination risk","text":"Hallucination is most dangerous where:","options":["Users can instantly check the answer against the source","Verification is hard, such as medicine, law and citations","The text is short enough to verify at a glance","Every reader is a domain expert who spots errors"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fluency makes wrong answers persuasive."},{"id":"zya18_9_2","topic":"Privacy","text":"Text entered into an AI service:","options":["May be stored, reviewed or used for training depending on the terms","Is legally guaranteed to be deleted within the hour","Stays on your machine - the service only sees embeddings","Is encrypted so thoroughly the provider cannot read it"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why personal and proprietary data should not be pasted in."},{"id":"zya18_9_3","topic":"Accountability","text":"When a deployed AI system produces a harmful output:","options":["The model itself bears the responsibility for its words","No one, since generated text has no legal author","Only the user who typed the prompt that triggered it","A person or organization remains accountable"],"correct":3,"explain":""The model did it" is not a defence."},{"id":"zya18_9_4","topic":"Transparency","text":"Users should be told:","options":["Only the model's version number, for support tickets","Nothing, since disclosure would reduce the system's usefulness","When they are interacting with an AI and when it made a decision about them","Only the total cost of the tokens their question used"],"correct":2,"explain":"Especially for consequential decisions."},{"id":"zya18_9_5","topic":"Testing for fairness","text":"A system should be tested:","options":["On its average accuracy, since averages capture fairness","Across the groups it will actually be used on","Once before launch, since behavior never drifts","Only by its developers, who know it best"],"correct":1,"explain":"Aggregate accuracy can hide poor performance for a subgroup."},{"id":"zya18_9_6","topic":"Human review","text":"Decisions materially affecting someone should:","options":["Have meaningful human review and a route to contest them","Be fully automated so no human bias can enter them","Be kept confidential so the criteria cannot be gamed","Be randomized so no group is systematically favored"],"correct":0,"explain":"Automation without recourse is the core fairness failure."},{"id":"zya18_9_7","topic":"Environmental cost","text":"Training and serving large models:","options":["Run carbon-neutral because data centers are all renewable","Cost only electricity, which is negligible at scale","Consume resources only during the initial training run","Consume substantial energy and water"],"correct":3,"explain":"A real consideration in deployment decisions."},{"id":"zya18_9_8","topic":"Appropriate reliance","text":"The balanced position is:","options":["Trusting the tool fully once it has passed a demo","Refusing the tool entirely until it is provably perfect","Neither refusing a useful tool nor trusting it beyond what it can do","Using it only where mistakes could never matter"],"correct":2,"explain":"That calibration is the actual skill being assessed."},{"id":"zyf18_9_0","topic":"Accountability","text":"Responsibility for a deployed AI system's output rests with:","options":["The model vendor alone, wherever the system is used","Whoever typed the specific prompt that produced it","No one, because statistical output has no author","The people and organization deploying it"],"correct":3,"explain":""The model did it" is not a defence."},{"id":"fs_z18_9_1","topic":"Automation bias","text":""Automation bias" is the failure mode of:","options":["Training data that contains no automation examples","Trusting a confident, fluent output more than it deserves","A model refusing to automate a task","Code that runs slower when automated"],"correct":1,"explain":"Automation bias is over-trusting machine output precisely because it sounds fluent and sure of itself. It is the reason human review must be genuinely critical wherever decisions materially affect people."},{"id":"fs_z18_9_2","topic":"Attribution risk","text":"The ATTRIBUTION risk of generative AI is that:","options":["Generated text cannot be copyrighted by anyone","Generated work may closely reproduce material from its training data","Outputs are always labelled with their source","The model credits the wrong programmer in comments"],"correct":1,"explain":"Because the model learned from existing material, its output can closely mirror that material - raising questions about credit and ownership. It is one of the listed risk categories alongside bias, privacy, accuracy, and accountability."},{"id":"fs_z18_9_3","topic":"Explainability","text":"Explainability - being able to say WHY a model produced an output - matters most when:","options":["The decision must be justified to the person it affects, as in lending or hiring","The output is creative writing, where style matters far more than reasons","The system only runs in overnight batch jobs that nobody reads live","The model is small enough that engineers can audit it entirely by hand"],"correct":0,"explain":"Wherever a decision materially affects someone, it must be justifiable - and learned systems are hard to explain, which is a real cost in those settings. That is also why rules still win where reasoning must be shown."},{"id":"fs_z18_9_4","topic":"Deployment decisions","text":"Deciding whether to deploy a model that screens job applicants is ultimately:","options":["The model's own decision to make","A human and legal judgment that code quality cannot settle","A purely technical question about test coverage","Automatic, once accuracy exceeds 90 percent"],"correct":1,"explain":"A model trained on biased hiring data reproduces that bias no matter how well the surrounding Java is written. Fairness, accountability, and legality are human questions - which is precisely why the deployment decision is not a technical one."},{"id":"zf4799","topic":"","text":"Model bias originates chiefly from:","options":["the GPU","patterns and gaps in the training data","the JSON","user typing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Models mirror their data - including its skews.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4800","topic":"","text":"Sending user data to an AI API raises:","options":["privacy duties about data leaving you","no issues","only speed issues","GUI questions"],"correct":0,"explain":"Personal data in prompts is data disclosed to a third party.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4801","topic":"","text":"Hallucination risk means deployed systems should:","options":["trust fluent text","verify or ground factual outputs","disable output","avoid text"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fluency is not accuracy - design for the gap (RAG, review, checks).","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4802","topic":"","text":"Presenting AI-generated work as entirely your own, against policy:","options":["is standard","is required","is an integrity violation","is untraceable"],"correct":2,"explain":"Disclosure norms exist; follow the applicable ones.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4803","topic":"","text":"Prompt injection is:","options":["a vaccine","faster prompting","a GUI attack","input that hijacks the instructions"],"correct":3,"explain":""Ignore prior instructions and..." - treat user text as untrusted.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4804","topic":"","text":"Defending against prompt injection includes:","options":["separating instructions from user data","longer prompts","no users","bigger models"],"correct":0,"explain":"The untrusted-input discipline, extended to prompts.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4805","topic":"","text":"Over-reliance on AI answers in learning risks:","options":["nothing","skill atrophy and unchecked errors","faster learning always","disk usage"],"correct":1,"explain":"The tool amplifies; it should not replace the thinking being trained.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4806","topic":"","text":"Copyright questions around generated content mean:","options":["content is ownerless","all is public domain","usage and attribution rules apply","lawyers are obsolete"],"correct":2,"explain":"The legal landscape is live - know your context’s rules.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4807","topic":"","text":"An AI feature that fails for some user groups more than others indicates:","options":["user error","randomness","a GPU fault","bias to measure and mitigate"],"correct":3,"explain":"Test across populations; fairness is an engineering requirement.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4808","topic":"","text":"Environmental cost of large models is relevant because:","options":["training and serving cost real energy","code is weightless","it is not","GPUs are solar"],"correct":0,"explain":"Efficiency choices (caching, smaller models) have footprint impact.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4809","topic":"","text":"The responsible-use summary of the unit is:","options":["tools decide","powerful tool, human accountability","risk is zero","ethics is optional"],"correct":1,"explain":"You ship it, you own its behavior - AI included.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u8/z8_4":[{"id":"zyf8_4_0","topic":"Garbage collection trigger","text":"An object becomes collectable when:","options":["Its last method call has finished executing","The method that created it has returned","No reference chain from a live root reaches it","It has not been used for a fixed period"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reachability is the criterion."},{"id":"zyf8_4_1","topic":"Automatic reclamation","text":"Java reclaims unused objects:","options":["Automatically, at times the JVM chooses","Only when the programmer calls free()","Immediately when the last reference is dropped","When the enclosing method's frame is popped"],"correct":0,"explain":"You cannot force or schedule it reliably."},{"id":"zyf8_4_2","topic":"System.gc","text":"Calling System.gc():","options":["Is only a suggestion the JVM may ignore","Frees the stack","Is required","Guarantees immediate collection"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is almost never appropriate in application code."},{"id":"zyf8_4_3","topic":"Reference count fallacy","text":"Two objects referencing only each other are:","options":["Still collectable","Merged","Kept forever","Leaked permanently"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reference counting would leak here; tracing does not. Java traces reachability from roots."},{"id":"zyf8_4_4","topic":"Setting null","text":"Assigning null to a reference:","options":["Frees the object's memory immediately and completely","Clears every field of the object before releasing it","Removes one link, allowing collection if it was the last","Has no effect on the garbage collector at all"],"correct":2,"explain":"The collector decides when."},{"id":"zyf8_4_5","topic":"Local objects","text":"An object created in a method and not stored elsewhere becomes collectable:","options":["When the method returns and the local reference disappears","As soon as the object's constructor finishes","Only after the whole program has terminated","Immediately after its last field is written"],"correct":0,"explain":"Assuming no reference escaped."},{"id":"zyf8_4_6","topic":"Leak in Java","text":"A Java memory leak usually means:","options":["The garbage collector failing to run often enough to keep up","Memory freed twice by mistake somewhere in the code","Unneeded objects still reachable, often from a growing collection","Objects allocated on the stack rather than on the heap"],"correct":2,"explain":"A cache that is never trimmed is the classic case."},{"id":"zyf8_4_7","topic":"Finalizers","text":"Relying on finalize() for cleanup is:","options":["Discouraged","Required","Automatic","Recommended"],"correct":0,"explain":"Use try-with-resources for deterministic cleanup. It may run late or never."},{"id":"zyf8_4_8","topic":"Effect on performance","text":"Creating many short-lived objects in a loop:","options":["Adds allocation and collection cost","Prevents collection","Is impossible","Is free"],"correct":0,"explain":"Which is why StringBuilder beats repeated concatenation."},{"id":"fs_z8_4_1","topic":"Setting a reference to null","text":"What does this program print?

static class Tag {
    String n;
    Tag(String n) { this.n = n; }
}

Tag a = new Tag("Cy");
Tag b = a;
a = null;
System.out.println(b.n);","options":["null","Nothing; it throws a NullPointerException","It does not compile","Cy"],"correct":3,"explain":"a = null removes one path to the object, not the object itself. b still refers to it, so it is still reachable and still usable."},{"id":"fs_z8_4_2","topic":"Reachability after reassignment","text":"Consider these two statements:

Student s = new Student("Ana");
s = new Student("Ben");

Immediately after the second statement, how many of the two Student objects are eligible for garbage collection?","options":["Both of them","Exactly one, the Ben object","Exactly one, the Ana object","Neither of them"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reassigning s drops the only reference to the Ana object, so it becomes unreachable. The Ben object is still held by s and stays alive."},{"id":"fs_z8_4_3","topic":"Releasing scarce resources","text":"A program opens a file and finishes with it. What is the correct way to release the file handle?","options":["Call close() on the stream, or open it in a try-with-resources statement","Set the stream variable to null and let the collector release the handle","Call System.gc() so the collector runs immediately","Override finalize() so the handle is released when the object is collected"],"correct":0,"explain":"The collector manages memory, not operating-system resources, and its timing is unspecified. close() is what releases the handle, and finalize() is deprecated and may never run."},{"id":"fs_z8_4_4","topic":"Roots of reachability","text":"Every local variable that referred to a particular object has gone out of scope. Which of these would still keep the object alive?","options":["The fact that the object refers to itself through one of its own fields","A comment in the source code mentioning the object","A static field that still refers to it","Another object on the heap that refers to it but is itself unreachable"],"correct":2,"explain":"Reachability is measured from live roots such as static fields and the locals of running methods. A reference held by an unreachable object, or a self-reference, keeps nothing alive."},{"id":"fs_z8_4_5","topic":"Forcing collection","text":"Which of these frees an object's memory at the moment it is executed?","options":["None of them; Java gives no way to reclaim an object's memory on demand","System.gc()","obj.finalize()","Assigning null to every reference to the object"],"correct":0,"explain":"System.gc() is only a suggestion the JVM may ignore, finalize() is deprecated with no guarantee it runs, and clearing references only makes an object eligible. When the memory is actually reclaimed is up to the JVM."},{"id":"zf2509","topic":"","text":"The garbage collector reclaims:","options":["unreachable heap objects","all old objects","random objects","stack frames"],"correct":0,"explain":"Reachability is the sole criterion.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2510","topic":"","text":"An object becomes ELIGIBLE for collection when:","options":["its last reference is dropped","it gets old","its fields are zero","its method ends"],"correct":0,"explain":"No path from live variables means no way to ever use it again.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2511","topic":"","text":"Eligible objects are collected:","options":["immediately","at some collector-chosen time","never","at midnight"],"correct":1,"explain":"GC runs when the JVM decides; eligibility just marks candidates.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2512","topic":"","text":"p = null; when p held the only reference:","options":["destroys it that instant","keeps it alive","makes the object collectable","throws"],"correct":2,"explain":"Dropping the last reference is the letting-go.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2513","topic":"","text":"Java’s GC removes which classic C/C++ bug family?","options":["off-by-one loops","typos","slow algorithms","use-after-free and forgotten frees"],"correct":3,"explain":"No manual delete means no double-free or dangling-pointer crashes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2514","topic":"","text":"System.gc() is:","options":["a suggestion the JVM may ignore","a guaranteed immediate collection","required cleanup","a compile error"],"correct":0,"explain":"You cannot command collection - only hint.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2515","topic":"","text":"GC cost shows up as:","options":["compile slowness","occasional collector pauses","disk usage","network lag"],"correct":1,"explain":"The convenience is paid in background CPU and pauses.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2516","topic":"","text":"Objects referencing EACH OTHER but reachable from nowhere else:","options":["live forever","crash the JVM","are still collectable","become static"],"correct":2,"explain":"Cycle-immunity is why tracing beats naive reference counting.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2517","topic":"","text":"A long-lived list you keep adding to but never trimming:","options":["is collected anyway","is compressed","is illegal","defeats GC - it all stays reachable"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Java-style leak: reachable-but-useless data.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2518","topic":"","text":"After collection, the reclaimed memory is:","options":["available for new allocations","returned to the disk","lost","zeroed on screen"],"correct":0,"explain":"The heap space cycles back into service.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf2519","topic":"","text":"The programmer’s GC-era responsibility is:","options":["calling free","dropping references when done","counting objects","disabling the GC"],"correct":1,"explain":"Scope tightly, clear collections, drop references - the collector does the rest.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_1":[{"id":"zyf17_1_0","topic":"Linear search cost","text":"Linear search on n elements is:","options":["O(log n)","O(n^2)","O(1)","O(n)"],"correct":3,"explain":"It may examine every element."},{"id":"zyf17_1_1","topic":"Not-found return","text":"Linear search conventionally returns:","options":["null","The length","0","-1"],"correct":3,"explain":"Matching indexOf across the Java library."},{"id":"zyf17_1_2","topic":"Precondition","text":"Linear search requires the data to be:","options":["Numeric","Unique","Sorted","In any order"],"correct":3,"explain":"Which is its one advantage over binary search."},{"id":"zyf17_1_3","topic":"Best case","text":"Linear search is O(1) when:","options":["The array is empty","Never","The array is sorted","The target is the first element"],"correct":3,"explain":"The worst case remains O(n)."},{"id":"zyf17_1_4","topic":"Average comparisons","text":"On average, a successful linear search examines about:","options":["n","log n","1","n/2"],"correct":3,"explain":"Half the elements."},{"id":"zyf17_1_5","topic":"Comparing objects","text":"Searching an array of Strings should compare with:","options":["compareTo only",">","==","equals"],"correct":3,"explain":"== compares references, not contents."},{"id":"zyf17_1_6","topic":"Algorithm definition","text":"An algorithm is:","options":["A program written in a specific language","A way of organising data for efficient access","A diagram of the steps drawn with symbols","A finite, unambiguous procedure that solves a problem"],"correct":3,"explain":"It is language-independent."},{"id":"zyf17_1_7","topic":"Why measure cost","text":"Two correct algorithms are compared by:","options":["How many lines of source code each needs","How their runtime grows with input size","How readable each one is to a new developer","How much memory the compiled program occupies"],"correct":1,"explain":"Correctness is necessary but not sufficient."},{"id":"zyf17_1_8","topic":"Search on a linked list","text":"Binary search is unsuitable for a linked list because:","options":["Linked lists cannot be kept in sorted order","Linked lists have no way to compare elements","The list's length cannot be determined at all","There is no O(1) access to the middle element"],"correct":3,"explain":"The halving advantage disappears without random access."},{"id":"fs_z17_1_1","topic":"Linear search trace","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42};
System.out.println(linearSearch(a, 15));

where linearSearch is:

static int linearSearch(int[] a, int target) {
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] == target) return i;
  }
  return -1;
}","options":["15","-1","2","3"],"correct":2,"explain":"Linear search returns the INDEX where the target is found, not the value. 15 sits at index 2 (indexes 0, 1, 2), so 2 is printed."},{"id":"fs_z17_1_2","topic":"Linear search miss","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {2, 4, 6, 8};
System.out.println(linearSearch(a, 7));

where linearSearch is:

static int linearSearch(int[] a, int target) {
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] == target) return i;
  }
  return -1;
}","options":["It throws an exception","-1","0","3"],"correct":1,"explain":"7 is not in the array, so the loop finishes without returning and the method returns -1, the conventional not-found value. 0 cannot signal not-found because 0 is a valid index."},{"id":"fs_z17_1_3","topic":"Sort once, search many","text":"An unsorted array of a million elements will be searched thousands of times for different values. The best plan is:","options":["Use linear search for every lookup, since the data starts unsorted","Use binary search directly on the unsorted array","Re-sort the array before each individual search","Sort it once, then use binary search for every lookup"],"correct":3,"explain":"Sorting once buys O(log n) binary searches forever after: about 20 comparisons per lookup instead of up to a million. Binary search on unsorted data is incorrect, and re-sorting per search throws the investment away."},{"id":"fs_z17_1_4","topic":"Search preconditions","text":"Which statement about search preconditions is true?","options":["Neither search works on unsorted data","Linear search needs no precondition, while binary search requires sorted data","Both linear and binary search require sorted data","Binary search needs no precondition, while linear search requires sorted data"],"correct":1,"explain":"Linear search just checks every element in turn, so it works on any traversable data, sorted or not. Binary search's halving logic is only correct when the array is sorted."},{"id":"fs_z17_1_5","topic":"Linear search duplicates","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {5, 3, 5, 1};
System.out.println(linearSearch(a, 5));

where linearSearch is:

static int linearSearch(int[] a, int target) {
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] == target) return i;
  }
  return -1;
}","options":["2","0 and then 2","-1","0"],"correct":3,"explain":"The method returns as soon as the first match is found, so with duplicates linear search reports the FIRST occurrence: index 0. It never reaches the 5 at index 2."},{"id":"zf4612","topic":"","text":"Linear search examines:","options":["the middle first","each element in turn until the end","only the ends","a random sample"],"correct":1,"explain":"The straightforward scan - no preconditions.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4613","topic":"","text":"Linear search on unsorted data:","options":["fails","sorts first","works fine","needs hashing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Order-independence is its virtue; cost is its vice.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4614","topic":"","text":"Worst-case comparisons of linear search over n items:","options":["1","n/2 exactly always","log n","n"],"correct":3,"explain":"The target may be last - or absent entirely.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4615","topic":"","text":"Searching 10,000 items linearly, absent target, makes:","options":["10,000 comparisons","1","100","14"],"correct":0,"explain":"Every element gets checked before concluding not-found.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4616","topic":"","text":"An algorithm, formally, is:","options":["any Java file","finite, unambiguous solving steps","a flowchart only","a loop"],"correct":1,"explain":"The unit’s foundation definition.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4617","topic":"","text":"Two correct algorithms for one problem can differ in:","options":["their answers","their correctness","the work they perform (efficiency)","nothing"],"correct":2,"explain":"Same outputs, wildly different costs - the reason analysis exists.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4618","topic":"","text":"Returning the INDEX from a search (vs true/false) is useful because:","options":["booleans are slow","indexes are smaller","it is not","callers can then USE the found element"],"correct":3,"explain":"Position enables follow-up work; -1 signals absence.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4619","topic":"","text":"Early exit (return at the match) changes linear search’s BEST case to:","options":["1 comparison","n","log n","0"],"correct":0,"explain":"First-element hits end instantly; worst case stays n.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4620","topic":"","text":"The average case for a present, uniformly placed target is about:","options":["n / 2 comparisons","n","1","log n"],"correct":0,"explain":"On average the target sits in the middle of the scan.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4621","topic":"","text":"Choosing search algorithms is a preview of the unit because:","options":["search is rare","search shows efficiency gaps simply","sorting is easier","it is not"],"correct":1,"explain":"Linear vs binary: the same question, orders-of-magnitude apart.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4622","topic":"","text":"A phone contact list searched by scrolling from A is:","options":["binary search","hashing","linear search in daily life","sorting"],"correct":2,"explain":"The algorithms name what we already do.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u17/z17_5":[{"id":"zyf17_5_0","topic":"Stable sort","text":"A stable sort guarantees:","options":["The sort never takes more than O(n log n)","The sort uses no extra memory at all","No two elements ever compare as equal","Equal elements keep their original relative order"],"correct":3,"explain":"It matters when sorting by one field after another."},{"id":"zyf17_5_1","topic":"In place","text":"An in-place sort uses extra space of:","options":["O(log n)","O(n^2)","O(n)","O(1)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Merge sort is not in place."},{"id":"zyf17_5_2","topic":"Comparison lower bound","text":"Any comparison-based sort is at best:","options":["O(log n)","O(1)","O(n)","O(n log n)"],"correct":3,"explain":"A proven bound, which is why faster sorts must avoid comparisons."},{"id":"zyf17_5_3","topic":"Sorting an int array","text":"Arrays.sort on int[] uses:","options":["Bubble sort","Merge sort","TimSort","Dual-pivot quicksort"],"correct":3,"explain":"Stability is irrelevant for primitives. Fast but not stable."},{"id":"zyf17_5_4","topic":"Sorting objects","text":"Arrays.sort on Object[] uses:","options":["Selection sort","Insertion sort only","Quicksort","TimSort"],"correct":3,"explain":"Stability matters once elements carry multiple fields. Is stable."},{"id":"zyf17_5_5","topic":"Custom ordering","text":"Sorting your own class requires:","options":["equals and hashCode overridden on the class","Comparable, or a Comparator supplied at the call","The class to be declared final and immutable","A toString that returns a sortable String"],"correct":1,"explain":"Otherwise ClassCastException at runtime."},{"id":"zyf17_5_6","topic":"Why sorting matters","text":"Sorting is valuable partly because it enables:","options":["Constant-time lookup by key, as a hash table does","Insertion at the front of a list in constant time","Removal of every duplicate without any comparison","Binary search, adjacency of duplicates, and easy merging"],"correct":3,"explain":"It is an enabling step, not just an output format."},{"id":"zyf17_5_7","topic":"Descending order","text":"To sort a List in reverse:","options":["Call reverse() on the list before sorting","Pass Comparator.reverseOrder() to sort","Negate every element before sorting it","Sort normally, then call Collections.shuffle"],"correct":1,"explain":"Or supply a custom comparator."},{"id":"fs_z17_5_1","topic":"Sort cost classes","text":"Which group contains ONLY O(n^2) sorting algorithms?","options":["Quicksort, merge sort, Collections.sort","Selection sort, insertion sort, bubble sort","Merge sort, quicksort, selection sort","Insertion sort, merge sort, bubble sort"],"correct":1,"explain":"The three simple sorts - selection, insertion, bubble - are quadratic. Merge sort and quicksort (on average) are O(n log n), which is why libraries build on them instead."},{"id":"fs_z17_5_2","topic":"Stability in practice","text":"A list of students is sorted by name, then sorted again by grade using a STABLE sort. Afterwards:","options":["Students with equal grades are in random order","The list is in name order only","The second sort has no effect","Students with equal grades are still in name order"],"correct":3,"explain":"A stable sort preserves the relative order of equal elements, so the earlier name ordering survives within each grade group. An unstable sort would scramble it - this two-key scenario is exactly why stability matters."},{"id":"fs_z17_5_3","topic":"Merge sort vs quicksort","text":"Compared with quicksort, merge sort:","options":["Guarantees O(n log n) even in the worst case, but needs O(n) extra memory","Is faster in every case and uses less memory","Has an O(n^2) worst case but sorts in place","Is unstable but needs no extra memory"],"correct":0,"explain":"Merge sort's even splitting means no input is bad, at the price of an O(n) temporary array. Quicksort is the mirror image: in place, but with an O(n^2) worst case. The third option describes quicksort, not merge sort."},{"id":"fs_z17_5_4","topic":"Arrays.sort trace","text":"What does this print?

int[] a = {5, 1, 4, 2};
Arrays.sort(a);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));","options":["[1, 2, 4, 5]","[5, 4, 2, 1]","[5, 1, 4, 2]","It does not compile - int[] cannot be sorted"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arrays.sort sorts the array in place into ascending order, and Arrays.toString shows the result. For primitives Java uses a dual-pivot quicksort under the hood."},{"id":"fs_z17_5_5","topic":"Collections.sort trace","text":"What does this print?

List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Cara", "Abe", "Ben"));
Collections.sort(names);
System.out.println(names);","options":["[Abe, Ben, Cara]","[Cara, Abe, Ben]","[Cara, Ben, Abe]","It does not compile - Strings need a Comparator"],"correct":0,"explain":"Collections.sort orders the list by the elements' natural ordering; String's compareTo is alphabetical. No Comparator is needed because String implements Comparable."},{"id":"fs_z17_5_6","topic":"Reverse-order sort trace","text":"What does this print?

List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(3, 1, 2));
nums.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder());
System.out.println(nums);","options":["[3, 2, 1]","[1, 2, 3]","[2, 1, 3]","[3, 1, 2]"],"correct":0,"explain":"Comparator.reverseOrder() inverts the natural ordering, so the list is sorted descending: 3, 2, 1. Without the comparator, sort would give [1, 2, 3]."},{"id":"zf4655","topic":"","text":"Sorting means:","options":["arranging elements into order","removing duplicates","searching","hashing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Ascending or descending, by the chosen key.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4656","topic":"","text":"Sorting pays off because:","options":["sorted data is smaller","sorted data unlocks fast search","it is required","loops need it"],"correct":1,"explain":"Binary search, ranges, ranked output - all downstream of order.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4657","topic":"","text":"The two classic simple sorts are:","options":["merge and quick","hash and tree","selection sort and insertion sort","linear and binary"],"correct":2,"explain":"The O(n^2) teaching pair, before the fast ones.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4658","topic":"","text":"The two classic FAST sorts are:","options":["selection and insertion","bubble and gnome","linear and binary","merge sort and quicksort"],"correct":3,"explain":"O(n log n) - the practical class.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4659","topic":"","text":"Simple sorts’ O(n^2) means 10x more data takes:","options":["about 100x longer","10x","2x","the same"],"correct":0,"explain":"The quadratic scaling penalty.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4660","topic":"","text":"Sorting an already-sorted array is a case where:","options":["algorithms differ","all sorts are instant","all sorts fail","order breaks"],"correct":0,"explain":"Insertion sort flies on sorted input; selection sort does not care.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4661","topic":"","text":"A sort’s "key" is:","options":["a password","the field/value elements are ordered by","the array index","a hash"],"correct":1,"explain":"Sort people by NAME or by AGE - same data, different keys.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4662","topic":"","text":"Sorting objects in Java leverages:","options":["== chains","field guessing","compareTo (Comparable) or a Comparator","toString"],"correct":2,"explain":"Unit 13’s interfaces power unit 17’s algorithms.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4663","topic":"","text":"A stable sort:","options":["never crashes","is always fast","sorts twice","keeps equal keys in original order"],"correct":3,"explain":"Sort by name then stably by grade: same-grade students stay alphabetical.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4664","topic":"","text":"Sorting cost matters even at n = 100,000 because:","options":["n^2 there is ten billion operations","it does not","memory fails first always","Java forbids it"],"correct":0,"explain":"Simple sorts stop being viable well within real data sizes.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4665","topic":"","text":"The library call for sorting a list is:","options":["list.order()","Collections.sort(list)","sort list","Arrays.list()"],"correct":1,"explain":"Use the tuned built-in in practice; learn the algorithms for understanding.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/u19/z19_4":[{"id":"zyf19_4_0","topic":"First argument","text":"The first command-line argument is:","options":["args.length","args[1]","args[0]","The program name"],"correct":2,"explain":"Unlike C, the program name is not included."},{"id":"zyf19_4_1","topic":"Argument type","text":"Command-line arguments arrive as:","options":["Objects","ints","Strings","chars"],"correct":2,"explain":"Numbers need Integer.parseInt or Double.parseDouble."},{"id":"zyf19_4_2","topic":"Missing arguments","text":"Reading args[0] when none were supplied throws:","options":["Nothing","NullPointerException","ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","NumberFormatException"],"correct":2,"explain":"Check args.length first."},{"id":"zyf19_4_3","topic":"No arguments","text":"Running with no arguments gives args.length of:","options":["-1","1","0","null"],"correct":2,"explain":"The array is empty but not null."},{"id":"zyf19_4_4","topic":"Parsing a number","text":"Converting args[1] to an int uses:","options":["Double.parse","(int) args[1]","Integer.parseInt(args[1])","args[1].toInt()"],"correct":2,"explain":"And it can throw NumberFormatException."},{"id":"zyf19_4_5","topic":"Usage message","text":"When arguments are missing the program should:","options":["Guess","Crash","Print a usage line and exit cleanly","Prompt with Scanner"],"correct":2,"explain":"Far more helpful than a stack trace."},{"id":"zyf19_4_6","topic":"Quoted arguments","text":"An argument containing spaces must be:","options":["Joined with commas, which the shell converts to spaces","Split manually inside main by scanning for capital letters","Rejected, since arguments cannot contain spaces at all","Quoted on the command line so it arrives as one element"],"correct":3,"explain":"The shell does the splitting before Java sees it."},{"id":"zyf19_4_7","topic":"Order","text":"Arguments appear in args:","options":["Randomly","Alphabetically","In the order typed","Reversed"],"correct":2,"explain":"Positional meaning is up to your program."},{"id":"zyf19_4_8","topic":"Where they come from","text":"args is populated by:","options":["The IDE only","Scanner","The JVM, from the command line","A file"],"correct":2,"explain":"IDEs provide a run-configuration field for them."},{"id":"fs_z19_4_1","topic":"args contents","text":"A program is run as:

java Prog alpha 7

