What prints?","options":["58","13","5 8","A NumberFormatException is thrown"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arguments are always Strings, and + on Strings concatenates: "5" + "8" is "58". No exception occurs - the program is quietly wrong, which is why numeric arguments must go through Integer.parseInt."},{"id":"fs_z19_4_4","topic":"Length excludes program name","text":"After running java Prog a b c, the value of args.length is:","options":["2 - the first argument is skipped","0 until the arguments are parsed","3 - the class name is not counted","4 - the class name occupies slot 0"],"correct":2,"explain":"args holds exactly the three arguments a, b, c. Java differs from C here: there is no program-name entry, so args[0] is the first real argument and length counts only the arguments."},{"id":"fs_z19_4_5","topic":"Bad numeric argument","text":"What does this print?

