}
System.out.println(grade);","options":["C","A","B","F"],"correct":0,"explain":"The first true branch wins: 95 >= 70, so grade is 'C' and the later tests never run. An else-if grade chain must be ordered highest threshold first, or every passing score collapses to the lowest grade."},{"id":"fs_z19_7_5","topic":"printf specifier for char","text":"In System.out.printf("Average: %.2f Grade: %c%n", avg, grade); the char variable grade is matched by:","options":["%c","%s","%d","%f"],"correct":0,"explain":"%c is the conversion for a single char; %.2f formats the double average. %d is for integers and %s for Strings - mismatching a specifier with its argument type is a favourite planted error."},{"id":"fs_z19_7_6","topic":"Rounding at the boundary","text":"Cutoffs say "90 and above is an A". A student's average computes to 89.5. Unless the program explicitly rounds BEFORE grading, the grade assigned is:","options":["B - 89.5 fails the avg >= 90 test","A - 89.5 displays as 90 when formatted","A - >= rounds automatically","It depends on the printf format used"],"correct":0,"explain":"The comparison sees the actual value 89.5, which is less than 90 - formatting affects only what is displayed, never what is compared. If the specification wants 89.5 to earn an A, the program must round before the chain."},{"id":"zf4877","topic":"","text":"A weighted grade with exams 60% and homework 40%: exam 90, homework 80 gives:","options":["85.0","88.0","86.0","170.0"],"correct":2,"explain":"90 x 0.6 + 80 x 0.4 = 54 + 32.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4878","topic":"","text":"Weights in such formulas must:","options":["each be 0.5","be integers","be equal","sum to 1.0 (100%)"],"correct":3,"explain":"Otherwise the "average" silently inflates or deflates.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4879","topic":"","text":"Score 86 through the ladder >=90 A, >=80 B, >=70 C maps to:","options":["B","A","C","D"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fails the A gate, passes the B gate - unit 3’s ladder at work.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4880","topic":"","text":"Dropping the lowest of quiz scores {7, 9, 4, 8} leaves an average of:","options":["8.0","7.0","6.0","9.0"],"correct":0,"explain":"Drop the 4; (7+9+8)/3 = 8.0.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4881","topic":"","text":"Finding that lowest quiz uses:","options":["sorting always","the running-minimum scan","a map","recursion"],"correct":1,"explain":"Unit 5’s champion pattern in a gradebook.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4882","topic":"","text":"Reading scores until -1 uses:","options":["a fixed for","do-nothing","a sentinel-controlled while loop","args"],"correct":2,"explain":"The unit-4 sentinel pattern feeding the calculation.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4883","topic":"","text":"Storing per-student scores for later statistics uses:","options":["single variables","strings","nothing","an ArrayList (or array) as fits"],"correct":3,"explain":"Collections carry the data from input phase to analysis phase.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4884","topic":"","text":"An average printed as 86.66666667 wants:","options":["printf with %.2f","casting to int","substring","rounding by eye"],"correct":0,"explain":"Presentation-quality numbers via unit 9 formatting.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4885","topic":"","text":"Validating scores into 0..100 before averaging prevents:","options":["all bugs","typos like 900 wrecking the average","slow input","loops"],"correct":1,"explain":"One guarded read protects every downstream statistic.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4886","topic":"","text":"A curve adding 5 points but capping at 100 is:","options":["score + 5 always","Math.max(score, 5)","Math.min(score + 5, 100)","score * 1.05 always"],"correct":2,"explain":"The cap keeps curved scores in-domain.","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"zf4887","topic":"","text":"The exercise integrates:","options":["GUIs","recursion","generics","five units in one program"],"correct":3,"explain":"A one-screen program touching five units - that is why it is practice.","difficulty":"medium"}],"D286/ch6/s3":[{"id":"q1","topic":"Array init trap","text":"Finding the max of an array: why initialize max = arr[0] instead of max = 0?","options":["An empty array would incorrectly return arr[0]","arr[0] is guaranteed to be the largest element","Starting from 0 runs the loop one time too many","An all-negative array would incorrectly return 0"],"correct":3,"explain":"With max=0 and array {-5,-2,-9}, no element beats 0 → wrong answer 0. Starting from arr[0] is always correct.","__rebal":1},{"id":"q2","topic":"In-place reverse","text":"When reversing an array in place with swaps, the loop must run:","options":["Only to the middle (length / 2)","From the end backward","Twice through the array","Through the entire array"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each iteration swaps a pair (i and length-1-i). Going past the middle swaps everything back to the original.","distractors":{"1":"Direction doesn't fix the double-swap issue.","2":"Twice = original order restored.","3":"Full traversal undoes the work."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"q3","topic":"ArrayList removal","text":"Removing elements from an ArrayList while looping forward causes:","options":["Automatic re-indexing that works fine","Skipped elements","The list to become immutable","A compile error"],"correct":1,"explain":"After remove(i), the next element slides INTO index i, but the loop moves to i+1 — skipping it. Iterate backward (or use removeIf). Indices shift left after each removal.","distractors":{"1":"The shifting is exactly the problem.","2":"Lists stay mutable.","3":"Compiles fine; logic bug."},"__rebal":1},{"id":"q7","topic":"toString","text":"System.out.println(studentObject); prints something useful when:","options":["The object is static","The class overrides toString()","You call .print() instead","The class has a getName() method"],"correct":1,"explain":"println calls toString() automatically. Without an override you get the class name + hash (Student@1a2b3c).","distractors